(function(){var O={};["SQE_CBA", "SQE_BERT", "SQE_MINDMAPS", "SQE_CHECKS"].forEach(function(g){O[g]=window[g];});window.SQE_LATE_O=O;})();
;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["blp"] = [
 {"t":"business-structures","q":"Kwame subscribed for 20,000 ordinary shares of £1 each in Fenwick Joinery Ltd when it was formed, agreeing to pay £1 per share. He has so far paid 25p per share and the company has made no call for the balance. Fenwick Joinery Ltd has now gone into insolvent liquidation owing £90,000 to unsecured creditors. Kwame gave no guarantees.\n\nWhat is the extent of Kwame's personal liability to contribute to the company's assets?","o":["Nothing, because no call was made before the liquidation began.","£20,000, being the full nominal value of his shares.","£15,000, being the amount unpaid on his shares.","£90,000, because the company cannot pay its creditors in full.","Nothing, because a shareholder's liability ends when the company is wound up."],"a":2,"e":"A shareholder's liability is limited to the amount **unpaid** on their shares (75p × 20,000 = £15,000). The liquidator can call for that unpaid amount even though the directors never made a call. Kwame is not liable for the full nominal value (he has already paid £5,000 of it) or for the company's debts beyond the unpaid amount, because the company is a separate person with its own debts."},
 {"t":"business-structures","q":"Imogen is the sole director and shareholder of Larch Interiors Ltd. When the company opened a £60,000 overdraft facility, the bank required Imogen to sign a personal guarantee, which she did. Larch Interiors Ltd has now entered insolvent liquidation with the full £60,000 owing to the bank.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Imogen about the bank's position?","o":["The bank can recover the £60,000 from her personally under the guarantee.","She is protected by limited liability, so the bank can only prove in the liquidation.","The bank can recover from her only if she is found guilty of wrongful trading.","The guarantee is unenforceable because she signed it on the company's behalf.","The bank must first ask the court to pierce the corporate veil before suing her."],"a":0,"e":"Limited liability protects shareholders from the **company's** debts, but a **personal guarantee** is Imogen's own contract with the bank, so she is liable on it in her own right. Wrongful trading and veil piercing are irrelevant because the bank is suing on a separate contract, not trying to make her answer for the company's debt as a shareholder."},
 {"t":"business-structures","q":"Owain transferred his timber business to Owain Timber Ltd in return for all its shares. He is the company's only shareholder and director. The timber stock is owned by the company, but Owain insured it against fire in his own name and has paid the premiums personally. The stock has been destroyed by fire and the insurer refuses to pay Owain.\n\nWhich of the following best explains whether the insurer is entitled to refuse?","o":["No, because as sole shareholder and director Owain is in substance the owner of the stock.","No, because the insurer accepted premiums from Owain knowing he ran the business.","Yes, but only because the company, rather than Owain, should have paid the premiums.","Yes, because the stock belongs to the company, so Owain has no insurable interest in it.","No, because the court will pierce the corporate veil where a company has one shareholder."],"a":3,"e":"A company is a **separate legal person**, so the stock belongs to the company and not to Owain, even though he owns all the shares. A shareholder has no legal or equitable interest in the company's assets, so he had no **insurable interest** (Macaura). Owning and controlling the company is not a ground for piercing the veil, and who paid the premiums does not change who owns the stock."},
 {"t":"business-structures","q":"Femi formed Femi Tiles Ltd and was its majority shareholder and only director. When the company was solvent, he lent it £80,000 secured by a properly registered floating charge over all its assets. Three years later the company went into insolvent liquidation. The unsecured trade creditors argue that Femi cannot rank ahead of them because the company was really just his business.\n\nWhat is the best advice on the creditors' argument?","o":["It will succeed, because a director-shareholder's loan always ranks behind outside creditors.","It will fail, because the company is a separate person and can validly grant security to its own shareholder.","It will succeed, because the court will treat the company as Femi's agent or nominee and will disregard the charge he took over its assets.","It will fail, but only because the creditors were aware that Femi controlled the company.","It will succeed, because a floating charge in favour of a director is void in a liquidation."],"a":1,"e":"Because a company is a **separate legal person**, it can contract with, borrow from and grant security to its own shareholder, who then ranks as a **secured creditor** like anyone else (the principle in Salomon). Control of the company does not make it his agent. A charge to a director can be challenged only under the specific clawback rules (e.g. a floating charge created within 2 years of insolvency), which do not apply to a charge granted three years earlier when the company was solvent."},
 {"t":"business-structures","q":"Harbour Fixings Ltd failed to pay a supplier £35,000 for goods. Callum is the company's sole shareholder and director and makes every decision. The company was formed with share capital of only £100 and has always had very few assets. The supplier wants to sue Callum personally, arguing that the company is merely his creature.\n\nWhat is the best advice to the supplier?","o":["Callum is personally liable because he has total control of the company's decisions.","Callum is personally liable because the company was deliberately undercapitalised.","The court will pierce the veil because the company has only one shareholder.","The supplier can sue Callum because directors are personally liable on company contracts.","Only the company is liable, as control and low capital do not justify piercing the veil."],"a":4,"e":"The court will pierce the corporate veil only in narrow cases, where a person deliberately uses a company to **evade an existing legal obligation** or frustrate its enforcement (Prest). Owning and controlling a company, having one shareholder, or trading with little capital are all lawful and are not enough. The contract was made by the company, so the supplier's claim is against the company only."},
 {"t":"business-structures","q":"Tamsin runs a successful software business through Tamsin Code Ltd, a private limited company. She now wants to raise £3 million to expand by advertising shares in the company for sale to members of the public.\n\nWhat is the best advice on how she can lawfully do this?","o":["The company can offer the shares to the public if a special resolution is passed first.","The company must re-register as a public limited company before shares are offered to the public.","The company can offer the shares to the public if its articles are amended to permit it.","The company can offer the shares to the public, provided each investor buys at least £50,000 of shares.","The company can offer the shares to the public, provided the directors file a statement of capital."],"a":1,"e":"A **private** company is prohibited from offering its securities to the public (CA 2006 s.755). To raise money from the public, the company must **re-register as a plc**, which requires a special resolution, at least £50,000 of allotted share capital (a quarter paid up), two directors and a qualified secretary. A special resolution or change of articles alone does not lift the prohibition."},
 {"t":"business-structures","q":"Rhys is a sole trader whose design business makes taxable profits of about £220,000 a year. He needs to keep about £150,000 of profit in the business each year to fund expansion and lives on the rest. He asks whether incorporating would reduce the tax on the profits he keeps in the business.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["No: a company's profits are taxed at the same income tax rates as a sole trader's, so incorporating makes no difference.","Yes: a company pays no tax at all on profits until they are paid out as dividends.","No: as a sole trader he is taxed only on the profits he draws out of the business, not on retained profits.","Yes: a company pays only National Insurance on its profits, not corporation tax.","Yes: retained profits would bear corporation tax at no more than 25%, rather than income tax at up to 45%."],"a":4,"e":"A sole trader is taxed on **all** the business's profits as they arise, whether or not he takes them out, at income tax rates of up to 45% plus Class 4 NICs. A company pays **corporation tax** on its profits (19% to 25%), and the owner pays income tax only on what is extracted as salary or dividends. So for a high earner who reinvests profits, a company often saves tax on retained profits. A company does pay tax on undistributed profits, and a sole trader is not taxed only on drawings."},
 {"t":"business-structures","q":"Gemma has run a florist's shop for five years under the name \"Bloom & Petal\". She has never formed a company or registered anything at Companies House, and she employs two assistants. The business owes a wholesaler £28,000 that it cannot pay. Gemma owns her own home.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Gemma?","o":["Only the business's assets can be used to pay the debt, because it trades under a separate name.","She is liable only up to the value of the shop's stock and equipment.","She is personally liable for the whole debt and her home and other personal assets are at risk.","She is not personally liable because employing staff gives the business separate legal personality.","She is liable only if she signed a personal guarantee for the wholesaler's account."],"a":2,"e":"A sole trader has **no separate legal personality**: the business and its debts are Gemma's own, so she has **unlimited personal liability** and her personal assets, including her home, are at risk; the creditor could petition for her bankruptcy (the debt exceeds £5,000). Trading under a business name and employing staff do not create a separate legal person or limit her liability, and no guarantee is needed because the debt is already hers."},
 {"t":"business-structures","q":"Aled and Bea, two accountants, want to run a practice together. They want protection from personal liability for the business's debts. They are also keen that details of the business's finances are not made publicly available, and they are willing to accept unlimited liability if that is the only way to keep their finances private.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the choice they face?","o":["An LLP gives limited liability but must file public accounts; a partnership is private but liability is unlimited.","An LLP would give them limited liability and, like a general partnership, need not file any accounts publicly.","A general partnership would give them limited liability if they register it at Companies House.","A private limited company would give them limited liability and need not file accounts if it has only two shareholders.","A general partnership and an LLP both give limited liability, but only an LLP must file accounts."],"a":0,"e":"An **LLP** is a separate legal person whose members have **limited liability**, but the price is publicity: it must register at Companies House and **file accounts** each year, which are public. A **general partnership** has no filing requirements, so its finances stay private, but the partners have **unlimited** joint liability for its debts. A company gives limited liability but must also file accounts, whatever its number of shareholders."},
 {"t":"business-structures","q":"Lowri was the sole shareholder and director of Aerial Crop Ltd. She was also employed by the company, under a contract she signed on its behalf, as its chief pilot. She was killed in a flying accident while working. The company's employer's liability insurer refuses to pay compensation to her family, arguing that she could not be an employee of a company she owned and controlled.\n\nIs the insurer's argument correct?","o":["Yes, because a person cannot enter into a contract with themselves.","Yes, because a sole director cannot sign a contract on behalf of the company in their own favour.","No, but only because the shareholders approved her service contract by special resolution.","No, because the company is a separate legal person that can validly employ its own controlling shareholder.","Yes, because a controlling shareholder is treated as self-employed for all purposes."],"a":3,"e":"Because the company is a **separate legal person**, the contract of employment was between two different persons, the company and Lowri, even though she signed for the company (Lee v Lee's Air Farming). She could therefore be the company's employee, and the insurer must pay. No special resolution is needed to make such a contract valid."},
 {"t":"formation","q":"Sunil has agreed to sign a major supply contract tomorrow morning and the customer insists on contracting with a limited company. He has not yet formed a company. Same-day incorporation is not possible today.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Sunil?","o":["Buy an existing off-the-shelf company, then change its name, articles and officers as needed.","Sign the contract tomorrow in the name of the company to be formed, which will automatically adopt it on incorporation.","Sign the contract tomorrow as agent of the company, so that he incurs no personal liability.","File an incorporation application today, which allows the company to contract as soon as it is filed.","Ask the customer to sign with him as a sole trader, and transfer the contract to a company later without consent."],"a":0,"e":"An **off-the-shelf company** already exists, so it can sign the contract immediately; Sunil can then change its name, articles, directors and shareholders. A contract made for an unformed company binds the person who signs it personally (s.51), and the company cannot ratify or automatically adopt it. A company exists only from the date on its certificate of incorporation, not when the application is filed, and a contract cannot be transferred to a company without the other party's consent."},
 {"t":"formation","q":"Priya is forming Lantern Studio Ltd. To keep her address private, she wants to use a PO box as the company's registered office and to give no email address to Companies House, as she does not want unwanted contact.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["She can use a PO box, but must also give a registered email address, which will appear on the public register.","She can use a PO box and need not give an email address, as both are optional for private companies.","She cannot use a PO box, but giving an email address is optional unless the company has more than one director.","She cannot use a PO box, and she must give a registered email address, which is not made public.","She can use a PO box if she also files a statement of lawful purpose."],"a":3,"e":"Since 4 March 2024 (ECCTA 2023), every company must have an **appropriate registered office address**, where documents will come to the attention of someone acting for the company and delivery can be acknowledged; a **PO box is not appropriate**. Every company must also give Companies House a **registered email address**, which is used for contact and is **not** published. Priya could instead use a service address such as her accountant's or solicitor's office, if it meets the test."},
 {"t":"formation","q":"In December 2026, the board of Tidewell Ltd appoints Grace as an additional director. Grace has not verified her identity with Companies House, but she starts attending board meetings and signs a contract on the company's behalf.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Her appointment is void, so the contract she signed does not bind the company.","Acting without verification is an offence, but the contract she signed is not invalid for that reason.","Identity verification applies only to directors appointed before 18 November 2025, so there is no problem.","She has 12 months from her appointment in which to verify her identity, and may act freely in the meantime.","Only the company commits an offence; Grace has no personal liability for acting without verification."],"a":1,"e":"Since 18 November 2025, every **new director** must have **verified their identity** with Companies House (directly or through an authorised corporate service provider), and form **AP01** must confirm it. Acting as a director without verified identity is an **offence** by both the director and the company, but it **does not invalidate** the director's acts, so the contract stands. The 12-month transition applied only to existing directors and ended on 17 November 2026."},
 {"t":"formation","q":"Halden Ltd was incorporated in 2015. Its new company secretary asks, in December 2026, which of the following statutory registers the company itself must now keep and update: the register of members, the register of directors, the register of directors' residential addresses and the register of people with significant control (PSCs).\n\nWhat is the correct advice?","o":["All four registers, as they must be available for inspection at the registered office.","Only the registers of directors and PSCs; the register of members is now kept by Companies House.","None of them, as all company registers have been moved to Companies House.","Only the register of PSCs, because of its importance in preventing money laundering.","Only the register of members; director and PSC details are held only at Companies House."],"a":4,"e":"Under ECCTA 2023, since **18 November 2025** companies no longer keep their own registers of **directors**, directors' residential addresses, secretaries and **PSCs**: that information is held only on the Companies House register. But every company must still keep its own **register of members**, and since 26 January 2026 a private company can no longer opt to keep it at Companies House instead."},
 {"t":"formation","q":"Dev and Ruth incorporated Quarry Lane Ltd online last month. They did not register any articles of association of their own and did not choose any articles from a menu; they simply completed the application.\n\nWhat articles govern the company?","o":["The company has no articles until the shareholders adopt some by special resolution.","The Model Articles for private companies limited by shares apply in full by default.","The directors may decide which provisions of the Model Articles apply.","Only the Model Articles on directors' powers apply; the rest must be adopted by ordinary resolution.","The memorandum of association acts as the company's articles."],"a":1,"e":"If a company limited by shares does not register its own articles, the relevant **Model Articles** apply automatically **in full** (CA 2006 s.20). The same is true of any parts of the Model Articles that registered articles do not exclude or modify. The memorandum is now only a short statement that the subscribers wish to form a company; it is not the constitution."},
 {"t":"formation","q":"Cycleway Ltd's articles restrict its objects to \"the manufacture and sale of bicycles\". The directors sign a contract on the company's behalf to buy a fleet of delivery vans for a new van-hire business. The seller did not read the articles. The company now wants to escape the contract.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["The contract is void, because the company has no capacity to act outside its objects.","The contract is voidable at the company's option, because the seller is deemed to know the contents of the articles.","The company is bound only if the shareholders ratify the contract by special resolution.","The contract is void unless the seller can prove it acted in good faith and the directors had actual authority.","The company is bound, because its capacity is not limited by its articles, but the directors may be in breach."],"a":4,"e":"A company's capacity is **not limited** by anything in its constitution: the validity of its acts cannot be questioned on the ground of a restriction in its objects (CA 2006 s.39), and there is no deemed notice of the articles. The company is therefore bound. But the directors must act within the company's constitution (s.171), so by acting outside the objects they may be liable to the company for breach of duty."},
 {"t":"formation","q":"The articles of Mapleton Ltd state that \"Hannah Okafor shall be the company's accountant for as long as she wishes\". Hannah is also a shareholder. The board has now appointed a different accountant. Hannah wants to rely on the articles to keep her appointment.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Hannah?","o":["She will succeed, because the articles are a binding contract between the company and each of its members.","She will succeed, because the articles can be changed only by special resolution, and none was passed.","She is unlikely to succeed, because the articles are enforceable only for rights held as a member.","She will succeed, provided she holds at least 25% of the voting shares.","She is unlikely to succeed, because the articles bind only the directors, not the company."],"a":2,"e":"The articles form a statutory contract between the company and its members (CA 2006 s.33), but only in respect of rights and obligations held **as members**, such as voting and dividend rights. A right to be the company's accountant is an **outsider right**, held in a different capacity, so Hannah cannot enforce it through the articles, even though she is a shareholder (Eley). She would need a separate contract with the company."},
 {"t":"formation","q":"The articles of Pembrook Ltd contain a provision that the clause giving Sion the right to appoint one director \"may be amended only with the consent of all the members\". The company's other members, who hold 85% of the shares, want to delete the clause and pass a special resolution to do so. Sion votes against.\n\nWhat is the effect of the special resolution?","o":["It does not remove the clause, because the entrenched condition of unanimity is not met.","It removes the clause, because a special resolution passed by 75% can always change the articles.","It removes the clause, but Sion is entitled to damages for loss of his right.","It removes the clause, because entrenchment is permitted only in a public company.","It is invalid only if Sion obtains a court order within 21 days."],"a":0,"e":"A company's articles may contain **provisions for entrenchment**: specified provisions may be amended only if conditions more restrictive than a special resolution, such as unanimity, are met (CA 2006 s.22). The condition here (consent of all members) is not satisfied because Sion objects, so the 85% special resolution cannot remove the clause. Entrenchment is available to private companies, and the provision binds without any court application."},
 {"t":"formation","q":"Larkfield Ltd, a private company, has one director and an issued share capital of 20,000 £1 ordinary shares, all fully paid. It has no company secretary. It wants to re-register as a public limited company.\n\nWhich of the following best states what it must do?","o":["Pass an ordinary resolution and increase its allotted share capital to at least £50,000, fully paid up.","Pass a special resolution and appoint a company secretary; a plc may have only one director.","Pass a special resolution only; the share capital and officer requirements apply only to companies formed as plcs.","Pass a special resolution, raise allotted capital to £50,000 (a quarter paid up), and appoint a second director and a secretary.","Pass a special resolution, appoint a second director and obtain a trading certificate; its existing capital is enough."],"a":3,"e":"To re-register as a plc, a private company needs a **special resolution**, allotted share capital of at least **£50,000** nominal, with each share paid up to at least **one quarter** of its nominal value plus the whole of any premium, at least **two directors** and a **qualified company secretary** (CA 2006 ss.90-91, 154, 271). Larkfield's £20,000 is too little, and it has only one director and no secretary. A trading certificate is needed only by a company formed as a plc from the start."},
 {"t":"formation","q":"Brightwater plc was incorporated as a public company last week. Before obtaining a trading certificate from Companies House, its directors signed a contract on its behalf to buy equipment for £120,000. The company has still not applied for the certificate.\n\nWhat is the legal position?","o":["The contract is void because a plc cannot do business before obtaining a trading certificate.","The contract is voidable at the seller's option until the trading certificate is obtained.","The contract is valid, but there is an offence and the directors may have to indemnify the seller for loss.","The contract is valid and there are no consequences, because a trading certificate is an administrative formality.","The contract binds the directors personally, but not the company, as for a pre-incorporation contract."],"a":2,"e":"A company formed as a plc must not do business or borrow until it has a **trading certificate** (CA 2006 s.761). Breach is an offence by the company and its officers in default, but the transaction remains **valid** (s.767). If the company fails to comply with its obligations within **21 days** of being called on to do so, the directors are **jointly and severally liable** to indemnify the other party for any loss. This is not a pre-incorporation contract, because the company already exists."},
 {"t":"directors","q":"Three of the four shareholders of Ash Ltd, who together hold 80% of the shares, want to remove Jonah as a director as quickly as possible. Jonah holds the other 20% and has no weighted voting rights. The shareholders propose to circulate a written resolution to remove him, to be signed by all three of them.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["The written resolution will be effective if signed by holders of at least 75% of the voting rights.","The written resolution will be effective if signed by holders of more than 50% of the voting rights.","The written resolution will be effective only if Jonah also signs it.","He cannot be removed by written resolution; an ordinary resolution at a meeting, on special notice, is needed.","A director can be removed only by special resolution, which may be passed as a written resolution."],"a":3,"e":"A director may be removed by **ordinary resolution at a meeting** (CA 2006 s.168), but **special notice** (28 days) must be given to the company and the written resolution procedure **cannot** be used (s.288(2)). This protects the director's right to make written representations and to be heard at the meeting (s.169). A special resolution is not required, and 80% would be enough for an ordinary resolution passed at a meeting."},
 {"t":"directors","q":"Tegan is a director of Ruskin Ltd under a service contract with a fixed term that has three years left to run. The shareholders validly pass an ordinary resolution at a general meeting to remove her as a director. The service contract makes her employment conditional on her remaining a director.\n\nWhat is the effect of the resolution?","o":["She remains a director until her service contract expires, because the contract overrides the resolution.","She ceases to be a director, but she may claim damages from the company for breach of her service contract.","She ceases to be a director and has no claim, because removal under statute cannot be a breach of contract.","The resolution is invalid unless the company first pays her the full value of the remaining term.","She ceases to be a director only if the shareholders also pass a special resolution terminating her contract."],"a":1,"e":"Removal under s.168 is effective **despite anything in any agreement** between the company and the director. But s.168(5) preserves the director's right to **compensation or damages** for termination of their contract, so Tegan may sue for breach of her service contract (subject to her duty to mitigate her loss). Payment is not a condition of the removal being valid."},
 {"t":"directors","q":"Holly holds 55% of the shares in Brackley Ltd and has told the board that she intends to call a meeting to remove two of the directors. To prevent this, the board allots new shares to a friendly investor, which reduces Holly to 40%. The directors honestly believe that keeping the current board in office is best for the company.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the directors' position?","o":["They are not in breach, because they honestly believed their action would promote the success of the company.","They are not in breach, because directors have an unrestricted power to allot shares under the articles.","They are in breach only of their duty to exercise reasonable care, skill and diligence.","They are not in breach unless the allotment was at an undervalue.","They are in breach of their duty to use powers only for their proper purpose."],"a":4,"e":"Directors must exercise their powers only for the **purposes for which they are conferred** (CA 2006 s.171(b)). The power to allot shares exists to raise capital, not to manipulate control or defeat a majority shareholder (Howard Smith v Ampol). An improper purpose is a breach **even if** the directors honestly believe it is in the company's interests, so their good faith under s.172 is no defence."},
 {"t":"directors","q":"Marcus, the purchasing director of Kerne Foods Ltd, accepts a free two-week luxury holiday from a packaging supplier. The supplier hopes that Marcus will favour it when the company next renews its packaging contract. The other directors know about the holiday and have passed a board resolution approving it.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["Marcus is not in breach, because the board has authorised the benefit.","Marcus is in breach of his duty not to accept benefits from third parties; the board cannot authorise it.","Marcus is not in breach unless the company actually awards the contract to the supplier.","Marcus is in breach only of his duty to declare an interest in a proposed transaction.","Marcus is not in breach, because the holiday was given to him personally and not to the company."],"a":1,"e":"A director must not accept a benefit from a third party conferred because of his being a director, unless it cannot reasonably be regarded as likely to give rise to a conflict of interest (CA 2006 s.176). A holiday given to influence a contract plainly risks a conflict. Unlike a s.175 conflict, a s.176 benefit **cannot be authorised by the board**; only the members can approve or ratify it. No contract need be awarded for the breach to occur."},
 {"t":"directors","q":"Ffion is one of three directors of Cobalt Print Ltd. The company proposes to buy paper from Glendale Paper Ltd, of which Ffion's husband is managing director. Ffion has not made any declaration, but at the last board meeting the other two directors discussed her husband's role at Glendale with her, and minuted that they knew of it.\n\nMust Ffion declare her interest before the company enters the contract?","o":["Yes, she must declare it at a board meeting, as a written notice cannot be used.","Yes, because a director must always declare any interest of a spouse in a proposed transaction.","No, because an interest held through a spouse can never be a director's interest.","Yes, and she must also obtain approval of the transaction by ordinary resolution of the shareholders.","No, because the other directors are already aware of her interest."],"a":4,"e":"A director who is directly or indirectly interested in a proposed transaction must declare the nature and extent of the interest (CA 2006 s.177), and an interest through a spouse can be an indirect interest. But no declaration is needed if, or to the extent that, the other directors are **already aware** of it (s.177(6)(b)). Here they are, as the minutes show. Shareholder approval is not required for an ordinary supply contract of this kind."},
 {"t":"directors","q":"Nadim is a director of Aspen Robotics Ltd, a private company with the Model Articles. A customer offered the company a small contract, but the board decided not to take it. Nadim now wants to take the contract personally. The other two directors are happy for him to do so.\n\nHow can Nadim avoid breaching his duty to avoid conflicts of interest?","o":["The conflict can be authorised only by a special resolution of the shareholders.","No authorisation is needed, because the company has rejected the opportunity.","The other two directors can authorise it, with Nadim not counting or voting.","Nadim can authorise the conflict himself by declaring his interest to the board.","The conflict can never be authorised, because it involves the exploitation of a corporate opportunity."],"a":2,"e":"Under CA 2006 s.175, a director must avoid situations where his interests conflict with the company's, and it is **irrelevant** whether the company could or would take the opportunity itself. In a **private company**, the conflict can be authorised by the **independent directors**, unless the constitution prevents it (s.175(5)(a)), and the authorisation is effective only if the interested director is not counted in the quorum and does not vote (s.175(6)). Shareholders could also authorise it by ordinary resolution, but a special resolution is not required."},
 {"t":"directors","q":"Victor owns 70% of the shares in Pellow Ltd but has never been appointed a director. The two appointed directors routinely do whatever he tells them to do about the company's business, including which contracts to sign. Pellow Ltd has entered insolvent liquidation and the liquidator wants to make Victor contribute to its assets.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Victor's position?","o":["He is a shadow director, so the general duties can apply to him, as can wrongful trading.","He cannot be liable as a director, because he was never validly appointed.","He is liable only as a shareholder, up to any amount unpaid on his shares.","He is a shadow director only if he received a salary from the company.","He is a de facto director, and so owes no duties until he is formally appointed."],"a":0,"e":"A **shadow director** is a person in accordance with whose directions or instructions the directors are **accustomed to act** (CA 2006 s.251). The general duties apply to a shadow director where and to the extent that they are capable of applying (s.170(5)), and shadow directors can be liable for **wrongful trading** (IA 1986 s.214(7)) and be disqualified. Formal appointment and payment are not required."},
 {"t":"directors","q":"Gwilym was disqualified from acting as a director for six years after a previous company's insolvent liquidation. Two years later, without leave of the court, he became involved in the management of Ironside Trading Ltd, making all its key decisions. The company incurred debts of £70,000 while he was involved and has now gone into insolvent liquidation.\n\nWhat is Gwilym's position?","o":["He commits a criminal offence, but has no personal liability because the company is a separate legal person.","He is personally liable only if he was formally appointed as a director of Ironside Trading Ltd.","He has no liability, because his disqualification applied only to the company that previously failed.","He commits an offence and is personally liable for debts incurred while he was involved.","He is personally liable only for debts incurred after the liquidator was appointed."],"a":3,"e":"A disqualification order bans the person from being a director or being concerned or taking part in the **management of any company** without leave of the court, not just the failed company. Breach is a **criminal offence** (CDDA 1986 s.13), and a person who is involved in management in breach is **personally liable**, jointly and severally with the company, for the company's debts incurred while he was so involved (s.15). Formal appointment is not required."},
 {"t":"directors","q":"The articles of Westfield Supplies Ltd provide that any contract worth over £50,000 must be approved by the board. Ingrid, the managing director, signs an £80,000 contract with a supplier on the company's behalf without board approval. The supplier did not know of the restriction and acted in good faith. The board now wants to reject the contract.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["The company is not bound, because the supplier is deemed to know the contents of the articles.","The company is not bound, because Ingrid acted outside her actual authority.","The company is bound, because Ingrid had apparent authority and constitutional limits do not affect the supplier.","The company is bound only if the shareholders ratify the contract by special resolution.","The company is not bound unless the supplier checked the articles at Companies House before contracting."],"a":2,"e":"A managing director has **apparent (ostensible) authority** to make contracts of the kind an MD usually makes, and the supplier was entitled to rely on it. A person dealing with a company in **good faith** is not affected by limitations on the directors' powers in the constitution, and is not bound to enquire about them (CA 2006 s.40). There is no deemed notice of the articles. Ingrid may, however, be liable to the company for exceeding her authority (s.171)."},
 {"t":"directors","q":"Sophie is a director of Haverly Ltd and holds 60% of its shares. She negligently approved a transaction that cost the company £45,000. A general meeting is held to ratify her breach by ordinary resolution. Sophie votes her 60% in favour, and the holders of the other 40% all vote against.\n\nIs the breach ratified?","o":["No, because Sophie's own votes must be disregarded on a resolution to ratify her breach.","Yes, because an ordinary resolution needs only a simple majority of the votes cast.","Yes, because negligence can be ratified by any resolution of the shareholders.","No, because a breach of duty can be ratified only by special resolution.","No, because a breach of the duty of care and skill can never be ratified."],"a":0,"e":"A director's negligence or breach of duty can be ratified by **ordinary resolution** of the members (CA 2006 s.239), but on that resolution the votes of the **director** (as a member) and any member **connected** with them are **disregarded**. Without Sophie's votes, only votes against were cast, so the resolution fails. A special resolution is not needed, and negligence is capable of ratification."},
 {"t":"decision-making","q":"The board of Corran Ltd, a private company with the Model Articles, wants to hold a general meeting as soon as possible. Notice of the meeting will be sent to all members by email on Monday 1 March 2027. The members will not agree to short notice, and the articles say nothing about deemed delivery.\n\nWhat is the earliest date on which the general meeting can be held?","o":["Monday 15 March 2027.","Thursday 18 March 2027.","Tuesday 16 March 2027.","Wednesday 17 March 2027.","Monday 22 March 2027."],"a":1,"e":"A private company general meeting needs **14 clear days'** notice (CA 2006 s.307), excluding the day the notice is given and the day of the meeting (s.360). An email is deemed received **48 hours** after it is sent, ignoring non-working days (s.1147), so on Wednesday 3 March. The 14 clear days run from 4 March to 17 March, so the earliest meeting date is **18 March**. The other dates either ignore the 48-hour rule or fail to exclude both days."},
 {"t":"decision-making","q":"Tarn Ltd, which has the Model Articles unamended, has four directors. At a properly convened board meeting attended by all four, two directors vote in favour of a proposal to open a new warehouse and two vote against. The chair of the board, who is not conflicted, voted in favour and now wishes to use a casting vote.\n\nWhat is the outcome?","o":["The resolution fails, because a board resolution requires the support of at least three of the four directors.","The resolution fails, because the chair has already used their vote.","The decision must be referred to the shareholders, because the board is deadlocked.","The resolution is passed only if the company secretary also votes in favour.","The resolution is passed, because the chair has a casting vote where the votes are equal."],"a":4,"e":"Under the Model Articles for private companies (art.13), if the numbers of votes for and against a proposal at a directors' meeting are **equal**, the **chair has a casting vote**, in addition to their ordinary vote, unless the chair is not counted for quorum or voting purposes on that matter. The chair here is not conflicted, so the casting vote breaks the deadlock and the resolution is passed."},
 {"t":"decision-making","q":"Ardent Ltd has three shareholders: Hamid (45%), Iona (10%) and Jake (45%). On 1 February 2027 the directors circulate a written ordinary resolution with no lapse period stated in the articles. Hamid signs and returns it on 5 February. Iona signs and returns it on 3 March. Jake does not respond.\n\nWhat is the status of the resolution?","o":["It was passed on 3 March, when holders of more than 50% of the votes had agreed.","It has not been passed, because it lapsed before Iona agreed, leaving only 45% in time.","It was passed on 5 February, because Hamid holds the largest shareholding.","It has not been passed, because a written ordinary resolution needs the agreement of all eligible members.","It was passed on 3 March, because a written resolution never lapses unless the articles say so."],"a":1,"e":"A written ordinary resolution is passed when members holding a **simple majority of the total voting rights of eligible members** have signified agreement (CA 2006 s.296). If the articles specify no period, it **lapses** at the end of **28 days** beginning with the circulation date (s.297), so on 28 February. Iona's agreement came too late, leaving only Hamid's 45%, so the resolution has failed."},
 {"t":"decision-making","q":"Sabine holds 7% of the paid-up voting shares in Ellwood Ltd. She is concerned about the directors' management of the company and wants a general meeting held to consider a resolution to remove the managing director. The board refuses to call one.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Sabine?","o":["She cannot require a meeting, because only holders of at least 10% of the votes can do so.","She can call the meeting herself immediately, without first asking the directors.","She can require a meeting only if she obtains the agreement of a majority in number of the members.","She can require a meeting, but the directors may hold it at any time within the next 12 months.","She can require the directors to call a meeting, which they must call within 21 days."],"a":4,"e":"Members holding at least **5%** of the paid-up voting capital can require the directors to call a general meeting (CA 2006 s.303). The directors must call it within **21 days** of the request, for a date not more than **28 days** after the notice convening it (s.304). If they fail to do so, the requesting members may call it themselves at the company's expense (s.305). Here, removing a director would also require special notice."},
 {"t":"decision-making","q":"Penhallow Ltd has the Model Articles. At a general meeting attended by three members, an ordinary resolution is put to a show of hands. Kieran, who holds 60% of the voting shares, votes in favour; the other two members, holding 25% and 15%, vote against. The chair declares the resolution lost. Kieran immediately demands a poll.\n\nWhat is the outcome?","o":["No poll can be held, because the result of a show of hands is final once declared.","A poll can be demanded only by the chair, so the resolution remains lost.","A poll must be held on a 10% holder's demand, and the resolution will pass on the poll.","A poll can be held only if at least two members demand it, so the resolution remains lost.","A poll must be held, but the resolution will need 75% of the votes cast to pass."],"a":2,"e":"On a **show of hands** each member has one vote, so Kieran was outvoted two to one. Under the Model Articles (art.44) a **poll** may be demanded by the chair, the directors, two or more persons entitled to vote, or a person or persons representing at least **10%** of the voting rights. On a poll each share carries a vote, so Kieran's 60% passes the ordinary resolution, which needs only a simple majority."},
 {"t":"decision-making","q":"The board of Linden Ltd proposes to give its finance director, Ravi, a new service contract for a fixed term of three years, which the company cannot terminate by notice during that time.\n\nWhat approval is required?","o":["An ordinary resolution of the members, passed before the contract is entered into.","A special resolution of the members, because the term exceeds two years.","A board resolution only, with Ravi not voting.","No approval, because service contracts are a matter for the directors under the Model Articles.","An ordinary resolution of the members, which may be passed within six months after the contract is signed."],"a":0,"e":"A director's service contract with a **guaranteed term of more than two years** (one the company cannot terminate by notice, or can only in specified circumstances) needs **prior approval by ordinary resolution** of the members (CA 2006 s.188). A memorandum of the proposed terms must be available to members (on a written resolution, sent with it; at a meeting, available for 15 days before and at the meeting). Without approval the term is void and the contract is terminable on reasonable notice."},
 {"t":"decision-making","q":"The board of Ashby Ltd agrees to lend £25,000 to one of its directors, Bethany, to help her renovate her home. The company is not a money-lending company and its members have not been asked to approve the loan.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["No approval is needed, because loans of up to £50,000 are exempt.","No approval is needed, because the board can authorise loans to directors under the Model Articles.","The loan needs approval by special resolution, without which it is void.","It needs prior approval by ordinary resolution; without it, the loan is voidable by the company.","The loan is lawful without approval, provided Bethany pays interest at a commercial rate."],"a":3,"e":"A company may not make a loan to a director unless it has been approved by **ordinary resolution** of the members (CA 2006 s.197). The exemptions do not apply: £25,000 exceeds the £10,000 limit for **minor loans** (s.207), and the £50,000 exemption (s.204) covers only expenditure on **company business**, not a home renovation. Without approval the loan is **voidable**, and the director must account for gains and indemnify losses (s.213). Charging interest does not remove the need for approval."},
 {"t":"decision-making","q":"On 2 June the shareholders of Morven Ltd passed a special resolution adopting new articles of association. The company secretary asks what must now be sent to Companies House.\n\nWhat is the correct advice?","o":["A copy of the special resolution only, within 28 days of the resolution.","A copy of the new articles only, within 14 days of the resolution.","A copy of the special resolution and a copy of the new articles, each within 15 days of the resolution.","Nothing until the next confirmation statement, which must record the change.","A copy of the special resolution and a copy of the new articles, both within one month after the date on which the resolution was passed."],"a":2,"e":"A copy of every **special resolution** must be delivered to Companies House within **15 days** after it is passed (CA 2006 ss.29-30). Where articles are amended, a copy of the **amended articles** must also be sent within **15 days** after the amendment takes effect (s.26). Waiting until the next confirmation statement would be a breach, and the 14-day and 28-day periods relate to other filings."},
 {"t":"decision-making","q":"Ottery Ltd proposes to buy a commercial unit worth £180,000 from Neil, one of its directors. Neil also holds 70% of the company's shares. An ordinary resolution approving the purchase is put to a general meeting before the contract is signed. Neil votes all his shares in favour; the other shareholders vote against.\n\nIs the purchase validly approved?","o":["Yes, because Neil may vote his shares on a resolution to approve the transaction.","No, because Neil's votes must be disregarded as he is the director involved.","No, because a substantial property transaction requires a special resolution.","No, because the approval must be given by written resolution, not at a meeting.","Yes, but only because the asset is worth less than 10% of the company's net assets."],"a":0,"e":"Buying a non-cash asset worth over **£100,000** from a director is a **substantial property transaction** needing prior approval by **ordinary resolution** (CA 2006 s.190). Unlike a resolution to ratify a director's breach (s.239) or a buyback (s.695), there is **no rule disregarding the interested director's votes** as a shareholder, so Neil's 70% carries the resolution. As the asset exceeds £100,000, the 10% test is irrelevant."},
 {"t":"decision-making","q":"Dafydd is leaving the board of Tresco Ltd. His service contract entitles him to six months' pay in lieu of notice, which is £40,000. The board wants to pay him £70,000 in total, describing the extra £30,000 as \"a thank-you for long service\". The members have passed no resolution.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["The whole £70,000 needs approval by ordinary resolution, because it is paid on leaving office.","The whole £70,000 can be paid without approval, because the board has power to fix directors' remuneration.","The extra £30,000 needs approval by special resolution.","Only the extra £30,000 needs approval, by ordinary resolution of the members.","The whole £70,000 can be paid without approval, because it is less than £100,000."],"a":3,"e":"A **payment for loss of office** to a director needs approval by **ordinary resolution** of the members (CA 2006 s.217). But a payment made in good faith to discharge an **existing legal obligation**, such as contractual pay in lieu of notice, is exempt (s.220), so the £40,000 needs no approval. The extra £30,000 is gratuitous and does need approval; the small-payment exemption covers only £200."},
 {"t":"shareholders-minority","q":"Clara holds 30% of the voting shares in Briarwood Ltd. The other 70% are held by a single shareholder who is in dispute with her. There are no special voting rights in the articles and no shareholders' agreement.\n\nWhich of the following can Clara prevent on her own?","o":["The removal of a director from office.","The appointment of a new director.","The declaration of a final dividend recommended by the directors.","The grant to the directors of authority to allot shares.","A change to the company's articles of association."],"a":4,"e":"Holding **more than 25%** of the votes lets a member **block special resolutions**, which need 75% of the votes, such as a change to the articles (s.21). Removing or appointing a director, declaring a dividend and giving s.551 authority to allot all need only an **ordinary resolution** (more than 50%), which the 70% holder can pass alone."},
 {"t":"shareholders-minority","q":"Ewan owns 20% of the shares in Hollin Ltd. A director, Petra, dishonestly diverted £400,000 of company funds to herself. As a result, the value of Ewan's shares has fallen by £80,000. Ewan wants to sue Petra personally for £80,000.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Ewan?","o":["He can recover £80,000 personally, because Petra's breach directly reduced the value of his shares.","He cannot recover the fall in value personally; the claim is the company's, pursued by a derivative claim.","He can recover £80,000 personally, but only if the company has decided not to sue Petra.","He cannot sue at all, because directors owe their duties to the company, which alone decides whether to sue.","He can recover £80,000 personally by an unfair prejudice petition seeking damages against Petra."],"a":1,"e":"Directors owe their duties to the **company** (CA 2006 s.170), so the proper claimant for the £400,000 is the company. A shareholder cannot recover a fall in share value that merely **reflects the company's loss** (the reflective loss rule). Ewan's route is a **derivative claim** on the company's behalf, which needs the court's permission (ss.260-264); any recovery goes to the company, restoring the value of his shares."},
 {"t":"shareholders-minority","q":"Mira holds 15% of the shares in Dunmore Ltd as a passive investor; she has never been a director or involved in management, and there was no understanding that she would be. The board has lawfully decided, after taking professional advice, to open a new branch, which Mira considers commercially unwise. She asks whether she can petition on the ground of unfair prejudice.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["A petition is likely to succeed, because any decision that may reduce the value of her shares is unfairly prejudicial.","A petition is likely to succeed, because a minority shareholder has a right to be consulted on major business decisions.","A petition is likely to fail, because only members holding at least 25% of the issued shares are entitled to present an unfair prejudice petition.","A petition is likely to succeed, and the court will usually order the majority to buy her shares.","A petition is likely to fail, because a lawful commercial decision that a shareholder disagrees with is not unfairly prejudicial conduct."],"a":4,"e":"A member may petition if the company's affairs are conducted in a manner **unfairly prejudicial** to members' interests (CA 2006 s.994). The courts will not interfere with **bona fide commercial decisions** taken lawfully by the directors, and a passive investor has no legitimate expectation of a say in management. There is no minimum shareholding for a petition, but on these facts there is no unfair prejudice."},
 {"t":"shareholders-minority","q":"Seren and Tomos each own 50% of Mostyn Ltd, which they set up on the basis that both would manage the business. They have fallen out and are deadlocked. Tomos has offered in writing to buy Seren's shares at a fair value fixed by an independent valuer, with no discount for her minority stake. Seren rejects the offer and petitions for the company to be wound up on the just and equitable ground.\n\nWhat is the most likely outcome?","o":["The court will order winding up, because deadlock between equal shareholders always justifies it.","The court will order winding up, because a quasi-partnership must be dissolved once trust breaks down.","The court may refuse the order, as Seren is unreasonably rejecting a fair alternative remedy.","The court must refuse the petition, because only a creditor can petition for winding up.","The court will instead make an unfair prejudice order, even though Seren has not asked for one."],"a":2,"e":"Winding up on the **just and equitable** ground (IA 1986 s.122(1)(g)) is a remedy of last resort. Deadlock in a quasi-partnership can justify it, but the court may **refuse** an order if some other remedy is available and the petitioner is **acting unreasonably** in seeking winding up instead (s.125(2)). Rejecting a fair, independently valued offer for her shares is likely to be unreasonable. Members can present such a petition, and the court does not make an unfair prejudice order of its own motion."},
 {"t":"shareholders-minority","q":"Kemi holds 6% of the voting shares in Fairlop Ltd. A general meeting has been called to consider a resolution to sell the company's main factory. Kemi wants every member to receive a short statement of her reasons for opposing the sale before the meeting.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["She can require the company to circulate a statement of up to 1,000 words, as she holds 5% or more.","She cannot require circulation, because only the directors may send material to members about a resolution.","She can require circulation only if she holds at least 10% of the voting rights.","She can require circulation only with the consent of a majority of the board.","She can require circulation of a statement of any length, at the directors' personal expense."],"a":0,"e":"Members holding at least **5%** of the total voting rights (or at least 100 members with the required holding) can require the company to circulate a statement of **not more than 1,000 words** about a proposed resolution or other business at a general meeting (CA 2006 s.314). Kemi qualifies on her own. The directors' consent is not needed."},
 {"t":"shareholders-minority","q":"Bryony holds 30% of the ordinary shares in Coldharbour Ltd, which has only one class of shares and the Model Articles. The board allotted 50,000 new ordinary shares to an outside investor for cash without first offering any to the existing shareholders. Pre-emption rights had not been excluded or disapplied. Bryony's percentage has fallen as a result.\n\nWhat remedy does Bryony have?","o":["The allotment is void, so the investor must return the shares to the company.","The allotment is voidable at Bryony's option, so she can require the shares to be cancelled.","She has no remedy, because the directors had authority to allot under the Model Articles.","The company and responsible officers must compensate her, if she claims within two years.","She can require the investor to transfer 30% of the new shares to her at the issue price."],"a":3,"e":"Before allotting ordinary shares **for cash**, the company must first offer them to existing ordinary shareholders pro rata (CA 2006 s.561). If it does not, the **company and every officer** who knowingly authorised or permitted the breach are **jointly and severally liable to compensate** the shareholders for their loss (s.563), and claims must be brought within **two years**. The allotment itself remains **valid**, and having authority to allot does not excuse a breach of pre-emption rights."},
 {"t":"shareholders-minority","q":"Arthur held 40% of the shares in Glasbury Ltd, which has the Model Articles, and died last month. His executor, Nell, has obtained probate. A general meeting is to be held next week, and Nell wants to attend and vote Arthur's shares. She has not yet asked to be registered as a member or to have the shares transferred.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["She can attend and vote at once, because the shares vest in her automatically on probate.","She cannot vote and is not entitled to any dividends until she is registered.","She can elect to be registered or to transfer the shares, but cannot vote until registered.","She must sell the shares to the other members within 14 days of probate.","The directors can refuse to recognise her title because Arthur's shares lapse on his death."],"a":2,"e":"On death, title to the shares passes by **transmission** to the personal representative, who can elect either to be **registered** as the holder or to have the shares **transferred** to someone else (Model Articles art.27). Until registered, the personal representative has the same rights as the holder, such as to dividends, but is **not entitled to attend or vote** at general meetings in respect of those shares (art.27(3))."},
 {"t":"shareholders-minority","q":"Lucy, a shareholder in Rowan Bank Ltd, which has the Model Articles unamended, has sold all her shares to Daniel. She has given him a signed stock transfer form and the share certificate. Daniel's application to register the transfer has been considered by the board, who do not want him as a member.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["The directors may refuse, but must give Daniel notice and reasons within two months.","The directors must register the transfer, because Model Articles shares are freely transferable.","The directors may refuse to register the transfer, and need give no notice or reasons.","The directors may refuse to register only if the shares are partly paid.","Daniel becomes a member when he receives the stock transfer form, so the directors' approval is irrelevant."],"a":0,"e":"Under the Model Articles (art.26(5)), the directors have a discretion to **refuse to register** a transfer. If they refuse, the company must send the transferee **notice of the refusal** with **reasons** as soon as practicable and within **two months** of the transfer being lodged (CA 2006 s.771). A buyer becomes a member only when **entered in the register of members**, so until then Lucy remains the member."},
 {"t":"shareholders-minority","q":"Three founders are setting up Kestle Ltd and will each hold one-third of the shares. Each wants a personal right to veto any future allotment of shares and any change in the nature of the business. They want these arrangements kept private and binding only between themselves.\n\nWhich of the following best achieves their aims?","o":["Special articles of association containing the veto rights, filed at Companies House.","An ordinary resolution of the shareholders recording the veto rights.","A board resolution recording the veto rights, kept with the company's minutes.","A shareholders' agreement between the three founders, containing the veto rights as personal obligations.","Reliance on the statutory rules, which give every one-third shareholder these veto rights automatically."],"a":3,"e":"A **shareholders' agreement** is a private contract between the parties, so it is not filed at Companies House, and it can give each founder **personal veto rights** enforceable against the others (for example, by agreeing how they will vote). **Articles** are public and can be changed by special resolution. A one-third holder can block special resolutions under the statutory rules, but not ordinary resolutions, so the law does not give them these vetoes."},
 {"t":"shareholders-minority","q":"In 2024, Ruth, a director of Haddon Ltd, made a secret profit of £120,000 in breach of her duty to avoid conflicts. In 2026 Jamal bought 15% of the company's shares. He now discovers the breach and wants to bring a derivative claim against Ruth. The board, which Ruth controls, refuses to sue.\n\nCan Jamal bring a derivative claim?","o":["No, because he was not a member when the breach occurred.","Yes, he can seek the court's permission to bring one, even though the breach occurred before he became a member.","No, because only holders of at least 25% of the shares can bring a derivative claim.","Yes, and he does not need the court's permission to continue the claim, because the board that refused to sue is controlled by the wrongdoer.","No, because a derivative claim can only be brought against a director who is no longer in office."],"a":1,"e":"A derivative claim may be brought by a **member** in respect of a cause of action vested in the company, and it is **immaterial** whether the cause of action arose **before or after** the person became a member (CA 2006 s.260(4)). There is no minimum shareholding, but the member must obtain the **court's permission** to continue the claim (s.261), whoever controls the board."},
 {"t":"partnerships","q":"Zara joined the partnership of Holt & Pryce, a firm of surveyors, on 1 May. In March, before she joined, the firm ordered office equipment worth £22,000 which has not been paid for. The firm cannot pay and the supplier is now claiming against all the current partners, including Zara.\n\nWhat is Zara's liability for the debt?","o":["She is liable for the whole debt, because all current partners are jointly liable for the firm's debts.","She is not liable, because a partner is not liable for debts of the firm incurred before she became a partner.","She is liable for a share of the debt in proportion to her share of the profits.","She is liable only if the equipment is still used by the firm.","She is liable unless she placed a notice in the London Gazette when she joined."],"a":1,"e":"A person admitted as a partner into an existing firm does **not** become liable to creditors for anything done **before** she became a partner (PA 1890 s.17(1)). The debt was incurred in March, before Zara joined, so the supplier must look to the partners at that time. A new partner can take on old debts only by agreement with the creditor and partners (novation)."},
 {"t":"partnerships","q":"Nadia is an employee of Fenton & Grey, a firm of architects. With the partners' agreement, the firm's website and brochures describe her as \"Partner\". Relying on this, a printing company gave the firm 60 days' credit for £15,000 of work. The firm cannot pay.\n\nCan the printing company recover the debt from Nadia?","o":["No, because she is only an employee and has no share in the profits.","No, because only the partners who authorised the website are liable.","Yes, but only for a share of the debt matching the number of people described as partners.","No, because holding out applies only to former partners who have retired.","Yes, because she allowed herself to be held out as a partner and the printer relied on it."],"a":4,"e":"A person who **represents herself**, or **knowingly allows herself to be represented**, as a partner is liable as if she were a partner to anyone who has **given credit to the firm on the faith of** that representation (PA 1890 s.14). Nadia consented to the description and the printer relied on it. Holding out is not limited to retired partners, and her lack of a profit share is irrelevant."},
 {"t":"partnerships","q":"Kit, Liam and Mona are partners in a firm letting residential flats, with no written partnership agreement. Kit and Liam want the firm to stop residential lettings and instead develop and sell commercial property. Mona objects.\n\nCan Kit and Liam make the change?","o":["Yes, because ordinary matters are decided by a majority of the partners.","Yes, because partners holding a majority of the capital can decide any matter.","No, because a change in the nature of the business needs every partner's consent.","No, because Mona can veto any decision of the firm.","Yes, provided they first give Mona three months' notice of the change."],"a":2,"e":"Under the default rules, ordinary matters connected with the partnership business may be decided by a **majority** of the partners, but **no change may be made in the nature of the partnership business** without the **consent of all** existing partners (PA 1890 s.24(8)). Switching from lettings to property development is a change in nature, so Mona can block it. She cannot, however, veto ordinary matters."},
 {"t":"partnerships","q":"Rhian, Sam and Tobias are partners under a written agreement that says nothing about expulsion. Rhian and Sam are frustrated that Tobias repeatedly arrives late to client meetings, and they vote to expel him from the partnership.\n\nWhat is the effect of their vote?","o":["It has no effect, because there is no express power of expulsion in the agreement.","Tobias is expelled, because a majority of the partners have voted for it.","Tobias is expelled, but the firm must pay him the value of his share within 12 months.","Tobias is expelled only if his conduct amounts to a breach of the duty of good faith.","It dissolves the partnership automatically as regards all three partners."],"a":0,"e":"**No majority** of the partners can expel any partner unless a power to do so has been conferred by **express agreement** between the partners (PA 1890 s.25). The agreement contains no such power, so the vote is ineffective. Rhian and Sam might instead consider dissolution (by notice if it is a partnership at will, or by applying to the court), but the vote itself neither expels Tobias nor dissolves the firm."},
 {"t":"partnerships","q":"Ivy, Jasper and Kai ran a bakery business in partnership without any written agreement. Kai has died. Ivy and Jasper want to carry on the business together.\n\nWhat is the effect of Kai's death on the partnership?","o":["The partnership continues automatically between Ivy and Jasper, with Kai's share passing to them.","The partnership continues, with Kai's personal representatives becoming partners in his place.","The partnership is dissolved only if Kai's personal representatives give notice of dissolution.","The partnership is dissolved as regards all the partners, unless they had agreed otherwise.","The partnership continues until the end of the current accounting year and then dissolves."],"a":3,"e":"Subject to any agreement between the partners, every partnership is **dissolved** as regards **all** the partners by the **death** of any partner (PA 1890 s.33). With no agreement, Kai's death dissolves the firm; Ivy and Jasper can continue in a new partnership, but Kai's estate is entitled to his share of the old firm's assets. Personal representatives do not become partners. This is why partnership agreements usually provide that the firm continues on death."},
 {"t":"partnerships","q":"Omar is a partner in a firm of plumbers. Without telling his partners, he has been running a separate plumbing business of his own in the same town, competing with the firm for customers, and has made profits of £30,000 from it.\n\nWhat is the best advice to the other partners?","o":["Omar must account only for profits from customers who had previously used the firm.","Omar must pay the firm damages, but may keep his profits.","Omar must account to the firm for all the profits he made from the competing business.","Omar's conduct is a matter of conscience only, as partners owe each other no legal duties.","Omar is in breach only if the partnership agreement contains a non-compete clause."],"a":2,"e":"Partners owe each other duties of good faith. If a partner, **without the consent** of the other partners, carries on any business of the same nature as and **competing with the firm**, he must **account for and pay over to the firm all profits** made by him in that business (PA 1890 s.30). No express clause is needed, and the duty extends to all profits of the competing business."},
 {"t":"partnerships","q":"Penny and Quentin are partners in a delivery business. Last year a van was bought with money from the firm's bank account and has been used only for firm deliveries, but it was registered in Quentin's name. Quentin now claims the van belongs to him personally.\n\nWho owns the van?","o":["The partnership, because property bought with the firm's money is presumed to be the firm's.","Quentin, because the van is registered in his name.","Quentin and Penny as joint owners in their personal capacities, not as partnership property.","Quentin, unless the partnership agreement expressly says vehicles are partnership property.","The partnership, but only if Penny contributed more than half the price."],"a":0,"e":"Property **bought with money belonging to the firm** is deemed to have been bought on account of the firm, unless the contrary intention appears (PA 1890 s.21). The van was paid for from the firm's account and used only for the business, so it is **partnership property**, held for the purposes of the partnership (s.20), whoever's name is on the registration document."},
 {"t":"partnerships","q":"Ursula is a partner in a firm of dentists. Without consulting her partners, she signs a contract in the firm's name to buy a racehorse for £40,000 from a breeder, telling him it will be \"a good investment for the practice\". The partners refuse to pay.\n\nCan the breeder enforce the contract against the firm?","o":["Yes, because every partner is an agent of the firm with authority to make any contract.","Yes, because the breeder was not told that Ursula lacked authority.","No, but only because the contract was worth more than £10,000.","No, because buying a racehorse is not within the usual business of a dental practice.","Yes, because Ursula signed in the firm's name rather than her own."],"a":3,"e":"Every partner is an agent of the firm and binds it when doing an act for **carrying on in the usual way business of the kind carried on by the firm** (PA 1890 s.5). Buying a racehorse is not usual business for a dental practice, so Ursula had neither actual nor apparent authority, and the firm is not bound. Ursula may be personally liable to the breeder for breach of warranty of authority."},
 {"t":"partnerships","q":"Pine Grove LLP's members' agreement provides that Viktor, a member, may not sign contracts on the LLP's behalf. Viktor signs a contract with a supplier on behalf of the LLP. The supplier knew Viktor was a member of the LLP but did not know of the restriction.\n\nIs the LLP bound by the contract?","o":["No, because Viktor had no actual authority under the members' agreement.","Yes, because the supplier did not know that Viktor lacked authority and knew he was a member.","No, because the members' agreement is registered at Companies House and the supplier is deemed to know of it.","Yes, but Viktor is jointly and severally liable with the LLP for the debt.","No, unless the other members ratify the contract by unanimous agreement."],"a":1,"e":"Every member of an LLP is its **agent** (LLPA 2000 s.6). The LLP is not bound only if the member had **no authority** and the other party **knows** that, or does not know or believe him to be a member. The supplier knew Viktor was a member and did not know of the restriction, so the LLP is bound. The members' agreement is a private document, and Viktor is not personally liable on the LLP's contract (though he may be liable to the LLP for breaching the agreement)."},
 {"t":"partnerships","q":"Ada, Ben and Cora formed Riverside Design LLP last year. They have no members' agreement. Ada contributed £60,000 of capital and Ben and Cora contributed £10,000 each. Ada also works more hours than the others and claims she is entitled to a salary.\n\nHow will profits be shared under the default rules?","o":["In proportion to their capital contributions, with no salary for Ada.","Equally between the three members, after Ada is paid a reasonable salary.","In proportion to the hours each member works.","In proportion to their capital contributions, after 5% interest on Ada's capital.","Equally between the three members, with no salary for Ada."],"a":4,"e":"Where there is no members' agreement, the **default rules** in the LLP Regulations 2001 (reg.7) apply: all members are entitled to **share equally** in the capital and profits, and **no member is entitled to remuneration** for acting in the business. So profits are split equally, regardless of the different capital contributions and hours worked."},
 {"t":"equity-finance","q":"Harlech Ltd was incorporated in 2018 with the Model Articles unamended and has one class of ordinary shares. The board wants to allot 10,000 new ordinary shares to an outside investor for £50,000 in cash. The existing shareholders do not want to be offered the shares.\n\nWhat shareholder resolution(s), if any, are required?","o":["An ordinary resolution to give the directors authority to allot and a special resolution to disapply pre-emption rights.","An ordinary resolution to give the directors authority to allot only; pre-emption does not apply to an outsider.","None, because the Model Articles give the directors authority and exclude pre-emption rights.","A special resolution to give the directors authority to allot only.","A special resolution to disapply pre-emption rights; no resolution is needed for the directors' authority to allot."],"a":4,"e":"In a private company with **one class** of shares, the directors may allot more shares of that class **without a resolution** unless the articles prohibit it (CA 2006 s.550), and the Model Articles do not. But this is an allotment of ordinary shares **for cash**, so **statutory pre-emption rights** apply (s.561). They can be disapplied by **special resolution** (s.569), or the existing members could waive them in writing; the Model Articles do not exclude them."},
 {"t":"equity-finance","q":"Mabel Ltd, a private company, has ordinary shares and preference shares in issue. Its articles are the Model Articles with the addition of the preference share rights. The board wants to allot 5,000 new ordinary shares for cash to an outside investor, and the existing shareholders will not take them up.\n\nWhat shareholder resolution(s) are required?","o":["A special resolution to disapply pre-emption rights only, as the directors already have authority to allot.","An ordinary resolution to give the directors authority to allot only.","An ordinary resolution to give the directors authority to allot, and a special resolution to disapply pre-emption rights.","A special resolution to give the directors authority to allot, and an ordinary resolution to disapply pre-emption rights.","No resolutions, because a company with more than one class of shares is outside the statutory rules."],"a":2,"e":"Because the company has **more than one class** of shares, the s.550 authority is unavailable, so the directors need authority under **s.551**, given by the articles or by **ordinary resolution** (stating the maximum number of shares and an expiry date of no more than five years). The shares are ordinary shares issued **for cash**, so pre-emption rights apply and must be disapplied by **special resolution** (s.570 or s.571) unless waived."},
 {"t":"equity-finance","q":"Corbel Ltd has only ordinary shares in issue and the Model Articles. It wants to raise money by allotting, for cash, a new class of preference shares to a new investor. The preference shares will carry a fixed 6% dividend and a right to repayment of their nominal value on a winding up, with no other right to share in profits or capital.\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes the position?","o":["The directors need authority by ordinary resolution, but statutory pre-emption rights do not apply to these shares.","No authority is needed because the company is a private company, but pre-emption rights must be disapplied by special resolution.","The directors need authority by ordinary resolution, and pre-emption rights must be disapplied by special resolution.","No resolutions are needed, because preference shares are treated as debt.","The directors need authority by special resolution, and the existing ordinary shareholders must be offered the shares first."],"a":0,"e":"Section 550 lets directors allot only more shares **of the existing single class**; creating a **new class** needs authority under **s.551** by ordinary resolution (or the articles). Pre-emption rights apply only to **equity securities**, and shares whose rights to dividends and capital are limited to a **fixed amount** are not ordinary shares (s.560), so these preference shares fall **outside** s.561. A new class of shares may also need the articles to set out its rights."},
 {"t":"equity-finance","q":"On 3 May, the board of Tallis Ltd allotted 2,000 new ordinary shares to Esme, who paid for them in full. The company secretary asks what the company must now do.\n\nWhat is the correct advice?","o":["File form SH01 within 15 days, and register Esme in the register of members within 28 days.","Register Esme in the register of members within one month; no filing at Companies House is required.","File form SH01 with the next confirmation statement, and register Esme within two months.","File form SH01 within one month, and enter Esme in the register of members within two months.","Notify Companies House within 14 days, and issue a share certificate within one month."],"a":3,"e":"After an allotment, the company must deliver a **return of allotment** with a **statement of capital** (form **SH01**) to Companies House within **one month** (CA 2006 s.555). It must register the allotment in the **register of members** as soon as practicable and in any event within **two months** (s.554), and issue a **share certificate** within two months (s.769)."},
 {"t":"equity-finance","q":"The directors of Wexcombe Ltd, keen to attract an investor, allot 10,000 ordinary shares of £1 each to her at 80p per share, which she pays in cash.\n\nWhat is the legal position?","o":["The allotment is valid because directors may fix any issue price they consider commercially justified.","The allotment is void, so the company must repay the investor's £8,000.","The issue at a discount is unlawful, so the investor must pay the £2,000 plus interest.","The allotment is valid if approved by special resolution of the existing shareholders.","Only the directors are liable, to pay the £2,000 shortfall to the company from their own funds."],"a":2,"e":"A company's shares must **not be allotted at a discount** to their nominal value (CA 2006 s.580). If they are, the allottee is **liable to pay** the company the amount of the discount (here 20p × 10,000 = £2,000) with **interest** (s.580(2)). The allotment itself is not void, and no resolution can authorise a discount. The directors may also commit an offence."},
 {"t":"equity-finance","q":"Glyndwr Ltd has agreed to buy back the shares of a retiring shareholder, Huw, for £90,000 out of its distributable profits. To help its cash flow, the board proposes to pay Huw £30,000 now and the balance in two annual instalments. The shares are fully paid.\n\nIs this arrangement lawful?","o":["No, because the company must pay for shares bought back in full at the time of purchase.","Yes, provided the instalments are approved by ordinary resolution.","Yes, because payment terms in a buyback are a matter for agreement between the company and the seller.","No, because a buyback must be funded from a fresh issue of shares.","Yes, provided Huw's votes are excluded on the resolution approving the contract."],"a":0,"e":"Shares may be bought back only if they are **fully paid**, and the company must **pay for them on purchase** (CA 2006 s.691(2)), so instalments are not allowed (except where a private company buys shares for an employees' share scheme). Distributable profits are a proper source of funding, and excluding Huw's votes is a separate requirement that does not cure the payment problem."},
 {"t":"equity-finance","q":"Nina subscribed £20,000 for her shares in Quayside Ltd, an unquoted trading company, three years ago. She has never inherited any shares. The company now buys back all her shares for £100,000, wholly for the benefit of its trade, and she leaves the company. She is UK resident.\n\nHow will Nina be taxed on the buyback?","o":["The whole £100,000 will be taxed as a capital gain, with BADR available.","The £80,000 will be taxed as a capital gain, because all her shares were bought back.","The whole £100,000 will be taxed as employment income.","The £80,000 above her subscription price will be taxed as dividend income.","No tax is payable, because a buyback is a return of capital."],"a":3,"e":"By default, a buyback payment is treated as a **distribution**, so the amount above the sum originally **subscribed** (£100,000 − £20,000 = £80,000) is taxed as **dividend income**. **Capital treatment** (CGT) requires all its conditions to be met, including that the seller has owned the shares for at least **five years** (three if inherited). Nina has owned hers for only three years and did not inherit them, so capital treatment is unavailable."},
 {"t":"equity-finance","q":"Fairway Ltd wants to raise money by issuing preference shares that it can redeem in five years' time. The directors suggest that, to keep things simple, the company should also convert all its existing ordinary shares into redeemable shares. The articles do not exclude or restrict redeemable shares.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["The company cannot issue redeemable shares at all unless its articles expressly authorise it.","The company can issue redeemable shares, but not if it would leave no non-redeemable shares in issue.","The company can make all its shares redeemable, provided they are fully paid.","The company can issue redeemable shares only if they are redeemed out of capital.","The company can make all its shares redeemable if a special resolution is passed."],"a":1,"e":"A **private company** may issue redeemable shares unless its articles exclude or restrict this (CA 2006 s.684), so no express authority is needed. But redeemable shares **cannot be issued at a time when there are no non-redeemable shares** in issue (s.684(4)), so the company cannot make all its shares redeemable. Redemption is normally funded from distributable profits or a fresh issue; capital is only a fallback."},
 {"t":"equity-finance","q":"Lowmoor Ltd has ordinary shares and cumulative preference shares carrying a fixed 8% dividend. The articles contain no procedure for varying class rights. The ordinary shareholders want to reduce the preference dividend to 5%.\n\nWhat approval is needed?","o":["A special resolution of all the company's shareholders voting together.","An ordinary resolution of the preference shareholders only.","The unanimous consent of all the preference shareholders.","A board resolution, because dividend rates are fixed by the directors.","A special resolution of the preference class, or 75% of that class in writing."],"a":4,"e":"Reducing the preference dividend is a **variation of class rights**. Where the articles contain no provision, the rights can be varied only with the consent of the holders of that class, given either in writing by holders of at least **three-quarters in nominal value** of the class or by a **special resolution** at a separate general meeting of the class (CA 2006 s.630). A resolution of all shareholders together is not enough, because the ordinary shareholders could outvote the preference holders."},
 {"t":"equity-finance","q":"Pentre Ltd, a private company with distributable profits of only £2,000, has articles that expressly permit buybacks of shares out of capital under the de minimis exception. Its fully paid share capital at the start of this financial year was £400,000 nominal. A small shareholder, Aled, wants to sell back shares for £12,000 in cash. The company has made no other buybacks this year.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["The company must follow the full out-of-capital procedure, including a directors' statement and auditor's report.","The company can pay £12,000 out of capital under the de minimis exception, as the limit is £15,000.","The company can buy back shares only to the value of its distributable profits of £2,000.","The company can buy the shares out of capital without any shareholder approval of the contract.","The company can use the de minimis exception for up to £20,000, being 5% of its share capital."],"a":1,"e":"If its **articles authorise** it, a private company may buy back shares **out of capital in cash** without the full out-of-capital procedure, up to the **lower of £15,000** or the nominal value of **5%** of its fully paid share capital in a financial year (CA 2006 s.692(1ZA)). Here 5% is £20,000, so the limit is **£15,000**, and £12,000 is within it. The terms of the buyback contract still need approval by ordinary resolution (s.694)."},
 {"t":"debt-security","q":"On 3 March 2027, Ellis Engineering Ltd granted Northgate Bank plc a fixed charge over its factory. The bank's solicitors want to register the charge at Companies House.\n\nWhat is the last day on which the charge can be delivered for registration without a court order?","o":["17 March 2027.","23 March 2027.","24 March 2027.","31 March 2027.","3 April 2027."],"a":2,"e":"A charge must be delivered for registration (form **MR01** with a certified copy of the charge) within **21 days beginning with the day after the date of its creation** (CA 2006 s.859A(4)). The period begins on 4 March and its 21st day is **24 March**. A deadline of 23 March would wrongly count the day of creation, and the other dates use a 14-day or one-month period."},
 {"t":"debt-security","q":"Fernhill Ltd granted a fixed charge over its warehouse to Redbrook Finance to secure a £300,000 loan. Redbrook's solicitors overlooked registration, and the 21-day period has now expired. Fernhill Ltd is solvent and trading normally.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Redbrook Finance?","o":["Apply to court to extend the period; meanwhile the charge is void against a liquidator and creditors.","Register the charge now with a late filing fee, which validates it from its date of creation.","Do nothing, because the charge is valid against the company and its creditors until the company becomes insolvent.","Take a fresh charge, which will rank ahead of any charge created in the meantime.","Apply to the court to extend the period, which will make the charge valid from its date of creation against all parties."],"a":0,"e":"An unregistered charge is **void against a liquidator, administrator and creditors** of the company (s.859H), and the **money secured becomes immediately payable** (s.859H(4)). The court can **extend the period** for registration if the failure was accidental and not prejudicial to creditors or shareholders (s.859F), usually on terms protecting intervening rights. A fresh charge is an option but ranks from its own creation date, and registration cannot simply be done late."},
 {"t":"debt-security","q":"Kingsley Ltd granted Bank A a fixed charge over its freehold offices on 1 May, which was registered at Companies House on 18 May. It granted Bank B a fixed charge over the same offices on 5 May, which was registered on 8 May. Neither charge contains any provision about priority, and neither bank knew of the other's charge.\n\nWhich bank's charge has priority?","o":["Bank B's charge, because it was registered at Companies House first.","Bank B's charge, because a later chargee without notice takes priority.","They rank equally, sharing the proceeds in proportion to their debts.","Bank A's charge, because both were registered in time and A's was created first.","Bank A's charge, but only if Bank B had actual notice of it."],"a":3,"e":"As between two **fixed charges** over the same property, both **registered within the 21-day period**, priority depends on the **order of creation**, not the order of registration. Bank A's charge was created first, so it ranks ahead. Registering first gives no advantage while the earlier charge is still within its registration period. (For registered land, registration at HM Land Registry also affects priority, but no such facts are given.)"},
 {"t":"debt-security","q":"Westmere Ltd granted its bank a floating charge over all its assets and undertaking. The charge says nothing about crystallisation. The company has now permanently ceased to carry on business, but no liquidator, administrator or receiver has been appointed.\n\nWhat is the effect on the floating charge?","o":["Nothing happens until the bank appoints a receiver or administrator.","It becomes void, because the company no longer has a business for the charge to float over.","It crystallises automatically on the cessation of business and attaches to the assets.","It crystallises only when the company enters liquidation.","It converts into a mortgage over the company's land only."],"a":2,"e":"A floating charge **crystallises** on the appointment of a receiver, administrator or liquidator, on any event specified in the charge, and also, by implication, on the company **ceasing to carry on business**. On crystallisation the charge **attaches** to the assets the company then owns and operates like a fixed charge, so the company can no longer deal with them. The charge is not void."},
 {"t":"debt-security","q":"Brecon Data Ltd has a £2 million loan from Bank A. Its loan agreement with Bank B, for a separate £500,000 facility, contains a clause allowing Bank B to demand immediate repayment if the company defaults on any other borrowing. Brecon Data Ltd has just missed a repayment to Bank A, but has made every payment due to Bank B.\n\nCan Bank B demand immediate repayment of its loan?","o":["Yes, because the default on Bank A's loan is an event of default under Bank B's agreement.","No, because the company has made every payment due to Bank B.","No, because a lender can only accelerate a loan for a default in payment of its own loan.","Yes, but only if Bank A has already demanded repayment.","No, unless Bank B's loan is secured by a charge over the company's assets."],"a":0,"e":"A **cross-default** clause makes a default under **another** borrowing an **event of default** under this loan, so the lender can protect itself before its own payments are missed. On an event of default, the lender may typically **accelerate** (demand immediate repayment of) the loan and enforce any security. This clause applies even though no payment to Bank B has been missed, and whether or not the loan is secured."},
 {"t":"debt-security","q":"Tremain Ltd is borrowing £400,000 from Eastgate Bank to buy specialist machinery that will be bolted to the floor of its factory. The bank asks what kind of security over the machinery would best protect its position if the company became insolvent.\n\nWhat is the best advice to the bank?","o":["A floating charge over the machinery, because it ranks ahead of all other creditors, including fixed charge holders.","A floating charge over the machinery, because it lets the company sell the machinery freely while the bank keeps priority.","A fixed charge over the machinery, because it guarantees repayment in full whatever the machinery's value.","A fixed charge over the machinery, because it ranks ahead of the insolvency expenses, preferential creditors and the prescribed part.","No charge, because security over machinery cannot be registered at Companies House."],"a":3,"e":"A **fixed charge** attaches to a specific asset at once, and the company cannot deal with the asset without the lender's consent. On insolvency the fixed charge holder is paid from the proceeds of that asset **before** the expenses, preferential creditors and the **prescribed part**, which come out of **floating charge** assets only. A fixed charge does not guarantee full repayment if the asset is worth less than the debt, and charges over machinery can be registered."},
 {"t":"debt-security","q":"Hollybush Ltd granted Westcliff Bank a legal mortgage over its registered freehold factory. The bank delivered form MR01 to Companies House within 21 days but has not taken any steps at HM Land Registry.\n\nWhat further step should the bank take to protect its mortgage?","o":["None, because registration at Companies House protects the mortgage for all purposes.","Register the mortgage at HM Land Registry, so that it takes effect as a legal mortgage.","Give notice of the mortgage to all the company's other creditors.","Register the mortgage at the Land Charges Department, as it is a company charge.","None, because a legal mortgage over company property does not need to be registered anywhere."],"a":1,"e":"A charge created by a company must be registered at **Companies House** within 21 days, but a **legal mortgage of registered land** must also be **registered at HM Land Registry** to take effect as a legal mortgage and to protect its priority against later interests (Land Registration Act 2002). The two registration systems operate separately. The Land Charges Department deals with unregistered land."},
 {"t":"debt-security","q":"Calder Fabrics Ltd granted its bank a floating charge over all its assets, including its stock. Last week, before any crystallisation, the company sold a large consignment of fabric to a retailer in the ordinary course of business. The bank now claims that the fabric is still subject to its charge.\n\nIs the bank correct?","o":["Yes, because a floating charge attaches to all the company's assets from the date it was created.","Yes, because the retailer is deemed to have notice of the registered charge.","No, but only because the retailer paid the full market price.","Yes, unless the retailer obtained the bank's written consent to the sale.","No, because the company may deal with floating charge assets in the ordinary course of business."],"a":4,"e":"The defining feature of a **floating charge** is that, until it **crystallises**, the company can **deal with the charged assets in the ordinary course of business** without the lender's consent. A buyer of stock therefore takes it **free of the charge**, and the charge instead floats over whatever assets of that class the company then has, such as the sale proceeds or new stock."},
 {"t":"debt-security","q":"Imran is considering investing £200,000 in Delph Ltd. He wants a fixed return each year whether or not the company makes a profit, and he wants to rank ahead of the shareholders for repayment if the company is wound up. He does not want a vote in company decisions.\n\nWhich form of investment best meets his aims?","o":["Ordinary shares, which carry a right to dividends in priority to creditors.","A loan to the company, on which interest is payable whether or not the company makes profits.","Non-voting ordinary shares, which give a fixed annual dividend.","Preference shares, which entitle the holder to a dividend whether or not there are distributable profits.","Redeemable ordinary shares, which must be repaid before the company's creditors on a winding up."],"a":1,"e":"A **lender** is a **creditor**: interest is a debt payable whether or not the company makes profits, and on a winding up creditors are paid **before** shareholders. Shares of any kind, including preference shares, can receive dividends only out of **distributable profits**, and shareholders rank **after** all creditors on a winding up. A loan also carries no vote."},
 {"t":"debt-security","q":"The articles of Brookfield Ltd provide that the directors may not borrow more than £500,000 without the prior approval of the shareholders by ordinary resolution. Without seeking approval, the board borrows £700,000 from a bank. The bank did not read the articles and acted in good faith.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["The loan is void above £500,000, so the bank can recover only that amount.","The loan is void, because the bank is deemed to have notice of the borrowing limit in the articles.","The loan binds the company only if the shareholders ratify it by special resolution.","The loan binds the directors personally, but not the company.","The loan binds the company, because the bank acted in good faith, but the directors may be liable."],"a":4,"e":"In favour of a person dealing with the company in **good faith**, the directors' power to bind the company is deemed **free of any limitation** under the constitution (CA 2006 s.40), and the bank is not obliged to check the articles. The loan therefore binds the company in full. But the directors have acted outside the constitution, in breach of **s.171**, and may be liable to the company for any loss."},
 {"t":"distributions-accounts","q":"The directors of Copse Ltd, which has the Model Articles unamended, want to pay an interim dividend halfway through the financial year. Interim accounts show sufficient distributable profits. The shareholders have not been consulted.\n\nWhat approval is needed?","o":["A decision of the directors only, if the available profits justify the payment.","An ordinary resolution of the shareholders, on the recommendation of the directors.","A special resolution of the shareholders.","An ordinary resolution of the shareholders, with the directors' recommendation optional.","No approval is possible, because dividends can only be paid after the annual accounts are approved."],"a":0,"e":"Under the Model Articles (art.30), **final dividends** are declared by **ordinary resolution** of the shareholders, and may not exceed the amount the directors recommend. But the **directors** may decide to pay **interim dividends** themselves, if it appears to them that the profits available for distribution justify the payment. Interim accounts can be used to show this (CA 2006 s.838)."},
 {"t":"distributions-accounts","q":"Ferndale Ltd has accumulated realised profits brought forward of £100,000. In the current financial year it made a realised trading loss of £40,000 and has already paid an interim dividend of £20,000. It has no unrealised profits or losses.\n\nWhat is the maximum final dividend Ferndale Ltd can lawfully pay for the year?","o":["£60,000.","£80,000.","£100,000.","£40,000.","Nothing, because it made a loss this year."],"a":3,"e":"A company may only make a distribution out of **profits available for the purpose**: its **accumulated realised profits** less its **accumulated realised losses**, so far as not previously distributed (CA 2006 s.830). Here £100,000 − £40,000 − £20,000 already distributed = **£40,000**. The test is cumulative, so a loss in one year does not prevent a dividend if earlier profits cover it, but the interim dividend already paid must be deducted."},
 {"t":"distributions-accounts","q":"Grafton Ltd paid a dividend of £5,000 to Lena, an outside investor holding 5% of its shares. It later emerged that the company had insufficient distributable profits because of an accounting error. Lena had no involvement in the company's management and did not know, and had no reason to believe, that the dividend was unlawful.\n\nMust Lena repay the dividend?","o":["Yes, because every shareholder must repay an unlawful dividend whatever their state of knowledge.","Yes, but only if the company is now insolvent.","No, because she did not know, and had no reasonable grounds to believe, it was unlawful.","No, because once a dividend has been paid it can never be recovered by the company.","Yes, but only half of it, as the directors share responsibility."],"a":2,"e":"A member who receives an unlawful distribution is liable to repay it only if, at the time, they **knew or had reasonable grounds for believing** that it was made in contravention of the rules (CA 2006 s.847). Lena had neither, so she keeps it. The company's remedy is instead against the **directors** who authorised it, who may be liable for breach of duty."},
 {"t":"distributions-accounts","q":"Wildwood Ltd, a private company, has been trading for several years with an accounting reference date of 31 March. Its accounts are for the year ended 31 March 2026.\n\nBy when must Wildwood Ltd file these accounts at Companies House?","o":["31 December 2026.","30 September 2026.","31 January 2027.","31 March 2027.","30 June 2026."],"a":0,"e":"A **private company** must file its accounts within **9 months** of the end of its accounting reference period (CA 2006 s.442), so by **31 December 2026**. A public company has 6 months (30 September 2026). Special rules apply only to a company's first accounts (generally 21 months from incorporation if they cover more than 12 months)."},
 {"t":"distributions-accounts","q":"Calloway Ltd qualifies as a small company and has always claimed exemption from audit. Patrick holds 12% of its issued shares and is worried about how the company is being run. He wants the accounts for the current financial year to be audited. The financial year ends in four months' time.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Patrick?","o":["He cannot require an audit, because small companies are exempt from audit.","He can require an audit only if he obtains an ordinary resolution of the shareholders.","He can require an audit at any time up to the filing deadline for the accounts.","He can require an audit by notice given at least one month before the year end.","He can require an audit only if he holds more than 25% of the shares."],"a":3,"e":"Small companies are generally **exempt from audit** (CA 2006 s.477), but members holding at least **10%** of the nominal value of the issued share capital can **require an audit** by notice deposited at the registered office **no later than one month before the end of the financial year** (s.476). Patrick holds 12% and has time to give notice, so he can insist on an audit."},
 {"t":"distributions-accounts","q":"Marlowe Ltd, a private company, has an annual turnover of £12 million, a balance sheet total of £9 million and 40 employees on average. It met the same figures last year. For financial years starting on or after 6 April 2025, a small company must not exceed two of: turnover £15m, balance sheet total £7.5m and 50 employees. A medium-sized company must not exceed two of: turnover £54m, balance sheet total £27m and 250 employees.\n\nWhich size category does Marlowe Ltd fall into?","o":["Medium, because its balance sheet total exceeds the small company limit.","Small, because it does not exceed the turnover and employee limits.","Large, because it exceeds one of the small company limits.","Micro-entity, because it has fewer than 50 employees.","Medium, because a company must meet all three conditions to be small."],"a":1,"e":"A company qualifies for a size category if it meets **at least two of the three** conditions (in its first year, or generally in two consecutive years). Marlowe exceeds the small company balance sheet limit, but its turnover (£12m) and employees (40) are within the small limits, so it is **small**. Exceeding just one limit does not move it up a category, and it is far above the micro-entity limits."},
 {"t":"distributions-accounts","q":"Lucia and Dario are the directors of Penrose Ltd. They declared an interim dividend relying on management accounts prepared by the company's qualified accountant, which showed ample distributable profits. The accounts contained an error that the directors could not reasonably have spotted, and there were in fact no distributable profits. The company is now in liquidation.\n\nWhat is the best advice to the directors?","o":["They have no possible liability, because only the shareholders who received the dividend can be made to repay it.","They are strictly liable to repay the dividend and cannot be relieved from liability by the court.","They are liable only if the dividend exceeded 10% of the company's net assets.","They are liable only if they also received part of the dividend as shareholders.","They may be liable, but the court may relieve them as they acted honestly and reasonably."],"a":4,"e":"Directors who authorise an unlawful dividend may be liable to repay it to the company for **breach of duty**. But the court may **relieve** a director from liability, wholly or partly, if they acted **honestly and reasonably** and ought fairly to be excused (CA 2006 s.1157). Reasonable reliance on properly prepared accounts containing an error they could not have spotted is a strong ground for relief. Their liability does not depend on receiving any of the dividend."},
 {"t":"distributions-accounts","q":"Haddon Ltd has no distributable profits. Its board agrees to sell one of the company's buildings, worth £300,000, for £100,000 to Nell, who is the company's only shareholder. The transaction is described in the documents as a sale.\n\nWhat is the legal position?","o":["The sale is lawful, because a company can sell its assets to anyone at any price the directors agree.","The sale is an unlawful distribution, because the company has no distributable profits.","The sale is lawful, because a transaction labelled as a sale cannot be a distribution.","The sale is lawful because Nell is the only shareholder and has consented to it.","The sale is valid, but Nell must pay corporation tax on the £200,000 difference."],"a":1,"e":"A **distribution** includes **every description of distribution of a company's assets to its members**, in cash or otherwise (CA 2006 s.829), and the courts look at the **substance**, not the label. A transfer to a shareholder at an **undervalue** is a disguised distribution, which is **unlawful** if the company has no distributable profits (Aveling Barford). The sole shareholder's consent does not cure it, because the rules protect creditors."},
 {"t":"distributions-accounts","q":"The directors of Bramley Ltd ask how long the company must keep its accounting records, such as its invoices, ledgers and records of assets and liabilities. Bramley Ltd is a private company.\n\nWhat is the correct advice?","o":["At least six years from the end of the financial year.","At least two years from the date they are made.","At least ten years from the date they are made.","Only until the annual accounts for that year are filed at Companies House.","At least three years from the date they are made."],"a":4,"e":"Every company must keep **adequate accounting records** to show and explain its transactions and disclose its financial position with reasonable accuracy (CA 2006 s.386). A **private** company must keep them for **three years**, and a **public** company for **six years**, from the date they are made (s.388). Separately, records needed for tax (such as for corporation tax and VAT) must generally be kept for six years."},
 {"t":"distributions-accounts","q":"Larkin Ltd declared a final dividend of £30,000 by ordinary resolution at its general meeting in May. Before it was paid, the company entered insolvent liquidation in July. Erin, a shareholder, is owed £6,000 of the dividend and claims to rank alongside the trade creditors.\n\nHow will Erin's claim be treated in the liquidation?","o":["It ranks equally with the unsecured trade creditors, because a declared dividend is a debt.","It is a preferential debt, ranking ahead of the unsecured creditors.","It is owed to her as a member, so it ranks after the ordinary creditors.","It is extinguished, because a dividend cannot be paid once a company is in liquidation.","It ranks ahead of the unsecured creditors but after the floating charge holder."],"a":2,"e":"Once declared, a dividend becomes a **debt** owed by the company to the shareholder. But in a winding up, a sum due to a member **in her character as a member** by way of dividend is **not** treated as a debt ranking alongside other creditors; it is a **deferred** debt, paid only after the ordinary creditors (and statutory interest) are paid in full (IA 1986 s.74(2)(f))."},
 {"t":"corporate-insolvency","q":"Ridgeway Homes Ltd owns land worth £4 million and owes £2.5 million in total. However, its land cannot be sold quickly, it has almost no cash, and it has failed to pay several suppliers' invoices as they fell due over the last three months.\n\nIs Ridgeway Homes Ltd unable to pay its debts for insolvency purposes?","o":["No, because its assets exceed its liabilities, so it is balance sheet solvent.","No, because a company is insolvent only if a creditor has served a statutory demand.","Yes, but only if it also fails the balance sheet test within the next 12 months.","Yes, because it cannot pay its debts as they fall due, whatever its balance sheet shows.","No, because a company with land is never treated as unable to pay its debts."],"a":3,"e":"A company is **unable to pay its debts** if it fails either the **cash flow test** (it cannot pay its debts as they fall due: IA 1986 s.123(1)(e)) or the **balance sheet test** (its assets are worth less than its liabilities, taking into account contingent and prospective liabilities: s.123(2)). Failing one test is enough. Ridgeway fails the cash flow test, despite being balance sheet solvent, and no statutory demand is required."},
 {"t":"corporate-insolvency","q":"Ivo, a freelance designer, is owed £600 by Clearpath Media Ltd for work done three months ago. The debt is undisputed but unpaid. He wants to serve a statutory demand and then petition to wind the company up if it does not pay.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Ivo?","o":["He can serve a statutory demand and petition after 21 days if it is not paid.","He can serve a statutory demand, but must wait 3 months before petitioning.","He cannot rely on a statutory demand for under £750, but may prove inability to pay in other ways.","He can petition immediately, because an unpaid debt of any amount proves inability to pay.","He cannot petition in any circumstances, because only debts of over £5,000 can found a winding-up petition."],"a":2,"e":"A statutory demand founds a deemed inability to pay only if the company owes **more than £750** and fails to pay, secure or compound it within **3 weeks** (IA 1986 s.123(1)(a)). Ivo's £600 debt is too small. He could still petition if he can prove inability to pay in another way, such as an **unsatisfied execution** of a judgment (s.123(1)(b)) or evidence that the company cannot pay its debts as they fall due. The £5,000 level applies to bankruptcy petitions against individuals."},
 {"t":"corporate-insolvency","q":"Westbank plc holds a qualifying floating charge over the whole of the assets of Darrow Engineering Ltd, which is now enforceable because the company has defaulted. The bank wants an administrator appointed as quickly as possible and without a court hearing.\n\nWhat is the best advice to the bank?","o":["As a qualifying floating charge holder, it can appoint an administrator out of court.","It must apply to the court for an administration order, as only the court can appoint an administrator.","It can appoint an administrative receiver, who will act in the same way as an administrator.","It must ask the directors to appoint an administrator, as a creditor has no power to do so.","It can appoint an administrator out of court only with the consent of all the company's unsecured creditors."],"a":0,"e":"The holder of a **qualifying floating charge** (one over the whole or substantially the whole of the company's property) may appoint an administrator **out of court** once the charge is **enforceable**, by filing a notice of appointment (IA 1986 Sch.B1 para.14). For charges created on or after 15 September 2003, a floating charge holder generally **cannot** appoint an administrative receiver (s.72A), and the unsecured creditors' consent is not needed."},
 {"t":"corporate-insolvency","q":"Halcyon Retail Ltd entered administration last week. Its landlord is owed three months' rent and wants to forfeit the lease of the company's flagship shop by peaceably re-entering the premises. A supplier also wants to issue court proceedings to recover an unpaid invoice.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["The landlord can forfeit the lease, but the supplier needs the court's permission to sue.","Both can take action, because the moratorium protects only the company's secured creditors.","The supplier can sue, but the landlord needs the administrator's consent to forfeit.","Neither can act without the administrator's consent or the court's permission.","Both can take action once the administrator has been in office for 28 days."],"a":3,"e":"Once a company enters administration, a **moratorium** applies (IA 1986 Sch.B1 paras 42-43): no landlord may exercise a right of **forfeiture by peaceable re-entry**, no security may be enforced and no **legal process** may be started or continued against the company, without the **administrator's consent** or the **court's permission**. This protects the company while the administrator pursues the purpose of the administration."},
 {"t":"corporate-insolvency","q":"Shortly after his appointment, the administrator of Tidemill Ltd concludes that it is not reasonably practicable to rescue the company as a going concern, but that selling its business and assets as a going concern to a buyer would produce more for creditors than an immediate liquidation. He plans to sell the business and then move the company into liquidation.\n\nIs the administrator acting in accordance with the purpose of administration?","o":["No, because an administrator must always rescue the company as a going concern.","Yes, because rescue is not reasonably practicable, so a better result for creditors suffices.","No, because an administrator may only realise property to pay secured creditors.","Yes, but only if the shareholders first approve the sale of the business and assets by passing a special resolution.","No, because selling the business is a matter only for a liquidator."],"a":1,"e":"The administrator must perform his functions with the objective of (a) **rescuing the company** as a going concern, or, if that is not reasonably practicable, (b) achieving a **better result for the creditors as a whole** than an immediate winding up, or, failing both, (c) realising property to distribute to secured or preferential creditors (IA 1986 Sch.B1 para.3). A going-concern sale of the business is a typical way of achieving objective (b), and no shareholder approval is needed."},
 {"t":"corporate-insolvency","q":"The directors of Brightline Software Ltd believe the company is likely to become unable to pay its debts. They think it can be rescued as a going concern if it has a short breathing space from creditor action to negotiate with its main creditors. They want to stay in control of the business while this happens.\n\nWhich procedure best meets their aims?","o":["Administration, during which the directors remain in control under the supervision of the administrator.","A creditors' voluntary liquidation, which gives the company 28 days' protection while it trades on.","A members' voluntary liquidation, because the directors believe the company can be rescued.","Receivership, in which the receiver takes control of the company but the directors keep management powers.","A Part A1 moratorium, with the directors in control under the supervision of a monitor."],"a":4,"e":"A **Part A1 moratorium** gives the company a breathing space from most creditor action while the **directors stay in control**, supervised by a **monitor** (a licensed insolvency practitioner) who must consider it likely that the moratorium will result in the **rescue of the company as a going concern**. It lasts an initial **20 business days**, extendable. In administration, the **administrator** takes control, and liquidation ends the company rather than rescuing it."},
 {"t":"corporate-insolvency","q":"The directors of Eastbrook Ltd conclude that the company is insolvent and cannot continue trading. They want to put it into liquidation without a court petition. The shareholders are willing to cooperate.\n\nWhat procedure should be followed?","o":["The directors resolve to wind up, and the company is in liquidation from that date without any shareholder resolution.","The members pass a special resolution, and the creditors then decide on the liquidator.","The directors make a statutory declaration of solvency and the members pass a special resolution.","The members pass an ordinary resolution to wind up, and the members' nominee becomes liquidator.","The directors file a notice at court, and the Official Receiver becomes liquidator."],"a":1,"e":"A **creditors' voluntary liquidation** (CVL) starts with a **special resolution** of the members to wind up. The creditors must then be asked to make a decision on the **nomination of a liquidator** within **14 days** of the resolution, and if the members and creditors nominate different people, the **creditors' nominee** prevails (IA 1986 s.100). A declaration of solvency is used only in a members' voluntary liquidation, and the Official Receiver acts in compulsory liquidations."},
 {"t":"corporate-insolvency","q":"A winding-up order was made against Cornel Ltd on a creditor's petition. Its directors ask who will now act as liquidator and what happens to their own powers.\n\nWhat is the correct advice?","o":["The directors choose a licensed insolvency practitioner as liquidator and keep their powers to run the business.","The petitioning creditor becomes liquidator, and the directors keep their powers until the first creditors' meeting.","The court appoints an administrator, and the directors' powers are suspended for 12 months.","No liquidator is appointed until the creditors vote, and the directors keep their powers until then.","The Official Receiver becomes liquidator on the making of the order, and the directors' powers cease."],"a":4,"e":"In a **compulsory liquidation**, the **Official Receiver** becomes the liquidator when the winding-up order is made (IA 1986 s.136), although an insolvency practitioner may later be appointed in their place. The **directors' powers cease** and pass to the liquidator, who takes control of the company's assets in order to realise them and pay creditors."},
 {"t":"corporate-insolvency","q":"Hartley Ltd is in insolvent liquidation. It holds a lease of a warehouse with 15 years left to run, at a rent well above the market rate, with onerous repairing obligations. No buyer for the lease can be found.\n\nWhat can the liquidator do about the lease?","o":["Nothing, because the lease must be performed until it expires.","Surrender it to the landlord without the landlord's consent, ending all liability.","Disclaim it, leaving the landlord to prove for its loss as an unsecured creditor.","Assign it to a director of the company, who becomes personally liable for the rent.","Disclaim it, but only with the consent of every creditor of the company."],"a":2,"e":"A liquidator may **disclaim onerous property**, including an unprofitable contract or property that is unsaleable or gives rise to burdensome obligations (IA 1986 s.178). Disclaimer ends the company's rights and liabilities in the property. A person who suffers loss as a result, such as the landlord, may **prove** in the liquidation as an **unsecured creditor** for that loss. Creditors' consent is not required."},
 {"t":"corporate-insolvency","q":"Kelso Properties Ltd granted Alder Bank a fixed charge (a legal mortgage) over one of its let commercial buildings in 2022. The bank has no floating charge. The company has defaulted on the loan, and the bank wants someone appointed to collect the rents and, if necessary, sell the building.\n\nWhat is the best advice to the bank?","o":["Appoint a fixed charge receiver (an LPA receiver) over the building under the power in the mortgage.","Appoint an administrative receiver over all the company's assets.","Appoint an administrator out of court as a qualifying floating charge holder.","Petition for a compulsory winding up, as this is the only way to enforce a fixed charge.","Take possession of all the company's assets, including those not covered by the charge."],"a":0,"e":"A **fixed charge** or **LPA receiver** can be appointed by the lender under the mortgage (or the Law of Property Act 1925) to collect income from and sell the **charged property** only, for the benefit of that lender. An **administrative receiver** can generally be appointed only by a floating charge holder under a charge created before 15 September 2003, and a bank with no qualifying floating charge cannot appoint an administrator out of court."},
 {"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","q":"Eighteen months before it entered insolvent liquidation, Mercer Tools Ltd sold a van worth £40,000 for £10,000 to Kieran, the brother of one of its directors. The liquidator cannot find any evidence about whether the company was unable to pay its debts at the time of the sale.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the liquidator's position?","o":["He cannot challenge the sale, because it took place more than six months before the liquidation.","He can challenge the sale only if he proves that the company was unable to pay its debts at the time.","He may challenge it as a transaction at an undervalue, and insolvency at the time is presumed.","He may challenge the sale as a preference, because the company preferred a connected person.","He cannot challenge the sale, because a sale for some value cannot be a transaction at an undervalue."],"a":2,"e":"A **transaction at an undervalue** (IA 1986 s.238) is one where the company receives significantly less value than it gives, within **2 years** before the onset of insolvency. The company must have been unable to pay its debts at the time (or become so as a result), but this is **presumed** where the other party is a **connected person** (s.240(2)); a director's brother is connected. The court may make any order restoring the position. A sale for £10,000 of a £40,000 van is significantly undervalued."},
 {"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","q":"Eight months before Ashdown Ltd entered insolvent liquidation, and while it was unable to pay its debts, it paid £20,000 to Belfry Supplies, an unconnected trade supplier, in full settlement of an old invoice. The directors admit they wanted Belfry to be paid ahead of other creditors because of a long friendship with its owner.\n\nCan the liquidator recover the payment as a preference?","o":["No, because the payment was made outside the six-month period for an unconnected person.","Yes, because the directors admit they had a desire to prefer Belfry.","Yes, because a preference can be challenged if made within two years before the onset of insolvency.","No, because paying a genuine trade debt can never be a preference.","Yes, but only if Belfry knew that the company was insolvent."],"a":0,"e":"A **preference** (IA 1986 s.239) can be challenged only if it was given within the **relevant time**: **six months** before the onset of insolvency for an unconnected person, or **two years** for a connected person (s.240). Belfry is unconnected and the payment was eight months before, so it falls outside the period, despite the admitted desire to prefer. Paying a genuine debt can be a preference if made in time, and the recipient's knowledge is not a requirement."},
 {"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","q":"Three months before Trent Logistics Ltd entered insolvent liquidation, while it was unable to pay its debts, it paid £30,000 of arrears to its fuel supplier, an unconnected company. The supplier had told the company it would stop all deliveries unless the arrears were paid. The directors paid only to keep the fuel supplies coming so that the business could keep trading.\n\nIs the payment likely to be set aside as a preference?","o":["Yes, because it improved the supplier's position compared with other creditors within six months of insolvency.","Yes, because any payment to a creditor while insolvent is a preference.","No, because the supplier is unconnected, so a preference must be made within two months.","No, because the company paid out of commercial need, not a desire to prefer the supplier.","Yes, because the desire to prefer is presumed for all payments to trade suppliers."],"a":3,"e":"For a payment to be a **preference** (IA 1986 s.239), the company must have been **influenced by a desire** to put the creditor in a better position on insolvency. A payment made for **genuine commercial reasons**, such as to keep essential supplies flowing under pressure from the creditor, is usually not made with that desire (Re MC Bacon). The desire is **presumed** only where the creditor is a **connected person**, which the supplier is not."},
 {"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","q":"Gwen is a director of Pellam Ltd, which has now entered insolvent liquidation. Nine months before the liquidation, the company's accountant told her in writing that there was no realistic prospect of the company avoiding insolvent liquidation. Gwen carried on trading in the hope that things would improve, and the company's debts increased by £150,000 during that time.\n\nWhat is the liquidator most likely to be able to do?","o":["Apply for an order for fraudulent trading, because carrying on trading while insolvent is always fraudulent.","Seek a contribution from Gwen for wrongful trading, unless she took every step to minimise loss.","Nothing, because a director is protected by the company's limited liability.","Apply for an order that Gwen pay all the company's debts, including those incurred before the warning.","Apply for an order only if Gwen received a salary during the nine months."],"a":1,"e":"A director is liable for **wrongful trading** (IA 1986 s.214) if, before the liquidation, she **knew or ought to have concluded** that there was **no reasonable prospect** of avoiding insolvent liquidation, unless she proves she took **every step** to minimise the potential loss to creditors. The court can order her to contribute to the company's assets, usually measured by the increase in net deficiency after that date. **Fraudulent trading** requires **intent to defraud**, and hope that things will improve is not a defence."},
 {"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","q":"During its final months of trading, the directors of Spire Holidays Ltd took customers' deposits for holidays they knew the company would never provide, intending to use the money to pay themselves. Lars, the company's external marketing consultant, knowingly helped design the scheme and shared in the proceeds. Spire Holidays Ltd is now in insolvent liquidation.\n\nWho can the liquidator ask the court to order to contribute to the company's assets for fraudulent trading?","o":["Only the directors, because only directors can be liable for fraudulent trading.","Only Lars, because the directors are protected by limited liability.","No one, because fraudulent trading is only a criminal offence.","Only the directors who signed the company's accounts.","The directors and Lars, because anyone knowingly a party to the fraud can be liable."],"a":4,"e":"If in a winding up it appears that the company's business has been carried on **with intent to defraud creditors** or for any fraudulent purpose, the court may order **any persons who were knowingly parties** to it to contribute to the company's assets (IA 1986 s.213). Unlike wrongful trading, liability is **not limited to directors**, so Lars may be liable too. Fraudulent trading is also a criminal offence (CA 2006 s.993), but that does not exclude the civil claim."},
 {"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","q":"The liquidator of Corwen Ltd discovers that, a year before the company went into liquidation, its director, Bryn, paid himself a £60,000 bonus that the board never authorised and to which he had no contractual entitlement. The liquidator wants a quick, summary way to recover the money.\n\nWhich remedy is most appropriate?","o":["A wrongful trading claim, because Bryn received the money while the company was trading.","A misfeasance application, asking the court to order Bryn to repay the money for breach of duty.","A claim to set aside the payment as a preference.","A petition for an unfair prejudice order.","A claim to set aside the payment as an invalid floating charge."],"a":1,"e":"**Misfeasance** proceedings (IA 1986 s.212) give the liquidator a **summary procedure** to ask the court to order a director (or other officer) who has misapplied or retained company money or property, or been guilty of a breach of duty, to repay or restore it or to contribute compensation. An unauthorised bonus is a breach of duty. Wrongful trading and preferences deal with different wrongs, and an unfair prejudice petition is a shareholder's remedy."},
 {"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","q":"Nerys was a director of Honeycomb Bakery Ltd until it went into insolvent liquidation in March 2025. In June 2026 she became a director of a new company, Honeycomb Bakes Ltd, which carries on a similar business. She did not apply to the court for leave and none of the statutory exceptions applies. The new company has now run up debts it cannot pay.\n\nWhat is Nerys's position?","o":["She has no liability, because the new company is a separate legal person.","She commits an offence, but has no personal liability for the new company's debts.","She is liable only if the new company bought assets from the liquidator of the old company.","She has no liability, because the restriction applies only if the new company uses an identical name.","She commits an offence and is personally liable for the new company's debts while in breach."],"a":4,"e":"A director of a company that has gone into **insolvent liquidation** must not, for **five years**, be a director of, or involved in, a company with the **same or a similar name** (a **prohibited name**) without the court's leave or an applicable exception (IA 1986 s.216). \"Honeycomb Bakes\" is similar enough to suggest an association. Breach is a **criminal offence**, and the person is **personally liable** for the new company's debts incurred while acting in breach (s.217)."},
 {"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","q":"Ivan, an employee of Quarry Stone Ltd, is owed £2,000 in unpaid wages for his last two months of work and £600 of accrued holiday pay when the company enters insolvent liquidation.\n\nHow will Ivan's claims rank in the liquidation?","o":["The whole £2,600 is an ordinary preferential debt.","£800 is preferential and the remaining £1,800 (including all the holiday pay) is unsecured.","£1,400 is preferential (£800 wages plus £600 holiday pay) and £1,200 is unsecured.","The whole £2,600 is unsecured, because employees are not preferential creditors.","The whole £2,600 is a secondary preferential debt, ranking after HMRC."],"a":2,"e":"Employees' unpaid wages for the **4 months** before the insolvency are **ordinary preferential debts** up to **£800** per employee, and **accrued holiday pay** is also preferential, with **no cap** (IA 1986 Sch.6). So £800 + £600 = £1,400 is preferential. The excess wages of £1,200 are an **unsecured** claim. Secondary preferential status applies to certain HMRC debts, not employees."},
 {"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","q":"Belmont Ltd has entered insolvent liquidation. It owes HMRC £40,000 of VAT, £25,000 of PAYE income tax and employees' NICs deducted from wages, £15,000 of employer's NICs and £50,000 of corporation tax.\n\nWhich of these debts are secondary preferential debts?","o":["The VAT and the PAYE income tax and employees' NICs only (£65,000).","All of the debts owed to HMRC (£130,000).","The corporation tax only (£50,000).","The VAT, PAYE income tax, employees' NICs and employer's NICs (£80,000).","None of them, because HMRC ranks as an ordinary unsecured creditor."],"a":0,"e":"Since 1 December 2020, HMRC is a **secondary preferential creditor** for taxes the company **collected or deducted on behalf of others**, mainly **VAT**, **PAYE income tax** and **employees' NICs**. Taxes the company owes **in its own right**, such as **corporation tax** and **employer's NICs**, remain **unsecured** debts. Secondary preferential debts rank after ordinary preferential debts (such as employees' wages)."},
 {"t":"insolvency-clawback-priority","q":"Eighteen months before Aspley Ltd went into insolvent liquidation, its managing director, Rose, who had earlier lent the company £100,000 unsecured, asked it to grant her a floating charge over all its assets to secure that loan. The company did so but received no new money or other value. The liquidator has evidence that the company was able to pay its debts at the time the charge was created.\n\nIs the floating charge valid against the liquidator?","o":["Yes, because the company was able to pay its debts when the charge was created.","Yes, because the charge was created more than 12 months before the onset of insolvency.","No, but only if the liquidator proves that Rose desired to be preferred.","No, because it was given to a connected person within two years without new value.","Yes, because it was properly registered at Companies House within 21 days."],"a":3,"e":"A floating charge is **invalid** except to the extent of new value given at or after its creation (IA 1986 s.245). For a charge in favour of a **connected person** (such as a director), the relevant time is **two years** before the onset of insolvency, and there is **no requirement** that the company was unable to pay its debts at the time. For an unconnected person the period is 12 months and insolvency at the time must be shown. Registration does not cure invalidity."},
 {"t":"personal-insolvency","q":"Naomi is owed £3,500 by Owen, a self-employed decorator, under an unpaid invoice that Owen does not dispute. She wants to petition for his bankruptcy. Owen also owes an undisputed £2,000 to another creditor, Rory, who would be willing to join her.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Naomi?","o":["She cannot petition alone for under £5,000, but she and Rory could petition jointly.","She can petition alone, because any unpaid debt shows that Owen cannot pay.","She can petition alone, provided she first serves a statutory demand for £3,500.","Neither she nor Rory can petition, because each debt must individually be at least £5,000.","She can petition alone, because the bankruptcy level is £750."],"a":0,"e":"A creditor's petition requires the debt, or the **total debts of the petitioning creditors**, to be at least the **bankruptcy level** of **£5,000**, and to be for a liquidated sum that the debtor appears unable to pay (IA 1986 s.267). Naomi's £3,500 is not enough alone, but creditors can **petition jointly**, and £3,500 + £2,000 = £5,500. The £750 figure applies to winding-up petitions against companies."},
 {"t":"personal-insolvency","q":"Ruby has been served with a statutory demand by a builder for £9,000. She genuinely disputes the debt on substantial grounds, as she says the building work was defective and she has photographs and a surveyor's report showing this. The demand was served 10 days ago.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Ruby?","o":["She should do nothing, because a disputed statutory demand automatically lapses after 21 days.","She should pay the debt in full, because a statutory demand cannot be challenged.","She should apply to set aside the demand, but only after a bankruptcy petition is presented.","She should apply to court to set the demand aside, normally within 18 days of service.","She should apply to the Adjudicator to cancel the demand within 28 days."],"a":3,"e":"A debtor may apply to the court to **set aside** a statutory demand, normally within **18 days** of service (Insolvency Rules 2016), and the court will generally do so where the debt is **disputed on substantial grounds**. If she does nothing, after **21 days** unpaid the demand allows the builder to petition, as she will appear unable to pay. A disputed demand does not lapse, and the Adjudicator deals with debtors' own bankruptcy applications, not statutory demands."},
 {"t":"personal-insolvency","q":"Sasha owes £3,000 to credit card companies and has no realistic prospect of paying. She has no assets and wants to make herself bankrupt as simply as possible, without going to court.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Sasha?","o":["She cannot be made bankrupt, because her debts are below £5,000.","She can apply online to the Adjudicator, with no minimum debt and no court hearing.","She must petition the county court and attend a hearing before a judge.","She must persuade one of her creditors to petition for her bankruptcy.","She can apply to the Official Receiver, who will make the order after a creditors' meeting."],"a":1,"e":"A debtor can make a **debtor's application** for their own bankruptcy **online** to the **Adjudicator** (IA 1986 ss.263H-263O), with a fee. There is **no minimum debt**, but the debtor must be **unable to pay** their debts. The Adjudicator decides within 28 days, with **no court hearing**. The £5,000 level applies only to creditors' petitions."},
 {"t":"personal-insolvency","q":"Tariq was made bankrupt on 1 February 2026. He has cooperated fully with the trustee and there has been no application to suspend his discharge or for a bankruptcy restrictions order. In September 2026 he agreed an income payments agreement to pay £300 a month for three years.\n\nWhat is Tariq's position on 1 February 2027?","o":["He is discharged, and all his assets are returned to him by the trustee.","He remains bankrupt until the income payments agreement ends in September 2029.","He is discharged only if he applies to the court and his creditors have been paid in full.","He is discharged, and the income payments agreement ends automatically.","He is discharged, but the income payments agreement continues and his estate stays with the trustee."],"a":4,"e":"A bankrupt is **automatically discharged** **one year** after the bankruptcy order (IA 1986 s.279), unless discharge is suspended. Discharge **releases** him from most bankruptcy debts, but the **estate** that vested in the trustee stays with the trustee to be realised. An **income payments agreement** can last up to **three years** and may continue **after discharge**, so it does not end on 1 February 2027."},
 {"t":"personal-insolvency","q":"Uma was made bankrupt in March 2026. In August 2026, before her discharge, her aunt died and left her £50,000. She has told her trustee about the inheritance.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["Uma keeps the inheritance, because it was received after the bankruptcy order was made.","The trustee can claim the inheritance by written notice within 42 days of learning of it.","The inheritance vests automatically in the trustee, without any notice being needed.","Uma keeps the inheritance unless the court makes an income payments order.","The trustee can claim the inheritance only after Uma is discharged."],"a":1,"e":"Property the bankrupt acquires **after** the bankruptcy order but **before discharge** is **after-acquired property**. It does not vest in the trustee automatically, but the trustee may **claim** it by **written notice** (IA 1986 s.307), given within **42 days** of first learning of it. The bankrupt must notify the trustee of it within 21 days. An income payments order deals with surplus income, not capital such as an inheritance."},
 {"t":"personal-insolvency","q":"Victor, an undischarged bankrupt, has been invited by a friend to become a director of the friend's new company, Lantern Events Ltd. He has not applied to the court.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Victor?","o":["He can become a director, provided he discloses his bankruptcy to the other directors.","He can become a director, provided he holds no shares in the company.","He can become a director once the trustee consents in writing.","He can become a director, because the restriction applies only to companies formed before his bankruptcy.","He must not act as a director without the court's leave, or he commits an offence."],"a":4,"e":"While undischarged, a bankrupt commits a **criminal offence** if he acts as a **director** of a company or directly or indirectly takes part in or is concerned in its **promotion, formation or management** without the **leave of the court** (CDDA 1986 s.11). Disclosure to the other directors or the trustee's consent is not enough. He is also restricted, for example, from obtaining credit of over £500 without disclosing his bankruptcy."},
 {"t":"personal-insolvency","q":"Three years before she was made bankrupt, Willa gave her daughter a holiday cottage worth £200,000 for nothing. Her trustee in bankruptcy wants to recover the cottage. Willa says she was solvent at the time of the gift, but has no evidence of this.\n\nWhat is the trustee's position?","o":["He cannot challenge the gift, because it was made more than two years before the bankruptcy.","He can challenge the gift only if he proves that Willa was insolvent at the time.","He can challenge it as a transaction at an undervalue, and her insolvency at the time is presumed.","He cannot challenge the gift, because gifts to family members are exempt.","He can challenge the gift as a preference, because the daughter was preferred over the creditors."],"a":2,"e":"A trustee may challenge a **transaction at an undervalue**, including a gift, made within **five years** before the bankruptcy petition (IA 1986 ss.339, 341). If it was made **more than two years** before, the trustee must show the individual was insolvent at the time (or became so as a result), but this is **presumed** where the recipient is an **associate**, such as a child (s.341(2)). Willa must rebut the presumption. A gift to a daughter is not a preference, as the daughter was not a creditor."},
 {"t":"personal-insolvency","q":"Eighteen months before he was made bankrupt, while he was already unable to pay his debts, Xavier repaid in full a £15,000 loan from his brother, Yusuf. His other creditors remain unpaid. Xavier says he simply wanted to pay off his family first.\n\nCan Xavier's trustee recover the payment from Yusuf?","o":["Yes, as a preference to an associate within two years, with the desire to prefer presumed.","No, because a preference can be challenged only if made within six months before the bankruptcy.","No, because repaying a genuine loan can never be a preference.","Yes, but only as a transaction at an undervalue.","No, because Yusuf did not know Xavier was insolvent."],"a":0,"e":"A payment that puts a creditor in a better position on bankruptcy is a **preference** (IA 1986 s.340) if the debtor was **influenced by a desire** to prefer and was insolvent at the time. For a preference to an **associate** (a brother is an associate under s.435) the relevant time is **two years** (six months for others), and the desire to prefer is **presumed**. Repaying a debt for full value is not a transaction at an undervalue."},
 {"t":"personal-insolvency","q":"Zoe, a bankrupt, earns a salary well above what she and her family reasonably need to live on. Her trustee wants a share of her surplus income for the creditors, but Zoe will not agree to pay anything voluntarily. Her discharge is due in two months.\n\nWhat is the best advice to the trustee?","o":["Wait until her discharge, then apply for an income payments order lasting up to five years.","Claim her whole salary each month, because all income of a bankrupt vests in the trustee.","Nothing can be done, because a bankrupt's income can only be taken with her agreement.","Apply for an income payments order before discharge, lasting up to three years.","Apply to the Adjudicator to extend her bankruptcy until her surplus income has paid her debts."],"a":3,"e":"Where the bankrupt will not enter an **income payments agreement**, the trustee can apply to the court for an **income payments order** (IA 1986 s.310), requiring payment of income above what is reasonably needed for the domestic needs of the bankrupt and family. It must be made **before discharge** and can last up to **three years** from the date of the order, so it may continue after discharge. The trustee cannot take the whole salary."},
 {"t":"personal-insolvency","q":"Abel has been made bankrupt. His creditors include an unsecured bank overdraft of £30,000, unpaid trade suppliers owed £20,000, and his wife, Bella, who lent him £25,000 for his business two years ago. The trustee has realised assets and paid the bankruptcy expenses.\n\nHow does Bella's claim rank?","o":["It ranks equally with the bank and the trade suppliers as an unsecured debt.","It is a preferential debt, ranking ahead of the bank and the suppliers.","It is postponed until the other creditors are paid in full, with interest.","It ranks ahead of the bank, because she lent money for the business.","It cannot be claimed at all, because loans between spouses are unenforceable."],"a":2,"e":"In bankruptcy, a debt owed to the bankrupt's **spouse or civil partner** for credit provided is a **postponed** debt (IA 1986 s.329). It ranks **after** the ordinary unsecured creditors and after **statutory interest** on their debts, so Bella will be paid only if there is a surplus. The bank and suppliers rank equally as ordinary unsecured creditors."},
 {"t":"income-tax","q":"Clive is a sole trader whose only income in 2026/27 is trading profit of £110,000. He has no reliefs to claim. The personal allowance for 2026/27 is £12,570, reduced by £1 for every £2 of adjusted net income above £100,000.\n\nWhat personal allowance is Clive entitled to for 2026/27?","o":["£12,570.","£2,570.","£5,000.","£7,570.","Nil."],"a":3,"e":"The personal allowance is reduced by **£1 for every £2** of adjusted net income above **£100,000**. Clive's income exceeds £100,000 by £10,000, so the allowance is reduced by £5,000, from £12,570 to **£7,570**. It is lost entirely only at £125,140. Deducting the whole £10,000 excess (giving £2,570) applies the wrong ratio."},
 {"t":"income-tax","q":"Orla is employed on a salary of £50,270 in 2026/27 and receives dividends of £10,000 from shares in a private company. She has no other income and no reliefs. The personal allowance is £12,570, the basic rate band is £37,700, the dividend allowance is £500, and dividends are taxed at 10.75% (basic rate), 35.75% (higher rate) and 39.35% (additional rate).\n\nHow much income tax is payable on Orla's dividends?","o":["£3,575.00.","£3,396.25.","£1,021.25.","£1,075.00.","£3,738.25."],"a":1,"e":"After the personal allowance, Orla's salary gives taxable non-savings income of £37,700, which uses up the **whole basic rate band**, so her dividends fall in the **higher rate band**. The first **£500** is covered by the dividend allowance (taxed at 0%), and the remaining £9,500 is taxed at **35.75%** = **£3,396.25**. Ignoring the allowance gives £3,575, and using the basic dividend rate gives the lower figures."},
 {"t":"income-tax","q":"Paulo is employed on a salary of £30,000 in 2026/27 and receives £1,500 of interest on his bank savings account. He has no other income. The personal allowance is £12,570, the basic rate band is £37,700, the personal savings allowance is £1,000 for a basic rate taxpayer, and savings income is taxed at 20% in the basic rate band. The starting rate for savings applies only where taxable non-savings income is below £5,000.\n\nHow much income tax is payable on Paulo's interest?","o":["£300.","£0.","£200.","£150.","£100."],"a":4,"e":"Paulo's taxable non-savings income is £17,430, so the **starting rate for savings** is not available and his total income keeps him a **basic rate** taxpayer. He therefore has a **personal savings allowance** of **£1,000**, taxed at 0%, and the remaining £500 of interest is taxed at **20%** = **£100**. Taxing the whole £1,500 at 20% gives £300."},
 {"t":"income-tax","q":"Rania is an employed sales consultant whose permanent workplace is her employer's office in Leeds. Each day she drives from home to the office, then to visit clients at their premises, and then home. Her employer does not reimburse her travel costs.\n\nWhich of her travel costs can she deduct from her employment income?","o":["All her travel costs, because she needs her car for her job.","Only the costs of travelling between the office and the clients' premises.","Only the costs of travelling between her home and the office.","None, because employees cannot deduct travel costs from employment income.","All her travel costs except the final journey home."],"a":1,"e":"An employee can deduct travel costs incurred in the **performance of the duties** of the employment, such as business journeys from the office to clients (ITEPA 2003 s.338), but **not ordinary commuting** between home and a **permanent workplace**. So the home-to-office and final journeys home are commuting and not deductible. The fact that she needs a car for work does not make commuting deductible."},
 {"t":"income-tax","q":"Simone was dismissed by her employer after working her full contractual notice period, for which she was paid her normal salary. Her contract contains no provision for any termination payment. Her employer then paid her £50,000 as compensation for loss of her employment.\n\nHow much of the £50,000 is taxable as employment income?","o":["Nil, because compensation for loss of employment is always tax-free.","£50,000, because all payments from an employer are taxable earnings.","£30,000, because only the first £20,000 is exempt.","£25,000, because half of a termination payment is exempt.","£20,000, because the first £30,000 of the payment is exempt."],"a":4,"e":"A genuine **non-contractual** payment of compensation for loss of employment is **exempt up to £30,000**, and only the **excess** is taxed as employment income (ITEPA 2003 ss.401-403). Simone worked her full notice, so no post-employment notice pay needs to be carved out first, and the payment is not contractual. The taxable amount is £50,000 − £30,000 = **£20,000**."},
 {"t":"income-tax","q":"Tobias is an employee of Lyle Ltd. During 2026/27 the company provides him with one mobile phone, free parking at his workplace, and an interest-free loan of £25,000 to help him buy a car. Tobias is not a shareholder.\n\nWhich of these is a taxable benefit in kind for Tobias?","o":["The mobile phone only.","The workplace parking only.","The interest-free loan only.","The mobile phone and the workplace parking.","All three."],"a":2,"e":"An employee is taxed on **benefits in kind** at their cash equivalent, but some benefits are **exempt**, including **one mobile phone** and **workplace parking**. A **beneficial (cheap or interest-free) loan** is taxable where the total loans exceed **£10,000**, on the difference between the interest paid and the **official rate**. Tobias's £25,000 loan exceeds the limit, so it is the only taxable benefit."},
 {"t":"income-tax","q":"Ursula has run a gift shop as a sole trader for 20 years, making good profits. She ceased trading on 31 March 2027 and made a trading loss of £30,000 in her final 12 months of trading. She has little other income in 2026/27.\n\nWhich loss relief is most likely to produce the best result for her?","o":["Terminal loss relief, against the trade's profits of the final year and three previous years.","Early years relief, setting the loss against her total income of the three previous tax years, earliest first.","Carry forward relief, setting the loss against future profits of the same trade.","Relief against her total income of the following tax year.","Incorporation relief, setting the loss against future dividends."],"a":0,"e":"A loss made in the **final 12 months** of trading qualifies for **terminal loss relief** (ITA 2007 s.89), which carries the loss back against the profits **of the same trade** of the final tax year and the **three previous** tax years, **latest first**, producing a tax repayment from her profitable years. Early years relief is only for the first four tax years of a trade, carry forward is useless after cessation, and incorporation relief applies only where the business is transferred to a company for shares."},
 {"t":"income-tax","q":"Victoria became a partner in a firm of accountants on 6 April 2026. She borrowed £60,000 from a bank to fund her capital contribution to the partnership, and paid interest of £3,600 on the loan in 2026/27.\n\nHow is the interest treated for income tax purposes?","o":["It is a deductible expense of the firm, reducing the firm's trading profit.","It is not deductible, because it is a personal expense.","It is deductible only from any savings income she receives.","It is qualifying interest, deducted from her total income as a relief.","It is deductible only after her personal allowance has been applied."],"a":3,"e":"Interest on a loan to **buy an interest in a partnership**, or to contribute capital or lend money to it, is **qualifying interest** when paid by a partner. It is deducted from the taxpayer's **total income** at Step 2 of the calculation to give **net income** (subject to the cap on reliefs, which this amount does not reach). It is Victoria's own loan, not an expense of the firm."},
 {"t":"income-tax","q":"Willow is a self-employed photographer. Her income tax and Class 4 NICs liability for 2025/26 was £8,000, none of it collected at source. She files her tax returns online.\n\nWhen must Willow make her payments for the 2026/27 tax year?","o":["A single payment on 31 January 2028.","Payments on account of £4,000 on 31 July 2027 and 31 January 2028, with any balancing payment on 31 July 2028.","Payments on account of £4,000 on 31 January 2027 and 31 July 2027, with any balancing payment on 31 January 2028.","A single payment on 31 October 2027.","Quarterly payments of £2,000 during the tax year."],"a":2,"e":"Under **self-assessment**, a taxpayer whose previous year's liability exceeded £1,000 (and was not mostly collected at source) makes **two payments on account**, each **50% of the previous year's** liability, on **31 January in the tax year** and **31 July after it**, so £4,000 on 31 January 2027 and 31 July 2027. Any **balancing payment** is due on the following **31 January** (2028), the same date as the online return."},
 {"t":"income-tax","q":"Xena holds debentures issued by Rook Ltd, a trading company. In 2026/27 the company pays her yearly interest of £8,000 gross. Rook Ltd sends her £6,400 and accounts to HMRC for the balance. Xena is a higher rate taxpayer.\n\nWhich of the following correctly describes the income tax position?","o":["Xena is taxed on £8,000 of savings income, with a credit for the £1,600 deducted.","Xena is taxed on £6,400 of savings income, with no credit, as the company has already paid the tax.","Xena has no further tax to pay, because the company deducted tax at source.","Xena is taxed on £8,000 as dividend income, with a credit for £1,600.","Xena is taxed on £8,000 of non-savings income, with no credit."],"a":0,"e":"Interest on debentures is **savings income**. A company paying **yearly interest** to an individual must generally **deduct basic rate tax (20%) at source**, here £1,600, and pay it to HMRC (ITA 2007 s.874). Xena is taxed on the **gross** £8,000 and gets a **credit** for the £1,600 deducted. As a higher rate taxpayer she has a £500 personal savings allowance and will owe further tax at 40% on most of the interest, so the deduction does not settle her liability."},
 {"t":"cgt","q":"In 2026/27, Yasmin sold an investment building for £420,000, paying estate agent's fees of £8,000. She bought it some years ago for £200,000, paying legal fees of £5,000 on the purchase. While she owned it, she spent £50,000 building an extension and £10,000 on repainting and general repairs.\n\nWhat is Yasmin's gain before the annual exempt amount?","o":["£147,000.","£157,000.","£162,000.","£165,000.","£220,000."],"a":1,"e":"The gain is the **disposal proceeds** (£420,000) **less incidental costs of disposal** (£8,000), the **acquisition cost** and its incidental costs (£200,000 + £5,000), and **enhancement expenditure** (£50,000): £420,000 − £8,000 − £205,000 − £50,000 = **£157,000**. The £10,000 of repairs and repainting is **not** enhancement expenditure, so deducting it (giving £147,000) is wrong."},
 {"t":"cgt","q":"Zack gives his son 2,000 shares in a company listed on the London Stock Exchange. Zack holds less than 1% of the company's shares and is not an officer or employee of it. He bought the shares for £40,000, and they are worth £100,000 on the date of the gift.\n\nWhat are the capital gains tax consequences for Zack?","o":["He makes no gain, because he received nothing for the shares.","He makes a gain of £60,000, but can defer it by claiming gift relief jointly with his son.","He makes no gain, because transfers to family members are on a no gain/no loss basis.","He makes no gain, because CGT on gifts is replaced by inheritance tax.","He makes a chargeable gain of £60,000, as the gift is a disposal at market value."],"a":4,"e":"A **gift** is a disposal, and a disposal to a **connected person** (such as a child) is treated as made at **market value**, so Zack's gain is £100,000 − £40,000 = **£60,000**. **Gift relief** is available only for business assets, unquoted trading company shares, or listed shares in the donor's **personal company** (5% of the votes), so not here. The no gain/no loss rule applies only to **spouses and civil partners**."},
 {"t":"cgt","q":"Alys, who is married and lives with her husband, Bryn, transfers to him a holiday home that she bought for £150,000 and that is now worth £350,000. Two years later, Bryn sells it to a third party for £380,000.\n\nWhat are the capital gains tax consequences?","o":["Alys makes a gain of £200,000 on the transfer, and Bryn makes a gain of £30,000 on the sale.","Alys makes no gain or loss, and Bryn makes a gain of £230,000 on the sale.","Neither makes a gain, because the property was transferred between spouses.","Alys makes no gain, and Bryn makes a gain of £30,000, using the value at the date of transfer.","Alys makes a gain of £230,000 when Bryn sells, because the gain is attributed back to her."],"a":1,"e":"A disposal between **spouses or civil partners** living together is treated as made for a consideration giving **no gain and no loss** (TCGA 1992 s.58). Alys pays nothing, but Bryn takes over her **original base cost** of £150,000. When he sells, his gain is £380,000 − £150,000 = **£230,000**. The spouse exemption only defers the gain; it does not eliminate it on a later sale to a third party."},
 {"t":"cgt","q":"Carys died in 2025 owning shares that she had bought for £50,000. They were worth £300,000 at her death and passed under her will to her nephew, Dewi. In 2026/27 Dewi sells the shares for £320,000.\n\nWhat are the capital gains tax consequences?","o":["CGT was payable by Carys's estate on a gain of £250,000, and Dewi makes a gain of £20,000.","Dewi makes a gain of £270,000, using Carys's original acquisition cost.","No CGT is payable by anyone, because the shares were inherited.","Dewi makes no gain, because inherited assets are exempt assets.","No CGT arose on Carys's death, and Dewi makes a gain of £20,000."],"a":4,"e":"**Death is not a disposal** for CGT, so no CGT arises on Carys's death (although inheritance tax may be payable). The beneficiary acquires the asset at its **market value at the date of death** (probate value), so the gain built up during Carys's lifetime is wiped out. Dewi's gain is £320,000 − £300,000 = **£20,000**."},
 {"t":"cgt","q":"In 2026/27 Ezra has taxable income (after his personal allowance) of £32,700. He sells a let property (not residential) for a gain of £23,000, which does not qualify for any relief. He has no other disposals or losses. The annual exempt amount is £3,000, the basic rate band is £37,700, and the CGT rates are 18% on gains within the basic rate band and 24% above it.\n\nWhat is Ezra's capital gains tax liability?","o":["£4,800.","£3,600.","£4,500.","£5,520.","£4,140."],"a":2,"e":"The taxable gain is £23,000 − £3,000 annual exempt amount = £20,000, added **on top of** Ezra's taxable income. He has £37,700 − £32,700 = **£5,000** of basic rate band left, so £5,000 is taxed at **18%** (£900) and the remaining £15,000 at **24%** (£3,600): **£4,500**. Taxing all £20,000 at 24% gives £4,800, and forgetting the exempt amount gives higher figures."},
 {"t":"cgt","q":"Fatima has run a bakery as a sole trader for six years. In 2026/27 she sells the whole business, including the freehold shop and goodwill, as a going concern to a buyer, realising gains of £400,000. She has never claimed business asset disposal relief (BADR) before and is a higher rate taxpayer. For 2026/27 the BADR rate is 18% and the main CGT rates are 18% and 24%.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the tax treatment of her gains?","o":["BADR is available, so her gains (after the annual exempt amount) are taxed at 18%.","BADR is not available, because the business includes land.","BADR is available, so her gains are taxed at 10%.","BADR is not available, so her gains are taxed at 24% as a higher rate taxpayer.","BADR is available only on the goodwill, with the gain on the shop taxed at 24%."],"a":0,"e":"A disposal of the **whole or part of a business** that the individual has owned throughout the **two years** before the disposal is a qualifying disposal for **BADR**, which covers the gains on assets used in the business, including premises and goodwill, up to the **£1 million** lifetime limit. For disposals from 6 April 2026 the BADR rate is **18%**, which helps Fatima as a higher rate taxpayer, who would otherwise pay 24%. The 10% rate applied only before 6 April 2025."},
 {"t":"cgt","q":"Gareth owns 30% of the ordinary shares and votes in Harlow Ltd, an unquoted trading company, and has done so for ten years. He has never been a director or employee of the company, but is entitled to 30% of its profits and assets. He sells all his shares this year at a large gain.\n\nIs business asset disposal relief (BADR) available?","o":["Yes, because he holds at least 5% of the shares and votes in a trading company.","Yes, because he has held the shares for more than two years.","No, because BADR is available only on a disposal of the whole company.","No, because he has not been an officer or employee of the company.","Yes, but only on 5% of the shares sold."],"a":3,"e":"For BADR on shares, throughout the **two years** before the disposal the company must be a **trading company** and the individual's **personal company** (at least **5%** of the ordinary share capital and votes, plus an economic interest), **and** the individual must be an **officer or employee** of the company (or a group company). Gareth meets the shareholding conditions but has never been an officer or employee, so no BADR is available."},
 {"t":"cgt","q":"Hannah is retiring from Islay Ltd, an unquoted trading company, where she has been a director for 15 years. She sells her 25% shareholding, which qualifies for business asset disposal relief (BADR). At the same time, she sells the workshop she has owned personally for 12 years and has let the company use, rent-free, for its trade throughout that time.\n\nIs BADR available on the gain on the workshop?","o":["No, because BADR is available only on the disposal of shares, not on assets owned personally.","No, because the workshop was not owned by the company.","Yes, as an associated disposal made on her withdrawal from the business, with no rent charged.","Yes, but only if she sells the workshop to the company itself.","No, because the workshop was used by the company rent-free, which prevents relief."],"a":2,"e":"An **associated disposal** qualifies for BADR where an individual disposes of an asset owned personally and **used in the business** of their personal company, as part of their **withdrawal** from the business, alongside a qualifying disposal of at least **5%** of the company's shares. The asset must have been used in the business for the two years before (and owned for three years if acquired after 13 June 2016). Relief is **restricted** where **rent** was charged; Hannah charged none, so full relief is available."},
 {"t":"cgt","q":"Ivor, a sole trader, sold his freehold workshop in May 2022 at a gain of £120,000. He used the full proceeds to buy a new freehold workshop for use in the same trade in November 2025.\n\nCan Ivor claim rollover relief on the gain?","o":["No, because the new workshop was bought more than three years later.","Yes, because both assets are land and buildings used in his trade.","Yes, because he reinvested all the proceeds.","No, because rollover relief is available only to companies.","Yes, but the gain is only held over for ten years."],"a":0,"e":"**Rollover relief** requires the new qualifying asset to be acquired within the period beginning **one year before** and ending **three years after** the disposal of the old asset (TCGA 1992 s.152). The old workshop was sold in May 2022, so the new one had to be bought by May 2025. Buying it in November 2025 is too late, even though both are qualifying assets and all the proceeds were reinvested. Rollover relief is available to individuals as well as companies."},
 {"t":"cgt","q":"Jade transfers her sole trader business as a going concern, with all its assets, to a new company, Jade Designs Ltd. The business is worth £500,000 and the transfer realises gains of £200,000. In return, the company issues her shares worth £400,000 and credits £100,000 to a loan account in her name. She claims incorporation relief.\n\nHow much of the gain is chargeable now?","o":["Nil.","£100,000.","£160,000.","£40,000.","£200,000."],"a":3,"e":"**Incorporation relief** (TCGA 1992 s.162) defers the gain on transferring a business to a company as a going concern with all its assets, but only to the extent that the consideration is **shares**. The deferred gain is £200,000 × (£400,000 ÷ £500,000) = **£160,000**, which is deducted from the base cost of her shares. The balance of **£40,000**, matching the loan account, is chargeable now."},
 {"t":"corporation-tax","q":"Kestrel Ltd has one associated company. For its 12-month accounting period ending 31 March 2027 it has taxable total profits of £40,000. With no associated companies, the small profits rate of 19% applies to profits up to £50,000 and the main rate of 25% to profits over £250,000, with marginal relief between those limits calculated as 3/200 × (upper limit − profits).\n\nWhat is Kestrel Ltd's corporation tax liability?","o":["£7,600.","£10,000.","£9,325.","£8,500.","£8,725."],"a":4,"e":"The limits are **divided equally** between associated companies, so for Kestrel the lower limit is **£25,000** and the upper limit **£125,000**. Profits of £40,000 fall in the marginal relief band: tax at 25% is £10,000, less marginal relief of 3/200 × (£125,000 − £40,000) = £1,275, giving **£8,725**. Applying 19% (£7,600) ignores the associated company."},
 {"t":"corporation-tax","q":"Larkspur Ltd prepared one set of accounts for an 18-month period from 1 January 2026 to 30 June 2027.\n\nHow will this period be treated for corporation tax purposes?","o":["As one accounting period of 18 months.","As two accounting periods: 1 January 2026 to 31 December 2026, and 1 January 2027 to 30 June 2027.","As two accounting periods of nine months each.","As two accounting periods: 1 January 2026 to 31 March 2026, and 1 April 2026 to 30 June 2027.","As one accounting period ending 31 March 2027, with the remaining profit added to the next period."],"a":1,"e":"An **accounting period** for corporation tax can **never exceed 12 months**. Where a period of account is longer, it is split into an accounting period of the **first 12 months** and a second accounting period for the **remainder**: here 1 January to 31 December 2026, and 1 January to 30 June 2027. Financial years (starting 1 April) matter only for apportioning profits between rates."},
 {"t":"corporation-tax","q":"In 2026, Moorhen Ltd sells a warehouse used in its trade for £600,000. It bought the warehouse in 2012 for £250,000. The indexation allowance from 2012 to December 2017 is £30,000. It does not reinvest the proceeds.\n\nWhat is Moorhen Ltd's chargeable gain?","o":["£347,000, after deducting the annual exempt amount.","£350,000, because companies get no indexation allowance.","£317,000, after deducting the annual exempt amount from the indexed gain.","£320,000, taxed at the capital gains tax rate of 24%.","£320,000, taxed as part of its profits at the corporation tax rate."],"a":4,"e":"A company's chargeable gain is computed like an individual's, but it may deduct an **indexation allowance**, which is **frozen at December 2017**, and it has **no annual exempt amount**. So the gain is £600,000 − £250,000 − £30,000 = **£320,000**. Companies do not pay CGT: the gain forms part of their **total profits** and is taxed at the **corporation tax** rate."},
 {"t":"corporation-tax","q":"In its latest accounting period, Nettle Ltd paid: directors' salaries of £120,000 with employer's NICs on them of £17,000; dividends of £50,000 to its shareholders; £6,000 entertaining prospective customers; and a £2,000 fine for breaching health and safety rules.\n\nWhich of these payments are deductible in calculating Nettle Ltd's trading profits?","o":["The directors' salaries, employer's NICs and dividends.","The directors' salaries, employer's NICs and customer entertaining.","The directors' salaries and the employer's NICs only.","All of the payments, because they were all incurred by the company.","The directors' salaries only."],"a":2,"e":"Expenditure is deductible only if it is **revenue** in nature and incurred **wholly and exclusively** for the purposes of the trade (CTA 2009 s.54). **Salaries** and **employer's NICs** are deductible. **Dividends** are a distribution of profit, not an expense, so are not deductible. **Business entertainment** of customers and **fines** for breaking the law are specifically not deductible."},
 {"t":"corporation-tax","q":"Osprey Ltd, a trading company, has held 15% of the ordinary shares in Plover Ltd, also a trading company, for the last three years. Osprey Ltd now sells the whole holding at a gain of £500,000.\n\nHow will the gain be taxed?","o":["It will be exempt under the substantial shareholding exemption.","It will be taxed at the main corporation tax rate of 25%.","It will be taxed as a capital gain at 24%, after the annual exempt amount.","It will qualify for business asset disposal relief at 18%.","It will be exempt only if Osprey Ltd reinvests the proceeds in other shares within three years."],"a":0,"e":"The **substantial shareholding exemption** makes a company's gain on selling shares **exempt** if it has held at least **10%** of the ordinary share capital for a continuous **12-month** period in the **six years** before the sale, and the company sold is a **trading company** (TCGA 1992 Sch.7AC). Osprey's 15% held for three years in a trading company qualifies. BADR, CGT rates and the annual exempt amount apply to individuals, not companies."},
 {"t":"corporation-tax","q":"Quail Ltd owns 70% of the ordinary shares in Robin Ltd. In the year to 31 March 2027, Robin Ltd made a trading loss of £100,000, and Quail Ltd made taxable profits of £300,000. The directors of both companies want Robin Ltd's loss to reduce Quail Ltd's tax bill for that year.\n\nCan Robin Ltd surrender its loss to Quail Ltd?","o":["Yes, because Quail Ltd controls Robin Ltd by holding more than 50% of its shares.","Yes, but only 70% of the loss can be surrendered.","No, because group relief can only be claimed for capital losses.","No, because group relief requires at least a 75% shareholding.","Yes, provided the loss is first carried back against Robin Ltd's profits of the previous year."],"a":3,"e":"**Group relief** lets a company surrender current-period trading losses to another company in the same **75% group**: one must own (directly or indirectly) at least **75%** of the other's ordinary share capital, with corresponding rights to profits and assets, or both must be 75% subsidiaries of a third company. A 70% holding is not enough, so no part of the loss can be surrendered, even though Quail controls Robin."},
 {"t":"corporation-tax","q":"Sorrel Ltd is not a large company and has an accounting period ending 31 December 2026. It has no associated companies.\n\nWhen must it pay its corporation tax for that period and file its company tax return?","o":["Pay by 31 December 2027 and file the return by 1 October 2027.","Pay and file by 31 January 2028.","Pay by 1 October 2027 and file the return by 31 December 2027.","Pay in quarterly instalments during 2026 and file by 31 December 2027.","Pay by 30 September 2027 and file the return by 30 June 2027."],"a":2,"e":"A company that is not \"large\" must pay its corporation tax **9 months and 1 day** after the end of the accounting period, so by **1 October 2027**. The company tax return (**CT600**) must be filed within **12 months** after the end of the accounting period, so by **31 December 2027**. Quarterly instalments apply only to large companies, and 31 January is the self-assessment date for individuals."},
 {"t":"corporation-tax","q":"Tansy Ltd ceased trading on 31 March 2027. In its final 12-month accounting period it made a trading loss of £180,000 and had no other income or gains. In each of the previous three accounting periods it made taxable total profits of £70,000.\n\nHow can Tansy Ltd obtain relief for the loss?","o":["By terminal loss relief, carrying it back three years against total profits, latest first.","By carrying the loss back against total profits of the previous 12 months only, with the rest lost.","By carrying the loss forward against future profits of the same trade.","By carrying the loss back against total profits of the previous three years, earliest year first.","By setting the loss against the shareholders' income from the company."],"a":0,"e":"A company's trading loss can normally be set against the current period's total profits and then **carried back 12 months** (CTA 2010 s.37). But a loss of the **final 12 months** of trading qualifies for **terminal loss relief**, which extends the carry-back to **three years**, taking the **latest year first** (s.39). Carrying forward is useless because the trade has ceased, and company losses cannot be set against shareholders' income."},
 {"t":"corporation-tax","q":"In June 2026, Umber Ltd, a trading company, spent £300,000 on new and unused manufacturing machinery (main-rate plant and machinery, not cars) for use in its trade. It has made no other capital expenditure this year.\n\nWhat is the most beneficial capital allowance claim available to Umber Ltd for its accounting period ending 31 December 2026?","o":["A writing down allowance of 14%, giving £42,000.","A writing down allowance of 18%, giving £54,000.","A first-year allowance of 40%, giving £120,000, with no other option.","A 100% deduction of £300,000 under full expensing.","No allowance, because the machinery is capital expenditure."],"a":3,"e":"Capital expenditure is not deductible as an expense, but **capital allowances** give relief for plant and machinery. A company buying **new and unused main-rate plant and machinery** can claim **full expensing**, a **100%** first-year deduction, and the £300,000 is also within the **£1 million annual investment allowance**. Either way, the whole £300,000 is deducted this period. The 14% writing down allowance applies only to remaining pool balances."},
 {"t":"corporation-tax","q":"Viola Ltd is a close company. In May 2026 it lent £30,000 to Wren, a 40% shareholder who is not an employee or director. The company paid a tax charge under s.455 on the loan because it was not repaid within nine months of the end of the accounting period. The company has now written the loan off.\n\nWhat are the tax consequences of the write-off?","o":["Wren has no tax to pay, and the company can deduct the £30,000 as a trading expense.","Wren is taxed on £30,000 as dividend income, and the s.455 tax becomes repayable.","Wren is taxed on £30,000 as employment income, and the company keeps paying the s.455 tax.","Wren is taxed on £30,000 as a capital gain, and the company loses the s.455 tax.","Neither is taxed further, because the s.455 tax has already been paid."],"a":1,"e":"Where a close company **writes off** a loan to a participator, the participator is taxed on the amount as if it were **dividend income** (ITTOIA 2005 s.415). The s.455 charge is a temporary tax that is **repayable** when the loan is repaid, released or written off. The write-off is not a deductible expense for the company. Wren is not an employee, so it is not employment income."},
 {"t":"vat-iht-bpr","q":"Adrian dies in November 2026. His estate includes his share in a trading partnership, worth £3 million, which he has owned for 12 years. He made no lifetime gifts and has no transferred allowance from a spouse. Under the rules for transfers from 6 April 2026, 100% business property relief applies to the first £2.5 million of qualifying property and 50% relief to the excess.\n\nWhat value of the partnership share remains chargeable to inheritance tax after business property relief?","o":["Nil.","£250,000.","£500,000.","£1,500,000.","£3,000,000."],"a":1,"e":"A **partnership share** is an interest in a business, which qualifies for **100%** BPR, but since 6 April 2026 100% relief is limited to the first **£2.5 million** of qualifying property. The excess £500,000 gets **50%** relief, leaving **£250,000** chargeable (before applying any available nil-rate band). Ignoring the allowance gives nil, and giving no relief on the excess gives £500,000."},
 {"t":"vat-iht-bpr","q":"Bianca started a catering business in 2025 and is not registered for VAT. Her taxable turnover so far has been small. On 10 May 2027 she signs a contract to cater a festival, under which she will make standard-rated supplies of £95,000 within the next 30 days.\n\nWhat must Bianca do?","o":["Notify HMRC within 30 days of the end of May 2027, and she will be registered from 1 July 2027.","Nothing until her taxable turnover for the previous 12 months exceeds £90,000 at a month end.","Register voluntarily if she wishes, as registration is optional for a single contract.","Notify HMRC within 30 days of completing the contract, and she will be registered from that date.","Notify HMRC within the 30-day period starting 10 May 2027, and be registered from 10 May 2027."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **future test**, a person must register if at any time there are reasonable grounds to believe that the value of taxable supplies in the **next 30 days alone** will exceed **£90,000**. Bianca must notify HMRC by the **end of that 30-day period**, and she is registered from the **beginning** of the period (10 May 2027). The historic test, based on a rolling 12 months, is separate and gives a later date."},
 {"t":"vat-iht-bpr","q":"Cedric runs a VAT-registered building supplies business making only standard-rated supplies. In a VAT quarter he charged output tax of £20,000. He paid VAT of £8,000 on stock, £4,000 on a new delivery van, £600 on entertaining prospective customers, and £5,000 on a car for his sales manager, who also uses it privately. He holds valid VAT invoices for all of them.\n\nHow much VAT must Cedric pay to HMRC for the quarter?","o":["£2,400.","£3,000.","£8,000.","£7,400.","£20,000."],"a":2,"e":"A registered business pays **output tax less recoverable input tax**. VAT on **stock** (£8,000) and the **van** (£4,000) is recoverable, totalling £12,000. VAT on **business entertainment** of customers and on a **car** that is not used exclusively for business is **blocked** and cannot be recovered. So Cedric pays £20,000 − £12,000 = **£8,000**. Recovering all the input tax would give £2,400."},
 {"t":"vat-iht-bpr","q":"Dara, a VAT-registered plumber, agrees to fit a new boiler for a homeowner for \"£1,200\". Nothing was said about VAT, and the supply is standard-rated at 20%.\n\nHow much VAT must Dara account for, and how much of the price does he keep?","o":["VAT of £200, keeping £1,000.","VAT of £240, keeping £1,200, as he can add VAT to the agreed price.","VAT of £240, keeping £960.","No VAT, because the homeowner is not VAT-registered.","VAT of £200, keeping £1,200, as the homeowner must pay the VAT on top."],"a":0,"e":"VAT is charged on the **consideration** for the supply, and where a price is agreed with no mention of VAT it is treated as **VAT-inclusive**. The VAT element at 20% is **1/6** of the inclusive price (20/120), so £1,200 ÷ 6 = **£200**, and Dara keeps £1,000. He cannot add VAT on top of the agreed price, and the customer's status does not affect whether VAT is due."},
 {"t":"vat-iht-bpr","q":"Elan, a VAT-registered consultant, completed a piece of advisory work for a client on 10 June. He issued a VAT invoice on 20 June, and the client paid on 15 July. Elan has made no election about the 14-day rule.\n\nWhat is the tax point for the supply?","o":["10 June, because services are supplied when they are performed.","15 July, because VAT is due only when payment is received.","30 June, being the end of the month in which the invoice was issued.","20 June, because the invoice was issued within 14 days after performance.","24 June, being 14 days after the services were performed."],"a":3,"e":"The **basic tax point** for services is when they are **performed** (10 June). But if a **VAT invoice** is issued **within 14 days after** the basic tax point, and no earlier tax point has arisen through an earlier invoice or payment, the **invoice date** becomes the **actual tax point**. The invoice was issued on 20 June, within 14 days, so that is the tax point. Payment on 15 July is irrelevant because it came later."},
 {"t":"vat-iht-bpr","q":"Faye, a VAT-registered sole trader, is selling her shop business as a going concern, including its stock, equipment and goodwill, to Gil, who is VAT-registered and will carry on the same kind of business from the same premises. The assets would normally be standard-rated.\n\nMust Faye charge VAT on the sale?","o":["Yes, on the whole price at the standard rate, because the assets are standard-rated.","Yes, but only on the stock and equipment, not the goodwill.","No, because a transfer of a going concern is outside the scope of VAT.","No, because the sale of a business is an exempt supply.","Yes, but Gil can reclaim it, so the question of VAT is irrelevant."],"a":2,"e":"The sale of a business (or part of it) as a **transfer of a going concern** (TOGC) to a buyer who is or becomes VAT-registered and uses the assets to carry on the **same kind of business** is treated as **neither a supply of goods nor services**, so it is outside the scope of VAT. This is not the same as an exempt supply, and no VAT should be charged on any part of the price."},
 {"t":"vat-iht-bpr","q":"Hector dies in December 2026. His estate includes shares in a trading company traded on the Alternative Investment Market (AIM), which are not listed on a recognised stock exchange. They are worth £400,000, and he has owned them for five years. He made no lifetime gifts.\n\nWhat business property relief is available on the AIM shares?","o":["50% relief, giving relief of £200,000.","100% relief, giving relief of £400,000.","No relief, because the shares are traded on a stock market.","100% relief, but only if Hector owned more than 25% of the company.","50% relief, but only if Hector controlled the company."],"a":0,"e":"Shares **admitted to trading** on a recognised stock exchange but **not listed**, such as **AIM** shares, attract **50%** business property relief for transfers on or after **6 April 2026** (previously 100%), whatever the size of the holding. Hector's shares give relief of £200,000. Control is required for 50% relief only on **listed** shares."},
 {"t":"vat-iht-bpr","q":"Isla dies in October 2026. She owned 40% of the shares in Juniper Ltd, an unquoted trading company, worth £600,000. She also owned personally the factory used by Juniper Ltd for its trade, worth £400,000. She had owned both for over ten years and made no lifetime gifts. No one else connected to her held shares.\n\nWhat business property relief is available?","o":["100% relief on the shares and 50% relief on the factory.","50% relief on both the shares and the factory.","100% relief on both the shares and the factory.","100% relief on the shares and no relief on the factory.","No relief on the shares and 50% relief on the factory."],"a":3,"e":"**Unquoted shares** in a trading company qualify for **100%** relief whatever the size of the holding, and £600,000 is well within the £2.5 million allowance. But land or buildings owned personally and used by a **company** qualify for **50%** relief only if the transferor **controlled** the company (more than 50% of the votes). With 40%, Isla did not, so the factory gets **no relief**."},
 {"t":"vat-iht-bpr","q":"Kenji dies in November 2026 owning all the shares in Kenji Estates Ltd, an unquoted company he has owned for 20 years. The company's business consists almost entirely of letting residential flats to tenants on long-term tenancies and collecting the rents.\n\nWhat business property relief is available on the shares?","o":["100% relief, because the shares are unquoted.","None, because the company's business is mainly holding investments.","50% relief, because Kenji controlled the company.","100% relief on the first £2.5 million and 50% on the excess.","100% relief, because Kenji had owned the shares for more than two years."],"a":1,"e":"No business property relief is available for a business, or shares in a company whose business, consists **wholly or mainly** of dealing in land or securities or **making or holding investments** (IHTA 1984 s.105(3)). Letting property to tenants and collecting rent is holding investments, so the shares get **no relief**, even though they are unquoted and were owned for over two years."},
 {"t":"vat-iht-bpr","q":"Nia's husband, Osian, bought 20% of the shares in Pentre Ltd, an unquoted trading company, in 2015. He died in March 2026 and left the shares to Nia. Nia died in December 2026, nine months later, leaving the shares to her daughter. Neither made any lifetime gifts.\n\nIs business property relief available on the shares in Nia's estate?","o":["No, because Nia owned the shares for less than two years.","No, because business property relief was already given on Osian's death.","Yes, but only at 50%, because Nia owned the shares for less than two years.","No, because a 20% holding is not a controlling interest.","Yes, because Osian's period of ownership counts towards the two-year condition."],"a":4,"e":"Business property relief normally requires the transferor to have **owned** the property for the **two years** before the transfer (IHTA 1984 s.106). But where the transferor inherited the property on the death of their **spouse or civil partner**, the spouse's period of ownership is **added** to their own (s.108). Osian owned the shares from 2015, so the condition is met and the unquoted shares get **100%** relief, whatever the size of the holding. Relief is not lost simply because the shares passed between spouses."}
];

;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["dr"] = [
 {"t": "dr-options", "q": "Willow Interiors Ltd has supplied furniture to a hotel group, Brackley Hotels Ltd, for twelve years. The hotel group says the latest delivery was late and is withholding £35,000. Both companies want to keep trading together, want a quick and cheap outcome kept out of the public eye, and Brackley Hotels Ltd would accept a discount on future orders and a written apology. There is no arbitration clause.\n\nWhich method of dispute resolution best meets the parties' aims?", "o": ["Litigation, because a judge can order an apology and a discount on future orders.", "Arbitration, because an arbitrator can be appointed without the agreement of both parties.", "Mediation, because it is quick, private and can produce terms a court could not order.", "Expert determination, because an expert's binding decision will preserve the relationship.", "Early neutral evaluation, because the evaluator's view will bind both companies."], "a": 2, "e": "Mediation is voluntary, confidential and cheap, tends to preserve commercial relationships, and lets the parties agree outcomes a court cannot order, such as an apology or a future discount. A court gives a money judgment, not an apology. Arbitration needs both parties' agreement, and early neutral evaluation is non-binding by definition."},
 {"t": "dr-options", "q": "Hana runs a design agency. She has just learned that Owen, a designer who left her firm last week, is about to email her client list and confidential pricing to a competitor. Her contract with Owen contains no arbitration clause. She wants the disclosure stopped within days.\n\nWhich route should Hana's solicitor recommend?", "o": ["Litigation, because the court can grant an urgent interim injunction.", "Mediation, because a mediator can order Owen not to disclose the information.", "Arbitration, because an arbitrator can be appointed without Owen's agreement.", "Negotiation, because a without prejudice letter will bind Owen not to disclose.", "Expert determination, because an expert can decide the matter within days."], "a": 0, "e": "Only the court has full coercive powers, including urgent interim injunctions, which can even be granted before a claim is issued. A mediator cannot impose any outcome, and arbitration (and expert determination) needs the parties' agreement, which Owen has not given. A without prejudice letter binds nobody."},
 {"t": "dr-options", "q": "A supply contract between Corran Ltd and Duval SA provides for arbitration seated in London under the LCIA Rules. The tribunal has made its award against Corran Ltd. Corran Ltd believes the tribunal misinterpreted a clause of the contract, which is governed by English law. The hearing was conducted fairly.\n\nCan Corran Ltd appeal to the court on the point of law?", "o": ["Yes, because an appeal on a point of English law is always available, without any need for permission.", "Yes, because a misinterpretation of the contract is a serious irregularity.", "Yes, but only within six months of the award, with the court's permission.", "No, because an arbitration award can never be challenged in the courts.", "No, because choosing the LCIA Rules usually excludes the right to appeal on a point of law."], "a": 4, "e": "An appeal on a point of law (s.69 AA 1996) needs all parties' agreement or the court's leave, and the parties can exclude it; adopting institutional rules such as the LCIA Rules usually does exclude it. A wrong reading of a contract is an error of law, not a serious irregularity (s.68), which concerns unfair process. Awards can be challenged in limited cases, but any challenge must be brought within 28 days."},
 {"t": "dr-options", "q": "An arbitral tribunal made its final award against Ferris Ltd on 2 March. Ferris Ltd now believes the tribunal refused to let it respond to new evidence produced by the other side at the hearing, which caused it substantial injustice. There is no arbitral process of appeal or review. It is 20 April.\n\nWhat is the position on Ferris Ltd's proposed challenge?", "o": ["The challenge is in time, because a challenge may be brought within three months.", "The challenge is out of time unless the court extends the 28-day limit.", "The challenge is in time, because time runs only from when Ferris Ltd took advice.", "The challenge must be made to the tribunal, which may reopen its findings of fact.", "The challenge is barred, because a serious irregularity can never be raised in court."], "a": 1, "e": "A challenge for serious irregularity causing substantial injustice (s.68 AA 1996) is possible, but any court challenge must be brought within 28 days of the award (s.70(3)). By 20 April that period has passed, so Ferris Ltd needs an extension from the court. Time does not run from when advice was taken, and the court does not reopen the tribunal's findings of fact."},
 {"t": "dr-options", "q": "Gwen is appointed sole arbitrator in an arbitration that began in October 2025 between Halcyon Ltd and Ives Ltd. Halcyon Ltd later discovers that Gwen did paid consultancy work for Ives Ltd for several years until shortly before her appointment. She never mentioned this.\n\nWhich statement best describes Gwen's position?", "o": ["She owed no duty of disclosure, because the parties could and should have investigated her past work themselves.", "She had to disclose only matters of which she was actually aware on the date of her appointment, and nothing later.", "She owed a duty of disclosure only if the arbitration agreement expressly imposed one.", "She had a statutory duty to disclose anything that might reasonably cause justifiable doubts about her impartiality.", "She owed a duty of disclosure only once the hearing had started, not before her appointment."], "a": 3, "e": "For arbitrations started on or after 1 August 2025, the Arbitration Act 2025 (new s.23A AA 1996) puts arbitrators under a statutory duty to disclose circumstances that might reasonably give rise to justifiable doubts about their impartiality. The duty applies before appointment and throughout, and covers matters they ought reasonably to be aware of, not only actual knowledge. It does not depend on the arbitration agreement."},
 {"t": "dr-options", "q": "In an arbitration seated in London which began in January 2026, the claimant, Jessop Ltd, has brought a claim for unpaid invoices. The respondent's only defence is a set-off which, on the documents, has no realistic chance of success. The arbitration agreement says nothing about early determination. Jessop Ltd wants the defence dealt with quickly.\n\nWhat can Jessop Ltd do?", "o": ["Apply to the court for summary judgment, because a tribunal has no power to decide issues early.", "Nothing, because a tribunal must always hold a full hearing on every defence raised.", "Apply to the court to strike out the defence, because the court closely supervises all of the tribunal's procedure.", "Apply to the tribunal to decide the defence summarily on the basis that it has no real prospect of succeeding.", "Ask an emergency arbitrator to give a final award, which cannot be challenged."], "a": 3, "e": "Since the Arbitration Act 2025 (new s.39A AA 1996), unless the parties agree otherwise, the tribunal may on a party's application decide a claim, defence or issue summarily if that party has no real prospect of succeeding, after giving the parties a reasonable opportunity to make representations. The court does not decide the merits of an arbitrated dispute, and an emergency arbitrator deals with urgent interim matters, not final awards."},
 {"t": "dr-options", "q": "Kezia and Lomax Ltd attend a mediation of Kezia's claim for unpaid commission. At the end of the day they shake hands on a payment of £18,000, and the mediator says he will draft the written settlement agreement. The mediation agreement provides that no settlement is binding until written and signed. The next morning Lomax Ltd says it will pay only £12,000.\n\nWhat is Kezia's position?", "o": ["She can enforce the £18,000 settlement as a binding oral contract, because the parties shook hands on it.", "She has no binding settlement, because nothing is binding until a written settlement agreement is signed.", "She can ask the mediator to make a binding award of £18,000.", "She can rely at trial on Lomax Ltd's agreement to pay £18,000 as an admission of liability.", "She can apply for a consent order in the agreed terms without Lomax Ltd's agreement."], "a": 1, "e": "A mediator cannot impose an outcome, and a mediated settlement is binding only once the written settlement agreement is signed, as the mediation agreement here makes clear. What is said at mediation is without prejudice, so the offer cannot be used at trial as an admission. A consent order requires both parties' consent."},
 {"t": "dr-options", "q": "Moss Ltd issued a defended specified money claim for £4,200 against Nadia, which was allocated to the small claims track and automatically referred to the free one-hour telephone mediation service. Nadia did not join the mediation call and has given no reason.\n\nWhat is the most likely consequence for Nadia?", "o": ["None, because mediation is always voluntary and attendance cannot be required.", "The claim will be transferred automatically to the fast track.", "Moss Ltd must arrange and pay for a private mediation instead.", "Nadia will be ordered to pay Moss Ltd's full legal costs on the indemnity basis.", "The court may impose a sanction, which can include striking out her defence."], "a": 4, "e": "Since 22 May 2024 most defended specified money claims on the small claims track are automatically referred to the HMCTS Small Claims Mediation Service, and attendance is compulsory. A party who fails to attend without good reason can face sanctions, including having its claim or defence struck out. Costs on the small claims track are very limited, so full indemnity costs is not the likely order."},
 {"t": "dr-options", "q": "Pryce Ltd sues Quarry Homes Ltd for £300,000 over defective building work. At the first case management conference the judge proposes to stay the claim for two months and order the parties to attend mediation. Pryce Ltd objects that it has an absolute right to a trial and cannot be made to mediate.\n\nWhich statement best describes the court's power?", "o": ["The court cannot order ADR; it can only penalise in costs a party who unreasonably refuses it.", "The court can order ADR only if both parties consent, because mediation must always be voluntary.", "The court can order ADR if the order is proportionate and does not impair the essence of the right to a trial.", "The court can order ADR only in claims allocated to the small claims track, through its mediation service.", "The court can order ADR and enter judgment against any party that fails to reach a settlement."], "a": 2, "e": "The Court of Appeal decided in 2023 (Churchill) that a court can lawfully order parties to engage in non-court dispute resolution, or stay proceedings for that purpose, if the order is proportionate and does not impair the very essence of the right to a fair trial. From 1 October 2024 the CPR expressly allow this (CPR 3.1) and the overriding objective includes promoting ADR. Nobody can be forced to settle, and failing to settle does not lead to judgment."},
 {"t": "dr-options", "q": "Rafferty Ltd is a subcontractor on a construction project. The main contractor has refused to pay a £90,000 interim payment, alleging defects. Rafferty Ltd needs a fast decision so that it is paid now, and accepts that the dispute may later be finally decided by a court.\n\nWhich process best meets Rafferty Ltd's needs?", "o": ["Mediation, because the mediator will decide within 28 days and the decision is final.", "Early neutral evaluation, because the evaluator's opinion must be paid within 28 days.", "Adjudication, because it gives a fast decision that is binding until the dispute is finally determined.", "Arbitration under a Tomlin order, because the court will stay the claim on confidential terms.", "An ombudsman complaint, because ombudsmen decide commercial construction payment disputes free of charge."], "a": 2, "e": "Adjudication is the fast (usually 28-day) process used in construction disputes; the decision is binding until the dispute is finally decided by litigation or arbitration, so the contractor must 'pay now, argue later'. A mediator decides nothing, and early neutral evaluation is non-binding. Ombudsman schemes deal with consumer complaints, not commercial construction disputes."},
 {"t": "dr-limitation", "q": "In June 2016 Sefton Ltd completed an extension for Talia under a building contract executed as a deed. In August 2026 Talia discovers that the steelwork does not comply with the contract specification, which was a breach when the work was done. She wants to sue for breach of contract.\n\nIs Talia's contract claim in time?", "o": ["No, because the limitation period for any breach of contract claim is 6 years from the breach.", "Yes, because the limitation period for a contract made by deed is 12 years from the breach.", "Yes, because time ran only from when she discovered the breach in August 2026.", "No, because the 15-year longstop runs from completion of the work.", "Yes, but only if the court exercises its discretion to disapply the limitation period."], "a": 1, "e": "A claim on a contract made by deed (a specialty) has a 12-year limitation period from the breach (LA 1980 s.8), so Talia has until June 2028. The 6-year period applies to simple contracts only. Discovery is irrelevant in contract, the 15-year longstop applies to latent damage in negligence, and the court's discretion to disapply is only for personal injury claims."},
 {"t": "dr-limitation", "q": "In March 2018 an architect negligently designed the drainage for Uma's new house. The drainage worked until February 2021, when it failed and flooded the house, causing immediate and obvious damage. Uma issued a claim in negligence against the architect in October 2026.\n\nIs Uma's negligence claim in time?", "o": ["No, because time ran from the negligent design in March 2018 and expired in March 2024.", "No, because the claim had to be brought within 3 years of the flood.", "Yes, because the longstop of 15 years from the flood has not expired.", "No, because a claim in negligence against a professional is a contract claim.", "Yes, because time in negligence runs from when the damage occurred in February 2021."], "a": 4, "e": "Negligence is not complete until damage occurs, so the 6-year period (LA 1980 s.2) runs from February 2021 and ends in February 2027. A contract claim against the architect would have expired 6 years after the breach in 2018, but Uma can sue in tort. The 3-year period is for personal injury or latent damage knowledge, and the 15-year longstop runs from the negligent act, not the damage."},
 {"t": "dr-limitation", "q": "Viktor worked in a joinery workshop from 2015 to 2022. He had a mild cough from 2020 but thought it unimportant. On 14 March 2024 a consultant diagnosed a serious lung condition and told him it was caused by dust exposure at the workshop. He wants to sue his former employer.\n\nWhat is the latest date by which Viktor should issue his claim?", "o": ["14 March 2027, three years from his date of knowledge.", "The third anniversary of when his mild cough began in 2020.", "The third anniversary of the date he left the workshop in 2022.", "14 March 2030, six years from his date of knowledge.", "There is no deadline, because the court will always disapply the period for a disease."], "a": 0, "e": "A personal injury claim must be brought within 3 years of the later of the injury and the claimant's date of knowledge (LA 1980 ss.11 and 14). Knowledge requires knowing the injury was significant and attributable to the defendant's acts, which Viktor first knew on 14 March 2024. The 6-year period is for non-PI claims, and the court's discretion (s.33) is never automatic."},
 {"t": "dr-limitation", "q": "On 1 June 2021 Wanda was injured when a shop's sign fell on her. She knew immediately that the injury was serious, that it was caused by the badly fixed sign and that the shop owner was responsible. She did not realise she could sue until a friend told her in April 2025. She issues a claim in May 2026.\n\nWhich statement best describes the limitation position?", "o": ["The claim is in time, because her date of knowledge was April 2025.", "The claim is in time, because the period for injuries caused by a defective structure is 6 years.", "The claim is out of time, but the court may allow it to proceed if that would be equitable.", "The claim is out of time and the court has no power to allow it to proceed.", "The claim is in time, because time does not run until the claimant takes legal advice."], "a": 2, "e": "Wanda knew all the relevant facts on 1 June 2021, so the 3-year period expired on 1 June 2024; not knowing she had a legal claim is ignorance of the law, which does not postpone the date of knowledge. Because this is a personal injury claim, the court has a discretion to disapply the period if it would be equitable (LA 1980 s.33), weighing the delay, its reasons and its effect on the evidence."},
 {"t": "dr-limitation", "q": "Xander's company ordered a printing machine under an ordinary written contract. The seller delivered a defective machine on 10 May 2020. Xander's solicitor overlooked the file and issued the contract claim on 17 May 2026. The defendant pleads limitation. Xander asks whether the court can allow the claim to continue because the delay was only a week.\n\nWhat is the correct advice?", "o": ["The court may disapply the period because the delay was short and caused no prejudice.", "The claim is in time because the limitation period runs from discovery of the defect.", "The claim is in time because the period ends 6 years after the end of the month of breach.", "The claim is time-barred, as there is no discretion to extend the limitation period for a contract claim.", "The court will extend time, because the solicitor's oversight was not Xander's own fault and he should not suffer."], "a": 3, "e": "A simple contract claim must be issued within 6 years of the breach (LA 1980 s.5), here by 10 May 2026. The court's discretion to disapply the limitation period (s.33) is available only for personal injury and fatal accident claims, so a one-week delay in a contract claim is fatal once limitation is pleaded. Xander may have a claim against his solicitor instead."},
 {"t": "dr-limitation", "q": "Yew Supplies Ltd sold goods to Zeller Ltd and the price of £40,000 fell due on 1 June 2019. Zeller Ltd paid nothing, but on 5 February 2024 its finance director signed and sent a letter saying, 'We accept we owe you £40,000 and will pay when we can.' Yew Supplies Ltd issued a claim for the debt on 1 October 2026.\n\nIs the claim in time?", "o": ["No, because the 6-year period ran from June 2019 and expired in June 2025.", "Yes, because the signed written acknowledgment restarted the 6-year period from February 2024.", "Yes, because time for a debt claim runs only from the creditor's final demand.", "No, because an acknowledgment of a debt must be accompanied by a part payment.", "Yes, because the limitation period for recovering a commercial debt is 12 years from when it fell due."], "a": 1, "e": "A written acknowledgment of a debt signed by the debtor, made before the limitation period expires, restarts the period from the date of the acknowledgment (LA 1980 ss.29-30). The original period would have ended in June 2025, but the February 2024 letter started a fresh 6 years. An acknowledgment alone is enough; a part payment is an alternative, not an extra requirement."},
 {"t": "dr-limitation", "q": "Abbott & Co supplied services to Bella and invoiced £12,000, due on 1 March 2018. Bella never paid. On 1 May 2025 Bella emailed and signed a letter admitting that she owed the full £12,000. Abbott & Co now wants to sue.\n\nCan Abbott & Co bring a claim for the debt?", "o": ["Yes, because the acknowledgment restarted the 6-year period from May 2025.", "Yes, because a debt claim has no limitation period once the debtor admits it.", "Yes, but only if the court exercises its discretion to disapply the limitation period.", "No, because the limitation period expired in March 2024 and a later acknowledgment cannot revive it.", "No, because an acknowledgment sent by email is never effective for limitation purposes, even if signed."], "a": 3, "e": "The 6-year contract period ran from 1 March 2018 and expired in March 2024. An acknowledgment or part payment restarts time only if it is made before the period expires; once a debt claim is time-barred it cannot be revived. The discretion to disapply is only for personal injury claims."},
 {"t": "dr-limitation", "q": "In 2016 Cotton Builders Ltd built a house for Dilys. Its site manager knew the foundations were too shallow but covered them over and told Dilys they complied with the plans. Dilys discovered the problem in April 2025, when she first had any reason to investigate, and wants to sue.\n\nWhen did the limitation period start to run?", "o": ["In 2016, because time in contract runs from the breach even if the breach is undiscovered.", "In April 2025, because a relevant fact was deliberately concealed by the defendant.", "In 2016, because deliberate concealment only affects personal injury claims.", "When the foundations first caused visible damage, whenever that was.", "When Dilys first took legal advice about her claim."], "a": 1, "e": "Where a relevant fact has been deliberately concealed by the defendant, time does not start until the claimant discovered the concealment or could with reasonable diligence have discovered it (LA 1980 s.32). Covering over defects known to be in breach is deliberate concealment, so time runs from April 2025. Section 32 applies to all claims, not just personal injury."},
 {"t": "dr-limitation", "q": "On 10 February 2025 a local newspaper published an article which Eamon says falsely accused him of fraud. He took no action until he instructed solicitors in March 2026, and they issued a libel claim on 1 April 2026.\n\nWhich statement best describes the limitation position?", "o": ["The claim is in time, because all claims in tort, including libel, may be brought within 6 years of the wrongful act.", "The claim is in time, because time runs only from when Eamon first instructed solicitors.", "The claim is out of time and the court has no power at all to allow it to proceed.", "The claim is in time, because the period for defamation is 3 years from publication.", "The claim is out of time, as the period is 1 year from publication, but the court has a discretion to allow it."], "a": 4, "e": "Defamation (libel and slander) has a 1-year limitation period from publication (LA 1980 s.4A), which expired on 10 February 2026. The court has a special discretion to allow a defamation claim to proceed out of time (s.32A), so the claim is not necessarily lost. The general 6-year tort period does not apply."},
 {"t": "dr-limitation", "q": "Fforde Ltd and Garnett Ltd each contributed to a warehouse fire. The warehouse owner sued only Fforde Ltd, and judgment for £500,000 was given against Fforde Ltd on 1 September 2025. Fforde Ltd now wants to claim a contribution from Garnett Ltd.\n\nBy when must Fforde Ltd bring its contribution claim?", "o": ["Within 6 years of the date of the fire.", "By 1 September 2026, one year from the judgment.", "By 1 September 2027, two years from the judgment.", "Within 3 years of the date of the fire.", "By 1 September 2031, six years from the judgment."], "a": 2, "e": "A claim for contribution between people liable for the same damage must be brought within 2 years of the date on which the right to contribution arose, which is the date of the judgment (or settlement) fixing the claimant's liability (LA 1980 s.10). Time does not run from the fire. The 6-year period applies to the original tort claim, not to contribution."},
 {"t": "dr-pre-action", "q": "Harrow Signs Ltd supplied signage to Ivydene Ltd under a written contract. Ivydene Ltd has refused to pay £22,000, saying the signs were poorly made. Harrow Signs Ltd sends a letter before claim. The issues are straightforward.\n\nWithin what time should Ivydene Ltd normally respond?", "o": ["Within 30 days, using the reply form and financial statement required by the Debt Claims Protocol.", "Within 21 days, with a full response on liability within 3 months after that.", "Within 4 months of the date of the letter before claim being received.", "Within 14 days, a reasonable time for a simple case under the general Practice Direction.", "Within 28 days, followed by a compulsory pre-action meeting of the parties."], "a": 3, "e": "This is a business-to-business contract claim, so no specific protocol applies and the parties follow the Practice Direction on Pre-Action Conduct, which requires a response within a reasonable time: 14 days in a straightforward case and no more than 3 months in a very complex one. The Debt Claims Protocol (30 days) applies only where the debtor is an individual, and the other time limits belong to specific protocols."},
 {"t": "dr-pre-action", "q": "Jemima instructs a solicitor to sue Kirby & Co, chartered surveyors, whose survey report failed to mention serious damp in the house she bought. Her solicitor sends a letter of claim under the relevant pre-action protocol.\n\nWhat should Kirby & Co do in response?", "o": ["Acknowledge within 21 days, then send a letter of response within 3 months of acknowledging.", "Send a full letter of response within 14 days of receiving the letter of claim.", "Acknowledge within 14 days and send a full response within 4 months of the date of the letter of claim.", "Respond within 30 days, using the reply form and the financial statement.", "Respond within 28 days and then arrange a pre-action meeting with Jemima."], "a": 0, "e": "Claims against professionals such as surveyors fall under the Professional Negligence Pre-Action Protocol: the professional acknowledges the letter of claim within 21 days and sends a letter of response within 3 months of the acknowledgment. The 14-day and 4-month limits belong to the Clinical Disputes Protocol, 30 days to the Debt Claims Protocol, and 28 days plus a meeting to the Construction Protocol."},
 {"t": "dr-pre-action", "q": "Lorna believes that a hospital trust's negligent surgery caused her nerve damage. Her solicitor sends a letter of claim to the trust under the Clinical Disputes Pre-Action Protocol on 1 March.\n\nBy when should the trust send its letter of response?", "o": ["Within 21 days of the letter of claim, with no separate acknowledgment.", "Within 3 months of acknowledging the letter of claim.", "Within 4 months of the letter of claim, having acknowledged it within 14 days.", "Within 14 days, because medical claims must be dealt with urgently.", "Within 6 months, because medical records must first be obtained."], "a": 2, "e": "Under the Clinical Disputes Pre-Action Protocol the defendant acknowledges the letter of claim within 14 days and sends a reasoned letter of response within 4 months of the letter of claim. The 3-months-from-acknowledgment rule belongs to the Personal Injury and Professional Negligence Protocols. Here the response is due by 1 July."},
 {"t": "dr-pre-action", "q": "Marcus was injured at work. His solicitor sent a proper letter of claim under the Personal Injury Pre-Action Protocol. The employer's insurer ignored it and every follow-up letter, so Marcus had to issue proceedings without knowing the defendant's position. He wins at trial.\n\nWhat sanction may the court impose on the defendant for its failure to follow the protocol?", "o": ["Deprive Marcus of interest on his damages for the period of delay.", "Strike out its defence automatically, because ignoring a pre-action protocol is an abuse of process.", "Order it to pay damages double the amount awarded at trial.", "Refer the insurer to the Solicitors Regulation Authority for misconduct.", "Order it to pay interest on the damages at a higher rate, up to 10% above base rate, for a period."], "a": 4, "e": "Where a defendant's non-compliance with a protocol is substantial, the court may order it to pay costs (possibly on the indemnity basis) and interest on damages at a higher rate, up to 10% above base rate (PD-PAC). Depriving a party of interest is the sanction for a claimant at fault. Striking out for pre-action failings alone is very unusual, and the court cannot multiply damages."},
 {"t": "dr-pre-action", "q": "Nash Ltd issued a £70,000 claim against Orme Ltd. Before issue it refused to provide Orme Ltd with the key invoices and correspondence it relied on, although Orme Ltd asked for them several times, so Orme Ltd could not assess the claim. Nash Ltd wins at trial.\n\nWhat sanction is the court most likely to consider against Nash Ltd?", "o": ["Ordering Orme Ltd to pay interest on the damages at up to 10% above base rate.", "Depriving Nash Ltd of interest on its damages, or awarding interest at a lower rate.", "Setting aside Nash Ltd's judgment and ordering a retrial of the whole claim.", "Ordering Nash Ltd to pay a fine to the court for its pre-action conduct.", "Refusing to enforce Nash Ltd's judgment until it has provided the documents."], "a": 1, "e": "Where a claimant has substantially failed to comply with pre-action requirements, the court may deprive it of interest on damages for a period or award interest at a lower rate, and may also make costs orders against it (PD-PAC). Enhanced interest is the sanction against a defendant at fault. The judgment itself is not undone."},
 {"t": "dr-pre-action", "q": "Peake Ltd sent a proper letter before claim to Quinn Ltd in a simple contract dispute. Quinn Ltd's detailed response arrived three days after the 14 days allowed. It made no difference to how the dispute progressed. Peake Ltd later wins at trial and asks the court to penalise Quinn Ltd for its late response.\n\nWhat is the court most likely to do about the late response?", "o": ["Order Quinn Ltd to pay Peake Ltd's costs of the whole claim on the indemnity basis.", "Order Quinn Ltd to pay interest at 10% above base rate from the date of the letter.", "Strike out Quinn Ltd's defence, as any breach of the Practice Direction is serious.", "Stay the judgment until Quinn Ltd explains the delay.", "Impose no sanction, because the court looks only at substantial non-compliance."], "a": 4, "e": "The court considers whether there has been substantial non-compliance with pre-action requirements; minor or technical slips, such as a response a few days late that caused no prejudice, will not attract a sanction. Indemnity costs, enhanced interest and strike-out are reserved for serious failures. Quinn Ltd will still pay costs as the losing party, but not as a penalty for the late response."},
 {"t": "dr-pre-action", "q": "Rees Ltd, a main contractor, wants to sue Selby Engineering Ltd over defective steelwork on a new warehouse. Its solicitor sends a letter of claim under the Construction and Engineering Pre-Action Protocol.\n\nWhat should happen next under the protocol?", "o": ["Selby Engineering Ltd should acknowledge within 21 days and respond within 3 months of acknowledging.", "Selby Engineering Ltd should respond within 28 days, after which the parties should hold a pre-action meeting.", "Selby Engineering Ltd should respond within 30 days using the reply form.", "Rees Ltd may issue proceedings immediately, because construction claims are excluded from the pre-action conduct rules.", "Selby Engineering Ltd should respond within 4 months of the letter of claim."], "a": 1, "e": "Under the Construction and Engineering Pre-Action Protocol the defendant sends a letter of response within 28 days (the parties can agree to extend a step, but by no more than 28 days in total), and the parties then hold a pre-action meeting to try to resolve or narrow the issues. The other time limits come from the Personal Injury, Professional Negligence, Debt Claims and Clinical Disputes protocols."},
 {"t": "dr-pre-action", "q": "Tilly, who is employed as a nurse, owes £6,500 to Ubique Finance Ltd under a loan agreement. The company sent her a letter of claim with the information sheet, reply form and financial statement required by the Debt Claims Protocol. Within the time allowed Tilly returned the reply form saying she is seeking advice from a free debt advice charity.\n\nWhat should Ubique Finance Ltd do?", "o": ["Issue proceedings immediately, because Tilly has not disputed that she owes the debt.", "Issue proceedings once 14 days have passed since the letter of claim.", "Apply for a stay of the protocol so it can issue proceedings early.", "Allow Tilly a reasonable period to obtain debt advice before starting proceedings.", "Treat the reply form as an admission and request judgment."], "a": 3, "e": "The Debt Claims Protocol applies because a business is claiming a debt from an individual; the debtor has 30 days to reply. If the debtor says they are seeking debt advice, the creditor should allow reasonable extra time and should not start proceedings while the debtor is genuinely seeking advice. A reply form is not a court admission on which judgment can be requested."},
 {"t": "dr-pre-action", "q": "Vale Ltd's solicitor drafts a letter before claim to Wix Ltd that reads in full: 'Our client is owed money by you for the work done last year. Pay within 14 days or we will issue proceedings.' No documents are enclosed.\n\nWhat is the main deficiency in the letter?", "o": ["It does not give details of the claim, how the sum is calculated or the key documents.", "It should have been sent by the client personally rather than by a solicitor.", "It must give Wix Ltd at least 3 months to respond, whatever the complexity.", "It must be marked without prejudice, or it will not be valid at all.", "It must enclose a draft claim form and draft particulars of claim."], "a": 0, "e": "Under the Practice Direction on Pre-Action Conduct, a letter before claim should give concise details of the claim, say what the claimant wants and, if money is claimed, how the amount is calculated, and enclose or list the key documents relied on. Fourteen days is a reasonable period in a simple case. A letter before claim is an open letter, and no draft court documents are required."},
 {"t": "dr-pre-action", "q": "Yorke Ltd and Zara Ltd are in a contract dispute and both want to try mediation, but the limitation period for Yorke Ltd's claim expires in three weeks. Neither wants the expense of court proceedings at this stage.\n\nWhat is the best way to protect Yorke Ltd's position while the parties mediate?", "o": ["Rely on the pre-action protocol, which stops the limitation period running.", "Do nothing, because time stops running once the parties agree to mediate.", "Agree in writing a standstill agreement stopping time running while they mediate.", "Ask the mediator to certify that the claim is in time.", "Send a letter before claim, which counts as starting proceedings for limitation purposes."], "a": 2, "e": "Pre-action protocols and mediation do not stop the limitation period running. The parties can avoid issuing proceedings by agreeing in writing to a standstill agreement, which pauses time for an agreed period; the alternative is to issue protectively and seek a stay. A claim is brought only when the claim form is issued, not when a letter is sent."},
 {"t": "dr-parties-law", "q": "Aldous Consulting Ltd, a management consultancy whose central administration is in London, agrees to provide consultancy services to Baeza SL, a company based in Madrid. The contract contains no choice of law clause and has no particular connection with any other country. Baeza SL refuses to pay and Aldous Consulting Ltd sues in England.\n\nWhich law governs the contract?", "o": ["Spanish law, because the services were received in Spain.", "Spanish law, because it is the law of the habitual residence of the party paying for the services.", "English law, because the claim is heard in an English court.", "Whichever law the English court considers fairer to the parties.", "English law, because it is the law of the service provider's habitual residence."], "a": 4, "e": "Under Rome I (Art 4(1)(b)), a contract for the provision of services with no choice of law is governed by the law of the service provider's habitual residence, which for a company is its place of central administration: London. The law of the court hearing the case governs procedure, not the contract, and there is no general fairness test."},
 {"t": "dr-parties-law", "q": "Ciaran, who lives and works in New York, contracted with Dunmore Ltd, an English company, to buy a racehorse. Dunmore Ltd says Ciaran owes it £400,000. The contract has no jurisdiction clause. While Ciaran is in London for a three-day race meeting, Dunmore Ltd's process server hands him the claim form at his hotel.\n\nDoes the English court have jurisdiction over the claim?", "o": ["No, because a defendant who lives abroad can only be sued in the courts where he lives.", "No, because the court's permission is always needed before suing a foreign resident.", "Yes, because he was served in England, subject to any argument that another forum is more appropriate.", "Yes, but only because the EU Brussels rules give jurisdiction to the claimant's courts.", "No, because service on a defendant during a short visit is not valid service."], "a": 2, "e": "Under the common law rules, which apply since the Brussels regime stopped applying to proceedings started from 1 January 2021, the English court has jurisdiction over a defendant served with the claim form while present in England and Wales, even on a short visit. The defendant may still ask the court to decline jurisdiction because another country's courts are clearly more appropriate (forum non conveniens). Permission is needed only to serve outside the jurisdiction."},
 {"t": "dr-parties-law", "q": "Edda, who lives in Bristol, buys a camera from Fotohaus GmbH, a German retailer whose English-language website targets UK customers and delivers to the UK. The online terms say the contract is governed by German law. The camera is faulty and Edda wants to rely on her rights under English consumer law.\n\nWhich statement best describes the applicable law?", "o": ["German law applies in full, because the parties expressly chose it.", "German law applies, but it cannot deprive Edda of the mandatory consumer protections of English law.", "English law applies in full, because any choice of law clause is void in a cross-border consumer contract.", "German law applies, because it is the law of the seller's habitual residence.", "The court will apply whichever law gives the higher damages."], "a": 1, "e": "Under Rome I Art 6, where a business directs its activities to the consumer's country, the parties may choose the governing law, but the choice cannot deprive the consumer of the protection of the mandatory rules of the law of their habitual residence. So the German-law clause is valid but Edda keeps her English consumer protections. The seller's law is the default rule for business sales of goods, not consumer contracts."},
 {"t": "dr-parties-law", "q": "Ffion, who lives in Cardiff, is on holiday in Spain when her hire car is struck by a car driven by Gaston, who lives in France. Ffion is injured. She sues Gaston in England in negligence. There is no agreement between them about the applicable law and no pre-existing relationship.\n\nWhich law will apply to the claim?", "o": ["English law, because Ffion lives in Wales and is suing in England.", "French law, because it is the law of the defendant's habitual residence.", "English law, because procedure and liability are governed by the law of the court.", "Spanish law, because it is the law of the country where the damage occurred.", "French law, because the parties do not share a habitual residence."], "a": 3, "e": "Under Rome II, the general rule is that the law of the country where the damage occurs applies (Art 4(1)). The common habitual residence exception (Art 4(2)) does not apply because Ffion and Gaston live in different countries. Only procedure is governed by the law of the court hearing the case."},
 {"t": "dr-parties-law", "q": "Hartley & Penn is a partnership of three architects. A client, Ines, wants to sue the practice for negligent design work.\n\nIn whose name should the claim be brought against the practice?", "o": ["Against the partnership in its firm name, Hartley & Penn.", "Against the senior partner only, as the person who represents the firm.", "Against each partner individually, because a partnership cannot be sued.", "Against the partner who did the design work only.", "Against the firm's professional indemnity insurer."], "a": 0, "e": "A partnership can sue and be sued in its firm name, and the partners can be required to disclose the names of all the partners. Partners are jointly liable for the firm's debts, so suing the firm is the straightforward course. A claimant generally cannot sue the defendant's insurer directly in a negligence claim of this kind."},
 {"t": "dr-parties-law", "q": "Jago Ltd is owed £80,000 by Kestle Ltd for goods supplied. Before Jago Ltd issues proceedings, Kestle Ltd goes into administration.\n\nWhat must Jago Ltd do if it wishes to start a claim against Kestle Ltd?", "o": ["Nothing different, because administration does not affect the rights of creditors to bring claims.", "Obtain the consent of Kestle Ltd's directors.", "Obtain the administrator's consent or the court's permission, because of the moratorium.", "Wait until the administration ends, because no claim can ever be brought.", "Serve a statutory demand before starting any claim."], "a": 2, "e": "When a company enters administration a moratorium applies: no legal proceedings can be started or continued against it without the administrator's consent or the court's permission. The directors' powers are largely displaced by the administrator. In practice Jago Ltd will usually be an unsecured creditor, so suing may not be worthwhile."},
 {"t": "dr-parties-law", "q": "Lowri, aged 17, was injured when she fell through a rotten floorboard in a rented holiday cottage. She wants to bring a personal injury claim now rather than wait until she turns 18. Her father is willing to help.\n\nHow should the claim be brought?", "o": ["By her father in his own name, as the claim belongs to her parents until she is 18.", "By Lowri in her own name, because a 17-year-old has full capacity to litigate.", "It cannot be brought until she turns 18, when limitation begins to run.", "By the Official Solicitor, who must act for all claimants under 18.", "By Lowri through a litigation friend, such as her father, unless the court lets her act alone."], "a": 4, "e": "A child (under 18) must conduct proceedings through a litigation friend, usually a parent, unless the court permits the child to act without one (CPR 21). A litigation friend acting for a claimant must have no interest adverse to the child and must undertake to pay any costs the child may be ordered to pay. The claim is the child's, and she need not wait until 18, although time does not run against her until then."},
 {"t": "dr-parties-law", "q": "Mabel wins her claim for damages against Noble Ltd. She funded it with a conditional fee agreement providing for a success fee of 50% of her solicitor's base costs. Noble Ltd is ordered to pay her costs.\n\nWho pays the success fee?", "o": ["Noble Ltd, because it was ordered to pay Mabel's costs.", "Mabel, because a success fee is not recoverable from the losing party.", "Mabel's after-the-event insurer, because it covers all her own costs.", "Nobody, because success fees are no longer lawful.", "Noble Ltd, but only if the court finds the success fee reasonable and proportionate."], "a": 1, "e": "Under a conditional fee agreement the solicitor receives normal fees plus a success fee if the client wins, up to 100% of base costs. Since April 2013 the success fee is not recoverable from the losing opponent, so the client pays it, usually out of damages. After-the-event insurance mainly covers the risk of paying the other side's costs and disbursements."},
 {"t": "dr-parties-law", "q": "Oriel Ltd supplied office equipment to Pask Ltd. The written contract says nothing about interest. Pask Ltd has not paid a £20,000 invoice that fell due four months ago. Oriel Ltd wants to claim interest as well as the price.\n\nOn what basis is Oriel Ltd most likely to be entitled to interest?", "o": ["Interest at the contractual rate, which the court will imply at 10%.", "No interest, because interest can only be claimed where the contract provides for it.", "Judgment interest at 8% from the date the invoice fell due.", "Statutory interest at 8% above base rate under the Late Payment of Commercial Debts (Interest) Act 1998.", "Interest at the Bank of England base rate only, under the Late Payment of Commercial Debts (Interest) Act 1998."], "a": 3, "e": "For debts under contracts between businesses for goods or services, the Late Payment of Commercial Debts (Interest) Act 1998 gives statutory interest at 8% above base rate, plus fixed compensation. Interest must still be claimed and pleaded. Judgment interest runs only from judgment, and the court will not invent a contractual rate."},
 {"t": "dr-parties-law", "q": "Rhodri is the claimant in a County Court claim being heard in Swansea. He and two of his witnesses wish to give their evidence in Welsh. The defendant and its solicitors speak only English.\n\nWhat should Rhodri's solicitor do?", "o": ["Tell the court early, at the latest in the directions questionnaire, so it can make arrangements.", "Apply for the defendant's consent, because Welsh can only be used if all parties agree.", "Apply for the court's permission, which is given only if Rhodri cannot speak English.", "Arrange and pay for an interpreter himself, because the court will not provide one.", "Do nothing until the trial, when the judge will decide whether Welsh may be used."], "a": 0, "e": "Any party or witness may speak Welsh in legal proceedings in Wales (Welsh Language Act 1993) without needing anyone's permission. Under the Practice Direction on the use of the Welsh language, the parties must tell the court early, and at the latest in the directions questionnaire, so the case can be listed before a Welsh-speaking judge or with simultaneous translation; the court arranges any interpreter. Only a hearing wholly in Welsh without notice needs everyone's agreement."},
 {"t": "dr-where-to-start", "q": "Sorcha was injured when she slipped on a wet floor in a supermarket. Her solicitor values her claim at £45,000. Sorcha would like the claim heard by a High Court judge.\n\nWhere must the claim be issued?", "o": ["In the County Court, because a personal injury claim worth less than £50,000 must start there.", "In the High Court, because all personal injury claims over £25,000 may start there.", "In either court, because the claimant may choose where to issue any claim.", "In the High Court, because the claimant has stated a clear preference for a High Court judge to hear it.", "In the County Court, but only if the defendant agrees."], "a": 0, "e": "A personal injury claim worth less than £50,000 must be started in the County Court. Only a personal injury claim of £50,000 or more may start in the High Court, and then only if it justifies a High Court judge. The claimant's preference does not override the threshold, and the defendant's agreement is irrelevant."},
 {"t": "dr-where-to-start", "q": "Tobias suffered severe brain injuries in a road accident. His claim is valued at £1.2 million, involves complex medical evidence on causation and requires a large care package.\n\nWhere may the claim be issued?", "o": ["Only in the County Court, because all personal injury claims must start there.", "Only in the High Court, because claims over £1 million must start there.", "In the High Court, because its value and complexity justify a High Court judge.", "Only in the Court of Appeal, because of the very high value of the claim.", "In the County Court only, unless the defendant consents to the High Court."], "a": 2, "e": "A personal injury claim worth £50,000 or more may be issued in the High Court if the claimant believes it ought to be dealt with by a High Court judge because of its financial value, complexity or public importance (PD 7A). A claim of this value and complexity justifies that. No value obliges the claimant to use the High Court, and the Court of Appeal is not a first-instance court."},
 {"t": "dr-where-to-start", "q": "Ursula, a local councillor, says a neighbour posted a defamatory statement about her on a community website. She estimates her damages at £8,000. The parties have made no agreement about which court should hear the claim.\n\nIn which court should Ursula issue her claim?", "o": ["The County Court, because the claim is worth less than £100,000.", "The County Court small claims track, because the damages claimed are worth less than £10,000.", "Either court, at Ursula's choice, because defamation has no value threshold.", "The County Court, because claims involving local disputes must start locally.", "The High Court, because the County Court cannot hear defamation unless the parties agree."], "a": 4, "e": "Defamation claims (libel and slander) are issued in the High Court, in the King's Bench Division's Media and Communications List, whatever their value; the County Court has no jurisdiction unless the parties agree. The ordinary £100,000 threshold does not apply. The track is a separate question from the court."},
 {"t": "dr-where-to-start", "q": "Valentine Ltd's warehouse was damaged by a fire caused by a contractor's negligence. Its loss is £110,000. The contractor intends to argue that Valentine Ltd was 50% to blame for storing chemicals carelessly.\n\nHow should the claim be valued for deciding where it may be issued?", "o": ["At £55,000, because the court deducts the defendant's alleged share of blame from the loss.", "At £110,000, because any possible finding of contributory negligence is disregarded.", "At £55,000 until the court decides the contributory negligence issue.", "At £110,000 plus interest and costs.", "At whatever figure the defendant states in its defence."], "a": 1, "e": "When valuing a claim for the court thresholds and allocation, the court disregards any possible finding of contributory negligence, interest and costs (CPR 16.3(6)). The claim is worth £110,000, so it may be issued in the High Court if the case justifies a High Court judge, or in the County Court. The defendant's view does not decide the value."},
 {"t": "dr-where-to-start", "q": "Wexley Ltd claims £90,000 from Yarrow Ltd for unpaid invoices. Yarrow Ltd has said that if it is sued it will counterclaim £40,000 for defective goods. Wexley Ltd would prefer the High Court.\n\nWhere must Wexley Ltd issue the claim?", "o": ["In the County Court, because the claim is worth £90,000 and any counterclaim is disregarded.", "In the High Court, because the claim and the counterclaim together exceed the £100,000 threshold.", "In either court, because the total amount in dispute is £130,000.", "In the County Court, because the claim is worth only £50,000 after the counterclaim.", "In the High Court, because the court must take the counterclaim into account."], "a": 0, "e": "A money claim worth £100,000 or less must be started in the County Court. In valuing the claim the court disregards any counterclaim or set-off the defendant may raise (CPR 16.3(6)), so the value is £90,000. The counterclaim neither increases nor reduces the claimant's value for this purpose."},
 {"t": "dr-where-to-start", "q": "Zainab is injured when she trips over a broken paving slab outside a shop. Her solicitor expects her to recover about £18,000 in total, of which about £6,000 is for pain, suffering and loss of amenity. The claim is not a road traffic claim.\n\nWhich statement of value should the claim form contain?", "o": ["Over £25,000, with over £5,000 for pain, suffering and loss of amenity.", "Not over £10,000, because only the pain, suffering and loss of amenity figure counts.", "Over £100,000, because that is the figure required for a High Court claim.", "Over £10,000 but not over £25,000, with over £1,500 for pain, suffering and loss of amenity.", "That she cannot say how much she expects to recover, as damages are unspecified."], "a": 3, "e": "For an unspecified claim, the statement of value states the band the claimant expects (here more than £10,000 but not more than £25,000), and in a personal injury claim whether damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity are expected to be more or less than £1,500 (CPR 16.3). The £5,000 figure applies to most road traffic claims. 'Cannot say' is for claims that genuinely cannot be valued."},
 {"t": "dr-where-to-start", "q": "Bellwether Ltd is a property developer with a dispute worth £3 million with its building contractor about defective structural work on a new office block. The case will involve detailed engineering evidence.\n\nWhich court is most suitable for the claim?", "o": ["The Commercial Court, because it hears all claims over £1 million.", "The Technology and Construction Court, as part of the Business and Property Courts of the High Court.", "The County Court small claims track, because construction claims are always referred to mediation first.", "The Admiralty Court, because it hears technically complex claims.", "The Intellectual Property Enterprise Court, because it hears technical disputes cheaply."], "a": 1, "e": "The Technology and Construction Court (CPR 60), one of the Business and Property Courts, hears building, engineering and other technically complex disputes. The Commercial Court deals with complex commercial matters such as trade, shipping, banking and insurance, the Admiralty Court with shipping, and IPEC with smaller intellectual property disputes."},
 {"t": "dr-where-to-start", "q": "Cobalt Ltd is owed £150,000 by a customer for goods delivered. The customer has no defence it has put in writing, the facts are simple and the case raises no point of wider importance. Cobalt Ltd's director wants to issue in the High Court because 'it's a big claim'.\n\nWhat is the best advice?", "o": ["The claim must be issued in the High Court because it is worth more than £100,000.", "The claim must be issued in the County Court because debt claims cannot be heard in the High Court.", "The claim may be issued in the High Court only with the defendant's consent.", "The claim must be issued in the County Court because claims under £250,000 must start there.", "The County Court is appropriate, as nothing about the claim justifies a High Court judge."], "a": 4, "e": "A money claim worth more than £100,000 may start in the High Court only if the claimant believes it should be dealt with by a High Court judge because of its financial value, complexity or public importance (PD 7A para 2.4). A high value alone does not oblige or necessarily justify the High Court; a simple £150,000 debt is often issued in the County Court. Starting in the High Court unjustifiably risks a costs penalty."},
 {"t": "dr-where-to-start", "q": "Dacre Ltd is about to obtain a County Court judgment for £8,000 against a trader who owns valuable stock and equipment. Dacre Ltd intends to enforce by taking control of the trader's goods.\n\nHow must the judgment be enforced by taking control of goods?", "o": ["In the County Court by a warrant of control, because the judgment is a County Court judgment.", "In the County Court only, because judgments under £10,000 must be enforced there.", "In the High Court by a writ of control, after transferring the judgment there.", "By an attachment of earnings order in the High Court.", "In either court, at Dacre Ltd's choice, because the debt is over £600."], "a": 2, "e": "A judgment for £5,000 or more to be enforced by taking control of goods must be enforced in the High Court (unless it arises under a regulated consumer credit agreement), so a County Court judgment is transferred to the High Court and a writ of control is executed by a High Court Enforcement Officer. Either court can be used only for judgments between £600 and £4,999.99, and attachment of earnings is a County Court remedy."},
 {"t": "dr-where-to-start", "q": "Elan, a freelance illustrator, says a small company has copied one of her designs on its greetings cards. She estimates her loss at £15,000 and is anxious to keep her costs and her exposure to the other side's costs low.\n\nWhich court is most suitable for her claim?", "o": ["The Intellectual Property Enterprise Court, which has caps on damages and recoverable costs.", "The Commercial Court, because copyright is a commercial matter.", "The Technology and Construction Court, because the dispute concerns a design.", "The Court of Appeal, because intellectual property is a specialist area.", "The Administrative Court, because the claim involves a breach of her statutory intellectual property rights."], "a": 0, "e": "The Intellectual Property Enterprise Court (IPEC) is a cheaper forum for smaller intellectual property disputes, with caps on damages and on recoverable costs, and a small claims track for low-value claims. It suits an individual claimant worried about costs. The other courts listed deal with different types of claim, and the Court of Appeal is not a first-instance court."},
 {"t": "dr-issue-service", "q": "Farrah's claim form was issued on 2 February. Her solicitor posted it by first class post to the defendant's home on Wednesday 4 March. There are no bank holidays that week.\n\nOn what date is the claim form deemed served?", "o": ["Thursday 5 March, the next business day after posting.", "Wednesday 4 March, the day it was posted.", "Monday 9 March, the third business day after posting.", "Friday 6 March, the second business day after posting.", "The day the defendant actually received it."], "a": 3, "e": "A claim form is deemed served on the second business day after the step required for the chosen method was completed, whatever the method (CPR 6.14). For first class post the step is posting, so a claim form posted on Wednesday is deemed served on Friday. The actual date of receipt does not matter."},
 {"t": "dr-issue-service", "q": "Gideon's claim form has already been served. His solicitor personally hands the defendant the separate particulars of claim at 5.15pm on Tuesday 9 June. There are no bank holidays that week.\n\nWhen are the particulars of claim deemed served?", "o": ["Tuesday 9 June, because personal service takes effect immediately.", "Wednesday 10 June, because they were served after 4.30pm.", "Thursday 11 June, the second business day after service.", "Friday 12 June, three days after service.", "Monday 15 June, the next working week."], "a": 1, "e": "Documents other than the claim form follow CPR 6.26: personal service is deemed to take place that day if done before 4.30pm on a business day, and otherwise on the next business day. Service at 5.15pm on Tuesday is therefore deemed to be on Wednesday. The 'second business day' rule applies only to claim forms."},
 {"t": "dr-issue-service", "q": "Hester's claim form was issued on 3 March and served on 17 March, stating that particulars of claim would follow. Her solicitor wants to serve the particulars of claim separately.\n\nWhat is the time limit for serving the particulars of claim?", "o": ["Within 4 months of the date of issue of the claim form, whenever the claim form itself was served.", "Within 28 days after service of the claim form on the defendant.", "Within 14 days after the defendant files an acknowledgment of service.", "At any time before the first case management conference is held.", "Within 14 days of serving the claim form, and no later than the claim form deadline."], "a": 4, "e": "Particulars of claim served separately must be served within 14 days after service of the claim form, and in any event no later than the latest time for serving the claim form (CPR 7.4). The 4-month limit applies to the claim form itself. The defendant's time to respond then runs from service of the particulars."},
 {"t": "dr-issue-service", "q": "Idris issued a claim form on 5 January. His solicitor overlooked the file and did not try to serve it until 8 May, when he realised the deadline had passed. The limitation period has now expired. Idris applies promptly for an extension of time for service.\n\nIs the court likely to grant the extension?", "o": ["Yes, because the court applies the overriding objective to every application to extend time for service.", "Yes, because he applied promptly as soon as he noticed the error.", "No, because late extensions need all reasonable steps to have been taken, or a court failure.", "Yes, because the defendant will suffer no prejudice from a short delay.", "No, because the time for serving a claim form can never be extended."], "a": 2, "e": "Where an application to extend time for serving the claim form is made after the 4-month period has expired, the court may extend time only if the court was responsible for service and failed to do it, or the claimant took all reasonable steps to serve but was unable to, and in either case the claimant acted promptly (CPR 7.6(3)). A solicitor's oversight satisfies neither condition, so the claim form has expired. An extension is possible if applied for before expiry."},
 {"t": "dr-issue-service", "q": "Jonah issued a claim form against Kai on 1 April. Kai has moved out of his last known address, refuses to say where he now lives and has twice avoided a process server at his workplace. It is now 20 July.\n\nWhat should Jonah's solicitor do?", "o": ["Wait until the 4 months expire and then apply to dispense with service.", "Email the claim form to Kai, which is valid service if he has an email address.", "Apply before the 4 months expire to extend time or for permission to use another method.", "Do nothing, because the 4-month period automatically stops running while a defendant evades service.", "Issue a fresh claim form, which automatically gives a further 4 months."], "a": 2, "e": "Applying before the deadline means the court applies the overriding objective, and a defendant's evasion is a good reason to extend time (CPR 7.6(2)) or to permit service by an alternative method or at an alternative place (CPR 6.15). Email service needs prior written agreement, and dispensing with service is only for exceptional cases. Time does not stop running because of evasion."},
 {"t": "dr-issue-service", "q": "Lumen Ltd issued a claim against Marlowe Ltd, a company registered in England. Marlowe Ltd's registered office is at its accountants' offices in Leeds, but it trades from a factory in Hull. Lumen Ltd posts the claim form first class to the registered office in Leeds. Marlowe Ltd has not nominated solicitors to accept service.\n\nHas the claim form been validly served?", "o": ["Yes, because a company can be served by post at its registered office.", "No, because a company must be served at its principal place of business.", "No, because a company must be served personally on a director.", "Yes, but only if the accountants agreed in writing to accept service.", "No, because the registered office has no connection with the claim."], "a": 0, "e": "A company registered in England and Wales can be served by leaving the document at, or posting it to, its registered office (Companies Act 2006 s.1139), as well as by the CPR methods, such as service at its principal office or a place of business connected with the claim. Personal service on a senior officer is only one option, and the registered office needs no connection with the claim."},
 {"t": "dr-issue-service", "q": "Nerys served her claim form and particulars of claim on the defendant herself. The defendant has filed nothing and the time for responding has passed. Nerys asks the court to enter default judgment, but she has not filed anything to show that the documents were served.\n\nWhat must Nerys do before she can obtain default judgment?", "o": ["Nothing, because the court presumes service once the claim form is issued.", "Serve the claim form again personally, because postal service is not enough for default judgment.", "Ask the defendant to confirm in writing that it received the documents.", "Apply for an order for alternative service.", "File a certificate of service, without which default judgment is not available."], "a": 4, "e": "A claimant who serves the claim form itself must file a certificate of service within 21 days of service of the particulars, unless all defendants have filed an acknowledgment of service, and cannot obtain default judgment without it (CPR 6.17). The defendant's confirmation is not needed and the court does not presume service."},
 {"t": "dr-issue-service", "q": "Orrin Ltd, an English company, wants to sue Pell Inc, a company based only in New York, for negligent misstatement. Pell Inc's advice was received and acted on in London, where Orrin Ltd suffered its loss. There is no contract or jurisdiction clause between them.\n\nWhat must Orrin Ltd do to serve the claim form on Pell Inc in New York?", "o": ["Serve the claim form by post in New York without permission, because damage was suffered in England.", "Get the court's permission, showing a gateway, a serious issue to be tried and England is the proper place.", "Serve the claim form within 4 months of issue, the same as service within England.", "Serve it on Pell Inc's nearest customer in England, which counts as its agent.", "Nothing special, because the English court has jurisdiction over any defendant who causes loss in England."], "a": 1, "e": "With no jurisdiction clause or other ground for serving without permission, the claimant needs the court's permission to serve out (CPR 6.36). It must show a good arguable case within a PD 6B gateway (here, damage sustained in England from a tort), a serious issue to be tried on the merits, and that England and Wales is the proper place to bring the claim. The claim form must then be served within 6 months of issue."},
 {"t": "dr-issue-service", "q": "Quill Developments Ltd has served its claim against Ravel Builders Ltd. It now wants to add Sable Ltd, a second contractor, as an additional defendant. Sable Ltd does not agree to be added.\n\nWhat does Quill Developments Ltd need in order to add Sable Ltd as a defendant?", "o": ["Sable Ltd's written consent, filed with the court.", "Nothing, because a claimant may add defendants at any time before trial.", "Ravel Builders Ltd's written consent only.", "The court's permission, because the claim form has already been served.", "Permission from the court and Sable Ltd's written consent."], "a": 3, "e": "Before the claim form is served, parties can be added without permission; after service, the court's permission is needed (CPR 19.4). The court may add a party where it is desirable so that it can resolve all the matters in dispute. Written consent is needed only from a person being added as a claimant, not as a defendant."},
 {"t": "dr-issue-service", "q": "Tamara sued Upton Ltd for negligent damage to her property, issuing within the limitation period. After limitation expired, she discovered that Venn Ltd, another contractor working on the site, was also partly responsible. She applies to add Venn Ltd as a second defendant.\n\nWill the court allow Venn Ltd to be added?", "o": ["Yes, because the original claim was issued within the limitation period.", "Yes, because the court may add any party whose presence is desirable.", "Yes, because the court can disapply the limitation period in any negligence claim if it is equitable.", "No, because adding Venn Ltd is not necessary within the narrow post-limitation rules.", "No, because parties can never be added once the claim form has been served."], "a": 3, "e": "After limitation has expired, a party can be added or substituted only if the period was current when proceedings started and the addition is necessary: substituting a party named by mistake, where the claim cannot properly be carried on without the new party, or where the original party has died or become bankrupt (CPR 19.6; LA 1980 s.35). Tamara named Upton Ltd correctly and can carry on against it, so none applies. The discretion to disapply limitation exists only in personal injury claims."},
 {"t": "dr-responding", "q": "Wilma's particulars of claim were deemed served on the defendant on 2 March. The defendant has filed neither an acknowledgment of service nor a defence.\n\nFrom what date can Wilma first request default judgment?", "o": ["16 March, the fourteenth day after service.", "31 March, after 28 days from service.", "17 March, once the 14 days for responding have expired.", "3 March, the day after service, because nothing has been filed.", "27 April, after the maximum agreed extension of 56 days."], "a": 2, "e": "Where the defendant files nothing, it had 14 days after service of the particulars to file an acknowledgment of service or defence, which ends on 16 March (the day of service is not counted). Default judgment is available once that period has expired, so from 17 March. The 28-day period applies only if an acknowledgment of service is filed."},
 {"t": "dr-responding", "q": "Xavier's particulars of claim were deemed served on Yolanda on 1 June. Yolanda filed an acknowledgment of service on 10 June. Xavier obtained default judgment on 20 June. Yolanda applies promptly to set it aside, but her draft defence is weak.\n\nWhat will the court do?", "o": ["Refuse to set it aside, because Yolanda has shown no real prospect of successfully defending the claim.", "Set it aside only if Yolanda pays the whole claim into court.", "Refuse to set it aside, because she should have filed her defence within 14 days.", "Vary the judgment to allow Yolanda to pay by instalments.", "Set the judgment aside, because it was wrongly entered before the time for the defence had expired."], "a": 4, "e": "Having filed an acknowledgment of service, Yolanda had 28 days after service of the particulars (until 29 June) to file her defence, so default judgment on 20 June was entered too early. A judgment wrongly entered must be set aside (CPR 13.2), whatever the merits of the defence. The real-prospect test applies only to the court's discretion under CPR 13.3."},
 {"t": "dr-responding", "q": "Zest Ltd claims £8,000 for unpaid invoices from Amis Ltd. Amis Ltd files an admission of £5,000 of the claim and a defence to the remaining £3,000.\n\nWhat choice does Zest Ltd now have?", "o": ["Accept £5,000 in full satisfaction, or reject it and continue the claim.", "Enter judgment for £5,000 automatically, without telling the court whether it accepts the admission.", "Enter judgment for the full £8,000, because Amis Ltd has admitted liability.", "Apply for default judgment, because Amis Ltd has not defended the whole claim.", "Nothing, because the court will decide the amount at a disposal hearing."], "a": 0, "e": "Where a defendant admits part of a specified sum, the claimant must tell the court whether it accepts the lesser amount in full satisfaction of the claim (and enters judgment for it) or rejects it, in which case the claim continues as a defended claim (CPR 14). Default judgment is not available where a defence has been filed."},
 {"t": "dr-responding", "q": "Bramwell Ltd is sued in England by Cato Ltd. Bramwell Ltd believes the contract gives the courts of France exclusive jurisdiction. It filed an acknowledgment of service stating that it intended to contest jurisdiction, but then did nothing further for three weeks.\n\nWhat is the effect of Bramwell Ltd's delay?", "o": ["Nothing, because it can raise jurisdiction in its defence at any time.", "The claim is automatically stayed until the French court decides jurisdiction.", "Cato Ltd must apply to the court for a declaration that it has jurisdiction.", "It is treated as accepting jurisdiction, because it did not apply within 14 days.", "It can still apply at any time before the case management conference."], "a": 3, "e": "A defendant who wants to dispute jurisdiction must file an acknowledgment of service and then apply within 14 days after filing it, with evidence (CPR Part 11). If it files an acknowledgment but does not apply in time, it is treated as having accepted that the court has jurisdiction. Raising jurisdiction in a defence instead risks being treated as submitting to the court."},
 {"t": "dr-responding", "q": "Della sues a builder for damages for poor workmanship, an unspecified sum. The builder files no acknowledgment of service or defence, and the time for doing so has expired.\n\nWhat judgment can Della obtain by request?", "o": ["Judgment for the sum her solicitor estimates in the particulars of claim.", "Judgment on liability, with the amount of damages decided by the court at a later hearing.", "No judgment, because default judgment is not available where the damages claimed are unspecified.", "Judgment for the maximum amount in her statement of value.", "Summary judgment without a hearing on both liability and amount."], "a": 1, "e": "Where the claim is for an unspecified sum, a request for default judgment produces judgment on liability, with the amount decided at a disposal hearing or other hearing (CPR 12). Judgment for a fixed amount by request is available only for a specified sum. Summary judgment is a separate procedure based on the merits."},
 {"t": "dr-responding", "q": "Eldon was knocked off his bicycle by a car driven by Fern, aged 17. Fern has been properly served but has filed nothing, and the time for doing so has passed. Eldon wants default judgment.\n\nHow can Eldon obtain default judgment?", "o": ["By filing a request, as for any claim where no defence is filed.", "He cannot, because default judgment is never available against a defendant who is a child.", "By request, but only after waiting a further 28 days.", "By serving a notice on Fern's parents requiring them to defend.", "By making an application to the court, because the defendant is a child."], "a": 4, "e": "Default judgment is usually obtained by an administrative request, but an application to a judge, with evidence, is required in certain cases, including where the defendant is a child or protected party (CPR 12). The court will want to ensure that a litigation friend is appointed for the child. Default judgment is not barred; it simply needs an application."},
 {"t": "dr-responding", "q": "Fairfax Ltd sued Gale Ltd for £60,000. After disclosure, Fairfax Ltd realises its claim is weak and files and serves a notice of discontinuance. It argues that each side should bear its own costs because the claim was brought in good faith.\n\nWhat is the likely costs position?", "o": ["Fairfax Ltd pays Gale Ltd's costs to the date of the notice, unless the court orders otherwise.", "Each party bears its own costs, because the claim was brought in good faith.", "Gale Ltd must pay Fairfax Ltd's costs, because the discontinuance has saved it the expense of a trial.", "Costs will be decided as if the case had gone to trial and Fairfax Ltd had won.", "No order for costs can be made, because there has been no judgment or trial."], "a": 0, "e": "Unless the court orders otherwise, a claimant who discontinues is liable for the defendant's costs up to the date the notice of discontinuance was served (CPR 38.6). To avoid this the claimant must show a good reason, such as a change of circumstances caused by the defendant's conduct; good faith alone is not enough."},
 {"t": "dr-responding", "q": "Hollis has a personal injury claim against Ince Ltd. Ince Ltd has made an interim payment of £20,000 under a court order. Hollis now wants to discontinue the claim. Ince Ltd has not agreed to the discontinuance.\n\nCan Hollis discontinue?", "o": ["Yes, at any time, simply by filing and serving a notice of discontinuance on Ince Ltd.", "Yes, but only if he repays the interim payment first.", "Only with the court's permission, as he has had an interim payment without written consent.", "No, because a claimant who has received an interim payment can never discontinue.", "Only if the claim has not yet been allocated to a track."], "a": 2, "e": "A claimant can usually discontinue at any time by filing and serving a notice, but permission is needed where an interim injunction has been granted or the claimant has received an interim payment, unless the defendant consents in writing (CPR 38.2). The court may make orders about repaying the interim payment when deciding the application."},
 {"t": "dr-responding", "q": "Jules sued Keane Ltd for breach of contract. In its defence, Keane Ltd admitted that it was in breach. Two months later Keane Ltd's new solicitors want to withdraw the admission and deny breach. Jules does not agree.\n\nCan Keane Ltd withdraw its admission?", "o": ["Yes, at any time before trial by amending its defence.", "Only with the court's permission, which will weigh all the circumstances.", "Yes, because an admission in a defence is not binding until judgment.", "No, because an admission can never be withdrawn once proceedings have started.", "Yes, if Keane Ltd files a witness statement explaining its change of mind."], "a": 1, "e": "An admission made in the proceedings can be withdrawn only with the court's permission (CPR 14.2(11)). The court will decide, looking at all the circumstances, including the reasons, the prejudice to each party and the stage of the case. There is no automatic right to withdraw."},
 {"t": "dr-responding", "q": "Lacey Ltd and Moreton Ltd settle a claim three weeks before trial. Moreton Ltd will pay £150,000 in instalments, and the parties will enter a new five-year supply agreement. Both want the terms kept confidential.\n\nHow should the settlement be recorded?", "o": ["In a consent order setting out all the terms, because only a public court order can be enforced.", "In a notice of discontinuance filed by Lacey Ltd.", "In a default judgment entered by consent.", "In a Tomlin order, staying the claim on terms in a confidential schedule with liberty to apply.", "In a Part 36 offer and acceptance, which automatically keeps all terms confidential."], "a": 3, "e": "A Tomlin order stays the claim on terms set out in a schedule that is not part of the public order, with liberty to apply to the court to enforce them. It suits settlements needing confidentiality or including terms a court could not itself order, such as a new supply agreement. A consent order puts the terms in the public order, and discontinuance would leave the claimant liable for costs with no enforceable terms."},
 {"t": "dr-statements-of-case", "q": "Nina's particulars of claim allege that the contract with Orton Ltd contained an express term that delivery would be made by 1 May. Orton Ltd's defence deals with every other paragraph but says nothing about delivery dates or this term, and sets out no case on that issue.\n\nWhat is the effect of the defence's silence on the delivery term?", "o": ["Orton Ltd is taken to deny the term.", "Orton Ltd is taken to admit the term.", "Nina must prove the term at trial, because silence is never an admission.", "The court will strike out the paragraph of the particulars of claim.", "Orton Ltd may raise its case on the term for the first time at trial."], "a": 1, "e": "A defendant who fails to deal with an allegation is taken to admit it, unless it has set out in its defence the nature of its case on that issue, in which case the allegation is taken as requiring proof (CPR 16.5(5)). Orton Ltd says nothing about the issue, so it is taken to admit the term. A party cannot run an unpleaded case at trial without amending."},
 {"t": "dr-statements-of-case", "q": "Pax Ltd's lorry collided with Quentin's van. Quentin's particulars allege that the lorry driver was driving too fast. Pax Ltd's draft defence says only: 'Paragraph 4 is denied.' Its driver says he was within the limit and that Quentin pulled out without looking.\n\nWhat should the defence say about paragraph 4?", "o": ["Deny it, giving reasons and setting out Pax Ltd's own version of events.", "Say 'Paragraph 4 is denied', because a bare denial is sufficient.", "Not admit it, and require Quentin to prove it.", "Admit it, but say Quentin was also at fault.", "Say nothing, so that Quentin is forced to prove it."], "a": 0, "e": "Where a defendant denies an allegation it must state its reasons and, if it intends to put forward a different version of events, set out its own version (CPR 16.5(2)). Pax Ltd knows its case, so a bare denial or a non-admission is not appropriate. Saying nothing risks the allegation being taken as admitted."},
 {"t": "dr-statements-of-case", "q": "Rowena sues Sefton Ltd for personal injury. Sefton Ltd's defence alleges that Rowena was contributorily negligent because she was not wearing a seatbelt. Rowena's solicitor does not file a reply.\n\nWhat is the effect of not filing a reply?", "o": ["Rowena is taken to admit that she was not wearing a seatbelt.", "Sefton Ltd can now obtain default judgment on the contributory negligence issue raised in its defence.", "Rowena cannot dispute contributory negligence at trial.", "The claim will be stayed until a reply is filed.", "Nothing adverse, because a reply is optional and failing to file one is not an admission."], "a": 4, "e": "A reply is optional; if one is filed it goes with the claimant's directions questionnaire. A claimant who does not file a reply is not taken to admit the matters raised in the defence (CPR 16.7), so Rowena can dispute contributory negligence at trial. Default judgment is relevant to a missing defence to counterclaim, not a reply."},
 {"t": "dr-statements-of-case", "q": "Thorne Ltd sued Usk Ltd for the price of goods. Usk Ltd filed a defence and counterclaim for £30,000 damages for late delivery. Thorne Ltd's solicitors thought no response was needed and filed nothing. Six weeks have passed since the counterclaim was served.\n\nWhat can Usk Ltd do?", "o": ["Nothing, because a claimant's silence on a counterclaim is not an admission.", "Apply to strike out Thorne Ltd's claim automatically.", "Request that Thorne Ltd file an acknowledgment of service to the counterclaim within a further 14 days.", "Obtain default judgment on its counterclaim, because Thorne Ltd has filed no defence to it.", "Proceed to trial, where the counterclaim will be treated as disputed."], "a": 3, "e": "A claimant must file a defence to counterclaim within 14 days of service of the counterclaim; if it does not, the defendant can obtain default judgment on the counterclaim. There is no acknowledgment of service to a counterclaim. This differs from a reply, which is optional."},
 {"t": "dr-statements-of-case", "q": "Vashti sues Wren Ltd for misrepresentation. Her particulars plead that Wren Ltd's statements were negligent. Shortly before trial her counsel tells her solicitor that he intends to argue at trial that Wren Ltd's director lied deliberately.\n\nWhat must happen before Vashti can allege fraud at trial?", "o": ["Her statement of case must be amended to set out the allegation of fraud specifically.", "Nothing, because counsel can raise any point that the evidence supports.", "She must serve a witness statement from the director admitting the fraud.", "She must obtain a criminal conviction of the director first.", "She must serve a Part 18 request on Wren Ltd asking whether it was fraudulent."], "a": 0, "e": "An allegation of fraud must be specifically set out in the statement of case (PD 16 para 8.2), and a party can rely at trial only on matters it has pleaded. Vashti must amend (with consent or the court's permission) before she can run a fraud case. A criminal conviction is not required for civil fraud."},
 {"t": "dr-statements-of-case", "q": "Xenia's particulars of claim against Yates Ltd were served two months ago. She now wants to add a further allegation of breach that arises from the same facts. Limitation has not expired. Yates Ltd will not agree to the amendment.\n\nHow can Xenia amend her particulars of claim?", "o": ["At any time without permission, because limitation has not expired.", "Only with the court's permission, as Yates Ltd will not consent.", "Only by discontinuing and issuing a new claim.", "By serving the amended particulars, which Yates Ltd may then challenge.", "Only at trial, by asking the judge to allow the new allegation."], "a": 1, "e": "A statement of case can be amended without permission before it is served, but after service only with the written consent of all the other parties or the court's permission (CPR 17.1). The court considers the overriding objective, whether the amendment has a real prospect of success and the stage of the case. The amending party usually pays the costs of and arising from the amendment."},
 {"t": "dr-statements-of-case", "q": "Zander Ltd's defence alleges that the claimant 'failed to mitigate its loss' but gives no details. The claimant cannot tell what Zander Ltd says it should have done differently and wants to know before preparing its witness evidence.\n\nWhat should the claimant do first?", "o": ["Apply immediately for a court order requiring Zander Ltd to give details.", "Apply for summary judgment on the whole defence.", "Serve a written request for further information, giving a reasonable time to reply.", "Treat the mitigation allegation as withdrawn because it lacks details.", "Serve a notice to admit facts on Zander Ltd."], "a": 2, "e": "Under Part 18 a party can ask another to clarify any matter in dispute or give additional information. It should first make a concise written request, giving a reasonable time to respond, and apply to the court only if there is no adequate response (PD 18). The response must be verified by a statement of truth and forms part of the statement of case."},
 {"t": "dr-statements-of-case", "q": "Agate Ltd, a company, is about to issue a claim. Its particulars of claim need to be verified by a statement of truth. The finance manager, a junior employee who knows the facts best, offers to sign it.\n\nWho may sign the statement of truth on Agate Ltd's behalf?", "o": ["Any employee who has knowledge of the facts.", "Only the company secretary, as the company's legal officer.", "A person holding a senior position in the company, such as a director, or its solicitor on its behalf.", "Only counsel instructed to draft the particulars.", "Nobody, because a statement of case issued by a company does not need to be verified by a statement of truth."], "a": 2, "e": "A statement of case must be verified by a statement of truth signed by the party, its litigation friend, or its legal representative on its behalf; for a company, the person signing must hold a senior position, such as a director (PD 22). A junior employee does not qualify, however well they know the facts. A false statement without honest belief is contempt of court."},
 {"t": "dr-statements-of-case", "q": "Bryn is preparing particulars of claim for a personal injury claim arising from an accident at work. He relies on a report from an orthopaedic surgeon and claims for lost earnings, care and future surgery.\n\nWhich of the following must be attached to or included with the particulars of claim?", "o": ["The witness statements of everyone who saw the accident.", "The defendant's insurance policy.", "A copy of the letter of claim sent under the pre-action protocol only.", "A costs budget for the whole claim.", "A copy of the medical report and a schedule of past and future expenses and losses."], "a": 4, "e": "In a personal injury claim the particulars of claim must state the claimant's date of birth and brief details of the injuries, attach a medical report where the claimant relies on medical evidence, and attach a schedule of past and future expenses and losses (PD 16). Witness statements are exchanged later under directions, and costs budgets are filed separately."},
 {"t": "dr-statements-of-case", "q": "Cottam Ltd is sued by a customer over a faulty heating system. Two months after filing its defence, Cottam Ltd decides it wants to claim an indemnity from Dunn Ltd, the manufacturer of the boiler, which is not a party to the claim.\n\nWhat does Cottam Ltd need to bring the additional claim against Dunn Ltd?", "o": ["Nothing, because an additional claim against a non-party never needs permission.", "The court's permission, because the claim was not issued before or at the same time as its defence.", "The claimant's written consent to the additional claim.", "Dunn Ltd's written consent to be joined.", "A new claim form issued as a separate action, which can never be linked to the customer's existing claim."], "a": 1, "e": "A defendant can make an additional (Part 20) claim against a non-party without permission if it is issued before or at the same time as the defence is filed; otherwise it needs the court's permission (CPR 20.7). Once issued, it must be served on the third party within 14 days with copies of the statements of case. Neither the claimant's nor the third party's consent is required."},
 {"t": "dr-interim", "q": "Ewart sues his former accountants, Finch & Co, alleging negligent tax advice given in 2023. Finch & Co's evidence includes a letter, signed by Ewart, confirming that he received independent advice from another firm and chose to ignore Finch & Co's warnings. Ewart has produced no evidence to the contrary.\n\nWhat application should Finch & Co consider?", "o": ["Default judgment, because Ewart's claim is weak.", "An interim payment from Ewart towards its costs.", "Summary judgment, but only if Ewart agrees, because otherwise only claimants can apply for it.", "A freezing injunction over Ewart's assets.", "Summary judgment against Ewart, on the basis that his claim has no real prospect of success."], "a": 4, "e": "Summary judgment can be given against a claimant or a defendant on the whole claim or an issue if that party has no real prospect of succeeding and there is no other compelling reason for a trial (CPR 24.3). A defendant can therefore apply against a hopeless claim. Default judgment is only for a failure to respond, and interim payments are made by defendants towards damages."},
 {"t": "dr-interim", "q": "Gemma claims that Harlan Ltd agreed orally to pay her a £50,000 bonus. Harlan Ltd's director says no such agreement was made. There are no documents, and the outcome depends on whose account of a private meeting is believed. Gemma applies for summary judgment.\n\nWhat is the likely outcome of the application?", "o": ["Dismissed, because disputed facts on conflicting evidence need a trial.", "Granted, because Gemma's evidence is signed by a statement of truth.", "Granted, because Harlan Ltd has no documents to support its denial.", "Granted on condition that Harlan Ltd pays £50,000 into court.", "Dismissed, because a claimant can never apply for summary judgment."], "a": 0, "e": "On a summary judgment application the court does not conduct a mini-trial; it will not resolve genuinely disputed facts on paper where the outcome depends on whose oral evidence is believed. Harlan Ltd's defence is realistic, not fanciful, so the application will be dismissed and the case will go to trial with directions. Claimants can apply against a hopeless defence."},
 {"t": "dr-interim", "q": "Ivo sues Jasper Ltd for £80,000. On Ivo's summary judgment application, the judge considers that Jasper Ltd's defence of a variation of the contract may possibly succeed, but that success is improbable.\n\nWhat order is the judge most likely to make?", "o": ["Summary judgment for Ivo, because the defence is improbable.", "Dismissal of the application with no conditions, because the defence may succeed.", "A conditional order that Jasper Ltd pays a sum into court or loses its defence.", "An order that Jasper Ltd make an interim payment directly to Ivo.", "A stay of the claim pending mediation."], "a": 2, "e": "Where it appears possible that a claim or defence may succeed but improbable that it will, the court may make a conditional order, for example requiring the party to pay a sum into court or take a specified step, failing which its statement of case will be struck out. Summary judgment needs no real prospect, which is not established if success is possible."},
 {"t": "dr-interim", "q": "Kendra issues a claim for £30,000 against Linden Ltd and serves the claim form and particulars of claim. The next day, before Linden Ltd has filed anything, Kendra wants to apply for summary judgment.\n\nCan Kendra apply for summary judgment now?", "o": ["Yes, a claimant can apply for summary judgment at any time after issue.", "Not without permission, because the defendant has not yet filed an acknowledgment or defence.", "No, a claimant can apply only after the case has been allocated to a track.", "Yes, but only if the claim is for a specified sum.", "No, she must wait until the defendant has filed both a defence and its directions questionnaire."], "a": 1, "e": "A claimant may not apply for summary judgment until the defendant has filed an acknowledgment of service or a defence, unless the court gives permission or a rule allows it (CPR 24.4(1)). If the claimant applies before the defence is filed, the defendant need not file a defence before the hearing. There is no need to wait for allocation."},
 {"t": "dr-interim", "q": "Mira was seriously injured by a falling scaffold pole. The scaffolding company has admitted liability but alleges she was 25% to blame for walking under the scaffold. Her full damages are likely to be about £200,000. She needs money now to adapt her home.\n\nWhat is the maximum interim payment the court can order?", "o": ["The full £200,000, because liability is admitted.", "£150,000 in full, because the court must pay the likely net award in advance.", "No more than £50,000, the amount of the contributory negligence reduction.", "A reasonable proportion of about £150,000, the likely award after the 25% reduction.", "Nothing, because contributory negligence is in dispute."], "a": 3, "e": "An interim payment must not be more than a reasonable proportion of the likely amount of the final judgment, taking into account contributory negligence and any set-off or counterclaim. Here the likely judgment is about £150,000 after a 25% reduction, and the court will order a cautious proportion of that. The admission of liability satisfies a condition for an interim payment even though contributory negligence is disputed."},
 {"t": "dr-interim", "q": "Before trial of Nell's personal injury claim, the defendant's insurer made a voluntary interim payment of £40,000. At the start of the trial on liability and quantum, Nell's counsel wants to tell the judge about the payment to show the insurer accepted the claim was serious.\n\nMay counsel tell the trial judge about the interim payment?", "o": ["No, unless the defendant agrees, until all questions of liability and quantum have been decided.", "Yes, because the payment is an admission of liability that the trial judge must take into account.", "Yes, because voluntary payments are not covered by the secrecy rule.", "No, and the payment can never be taken into account in the final judgment.", "Yes, but only in the opening speech, not in evidence."], "a": 0, "e": "The fact that an interim payment has been made, whether voluntarily or under an order, must not be disclosed to the trial judge until all questions of liability and quantum have been decided, unless the defendant agrees. At the end the court adjusts the final judgment to take account of the payment and can order repayment of any overpayment."},
 {"t": "dr-interim", "q": "Ottilie applies without notice for an interim injunction to stop a former business partner using her customer database. Her solicitor knows that Ottilie herself had shared parts of the database with a third party last year, which weakens her case. Ottilie wants this left out of her evidence.\n\nWhat should her solicitor advise?", "o": ["Facts against Ottilie can be left out, because the respondent can raise them itself at the return date.", "Facts against Ottilie need only be disclosed if the judge asks about them.", "The application should be made on notice instead, so that nothing need be disclosed.", "Facts against Ottilie are privileged and must not be disclosed.", "She must make full and frank disclosure, including facts against her, or the order may be discharged."], "a": 4, "e": "An applicant for a without notice order owes a duty of full and frank disclosure: it must tell the court everything material, including points against itself. Breach can lead to the order being discharged. On a contested application on notice, the respondent can make its own points, but that does not justify misleading the court on a without notice application."},
 {"t": "dr-interim", "q": "Pembroke Ltd obtains an interim injunction stopping Quince Ltd from selling a product that allegedly infringes Pembroke Ltd's design rights. At trial Quince Ltd wins, having lost substantial sales while the injunction was in force.\n\nHow is Quince Ltd protected for its lost sales?", "o": ["By suing Pembroke Ltd in a new claim for malicious prosecution.", "By claiming the lost sales from the court, which granted the injunction.", "By enforcing the cross-undertaking in damages Pembroke Ltd gave.", "It is not protected, because the injunction was lawfully granted.", "By claiming the lost sales from Pembroke Ltd's solicitors as wasted costs."], "a": 2, "e": "An applicant for an interim injunction almost always gives a cross-undertaking in damages: a promise to the court to compensate the respondent for loss caused by the injunction if it later turns out it should not have been granted. Quince Ltd can ask the court to enforce it. The court may require the undertaking to be fortified if the applicant's means are doubtful."},
 {"t": "dr-interim", "q": "Rosalind owns a small independent cinema. She seeks an interim injunction to stop a developer starting work which she says will block the only access to her premises in breach of a right of way. Each side's losses would be very hard to calculate if it wins at trial but the interim decision goes against it. At present the access is open.\n\nHow is the court likely to approach the application?", "o": ["Weigh the balance of convenience and, if it is evenly balanced, preserve the status quo by granting the injunction.", "Refuse the injunction, because Rosalind has not yet proved her right of way on the balance of probabilities at trial.", "Grant the injunction only if Rosalind shows she is certain to win at trial.", "Refuse the injunction, because a developer's losses always outweigh a small business's.", "Grant the injunction without any undertaking, because her loss is hard to calculate."], "a": 0, "e": "Under the American Cyanamid approach the court first asks whether there is a serious question to be tried (it does not decide the merits). Where damages would not adequately compensate either side, it weighs the balance of convenience, and if that is evenly balanced it is prudent to preserve the status quo, here the open access. The applicant must still give a cross-undertaking in damages."},
 {"t": "dr-interim", "q": "Sutton Ltd has strong evidence, including emails, that its former sales director has taken its confidential pricing files and is using them in a competing business. There is clear evidence that he has the files at his home and that he has previously deleted documents when challenged. Sutton Ltd fears he will destroy the evidence if warned.\n\nWhich order should Sutton Ltd seek?", "o": ["A freezing injunction, preventing him from dealing with the files.", "An order for specific disclosure, on notice to the former director.", "Summary judgment, so that the files are returned without a trial.", "A search order, made without notice and supervised by an independent solicitor.", "A witness summons requiring the former director to bring the files to court."], "a": 3, "e": "A search order requires the respondent to allow the applicant's representatives to enter premises to search for, copy and remove evidence. It needs an extremely strong prima facie case, very serious potential damage, clear evidence that the respondent has incriminating material and a real possibility it will be destroyed. It is made without notice and executed under an independent supervising solicitor. A freezing injunction protects assets, not evidence."},
 {"t": "dr-case-management", "q": "Tarquin claims £18,000 from a garage for negligent repairs to his car. The trial is expected to last one day, and each party wants to rely on a written report from one motor engineer.\n\nTo which track is the claim most likely to be allocated?", "o": ["The small claims track.", "The fast track.", "The intermediate track.", "The multi-track.", "The fast track, but only if both parties agree."], "a": 1, "e": "The fast track is the normal track for claims between £10,000 and £25,000 where the trial will last no more than one day and expert evidence is limited to one expert per party in any field, in no more than two fields. This claim fits all three limits. Allocation is decided by the court, not by agreement."},
 {"t": "dr-case-management", "q": "Ulric claims £22,000 for defective building work. The case involves a large number of disputed factual issues, and the parties agree that the trial will need three days.\n\nTo which track is the claim most likely to be allocated?", "o": ["The fast track, because the claim is worth less than £25,000.", "The small claims track, because building disputes worth under £25,000 are usually referred to mediation.", "The multi-track, because any trial over one day must go there.", "The fast track, with the trial split over three separate days.", "The intermediate track, because the trial will last longer than one day but no more than three."], "a": 4, "e": "A claim is suitable for the fast track only if the trial will last no more than one day. A claim worth not more than £100,000 that cannot go on the small claims or fast track may go to the intermediate track, provided the trial will last no more than three days and other limits are met. A three-day trial fits that track, so the claim moves up from the fast track."},
 {"t": "dr-case-management", "q": "Verity tripped on a defective step in a shopping centre. Her total damages are likely to be about £8,000, of which £3,000 is for pain, suffering and loss of amenity. The claim is not a road traffic claim.\n\nTo which track is the claim most likely to be allocated?", "o": ["The small claims track, because the total claim is under £10,000.", "The fast track, because her damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity exceed £1,500.", "The small claims track, because the damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity are under £5,000.", "The intermediate track, because personal injury claims cannot be small claims.", "The multi-track, because personal injury claims need expert medical evidence."], "a": 1, "e": "A personal injury claim is on the small claims track only if the total is up to £10,000 and damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity are no more than £1,500. The £5,000 limit applies only to road traffic claims within the whiplash reforms. Verity's pain and suffering damages exceed £1,500, so her claim goes to the fast track."},
 {"t": "dr-case-management", "q": "Wilf, an adult car driver, suffered a whiplash injury when another driver ran into the back of his car. His damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity are likely to be £4,000, and his total claim about £6,500. The claim falls within the whiplash reforms.\n\nTo which track is the claim most likely to be allocated?", "o": ["The small claims track.", "The fast track, because damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity exceed £1,500.", "The intermediate track, because whiplash claims need expert evidence.", "The multi-track, because road traffic claims are complex.", "The fast track, because all personal injury claims start there."], "a": 0, "e": "For road traffic accident claims within the whiplash reforms under the Civil Liability Act 2018, the small claims limit for damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity is £5,000 (rather than £1,500), with a total limit of £10,000. Wilf's claim is within both limits, so it is a small claim."},
 {"t": "dr-case-management", "q": "Xanthe brings a clinical negligence claim against a hospital trust worth about £60,000. The trust denies both breach of duty and causation. The trial is expected to last three days.\n\nTo which track is the claim most likely to be allocated?", "o": ["The intermediate track, because the claim is worth under £100,000 and the trial lasts three days.", "The fast track, because the trial lasts no more than three days.", "The small claims track, because liability is disputed.", "The multi-track, because clinical negligence with disputed liability is excluded from the intermediate track.", "The intermediate track, with a costs budget."], "a": 3, "e": "The intermediate track generally covers claims of £25,000 to £100,000 with a trial of up to three days, but certain claims are excluded and go to the multi-track, including most clinical negligence claims (unless breach and causation are admitted). Costs budgeting does not apply on the intermediate track in any event."},
 {"t": "dr-case-management", "q": "Yasmin's solicitor served the claimant's expert report six weeks after the date ordered. As a result, the defendant's expert could not respond in time and the trial date has been lost. The only explanation is that the solicitor was very busy with other cases. Yasmin applies for relief from sanctions.\n\nIs relief likely to be granted?", "o": ["Yes, because the court must always grant relief where the breach was the solicitor's fault, not the client's.", "Yes, because the defendant can recover its wasted costs.", "Probably not, because the breach is serious and significant and pressure of work is not a good reason.", "Yes, because relief is automatic for breaches of less than three months.", "No, because relief from sanctions is never available for expert evidence."], "a": 2, "e": "Under the Denton approach the court considers (1) the seriousness and significance of the breach, (2) why it occurred and (3) all the circumstances, giving particular weight to efficient, proportionate litigation and enforcing compliance (CPR 3.9). A breach that loses a trial date is serious and significant, and pressure of work is rarely a good reason, so relief is likely to be refused."},
 {"t": "dr-case-management", "q": "The court ordered the parties to give standard disclosure by 1 March. On 20 February the parties agree in writing to extend the deadline by 14 days. The trial is fixed for six months later and the extension will not affect any hearing date.\n\nIs the agreed extension effective?", "o": ["No, because only the court can extend a time limit in a court order.", "Yes, because parties may agree in writing up to 28 days' extension that risks no hearing date.", "No, because extensions by agreement apply only to the time for filing a defence.", "Yes, but only if the parties first notify the court and the court then approves it.", "Yes, because the parties may agree any length of extension, provided it does not affect the trial date."], "a": 1, "e": "The parties may agree in writing to extend a time limit by up to 28 days without the court's involvement, provided the extension does not put at risk any hearing date (CPR 3.8(4)). This is separate from the 28-day defence extension under CPR 15.5. A longer extension needs a court order."},
 {"t": "dr-case-management", "q": "The court made an order that unless the defendant served its list of documents by 4pm on 10 May, its defence would be struck out. The defendant served the list on 12 May without explanation.\n\nWhat is the position?", "o": ["The claimant must apply for an order striking out the defence.", "Nothing happens, because the list was served only two days after the deadline and caused no prejudice.", "The court will fix a hearing to decide whether the order should take effect.", "The defendant can cure the default by paying the claimant's costs.", "The defence is struck out automatically; the defendant must seek relief from sanctions."], "a": 4, "e": "An unless order states that unless a party does something by a set time, a specified sanction will follow; the sanction takes effect automatically on non-compliance without any further order. The defaulting party must apply promptly for relief from sanctions, which the court will decide using the Denton approach."},
 {"t": "dr-case-management", "q": "Ashby Ltd's claim has been served with a notice of proposed allocation. Ashby Ltd failed to file its directions questionnaire by the date given.\n\nWhat is the court most likely to do?", "o": ["Order Ashby Ltd to file it within 7 days, failing which its claim will be struck out.", "Strike out Ashby Ltd's claim immediately without warning.", "Allocate the claim to the multi-track as a penalty.", "Enter default judgment for the defendant.", "Allocate the claim without the questionnaire and take no further action."], "a": 0, "e": "If a party fails to file its directions questionnaire, the court will usually order it to do so within 7 days, failing which its claim or defence may be struck out. Immediate strike-out without warning is not the usual first step, and allocation is based on the case's features, not used as a penalty."},
 {"t": "dr-case-management", "q": "Bonner Ltd's claim form states a value of £45,000. Because the trial is expected to last five days, both parties expect the claim to be allocated to the multi-track. Bonner Ltd's solicitor asks when its costs budget must be filed.\n\nWhen must the costs budget be filed?", "o": ["At least 21 days before the first case management conference.", "Not at all, because costs budgeting applies only to claims over £100,000.", "With its directions questionnaire, because the stated value is under £50,000.", "At least 7 days before the trial.", "Not at all, because fixed recoverable costs apply to all claims valued under £100,000."], "a": 2, "e": "Costs budgeting applies to most multi-track claims with a value under £10 million. Where the stated value on the claim form is less than £50,000, the budget (Precedent H) must be filed with the directions questionnaire; otherwise it is due at least 21 days before the first case management conference (CPR 3.13). Fixed recoverable costs apply on the fast and intermediate tracks, not the multi-track."},
 {"t": "dr-evidence", "q": "Cara sues Drew for injuries suffered when his dog knocked her off her bicycle. Drew's defence alleges that Cara was contributorily negligent because she was cycling at night without lights.\n\nWho bears the burden of proving contributory negligence, and to what standard?", "o": ["Drew, on the balance of probabilities.", "Cara, on the balance of probabilities, because she is the claimant.", "Drew, beyond reasonable doubt.", "Cara, beyond reasonable doubt.", "Neither party, because the judge decides contributory negligence of their own motion."], "a": 0, "e": "The party who asserts a fact must prove it. The claimant proves the elements of her claim, but the defendant bears the burden on its defences, such as contributory negligence. The civil standard is the balance of probabilities: whether the fact is more likely than not."},
 {"t": "dr-evidence", "q": "Elgar Ltd alleges that its former finance director, Fay, fraudulently diverted company funds. Fay argues that, because fraud is a serious allegation, Elgar Ltd must prove it to a higher standard than in an ordinary civil claim.\n\nWhat standard of proof applies to the fraud allegation?", "o": ["Beyond reasonable doubt, because fraud is also a criminal offence.", "A heightened civil standard for dishonesty, somewhere between the balance of probabilities and beyond reasonable doubt.", "Clear and convincing evidence, which is the standard for all allegations of dishonesty.", "The balance of probabilities, though the seriousness of the allegation is relevant to whether it is proved.", "The balance of probabilities, but only if Fay has first been convicted of fraud."], "a": 3, "e": "There is one civil standard of proof, the balance of probabilities, even for serious allegations such as fraud. The seriousness of the allegation, and the inherent probability of it, are simply factors the court takes into account in deciding whether it is proved. No criminal conviction is required."},
 {"t": "dr-evidence", "q": "In a breach of contract claim, the claimant disclosed a letter which appears to be signed by the defendant's managing director agreeing to a price increase. The defendant believes the signature was forged. Disclosure took place three days ago, and witness statements are due next month.\n\nWhat should the defendant do to challenge the letter's authenticity?", "o": ["Nothing until trial, when it can cross-examine on the letter.", "Serve a notice requiring the letter to be proved at trial, or it is taken as genuine.", "Apply to strike out the claimant's case, because forged evidence is an abuse.", "Serve a notice to admit facts on the claimant.", "Ask the judge at the pre-trial review to exclude the letter."], "a": 1, "e": "A party is deemed to admit the authenticity of a document disclosed to it unless it serves a notice requiring the document to be proved at trial (CPR 32.19). The notice must be served by the later of the date for serving witness statements or 7 days after disclosure of the document. A notice to admit facts is used to ask the other side to admit facts, not to challenge documents."},
 {"t": "dr-evidence", "q": "Gil's solicitor failed to serve the statement of Gil's only eyewitness by the date ordered, and served it three weeks late. No relief from sanctions has been sought.\n\nWhat is the consequence for the witness's evidence?", "o": ["The statement is automatically admitted as hearsay instead.", "There is no consequence, because the defendant has now received the statement.", "Gil's claim is automatically struck out.", "The witness may give evidence, but Gil must pay the defendant's costs of the trial.", "The witness may not give oral evidence at trial unless the court gives permission."], "a": 4, "e": "If a witness statement is not served within the time ordered, the witness may not be called to give oral evidence unless the court gives permission (CPR 32.10). This is an automatic sanction, and Gil must apply for relief, which the court will consider under the Denton approach. It does not strike out the claim."},
 {"t": "dr-evidence", "q": "Hector wants to rely on a written statement from his late father, who saw the events in question but died before trial. Hector's solicitor forgets to serve a hearsay notice.\n\nWhat is the effect of the failure to serve a hearsay notice?", "o": ["The statement is inadmissible, because notice is a condition of admissibility.", "The statement is admissible only if the defendant consents.", "The statement remains admissible, but the failure may affect the weight given to it and costs.", "The statement is inadmissible, because the maker cannot be cross-examined.", "The statement is admissible only if Hector himself gives oral evidence confirming its contents at trial."], "a": 2, "e": "Hearsay is admissible in civil proceedings (Civil Evidence Act 1995 s.1). A party relying on hearsay where the maker will not be called should serve a hearsay notice, but failure to do so does not make the evidence inadmissible (s.2(4)); it may affect the weight the court gives the evidence and costs. The court also considers the s.4 weight factors."},
 {"t": "dr-evidence", "q": "Iona serves a hearsay notice saying she will rely on the statement of a delivery driver, Jed, without calling him, because he now works abroad. The defendant believes Jed's account is mistaken and wants to question him on it.\n\nWhat should the defendant do?", "o": ["Wait until trial and then object to Jed's statement as inadmissible hearsay.", "Serve a notice to admit facts on Iona no later than 21 days before the trial.", "Apply within 14 days of the hearsay notice for permission to call Jed for cross-examination.", "Apply for a witness summons requiring Jed to travel back from abroad to attend.", "Nothing, because a hearsay statement cannot be challenged by the other side."], "a": 2, "e": "Where a party relies on hearsay without calling the maker, the other party can apply for permission to call the maker and cross-examine him on the statement (Civil Evidence Act 1995 s.3; CPR 33.4). The application must be made within 14 days after service of the hearsay notice. The defendant can also attack the maker's credibility on giving notice within the same period."},
 {"t": "dr-evidence", "q": "Kofi's expert engineer has sent a draft report. Part of it is unhelpful to Kofi's case. Kofi asks his solicitor to tell the expert to delete that paragraph before the report is served, pointing out that Kofi is paying the expert's fees.\n\nWhat should happen?", "o": ["The expert must delete the paragraph, because experts act on the instructions of the client who pays them.", "The expert may delete the paragraph if Kofi signs the statement of truth.", "The paragraph may be deleted because draft reports are privileged.", "The expert must refuse, as an expert's overriding duty is to the court, not the payer.", "The expert may delete the paragraph if it is moved to a separate privileged letter."], "a": 3, "e": "An expert's overriding duty is to help the court on matters within their expertise, and it overrides any obligation to the person instructing or paying them (CPR 35.3). Expert evidence must be independent, objective and unbiased, so the expert must not remove an honest opinion to suit the client. The report's statement of truth is signed by the expert."},
 {"t": "dr-evidence", "q": "In a claim about subsidence, the court has directed that expert evidence be given by a single joint expert. Lyle, the claimant, wants to send the expert some additional instructions about the history of the property.\n\nWhat must Lyle do?", "o": ["Nothing more, because each party may instruct the expert privately.", "Obtain the court's permission before giving any instructions.", "Obtain the other party's agreement to the content of the instructions first.", "Send the instructions to the court, which will forward them to the expert.", "Send a copy of his instructions to the other party at the same time."], "a": 4, "e": "Where the court has directed a single joint expert, each party may give instructions to the expert, but must at the same time send a copy to the other parties (CPR 35.8). Agreement to the content is not required, and the court does not act as a go-between. The parties are jointly and severally liable for the expert's fees unless the court orders otherwise."},
 {"t": "dr-evidence", "q": "The court gave Mona permission to rely on a named orthopaedic surgeon. His report was unfavourable to her, and she now applies for permission to rely instead on a different surgeon whom she expects to be more helpful.\n\nHow is the court likely to deal with the application?", "o": ["Grant permission unconditionally, because a party may choose its own expert.", "Grant permission only on condition that she discloses the first surgeon's report.", "Refuse permission, because a party may never change expert once permission is given.", "Grant permission on condition that the first report is destroyed.", "Grant permission, and order that the first report remains privileged."], "a": 1, "e": "A party wanting to change expert after obtaining permission, especially to avoid an unfavourable report ('expert shopping'), will usually be given permission only on condition that it discloses the first expert's report. This prevents a party hiding unhelpful expert evidence. Changing expert is not prohibited outright."},
 {"t": "dr-evidence", "q": "The court directed the parties' surveyors to discuss the issues and prepare a joint statement. In the statement, the experts agree that the claimant's property lost value of £40,000. The defendant had not agreed to be bound by anything the experts agreed and now wishes to argue the loss was £25,000.\n\nIs the defendant bound by the experts' agreement?", "o": ["Yes, because the joint statement is binding on the court and the parties.", "Yes, because the defendant's expert had authority to settle the issue.", "No, unless the parties have expressly agreed to be bound by the experts' agreement.", "No, but the defendant can now call a new expert without permission.", "Yes, unless the defendant applies within 7 days to set the statement aside."], "a": 2, "e": "The court may direct experts to discuss the issues and produce a joint statement of what is agreed and disagreed (CPR 35.12). Any agreement between the experts does not bind the parties unless they expressly agree to be bound by it. The defendant can still argue its case, but calling another expert would require the court's permission."},
 {"t": "dr-disclosure", "q": "Two years before any dispute arose, the in-house lawyer of Navarro Ltd wrote a confidential email to the managing director advising whether a supply contract could be terminated early. Navarro Ltd is now being sued under that contract and has listed the email for standard disclosure.\n\nCan Navarro Ltd withhold the email from inspection?", "o": ["No, because litigation was not in contemplation when it was written.", "No, because in-house lawyers are employees and cannot give privileged advice.", "Yes, because all confidential documents can be withheld from inspection.", "Yes, under legal advice privilege, which does not need litigation to be in contemplation.", "No, because the email is relevant and adversely affects Navarro Ltd's case, so it must be inspected."], "a": 3, "e": "Legal advice privilege protects confidential communications between a client and its lawyer, including an in-house lawyer, made for the dominant purpose of giving or receiving legal advice, whether or not litigation is contemplated. The document must still be listed, but inspection can be refused. Confidentiality alone is not a ground for withholding, and relevance does not defeat privilege."},
 {"t": "dr-disclosure", "q": "Orla is in a dispute with HMRC about the tax treatment of a business transaction. She wants to withhold from inspection a letter of advice from her chartered accountant on the tax law involved, written before any dispute arose.\n\nIs the accountant's letter privileged?", "o": ["Yes, because it contains advice on the law.", "No, because legal advice privilege does not cover advice from accountants.", "Yes, because it is protected by litigation privilege.", "Yes, because accountants owe a professional duty of confidence.", "No, because tax advice is never privileged, even when given by a solicitor."], "a": 1, "e": "Legal advice privilege applies only to communications with lawyers (solicitors, barristers, in-house and foreign lawyers); it does not cover legal advice from other professionals such as accountants advising on tax. Litigation privilege does not apply because no litigation was in contemplation when the letter was written. A solicitor's tax advice would be privileged."},
 {"t": "dr-disclosure", "q": "After a customer wrote threatening to sue over a scalding accident in its café, Pinto Ltd's solicitors interviewed an independent customer who witnessed the accident and took a written statement from her, to help defend the threatened claim.\n\nCan Pinto Ltd withhold the statement from inspection?", "o": ["No, because communications between solicitors and third parties such as witnesses are never privileged.", "Yes, because it is protected by legal advice privilege as it was taken by solicitors.", "No, because the witness is independent of both parties and owes them no confidence.", "No, because statements from witnesses of fact must always be disclosed for inspection.", "Yes, under litigation privilege, as it was obtained for the dominant purpose of a threatened claim."], "a": 4, "e": "Litigation privilege protects confidential communications between a lawyer or client and a third party, such as a witness, made when litigation is reasonably in contemplation, for the dominant purpose of that litigation. A claim had been threatened and the statement was taken to defend it. Legal advice privilege covers only lawyer-client communications."},
 {"t": "dr-disclosure", "q": "Ruskin Ltd is ordered to give standard disclosure. Several relevant invoices and emails are held by its external accountants, who have kept the company's financial records under their engagement letter. The accountants will return the records to Ruskin Ltd on request.\n\nMust Ruskin Ltd disclose the documents held by its accountants?", "o": ["Yes, because the documents are within its control, as it has a right to possession of them.", "No, because they are not in Ruskin Ltd's physical possession.", "No, because documents held by a third party can only be obtained by an application for non-party disclosure.", "Yes, but only if the accountants consent to disclosure.", "No, because accountants' working papers are privileged."], "a": 0, "e": "A party must disclose documents that are or have been in its control, which includes documents it has a right to possess or to inspect or copy, such as files held by its agent or accountant (CPR 31.8). Physical possession is not necessary. The documents here are the company's own records, not privileged advice."},
 {"t": "dr-disclosure", "q": "Sefton Ltd suffered losses when a machine supplied by Tanner Ltd failed. Before deciding whether to sue, Sefton Ltd asks Tanner Ltd for its factory test records for the machine, which Sefton Ltd believes will show whether the fault was present at delivery. Tanner Ltd refuses.\n\nWhat can Sefton Ltd do?", "o": ["Nothing, because no disclosure can be ordered until proceedings have started.", "Apply for non-party disclosure, because no claim has yet been issued and Tanner Ltd is not yet a party.", "Apply for pre-action disclosure, as both are likely parties and early disclosure is desirable.", "Apply for specific disclosure under the court's case management powers.", "Issue a witness summons requiring Tanner Ltd to produce the records."], "a": 2, "e": "Before proceedings start, the court can order disclosure if the applicant and respondent are likely to be parties to subsequent proceedings, the documents would fall within standard disclosure if proceedings had started, and early disclosure is desirable to dispose fairly of the anticipated proceedings, help resolve the dispute without proceedings or save costs (CPR 31.16). The applicant usually pays the respondent's costs."},
 {"t": "dr-disclosure", "q": "Ulla is suing a taxi company for injuries suffered in a crash. She believes that a garage, which is not a party, holds service records showing that the taxi's brakes were faulty. Proceedings have been issued.\n\nWhat should Ulla do to obtain the garage's records?", "o": ["Apply for non-party disclosure, as the records are likely to support her case and are necessary.", "Apply for pre-action disclosure, because the garage is not a party.", "Ask the taxi company to include the garage's records in its own list, because they relate to its taxi.", "Serve a notice to prove documents on the garage.", "Apply for specific disclosure against the garage."], "a": 0, "e": "After proceedings have started, the court can order a non-party to disclose documents if they are likely to support the applicant's case or adversely affect another party's case, and disclosure is necessary to dispose fairly of the claim or to save costs (CPR 31.17). Pre-action disclosure and specific disclosure are orders against parties or likely parties. The applicant usually pays the non-party's costs."},
 {"t": "dr-disclosure", "q": "Varga Ltd served a witness statement from its sales manager which says, 'I confirmed the new price in my letter to the claimant dated 4 May.' The letter was not included in Varga Ltd's list of documents. The claimant wants to see it.\n\nWhat can the claimant do?", "o": ["Nothing, because only documents in the list of documents can be inspected.", "Apply for a search order to obtain the letter.", "Apply for non-party disclosure against the sales manager.", "Wait until trial and ask the sales manager to produce it in the witness box.", "Ask to inspect it, as a document referred to in a witness statement may be inspected."], "a": 4, "e": "A party may inspect, at any time, a document mentioned in a statement of case, witness statement, witness summary or expert report (CPR 31.14), subject to any claim to privilege. The claimant simply makes a written request. A search order is a drastic remedy for preserving evidence at risk of destruction."},
 {"t": "dr-disclosure", "q": "Before trial, Wyatt made a written offer to Xu Ltd headed 'without prejudice save as to costs' to accept £40,000 in settlement. Xu Ltd rejected it. At trial Wyatt is awarded £45,000.\n\nHow can the offer be used?", "o": ["It can be shown to the judge during the trial as evidence that Xu Ltd was liable.", "It can be shown to the judge on costs after judgment, without automatic Part 36 consequences.", "It cannot be shown to the judge at any stage, because it was made without prejudice and is privileged.", "It entitles Wyatt to indemnity costs and enhanced interest automatically.", "It can be used only if Xu Ltd agrees to waive privilege."], "a": 1, "e": "A 'without prejudice save as to costs' (Calderbank) offer is hidden from the judge on liability but can be shown once the case is decided, when the court takes it into account under its general costs discretion (CPR 44.2). It does not carry the automatic consequences of a Part 36 offer, such as indemnity costs and enhanced interest."},
 {"t": "dr-disclosure", "q": "During disclosure, Yeo Ltd's solicitors mistakenly included in the documents sent to the claimant a letter of advice from counsel on the weaknesses of Yeo Ltd's defence. The letter was clearly marked 'privileged and confidential: counsel's advice'. The claimant's solicitor has read it.\n\nWhat is the position on the claimant's use of the letter?", "o": ["The claimant may use it freely, because privilege has been waived by Yeo Ltd's disclosure of the letter.", "The claimant may use it because Yeo Ltd's solicitors were negligent.", "The claimant may use it, but only for the purposes of costs.", "Only with the court's permission, which is likely to be refused as the mistake was obvious.", "The claimant's solicitor must withdraw from acting for the claimant immediately."], "a": 3, "e": "Where a party inadvertently allows a privileged document to be inspected, the receiving party may use it only with the court's permission (CPR 31.20). Where the mistake would have been obvious to a reasonable solicitor, as here, the court will usually grant an injunction preventing its use. The receiving solicitor should return the document and tell the other side."},
 {"t": "dr-disclosure", "q": "Zoe and Arden Ltd exchanged without prejudice emails to settle Zoe's claim. Zoe says that Arden Ltd's last email accepted her offer of £25,000 and a binding settlement was made. Arden Ltd says negotiations were still continuing and refuses to pay.\n\nCan the court look at the without prejudice emails?", "o": ["Yes, because they are admissible to decide whether a settlement was actually reached.", "No, because without prejudice communications can never be shown to the court.", "Yes, but only if both parties agree to waive the protection.", "No, unless Zoe can show that Arden Ltd acted with unambiguous impropriety.", "Yes, but only after the court has decided the original claim."], "a": 0, "e": "Without prejudice communications are generally inadmissible, but there are exceptions, including where the issue is whether the negotiations resulted in a concluded settlement and on what terms. The court can therefore look at the emails to decide whether Zoe's offer was accepted. Unambiguous impropriety is a separate exception, and joint waiver is not needed here."},
 {"t": "dr-trial", "q": "Beatrix is a trainee solicitor appearing at a fast track trial in the County Court before a male District Judge.\n\nHow should she address the judge in court?", "o": ["As 'Your Honour'.", "As 'Judge'.", "As 'My Lord'.", "As 'Master'.", "As 'Your Worship'."], "a": 1, "e": "A District Judge (and Deputy District Judge) is addressed as 'Judge' (since December 2022; 'Sir' or 'Madam' is the old form). 'Your Honour' is used for Circuit Judges and Recorders, 'My Lord' or 'My Lady' for High Court and appellate judges, and 'Master' for a Master of the High Court in London."},
 {"t": "dr-trial", "q": "Corvin Ltd is the claimant in a multi-track claim listed for a five-day trial starting on Monday 15 June. The court has made no special order about the trial bundle.\n\nWho must file the trial bundle, and when?", "o": ["The defendant, not more than 7 and not less than 3 days before the trial.", "The court, which prepares the bundle from the court file.", "Each party separately, on the first day of the trial.", "Corvin Ltd, at least 14 days before the trial.", "Corvin Ltd, not more than 7 and not less than 3 days before the start of the trial."], "a": 4, "e": "Unless the court orders otherwise, the claimant is responsible for preparing and filing the trial bundle, not more than 7 and not less than 3 days before the start of the trial (PD 39A). The bundle should be paginated, indexed and agreed if possible, and contain the statements of case, witness statements, expert reports and other necessary documents."},
 {"t": "dr-trial", "q": "At trial, Dale's witness, Esther, is cross-examined about her eyesight. Dale's advocate wants to re-examine her. He would also like to ask her about a conversation she overheard after the accident, which was not mentioned in her witness statement or raised in cross-examination.\n\nWhat may Dale's advocate do in re-examination?", "o": ["Ask any questions he likes, including about the new conversation, because Esther is his own client's witness.", "Ask leading questions about her eyesight to repair the damage done.", "Ask open questions on matters arising from the cross-examination, but not raise the new conversation.", "Ask about the new conversation, because re-examination is the time to amplify the witness statement.", "Nothing, because re-examination is permitted only with the court's permission."], "a": 2, "e": "Re-examination is limited to matters arising out of the cross-examination, to clarify or repair damage done; no new topics may be introduced. Because Esther is the advocate's own witness, leading questions are not allowed. Adding new matters to a witness's evidence in chief (amplification) needs the court's permission and should be done before cross-examination."},
 {"t": "dr-trial", "q": "At the end of a two-day trial, both parties have called all their evidence. The judge invites closing speeches.\n\nIn what order are closing speeches normally made?", "o": ["The claimant first, with the defendant having the last word.", "The claimant first, followed by a reply from the defendant and a final reply from the claimant.", "Whichever party the judge considers to have the stronger case first.", "The defendant first, with the claimant having the last word.", "The defendant first, with the claimant having no right to reply."], "a": 3, "e": "At a civil trial the claimant normally opens, then each side calls its evidence; in closing, the defendant normally speaks first and the claimant has the last word. Advocates summarise the evidence, comment on credibility and make legal submissions."},
 {"t": "dr-trial", "q": "Fenella is cross-examining the defendant's main witness. The witness's statement says the claimant's car was travelling at 50 mph. Fenella's client, the claimant, will say she was travelling at 25 mph, but Fenella does not challenge the witness on speed.\n\nWhat is the risk for Fenella's client?", "o": ["None, because the claimant can give her own evidence on speed later.", "The claimant's evidence on speed will become inadmissible hearsay.", "The judge will order a retrial on the question of speed.", "The court may treat his evidence on speed as accepted, as it was not challenged.", "The defendant must call further evidence to prove the speed of the car."], "a": 3, "e": "A cross-examining advocate must put its party's case to each opposing witness on every point where its own evidence differs, so that the witness has a chance to respond. Failing to challenge a witness's evidence may be treated as accepting it. The claimant can still give evidence, but its weight may be undermined."},
 {"t": "dr-trial", "q": "Gareth is acting for the claimant. While preparing his closing submissions, he finds a recent Court of Appeal decision that is directly against his client's argument. The defendant's advocate has not mentioned it.\n\nWhat should Gareth do?", "o": ["Say nothing, because it is for the defendant's advocate to find the authorities.", "Mention it only if the judge asks whether there are any adverse authorities.", "Tell the defendant's advocate privately, but not the court.", "Draw the decision to the court's attention, even though it is against his client.", "Seek his client's instructions and follow them, because his duty is to the client."], "a": 3, "e": "A solicitor must draw the court's attention to relevant cases and statutory provisions likely to have a material effect on the outcome, including those against the client (SRA Code of Conduct para 2.7). The duty to the court overrides the duty to the client, so client instructions cannot authorise silence. Gareth can then argue that the case is distinguishable."},
 {"t": "dr-trial", "q": "In a fast track claim, the court sent each party a pre-trial checklist to be returned by a fixed date. Neither party returned it.\n\nWhat will the court usually do?", "o": ["Order that unless one is filed within 7 days, the whole claim and defence are struck out.", "Strike out the claim immediately, because the claimant must always file first.", "List the trial anyway and take no further action.", "Strike out the defence and enter judgment for the claimant.", "Transfer the claim to the small claims track."], "a": 0, "e": "If no party files a pre-trial checklist, the court will usually order that unless one is filed within 7 days, the claim, defence and any counterclaim will be struck out. If only one party fails, the court gives listing directions it thinks appropriate, possibly with a sanction against that party."},
 {"t": "dr-trial", "q": "Harriet's key witness is part-way through cross-examination when the court adjourns for lunch. The witness asks Harriet how she thinks the morning went and whether she should explain one of her answers differently after lunch.\n\nHow should Harriet respond?", "o": ["Advise the witness how to improve her answer, because she is Harriet's own witness and not the other side's.", "Discuss the evidence, but only if the other side's advocate is told afterwards.", "Ask the judge after lunch for permission to coach the witness.", "Tell the witness what the other side's next questions are likely to be.", "Decline to discuss the evidence with the witness until she has finished giving evidence."], "a": 4, "e": "An advocate must not discuss the evidence with a witness who is part-way through giving evidence, and must not seek to influence the substance of evidence (SRA Code of Conduct para 2.2). Coaching a witness is never permitted. Harriet may speak to the witness only about matters unrelated to her evidence."},
 {"t": "dr-trial", "q": "Iris wins a multi-track claim after a seven-day trial and is awarded her costs. The costs will be decided by detailed assessment, which will take several months. Her solicitor asks the judge to order something in the meantime.\n\nWhat is the judge most likely to order?", "o": ["Nothing, because no costs can be paid until the detailed assessment is complete.", "Payment of the full amount of Iris's bill within 14 days.", "A reasonable sum on account of costs, unless there is good reason not to.", "Summary assessment of all the costs, because the trial has ended.", "Fixed recoverable costs, because the claim was under £10 million."], "a": 2, "e": "Where the court orders a party to pay costs subject to detailed assessment, it will order that party to pay a reasonable sum on account of costs unless there is good reason not to (CPR 44.2(8)). Summary assessment is normally for hearings lasting one day or less, and fixed recoverable costs apply on the fast and intermediate tracks, not the multi-track."},
 {"t": "dr-trial", "q": "At the close of the claimant's evidence, the defendant's advocate believes the claimant has not proved its case. She wants to make a submission of no case to answer.\n\nWhat will the judge normally require before ruling on the submission?", "o": ["That the defendant serves written notice 7 days before trial.", "That the defendant elects not to call any evidence of its own.", "That the claimant agrees to the submission being made.", "Nothing, because a defendant may make the submission and then call evidence if it fails.", "That the defendant pays the claimant's costs of the trial so far."], "a": 1, "e": "A submission of no case to answer, made at the end of the claimant's case, is rare in civil trials, and the judge will usually require the defendant to elect not to call any evidence before ruling on it. If the submission fails, the defendant cannot then call evidence, so the risk is significant."},
 {"t": "dr-costs", "q": "On 1 March a defendant made a valid Part 36 offer of £60,000 with a 21-day relevant period, ending on 22 March. The claimant did not respond until 30 April, when she accepted the offer.\n\nWhat is the likely costs position, unless the court orders otherwise?", "o": ["She recovers her costs to 22 March and pays the defendant's costs from then to 30 April.", "The claimant recovers all her costs up to the date of acceptance on 30 April.", "Each party bears its own costs, because the claim has now settled by agreement between the parties.", "The claimant pays all the defendant's costs of the claim.", "The claimant recovers her costs on the indemnity basis up to 30 April."], "a": 0, "e": "Where a defendant's Part 36 offer is accepted after the relevant period has expired, unless the court orders otherwise, the claimant recovers its costs up to the end of the relevant period and pays the defendant's costs from then until acceptance, with interest on those costs. Acceptance within the period would have given her all her costs up to acceptance."},
 {"t": "dr-costs", "q": "A defendant makes a valid Part 36 offer of £35,000 with a 21-day relevant period. The claimant accepts it by written notice on day 15.\n\nWhat is the effect of the acceptance?", "o": ["The claim continues until the court has approved the terms of the settlement.", "The claim is stayed, but each party bears its own costs.", "The claim is stayed and she recovers her costs to the date of acceptance on the standard basis.", "The claim is stayed and the claimant recovers all her costs up to the date of acceptance on the indemnity basis.", "Judgment is entered for £35,000 plus enhanced interest."], "a": 2, "e": "Where a Part 36 offer is accepted within the relevant period, the claim is stayed on the terms of the offer and the claimant is entitled to its costs up to the date of acceptance, assessed on the standard basis if not agreed (CPR 36.13). Court approval is needed only for a child or protected party. Indemnity costs and enhanced interest are consequences of a claimant beating its own offer at trial."},
 {"t": "dr-costs", "q": "In a £50,000 claim, the defendant wrote 'This offer is made under Part 36' and offered £30,000, payable 28 days after acceptance, with a 21-day relevant period. The claimant rejected it and was awarded £28,000 at trial.\n\nWhat is the costs position?", "o": ["The claimant must pay the defendant's costs from the end of the relevant period, as under Part 36.", "The claimant must pay the defendant's costs of the whole claim on the indemnity basis.", "The offer must be ignored entirely, because it did not comply with Part 36.", "The defendant must pay enhanced interest because its offer was invalid.", "It is not a valid Part 36 offer, so no automatic consequences follow, but it may affect costs."], "a": 4, "e": "A defendant's Part 36 offer to settle a money claim must be an offer to pay a single sum within 14 days of acceptance; an offer to pay later is not treated as a Part 36 offer unless the claimant accepts it (CPR 36.6). The automatic Part 36 consequences therefore do not apply, but the court can still consider the offer as an admissible offer when exercising its discretion on costs (CPR 44.2)."},
 {"t": "dr-costs", "q": "On 1 June a defendant made a valid Part 36 offer of £80,000 with a 21-day relevant period. On 10 June it served written notice withdrawing the offer. On 15 June the claimant served written notice accepting it.\n\nIs the acceptance effective?", "o": ["No, because the offer was withdrawn on 10 June.", "Yes, because a withdrawal served within the relevant period only takes effect at its end.", "No, because a Part 36 offer can be withdrawn at any time without notice.", "Yes, but only if the court gives permission to accept an offer that has been withdrawn by written notice.", "Yes, because a Part 36 offer can never be withdrawn."], "a": 1, "e": "A Part 36 offer can be withdrawn by written notice, but if the notice is served during the relevant period, the withdrawal takes effect only at the end of that period, and if the offeree accepts before then, the withdrawal has no effect (CPR 36.9). The relevant period here ended on 22 June, so acceptance on 15 June is effective."},
 {"t": "dr-costs", "q": "Jem wins his small claims track claim for £4,500 against a builder, who defended reasonably. Jem's solicitor charged him £3,000 for the work. Jem asks the court to order the builder to pay his legal costs.\n\nWhat costs is Jem likely to recover?", "o": ["His full solicitor's costs of £3,000, because the loser pays the winner's costs.", "His solicitor's costs, assessed on the standard basis.", "Fixed recoverable costs under the fast track bands.", "Only limited sums, such as the fixed issue costs, court fees and limited witness expenses.", "Costs under Part 36, because he made an offer before the hearing."], "a": 3, "e": "On the small claims track, recoverable costs are very limited: fixed issue costs, court fees, limited witness expenses (such as loss of earnings up to £95 a day) and expert fees (up to £750), with further costs only where a party has behaved unreasonably (CPR 27.14). The builder defended reasonably. Part 36 does not apply on the small claims track."},
 {"t": "dr-costs", "q": "The claimant applied to the court to compel the defendant to give further disclosure. The judge granted the application and ordered 'claimant's costs in the case'. The claimant later loses at trial.\n\nWhat is the effect of the costs order on the costs of the disclosure application?", "o": ["Neither side recovers its costs of the application from the other.", "The claimant recovers its costs of the application, because it won the application.", "The claimant must pay the defendant's costs of the application, because it lost at trial.", "Each party's costs of the application will be decided again at a separate hearing.", "The claimant recovers its costs of the application on the indemnity basis."], "a": 0, "e": "A 'claimant's costs in the case' order means the claimant gets its costs of the application only if it is awarded its costs at the end of the case; if it loses, it does not pay the other side's costs of that application. 'Costs in the case' would have meant the loser at trial paying both sides' costs of the application, and 'costs in any event' would have given the claimant its costs whatever the outcome."},
 {"t": "dr-costs", "q": "At an interim hearing, the judge cannot decide who should pay the costs because the merits depend on facts still to be decided. She orders 'costs reserved'. No further order about those costs is made at the trial, which the defendant wins.\n\nWho pays the costs of the interim hearing?", "o": ["Each party bears its own, because no order was made.", "The claimant, because reserved costs become costs in the case.", "The defendant, because the decision was postponed at its request.", "Nobody, because reserved costs lapse at the end of the case.", "The claimant, but only on the indemnity basis."], "a": 1, "e": "'Costs reserved' means the decision on costs is postponed to a later occasion; if no later order is made, the costs become costs in the case (PD 44). Since the defendant won and is entitled to its costs, the claimant pays the costs of the interim hearing. The indemnity basis would need a special order."},
 {"t": "dr-costs", "q": "Kerensa wins a claim and is awarded her costs on the standard basis. On assessment, the costs judge has some doubt about whether 30 hours spent preparing witness statements were reasonably incurred.\n\nHow will the costs judge resolve the doubt?", "o": ["In favour of Kerensa, because she won the claim.", "In favour of Kerensa, provided the work was not plainly unnecessary.", "By allowing the costs in full, because proportionality does not apply to costs assessed after a trial.", "In favour of the paying party, and the costs must also be proportionate to the matters in issue.", "By referring the question to the trial judge for decision."], "a": 3, "e": "On the standard basis, any doubt about whether costs were reasonably incurred or reasonable in amount is resolved in favour of the paying party, and the court allows only costs that are proportionate, even if they were reasonably incurred (CPR 44.3). On the indemnity basis doubt is resolved in favour of the receiving party and proportionality does not apply."},
 {"t": "dr-costs", "q": "Lockwood Ltd, a company with no assets, brought a claim against Marsh Ltd which failed at trial. Lockwood Ltd cannot pay the £120,000 costs order. Its sole director and shareholder, Nigel, personally funded and controlled the claim, which would have benefited him alone.\n\nWhat can Marsh Ltd do to recover its costs?", "o": ["Nothing, because a company's director is never personally liable for its costs.", "Apply for a wasted costs order against Nigel.", "Apply for security for costs against Lockwood Ltd now that the trial is over.", "Enforce the costs order against Nigel automatically as the company's director.", "Seek a non-party costs order against Nigel, after adding him as a party for costs."], "a": 4, "e": "The court can order a person who is not a party to pay costs (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.51), and does so where, for example, a director controlled and funded litigation for his own benefit. The non-party must be added as a party for costs purposes and given a reasonable opportunity to attend a hearing (CPR 46.2). Wasted costs orders are made against legal representatives, and security for costs must be sought before trial."},
 {"t": "dr-costs", "q": "Oona, a retired teacher living in Leeds, sues a builder for £90,000. She has very little money and would struggle to pay the builder's costs if she lost. The builder applies for security for costs on the ground of her lack of means.\n\nIs the court likely to order security for costs?", "o": ["Yes, because any claimant who may be unable to pay the defendant's costs can be ordered to give security.", "Yes, because security is ordered in all claims over £50,000.", "No, because a poor individual living in England and Wales cannot be ordered to give security.", "No, because security for costs can be ordered only against a defendant.", "Yes, provided the builder shows that the claim is weak."], "a": 2, "e": "Security for costs can be ordered only if it is just and one of the conditions in CPR 25 applies, such as the claimant being resident out of the jurisdiction, or a company with reason to believe it will be unable to pay costs. Inability to pay is a condition only for companies and nominal claimants, so a poor individual resident in England and Wales cannot be ordered to give security on that ground. Security is ordered against claimants, not defendants."},
 {"t": "dr-appeals", "q": "A District Judge sitting in the County Court refuses Penrose Ltd's application to set aside a default judgment. Penrose Ltd wants to appeal.\n\nWho will hear Penrose Ltd's appeal?", "o": ["A High Court Judge.", "The Court of Appeal.", "Another District Judge.", "A Master of the High Court.", "A Circuit Judge."], "a": 4, "e": "An appeal goes to the next level of judge up. A first appeal from a District Judge of the County Court is heard by a Circuit Judge, whether the decision was interim or final (Access to Justice Act 1999 (Destination of Appeals) Order 2016). A High Court Judge hears appeals from Circuit Judges and Masters."},
 {"t": "dr-appeals", "q": "A Master in the High Court in London makes an order refusing Quentin permission to amend his particulars of claim. Quentin believes the Master applied the wrong legal test.\n\nTo whom should Quentin's appeal be made?", "o": ["A Circuit Judge.", "The Court of Appeal.", "A High Court Judge.", "The Supreme Court.", "A different Master of the High Court."], "a": 2, "e": "Appeals from a Master or a District Judge of the High Court go to a High Court Judge, following the rule that an appeal goes to the next level of judge up. The Court of Appeal hears first appeals from High Court Judges and second appeals. Permission to appeal will still be required."},
 {"t": "dr-appeals", "q": "A District Judge gave judgment against Rania in a fast track claim. She appealed to a Circuit Judge, who dismissed her appeal. Rania wants to appeal further.\n\nWhere would any further appeal go, and who could give permission?", "o": ["To a High Court Judge, who gives permission if there is a real prospect of success.", "To the Court of Appeal, with permission available from the Circuit Judge.", "None, because a second appeal is never possible in civil proceedings.", "To the Court of Appeal, which alone gives permission and applies a stricter test.", "To the Supreme Court, because the case has already had one appeal."], "a": 3, "e": "A second appeal always goes to the Court of Appeal, and only the Court of Appeal can give permission (CPR 52.7). Permission is given only if the appeal would have a real prospect of success and raises an important point of principle or practice, or there is some other compelling reason for the Court of Appeal to hear it. This is much stricter than the test for a first appeal."},
 {"t": "dr-appeals", "q": "Saul has just lost a trial before a Circuit Judge. The judge has given judgment orally and is about to deal with costs. Saul's solicitor believes the judge made an error of law.\n\nWhat should Saul's solicitor do first?", "o": ["File an appellant's notice at the appeal court without asking the trial judge.", "Ask the trial judge for permission to appeal at the hearing at which the decision is given.", "Write to the trial judge within 21 days asking for permission.", "Apply directly to the Court of Appeal for permission, because the appeal is on a point of law only.", "Do nothing, because permission is not needed for an appeal on a point of law."], "a": 1, "e": "Permission to appeal is required for almost all appeals, and the usual first step is to ask the lower court at the hearing at which the decision is made (or at an adjourned hearing for that purpose). If the trial judge refuses, the appellant can ask the appeal court in the appellant's notice. The appeal from a Circuit Judge goes to a High Court Judge, not the Court of Appeal."},
 {"t": "dr-appeals", "q": "Tegid asks the trial judge for permission to appeal against a final judgment. He accepts that his appeal has no real prospect of success, but argues that the case raises a point of law of public importance on which there are conflicting first-instance decisions.\n\nCan the judge give permission?", "o": ["No, because permission requires a real prospect of success in every case.", "No, because only the Court of Appeal can give permission on a point of law.", "Yes, but only if the other party consents.", "Yes, because permission is automatic on a point of law.", "Yes, because a compelling reason for an appeal can justify permission."], "a": 4, "e": "Permission for a first appeal may be given only where the appeal would have a real prospect of success, or there is some other compelling reason for it to be heard (CPR 52.6), such as an issue of public importance or a need to clarify the law. The test is alternative, so a compelling reason can justify permission without a real prospect of success."},
 {"t": "dr-appeals", "q": "Ursula has obtained judgment for £200,000 against Varley Ltd. Varley Ltd has been given permission to appeal and has filed its appellant's notice. Ursula starts enforcement proceedings, and Varley Ltd says she cannot do so while the appeal is pending.\n\nWho is right?", "o": ["Ursula, because an appeal does not stay the judgment unless the court orders a stay.", "Varley Ltd, because filing an appellant's notice automatically stays enforcement of the judgment.", "Varley Ltd, because permission to appeal automatically stays enforcement.", "Ursula, but only once the appeal has been dismissed.", "Neither, because enforcement is suspended until 21 days after judgment."], "a": 0, "e": "An appeal does not operate as a stay of the lower court's order unless the appeal court or the lower court orders otherwise (CPR 52.16). Varley Ltd must apply for a stay if it wants to prevent enforcement. Until then, the judgment debt is payable and can be enforced."},
 {"t": "dr-appeals", "q": "Wynn Ltd won at trial. The defendant has appealed. Wynn Ltd wants the appeal court to uphold the judgment, but for an additional reason that the trial judge rejected.\n\nWhat should Wynn Ltd do?", "o": ["Nothing, because the appeal court will consider all possible reasons automatically.", "File its own appellant's notice within 21 days of the decision.", "Raise the point orally at the appeal hearing without notice.", "File a respondent's notice, generally within 14 days.", "Apply to the trial judge to amend the judgment."], "a": 3, "e": "A respondent who wants the appeal court to uphold the decision for reasons different from or additional to those given by the lower court must file a respondent's notice (CPR 52.13), generally within 14 days of being served with the appellant's notice or notified that permission has been granted. An appellant's notice is for a party seeking to change the order."},
 {"t": "dr-appeals", "q": "After losing at trial, Xerxes finds an email in his own archive which he says proves his case. It was in his possession throughout, and a reasonable search before trial would have found it. He seeks to rely on it on appeal.\n\nIs the appeal court likely to admit the email?", "o": ["Yes, because an appeal is a complete rehearing of the whole case.", "Yes, because it would probably have had an important influence on the result.", "Yes, provided he files it with his appellant's notice and skeleton argument.", "No, because the appeal court never admits any evidence.", "No, because it could have been obtained with reasonable diligence for the trial."], "a": 4, "e": "An appeal is a review, not a rehearing, and the appeal court will not normally receive evidence not before the lower court. Fresh evidence is generally admitted only if it could not have been obtained with reasonable diligence for the trial, would probably have had an important influence on the result, and is apparently credible. The email fails the first requirement."},
 {"t": "dr-appeals", "q": "At trial, the judge refused to allow Yolande's counsel to cross-examine the defendant's only witness, saying that time was short. The judge then accepted the witness's evidence in full and found against Yolande.\n\nOn what basis is Yolande's appeal most likely to succeed?", "o": ["The decision was unjust because of a serious procedural irregularity.", "The judge's findings of fact were based on an assessment of the witness, which the appeal court will review afresh.", "The trial should be reheard because Yolande disagrees with the result.", "The judge made a case management decision, which is always open to appeal.", "The appeal court will substitute its own view of the witness's credibility."], "a": 0, "e": "An appeal will be allowed where the lower court's decision was wrong or unjust because of a serious procedural or other irregularity (CPR 52.21(3)). Refusing any cross-examination of the only opposing witness is a serious irregularity that makes the decision unjust. Appeal courts do not rehear cases or substitute their own view of credibility."},
 {"t": "dr-appeals", "q": "At a case management conference, a District Judge refused the claimant's request for permission to rely on a second expert, finding it disproportionate. The claimant thinks another judge might have allowed it and wants to appeal.\n\nHow is the appeal court likely to approach the appeal?", "o": ["It will rehear the application and decide it afresh.", "It will allow the appeal if it would have made a different decision.", "It will interfere only if the decision was plainly wrong or based on an error of principle.", "It will refuse to hear the appeal, because case management decisions can never be the subject of an appeal.", "It will allow the appeal automatically, because expert evidence is important."], "a": 2, "e": "Appeal courts are slow to interfere with case management and other discretionary decisions. They will do so only if the decision was plainly wrong, outside the generous range of reasonable decisions, or based on an error of principle. An appeal is a review, so the fact that another judge might have decided differently is not enough."},
 {"t": "dr-enforcement", "q": "Arlo has a £10,000 judgment against Bede, who is unemployed and rents his flat. An order to obtain information has revealed that Bede has £11,500 in a current account in his sole name, from an inheritance.\n\nWhich method of enforcement is most suitable?", "o": ["An attachment of earnings order.", "A charging order over Bede's flat.", "A third party debt order against the bank.", "A writ of control over Bede's household goods.", "A bankruptcy petition, because the debt exceeds £5,000."], "a": 2, "e": "A third party debt order captures money owed to the debtor by a third party, almost always a credit balance in a bank account; the interim order freezes the money and the bank then pays the creditor. Attachment of earnings needs an employed debtor, a charging order needs land the debtor owns, and household goods needed for basic domestic needs are exempt."},
 {"t": "dr-enforcement", "q": "Celyn obtains an interim third party debt order against her judgment debtor's bank account. When the order is served on the bank, the account holds £300. The next day, the debtor's salary of £2,400 is paid into the account.\n\nHow much is caught by the interim order?", "o": ["£300, because the order catches only money in the account when it is served.", "£2,700, because the order freezes the account until the final hearing.", "£2,400, because salary payments are always caught.", "Nothing, because an account holding salary cannot be frozen.", "The full judgment debt, which the bank must pay from its own funds."], "a": 0, "e": "An interim third party debt order catches only money in the account at the time the order is served on the bank; it does not catch later deposits or overdrawn accounts. To capture the debtor's salary, an attachment of earnings order would be more effective. The bank never pays from its own funds."},
 {"t": "dr-enforcement", "q": "Dmitri has a £6,000 judgment against Elin alone. He discovers that Elin has £20,000 in a joint savings account with her husband, who is not a party to the judgment.\n\nCan Dmitri obtain a third party debt order against the joint account?", "o": ["Yes, for half the balance, because Elin owns half the account.", "Yes, for the full judgment debt, because Elin can withdraw all the money.", "Yes, but only with the husband's written consent.", "No, as a joint account is caught only if all the holders are judgment debtors.", "No, because third party debt orders apply only to current accounts."], "a": 3, "e": "A third party debt order does not catch money in a joint account unless the judgment is against all the account holders, because the debt is owed by the bank to the account holders jointly. Dmitri should consider other methods, such as an order to obtain information to find Elin's other assets."},
 {"t": "dr-enforcement", "q": "An enforcement agent attends the home of Fergus, a self-employed graphic designer, under a warrant of control for a £3,000 judgment. Fergus's only valuable items are the laptop and design software he uses for work, worth £1,100 in total, and his cooker and fridge.\n\nWhich of these items can the agent take control of?", "o": ["The laptop and software, because they are worth more than £1,000.", "The cooker and fridge, because they are not used for work.", "All of them, because the debtor has no other assets.", "The laptop only, because business equipment is never exempt for the self-employed.", "None of them, because they are exempt goods."], "a": 4, "e": "Exempt goods include items necessary for the debtor's personal use in their business or trade, up to an aggregate value of £1,350, and items necessary for the basic domestic needs of the debtor and household, such as a cooker and fridge. Fergus's work equipment is worth £1,100, within the limit, so none of these items can be taken."},
 {"t": "dr-enforcement", "q": "Gwyneth has a judgment for £9,000 against Hal, a self-employed courier. An enforcement agent finds a valuable car at Hal's home. Hal shows that the car is on hire purchase from a finance company and the instalments are still being paid.\n\nCan the agent take control of the car?", "o": ["Yes, because Hal is in possession of the car.", "No, because hire purchase goods belong to the finance company.", "Yes, because a car is never an exempt item.", "Yes, but only if Hal has missed an instalment.", "No, because cars can never be taken under a writ of control."], "a": 1, "e": "An enforcement agent can take only goods belonging to the debtor. Goods on hire purchase or leased belong to the finance company, so they are third-party goods and cannot be taken. Cars can be taken if they belong to the debtor and are not exempt, but possession alone is not ownership."},
 {"t": "dr-enforcement", "q": "Imogen has a County Court judgment for £12,000 against a shop owner who has valuable stock. She wants to enforce by taking control of the shop's goods.\n\nHow must she enforce?", "o": ["Transfer it to the High Court for a writ of control by a High Court Enforcement Officer.", "Obtain a warrant of control in the County Court, executed by County Court bailiffs, as it is a County Court judgment.", "Obtain an attachment of earnings order against the shop owner.", "Obtain a charging order over the stock.", "Apply to the County Court for a writ of control executed by the court's own staff."], "a": 0, "e": "A judgment of £5,000 or more to be enforced by taking control of goods must be enforced in the High Court, unless it arises under a regulated consumer credit agreement. A County Court judgment is transferred to the High Court for this purpose and a writ of control is executed by a High Court Enforcement Officer. Charging orders secure debts on land or securities, not stock."},
 {"t": "dr-enforcement", "q": "An attachment of earnings order has been made against Jasper, a warehouse worker. It sets a normal deduction rate of £150 a month and a protected earnings rate of £1,400 a month. In March Jasper works reduced hours and earns only £1,300.\n\nHow much should his employer deduct in March?", "o": ["£150, the normal deduction rate, regardless of his earnings.", "£100, the difference between £1,400 and £1,300.", "£75, half the normal deduction rate.", "Nothing, because his earnings are below the protected earnings rate.", "£150, with the shortfall carried forward as arrears against Jasper personally."], "a": 3, "e": "Under an attachment of earnings order the court sets a normal deduction rate and a protected earnings rate, the minimum the debtor must be left with to meet basic needs. If his earnings in a pay period are below the protected earnings rate, nothing is deducted. The employer simply deducts what it can within the order."},
 {"t": "dr-enforcement", "q": "Kestrel Ltd has a £25,000 judgment against Lark Trading Ltd but does not know what assets Lark Trading Ltd has. Kestrel Ltd obtains an order to obtain information.\n\nWhat is the effect of the order?", "o": ["Its bank must disclose the balances of all the company's accounts to Kestrel Ltd within 14 days.", "The company must pay the whole judgment debt within 7 days of service.", "A director or other officer must attend court and answer questions on oath about its means.", "Its auditors must attend court and give evidence about its accounts.", "The company must file up-to-date accounts at Companies House within 14 days."], "a": 2, "e": "An order to obtain information (CPR 71) requires the judgment debtor, or where it is a company, an officer such as a director, to attend court and answer questions on oath about its means and produce relevant documents. It is a fact-finding tool to help choose a method of enforcement. Failure to attend or answer can lead to a suspended committal order."},
 {"t": "dr-enforcement", "q": "Mabli obtained a final charging order over Nye's house to secure a £30,000 judgment debt for unpaid building work. A year later Nye has still paid nothing.\n\nWhat can Mabli do to obtain payment from the house?", "o": ["Apply for an order for sale of the house, which the court has a discretion to make.", "Nothing, because a charging order only secures the debt and can never produce payment.", "Take possession of the house herself under the charging order.", "Apply for an attachment of earnings order over the house.", "Register a second charging order, which automatically forces a sale."], "a": 0, "e": "A charging order secures the debt on the debtor's land but does not itself produce money. If the debt remains unpaid, the creditor can apply, usually by a separate Part 8 claim, for an order for sale so that the debt is paid from the proceeds. The court has a discretion and will consider the interests of the debtor's family and any co-owners."},
 {"t": "dr-enforcement", "q": "Olwen has a judgment for £4,000 against Pryor, an individual who has ignored all demands for payment. Olwen wants to put maximum pressure on Pryor by threatening bankruptcy.\n\nCan Olwen present a bankruptcy petition against Pryor based on this debt?", "o": ["Yes, once a statutory demand has gone unpaid for 21 days.", "No, because the debt is below the £5,000 minimum for a bankruptcy petition.", "Yes, because any unpaid judgment debt, whatever its size, can found a bankruptcy petition.", "Yes, because the minimum debt for an individual is £750.", "No, because a judgment creditor can never use insolvency proceedings."], "a": 1, "e": "A bankruptcy petition against an individual requires a debt of at least £5,000; the £750 minimum applies to a winding-up petition against a company. Olwen's £4,000 debt is too small, so she should use another method, such as an order to obtain information followed by a suitable enforcement method."}
];

;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["ls"] = [
 {"t":"sra-regulatory-role","q":"Harriet, a retired bank manager who is not a lawyer, helps her elderly neighbour transfer his registered house to his daughter as a gift. She prepares the TR1 transfer and lodges the application at HM Land Registry. She charges nothing and receives no other benefit.\n\nHas Harriet unlawfully carried on a reserved legal activity?","o":["Yes, because preparing an instrument transferring land is a reserved instrument activity whoever does it.","No, because preparing instruments of transfer is a reserved activity only when done for a fee, gain or reward.","Yes, because lodging documents at HM Land Registry is reserved to solicitors.","No, because a gift of land is not a transfer of an interest in land.","No, because Harriet has professional experience of property transactions."],"a":1,"e":"Reserved instrument activities (preparing instruments transferring or creating interests in land, and lodging documents at the Land Registry) are reserved under the Legal Services Act 2007 only when done for a fee, gain or reward. Harriet acted for nothing, so she has not carried on a reserved activity. A gift of land is still a transfer of an interest in land, and professional experience does not make someone an authorised person."},
 {"t":"sra-regulatory-role","q":"Ian, a solicitor at a high street firm, dishonestly transferred £60,000 from the client account to pay his personal debts. The SRA has investigated and concluded that he should no longer be allowed to practise.\n\nWhich body can strike Ian off the roll of solicitors?","o":["The SRA, as the regulator of solicitors.","The Legal Ombudsman, following a complaint by the client.","The Law Society, as the approved regulator.","The Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal, on a case brought by the SRA.","The Legal Services Board, as the oversight regulator."],"a":3,"e":"Only the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal (or the High Court) can strike off or suspend a solicitor. The SRA investigates and brings the case to the SDT; its own powers are limited to sanctions such as rebukes, fines, conditions and intervention. The Legal Ombudsman deals with poor service, and the Law Society has delegated its regulatory functions to the SRA."},
 {"t":"sra-regulatory-role","q":"Lomax LLP, a traditional (non-ABS) firm, is found by the SRA to have seriously failed to carry out required customer due diligence on high-risk clients, in breach of the Money Laundering Regulations 2017. The SRA decides to deal with the matter itself rather than refer it to the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal.\n\nWhat is the maximum financial penalty the SRA can impose on the firm?","o":["There is no upper limit, because the breach relates to economic crime.","£25,000, the cap on SRA fines for traditional firms.","£250 million, the cap that applies to licensed bodies.","£50,000, double the normal cap for serious breaches.","None, because the SRA cannot fine firms; only the Tribunal can."],"a":0,"e":"The SRA's fines for solicitors and traditional firms are normally capped at £25,000. Since 2024, under the Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act 2023, the SRA can impose unlimited fines for conduct relating to economic crime, which includes money laundering failures. The £250 million cap applies to ABSs in other cases."},
 {"t":"sra-regulatory-role","q":"Mr Osei is unhappy with Ridley Solicitors, which handled his house sale. The solicitor took weeks to answer emails, and an error in the completion statement cost him £800. He wants compensation. There is no suggestion of dishonesty or other misconduct.\n\nWhat is the correct route for his complaint?","o":["Report the solicitor to the SRA, which will investigate and order the firm to pay him compensation for his loss.","Apply to the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal for compensation.","Complain directly to the Legal Ombudsman without first contacting the firm.","Claim on the SRA Compensation Fund.","Complain to the firm first and then, if unresolved, to the Legal Ombudsman."],"a":4,"e":"Delay, poor communication and mistakes are service issues. The client must first use the firm's complaints procedure, and can then go to the Legal Ombudsman, which can order compensation (up to £50,000). The SRA deals with conduct and does not award compensation to individual complainants, and the Compensation Fund is for dishonesty or failure to account."},
 {"t":"sra-regulatory-role","q":"Ms Patel complained to her solicitors about poor service on her divorce. The firm sent her its final response nine months ago; it explained her right to complain to the Legal Ombudsman and the time limit for doing so. The poor service happened 14 months ago. She has only now decided to complain to the Ombudsman, and there are no exceptional circumstances.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her position?","o":["She can complain, because the Ombudsman's time limit is six years from the poor service.","She can complain, because the firm's response did not resolve her complaint.","She is likely to be out of time, as she had six months from the firm's final response.","Her complaint is in time, because she has a year from the firm's final response in which to go to the Ombudsman.","She must first complain to the SRA, which will refer her to the Ombudsman."],"a":2,"e":"A complaint to the Legal Ombudsman must normally be made within six months of the firm's final response (provided the response explained this time limit), and within one year of the problem or of when the client should reasonably have known of it. Ms Patel is outside both limits. The SRA does not act as a gateway to the Ombudsman."},
 {"t":"sra-regulatory-role","q":"Tom acts for a client in a contract dispute. The client instructs him to send the other side a letter that Tom knows contains a false statement of fact, which would help the client's case. The client says that acting in his best interests requires Tom to send it.\n\nHow should Tom resolve the conflict between the SRA Principles?","o":["Send the letter, because the duty to act in the client's best interests takes priority over the other Principles when they conflict.","Send the letter, but add a note that the facts have not been checked.","Refuse to send it, because honesty and public trust take precedence over the client's interests.","Refuse to send it only if the other side is unrepresented.","Send the letter, because it is the client's decision and not the solicitor's."],"a":2,"e":"Where the SRA Principles conflict, those that safeguard the wider public interest (such as the rule of law, public trust and confidence, honesty and integrity) take precedence over the client's interests. Knowingly sending a false statement would breach the duties of honesty and integrity and would undermine public trust. The client's instructions cannot override this, whether or not the other side is represented."},
 {"t":"sra-regulatory-role","q":"QuickDocs Ltd, an unregulated online business with no authorised lawyers, offers customers a service in which its staff take their statutory declarations (for example, to confirm a change of name) by video call for £15.\n\nWhich of the following best describes this service?","o":["It is lawful, because a statutory declaration is not a court document.","It is lawful, because the service is provided online.","It is lawful, provided the customer is told that the business is not regulated.","It is lawful, because only the swearing of affidavits for use in court proceedings is a reserved legal activity.","It is unlawful, because taking statutory declarations is a reserved legal activity."],"a":4,"e":"The administration of oaths, which covers taking affidavits and statutory declarations, is one of the six reserved legal activities under the Legal Services Act 2007. Only authorised or exempt persons may carry it on, and doing so when not entitled is a criminal offence. The method of delivery and disclaimers about regulation make no difference."},
 {"t":"sra-regulatory-role","q":"Una is a paralegal (not an authorised person) at Vine Solicitors. She has her own caseload of County Court debt claims, which she issues, conducts and settles without any solicitor reviewing her files. No authorised person at the firm has overall responsibility for her cases, and there are no supervision arrangements.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Una is unlawfully conducting litigation, because no authorised person retains responsibility for the litigation and supervises her.","Una may lawfully conduct the claims, because she works for an SRA-authorised firm.","Una may lawfully conduct the claims, because debt claims are not reserved litigation.","Una may lawfully conduct the claims, provided the clients consent.","Una is acting unlawfully only when she appears in court as an advocate."],"a":0,"e":"Conduct of litigation is a reserved activity. Following the Court of Appeal in Mazur (2026), an unauthorised person may carry out litigation tasks for and on behalf of an authorised individual who retains responsibility for the litigation and has appropriate supervision and control in place. Here no authorised person has responsibility or supervises her, so she is carrying on a reserved activity unlawfully; working for an authorised firm or having client consent is not enough."},
 {"t":"sra-regulatory-role","q":"Wanda, a solicitor with a practising certificate, takes a job at an unregulated employment advice company. She will advise members of the public on employment disputes and represent them in the employment tribunal.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her position?","o":["She cannot do this work, because solicitors may practise only through SRA-authorised firms.","She can do this work, and her clients will have the same protections, including compulsory insurance and the Compensation Fund, as clients of an SRA-authorised firm.","She can do this work, but she is no longer bound by the SRA Code of Conduct.","She can do this work, but remains bound by the SRA Principles and Code and must tell clients they lack SRA protections such as compulsory insurance.","She can do this work only if the company obtains a licence as an alternative business structure."],"a":3,"e":"Solicitors may work in unregulated businesses providing non-reserved services, and employment advice and tribunal representation are not reserved. Wanda remains personally bound by the SRA Principles and Code, and under Code paras 8.10-8.11 she must ensure clients understand that the business is not SRA-regulated and that they lack compulsory PII and Compensation Fund protection. An ABS licence would be needed only to provide reserved activities with non-lawyer owners."},
 {"t":"sra-regulatory-role","q":"Yasmin is a self-employed solicitor who wants to offer probate applications and conveyancing to the public in her own name, without setting up an authorised firm. She plans to employ an assistant and to hold clients' completion money in her own bank account.\n\nCan she do this?","o":["Yes, because any solicitor with a practising certificate can carry out reserved activities in her own name.","No, because as a freelance solicitor she may not employ staff or hold client money other than fees or disbursements.","Yes, provided she has adequate and appropriate insurance.","No, because freelance solicitors cannot carry out any reserved activities.","Yes, provided she opens a separate client account for the completion money and complies with the SRA Accounts Rules in operating it."],"a":1,"e":"A freelance solicitor may carry out reserved activities without being an authorised firm only if she meets strict conditions: she employs no one, holds no client money other than money for fees or disbursements on account, and has adequate and appropriate insurance. Yasmin's plan breaks the first two conditions, so she would need to set up an authorised sole practice. Insurance alone is not sufficient."},

 {"t":"pii-other-providers","q":"Kent & Co LLP has professional indemnity insurance at the minimum level required by the SRA. Through negligence, it causes a client a loss of £3.6 million on a commercial property transaction.\n\nWhat is the insurer obliged to pay under the minimum terms?","o":["£2 million plus defence costs, because that is the minimum for all firms.","£3.6 million, because the insurer must indemnify the client in full.","Nothing, unless the firm has bought top-up cover.","£3 million, including defence costs.","£3 million, with defence costs paid on top."],"a":4,"e":"The minimum cover for a firm with limited liability (an LLP or company) is £3 million for each and every claim; for other firms it is £2 million. Defence costs are paid in addition to the indemnity limit. The client must look to the LLP itself for the remaining £600,000, which is why firms doing high-value work should consider top-up cover."},
 {"t":"pii-other-providers","q":"Lane & Moss, a partnership, negligently misses a limitation date, and a client's claim worth £150,000 is lost. The firm's PII policy has an excess of £25,000 for each claim.\n\nHow will the client's loss be met?","o":["The insurer pays the client £150,000 in full and recovers the £25,000 excess from the firm.","The insurer pays the client £125,000, and the client must recover £25,000 from the partners.","The insurer pays £125,000 and the client must apply to the Compensation Fund for the rest.","The firm must pay the first £25,000 to the client before the insurer pays anything.","The insurer pays £150,000 only if the firm has already paid the excess to the insurer."],"a":0,"e":"Under the minimum terms, a firm may agree an excess with its insurer, but the excess cannot be deducted from the payment to the claimant. The insurer pays the client in full and then recovers the excess from the firm. The Compensation Fund covers dishonesty and failure to account, not negligence."},
 {"t":"pii-other-providers","q":"When renewing its PII, Mill Solicitors failed to tell its insurer that it had recently started high-risk investment fraud work. A client from that work now has a £400,000 negligence claim against the firm. The insurer discovers the non-disclosure.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the insurer's position?","o":["It can avoid the policy, because the firm failed to disclose a material fact.","It can reduce its payment to the client in proportion to the extra risk not disclosed.","It must pay the client, but can then seek to recover the money from the firm.","It must pay the client only if the non-disclosure was innocent.","It can refuse the claim, leaving the client to apply to the Compensation Fund."],"a":2,"e":"The minimum terms prevent a participating insurer from avoiding, repudiating or cancelling the policy, or reducing the payment to the claimant, because of the firm's non-disclosure or misrepresentation. The insurer must pay the client and can then pursue the firm to recover its outlay. This protects clients whatever the firm's own failings."},
 {"t":"pii-other-providers","q":"In 2021, Nash Solicitors made a mistake on a client's lease, insured at the time with Insurer A. In 2024 the firm moved its cover to Insurer B. The firm knew nothing of the mistake until the client made a claim against it in 2026.\n\nWhich insurer must deal with the claim?","o":["Insurer A, because the policy in force when the mistake was made responds.","Both insurers equally, because the mistake and the claim fall in different policy years.","Insurer A, unless the firm told Insurer B about the mistake when it switched.","Insurer B, because the policy in force when the claim is first made against the firm responds.","Neither insurer, because the claim relates to work done under an expired policy."],"a":3,"e":"SRA minimum terms cover is written on a claims-made basis: the policy in force when the claim is first made against the firm pays, not the policy in force when the mistake happened. The claim was made in 2026, when Insurer B was on risk. This is why continuous cover and run-off cover matter."},
 {"t":"pii-other-providers","q":"Oak Solicitors could not find an insurer willing to renew its PII by the renewal date. Thirty-five days have now passed and it still has no new policy.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It may carry on practising normally, including taking new instructions, for up to 90 days while it looks for new cover.","It is in the cessation period, so it must not take new instructions and must close if still uninsured at its end.","It must close immediately, because it has no current insurance.","It may carry on normally, because the previous policy continues until a new one is found.","It may take new instructions, but only from existing clients."],"a":1,"e":"If a firm fails to renew, its old policy continues for an extended policy period of 30 days, followed by a cessation period of a further 60 days. After 35 days Oak is in the cessation period, during which it must not take on new work, and it must close if it has no cover by the end. It cannot carry on normally for the whole 90 days."},
 {"t":"pii-other-providers","q":"Pallas plc, a large company with an annual turnover of £50 million, lost £1 million when a solicitor at its former law firm stole money held for it in the client account. The firm's PII does not respond to the loss, and the solicitor has no assets. Pallas plc asks whether it can claim on the SRA Compensation Fund.\n\nWhich of the following best describes its position?","o":["It is unlikely to receive a grant, because the Fund is generally not available to businesses with a turnover of £2 million or more.","It is entitled to a grant of up to £2 million, because the loss was caused by dishonesty.","It is entitled to a grant of £1 million, because there is no other remedy.","It can apply only once it has sued the solicitor personally to judgment.","It can claim only if the firm also failed to have PII."],"a":0,"e":"The Compensation Fund is a discretionary fund of last resort for losses caused by dishonesty or failure to account. It is available to individuals and smaller businesses, charities and trusts (broadly those with turnover, income or assets below £2 million); larger businesses are expected to protect themselves. No applicant has a right to a payment."},
 {"t":"pii-other-providers","q":"Ola used Speedy Wills Ltd, an unregulated will-writing company that employs a solicitor, Pam. A Speedy Wills employee stole £30,000 that Ola had paid to the company to hold towards inheritance tax. The company has collapsed with no assets.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ola's position?","o":["She can claim on the SRA Compensation Fund, because a practising solicitor is employed by the company and the loss involved client money.","She can claim against the company's compulsory PII under the SRA minimum terms.","She can complain to the Legal Ombudsman, who can order the SRA to compensate her.","She can claim on the Compensation Fund, because the loss was caused by dishonesty.","She has no claim on the Compensation Fund or compulsory PII, because the company is not regulated by the SRA."],"a":4,"e":"Compulsory PII and the Compensation Fund protect clients of SRA-authorised firms only. Clients of unregulated businesses do not have them, even if a solicitor works there, which is why the solicitor must explain this to clients (Code paras 8.10-8.11). The Legal Ombudsman generally deals with authorised persons and cannot order the SRA to pay."},
 {"t":"pii-other-providers","q":"Bright Law Ltd is an alternative business structure licensed by the SRA. Quentin, a non-lawyer investor, proposes to buy 15% of its shares. He will not be a manager of the firm.\n\nWhat is required before Quentin acquires the shares?","o":["Nothing, because only managers of an ABS need regulatory approval.","SRA approval of Quentin as a person holding a material interest.","Approval from the Legal Services Board.","Nothing, because owners need approval only if they hold more than 25% of the shares.","The consent of all the firm's clients."],"a":1,"e":"Anyone who holds a material interest in an ABS, generally 10% or more of the shares or voting rights, must be approved by the licensing authority as fit and proper. A 15% holding crosses that threshold, so the SRA must approve Quentin even though he will not be a manager. The 25% figure is the beneficial owner threshold for money laundering due diligence, not ABS ownership."},
 {"t":"pii-other-providers","q":"Rita was persuaded by a claims management company to pursue a claim about a mis-sold insurance policy. She is unhappy with the company's service and fees, and the company has not resolved her complaint.\n\nWhere can she take her complaint next?","o":["The Legal Ombudsman, because claims management is a legal service.","The SRA, because the company handles legal claims.","The Financial Ombudsman Service, because claims management companies are regulated by the FCA.","Nowhere, because claims management companies are unregulated.","The Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal."],"a":2,"e":"Since April 2019 claims management companies have been regulated by the Financial Conduct Authority, and complaints about them go to the Financial Ombudsman Service, not the Legal Ombudsman. They are regulated, but not as lawyers, so the SRA and the SDT have no role."},
 {"t":"pii-other-providers","q":"Sam wants advice and representation at a county court hearing next month. He would like to instruct a barrister directly, to avoid the cost of also instructing a solicitor.\n\nWhich of the following best describes his position?","o":["He cannot, because barristers may only be instructed through a solicitor.","He can, but the barrister may not represent him in court.","He can, but only if he is a business client.","He can instruct a barrister who is authorised to accept public access instructions.","He can instruct any barrister directly, because barristers have rights of audience in all courts."],"a":3,"e":"Barristers regulated by the Bar Standards Board who are authorised for public access work can take instructions directly from members of the public without a solicitor. Not every barrister is so authorised, so Sam must choose one who is. A public access barrister can both advise and appear in court."},

 {"t":"equality-act","q":"Una, an associate at Ash LLP, has multiple sclerosis. She has had several short absences for treatment and flare-ups. The firm gives her a formal warning under its absence policy, which is applied to all staff in the same way.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Una's likely claim?","o":["Direct disability discrimination, which cannot be justified.","Indirect disability discrimination only, because the absence policy is a provision applied to everyone in the same way.","Discrimination arising from disability, unless the warning was a proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim.","No claim, because the firm applies the same absence policy to all staff.","Harassment, because the warning created a hostile environment."],"a":2,"e":"Multiple sclerosis is a disability from diagnosis. Treating Una unfavourably because of something arising in consequence of her disability (her disability-related absences) is discrimination arising from disability under EA 2010 s.15, unless the firm shows it was a proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim or it did not know of the disability. Applying the same policy to everyone is no defence; the firm may also have to make reasonable adjustments to the policy."},
 {"t":"equality-act","q":"Xavier, a former associate at Yew LLP, brought an employment tribunal claim alleging race discrimination, which he lost. He brought the claim in good faith. When a prospective employer asks Yew LLP for a reference, the managing partner refuses to give one 'because Xavier sued us'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Xavier's position?","o":["He has no claim, because his discrimination claim failed.","He has no claim, because he is no longer an employee.","He has a claim for direct race discrimination, because the refusal is connected with his earlier race claim.","He has a claim for harassment related to race.","He has a claim for victimisation, because he suffered a detriment for bringing a claim under the Act."],"a":4,"e":"Bringing proceedings under the Equality Act is a protected act, and subjecting someone to a detriment because of it is victimisation (s.27). It does not matter that the original claim failed, provided it was not a false allegation made in bad faith. Former employees are protected against post-employment victimisation, such as refusing a reference."},
 {"t":"equality-act","q":"Zoe applies for a training contract at Birch Solicitors. The recruiting partner wrongly believes she is gay, based on comments on her social media, and rejects her application for that reason. Zoe is not gay.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Zoe's position?","o":["She has no claim, because she does not have the protected characteristic.","She has a claim for direct discrimination because of sexual orientation, by perception.","She has a claim for indirect discrimination, because the partner applied a criterion to her.","She has a claim for harassment, because the decision was offensive to her.","She has a claim for direct discrimination, but the firm can justify it as proportionate."],"a":1,"e":"Direct discrimination covers less favourable treatment 'because of' a protected characteristic, including where the discriminator wrongly perceives the person to have it. So Zoe can claim even though she is not gay. Direct discrimination cannot be justified except in age cases."},
 {"t":"equality-act","q":"Cedar LLP refuses to consider anyone over 60 for its new partner intake, saying it wants partners who will stay for at least ten years. Colin, aged 62, is turned down on this ground alone.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It is direct age discrimination, but the firm may justify it if it is a proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim.","It is direct age discrimination, which can never be justified.","It is indirect age discrimination only, because the rule is applied in the same way to every candidate for the partner intake.","It is lawful, because age is not protected in relation to partnership.","It is lawful positive action in favour of younger candidates."],"a":0,"e":"A rule that expressly turns on age is direct age discrimination. Age is the one characteristic where direct discrimination can be justified, if the employer shows a proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim; a blanket bar is hard to justify. Partnership decisions are covered by the Act, and positive action concerns under-represented groups, not excluding older people."},
 {"t":"equality-act","q":"Dale Solicitors' office is on the first floor with no lift. Ellen, a prospective client who uses a wheelchair, telephones to make an appointment about her late mother's estate. The receptionist tells her the firm cannot see her because she cannot get up the stairs.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It is not in breach, because it cannot be expected to install a lift.","It is not in breach, because the duty to make reasonable adjustments arises only once a client has actually instructed the firm.","It is in breach only if it had previously been told about Ellen's disability.","It is likely to be in breach, because it must take reasonable steps, such as meeting Ellen somewhere accessible.","It can meet Ellen elsewhere, but may charge her the extra cost of doing so."],"a":3,"e":"Service providers must take reasonable steps to avoid the substantial disadvantage a physical feature causes disabled people, for example by meeting the client at an accessible location (EA 2010 ss.20-21). For service providers the duty is anticipatory, owed to disabled people generally before any particular client asks. The cost of a reasonable adjustment cannot be passed to the disabled person."},
 {"t":"equality-act","q":"Faisal, a Muslim trainee, is repeatedly the target of 'jokes' about his religion made by a senior associate in team meetings. The associate says it is just banter and that others laugh. Faisal finds it humiliating and has complained.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Faisal's likely claim?","o":["No claim, because the associate did not intend to offend.","No claim, because the comments were made to the whole team in meetings rather than directed at Faisal alone.","Victimisation, because Faisal has been treated badly.","Harassment related to religion, because the conduct was unwanted and had a humiliating effect on him.","Indirect religious discrimination, because the jokes were told to everyone."],"a":3,"e":"Harassment is unwanted conduct related to a protected characteristic that has the purpose or effect of violating dignity or creating an intimidating, hostile, degrading, humiliating or offensive environment (s.26). Effect is enough, so intention is irrelevant; the tribunal considers Faisal's perception, the circumstances and whether it was reasonable for the conduct to have that effect. Victimisation would require a detriment because of a protected act."},
 {"t":"equality-act","q":"An associate at Gale LLP sexually harassed a trainee at the firm's office party. The firm had a written anti-harassment policy, but it had never trained staff on it and had ignored two earlier complaints about the same associate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gale LLP's liability?","o":["It is not liable, because the harassment happened at a social event.","It is likely to be liable, because it cannot show that it took all reasonable steps to prevent the harassment.","Only the associate can be liable, because the firm did not approve of his conduct.","It is not liable, because it had a written anti-harassment policy.","It is liable only if a partner knew of the harassment at the time."],"a":1,"e":"An employer is vicariously liable for discrimination and harassment by employees in the course of employment, which can include work social events, whether or not it knew or approved (s.109). Its only defence is that it took all reasonable steps to prevent it; a policy with no training and ignored complaints falls far short. The associate may also be personally liable, and compensation may be increased for breach of the duty to prevent sexual harassment."},
 {"t":"equality-act","q":"Holt Solicitors has very few partners from ethnic minority backgrounds. In its next promotion round, two associates are equally qualified for one partnership. The firm wants to choose the associate from an ethnic minority background for that reason.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It is unlawful, because selection on the basis of race is always direct discrimination.","It is lawful, and the firm could also choose a less qualified ethnic minority candidate for the same reason.","It is lawful only if the firm sets a quota for ethnic minority partners.","It is unlawful unless the other associate consents.","It is likely to be lawful positive action, as the candidates are equally qualified."],"a":4,"e":"Positive action allows an employer, in recruitment or promotion, to prefer a candidate from an under-represented group where the candidates are equally qualified (the 'tie-breaker', EA 2010 s.159), if this is proportionate. Choosing a less qualified candidate, or operating quotas, would be unlawful positive discrimination."},
 {"t":"equality-act","q":"Iris, who is single, asks Jade Solicitors to act on her house purchase. The partner refuses, saying the firm 'prefers to act for married couples'. Iris wants to bring a claim under the Equality Act 2010 for discrimination because of her marital status.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her claim under the Act?","o":["It will fail, because marriage and civil partnership are protected only in work, and being single is not protected.","It will succeed as direct discrimination because of marriage.","It will succeed as indirect sex discrimination.","It will succeed, because the firm has no legitimate reason for refusing.","It will fail, because firms may choose their clients freely for any reason."],"a":0,"e":"Marriage and civil partnership is a protected characteristic only in the employment field, not in the provision of services, and being single is not protected at all. So a claim under the Act on that basis fails. But firms are not free to refuse clients for any reason: refusing because of a protected characteristic would be unlawful, and this refusal may still breach the SRA Code's prohibition on unfair discrimination."},
 {"t":"equality-act","q":"Kara, a solicitor, was diagnosed with cancer last month. She is still working normally and has no symptoms yet. Her firm removes her from a major case, saying it cannot rely on her, and she asks whether she is protected under the Equality Act 2010.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her position?","o":["She is not disabled, because the condition has not yet had a substantial effect on her day-to-day activities.","She is not disabled, because her condition has not lasted 12 months.","She is disabled under the Act from the date of diagnosis, because cancer is treated as a disability.","She is disabled only if her cancer is likely to be terminal.","She is protected only once she has asked for reasonable adjustments."],"a":2,"e":"Disability normally requires a physical or mental impairment with a substantial and long-term adverse effect on day-to-day activities (s.6). But cancer, HIV and multiple sclerosis are deemed disabilities from diagnosis, regardless of current effects. Removing Kara from the case because of her cancer is likely to be direct disability discrimination."},

 {"t":"aml-framework-cdd","q":"Lark Solicitors acts for Mr Nolan in defending a breach of contract claim brought by a former supplier. The work involves no property, company or financial transaction. A trainee asks whether the firm must carry out customer due diligence under the Money Laundering Regulations 2017.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["Yes, because the Regulations apply to all work done by solicitors.","Yes, because litigation could be used to launder money.","No, and the firm is also outside the scope of all the money laundering offences under POCA for this work.","No, because pure litigation is outside the Regulations, though the POCA principal offences still apply.","Only if the claim is worth more than the occasional transaction threshold."],"a":3,"e":"The MLR 2017 apply to independent legal professionals only when they participate in financial or real property transactions such as conveyancing, company and business sales, managing client money, or creating trusts and companies. Pure litigation is outside their scope. However, the POCA principal offences (ss.327-329) apply to everyone, so the firm must still not become involved in laundering."},
 {"t":"aml-framework-cdd","q":"Mabel, a new client, asks Oak Solicitors to buy a £2 million London house through a chain of three overseas companies, each owned by the next. She can give no commercial, tax or other reason for the structure, which is unusual for a residential purchase.\n\nWhat level of due diligence must the firm apply?","o":["Simplified due diligence, because the companies are only holding vehicles.","Enhanced due diligence, because the structure is unusually complex with no apparent purpose.","Standard due diligence on Mabel only, because she is the client.","No due diligence until the firm actually suspects money laundering.","Standard due diligence, because residential conveyancing is low risk."],"a":1,"e":"Enhanced due diligence is required for transactions that are unusually complex or unusually large, or have no apparent economic or legal purpose (MLR 2017 reg 33). A layered overseas ownership chain with no explanation is a classic high-risk indicator. The firm must also identify and take reasonable measures to verify the beneficial owners through the chain, and consider whether the facts give grounds for suspicion."},
 {"t":"aml-framework-cdd","q":"Pine Solicitors is instructed by Quarry plc, a company listed on the London Stock Exchange, to act on its purchase of a warehouse. The firm's risk assessment rates the matter as low risk. A trainee suggests that no due diligence is needed at all.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The trainee is right, because companies listed on a regulated market are exempt from the Regulations altogether.","The trainee is right, provided the firm records its risk assessment.","The firm must apply enhanced due diligence, because it is a large transaction.","The firm must identify and verify every shareholder holding more than 25%.","The firm may apply simplified due diligence, but must still establish identity and monitor."],"a":4,"e":"Where the firm assesses a relationship as low risk, for example a client listed on a regulated market, it may apply simplified due diligence (reg 37). SDD reduces the extent and timing of checks but is not 'no CDD': identity must still be established and ongoing monitoring continues. It is not available if there is any suspicion."},
 {"t":"aml-framework-cdd","q":"Vale Solicitors acted for Wes on a one-off house purchase that completed in March 2020. Wes has not instructed the firm since. The office manager wants to destroy the copies of Wes's identity documents and other customer due diligence records in April 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligation under the Money Laundering Regulations 2017?","o":["It may destroy them, because the Regulations require retention for five years after completion.","It must keep the CDD records for six years from completion, to match the limitation period for contract claims.","It must keep the CDD records for 15 years, because the matter involved land.","It must keep the CDD records permanently.","It must keep the records until Wes consents to their destruction."],"a":0,"e":"Regulation 40 requires CDD records and supporting transaction records to be kept for five years after the end of the business relationship or the completion of the occasional transaction. The purchase completed in March 2020, so the five years have passed by April 2026. The firm may have other reasons to keep the file (such as potential negligence claims), but the Regulations themselves do not require longer retention."},
 {"t":"aml-framework-cdd","q":"The trustees of a family discretionary trust instruct Xen Solicitors to buy an investment property for the trust. As part of customer due diligence, the firm must identify the beneficial owners of the trust.\n\nWho must the firm identify?","o":["Only the trustees, because they are the legal owners and the client.","Only the beneficiaries who have received payments in the last year.","The settlor, the trustees, the beneficiaries (or class) and anyone else controlling the trust.","Only the settlor, because the settlor created the trust.","Only individuals entitled to more than 25% of the trust capital, as for the beneficial owners of a company."],"a":2,"e":"For a trust, the beneficial owners include the settlor, the trustees, the beneficiaries (or the class of beneficiaries, where they are not yet determined) and any other individual who has control over the trust. The firm must identify them and take reasonable measures to verify their identity and understand the control structure. The more-than-25% test applies to companies."},
 {"t":"aml-framework-cdd","q":"Yara, a serving UK Member of Parliament, instructs Zinc Solicitors on the purchase of a family home funded by her salary and a mortgage. There are no other risk factors. A partner says that, since 2024, UK politicians are no longer treated as politically exposed persons.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The partner is right, so standard due diligence is enough.","The partner is right, but the firm must still check Yara's source of funds.","Yara is a PEP, and the firm must apply the same level of enhanced due diligence as for a foreign PEP.","Yara is not a PEP, because MPs do not hold prominent public functions.","Yara is still a PEP and EDD applies, but as a domestic PEP her starting risk is lower."],"a":4,"e":"MPs hold prominent public functions and are PEPs, whether in the UK or abroad. Since January 2024 firms must treat a domestic PEP as presenting a lower starting level of risk than a foreign PEP, absent other risk factors. Enhanced due diligence still applies (senior management approval, source of wealth and funds, enhanced monitoring), but it may be less extensive."},
 {"t":"aml-framework-cdd","q":"Ahmed stepped down as a government minister in an overseas country four months ago. He now instructs Bell Solicitors to buy a London flat.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["Ahmed must still be treated as a PEP for at least 12 months after leaving office.","Ahmed is no longer a PEP, because he has left office.","Ahmed is a PEP only if he is likely to return to office.","Ahmed must be treated as a PEP for the rest of his life, because he once held a prominent public function.","Ahmed is a PEP only if the flat is worth more than £1 million."],"a":0,"e":"A person who has ceased to hold a prominent public function must continue to be treated as a PEP for at least 12 months afterwards, or longer if the firm considers the risk requires it. Ahmed left office four months ago, so EDD applies: senior management approval, source of wealth and source of funds, and enhanced ongoing monitoring. The value of the property does not determine PEP status."},
 {"t":"aml-framework-cdd","q":"Eli asks Fern Solicitors to act on his one-off purchase of a lock-up garage for £9,000. He wants to pay the whole price in cash and gives inconsistent explanations of where the cash came from. The partner says the firm need not carry out due diligence because the transaction is below the occasional transaction threshold.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the partner's view?","o":["The partner is right, because occasional transactions below the threshold are outside CDD.","The partner is right, but the firm must refuse to accept cash.","The partner is wrong, because CDD applies whenever money laundering is suspected.","The partner is wrong, because CDD applies to all property transactions over £5,000.","The partner is right, provided the firm keeps a record of Eli's explanations."],"a":2,"e":"Under reg 27, CDD is required not only when establishing a business relationship or carrying out an occasional transaction above the threshold, but also whenever the firm suspects money laundering or terrorist financing, whatever the amount. Large cash and inconsistent explanations of its source are red flags. The firm should also consider reporting its suspicion to the MLRO."},
 {"t":"aml-framework-cdd","q":"Gwen, a well-known local businesswoman with no risk factors, asks Hale Solicitors to start urgent work on a new commercial lease for her shop. The firm has identified her but has not yet completed verification, which will take two more days. Waiting would hold up the negotiations.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["The firm must not do any work until verification is complete, in every case.","The firm may complete the transaction and verify her identity afterwards, because she is a well-known local businesswoman with no risk factors.","The firm may proceed without verification, because the risk is low.","The firm may complete verification during establishment of the relationship, as this avoids interrupting business and the risk is low.","The firm may rely on Gwen's own statement of her identity instead of verification."],"a":3,"e":"Verification must normally be completed before the business relationship is established, but reg 30 allows it to be completed during establishment where that is necessary not to interrupt the normal conduct of business and there is little risk of money laundering, provided it is finished as soon as practicable. This does not permit completing the transaction first or skipping verification altogether."},
 {"t":"aml-framework-cdd","q":"Ivy is a sole practitioner doing residential conveyancing, with two members of staff. She asks who must act as her firm's money laundering compliance officer and nominated officer (MLRO).\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["Two different people, because the roles must always be held separately.","Ivy can perform both roles herself.","An external compliance consultant, because a sole practitioner cannot report on her own firm.","The SRA, which acts as nominated officer for small firms.","No one, because sole practitioners are exempt from appointing officers."],"a":1,"e":"Firms in scope must appoint a money laundering compliance officer at senior management level and a nominated officer to receive internal reports and make SARs to the NCA (reg 21). In a small firm the same person can hold both roles. The SRA is the firm's AML supervisor, not its nominated officer."},

 {"t":"poca-offences-reporting","q":"The MLRO at Gold Solicitors submits a SAR to the NCA requesting a defence against money laundering for a client's property sale. On the fifth working day of the notice period, the NCA refuses consent. Nothing more is heard. The client is pressing to complete.\n\nWhen may the firm lawfully proceed with the sale?","o":["Once 31 days have passed from the refusal, if the moratorium has not been extended by the court.","Immediately, because the 7 working day notice period has not yet ended.","After the end of the 7 working day notice period.","Never, because a refusal of consent permanently prevents the transaction.","Once the client has given the firm an indemnity against any liability."],"a":0,"e":"If the NCA refuses consent within the 7 working day notice period, a 31-day moratorium period begins on the day of refusal, during which the firm must not proceed. If nothing more happens (and the court does not extend the moratorium) by the end of that period, consent is treated as given. A client indemnity is no defence to a POCA offence."},
 {"t":"poca-offences-reporting","q":"Ian, a solicitor, is defending Jed in a criminal trial for theft. Ian suspects that the £8,000 Jed paid on account of his fees may come from earlier thefts. The fee is normal for the work, which Ian carries out properly.\n\nHas Ian committed the s.329 offence (acquisition, use and possession) by receiving the money?","o":["Yes, because he suspected that the money was criminal property.","Yes, unless he reported his suspicion to the police before the trial.","No, because normal fees for genuine legal work are adequate consideration.","No, because criminal defence work is outside the regulated sector.","No, because s.329 applies only to the person who committed the original crime."],"a":2,"e":"Section 329 is committed by anyone who acquires, uses or possesses criminal property, but there is a defence where adequate consideration was given. Normal fees for genuine legal services provide adequate consideration, unless the solicitor knows or suspects that the services themselves help the criminal. The principal offences apply to everyone, so the fact that criminal defence is outside the regulated sector is not the reason."},
 {"t":"poca-offences-reporting","q":"Kerry, a litigator, acts for a client in a genuine boundary dispute. During settlement negotiations she comes to suspect that the other side paid for building works with money on which VAT was deliberately not declared. The parties are about to agree a consensual settlement.\n\nDoes Kerry commit the s.328 arrangements offence if she concludes the settlement?","o":["Yes, because she suspects that criminal property is involved.","Yes, unless she first obtains consent (a defence against money laundering) from the NCA before settling.","No, because she is not in the regulated sector.","No, because tax evasion is not criminal conduct for POCA purposes.","No, because the arrangements offence does not cover settling genuine legal proceedings."],"a":4,"e":"In Bowman v Fels the Court of Appeal held that s.328 was not intended to cover the ordinary conduct of legal proceedings or their consensual settlement. So Kerry does not need consent to settle genuine litigation, although sham litigation set up to launder money is still caught. Tax evasion is criminal conduct, and s.328 applies whether or not she is in the regulated sector."},
 {"t":"poca-offences-reporting","q":"Leo's client proposes to buy a company using £200,000 in cash whose source he will not explain. Before any report has been made, Leo tells the client that going ahead could amount to a money laundering offence and tries to persuade him not to do it.\n\nHas Leo committed the offence of tipping off?","o":["Yes, because he has warned the client about money laundering.","No, because he has not revealed any report or investigation, and may try to dissuade a client from offending.","Yes, unless he obtained the MLRO's permission first.","No, because tipping off can be committed only by an MLRO.","Yes, because any discussion of money laundering with a client is likely to prejudice a future investigation by the NCA."],"a":1,"e":"Tipping off (s.333A) means disclosing that a SAR has been made or that an investigation is contemplated or under way, where that is likely to prejudice an investigation. Leo has done neither. In any event, a legal adviser who discloses to a client in order to dissuade them from committing an offence has a defence (s.333D). Leo should still consider whether to report his suspicion to the MLRO."},
 {"t":"poca-offences-reporting","q":"Mona instructs a solicitor to buy a London flat using income that she earned from an activity carried on entirely in another country. The solicitor knows that the activity was lawful in that country, but that it would be an offence in the UK punishable by a maximum of six months' imprisonment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the solicitor's position under the principal money laundering offences?","o":["The money is criminal property, so proceeding would be an arrangements offence unless the solicitor first obtains consent from the NCA.","The money is criminal property only if Mona has been convicted of an offence.","The overseas conduct defence cannot apply, because the conduct would be an offence in the UK.","The solicitor has a defence, as the conduct was lawful abroad and would carry no more than 12 months in the UK.","The solicitor has a defence only if the other country follows the FATF Recommendations."],"a":3,"e":"Conduct abroad that would be an offence in the UK is criminal conduct. But there is a defence to the principal offences where the person knows or believes on reasonable grounds that the conduct occurred abroad, was lawful there, and is not of a kind that would carry more than 12 months' imprisonment in the UK. All three conditions are met here."},
 {"t":"poca-offences-reporting","q":"Mo, the MLRO at Nash LLP, a firm in the regulated sector, receives an internal report from a fee earner setting out clear grounds to suspect that a client is laundering the proceeds of fraud. Mo decides not to make a SAR because the client is the firm's biggest source of fees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mo's position?","o":["Mo commits no offence, because the fee earner has made an internal report and the firm's duty is satisfied.","Mo commits the offence of tipping off.","Mo commits no offence unless the transaction completes.","Mo commits the s.331 offence of failing to pass on the report to the NCA as soon as practicable.","Only the fee earner commits an offence, under s.330."],"a":3,"e":"A nominated officer in the regulated sector who receives an internal report and knows or suspects, or has reasonable grounds to know or suspect, money laundering must pass it to the NCA as soon as practicable; failing to do so is an offence under s.331. Commercial reasons are no excuse. The fee earner satisfied his own s.330 duty by reporting to Mo."},
 {"t":"poca-offences-reporting","q":"Pat tells his solicitor that £3,000 of the £20,000 deposit for his flat comes from housing benefit he claimed while working, without telling the council. The rest comes from his salary.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the deposit?","o":["None of it is criminal property, because £3,000 is below any minimum value.","It includes criminal property, because the £3,000 is a benefit from benefit fraud.","It is criminal property only if Pat has been convicted of benefit fraud.","None of it is criminal property, because benefit fraud is a civil matter dealt with by recovering the overpayment.","None of it is criminal property, because Pat told his solicitor openly about the benefits."],"a":1,"e":"Criminal property is property that constitutes or represents, in whole or part, a person's benefit from criminal conduct, where the alleged offender knows or suspects this (POCA s.340). Benefit fraud is criminal conduct, there is no minimum value and no conviction is needed. The solicitor should report to the MLRO and not proceed without consent."},
 {"t":"poca-offences-reporting","q":"Quinn, an employment solicitor, is advising a client on an unfair dismissal claim, which is not regulated-sector work. Over coffee, and not in connection with any legal advice, the client mentions that a friend launders money through a car wash. Quinn takes no part in any transaction and does not report it.\n\nHas Quinn committed the s.330 offence of failing to disclose?","o":["No, because the information did not come to him in the course of business in the regulated sector.","Yes, unless he reports to his firm's MLRO.","He has committed the s.328 offence by continuing to act for the client.","Yes, because any suspicion of money laundering must always be reported.","No, but only because the information is privileged."],"a":0,"e":"The s.330 offence applies only where the information came to the person in the course of business in the regulated sector. Employment advice and litigation are outside it, so Quinn commits no s.330 offence. He is not concerned in any arrangement, so s.328 is not committed either, and the information was not given in privileged circumstances, so privilege is not the reason."},
 {"t":"poca-offences-reporting","q":"Rae, a solicitor at a firm doing only family work, learns from a client that the police have begun a money laundering investigation into him. The client asks Rae to shred her file notes recording his instructions about moving money overseas, and Rae does so.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rae's position?","o":["She commits no offence, because she is not in the regulated sector.","She commits the offence of tipping off.","She commits no offence, because the file notes are privileged.","She commits the s.330 offence of failing to disclose.","She commits the offence of prejudicing an investigation, by destroying documents relevant to it."],"a":4,"e":"Under s.342 anyone, not only those in the regulated sector, commits an offence if, knowing or suspecting that a money laundering investigation is under way, they falsify, conceal or destroy documents relevant to it. Tipping off is a regulated-sector offence about disclosing a SAR or investigation. Privilege does not permit destroying evidence, and would not protect communications made to further a crime."},
 {"t":"poca-offences-reporting","q":"Sol, a new conveyancing assistant, did not actually suspect anything when a client bought three flats in quick succession using cash from unexplained sources, although a reasonable conveyancer would have been suspicious. His firm had given him no money laundering training. He made no report.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sol's position under s.330?","o":["He commits the offence, because the test is objective.","He commits the offence, unless the client was eventually convicted.","He has a defence, as he did not actually suspect and had not been given the required training.","He has a defence only if he reported to the NCA within 7 working days.","He has no liability under s.330, but commits the arrangements offence instead because he continued to act."],"a":2,"e":"Section 330 can be committed on an objective basis (reasonable grounds to suspect), but an employee who did not actually know or suspect has a defence if the employer had not provided the required AML training. Sol meets both conditions. The s.328 offence needs actual knowledge or suspicion, which he did not have; the firm itself may be in breach of the MLR 2017 training requirements."},

 {"t":"financial-services","q":"Tess instructs Uplands Solicitors, which is not authorised by the FCA, to advise on and handle the sale of a block of flats she owns as an investment, including advising whether the price offered is a good one. No mortgage, shares or other investments are involved.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the regulatory position?","o":["It is a regulated activity, because the flats are held as an investment.","It is a regulated activity, but the firm can rely on the Part 20 exemption.","It is a regulated activity, unless the firm introduces Tess to an independent financial adviser.","It is regulated only if the firm is paid a commission by the buyer.","It is not a regulated activity, because land is not a specified investment."],"a":4,"e":"An activity is regulated under FSMA only if it involves a specified investment. Land and buildings themselves are not specified investments, even if held as an investment, so advising on and selling the flats is not regulated. Arranging or advising on a regulated mortgage would be different."},
 {"t":"financial-services","q":"Vince has inherited £300,000 and asks his solicitor which investment funds he should put it into. The solicitor, at a firm not authorised by the FCA, gives no advice on any particular investment, but arranges a meeting for him with an FCA-authorised independent financial adviser.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the solicitor's position?","o":["The solicitor has carried on a regulated activity and needs the Part 20 exemption.","The solicitor has breached the general prohibition, because the matter concerns investments and the firm is not authorised by the FCA.","The solicitor has not carried on a regulated activity, because introductions to an authorised adviser are excluded.","The solicitor has carried on a regulated activity, but only if the adviser pays a referral fee.","The solicitor must be authorised by the FCA to make the introduction."],"a":2,"e":"Introducing a client to an authorised person, such as an independent financial adviser, for independent advice or discretionary management is excluded from regulation (RAO art 33). Because an exclusion applies, no authorisation or exemption is needed. This is the usual correct course when a client asks a non-authorised solicitor for investment recommendations."},
 {"t":"financial-services","q":"During a probate matter, a beneficiary asks Wren, a solicitor at a firm not authorised by the FCA, whether in general terms government bonds are less risky than shares. Wren explains the general differences in risk but does not recommend any particular investment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Wren's position?","o":["She has not carried on a regulated activity, because generic advice is not advising on investments.","She has carried on a regulated activity, because she advised on specified investments.","She has carried on a regulated activity, but it is excluded because it was a necessary part of the probate work.","She needs the Part 20 exemption, because the advice was incidental to probate.","She has breached the general prohibition, because she is not authorised by the FCA."],"a":0,"e":"'Advising on investments' means advice on the merits of buying, selling, subscribing for or underwriting a particular investment. Generic advice, such as explaining that shares are riskier than gilts, is not a regulated activity at all. So no exclusion or exemption is needed."},
 {"t":"financial-services","q":"Yew Solicitors, an SRA firm not authorised by the FCA, arranges after-the-event insurance for a litigation client under the Part 20 exemption. The insurer pays the firm a commission of £400. The firm proposes to keep it without telling the client.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The firm may keep the commission, because it is small.","The firm may keep the commission, because arranging insurance is not a regulated activity.","The firm may keep the commission if it is disclosed in the general terms of business published on its website.","The firm cannot rely on the exemption unless it accounts to the client for the commission.","The firm must pay the commission to the opponent."],"a":3,"e":"One condition of the Part 20 exemption (FSMA s.327) is that the firm must not receive any pecuniary reward from anyone other than the client for the activity unless it accounts to the client for it. Keeping the commission undisclosed would take the firm outside the exemption and into a breach of the general prohibition. Arranging ATE insurance is insurance distribution, a regulated activity."},
 {"t":"financial-services","q":"In divorce proceedings, Zane's solicitor, at an SRA firm that is not authorised by the FCA, advises Zane to surrender his endowment life policy and use the proceeds to buy units in a named unit trust.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the solicitor's position?","o":["This is permitted under Part 20, because the advice is incidental to the divorce and the firm is regulated by the SRA.","This is not permitted under Part 20, because exempt firms may not recommend buying a retail investment product.","This is excluded from regulation, because it is a necessary part of the legal advice.","This is permitted, provided the firm receives no commission.","This is permitted, because life policies are not specified investments."],"a":1,"e":"Advising on the merits of selling a particular life policy and buying particular units is advising on investments, and no exclusion applies. The SRA Financial Services (Scope) Rules prohibit exempt firms from recommending a client to buy a retail investment product, so Part 20 cannot be used. The solicitor should introduce Zane to an authorised independent financial adviser instead."},
 {"t":"financial-services","q":"Abby, a partner at an SRA firm not authorised by the FCA, is one of the executors of a client's estate. She and her co-executor decide to sell the estate's shares in a listed company through a stockbroker. Abby receives no separate payment for the sale beyond the firm's normal charges for administering the estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Abby is carrying on a regulated activity that requires FCA authorisation.","The activity is excluded, because Abby acts as a personal representative and is not separately paid.","The activity is excluded only if the beneficiaries consent in writing.","The activity can be carried on only under the Part 20 exemption, because no exclusion applies to the sale of listed shares.","The activity is excluded, because shares in an estate are not specified investments."],"a":1,"e":"Dealing, arranging, managing or safeguarding by a trustee or personal representative acting in that capacity is excluded (RAO art 66), provided they are not separately remunerated for the activity. Shares are specified investments, so the exclusion is what takes this outside regulation. Selling through an authorised stockbroker without advice would also fall within arts 22/29."},
 {"t":"financial-services","q":"Bryn Solicitors agrees that a client may pay a £4,800 bill in 12 equal monthly instalments, with interest charged at 5% a year.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the arrangement?","o":["It is exempt, because there are no more than 12 instalments.","It is exempt, because the credit relates to legal fees.","It is exempt, provided the instalments are paid within 12 months.","It is not exempt, because interest is charged, so the firm may be providing regulated credit.","It is not exempt, because any instalment arrangement for fees is regulated."],"a":3,"e":"Allowing a client to pay fees by instalments can be providing credit, a regulated activity. It is exempt only if repayable in 12 or fewer instalments within 12 months with no interest or other charges. Charging interest takes this arrangement outside the exemption."},
 {"t":"financial-services","q":"Cole Solicitors, which is not authorised by the FCA, plans to send a mass email to all its clients recommending that they buy shares in a new technology company in which one of its partners has invested.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the email?","o":["It is lawful, because the firm is regulated by the SRA.","It is lawful, because it is a one-off communication.","It is lawful, provided the email says the firm is not authorised by the FCA.","It is lawful, because communications by firms regulated by the SRA and exempt under Part 20 are always exempt.","It is unlawful unless exempt or approved by an authorised person, as it is a financial promotion."],"a":4,"e":"FSMA s.21 prohibits communicating, in the course of business, an invitation or inducement to engage in investment activity unless the communicator is authorised, the content is approved by a suitably authorised person, or an exemption applies. A mass email recommending shares is not a one-off tailored communication or incidental to legal services. Breach is a criminal offence."},
 {"t":"financial-services","q":"Della asks her solicitor to sell her shares in a listed company. She has already decided to sell and asks for no advice. The solicitor, at a firm not authorised by the FCA, instructs an FCA-authorised stockbroker to carry out the sale and receives no commission.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the regulatory position?","o":["The activity is excluded, because it is done through an authorised person and no advice was sought or given.","The activity requires FCA authorisation, because selling listed shares as agent for a client is dealing in investments.","The activity is excluded only because it is a necessary part of other legal services.","The activity is regulated, and the firm must rely on Part 20.","The activity is excluded, because listed shares are not specified investments."],"a":0,"e":"Shares are specified investments, and acting for Della in the sale is dealing as agent or arranging. But where the transaction is entered into through an authorised person and the client has not sought or received advice from the firm, the activity is excluded (RAO arts 22 and 29). Because an exclusion applies, the firm does not need to rely on the Part 20 exemption."},
 {"t":"financial-services","q":"Eden Solicitors carries on some regulated activities, such as arranging ATE insurance, under the Part 20 exemption. A new client asks what the firm must tell clients about its regulatory status for this work.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["Nothing, because firms regulated by the SRA need not disclose their FCA status when acting under the Part 20 exemption.","That the firm is authorised by the FCA.","In writing, that it is not FCA-authorised, that it is regulated by the SRA, and how to complain.","That the firm is regulated by the Financial Ombudsman Service.","Only that the firm holds professional indemnity insurance."],"a":2,"e":"The SRA Financial Services (Conduct of Business) Rules require exempt firms to give clients a written status disclosure: that the firm is not authorised by the FCA, that it is regulated by the SRA, and how to complain, including to the Legal Ombudsman. Claiming FCA authorisation would be misleading. The Financial Ombudsman Service is not a regulator."},

 {"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","q":"Fay Solicitors quoted Gil a fixed fee of £1,500 plus VAT for drafting a commercial lease, defining the scope as including one round of negotiations. The negotiations stayed within that scope but took far longer than expected. The firm now wants to bill £2,400 plus VAT.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The firm can bill £2,400, because the time actually spent was greater.","The firm can bill only £1,500 plus VAT, because a fixed fee for the work described is binding.","The firm can bill £2,400 if it tells Gil promptly that costs have risen.","The firm can bill £2,400, because a fixed fee is only an estimate.","The firm can bill the extra, because fixed fees do not cover lengthy negotiations."],"a":1,"e":"A fixed fee or quotation is binding for the work described: the firm bears the risk if the work takes longer than expected. Only work outside the defined scope can be charged separately, if agreed. An estimate, by contrast, is not binding but must be realistic and updated."},
 {"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","q":"Ivy wants to bring financial remedy proceedings against her former husband on their divorce. She cannot afford to pay privately and asks her solicitor whether she can enter into a conditional fee agreement.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["Yes, provided the agreement is in writing.","Yes, provided the success fee does not exceed 100% of base costs.","Yes, provided the success fee is capped at 25% of what she recovers.","No, because a conditional fee agreement cannot be used in family proceedings.","No, because conditional fee agreements are available only in personal injury claims."],"a":3,"e":"Under the Courts and Legal Services Act 1990 s.58, a CFA must be in writing, must state the success fee percentage, and must not relate to criminal or family proceedings. Financial remedy proceedings on divorce are family proceedings, so a CFA is not available. CFAs are not limited to personal injury."},
 {"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","q":"Jake brings a breach of contract claim against a former supplier under a CFA with a 50% success fee. He wins. His solicitor's base costs are £40,000, and the court orders the supplier to pay Jake's costs, which are assessed at the full £40,000.\n\nHow much must Jake pay his solicitor himself?","o":["£20,000, because the success fee is not recoverable from the supplier.","Nothing, because the supplier must pay all of his costs.","£40,000, because a client must pay both base costs and the success fee himself.","£60,000, because recovered costs do not count towards what he owes his solicitor.","£10,000, because the supplier and Jake share the success fee equally."],"a":0,"e":"Under a CFA the winning client owes base costs plus the success fee, here £40,000 plus £20,000. Since April 2013 (LASPO s.44) the success fee is not recoverable from the losing opponent, though base costs are. The supplier pays the £40,000 base costs, leaving Jake to pay the £20,000 success fee, usually from his damages."},
 {"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","q":"Kai enters into a damages-based agreement for a commercial contract claim, under which his solicitor will receive 55% of any damages, including VAT and counsel's fees. He wins £200,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the solicitor's entitlement?","o":["£110,000, because the parties agreed 55%.","£100,000, because the court will reduce the payment to the 50% cap that applies to this kind of claim.","£70,000, because the cap for this kind of claim is 35%.","£50,000, because the cap for this kind of claim is 25%.","Nothing under the agreement, because it breaches the 50% cap and is unenforceable."],"a":4,"e":"For civil claims other than personal injury and employment matters, a DBA payment (including VAT and counsel's fees) cannot exceed 50% of the sums recovered. A DBA that does not comply with the Damages-Based Agreements Regulations 2013 is unenforceable; the court does not rewrite it down to the cap. The 35% and 25% caps apply to employment and personal injury claims."},
 {"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","q":"Lina's personal injury claim succeeds under a damages-based agreement. She recovers £60,000 for pain, suffering and loss of amenity, £20,000 for past loss of earnings (after deduction of recoverable benefits) and £100,000 for future care.\n\nWhat is the maximum payment the solicitor can receive under the DBA?","o":["£45,000, being 25% of all her damages.","£25,000, being 25% of her damages for future care.","£20,000, being 25% of her damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity and past loss.","£40,000, being 50% of her damages excluding future care.","£15,000, being 25% of her damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity only."],"a":2,"e":"In personal injury claims, the DBA payment is capped at 25% of damages for pain, suffering and loss of amenity and past financial loss, net of recoverable benefits. Future loss is excluded to protect money needed for future care. 25% of £80,000 is £20,000."},
 {"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","q":"Mia's solicitor gave her a written estimate of £8,000 for her probate dispute. Halfway through the matter, it becomes clear because of new evidence that costs will be about £14,000.\n\nWhat should the solicitor do?","o":["Nothing, because it was only an estimate.","Tell Mia when the final bill is sent.","Tell Mia promptly, give her a revised estimate and seek her instructions.","Stop work once costs reach £8,000.","Charge no more than £8,000, because a written estimate is binding on the firm once given."],"a":2,"e":"SRA Code para 8.7 requires the best possible information about pricing and likely overall cost, both at engagement and as the matter progresses. An estimate is not binding, but it must be updated promptly when it will be exceeded so the client can decide how to proceed. Waiting until the bill, or simply stopping work, does not meet this duty."},
 {"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","q":"Ned instructs Opal Solicitors under a private retainer at £250 an hour to bring a claim. He wins, and the court orders the defendant to pay his costs. On assessment the defendant is ordered to pay £18,000, but Opal's bill to Ned under the retainer is £25,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Opal can recover only £18,000, the amount assessed against the defendant.","Opal can recover nothing from Ned, because he won the claim.","Opal must recover the full £25,000 from the defendant.","Ned can refuse to pay the £7,000 difference, because costs follow the event and the defendant lost the claim.","Ned remains liable for the £7,000 difference, because he is primarily liable for his own solicitor's costs."],"a":4,"e":"The client is primarily liable to pay his own solicitor under the retainer, whatever is recovered from the other side. A winner rarely recovers all its costs on assessment, so the shortfall falls on the client. Ned should have been warned about this costs risk at the outset; he can still challenge the bill itself if he thinks it unreasonable."},
 {"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","q":"Pia pays Quill Solicitors £3,000 on account of future costs before any work is done. The firm wants to pay it straight into its office account to help with cash flow.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The firm may do so, because the money will be used for Pia's costs.","The firm must hold it in client account until a bill is delivered, because it is client money.","The firm may do so, provided it records the money as belonging to Pia in a ledger kept for her matter.","The firm must return it, because firms may not ask for money on account.","The firm may do so once it has started work."],"a":1,"e":"Money paid on account of costs before a bill is delivered is client money. Under the SRA Accounts Rules it must be held in client account until the firm has delivered a bill (or written notification of costs) or spends it on a disbursement. Firms are entitled to ask for money on account, but cannot use it as their own in the meantime."},
 {"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","q":"Rory's solicitor proposes an agreement for a commercial claim under which Rory pays a reduced hourly rate whatever the outcome, plus 30% of any damages if he wins.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the proposed agreement?","o":["It is permitted, because it is a discounted conditional fee agreement.","It is permitted, because a damages-based agreement may include any payment the parties agree.","It is permitted, provided the total paid to the solicitor, including the hourly fees, does not exceed 50% of the damages.","It is generally regarded as not permitted, because a DBA lawyer's only payment on a win is the percentage.","It is permitted only in employment tribunal claims."],"a":3,"e":"A payment calculated as a percentage of damages makes this a DBA, not a CFA (a CFA success fee is an uplift on costs). Such 'hybrid' DBAs, combining fees payable regardless of outcome with a percentage on success, are generally regarded as not permitted under the current Regulations. A discounted CFA (lower rate if lost, full rate plus success fee if won) would be permitted."},
 {"t":"funding-private-cfa-dba","q":"Sian receives a bill of £12,000 from her solicitors for work on a dispute and thinks it is far too high. She wants the court to check it. The bill was delivered two weeks ago.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her options?","o":["She can apply for assessment under the Solicitors Act 1974, as of right if within one month of delivery.","She can only complain to the Legal Ombudsman, because the court has no power to review a solicitor's own bill.","She must pay the bill first and then sue the firm in negligence.","She can apply to the SRA to reduce the bill.","She must wait eight weeks before taking any action about the bill."],"a":0,"e":"A client can ask the court to assess a solicitor's bill under the Solicitors Act 1974, and is entitled to an assessment as of right if the application is made within one month of delivery of the bill. She can also complain to the firm and then the Legal Ombudsman about excessive fees. The SRA does not reduce individual bills."},

 {"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","q":"Tia receives Universal Credit and has no savings. She wants legal aid to apply for a child arrangements order so that she can spend time with her son, who lives with his father. There is no evidence of domestic abuse or child abuse.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her eligibility for civil legal aid?","o":["She is eligible, because she is passported through the means test.","She is eligible, because cases involving children are always in scope.","She is eligible, provided her case has at least moderate prospects of success, as she passes the means test.","She is not eligible, because private child arrangements cases without abuse are generally out of scope.","She is not eligible, because people on Universal Credit must pay a contribution."],"a":3,"e":"Civil legal aid is available only for cases in scope under LASPO Schedule 1. Private family disputes such as child arrangements are generally out of scope unless there is evidence of domestic abuse or child abuse. Because scope fails, means and merits do not matter, though exceptional case funding could be considered if her human rights were at stake."},
 {"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","q":"Uma had legal aid to defend her landlord's possession claim and to bring a counterclaim for disrepair. She wins £15,000 damages for disrepair, but the landlord pays only part of her costs. The Legal Aid Agency has paid £9,000 in costs that were not recovered from the landlord.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect on Uma's damages?","o":["The statutory charge attaches to the £15,000 for the unrecovered costs, so she may keep about £6,000.","She keeps the full £15,000, because legal aid is free to those who qualify.","She must repay the £9,000 from her other income in instalments.","She keeps the full £15,000, because the statutory charge applies only to land recovered or preserved, not money.","Her solicitor must waive the unrecovered costs."],"a":0,"e":"Under LASPO s.25, the LAA has a first charge on money or property recovered or preserved in the proceedings, to recoup legal aid costs not recovered from the opponent. Uma's £15,000 damages are subject to the charge for the £9,000. She should have been warned about the statutory charge at the outset."},
 {"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","q":"Vera, who is legally aided, brings a claim for judicial review against a local authority and loses. The authority asks the court to order her to pay its costs of £30,000. Vera has very limited means.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her liability for the authority's costs?","o":["She must pay all £30,000, because the loser pays the winner's costs.","The Legal Aid Agency must pay the authority's costs in full.","She can be ordered to pay only what is reasonable, having regard to the parties' resources and conduct.","She cannot be ordered to pay anything, because legally aided parties are wholly immune from adverse costs orders.","She must pay half the costs, as a fixed proportion for legally aided parties."],"a":2,"e":"Costs protection under LASPO s.26 limits a legally aided party's liability for the opponent's costs to an amount that is reasonable having regard to all the circumstances, including the financial resources of all the parties and their conduct. In practice this is often nil, but she is not automatically immune. There is no fixed proportion."},
 {"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","q":"Wyn needs legal advice and representation in a complex case that is outside the scope of civil legal aid. Because of his circumstances he cannot present the case effectively himself, and refusing funding would risk a breach of his right to a fair trial.\n\nWhich of the following best describes his position?","o":["No legal aid is possible, because the case is out of scope.","Legal aid is available only if the case raises an issue of significant wider public interest beyond his own.","He must first try to obtain a conditional fee agreement.","Legal aid is available only once the case has started in court.","He may apply for exceptional case funding, because refusal would risk breaching his Convention rights."],"a":4,"e":"Exceptional case funding under LASPO s.10 allows legal aid for out-of-scope cases where refusing it would breach, or risk breaching, the applicant's rights under the European Convention on Human Rights, such as the Article 6 right to a fair trial. He must still satisfy the means and merits tests."},
 {"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","q":"Xia has a housing case that is within the scope of civil legal aid, and she passes the means test. Her solicitor assesses her prospects of success as poor, at about 40%. The case has no wider public interest and is not of overwhelming importance to her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her application for full representation?","o":["She will get full representation, because housing cases are in scope.","She is unlikely to get full representation, because her case fails the merits test.","She will get full representation once she pays a contribution.","She will get full representation, because she passes the means test.","She will get full representation, but must pay her opponent's costs if she loses."],"a":1,"e":"Legal aid requires the case to be in scope, the client to pass the means test and the case to pass the merits test. For full representation, prospects must generally be at least moderate (50% or more), with marginal prospects acceptable only in special categories; poor prospects (below 45%) fail. Paying a contribution relates to means, not merits."},
 {"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","q":"Yusuf brings a personal injury claim under a CFA, with after-the-event insurance to cover his disbursements. He wins. His solicitor asks whether the ATE premium can be recovered from the defendant.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["Yes, because a winning claimant recovers all reasonable funding costs, including the ATE premium, from the loser.","Yes, because personal injury claims are an exception to the general rule.","Yes, but only half of it.","No, unless the defendant agreed in advance to pay it.","No, because ATE premiums are generally not recoverable from the loser, so it comes from his damages."],"a":4,"e":"Since 1 April 2013 (LASPO), ATE premiums, like CFA success fees, are generally not recoverable from the losing party. The client bears the premium, usually out of the damages. The only significant exception is the part of a premium in clinical negligence cases covering expert reports on liability and causation."},
 {"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","q":"Zest Ltd is bringing a large competition damages claim. A litigation funder offers to fund it in return for three times the amount it invests, subject to an overall cap linked to the damages recovered. The agreement does not comply with the Damages-Based Agreements Regulations 2013.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the enforceability of the funding agreement?","o":["It is unenforceable, because any funding agreement whose return is capped by reference to the damages is a DBA.","It is likely to be enforceable, because a return set as a multiple of the investment is not a DBA.","It is unenforceable, because third-party funding of litigation is unlawful.","It is enforceable only if approved by the Association of Litigation Funders.","It is enforceable only if the funder is regulated by the FCA."],"a":1,"e":"PACCAR held that a funding agreement under which the funder's return is calculated as a percentage of damages is a DBA, unenforceable unless it complies with the Regulations. In Sony v Neill (2025) the Court of Appeal held that agreements calculating the return as a multiple of the funder's investment are not DBAs, even where the return is capped by reference to damages. Funders are self-regulated, not FCA-authorised."},
 {"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","q":"Arlo's commercial claim is being financed by a litigation funder. The defendant makes a settlement offer that Arlo wants to accept, but the funder instructs Arlo's solicitor to reject it because it hopes for a larger return.\n\nWhat should the solicitor do?","o":["Follow the funder's instructions, because it is paying the costs.","Accept the offer only if the funder agrees, because the funder bears the costs risk of the claim.","Withdraw from acting immediately because of the conflict.","Act on Arlo's instructions, because the solicitor's duties are owed to Arlo.","Ask the court to decide whether the offer should be accepted."],"a":3,"e":"The funded party remains the solicitor's client and must give instructions; the solicitor's duties are owed to the client, not the funder, and the solicitor must act in the client's best interests. Funders should not control the litigation under the voluntary Association of Litigation Funders Code. Any consequences under the funding agreement are a matter between Arlo and the funder, on which he may need advice."},
 {"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","q":"Bea, a member of a trade union, has home insurance that includes legal expenses cover. She wants to bring a claim against her employer for unpaid wages, and asks her solicitor how to fund it.\n\nWhat is the best first advice?","o":["Check whether her union or her legal expenses insurance will fund the claim.","Apply for civil legal aid, because employment claims are in scope.","Enter into a damages-based agreement immediately, because it is the cheapest option.","Seek third-party litigation funding.","Fund the claim privately, because insurance never covers employment claims."],"a":0,"e":"A solicitor should always check first whether the client already has funding, such as before-the-event insurance or trade union support; failing to do so could breach the duty to give the best possible costs information. Most employment claims (other than discrimination) are outside legal aid scope, and third-party funding is for high-value claims."},
 {"t":"funding-legal-aid-tpf-lei","q":"Cal receives Universal Credit and has £60,000 in savings. He has a housing case that is within the scope of civil legal aid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the means test applies to him?","o":["He is automatically eligible on both income and capital, because he receives Universal Credit.","He is ineligible, because people receiving benefits cannot have savings.","He automatically satisfies the income test, but his capital must still be assessed.","He does not need to satisfy any means test, because housing cases are not means tested.","He is eligible, but only for initial advice and not for representation."],"a":2,"e":"Applicants receiving certain means-tested benefits, including Universal Credit, are 'passported' through the income test. Their capital is still assessed, so Cal's £60,000 savings may make him ineligible or require a contribution, depending on the limits. Housing cases are means tested; the non-means-tested exceptions include care proceedings for parents and children."},

 {"t":"transparency-client-care","q":"Dawn Solicitors acts only for individuals on personal injury and clinical negligence claims. Its website sets out its complaints procedure and SRA regulatory details but gives no price information.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position under the SRA Transparency Rules?","o":["It breaches the rules, because it must publish prices for all services offered to individuals.","It breaches the rules, because personal injury is a listed service.","It breaches the rules unless it publishes its average costs.","It complies only if it publishes its usual CFA success fee.","It complies, because personal injury and clinical negligence are not listed services."],"a":4,"e":"The duty to publish price information applies only to listed services, such as residential conveyancing, uncontested probate, certain motoring offences, unfair or wrongful dismissal claims, immigration (excluding asylum), debt recovery up to £100,000 and licensing. Personal injury and clinical negligence are not listed. The firm must still publish its complaints procedure and regulatory information, which it does."},
 {"t":"transparency-client-care","q":"Elm LLP acts for businesses defending unfair dismissal claims in the employment tribunal. Its website gives its hourly rates but no other price information.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position under the SRA Transparency Rules?","o":["It complies, because it acts only for businesses.","It breaches the rules, because defending dismissal claims for businesses is a listed service.","It complies, because employment tribunal work is contentious.","It breaches the rules only if it also acts for employees.","It complies, because publishing its hourly rates is sufficient price information for employment work."],"a":1,"e":"For businesses, defending employment tribunal claims for unfair or wrongful dismissal is a listed service. The firm must publish the total or average cost (or a range), the basis of charges, likely disbursements and VAT, the experience and qualifications of those doing the work, what is included, and the key stages and timescales. An hourly rate alone is not enough."},
 {"t":"transparency-client-care","q":"Fir Solicitors advertises its defence of speeding offences (summary-only, dealt with at a single hearing in the magistrates' court) at '£600 plus VAT'. The website lists what is included, states that there are no disbursements and sets out the key stages and timescales, but says nothing about who will do the work.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It complies, because criminal and motoring work is exempt from the price publication requirements of the rules.","It complies, because a fixed fee has been published.","It breaches the rules only if VAT is not mentioned.","It breaches the rules, because it must state the experience and qualifications of those doing the work.","It complies, because motoring offences are not a listed service."],"a":3,"e":"Summary-only motoring offences dealt with at a single hearing are a listed service, so full price information must be published. That includes the experience and qualifications of anyone carrying out the work and of their supervisors. Publishing a fixed fee, inclusions, disbursements and timescales is not enough without it."},
 {"t":"transparency-client-care","q":"Gill complained to Holt Solicitors about delay on her matter. Eight weeks have now passed and the complaint has not been resolved. The firm intends to carry on investigating.\n\nWhat must the firm do now?","o":["Tell Gill in writing of her right to complain to the Legal Ombudsman, the time limit and contact details.","Nothing, until it has completed its investigation.","Refer the complaint to the SRA.","Tell Gill she must wait for the firm's final response before she is entitled to go to the Legal Ombudsman.","Offer Gill compensation to close the complaint."],"a":0,"e":"Under SRA Code para 8.4, if a complaint is not resolved to the client's satisfaction within 8 weeks, the firm must tell the client in writing of any right to complain to the Legal Ombudsman, the time frame and the Ombudsman's full contact details. After 8 weeks Gill can go to the Ombudsman without waiting for a final response. Service complaints do not go to the SRA."},
 {"t":"transparency-client-care","q":"Ian, a solicitor, visits a hospital accident and emergency department and hands his business card to patients injured in road accidents, offering to act for them on their claims. None of them is a current or former client.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ian's conduct?","o":["It is permitted, provided he does not charge for the first meeting.","It is permitted, because accident victims need advice.","It breaches the Code, because unsolicited approaches to the public are prohibited, except to current or former clients.","It is permitted, provided the hospital consents.","It breaches the Code only if he mentions a no win, no fee arrangement, which would make the approach misleading publicity."],"a":2,"e":"SRA Code para 8.9 prohibits unsolicited approaches to members of the public, such as approaching people in the street or at hospitals, to advertise legal services, except to current or former clients. Approaching injured people in A&E is a clear example. A free first meeting or the hospital's consent does not change this."},
 {"t":"transparency-client-care","q":"Jo, a consumer, instructs Kite Solicitors by email on her lease extension, without ever meeting them. The firm gives her written information about her 14-day right to cancel and, at her express written request, starts work immediately. Five days later she cancels.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["She can cancel, but must pay for the work done up to cancellation.","She cannot cancel, because work has already started.","She can cancel and pays nothing, because the 14 days had not expired.","She cannot cancel, because legal services are excluded from the Regulations.","She can cancel only if the firm agrees."],"a":0,"e":"A contract made at a distance with a consumer can be cancelled within 14 days under the Consumer Contracts Regulations 2013. Where the consumer expressly requests that work start within the cancellation period and then cancels, she must pay for the work done up to cancellation. She would pay nothing only if the firm had failed to give the required information or obtain her express request."},
 {"t":"transparency-client-care","q":"Lark Solicitors, an SRA-authorised firm, does not display the SRA clickable logo on its website, although its letterhead and website both state that it is authorised and regulated by the SRA and give its SRA number.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It complies, because the regulatory statement and SRA number on its letterhead and website are sufficient by themselves.","It complies, because the logo is optional for small firms.","It breaches the rules only if it is an ABS.","It breaches the Transparency Rules, because every authorised firm must display the SRA clickable logo.","It complies, because its SRA number is on the website."],"a":3,"e":"Every SRA-authorised firm, including ABSs and small firms, must display the SRA clickable logo (digital badge) prominently on its website, in addition to stating that it is authorised and regulated by the SRA. The badge links to the SRA register and explains the protections clients have. A written statement and SRA number do not replace it."},
 {"t":"transparency-client-care","q":"Mina's solicitor made a mistake when preparing her will 18 months ago. She could not reasonably have known about it until two months ago, when she discovered it and complained to the firm. The firm sent its final response last week, explaining her right to complain to the Legal Ombudsman.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether she can complain to the Ombudsman?","o":["She is out of time, because more than one year has passed since the mistake.","She is out of time, because she must complain to the Ombudsman within 8 weeks of the mistake.","She is in time, provided she complains within six months of the final response.","She is in time only if the firm agrees to the Ombudsman investigating.","She is in time, because there is no time limit for complaints about wills."],"a":2,"e":"A complaint to the Legal Ombudsman must be made within six months of the firm's final response and within one year of the act or omission, or of when the complainant should reasonably have known there was cause for complaint. Mina could not have known until two months ago, so the one-year limit is satisfied, and she has six months from the final response. The firm's agreement is not needed."},
 {"t":"transparency-client-care","q":"Nest Solicitors advertises 'Residential conveyancing from £499 – no hidden extras'. In practice, every client is also charged a £150 'administration fee' and a £60 'ID check fee' by the firm.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It complies, because the word 'from' shows the price may vary.","It complies, because administration fees are disbursements.","It breaches the rules only if clients complain.","It complies, provided the fees are shown in the client care letter.","It breaches the Code, because publicity must be accurate and not misleading about charges."],"a":4,"e":"SRA Code para 8.8 requires publicity to be accurate and not misleading, including about charges. Advertising 'no hidden extras' while charging every client additional firm fees is misleading, and the Transparency Rules also require conveyancing price information to be accurate and complete. The firm's own administration and ID fees are not disbursements (payments to third parties), and later disclosure does not cure misleading publicity."},
 {"t":"transparency-client-care","q":"Oasis Solicitors does only asylum claims and First-tier Tribunal asylum appeals, all funded by legal aid. Its website contains no price information.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligations under the SRA Transparency Rules?","o":["It must publish price information, because immigration is a listed service.","It need not publish prices for this work, but must publish complaints and regulatory information.","It need publish nothing at all, because firms doing only legal aid work are exempt from the Transparency Rules.","It must publish price information for asylum appeals only.","It must publish the legal aid rates it is paid."],"a":1,"e":"The immigration listed service expressly excludes asylum, and the price rules do not apply to publicly funded work in any event. But the duty to publish complaints information (including how to complain to the Legal Ombudsman and the SRA) and regulatory information applies to every SRA-authorised firm. So Oasis must publish those, though not prices."}
];

;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["contract"] = [
 {"t":"offer-acceptance","q":"Calder Auctions holds an auction of used catering equipment advertised as 'without reserve'. When a commercial oven worth about £6,000 comes up, Nadia is the highest bidder at £900. The auctioneer refuses to knock the oven down to her because the price is too low and withdraws it from the sale.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nadia's legal position?","o":["She has no claim, because a bid is only an offer and the auctioneer was free to reject it.","She can claim damages from the auctioneer, because by holding the auction without reserve he promised to sell to the highest genuine bidder.","She can compel the owner to sell her the oven, because in an auction without reserve the sale is complete as soon as the highest bid is made, without the fall of the hammer.","She has no claim, because the auction catalogue was only an invitation to treat.","She can claim damages from the owner, because the advertisement was an offer to sell the oven to the public."],"a":1,"e":"At an auction without reserve the auctioneer makes a collateral unilateral promise to sell to the highest genuine bidder, which the bidder accepts by bidding (Barry v Davies). Nadia can recover damages from the auctioneer, measured by the difference between her bid and the oven's value. It is true that a bid is normally only an offer, but that does not defeat the collateral contract; and there is no main contract of sale with the owner to enforce."},
 {"t":"offer-acceptance","q":"On Monday, Brennan Steel emails Oriel Ltd offering to sell 40 tonnes of steel at £600 a tonne. On Tuesday, Oriel replies: 'Would you accept delivery spread over two months?' Brennan does not answer. On Wednesday, Oriel emails: 'We accept your offer of Monday.' Brennan, which has had a better offer, says there is no contract.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether a contract was made?","o":["Yes, because Oriel's Tuesday email was a mere request for information and did not end the offer.","No, because Oriel's Tuesday email proposed a different delivery arrangement, so it was a counter-offer that destroyed Monday's offer.","No, because Brennan never answered Oriel's Tuesday email, so the offer lapsed.","Yes, but on terms that delivery is spread over two months.","No, because acceptance by email takes effect only when the offeror reads it."],"a":0,"e":"Asking whether a different delivery arrangement would be acceptable is a request for information, not a counter-offer, so Monday's offer stayed open and Wednesday's email accepted it on the original terms (Stevenson v McLean). A counter-offer would have to propose a new term as part of the reply. The delivery query never became a term, so the contract is on the original terms."},
 {"t":"offer-acceptance","q":"Joseph writes to his sister Leah offering to buy her vintage motorbike for £4,000, adding: 'If I hear nothing from you by the end of the month, I will take it that the bike is mine.' Leah decides to accept but does not reply. She tells her husband that the bike is sold to Joseph. Before the end of the month, Leah's husband sells the bike to a dealer by mistake.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Joseph's position?","o":["He can sue Leah for breach, because she decided to accept and told a third party.","He can sue Leah for breach, because as offeror he waived the need for communication of acceptance, and she clearly decided to accept.","He can recover the bike from the dealer, because the property passed to him at the end of the month.","He has no claim, because a contract between family members is presumed not to be legally binding.","He has no claim, because silence cannot amount to acceptance and Leah never communicated acceptance to him."],"a":4,"e":"An offeror cannot impose a contract on the offeree by saying that silence will count as acceptance; acceptance must normally be communicated (Felthouse v Bindley). Telling her husband is not communication to Joseph. The family presumption is not the answer: a sale of property for a market price between adult siblings is a commercial-type transaction where intention can readily be found; the problem here is acceptance."},
 {"t":"offer-acceptance","q":"At 9.30 am on a weekday, Fairlie Ltd emails Gant Ltd offering to supply office furniture for £15,000. At 10.15 am, during normal office hours, Gant sends an email accepting the offer, which arrives in Fairlie's inbox at once. Fairlie's sales manager does not open it until 3 pm. At 11 am Fairlie had emailed Gant withdrawing the offer.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is no contract, because the acceptance was not read until after the revocation was sent.","There is no contract, because an emailed revocation takes effect when it is sent.","There is a contract, because the postal rule applies to email acceptances.","There is a contract, because the acceptance took effect when it arrived in Fairlie's inbox during office hours, before the revocation.","There is no contract, because acceptance by email takes effect only when it is actually opened and read, which here was after the revocation."],"a":3,"e":"Instantaneous communications such as email take effect when received; where they arrive during business hours they are treated as received then, whether or not they are read (Entores; The Brimnes). The acceptance at 10.15 am came before the 11 am revocation, so a contract was made. Saying the postal rule applies to email reaches the right result for the wrong reason."},
 {"t":"offer-acceptance","q":"On 1 May, Amira offers in writing to sell her classic car to Callum for £28,000, saying the offer is open until 8 May. On 5 May, Callum hears from Ruth, a reliable local estate agent, that Amira has agreed to sell the car to someone else. On 6 May, Callum delivers a written acceptance to Amira.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is a contract, because Amira promised to keep the offer open until 8 May.","There is a contract, because only Amira herself could communicate a revocation.","There is no contract, because an offer stated to be open until a fixed date can be accepted only on that date, so the acceptance was premature.","There is no contract, because Callum knew from a reliable source that the offer had been withdrawn before he accepted.","There is a contract, because selling to someone else is not a revocation unless it is completed."],"a":3,"e":"Revocation must be communicated, but it can be communicated by a reliable third party, and once the offeree knows the offeror no longer intends to sell, he cannot accept (Dickinson v Dodds). Amira's promise to keep the offer open was not binding because Callum gave nothing for it (it was not an option). Nothing requires an acceptance to wait until the closing date."},
 {"t":"offer-acceptance","q":"Halford Homes tells Sian, a self-employed decorator, that it will pay her £3,000 if she repaints all 12 show flats on its site by the end of June. Sian is not obliged to do the work. She buys materials and finishes 8 flats. Halford then tells her that it has found someone cheaper and withdraws its offer.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Halford's position?","o":["It can withdraw at any time before Sian completes all 12 flats, because acceptance of a unilateral offer requires full performance.","It can withdraw, but must pay Sian £2,000 for the 8 flats completed.","It cannot withdraw once Sian has started performance, because there is an implied promise not to revoke while she is performing.","It cannot withdraw, because Sian accepted the offer when she bought the materials.","It can withdraw, because Sian never communicated her acceptance before starting."],"a":2,"e":"This is a unilateral offer (a promise in return for an act). Although acceptance is complete only on full performance, once the offeree has begun to perform the offeror cannot revoke, as there is an implied collateral promise not to do so (Errington v Errington; Daulia v Four Millbank Nominees). Communication of acceptance is waived for unilateral offers, and buying materials is preparation, not performance."},
 {"t":"offer-acceptance","q":"Kerr Ltd sends Linden Ltd a purchase order for machine parts 'on the buyer's standard terms', which have no limit of liability. Linden replies with an acknowledgement 'accepted on the seller's standard terms printed overleaf', which limit its liability to the price. Kerr does not respond, but takes delivery of the parts and pays for them. The parts prove defective.\n\nWhich terms govern the contract?","o":["Kerr's terms, because the purchase order was the offer, and Linden accepted it by conduct when it delivered the parts to Kerr.","Linden's terms, because its acknowledgement was a counter-offer which Kerr accepted by taking delivery.","Kerr's terms, because a buyer's terms take priority in a sale of goods.","Neither set, because the parties never agreed terms and there is no contract.","Both sets, with any conflicting terms struck out."],"a":1,"e":"Linden's acknowledgement on different terms was a counter-offer that rejected Kerr's order; Kerr accepted it by conduct when it took delivery without objection, so Linden's terms apply (the 'last shot', Butler Machine Tool v Ex-Cell-O). English law does not strike out conflicting terms, and there is a contract because the parties performed."},
 {"t":"offer-acceptance","q":"On 2 March, Greenway Farms writes to Petra's Bakery offering to sell 500 kg of fresh strawberries at £3 per kg. The letter states no time limit for acceptance. Petra posts an acceptance on 20 April. Strawberry prices have risen sharply in the meantime.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is a contract, because an offer without a time limit stays open until it is revoked, and Greenway never revoked it.","There is a contract, because Petra's acceptance took effect when it was posted.","There is no contract, because acceptance of an offer made by letter must be sent by return of post.","There is a contract, but Greenway may charge the current market price.","There is no contract, because the offer lapsed after a reasonable time, which for perishable goods is short."],"a":4,"e":"An offer with no stated time limit lapses after a reasonable time, judged by the subject matter; for perishable goods with volatile prices that is short (Ramsgate Victoria Hotel v Montefiore). Seven weeks is far too long, so there was nothing left to accept. The postal rule only helps if there is still a live offer when the letter is posted."},
 {"t":"offer-acceptance","q":"Holt Engineering invites tenders for a maintenance contract. Its invitation states: 'Acceptance will be sent by post to the address on the tender form.' Vale Services submits a tender. Holt emails its acceptance to Vale's managing director, who receives it the same day. Vale then tries to withdraw its tender, arguing that acceptance was not made by post.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is no contract, because a tender is only an invitation to treat.","There is no contract, because Holt did not use the method of acceptance it had specified.","There is a contract, because the stated method was not said to be the only permitted method and email was no less advantageous to Vale.","There is a contract, but only once a confirming letter is posted.","There is no contract, because Vale was free to withdraw its tender at any time before a formal written contract was signed by both parties."],"a":2,"e":"Where a prescribed method of acceptance is not stated to be exclusive, another method that is no less advantageous to the offeror is effective (Manchester Diocesan Council for Education v Commercial and General Investments). Here the tender was the offer, and email reached Vale faster than post. An invitation to tender is an invitation to treat, but the tender itself is an offer."},
 {"t":"offer-acceptance","q":"Mr Doyle posts notices offering a £500 reward to anyone who returns his lost spaniel. Ines has seen one of the notices. She finds the dog and returns it, saying that she did so mainly because she felt sorry for the animal rather than for the money.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ines can claim the reward?","o":["Yes, because she knew of the offer when she performed the act, and her motive is irrelevant.","No, because she did not tell Mr Doyle in advance that she was accepting his offer.","No, because her main motive was not to obtain the reward.","No, because a reward notice is an invitation to treat.","Yes, but only if she had read the notice immediately before finding the dog."],"a":0,"e":"A unilateral offer is accepted by performing the act with knowledge of the offer; the acceptor's motive does not matter (Williams v Carwardine). Knowledge is required (R v Clarke), and Ines had it. Advance notice of acceptance is waived for unilateral offers, and a reward notice is a classic offer to the world."},
 {"t":"consideration","q":"While his aunt Gwen was in hospital, Rhys repaired the damaged fence at her house without being asked. When Gwen came home and saw the work, she was delighted and promised to pay Rhys £800. She has now refused to pay.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Rhys can enforce Gwen's promise?","o":["Yes, because Gwen received a practical benefit from the work.","Yes, because Gwen's promise was made after she had seen and accepted the work, so the work and the promise form a single transaction.","No, because the work was done before the promise was made and was not requested, so it is past consideration.","No, because agreements between family members are never legally binding.","Yes, because the work was worth at least £800."],"a":2,"e":"Consideration must not be past: an act completed before the promise is made does not support it (Re McArdle). The exception in Pao On v Lau Yiu Long needs the act to have been done at the promisor's request with payment understood; Rhys acted unasked. Practical benefit only matters where there is an existing duty to the promisor, and family agreements are presumed non-binding, not never binding."},
 {"t":"consideration","q":"Tomas agrees in writing to sell his car, worth about £9,000, to his friend's business, Quickcab Ltd, for £50. Before the handover, Tomas changes his mind and argues that there is no binding contract because the price is ridiculously low.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The contract is binding, but the court will substitute a reasonable price.","The contract is void, because £50 is not real consideration for a car worth £9,000.","The contract is voidable, because the price shows that Tomas was under undue influence.","There is no contract, because gifts disguised as sales lack intention to create legal relations.","The contract is binding, because consideration must be sufficient but need not be adequate."],"a":4,"e":"The courts do not weigh the value of consideration: it must have some value in the eyes of the law (be sufficient) but need not match the value of the promise (be adequate) (Chappell v Nestlé). An undervalue might be evidence of undue influence or fraud, but there are no such facts here. The court does not rewrite the price."},
 {"t":"consideration","q":"Wexford Rovers FC asks the local police force to station 20 officers inside its stadium for every home match, which is more than the police consider necessary to keep public order. The club promises to pay for the extra officers. After the season, the club refuses to pay, arguing that the police were only doing their public duty.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the police can recover payment?","o":["No, because performing a public duty can never be consideration.","No, because the club's promise was gratuitous.","Yes, but only for officers on duty outside the stadium.","Yes, because the police provided more than their public duty required, at the club's request.","No, because a promise to a public body requires a deed."],"a":3,"e":"Doing no more than an existing public duty is not good consideration (Collins v Godefroy), but doing more than the duty requires is (Glasbrook v Glamorgan CC). The extra officers inside the ground, requested by the club, went beyond what the police judged necessary, so the club's promise is enforceable. A deed is not needed."},
 {"t":"consideration","q":"Clayton Hauliers has a contract with Eastfield Ltd to deliver machinery to a construction site owned by Dunmore Developments. Dunmore, worried about delays, promises Clayton a £2,000 bonus if it delivers on time under its contract with Eastfield. Clayton delivers on time. Dunmore refuses to pay, saying Clayton was already bound to deliver.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Clayton can enforce Dunmore's promise?","o":["Yes, because performing an existing contractual duty owed to a third party is good consideration for a new promise.","No, because Dunmore is not a party to the delivery contract.","Yes, but only if Dunmore can show it obtained a practical benefit from the on-time delivery that Eastfield did not also obtain.","No, because Clayton only did what it was already contractually bound to do.","No, because the bonus was a penalty."],"a":0,"e":"Performing, or promising to perform, a duty already owed to a third party is good consideration for a fresh promise from someone else (Scotson v Pegg; Pao On). Clayton's duty was owed to Eastfield, not Dunmore, so delivery is consideration for Dunmore's promise. Practical benefit is the test where the existing duty is owed to the promisor himself."},
 {"t":"consideration","q":"Gareth owes Hollins Ltd £10,000, due now. Gareth says he is short of money, and Hollins agrees in writing to accept £6,000 'in full and final settlement'. Gareth pays the £6,000 on the due date. A month later Hollins sues for the remaining £4,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hollins' claim?","o":["It fails, because Hollins agreed in writing to accept less.","It fails, because Hollins received the practical benefit of being paid promptly.","It succeeds, because part payment of a debt on the due date is not consideration for a promise to forgive the balance.","It succeeds, but only if Hollins can show that Gareth could have paid the full £10,000 on the due date had he wanted to.","It fails, because Hollins is estopped from going back on its promise."],"a":2,"e":"Paying less than the debt, on the due date and in the same way, gives no consideration for the creditor's promise to accept less (Pinnel's Case; Foakes v Beer). Practical benefit does not apply to part payment of debts (Re Selectmove). Writing alone does not make a promise binding unless it is a deed, and estoppel needs facts making it inequitable to go back on the promise, which are not shown."},
 {"t":"consideration","q":"Keswick Ltd owes Abbott Supplies £20,000, due on 30 September. In July, Abbott, needing cash urgently, asks Keswick to pay £17,000 on 1 August in full settlement. Keswick does so. In October, Abbott claims the remaining £3,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Abbott's claim?","o":["It fails, because paying a lesser sum early, at the creditor's request, is good consideration for discharging the whole debt.","It succeeds, because part payment of a debt is never good consideration.","It succeeds, because Abbott agreed to accept less only because it urgently needed cash, so its promise to forgo £3,000 is not binding.","It fails, because Keswick has given Abbott a practical benefit.","It succeeds, unless Keswick can rely on promissory estoppel."],"a":0,"e":"Under the exceptions in Pinnel's Case, part payment discharges the whole debt if the debtor provides something different at the creditor's request, such as paying earlier or at a different place. Abbott asked for early payment, so the £3,000 is not recoverable. Practical benefit is not the reason (Re Selectmove), and there was no pressure from Keswick."},
 {"t":"consideration","q":"Pryce Builders completes work for Mrs Lowe worth £8,000. Mrs Lowe, knowing that Pryce is close to insolvency, says she will pay only £5,000 by cheque, 'take it or leave it, or you will get nothing'. Pryce reluctantly accepts the cheque 'in full settlement'. It now sues for the remaining £3,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Pryce's claim?","o":["It fails, because accepting a cheque instead of cash is something different from the debt, which can discharge the balance if the creditor agrees to it.","It succeeds, because a cheque is not something different and Mrs Lowe cannot rely on estoppel after extracting the promise by pressure.","It fails, because Pryce agreed to the settlement.","It succeeds, but only if Pryce returns the cheque.","It fails, because Mrs Lowe can rely on promissory estoppel."],"a":1,"e":"Payment by cheque is not a different form of payment that can discharge the balance (D and C Builders v Rees). Promissory estoppel is equitable and is not available to a debtor who obtained the creditor's promise by taking unfair advantage of its financial difficulty. So Pryce can recover the £3,000."},
 {"t":"consideration","q":"During their divorce, Martin promised his former wife Clare that he would pay her £300 a month maintenance. Clare gave nothing in return, but relying on the promise she did not apply to the court for a maintenance order. Martin has never paid. Clare sues Martin for the unpaid sums, relying on promissory estoppel.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Clare's claim?","o":["It succeeds, because she relied on the promise to her detriment.","It succeeds, because the promise was made in a family context during divorce proceedings, where consideration is not required for maintenance promises.","It fails, because Clare did not rely on the promise.","It fails, because promissory estoppel can be used only as a defence and cannot create a cause of action.","It succeeds, but she can recover only arrears for the last six months."],"a":3,"e":"Promissory estoppel is a 'shield, not a sword': it can prevent a promisor enforcing strict legal rights, but it cannot found a claim on a promise unsupported by consideration (Combe v Combe). Clare did rely on the promise, so lack of reliance is not the reason; the problem is that she is using estoppel to sue."},
 {"t":"consideration","q":"Mercer Ltd buys goods from Nash Ltd on terms that interest is payable on late invoices. During a recession Nash writes to Mercer: 'We will not charge interest on late payments until further notice.' Mercer relies on this and pays late for a year. Nash then gives Mercer reasonable written notice that interest will be charged on invoices paid late after the end of the notice period. It also claims interest for the past year.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nash's position?","o":["It can claim interest both for the past year and for the future, because its waiver was not supported by any consideration from Mercer.","It cannot claim interest for the past year, but it can charge interest on late payments after the reasonable notice expires.","It cannot claim interest for the past year or the future, because its waiver permanently varied the contract.","It can claim interest for the past year, because Mercer suffered no detriment by paying late.","It cannot claim future interest unless Mercer agrees to the change."],"a":1,"e":"Promissory estoppel prevents a promisor going back on a promise not to enforce its rights where the promisee has relied on it, but it usually only suspends those rights: the promisor can restore them for the future by giving reasonable notice (Tool Metal v Tungsten Electric). Nash is bound for the period of the waiver but can charge interest after the notice. Detriment in the strict sense is not required; reliance is enough."},
 {"t":"consideration","q":"Owen owes Rapid Loans Ltd £12,000. Owen's mother offers Rapid Loans £8,000 of her own money if it will accept that sum in full settlement of Owen's debt. Rapid Loans agrees and takes her money. It now sues Owen for the remaining £4,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rapid Loans' claim?","o":["It succeeds, because part payment of a debt is not good consideration.","It succeeds, because Owen himself gave no consideration.","It succeeds, but only once Owen's mother has been repaid.","It fails, because Owen's mother has a right to enforce the settlement under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999 and can stop the claim.","It fails, because accepting part payment from a third party in full settlement prevents the creditor suing the debtor for the balance."],"a":4,"e":"Where a third party pays part of a debt and the creditor accepts it in full settlement, the creditor cannot sue the debtor for the balance, because that would be a fraud on the third party (Hirachand Punamchand v Temple). This is an exception to the rule in Pinnel's Case. The 1999 Act is irrelevant: the question is whether Owen can resist the claim."},
 {"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","q":"Three flatmates, Ash, Bea and Cal, enter a weekly online lottery in Ash's name. They take it in turns to pay the stake and agree that any winnings will be shared equally. They win £9,000. Ash says it was his account and keeps the money.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Bea and Cal can claim a share?","o":["No, because agreements between friends are conclusively presumed not to be legally binding.","Yes, because pooling money on a shared understanding rebuts the presumption against intention to create legal relations.","Yes, but only Bea, because it was her turn to pay the stake that week.","No, because a gambling agreement is unenforceable.","No, because nothing was put in writing."],"a":1,"e":"Social arrangements are presumed not to be legally binding, but the presumption can be rebutted; where people pool money in a joint venture on the basis that winnings will be shared, the court will find intention (Simpkins v Pays). The presumption is rebuttable, not conclusive, and writing is not required. Each is entitled to a third."},
 {"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","q":"Kamal and Rena are happily married and living together. Kamal tells Rena that he will give her £200 a month to spend on herself. After six months he stops paying. They remain together. Rena wants to sue for the unpaid sums.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rena's position?","o":["She can sue, because Kamal made a clear promise which she accepted.","She cannot sue, because contracts between spouses are void.","She can sue, because by remaining married to Kamal and continuing to live with him she gave consideration for his promise.","She cannot sue, because agreements between spouses living together are presumed not to be intended to be legally binding.","She can sue, but only if she can prove that she relied on the money."],"a":3,"e":"Agreements between spouses living together amicably are presumed not to create legal relations (Balfour v Balfour), and nothing here rebuts the presumption. The position is different where spouses are separating (Merritt v Merritt). Contracts between spouses are not void; they simply need intention to be shown."},
 {"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","q":"Ardent Ltd and Brockley Ltd sign a written distribution agreement after detailed negotiations. Clause 20 states: 'This agreement is not entered into as a formal or legal agreement and shall not be subject to the jurisdiction of the courts. It records the honourable intentions of the parties.' Brockley stops supplying goods and Ardent sues for breach.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ardent's claim?","o":["It succeeds, because commercial agreements are always legally binding.","It succeeds, because a clause ousting the jurisdiction of the courts is void.","It fails, because there is no consideration.","It succeeds, because the agreement is in writing and signed.","It fails, because the clause clearly rebuts the presumption that the parties intended to create legal relations."],"a":4,"e":"In commercial agreements intention is presumed, but clear express words, such as an 'honour clause', can rebut the presumption (Rose and Frank v Crompton Bros). Signing and writing do not override such a clause. Commercial agreements are only presumed, not always, to be binding."},
 {"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","q":"Skyline Airways tells pilots who are being made redundant that, if they leave voluntarily, it will make them an 'ex gratia' payment equal to their pension contributions. Pilot Dev leaves on that basis. Skyline now refuses to pay, arguing that 'ex gratia' shows it never intended a legal obligation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Dev's position?","o":["He cannot enforce the promise, because 'ex gratia' means the payment is voluntary.","He can enforce it, because 'ex gratia' only means that Skyline does not admit a prior liability; it does not rebut the presumption of intention.","He cannot enforce the promise, because arrangements between an employer and staff who are leaving are treated in the same way as domestic arrangements.","He can enforce the promise only if it was put in writing.","He cannot enforce the promise, because he gave no consideration."],"a":1,"e":"In a commercial or employment setting the presumption of intention is strong, and the party denying it must prove otherwise. 'Ex gratia' simply means the payer does not admit a pre-existing liability; it does not show that the promise itself was not intended to bind (Edwards v Skyways). Dev gave consideration by leaving voluntarily."},
 {"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","q":"Northgate Bank lends £5 million to Orbis Trading Ltd. Orbis's parent company refuses to give a guarantee but writes to the bank: 'It is our policy to ensure that Orbis is at all times in a position to meet its liabilities to you.' Orbis becomes insolvent. The bank sues the parent company.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the bank's claim?","o":["It succeeds, because the parent company made the statement in a commercial context, where intention to create legal relations is presumed.","It succeeds, because the letter takes effect as a guarantee.","It fails, because the letter stated the parent's current policy and did not contain a promise about its future conduct.","It fails, because the letter is unsigned.","It fails, because a parent company has no capacity to undertake liability for a subsidiary."],"a":2,"e":"Whether a letter of comfort is binding depends on its wording. A statement of present policy is not a contractual promise about the future, especially where the parent had refused to give a guarantee (Kleinwort Benson v Malaysia Mining Corporation). The presumption of intention applies to promises; the problem here is that there was no promise."},
 {"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","q":"Patel Motors agrees to sell a van to Harley Ltd for £18,000, 'the balance to be paid on hire-purchase terms'. There are many different kinds of hire-purchase terms, and the parties never discuss which would apply. Before delivery, Patel refuses to proceed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether there is a binding contract?","o":["Yes, because the court will imply the most usual hire-purchase terms.","Yes, because the phrase is meaningless and can be ignored.","Yes, because the price was agreed and that is the only essential term.","No, because the phrase is too vague for the court to give it a definite meaning, so the agreement is incomplete.","No, because Harley never paid a deposit."],"a":3,"e":"A term so vague that the court cannot give it a definite meaning leaves the agreement uncertain, so there is no contract (Scammell v Ouston). A meaningless subsidiary phrase can sometimes be severed (Nicolene v Simmonds), but here the payment terms were essential and the parties clearly intended them to matter. There was no trade usage or previous dealing to fill the gap."},
 {"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","q":"Ruth, a sole trader, telephones Orchard Wholesale and orders 200 boxes of apples, which are delivered the next day. Neither party mentions the price, and there is no previous dealing or price list. Ruth now says there was no contract because no price was agreed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is a contract, and Ruth must pay a reasonable price.","There is a contract, and Orchard can charge whatever price it chooses.","There is no contract, but Orchard can recover the apples.","There is no contract, because price is an essential term which was never agreed.","There is a contract, but only once a price is agreed between the parties."],"a":0,"e":"Where a contract for the sale of goods does not fix the price and there is no machinery or course of dealing to fix it, the buyer must pay a reasonable price (Sale of Goods Act 1979 s.8). The parties have also performed, which makes the court readier to find a contract. Orchard cannot set any price it likes."},
 {"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","q":"Edgar, who has advanced dementia, signs a contract with a home improvements company for a new kitchen at a fair price. The company's salesperson spent an hour with him and noticed nothing unusual. Edgar's daughter later wants to avoid the contract on the ground of his lack of mental capacity.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The contract is binding, unless the company knew or ought to have known of Edgar's incapacity.","The contract is voidable at Edgar's option, whatever the company knew.","The contract is void, because Edgar lacked mental capacity.","The contract is binding, because a person who signs is always bound.","The contract is unenforceable, because contracts for home improvements must be made in writing."],"a":0,"e":"A contract made by a person lacking mental capacity is binding unless the other party knew, or ought to have known, of the incapacity, in which case it is voidable (Hart v O'Connor). It is not void. On these facts, a fair price and nothing noticeable, Edgar is likely bound; necessaries would in any event need a reasonable price."},
 {"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","q":"Kai, aged 16, signs a two-year training contract with Northfield United Football Club. The contract provides coaching and education and pays a modest wage, but allows the club to fine him for breaches of discipline. Kai wants to leave after three months and argues that, as a minor, he is not bound.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Kai is not bound, because contracts with minors are unenforceable against them.","Kai is bound, but only once he reaches 18.","Kai is bound, because a contract of employment or training that is, taken as a whole, substantially for his benefit is binding on a minor.","Kai is not bound, because a training contract is a continuing contract of the kind that a minor can repudiate at any time before he reaches 18.","Kai is not bound, because the disciplinary fines make the contract harsh."],"a":2,"e":"Contracts of service, apprenticeship and training are binding on a minor if, taken as a whole, they are substantially for the minor's benefit, even if some terms are onerous (Doyle v White City Stadium; Roberts v Gray). Training, education and wages point strongly to benefit. Continuing contracts such as leases and shareholdings are a different category."},
 {"t":"intention-certainty-capacity","q":"Ffion, aged 17, takes a one-year lease of a flat for £700 a month. After four months she repudiates the lease and moves out, still aged 17. She had not paid the rent for the fourth month. The landlord claims the rent for the fourth month and for the remaining eight months.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ffion's liability?","o":["She is liable for all twelve months, because a lease of a home is a contract for necessaries, which binds a minor in the same way as an adult.","She is liable for nothing, because contracts with minors are void.","She is liable for nothing, because she repudiated before she turned 18.","She is liable for the remaining eight months, but not the fourth month.","She is liable for the rent for the fourth month, which fell due before she repudiated, but not for rent falling due afterwards."],"a":4,"e":"A lease is a continuing contract that binds a minor unless repudiated before, or within a reasonable time after, turning 18. Repudiation releases the minor from future obligations, but liabilities that accrued before repudiation remain. Minors' contracts are not void, and a lease is treated as a continuing contract rather than one for necessaries at an agreed price."},
 {"t":"privity-third-parties","q":"Brookside Developments contracts with Hardy Construction to build a block of flats. The contract states: 'Hardy shall be liable to the future purchasers of each flat for any structural defect appearing within ten years.' Mei, who bought a flat two years after the contract was signed, discovers a serious structural defect.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Mei can sue Hardy under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999?","o":["No, because she did not exist as a purchaser when the contract was made.","No, because she was not named in the contract.","Yes, because she is identified as a member of a class in the contract, and the term purports to confer a benefit on her.","Yes, but only if Brookside agrees to join the claim.","No, because the 1999 Act applies only to terms that expressly state that the third party may enforce them, and this term does not say so."],"a":2,"e":"Under s.1 the third party may be identified by name, as a member of a class or by description, and need not exist when the contract is made (s.1(3)). The term purports to confer a benefit on 'future purchasers', so s.1(1)(b) applies, and nothing suggests the parties did not intend Mei to enforce it. An express statement of the right is one route (s.1(1)(a)), but not the only one."},
 {"t":"privity-third-parties","q":"Corran Ltd contracts with Delta Logistics to deliver goods to Corran's customer, Ellis plc. The contract states that Delta will pay Ellis £500 for every day the delivery is late. It also states: 'Nothing in this agreement shall confer any right on any person who is not a party to it.' Delivery is ten days late. Ellis wants to sue Delta for £5,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ellis's position?","o":["Ellis can sue, because the term purports to confer a benefit on it.","Ellis can sue, because it is expressly named in the contract.","Ellis cannot sue, because the late payment term is a penalty.","Ellis can sue, but only for its actual loss.","Ellis cannot sue, because the contract shows that the parties did not intend the term to be enforceable by a third party."],"a":4,"e":"Where a term purports to benefit a third party, there is a presumption that it may enforce it, but this is displaced if on a proper construction the parties did not intend it to be enforceable by the third party (s.1(2)). A clear exclusion clause does exactly that. Corran, as promisee, can still sue Delta."},
 {"t":"privity-third-parties","q":"Fenwick hires Glenn Builders to repair her house. The contract obliges Glenn to 'carry out all works in a good and workmanlike manner'. Nothing else in the contract refers to anyone other than the parties. Fenwick's lodger, Hamid, has his belongings damaged by poor work and wants to sue Glenn for breach of contract under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hamid's position?","o":["He can sue, because the work benefited everyone living in the house.","He can sue, because he can prove from outside evidence that Glenn knew he lived in the house and would be affected by the work.","He cannot sue under the Act, because he is not expressly identified in the contract by name, class or description.","He can sue, but only for the cost of repairing the work.","He cannot sue, because a lodger has no interest in land."],"a":2,"e":"The third party must be expressly identified in the contract itself by name, class or description (s.1(3)); outside evidence that the parties had him in mind is not enough (Avraamides v Colwill). Hamid may have a claim in tort for negligence, but not under the 1999 Act."},
 {"t":"privity-third-parties","q":"Imogen contracts with Jarrow Gardens Ltd for Jarrow to maintain the garden of her elderly father, Ken, for two years, and the contract expressly says Ken may enforce it. Ken writes to Jarrow saying he agrees to the arrangement, and Jarrow receives the letter. Imogen and Jarrow later agree to cancel the contract without asking Ken. The contract says nothing about variation or cancellation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the cancellation on Ken?","o":["It is effective, because only the contracting parties can decide whether their contract continues, and Ken was never a party to it at all.","It is effective, because Ken has not yet relied on the contract.","It is not effective, because a contract benefiting a third party can never be cancelled.","It is not effective against Ken, because once Jarrow received his assent the parties could not cancel his right without his consent.","It is effective, but Ken may claim damages from Imogen."],"a":3,"e":"Under s.2(1), once the third party has communicated his assent to the promisor (or the promisor knows he has relied on the term, or ought to have foreseen reliance and he has relied), the parties cannot cancel or vary his rights without his consent, unless the contract provides otherwise. Ken's assent was received by Jarrow, so reliance does not need to be shown. Cancellation is possible with Ken's consent or under an express term."},
 {"t":"privity-third-parties","q":"Lorna contracts with Moss Caterers to provide food for her son Niall's wedding, with an express term that Niall may enforce the catering obligations. On 1 June Niall posts a letter to Moss assenting to the term. On 2 June, Lorna and Moss agree to vary the contract to remove Niall's right. Niall's letter reaches Moss on 3 June. Niall has not otherwise relied on the term.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the variation?","o":["It is ineffective, because Niall's assent took effect when he posted it.","It is effective, because Niall's assent counts only when received by the promisor, and that happened after the variation.","It is ineffective, because a contract for a wedding is for the benefit of both spouses.","It is effective, because a third party's rights can always be removed by the contracting parties at any time before performance is due.","It is ineffective, because Moss knew Niall intended to rely on the term."],"a":1,"e":"For s.2, assent must be communicated to the promisor, and if sent by post it is not treated as received until it actually arrives (s.2(2)(b)); the postal rule does not apply. As the variation came first and Niall had not relied, it was effective. Nothing suggests Moss knew of any reliance."},
 {"t":"privity-third-parties","q":"Oakley contracts with Priory Removals to move furniture to his daughter Quinn's new flat. The contract expressly entitles Quinn to enforce Priory's obligations, and it limits Priory's liability for damaged items to £1,000. A dresser worth £3,000 belonging to Quinn is damaged by Priory's carelessness. Quinn sues Priory under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Quinn's position?","o":["She can recover £3,000, because the limitation clause binds only Oakley.","She can recover nothing, because she is not a party to the contract.","She can recover £3,000, because a limitation clause in a contract can never bind a third party who did not sign that contract.","She can recover up to £1,000 only if she signed the contract.","She can recover up to £1,000, because Priory can rely against her on the limitation clause in the contract."],"a":4,"e":"A third party's right to enforce a term is subject to the other terms of the contract, and the promisor can rely on any defence that would have been available against the promisee in relation to that term (ss.1(4) and 3). So the £1,000 cap applies to Quinn. She does not need to sign the contract to have, or be subject to, these rights."},
 {"t":"privity-third-parties","q":"Ravi books and pays for a two-week package holiday for himself, his wife and their two children. The hotel is filthy and the promised facilities do not exist, ruining the holiday for the whole family. Ravi sues the tour operator for damages.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the damages Ravi can recover?","o":["Damages for the whole family's disappointment, because in a family holiday contract the booker can recover for the losses of those he contracted for.","Only damages for his own loss, because the rest of the family were not parties to the contract.","Nothing for disappointment, because damages for mental distress are not recoverable in contract, and the rest of the family were not parties to it in any event.","Only the price of the holiday for each family member.","Damages for the family only if each family member brings a separate claim."],"a":0,"e":"The promisee can generally recover only for his own loss, but in a family holiday contract the person who booked can recover for the disappointment of the family members he contracted for (Jackson v Horizon Holidays, later treated as a special case in Woodar v Wimpey). Distress damages are recoverable where the object of the contract is pleasure or enjoyment."},
 {"t":"privity-third-parties","q":"Stan sells his window-cleaning business to his nephew Tariq. In return, Tariq promises Stan that, after Stan dies, he will pay Stan's widow, Una, £100 a week. The contract expressly excludes the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999. Stan dies, and Tariq makes one payment and then stops. Una is Stan's administrator.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Una can enforce the promise?","o":["As administrator of Stan's estate, she can seek an order for specific performance requiring Tariq to pay her.","She can sue Tariq for damages in her own name under the 1999 Act.","She cannot enforce it, because the only party who could have done so was Stan, who has died.","She can obtain only nominal damages as administrator, because the estate has suffered no loss.","She can sue in her own name as the person intended to benefit."],"a":0,"e":"Una cannot sue in her personal capacity because she is not a party and the 1999 Act is excluded. But as administrator she steps into Stan's shoes as promisee, and because damages to the estate would be nominal and inadequate, the court can order specific performance in her favour (Beswick v Beswick). The fact that the estate would recover only nominal damages is the very reason specific performance is appropriate."},
 {"t":"privity-third-parties","q":"Vista Leisure owns a seaside pier. Wexley Paints tells Vista that its paint will last ten years. Relying on that, Vista requires its painting contractor to buy and use Wexley's paint for the pier. The contractor buys the paint from Wexley. The paint peels after six months.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Vista's position against Wexley?","o":["Vista has no claim, because it did not buy the paint from Wexley.","Vista can sue Wexley on a collateral contract: Wexley's promise was given in return for Vista causing the contractor to buy the paint.","Vista can sue only through its contractor.","Vista can sue under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999, because the contract of sale between the contractor and Wexley benefited it.","Vista can sue only in the tort of negligence."],"a":1,"e":"A collateral contract can arise where one party promises something in return for the other procuring a third person to enter a main contract with the promisor (Shanklin Pier v Detel Products). Vista's consideration was instructing its contractor to buy Wexley's paint. The 1999 Act would need the contract of sale to identify and purport to benefit Vista, which the facts do not show."},
 {"t":"privity-third-parties","q":"Ashby Ltd has a five-year contract to supply cleaning services to Beaumont plc. Ashby wants to sell its business and arranges for Cleanco Ltd to take over performance of the cleaning obligations, releasing Ashby from them. Beaumont has not been asked and does not agree.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Ashby can transfer its obligations by giving written notice to Beaumont.","Ashby can transfer its obligations only if Cleanco is equally competent.","Ashby can transfer its obligations to Cleanco, because contracts for services are freely assignable without the other party's consent.","Ashby cannot transfer the burden of its obligations without Beaumont's agreement; this requires a novation.","Ashby can transfer both the benefit and the burden by a written assignment."],"a":3,"e":"The benefit of a contract (for example, the right to payment) can generally be assigned, and a legal assignment needs writing and notice to the debtor (LPA 1925 s.136). The burden cannot be transferred without the other party's consent: a new contract, called a novation, is needed. Notice alone or a written assignment cannot release Ashby from its obligations."},
 {"t":"express-terms","q":"Hanna, who runs a small café, signs a hire agreement for a coffee machine without reading it. The agreement contains a clause excluding liability for any loss of profits caused by breakdown. The machine breaks down and Hanna loses a week's profits. She argues that she is not bound by a clause she did not read.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The clause is incorporated, because a person who signs a contractual document is generally bound by its terms whether or not she read them.","The clause is not incorporated, because the hire company did not specifically draw it to her attention before she signed the hire agreement.","The clause is not incorporated, because Hanna did not read it.","The clause is incorporated only if it is reasonable.","The clause is not incorporated, because Hanna is not a large business."],"a":0,"e":"A signature binds the signer to the terms of a contractual document, read or not, unless there was fraud or misrepresentation (L'Estrange v Graucob). Reasonableness under UCTA is a separate question about whether the clause is effective, not whether it is incorporated."},
 {"t":"express-terms","q":"Petra takes a wedding dress to Ivory Cleaners. The assistant asks her to sign a form, and when Petra asks what it is, the assistant says it excludes liability only for damage to beads and sequins. In fact the form excludes liability for 'any damage however caused'. The dress comes back badly stained.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ivory can rely on the clause?","o":["Yes, because Petra signed the form.","No, because the assistant misrepresented the effect of the clause, so Ivory can rely on it only for beads and sequins.","No, because cleaning contracts cannot exclude liability.","Yes, because staining is covered by 'any damage however caused'.","No, because the form was not given to Petra in advance."],"a":1,"e":"The signature rule does not apply where the party relying on the clause misrepresented its effect; the clause is then limited to what was represented (Curtis v Chemical Cleaning and Dyeing). Ivory can rely on it only for damage to beads and sequins, so it cannot rely on it for the stain."},
 {"t":"express-terms","q":"Lenses Ltd, a photo library, sends 40 photographs to Studio Nine, an advertising agency, which has asked to see them. The photos come with a delivery note whose ninth condition, in ordinary print, imposes a holding fee of £5 per photo per day after 14 days. That is many times the usual rate. Studio Nine forgets to return the photos for a month and is billed £3,200.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the fee is payable?","o":["Yes, because the delivery note was a contractual document received before the photos were used, so all its conditions were incorporated.","Yes, but only at the usual market rate.","Yes, because Studio Nine is a business and is taken to have read the terms.","No, because terms in a delivery note can never be part of a contract.","No, because a particularly onerous or unusual term must be fairly brought to the other party's attention, and this one was not."],"a":4,"e":"The more onerous or unusual a term, the more must be done to bring it fairly and reasonably to the other party's attention; a general set of conditions in ordinary print is not enough for an exceptionally harsh term (Interfoto v Stiletto). The fee is not incorporated. The court does not substitute a market rate under the incorporation rules."},
 {"t":"express-terms","q":"Deckchairs on Brighton beach are stacked beside a sign: 'Deckchairs £3 per hour. Pay the attendant.' When Oscar pays, the attendant gives him a ticket. On the back, which Oscar does not read, the ticket states that the council is not liable for any accident. The chair collapses because of poor maintenance and Oscar is hurt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the council can rely on the ticket?","o":["Yes, because Oscar accepted the ticket and should have read it.","Yes, because exclusion clauses printed on tickets are always incorporated.","No, because Oscar did not sign the ticket.","No, because the ticket was a mere receipt which a reasonable person would not expect to contain contractual terms.","Yes, but only for damage to Oscar's property."],"a":3,"e":"A document is incorporated by notice only if a reasonable person would expect it to contain contractual terms; a ticket that appears to be a mere receipt for payment does not qualify (Chapelton v Barry UDC). In any event, liability for personal injury caused by negligence cannot be excluded against a consumer (CRA 2015 s.65)."},
 {"t":"express-terms","q":"Hal, a private individual, buys a second-hand car from Prestige Motors, a car dealer. Before the sale, the salesman tells Hal that the car has done only 20,000 miles since a new engine was fitted. That is false; the salesman had not checked. The written contract does not mention the mileage.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of the salesman's statement?","o":["It is a mere puff with no legal effect.","It is a representation only, because it was not included in the written contract, which the parties intended to record all the terms.","It is likely to be a term, because it was made by a dealer with special knowledge and skill and was important to the buyer.","It is a term only if Hal can show that the salesman knew it was false.","It is a statement of opinion, because the salesman did not check."],"a":2,"e":"Whether a statement is a term depends on the parties' objective intention; a statement by a party with special knowledge or skill, such as a dealer, is more likely to be a term (Dick Bentley v Harold Smith Motors, contrasted with Oscar Chess v Williams, where the private seller had no special knowledge). The absence of the statement from the written document does not stop it being a term or collateral warranty."},
 {"t":"express-terms","q":"Rhona, a florist, is negotiating to buy a refrigerated van from Coldline Ltd. She tells Coldline: 'If the refrigeration unit is more than three years old, I won't buy the van at all.' Coldline replies: 'It's only a year old.' Rhona buys the van. The unit is in fact five years old.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of Coldline's statement?","o":["It is a statement of opinion, because Coldline may not have known the unit's age.","It is a mere representation, because it was not put in writing.","It is a term, because Rhona made clear that the point was so important that she would not have contracted without it.","It is a mere representation, because Rhona could easily have checked the age of the refrigeration unit for herself before buying.","It is a term only if Coldline is a dealer in refrigerated vans."],"a":2,"e":"Where one party makes clear that a matter is so important that she would not contract without it, a statement on that matter is likely to be a term (Bannerman v White). Writing is not required for a term, and the opportunity to verify is relevant but does not outweigh this strong indication."},
 {"t":"express-terms","q":"Before buying a warehouse unit, Delta Ltd is told by the seller's director that the roof was replaced in 2022. The signed contract contains a clause stating: 'This contract contains the entire agreement between the parties and supersedes all prior statements and representations.' The roof was in fact not replaced and is leaking.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Delta's position?","o":["Delta can sue for breach of a term, because the statement was important.","Delta has no remedy, because the entire agreement clause excludes all liability for statements made before the contract, including misrepresentations.","Delta can sue for breach of contract and for misrepresentation.","Delta can rescind the contract, but only if the director knew the statement was false.","Delta cannot rely on the statement as a term, but may still claim for misrepresentation, which the clause does not by itself exclude."],"a":4,"e":"An entire agreement clause prevents a pre-contract statement from taking effect as a term or collateral warranty, but on its own it does not exclude liability for misrepresentation. That needs clear wording, and any such exclusion must also satisfy the reasonableness test (Misrepresentation Act 1967 s.3). Rescission for misrepresentation does not require fraud."},
 {"t":"express-terms","q":"Under a four-year distribution agreement with a manufacturer, Wren Ltd must send a sales representative to visit each of six named retailers every week. The agreement states: 'It shall be a condition of this agreement that the representative visits each retailer every week.' One week the representative misses one visit because of a car breakdown. The manufacturer wants to terminate the agreement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the manufacturer's position?","o":["It may terminate, because the parties expressly called the term a condition.","It may not terminate, because calling a term a condition is not conclusive if the parties cannot have meant any breach to allow termination.","It may terminate only if the missed visit was deliberate.","It may not terminate, because a single missed visit causes no loss.","It may terminate, because every obligation in a long-term distribution agreement is a condition, and here the parties also expressly used that word."],"a":1,"e":"Using the word 'condition' is strong evidence but not conclusive; where treating every breach as a right to terminate would be unreasonable (about 1,400 visits over four years), the court may conclude that the parties did not intend it to be a condition in the technical sense (Schuler v Wickman Machine Tool Sales). The manufacturer can claim damages but cannot terminate for this one missed visit."},
 {"t":"express-terms","q":"A commercial contract for the sale of grain requires the buyer, Fenton Ltd, to give the seller at least 15 days' notice of the date its ship will be ready to load. Fenton gives notice four days late. The delay causes the seller little or no loss, but the market price has fallen and the seller wishes to terminate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the seller's position?","o":["It cannot terminate, because the breach was minor and caused little loss.","It can terminate only if the four-day delay deprived it of substantially the whole benefit that it was intended to obtain from the contract.","It cannot terminate, because time is never of the essence in a sale of goods.","It can terminate, because in a commercial sale contract a time clause of this kind is treated as a condition.","It can claim damages only, because the notice clause is a warranty."],"a":3,"e":"In mercantile contracts, time clauses are usually treated as conditions, because certainty matters to commercial parties, so any breach allows termination regardless of its consequences (Bunge v Tradax). Asking whether the breach deprived the seller of substantially the whole benefit is the test for an innominate term, which does not apply here."},
 {"t":"express-terms","q":"Gamma Software contracts to supply a system to Horizon Ltd. The contract states that it may be varied 'only in writing signed by authorised signatories of both parties'. On a phone call, Gamma's account manager agrees with Horizon that the delivery date can be extended by six weeks. Gamma later relies on the extension when Horizon complains of late delivery.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the extension is binding?","o":["No, because a 'no oral modification' clause is effective, so an oral variation that does not meet its formalities does not bind.","Yes, because parties are always free to vary their contract orally, whatever the contract itself says about how variations must be made.","Yes, because Horizon received a practical benefit.","No, because a variation of a delivery date needs a deed.","Yes, because the account manager had apparent authority."],"a":0,"e":"The Supreme Court held that a 'no oral modification' clause is valid and effective, so an oral variation that does not comply is not binding (Rock Advertising v MWB Business Exchange Centres). An estoppel might exceptionally help, but it would need more than the informal promise itself. Practical benefit concerns consideration, not this formality."},
 {"t":"implied-terms","q":"Tilly leases a unit in a shopping centre from Mallward Estates. The lease says nothing about the other units. Several neighbouring shops close and stay empty, and Tilly's trade falls. She argues that the lease contains an implied term that Mallward will keep the centre fully let, because that would be reasonable.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The term will be implied, because it is reasonable and fair.","The term will be implied in law into all commercial leases.","The term will be implied, because it reflects what Tilly would reasonably have wanted, and it would make the lease much fairer to her as the tenant.","The term will not be implied, because terms can be implied only by statute.","The term will not be implied, because a term is implied in fact only if necessary for business efficacy or so obvious it goes without saying."],"a":4,"e":"A term is implied in fact only if it is necessary for business efficacy or so obvious that it goes without saying; reasonableness alone is not enough (Marks & Spencer v BNP Paribas). The lease works perfectly well without such a term, and a landlord cannot guarantee that other tenants will stay."},
 {"t":"implied-terms","q":"Harbour Council lets flats in a high-rise block. The tenancy agreements say nothing about the lifts, stairs or rubbish chutes, which remain under the council's control. The lifts often fail and the stairwell lights are broken. A tenant argues that the council is in breach of contract.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["A term is implied in law that the council will take reasonable care to keep the common parts reasonably fit for use.","There is a breach only if the council expressly promised to maintain the common parts.","The council is under an absolute duty to keep the common parts in working order at all times.","There is no breach, because only statute can impose repairing duties on landlords.","There is no breach, because the tenancy agreement imposes no obligation about the common parts."],"a":0,"e":"In a defined type of contract, such as a tenancy of flats in a block where the landlord keeps the common parts, the law implies a term as a necessary incident of the relationship: a duty to take reasonable care to maintain them (Liverpool CC v Irwin). The duty is one of reasonable care, not an absolute obligation."},
 {"t":"implied-terms","q":"Bryn, a private individual, advertises his classic car for sale as 'a 1972 model'. Ceri, another private individual, buys it after a test drive. Ceri later discovers it is a 1975 model. It is in good working order.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ceri's position?","o":["She has no claim, because private sellers give no implied terms.","She has a claim, because the implied term that goods correspond with their description applies even to private sales.","She has no claim, because the car is of satisfactory quality.","She has a claim under the Consumer Rights Act 2015.","She has a claim only if she can show that Bryn knew the description of the car was false when he advertised it for sale."],"a":1,"e":"The implied condition that goods correspond with their description (SGA 1979 s.13) applies to all sales by description, including private sales. Satisfactory quality and fitness for purpose (s.14) apply only where the seller sells in the course of a business. The CRA 2015 needs a trader, and liability under s.13 is strict."},
 {"t":"implied-terms","q":"Mariner Boats Ltd asks Sealcoat Ltd, a paint supplier, for a paint to protect the hulls of its boats in seawater, and relies on Sealcoat's recommendation. Sealcoat supplies a paint which is excellent for indoor use but which flakes off when kept in seawater for a few weeks.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mariner's position?","o":["It has no claim, because the paint is of satisfactory quality for its usual purposes, and a seller does not guarantee its suitability for unusual uses.","It has no claim, because Mariner is a business and caveat emptor applies.","It has a claim for breach of the implied term that the goods are reasonably fit for the particular purpose it made known to the seller.","It has a claim only under the Consumer Rights Act 2015.","It has a claim only if Sealcoat knew the paint was unsuitable."],"a":2,"e":"Where a business buyer makes known a particular purpose to a seller who sells in the course of a business, there is an implied term that the goods are reasonably fit for that purpose, unless the buyer did not rely, or it was unreasonable to rely, on the seller's skill (SGA 1979 s.14(3)). Mariner relied on Sealcoat's recommendation. Liability is strict, and the CRA does not apply between businesses."},
 {"t":"implied-terms","q":"Tarn Ltd buys a used forklift truck from Ridge Plant Hire for its warehouse. Before the sale, Ridge points out a large dent in the bodywork and a cracked seat, and the price is reduced to reflect them. After delivery, Tarn complains about the dent and the seat and argues that the forklift is not of satisfactory quality.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Tarn's position?","o":["Tarn can reject the forklift, because the defects make it unsatisfactory.","Tarn can claim damages, because satisfactory quality cannot be excluded.","Tarn has a claim, because a second-hand item must be as good as new.","Tarn has no claim about those defects, because they were specifically drawn to its attention before the contract was made.","Tarn has no claim, because the implied term of satisfactory quality does not apply at all to second-hand goods sold between businesses."],"a":3,"e":"The implied term of satisfactory quality does not extend to defects specifically drawn to the buyer's attention before the contract (SGA 1979 s.14(2C)(a)). The term does apply to second-hand goods, but the standard takes account of age and price; it does not require them to be as good as new."},
 {"t":"implied-terms","q":"Pinnacle Ltd engages Quarry Consulting to review its IT security. Quarry carries out the review with the care and skill expected of a competent IT consultant. However, its recommendations do not prevent a later cyber-attack, and Pinnacle claims Quarry is in breach of contract. The contract says nothing about the standard of the work.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Quarry's position?","o":["Quarry is in breach, because it impliedly guaranteed that the system would be secure.","Quarry is in breach, because the result was not fit for purpose.","Quarry is not in breach, because the implied term is to carry out the service with reasonable care and skill, which it did.","Quarry is not liable, because the Supply of Goods and Services Act 1982 does not imply any terms into contracts between two businesses.","Quarry is not liable, because there are no implied terms in contracts for services."],"a":2,"e":"In a business-to-business contract for services, the Supply of Goods and Services Act 1982 s.13 implies a term that the supplier will carry out the service with reasonable care and skill. It is a duty of care, not a guarantee of a particular result. As Quarry met the standard of a competent consultant, there is no breach."},
 {"t":"implied-terms","q":"Lena, a consumer, pays Warmhome Heating Ltd £180 to service her boiler. The engineer does the job carelessly and the boiler keeps cutting out. Lena wants to know what she can require Warmhome to do first.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Lena's primary remedy under the Consumer Rights Act 2015?","o":["She can require a price reduction of at least 50%.","She can reject the service and demand an immediate full refund, because it was not performed with reasonable care.","She can require Warmhome to replace the boiler.","She can claim only damages for breach of contract.","She can require Warmhome to perform the service again properly, within a reasonable time and at no cost to her."],"a":4,"e":"Where a service is not performed with reasonable care and skill (CRA 2015 s.49), the consumer's first remedy is repeat performance (s.55), at the trader's cost, within a reasonable time and without significant inconvenience. A price reduction is available if repeat performance is impossible or not done in time (s.56). There is no 'right to reject' a service, though damages remain available."},
 {"t":"implied-terms","q":"Four months ago, Omar, a consumer, bought a sofa from Comfort Homes for £1,500. A month ago a frame defect appeared; Comfort repaired it, but the same fault has now returned. Omar wants his money back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Omar's rights under the Consumer Rights Act 2015?","o":["He must allow Comfort a second attempt at repair before he can do anything else.","He can exercise the short-term right to reject and receive a full refund.","He can exercise the final right to reject, but Comfort may deduct an amount from the refund to reflect his four months' use of the sofa at home.","He can exercise the final right to reject and receive a full refund without any deduction for use, or ask for a price reduction instead.","He is limited to damages, because more than 30 days have passed."],"a":3,"e":"After one unsuccessful repair or replacement, the consumer can choose the final right to reject or a price reduction (CRA 2015 s.24). If the final right to reject is used within six months of delivery, no deduction for use is allowed (except for motor vehicles). The 30-day short-term right to reject has expired."},
 {"t":"implied-terms","q":"Gwen, a consumer, pays £60 to download a photo-editing app. It has a bug that causes it to crash whenever she saves a file. A week later she asks the developer for her money back by 'rejecting' the app.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gwen's rights under the Consumer Rights Act 2015?","o":["She can exercise the short-term right to reject within 30 days of the purchase and obtain a full refund of the £60.","She can require repair or replacement and, if that fails, a price reduction, which may be up to the full price.","She can reject the app and claim damages for her time.","She has no rights, because the CRA does not cover digital content.","She must accept a repair, but cannot ever obtain a price reduction."],"a":1,"e":"Digital content must be of satisfactory quality (CRA 2015 s.34). The remedies are repair or replacement, then price reduction (ss.43-44), which can be up to 100% of the price. There is no right to reject digital content that is not supplied on a tangible medium, so neither the 30-day right to reject nor rejection plus damages is available."},
 {"t":"implied-terms","q":"Iris, a private individual, sells her five-year-old bicycle to Jack, another private individual, for £200. She describes it accurately and honestly believes it to be in good condition. A week later the frame cracks because of a hidden defect.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Jack's position?","o":["He has no claim on quality, because satisfactory quality and fitness for purpose are implied only into sales in the course of a business.","He can claim under the Consumer Rights Act 2015.","He can reject the bicycle, because it was not of satisfactory quality, and the crack shows that the defect already existed at the time of sale.","He can claim damages for breach of the implied term as to description.","He can claim a price reduction under the Sale of Goods Act 1979."],"a":0,"e":"In a private sale, the implied terms of satisfactory quality and fitness for purpose do not apply (SGA 1979 s.14 needs a sale in the course of a business); the buyer must take care (caveat emptor). Only title, description and sample terms apply, and the bike was accurately described. The CRA needs a trader seller."},
 {"t":"exemption-clauses","q":"Pemberton Storage stores goods for Halden Ltd. The contract states: 'Pemberton is not liable for any loss of or damage to goods while in transit.' Halden's goods are damaged while sitting in Pemberton's warehouse because a Pemberton employee left a skylight open. Pemberton relies on the clause.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the clause protects Pemberton?","o":["Yes, because the clause covers any damage to the goods.","Yes, because Halden is a business and accepted the clause.","Yes, but only if the clause satisfies the requirement of reasonableness under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, which Pemberton must prove.","No, because a storage company can never exclude liability for negligence.","No, because exclusion clauses are construed strictly against the party relying on them, and this damage did not occur in transit."],"a":4,"e":"Before applying UCTA, the court asks whether the clause, properly construed, covers the loss; exclusion clauses are interpreted strictly, and any genuine ambiguity is resolved against the party relying on them (contra proferentem). Damage in the warehouse is not damage 'in transit'. Reasonableness only arises if the clause covers the loss."},
 {"t":"exemption-clauses","q":"Ellis, a sales representative for Gant Supplies, visits the warehouse of Keld Ltd to take an order. A sign at the entrance states: 'Keld Ltd accepts no liability for death or injury to visitors however caused.' Ellis is injured when a pallet stacked carelessly by a Keld employee falls on him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Keld can rely on the sign?","o":["Yes, because Ellis is a business visitor, not a consumer.","No, because a business cannot exclude or restrict liability for death or personal injury resulting from negligence.","No, because the notice was not signed by Ellis.","Yes, if the notice is reasonable in all the circumstances.","Yes, because Ellis voluntarily entered the warehouse having seen the sign."],"a":1,"e":"Under UCTA 1977 s.2(1), a person cannot by any contract term or notice exclude or restrict business liability for death or personal injury resulting from negligence. The reasonableness test (s.2(2)) applies only to other loss or damage. Awareness of a notice is not, of itself, voluntary acceptance of the risk (s.2(3))."},
 {"t":"exemption-clauses","q":"Calder Couriers collects goods from business customers on its standard terms. One term excludes liability for damage to goods caused by its employees' negligence. A Calder driver carelessly drops a crate of glassware belonging to Fallow Ltd, an interior design firm.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Calder can rely on the term?","o":["Yes, because the term is clear and Fallow agreed to it.","No, because negligence liability can never be excluded.","Yes, unless Fallow proves that the term fails to satisfy the requirement of reasonableness.","Yes, if Calder proves that the term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness.","No, because the Consumer Rights Act 2015 applies."],"a":3,"e":"Liability for negligence causing loss or damage other than death or personal injury can be excluded only so far as the term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness (UCTA 1977 s.2(2)). The burden of proving reasonableness is on the party relying on the clause (s.11(5)), not on Fallow. The CRA applies only to consumer contracts."},
 {"t":"exemption-clauses","q":"Granger Ltd sells a used printing press to Hythe Ltd. Its standard terms state: 'The seller gives no warranty that it has the right to sell the goods.' The press turns out to have been stolen, and the true owner reclaims it from Hythe.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Granger can rely on the term?","o":["Yes, because both parties are businesses.","Yes, if Granger shows that the term is reasonable.","No, because liability for breach of the implied term as to title cannot be excluded or restricted by any contract term.","No, because the term was not drawn to Hythe's attention.","Yes, because Hythe could have checked the ownership of the press."],"a":2,"e":"Liability for breach of the implied term that the seller has the right to sell (SGA 1979 s.12) cannot be excluded or restricted by reference to any contract term (UCTA 1977 s.6(1)). Other implied terms (description, quality, fitness, sample) can be excluded between businesses only if reasonable (s.6(1A)). So reasonableness is irrelevant here."},
 {"t":"exemption-clauses","q":"In 2023, Ivy Ltd contracted with Juniper Systems on Juniper's standard terms, which limit its liability to £10,000. At the time the limit was common in the market and Juniper's insurance covered only that amount. In 2026 a fault causes Ivy a loss of £200,000. Ivy argues that, given the size of the loss, the clause is unreasonable.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the reasonableness of the clause will be assessed?","o":["By reference to the circumstances when the contract was made, with the burden on Juniper to prove it reasonable.","By reference to the circumstances at the time of the breach, with the burden on Ivy to prove that it is unreasonable.","By reference to the size of the loss actually suffered, with the burden on Juniper.","By reference to the circumstances when the contract was made, with the burden on Ivy to prove that it is unreasonable.","By reference to whether Juniper acted honestly when the fault occurred."],"a":0,"e":"Under UCTA 1977 s.11(1) the clause must have been a fair and reasonable one to include, having regard to the circumstances which were, or ought reasonably to have been, known to or in the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made. The burden is on the party claiming the term is reasonable (s.11(5)). Hindsight about the size of the loss is not the test."},
 {"t":"exemption-clauses","q":"Fieldseed Ltd supplies cabbage seed to farmers on its standard terms, which limit its liability to replacing the seed or refunding its price. A farmer is supplied with the wrong seed, which produces a worthless crop; his loss is £60,000 and the seed cost £200. Fieldseed could have insured against such losses cheaply, the farmer could not have detected the defect, and in the past Fieldseed has often paid compensation above the limit.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the limitation is likely to be upheld?","o":["Yes, because limits of liability in standard terms agreed between businesses are presumed to be reasonable unless the buyer proves otherwise.","No, because the supplier was at fault, could have insured cheaply, often paid above the limit, and the buyer could not detect the defect.","Yes, because the limit was set out clearly in the contract.","No, because the clause is not incorporated into a contract for the sale of seed.","Yes, because the farmer is a business and should have insured himself."],"a":1,"e":"These are the same factors that led the House of Lords to hold a similar clause unreasonable in George Mitchell v Finney Lock Seeds: the availability of insurance to the supplier, the buyer's inability to detect the defect, the supplier's fault, and its practice of settling claims above the limit. Clarity goes to incorporation and construction, not reasonableness."},
 {"t":"exemption-clauses","q":"Nell, a consumer, buys a new television from Brightvision Ltd. Brightvision's standard terms state: 'All goods are sold as seen. Brightvision is not liable if goods are not of satisfactory quality.' The television develops a fault after a week.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the term?","o":["It is binding if it is fair under the Consumer Rights Act 2015.","It is not binding, because it was not individually negotiated.","It is binding, because Nell agreed to the standard terms.","It is binding only if Brightvision proves that the term satisfies the requirement of reasonableness in the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977.","It is not binding on Nell, because a trader cannot exclude liability for the statutory right that goods are of satisfactory quality."],"a":4,"e":"A term in a consumer contract that excludes or restricts the trader's liability for the statutory rights in ss.9-17 (including satisfactory quality) is blacklisted: it is not binding on the consumer (CRA 2015 s.31). The fairness test is irrelevant to blacklisted terms, and UCTA does not apply to consumer contracts."},
 {"t":"exemption-clauses","q":"Imani and Joe, a consumer couple, book a wedding venue 14 months ahead and pay a £2,000 deposit. The standard terms say that if they cancel at any time they must pay the full hire charge of £15,000. They cancel 12 months before the date, and the venue easily rebooks the date for the same price.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely status of the cancellation term?","o":["It is binding, because the hire charge is the price, which is a core term that cannot be assessed for fairness if it is transparent.","It is binding, because the couple agreed to it when they booked.","It is void under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977.","It is likely to be unfair and not binding, because it requires a consumer who cancels to pay a disproportionately high sum.","It is binding unless it is a penalty."],"a":3,"e":"A term is unfair if, contrary to good faith, it causes a significant imbalance in the parties' rights and obligations to the consumer's detriment (CRA 2015 s.62); requiring a consumer who cancels to pay a disproportionately high sum is on the grey list (Sch 2 para 5). The core terms exemption (s.64) covers the price for the service, not a sum payable on cancellation. UCTA does not apply to consumer contracts."},
 {"t":"exemption-clauses","q":"Kofi, a consumer, buys a broadband package. A term in the trader's standard contract states that the trader may 'adjust charges in line with costs'. It is unclear whether this permits increases only in line with inflation or any increase at all. The trader raises the monthly charge by 25%.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will interpret the term?","o":["It will adopt the interpretation most favourable to Kofi.","It will adopt the interpretation most favourable to the trader, because the trader drafted it.","It will strike the term out entirely as uncertain.","It will adopt whichever interpretation is commercially sensible for the trader.","It will refer the term to the Competition and Markets Authority."],"a":0,"e":"If a term in a consumer contract could have different meanings, the meaning most favourable to the consumer prevails (CRA 2015 s.69). That is a statutory version of the contra proferentem rule. The term may also be assessed for fairness, but the court does not automatically strike it out for ambiguity."},
 {"t":"exemption-clauses","q":"Lambert Ltd buys a warehouse unit from Moorside plc after Moorside's director tells it that there are no disputes with neighbours. That is untrue. The contract contains a clause: 'The seller shall not be liable for any misrepresentation made before this contract.' Lambert sues for misrepresentation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the clause?","o":["It is effective, because both parties are businesses.","It is void, because liability for misrepresentation can never be excluded.","It is effective only if Moorside shows that it satisfies the requirement of reasonableness.","It is effective unless Lambert proves that it is unreasonable.","It is effective, because the contract was for land, which is outside the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977."],"a":2,"e":"A term excluding or restricting liability for misrepresentation is of no effect unless it satisfies the reasonableness test in UCTA s.11, and the burden is on the party relying on it (Misrepresentation Act 1967 s.3). The land exception in UCTA Sch 1 does not apply to s.3 of the 1967 Act. An exclusion could never protect against the seller's own fraud."},
 {"t":"misrepresentation","q":"Farrell, a farmer who has never kept sheep on his land, tells Gina, a prospective buyer, 'In my opinion this land could carry 2,000 sheep.' Gina knows that Farrell has never used it for sheep. She buys the land, but it can carry only 800 sheep.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Gina has a claim for misrepresentation?","o":["Yes, because Farrell's statement was untrue and induced the contract.","Yes, because a seller is always taken to know the qualities of his own land, so his opinion implies facts that justify it.","No, because Gina should have obtained a survey.","No, because a statement of opinion honestly held by someone without special knowledge is not a statement of fact.","Yes, but only for negligent misstatement in tort."],"a":3,"e":"An honestly held opinion is not a statement of fact, especially where both parties know that the maker has no special knowledge to support it (Bisset v Wilkinson). The position would be different if the maker had superior knowledge or did not honestly hold the opinion. Failure to obtain a survey is not the reason for the outcome."},
 {"t":"misrepresentation","q":"Harrow Estates is selling a shop let to a tenant. When asked about the tenant, Harrow's director describes him as 'a most desirable tenant'. In fact the tenant has been in arrears of rent for months, which the director knows. The buyer relies on the description and buys the shop. The tenant soon becomes insolvent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the director's statement?","o":["It is a statement of opinion, so it cannot be a misrepresentation.","It is mere sales puff.","It is not actionable, because the buyer could have checked the rent account before buying and so was not entitled to rely on it.","It is a misrepresentation only if put in writing.","It is actionable, because an opinion given by someone with superior knowledge implies that he knows facts that justify it."],"a":4,"e":"An opinion by someone who is in a better position to know the facts carries an implied statement that he knows facts that justify it; if he does not, it is a misrepresentation of fact (Smith v Land and House Property Corp). That the buyer could have discovered the truth is not a defence (Redgrave v Hurd)."},
 {"t":"misrepresentation","q":"The directors of Tidewell Ltd persuade an investor to lend the company £200,000 by telling him the money will be used to buy new equipment and expand the business. In truth the directors always intended to use the money to pay off pressing debts, which they do.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the directors' statement?","o":["It is not a misrepresentation, because it was a statement about future intentions.","It is a misrepresentation of fact, because the directors misstated their present intention.","It is not a misrepresentation, because the investor would have lent the money anyway.","It is not a misrepresentation, because the money was used for the company's benefit.","It is a breach of contract only."],"a":1,"e":"A statement of intention is not normally a statement of fact, but if the maker does not actually hold that intention when making it, he misrepresents a present fact, namely the state of his mind (Edgington v Fitzmaurice). There are no facts suggesting the investor would have lent anyway."},
 {"t":"misrepresentation","q":"Selling a block of offices, Uplands Ltd tells a buyer: 'All six suites are currently let.' That is true, but Uplands knows that four of the tenants have given notice to leave at the end of the month. The buyer completes the purchase and four suites fall vacant.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is no misrepresentation, because the statement was literally true.","There is no misrepresentation, because silence is not a misrepresentation.","There is a misrepresentation only if the contract expressly mentioned the tenancies.","There is a misrepresentation, because a literally true statement that is misleading because of what is left out is a half-truth.","There is no misrepresentation, because the buyer should have asked about the tenants' notices, and silence is not a misrepresentation."],"a":3,"e":"Silence is generally not a misrepresentation, but a statement that is literally true yet misleading because of what it leaves out (a half-truth) can be one (Dimmock v Hallett; Nottingham Patent Brick v Butler). By saying the suites were let while concealing the notices, Uplands gave a misleading picture."},
 {"t":"misrepresentation","q":"Vane Holdings tells Westbury Ltd that its mine produces 5,000 tonnes of ore a year. Westbury does not rely on that figure but engages its own engineers, who investigate and report that the figure is accurate. Westbury buys the mine, relying on its engineers' report. The output is in fact far lower.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Westbury's position against Vane?","o":["It has a claim, because Vane's statement was false.","It has a claim for innocent misrepresentation only.","It has no claim for misrepresentation, because it relied on its own engineers' report rather than Vane's statement.","It has no claim, because statements about mine output are opinions.","It has a claim, because a representee need not show inducement."],"a":2,"e":"A misrepresentation must induce the contract. Where the claimant relies on its own investigation rather than on the statement, there is no inducement (Attwood v Small). Contrast Redgrave v Hurd, where the claimant had the opportunity to check but did not, and still relied on the statement."},
 {"t":"misrepresentation","q":"Ana buys a share in Clive's dental practice after Clive tells her that it brings in £120,000 a year. Clive offers her the accounts so she can check the figure, but she does not look at them. The accounts would have shown that the true figure is £60,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ana was induced by the misrepresentation?","o":["No, because she could easily have discovered the truth by reading the accounts that Clive offered her before she bought.","Yes, because having the means of discovering the truth does not stop a representee relying on a false statement.","Yes, but her damages will be reduced to nil for contributory negligence.","No, because she was careless in not checking.","Yes, but only if Clive's statement was fraudulent."],"a":1,"e":"Having the opportunity to discover the truth does not stop a representee relying on a false statement (Redgrave v Hurd). Ana relied on what Clive said. Contributory negligence is not available at all where the misrepresentation is fraudulent and, even where it can apply to a negligent misrepresentation, a failure to check would not reduce her damages to nil."},
 {"t":"misrepresentation","q":"Doran sells a hotel business to Eve, telling her that it has a turnover of £500,000 a year, which he knows is false. Eve buys the hotel. As well as the overpayment, she suffers losses from having to take out an expensive loan to keep the hotel going, which neither party could reasonably have foreseen.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the damages Eve can recover in the tort of deceit?","o":["All losses flowing directly from the fraudulent misrepresentation, whether or not they were foreseeable.","Only losses that were reasonably foreseeable at the time of the misrepresentation.","Only the difference between the price paid and the true value of the business.","Her expected profits, as though the statement had been true.","Only losses that were within the contemplation of both parties when the contract was made."],"a":0,"e":"In fraud, the claimant recovers all losses directly flowing from the misrepresentation, even if unforeseeable (Doyle v Olby; Smith New Court v Scrimgeour Vickers). The aim is to put her in the position she would have been in had the statement not been made, not the expectation measure used for breach of contract."},
 {"t":"misrepresentation","q":"Gregor sells a painting to Hayley, telling her it is by a particular 19th-century artist. He honestly believes this, with good reason, because a reputable gallery sold it to him with a certificate. It is later shown to be a copy. Hayley wants to rescind the contract a month later. The court considers that rescission would be harsh on Gregor and that the loss is small.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's powers?","o":["It can declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission.","It must award Hayley damages under the Misrepresentation Act 1967, because Gregor's statement was untrue.","It cannot award any remedy, because Gregor was not at fault.","It must order rescission, because Hayley acted promptly.","It can award damages for fraud."],"a":0,"e":"Where a misrepresentation is innocent (the maker had reasonable grounds for belief), the court has a discretion to declare the contract subsisting and award damages in lieu of rescission if it would be equitable to do so (s.2(2)). A claim under s.2(1) fails because Gregor can prove he had reasonable grounds to believe his statement."},
 {"t":"misrepresentation","q":"Ivan buys a van from Jade's Vans after being told that it has a new gearbox. Two weeks later his mechanic tells him that the gearbox is the original one. Ivan carries on using the van for his business for another three months, then says he wants to rescind.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ivan's position?","o":["He can rescind, because rescission is always available for misrepresentation, whatever the representee has done since the contract.","He cannot rescind, because the misrepresentation must have been fraudulent.","He cannot rescind or claim damages, because he has affirmed the contract.","He can rescind, but must pay for his use of the van.","He cannot rescind, because he affirmed the contract by using the van after learning the truth, but he may still claim damages."],"a":4,"e":"Affirmation is a bar to rescission: a representee who, knowing the truth, acts in a way that shows an intention to continue with the contract loses the right to rescind. Affirmation does not bar a claim for damages. Rescission is available for all types of misrepresentation, subject to the bars."},
 {"t":"misrepresentation","q":"Keiko buys a drawing from Lewin Gallery, which honestly and reasonably tells her it is by a named Victorian artist. Five years later, when she tries to sell it, she discovers that it is by someone else. She asks to rescind the contract and get her money back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Keiko can rescind?","o":["Yes, because time runs only from when she discovered the truth, and she asked to rescind as soon as she found out the drawing's true artist.","Yes, because rescission is never barred for innocent misrepresentation.","No, because for a non-fraudulent misrepresentation the right to rescind is lost after a reasonable time, which may run from the contract.","No, because an innocent misrepresentation never gives a right to rescind.","Yes, provided she returns the drawing in the same condition."],"a":2,"e":"Lapse of time is a bar to rescission. For a non-fraudulent misrepresentation, a reasonable time can run from the date of the contract rather than from discovery (Leaf v International Galleries, where five years was too long). For fraud, time runs from discovery. Innocent misrepresentation does give a right to rescind, subject to the bars."},
 {"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","q":"At an antiques fair, Nadine buys a painting from Owen for £20,000. Both believe it is by a well-known 18th-century artist. It turns out to be a good copy worth £1,000. Neither party made any statement about its authorship; the price reflected their shared belief.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the contract is void for common mistake?","o":["It is void, because both parties shared the same fundamental mistake about who painted it, and the price was based entirely on that belief.","It is not void, because a shared mistake as to quality does not void a contract unless it makes the subject matter essentially different.","It is void, because the painting is worth only 5% of the price.","It is voidable at Nadine's option.","It is not void, because common mistake applies only where the goods have ceased to exist."],"a":1,"e":"A common mistake as to quality makes a contract void only if it makes the subject matter essentially and radically different from what the parties believed (Bell v Lever Bros; Great Peace Shipping). The Court of Appeal in Leaf v International Galleries indicated that a shared mistake about a painting's artist would not meet that test. The doctrine is not limited to goods that have ceased to exist."},
 {"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","q":"Patrice gives Quentin £50,000 so that Quentin can place bets on a company's shares using inside information Quentin expects to receive, which would be the criminal offence of insider dealing. The inside information never arrives and no bets are placed. Quentin refuses to return the money, arguing that the agreement was illegal.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Patrice can recover the £50,000?","o":["No, because the court will never help a party to recover money that was paid under an agreement to commit a criminal offence.","Yes, because under the range of factors approach, ordering repayment would not harm the integrity of the legal system.","Yes, but only if Patrice withdrew from the scheme before Quentin acted.","No, because Patrice knew the scheme was illegal.","Yes, because insider dealing is not illegal if it is never carried out."],"a":1,"e":"The Supreme Court in Patel v Mirza replaced the old reliance test with a 'range of factors' approach, considering the purpose of the prohibition, other relevant public policies and proportionality. On almost identical facts it held the claimant could recover the money in unjust enrichment. The court does not automatically refuse help to a party who knew of the illegality."},
 {"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","q":"Ruth agrees to buy 'the cargo of grain on the ship Northern Star arriving from Riga' from Seth. There are two ships of that name sailing from Riga, one in May and one in August. Ruth means the May ship and Seth means the August ship. Nothing in their dealings indicates which ship is meant.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is a contract for the May cargo, because the buyer's understanding prevails.","There is a contract for the August cargo, because where the parties are at cross-purposes the seller's understanding of the goods prevails.","There is a contract, and the court will choose the more reasonable interpretation.","The contract is voidable at Ruth's option.","There is no binding contract, because the parties were at cross-purposes and there is no objective way of deciding which ship was agreed."],"a":4,"e":"Where the parties are at cross-purposes (mutual mistake), the court asks what a reasonable person would understand from their dealings; if the ambiguity is such that no meaning can be preferred, there is no contract (Raffles v Wichelhaus). Neither party's subjective understanding prevails."},
 {"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","q":"Una buys a quantity of oats from Victor after inspecting a sample. She assumes they are old oats, which her horses need, but Victor has said nothing about their age and has not been told why she wants them. They are new oats, and Una refuses to pay.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The contract is binding, because Una was mistaken only about the quality of the oats and Victor made no promise that they were old.","The contract is void, because Una was mistaken about a fundamental quality of the oats that made them completely useless for her horses.","The contract is void, because Victor knew that Una was mistaken.","The contract is voidable for misrepresentation.","The contract is binding only if Una's mistake was unreasonable."],"a":0,"e":"The court looks at the agreement objectively. A buyer's unilateral mistake about the quality of goods, which the seller has not induced or promised, does not affect the contract (Smith v Hughes). The position would differ if Una had believed Victor was promising the oats were old and he knew it. Silence is not a misrepresentation."},
 {"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","q":"After negotiations in which the price was always discussed per piece, Wilder Ltd sends Xavier a written offer to sell 10,000 vintage tiles at £1.20 per kilogram instead of per tile. At that price the tiles would cost a third of the negotiated amount. Xavier, who is in the trade and knows that tiles are always priced per piece, immediately accepts.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is no binding contract, because Xavier must have known that the offer did not reflect Wilder's true intention.","There is a binding contract at the price per kilogram, because Wilder made the offer.","There is a contract, but the court will rectify the price.","The contract is voidable for misrepresentation by Xavier.","There is a binding contract, because a party who makes a mistake in the price in its own written offer must bear that risk."],"a":0,"e":"A unilateral mistake as to the terms of the contract prevents a contract from arising if the other party knew, or must have known, of the mistake (Hartog v Colin and Shields). Xavier cannot 'snap up' an offer he must have realised was a mistake. Silence about the mistake is not a misrepresentation."},
 {"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","q":"A rogue applies in writing to Yardley Finance for a car on hire purchase. He uses a stolen driving licence and signs the agreement in the name of Mr Zak Hussain, a real person whose credit history Yardley checks. Yardley lets him take the car. The rogue sells it to Amy, who buys in good faith.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Amy owns the car, because Yardley's contract with the rogue was only voidable for fraud and had not been rescinded when she bought it in good faith.","Amy owns the car, because she bought in good faith.","Yardley can recover the car, because its contract was with Mr Hussain, the person named in the agreement, so the rogue had no title to pass.","Yardley can recover the car only if it rescinded the contract before Amy bought.","Amy and Yardley share ownership of the car."],"a":2,"e":"Where a contract is made in writing with a specific, identified person, the contract is with the person named in the document; if the rogue is not that person, there is no contract and the rogue acquires no title to pass on (Shogun Finance v Hudson). The 'voidable contract' rule applies to face-to-face dealings, not to a written contract with a named person."},
 {"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","q":"Bernard, an educated businessman, is asked by his nephew to sign a document, which the nephew says is a routine form. Bernard, who is in a hurry, signs without reading it. It is in fact a guarantee of the nephew's business debts. The creditor sues Bernard.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Bernard can rely on non est factum ('it is not my deed')?","o":["Yes, because he did not know what he was signing.","Yes, because his nephew misled him about the nature of the document, so he never intended to sign a guarantee of his nephew's business debts at all.","Yes, because a guarantee must be explained to the guarantor.","No, because the document must be radically different from what he believed, and the plea is not open to someone who signed carelessly.","No, because the plea applies only to deeds."],"a":3,"e":"Non est factum is very narrow: the signer must show that the document was radically different from what he believed, and that he was not careless in signing (Saunders v Anglia Building Society). An able adult who simply does not bother to read is careless. Any claim based on his nephew's deceit would be against the nephew."},
 {"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","q":"Corwin agrees to sell his shares in a company to Dex. Dex had threatened to have Corwin beaten up unless he agreed. Corwin also wanted to sell for commercial reasons of his own, and the threats were only one of the reasons he agreed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Corwin's position?","o":["He cannot avoid the contract, because he would have entered into it anyway for his own commercial reasons, so the threats made no difference.","He can avoid the contract only if the threats were the main reason he agreed.","The contract is void.","He can avoid the contract, because duress to the person need only be one of the reasons for entering the contract.","He can claim damages but cannot avoid the contract."],"a":3,"e":"Duress to the person makes a contract voidable, and it is enough that the threat was a reason for entering the contract; it need not be the main or only reason (Barton v Armstrong). Economic duress requires a stricter 'but for' causation. Duress makes contracts voidable, not void."},
 {"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","q":"Ernest, aged 88, relies entirely on his live-in carer, Fay, to manage his finances and pay his bills. He transfers his house, worth £400,000, which is nearly all he owns, to Fay as a gift. He had no independent advice. His son seeks to set the gift aside.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The son must prove that Fay actually pressured or coerced Ernest into making the gift, because undue influence is never presumed.","The gift cannot be challenged, because Ernest had mental capacity.","A presumption of undue influence arises, because Ernest trusted Fay with his affairs and the gift calls for explanation.","The relationship of carer and patient automatically means the gift is void.","The gift can be set aside only if Fay was dishonest."],"a":2,"e":"Presumed undue influence arises where the claimant proves a relationship of trust and confidence in fact and a transaction calling for explanation; the burden then passes to the other party to rebut it, typically by showing independent advice (Royal Bank of Scotland v Etridge (No 2)). The result is that the gift is voidable, not void, and actual pressure need not be proved."},
 {"t":"mistake-duress-ui-illegality","q":"Grace, a hairdresser, employs Henry at her salon in a market town. His contract says that, after leaving, he will not work as a hairdresser anywhere in the United Kingdom for five years. Henry leaves and opens a salon two streets away. Grace seeks to enforce the restriction.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The restriction is enforceable, because Henry agreed to it.","The restriction is enforceable, because Grace has a legitimate interest in protecting her client connection from a former employee.","The restriction is valid, because restraints in employment contracts are presumed to be reasonable.","The court will rewrite the restriction to cover the town for one year.","The restriction is void as an unreasonable restraint of trade, because its area and duration go far beyond what is needed."],"a":4,"e":"Restraint of trade clauses are void unless they protect a legitimate interest and are reasonable in area, time and activity. A UK-wide, five-year ban for a local hairdresser is far wider than needed to protect her client connection. The court may sever words if that does not require rewriting the clause (Tillman v Egon Zehnder), but it will not rewrite a new, narrower restriction."},
 {"t":"discharge-frustration","q":"Jasper agrees to build a garden studio for Kim for a lump sum of £20,000, payable on completion. When the studio is half built, Jasper abandons the job. Kim has no choice but to have another builder finish it on the half-built base. Jasper claims payment for the work he did.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Jasper's position?","o":["He can recover £10,000, because he did half the work.","He can recover a reasonable sum for the work done, because Kim has kept the benefit of his work and used it to finish the studio.","He can recover nothing unless the contract was frustrated.","He can recover the price less the cost of completion.","He can recover nothing, because the obligation was entire and Kim had no real choice whether to accept the partial work."],"a":4,"e":"Where a contract requires complete performance before payment (an entire obligation), a party who abandons the work recovers nothing. A claim for a reasonable sum would need the other party to have freely chosen to accept the partial performance; a landowner who is left with half-built work on her land has no real choice (Sumpter v Hedges). Price less cost of completion reflects the substantial performance doctrine, which needs nearly complete performance."},
 {"t":"discharge-frustration","q":"Lyle agrees to redecorate and furnish Maya's flat for £5,000, payable on completion. He completes the work, but a wardrobe door is warped and a bookshelf needs replacing; the defects will cost £300 to put right. Maya refuses to pay anything, arguing that the work is not complete.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Lyle's position?","o":["He can recover nothing, because the obligation to complete the work was entire and it has not been completely and exactly performed.","He can recover £5,000 in full, because the defects are trivial.","He can recover only a reasonable sum for the value of his work.","He can recover £4,700, because he has substantially performed, less the cost of remedying the defects.","He can recover nothing until he has fixed the defects."],"a":3,"e":"Where a party has substantially performed an entire obligation, he can recover the price less the cost of putting right the defects (Hoenig v Isaacs). Here the work is complete with minor defects. Lyle cannot claim the full price, because Maya is entitled to set off the cost of rectification."},
 {"t":"discharge-frustration","q":"A contract for the sale of 1,000 bottles of wine requires them to be delivered to Nantwich Wines by 1 March, and it is agreed that time is of the essence. The seller delivers on 8 March. Nantwich accepts the delivery, pays for it, and sells the wine to its customers. Two weeks later it tries to terminate the contract and recover the price.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nantwich's position?","o":["It can terminate, because the seller breached a condition.","It can terminate, because a repudiatory breach of a condition automatically brings the contract to an end, whatever the buyer does next.","It can terminate, but only if it returns the wine.","It cannot terminate, because time was not of the essence.","It cannot terminate, because it affirmed the contract by accepting and reselling the goods, though it can still claim damages."],"a":4,"e":"A breach of condition gives the innocent party an election: terminate or affirm. Once it affirms with knowledge of the breach, for example by accepting and dealing with the goods, it loses the right to terminate for that breach but keeps its claim for damages. A repudiatory breach does not end the contract automatically."},
 {"t":"discharge-frustration","q":"Owl Shipping charters a ship to Pell Ltd to load cargo at a foreign port within 45 days. Before the 45 days end, Pell tells Owl that it will not supply any cargo. Owl refuses to accept this and keeps the ship waiting at the port, hoping Pell will change its mind. Before the 45 days are up, war breaks out and loading becomes illegal.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Owl's position?","o":["Owl can claim damages for Pell's anticipatory breach.","Owl can claim the full charter fee, because Pell repudiated first.","Owl has no claim, because by affirming it kept the contract alive, and the contract was then frustrated, discharging both parties.","Owl can claim damages, but only for the time its ship was kept waiting.","Owl can claim under the Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943 for the whole of the charter fee that it would otherwise have earned."],"a":2,"e":"On an anticipatory breach the innocent party can accept it and sue at once, or affirm and wait for performance. If it affirms, the contract stays alive for both parties, and it takes the risk of later events: here the contract was frustrated, so Pell was discharged before any actual breach (Avery v Bowden). The 1943 Act deals with money paid or payable and benefits conferred, not lost profits."},
 {"t":"discharge-frustration","q":"Quill, a drummer, is engaged by a band under a contract requiring him to play up to seven nights a week for a year. After two months he suffers a serious illness, and his doctors advise that he must not play more than four nights a week for the foreseeable future. The band needs a full-time drummer.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect on the contract?","o":["The contract continues, but Quill's pay must be reduced.","The contract is frustrated, because Quill's illness means he cannot perform the contract in the way it requires.","Quill is in breach, because he can no longer perform.","The contract is frustrated only if Quill dies.","The band may terminate and claim damages for Quill's breach."],"a":1,"e":"Illness or incapacity in a contract for personal services can frustrate it if it makes performance radically different from what was agreed (Condor v The Baron Knights, on almost identical facts). Frustration is not breach, so neither party is liable for failing to perform after the frustrating event."},
 {"t":"discharge-frustration","q":"Rosie hires a balcony flat overlooking a city street for two days, at a high price, for the sole purpose of watching a royal procession. The procession is cancelled because the King is unwell. Both parties knew the purpose of the hire, although the contract does not mention the procession.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The contract is not frustrated, because Rosie can still use the flat.","The contract is not frustrated, because the procession was not mentioned in the contract and Rosie can still use the flat for two days.","Rosie is in breach if she does not pay.","The contract is frustrated, because the cancellation destroyed the foundation of the contract that both parties shared.","The contract is frustrated only if the owner cannot re-let the flat."],"a":3,"e":"A contract can be frustrated where the common purpose that formed its foundation, known to both parties, is destroyed, even if performance is still physically possible (Krell v Henry). The purpose does not need to be written into the contract. Contrast Herne Bay Steamboat v Hutton, where the trip still had some purpose."},
 {"t":"discharge-frustration","q":"Seacraft Ltd owns two barges. It contracts with Trent Ltd to transport goods using 'one of its barges' in June. One barge sinks in May. Seacraft has other contracts and chooses to use the remaining barge for another customer. It tells Trent that its contract is frustrated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The contract is not frustrated, because Seacraft's own choice to use its remaining barge for another customer makes it self-induced.","The contract is frustrated, because the sinking was not Seacraft's fault.","The contract is frustrated, because after the sinking Seacraft had no choice but to decide which of its customers would receive the remaining barge.","The contract is frustrated if Seacraft offers Trent a refund.","The contract is not frustrated, because frustration never applies to transport contracts."],"a":0,"e":"Frustration cannot be relied on where the failure to perform results from the party's own choice; the event is then self-induced (The Super Servant Two; Maritime National Fish v Ocean Trawlers). Seacraft could have performed with its remaining barge but chose to use it for someone else, so it is in breach."},
 {"t":"discharge-frustration","q":"Ullswater Ltd contracts to supply components to Vantage plc. The contract contains a clause providing that, if a strike at Ullswater's factory delays supply by more than 60 days, either party may terminate on notice with no liability. A strike at the factory delays supply by 90 days. Ullswater claims the contract is frustrated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The contract is frustrated, because a strike is outside the parties' control.","The contract is not frustrated, because the parties expressly provided for this event, so the clause governs the consequences.","Ullswater is in breach, because a strike can never frustrate a contract.","The contract is frustrated, because the delay exceeded 60 days.","The contract is frustrated only if Vantage agrees."],"a":1,"e":"Frustration does not apply where the contract itself makes provision for the event that has occurred; the parties have allocated the risk and the clause governs. Here either party may terminate under the clause. It is wrong to say a strike can never frustrate a contract: strikes can be frustrating events in an appropriate case."},
 {"t":"discharge-frustration","q":"Westmore Theatres hires a concert hall to Xander Promotions for four concerts. A week before the first concert, without any fault of either party, the hall is destroyed by fire.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect on the contract?","o":["Xander can choose whether to treat the contract as ended.","Westmore is in breach for failing to provide the hall.","The contract is frustrated, so both parties are automatically discharged from their future obligations from the time of the fire.","The contract is void from the start, because the hall no longer exists, so all money paid under it must be returned with interest.","The contract is suspended until the hall is rebuilt."],"a":2,"e":"Destruction of the specific thing essential to performance frustrates the contract (Taylor v Caldwell). Frustration ends the contract automatically from that moment; it does not depend on either party's election and does not make the contract void from the start. Money paid before the event is dealt with under the Law Reform (Frustrated Contracts) Act 1943."},
 {"t":"discharge-frustration","q":"Yewtree Ltd contracts to supply Zenith Ltd with 12 monthly deliveries of timber. Before any deliveries or payments have been made, both companies decide that the arrangement no longer suits them and agree in writing that the contract will end with no liability on either side.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the agreement?","o":["It is binding, because where neither party has performed, each party's release of the other is consideration for its own release.","It is binding only if made by deed.","It is not binding, because Zenith gave no consideration for Yewtree's release.","It is not binding, because a contract can be brought to an end only by performance, breach or frustration, not by later agreement.","It is binding only if one party pays compensation."],"a":0,"e":"A contract can be discharged by agreement. Where both parties still have obligations to perform, each gives consideration by releasing the other (bilateral discharge), so no deed or payment is required. A deed or fresh consideration ('accord and satisfaction') is needed only where one party has already fully performed."},
 {"t":"damages","q":"Archer Ltd contracts to buy 500 tonnes of steel from Brook Metals at £700 a tonne, for delivery on 1 May. Brook fails to deliver. On 1 May the market price is £820 a tonne, and Archer buys substitute steel elsewhere at that price.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the damages Archer can recover?","o":["£350,000, the contract price.","£410,000, the cost of the substitute steel.","£60,000 plus Archer's lost profit on reselling the steel.","Nominal damages only, because Archer obtained the steel elsewhere.","£60,000, the difference between the contract price and the market price on the delivery date."],"a":4,"e":"The aim of contract damages is to put the claimant in the position it would have been in had the contract been performed (expectation). Where there is an available market for goods not delivered, the measure is prima facie the difference between the contract price and the market price at the time they should have been delivered (SGA 1979 s.51(3)). Here that is £120 x 500 = £60,000. Archer has suffered a real loss, so damages are not nominal."},
 {"t":"damages","q":"Dena contracts with Eastside Pools to build a swimming pool 2.3 metres deep at its deepest point. The finished pool is only 2.0 metres deep. It is perfectly safe and usable, and the value of Dena's house is unaffected. Rebuilding to the agreed depth would cost £25,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Dena's likely damages?","o":["£25,000, because she is entitled to the cost of obtaining what she contracted for, whatever the effect on the value of her house.","Nothing, because the value of her house is unaffected.","A modest sum for her loss of amenity, because rebuilding would be out of all proportion to the benefit gained.","Nominal damages only.","An order for specific performance requiring Eastside to rebuild."],"a":2,"e":"Cost of cure is not awarded where it would be wholly disproportionate to the benefit of curing the defect; but the claimant may recover a modest sum for loss of amenity or pleasure (Ruxley Electronics v Forsyth, on very similar facts). Damages are not limited to diminution in value or nominal damages, and specific performance of building work is rarely ordered."},
 {"t":"damages","q":"Falcon Films hires Gil, a well-known actor, to star in a film. Before signing, Falcon spends £40,000 on scripts and location scouting. After signing, it spends another £30,000. Gil then pulls out, and the film is abandoned. Falcon cannot prove what profit, if any, the film would have made.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Falcon can recover?","o":["Nothing, because it cannot prove its lost profits.","Its wasted expenditure of £70,000, including pre-contract spending that Gil would have contemplated being wasted.","£30,000 only, because spending before the contract cannot be recovered.","Its expected profits, assessed by the court.","£40,000 only, because the £30,000 spent after signing was a failure to mitigate once Gil's commitment became doubtful."],"a":1,"e":"Where expectation loss is too speculative to prove, the claimant can claim reliance loss (wasted expenditure). This can include expenditure before the contract if it was within the defendant's contemplation as likely to be wasted if he broke the contract (Anglia Television v Reed). Falcon cannot recover both wasted expenditure and lost profits on the same item."},
 {"t":"damages","q":"Hari books a two-week luxury holiday for £4,000. The brochure promises a hotel with a private beach, spa and fine dining. In fact the hotel is half-built, the beach is a building site and the food is poor. Hari's holiday is ruined.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the damages Hari can recover?","o":["Only the difference in value between the holiday promised and the holiday received.","Damages for the difference in value and for his disappointment, because the object of the contract was to provide pleasure.","Nothing for distress, because damages for mental distress or disappointment are never recoverable for a breach of contract of any kind.","A refund of the price only.","Damages for distress only if Hari suffered a recognised psychiatric illness."],"a":1,"e":"Damages for distress and disappointment are generally not recoverable in contract (Addis v Gramophone), but an exception applies where a major or important object of the contract is to provide pleasure, relaxation or peace of mind, such as a holiday (Jarvis v Swans Tours). A recognised psychiatric illness is a requirement in tort for some claims, not here."},
 {"t":"damages","q":"Ingram Ltd, a small manufacturing company, contracts with Juno Logistics for the delivery of raw materials. Juno delivers late, and Ingram loses an order worth £15,000 in profit. Ingram's managing director suffers anxiety and sleepless nights as a result. Ingram claims its lost profit, and the director claims damages for his distress.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["Both the lost profit and damages for distress are recoverable.","Neither is recoverable, because the loss of the order was too remote.","The director can recover for his distress, because it was a foreseeable result of the breach, as well as the company's lost profit.","The lost profit may be recoverable if not too remote, but the director cannot recover for his distress.","Only the distress is recoverable, because lost profits are speculative."],"a":3,"e":"In an ordinary commercial contract, damages for mental distress are not recoverable (Addis v Gramophone; Hayes v Dodd). In any event, the director is not a party to the contract. The lost profit is recoverable if it passes the remoteness test in Hadley v Baxendale."},
 {"t":"damages","q":"Kestrel Laundry buys a large industrial boiler from Lomas Engineering for delivery on 1 June. Lomas knows that Kestrel is a laundry and needs the boiler immediately to run its business. It does not know that Kestrel has just won an exceptionally profitable government contract. The boiler is delivered five months late.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kestrel's damages for loss of profit?","o":["It can recover its ordinary lost profits, but not the exceptional profits on the government contract.","It can recover nothing for lost profits, because Lomas did not know about the government contract.","It can recover only the price of the boiler.","It can recover the profits from the government contract, but not its ordinary profits.","It can recover all lost profits, including on the government contract, because loss of profit was foreseeable."],"a":0,"e":"Under Hadley v Baxendale, losses are recoverable if they arise naturally from the breach (limb 1) or were within the reasonable contemplation of both parties when contracting because of special knowledge (limb 2). Lomas knew the boiler was needed for immediate use, so ordinary profits are recoverable, but not the exceptional profits it knew nothing about (Victoria Laundry v Newman Industries)."},
 {"t":"damages","q":"Mill Farm buys a feed hopper from Nolan Ltd. Both parties contemplate that, if the hopper is defective, there is a serious possibility that the pigs fed from it may become ill. The hopper's ventilator is left closed in breach of contract, the feed goes mouldy, and the pigs contract a rare disease from which 250 of them die. The extent of the harm was not foreseeable.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the loss of the pigs is recoverable?","o":["Yes, because the type of loss, illness of the pigs, was within the parties' contemplation, even though its extent was not.","No, because the death of 250 pigs was not within the contemplation of the parties.","No, because the particular disease was not contemplated.","Yes, because all losses caused by a breach are recoverable.","Yes, but only up to the price of the hopper."],"a":0,"e":"For remoteness, it is the type of loss that must be within the parties' reasonable contemplation, not its precise manner or extent (Parsons v Uttley Ingham). Illness of the pigs from defective feed was contemplated, so the full loss is recoverable. Not all losses caused by breach are recoverable, so it overstates the law to say they are."},
 {"t":"damages","q":"Orchard Power buys turbines from Prime Engineering. The turbines delivered are defective, and Orchard reasonably replaces them with new turbines from another supplier. The new turbines are much more efficient and save Orchard more in running costs than the losses caused by the defective ones.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the savings affect Orchard's damages?","o":["They do not affect damages, because the savings came from Orchard's own decision.","They increase damages, because Orchard had to buy new turbines.","They do not affect damages, because a claimant whose goods are defective is entitled to the full cost of a replacement, whatever its benefits.","They reduce damages only if Prime proves that Orchard acted unreasonably.","They reduce Orchard's damages, because a benefit arising from reasonable steps taken in mitigation must be taken into account."],"a":4,"e":"Where the claimant's reasonable steps to mitigate its loss actually reduce or eliminate that loss, the benefit is taken into account in assessing damages (British Westinghouse v Underground Electric Railways, on these facts). Unreasonableness is relevant to a failure to mitigate, which is not the issue here."},
 {"t":"damages","q":"Quayside Builders agrees to build a hotel for Royal Hospitality, with a clause requiring Quayside to pay £5,000 for each week of delay. When the contract was made, Royal expected to lose about £4,000 a week if the hotel opened late, and it also had a legitimate interest in opening on time for the tourist season. The hotel opens four weeks late, and Royal's actual loss is £3,000 a week.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the clause is enforceable?","o":["It is unenforceable, because £5,000 a week is more than Royal's actual loss of £3,000 a week, so it cannot be a genuine pre-estimate.","It is unenforceable, because it was intended to deter delay.","It is enforceable, because the sum is not out of all proportion to Royal's legitimate interest in timely completion.","It is enforceable only up to Royal's actual loss of £12,000.","It is unenforceable, because damages clauses in construction contracts are penalties."],"a":2,"e":"Under the modern test (Cavendish Square Holding v Makdessi; ParkingEye v Beavis), a clause triggered by breach is a penalty only if it imposes a detriment out of all proportion to the innocent party's legitimate interest in performance. A sum moderately above the expected loss, protecting a real commercial interest, is enforceable. That the sum exceeds the actual loss, or has some deterrent effect, does not make it a penalty."},
 {"t":"damages","q":"Silva hires plant machinery from Tower Hire for 12 months. The contract allows Silva to end the hire early at any time on payment of a fee equal to three months' hire charges. After five months, Silva ends the hire under that clause and argues that the fee is a penalty because it exceeds Tower's loss.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The fee is a penalty, because it exceeds Tower's loss.","The fee is a penalty, because it was intended to discourage early termination.","The fee is enforceable only up to Tower's actual loss.","The penalty rule does not apply, because the fee is payable on Silva's exercise of a contractual right, not on a breach.","The fee is unenforceable unless Tower can show that it was a genuine pre-estimate of the loss it would suffer from early termination."],"a":3,"e":"The penalty rule applies only to sums payable on breach of contract. A payment due on the exercise of a contractual option, such as a right to terminate early, is a primary obligation and is outside the rule (Makdessi). Silva was not in breach, so the fee is payable."},
 {"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","q":"Una contracts to buy a new car of a popular model from Victory Motors for £28,000. Before delivery, Victory tells her that it has sold that car to another customer at a higher price. Identical cars are readily available from other dealers at £29,500. Una seeks specific performance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether specific performance is likely to be ordered?","o":["Yes, because Una has a contract for a specific car.","No, because damages of £1,500 would be an adequate remedy, as identical cars are readily available.","Yes, because specific performance is available as of right for breach of a contract for the sale of goods.","No, because specific performance is never available for goods.","Yes, provided Una pays the price into court."],"a":1,"e":"Specific performance is discretionary and is not ordered where damages are an adequate remedy. For ordinary goods readily available on the market, damages (here the extra cost of a substitute) are adequate. It may be ordered for unique or unavailable goods, so it is wrong to say it is never available for goods."},
 {"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","q":"Westfield Shopping Centre leases its largest unit to Xtra Supermarkets for 35 years, with a covenant by Xtra to keep the supermarket open for retail trade during normal hours. Xtra's store loses money and it closes it, though it continues to pay the rent. Westfield seeks an order requiring Xtra to keep trading.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["An order will be granted, because damages are hard to assess.","An order will be granted, because Xtra expressly agreed to keep the store open, and damages for the closure of an anchor store are hard to assess.","No order will be granted, because the court will not normally order a party to carry on a business; Westfield is left to damages.","No order will be granted, because the covenant is void.","An order will be granted, but only for the first year."],"a":2,"e":"The courts will not usually order specific performance of an obligation to run a business, because it would require constant supervision and could cause the defendant losses out of proportion to the benefit to the claimant (Co-operative Insurance v Argyll Stores). The covenant is valid and damages are available."},
 {"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","q":"Yusuf exchanges contracts to sell his house to Zara. Before completion, Yusuf suffers a serious accident and becomes disabled, and his family support network is in the area. Selling would leave him unable to buy anywhere suitable nearby. He refuses to complete. Zara seeks specific performance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's approach?","o":["It must order specific performance, because the contract is for land.","It may refuse specific performance only if Zara knew of Yusuf's accident before exchange.","It must refuse specific performance, because Yusuf's change of circumstances frustrated the contract.","It must order specific performance, because hardship arising after the contract is irrelevant.","It may refuse specific performance on the ground of severe hardship, and leave Zara to her claim for damages."],"a":4,"e":"Although damages are generally regarded as inadequate for land, specific performance is discretionary and can be refused where it would cause severe hardship to the defendant, even if the hardship arose after the contract (Patel v Ali). The contract is not frustrated: it remains possible to perform, and Zara can claim damages."},
 {"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","q":"Alba agrees to buy a plot of land from Brent for £300,000. Brent refuses to complete. Alba does nothing for two and a half years, during which land prices in the area rise sharply. She then seeks specific performance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["Specific performance will be ordered, because land is unique.","Specific performance will be ordered, because Alba has brought her claim well within the six-year limitation period for contract claims.","Specific performance will be refused, and Alba also loses her right to damages.","Specific performance may be refused because of Alba's unreasonable delay, though she may still claim damages.","Specific performance will be ordered, but at the current market price."],"a":3,"e":"Equitable remedies can be refused on the ground of delay (laches), where the claimant has delayed unreasonably and it would be unjust to grant relief. Damages at common law are available as of right within the limitation period, so Alba keeps her damages claim. The court does not vary the contract price."},
 {"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","q":"Calder Accountants employs Dion as a senior accountant on a three-year contract. After a year Dion resigns without giving the required notice and joins a rival firm. Calder seeks an order compelling Dion to return to work for it for the rest of the contract.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the court will make such an order?","o":["No, because the court will not order specific performance of a contract for personal services so as to compel someone to work.","Yes, because Dion is in breach of a fixed-term contract and Calder's damages for losing a senior accountant would be difficult to assess.","Yes, but only for the notice period.","No, because Calder has already found a replacement.","Yes, because Dion's contract was for a fixed term."],"a":0,"e":"Courts will not order specific performance of contracts of employment or for personal services, as this would compel a person to work against his will (and, for employees, statute prevents an order compelling work: Trade Union and Labour Relations (Consolidation) Act 1992 s.236). Calder is limited to damages, and possibly an injunction to enforce a valid negative covenant if one exists."},
 {"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","q":"Ewan leases a shop from Fenland Estates. The lease contains a covenant not to use the premises as a restaurant or café. Ewan opens a café there. Fenland seeks an injunction to stop the café use.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will approach Fenland's application?","o":["It will grant an injunction only after the trial of a full claim for damages.","It will refuse an injunction unless Fenland proves that damages would be inadequate.","It will refuse an injunction, because Ewan would have to close his business.","It will grant a mandatory injunction requiring Ewan to sell the café.","It will grant a prohibitory injunction almost as a matter of course to restrain breach of a clear negative covenant."],"a":4,"e":"Where a party is breaking an express negative promise, the court will ordinarily grant a prohibitory injunction to restrain the breach without needing to consider whether damages are adequate, subject to its general discretion (Doherty v Allman). A mandatory injunction requires positive action and is harder to obtain."},
 {"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","q":"Gwynedd Software claims that Halo Ltd, a former distributor, is selling its software in breach of a distribution agreement. It seeks an interim injunction to stop the sales until trial. Halo argues that the claim may fail at trial.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the test the court will apply?","o":["Whether there is a serious question to be tried and, if so, where the balance of convenience lies.","Whether Gwynedd is certain to win at trial.","Whether Gwynedd has proved on the balance of probabilities, at the interim hearing, that it will succeed at trial.","Whether Halo is acting dishonestly.","Whether Gwynedd has suffered loss already."],"a":0,"e":"Under American Cyanamid v Ethicon, the court asks whether there is a serious question to be tried, then whether damages would be an adequate remedy for either party, and then where the balance of convenience lies. The claimant does not have to prove its case at the interim stage; the claimant usually gives a cross-undertaking in damages."},
 {"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","q":"Ivy guarantees her son's business loan from Jarvis Bank. Later, without telling Ivy, the bank and her son agree to double the loan and increase the interest rate. The son's business fails, and the bank calls on Ivy's guarantee for the original amount.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ivy's position?","o":["She is liable for the original amount only, because she did not agree to the increase.","She is liable for the full increased amount, because the guarantee covers all of the son's debts to the bank, whenever they were incurred.","She is liable only if the bank proves the variation did not harm her.","She is released from the guarantee, because the bank made a material variation to the principal contract without her consent.","She is liable for half the original amount."],"a":3,"e":"A guarantor is discharged if the creditor and the principal debtor make a material variation to the principal contract without the guarantor's consent, unless the variation is plainly insubstantial or cannot prejudice the guarantor (Holme v Brunskill). Doubling the loan and raising the interest rate are clearly material. Many professionally drafted guarantees contain clauses allowing variations, but none is mentioned here."},
 {"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","q":"Keane Ltd is reluctant to supply goods on credit to Lark Ltd. Lark's director, Mona, tells Keane over the phone: 'Go ahead and supply them. I will personally make sure you are paid in full, whatever happens, even if it turns out that Lark is not liable to pay you for any reason.' Nothing is put in writing. Lark does not pay and is insolvent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Keane can enforce Mona's promise?","o":["No, because a promise to pay another's debt must be evidenced in signed writing.","Yes, because Mona's promise is an indemnity, a primary obligation of her own, which does not need to be in writing.","No, because Mona gave no consideration.","Yes, but only if Lark is first sued to judgment.","No, because a director cannot be personally liable for a company's debts."],"a":1,"e":"A guarantee (a secondary promise to pay if the debtor does not) must be evidenced in writing signed by the guarantor (Statute of Frauds 1677 s.4). An indemnity is a primary obligation to keep the creditor harmless regardless of the debtor's liability, and does not need writing. Mona's promise to pay 'whatever happens, even if Lark is not liable' is an indemnity. Keane's supply of goods was the consideration."},
 {"t":"equitable-remedies-guarantees","q":"During email negotiations, Nathan, the owner of Orbit Ltd, sends Pace Supplies an email stating: 'I confirm that I will guarantee Orbit's payments for the goods.' The email ends with his typed name, 'Nathan'. Orbit fails to pay. Nathan argues that the guarantee is unenforceable because it was not signed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The guarantee is unenforceable, because it needed a handwritten signature.","The guarantee is unenforceable, because guarantees must be made by deed.","The guarantee is enforceable, because an email ending with the guarantor's typed name can satisfy the requirement of signed writing.","The guarantee is enforceable only if Pace gave Nathan personally some separate consideration of his own, apart from supplying the goods to Orbit.","The guarantee is unenforceable, because emails are not 'writing'."],"a":2,"e":"The writing and signature requirement in s.4 Statute of Frauds 1677 can be satisfied by an email, and a typed name at the end of the email can be a signature (Golden Ocean Group v Salgaocar Mining). A deed is not required. The supply of goods to Orbit is good consideration for the guarantee."}
];

;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["tort"] = [
 {"t":"negligence","q":"Rhys is driving through a town centre when he looks at his phone and hits Marta, a pedestrian on a zebra crossing. In defending Marta's claim, Rhys's insurer argues that the court must first decide whether it is fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty of care on a driver towards a pedestrian.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will approach the question of duty?","o":["It will apply the established duty that drivers owe to other road users, without asking whether a duty is fair, just and reasonable.","It will apply the three-stage test of foreseeability, proximity and whether it is fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty in this situation.","It will impose a duty only if Rhys assumed responsibility for Marta's safety before the accident happened.","It will impose a duty only if there is a close relationship of proximity, such as a contract, between Rhys and Marta.","It will consider only whether harm to Marta was reasonably foreseeable, because that is the sole test for any duty."],"a":0,"e":"Where a case falls within an **established category**, such as a driver and other road users, the court simply applies the existing duty (Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire). The **Caparo** factors (foreseeability, proximity, fair, just and reasonable) are used only for **novel** situations. No contract or assumption of responsibility is needed, and foreseeability alone is not the general test."},
 {"t":"negligence","q":"Kevin is walking along a canal towpath when he sees Ellie, a 6-year-old girl he does not know, fall into the water. He is a strong swimmer and could easily reach her, but he walks on. Ellie is pulled out by others but suffers brain damage from lack of oxygen.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Kevin owed Ellie a duty of care?","o":["He owed a duty, because it was reasonably foreseeable that Ellie would be harmed if he did not help.","He owed a duty, because he was a strong swimmer and could have rescued her easily, quickly and without any risk to himself.","He owed a duty, because Ellie was a young child and so particularly vulnerable to harm.","He owed no duty, because there is no general duty to act to prevent harm to a stranger that one has not caused.","He owed a duty, but only to call the emergency services, not to enter the water himself."],"a":3,"e":"English law imposes **no general duty to act** or to rescue: a mere **omission** (failing to make things better) does not give rise to a duty unless an exception applies, such as an assumption of responsibility, control over the claimant or the danger, or creating the danger. Kevin had no relationship with Ellie and did not create the danger. Foreseeability, ease of rescue and her vulnerability do not create a duty on their own."},
 {"t":"negligence","q":"Dr Shah calls an ambulance for her patient, Mrs Ortiz, who is having a severe asthma attack at home. The ambulance service accepts the call and gives Mrs Ortiz's name and address to a crew. For no good reason, the ambulance takes 45 minutes to cover a short distance. Mrs Ortiz suffers a respiratory arrest that a prompt arrival would have prevented.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the ambulance service's position?","o":["It owed no duty, because emergency services are immune from negligence claims for operational decisions about allocating ambulances.","It owed no duty, because a failure to arrive in time is an omission, and there is never a duty to act.","It owed Mrs Ortiz a duty of care, because by accepting the call for a named patient it assumed responsibility for her.","It owed no duty, because Dr Shah, not Mrs Ortiz, made the call to the ambulance service.","It owed a duty only if it had a statutory obligation to reach the patient within a set time."],"a":2,"e":"Although there is usually no duty to confer a benefit, an ambulance service that **accepts a 999 call** for a named individual assumes responsibility for her, so it owes a duty to arrive within a reasonable time (Kent v Griffiths). There is no blanket immunity for emergency services. It does not matter who made the call, and no statutory target is required."},
 {"t":"negligence","q":"Leanne calls 999 and tells the police that her ex-partner has just threatened to come round and kill her. Because of a careless error in the call centre, the call is graded as non-urgent and officers do not attend for an hour. In the meantime, her ex-partner attacks and seriously injures her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the police owed Leanne a duty of care?","o":["They owed a duty, because it was reasonably foreseeable that Leanne would be attacked if they did not attend.","They owed a duty, because the police are always liable in negligence for careless handling of emergency calls from members of the public.","They owed no duty, because the police are completely immune from claims in negligence.","They owed a duty, because their failure to attend promptly was a positive act that made things worse.","They probably owed no duty, because the police generally owe no duty to protect people from harm caused by third parties."],"a":4,"e":"The police generally owe **no duty to protect** individuals from harm caused by **third parties**: failing to prevent the attack is an omission (Michael v Chief Constable of South Wales). That is not the same as **immunity**: the police can be liable for harm caused by their own **positive acts**, such as knocking over a bystander during an arrest (Robinson). A delayed response does not turn an omission into a positive act."},
 {"t":"negligence","q":"Paolo negligently leaves a gas cylinder leaking in a block of flats. A fire starts. Bethany, a neighbour, runs in to bring out a trapped child and suffers serious burns. Paolo argues he owed Bethany no duty because she chose to put herself in danger.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Paolo's position?","o":["He owed no duty, because Bethany voluntarily accepted the risk of injury when she decided for herself to enter the burning building.","He owed no duty, because Bethany's decision to enter the building broke the chain of causation.","He owed Bethany a duty, because it was foreseeable that someone might try to rescue people endangered by his negligence.","He owed a duty only if Bethany was a trained rescuer, such as a firefighter or paramedic.","He owed a duty, but Bethany's damages will be halved because she chose to run into the fire."],"a":2,"e":"A person whose negligence creates danger owes a duty to a **rescuer**, because a rescue attempt is a foreseeable response to danger. The courts do not treat a reasonable rescue as consent (volenti) or as an intervening act breaking the chain of causation, and rescuers are rarely found contributorily negligent. Professional rescuers are owed a duty too, but a duty is not limited to them."},
 {"t":"negligence","q":"Toby, aged 12, and his friend are having a play-fight with plastic rulers in a school classroom. One ruler snaps and a fragment hits his friend in the eye, causing serious injury. Such play-fights are common among children of that age, and nothing suggested that a ruler might snap.\n\nWhat standard of care will the court apply to Toby?","o":["That of a reasonable adult, so he is in breach because an adult would have foreseen the risk.","That of a reasonable 12-year-old child, so he is probably not in breach of duty.","No standard at all, because a child under the age of 14 cannot be liable in negligence.","That of a reasonable 12-year-old with Toby's particular personality and experience.","That of a reasonable adult, because the standard of care is objective for everyone."],"a":1,"e":"A child is judged by the standard of a **reasonable child of the same age**, not an adult (Mullin v Richards, where two 15-year-old girls fencing with rulers were not liable). The standard is still **objective**: it is not tailored to Toby's own personality or experience. There is no minimum age for liability in negligence."},
 {"t":"negligence","q":"Jonas, a keen amateur, fits a new door handle in his house. He does the job carefully and in the way a reasonably competent amateur would, but the handle comes off when his visitor, Clare, pulls it, and she falls down the stairs. A professional joiner would have used longer screws and the handle would not have come off.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Jonas was in breach of duty?","o":["He was in breach, because anyone carrying out work on a building must meet the standard of a professional joiner doing the same job.","He was probably not in breach, because for a simple task like this he is judged by the standard of a reasonably competent amateur.","He was in breach, because the handle came off and so the work was not done to a safe standard.","He was not in breach, because an amateur doing work in their own home owes visitors no duty of care.","He was in breach, because he should have hired a professional joiner to do any work on his house."],"a":1,"e":"A person doing a **simple household task** that an amateur might reasonably undertake is judged by the standard of a **reasonably competent amateur** doing that task (Wells v Cooper). For more complex or specialist work the professional standard might apply, or it might be unreasonable to attempt it at all. The fact that the handle failed does not by itself show breach, and occupiers do owe visitors a duty."},
 {"t":"negligence","q":"Derek, a delivery driver, starts to feel dizzy and his vision blurs while driving. He thinks it will pass and carries on. Ten minutes later he loses consciousness at the wheel and his van hits a cyclist, Asha. Medical evidence shows he had a condition that caused gradual loss of consciousness.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Derek was in breach of duty?","o":["He was in breach, because he continued to drive after he became aware of symptoms that affected his ability to drive.","He was not in breach, because his loss of consciousness was caused by a medical condition beyond his control, which he had not been diagnosed with.","He was not in breach, because the standard of care is adjusted to take account of a driver's illness.","He was in breach only if he had previously been diagnosed with the medical condition.","He was not in breach, because a driver who loses consciousness cannot be judged by any standard at all."],"a":0,"e":"A driver who is struck by a **sudden, unforeseeable** incapacity, with no warning, is not in breach. But a driver who **continues to drive** after becoming aware of symptoms that impair their driving falls below the standard of the reasonable driver (Roberts v Ramsbottom). The standard is not lowered for illness, and no earlier diagnosis is needed."},
 {"t":"negligence","q":"After an exceptional storm, a factory floor is flooded and becomes slippery with oil. The owner, Ferris Ltd, spreads all the sawdust it has on the floor, but some areas remain slippery. The only way to remove the risk completely would be to close the factory. An employee, Gail, slips on an untreated area and is injured.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ferris Ltd was in breach of duty?","o":["It was in breach, because it failed to remove the risk of slipping completely before allowing work to continue.","It was in breach, because an employer must always close premises where any risk of injury remains.","It was not in breach, because the storm was an act of God for which no one can be held responsible.","It was in breach, because an employee was injured and the risk was one the employer knew about.","It was probably not in breach, because closing the factory would be out of proportion to the risk."],"a":4,"e":"In deciding breach, the court weighs the **risk** against the **cost and practicality** of precautions. Where the defendant has taken reasonable steps and the only further precaution (closing the factory) would be out of proportion to the risk, there is no breach (Latimer v AEC). The duty is to take reasonable care, not to remove every risk, and knowing of the risk does not of itself mean breach."},
 {"t":"negligence","q":"Mia is walking past Dock Ltd's warehouse when a heavy sack falls from an upper loading door and injures her. Mia does not know how it came to fall. Dock Ltd controlled the warehouse and the loading equipment, and sacks do not usually fall from loading doors if proper care is taken. Dock Ltd offers no explanation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Mia can prove breach?","o":["She cannot prove breach, because she does not know exactly what Dock Ltd did wrong.","She need not prove breach, because the burden of proof is reversed and Dock Ltd must disprove negligence on the balance of probabilities.","She need not prove breach, because a warehouse owner is strictly liable for anything that falls from it.","She may rely on an inference of negligence from the facts, which Dock Ltd would need to answer with an explanation.","She can prove breach only if Dock Ltd has been convicted of a criminal offence arising from the incident."],"a":3,"e":"Under **res ipsa loquitur** (\"the thing speaks for itself\"), where the thing that caused harm was under the defendant's control and the accident would not usually happen without negligence, the court may **infer** negligence even though the claimant cannot say exactly what went wrong (Scott v London and St Katherine Docks). It does not reverse the legal burden of proof or create strict liability: the defendant can rebut the inference by giving a reasonable explanation."},
 {"t":"causation-remoteness","q":"Arjun, a steel erector, falls from a height and is badly injured. His employer was in breach of duty by failing to provide him with a safety harness. The evidence shows that Arjun had always refused to wear harnesses when they were provided, and that he would not have worn one on the day of the accident.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the employer's liability for Arjun's injuries?","o":["It is liable, because it breached its duty by failing to provide a harness.","It is liable in full, because an employer's duty to provide equipment is non-delegable.","It is liable, but Arjun's damages will be reduced for contributory negligence because he always refused to wear harnesses.","It is liable, because it is impossible to know for certain what Arjun would have done.","It is not liable, because the injury would have happened even if a harness had been provided."],"a":4,"e":"The claimant must show, on the **balance of probabilities**, that **but for** the breach he would not have been injured. If he would not have worn the harness anyway, the failure to provide one made no difference, so causation fails (as in McWilliams v Sir William Arrol). Breach without causation gives no claim, and uncertainty is resolved on the balance of probabilities, not in the claimant's favour."},
 {"t":"causation-remoteness","q":"Petra worked for 20 years in a foundry and developed a lung disease caused by the gradual build-up of silica dust. Some of the dust came from a machine whose dust extractor her employer negligently failed to maintain. The rest came from processes where no precaution was possible. The negligent dust was a significant, but not the main, source.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the employer's liability?","o":["It is not liable, because Petra cannot show that the disease would not have happened but for the breach.","It is not liable, because most of the dust came from processes for which it was not at fault.","It is liable, because the dust from its breach made a material contribution to the disease.","It is liable only if Petra can show that the negligent dust was the main cause of the disease.","It is liable only for the loss of a chance of avoiding the disease, valued as a percentage."],"a":2,"e":"Where a disease results from a **cumulative** build-up from several sources, a defendant is liable if its breach made a **material contribution** (more than negligible) to the disease, even if it was not the only or main cause (Bonnington Castings v Wardlaw). The claimant need not satisfy the strict but-for test in this situation. Loss of a chance is not the approach for personal injury."},
 {"t":"causation-remoteness","q":"Graham has developed mesothelioma, a cancer that can be triggered by a single asbestos fibre. He was negligently exposed to asbestos by three successive employers over 30 years. Medical science cannot say which exposure caused the disease. One employer, Salt Ltd, is still trading; the other two have been dissolved.\n\nWhat can Graham recover from Salt Ltd?","o":["Full damages, because each negligent employer is liable for the whole of the damage.","Nothing, because he cannot prove on the balance of probabilities that Salt Ltd's asbestos caused the disease.","One-third of his damages, because Salt Ltd was one of three employers who exposed him.","A share of his damages proportionate to the length of his exposure while working for Salt Ltd.","Full damages only if Salt Ltd's exposure was the longest or the most intense of the three."],"a":0,"e":"In mesothelioma cases, an employer who **materially increased the risk** of the disease is liable even though but-for causation cannot be proved (Fairchild). Under the **Compensation Act 2006 s.3**, each responsible person is liable **jointly and severally** for the **whole** of the damage, so Graham can recover in full from Salt Ltd, which may seek contribution from any other responsible person or its insurer (Parliament reversed the proportionate-share approach for mesothelioma)."},
 {"t":"causation-remoteness","q":"Baby Leo is born prematurely and develops a serious eye condition. A doctor negligently gave him too much oxygen. The condition could have been caused by the excess oxygen or by any one of four other conditions common in premature babies, none of which involved negligence. The experts cannot say which was the cause.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Leo's claim against the hospital?","o":["It is likely to succeed, because the excess oxygen materially increased the risk of the eye condition.","It is likely to succeed, because the excess oxygen was the only negligent possible cause.","It is likely to succeed for a proportion of his damages, reflecting the one-in-five chance that the excess oxygen was the actual cause.","It is likely to succeed, because the burden of proof shifts to the hospital once negligence is shown.","It is likely to fail, because he cannot prove that the excess oxygen, rather than another cause, probably caused the condition."],"a":4,"e":"Where there are several **distinct possible causes** of an injury and only one is negligent, the claimant must still prove on the balance of probabilities that the negligent one caused it (Wilsher v Essex AHA). The material-increase-in-risk exception is confined to mesothelioma-type cases, and the burden of proof does not shift. Proportionate damages for a lost chance are not awarded in personal injury claims."},
 {"t":"causation-remoteness","q":"Nikos injures his back at work in 2023 because of his employer's breach of duty and can only do light work, losing part of his earnings. In 2025 he is diagnosed with an unrelated spinal disease, not connected with the accident, which would by then have left him completely unable to work anyway.\n\nHow will the later disease affect his damages for loss of earnings?","o":["They will not be affected, because the employer must take Nikos as it finds him.","They will not be affected, because the employer's breach was the first cause of his loss and a later illness cannot reduce liability.","They will be limited to his losses up to the time the disease would have stopped him working.","They will be extinguished entirely, because the disease would have prevented him working.","They will be increased, because the disease makes the effect of the back injury worse."],"a":2,"e":"Where a later **natural** illness, unrelated to the tort, would have caused the same loss anyway, the defendant is liable only for the loss up to that point; the \"vicissitudes of life\" are taken into account (Jobling v Associated Dairies). A later **tortious** event is treated differently and does not cut down the first defendant's liability (Baker v Willoughby). Losses before the disease remain recoverable."},
 {"t":"causation-remoteness","q":"Jodie breaks her leg in a road accident caused by Matt's negligent driving. At hospital, a junior doctor makes an ordinary error in setting the fracture, which means it heals less well than it should and she is left with a limp. The doctor's error was negligent but not grossly so.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the doctor's negligence breaks the chain of causation between Matt's driving and Jodie's limp?","o":["It breaks the chain, because the limp was caused by the doctor's negligence, not by Matt's driving.","It does not break the chain, because ordinary negligent medical treatment is not so unreasonable as to be a new cause.","It breaks the chain, because any negligent act by a third party is a new intervening act.","It does not break the chain, because a third party's act can never break the chain of causation.","It breaks the chain, because Matt could not have foreseen that Jodie would receive negligent medical treatment at the hospital."],"a":1,"e":"A third party's act breaks the chain of causation only if it is **so unreasonable or unforeseeable** that it eclipses the defendant's wrongdoing. Medical treatment is a foreseeable consequence of an injury, and ordinary negligent treatment does not usually break the chain; only treatment so grossly negligent as to be a completely inappropriate response might do so. A third party's act can sometimes break the chain, for example a deliberate wrongful act."},
 {"t":"causation-remoteness","q":"Sunita is a passenger on a coach when the driver negligently loses control on a steep hill. Believing the coach is about to crash over the edge, she jumps from the moving coach and breaks her ankle. In fact the driver regains control and the coach stops safely.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the coach company's liability for her broken ankle?","o":["It is not liable, because Sunita's decision to jump was a new intervening act that broke the chain of causation.","It is liable, because jumping was a reasonable reaction to the danger created by its driver's negligence.","It is not liable, because the coach did not in fact crash, so Sunita was never in real danger.","It is liable, but Sunita's damages will be reduced substantially because she chose to jump.","It is not liable, because Sunita voluntarily accepted the risk of injury when she jumped."],"a":1,"e":"Where a defendant's negligence puts the claimant in an **emergency**, a reasonable reaction in the \"agony of the moment\" does not break the chain of causation, even if, with hindsight, it was the wrong choice (Jones v Boyce). Only a claimant's **wholly unreasonable** conduct breaks the chain. A reasonable reaction is not contributory negligence and is not consent to the risk."},
 {"t":"causation-remoteness","q":"Lewis is splashed with molten metal at work because of his employer's negligence and suffers a small burn on his lip. The burn triggers a cancer in tissue that was already pre-malignant, and he dies three years later. A burn was foreseeable; cancer was not.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the employer's liability for Lewis's death?","o":["It is liable, because a burn was a foreseeable type of harm and it must take Lewis as it finds him.","It is not liable, because the cancer and death were not a reasonably foreseeable consequence of a small burn.","It is liable only for the burn itself, because the cancer was caused by Lewis's pre-existing condition.","It is not liable, because Lewis's pre-malignant condition was a new intervening cause of his death.","It is liable only if it knew about Lewis's pre-malignant condition before the accident."],"a":0,"e":"Under the remoteness test only the **type** of harm (here, a burn injury) need be foreseeable, not its extent. Under the **eggshell skull** rule, the defendant must take the claimant as it finds him, so it is liable for the full consequences, including the cancer and death (Smith v Leech Brain). The pre-existing condition is not a new intervening act, and the employer's knowledge of it is irrelevant."},
 {"t":"causation-remoteness","q":"Ffion works on a farm where her employer negligently allowed rats to infest the buildings. She catches a rare disease spread by contact with rat urine. The foreseeable risks of a rat infestation were rat bites and food contamination; the disease was not known to be caught in this way at the time.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely outcome of Ffion's claim for the disease?","o":["It succeeds, because some harm from the rats was foreseeable, so all harm that the rats in fact caused to Ffion is recoverable.","It succeeds, because under the eggshell skull rule the employer must take Ffion as it finds her.","It fails, because Ffion cannot prove that but for the infestation she would not have caught the disease.","It fails, because the disease was a different type of harm from the bites and contamination that were foreseeable.","It succeeds, because only the extent of harm, not its type, must be foreseeable."],"a":3,"e":"Damage is too **remote** if it is of a **different type** from the harm that was reasonably foreseeable (The Wagon Mound). On similar facts, a disease from rat urine was held to be a different kind of harm from the foreseeable bites and food poisoning (Tremain v Pike). The eggshell skull rule concerns the extent of harm to a vulnerable claimant, not a different type of harm, and causation in fact is not the problem."},
 {"t":"causation-remoteness","q":"Holt Ltd instructs solicitors on the purchase of a business. The solicitors negligently fail to advise Holt Ltd to seek a warranty protecting it against a particular liability. The liability later costs Holt Ltd £200,000. The court finds there was a real and substantial chance, assessed at 40%, that the seller would have agreed to give the warranty.\n\nWhat is Holt Ltd most likely to recover?","o":["£200,000, because the solicitors' negligence caused it to suffer the liability.","Nothing, because it cannot prove on the balance of probabilities that the seller would have agreed.","£100,000, because loss of a chance claims are always assessed at half of the full loss.","£80,000, being the value of the 40% chance it lost of obtaining the warranty.","Nothing, because a loss of a chance can never be recovered in negligence."],"a":3,"e":"Where the claimant's loss depends on what a **third party** would have done, the claimant must show a **real and substantial** (not speculative) chance, and damages are assessed as a **percentage** of the value of the lost opportunity (Allied Maples v Simmons & Simmons). So 40% of £200,000 is £80,000. This differs from personal injury claims, where loss of a chance below 50% is not recoverable."},
 {"t":"economic-loss","q":"Roadworks contractors negligently damage a water main in a high street. The council closes the street for two weeks while it is repaired. Kerry's café, on that street, suffers no physical damage, but she loses £6,000 of trade because customers cannot reach it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kerry's claim against the contractors?","o":["It will succeed, because her loss of trade was a reasonably foreseeable result of the contractors' negligence.","It will succeed, because she was in a close relationship of proximity to the contractors' works.","It will succeed, but only for the loss of profit and not for her loss of turnover.","It will probably fail, because it is pure economic loss caused by a careless act.","It will succeed, because businesses can always recover loss of trade caused by negligence."],"a":3,"e":"**Pure economic loss** (financial loss not resulting from physical damage to the claimant's person or property) caused by a careless **act** is generally **not recoverable** in negligence, even if it is foreseeable (Spartan Steel). Kerry suffered no physical damage, so her lost trade is pure economic loss. The exception for negligent misstatements and services (Hedley Byrne) does not apply."},
 {"t":"economic-loss","q":"Ben buys a house built six years ago by Brick Ltd. Cracks appear because Brick Ltd negligently laid inadequate foundations. No one has been injured and no other property has been damaged, but repairs will cost £45,000. Ben has no contract with Brick Ltd.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ben's claim in negligence against Brick Ltd?","o":["It will succeed, because Brick Ltd owed a duty to future owners to build the house carefully.","It will succeed, because the cracks are physical damage to Ben's property caused by negligence, not merely a loss in its value.","It will probably fail, because the cost of repairing a defect in the building itself is pure economic loss.","It will succeed, but only if Ben issues his claim within six years of buying the house.","It will succeed, because the building is a dangerous defect that threatens Ben's safety."],"a":2,"e":"The cost of repairing a **defect in the building itself**, or the loss in its value, is treated as **pure economic loss**, not damage to other property, so it is not generally recoverable from a builder in negligence (Murphy v Brentwood DC). A claim would arise if the defect caused **personal injury** or damage to **other property**. Ben should consider other routes, such as the Defective Premises Act 1972 or a building warranty."},
 {"t":"economic-loss","q":"Nadine leaves her job at Fenton Ltd. When she applies for a new job, the prospective employer asks Fenton Ltd for a reference. Fenton Ltd's HR manager carelessly confuses her with another employee and writes that Nadine was dismissed for dishonesty. The job offer is withdrawn.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Fenton Ltd owed Nadine a duty of care?","o":["It owed her no duty, because the reference was given to the prospective employer, not to Nadine.","It owed her a duty, because an employer giving a reference assumes responsibility to the employee for its accuracy.","It owed her no duty, because her only remedy for a damaging false statement is in defamation.","It owed her no duty, because the loss of a job offer is pure economic loss and is never recoverable in negligence.","It owed her a duty only if she had paid Fenton Ltd to provide the reference."],"a":1,"e":"An employer who provides a **reference** about an employee owes that **employee** a duty to take reasonable care in preparing it, and can be liable for economic loss caused by a careless reference (Spring v Guardian Assurance). This is an extension of the Hedley Byrne principle based on assumption of responsibility. A defamation claim might fail because of qualified privilege, which is why negligence matters, and no payment is required."},
 {"t":"economic-loss","q":"Mrs Price instructs her solicitor, Jamal, to change her will to leave her house to her granddaughter, Isla. Jamal carelessly delays for three months. Mrs Price dies before the new will is signed, and the house passes to others under her old will. Isla had no contact with Jamal.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Isla's position?","o":["She has no claim, because she was not Jamal's client and had no contract with him.","She has no claim, because her loss is pure economic loss and she did not rely on Jamal.","She has no claim, because only Mrs Price's estate can sue Jamal for his negligence.","She may recover only if she can show that she knew about, and relied on, the proposed change to the will.","She may recover from Jamal, because a will-drafting solicitor owes a duty to the intended beneficiary."],"a":4,"e":"A solicitor who negligently fails to carry out a client's will instructions owes a duty to the **intended beneficiary** who loses out (White v Jones). This extends the Hedley Byrne principle: the solicitor assumes responsibility for the task, and otherwise there would be no remedy because the estate has suffered no loss. Isla need not show reliance or knowledge of the change."},
 {"t":"economic-loss","q":"Bright & Co, accountants, are told by their client, Target Ltd, that Bidco Ltd is considering a takeover of Target Ltd and will rely on the accounts that Bright & Co are preparing in deciding how much to offer. Bright & Co carelessly overstate Target Ltd's profits. Bidco Ltd relies on the accounts and pays far too much.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Bright & Co owed Bidco Ltd a duty of care?","o":["They owed no duty, because statutory accounts are prepared only for the company's shareholders as a body.","They probably owed a duty, because they knew this bidder would rely on the accounts for this deal.","They owed no duty, because Bidco Ltd's loss is pure economic loss caused by a statement.","They owed no duty, because Bidco Ltd had no contract with Bright & Co.","They owed a duty only if Bidco Ltd had paid a fee for the accounts."],"a":1,"e":"A duty for a negligent statement arises where the maker knows the statement will be communicated to the claimant (as an individual or member of an identifiable class) for a **specific purpose or transaction**, and that the claimant is likely to rely on it (Caparo). Here the accountants knew of this bidder and this transaction, unlike the general investing public in Caparo. Hedley Byrne liability needs no contract or fee."},
 {"t":"economic-loss","q":"Atlas plc, a property investment company with its own in-house surveyors, is buying an office building for £15 million. It obtains a valuation from Grey Valuers, which contains a clear, prominent clause excluding liability for negligence. Atlas plc had the opportunity to negotiate the terms and chose not to commission a full structural survey.\n\nHow is a court most likely to treat the exclusion clause?","o":["As void, because liability for negligence can never be excluded by a business.","As void, because a disclaimer cannot prevent a duty of care arising under Hedley Byrne.","As unreasonable, because disclaimers on valuations are always unreasonable.","As effective only if Atlas plc signed a separate document agreeing to it.","As likely to be reasonable, given the parties' equal bargaining power and the type of deal."],"a":4,"e":"A disclaimer for negligent advice causing **economic loss** is subject to the **reasonableness** test under the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 (s.2(2)) when given by a business to another business. Disclaimers on cheap valuations of modest homes for ordinary buyers are usually unreasonable (Smith v Eric S Bush), but the courts indicated that the position may differ for **expensive commercial property** and sophisticated parties. Only liability for death or personal injury caused by negligence can never be excluded."},
 {"t":"economic-loss","q":"A lender asks Val, a valuer, to value a property as security for a loan. Val negligently values it at £1 million; its true value is £700,000. Relying on the valuation, the lender lends £800,000. The property market then falls sharply, the borrower defaults and the property sells for £400,000. The lender has lost £400,000.\n\nHow much can the lender most likely recover from Val?","o":["£300,000, the difference between the valuation and the true value at the time.","£400,000, the full loss the lender suffered on the loan.","£100,000, the difference between the loan and the property's true value.","£600,000, the difference between the valuation and the eventual sale price.","Nothing, because the lender's loss was caused by the fall in the property market."],"a":0,"e":"A valuer is liable only for loss within the **scope of the duty**, meaning the loss attributable to the information being wrong (South Australia Asset Management v York Montague, applied in Manchester Building Society v Grant Thornton). If the valuation had been correct, the lender would have had £300,000 less security, so recovery is capped at £300,000. The market fall is a risk the lender would have borne anyway, but it does not wipe out the claim entirely."},
 {"t":"economic-loss","q":"Natural Foods Ltd is a small company owned and run by its managing director, Mr Lowe. A franchisee buys a franchise after reading the company's brochure, which carelessly overstates likely profits and refers to Mr Lowe's experience. The franchisee never dealt with Mr Lowe personally. The company is now insolvent, so the franchisee sues Mr Lowe.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mr Lowe's liability?","o":["He is liable, because as managing director he is responsible for all statements made by his company.","He is liable, because the brochure referred to his personal experience and expertise.","He is unlikely to be liable, because he did not personally assume responsibility to the franchisee.","He is liable, because the company is insolvent and the franchisee has no other remedy.","He is liable, because a director who owns the company is treated as the company itself."],"a":2,"e":"A director is not personally liable for the company's negligent misstatement unless he **personally assumed responsibility** to the claimant, and the claimant reasonably relied on that (Williams v Natural Life Health Foods). References to his expertise in company material, with no personal dealings, are not enough. The company's insolvency does not create personal liability, and the company is a separate legal person from its owner."},
 {"t":"economic-loss","q":"A lorry driver negligently crashes into Kate's florist's shop, damaging the shop front and destroying £2,000 of flowers. The shop has to close for ten days for repairs, and Kate loses £3,000 of profit during that period.\n\nWhich of the following can Kate recover?","o":["The cost of repairs and the £2,000 of flowers only, because lost profit is pure economic loss.","The cost of repairs only, because the flowers and lost profit are economic loss.","The £3,000 of lost profit only, because the shop's insurer will pay for the physical damage.","The cost of repairs, the £2,000 of flowers and the £3,000 of lost profit.","Nothing, because a business can recover only if it has a contract with the defendant."],"a":3,"e":"**Physical damage** to the claimant's property is recoverable, and so is **consequential economic loss**, meaning financial loss that flows directly from that physical damage (such as profit lost while the damaged shop is closed). This is different from pure economic loss, which is not caused by damage to the claimant's own property. Insurance is not deducted from the claim against the wrongdoer, and no contract is needed."},
 {"t":"economic-loss","q":"Bank Ltd is lending to a borrower whose property is being released from an existing charge. The borrower's solicitor, Sam, sends Bank Ltd an email carelessly stating that the whole loan is to be repaid, when only part is. Bank Ltd, an experienced commercial lender with its own lawyers, does not check its file and releases its charge, suffering a large loss.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Sam owed Bank Ltd a duty of care?","o":["He probably owed no duty, because it was unreasonable for Bank Ltd to rely on him without checking.","He owed a duty, because a solicitor always owes a duty to anyone who relies on his statements.","He owed a duty, because it was foreseeable that Bank Ltd would act on his email.","He owed no duty, because a solicitor owes no duty to anyone except his own client in any circumstances.","He owed a duty, because Bank Ltd's loss resulted from a statement and not from an act."],"a":0,"e":"A duty for a negligent misstatement depends on an **assumption of responsibility** and **reasonable reliance**. A solicitor acting for one party does not normally assume responsibility to the other side, and it is not usually reasonable for a represented, sophisticated party to rely on the opponent's solicitor without checking (NRAM v Steel). Foreseeability of reliance is not enough. It is not an absolute rule, though; a duty can arise in exceptional cases, which is why the fourth option overstates it."},
 {"t":"psychiatric-harm","q":"Omar's wife is killed in a train crash caused by the operator's negligence. Omar was not there. He suffers deep grief, sadness and sleepless nights for many months, but medical experts agree that he has not developed any recognised psychiatric illness.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Omar can claim damages for psychiatric harm?","o":["He cannot, because grief and distress without a recognised psychiatric illness are not recoverable.","He can, because the loss of a spouse in an accident caused by negligence always gives the surviving spouse a claim for mental harm.","He can, because his grief was reasonably foreseeable as a result of his wife's death.","He can, but only if he can show that he was in physical danger himself.","He cannot, because only people who witness an accident can ever claim for mental harm."],"a":0,"e":"A claim for psychiatric harm requires a **recognised psychiatric illness** (such as post-traumatic stress disorder or clinical depression). Ordinary **grief, distress or anxiety**, however severe, is not enough. (Separately, as a spouse he could recover the fixed bereavement award under the Fatal Accidents Act 1976, which is a different claim.)"},
 {"t":"psychiatric-harm","q":"Leanne watches from the pavement as her adult brother is crushed by a lorry driven negligently onto the pavement. She was not in danger herself. She develops post-traumatic stress disorder. The lorry company argues that Leanne cannot claim.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Leanne's position?","o":["She can claim, because a sibling, like a parent or spouse, is presumed to have a close tie of love and affection with the victim.","She can claim only if she proves a close tie of love and affection with her brother, which is not presumed for siblings.","She cannot claim, because only parents, children and spouses can ever be secondary victims.","She can claim, because she saw the accident with her own unaided senses, which is all that is required.","She cannot claim, because she was not in any physical danger from the lorry."],"a":1,"e":"As a **secondary victim**, Leanne must satisfy all the **Alcock** controls. A close tie of love and affection is **presumed** only for parents and children, spouses and fiancé(e)s; a **sibling** must **prove** an especially close tie, though this is possible. She meets proximity and perception (present and seeing it), but that is not all that is required. Not being in danger makes her a secondary, not primary, victim; it does not bar the claim."},
 {"t":"psychiatric-harm","q":"Grace is waiting at a school gate when she sees a van mount the pavement through the driver's negligence and hit her 8-year-old son. She runs to him immediately. She was never in danger herself. She develops a recognised psychiatric illness.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Grace's claim?","o":["She will fail, because she was not in physical danger and so cannot be a primary victim.","She will fail, because a secondary victim must also have suffered some physical injury.","She is likely to succeed as a secondary victim, because she has a presumed close tie and witnessed the accident.","She will succeed only if she can show that a person of ordinary fortitude would not have suffered any illness.","She will fail, because a parent can claim only if she comes upon the immediate aftermath, not if she sees the accident itself happen."],"a":2,"e":"Grace is a **secondary victim** who meets the **Alcock** controls: a **presumed close tie** (parent and child), presence at the scene, and perceiving the **accident** with her own unaided senses, and a person of ordinary fortitude might well suffer psychiatric illness in these circumstances. No physical injury is needed. Being present at the accident itself is stronger, not weaker, than coming upon the aftermath."},
 {"t":"psychiatric-harm","q":"Callum, a passer-by, witnesses a horrific crash in which a stranger is killed when a negligently driven lorry hits his car. Callum was across the road and never in danger. He develops post-traumatic stress disorder.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Callum's claim?","o":["It will probably fail, because a bystander with no close tie to the victim cannot satisfy the secondary victim controls.","It will succeed, because he witnessed the accident with his own unaided senses and was close by when it happened, which is enough.","It will succeed, because post-traumatic stress disorder is a recognised psychiatric illness.","It will succeed if the accident was especially horrific, because then no close tie is required.","It will fail, because a bystander must be in physical danger to make any claim for mental harm."],"a":0,"e":"A **bystander** who has no **close tie of love and affection** with the victim fails the Alcock controls. Although the House of Lords in Alcock left open the possibility of a claim where an accident is especially horrific, later cases have not allowed such claims, so the claim will probably fail. Being in danger would make him a primary victim, but that is not the only way to claim."},
 {"t":"psychiatric-harm","q":"Hassan is at work when a police officer telephones to tell him that his wife has been killed in a road accident caused by a negligent driver. He does not see her body until a formal identification two days later. He develops a severe depressive illness.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hassan's claim against the driver?","o":["It will succeed, because he has a presumed close tie with his wife and has a recognised illness.","It will fail, because he was not present at the accident or its immediate aftermath and learned of it from another person.","It will succeed, because seeing his wife's body at the formal identification two days later counts as the immediate aftermath.","It will fail, because a spouse must prove a close tie of love and affection with the victim.","It will succeed, because the shock of hearing the news is a sudden event perceived by him."],"a":1,"e":"A secondary victim must be **present** at the accident or its **immediate aftermath** and perceive it with their **own unaided senses**. Being told by someone else, and seeing the body two days later at an identification, does not satisfy these controls (Alcock). His close tie with his wife is presumed, so that is not why the claim fails."},
 {"t":"psychiatric-harm","q":"Dev, an off-duty paramedic, helps at the scene of a stadium crush caused by the operator's negligence. He treats injured people in an area away from the crush where he is never in any physical danger and does not believe he is. He develops post-traumatic stress disorder.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Dev's claim?","o":["It will succeed, because rescuers are always treated as primary victims.","It will succeed, because it was foreseeable that a rescuer at a disaster would suffer psychiatric harm.","It will succeed, because he was acting in the course of a duty to help the public.","It will probably fail, because a rescuer not in danger is a secondary victim, and he had no close tie.","It will fail, because a professional rescuer can never recover for psychiatric harm."],"a":3,"e":"A rescuer is a **primary victim** only if he was in **physical danger** or reasonably believed he was. Otherwise he is treated like any other **secondary victim** and must satisfy the Alcock controls, which Dev cannot, as he had no close tie with the victims (White v Chief Constable of South Yorkshire). Professional rescuers are not barred as such; they are treated in the same way as others."},
 {"t":"psychiatric-harm","q":"Fran stops at a burning car crash caused by a negligent driver and crawls inside the wreck to pull out a trapped passenger, a stranger. Petrol is leaking and she reasonably believes the car may explode at any moment. It does not explode, and she is not physically hurt, but she develops post-traumatic stress disorder.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Fran's claim against the negligent driver?","o":["She cannot claim, because she had no close tie of love and affection with the trapped passenger.","She cannot claim, because the car did not in fact explode, so she was never in danger.","She cannot claim, because she voluntarily put herself in danger and so accepted the risk.","She can claim as a primary victim, because she reasonably believed she was in physical danger.","She can claim as a secondary victim, because she witnessed the accident with her own senses."],"a":3,"e":"A rescuer who is in **physical danger**, or **reasonably believes** she is, is a **primary victim**, so she need not satisfy the Alcock controls, such as a close tie. Her reasonable belief is enough even though the car did not explode. A reasonable rescue is not treated as consent to the risk."},
 {"t":"psychiatric-harm","q":"Martin, a social services manager, suffers a nervous breakdown caused by an excessive workload. When he returns to work, his employer promises extra support, but provides none and increases his caseload. Six months later he suffers a second breakdown and can no longer work.\n\nWhich of the following best describes his employer's liability for the second breakdown?","o":["It is not liable, because an employer is never liable for psychiatric harm caused by workload or stress at work, as opposed to physical injury.","It is not liable, because Martin chose to return to work and so accepted the risk of further harm.","It is liable only if Martin was in physical danger at work when the second breakdown occurred.","It is not liable, because stress claims require the employee to be a secondary victim.","It is likely to be liable, because after the first breakdown psychiatric harm to Martin was reasonably foreseeable."],"a":4,"e":"In **stress at work** cases, the key question is whether harm to this employee was **reasonably foreseeable**; an employer is usually entitled to assume an employee can cope unless it knows otherwise. After the first breakdown, the employer knew Martin was vulnerable and failed to take the steps it had promised, so it is likely to be liable (Walker v Northumberland CC; Hatton v Sutherland). Returning to work is not consent, and the primary and secondary victim categories do not govern these claims."},
 {"t":"psychiatric-harm","q":"Gareth, a firefighter, attends a crash and finds that the driver is his own son, Liam, who has been badly injured because of his own careless driving. No one else was involved. Gareth develops post-traumatic stress disorder and wants to claim against Liam's motor insurer.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gareth's claim?","o":["It will succeed, because Gareth has a presumed close tie with his son and attended the immediate aftermath of the crash in person.","It will succeed, because Liam's insurer must pay for any harm caused by Liam's careless driving.","It will fail, because a person who negligently injures only himself owes no duty to a relative who suffers psychiatric harm.","It will fail, because Gareth is a professional rescuer, and professional rescuers can never claim.","It will succeed, but Gareth's damages will be reduced because of his son's contributory negligence."],"a":2,"e":"A victim who negligently injures **himself** owes **no duty** of care to a relative who suffers psychiatric harm from witnessing it (Greatorex v Greatorex). Recognising such a duty would restrict people's freedom to take risks with themselves and could cause conflict within families. So Gareth's claim fails even though he might otherwise satisfy the Alcock controls."},
 {"t":"psychiatric-harm","q":"Asha's husband, Tom, is knocked down by a negligent driver while crossing a road. A neighbour phones Asha, who lives at the end of the same street. She runs there and arrives within five minutes, before the ambulance, and sees Tom lying injured and bleeding in the road. She develops post-traumatic stress disorder.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Asha's claim?","o":["She will fail, because she did not see the accident itself happen.","She will fail, because she learned of the accident from a telephone call from a neighbour.","She will fail, because she was never in any physical danger from the driver.","She will succeed only if she can prove an especially close tie with her husband.","She is likely to succeed, because she saw the immediate aftermath with her own senses."],"a":4,"e":"A secondary victim can satisfy the proximity controls by coming upon the **immediate aftermath** of the accident, such as arriving at the scene within minutes, before the victim has been moved or treated, and seeing it with her own unaided senses. Being told first by phone does not matter if she then perceives the immediate aftermath herself. Her close tie as a spouse is **presumed**."},
 {"t":"employers-liability","q":"Marsh Ltd appoints an experienced and competent safety manager to run its warehouse. The manager carelessly fails to ensure that shelving is securely fixed to the wall, and an employee, Priya, is injured when it collapses. Marsh Ltd argues that it discharged its duty by appointing a competent manager.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Marsh Ltd's position?","o":["It is not liable, because it took reasonable care in selecting an experienced and competent safety manager to run the warehouse.","It is not liable, because only the safety manager personally owes a duty to Priya.","It is liable only if Priya can show that it knew the shelving was not securely fixed.","It is liable, because an employer's duty to provide a safe place and system of work is non-delegable.","It is not liable, because the shelving collapse was caused by the act of another employee."],"a":3,"e":"An employer's duty to its employees is **personal and non-delegable**: it can delegate the performance of the duty, but not responsibility for it. If the person it relies on is careless, the employer is in breach (Wilsons & Clyde Coal v English; McDermid v Nash Dredging). Appointing a competent manager is no defence to a primary claim, and the employer need not have had personal knowledge."},
 {"t":"employers-liability","q":"Kyle works on a building site. His employer knows that a co-worker, Shane, has a long history of dangerous practical jokes and has been warned several times, but has taken no further action. Shane pushes Kyle off a low platform as a prank and Kyle breaks his wrist.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the employer's liability?","o":["It is not liable, because Shane's prank was outside the course of his employment.","It is not liable, because an employer cannot be responsible for the deliberate acts of an employee.","It is liable only if Shane himself has insufficient money to pay Kyle's damages.","It is not liable, because it had warned Shane several times about his behaviour.","It is liable, because it failed in its duty to provide competent fellow employees despite knowing of the risk."],"a":4,"e":"An employer's personal duty includes providing **reasonably competent fellow employees**. Where the employer knows that an employee is a danger to others and fails to take effective action, such as proper discipline or dismissal, it is in breach (Hudson v Ridge Manufacturing). This is **primary** liability, so whether the prank was in the course of employment (relevant to vicarious liability) does not matter, and repeated warnings that did not work are not enough."},
 {"t":"employers-liability","q":"Zoe is an experienced steel worker who knows the risks of molten metal. Her employer makes safety spats (protective coverings for the feet) available in the stores and has told her where they are, but she chooses not to collect them. Molten metal splashes onto her foot.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the employer's liability?","o":["It is probably not liable, because it took reasonable steps and an experienced worker chose not to use the equipment.","It is liable, because an employer must ensure that safety equipment is actually used by every employee, however experienced.","It is liable, because it should have issued the spats to Zoe personally and supervised her use of them.","It is liable in full, because Zoe's choice not to wear spats does not affect her damages.","It is liable, because the employer's duty to provide a safe system of work is strict."],"a":0,"e":"The employer's duty is to take **reasonable care**, and what is reasonable depends on the employee. For an **experienced** worker who knows the risks, making equipment available and telling her about it may be enough, without constant supervision (Qualcast v Haynes). The position is different for new or inexperienced workers, where instruction and supervision may be needed. The duty is not strict."},
 {"t":"employers-liability","q":"Oscar is injured at work in 2025 when an unguarded machine traps his hand. The machine did not comply with health and safety regulations requiring guards. His employer argues that he must prove negligence. Oscar's adviser says he can rely on breach of the regulations alone, without proving fault.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Oscar's position?","o":["He can recover simply by proving the regulations were breached, without proving any fault.","He must prove negligence, but the breach of the regulations will be relevant evidence of the standard of care.","He cannot recover at all, because breaches of health and safety regulations are matters for criminal law only.","He can recover only if the employer has been prosecuted and convicted for breaching the regulations.","He must prove that the employer deliberately ignored the regulations in order to recover."],"a":1,"e":"Since the **Enterprise and Regulatory Reform Act 2013 s.69** (for breaches from 1 October 2013), breach of most health and safety regulations does **not by itself** give a civil claim. The employee must prove **negligence**, but the regulations remain relevant in setting the standard of care a reasonable employer should meet. No prosecution or deliberate breach is needed."},
 {"t":"employers-liability","q":"Fiona's employer knows that she has sight in only one eye. She is given work hammering metal, which carries a small risk of flying fragments. The employer does not provide goggles to workers doing this job, as it is not common practice for employees with normal sight. A fragment hits Fiona's good eye and she is blinded.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the employer's liability?","o":["It is not liable, because it was not common practice to provide goggles for this work.","It is not liable, because the risk of a fragment hitting an eye was small.","It is not liable, because it owes the same duty to every employee, whatever their characteristics.","It is liable only if Fiona asked for goggles and the employer refused to provide them.","It is likely to be liable, because the grave risk to Fiona meant it should have given her goggles."],"a":4,"e":"The employer's duty is owed to **each individual employee**, and it must take account of characteristics it knows about. The **seriousness** of the potential harm to a worker with one good eye made it reasonable to provide goggles, even though the risk was small and goggles were not usual for others (Paris v Stepney BC). Common practice is relevant but not conclusive."},
 {"t":"employers-liability","q":"Tariq, aged 18, starts a new job in a food factory. On his first day he is told to clean a mixing machine. No one shows him how to isolate the power or explains the dangers. He reaches into the machine while it is still connected, it starts up and he is injured.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the employer's liability?","o":["It is not liable, because Tariq should have asked how to clean the machine safely before starting.","It is likely to be liable, because it failed to train and supervise him in a safe system of work.","It is not liable, because the machine itself was not defective in any way.","It is not liable, because Tariq voluntarily accepted the risk by reaching into the machine.","It is liable only if the employer has been prosecuted for failing to train Tariq."],"a":1,"e":"An employer must provide a **safe system of work**, which includes proper **training, instruction and supervision**, particularly for **young and inexperienced** workers. Sending a new worker to clean a dangerous machine without explaining how to isolate it is a clear breach. The machine need not be defective, and consent (volenti) rarely succeeds against employees. Any carelessness by Tariq might at most reduce damages for contributory negligence."},
 {"t":"employers-liability","q":"Halo Ltd hires Ned, a self-employed electrician with his own business and other customers, to rewire its office. Ned is injured when he falls from an unsafe ladder that he brought himself. He argues that Halo Ltd owed him the same personal duty that an employer owes its employees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ned's position?","o":["He is owed an employer's personal duty, because he was working on Halo Ltd's premises.","He is owed an employer's personal duty, because Halo Ltd was paying him for the work.","He is owed an employer's personal duty, but only in respect of equipment he brought onto the site himself.","He is not owed an employer's personal duty, because he is an independent contractor, not an employee.","He is owed an employer's personal duty if the work lasted more than one working day."],"a":3,"e":"The employer's personal, non-delegable duty is owed to **employees**. Ned is an **independent contractor** running his own business, so he is not owed that duty. He may have other claims, for example against Halo Ltd as **occupier** under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957, but a skilled visitor can be expected to guard against risks incidental to his trade, and the ladder was his own."},
 {"t":"employers-liability","q":"Bea works in a laboratory. Her employer has carried out proper risk assessments, provides suitable protective equipment, trains staff and supervises them closely. Despite this, a flask that had been properly inspected unexpectedly shatters and injures Bea. No one could reasonably have foreseen or prevented it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the employer's liability?","o":["It is liable, because an employer is strictly liable for any injury suffered at work.","It is liable, because the employer's duty to provide safe equipment is non-delegable.","It is not liable, because its duty is to take reasonable care, not to guarantee safety.","It is liable, because Bea was injured by equipment provided by her employer.","It is liable unless it can show that Bea caused the accident by her own carelessness."],"a":2,"e":"The employer's duty is to take **reasonable care** for employees' safety; it is **not** an absolute duty or a guarantee. Where the employer has taken all reasonable precautions and the accident could not reasonably have been foreseen or prevented, it is not liable. Non-delegability concerns who is responsible for a breach, not whether there was one, and the burden is on Bea to prove breach."},
 {"t":"employers-liability","q":"Callum is employed by a UK dredging company, which sends him to work on a tug operated by a Dutch company. The Dutch captain uses an unsafe system for releasing the tug's ropes, and Callum's leg is badly injured. Callum's employer had no control over how the captain worked.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Callum's employer's liability?","o":["It is not liable, because the unsafe system was operated by a captain employed by another company.","It is not liable, because it had no control over how the captain did his work.","It is liable, because its duty to provide a safe system of work cannot be delegated to the other company.","It is liable only if it was careless in choosing to send Callum to work for the Dutch company.","It is not liable, because Callum's only claim is against the Dutch company, which operated the tug and employed the captain."],"a":2,"e":"An employer's duty to provide a **safe system of work** is **non-delegable**. If it hands over the operation of the system to someone else, it remains liable if that system is unsafe, even if the other person is not its employee (McDermid v Nash Dredging). Lack of control is no defence, and liability does not depend on carelessness in choosing the other company. Callum may also have a claim against the Dutch company."},
 {"t":"employers-liability","q":"Gemma, a warehouse worker, is told by her employer to wear steel-capped boots on the loading bay, and the boots are provided free. She regularly wears trainers instead because they are more comfortable, and supervisors who see this sometimes remind her. A pallet is dropped on her foot through a co-worker's negligence. Boots would have reduced her injury.\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely outcome of Gemma's claim against her employer?","o":["She succeeds, but her damages are reduced for contributory negligence.","She fails completely, because she chose not to wear the boots she was given.","She succeeds in full, because the pallet was dropped through another employee's negligence.","She fails, because she voluntarily accepted the risk of injury by wearing trainers.","She succeeds in full, because an employer cannot rely on an employee's fault as a defence."],"a":0,"e":"The employer is liable (here vicariously, for the co-worker's negligence), but Gemma's own failure to take care for her safety by ignoring instructions to wear boots that would have reduced her injury is **contributory negligence**, so damages are reduced under the Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945. It does not defeat the claim, and volenti rarely succeeds against employees because of the economic pressures of employment."},
 {"t":"vicarious-liability","q":"Health checks for Pell Bank's job applicants are carried out by Dr Ross, a self-employed GP with her own practice and many other clients. Pell Bank pays her a fee for each check. Several applicants allege that Dr Ross assaulted them during the examinations. They sue Pell Bank.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Pell Bank is vicariously liable?","o":["It is, because Dr Ross carried out the examinations for the bank's benefit and at its request.","It is, because the bank chose Dr Ross and paid her for every examination she carried out.","It is not, because Dr Ross was an independent contractor running her own business.","It is, because the assaults were closely connected with the examinations the bank arranged.","It is, provided the applicants can show that Dr Ross has no insurance or assets."],"a":2,"e":"Vicarious liability requires, first, a relationship of **employment or one akin to employment**. A person carrying on their own **independent business**, with their own practice and other clients, is an **independent contractor**, so stage one fails and the close connection question does not arise (Various Claimants v Barclays Bank). Whether Dr Ross has insurance is irrelevant."},
 {"t":"vicarious-liability","q":"Errol is a prisoner who works in a prison kitchen for a small weekly payment, under the supervision of prison staff. He carelessly drops a heavy sack of rice onto the back of Mrs Hill, the civilian catering manager, injuring her. She sues the Ministry of Justice.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Ministry of Justice's position?","o":["It is not vicariously liable, because Errol was a prisoner and not an employee under a contract of employment.","It is vicariously liable, because Errol's relationship with it was akin to employment and the act was part of his work.","It is not vicariously liable, because Errol was paid only a nominal wage for his work.","It is not vicariously liable, because prisoners are compelled to work and do not choose to do so.","It is vicariously liable only if it was negligent in assigning Errol to work in the kitchen."],"a":1,"e":"A relationship can be **akin to employment** even without a contract of employment. Errol did work that was an integral part of the prison's activities, for its benefit, under its direction, and the risk of carelessness was created by assigning him the work (Cox v Ministry of Justice). The nominal pay and compulsion do not prevent this. Vicarious liability is strict, so no negligence by the Ministry is needed."},
 {"t":"vicarious-liability","q":"Khalid works at a petrol station kiosk run by Fuelco Ltd. A customer, Dan, asks politely whether he can print some documents. Khalid swears at him, orders him to leave, follows him to his car on the forecourt, and punches him, telling him never to come back to Fuelco's premises.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Fuelco Ltd is vicariously liable for the assault?","o":["It is not liable, because assaulting a customer is never within the course of an employee's employment.","It is not liable, because the assault happened outside the kiosk where Khalid worked.","It is not liable, because Fuelco Ltd did not authorise Khalid to use violence towards anyone.","It is likely to be liable, because the assault was closely connected with his job of serving customers.","It is liable only if Fuelco Ltd knew that Khalid had a violent temper when it employed him."],"a":3,"e":"For **close connection**, the court asks what the employee's job was, broadly, and whether the wrongful act was so closely connected with it that it is just to hold the employer liable. Khalid's job was to deal with customers, and the assault was an **unbroken sequence** from that interaction, ending with an order to keep away from the employer's premises (Mohamud v WM Morrison Supermarkets). Lack of authorisation does not prevent liability, and the employer's knowledge is irrelevant to vicarious liability."},
 {"t":"vicarious-liability","q":"Gordon is employed as a warden at a residential home for children with learning difficulties. His job involves living at the home and looking after the children, including putting them to bed. Over several years he sexually abuses several of the children. His employer, Hope Homes Ltd, did not know and argues that abuse cannot be part of any job.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hope Homes Ltd's position?","o":["It is not vicariously liable, because the abuse was a criminal act that Hope Homes Ltd would never have authorised.","It is not vicariously liable, because the abuse was done for Gordon's own purposes and not for his employer's.","It is likely to be liable, because the abuse was closely connected with the care Gordon was employed to give.","It is vicariously liable only if it was negligent in employing or supervising Gordon.","It is not vicariously liable, because it did not know about the abuse while it was happening."],"a":2,"e":"An employer can be vicariously liable for an employee's **deliberate criminal** acts where they are **closely connected** with the employment. Gordon was entrusted with the care of the children, and the abuse was inextricably bound up with the duties he was employed to perform (Lister v Hesley Hall). Vicarious liability is strict, so it does not depend on the employer's negligence or knowledge."},
 {"t":"vicarious-liability","q":"Lila is employed as a delivery driver by Swift Ltd. Halfway through her round, without permission, she drives 30 miles in the opposite direction to visit her boyfriend for the afternoon. On the way, she negligently crashes into a cyclist, Sam.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Swift Ltd is vicariously liable to Sam?","o":["It is probably not liable, because Lila had gone on a frolic of her own unconnected with her work.","It is liable, because Lila was driving Swift Ltd's van during her working hours.","It is liable, because Lila was an employee and caused the accident while at work.","It is liable, because an employer is responsible for all torts its employees commit while on duty.","It is not liable, because Swift Ltd had not expressly forbidden Lila to make personal journeys."],"a":0,"e":"An employer is not vicariously liable where the employee has embarked on a **frolic of her own**: a substantial departure for **purely personal** purposes, unconnected with the employer's business. A 30-mile detour in the opposite direction to visit her boyfriend is such a frolic. Using the employer's vehicle during working hours does not by itself make the act part of the job. An express prohibition is not needed for the act to fall outside the employment."},
 {"t":"vicarious-liability","q":"Robbie is employed by Insul Ltd at its base in Bristol. He is sent to do a week's urgent job at a power station in Wales and is paid his normal wages for the travelling time. Driving back home at the end of the week, he negligently crashes, injuring his colleague, Tom, who is his passenger.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Insul Ltd's vicarious liability?","o":["It is likely to be liable, because Robbie was travelling in paid working time to and from the job.","It is not liable, because an employee travelling home from work is never acting in the course of employment.","It is not liable, because Robbie was driving his own car and not a company vehicle.","It is not liable, because Robbie chose the route and the time of his journey home.","It is liable only if Insul Ltd had told Robbie to give Tom a lift home."],"a":0,"e":"Ordinary commuting is usually outside the course of employment, but an employee who is **paid for travelling time** to and from a distant site where his employer sent him is acting in the course of his employment while travelling (Smith v Stages). It does not matter that he used his own car or chose the route."},
 {"t":"vicarious-liability","q":"Mo is employed by Gas Fuels Ltd to deliver heating oil. While transferring oil from his tanker into a customer's storage tank, he lights a cigarette and carelessly throws away the match. It causes an explosion that damages the customer's garage. Smoking during deliveries is strictly against company rules.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Gas Fuels Ltd is vicariously liable?","o":["It is not liable, because smoking is a personal act and not part of Mo's job.","It is not liable, because Mo's smoking breached company rules and so was outside his employment.","It is not liable, because lighting a cigarette is a deliberate act and not a careless one.","It is liable, because Mo was doing his job of delivering oil, though in a careless way.","It is liable only if Gas Fuels Ltd had failed to train Mo in the dangers of smoking."],"a":3,"e":"An employer is vicariously liable where the employee does the job he is employed to do, even if he does it in a **careless or prohibited way**. Mo was delivering oil, and smoking while doing so was a negligent way of doing that work (Century Insurance v NI Road Transport Board). A breach of company rules does not take the act outside the employment where it is done in the course of the work. Vicarious liability does not depend on any fault by the employer."},
 {"t":"vicarious-liability","q":"Cora, a lorry driver employed by Haul Ltd, is driving carefully and within the speed limit when a child suddenly runs into the road from behind a parked van. Cora cannot stop in time and the child is injured. An expert confirms that no reasonable driver could have avoided the accident.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Haul Ltd's position?","o":["It is vicariously liable, because Cora was acting in the course of her employment when the child was injured.","It is not vicariously liable, because Cora committed no tort for which it could be liable.","It is vicariously liable, because vicarious liability is strict and does not depend on anyone's fault.","It is vicariously liable, because an employer is responsible for all accidents caused by its vehicles.","It is not vicariously liable, because the child was not a customer of Haul Ltd."],"a":1,"e":"Vicarious liability makes the employer liable for a **tort committed by the employee**. Cora was not negligent, so there is no tort and nothing for which Haul Ltd can be vicariously liable. Vicarious liability is strict in the sense that the **employer** need not be at fault, but the employee must still have committed a tort. The victim need not be a customer."},
 {"t":"vicarious-liability","q":"Plant Hire Ltd hires out a crane and its employed driver, Joe, to Build Ltd for a construction project. The hire terms say that Joe is to be treated as Build Ltd's employee, but Plant Hire Ltd still pays him and decides how the crane is to be operated. Joe negligently swings the crane into a passer-by.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely position?","o":["Build Ltd is vicariously liable, because the hire terms state that Joe is to be treated as its employee.","Neither company is vicariously liable, because Joe was working for one company on the other's project.","Build Ltd is vicariously liable, because the accident happened on its construction site.","Neither company is vicariously liable, because Joe is personally responsible for his own negligence.","Plant Hire Ltd is likely to remain vicariously liable, because it kept control over how Joe operated the crane."],"a":4,"e":"When an employee is lent to another business, the **general (lending) employer** is presumed to remain vicariously liable, and the presumption is hard to displace. It is displaced only if control over **how** the work is done has passed to the hirer (Mersey Docks and Harbour Board v Coggins & Griffith). The contractual label does not bind the injured third party. Joe is personally liable too, but that does not stop the employer being vicariously liable."},
 {"t":"vicarious-liability","q":"A local authority school arranges for its pupils' swimming lessons to be run by Splash Ltd, an independent company, using its own instructors and lifeguard. Through the lifeguard's negligence, Amy, aged 10, gets into difficulty and suffers brain injury. Splash Ltd has gone out of business. Amy sues the local authority.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the local authority's position?","o":["It is vicariously liable, because the lifeguard was its employee for the purposes of the lesson.","It is not liable, because it chose a competent independent contractor to provide the lessons.","It is not liable, because a school's duty to its pupils ends when they leave the school premises.","It is liable only if its own teachers were present and negligently failed to supervise the lesson.","It is likely to be liable, because it owed its pupils a non-delegable duty during lessons."],"a":4,"e":"The lifeguard was not employed by the authority, so vicarious liability does not apply. But a school owes its pupils a **non-delegable duty** because it has assumed responsibility for them, they are in its care, and it has delegated an integral part of that function to a contractor (Woodland v Swimming Teachers Association). Choosing a competent contractor is no defence, and the duty does not depend on where the lesson takes place."},
 {"t":"defences","q":"After an afternoon drinking together, Kieran agrees to go up in a small light aircraft piloted by his friend Tony, who he knows has drunk heavily and can barely stand. Tony crashes the plane through his drunken flying and Kieran is seriously injured.\n\nWhich defence is Tony most likely to rely on successfully?","o":["None, because the Road Traffic Act 1988 prevents volenti being used against a passenger.","Contributory negligence only, because volenti can never apply to a passenger in any vehicle, whether on land or in the air.","Illegality, because it is a crime to be a passenger in an aircraft flown by a drunk pilot.","Volenti, because Kieran freely accepted the obvious risk of flying with a very drunk pilot.","None, because Kieran's own drinking prevented him from properly understanding the risk."],"a":3,"e":"**Volenti** (consent) applies where the claimant, with full knowledge of the risk, freely agrees to accept it, including the risk of the defendant's negligence. Flying with an obviously very drunk pilot is a clear case (Morris v Murray). The Road Traffic Act 1988 s.149 bar on volenti applies only to vehicles that need compulsory motor insurance, not aircraft. Kieran was not so drunk that he could not appreciate the risk."},
 {"t":"defences","q":"Two brothers, both experienced shot-firers employed by a quarry company, decide to test detonators without taking cover, against their employer's express orders and in breach of statutory safety rules, because the proper method takes longer. An explosion injures one brother, who sues the employer, claiming it is vicariously liable for his brother's negligence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the most likely outcome?","o":["The claim succeeds in full, because volenti can never be used against an employee.","The claim succeeds, but damages are reduced by 50% for his contributory negligence.","The claim fails because the employer was not at fault and gave clear safety orders.","The claim succeeds in full, because the employer is vicariously liable for any breach of statutory rules.","The claim fails because of volenti, because he freely and knowingly agreed to the risk with his brother."],"a":4,"e":"Volenti rarely succeeds against employees, because economic pressure usually means they do not truly agree to risks. But where experienced employees **freely and deliberately** agree together to disobey orders and safety rules, a vicarious claim by one for the other's negligence is barred by volenti (ICI v Shatwell). The employer's lack of personal fault would not defeat a vicarious claim by itself."},
 {"t":"defences","q":"A horse-drawn delivery cart is negligently left unattended in a busy street by its driver. The horses bolt towards a crowd including children. Pete, a police officer, runs out and stops the horses but is trampled and injured. The defendant argues that Pete consented to the risk or was contributorily negligent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Pete's position?","o":["He recovers nothing, because he voluntarily accepted the risk of injury by running towards the horses.","He recovers reduced damages, because he chose to put himself in danger.","He recovers full damages, because a rescuer acting reasonably is neither volenti nor at fault.","He recovers nothing, because his act of rescue broke the chain of causation.","He recovers only if he was under a legal duty, as a police officer, to stop the horses."],"a":2,"e":"The courts are sympathetic to **rescuers**: a person who acts reasonably to rescue others from danger created by the defendant's negligence is not treated as consenting (volenti) or as breaking the chain of causation (Haynes v Harwood). Contributory negligence is rarely found against a rescuer unless he acted with a wholly unreasonable disregard for his own safety. A moral or social duty to act is enough; a legal duty is not required."},
 {"t":"defences","q":"Lily, aged 8, runs out between parked cars into the road to chase a ball and is hit by a car driven negligently fast by Ian. Ian argues that Lily's damages should be reduced because she did not look before running into the road.\n\nHow will the court assess whether Lily was contributorily negligent?","o":["By asking whether a reasonable adult pedestrian would have looked before running into the road.","By deciding that a child under 10 can never be contributorily negligent.","By asking what degree of care could reasonably be expected of an ordinary 8-year-old child.","By asking whether Lily's parents were negligent in allowing her to play near the road.","By reducing her damages by a fixed 25%, because she failed to look before crossing."],"a":2,"e":"A child's contributory negligence is judged by the standard of an **ordinary child of the same age**, not an adult. Young children are often found not contributorily negligent at all, or only to a small extent (Gough v Thorne). There is no fixed age below which it can never apply, any parental negligence is not attributed to the child, and the 25% figure is the guideline for seatbelt cases where the injury would have been prevented altogether."},
 {"t":"defences","q":"Nora is a front-seat passenger in a car driven by Hal. Hal negligently crashes into a wall. Nora was not wearing a seatbelt. Medical evidence shows that, because of the way the car was struck, her injuries would have been exactly the same if she had been wearing one.\n\nHow will her failure to wear a seatbelt affect her damages?","o":["It will not reduce them, because her failure made no difference to the injuries she suffered.","It will reduce them by 25%, because she did not wear a seatbelt as the law requires, whatever difference it would have made.","It will reduce them by 15%, because she failed to take care for her own safety.","It will defeat her claim completely, because she broke the law by not wearing a seatbelt.","It will reduce them by 50%, because she and Hal were equally responsible for her injuries."],"a":0,"e":"Contributory negligence reduces damages only where the claimant's fault **contributed to the damage**. The guideline figures are 25% where a belt would have prevented the injury altogether and 15% where it would have made the injury less severe; where it would have made **no difference**, there is **no reduction** (Froom v Butcher). Failing to wear a belt is not a bar to the claim."},
 {"t":"defences","q":"Jack is injured in a collision between his motorcycle and a car driven by Ruth. The judge finds that Ruth was negligent, but that Jack was riding very fast and was overwhelmingly to blame. Ruth's lawyer asks the court to reduce Jack's damages by 100% for contributory negligence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will respond?","o":["It will agree, because a court may reduce damages by any amount that reflects the claimant's share of the blame.","It will agree, because a claimant who is mainly to blame for an accident cannot recover anything.","It will refuse, because reductions for contributory negligence cannot exceed 50%.","It will refuse, because contributory negligence does not apply to road traffic accidents.","It will refuse, because if Ruth is liable at all, a 100% reduction for contributory negligence is not possible."],"a":4,"e":"Under the Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945, damages are reduced to the extent the court thinks **just and equitable**, having regard to the claimant's share in the responsibility. A **100% reduction** is not possible because it would mean the defendant was not responsible at all, which contradicts a finding of liability (Pitts v Hunt). There is no 50% ceiling, so a very large reduction (for example 75% or more) is possible."},
 {"t":"defences","q":"Sid, aged 18, rides pillion on a motorcycle driven by his friend Joe, who has no licence and has been drinking. Throughout the ride Sid encourages Joe to drive fast and to frighten other road users, and both are laughing. Joe crashes and Sid is injured. Sid sues Joe.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["The claim succeeds in full, because a passenger cannot be blamed for the way a driver behaves.","The claim fails for illegality, because they were engaged in a joint criminal enterprise.","The claim fails because of volenti, because Sid knew Joe had been drinking.","The claim succeeds, but damages are reduced by 25% for Sid's contributory negligence.","The claim succeeds in full, because Joe owed Sid a duty of care as his passenger."],"a":1,"e":"Where the claimant and defendant are engaged in a **joint criminal enterprise** and the claimant is injured in the course of it, the claim may be barred by **illegality** (Pitts v Hunt, where a pillion passenger encouraged reckless driving). Volenti cannot be used against a passenger in a vehicle requiring compulsory insurance (Road Traffic Act 1988 s.149). Contributory negligence is relevant only if the claim is not barred altogether."},
 {"t":"defences","q":"Ada is driving to work when a van driven negligently by Bill hits her car, injuring her. Ada's car tax had expired the previous week, and she was therefore committing a minor offence by driving. Bill argues that Ada cannot claim because she was acting illegally at the time.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ada's position?","o":["Her claim is barred, because no one can recover damages for injuries suffered while committing an offence.","Her claim is not barred, because her minor offence was incidental and did not cause her injuries.","Her claim is barred, because the defence of illegality applies to all criminal offences, however minor.","Her claim succeeds, but her damages are reduced by 25% because of her offence.","Her claim is barred, unless she pays the unpaid car tax before issuing proceedings."],"a":1,"e":"**Illegality** bars a claim where allowing it would harm the integrity of the legal system, considering the purpose of the rule broken, other public policies and **proportionality** (Patel v Mirza). A minor offence that is merely **incidental** to the injury, and did not cause it, does not bar a claim. It is not contributory negligence either, because it did not contribute to her damage."},
 {"t":"defences","q":"Mary is watching a horse show from a spectators' area when a top rider, competing at speed, misjudges a corner and his horse veers into the area, injuring her. The rider made an error of judgement in the heat of competition, but was not reckless.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome of Mary's claim against the rider?","o":["It will probably fail, because an error of judgement in competition is unlikely to be a breach.","It will fail, because spectators consent to all risks of injury at sporting events.","It will succeed, because a competitor owes spectators a duty to avoid all accidents.","It will succeed, because the rider's error caused the horse to enter the spectators' area.","It will fail, because a competitor owes no duty of care to spectators at all."],"a":0,"e":"A competitor owes spectators a duty of care, but in the **heat of competition** the standard takes account of the circumstances: an **error of judgement** is unlikely to be a breach unless it shows a **reckless disregard** for the spectators' safety (Wooldridge v Sumner). Spectators do not consent to the risk of a competitor's negligence, so volenti is not the reason the claim fails."},
 {"t":"defences","q":"Eve is hit by a car driven negligently by Owen as she steps out to cross a road without looking. The court assesses her full damages at £60,000 and finds that she was 30% responsible for her injuries.\n\nWhat will Eve recover?","o":["£60,000.","£18,000.","£30,000.","£42,000.","Nothing."],"a":3,"e":"Under the Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945, the court first assesses **full damages** and then reduces them by the claimant's share of responsibility. A 30% reduction from £60,000 is £18,000, so Eve recovers **£42,000**. £18,000 is the amount of the reduction, not the award, and contributory negligence reduces damages but never defeats the claim entirely."},
 {"t":"remedies","q":"Dan, aged 40, is left unable to work after an accident caused by Kim's negligence. He earned £35,000 a year gross, which was £27,000 a year after income tax and National Insurance. He would have worked for another 27 years. The appropriate Ogden multiplier for his future loss of earnings is 18.\n\nWhat is the correct figure for his future loss of earnings?","o":["£630,000.","£486,000.","£729,000.","£945,000.","£540,000."],"a":1,"e":"Future loss of earnings is calculated by the **multiplicand** (the **net** annual loss, after tax and National Insurance: £27,000) multiplied by the **multiplier** (18), giving **£486,000**. Using gross earnings (£35,000 x 18 = £630,000) or the number of years of loss (27) is wrong: the multiplier is lower than the number of years because the money is received early and can be invested, and life is uncertain."},
 {"t":"remedies","q":"Mrs Bell suffers serious injuries caused by a negligent driver. Her daughter, Jo, gives up her part-time job for a year to care for her at home, without payment. Mrs Bell claims the value of Jo's care as part of her damages.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Mrs Bell cannot recover anything, because Jo provided the care free of charge.","Mrs Bell cannot recover anything for the care, but Jo can bring her own claim against the driver for her lost earnings.","Mrs Bell can recover the value of the care, which she may keep for herself.","Mrs Bell can recover the reasonable value of the care, which she holds on trust for Jo.","Mrs Bell can recover only Jo's lost earnings, not the value of the care."],"a":3,"e":"The reasonable value of **gratuitous care** given by family or friends is recoverable by the injured claimant, who holds it **on trust** for the carer (Hunt v Severs). The carer has no claim of her own against the defendant. The award is assessed by reference to the value of the services, often taking into account the carer's lost earnings, but is not limited to them. It would not be recoverable if the carer were the defendant."},
 {"t":"remedies","q":"Sam is injured in a cycling accident caused by a negligent motorist. He chooses to have physiotherapy and an operation privately, at a cost of £12,000, instead of waiting for NHS treatment. The defendant's insurer says it will not pay for private treatment because NHS treatment was available.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sam's position?","o":["He can recover the reasonable cost of private treatment, even though NHS treatment was available.","He cannot recover the cost, because he should have used the free NHS treatment that was available to mitigate his loss.","He can recover only what the NHS treatment would have cost the NHS.","He can recover the cost only if the NHS waiting list was longer than six months.","He cannot recover the cost, because private medical expenses are not a recognised head of loss."],"a":0,"e":"A claimant may recover the reasonable cost of **private medical treatment**, and the possibility of using the NHS is disregarded (Law Reform (Personal Injuries) Act 1948 s.2(4)). Choosing private treatment is not a failure to mitigate. There is no waiting-list threshold."},
 {"t":"remedies","q":"Ruby suffers a head injury in an accident caused by a negligent driver. She has largely recovered, but medical experts say there is a 10% chance that she will develop epilepsy as a result of the injury at some point in the future. Her case is about to be settled.\n\nWhich of the following would best protect Ruby's position?","o":["An award of 10% of the damages she would receive if she had epilepsy, added to her lump sum.","An award of full damages for epilepsy now, because the risk is real and foreseeable.","An award of periodical payments, which can be increased if she develops epilepsy.","An award of provisional damages, allowing her to return to court for more if she develops epilepsy.","No award for the risk, because a 10% chance is less than the balance of probabilities."],"a":3,"e":"Where there is a chance that the claimant will develop a **serious disease** or suffer a **serious deterioration** in future, the court can award **provisional damages**: damages now on the assumption it will not happen, with the right to apply for further damages if it does (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.32A). This avoids under- or over-compensating her with a guess. Periodical payments deal with uncertainty about the length of future losses, not a risk of deterioration."},
 {"t":"remedies","q":"Faisal injures his back in an accident caused by his employer's negligence. Doctors recommend a routine, low-risk operation that would very probably allow him to return to his job within six months. He refuses, saying he does not like hospitals, and claims loss of earnings for the rest of his working life.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how his refusal is likely to affect his damages?","o":["He will not recover losses he could reasonably have avoided, if his refusal was unreasonable.","It will not affect his damages, because a claimant cannot be required to undergo medical treatment.","It will defeat his claim entirely, because he has failed to mitigate his loss.","It will reduce his damages by a fixed 25% for contributory negligence.","It will not affect his damages unless he caused the original accident."],"a":0,"e":"A claimant must take **reasonable steps to mitigate** his loss, which can include accepting reasonable medical treatment. Losses that could reasonably have been avoided are not recoverable, so if his refusal is unreasonable he will not recover losses after the date he would probably have returned to work. The burden is on the defendant to show the refusal was unreasonable. Failure to mitigate does not defeat the whole claim and is different from contributory negligence."},
 {"t":"remedies","q":"Hettie, aged 30, suffers catastrophic brain injuries in an accident caused by a negligent driver. She is permanently in a state of very limited awareness, does not feel pain and is unable to appreciate what she has lost.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her damages for non-pecuniary loss?","o":["She can recover nothing, because she is unaware of her condition and cannot suffer.","She can recover for loss of amenity, assessed objectively, but not for pain she does not feel.","She can recover in full for both pain and suffering and loss of amenity.","She can recover for pain and suffering only, because loss of amenity depends on her awareness.","She can recover only the costs of her care, not any non-pecuniary damages."],"a":1,"e":"Damages for **pain and suffering** depend on the claimant's awareness, so a claimant who feels no pain recovers nothing for that element (Lim Poh Choo). **Loss of amenity** (loss of the enjoyment of life) is assessed **objectively**, so it is recoverable even if she cannot appreciate the loss (H West & Son v Shephard). She will also recover pecuniary losses such as care costs."},
 {"t":"remedies","q":"Liam, aged 45, is killed instantly by a negligent driver. He earned £50,000 a year and supported his wife, Ana, who does not work. Ana wants to recover for the income Liam would have provided to the household over the coming years.\n\nUnder which claim can this loss best be recovered?","o":["A claim by Liam's estate under the Law Reform Act 1934 for his future lost earnings.","A claim by Ana for bereavement damages, which compensate for lost financial support.","A claim by Liam's estate for the earnings he would have made during the lost years.","A claim by Ana in her own right for psychiatric harm caused by losing her husband.","A claim by the dependants under the Fatal Accidents Act 1976 for loss of dependency."],"a":4,"e":"The financial support a deceased person would have provided is recovered by the **dependants** under the **Fatal Accidents Act 1976** as **loss of dependency** (the deceased's net income less what he would have spent on himself, times a multiplier). The **estate** under the 1934 Act can claim only losses up to death and funeral costs, not the lost years. **Bereavement damages** are a fixed sum for grief, not financial support."},
 {"t":"remedies","q":"Jess is killed by a negligent driver. She was not married but had lived with her partner, Sean, as a couple in the same household for three years before her death. She also leaves her mother, who lived nearby. Sean claims bereavement damages.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to bereavement damages?","o":["No one is entitled, because Jess was an adult who was not married or in a civil partnership.","Jess's mother is entitled, because she is Jess's closest blood relative.","Sean is entitled, because he lived with Jess as a couple for at least two years before her death.","Sean and Jess's mother share the award equally.","Sean is entitled only if he can prove he was financially dependent on Jess."],"a":2,"e":"Since 2020, a **cohabiting partner** who lived with the deceased as a couple in the same household for at least **two years** immediately before the death can claim **bereavement damages** under the Fatal Accidents Act 1976. Parents can claim only for an **unmarried minor** child, so Jess's mother (Jess being an adult) cannot. Financial dependency is relevant to a dependency claim, not to bereavement."},
 {"t":"remedies","q":"Owen was injured in a playground accident caused by the council's negligence on 1 June 2017, when he was 10 years old. He knew about his injury and its cause from the start. He is now 19.\n\nBy what date must he normally issue his personal injury claim?","o":["By 1 June 2020, three years after the accident.","By 1 June 2023, six years after the accident.","By his 25th birthday, because time runs for seven years from his 18th birthday.","At any time, because there is no limitation period for injuries to children.","By his 21st birthday, because time does not start to run until he reaches 18."],"a":4,"e":"The personal injury limitation period is **three years** (Limitation Act 1980 s.11). Time does not run against a **child** (under 18) until they reach **18** (s.28), so Owen has until his **21st birthday**. The six-year period applies to non-personal-injury claims in tort, and the court also has a discretion to extend time in personal injury cases (s.33)."},
 {"t":"remedies","q":"In March 2021, a builder negligently fitted a water pipe in Jamal's house. In June 2021 the pipe leaked and caused flood damage to his kitchen. No one was injured. Jamal has delayed taking action and now, in November 2026, wants to sue the builder in negligence for the property damage.\n\nIs Jamal's claim in time?","o":["No, because negligence claims must be brought within three years of the damage.","No, because time ran from March 2021, when the negligent work was done.","Yes, because negligence claims for property damage must be brought within six years of the damage.","Yes, because there is no time limit for claims in negligence for damage to property.","No, because a claim for property damage must be brought within one year of discovering it."],"a":2,"e":"A claim in tort for **property damage** (not personal injury) must be brought within **six years** (Limitation Act 1980 s.2). In negligence, time runs from when the **damage** occurred (June 2021), not when the careless act happened, so he has until June 2027. The three-year period applies to personal injury claims."},
 {"t":"occupiers-liability","q":"Mr Grant owns a block of flats and lets each flat to a tenant, but keeps control of the common staircase, which he is responsible for maintaining. Leah, visiting a tenant, trips on a loose stair rod on the common staircase and is injured.\n\nWho is most likely to owe Leah the common duty of care as occupier of the staircase?","o":["The tenant she was visiting, because she was the tenant's visitor.","No one, because a common staircase used by many people has no occupier.","All the tenants of the block jointly, because they all use the staircase.","The local authority, because the staircase is a public place.","Mr Grant, because he retained control of the common staircase."],"a":4,"e":"An **occupier** is anyone with a sufficient degree of **control** over premises; it does not depend on ownership or physical presence, and there can be more than one occupier (Wheat v E Lacon & Co). A landlord who retains control of **common parts** is the occupier of those parts. Leah was a lawful visitor to the building, so she is owed the common duty of care under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957."},
 {"t":"occupiers-liability","q":"Kim is a customer in a department store. Looking for a toilet, she ignores a door clearly marked \"Staff Only, No Customers\" and goes through into a storeroom, where she trips over a box left on the floor and is injured.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kim's status and the law that applies?","o":["She was a visitor throughout, because she entered the store lawfully as a customer.","She was a visitor, because she was looking for a toilet, which the store provides for customers, so she stayed within her permission.","She was a trespasser, so the store owed her no duty of care of any kind.","She was a trespasser in the storeroom, so any duty arises only under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984.","She was a visitor under the 1957 Act, but her damages will be reduced because she ignored the sign."],"a":3,"e":"A visitor who goes beyond the area of her permission becomes a **trespasser** for that part of the premises. A clearly marked \"Staff Only\" door limits the customer's permission, so in the storeroom Kim was a trespasser and the **Occupiers' Liability Act 1984** applies, with its narrower duty. Trespassers can be owed a duty under the 1984 Act if its conditions are met, so it is wrong to say no duty at all."},
 {"t":"occupiers-liability","q":"A council park contains a bush with shiny red berries that look like cherries but are highly poisonous. The bush is not fenced and there is no warning sign. The council knows children play in the park. Max, aged 7, eats some berries and becomes seriously ill.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the council's position?","o":["It is likely to be liable, because the berries were an allurement and children are less careful.","It is not liable, because Max should have known not to eat berries from a bush in a public park.","It is not liable, because the berries were a natural feature of the park, not a danger created by the council.","It is not liable, because Max became a trespasser when he picked berries he had no permission to take.","It is liable only if Max's parents were with him and could not have prevented him eating the berries."],"a":0,"e":"Under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 s.2(3)(a), an occupier must be prepared for **children to be less careful than adults**. An attractive danger that tempts children (an **allurement**), such as poisonous berries that look like cherries, requires greater precautions (Glasgow Corporation v Taylor). Natural features can still create liability, and picking the berries did not turn Max into a trespasser in the park."},
 {"t":"occupiers-liability","q":"A housing developer is building on an open site where local people often walk. Five-year-old Billy, out alone in the evening, falls into a trench and breaks his leg. The trench would be an obvious danger to an adult or older child. Local parents generally keep young children with them in the area.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the developer's position?","o":["It is liable, because an occupier must make its land completely safe for children of any age.","It is likely not liable, because it could expect parents not to let very young children go there alone.","It is liable, because Billy was too young to appreciate the danger of the trench.","It is not liable, because a child aged five cannot be a visitor under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957.","It is liable, because an occupier is responsible for all injuries to children on its land."],"a":1,"e":"In deciding what is reasonable towards very young children, an occupier may take into account that their **parents** are primarily responsible for them and would not normally let them go unaccompanied to obviously dangerous places (Phipps v Rochester Corporation). Where the occupier reasonably expects parents to supervise, it is not in breach for failing to guard against dangers to an unaccompanied young child. Young children can be visitors."},
 {"t":"occupiers-liability","q":"Josh, aged 30, is walking along a harbour wall. The wall's surface is covered in seaweed that is plainly slippery, and there is sea on both sides. There is no warning sign. Josh slips, falls onto rocks and is injured.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the harbour owner is liable to Josh under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957?","o":["It is liable, because it failed to put up a sign warning visitors that the harbour wall was slippery.","It is probably not liable, because there is no duty to warn an adult visitor about a risk that is obvious.","It is liable, because it should have cleaned the seaweed from the whole of the harbour wall.","It is not liable, because Josh was a trespasser on the harbour wall.","It is liable, but Josh's damages will be reduced by 50% because the risk was obvious."],"a":1,"e":"An occupier is generally **not required to warn** adult visitors of dangers that are **obvious**, such as a slippery, seaweed-covered harbour wall (Staples v West Dorset DC). The common duty of care requires reasonable care, not the removal of all natural risks. There is nothing to suggest Josh was a trespasser."},
 {"t":"occupiers-liability","q":"Pulse Gym Ltd displays a large notice at its entrance, also included in its membership terms, stating: \"The gym accepts no liability for any injury to members, however caused.\" Nia, a member, is injured when a weights machine collapses because the gym negligently failed to maintain it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the notice?","o":["It excludes liability, because Nia agreed to the membership terms and saw the notice.","It excludes liability, provided it is reasonable in all the circumstances.","It excludes liability, because it was prominently displayed at the entrance to the gym.","It has no effect, because a business cannot exclude liability for negligently caused injury.","It reduces the gym's liability by half, because Nia was warned that it accepted no liability."],"a":3,"e":"A business cannot exclude or restrict liability for **death or personal injury** caused by **negligence**: for a consumer such as a gym member, under the **Consumer Rights Act 2015 s.65** (and for non-consumers, the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 s.2(1)). The reasonableness test applies only to other kinds of loss, such as property damage. Prominence and agreement do not save such a term."},
 {"t":"occupiers-liability","q":"Vista Council hires a cleaning company to clean the steps of its town hall in winter. The cleaner leaves the steps covered in a thin film of ice. A visitor, Erin, slips and is injured. Anyone glancing at the steps could have seen they were icy. The council made no check after the cleaning.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the council's position?","o":["It is not liable, because it entrusted the cleaning to an independent contractor.","It is not liable, because the cleaning company is solely responsible for its own negligence.","It is not liable, because ice on steps in winter is an act of nature beyond its control.","It is liable only if it failed to check that the cleaning company was insured.","It is likely to be liable, because checking the work was easy and required no technical knowledge."],"a":4,"e":"Under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 s.2(4)(b), an occupier is not liable for a contractor's faulty work if it acted reasonably in entrusting the work, chose a competent contractor, and took reasonable steps to check the work was properly done. Where checking is **easy** and needs no specialist knowledge, as with icy steps, the occupier should have checked (Woodward v Mayor of Hastings), unlike technical work such as lift repairs."},
 {"t":"occupiers-liability","q":"A port company owns a slipway into a harbour. In summer, people sometimes swim from it, and the company knows this. At midnight in December, Tom climbs onto the slipway, dives into the water and hits a submerged object, suffering serious injury. The company had no reason to think anyone would be there at that time of night in winter.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the company owed Tom a duty under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984?","o":["It owed a duty, because it knew that people sometimes swam from the slipway.","It owed a duty, because the submerged object was a hidden danger, not an obvious one.","It probably owed no duty, because it had no reason to expect anyone there at that time.","It owed no duty, because trespassers are never owed a duty under the 1984 Act.","It owed a duty, but Tom's damages will be reduced because he was a trespasser."],"a":2,"e":"Under the 1984 Act s.1(3), a duty arises only if the occupier is aware of the danger, **knows or has reasonable grounds to believe** that a trespasser is or may come into its vicinity, and the risk is one it could reasonably be expected to protect against. This is judged at the **time** of the injury: summer swimming did not give reason to expect someone at midnight in mid-winter (Donoghue v Folkestone Properties). Trespassers can be owed a duty when all three conditions are met."},
 {"t":"occupiers-liability","q":"A power company surrounds an electricity substation with a high fence topped with barbed wire, and puts up large signs reading \"DANGER OF DEATH: HIGH VOLTAGE. KEEP OUT\" at regular intervals. Ryan, aged 25, climbs the fence to retrieve a drone and is badly burned.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the company's liability to Ryan under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984?","o":["It is liable, because the electricity in the substation was an extremely serious danger that could kill anyone who came into contact with it.","It is liable, because it must make its premises safe for anyone who might enter them.","It is probably not liable, because it took reasonable steps to warn and discourage entry.","It is liable, because Ryan had a good reason for entering, namely to retrieve his drone.","It is not liable, because the 1984 Act does not apply to adult trespassers."],"a":2,"e":"Under the 1984 Act s.1(4) and (5), where a duty arises, it is to take reasonable care to see that the trespasser is not injured, and it can be discharged by taking reasonable steps to **give warning** of the danger or to **discourage** people from incurring the risk. A high fence with barbed wire and clear danger signs is likely to be enough for an adult. His reason for entering does not change that, and the Act protects adult trespassers too."},
 {"t":"occupiers-liability","q":"A public footpath crosses Farmer Hughes's field. The surface of the path has become rutted through ordinary wear. Lena, walking along the footpath, trips in a rut and breaks her ankle. She wants to claim against Farmer Hughes as occupier.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Lena's position?","o":["She is owed no duty under the 1957 Act, because users of a public right of way are not visitors.","She is owed the common duty of care, because she was lawfully on the land.","She is owed the common duty of care, because Farmer Hughes was aware that people used the path.","She is owed a duty only if Farmer Hughes had given her express permission to walk on the path.","She is owed the common duty of care, but her damages will be reduced because ruts are obvious."],"a":0,"e":"A person using a **public right of way** is exercising a public right, not the occupier's permission, so they are **not a visitor** under the 1957 Act (McGeown v NI Housing Executive). The 1984 Act also excludes people using the highway, so the landowner owes no occupiers' liability duty for ordinary wear of the path surface. Being lawfully on land does not by itself make someone a visitor."},
 {"t":"product-liability","q":"Megan buys a sealed bottle of lemonade from a corner shop and gives it to her friend Ivy. Ivy drinks it and becomes ill because a decomposed insect was in the bottle. The shop could not have seen inside the opaque, sealed bottle. Ivy wants to sue in negligence.\n\nWho is Ivy's best defendant in negligence?","o":["The shop, because it sold the lemonade and so is responsible for its condition.","Megan, because she bought the lemonade and gave it to Ivy.","No one, because Ivy did not buy the lemonade and so has no contract with anyone.","The manufacturer, because no intermediate examination of the sealed bottle was possible.","The shop, because a retailer is strictly liable in negligence for goods it sells."],"a":3,"e":"A manufacturer owes a duty of care to the **ultimate consumer** where the product reaches them in the form it left the manufacturer, with **no reasonable possibility of intermediate examination** (Donoghue v Stevenson). The shop could not inspect a sealed opaque bottle, so it was not negligent. No contract is needed in negligence, and there is no strict liability in negligence."},
 {"t":"product-liability","q":"Roy buys a powerful oven cleaner. The label clearly states, in large print: \"Corrosive. Always wear rubber gloves. Causes severe burns to skin.\" Roy uses it without gloves and suffers burns to his hands. He sues the producer under the Consumer Protection Act 1987, arguing the product is defective.\n\nWhich of the following best describes his claim?","o":["It will succeed, because the product caused him injury and the Act imposes strict liability.","It will probably fail, because, given the clear warnings, the product was not defective.","It will succeed, because a product that can burn skin is defective whatever warnings it carries.","It will fail, because Roy voluntarily accepted the risk by reading the label.","It will succeed, but his damages will be reduced by 25% for not wearing gloves."],"a":1,"e":"Under the Consumer Protection Act 1987 s.3, a product is **defective** if its safety is not such as persons generally are entitled to expect, taking into account all the circumstances, including any **instructions and warnings**. A necessarily corrosive cleaner with clear warnings is likely to meet that expectation, so it is not defective. Strict liability removes the need to prove fault, but not the need to prove a defect."},
 {"t":"product-liability","q":"Alan buys a branded cordless drill from Toolbox, a DIY shop. A defect causes it to explode, injuring him. The drill has no information about who made or imported it. Alan writes to Toolbox within a reasonable time asking it to identify the producer or the business that supplied it to the shop. Toolbox does not reply.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Toolbox's liability under the Consumer Protection Act 1987?","o":["It is liable, because it failed to identify its supplier or the producer within a reasonable time when asked.","It is not liable, because a retailer is never liable under the Act for selling a defective product that someone else made.","It is not liable, because it did not manufacture the drill or put its own name on it.","It is liable, because every supplier in the chain is liable under the Act for any defect.","It is liable only if it knew, or ought to have known, that the drill was defective."],"a":0,"e":"Under the Consumer Protection Act 1987 s.2(3), a **supplier** (such as a retailer) becomes liable if it is asked by the injured person, within a reasonable time, to identify the producer, own-brander, importer or its own supplier, and **fails to do so** within a reasonable time. Simply selling a product does not make a retailer liable, which is why the answer depends on its failure to reply. Liability is strict, so knowledge of the defect is irrelevant."},
 {"t":"product-liability","q":"Gita buys a defective toaster for her own home. A fault causes it to catch fire, scorching a kitchen cupboard. The cost of repairing the cupboard is £250. Gita is not injured. She wants to sue the producer under the Consumer Protection Act 1987.\n\nWhat can Gita recover under the Act?","o":["£250 for the cupboard, because it is private property used in her home.","£250 for the cupboard and the cost of the toaster itself.","Nothing, because property damage claims of £275 or less cannot be brought under the Act.","The cost of the toaster only, because it was the defective product.","£250 less an excess of £275, which means she must pay the difference to the producer."],"a":2,"e":"Under the Consumer Protection Act 1987 s.5(4), no damages are recoverable for **property damage** unless the amount claimed **exceeds £275**. Damage to the defective product itself is never recoverable under the Act. Gita may instead consider a claim in **negligence** against the producer (where there is no threshold, but she must prove fault), or against the seller in contract."},
 {"t":"product-liability","q":"In 2014, Medico Ltd put into circulation a batch of defective heart valves. A valve is implanted in Pat in 2015. It fails, injuring her, in 2026, more than 11 years after it was put into circulation. She learns of the defect immediately and wants to sue Medico Ltd.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her position?","o":["She may sue under the Consumer Protection Act 1987, because she issued within three years of the injury.","She may sue under the Consumer Protection Act 1987 if the court exercises its discretion to extend time.","A claim under the Consumer Protection Act 1987 is barred by the 10-year longstop, but she may sue in negligence.","She cannot sue at all, because more than 10 years have passed since the valve was put into circulation.","She may sue under the Consumer Protection Act 1987, because the 10-year period runs from implantation."],"a":2,"e":"A claim under the Consumer Protection Act 1987 is subject to an absolute **10-year longstop** running from when the product was **put into circulation**, which the court's s.33 discretion cannot override (Limitation Act 1980 s.11A). Her CPA claim is therefore barred. But a claim in **negligence** for personal injury is subject only to the three-year period from injury or knowledge, so it remains open, although she must prove fault."},
 {"t":"product-liability","q":"Purely Juice Ltd produces bottled smoothies with tamper-evident seals. After the bottles leave the factory and are on a supermarket shelf, a customer secretly opens one and adds a harmful substance, then replaces the cap. Another customer, Hugo, buys it and is made ill. He sues Purely Juice Ltd under the Consumer Protection Act 1987.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Purely Juice Ltd's position?","o":["It is liable, because the Act imposes strict liability on producers for all defects.","It is liable, because it should have designed a seal that could not be tampered with.","It has a defence only if it can show that it took all reasonable care in producing the drink.","It is liable, but can recover a contribution from the supermarket that sold the drink.","It has a defence, because the defect did not exist in the product when it supplied it."],"a":4,"e":"Under the Consumer Protection Act 1987 s.4(1)(d), it is a defence for the producer to show that the **defect did not exist** in the product at the relevant time, meaning when it supplied the product. Tampering after it left the producer creates the defect later. Strict liability means fault need not be proved, but this specific defence is available. Taking reasonable care is not itself a defence under the Act."},
 {"t":"product-liability","q":"Volt Ltd manufactures a brake component for Carco Ltd's new car, exactly to Carco Ltd's design and specification. The design is flawed, and the component fails, causing a crash that injures the driver, Fay. She sues Volt Ltd and Carco Ltd under the Consumer Protection Act 1987.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Volt Ltd's position?","o":["It is liable, because it produced the defective component that caused the crash.","It is liable jointly with Carco Ltd, because both companies are producers of the defective product that caused the crash.","It has a defence only if it can show that it did not know of the design flaw.","It has a defence, because the defect is wholly due to the design of the car it made the part for.","It is not liable, because component manufacturers are not producers under the Act."],"a":3,"e":"Component manufacturers are **producers** under the Act, but s.4(1)(f) gives a defence where the defect is **wholly attributable to the design of the subsequent product** (the car) or to compliance with instructions given by its producer. Because Volt Ltd followed Carco Ltd's flawed design exactly, it has a defence, and Carco Ltd remains liable. Knowledge is irrelevant under the Act."},
 {"t":"product-liability","q":"Adam buys woollen underwear from a shop and develops severe dermatitis. Tests show that the underwear contained an excess of a chemical used in its manufacture. Adam cannot say exactly what went wrong in the manufacturer's process. He sues the manufacturer in negligence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Adam can establish breach of duty?","o":["He must identify the precise step in the manufacturing process that was carried out negligently, and by whom.","The court may infer negligence from the presence of the excess chemical, without his proving exactly how it occurred.","He need not prove anything, because manufacturers are strictly liable in negligence.","He can establish breach only if the manufacturer has been convicted of a criminal offence.","He cannot establish breach, because he did not buy the underwear directly from the manufacturer."],"a":1,"e":"In negligence, the claimant must prove breach, but where a defect of this kind exists in a product, the court may **infer** that it resulted from negligence in manufacture, without the claimant showing exactly what went wrong (Grant v Australian Knitting Mills). The manufacturer can try to rebut the inference. Negligence is not strict liability (that is the advantage of the Consumer Protection Act), and no contract is needed."},
 {"t":"product-liability","q":"Nell buys a defective electric scooter. While her neighbour, Rob, is walking past on the pavement, the scooter's battery explodes and Rob is burned. Rob never bought or used the scooter. He sues the producer under the Consumer Protection Act 1987.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rob's claim?","o":["He cannot claim, because only the person who bought the product can claim under the Act.","He cannot claim, because only the user of the product can claim under the Act.","He can claim only in negligence, because the Act is limited to consumers of the product.","He can claim only if Nell first brings a claim for her own losses under the Act.","He can claim, because the Act protects anyone who suffers damage caused by a defective product."],"a":4,"e":"Under the Consumer Protection Act 1987 s.2(1), where damage is caused by a defect in a product, the producer is liable to **any person** who suffers that damage, not only buyers or users. A bystander injured by a defective product can therefore claim. Rob needs no contract and does not depend on Nell bringing a claim."},
 {"t":"product-liability","q":"Kerry's car was made and put into circulation by AutoMax Ltd in 2018. It met all safety standards at the time. In 2023 AutoMax Ltd introduced a new automatic emergency braking system on its later models. In 2026 Kerry crashes in circumstances where the new system might have reduced her injuries. She argues that her car is defective under the Consumer Protection Act 1987.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her argument?","o":["It is likely to fail, because a product is not defective merely because safer products were put into circulation later.","It is likely to succeed, because her car was less safe than the later models made by the same producer.","It is likely to succeed, because people are entitled to expect cars to have the latest safety features.","It is likely to succeed, because liability under the Act is strict once a product causes injury.","It is likely to fail, because the Act applies only to defects in a product's manufacture, not to defects in its design."],"a":0,"e":"In deciding whether a product is defective, the court considers the safety people were entitled to expect **at the time the producer supplied it**, and the Consumer Protection Act 1987 s.3(2) provides that a product is **not** to be treated as defective merely because a **safer product** was put into circulation **later**. Strict liability still requires proof of a defect, and the Act covers design defects as well as manufacturing defects."},
 {"t":"nuisance-rylands","q":"Dana runs a business that stores specialist photographic paper, which is extremely sensitive to heat. Her neighbour, Ian, uses an ordinary heating system in his cellar. The warmth that passes through the wall would not affect ordinary paper or goods, but it damages Dana's paper.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Dana can succeed in private nuisance?","o":["She will probably fail, because only her abnormally sensitive use is affected.","She will succeed, because Ian's heating caused physical damage to her property.","She will succeed, because the heat passed from Ian's land into hers.","She will succeed, because Ian's heating was a continuing interference with her land.","She will fail, because heat cannot be the basis of a claim in private nuisance."],"a":0,"e":"An interference is not a nuisance if it would not have affected an **ordinary use** of the property and harms only an **abnormally sensitive** use (Robinson v Kilvert, where heat from a neighbour's cellar damaged sensitive brown paper). Physical damage or an emanation across the boundary does not by itself make an interference unreasonable. Heat can be a nuisance if it would affect ordinary uses."},
 {"t":"nuisance-rylands","q":"Eric owns a house in a heavily industrial area. Fumes from a nearby copper works damage the trees and shrubs in his garden. The works operator argues that, because the area is industrial, Eric must put up with the fumes.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Eric's position in private nuisance?","o":["He will fail, because in an industrial area residents must accept the effects of industry.","He will fail, because the copper works were there before he bought his house.","He will succeed only if he can show the operator was negligent in running the works.","The character of the area is irrelevant, because the fumes caused physical damage.","He will succeed only if the fumes also make him personally ill."],"a":3,"e":"The **character of the neighbourhood** (locality) is relevant to nuisances causing personal discomfort, such as noise or smells, but **not** where the interference causes **material physical damage** to property (St Helen's Smelting v Tipping). Coming to the nuisance is not a defence, negligence need not be proved, and personal injury is not recoverable in private nuisance in any case."},
 {"t":"nuisance-rylands","q":"Rail Co owns an embankment where Japanese knotweed has grown for years. Rail Co knows that knotweed spreads and causes problems for neighbours, but takes no action. The knotweed's roots spread under the boundary onto Bill's land, interfering with his ability to use and enjoy his garden. Rail Co did not plant the knotweed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rail Co's liability in private nuisance?","o":["It is not liable, because it did not plant the knotweed and so did not create the nuisance.","It is not liable, because the knotweed is a natural growth that spread by itself, and no one can be responsible for natural growths.","It is likely to be liable, because it knew of the knotweed risk and failed to take reasonable steps.","It is not liable unless the knotweed has caused structural damage to Bill's house.","It is liable only if it was negligent in buying land on which knotweed was growing."],"a":2,"e":"An occupier can be liable in private nuisance for a hazard on its land that it did **not create**, including a natural one, if it **knew or ought to have known** of it and failed to take **reasonable steps** to deal with it (Leakey v National Trust). Encroachment by knotweed that interferes with the neighbour's use and enjoyment of the land is actionable even without structural damage (Williams v Network Rail)."},
 {"t":"nuisance-rylands","q":"A motocross track has operated for many years near Karen's home, making loud noise most weekends. The court finds that the noise is a private nuisance. The track employs several local people and has planning permission. The track owner asks the court to award damages instead of an injunction.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's approach to the remedy?","o":["It may award damages instead of an injunction, taking account of factors such as the public interest.","It must grant an injunction, because once a nuisance is proved the claimant is entitled to one.","It must award damages only, because the track has planning permission.","It must refuse any remedy, because the track brings jobs and economic benefit to the area.","It must grant an injunction unless the damages would be very small and easily calculated."],"a":0,"e":"An **injunction** is a discretionary remedy. The court may award **damages in lieu** (instead) of an injunction, and should take a flexible approach, considering all the circumstances, including the **public interest** and any planning permission (Coventry v Lawrence). Planning permission and public benefit are not defences to liability, but they can affect the remedy. The court is not bound by rigid rules either way."},
 {"t":"nuisance-rylands","q":"For 25 years, a confectioner has used noisy grinding machines in his kitchen, next to the garden of a doctor's house. No one has complained. The doctor then builds a consulting room at the end of his garden, against the kitchen wall, and finds the noise makes his work impossible. He sues in private nuisance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the confectioner's argument that he has a right by prescription to make the noise?","o":["It will succeed, because he has made the noise continuously for more than 20 years.","It will succeed, because the doctor came to the nuisance by choosing to build the consulting room there.","It will fail, because a right to commit a nuisance can never be acquired by prescription.","It will fail, because the noise only became a nuisance to the doctor when the consulting room was built.","It will succeed, because the doctor did not complain during the previous 25 years."],"a":3,"e":"A right to commit a private nuisance can be acquired by **prescription** after **20 years**, but time runs only from when the activity became an **actionable nuisance to the claimant** (Sturges v Bridgman). Here that was when the consulting room was built, so 20 years have not passed. **Coming to the nuisance** is not a defence."},
 {"t":"nuisance-rylands","q":"Quarry Ltd's lorries regularly park along both sides of a public road for several weeks, blocking it. Members of the public are inconvenienced. Sally runs a café on the road and loses most of her trade because customers cannot reach it. She sues Quarry Ltd.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sally's position?","o":["She cannot sue, because a public nuisance can be pursued only by the Attorney General or the local authority on behalf of the public.","She can sue in private nuisance only, because she owns land next to the road.","She can sue in public nuisance, because she has suffered special damage beyond that suffered by the public generally.","She cannot sue, because her loss of trade is pure economic loss, which is never recoverable in nuisance.","She can sue in public nuisance only if she also suffered personal injury."],"a":2,"e":"**Public nuisance** is an act that materially affects the reasonable comfort and convenience of a class of the public, such as an unreasonable obstruction of a highway. An individual can sue in tort only if she suffered **special damage** over and above that suffered by the public generally, such as substantial loss of trade (Tate & Lyle v GLC). Economic loss can be recovered as special damage in public nuisance, and personal injury is not required."},
 {"t":"nuisance-rylands","q":"A water pipe running from the mains supply to a council block of flats bursts without any negligence. The escaping water causes an embankment to collapse, exposing a gas main owned by Transgas plc, which spends money making it safe. Transgas plc sues the council under the rule in Rylands v Fletcher.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["The claim will succeed, because the water escaped from the council's land and caused damage.","The claim will fail, because supplying water to flats by pipe is an ordinary use of land.","The claim will succeed, because water is a thing likely to do mischief if it escapes.","The claim will fail, because the rule in Rylands v Fletcher requires proof of negligence.","The claim will succeed, because liability under the rule is strict and there are no defences."],"a":1,"e":"The rule in Rylands v Fletcher requires a **non-natural use** of land, meaning an **extraordinary or unusual** use, not an ordinary domestic or commercial one. Supplying water to a block of flats through a normal pipe is an ordinary use, so the claim fails (Transco v Stockport MBC). Liability under the rule is strict (no negligence needed), but there are defences, and escape alone is not enough."},
 {"t":"nuisance-rylands","q":"Over many years, a tannery spills small quantities of a chemical solvent on its concrete floor. At the time, no one could reasonably have foreseen that it would seep through and contaminate groundwater. The solvent eventually reaches a water company's borehole a mile away, making the water unusable. The water company sues under the rule in Rylands v Fletcher.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["The claim will succeed, because liability under the rule is strict, so foreseeability is irrelevant.","The claim will fail, because the type of damage caused was not reasonably foreseeable.","The claim will succeed, because storing solvents is a non-natural use of land.","The claim will fail, because the borehole was a mile away from the tannery.","The claim will fail, because the water company was not an occupier of neighbouring land."],"a":1,"e":"Liability under Rylands v Fletcher is strict in the sense that reasonable care is no defence, but the damage must be of a **type that was reasonably foreseeable** (Cambridge Water v Eastern Counties Leather). Contamination of groundwater was not foreseeable at the time, so the claim fails even though storing solvents may be a non-natural use. Distance and being a neighbouring occupier are not requirements in themselves; the claimant needs an interest in the land affected."},
 {"t":"nuisance-rylands","q":"Chem Ltd lawfully stores a large quantity of acid in a secure tank on its industrial site, which is a non-natural use of land. One night, an intruder breaks through the fence and deliberately opens the tank valve. The acid escapes onto a neighbouring farm, destroying crops. Chem Ltd could not reasonably have prevented the intrusion.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Chem Ltd's liability under the rule in Rylands v Fletcher?","o":["It is liable, because liability under the rule is strict and the acid escaped from its land.","It is liable, because storing acid is a non-natural use and the damage was foreseeable.","It has a defence only if it can show the farmer contributed to the damage.","It is liable, because an occupier is responsible for the acts of anyone who enters its land.","It has a defence, because the escape was caused by the unforeseeable deliberate act of a stranger."],"a":4,"e":"Although liability under Rylands v Fletcher is strict, the **act of a stranger** over whom the defendant had no control, and which it could not reasonably have foreseen or prevented, is a defence (Rickards v Lothian). The intruder's deliberate act caused the escape, so Chem Ltd is not liable under the rule. Other defences include act of God, statutory authority and the claimant's consent or default."},
 {"t":"nuisance-rylands","q":"An art gallery builds a public viewing platform on its top floor. Hundreds of thousands of visitors a year use it, and many stare, photograph and wave into the living areas of neighbouring glass-walled flats, a few metres away. The flat owners sue in private nuisance. The gallery argues that there is no right to privacy in nuisance and that overlooking can never be a nuisance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["The gallery will succeed, because overlooking from a neighbour's land can never amount to a nuisance, however intrusive it is.","The gallery will succeed, because the flat owners chose to buy flats with glass walls.","The flat owners will succeed only if they can prove the gallery acted maliciously.","The gallery will succeed, because the platform is a reasonable use of land in a city centre.","The flat owners are likely to succeed, because constant visual intrusion of this kind can be a nuisance."],"a":4,"e":"The Supreme Court has held that constant **visual intrusion** of this kind, caused by a use of land that was not a common and ordinary use, can amount to a **private nuisance** (Fearn v Board of Trustees of the Tate Gallery). The flat owners were not required to take steps (such as putting up blinds) to protect themselves, and an abnormally sensitive use argument did not apply to ordinary residential living. Malice is not required. This is different from merely being overlooked by an ordinary neighbouring building."}
];

;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["legalsys"] = [
 {"t":"courts-judiciary","q":"The Home Office is defending a judicial review claim in the High Court. Before judgment is given, a Home Office minister asks his private secretary to arrange a private meeting with the trial judge so that he can explain how important the policy is to the government. The claimant's lawyers know nothing about the request.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the minister's request?","o":["It is lawful, provided the minister puts the government's position in writing to the judge afterwards as well.","It is lawful, because the Lord Chancellor, as head of the judiciary, may authorise private contact between ministers and judges in such cases.","It breaches the statutory duty on ministers not to seek to influence particular judicial decisions through special access to the judiciary.","It breaches only a political convention, which the courts cannot enforce against a minister.","It is lawful, because the government is a party to the claim and is entitled to put its case to the judge."],"a":2,"e":"Under s.3 Constitutional Reform Act 2005, ministers must uphold the independence of the judiciary and must not seek to influence particular judicial decisions through special access to judges. A party puts its case only in open court through its lawyers, not by private meetings. The duty is statutory, not merely a convention, and the Lord Chancellor is no longer head of the judiciary (the Lord Chief Justice is)."},
 {"t":"courts-judiciary","q":"A High Court judge has given several judgments against the government on immigration policy. The Prime Minister tells colleagues that the judge should be removed from office and asks the Lord Chancellor how this can be done. There is no suggestion of any misconduct by the judge.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how a High Court judge may be removed from office?","o":["Only by the monarch on an address presented by both Houses of Parliament.","By the Prime Minister advising the monarch to dismiss the judge.","By the Lord Chancellor, after consulting the Lord Chief Justice and the Prime Minister.","By the Judicial Appointments Commission, which appointed the judge.","By a resolution of the House of Commons alone."],"a":0,"e":"Senior judges hold office during good behaviour and can be removed only by the monarch on an address (a formal request) passed by both Houses of Parliament (s.11(3) Senior Courts Act 1981). This security of tenure protects judicial independence, so neither the Prime Minister nor the Lord Chancellor can dismiss a judge. The Judicial Appointments Commission selects judges but has no power to remove them, and a Commons vote alone is not enough."},
 {"t":"courts-judiciary","q":"Kamal, a trainee solicitor, is drafting a client briefing on the court system. He writes that 'the Lord Chancellor, as head of the judiciary, is responsible for the deployment and discipline of judges in England and Wales'. His supervisor asks him to correct the sentence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is head of the judiciary of England and Wales?","o":["The Lord Chancellor, because he is the senior government minister responsible for the courts.","The Lord Chief Justice, who is President of the Courts of England and Wales.","The President of the Supreme Court, because the Supreme Court is the highest court.","The Master of the Rolls, because he heads the Court of Appeal (Civil Division).","The Attorney General, because he is the government's chief legal adviser."],"a":1,"e":"Since the Constitutional Reform Act 2005, the Lord (or Lady) Chief Justice is President of the Courts of England and Wales and head of the judiciary (s.7 CRA 2005). The Lord Chancellor is a government minister who must uphold judicial independence but is no longer a judge. The Supreme Court is a UK-wide court whose President does not head the England and Wales judiciary, and the Master of the Rolls heads only the Civil Division of the Court of Appeal."},
 {"t":"courts-judiciary","q":"Darren was convicted by lay magistrates of a summary-only offence after a trial in which he gave evidence that he was elsewhere at the time. He says the magistrates simply got the facts wrong and wants his case heard again by a different court.\n\nTo which court should Darren appeal against his conviction?","o":["The Court of Appeal (Criminal Division).","The King's Bench Division of the High Court.","The County Court.","The Crown Court.","The Supreme Court."],"a":3,"e":"Although the Crown Court is mainly a trial court for indictable cases, it also hears appeals from the magistrates' courts, including appeals against conviction on the facts. The Court of Appeal (Criminal Division) hears appeals from the Crown Court, not directly from magistrates. The High Court's role in criminal appeals from magistrates is limited to points of law, and the County Court is a civil court."},
 {"t":"courts-judiciary","q":"Bryn Valley Council decides to close a public library. A group of residents believes the council acted beyond its statutory powers and failed to consult as the relevant Act requires. They want a court to rule on whether the decision was lawful.\n\nIn which court should the residents' claim be brought?","o":["The County Court, because the claim is against a local council.","The Chancery Division of the High Court, because public property is involved.","The Court of Appeal, because it supervises the decisions of public bodies.","The First-tier Tribunal, because it hears disputes between individuals and the state about public decisions.","The Administrative Court, which is part of the King's Bench Division of the High Court."],"a":4,"e":"A challenge to the lawfulness of a public body's decision is a claim for judicial review, which is heard in the Administrative Court, part of the King's Bench Division of the High Court. The County Court has no general judicial review jurisdiction, and the Chancery Division deals with matters such as trusts, land and companies. The Court of Appeal hears appeals, not first-instance judicial review claims."},
 {"t":"courts-judiciary","q":"Sofia is a solicitor who qualified a year ago and does not hold any higher rights of audience qualification. She represented Callum at his trial in the magistrates' court, where he was convicted. Callum now appeals against his conviction to the Crown Court and wants Sofia to represent him at the appeal hearing.\n\nMay Sofia act as Callum's advocate at the appeal?","o":["No, because a solicitor without higher rights may never appear as an advocate in the Crown Court, whatever the type of hearing.","Yes, but only if the Crown Court judge gives her permission at the start of the hearing.","Yes, because a solicitor without higher rights may act on an appeal from magistrates where she acted for the client below.","No, because an appeal hearing in the Crown Court counts as a trial on indictment.","Yes, because every solicitor may exercise rights of audience in all courts once admitted."],"a":2,"e":"Solicitors are authorised on admission to exercise rights of audience in all courts, but need the higher rights qualification to act as advocates at trials and substantive hearings in the higher courts. There is an exception for appeals from, and committals for sentence by, a magistrates' court where the solicitor or her firm represented the client below. An appeal from the magistrates is not a trial on indictment, and no permission from the judge is needed."},
 {"t":"courts-judiciary","q":"Lena is bringing a County Court claim against her former landlord without a lawyer. Her friend Ravi, who has no legal qualifications, has helped her prepare. At the hearing Ravi tells the judge he intends to make Lena's submissions and cross-examine the landlord on her behalf.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ravi's position?","o":["He may sit with Lena, take notes and quietly advise her, but may address the court only with the judge's permission.","He may address the court and cross-examine, because Lena has chosen him to represent her.","He may not attend the hearing at all, because only authorised persons such as solicitors may assist a party during a hearing.","He may address the court, because rights of audience are reserved only in the higher courts.","He may cross-examine witnesses but may not make legal submissions to the judge."],"a":0,"e":"Ravi is acting as a McKenzie friend (a lay helper). He may support Lena by sitting with her, taking notes and quietly advising, but he has no right of audience; exercising a right of audience for someone else is a reserved legal activity under the Legal Services Act 2007. The court has a discretion to allow a lay person to address it in an exceptional case. Lena herself, as a litigant in person, can always address the court in her own case."},
 {"t":"courts-judiciary","q":"Max runs a 'claims consultancy'. He is not a solicitor, barrister or other authorised person and is not exempt. For a fee, he issues County Court claims in his clients' names, signs the claim forms and files documents with the court on their behalf. He never speaks in court; clients attend hearings alone.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Max's position?","o":["He is acting lawfully, because he never exercises any right of audience in court.","He is committing a criminal offence by carrying on the reserved activity of conducting litigation without being entitled.","He is acting lawfully, provided each client gives informed written consent to his role.","He is in breach of the SRA's rules, but commits no criminal offence because he is not a solicitor or other authorised person.","He is acting lawfully, because County Court claims are outside the reserved legal activities."],"a":1,"e":"Conducting litigation (issuing proceedings and taking formal steps in them) is a reserved legal activity under s.12 Legal Services Act 2007, separate from exercising a right of audience. Carrying on a reserved activity when not authorised or exempt is a criminal offence (s.14 LSA 2007). It does not matter that Max never speaks in court or that his clients consent, and the SRA regulates solicitors rather than people like Max."},
 {"t":"courts-judiciary","q":"Grace lost an appeal on a point of law about her disability benefit in the First-tier Tribunal (Social Entitlement Chamber). She then appealed to the Upper Tribunal, which also decided against her on the point of law. She wants to take the matter further.\n\nTo which court would any further appeal from the Upper Tribunal lie?","o":["The Supreme Court directly.","The Administrative Court of the High Court.","The Employment Appeal Tribunal.","The Crown Court.","The Court of Appeal."],"a":4,"e":"Appeals from the Upper Tribunal (and from the Employment Appeal Tribunal) go to the Court of Appeal. The Upper Tribunal is a superior court of record, so its decisions are not ordinarily challenged by judicial review in the Administrative Court, and the ordinary route upwards is to the Court of Appeal rather than straight to the Supreme Court. The Employment Appeal Tribunal deals only with appeals from employment tribunals."},
 {"t":"courts-judiciary","q":"During Tomasz's trial on indictment for robbery in the Crown Court, the judge refuses a defence application to exclude evidence of an identification. Tomasz's solicitor wants to challenge the ruling straight away by seeking judicial review in the High Court.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether judicial review is available?","o":["Yes, because the Crown Court is an inferior court and all of its decisions are therefore subject to judicial review.","Yes, because judicial review is available against any court below the Court of Appeal.","No, because the judge acted in good faith and so has judicial immunity.","No, because decisions of the Crown Court relating to trial on indictment are not subject to judicial review.","Yes, but only with the permission of the trial judge."],"a":3,"e":"The Crown Court is one of the Senior Courts and a superior court of record. Its decisions are not subject to judicial review except in matters not relating to trial on indictment (s.29(3) Senior Courts Act 1981). A ruling during a jury trial is challenged, if at all, on appeal after conviction. Judicial immunity protects judges from being sued personally; it is not the reason judicial review is unavailable."},
 {"t":"precedent","q":"The Court of Appeal (Civil Division) is hearing an appeal about the duty of care owed by surveyors. One of its own decisions from 2019 is directly in point and supports the respondent. Counsel for the appellant argues that the 2019 decision was badly reasoned and has been heavily criticised by academics. The 2019 court considered all relevant statutes and authorities.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Court of Appeal's position?","o":["It may depart from the 2019 decision, because it is free to correct its own earlier mistakes.","It may depart from the 2019 decision, because academic criticism of its reasoning makes the decision per incuriam.","It must refer the question to the Supreme Court before deciding the appeal.","It is bound to follow its 2019 decision, because none of the recognised exceptions allowing it to depart applies.","It may treat the 2019 decision as persuasive only, because it is a decision of the same court."],"a":3,"e":"The Court of Appeal (Civil Division) is bound by its own previous decisions, subject only to the three exceptions in Young v Bristol Aeroplane: conflicting Court of Appeal decisions, a decision that cannot stand with a later Supreme Court decision, and a decision made per incuriam. Per incuriam means made in ignorance of a binding statute or authority, not merely badly reasoned or criticised. There is no general power to depart because the earlier decision is thought wrong, and no procedure to refer the point to the Supreme Court."},
 {"t":"precedent","q":"In 2018 the Court of Appeal decided a point of contract law in favour of buyers. In 2024 the Supreme Court, in a different case, laid down a principle as part of its ratio that cannot stand with the 2018 decision, although it did not mention it. The same point now comes before the Court of Appeal.\n\nHow should the Court of Appeal approach its 2018 decision?","o":["It must follow its 2018 decision, because the Supreme Court did not expressly overrule it.","It may choose freely between its 2018 decision and the Supreme Court's reasoning.","It must follow its 2018 decision unless the Supreme Court's statement was obiter.","It must distinguish the Supreme Court decision, because that case concerned different facts and did not mention the 2018 decision.","It must not follow its 2018 decision, because the decision cannot stand with the later Supreme Court decision."],"a":4,"e":"One of the Young v Bristol Aeroplane exceptions is that the Court of Appeal must not follow its own earlier decision if it cannot stand with a later decision of the House of Lords or Supreme Court, even if that decision did not expressly overrule it. The Supreme Court binds the Court of Appeal, and here the principle formed part of its ratio, so the Court of Appeal has no free choice. Distinguishing is not appropriate where the binding principle applies to the point in issue."},
 {"t":"precedent","q":"A district judge in the County Court is hearing a claim by Nadia against a garage. The garage relies on a Court of Appeal decision that a particular exclusion clause was valid. Nadia shows that, unlike in the Court of Appeal case, she was never given a copy of the terms before the contract was made, which the judge accepts is a legally significant difference.\n\nWhat may the district judge do with the Court of Appeal decision?","o":["Overrule it, because it was decided on different facts.","Distinguish it, because the material facts of Nadia's case are different.","Ignore it, because Court of Appeal decisions are only persuasive in the County Court.","Refer it to the Court of Appeal and adjourn until that court decides.","Follow it regardless, because a lower court can never avoid a higher court's decision."],"a":1,"e":"Distinguishing is available to any court, however low in the hierarchy: if the material facts (the facts that matter legally) are different, the earlier ratio does not apply. Only a higher court (or one free to depart from its own decisions) can overrule a decision. Court of Appeal decisions bind the County Court, so the judge could not simply ignore it if the facts were the same."},
 {"t":"precedent","q":"In a negligence case, the Supreme Court decided the claim on the ground that no duty of care was owed. One Justice added that, had the claimant been a child, the court would have been minded to find a duty. A High Court judge is now hearing a claim on similar facts brought by a child.\n\nWhat is the status of the Justice's remark in the High Court?","o":["It is obiter dictum and so persuasive only, although it carries considerable weight.","It binds the High Court, because it was said by a Justice of the Supreme Court, the highest court.","It binds the High Court, because no other Justice disagreed with it.","It has no relevance at all, because it was not necessary for the decision.","It binds the High Court only if the Court of Appeal later approves it."],"a":0,"e":"Only the ratio decidendi (the principle needed for the decision on the material facts) binds later courts. A remark about hypothetical facts (here, a child claimant) is obiter dictum and is persuasive only, though a statement by a Supreme Court Justice is highly persuasive. It is not binding just because of the seniority of the court or because nobody disagreed."},
 {"t":"precedent","q":"Mr Justice Patel, sitting alone in the King's Bench Division, is asked to follow a decision given last year by another High Court judge sitting alone on the same point of statutory interpretation. Mr Justice Patel thinks the earlier decision is wrong.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mr Justice Patel's position?","o":["He is bound by the earlier decision, because the High Court is bound by its own decisions.","He is bound by the earlier decision unless one of the exceptions that apply to the Court of Appeal's own decisions applies.","He is not bound by it, although he would normally follow it for consistency unless convinced it is wrong.","He must refer the point to a Divisional Court because two High Court judges disagree.","He may overrule the earlier decision, which will then cease to be law."],"a":2,"e":"A High Court judge sitting alone is not bound by the decisions of other High Court judges, but normally follows them for consistency unless convinced they are wrong. The Young exceptions are about the Court of Appeal (and Divisional Courts) being bound by their own decisions. A judge of equal rank cannot overrule another; a conflict is resolved on appeal."},
 {"t":"precedent","q":"The High Court is deciding a tort claim. A Court of Appeal decision from 2015 is directly in point and favours the defendant. The claimant relies on a later decision of the Judicial Committee of the Privy Council, on an appeal from a Commonwealth country, which reaches the opposite result. The Privy Council gave no direction that its decision should be treated as representing English law.\n\nWhich decision should the High Court follow?","o":["The Court of Appeal decision, because Privy Council decisions are only persuasive in England and Wales.","The Privy Council decision, because it is the more recent decision.","The Privy Council decision, because it is staffed by Supreme Court Justices and so binds all the lower courts.","Either decision, because the High Court may choose between conflicting appellate decisions.","Neither decision, because the High Court must decide the point afresh."],"a":0,"e":"The Privy Council is not part of the English court hierarchy, so its decisions are persuasive, not binding. Under the narrow Willers v Joyce exception, a lower court should follow a Privy Council decision instead of an earlier conflicting English appellate decision only where the Privy Council expressly directs that its decision represents English law; there was no such direction here. The High Court remains bound by the Court of Appeal."},
 {"t":"precedent","q":"In 2022, Fenwick Ltd entered into a commercial arrangement relying on a long-standing Court of Appeal decision on the law of restitution. In 2026 the Supreme Court, in a different case, overrules that decision. Fenwick Ltd argues that the new rule should apply only to arrangements made after 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the Supreme Court's decision on Fenwick Ltd's arrangement?","o":["The new rule applies only to arrangements made after the Supreme Court's decision, because the courts may not act retrospectively.","The new rule applies to the 2022 arrangement only if Parliament passes an Act confirming it.","The old rule continues to govern all arrangements made before the overruling, by statute.","The 2022 arrangement is void, because it was based on an overruled decision.","The new rule is treated as always having been the law, so it applies to the 2022 arrangement."],"a":4,"e":"Under the declaratory theory, judges declare what the law always was, so an overruling decision operates retrospectively and applies to past transactions. The Supreme Court has said it could, in an exceptional case, overrule only for the future (prospective overruling), but it has never done so. No Act is needed for the overruling to take effect, and the arrangement is not automatically void; the new rule simply governs it."},
 {"t":"precedent","q":"Jasmine won her breach of contract claim against Arden Ltd in the High Court. Arden Ltd appealed, and the Court of Appeal allowed the appeal and entered judgment for Arden Ltd in the same proceedings.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the Court of Appeal has done to the High Court's decision?","o":["It has overruled the decision.","It has distinguished the decision.","It has reversed the decision.","It has disapproved the decision.","It has departed from the decision under the Practice Statement."],"a":2,"e":"Reversing is when a higher court, on appeal in the same case, changes the lower court's decision. Overruling is when a higher court decides that the rule laid down in an earlier, different case was wrong. Distinguishing involves different material facts in a later case, and disapproving is a usually obiter comment that an earlier decision was wrong without overruling it."},
 {"t":"precedent","q":"Kieran appeals to the Court of Appeal (Criminal Division) against his conviction. The trial judge directed the jury according to an earlier decision of the Criminal Division. Kieran shows that the earlier court misunderstood the law, and that following it would mean his conviction stands when it would otherwise be quashed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Court of Appeal's position?","o":["It is bound by its earlier decision in exactly the same way as the Civil Division, so the conviction must stand, however unjust the result.","It may depart from its earlier decision, because the law was misunderstood and following it would cause injustice to the defendant.","It may depart from its earlier decision only if the Supreme Court has since overruled it.","It must certify a point of law and refer the case to the Criminal Cases Review Commission.","It may depart from its earlier decision only if that decision was a majority rather than a unanimous one."],"a":1,"e":"The Criminal Division is generally bound by its own decisions and applies the Young exceptions, but it has extra flexibility: it may depart where the law was misapplied or misunderstood in an earlier case and following it would cause injustice to the defendant, because liberty is at stake. This is wider than the Civil Division's position. Whether the earlier decision was unanimous makes no difference."},
 {"t":"precedent","q":"In 2026, the High Court is hearing an employment claim that turns on the meaning of regulations implementing an EU directive, which remain in force as assimilated law. A 2017 decision of the Court of Justice of the European Union (CJEU) interpreting the directive is directly in point. The High Court judge thinks the CJEU decision is wrong and that the point is of general public importance.\n\nWhat is the best course open to the High Court?","o":["Depart from the CJEU decision, because CJEU decisions no longer bind any UK court.","Refer the question of interpretation to the CJEU for a preliminary ruling.","Depart from the CJEU decision, having regard to any changes of circumstances since 2017.","Follow the CJEU decision, because only the Court of Appeal and Supreme Court (and equivalents) may depart from it.","Treat the CJEU decision as persuasive only, because it is the decision of a foreign court and the UK has left the EU."],"a":3,"e":"CJEU decisions made before the end of 2020 are assimilated EU case law. Lower courts, including the High Court, remain bound by them; only the Court of Appeal and Supreme Court (and equivalents) may depart, applying the Practice Statement test (s.6(4)-(5) EUWA 2018 and the Relevant Court Regulations 2020). There is no procedure for a lower court to refer the point to an appeal court, however important it is; the point can go higher only on an ordinary appeal. The statutory factors such as changes of circumstances, and the reference procedure, were in s.6 of the REUL Act 2023, which never came into force. UK courts can no longer refer questions to the CJEU, and it is only post-2020 CJEU decisions that are not binding."},
 {"t":"legislation-structure","q":"The (fictional) Coastal Paths (Wales) Act 2026, an Act of Senedd Cymru, contains no provision about commencement. The King signed the Letters Patent giving Royal Assent on Monday 6 July 2026, and they were notified to the Clerk of the Senedd on Wednesday 8 July 2026. Owain did something on Wednesday 8 July that the Act prohibits.\n\nWhen did the Act come into force?","o":["At the beginning of Monday 6 July 2026.","At the beginning of Tuesday 7 July 2026.","At the beginning of Wednesday 8 July 2026.","On a day to be appointed by the Welsh Ministers by order.","At the beginning of Thursday 9 July 2026."],"a":4,"e":"An Act of the Senedd receives Royal Assent when the Letters Patent are notified to the Clerk of the Senedd (s.115 GoWA 2006), so Royal Assent was on 8 July, not when the King signed. If an Act of the Senedd says nothing about commencement, it comes into force at the beginning of the day after Royal Assent (s.30 Legislation (Wales) Act 2019), which is 9 July. The Westminster rule (the start of the day of Royal Assent) does not apply to Senedd Acts, so Owain's conduct on 8 July was not caught."},
 {"t":"legislation-structure","q":"Section 4 of the (fictional) Tenant Deposits Act 2025 requires landlords to protect 'any deposit paid in money'. The Explanatory Notes published with the Act say that section 4 'is intended to cover deposits paid in money or in goods'. Hamid, a landlord, took a valuable painting as a deposit and did not protect it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of the Explanatory Notes?","o":["They are not part of the Act and have no legal force, though a court may use them to understand the context.","They are part of the Act and override the wording of section 4 where the two differ.","They are part of the Act and carry the same weight as a Schedule.","They may be used only if the minister read them out in Parliament during the Bill's passage through both Houses.","They must be ignored entirely, because courts may never look at material outside the Act."],"a":0,"e":"Explanatory Notes are written by the government department and published with the Act, but they are not enacted and have no legal force. Courts may use them as an external aid to show context and purpose, but they cannot displace a meaning that is clear from the words of the Act read in context. Schedules, by contrast, are part of the Act."},
 {"t":"legislation-structure","q":"The (fictional) School Meals (Wales) Act 2026 is an Act of Senedd Cymru. Its extent section states that it extends to England and Wales. Section 1 requires every maintained school to provide free breakfasts. Bethan, who lives in Bristol, says that because the Act extends to England, her child's maintained school in Bristol must provide free breakfasts.\n\nIs Bethan correct?","o":["Yes, because the Act extends to England and Wales and section 1 applies to every maintained school without exception.","No, because although the Act extends to England and Wales, an Act of the Senedd applies only in relation to Wales.","Yes, unless the UK Parliament passes an Act disapplying it in England.","No, because an Act of the Senedd extends to Wales only and forms no part of the law of England.","No, because the Act cannot come into force in England until the Secretary of State consents."],"a":1,"e":"Extent (which legal system a law belongs to) differs from application (what it actually governs). Because England and Wales are a single jurisdiction, an Act of the Senedd extends to England and Wales, but it is within competence only if it applies in relation to Wales (s.108A(2) GoWA 2006). So the Act forms part of the law of England and Wales but does not govern a school in Bristol."},
 {"t":"legislation-structure","q":"A client asks about the (fictional) Rail Fares Act 2025. Its enacting formula states that it is enacted by the King 'by and with the advice and consent of the Commons in this present Parliament assembled, in accordance with the provisions of the Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949'. The client says this means the Act is not proper law.\n\nWhat does the enacting formula show?","o":["The Act is a private Act affecting only particular rail companies.","The Act was passed by the Commons alone and still needs the Lords' approval before any of it can be brought into force.","The Act was passed without the Lords' consent under the Parliament Acts, and it is valid primary legislation.","The Act is secondary legislation made under powers in the Parliament Acts.","The Act was passed without Royal Assent, which is not required under the Parliament Acts."],"a":2,"e":"An Act passed without the Lords' consent under the Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949 uses a different enacting formula referring to the Commons and those Acts. Such an Act is valid primary legislation (R (Jackson) v Attorney General). It still receives Royal Assent, and it is not secondary legislation or a private Act."},
 {"t":"legislation-structure","q":"The (fictional) Letting Agents Act 2025 is an Act of the UK Parliament. Section 60 states: 'This Act extends to England and Wales.' Section 3 provides that 'any letting agent who charges a tenant a fee for a reference commits an offence'. Nothing in the Act limits section 3 to England. Carys runs a letting agency in Swansea and charges reference fees.\n\nDoes section 3 apply to Carys?","o":["Yes, because the Act extends to England and Wales and nothing in section 3 limits its application to England.","No, because Westminster Acts never apply to devolved matters in Wales.","No, because a Westminster Act that extends to England and Wales applies only in England unless it says otherwise.","Yes, but only once Senedd Cymru has passed a legislative consent motion.","No, because the extent section does not mention Wales separately from England."],"a":0,"e":"Extent tells you which legal systems the Act forms part of; application depends on the wording of each provision. Here the Act extends to England and Wales and section 3 is not limited to England (for example, by words such as 'in England'), so it applies in Wales too. Westminster remains free to legislate for Wales, and the Sewel convention is political, so a consent motion is not a legal condition of the Act applying."},
 {"t":"legislation-structure","q":"Section 8 of the (fictional) Water Quality Act 2025 states: 'Schedule 2 has effect.' Paragraph 4 of Schedule 2 requires water companies to publish monthly sewage discharge data. Northbrook Water plc argues that it need not comply because Schedule 2 is only a technical appendix at the end of the Act, not one of its sections.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of paragraph 4 of Schedule 2?","o":["It has legal force only if and when the minister makes separate regulations bringing Schedule 2 into effect.","It is a guide to interpreting section 8, but does not itself impose duties.","It has legal force only where it is consistent with the Explanatory Notes.","It is as much part of the Act, and has the same legal force, as any section in the body of the Act.","It is secondary legislation, which can be quashed by the High Court."],"a":3,"e":"Schedules come at the end of an Act and are introduced by a section in the body (here, s.8), but they are part of the Act and have the same legal force as the sections. Schedules often contain detailed or technical provisions, amendments and repeals. They are not secondary legislation and do not depend on the Explanatory Notes."},
 {"t":"legislation-structure","q":"The (fictional) Litter Control (Wales) Act 2025, an Act of Senedd Cymru, gives the Welsh Ministers power to make regulations about fixed penalty amounts. The Welsh Ministers make the Litter Control (Fixed Penalties) (Wales) Regulations 2026, numbered 'SI 2026/310 (W. 52)'. A council officer asks what kind of law the Regulations are.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Regulations?","o":["An Act of Senedd Cymru, because they were made in Wales by the Welsh Government.","Primary legislation, because they were made under an Act of the Senedd rather than an Act of Parliament.","A Measure of the Senedd, which ranks between primary and secondary legislation.","A local Act, because they apply only within Wales.","A Welsh statutory instrument: secondary legislation made under a power in a parent Act."],"a":4,"e":"Law made by ministers under a power in a parent (enabling) Act is secondary legislation, usually in the form of a statutory instrument. Welsh Ministers make Welsh statutory instruments, numbered with a 'W.'. Acts of Senedd Cymru are primary legislation made by the Senedd itself, and Measures were the name for earlier Welsh primary legislation (2007-2011)."},
 {"t":"legislation-structure","q":"Section 12 of the (fictional) Animal Welfare Act 2025 sits under the cross-heading 'Dogs'. It makes it an offence to keep 'any animal' in a vehicle in hot weather without adequate ventilation. Imogen left her cat in a hot car. She argues that, because of the heading, section 12 applies only to dogs.\n\nHow is a court most likely to treat the heading?","o":["It must limit section 12 to dogs, because headings are binding parts of the Act.","It may consider the heading, but it carries little weight against the clear words 'any animal'.","It must ignore the heading completely, because headings are not part of the Act.","It must ask the minister responsible for the Act what the heading was intended to mean.","It must give the heading and the words of the section equal weight and so choose the narrower of the two meanings."],"a":1,"e":"Headings and cross-headings may be considered as an aid to interpretation, but they carry little weight, and they cannot override clear words in the section itself. 'Any animal' plainly covers a cat, so Imogen's argument is likely to fail. Courts do not ask ministers what words mean."},
 {"t":"legislation-structure","q":"The (fictional) Short-Term Lets Act 2025 is an Act of the UK Parliament. Section 50 states: 'This Act extends to England and Wales only.' Moira lets out holiday flats in Edinburgh and wants to know whether she must comply with the registration duty in section 2.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Moira's position?","o":["She must comply, because Acts of the UK Parliament always apply throughout the whole of the United Kingdom.","She must comply unless the Scottish Parliament passes an Act disapplying it.","She need not comply, but only because short-term lets are a devolved matter in Scotland.","She need not comply, because the Act does not extend to Scotland and so forms no part of Scots law.","She must comply, because extent concerns only which courts can hear disputes under the Act."],"a":3,"e":"The UK has three legal jurisdictions: England and Wales, Scotland, and Northern Ireland. The extent section tells you which of them an Act forms part of; an Act that extends to England and Wales only is not part of Scots law and so does not govern Moira's Edinburgh flats. Westminster could legislate for Scotland, but it has not done so here; the reason is extent, not devolution."},
 {"t":"legislation-structure","q":"A Bill before Senedd Cymru on the regulation of tattoo parlours contains a final provision stating: 'This Act extends to England and Wales and to Scotland.' The Bill is passed and receives Royal Assent without anyone noticing the provision.\n\nWhat is the legal effect of that provision?","o":["It is valid, because the Act received Royal Assent.","It is valid, unless the Scottish Parliament objects within four weeks.","It is outside the Senedd's legislative competence, so it is not law.","It is valid, but the Act applies in Scotland only with the Secretary of State's consent.","It makes the whole Act void, including the provisions about Wales."],"a":2,"e":"A provision of an Act of the Senedd is outside its legislative competence, and so is not law, if it extends otherwise than to England and Wales (s.108A(1) and (2)(a) GoWA 2006). Royal Assent does not cure a lack of competence. The rest of the Act is unaffected, because an Act is not law only 'so far as' a provision is outside competence."},
 {"t":"statutory-interpretation","q":"The (fictional) Fireworks Safety Act 2025 makes it an offence to 'sell' fireworks to a person under 16. Leon gave fireworks free of charge to a 14-year-old at a party. The magistrates acquitted him, holding that 'sell' must be given its ordinary meaning of transferring goods for a price, even though this leaves a gap that Parliament may not have intended.\n\nWhich approach to interpretation did the magistrates use?","o":["The literal rule.","The golden rule.","The mischief rule.","The purposive approach.","The ejusdem generis rule."],"a":0,"e":"The literal rule gives words their ordinary and natural meaning, even if the result seems harsh or leaves a gap, on the basis that it is for Parliament, not judges, to fix bad results. The golden rule would depart from the literal meaning only to avoid absurdity, while the mischief rule and purposive approach would look at the problem the Act was meant to cure or its overall purpose. Ejusdem generis is a rule of language about general words following a list."},
 {"t":"statutory-interpretation","q":"The (fictional) Schools Protection Act 2025 makes it an offence to protest 'within 50 metres of' a school during school hours. Priya held a protest inside a school's playground. She argues that 'within 50 metres of' a school does not include being inside the school itself. The court rejects this, saying it would be absurd if protesting near a school were an offence but protesting inside it were not.\n\nWhich approach to interpretation did the court use?","o":["The literal rule.","The golden rule.","The mischief rule.","The noscitur a sociis rule.","The expressio unius est exclusio alterius rule."],"a":1,"e":"The golden rule allows a court to depart from the literal meaning of words to avoid an absurd result; reading 'within 50 metres of' a school to include inside the school is like the approach in Adler v George ('in the vicinity of'). The literal rule would have produced the absurd result. The court did not reason from the problem the Act was passed to cure, which would be the mischief rule."},
 {"t":"statutory-interpretation","q":"Before 2024, the common law did not cover people who harassed patients by flying drones over hospital grounds. The (fictional) Hospital Grounds Act 2024 makes it an offence to cause distress to patients 'in hospital grounds'. Tariq flew a drone from outside the grounds over a hospital garden, distressing patients. The court asked what defect in the earlier law the Act was passed to cure, and convicted him.\n\nWhich approach to interpretation did the court use?","o":["The literal rule.","The golden rule.","The mischief rule.","The ejusdem generis rule.","The presumption against extra-territorial effect."],"a":2,"e":"The mischief rule (from Heydon's Case) asks what the common law was before the Act, what defect it failed to cover, what remedy Parliament provided and why, and then reads the Act to suppress the mischief. As in Smith v Hughes, where soliciting from balconies counted as soliciting 'in a street', the court read the Act to cover conduct aimed at the protected area. A literal reading might have required Tariq to be in the grounds himself."},
 {"t":"statutory-interpretation","q":"The (fictional) Public Parks Act 2025 makes it an offence to carry 'pistols, rifles, shotguns or other weapons' in a park. Dominic was arrested in a park carrying a kitchen knife in his bag. The Act contains no definition of 'weapons'.\n\nApplying the ejusdem generis rule, how is a court most likely to interpret 'other weapons'?","o":["As covering any article that could be used as a weapon, so the knife is covered.","As covering only the three types of weapon expressly listed, so the words 'other weapons' have no effect.","As covering bladed articles, because the word 'weapons' is the widest word in the list.","As ambiguous, so the court must consult Hansard to decide whether knives are covered.","As limited to weapons of the same kind as those listed, namely firearms, so the knife is not covered."],"a":4,"e":"Ejusdem generis ('of the same kind') means that general words following a list of specific words are limited to things of the same kind as the list. Pistols, rifles and shotguns form a class of firearms, so 'other weapons' is likely to be read as other firearms, which does not include a kitchen knife. The general words still have some effect (for example, other kinds of gun), so they are not ignored altogether."},
 {"t":"statutory-interpretation","q":"A (fictional) local Act requires a licence for 'cinemas and other places of entertainment'. Orla runs a bowling alley without a licence. She argues that, applying the ejusdem generis rule, 'other places of entertainment' covers only places like cinemas.\n\nHow is a court most likely to deal with Orla's argument?","o":["Accept it, because ejusdem generis always limits general words to things like the specific word that comes before them.","Accept it, because criminal statutes must always be read in favour of the defendant.","Reject it, because one specific word does not create a class, so the general words keep their wider meaning.","Reject it, because ejusdem generis applies only to Acts of Parliament, not local Acts.","Accept it, because the expressio unius rule excludes everything not expressly mentioned."],"a":2,"e":"Ejusdem generis needs at least two specific words to form a class. Where there is only one specific word ('cinemas'), there is no class to limit the general words, so they keep their wider meaning (as in Allen v Emmerson, where 'theatres and other places of amusement' covered a funfair). A bowling alley is therefore likely to be covered."},
 {"t":"statutory-interpretation","q":"The (fictional) Livestock Movement Act 2025 requires a movement licence for moving 'cattle, sheep and pigs' between farms. Gwen moves a herd of goats between her farms without a licence. The Act contains no general words after the list and no definition section.\n\nHow is a court most likely to decide whether Gwen needed a licence?","o":["She did, because goats are livestock of the same kind as those listed.","She did, because the purpose of disease control requires all livestock to be covered.","She did not, because the court must apply the golden rule to avoid an absurd result.","She did not, because expressly listing particular animals implies that others, such as goats, are excluded.","She did, because the singular and plural rule in the Interpretation Act 1978 extends the list to other farm animals."],"a":3,"e":"The expressio unius est exclusio alterius rule ('expressing one thing excludes others') means that expressly mentioning particular things excludes others not mentioned. With no general words like 'or other animals', there is nothing for ejusdem generis to work on. The Interpretation Act rule that the singular includes the plural does not add new categories to a list."},
 {"t":"statutory-interpretation","q":"A section of a 2023 Act is ambiguous. In the debate on the Bill, Imran, a backbench MP who neither introduced nor promoted the Bill, made a clear statement about what he believed the section meant. The minister in charge of the Bill said nothing on the point. Counsel wants the court to use Imran's statement to resolve the ambiguity.\n\nMay the court rely on Imran's statement?","o":["No, because Hansard may be used only for clear statements by a minister or other promoter of the Bill.","Yes, because the section is ambiguous, so any clear statement made in a Hansard debate on the Bill may be used.","Yes, because statements made in Parliament are absolutely privileged.","No, because Hansard may never be referred to in interpreting legislation.","Yes, provided the statement was not contradicted by anyone else in the debate."],"a":0,"e":"Under Pepper v Hart, a court may refer to Hansard only where all three conditions are met: the legislation is ambiguous, obscure or leads to absurdity; the material relied on consists of statements by a minister or other promoter of the Bill; and those statements are clear. Here the first and third conditions may be met, but Imran was an ordinary backbencher, not the promoter, so his statement cannot be used. The ban on Hansard was relaxed, not absolute."},
 {"t":"statutory-interpretation","q":"The (fictional) Prisons Act 2024 gives the Secretary of State a general power to make rules 'for the regulation and management of prisons'. Using this power, she makes a rule that prison staff may open and read all letters between prisoners and their solicitors. The Act says nothing specific about legal correspondence.\n\nHow is a court most likely to approach the rule?","o":["It is lawful, because the power to regulate prisons is broad enough to cover all prison correspondence, including legal letters.","It is likely to be unlawful, because general words are presumed not to authorise interference with fundamental rights.","It is lawful, because courts cannot question rules made under an Act of Parliament.","It is unlawful only if the Secretary of State failed to make a statement of compatibility when making it.","It is lawful, because prisoners lose the protection of fundamental rights while serving a sentence."],"a":1,"e":"Under the principle of legality, Parliament is presumed not to override fundamental rights by general or ambiguous words; it must 'squarely confront what it is doing' (ex p Simms). A general rule-making power is unlikely to authorise reading privileged letters between prisoners and their lawyers. Rules made under an Act are secondary legislation, which the courts can quash if they go beyond the parent Act."},
 {"t":"statutory-interpretation","q":"The (fictional) Online Safety (Amendment) Act 2026 came into force on 1 March 2026 and creates a new offence of sending certain types of message. It says nothing about events before that date. Ruth is charged with the offence for messages she sent in December 2025.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how a court will approach the charge?","o":["Ruth can be convicted, because the Act applies to all messages that are still accessible online.","Ruth can be convicted, because the presumption against retrospectivity applies only to civil liability, not to new offences.","Ruth can be convicted, provided the prosecution proves she knew the Bill was going through Parliament.","Ruth cannot be convicted, because the Act is presumed not to have retrospective effect, especially for criminal liability.","Ruth cannot be convicted, but only if she can show the messages caused no harm."],"a":3,"e":"Courts presume that an Act does not apply to past events unless it clearly says so, and this presumption is especially strong for criminal liability. Article 7 ECHR (no punishment without law) also forbids convicting someone of an offence that did not exist when they acted. The Act contains no clear words making it retrospective, so Ruth's December 2025 messages are not caught."},
 {"t":"statutory-interpretation","q":"An amending Act inserted words into a section of an earlier Act. Through an obvious drafting slip, the amendment appears to remove a right of appeal that every other part of the Act, and its stated purpose, show Parliament meant to keep. The court is sure what the provision was meant to achieve, that the drafter and Parliament failed by mistake to give effect to it, and what words Parliament would have used.\n\nWhat may the court do?","o":["Apply the words literally and leave Parliament to correct the error, because courts may never add any words to a statute.","Declare the amendment invalid and apply the earlier Act as it stood before amendment.","Make a declaration of incompatibility and leave the section to be corrected by remedial order.","Refer the section back to Parliament for correction before deciding the case.","Read the section with words added to correct the error, because the conditions for doing so are met."],"a":4,"e":"Under the purposive approach, a court may read in words to correct an obvious drafting error, but only if it is sure of the intended purpose, that by inadvertence the drafter and Parliament failed to give effect to it, and of the substance of what Parliament would have enacted (Inco Europe v First Choice Distribution). All three conditions are met here. Courts cannot declare an Act invalid, and a declaration of incompatibility concerns Convention rights, not drafting errors."},
 {"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","q":"Hollins Quarry Ltd is affected by a private Act of Parliament passed in 2025 that allows a rival company to build a railway across land next to its quarry. Hollins Quarry Ltd has evidence that the rival company obtained the Act by deliberately misleading Parliament about the environmental effects. It asks the High Court to declare the Act invalid.\n\nHow is the High Court most likely to respond?","o":["It will refuse to investigate how the Act was passed and will treat it as valid.","It will declare the Act invalid, because Parliament was deliberately misled.","It will make a declaration of incompatibility, leaving the Act in force.","It will quash the Act by judicial review, because private Acts are subject to review.","It will refer the question of validity to the Supreme Court."],"a":0,"e":"Under the enrolled Act rule, once an Act has been passed the courts will not look into the procedure by which it was passed, even if Parliament was misled (British Railways Board v Pickin, which also concerned a private Act). This follows from parliamentary sovereignty and Article 9 Bill of Rights 1689. Acts of Parliament, including private Acts, are not subject to judicial review, and a declaration of incompatibility is only for conflicts with Convention rights."},
 {"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","q":"The (fictional) Offshore Fishing Act 2025 makes it an offence to fish in a defined area of sea. Under international law, part of that area lies outside UK territorial waters. Lars, a foreign trawler captain, is prosecuted for fishing in that part of the area. He argues that the Act cannot apply to him because it breaches international law.\n\nHow is the court most likely to deal with Lars's argument?","o":["It will refuse to apply the Act, because international law takes priority over domestic legislation.","It will refuse to apply the Act, because all Acts are presumed not to have extra-territorial effect, whatever their wording.","It will apply the Act, because an Act of Parliament is valid and must be applied even if it breaches international law.","It will make a declaration that the Act is invalid so far as it breaches international law.","It will stay the prosecution until an international court has ruled on the dispute."],"a":2,"e":"Parliament can make or unmake any law, and no court can set aside an Act because it breaches international law (Mortensen v Peters, a similar fishing case). The presumption that Parliament does not intend to breach international law, or to legislate extra-territorially, is only a starting point that clear words displace. Here the Act defines the area expressly, so the court must apply it."},
 {"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","q":"Section 9 of the (fictional) Licensing Reform Act 2025, an ordinary Act, is inconsistent with a provision of the Human Rights Act 1998. The 2025 Act does not mention the Human Rights Act and contains no words showing that Parliament intended to change it. A claimant argues that, as the later Act, section 9 has impliedly repealed the relevant part of the Human Rights Act.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["The Human Rights Act is impliedly repealed to the extent of the inconsistency, because the later Act always prevails over the earlier.","The 2025 Act is invalid to the extent of the inconsistency, because the Human Rights Act is entrenched.","The court must make a declaration of incompatibility and apply section 9.","The Human Rights Act is not impliedly repealed, because constitutional statutes need express or unmistakably clear words.","The court must apply whichever provision is more favourable to the claimant."],"a":3,"e":"The ordinary rule is implied repeal: a later Act prevails over an earlier inconsistent one. But some Acts, such as the Human Rights Act 1998, the devolution Acts and the Bill of Rights 1689, are constitutional statutes, which cannot be impliedly repealed and need express or unmistakably clear words (Thoburn v Sunderland City Council). The HRA is not entrenched in the sense that it invalidates later Acts; Parliament could repeal it by clear words."},
 {"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","q":"In 2025 the Supreme Court held that the government must pay compensation to Ridgeway Farms Ltd for crops destroyed during a military exercise. In 2026 Parliament passes the (fictional) Military Exercises (Compensation) Act 2026, which provides that no compensation shall be payable, or shall ever have been payable, for such damage. Ridgeway Farms Ltd has not yet been paid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the 2026 Act?","o":["It is valid and removes the right retrospectively, because Parliament can legislate to reverse a court decision.","It is invalid, because Parliament cannot overturn a decision of the Supreme Court.","It is valid, but applies only to damage caused after it comes into force.","It is valid, but the court will strike it down because it breaches the rule of law.","It is invalid, because retrospective legislation can be made only by statutory instrument approved by both Houses of Parliament."],"a":0,"e":"Because Parliament is sovereign, it can change the law even to reverse a court decision, and can do so retrospectively if it uses clear words, as it did in the War Damage Act 1965 after Burmah Oil v Lord Advocate. The presumption against retrospectivity is displaced by the express words 'or shall ever have been payable'. The courts cannot strike down an Act for breaching the rule of law."},
 {"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","q":"The High Court ordered the Home Secretary not to remove Abdi from the UK while his asylum claim was being considered. The Home Secretary, believing the order was wrongly made, allowed Abdi's removal to go ahead. Abdi's lawyers apply to have the Home Secretary held in contempt of court.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's power?","o":["The court has no power, because ministers of the Crown are immune from contempt proceedings.","The court has no power, because the separation of powers prevents courts controlling ministers.","The court may find the Home Secretary in contempt in his official capacity, as ministers are subject to court orders.","The court may only report the matter to Parliament, which alone can discipline a minister.","The court may find the Home Secretary in contempt only if he acted in bad faith or for some personal reason of his own."],"a":2,"e":"In M v Home Office the House of Lords held that a minister who breached a court order not to deport an asylum seeker could be found in contempt of court in his official capacity. This reflects the rule of law (government officials are subject to the ordinary law) and the courts' role in holding the executive to account. The separation of powers does not prevent the courts controlling the executive's legality."},
 {"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","q":"Following a general election, the new Prime Minister wants to appoint 110 ministers from among MPs, so that more of the government's MPs are bound to support it in votes. All of them would continue to sit and vote in the House of Commons.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the legal position?","o":["The plan is lawful, because the appointment of ministers is a prerogative power that statute does not limit.","No more than 95 holders of ministerial office may sit and vote in the House of Commons at any one time.","The plan is lawful, but breaches the convention of collective ministerial responsibility.","The plan is unlawful, because ministers must sit in the House of Lords rather than the Commons.","The plan is lawful, provided the House of Commons approves each appointment."],"a":1,"e":"The House of Commons Disqualification Act 1975 limits the number of ministers who may sit and vote in the Commons to 95. This is one of the checks on the overlap between the executive and the legislature in the UK's partial separation of powers. Ministers sit in either House by convention, and the Commons does not approve individual appointments."},
 {"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","q":"A government department refuses applications for a hardship grant by applying detailed eligibility rules in an internal manual. The manual is not published, and applicants cannot find out what the rules are, so they cannot tell whether they qualify or what evidence to provide.\n\nWhich principle of the rule of law is most directly engaged?","o":["That no one may be punished except for a breach of law established in the ordinary courts.","That the state must comply with its obligations in international law.","That Parliament can make or unmake any law.","That rights in the UK derive from judicial decisions rather than a written constitution.","That the law must be accessible, intelligible, clear and predictable."],"a":4,"e":"Lord Bingham's first principle of the rule of law is that the law must be accessible and, so far as possible, intelligible, clear and predictable, so that people can know their rights and obligations. Secret rules applied to applicants breach this principle. No one is being punished here, and parliamentary sovereignty is a separate constitutional principle, not part of the rule of law."},
 {"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","q":"The (fictional) Courts and Tribunals Fees Act 2025 gives the Lord Chancellor a general power to set fees for proceedings. Using it, he makes an order setting fees of up to £3,000 to bring a claim in the employment tribunal. Evidence shows that claims have fallen by 75% because many low-paid workers cannot afford the fees.\n\nHow is a court most likely to treat the fees order?","o":["As lawful, because the Act gives the Lord Chancellor an unrestricted power to set fees.","As unlawful, because it effectively prevents access to justice, which general words do not authorise.","As lawful, because a court can never review secondary legislation that has been made under an Act of Parliament.","As valid, but the court will make a declaration of incompatibility.","As lawful, unless Parliament annulled it within 40 days."],"a":1,"e":"Access to the courts is inherent in the rule of law and is a fundamental right, which general words in an Act are presumed not to remove. In R (UNISON) v Lord Chancellor, employment tribunal fees that effectively prevented access to justice were held unlawful and the fees order was quashed. A fees order is secondary legislation, which the courts can review and quash; declarations of incompatibility are for primary legislation."},
 {"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","q":"A former Cabinet minister plans to publish a book revealing details of Cabinet discussions held six months ago about a sensitive policy that is still under negotiation, including the views of individual ministers. The government applies for an injunction to stop publication, relying on the convention of collective ministerial responsibility.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's approach?","o":["It will enforce the convention directly by injunction, because conventions are binding rules of the constitution.","It will refuse to consider the convention at all, because conventions are irrelevant in legal proceedings.","It must grant an injunction, because Cabinet discussions are protected by parliamentary privilege.","It will not enforce the convention as such, but may recognise it when applying the law of confidence.","It will refuse an injunction, because ministers waive confidentiality when they leave office."],"a":3,"e":"Courts do not enforce conventions, but they may recognise them when deciding a legal issue. In Attorney General v Jonathan Cape, the court accepted that collective responsibility makes Cabinet discussions confidential, which the law of confidence can protect, but refused an injunction because about ten years had passed. Where discussions are recent and still sensitive, the court may restrain publication under the law of confidence. Cabinet meetings are not proceedings in Parliament."},
 {"t":"sovereignty-rule-of-law","q":"A High Court judge is hearing a claim under the (fictional) Benefit Overpayments Act 2025. The words of the relevant section are clear and plainly require the claimant to repay an overpayment caused by an official's mistake. The judge considers the result deeply unjust and proposes to decline to apply the section. No Convention right is engaged.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the judge's position?","o":["He may decline to apply the section, because judges have an inherent power to prevent injustice in individual cases.","He may decline to apply the section under the golden rule, because any unjust result is absurd.","He may strike down the section, because it breaches the rule of law.","He may decline to apply the section if the government does not object.","He must apply the clear words, because judges may not refuse to apply an Act because they think the result unjust."],"a":4,"e":"Judges must respect Parliament's law-making role: they interpret and apply Acts and must not invent reasons to avoid applying plain words because they consider the result unjust (Duport Steels v Sirs). The golden rule applies to absurd or repugnant results where the words allow another reading, not simply where an outcome seems harsh. No court can strike down an Act, and with no Convention right engaged, s.3 HRA does not help."},
 {"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","q":"The (fictional) Immigration Control Act 2024 gives the Home Secretary power to make deportation orders. A deportation order against Viktor is signed by a senior civil servant in the Home Office, in the Home Secretary's name. The Home Secretary never saw the file. The Act does not require the Home Secretary to decide personally. Viktor argues the order is invalid.\n\nIs Viktor's argument likely to succeed?","o":["Yes, because statutory powers given to a minister may be exercised only by the minister personally, whatever the Act says.","Yes, because delegation by a minister to an official is always unlawful delegation.","No, because officials may exercise a minister's powers in the minister's name, and the minister answers to Parliament.","No, because deportation is a prerogative power that does not need to be exercised by a minister.","Yes, unless the Home Secretary later ratifies the decision in Parliament."],"a":2,"e":"Under the Carltona principle, civil servants can exercise a minister's powers in the minister's name, because ministers cannot personally take every decision and remain accountable to Parliament for those taken by their officials. This is an exception to the rule against unlawful delegation. It does not apply where the statute requires the minister personally to decide, but the Act here does not."},
 {"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","q":"Parliament passes the (fictional) Flood Victims Compensation Act 2024, which sets up a statutory compensation scheme to come into force on a day appointed by the Secretary of State. The Secretary of State announces that she will never bring the scheme into force and instead sets up a cheaper scheme under the royal prerogative.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lawfulness of the Secretary of State's action?","o":["It is lawful, because the Secretary of State has an unlimited discretion over when to commence an Act.","It is lawful, because the prerogative scheme provides compensation and so achieves the Act's purpose.","It is unlawful only if the prerogative scheme pays less than the statutory scheme would have paid in every single case.","It is unlawful, because the prerogative cannot be used to frustrate the will of Parliament expressed in a statute.","It is lawful, because decisions about compensation schemes are non-justiciable."],"a":3,"e":"A minister has a discretion about when to bring a statutory provision into force, but cannot use the prerogative to frustrate Parliament's will. In R v Home Secretary ex p Fire Brigades Union, a decision never to commence a statutory compensation scheme and to replace it with a cheaper prerogative scheme was an abuse of power. Criminal injuries compensation was treated as justiciable."},
 {"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","q":"The UK is party to a (fictional) treaty on environmental standards. An Act of Parliament gives individuals rights in domestic law that depend on the UK remaining a party to the treaty. The government announces that it will withdraw from the treaty using the royal prerogative, which would remove those statutory rights, without seeking an Act of Parliament.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the legal position?","o":["The government may withdraw using the prerogative, because making and unmaking treaties is a prerogative power.","The government may withdraw using the prerogative, provided the treaty is first laid before Parliament for 21 sitting days.","An Act of Parliament is needed, because the prerogative cannot be used to remove rights created by Parliament.","The government may withdraw using the prerogative, because the courts cannot review decisions about treaties.","The government needs only a resolution of the House of Commons approving withdrawal."],"a":2,"e":"Treaty-making is a prerogative power, but in Miller 1 the Supreme Court held that the prerogative cannot be used to change domestic law or remove rights that Parliament has created. Because withdrawal would remove statutory rights, an Act of Parliament is required, just as it was for giving notice to leave the EU. The 21-day procedure in the Constitutional Reform and Governance Act 2010 concerns ratification, and a Commons resolution is not an Act."},
 {"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","q":"The government decides, under the royal prerogative, to deploy troops overseas as part of a multinational peacekeeping force. A campaign group believes the decision is unwise and seeks judicial review, arguing that the government failed to consider the risks to civilians.\n\nHow is the court most likely to treat the claim?","o":["As justiciable, because every exercise of the prerogative can be reviewed on ordinary grounds.","As non-justiciable, because decisions about deploying the armed forces are unsuitable for judicial review.","As justiciable, but only on the ground of procedural impropriety.","As non-justiciable, because prerogative powers can never be judicially reviewed.","As justiciable, because the campaign group has a sufficient interest."],"a":1,"e":"Since the GCHQ case, the exercise of prerogative powers can be judicially reviewed, but whether a court will review depends on the subject matter. Defence and the deployment of the armed forces are among the excluded categories involving high policy judgments unsuited to courts. Standing alone does not make a non-justiciable matter reviewable."},
 {"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","q":"The government claims that the royal prerogative gives it a power to regulate cryptocurrency exchanges, an activity that did not exist until recently. No statute or court decision has recognised any such prerogative power, and the government wants to use it to impose licence requirements on exchanges.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the government's claim?","o":["It will fail, because new prerogative powers cannot be created and the prerogative cannot be extended to new areas.","It will succeed, because the prerogative adapts to cover new activities affecting the national interest.","It will succeed, provided Parliament does not object within 40 days.","It will fail only if a statute already regulates cryptocurrency exchanges.","It will succeed, because whether a prerogative power exists is a matter for the government to decide, not the courts."],"a":0,"e":"The prerogative is the residue of old common law powers of the Crown; it can shrink but cannot grow. In BBC v Johns the Court of Appeal said it was '350 years and a civil war too late' to extend the prerogative. Whether a prerogative power exists, and its extent, is for the courts to decide (Case of Proclamations), and the King cannot change the law or create new obligations by prerogative."},
 {"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","q":"The Home Secretary decides to supply specialist riot equipment to police forces from a central store, using the prerogative power to keep the peace. A police authority argues that she can act only under the Police Act, which deals with police funding but does not expressly cover the supply of equipment or displace the prerogative.\n\nIs the Home Secretary's decision likely to be lawful?","o":["No, because once Parliament has legislated on policing at all, every prerogative power in that area is abolished.","No, because the prerogative cannot be used where a statute deals with the same general subject.","Yes, because the prerogative always takes priority over statute in matters of public order.","No, because the prerogative to keep the peace was abolished by the Police Act.","Yes, because the prerogative to keep the peace exists and the statute does not cover the same ground."],"a":4,"e":"In R v Home Secretary ex p Northumbria Police Authority, the Home Secretary could supply plastic bullets to police forces under the prerogative to keep the peace, because the Police Act 1964 did not expressly displace it. The De Keyser principle applies only where a statute covers the same ground, putting the prerogative into abeyance. Statute always takes priority over the prerogative where they do overlap."},
 {"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","q":"The Prime Minister asks the King to dissolve Parliament and call a general election two years after the last one. An opposition MP claims the request is an abuse of power because it is designed to take advantage of a temporary lead in the polls, and seeks judicial review of the decision.\n\nHow is the court most likely to respond?","o":["It will refuse to consider the claim, because statute provides that the courts may not question the dissolution power.","It will review the decision on ordinary grounds, because the dissolution power was reviewed in the earlier prorogation case.","It will quash the dissolution if the Prime Minister cannot give a reasonable justification for it.","It will refuse the claim, because the MP has no sufficient interest.","It will make a declaration that the dissolution was unlawful but leave it in effect."],"a":0,"e":"The Dissolution and Calling of Parliament Act 2022 revived the prerogative power to dissolve Parliament, and s.3 contains an ouster clause: a court may not question the exercise or purported exercise of the power, any decision about it, or its limits or extent. Miller 2 concerned prorogation (suspending Parliament), which remains an ordinary reviewable prerogative. Standing is not the reason the claim fails."},
 {"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","q":"The Prime Minister advises the King to prorogue Parliament for seven weeks during a national crisis, preventing debates and scrutiny of the government's emergency measures. No reason for the length of the prorogation is given. A claimant seeks judicial review.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court is likely to decide the claim?","o":["The claim is non-justiciable, because prorogation is a political decision for the Prime Minister and the monarch to make alone.","The claim must be refused, because statute prevents the courts questioning prorogation.","The advice is lawful, because the King, not the Prime Minister, prorogued Parliament.","The advice is unlawful, but the prorogation remains effective until Parliament is recalled.","The advice is unlawful as it frustrates Parliament's functions without reasonable justification, so the prorogation is void."],"a":4,"e":"In Miller 2 the Supreme Court held that the limits of the power to prorogue are justiciable, and that a prorogation is unlawful if it frustrates or prevents, without reasonable justification, Parliament's ability to carry out its functions as a legislature and as the body that scrutinises the executive. With no justification, the advice was unlawful and the prorogation void, as if it had never happened. The statutory ouster in s.3 DCPA 2022 covers dissolution, not prorogation."},
 {"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","q":"The Cabinet decides to build a new airport runway. Sarah, a junior minister, gives a newspaper interview publicly attacking the decision but says she will not resign. A constituent of Sarah's asks a court to order her to resign or to stop speaking against government policy.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Sarah has breached the convention of individual ministerial responsibility, and the court may order her to resign.","Sarah has broken no rule, because collective responsibility applies only to Cabinet ministers.","Sarah has breached a statutory duty in the Ministerial Code, which the court may enforce by injunction.","Sarah has breached the convention of collective responsibility, but no court will order her to resign.","Sarah has committed a contempt of Parliament, which the court may punish."],"a":3,"e":"Collective ministerial responsibility is a convention that requires ministers to support government decisions publicly or resign, and it applies to all ministers, not just Cabinet members. Conventions are not enforced by the courts; breach has political consequences such as dismissal by the Prime Minister. The Ministerial Code is not law, and individual responsibility concerns a minister's own department and conduct."},
 {"t":"crown-executive-prerogative","q":"Ffion has suffered long delays and lost documents in her dealings with a government department, causing her financial loss. She has no legal claim against the department. She writes directly to the Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman asking it to investigate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ffion's position?","o":["The Ombudsman must investigate her complaint, and its findings bind the department in exactly the same way as a court order.","A complaint about a department must be referred to the Ombudsman by an MP, and any recommendation is not legally binding.","She must first bring judicial review proceedings before the Ombudsman can investigate.","The Ombudsman cannot investigate, because delay and lost documents are not maladministration.","She may complain directly to the Ombudsman, and can enforce any recommendation in the County Court."],"a":1,"e":"The Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman investigates complaints of maladministration (poor administration such as delay or failure to follow procedure) causing injustice. Complaints about government departments must be referred by an MP (the 'MP filter' under the Parliamentary Commissioner Act 1967). The Ombudsman's recommendations are not legally binding, although departments usually comply."},
 {"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","q":"The government introduces its (fictional) Rivers Restoration Bill in the House of Lords. The Lords pass it, but the House of Commons later amends it heavily and the Lords refuse to accept the amendments. The government wants to use the Parliament Acts in the next session to pass the Bill in the form approved by the Commons without the Lords' consent.\n\nCan the Parliament Acts be used?","o":["No, because the Parliament Acts cannot be used for a Bill that started in the House of Lords.","Yes, provided the Commons passes the Bill again in the next session at least a year after its second reading.","Yes, because the Parliament Acts apply to any public Bill that the Lords reject.","No, because the Parliament Acts can be used only for Money Bills.","Yes, provided the Speaker certifies that the Bill implements a manifesto commitment."],"a":0,"e":"The Parliament Acts 1911 and 1949 allow most public Bills to receive Royal Assent without the Lords' consent, but they cannot be used for a Bill that starts in the House of Lords, a Bill to extend the life of Parliament beyond five years, private Bills, or delegated legislation. This Bill started in the Lords, so the Parliament Acts are unavailable. The manifesto point relates to the Salisbury convention, not the Parliament Acts."},
 {"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","q":"The Speaker certifies the (fictional) Finance (Income Tax Rates) Bill as a Money Bill, because it deals only with national taxation. The Bill passes the Commons and is sent to the Lords six weeks before the end of the session. Five weeks later the Lords have still not passed it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Bill's position?","o":["It cannot become law in this session, because the Lords can delay any public Bill, including a Money Bill, for about one year.","It can receive Royal Assent without the Lords' consent, because the Lords have not passed it within one month.","It must be passed again by the Commons in the next session before it can receive Royal Assent.","It falls at the end of the session, because Money Bills need the Lords' approval.","It can receive Royal Assent only if the Lords expressly vote it down."],"a":1,"e":"Under the Parliament Act 1911, if the Lords do not pass a Bill certified by the Speaker as a Money Bill within one month of receiving it (where it was sent up at least a month before the end of the session), it may receive Royal Assent without them. The one-year delay and two-session requirement apply to other public Bills. A Lords vote against the Bill is not needed."},
 {"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","q":"An Act allows the Secretary of State to make regulations on food labelling, subject to annulment by a resolution of either House. The regulations are made and laid before Parliament. A group of MPs criticises them in a Commons debate, but no motion to annul them is passed in either House within 40 days.\n\nWhat is the status of the regulations?","o":["They lapse, because they were not approved by a resolution of both Houses within 40 days of being laid before Parliament.","They remain law only if the Lords Secondary Legislation Scrutiny Committee approves them.","They lapse, because a Commons debate criticising them amounts to a rejection.","They remain law, because under the negative procedure they are annulled only if either House resolves to annul them.","They remain law, but can be amended by either House at any time."],"a":3,"e":"Under the negative procedure (the most common), an SI becomes or stays law unless either House annuls it within 40 days. Criticism in a debate without a successful annulment motion has no legal effect. Approval by both Houses is the affirmative procedure, and Parliament can accept or reject an SI but cannot amend it."},
 {"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","q":"An Act provides that regulations setting new licence fees 'may not be made unless a draft has been laid before and approved by a resolution of each House of Parliament'. The minister lays a draft, which the Commons approves. Before the Lords vote on it, the minister makes the regulations and they are published.\n\nWhat is the legal status of the regulations?","o":["They are lawful, because the Commons has primacy and its approval is enough.","They are unlawful, because under the draft affirmative procedure they could be made only after approval by both Houses.","They are lawful, but will lapse if the Lords vote against them within 40 days.","They are lawful, because the Lords cannot reject secondary legislation.","They are lawful, because the courts cannot review any failure to follow parliamentary procedure on secondary legislation."],"a":1,"e":"Under the draft affirmative procedure, a statutory instrument must be approved by a resolution of both Houses before it is made (Commons-only approval applies only where the parent Act says so, typically for financial matters). Making the regulations without the Lords' approval fails to follow a mandatory procedure in the parent Act, so they are procedurally ultra vires and can be quashed. Courts can review secondary legislation, unlike Acts."},
 {"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","q":"An Act requires the Secretary of State, before making regulations on industry training levies, to consult 'organisations representing employers in the industry concerned'. She makes regulations imposing a levy on the horticulture industry without consulting the National Growers' Association, a well-known body representing horticultural employers.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the regulations?","o":["They are procedurally ultra vires because a mandatory consultation requirement was not met.","They are valid, because consultation is a matter of good practice and not a legal requirement.","They are valid, because once regulations are laid before Parliament the courts cannot question them.","They are substantively ultra vires because the minister had no power to impose a levy.","They are valid, but the Secretary of State must consult before making any future regulations."],"a":0,"e":"Secondary legislation is procedurally ultra vires where the minister fails to follow a mandatory procedure in the parent Act, such as required consultation. In Aylesbury Mushrooms an order was held not binding on a mushroom growers' association that should have been consulted but was not. The power to impose a levy existed, so the defect is procedural rather than substantive."},
 {"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","q":"An Act gives the Secretary of State a broad power to make regulations about the allocation of social housing. Regulations made under it exclude all same-sex couples from joint tenancies. The High Court finds the regulations incompatible with Articles 8 and 14 ECHR. Nothing in the Act requires such an exclusion.\n\nWhat can the High Court do?","o":["Only make a declaration of incompatibility, because courts cannot set aside legislation.","Only make a declaration of incompatibility, because the regulations were approved by both Houses before they came into force.","Quash the regulations, because they are secondary legislation that the parent Act does not require to be incompatible.","Nothing, because the regulations are within the broad power given by the Act.","Strike down the parent Act as well as the regulations."],"a":2,"e":"Courts cannot quash an Act of Parliament, but they can quash secondary legislation that is incompatible with Convention rights, unless the parent Act itself requires the incompatibility. Because the Act gives a broad discretion that could have been used compatibly, the regulations can be quashed. A declaration of incompatibility is the remedy for primary legislation (or secondary legislation whose incompatibility the parent Act makes unavoidable)."},
 {"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","q":"Section 20 of the (fictional) Licensing Act 2023 allows the minister by regulations to 'amend this Act' to update licence categories. The minister uses the power to make regulations that amend the Gambling Act 2005, a different Act, to remove certain licensing exemptions.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the regulations?","o":["They are valid, because a Henry VIII power allows a minister to amend any Act of Parliament.","They are valid, because regulations approved by Parliament have the same status as an Act.","They are ultra vires, because secondary legislation can never amend primary legislation, whatever the parent Act says.","They are ultra vires, because a power to amend primary legislation is read narrowly and covers only the 2023 Act.","They are valid, provided they were made by the affirmative procedure."],"a":3,"e":"A provision letting ministers amend or repeal primary legislation by regulations is a Henry VIII power. Courts interpret such powers narrowly and require clear authority. The power here allows amendment of 'this Act' only, so regulations amending the Gambling Act 2005 go beyond it. Secondary legislation can amend primary legislation where the parent Act clearly authorises it, so the statement that it never can is wrong."},
 {"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","q":"The Home Secretary is about to introduce a government Bill in the House of Commons containing powers to detain migrants. She considers that she cannot honestly state that the Bill is compatible with Convention rights, but the government wishes to proceed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes her position?","o":["She cannot introduce the Bill, because every government Bill must be certified as compatible with Convention rights before introduction.","She must first obtain a declaration from the High Court that the Bill is compatible.","She may proceed, but the Act will be invalid if it is later found to be incompatible.","She need make no statement, because the requirement applies only to Private Members' Bills.","She may make a statement that, although she cannot make a statement of compatibility, the government wishes to proceed."],"a":4,"e":"Under s.19 Human Rights Act 1998, before second reading of a government Bill in each House, the minister in charge must make either a statement of compatibility or a statement that, although one cannot be made, the government wishes the House to proceed. Parliament can then pass the Bill, and the resulting Act would be valid even if incompatible; the most a court could do is make a declaration of incompatibility."},
 {"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","q":"Clive, the managing director of a retail company, gives oral evidence to a House of Commons select committee inquiry into supermarket pricing. During his evidence he accuses a rival company of fixing prices. The rival company says the allegation is false and wishes to sue Clive for defamation for what he said to the committee.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the rival company's position?","o":["It can sue, because only MPs and peers are protected by parliamentary privilege.","It can sue, because select committee evidence is protected only by qualified privilege.","It cannot sue, because evidence to a select committee is a proceeding in Parliament and absolutely privileged.","It cannot sue unless the committee first gives permission for legal proceedings to be brought about the evidence.","It can sue, because the allegation was made by a witness rather than in a debate."],"a":2,"e":"Article 9 Bill of Rights 1689 protects 'proceedings in Parliament' from being impeached or questioned in any court. Proceedings in Parliament include committee proceedings and evidence given to committees, so witnesses as well as members are protected. The privilege is absolute, so no defamation claim can be brought on that evidence; repetition outside Parliament would not be protected."},
 {"t":"parliament-legislation-privilege","q":"During a Commons debate, an MP accuses a named property developer of bribing council officials. The next morning, the Daily Courier publishes a fair and accurate report of the debate, including the accusation. The developer wants to sue the newspaper for defamation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the newspaper's position?","o":["The report has absolute privilege under Article 9 of the Bill of Rights, so no claim can ever be brought.","The report has no protection, because privilege belongs only to the MP who spoke.","The report has absolute privilege, because it was published by order of the House.","The report is protected only if the newspaper first obtained the MP's consent.","The report has qualified privilege, so the newspaper is protected unless it published with malice."],"a":4,"e":"Fair and accurate reports of parliamentary proceedings, such as press reports, have qualified privilege, which protects the publisher unless it acted with malice. Absolute privilege attaches to the proceedings themselves (Article 9) and to papers published by order of either House, such as Hansard (Parliamentary Papers Act 1840). A newspaper report is not published by order of the House."},
 {"t":"devolution-wales","q":"Senedd Cymru has just passed a Bill on the licensing of private water supplies. The UK Government has reasonable grounds to believe that the Bill would have an adverse effect on the operation of the law as it applies in England, but does not think it is outside the Senedd's competence. The Bill was passed ten days ago.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the UK Government can do?","o":["The Secretary of State for Wales may, within four weeks, make an order prohibiting submission for Royal Assent.","The Attorney General may make an order prohibiting the Bill from being submitted for Royal Assent.","The Counsel General for Wales must refer the Bill to the Supreme Court whenever the UK Government asks him to do so.","The Secretary of State for Wales may ask the King to refuse Royal Assent once the Bill is submitted.","Nothing, because a Bill within competence cannot be stopped once the Senedd has passed it."],"a":0,"e":"Under s.114 GoWA 2006, within four weeks of a Bill being passed, the Secretary of State for Wales may make an order prohibiting its submission for Royal Assent if there are reasonable grounds to believe it would have an adverse effect on a reserved matter or on the operation of the law as it applies in England (or would be incompatible with international obligations or the interests of defence or national security). The Attorney General's role is to refer competence questions to the Supreme Court, not to make s.114 orders. Royal Assent is not refused by the monarch in practice."},
 {"t":"devolution-wales","q":"When a Senedd Bill on the regulation of dog breeding was introduced, the Presiding Officer stated that in her view it would be within the Senedd's legislative competence. After the Bill is passed, the Attorney General considers that it relates to a reserved matter and wants the question decided.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Attorney General's position?","o":["He cannot challenge the Bill, because the Presiding Officer's statement on introduction conclusively settles the question of competence.","He may challenge the Bill only after it has received Royal Assent, by bringing judicial review.","He may refer the question to the Supreme Court within four weeks of the Bill being passed, as the statement is not binding.","He may make an order prohibiting the Bill from being submitted for Royal Assent.","He must ask the Counsel General for Wales to make the reference on his behalf."],"a":2,"e":"On introduction, the member in charge and the Presiding Officer must each state their view on competence, but these statements are not binding. Under s.112 GoWA 2006, the Attorney General or the Counsel General may refer the question whether a Bill is within competence to the Supreme Court within four weeks of it being passed, and only the Supreme Court decides conclusively. The power to make a prohibition order belongs to the Secretary of State for Wales under s.114."},
 {"t":"devolution-wales","q":"A Senedd Bill on consumer protection includes a provision changing the general rules of contract law in Wales, so that every contract (whatever its subject) must be in writing to be enforceable. The provision is not incidental to, or consequential on, any other provision about a devolved matter.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the provision?","o":["It is within competence, because private law is not listed as a reserved matter.","It is within competence, because consumer protection is a devolved matter.","It is outside competence only if the Secretary of State for Wales makes an order against it within four weeks.","It is outside competence, because it modifies the private law in breach of a restriction in GoWA 2006.","It is within competence, because it applies only in relation to Wales."],"a":3,"e":"Under s.108A(2)(d) GoWA 2006, a provision is outside competence if it breaches a restriction in Schedule 7B. One restriction is that an Act of the Senedd may not modify the private law (such as contract, tort and property) except where this is incidental to, or consequential on, a provision about a devolved matter. A general change to all contracts is not incidental, so the provision is not law, even though it applies only in Wales."},
 {"t":"devolution-wales","q":"A Senedd Bill sets minimum rates of pay and conditions for workers on Welsh farms, with the aim of supporting the rural economy and the agricultural industry in Wales. The Attorney General argues that the Bill relates to employment rights, a reserved matter. Agriculture is not reserved.\n\nHow is the Supreme Court likely to decide whether the Bill relates to a reserved matter?","o":["By asking whether the Bill touches on a reserved matter in any way at all, in which case it is wholly outside competence.","By looking at the Bill's purpose, having regard to its effect, so a Bill aimed at agriculture may be within competence.","By deferring to the Presiding Officer's statement on competence made when the Bill was introduced.","By applying a presumption that any doubt must be resolved in favour of the UK Parliament.","By asking whether the UK Parliament has legislated on the same subject in England."],"a":1,"e":"Whether a provision 'relates to' a reserved matter is decided by reference to its purpose, having regard to its effect in all the circumstances (s.108A(6) GoWA 2006). A provision that only touches a reserved matter incidentally may still be within competence. In the Agricultural Sector (Wales) Bill reference, a Welsh scheme for agricultural wages was held to relate to agriculture and so was within competence."},
 {"t":"devolution-wales","q":"An Act of Senedd Cymru allows Welsh councils to seize and sell abandoned vehicles without any notice to the owner and without any compensation. Erin's car was seized and sold under the Act. A court finds that the provision is incompatible with her right to the protection of property under Article 1 of Protocol 1 ECHR.\n\nWhat is the effect of that finding on the provision?","o":["The provision remains valid, and the court may make a declaration of incompatibility.","The provision remains valid until the Senedd repeals it.","The provision remains valid, because Acts of the Senedd have the same status as Acts of Parliament.","The provision is valid but must be referred to the Secretary of State for Wales for amendment.","The provision is outside the Senedd's competence and so is not law."],"a":4,"e":"A provision of an Act of the Senedd is outside legislative competence, and so is not law, if it is incompatible with Convention rights (s.108A(2)(e) GoWA 2006). This differs from an Act of the UK Parliament, which remains valid and can only receive a declaration of incompatibility (s.4 HRA). Acts of the Senedd do not share Westminster's sovereignty."},
 {"t":"devolution-wales","q":"An Act of Senedd Cymru gives Welsh Ministers power to 'issue directions to any public body about flood defences'. Read widely, the words could cover UK Government departments and other bodies whose functions relate to reserved matters, which would be outside competence. Read narrowly, they cover only Welsh devolved bodies.\n\nHow should a court interpret the provision?","o":["As widely as its words allow, and then declare it not law in its entirety.","In the way that best gives effect to the Welsh Government's intention as stated in the Senedd.","As narrowly as is required for it to be within competence, covering only bodies the Senedd could direct.","As covering all public bodies, because Acts of the Senedd are read in exactly the same way as Acts of the UK Parliament.","By referring the question of meaning to the Secretary of State for Wales."],"a":2,"e":"Under s.154 GoWA 2006, a provision of an Act of the Senedd that could be read as outside competence must be read as narrowly as is required for it to be within competence, if such a reading is possible, and has effect accordingly. Here a narrow reading limited to devolved Welsh bodies is available, so the provision is valid on that reading."},
 {"t":"devolution-wales","q":"Rhodri is prosecuted in a magistrates' court in Cardiff for an offence created by an Act of Senedd Cymru that received Royal Assent two years ago. He argues that the provision creating the offence is about a reserved matter and so was never within the Senedd's competence. The prosecution says competence can be challenged only before Royal Assent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Rhodri cannot raise competence, because a Bill not referred to the Supreme Court before Royal Assent is conclusively within competence.","Rhodri may raise competence only by bringing a separate claim in the Supreme Court.","Rhodri cannot raise competence, because only the law officers may question the validity of an Act of the Senedd.","Rhodri may raise competence as a devolution issue in the proceedings; if the provision is outside competence, it is not law.","Rhodri may raise competence, but the most the court can do is make a declaration of incompatibility."],"a":3,"e":"Competence can be decided after Royal Assent as a 'devolution issue' arising in ordinary litigation, including criminal proceedings (GoWA 2006 Schedule 9), and law officers can also refer such issues to the Supreme Court. A provision of an Act of the Senedd that is outside competence is not law (s.108A(1)). A pre-Assent reference is optional, so the lack of one does not settle competence."},
 {"t":"devolution-wales","q":"A Senedd Bill would create a separate system of Welsh civil courts, with its own judges, to hear all civil claims arising in Wales instead of the County Court and High Court.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether this provision would be within the Senedd's competence?","o":["It would be outside competence, because the single legal jurisdiction and the courts are reserved matters.","It would be within competence, because the administration of justice and the courts in Wales are devolved matters.","It would be within competence, provided the UK Parliament passes a legislative consent motion.","It would be outside competence only if it applied to criminal as well as civil claims.","It would be within competence, because it applies only in relation to Wales."],"a":0,"e":"Schedule 7A GoWA 2006 reserves the single legal jurisdiction of England and Wales and the courts (as well as most tribunals and most of justice and policing). A provision that relates to a reserved matter is outside competence (s.108A(2)(c)), even if it applies only in Wales. Legislative consent motions are passed by the devolved legislature, not by the UK Parliament, and do not confer competence."},
 {"t":"devolution-wales","q":"The UK Government is preparing a Bill that changes immigration rules throughout the UK, including in Wales. A Welsh Government minister says that, under the Sewel convention, the Bill cannot lawfully apply in Wales unless the Senedd first passes a legislative consent motion.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The minister is right: the Bill will be invalid in Wales unless the Senedd consents.","The minister is wrong: immigration is reserved, so the convention does not apply, and it is not legally enforceable.","The minister is right, because the Sewel convention is now set out in statute and so legally binds the UK Parliament.","The minister is wrong only because Parliament may override the convention by a two-thirds majority.","The minister is right, but only for provisions that apply exclusively to Wales."],"a":1,"e":"The Sewel convention is that the UK Parliament will not normally legislate with regard to devolved matters without the Senedd's consent. Immigration is reserved, so the convention is not engaged. In any case, although the convention is recognised in statute (s.107(6) GoWA 2006), it is a political convention that the courts will not enforce (Miller 1), and Westminster's power to legislate for Wales is preserved (s.107(5))."},
 {"t":"devolution-wales","q":"Following a change of government, a Bill is introduced in the UK Parliament to abolish Senedd Cymru and the Welsh Government and return all their functions to Westminster. No referendum is proposed. Campaigners say that this would be illegal.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Abolition is legally impossible, because the Senedd is a permanent part of the constitution that only the Senedd can abolish.","Abolition requires the consent of the Senedd by legislative consent motion, which the courts will enforce.","Abolition would be valid only if approved by the Supreme Court on a reference.","Abolition requires no referendum, because the devolution statutes contain no provision about the Senedd's permanence.","Abolition without a referendum would breach a statutory commitment, but Parliament cannot bind its successors."],"a":4,"e":"Section A1 GoWA 2006 (inserted by the Wales Act 2017) declares the Senedd and the Welsh Government a permanent part of the UK's constitutional arrangements, not to be abolished except on a decision of the people of Wales in a referendum. That is a strong political commitment, but because of parliamentary sovereignty it is doubtful that it legally binds a future Parliament. The Sewel convention is not legally enforceable, and the Supreme Court has no role in approving Acts of Parliament."},
 {"t":"public-order","q":"At noon, workers at a car factory learn that it will close the next day. At 2pm, Gareth, a union representative, leads 300 workers on a march through the town centre to the company's offices to protest. No notice is given to the police. Gareth is later charged with organising a procession without giving notice.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gareth's position?","o":["He is guilty, because written notice must be given at least six clear days before any procession.","He is not guilty, because no notice is required where it is not reasonably practicable to give any advance notice.","He is guilty, because notice must always be given before the procession starts, even if only a short time beforehand.","He is not guilty, because notice is needed only for processions of more than 500 people.","He is not guilty, because a procession organised by a trade union is exempt from the notice requirement."],"a":1,"e":"Section 11 Public Order Act 1986 requires six clear days' written notice of a procession to demonstrate support or opposition, publicise a cause or mark an event, or as soon as reasonably practicable if that is not possible. But no notice is required at all if it is not reasonably practicable to give any advance notice, as with a genuinely spontaneous march. There is no size threshold and no trade union exemption."},
 {"t":"public-order","q":"Ayesha gave proper written notice of a march, including its proposed route. On the day, a police inspector directed the marchers to use a different street because of a burst water main on the notified route. Ayesha complied. She is later charged with an offence because the procession took a different route from that in the notice.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ayesha's position?","o":["She is guilty, because an organiser commits an offence whenever the route differs from the notice.","She is guilty unless she gave fresh written notice of the new route before the march.","She has a defence only if she can show that the burst water main made the original route dangerous.","She has a defence, because the difference arose from a direction given by a police officer.","She is not guilty, because notice requirements apply only to the date and time, not the route."],"a":3,"e":"Each organiser commits an offence if the procession takes place on a different date or time, or by a different route, from that notified. But it is a defence that the difference arose from circumstances beyond the organiser's control or from something done with the agreement, or by the direction, of a police officer. Following the inspector's direction gives Ayesha a defence without the need for fresh notice."},
 {"t":"public-order","q":"A march against a new incinerator is under way. As the marchers approach a busy hospital entrance, the most senior police officer present, a superintendent, reasonably believes that the procession may result in serious disruption to the life of the community. She tells the organisers by loudhailer that the march must take a different route. The organisers argue that conditions must be in writing and imposed by the chief constable.\n\nIs the condition lawful?","o":["No, because conditions on a procession may be imposed only by the chief officer of police, whether in advance or at the scene.","No, because all conditions on processions must be given in writing.","Yes, but only if the Home Secretary consents to the change of route.","No, because conditions may be imposed only on the ground of serious public disorder.","Yes, because at the scene the most senior officer present may impose conditions, which need not be in writing."],"a":4,"e":"Under s.12 Public Order Act 1986, for a procession that is being held, the 'senior police officer' is the most senior officer present at the scene; in advance, it is the chief officer (or an officer the chief officer has authorised). Conditions given in advance must be in writing, but conditions imposed at the scene need not be. Serious disruption to the life of the community is one of the statutory triggers, and the Home Secretary's consent is needed only for a ban."},
 {"t":"public-order","q":"Police impose a condition on an environmental protest requiring it to end by 4pm. The condition is announced repeatedly by loudhailer, displayed on large signs and posted on the police force's social media. Jamal remains after 4pm and is charged with failing to comply with the condition. He says he did not personally hear or read the condition.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the test for Jamal's liability?","o":["He commits an offence only if the prosecution proves he knowingly failed to comply with the condition.","He commits an offence only if the condition was served on him personally in writing.","He commits an offence if he knew or ought to have known of the condition, subject to a statutory defence.","He commits no offence, because only organisers can be liable for breaching conditions.","He commits an offence regardless of his knowledge, because the offence is one of strict liability."],"a":2,"e":"Under ss.12 and 14 Public Order Act 1986, as amended by the Police, Crime, Sentencing and Courts Act 2022, it is an offence to organise or take part in a procession or assembly and fail to comply with a condition that the person knows or ought to know has been imposed. This replaced the old requirement that the person 'knowingly' failed to comply. With the condition widely publicised, Jamal ought to have known of it, subject to the defence of circumstances beyond his control."},
 {"t":"public-order","q":"The Chief Constable of a county police force (outside London) reasonably believes that, because of particular circumstances, conditions will not be sufficient to prevent serious public disorder at a series of planned marches. He issues an order himself banning all public processions in the county for two months.\n\nIs the ban lawful?","o":["Yes, because the chief officer has a reasonable belief that conditions will not be sufficient.","No, because outside London the chief officer must apply to the council, which needs the Home Secretary's consent.","Yes, because a ban lasting less than three months needs no one else's approval.","No, because processions may be banned only to prevent serious disruption to the life of the community, not disorder.","No, because only the Home Secretary can ban processions outside London."],"a":1,"e":"Under s.13 Public Order Act 1986, outside London the chief officer cannot ban processions himself: he must apply to the local council, which may make an order prohibiting processions (or a class of them) for up to three months, with the Home Secretary's consent. In London, the Commissioner makes the order directly with the Home Secretary's consent. The only trigger for a ban is serious public disorder, which is present here."},
 {"t":"public-order","q":"About 30 people intend to gather peacefully on a public road next to an ancient stone circle on private farmland, for a solstice celebration. They will stand on the road verge without obstructing traffic. A trespassory assembly order is in force for the area. Police tell them that their gathering on the road is a prohibited trespassory assembly.\n\nAre the police correct?","o":["Yes, because any assembly of 20 or more people within the area of an order is prohibited.","Yes, because the public has only a limited right of access to the highway, which allows passing and repassing only.","Probably not, because a peaceful, non-obstructive assembly may be a reasonable use of the highway.","Probably not, because trespassory assembly orders apply only to processions.","Yes, because the gathering is near a monument of historical importance."],"a":2,"e":"Trespassory assembly orders under ss.14A-14C Public Order Act 1986 prohibit assemblies of 20 or more people on land to which the public has no or only limited access, held without the occupier's permission. In DPP v Jones, the House of Lords held that a peaceful, non-obstructive assembly on the highway can be a reasonable use of the highway, so it is not trespassory. The order therefore does not catch the group while they stay on the road."},
 {"t":"public-order","q":"About 150 people plan a static rally in a city park. The senior police officer reasonably believes the rally may result in serious disruption to the life of the community and, to prevent this, imposes a condition that the rally must start no earlier than 11am, after the morning rush hour. The organisers argue that conditions on assemblies may relate only to the place, the maximum number of people and the maximum duration.\n\nIs the organisers' argument correct?","o":["No, because since 2022 the senior officer may impose any conditions that appear necessary to prevent the harm.","Yes, because conditions on assemblies are limited to place, numbers and duration.","Yes, because conditions about timing may be imposed only on processions.","No, because assemblies are subject to the same notice requirements as processions.","No, because the police have power to ban an assembly outright and so may impose any lesser restriction they think fit."],"a":0,"e":"Before the Police, Crime, Sentencing and Courts Act 2022, conditions on public assemblies under s.14 Public Order Act 1986 were limited to the place, maximum duration and maximum numbers. Since 2022, the senior officer may impose any conditions that appear necessary to prevent the disorder, damage, disruption, impact or intimidation. Assemblies still need no advance notice and cannot be banned under s.13."},
 {"t":"public-order","q":"Each morning Keith stands alone outside a primary school with a megaphone, protesting about a planning decision. The noise disrupts lessons for several hours. The senior police officer reasonably believes that the noise may result in serious disruption to the school's activities and imposes a condition banning Keith from using the megaphone near the school.\n\nIs the condition lawful?","o":["No, because public order conditions require an assembly of at least two people.","No, because conditions may be imposed only on processions, and on assemblies of 20 or more people, not on individuals.","Yes, because a one-person protest may be banned altogether if it causes any disruption.","No, because a protest about a planning decision is protected absolutely by Article 10 ECHR.","Yes, because conditions may be imposed on a one-person protest whose noise may seriously disrupt a nearby organisation."],"a":4,"e":"Section 14ZA Public Order Act 1986 (added by the Police, Crime, Sentencing and Courts Act 2022) allows conditions on a protest carried on by one person where its noise may cause serious disruption to the activities of an organisation nearby or have a significant impact on people nearby. Article 10 is a qualified right, so proportionate restrictions to protect others' rights are permitted. There is no power to ban a one-person protest."},
 {"t":"public-order","q":"Three members of a religious group preach peacefully on the steps of a town hall. A crowd gathers and some members of the crowd become hostile and start shouting threats at the preachers. A police officer, fearing violence, asks the preachers to stop. When they refuse, he arrests them to prevent a breach of the peace. He takes no action against the hostile members of the crowd.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lawfulness of the arrests?","o":["They are lawful, because the officer reasonably feared a breach of the peace and the preachers refused his request to stop.","They are lawful, because preaching likely to provoke a hostile reaction is itself a breach of the peace.","They are unlawful, because breach of the peace is a criminal offence that requires a warrant for arrest.","They are likely to be unlawful, because the preachers acted lawfully and the threat of violence came from others.","They are lawful, because the police may always act against the speakers to protect public order."],"a":3,"e":"Action to prevent a breach of the peace should normally be taken against the person causing the threat of violence, not those acting lawfully. In Redmond-Bate v DPP, the arrest of peaceful preachers who had attracted a hostile crowd was unlawful, because 'freedom only to speak inoffensively is not worth having'. Action against people acting lawfully is justified only in extreme cases where it is necessary and there is no other way to prevent an imminent breach."},
 {"t":"public-order","q":"During a bitter industrial dispute, police learn that a large group of strikers is driving towards a factory where violent clashes occurred the day before. Officers stop the group's vehicles at a roundabout two miles from the factory, a few minutes' drive away, and tell them to turn back, warning that anyone who continues will be arrested.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lawfulness of the police action?","o":["It is likely to be lawful, because a breach of the peace was reasonably believed to be imminent.","It is unlawful, because police may act only once a breach of the peace has actually begun.","It is unlawful, because breach of the peace powers cannot be used against people travelling in vehicles.","It is lawful, because breach of the peace is a criminal offence and the strikers were about to commit it.","It is unlawful, because the strikers had not yet been asked to disperse under the Public Order Act 1986."],"a":0,"e":"Police (and anyone) may take reasonable steps to prevent a breach of the peace that they reasonably believe is imminent. In Moss v McLachlan, stopping striking miners a few miles (minutes by car) from collieries where violence was feared was lawful because a breach was imminent. This contrasts with Laporte, where coaches stopped far from the protest site were held unlawful. Breach of the peace is not itself a criminal offence."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-procedure","q":"On 2 February, a government agency awarded a large grant to Westfield Arts Trust under a statutory scheme. The Trust immediately signed building contracts and began work in reliance on the grant. Linden Arts, an unsuccessful applicant, believes the agency misapplied the scheme. It files a judicial review claim on 28 April, explaining that it took time to raise funds for the claim.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely response to the timing of the claim?","o":["The court may refuse permission or relief because the claim was not brought promptly, even within three months.","The claim is in time and cannot be refused on grounds of delay, because it was filed within the three-month limit.","The claim is out of time, because it was not filed within six weeks of the decision.","The claim is in time, because time runs only from when Linden Arts had the funds to bring it.","The claim is out of time, because judicial review claims must be filed within 30 days."],"a":0,"e":"Under CPR 54.5(1), a claim must be filed promptly and in any event not later than three months after the grounds first arose. Filing within three months does not guarantee the claim is in time: the court may refuse permission or relief for undue delay where it would cause substantial hardship or prejudice to others, such as a third party who has relied on the decision (s.31(6) Senior Courts Act 1981). Six weeks applies to planning decisions and 30 days to procurement decisions, neither of which applies here."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-procedure","q":"Cedar Homes Ltd's planning challenge has reached the Administrative Court and permission has been granted. Shortly before the hearing, the claimant learns that the council holds an internal email showing that the planning committee was told to refuse the application for a reason not mentioned in its decision letter. The council has not disclosed the email.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the council's obligation?","o":["It need disclose the email only if the claimant obtains a court order for standard disclosure.","It is under a duty of candour to disclose all relevant material and explain the true reasons for its decision.","It need not disclose the email, because internal communications within a council are protected by legal professional privilege.","It need disclose the email only if it intends to rely on it at the hearing.","It need not disclose the email, because judicial review is decided on the decision letter alone."],"a":1,"e":"A public body defending a judicial review claim owes a duty of candour: it must disclose all relevant material and explain the true reasons for its decision, putting its 'cards face upwards on the table'. Formal disclosure orders are rare in judicial review precisely because of this duty. An internal email about the reasons for a decision is not legal advice and so is not covered by legal professional privilege."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-procedure","q":"A county council awarded a major road maintenance contract to a rival bidder after a public procurement process. Highways Plus Ltd, an unsuccessful bidder, knew of the grounds for challenge on the day the award was announced. It plans to start judicial review proceedings seven weeks later.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the time limit that applies?","o":["The claim must be brought promptly and within three months, so it will be in time.","The claim must be brought within six weeks of the date of the award, as for planning decisions, so it will be out of time by one week.","It must be brought within 30 days of when the company knew or ought to have known of the grounds, so it is out of time.","The claim must be brought within one year, so it will be in time.","There is no fixed time limit for procurement challenges, only a requirement of promptness."],"a":2,"e":"For public procurement decisions, a judicial review claim must be brought within 30 days from when the claimant knew or ought to have known of the grounds (CPR 54.5(6A) and the Procurement Act 2023). This special limit is shorter than the general three-month limit and the six-week planning limit. Seven weeks is well outside 30 days."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-procedure","q":"The government decides to fund a large dam project overseas from the UK aid budget, despite official advice that the project is not economically sound. Clearwater, a well-known aid charity with expertise in development funding, seeks judicial review. No individual is directly affected, and it is unlikely that anyone else would bring a challenge.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Clearwater has standing?","o":["It has no standing, because only a person who is directly affected by a decision has a sufficient interest to bring a claim.","It has no standing, because it must show it is a victim under the Human Rights Act 1998.","It has standing only if its members include taxpayers who have contributed to the aid budget.","It has no standing, because decisions about overseas aid are not amenable to judicial review.","It is likely to have standing, given the issue's importance, the lack of any other challenger and its expertise."],"a":4,"e":"The court must not grant permission unless the claimant has a 'sufficient interest' (s.31(3) Senior Courts Act 1981). The modern approach is generous to pressure groups on matters of public importance: in ex p World Development Movement, factors included vindicating the rule of law, the importance of the issue, the absence of any other responsible challenger and the group's expertise. The victim test applies only to claims under the Human Rights Act."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-procedure","q":"Nkechi was employed as a finance officer by an NHS trust under an ordinary contract of employment. She was dismissed after a disciplinary hearing that she believes was unfair. She wants to bring judicial review proceedings against the trust, arguing that it is a public body.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether judicial review is available?","o":["It is unlikely to be available, because her claim concerns her contract of employment, a private law matter.","It is available, because every decision of a public body, including dismissal decisions, is amenable to judicial review.","It is available, because NHS trusts exercise statutory powers.","It is unavailable, because NHS trusts are private companies.","It is available, but only after she has exhausted the employment tribunal process."],"a":0,"e":"Amenability depends not only on whether the body is public but also on whether the decision has a sufficient public law element. In ex p Walsh, a nurse dismissed by an NHS body could not use judicial review because his claim concerned his contract of employment, a private law matter. Nkechi's remedy lies in an employment tribunal claim or a contract claim."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-procedure","q":"Ollie's application for a taxi driver's licence was refused by a council licensing committee. The governing Act gives a right of appeal to the magistrates' court against any refusal, which can consider the merits afresh. Ollie instead wants to apply immediately for judicial review, saying the committee misunderstood the law.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Ollie?","o":["He may choose freely between the appeal and judicial review, because both remedies are available to him.","He must bring judicial review, because only the High Court can decide questions of law.","He should normally use the statutory appeal first, because judicial review is a remedy of last resort.","He must bring both the appeal and judicial review at the same time to protect his position.","He cannot appeal, because licensing decisions can be challenged only by judicial review."],"a":2,"e":"Judicial review is a remedy of last resort. Where an adequate alternative remedy exists, such as a statutory appeal, the claimant should normally use it first; otherwise the court may refuse permission or relief. An appeal that can consider the merits afresh, including any error of law, is an adequate alternative remedy here."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-procedure","q":"Petra is prosecuted in the magistrates' court for breaching a railway byelaw. She argues that the byelaw was made beyond the powers of the parent Act and so is invalid. The prosecution says that she should have challenged the byelaw by judicial review within the time limit, and that she cannot raise its validity now.\n\nMay Petra raise the validity of the byelaw as a defence?","o":["No, because public law issues may be raised only by judicial review in the Administrative Court, not as a defence.","Yes, because a defendant in criminal proceedings may argue that the byelaw she is charged under is unlawful.","No, because the time limit for challenging the byelaw by judicial review has expired.","Yes, but only if she first obtains permission from the High Court.","No, because magistrates' courts have no power to consider the validity of secondary legislation."],"a":1,"e":"The rule that public law challenges should normally be brought by judicial review (O'Reilly v Mackman) is applied flexibly. In Boddington v British Transport Police, the House of Lords held that a defendant may raise the unlawfulness of a byelaw as a defence in criminal proceedings. It would be unjust to convict someone under an invalid law simply because the judicial review time limit had passed."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-procedure","q":"Hugo's application for permission to apply for judicial review was considered on the papers and refused. The judge did not record the claim as totally without merit. Hugo was served with the refusal four days ago and wants to continue.\n\nWhat is Hugo's next step?","o":["He must appeal to the Court of Appeal within 21 days, because a refusal of permission by the High Court on the papers is final.","He may file a fresh claim form on the same grounds within three months of the original decision.","He has no further remedy, because permission refused on the papers is final.","He may ask for the decision to be reconsidered at an oral hearing, filing his request within seven days of service.","He may ask for an oral hearing within 14 days, but only with the defendant's consent."],"a":3,"e":"If permission is refused on the papers, the claimant may request that the decision be reconsidered at an oral hearing, filing the request within seven days of service of the refusal (CPR 54.12). That right is not available if the judge records the claim as totally without merit; the claimant must then seek permission to appeal from the Court of Appeal. The defendant's consent is not needed."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-procedure","q":"Marchmont Residents' Group has issued a judicial review claim challenging a council's grant of planning permission to Oakridge Developments Ltd. The claim form names the council as defendant. Oakridge Developments Ltd is directly affected because it holds the permission.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the requirements for service of the claim form?","o":["It must be served only on the council, which must then notify Oakridge Developments Ltd.","It must be served on the council within 21 days of issue; Oakridge Developments Ltd need not be served.","It must be served on the council and on Oakridge Developments Ltd within 14 days of the court granting permission to proceed.","It need not be served at all, because the court serves judicial review claims on the defendant.","It must be served on the council and on Oakridge Developments Ltd as an interested party within seven days of issue."],"a":4,"e":"The claim form must be served on the defendant and on any interested party (a person directly affected by the claim, such as the developer holding the challenged permission) within seven days of the date of issue (CPR 54.7). Those served who wish to take part must then file an acknowledgment of service within 21 days of service (CPR 54.8)."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-procedure","q":"On 1 February, Kingsley Borough Council decided to close a day centre for older people. Gloria, who uses the centre, did not find out about the decision until 1 April. She files a judicial review claim on 20 May. The decision is not a planning or procurement decision. The council agreed in correspondence that it would not take any point on time.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the timing of Gloria's claim?","o":["The claim is in time, because time ran from 1 April, when Gloria found out about the decision.","The claim is in time, because the council has agreed not to take any point on time.","The claim is out of time, and the court has no power to extend the time limit.","Time ran from 1 February, so the claim is late unless the court extends time; the council's agreement cannot extend it.","The claim is in time, because the time limit is six months for decisions that affect vulnerable people such as older users."],"a":3,"e":"Time runs from when the grounds first arose, normally the date of the decision, not when the claimant found out about it (CPR 54.5(1)). From 1 February, three months expired on 1 May, so the claim is late. The parties cannot extend time by agreement (CPR 54.5(2)); only the court can extend time, for good reason."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-grounds","q":"A council has a statutory power to revoke street trading licences 'where the licence holder has breached the licence conditions'. A group of traders publicly criticised the council leader. The council then revokes their licences, although none of them has breached any condition, and an internal memo shows the aim was to silence criticism of the leader.\n\nOn which ground is the decision most likely to be challenged?","o":["Irrationality, because the decision was unpopular with local residents.","Procedural impropriety, because the traders were not given reasons in writing.","Legitimate expectation, because the traders expected their licences to continue.","Illegality, because the council unlawfully delegated its power.","Illegality, because the power was used for an improper purpose."],"a":4,"e":"A power given for one purpose must not be used for another. Using a licence revocation power to silence critics, rather than to deal with breaches of licence conditions, is an improper purpose and so a form of illegality (compare Padfield and Porter v Magill). Nothing suggests delegation, and unpopularity alone does not make a decision irrational."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-grounds","q":"A council has a statutory power to manage its land 'for the benefit of the area'. Councillors vote to ban the use of its parkland for a long-established rural sport because a majority find the sport morally offensive. They do not consider whether a ban would benefit the area, and the minutes record moral objection as the only reason.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lawfulness of the ban?","o":["It is likely to be unlawful, because the council relied on an irrelevant consideration and ignored the power's purpose.","It is lawful, because councillors are elected and so are entitled to act on their own moral views when managing land.","It is lawful, because the court cannot interfere with how a council manages its own land.","It is unlawful only if no reasonable council could have considered the sport offensive.","It is lawful, because the council has an unlimited discretion to manage its land."],"a":0,"e":"A decision-maker must take into account considerations the statute requires and must not rely on irrelevant ones. In R v Somerset CC ex p Fewings, a ban on stag hunting on council land based on councillors' moral repugnance was quashed because they had not asked whether the ban would benefit the area, which was the purpose of the statutory power. The issue is illegality, not the higher threshold of irrationality."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-grounds","q":"A council licensing committee refuses Callum's application for a late-night alcohol licence, giving detailed reasons based on police evidence of disorder in the area. Callum has strong evidence that the area is safer than the police suggest, and he believes most reasonable committees would have granted the licence. There is no procedural defect and no error of law.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Callum's prospects in a judicial review on the ground of irrationality?","o":["Strong, because the court will grant the licence if it finds the committee's decision was wrong.","Weak, because the decision must be so unreasonable that no reasonable authority could have reached it.","Strong, because irrationality is made out whenever the decision-maker gave too much weight to one piece of evidence.","Weak, because licensing decisions are not amenable to judicial review.","Strong, because the court will apply a proportionality test to all licensing decisions."],"a":1,"e":"Wednesbury unreasonableness requires a decision so unreasonable that no reasonable authority could ever have come to it, a high threshold. Judicial review is supervisory, so the court does not decide whether the decision was right on the merits or reweigh the evidence. A reasoned decision based on police evidence is very unlikely to meet the test; proportionality applies in human rights cases, not as a general ground."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-grounds","q":"A council planning committee grants planning permission for a large retail park to Greendale Developments plc. It later emerges that Councillor Hughes, who chaired the meeting and voted in favour, owns a substantial shareholding in Greendale Developments plc. There is no evidence that his vote was influenced by this.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The decision stands unless the claimant proves that Councillor Hughes was actually biased.","The decision stands, because one councillor's vote cannot invalidate a committee decision.","The decision is liable to be quashed only if a fair-minded observer would think Councillor Hughes's vote changed the outcome.","The decision is liable to be quashed, because a direct financial interest automatically disqualifies the decision-maker.","The decision stands, because councillors are elected and expected to have local interests."],"a":3,"e":"Under the rule against bias, a decision-maker with a direct financial or proprietary interest in the outcome is automatically disqualified, with no need to show actual bias or even a real possibility of bias (Dimes v Grand Junction Canal, where the Lord Chancellor owned shares in a party). The 'fair-minded and informed observer' test applies to apparent bias where there is no such interest. Whether the vote changed the outcome is not the test."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-grounds","q":"A statutory regulator revokes Daniel's licence to operate a care agency after receiving complaints from two former clients. Daniel was not told about the complaints or what was alleged, and was given no opportunity to respond before the revocation. The statute says nothing about the procedure to be followed.\n\nOn which ground is Daniel most likely to challenge the revocation?","o":["Irrationality, because no reasonable regulator could rely on complaints made by former clients without checking them.","Illegality, because the regulator had no power to revoke licences.","Legitimate expectation, because he expected his licence to be renewed.","No ground is available, because the statute does not require any hearing.","Procedural impropriety, because fairness required him to be told the case and given a chance to respond."],"a":4,"e":"Even where the statute is silent, the common law requires fairness, especially where someone stands to lose an existing right or livelihood. In Ridge v Baldwin, a chief constable dismissed without being told the charges or given a chance to respond had his dismissal declared void. Fairness usually requires notice of the case to be met and an opportunity to respond; people losing an existing licence get more protection than first-time applicants."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-grounds","q":"A council is under a statutory duty to consult before changing its library service. It publishes a consultation document setting out a single proposal to close five libraries, without mentioning that it had considered other options, such as reduced opening hours. The council's cabinet had already signed contracts to sell two of the buildings before the consultation began.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the consultation?","o":["It is lawful, because the council published a consultation document and invited responses, as the statute requires.","It is lawful, because a council need consult only on its preferred option.","It is likely to be unlawful, because consultation must be at a formative stage and give enough information.","It is unlawful only if the council failed to allow at least twelve weeks for responses.","It is lawful, because the duty to consult does not require the council to change its mind."],"a":2,"e":"Under the Gunning principles, approved in R (Moseley) v Haringey LBC, consultation must take place while proposals are at a formative stage, give sufficient reasons and information for intelligent consideration and response, allow adequate time, and be conscientiously taken into account. Signing contracts before consulting shows the decision was not formative, and in Moseley, presenting only one option without mentioning alternatives was unfair. There is no fixed twelve-week rule."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-grounds","q":"For fifteen years, a port authority has always consulted the Harbour Fishermen's Association before changing mooring charges, although no statute or written promise requires it. This year it doubles the charges without any consultation. The Association seeks judicial review.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Association's strongest argument?","o":["The authority breached a statutory duty to consult the Association.","The authority's settled practice of consulting created a procedural legitimate expectation of consultation.","The doubling of charges was irrational, because any large increase is unreasonable.","The Association has a substantive legitimate expectation that the mooring charges will never be increased without its agreement.","The authority fettered its discretion by following a policy on mooring charges."],"a":1,"e":"A legitimate expectation may arise from a clear promise or from a settled, regular practice that the claimant can reasonably expect to continue, as with the practice of consulting unions in the GCHQ case. Here the expectation is procedural (to be consulted), not of a particular outcome such as frozen charges. There is no statutory duty, and a large increase is not in itself irrational."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-grounds","q":"Beacon Holdings Ltd asked HM Revenue and Customs for guidance on the tax treatment of a proposed transaction. In its request it did not mention a key feature that made the transaction unusual. HMRC replied that the transaction would be taxed favourably. HMRC later discovers the full facts and taxes the transaction in the ordinary way. Beacon Holdings Ltd claims a legitimate expectation.\n\nIs the claim likely to succeed?","o":["Yes, because HMRC made a clear and unambiguous statement in writing, which Beacon Holdings Ltd then relied on.","Yes, because a public body can never depart from guidance it has given in writing.","No, because legitimate expectation cannot arise in tax matters.","No, because a claimant who has not fully disclosed the relevant facts cannot rely on the guidance it received.","Yes, provided Beacon Holdings Ltd can show it would suffer financial loss."],"a":3,"e":"A legitimate expectation generally requires a clear, unambiguous representation devoid of relevant qualification, given to a claimant who has disclosed all relevant facts. In R v IRC ex p MFK Underwriting Agents, claimants who had not fully disclosed the nature of their transactions could not rely on Revenue guidance. Tax matters are not excluded from the doctrine; the problem here is the non-disclosure."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-grounds","q":"Zainab's application for a market stall licence was refused by a council. On judicial review, the court finds that the council failed to consider a relevant matter. Zainab asks the court to grant her the licence and to award her damages for the profits she has lost. She has no claim in tort or under the Human Rights Act.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the relief she is most likely to obtain?","o":["A quashing order, after which the council must decide again lawfully, with no award of damages.","An order granting her the licence, because the court has found the refusal unlawful.","A quashing order and damages for all her lost profits, because the council's decision was unlawful.","A declaration that she is entitled to a licence, together with damages.","No remedy at all, because a council's licensing decisions are final."],"a":0,"e":"Judicial review is supervisory, so the usual remedy is a quashing order and the body decides again lawfully, possibly reaching the same result; the court does not grant the licence itself. Damages may be claimed in judicial review only where the claimant would have a claim in private law (such as tort) or under the HRA (s.31(4) Senior Courts Act 1981). There is no general right to damages for unlawful administrative action."},
 {"t":"judicial-review-grounds","q":"A (fictional) statute establishes a Compensation Board and provides that 'no determination of the Board shall be called in question in any court of law'. The Board rejects Ilse's claim after misinterpreting the statutory test for eligibility, an error of law. Ilse seeks judicial review. The Board argues that the clause prevents any challenge.\n\nHow is the court most likely to treat the clause?","o":["As protecting the decision, because the courts must always give effect to the clear words of an Act of Parliament.","As protecting the decision unless Ilse brought her claim within six weeks.","As not protecting the decision, because a decision based on an error of law is not a true determination.","As invalid, because Parliament has no power to restrict judicial review.","As protecting the decision, unless Ilse shows the Board acted in bad faith."],"a":2,"e":"Total ouster clauses are read very narrowly because access to the courts is fundamental to the rule of law. In Anisminic v Foreign Compensation Commission, the House of Lords held that a decision based on an error of law was only a 'purported' determination, a nullity, so the clause 'shall not be called in question' did not protect it. The court reads the clause narrowly rather than declaring it invalid; time-limited (six-week) ouster clauses are a different type and are upheld."},
 {"t":"human-rights-act","q":"A police force introduces a policy of keeping DNA samples taken from children who are arrested but never charged. Children First, a national children's charity, believes the policy breaches Article 8 ECHR. None of the charity's staff or members has had a sample taken. It wants to bring a claim against the police force under the Human Rights Act 1998.\n\nCan Children First bring the claim under the Human Rights Act 1998?","o":["Yes, because it has a sufficient interest in the matter as a national children's charity.","Yes, because the Act allows representative bodies such as charities to bring claims on behalf of the people affected.","No, because a police force is not a public authority for the purposes of the Act.","Yes, provided it brings the claim within one year of the policy being introduced.","No, because only a person who is, or would be, a victim of the unlawful act may bring proceedings under the Act."],"a":4,"e":"Under s.7(1) HRA, a person may bring proceedings, or rely on a Convention right, only if they are (or would be) a victim of the unlawful act, meaning directly affected. A pressure group is not a victim merely because it cares about the issue. The wider 'sufficient interest' test applies to ordinary judicial review, which the charity could still bring on non-HRA grounds. The police are a core public authority."},
 {"t":"human-rights-act","q":"Fourteen months ago, a council officer entered Dilys's home without lawful authority and searched it, in breach of her Article 8 rights. Dilys delayed bringing a claim because she was seriously ill in hospital for most of that time. She now wants to bring a claim against the council in the County Court under the Human Rights Act 1998.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the time limit for her claim?","o":["It must normally be brought within one year, but the court may allow longer if that is equitable in all the circumstances.","The claim is out of time and cannot proceed, because the one-year limit is absolute.","The claim must be brought promptly and within three months, so it is out of time.","The claim is in time, because the limit for Human Rights Act claims is six years.","The claim is in time, because time runs only from when Dilys recovered from her illness and was able to give instructions."],"a":0,"e":"Under s.7(5) HRA, proceedings must be brought within one year beginning with the date of the act complained of, or such longer period as the court considers equitable having regard to all the circumstances, subject to any stricter limit for the procedure used. A serious illness may make it equitable to extend time. The three-month limit applies only where the claim is brought by judicial review, which this ordinary County Court claim is not."},
 {"t":"human-rights-act","q":"In a possession claim in the County Court, a district judge concludes that a section of a 1988 Act cannot be read compatibly with the tenant's Article 8 rights, despite trying every possible interpretation. The tenant asks the district judge to make a declaration of incompatibility.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the district judge's position?","o":["The district judge may make a declaration of incompatibility, and must then refuse to apply the incompatible section of the Act.","The district judge cannot make a declaration of incompatibility, as only the higher courts can, and must apply the section.","The district judge may disapply the section, because it is incompatible with a Convention right.","The district judge must adjourn the case and refer the question to the European Court of Human Rights.","The district judge may make a declaration of incompatibility, which binds the landlord."],"a":1,"e":"Under s.4(5) HRA, only the higher courts (the Supreme Court, Privy Council, Court of Appeal, High Court and certain others) can make a declaration of incompatibility; the County Court, magistrates and most tribunals cannot. Where primary legislation cannot be read compatibly, a court must still apply it, because s.3 does not affect its validity. Even a declaration would not bind the parties or affect the section's operation."},
 {"t":"human-rights-act","q":"In a case about Article 6 ECHR, counsel for the claimant shows the Supreme Court a recent decision of a Chamber of the European Court of Human Rights which supports the claimant. The Supreme Court considers that the Strasbourg decision misunderstood important safeguards in English criminal procedure. There is no clear and constant line of Strasbourg authority on the point.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Supreme Court's obligation?","o":["It must follow the Strasbourg decision, because UK courts are bound by the European Court of Human Rights.","It must follow the Strasbourg decision unless Parliament has expressly legislated to the contrary.","It may ignore the Strasbourg decision entirely, because Strasbourg case law has no status in UK courts.","It must take the Strasbourg decision into account but is not bound by it, and may decline to follow it.","It must refer the question back to the Grand Chamber of the European Court of Human Rights."],"a":3,"e":"Under s.2 HRA, courts must 'take into account' Strasbourg case law, which means they are not bound by it. They normally follow a clear and constant line of Strasbourg authority, but may decline to follow a decision that misunderstands an aspect of UK law, as the Supreme Court did in R v Horncastle on hearsay evidence. UK courts cannot refer questions to Strasbourg."},
 {"t":"human-rights-act","q":"Intelligence officers question Farid, a terrorist suspect, for days while depriving him of sleep and subjecting him to treatment that reaches the minimum level of severity for inhuman and degrading treatment. The government argues that the treatment was a proportionate response to an imminent threat to national security.\n\nHow will the court approach the government's argument?","o":["It will accept it if the treatment was necessary in a democratic society to protect national security from an imminent threat.","It will reject it, because Article 3 is absolute and ill-treatment reaching the minimum level of severity cannot be justified.","It will apply the four-stage proportionality test to decide whether the treatment was justified.","It will accept it if the government has made a valid derogation from Article 3.","It will accept it, because the court must defer to the government on national security."],"a":1,"e":"Article 3 (prohibition of torture and inhuman or degrading treatment) is absolute: there are no exceptions, no justification and no derogation is possible, even in an emergency (Article 15). So there is no proportionality question; any treatment reaching the minimum level of severity is a breach whatever the reason. The 'necessary in a democratic society' test applies to qualified rights such as Articles 8-11."},
 {"t":"human-rights-act","q":"After complaints about loud music from one busker outside a block of flats, a city council makes a byelaw banning all street performance anywhere in the city centre at all times. Tom, a street magician who performs silently, is fined under the byelaw. He argues that it breaches his Article 10 right to freedom of expression.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["The ban is lawful, because it is prescribed by law and pursues a legitimate aim.","The ban is lawful, because street performance, especially silent magic, is not a form of expression protected by Article 10.","The ban is unlawful, because Article 10 is an absolute right.","The ban is probably disproportionate, because less intrusive measures, such as noise limits, could achieve the aim.","The ban is lawful, because councils are not public authorities under the Human Rights Act 1998."],"a":3,"e":"Article 10 is a qualified right: an interference is justified only if prescribed by law, for a legitimate aim and proportionate. Under the Bank Mellat questions, a blanket ban across the whole city centre is unlikely to pass the 'less intrusive measure' stage, because targeted measures such as noise limits would deal with the problem. Having a legal basis and a legitimate aim is not enough on its own."},
 {"t":"human-rights-act","q":"A council's policy on access to its family support service treats unmarried fathers less favourably than unmarried mothers. Kwame, an unmarried father, accepts that the denial of support does not, on its own, breach his Article 8 right to respect for family life, but the policy falls within the ambit of family life. He argues the policy breaches Article 14.\n\nCan Kwame rely on Article 14?","o":["No, because Article 14 can be relied on only if another Convention right has been breached.","No, because Article 14 is not one of the Convention rights that the Human Rights Act 1998 gives effect to in domestic law.","Yes, because Article 14 applies where the facts fall within the ambit of another Convention right, even if not breached.","Yes, because Article 14 is a free-standing right against all discrimination by public authorities.","No, because discrimination on grounds of sex is dealt with only by the Equality Act 2010."],"a":2,"e":"Article 14 (prohibition of discrimination) is not free-standing: it applies only to the enjoyment of another Convention right. But the other right need not itself be breached; it is enough that the facts fall within its ambit. Because the policy falls within the ambit of Article 8, Kwame can rely on Article 14, which is one of the Convention rights scheduled to the HRA."},
 {"t":"human-rights-act","q":"Riverside Borough Council dismisses Anwen, a council employee, after reading her private messages on a personal phone without her consent. The council argues that employment is a private matter and that it is bound by the Human Rights Act 1998 only when exercising public functions.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether section 6 of the Human Rights Act 1998 applies?","o":["It does not apply, because dismissing an employee is an act of a private nature.","It applies only if the council's employment decisions are made under statutory powers.","It does not apply, because employment disputes with a council must be brought in the employment tribunal, not the courts.","It applies only if the council receives public funding for the post Anwen held.","It applies, because a council is a core public authority bound in everything it does, including employment."],"a":4,"e":"A local council is a core public authority under s.6 HRA, bound to act compatibly with Convention rights in everything it does, including 'private' acts such as employment and contracts. The rule that only public functions are covered, and not acts of a private nature (s.6(5)), applies to hybrid authorities, such as private companies running prisons or some housing associations. The forum for the claim does not affect whether s.6 applies."},
 {"t":"human-rights-act","q":"Evan, a prisoner, succeeds in a claim that a prison disciplinary hearing breached his Article 6 right to a fair hearing. The court quashes the disciplinary finding and declares that his rights were breached. Evan cannot show that a fair hearing would have produced a different outcome. He also claims substantial damages, arguing that damages should match those awarded for comparable torts.\n\nWhich of the following best describes his claim for damages?","o":["Damages may be awarded only if necessary to afford just satisfaction, and the finding of a breach may be enough.","Damages must be awarded whenever a Convention right is breached, at the level awarded in tort.","Damages must be awarded at the level awarded in tort, because the claim is brought in a domestic court in England.","Damages cannot be awarded under the Human Rights Act 1998 in any circumstances.","Damages must be awarded because the court has already granted other relief."],"a":0,"e":"Under s.8 HRA, damages may be awarded only if, taking account of all the circumstances including any other remedy granted, an award is necessary to afford just satisfaction, and the court must follow Strasbourg principles (s.8(3)-(4)). Awards are usually modest and follow Strasbourg scales, not tort levels. In R (Greenfield), a prisoner denied a fair disciplinary hearing received no damages because the finding of a violation was enough."},
 {"t":"human-rights-act","q":"A council evicts Marta from temporary housing. A court later finds that the eviction interfered with her Article 8 rights. However, the eviction was required by a section of an Act of Parliament that left the council no choice, and the section cannot be read compatibly with Article 8.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the council's position?","o":["The council has acted unlawfully and must pay damages, because councils are core public authorities bound in all they do.","The council has acted unlawfully, because s.3 requires the court to read the section compatibly.","The council has not acted unlawfully, because it could not have acted differently as a result of primary legislation.","The council has not acted unlawfully, because Article 8 does not apply to temporary housing.","The council has acted unlawfully, but only if the section was passed after the Human Rights Act 1998."],"a":2,"e":"Under s.6(2) HRA, an act of a public authority is not unlawful if, as a result of primary legislation, the authority could not have acted differently, or it was giving effect to primary legislation that cannot be read compatibly. s.3 applies only 'so far as it is possible', and here it is not. The remedy, if any, would be a declaration of incompatibility in the High Court or above, which applies whenever the Act was passed."},
 {"t":"eu-law-brexit","q":"In 2026, Tarrant Logistics Ltd is in dispute about a provision of an EU regulation that became part of UK law at the end of 2020 and is now assimilated direct legislation. The provision conflicts with a section of a 2015 Act of Parliament. Tarrant Logistics Ltd argues that the assimilated provision prevails because it derives from EU law.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how a court will resolve the conflict?","o":["The assimilated provision prevails, because EU-derived law still takes priority over Acts of Parliament passed before the end of 2020.","The assimilated provision prevails, because the later law always impliedly repeals the earlier one.","The 2015 Act prevails, because assimilated direct legislation is now read subject to domestic enactments, whenever made.","The court must disapply the 2015 Act, applying the principle of supremacy of EU law.","The court must refer the conflict to the Court of Justice of the European Union."],"a":2,"e":"From 1 January 2024, the Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023 abolished the principle of supremacy of EU law and reversed the old priority rule. Assimilated direct legislation (converted EU regulations) must now be read and given effect subject to domestic enactments, whenever made, so the 2015 Act prevails. Before 2024, a pre-2021 Act could be disapplied in favour of conflicting retained EU law; that is no longer the case, and references to the CJEU are no longer possible."},
 {"t":"eu-law-brexit","q":"In 2026, Leah brings an equal pay claim against her employer. Instead of relying on the Equality Act 2010, her representative wants to rely directly on Article 157 of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union, arguing that it gives her a directly effective right that was saved after Brexit.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Leah can rely on Article 157?","o":["She can, because directly effective Treaty rights were saved as retained EU law and remain in force.","She cannot, because saved directly effective EU rights ceased to be recognised in UK law from 1 January 2024.","She can, because equal pay rights are protected by the Withdrawal Agreement.","She can, but only if the Court of Appeal agrees to depart from the Equality Act 2010.","She cannot, because Article 157 never had direct effect in the UK."],"a":1,"e":"Section 4 EUWA 2018 saved directly effective EU rights, such as equal pay under Article 157 TFEU, as retained EU law at the end of 2020. But the REUL Act 2023 provided that s.4 rights ceased to be recognised from 1 January 2024, unless restated in domestic law. Leah must rely on domestic law, such as the Equality Act 2010. Article 157 did have direct effect while the UK was a member."},
 {"t":"eu-law-brexit","q":"In 2026, the High Court is interpreting assimilated regulations that originally implemented an EU directive. Counsel relies on a 2023 decision of the Court of Justice of the European Union (CJEU) interpreting the same directive, which supports her client. The opposing side argues that the 2023 decision is irrelevant.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of the 2023 CJEU decision in the High Court?","o":["It binds the High Court, because the regulations implement an EU directive.","It binds the High Court, unless the Court of Appeal has departed from it.","It is not binding, but the High Court may have regard to it so far as it is relevant.","The High Court must ignore it, because UK courts may not consider any CJEU decisions made after Brexit.","It binds the High Court only if the regulations have not been amended since 2020."],"a":2,"e":"Under s.6 EUWA 2018, UK courts are not bound by CJEU decisions made after IP completion day (31 December 2020), but may have regard to them if relevant. It is pre-2021 CJEU decisions (assimilated EU case law) that bind the High Court and lower courts. Nothing prevents a court considering a post-2020 decision as persuasive material."},
 {"t":"eu-law-brexit","q":"In 2026, the Court of Appeal is hearing a commercial case that turns on the meaning of a provision of assimilated law derived from an EU regulation on product safety. The meaning is genuinely unclear, and the case does not concern citizens' rights or Northern Ireland. One party asks the court to refer the question to the Court of Justice of the European Union (CJEU) for a preliminary ruling.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the Court of Appeal's position?","o":["It cannot refer the question, because UK courts can no longer refer questions to the CJEU in a case like this.","It must refer the question, because the meaning of EU-derived law is unclear.","It may refer the question, because the Court of Appeal is the final court of appeal on this particular point of law.","It may refer the question only with the permission of the Supreme Court.","It may refer the question if both parties agree."],"a":0,"e":"Under s.6(1) EUWA 2018, UK courts cannot refer questions to the CJEU after IP completion day. The only limited routes that remain arise under the Withdrawal Agreement, such as citizens' rights references (until the end of 2028) and certain Northern Ireland matters, neither of which applies here. The court must decide the meaning itself, applying domestic principles of interpretation."},
 {"t":"eu-law-brexit","q":"In October 2026, a government minister wants to use the power in section 14 of the Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023 to revoke a piece of assimilated law and replace it with a new regulatory scheme. No other statutory power covers the change.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the minister's position?","o":["The minister can use the power, because ministers have a permanent power to revoke assimilated law by regulations.","The minister can use the power, but only with the consent of the devolved governments.","The minister cannot revoke assimilated law at all, because it is protected by the Withdrawal Agreement.","The minister can use the power, provided the regulations are made by the negative procedure.","The minister cannot use the power, because the section 14 power could not be used after 23 June 2026."],"a":4,"e":"The REUL Act 2023 gave ministers wide powers to restate (s.12) and to revoke or replace (s.14) assimilated law, but those powers could not be used after 23 June 2026. Further changes now need primary legislation or other existing powers (the update power in s.13 has no sunset). Assimilated law is ordinary domestic law and is not protected by the Withdrawal Agreement."},
 {"t":"eu-law-brexit","q":"In 2026, a county court case turns on the interpretation of assimilated regulations that implemented an EU directive. The Court of Justice of the European Union (CJEU) interpreted the directive in a judgment delivered on 10 November 2020. The claimant argues that, because the UK left the EU on 31 January 2020, the judgment is not binding.\n\nIs the CJEU judgment binding on the county court?","o":["Yes, because CJEU decisions made before 31 December 2020 bind the lower courts as assimilated case law.","No, because CJEU decisions made after exit day on 31 January 2020 are not binding on any court in the UK.","No, because no CJEU decisions are binding in UK courts after Brexit.","Yes, but only if the Supreme Court has approved it since 2020.","No, because county court judges may choose whether to follow CJEU decisions."],"a":0,"e":"Assimilated EU case law consists of CJEU decisions made up to IP completion day, 31 December 2020, the end of the transition period, not exit day. A judgment delivered on 10 November 2020 is therefore assimilated case law, which binds courts below the Court of Appeal. Only the Court of Appeal and Supreme Court (and equivalents) may depart from it."},
 {"t":"eu-law-brexit","q":"A law student writes that, in a well-known 1990 case, UK courts disapplied part of the Merchant Shipping Act 1988 because it conflicted with EU law, and that this proved Parliament was no longer sovereign while the UK was in the EU.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the Supreme Court has explained the courts' power to disapply Acts during EU membership?","o":["It showed that EU membership permanently transferred sovereignty to the EU institutions.","It was based on a constitutional convention that Parliament would not legislate contrary to EU law while the UK was a member.","It depended on the royal prerogative to make treaties, which overrode Acts of Parliament.","It flowed from Parliament's own choice in the European Communities Act 1972, so sovereignty was preserved in principle.","It meant that Acts passed while the UK was in the EU remain subject to EU law today."],"a":3,"e":"Before Brexit, s.2(4) of the European Communities Act 1972 required courts to disapply Acts conflicting with directly effective EU law, as in Factortame. In Miller 1 the Supreme Court explained this as Parliament's own choice in the 1972 Act, so sovereignty was preserved in principle: Parliament could, and did, repeal the 1972 Act. EU supremacy was abolished from 1 January 2024, and treaties do not override Acts."},
 {"t":"eu-law-brexit","q":"Murray Engineering Ltd manufactures machinery in Northern Ireland for sale in both Northern Ireland and Ireland. Its director assumes that, since Brexit, no EU rules apply to the company's goods because EU law has ceased to apply anywhere in the UK.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The director is right, because EU law ceased to apply throughout the UK at the end of 2020.","The director is wrong, because under the Windsor Framework certain EU rules on goods continue to apply in Northern Ireland.","The director is right, because all EU-derived law in the UK is now assimilated law.","The director is wrong, because EU law continues to apply throughout the UK until the end of 2028 under the Withdrawal Agreement.","The director is wrong, but only for goods sold to customers in Ireland."],"a":1,"e":"The Northern Ireland Protocol, amended in 2023 by the Windsor Framework, keeps Northern Ireland aligned with certain EU rules on goods to avoid a hard border on the island of Ireland. Those EU rules continue to apply in Northern Ireland, given effect through s.7A EUWA 2018, and the CJEU retains a role. The 2028 date relates to citizens' rights references, not the general application of EU law."},
 {"t":"eu-law-brexit","q":"Jakub, a Polish citizen, has lived in the UK since 2018 and holds settled status. In 2026, a tribunal hearing his appeal is unsure how a provision of the citizens' rights part of the Withdrawal Agreement should be interpreted. The point has not been decided before.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the tribunal's options?","o":["It cannot refer the question, because UK courts and tribunals can no longer make any references at all to the CJEU after Brexit.","It must refer the question to the Supreme Court, which alone may refer it to the CJEU.","It must refer the question to the Independent Monitoring Authority, which decides it.","It cannot refer the question, because the Withdrawal Agreement has no effect in domestic law.","It may refer the question to the CJEU, because references on citizens' rights remain possible until the end of 2028."],"a":4,"e":"Under the Withdrawal Agreement, UK courts and tribunals can still refer questions on the citizens' rights part to the CJEU for eight years after the end of the transition period, that is, until the end of 2028. This is an exception to the general rule that references are no longer possible. The Withdrawal Agreement has effect in domestic law through s.7A EUWA 2018, and the Independent Monitoring Authority oversees citizens' rights but does not decide cases."},
 {"t":"eu-law-brexit","q":"Harlow Chemicals Ltd is subject to (fictional) UK regulations made in 2005 to implement an EU directive on chemical storage. The regulations were not on the list of instruments revoked by the Retained EU Law (Revocation and Reform) Act 2023 and have not been amended. The company's director believes all EU-derived law automatically ceased to have effect at the end of 2023.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of the regulations in 2026?","o":["They ceased to have effect at the end of 2023, because all retained EU law was automatically revoked on that date.","They remain in force, but now have priority over any inconsistent Act of Parliament.","They ceased to have effect on IP completion day, when EU law stopped applying in the UK.","They remain in force as assimilated law, because only the listed instruments were revoked at the end of 2023.","They remain in force, but only until the Court of Appeal departs from them."],"a":3,"e":"The Bill that became the REUL Act 2023 originally proposed revoking all retained EU law automatically at the end of 2023 (a 'sunset'), but Parliament replaced this with a list of about 600 instruments revoked in Schedule 1. Other EU-derived law, such as these regulations, remains in force and since 1 January 2024 is called assimilated law. It is ordinary domestic law and does not take priority over Acts of Parliament."}
];

;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["wills"] = [
 {"t":"validity","q":"Aisha made a will at the age of 17, leaving her savings to her best friend. She was a student and was not a member of the armed forces. She died last month, aged 19, without having made any other will. Her parents are alive.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Aisha's estate will pass?","o":["Under the intestacy rules to her parents, because the will made at 17 is invalid.","Under the will to her friend, because she confirmed it by not revoking it after turning 18.","Under the will to her friend, because a will made at 17 is valid if properly witnessed.","Under the will to her friend, because she had capacity when she made it.","Under the intestacy rules, but only if her parents apply to set the will aside within six months."],"a":0,"e":"A person must be at least **18** to make a valid will (Wills Act 1837 s.7), unless they are a **privileged testator** (for example a member of the armed forces on actual military service). Reaching 18 does not validate an earlier will; she would have needed to make a **new will** (or re-execute it). The will is void, so the estate passes on **intestacy**: with no spouse or issue, her **parents** take."},
 {"t":"validity","q":"Bilal has paranoid schizophrenia and firmly but wrongly believes that his neighbours are spying on him through his television. When he made his will, he understood that he was making a will, knew the extent of his property and considered the claims of his wife and children, to whom he left everything. His estranged brother challenges the will.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the will?","o":["It is invalid, because any insane delusion at the time of the will deprives the testator of capacity.","It is valid only if a doctor certified his capacity when he signed it.","It is likely to be valid, because the delusion did not affect the dispositions in the will.","It is invalid unless Bilal's capacity is proved under the Mental Capacity Act 2005.","It is invalid, because a person with a diagnosed mental illness lacks testamentary capacity."],"a":2,"e":"Under the **Banks v Goodfellow** test, the testator must understand the nature of making a will, the extent of his property, and the claims he ought to consider, and no **disorder of the mind** must **influence** his dispositions. A delusion makes a will invalid only if it **affects the gifts** made. Bilal's delusion about his neighbours had nothing to do with leaving his estate to his family, so he had capacity. A medical certificate is good practice, not a legal requirement."},
 {"t":"validity","q":"Carmen, aged 82, has vascular dementia with fluctuating symptoms. On some days she is confused; on others she is lucid. Her solicitor visited her on a good day, took instructions and arranged for the will to be signed and witnessed the same afternoon. The solicitor's detailed file note records that Carmen discussed her property and family sensibly.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the will?","o":["It is valid only if confirmed by the Court of Protection.","It is valid only if a medical practitioner witnessed it.","It is invalid, because a person with a dementia diagnosis is presumed to lack capacity.","It is invalid, because a person whose capacity fluctuates can never make a valid will.","It is likely to be valid, because Carmen had capacity in a lucid interval when she signed."],"a":4,"e":"Testamentary capacity is judged at the **time of execution** (or at instructions, if the testator still understands that he is signing a will drawn up on those instructions). A person with fluctuating capacity can make a valid will in a **lucid interval**. Where there is real doubt, the propounder must prove capacity, and the solicitor's careful file note will be strong evidence. A medical witness (the 'golden rule') is good practice but not a requirement."},
 {"t":"validity","q":"Dafydd, aged 90, has advanced Alzheimer's disease and can no longer understand his property or his family relationships. His 1990 will leaves everything to his late wife, so it will fail and his estate will pass on intestacy. His attorney under a property and financial affairs lasting power of attorney wants him to have a new will benefiting his grandchildren.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how this can be achieved?","o":["Nothing can be done, because a person who lacks capacity cannot have a will made for them.","An application to the Court of Protection for a statutory will to be made on Dafydd's behalf.","The attorney can sign a new will on Dafydd's behalf under the lasting power of attorney.","Dafydd can sign a new will if a doctor witnesses it, applying the golden rule.","His family can agree a deed of variation now, before Dafydd dies."],"a":1,"e":"An **attorney** under a lasting power of attorney **cannot make a will** for the donor. Where a person **lacks testamentary capacity**, the **Court of Protection** can authorise a **statutory will** on their behalf under the Mental Capacity Act 2005, made in the person's best interests. The golden rule cannot cure a lack of capacity, and a deed of variation can only be made after death."},
 {"t":"validity","q":"Elinor, aged 94 and recovering from a stroke, asks her solicitor to prepare a new will cutting out one of her three children. The solicitor believes she probably has capacity but knows that her family may challenge the will.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the solicitor should do?","o":["Refuse to act, because a solicitor must not prepare a will for a client who may be challenged.","Prepare the will without further steps, because capacity is presumed for adults.","Ask the child being cut out to confirm that they agree with the new will.","Arrange for a medical practitioner to assess her capacity and ideally witness the will.","Arrange for Elinor to sign a lasting power of attorney instead of a will."],"a":3,"e":"The '**golden rule**' (Kenward v Adams) is that when an elderly or seriously ill testator makes a will, the solicitor should arrange for a **medical practitioner** to assess and record their capacity, and ideally to **witness** the will. Failure to do so does not invalidate the will, but following it provides strong evidence if the will is challenged. Refusing to act or involving the disinherited child would be inappropriate, and confidentiality must be kept."},
 {"t":"validity","q":"Faisal, who is registered blind, instructed a solicitor to prepare his will. The solicitor read the will aloud to Faisal before he signed it in front of two witnesses, but the attestation clause is in standard form and does not mention that Faisal was blind or that the will was read to him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what is needed to admit the will to probate?","o":["A new will, because a blind person cannot make a valid will.","Evidence that Faisal's signature was witnessed by a medical practitioner.","Confirmation from the beneficiaries that they consent to the will being proved.","Evidence, such as an affidavit, that the will was read over to Faisal before he signed.","Nothing further, because a duly executed will raises a presumption of knowledge and approval in every case."],"a":3,"e":"Normally **knowledge and approval** is presumed where a testator with capacity signs a duly executed will. Where the testator is **blind or illiterate**, the presumption does not arise, and the probate registry will require **evidence** (usually an affidavit under NCPR r.13) that the testator knew and approved the contents, such as proof that the will was **read over** to him. This is why the attestation clause should record such facts. A blind person can make a valid will."},
 {"t":"validity","q":"Gareth, aged 85, changed his will to leave more to his daughter Hana, who cares for him, and less to his son. Hana had told Gareth, truthfully, that his son rarely visited, and had often urged him to 'think about who really looks after you'. There is no evidence that she threatened or pressured him beyond this, and Gareth was mentally sharp.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the will can be set aside for undue influence?","o":["It will be set aside, because any attempt to persuade a testator invalidates the gifts that result.","It is unlikely to be set aside, because persuasion that falls short of coercion is not undue influence.","It is unlikely to be set aside, unless Gareth's son proves that the change was unfair.","It will be set aside, because a relationship of care and trust raises a presumption of undue influence.","It will be set aside, because Hana benefits from the change and must prove she did not influence it."],"a":1,"e":"In **probate**, undue influence means **coercion**: pressure that overpowers the testator's will so that he does not really intend the gift. **Persuasion**, appeals to affection or pointing out truthful facts are permissible. There is **no presumption** of undue influence for wills, even in a relationship of trust; the person alleging it must **prove** it. Unfairness alone is not a ground for setting a will aside."},
 {"t":"validity","q":"Imogen's will left her estate equally to her nephew Jake and her niece Kara. Shortly before Imogen made a new will leaving everything to Kara, Kara told Imogen that Jake had been convicted of stealing from elderly people, which Kara knew to be untrue. Imogen was shocked and changed her will because of this.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the new will?","o":["It is valid, but Jake can claim reasonable provision under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975.","It is valid, because Imogen had capacity and freely decided to change her will.","It is valid, because there is no presumption of undue influence in relation to wills.","It is invalid only if Kara prepared the new will herself.","It is likely to be invalid, because Kara's deliberate lies amount to fraud."],"a":4,"e":"A will (or gift) procured by **fraud** is invalid. **Fraudulent calumny** is a form of fraud where a person poisons the testator's mind by making **false statements** about another beneficiary's character, **knowing them to be false** (or not caring whether they are true), to induce a change in the will. Kara knowingly lied, so the new will is likely to be set aside, and the earlier will would stand. A nephew is unlikely to qualify under the 1975 Act."},
 {"t":"validity","q":"Lowri's son Marc telephones a solicitor and says his elderly mother wants to make a new will leaving her house to him. Marc says he will bring her to the appointment, sit in on the meeting and pay the fees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the solicitor should proceed?","o":["See Lowri alone and take her instructions directly, checking she is acting freely.","Take instructions from Marc, because he is a family member and is paying the fees.","Refuse to act, because a beneficiary has contacted the firm.","Allow Marc to attend the meeting, because Lowri is elderly and may need support.","Prepare the will on Marc's instructions and send it to Lowri to sign at home."],"a":0,"e":"A solicitor must act on the **client's** instructions and be satisfied that they reflect the client's **wishes** and are given **freely**. Where instructions come through a **beneficiary**, the solicitor should **see the testator alone** to check her capacity, her wishes and the absence of undue influence. Payment by a third party does not make him the client. Refusing to act is unnecessary if these steps are taken."},
 {"t":"validity","q":"Nerys's will was professionally drafted, rational on its face and properly signed and witnessed. It leaves everything to her carer. Her son challenges it and produces medical records showing that, in the month the will was signed, Nerys was repeatedly confused about where she lived and did not recognise him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who must prove what?","o":["The carer must prove capacity in every case, regardless of the son's evidence.","The son must prove that the carer exerted undue influence before capacity can be questioned.","The son's evidence raises a real doubt, so the carer must now prove that Nerys had capacity.","Capacity is conclusively presumed, because the will was professionally drafted and properly executed.","The son must prove beyond reasonable doubt that Nerys lacked capacity."],"a":2,"e":"The **propounder** of the will (the carer) bears the burden of proving capacity. A rational will that is **duly executed** raises a **presumption** of capacity, but if the challenger produces evidence raising a **real doubt**, the burden shifts back to the propounder to prove capacity on the **balance of probabilities**. The criminal standard does not apply, and the presumption is not conclusive."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Owain had a stroke and can no longer write his name. He signed his will by making a cross, in the presence of two witnesses who were in the room together. Both witnesses then signed in his presence. The attestation clause records that Owain signed by making his mark.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the will?","o":["It is valid only if a medical practitioner confirms Owain could not write.","It is invalid, because a testator who cannot write must have someone else sign for him.","It is valid, because a cross can be a signature if the testator intends it as one.","It is invalid, because a will must be signed with the testator's full name.","It is invalid, because a will signed with a mark must also be signed by a solicitor."],"a":2,"e":"Under Wills Act 1837 **s.9(a)**, the will must be **signed** by the testator, and a signature can be **any mark** intended as such: initials, a cross, a thumbprint. The cross was made in the presence of two witnesses together, who each then signed in his presence, so s.9 is satisfied. The attestation clause properly records the use of a mark, which helps with evidence of knowledge and approval."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Pam is too weak to hold a pen. With two witnesses present together, she asked her nephew Rhodri to sign the will for her. Rhodri wrote 'Pam Jones' on the will while Pam watched. The two witnesses then signed in Pam's presence. Rhodri receives nothing under the will.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the will?","o":["It is invalid, because a person signing for the testator must be a solicitor.","It is invalid, because a will must be signed personally by the testator.","It is invalid, because Rhodri should have signed his own name, not Pam's.","It is valid only if Rhodri also signs as a third witness.","It is valid, because Rhodri signed in Pam's presence and by her direction."],"a":4,"e":"Section 9(a) allows the will to be signed by **some other person in the testator's presence and by her direction**. That person can sign either the testator's name or their own. The signature was made in the presence of two witnesses together, who then signed in Pam's presence. Rhodri is not a beneficiary, so s.15 is not engaged. The attestation clause should record the circumstances."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Seb wrote his will by hand on a single sheet. It begins: 'This is the last will of me, Sebastian Ward.' It then sets out his gifts, and he did not sign at the bottom. He wrote it all in one sitting in front of two friends who were present throughout, then said, 'That's my will, please witness it,' and they signed it in his presence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the will?","o":["It may be valid, because a signature need not be at the end of the will.","It is invalid, because a will must be signed at the end.","It is invalid, because a handwritten will is not valid in England and Wales.","It is valid only if Seb's name was written after the gifts were set out.","It is invalid, because the witnesses did not see him write his name."],"a":0,"e":"A signature does not have to be at the **end** of the will. What matters under **s.9(b)** is that the testator **intended by the signature to give effect to the will**. Where the name at the top was written as part of **one continuous operation** of writing the will, intending it to validate the whole document, it can be his signature (Wood v Smith). A handwritten will is valid if it complies with s.9, and Seb acknowledged it to both witnesses together."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Tegan signed her will at home in front of her neighbour Uri and Uri's father, Victor, who were in the room together. Victor is blind. Both men then signed the will as witnesses in Tegan's presence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the will?","o":["It is valid, because Victor could hear Tegan sign and acknowledge the will.","It is valid, but Victor's signature is disregarded and the will is treated as witnessed by Uri alone.","It is invalid only if Victor is also a beneficiary under the will.","It is invalid, because a blind person cannot act as a witness.","It is valid, because both witnesses were present when Tegan signed."],"a":3,"e":"A witness must be able to **see** the testator sign (or acknowledge). A **blind** person **cannot** be a witness, because he is not 'present' in the visual sense required by **s.9(c)** (Re Gibson). There were therefore not two valid witnesses, so the will is invalid. A will witnessed by only one person cannot be valid."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Wanda signed her will in front of her neighbour and her neighbour's son, Xavi, aged 15, who were in the room together. Both then signed as witnesses in Wanda's presence. Xavi understood what he was doing. Neither witness benefits under the will.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the will?","o":["It is valid only if Xavi's parent consented to him acting as a witness.","It is valid, because the law sets no minimum age for a witness.","It is invalid, because a minor's signature can be confirmed only once he reaches 18.","It is invalid, because a witness must be at least 18.","It is invalid, because a witness must be at least 16."],"a":1,"e":"The Wills Act sets **no minimum age** for witnesses. A witness must be able to understand what they are witnessing and to see the testator sign. Xavi met these requirements, so the will is validly executed. It is not good practice to use a minor (they may be harder to trace or less reliable as a witness later), but it does not invalidate the will."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Yuki's will leaves £15,000 to her friend Zena and the residue to her brother. The will was properly signed by Yuki and witnessed by her neighbour Arlo and by Zena's husband, Ben. No one else witnessed it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of Ben's witnessing?","o":["The will is valid, but Ben is disqualified as a witness, so it must be re-executed.","The will is valid, but the £15,000 gift to Zena is void and falls into residue.","The will is invalid, because the spouse of a beneficiary witnessed it.","The will is valid and Zena receives the £15,000, because only the beneficiary personally is disqualified.","The will is valid, but Zena takes only half of the £15,000."],"a":1,"e":"Under Wills Act 1837 **s.15**, a gift to a **witness or the witness's spouse or civil partner** is **void**. The **will remains valid**, and Ben is still a good witness for s.9. The void gift falls into **residue**, so Yuki's brother takes the £15,000. The exception for **two other** independent witnesses does not apply, because only one other person witnessed the will."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Carys's will, signed in 2022, leaves £20,000 to her friend Dewi. The will was witnessed by Dewi's girlfriend, Eira, and by Carys's neighbour. Dewi and Eira married in 2024. Carys has just died.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Dewi takes the legacy?","o":["He does not take it, because his wife witnessed the will.","He does not take it, because a witness's partner is treated as a spouse for the purposes of s.15.","He takes it only if the neighbour confirms the will was properly executed.","He takes it, because s.15 applies only to a witness's spouse at the time the will was signed.","He does not take it, because the gift is void from the date of the marriage."],"a":3,"e":"Section **15** invalidates a gift to a witness's **spouse or civil partner**, judged at the **time the will is executed**. Dewi and Eira were not married when Eira witnessed the will in 2022, so the later marriage does not affect the gift. A cohabitant or fiancé(e) is not a spouse for this purpose. The will was validly executed by two witnesses."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"In 2020 Faith made a will leaving £10,000 to her friend Gwyn, who was one of the two witnesses. In 2025 Faith made a codicil changing her executors and confirming the will in all other respects. The codicil was witnessed by two neighbours who receive nothing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Gwyn takes the £10,000?","o":["He takes it, because the codicil he did not witness confirmed the will.","He does not take it, because a void gift cannot be revived by a later document.","He takes it only if the codicil expressly mentions the gift to him.","He takes only half of it, because the original gift was partly void.","He does not take it, because he witnessed the will that contains the gift."],"a":0,"e":"A gift to a witness is void under **s.15**. However, a **codicil** that **confirms** the will **republishes** it, so the will is treated as made at the date of the codicil. If the beneficiary did **not** witness the codicil, the gift is effectively confirmed by a document he did not witness, and it is **saved**. There is no need for the codicil to mention the gift specifically."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Hector's will was signed by him and by two witnesses and contains a standard attestation clause stating that it was signed by Hector in the presence of both witnesses, present at the same time, who then signed in his presence. Both witnesses have since died. Hector's daughter suggests that the will cannot be proved because no witness can give evidence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The will cannot be proved, because the death of a witness revokes the will.","The will can be proved only if all the beneficiaries agree.","The will cannot be proved, because a witness must give evidence of due execution.","The will can be proved only if a handwriting expert confirms the witnesses' signatures.","The will can be proved, because due execution is presumed."],"a":4,"e":"Where a will appears to be properly signed and contains a **proper attestation clause**, there is a **presumption of due execution** (omnia praesumuntur rite esse acta). The will can be admitted to probate without evidence from the witnesses. If there were **no** attestation clause or there was doubt about execution, the registrar would usually want an **affidavit of due execution** under NCPR r.12."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"In March 2025 Iris signed her will at home while her two witnesses watched her on a live video call from their own homes. The original was then posted to each witness, who signed it while Iris watched them on another video call. Iris has now died.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the will?","o":["It is invalid only because the witnesses signed on different days.","It is valid, because the witnesses had a clear line of sight to Iris over the video call.","It is invalid, because the witnesses were not physically present when Iris signed.","It is valid, because video witnessing is permanently permitted for wills in England and Wales.","It is valid, because the will was signed by Iris and both witnesses."],"a":2,"e":"Section 9 requires the witnesses to be **present** when the testator signs or acknowledges, which means **physical presence**. A temporary amendment allowed **video witnessing** for wills made between **31 January 2020 and 31 January 2024**, but it has **ended**. A will witnessed by video in March 2025 does not comply with s.9 and is invalid."},
 {"t":"executors","q":"Jodie's homemade will says: 'I want my sister Kate to pay my debts, collect in everything I own and deal with my estate. Everything else goes to my children.' The will does not use the word 'executor'. It was properly signed and witnessed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kate's position?","o":["She is not an executor, because a will must use the word 'executor' to appoint one.","She can apply only for letters of administration with will annexed, because no executor is appointed.","She is a trustee of the estate but not an executor.","She cannot act at all, because she is not a beneficiary under the will.","She is an executor according to the tenor of the will."],"a":4,"e":"An executor can be appointed **expressly** or by **implication**. An **executor according to the tenor** of the will is someone the will directs to carry out the functions of an executor, such as paying debts and administering the estate, without using the word 'executor'. Kate can therefore apply for **probate**. An executor need not be a beneficiary."},
 {"t":"executors","q":"Leo's will appoints five people as executors: his wife and his four adult children. All five are willing to act and want to apply for probate together.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["No more than four can take the grant, so one must stand aside, usually by having power reserved.","Only two can take probate, because the law requires exactly two personal representatives.","Only Leo's wife can take probate, because a spouse has priority over children.","None can take probate until one renounces, because the appointment of five executors is void.","All five can take probate, because the testator chose them all."],"a":0,"e":"No more than **four** personal representatives can take a grant in respect of the same property (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.114(1)). If five are named, up to four can apply, and the fifth can have **power reserved** (allowing them to apply later if a vacancy arises) rather than renouncing. There is no rule requiring exactly two, and a spouse has no priority over other named executors."},
 {"t":"executors","q":"Mona was appointed executor of her uncle's will. Before doing anything in the estate, she signed a form of renunciation, which was filed at the Probate Registry, and her co-executor obtained probate. A year later, after her co-executor died, Mona wishes to take over the administration.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mona's position?","o":["She can act automatically, because her renunciation lapsed when her co-executor died.","She can act only if all the beneficiaries sign a deed consenting to her appointment.","She can retract her renunciation only with the permission of a district judge or registrar.","She can retract her renunciation at any time simply by applying for probate.","She can never act, because a renunciation is irrevocable in all circumstances."],"a":2,"e":"An executor who has **renounced** gives up the right to a grant. A renunciation can be **retracted** only with the **leave** of a district judge or registrar (NCPR r.37), which is generally given only where it would benefit the estate (for example where no one else is available). It does not lapse automatically. If she had wanted to keep her options open, she should have had **power reserved** instead."},
 {"t":"executors","q":"Nikhil and his sister Ola are the executors of their father's will. Nikhil lives abroad and is too busy to deal with the estate now, but he wants to keep the option of acting later if Ola becomes unable to continue. He has not dealt with any estate assets.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Nikhil should do?","o":["Do nothing, because he will automatically become sole executor if Ola dies.","Renounce his executorship, which he can withdraw automatically if Ola stops acting.","Apply for probate jointly with Ola, because both executors must take the grant.","Allow Ola to take probate with power reserved to him, so he can act later.","Appoint Ola as his attorney, because an executor who does not act loses the right to act later."],"a":3,"e":"Where one executor does not want to act now but wants to keep the right to act later, the others can take probate with **power reserved** to him. He can later apply for a **grant of double probate** if needed. **Renunciation** gives up the right entirely and can be retracted only with leave. It is not necessary for all executors to take the grant."},
 {"t":"executors","q":"Pia was the sole executor of her mother Rose's will and obtained probate. Before she had finished administering Rose's estate, Pia died. Pia's own will appoints her husband Sam as her executor, and Sam has obtained probate of Pia's will.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who can complete the administration of Rose's estate?","o":["Sam, but only after obtaining letters of administration of Rose's estate.","Sam, who becomes Rose's executor through the chain of representation.","Rose's residuary beneficiaries, who must apply for a new grant of letters of administration.","No one until the court appoints a new administrator of Rose's estate.","Sam, but only if Rose's will names him as a substitute executor."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **chain of representation** (Administration of Estates Act 1925 **s.7**), the **executor of a sole (or last surviving) proving executor** who has obtained probate becomes the executor of the original testator as well. No further grant is needed. The chain is broken if a link is an **administrator** or if the executor dies without proving the will."},
 {"t":"executors","q":"Tariq was the sole executor of his aunt Una's will and obtained probate. He died intestate before finishing the administration, and his widow has obtained letters of administration of Tariq's estate. Some of Una's assets have not yet been distributed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who can deal with Una's remaining assets?","o":["A new grant for Una's unadministered estate, because the chain of representation is broken.","Tariq's widow, because the chain of representation passes through any personal representative.","Una's residuary beneficiaries, who can now collect the assets without a grant.","No one, because Una's remaining assets pass to Tariq's estate.","Tariq's widow, because as his administrator she automatically represents Una's estate."],"a":0,"e":"The **chain of representation** passes only through **executors who prove** the will. An **administrator** of the last proving executor does **not** become the original testator's representative, so the chain is broken. A new grant (letters of administration with will annexed, **de bonis non administratis**: 'of the goods not administered') is needed for Una's remaining estate. Una's assets do not belong to Tariq's estate."},
 {"t":"executors","q":"Vera's will appoints her two sons, Will and Xander, as executors. Will has had a severe brain injury and lacks the mental capacity to manage his affairs. Xander is willing to act.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how a grant can be obtained?","o":["No grant can be obtained until the Court of Protection appoints a deputy for Will.","Xander must apply for letters of administration with will annexed, because one executor lacks capacity.","Xander can take probate alone, with the application explaining that Will lacks capacity.","Xander cannot take probate unless Will joins in the application, because both executors must act.","Will can take probate with Xander, because his appointment in the will is valid."],"a":2,"e":"An executor who **lacks mental capacity** cannot take a grant. Where there is another executor who is able and willing, that executor can take **probate** alone, with the other's incapacity recorded in the application. A grant for the use of an incapable executor (for example to an attorney or deputy) is needed only if there is no capable executor."},
 {"t":"executors","q":"Yasmin is the executor of her father's will, and Zara is the administrator of her late brother's estate (he died intestate). Neither has yet obtained a grant. Both want to know whether they can begin dealing with the estates.\n\nWhich of the following best describes their authority before a grant?","o":["Both have authority from the date of death, but only for estates under £5,000.","Both have full authority from the date of death, because a grant only confirms their position.","Neither has any authority before a grant, so neither may take any step at all.","Zara has authority from the date of death, but Yasmin has none until probate is granted.","Yasmin may begin acting before the grant, but Zara has no authority until the grant."],"a":4,"e":"An **executor's** authority derives from the **will** and arises at **death**; the grant merely **confirms** it. She may therefore start collecting assets and taking steps, although she will need the grant to prove her title to many asset holders. An **administrator's** authority derives from the **grant** itself, so before it is issued Zara has no authority to act on behalf of the estate."},
 {"t":"executors","q":"Ahmed's will appoints his friend Bella as executor and leaves her £5,000. It also appoints his brother Cal as co-executor and leaves him nothing. Bella and Cal were the two witnesses to the will.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Both appointments are void, because an executor cannot witness the will appointing them.","Both appointments as executor are valid, but Bella's legacy is void because she witnessed the will.","Bella's appointment and legacy are both void, because she witnessed the will.","The will is invalid, because an executor witnessed it.","Bella's appointment and legacy are both valid, because an executor is exempt from the witnessing rule."],"a":1,"e":"An **executor can witness** the will, and the **appointment** as executor is not affected, because it is an office rather than a beneficial gift. However, s.15 Wills Act 1837 makes any **beneficial gift** to a witness **void**, so Bella loses the £5,000 (which falls into residue). The will itself remains valid."},
 {"t":"executors","q":"Dina's will appoints her sister Eve as executor and leaves the residue to Eve absolutely. Eve renounces probate, because she does not want the work of administering the estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of Eve's renunciation?","o":["Eve's renunciation makes the will invalid, so the estate passes on intestacy.","Eve keeps the right to probate, because a residuary beneficiary cannot renounce.","Eve loses the residue, which passes to the person entitled to the next grant.","Eve gives up the executorship but keeps the residue, and could still apply as residuary beneficiary.","Eve loses both the executorship and her entitlement to the residue."],"a":3,"e":"Renouncing as **executor** gives up the office, not the **benefit**: Eve still takes the residue. Under NCPR **r.20**, the residuary beneficiaries come after executors in the order of priority, so Eve could apply in that capacity for **letters of administration with will annexed**, unless she also renounces that right. A renunciation does not affect the will's validity."},
 {"t":"alterations-revocation","q":"Fran's will leaves '£10,000 to my nephew Gus'. After the will was executed, Fran used thick black marker to completely obliterate '£10,000 to my nephew Gus', so that the words cannot be read by any ordinary means. She did not sign or initial the change, and wrote nothing in its place.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect?","o":["Gus still takes £10,000, because the original words can be proved by extrinsic evidence such as a draft.","The legacy is revoked only if Fran's intention is confirmed by a codicil.","Gus still takes £10,000, because an unattested alteration after execution has no effect.","The legacy to Gus is revoked, because the words have been obliterated so that they are not apparent.","The whole will is revoked, because it has been deliberately damaged."],"a":3,"e":"Under Wills Act 1837 **s.21**, an unattested alteration made after execution is ineffective, **except** that if the original words have been **obliterated** so that they are **not apparent** (cannot be read by natural means), those words are **revoked**. Here there is no substitution on which a conditional revocation could depend, so the legacy is revoked and falls into residue. Extrinsic evidence such as a draft cannot be used to show 'apparent' words."},
 {"t":"alterations-revocation","q":"Hal's will leaves '£5,000 to my godson Ian'. After Hal's death, the will is found with a single neat line through '£5,000' and '£2,000' written above it in Hal's handwriting. The alteration is not signed or witnessed, and '£5,000' is still clearly readable.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ian's entitlement?","o":["£3,500, as a compromise between the two figures.","£5,000, because the alteration is unattested and the original words are apparent.","£2,000, because the change is in Hal's handwriting and shows his last intention.","Nothing, because the alteration revoked the original gift and the new gift is invalid.","£2,000, because alterations are presumed to have been made before the will was signed."],"a":1,"e":"An alteration made **after execution** must itself be executed in accordance with s.9 (signed or initialled by the testator and witnesses) to be effective (s.21). Alterations are **presumed** to have been made **after** execution unless shown otherwise. Since the original figure is still **apparent**, it stands, and Ian takes **£5,000**."},
 {"t":"alterations-revocation","q":"After signing her will, June changed a legacy to her cousin from £3,000 to £6,000 by crossing out the original figure and writing the new figure in the margin. She then signed her initials next to the change in the presence of two witnesses, present at the same time, who each initialled it in her presence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect?","o":["The cousin takes £3,000, because a will cannot be altered after it is signed.","The cousin takes £3,000, because initials are not a valid signature for an alteration.","The cousin takes £6,000 only if June also makes a codicil confirming the change.","The cousin takes nothing, because the legacy was revoked by the alteration.","The cousin takes £6,000, because the alteration was properly executed."],"a":4,"e":"Under **s.21**, an alteration made after execution is valid if it is **executed like a will**: signed (initials are enough) by the testator in the presence of two witnesses present at the same time, who then sign (or initial) in her presence. The alteration therefore takes effect and the cousin receives **£6,000**. No codicil is needed."},
 {"t":"alterations-revocation","q":"Kemal's 2015 will leaves 'my house' to his daughter Leyla. In 2019 he sold that house and bought a flat. In 2022 he made a codicil changing his executors and confirming the will. He still owned the flat when he died.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Leyla receives the flat?","o":["She receives the flat, because the codicil republished the will as at 2022.","She receives the proceeds of sale of the 2015 house, because the gift attaches to the proceeds.","She receives nothing, because a codicil cannot alter the meaning of the original will.","She receives the flat only if the codicil expressly mentions it.","She receives nothing, because the gift of the house adeemed when it was sold in 2019."],"a":0,"e":"A **codicil** confirming a will **republishes** it, so the will is treated as if made at the **date of the codicil**. In 2022 the description 'my house' applied to the flat Kemal then owned as his home, so the gift takes effect over the flat. Without the codicil, the gift would have **adeemed** when the original house was sold."},
 {"t":"alterations-revocation","q":"In 2018 Mia made a will leaving everything to her brother. In 2021 she made a new will, including a revocation clause, leaving everything to a charity. In 2024, having fallen out with the charity, Mia tore up the 2021 will, intending to revoke it and to bring back her 2018 will. She made no other will.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how her estate will pass?","o":["Under the 2018 will, because Mia's intention to revive it is clear.","On intestacy, unless her brother applies to court within six months to revive the 2018 will.","On intestacy, because the 2018 will was revoked in 2021 and is not revived by destroying the later will.","Under the 2018 will to her brother, because destroying the 2021 will revived it.","Under the 2021 will to the charity, because destruction does not revoke a will containing a revocation clause."],"a":2,"e":"A will once revoked can be **revived** only by **re-execution** or by a **codicil** showing an intention to revive it (Wills Act 1837 s.22). Destroying the later revoking will does **not** revive the earlier one, whatever the testator intended. The 2021 will was effectively revoked by destruction with intention, so Mia dies **intestate**."},
 {"t":"alterations-revocation","q":"Ned's 2019 will leaves his house to his daughter, £20,000 to his nephew and the residue to his wife. In 2025 Ned made a new will, properly executed, which leaves his house to his son. The 2025 will contains no revocation clause and says nothing else.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Ned's estate passes?","o":["The house is divided between his son and daughter, because the wills conflict.","The 2025 will revokes the whole 2019 will, so the rest of the estate passes on intestacy.","The son takes the house, and the rest of the 2019 will still takes effect.","The house passes to his daughter, because a later will without a revocation clause has no effect.","The 2025 will revokes the 2019 will entirely, so the son takes everything."],"a":2,"e":"A later will revokes an earlier one **expressly** (by a revocation clause) or **impliedly**, but only to the extent of any **inconsistency**. Without a revocation clause, both wills are read together: the 2025 will revokes only the gift of the house, so the son takes the house, while the other gifts in the 2019 will stand. Both documents are admitted to probate together."},
 {"t":"alterations-revocation","q":"Olive was in hospital and told her son Paolo on the telephone to go to her house and burn her will, as she wanted to revoke it. Paolo did so that evening at Olive's house, while she remained in hospital. Olive died a week later without making a new will.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The will is revoked, because it was destroyed on Olive's instructions with an intention to revoke.","The will is revoked, because a burnt will can never be proved.","The will is not revoked, because a will can be revoked only by a later will or codicil.","The will is revoked, but only if Paolo confirms Olive's instructions on oath.","The will is not revoked, because Paolo did not destroy it in Olive's presence."],"a":4,"e":"Under Wills Act 1837 **s.20**, a will can be revoked by burning, tearing or otherwise destroying it, by the testator **or by some person in her presence and by her direction**, with the intention to revoke. Destruction by Paolo **outside Olive's presence** is not effective. The will has not been revoked and its contents can be proved by a **copy** or other secondary evidence."},
 {"t":"alterations-revocation","q":"Priya made a will in 2020, which she kept in a drawer at home. She had full capacity until her death. After she died, her family searched thoroughly but could not find the original will. A copy is held on her solicitor's file.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It is presumed, unless rebutted, that Priya destroyed the will intending to revoke it.","There is a conclusive presumption that the will was revoked.","The will is presumed to have been lost accidentally, so the copy can be proved.","The will is valid only if the solicitor certifies that it was never revoked.","The copy can be proved automatically, because the solicitor's copy is equivalent to the original."],"a":0,"e":"Where a will last known to be in the **testator's possession** cannot be found at death, there is a **presumption** that the testator **destroyed it with the intention of revoking it**. The presumption is **rebuttable** by evidence (for example of continuing intention to benefit the beneficiaries or of accidental loss). If it is rebutted, the will can be proved from a **copy**; otherwise the estate passes as if there were no will."},
 {"t":"alterations-revocation","q":"In January 2025 Rhys made a will leaving everything to his fiancée Sian. The will states: 'This will is made in expectation of my marriage to Sian Jones and shall not be revoked by that marriage.' Rhys and Sian married in June 2025. Rhys died last month.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Rhys's estate passes?","o":["Under the will, but only up to the value of Sian's entitlement on intestacy.","Under the will, but only if Sian makes an application to court.","On intestacy, because marriage automatically revokes any earlier will.","Under the will to Sian, because it was made in expectation of marrying her.","On intestacy, unless Rhys confirmed the will by a codicil after the marriage."],"a":3,"e":"Marriage normally revokes a will (Wills Act 1837 s.18). Under **s.18(3)**, a will is **not revoked** by the marriage where it appears from the will that, at the time it was made, the testator was **expecting to marry a particular person** and intended the will not to be revoked by that marriage. The will names Sian and states that intention, so it stands."},
 {"t":"alterations-revocation","q":"Tom decided to revoke his will. He took a pair of scissors and cut his signature and the witnesses' signatures off the bottom of the will, then put the rest of it back in his desk. He died some months later without making a new will.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The will is revoked only if the cut-off signatures cannot be found.","The whole will is revoked, because the signatures are an essential part of it.","Only the signatures are revoked, and the remainder of the will can be proved.","The will is not revoked, because Tom did not destroy the whole document.","The will is not revoked, because a will can only be revoked by burning or tearing."],"a":1,"e":"Revocation by destruction under **s.20** requires an act of destruction plus an **intention to revoke**. Destruction of part of the will can revoke the **whole** will if the part destroyed is **essential**, such as the testator's **signature** or the witnesses' signatures. Cutting them off with intention to revoke therefore revokes the entire will, and Tom dies intestate."},
 {"t":"interpretation","q":"Ula's will leaves '£30,000 to be divided equally between my nephews'. Ula had three nephews when she made her will, but one, Vince, died before her, leaving a son. The will contains no other relevant provision.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the £30,000 is distributed?","o":["The two surviving nephews take £10,000 each, and Vince's £10,000 lapses into residue.","The two surviving nephews take £15,000 each, because this is a class gift.","The two surviving nephews take £10,000 each, and Vince's son takes £10,000 under the statutory substitution rule.","The two surviving nephews take £10,000 each, and Vince's £10,000 passes to his estate.","The whole gift lapses into residue, because one member of the class died."],"a":1,"e":"A gift to a **class** (here 'my nephews') is shared among those class members who are **alive at the testator's death** (unless the will says otherwise), so the death of one class member does not cause a lapse of his share; the survivors take the whole gift. The **s.33** substitution rule applies only to gifts to the testator's **children or remoter descendants**, not nephews."},
 {"t":"interpretation","q":"Wyn's will leaves 'my residuary estate to my friends Xia and Yusuf in equal shares'. Xia died before Wyn. The will contains no substitution or survivorship provision. Wyn's only relative is his sister.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the residue passes?","o":["Yusuf takes the whole residue by survivorship.","Yusuf takes half, and Xia's half passes to Xia's estate.","Yusuf takes half, and Xia's half falls back into residue and is shared by Yusuf.","Yusuf takes half, and Xia's half passes on partial intestacy.","Yusuf takes the whole residue, because a gift of residue to two people never lapses."],"a":3,"e":"The gift is to **named individuals** in **equal shares** (words of severance), not to a class or as joint tenants, so Xia's share **lapses** on her death before Wyn. A lapsed share of **residue** cannot fall back into residue; it passes on **partial intestacy**, here to Wyn's sister. Yusuf receives only his own half."},
 {"t":"interpretation","q":"Zak's will leaves 'my grand piano to my niece Amy'. A year before Zak died, the piano was destroyed in a house fire. The insurers paid £40,000 to Zak, which is still in his bank account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Amy's entitlement?","o":["Nothing, because the gift of the piano has adeemed.","Whatever replacement piano the PRs buy with the insurance money.","A share of residue equal to the piano's value.","£40,000, because the insurance money represents the piano.","£40,000, because a specific gift that fails is converted into a pecuniary legacy of its value."],"a":0,"e":"A **specific gift** of a particular item **adeems** (fails) if the item is **no longer in the estate** at death, for example because it was sold, given away or destroyed. The beneficiary has **no claim** to the proceeds or any insurance money, which form part of the general estate (usually residue). The will could have included a substitution clause, but it did not."},
 {"t":"interpretation","q":"Bea's will leaves '1,000 shares in Tesco plc to my nephew Cian'. At her death Bea owns no Tesco shares; she sold all her shares five years ago.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Cian's entitlement?","o":["He is entitled to nothing, because a gift of shares must refer to 'my' shares to be valid.","He is entitled to nothing, because the gift adeemed when Bea sold her Tesco shares.","He is entitled to the proceeds of sale of Bea's Tesco shares, if they can be traced.","He is entitled to a share of residue equal to the value of the shares when the will was made.","He is entitled to 1,000 Tesco shares or their value, because this is a general legacy."],"a":4,"e":"The wording '1,000 shares in Tesco plc', without '**my**', is a **general legacy**: a gift of property of a particular kind, not of a specific item owned by the testator. A general legacy cannot adeem; the PRs must **buy** the shares or pay their value from the estate. A gift of '**my** 1,000 shares in Tesco plc' would be **specific** and would have adeemed."},
 {"t":"interpretation","q":"Dev's will leaves 'my house to my nephew John'. Dev had two nephews called John: John Patel, his brother's son, whom he saw every week, and John Shah, his sister's son, whom he had not seen for 30 years. Dev's solicitor's file contains a note of Dev saying, 'I want my house to go to young John, who looks after me.'\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the file note can be used?","o":["It cannot be used, because the will must be interpreted from its words alone.","It cannot be used, because evidence of a testator's intention is never admissible.","It can be used, because the gift is ambiguous in the light of the surrounding circumstances.","It can be used only if both nephews agree.","It cannot be used, so the house is divided equally between the two nephews."],"a":2,"e":"Under Administration of Justice Act 1982 **s.21**, **extrinsic evidence**, including evidence of the testator's **intention**, is admissible to help interpret a will where any part of it is **meaningless**, or its language is **ambiguous** on its face or **in the light of surrounding circumstances**. 'My nephew John' is ambiguous when there are two such nephews, so the note can be used to identify John Patel."},
 {"t":"interpretation","q":"Erin's will leaves her residue 'equally between my grandchildren'. At her death she has three grandchildren: Fay, who was adopted by Erin's son; Gil, whose parents were never married; and Hal, born to Erin's married daughter.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who takes the residue?","o":["Hal and Gil, because an adopted child is treated as a child of the birth family only.","Hal and Fay, because a child whose parents were not married is excluded unless named.","All three, but Fay takes a smaller share because she was adopted.","Only Hal, because only grandchildren born to married parents are included.","All three grandchildren equally, because each of them is within the class."],"a":4,"e":"Unless the will shows a contrary intention, references to relationships include people whose parents were **not married** to each other (Family Law Reform Act 1987 s.1 and s.19), and an **adopted** child is treated as the child of the **adoptive** parents (Adoption and Children Act 2002 s.67). All three are Erin's grandchildren and share equally."},
 {"t":"interpretation","q":"In 2015 Imran made a will leaving 'all my shares to my daughter Jas'. At that time he owned shares in two companies. By his death he had sold those shares and bought shares in three different companies.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Jas's entitlement?","o":["She takes the shares Imran owned at his death.","She takes only shares in companies Imran held in 2015.","She takes the value the 2015 shares had at the date of the will.","She takes nothing, because the shares Imran owned in 2015 have all been sold.","She takes the proceeds of sale of the 2015 shares."],"a":0,"e":"Under Wills Act 1837 **s.24**, a will **speaks from death** as regards the **property** comprised in it, unless a contrary intention appears. A generic description such as 'all my shares' therefore covers the shares Imran owned at **death**, including those bought after the will. (Contrast a gift of a specific identified item, which may adeem if disposed of.)"},
 {"t":"interpretation","q":"Kira's will leaves £50,000 to her friend Lena 'if she survives me by 28 days', and the residue to her son. Kira and Lena were injured in the same accident. Kira died on 1 May, and Lena died on 10 May.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who receives the £50,000?","o":["It is divided equally between Lena's estate and Kira's son.","Lena's estate, because Lena survived Kira.","Kira's son, because the gift to Lena fails and falls into residue.","Lena's estate, because the 28-day condition applies only on intestacy.","Lena's children, under the statutory substitution rule."],"a":2,"e":"A **survivorship clause** makes the gift conditional on the beneficiary surviving the testator for the stated period. Lena survived Kira by only nine days, so the condition is not met and the gift **fails**, falling into **residue** for Kira's son. The s.33 substitution rule applies only to gifts to the testator's own children or remoter descendants."},
 {"t":"interpretation","q":"Mal, aged 70, and his son Nik, aged 40, were killed in the same plane crash. It is impossible to tell who died first. Mal's will leaves his estate to Nik, with no survivorship clause or substitution provision. Nik's will leaves everything to his wife, Ola.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who inherits Mal's estate?","o":["Nik's children, under the statutory substitution rule.","Mal's estate passes on intestacy, because the order of death cannot be established.","Ola and Mal's other relatives equally, because neither died first.","Ola, because Nik as the younger is presumed to have survived Mal.","Mal's estate passes on intestacy, because a gift without a survivorship clause lapses in a common accident."],"a":3,"e":"Where the order of deaths is uncertain, LPA 1925 **s.184** presumes that the **younger survived the elder**. Nik is therefore treated as surviving Mal, so the gift to him does **not lapse** and passes into Nik's estate, and then under his will to Ola. Section 33 does not apply because Nik did not die before Mal. A 28-day survivorship clause would have avoided this result."},
 {"t":"interpretation","q":"Pam's will leaves her residue 'to such of my children as survive me'. Her daughter Quin died before Pam, leaving a son, Rob. Pam's other child, Sol, survived her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who takes the residue?","o":["Sol takes half, and Quin's half passes on partial intestacy.","Sol takes it all, because the will excludes the s.33 substitution.","Sol takes half, and Quin's half passes to Quin's estate.","Rob takes all of it, because he represents the deceased child.","Sol and Rob take half each, because s.33 substitutes a predeceasing child's issue."],"a":1,"e":"Under Wills Act 1837 **s.33**, where a gift is to a **child** of the testator who dies before the testator leaving **issue**, the issue take in the child's place, **unless a contrary intention appears** in the will. A gift to 'such of my children **as survive me**' expressly limits the gift to surviving children, showing that contrary intention. Sol, the only surviving child, takes everything."},
 {"t":"intestacy","q":"Tariq died intestate in 2026. He is survived by his wife, Uma, and by both his parents. He had no children. His net estate is worth £900,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Tariq's estate passes?","o":["Uma takes the whole estate, because a surviving spouse takes everything where there are no issue.","Uma and his parents share the estate equally.","His parents take the estate, because a spouse inherits only where there are children.","Uma takes the personal chattels, £322,000 and half the residue, and his parents take the other half.","Uma takes £322,000 and the personal chattels, and his parents take the rest."],"a":0,"e":"Under the Administration of Estates Act 1925 s.46 (as amended in 2014), where the intestate leaves a **spouse or civil partner** but **no issue**, the spouse takes the **whole estate** absolutely, regardless of its value. Parents and siblings take nothing if there is a surviving spouse. The statutory legacy applies only where there are issue."},
 {"t":"intestacy","q":"Vic and Wendy married in 2005 and separated in 2019, but never divorced. Vic died intestate in 2026, leaving Wendy and his adult daughter. He had lived with his new partner, Xena, for six years. His net estate, excluding chattels, is £250,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the estate passes?","o":["His daughter and Xena share the estate equally.","His daughter takes the whole estate, because Vic and Wendy had separated.","Wendy takes the whole estate, because she is still his spouse.","Xena takes the estate, because she was living with Vic at his death.","Wendy and his daughter share the estate equally."],"a":2,"e":"A **separated** spouse is still a **spouse** for the intestacy rules until there is a final divorce order (or a judicial separation order). With **issue**, the spouse takes the personal chattels, the **statutory legacy of £322,000** and half of anything above that; here the estate is below £322,000, so Wendy takes it all. A **cohabitant** such as Xena has no entitlement on intestacy, although she may claim under the 1975 Act."},
 {"t":"intestacy","q":"Yara, a widow, died intestate in 2026. She had three children: Zed and Ali, who survived her, and Bina, who died in 2020, leaving two adult children, Cal and Dot. Yara's net estate is £300,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the distribution?","o":["Zed, Ali, Cal and Dot take £75,000 each.","Zed and Ali take £150,000 each, because Bina's share lapsed.","Zed and Ali take £100,000 each, and Bina's £100,000 passes to her estate.","Zed and Ali take £100,000 each, and Bina's £100,000 passes to the Crown.","Zed and Ali take £100,000 each, and Cal and Dot take £50,000 each."],"a":4,"e":"With no spouse, the estate passes to the **issue** on the **statutory trusts** (AEA 1925 s.47). It is divided into equal shares for each child who survives, and each child who died before the intestate leaving issue; the issue of a predeceased child take their parent's share between them (**per stirpes**). So each of the three branches takes £100,000, and Cal and Dot split Bina's share."},
 {"t":"intestacy","q":"Ed died intestate in 2026. He never married and had no children. He is survived by his mother and by his brother Finn. His father died years ago.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who inherits Ed's estate?","o":["Finn takes the whole estate, because siblings take before parents.","His mother takes the whole estate, because parents take before brothers and sisters.","His mother takes half and the other half passes to the Crown.","Finn takes the whole estate, because a parent inherits only if both parents survive.","His mother and Finn share the estate equally."],"a":1,"e":"Where there is no spouse or civil partner and no issue, the estate passes in the order set out in AEA 1925 s.46: **parents** (equally if both alive, or wholly to the survivor), then **brothers and sisters of the whole blood** (and their issue), then half-blood siblings, grandparents, uncles and aunts. A surviving parent therefore takes everything, to the exclusion of siblings."},
 {"t":"intestacy","q":"Gail died intestate in 2026. She was never married, had no children, and her parents, brothers and sisters all died before her without leaving any children. Her only surviving relatives are her grandmother, Hetty, and her aunt, Iris (her mother's sister).\n\nWhich of the following best describes who inherits Gail's estate?","o":["The estate passes to the Crown as bona vacantia.","Hetty takes a life interest and Iris takes the capital on her death.","Iris takes the whole estate, because aunts take before grandparents.","Hetty takes the whole estate as his grandparent.","Hetty and Iris share the estate equally."],"a":3,"e":"After parents and siblings (and their issue), the order is: **grandparents**, then **uncles and aunts of the whole blood** (and their issue), then uncles and aunts of the half blood. Hetty, as a grandparent, ranks above Iris and takes the whole estate. Only if there were no relatives in any class would the estate pass to the Crown."},
 {"t":"intestacy","q":"Jon died intestate in 2026. He had no spouse, issue, parents, siblings, grandparents or surviving uncles or aunts. His only living relative is his cousin Kim, the daughter of his late uncle (his father's full brother), who died in 2015.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who inherits Jon's estate?","o":["Kim can claim only under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975.","The estate passes to the Crown, because cousins are not entitled on intestacy.","Kim takes half, and the other half passes to the Crown.","Kim takes the whole estate, taking her late father's share on the statutory trusts.","The estate passes to the Crown, because Kim's father died before Jon."],"a":3,"e":"Uncles and aunts take on the **statutory trusts**, which means that if an uncle or aunt has died before the intestate, their **issue** (the intestate's **first cousins**, and their descendants) take their share. Kim therefore takes the whole estate. The estate passes to the Crown as **bona vacantia** only if there is no one in any of the entitled classes."},
 {"t":"intestacy","q":"Leah's will leaves £100,000 to her husband Max and the residue to her brother, who died before her without children. The residue, after debts, is £400,000 (personal chattels were given to Max separately). Leah has two adult children. She died in 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the £400,000 residue is distributed?","o":["Max takes £222,000 (the statutory legacy less his legacy) plus half the rest, and the children take the other half.","Max takes £322,000 plus £39,000, and the children share £39,000, with no deduction for his legacy.","The children take the whole £400,000, because Max has already received a legacy under the will.","Max takes the whole £400,000, because he is the surviving spouse.","The £400,000 passes to the brother's estate, because the gift of residue does not lapse."],"a":1,"e":"The gift of residue **lapses** (s.33 does not apply to a brother), so the residue passes on **partial intestacy** under the intestacy rules. Max, as spouse with issue, takes the **statutory legacy of £322,000** and **half** of the balance (£39,000), and the children share the other £39,000 on the statutory trusts. Since 2014, a spouse no longer has to bring benefits under the will into account against the statutory legacy."},
 {"t":"intestacy","q":"Nadia died intestate in 2026, survived by her husband, Owen, and their adult son. She owned their home, worth £450,000, in her sole name. Her net estate, including the home but excluding chattels, is £600,000. Owen wants to keep the home.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Owen's position?","o":["He cannot keep the home unless his son agrees, because it must be sold and the proceeds divided.","He can keep the home only by buying it from the estate at full market value with his own money.","He takes a life interest in the home, with the capital passing to his son on his death.","He takes the home automatically, because a spouse always inherits the matrimonial home.","He can require the PRs to appropriate the home towards his entitlement."],"a":4,"e":"Owen is entitled to £322,000 plus half of the remaining £278,000 (£139,000), a total of £461,000, plus chattels. Under the **Intestates' Estates Act 1952 Sch 2**, a surviving spouse can require the PRs to **appropriate** the **matrimonial home** in or towards his absolute interest, paying **equality money** if its value exceeds that interest. He must elect within **12 months** of the grant. The home does not pass automatically, and life interests for spouses were abolished in 2014."},
 {"t":"intestacy","q":"Pete died intestate in 2026, a widower. He had one biological son, Quinn, who was adopted by another family as a baby in 1995. Pete has no other children. His only other relative is his sister, Rosa.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who inherits Pete's estate?","o":["Rosa, because Quinn is not Pete's child in law.","Quinn, but only if he can show that Pete maintained him.","The Crown, because Pete left no issue.","Quinn, because he is Pete's biological child.","Quinn and Rosa equally, because both are related to Pete by blood."],"a":0,"e":"Under the **Adoption and Children Act 2002 s.67**, an adopted person is treated in law as the child of the **adopters** and **not** as the child of any other person. Quinn is therefore not Pete's 'issue' for intestacy purposes. With no spouse, issue or parents, the estate passes to Pete's **sister** Rosa."},
 {"t":"intestacy","q":"Sara died intestate in 2026, a widow. She is survived by her stepdaughter, Tia, whom she brought up from the age of four as her own but never adopted, and by her brother, Ugo. Sara had no children of her own.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who inherits Sara's estate?","o":["The Crown, because neither Tia nor Ugo is entitled.","Tia, because she was treated as a child of the family.","Ugo, because Tia is not Sara's issue.","Tia and Ugo equally.","Tia, because she has a stronger moral claim than Ugo."],"a":2,"e":"The intestacy rules follow **blood relationship and adoption**. A **stepchild** who was not adopted is **not** the intestate's issue and has no entitlement, however close the relationship. Sara's brother Ugo therefore takes the estate. Tia could, however, apply under the **Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975** as a person treated as a **child of the family**."},
 {"t":"outside-estate","q":"Vinny died in 2026. Many years ago he took out a life insurance policy on his own life and wrote it in trust for his two children. The policy has paid out £200,000. His will leaves everything to his second wife.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the policy money?","o":["It passes under Vinny's will to his second wife, because the policy was on his own life.","It is paid to Vinny's PRs, who must hold it on trust for the children.","It is paid to the trustees for the children, outside the estate.","It is shared between the children and the second wife under the intestacy rules.","It is paid to the children only after probate of Vinny's will is granted."],"a":2,"e":"A life policy **written in trust** belongs to the **trustees** from the outset, for the beneficiaries of the trust. On death the proceeds are paid to the trustees on production of a death certificate, **outside the estate**, so they do not pass under the will and **no grant** is needed. The proceeds are also outside the deceased's estate for inheritance tax."},
 {"t":"outside-estate","q":"Wren died in 2026. She had a life policy on her own life, not written in trust, which pays £150,000 to her estate. Her will leaves the residue of her estate to her nephew. Her husband asks whether the insurer can pay the money directly to him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the policy money?","o":["It passes outside the estate by survivorship to her husband.","It is paid to the nephew directly by the insurer without any grant.","It passes to her husband on intestacy, because life policies are not covered by a will.","It is paid directly to her husband, because a life policy always passes to the surviving spouse.","It is part of Wren's estate, passes under her will and needs a grant."],"a":4,"e":"A life policy on the deceased's own life that is **not written in trust** is payable to the **estate**. The proceeds form part of the estate, pass under the **will** (here into residue for the nephew), and the insurer will normally require a **grant** before paying. The proceeds are also part of the estate for inheritance tax."},
 {"t":"outside-estate","q":"Xavier died in 2026, a member of his employer's pension scheme. The scheme rules give the trustees discretion to pay a death-in-service lump sum to any of a wide class of beneficiaries. Xavier completed an expression of wishes form naming his partner, Yan. His will leaves everything to his children.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who receives the lump sum?","o":["The scheme trustees decide, guided but not bound by Yan's nomination.","Xavier's PRs receive it and must distribute it under the intestacy rules.","The children and Yan share it equally.","The children receive it under Xavier's will.","Yan receives it automatically under the expression of wishes form."],"a":0,"e":"Discretionary pension death benefits are paid by the **scheme trustees** at their discretion, so they pass **outside the estate**: the will does not govern them and no grant is needed. An **expression of wishes** (nomination) **guides** the trustees but is **not binding** on them. If the scheme rules made the benefit payable to the estate, it would pass under the will."},
 {"t":"outside-estate","q":"Zainab died in March 2026. She had £400,000 in a personal pension, which the scheme administrator may pay at its discretion to any of her family. She has not drawn any of it. Her executors are preparing the IHT account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the IHT treatment of the pension fund?","o":["It is taxed as a lifetime chargeable transfer made at her death.","It is part of her estate for IHT, because all pension funds are taxable on death.","It is part of her estate for IHT, because it was not written in trust.","It is generally outside her estate for IHT, because she died before 6 April 2027.","It is outside her estate for IHT only if it is paid to her spouse."],"a":3,"e":"For deaths **before 6 April 2027**, discretionary pension death benefits are generally **outside** the deceased's estate for IHT. The **Finance Act 2026** brings most **unused pension funds and death benefits** into the IHT estate for deaths **on or after 6 April 2027** (with exceptions, for example death-in-service benefits). Zainab died in March 2026, so the old rules apply."},
 {"t":"outside-estate","q":"Ada and her husband Ben had a joint bank account. Ada died in 2026, leaving a will giving everything to her daughter from a previous marriage. The joint account holds £30,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what happens to the joint account?","o":["It passes to Ada's daughter under the will.","It passes to Ben by survivorship, outside Ada's will.","Half passes to Ben by survivorship and half to Ada's daughter under the will.","It is frozen until a grant of probate is obtained and then passes under the will.","It passes to Ben only if he applies for letters of administration."],"a":1,"e":"A joint account held by spouses is normally held **jointly**, so on the death of one holder the balance passes to the survivor by **survivorship**, outside the will. The bank will usually release it to the survivor on production of the **death certificate**, without a grant. Ada's half share is still part of her estate for **IHT** (but exempt as passing to her spouse)."},
 {"t":"outside-estate","q":"Cleo and her brother Dan bought a house as tenants in common in equal shares. Cleo has died, and her will leaves her estate to her son Eli. Dan assumes the whole house is now his.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what happens to Cleo's share?","o":["It passes to Dan, because a will cannot pass an interest in co-owned land.","It passes under her will to Eli, because there is no survivorship.","It passes on intestacy, because Cleo's share is outside her estate.","It is divided between Dan and Eli equally.","It passes to Dan by survivorship, because co-owners of land always take by survivorship."],"a":1,"e":"Tenants in common each have a **distinct share** of the beneficial interest, and there is **no right of survivorship**. Cleo's half share therefore forms part of her **estate** and passes under her **will** to Eli. A grant will usually be needed so that her PRs can deal with her share. Dan, as surviving legal owner, holds the legal title on trust for himself and Cleo's estate."},
 {"t":"outside-estate","q":"Fern and Gus owned a house as beneficial joint tenants. After they separated, Fern served a written notice of severance on Gus, which he received. Fern died a month later, leaving a will giving everything to her mother.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what happens to Fern's interest in the house?","o":["Her share passes on intestacy, because severance revokes her will.","Gus and Fern's mother each take the whole house jointly.","Gus takes the whole house by survivorship, because a notice of severance is not effective until the house is sold.","Her half share passes under her will to her mother, because the notice severed the joint tenancy.","Gus takes the whole house, because Fern's will cannot override survivorship."],"a":3,"e":"A written **notice of severance** under LPA 1925 s.36(2) converts a beneficial joint tenancy into a **tenancy in common**. Once severed, there is no survivorship: Fern's **half share** forms part of her **estate** and passes under her **will**. (A will by itself cannot sever a joint tenancy, but here severance happened during her lifetime.)"},
 {"t":"outside-estate","q":"Hari was in hospital, knowing he was dying of cancer. He handed his nephew Ivan the keys and logbook of his vintage car, saying, 'If I don't come out of hospital, the car is yours.' Hari died a week later. His will leaves everything to charity.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to the car?","o":["Ivan is likely to be entitled under a donatio mortis causa.","Ivan and the charity equally.","The charity, because a gift of property on death must be made by will.","The charity, because the gift was conditional and so was never completed.","Ivan, but only if he can show that Hari signed a written transfer of the car."],"a":0,"e":"A **donatio mortis causa** (gift in contemplation of death) is valid where the donor makes the gift in **contemplation of imminent death**, it is **conditional on death**, and the donor **parts with dominion** over the property (for example by handing over the keys and documents). It takes effect on death **outside the will**. The courts apply the requirements strictly, but these facts satisfy them. It remains part of the estate for IHT."},
 {"t":"outside-estate","q":"Jade died in 2026, leaving her estate to her son. She owned a holiday cottage with her sister as beneficial joint tenants. The cottage passes to her sister by survivorship. The executors are preparing the IHT account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the treatment of Jade's interest in the cottage for IHT?","o":["It is excluded from her estate for IHT, because it passes outside her will.","It is excluded from her estate for IHT, because it passes to a close relative.","The whole value of the cottage is included in her estate for IHT.","It is included only if her sister agrees to pay the tax.","Her share of the cottage is included in her estate for IHT."],"a":4,"e":"Property passing by **survivorship** is outside the estate for **succession** purposes, but the deceased's **share** of joint property is part of her estate for **inheritance tax** (IHTA 1984 s.5). Jade's half share is therefore included (with a possible discount for co-owned land, as the co-owner is not her spouse). The sister, not the residuary estate, normally bears the tax on it."},
 {"t":"outside-estate","q":"Kofi died in 2026. Many years ago he made a statutory nomination of £4,000 held in a friendly society account in favour of his niece. His will leaves his entire estate to his brother.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what happens to the friendly society money?","o":["It is paid to the niece and is outside Kofi's estate for both succession and IHT.","It passes to the brother because a nomination is revoked by death.","It is paid to the niece, but is included in Kofi's estate for IHT.","It must be paid to Kofi's PRs, who will decide whether to honour the nomination.","It passes under the will to Kofi's brother, because a will overrides an earlier nomination."],"a":2,"e":"Some **statutory nominations** survive, for example of up to £5,000 in certain friendly societies. The nominated sum is paid **directly to the nominee**, **outside the will**, and the will cannot override it. However, the nominated money is still part of the deceased's estate for **inheritance tax**."},
 {"t":"grants","q":"Lena died intestate in 2026. She never married and had no children. She is survived by her mother and her sister. Her mother is willing to act.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who has the first right to a grant of representation?","o":["Her mother, who is entitled to a grant of probate.","Her sister, because a parent is too elderly to act.","Her sister, because siblings rank ahead of parents for a grant.","Her mother and sister jointly, because both are close relatives.","Her mother, who is entitled to letters of administration."],"a":4,"e":"Under NCPR **r.22**, the order of priority for a grant of **letters of administration** on intestacy follows entitlement: spouse or civil partner, children (or their issue), **parents**, then brothers and sisters of the whole blood. A person must have a **beneficial interest** to apply. The mother takes the whole estate, so she has the right to the grant; the sister has no interest. Probate is available only to executors."},
 {"t":"grants","q":"Mike's will appoints his friend Ned as sole executor and leaves a legacy of £5,000 to his cousin Ola and the residue to his partner, Pat. Ned died before Mike. Pat survived Mike but died two months later, before any grant was obtained. Pat's executor, Quinn, and Ola both wish to apply for the grant.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who has priority?","o":["Quinn, because the PR of a residuary beneficiary ranks above Ola.","Neither, because the will must now be treated as an intestacy.","Ola, because a living beneficiary always ranks ahead of the PR of a deceased beneficiary.","Ola and Quinn equally, because both represent people who benefit under the will.","Quinn, who can apply for probate of Mike's will."],"a":0,"e":"Where there is a will but no proving executor, priority follows NCPR **r.20**: (a) executor; (b) residuary beneficiary holding in trust; (c) other residuary beneficiaries; (d) the **PR of a residuary beneficiary**; (e) **other legatees** or creditors; (f) the PR of such a legatee or creditor. Quinn, in class (d), ranks ahead of Ola in class (e). The grant will be **letters of administration with will annexed**, as probate is only for executors."},
 {"t":"grants","q":"Rory died in 2026, leaving a will. His assets were: his home, owned with his wife as beneficial joint tenants; a joint bank account with his wife; his car, furniture and personal belongings; and £2,500 in a building society account, which the society will release on a death certificate. His wife is the sole beneficiary.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether a grant is needed?","o":["A grant of probate is needed, because Rory left a will.","A grant is needed to transfer the home into his wife's sole name.","No grant is needed, because none of the assets requires one.","A grant is needed, because the total value of his assets exceeds £5,000.","A grant is needed only for the joint bank account."],"a":2,"e":"A **grant** is needed only for assets that cannot otherwise be collected. The home and joint account pass to the wife by **survivorship** (she can register the death certificate at HM Land Registry for the house). **Personal chattels** can be handed over, and small balances can be released without a grant, under the Administration of Estates (Small Payments) Act 1965 limit of £5,000 or the institution's own limits. The existence of a will does not in itself require a grant."},
 {"t":"grants","q":"Sian's will appoints her brother Tom as sole executor. It leaves her estate on trust for her two children, aged 8 and 11, until they reach 25. Tom wishes to apply for the grant alone.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Tom can take letters of administration alone, but not probate.","Tom cannot act at all, because a trust for minors requires a trust corporation.","Tom can take probate only if the children's other parent consents.","Tom can take probate alone, because he is a sole executor.","Tom cannot take a grant alone, because there is a minority interest, so a second PR must join him."],"a":3,"e":"Under Senior Courts Act 1981 **s.114(2)**, where a **minority** or **life interest** arises, a grant of **administration** must normally be made to **at least two** individuals or a trust corporation. That rule does not apply to **probate**: a **sole executor** can always take probate, even where the will creates trusts for minors."},
 {"t":"grants","q":"Una died in 2026, domiciled and resident in England. Her gross estate is worth £1.8 million. She made no lifetime gifts and had no interest in any trust. After paying debts, she leaves everything to her husband, except a legacy of £100,000 to her nephew.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether her PRs must deliver a full IHT account (IHT400)?","o":["No, because estates passing mainly to a spouse never need any IHT reporting.","No, because it is an exempt excepted estate.","Yes, because the estate exceeds £1 million.","Yes, because the gross value of the estate is more than the nil rate band of £325,000.","Yes, because any estate that includes a gift to someone other than a spouse needs an IHT400."],"a":1,"e":"An **exempt excepted estate** is one with a **gross value of no more than £3 million** where, after deducting debts and only the **spouse/civil partner** and **charity** exemptions, the **net chargeable estate** is within the **nil rate band**. Here the chargeable estate is the £100,000 legacy, within £325,000. The estate is excepted, so no IHT400 is needed; the values are given in the probate application."},
 {"t":"grants","q":"Vic died in 2026, unmarried, leaving everything to his niece. His sole-name assets are worth £250,000. He also owned a house jointly with his brother as beneficial joint tenants; his half share is worth £150,000. He made no lifetime gifts.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Vic's estate is a low value excepted estate?","o":["It is not, because his share of joint property counts towards the limit.","It is, because the estate passes to a relative.","It is, because only the £250,000 passing under his will is counted, which is within the nil rate band.","It is, because property passing by survivorship is ignored for IHT.","It is not, because any estate containing land requires a full IHT account."],"a":0,"e":"For a **low value** excepted estate, the **gross value** of the estate must not exceed the **nil rate band** (£325,000), and the gross value **includes** the deceased's share of **joint property** passing by survivorship, as well as specified lifetime gifts. Vic's gross estate is £400,000, so it is not a low value excepted estate, and (as no exemption applies) the PRs must deliver an **IHT400**."},
 {"t":"grants","q":"Will died in 2026. He and his wife each owned 30% of the shares in a private company, Wilco Ltd. The rest is owned by unrelated investors. A 30% holding, valued alone, is worth much less per share than a controlling holding. Will's executors are valuing his shares for IHT.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Will's shares are valued?","o":["As a minority 30% holding valued on its own, because each spouse's shares are separate property.","At the price Will paid for them.","As a proportion of the combined 60% holding, under the related property rules.","At nil, because shares passing to a spouse are exempt from valuation.","As half of the value of the whole company, because spouses are treated as owning everything jointly."],"a":2,"e":"Under IHTA 1984 **s.161**, **related property**, including property owned by the deceased's **spouse or civil partner**, is taken into account in valuation. Will's 30% is valued as a proportion of the combined **60% controlling holding**, which is worth more per share. If the shares pass to the spouse, the spouse exemption will still apply, but the valuation rule matters for any non-exempt part."},
 {"t":"grants","q":"Xena died in 2026. She owned a house as tenant in common in equal shares with her brother, who lives there. The whole house is worth £600,000. Xena's will leaves her share to her daughter. Her executors are valuing her share for IHT.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely value of Xena's share?","o":["£600,000, because the whole value of co-owned land is included in each co-owner's estate.","Nil, because her brother is in occupation.","More than £300,000, because a share in a home has a premium value.","Exactly £300,000, because a half share is always worth half the value of the whole.","Less than £300,000, because a share of co-owned land is usually discounted."],"a":4,"e":"Assets are valued at **open market value** immediately before death (IHTA s.160). A **share of co-owned land** is hard to sell, especially with a co-owner in occupation, so where the co-owner is **not a spouse**, a **discount** (commonly 10-15%) is usually allowed. Where land is co-owned with a spouse, the related property rules mean no such discount is available."},
 {"t":"grants","q":"Yusuf has just died. His son Zaid believes his father lacked capacity when he made his last will, which leaves everything to a neighbour, and wants to stop the executor obtaining probate while he investigates.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate immediate step for Zaid?","o":["Write to the executor asking him not to apply for probate, which binds the registry.","Enter a caveat at the Probate Registry to prevent a grant being issued.","Ask the bank to freeze Yusuf's accounts, which prevents probate being granted.","Apply for letters of administration himself, which will take priority over the executor.","Bring a claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975."],"a":1,"e":"A person who wishes to challenge a will can enter a **caveat** at the Probate Registry. It prevents a **grant** being issued for **six months** (renewable), giving time to investigate and, if appropriate, begin a probate claim. A 1975 Act claim seeks provision from the estate rather than challenging the will's validity, and a letter to the executor does not bind the registry."},
 {"t":"grants","q":"Ali's will, which is valid, leaves his estate to his two adult sons equally. The will does not appoint any executor. Both sons wish to apply for a grant.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the grant they should obtain?","o":["No grant, because the sons are the only beneficiaries.","Probate, because the will is valid.","Letters of administration on intestacy, because there is no executor.","Letters of administration with will annexed.","Probate, but only if the court first appoints them as executors."],"a":3,"e":"Where there is a valid will but **no executor** is appointed (or none is able and willing to act), the grant is **letters of administration with will annexed**. Priority is decided by NCPR r.20, under which **residuary beneficiaries** come after executors. The will still governs who inherits. **Probate** is granted only to executors, and the estate is not an intestacy."},
 {"t":"iht-funding-incidence","q":"Bryony died on 14 March 2026. IHT is payable on her estate. Her executors ask when the tax must be paid to avoid interest.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the due date for the IHT on her death estate?","o":["14 September 2026, six months after the date of death.","31 March 2027, twelve months after the end of the month of death.","31 January 2027, the normal self-assessment date.","30 September 2026, six months after the end of the month of death.","Whenever the executors apply for the grant, whenever that is."],"a":3,"e":"IHT on the death estate is **due six months after the end of the month of death** (IHTA 1984 s.226). Bryony died in March, so the tax is due on **30 September 2026**, and **interest** runs on unpaid tax after that. The IHT400 account must be delivered within 12 months of the end of the month of death, but in practice the tax on non-instalment property is paid before the grant."},
 {"t":"iht-funding-incidence","q":"Caleb died in 2026. His estate includes his home, which does not qualify for any relief, and on which IHT of £120,000 is payable. His executors elect to pay this tax by instalments.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The tax on the home cannot be paid by instalments, because only business property qualifies.","It can be paid in 10 annual instalments, with interest on the outstanding balance.","The whole £120,000 must be paid before the grant, and instalments begin only afterwards.","The tax can be paid in 5 annual instalments, with interest only on late instalments.","The tax can be paid in 10 annual instalments free of interest, because land always qualifies for interest-free instalments."],"a":1,"e":"**Land** (including the deceased's home) is **instalment property**, so the IHT can be paid in **10 equal annual instalments**, starting six months after the end of the month of death, and need not be paid before the grant. Instalments are **interest-free** only for property qualifying for **APR or BPR** (and some shareholdings). For other land, **interest** runs on the **whole outstanding balance**."},
 {"t":"iht-funding-incidence","q":"Della died in 2026. IHT of £90,000 is payable on her bank accounts and investments before her executors can obtain the grant. The executors have no funds of their own and cannot sell her assets without the grant. Her bank holds £250,000 in her sole name.\n\nWhich of the following is usually the most appropriate way to fund the tax?","o":["Wait until after the grant to pay the tax, as interest is the only consequence.","Ask HMRC to accept the tax after the estate has been distributed.","Take out a commercial bank loan, because the deceased's own money cannot be used until the grant.","Sell Della's house before the grant to raise the money.","Ask the bank to pay HMRC directly under the Direct Payment Scheme."],"a":4,"e":"Under the **Direct Payment Scheme**, most banks and building societies will transfer funds from the **deceased's own accounts directly to HMRC** before the grant (using form IHT423). It is usually the first choice because it avoids borrowing. Land cannot be sold before the grant, and the grant will not be issued until the tax on non-instalment property is paid."},
 {"t":"iht-funding-incidence","q":"Ewan died in 2026. Since 1999 he had been entitled to the income for life of a trust fund set up by his father's will in 1999, which passes on his death to his sister. The trust fund is worth £500,000. IHT is payable on Ewan's death in respect of the trust fund.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is liable to pay the IHT on the trust fund?","o":["The trustees of the trust, out of the trust fund.","HMRC cannot collect tax on the trust fund, because it does not pass under Ewan's will.","Ewan's PRs, out of his free estate.","Ewan's residuary beneficiaries, personally.","Ewan's sister personally, before she receives the fund."],"a":0,"e":"Property in which the deceased had a **qualifying interest in possession** (such as a life interest under a pre-2006 will trust) is part of his estate for IHT. Liability for the tax on that property falls on the **trustees**, who pay it out of the **trust fund**, and the fund bears its own tax. The PRs are liable for the tax on the free estate."},
 {"t":"iht-funding-incidence","q":"Fatima died in 2026. She owned a flat jointly with her brother as beneficial joint tenants, and it passes to him by survivorship. Her will leaves her residuary estate to her son. IHT is payable on both the flat share and the free estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who bears the IHT attributable to Fatima's share of the flat?","o":["Her son, because the residuary beneficiary bears all the IHT on the estate.","Fatima's PRs, out of the free estate, with no recovery from her brother.","Her brother, because property passing by survivorship bears its own tax.","Her brother and son equally.","No one, because property passing by survivorship is not taxable."],"a":2,"e":"IHT on the **UK free estate** is borne by residue under IHTA s.211, but that rule applies only to property vesting in the **PRs**. Property **outside the free estate**, such as a share of joint property passing by **survivorship**, **bears its own tax**. The PRs and the surviving joint tenant are both liable to HMRC, but in the end the **brother** bears the tax attributable to the flat share."},
 {"t":"iht-funding-incidence","q":"Gethin's will leaves his house to his nephew Huw 'subject to inheritance tax', £50,000 to his friend free of tax, and the residue to his daughter. IHT is payable on the estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who bears the tax attributable to the house?","o":["The friend and the daughter in proportion to their gifts.","Gethin's PRs personally.","Huw, because the will provides that the gift of the house bears its own tax.","Huw and the daughter equally.","Gethin's daughter, because tax on UK property is always paid out of residue."],"a":2,"e":"By default (IHTA s.211), IHT on UK free estate is paid out of **residue**, so specific and pecuniary gifts are received **free of tax**. But the will can provide otherwise. A gift '**subject to tax**' bears its **own rateable share** of the IHT, so Huw bears the tax on the house. The friend's legacy is free of tax, and the daughter bears the tax on the rest."},
 {"t":"iht-funding-incidence","q":"Four years before his death in 2026, Ifan gave £400,000 to the trustees of a discretionary trust, which was a lifetime chargeable transfer on which the trustees paid lifetime IHT. Because of Ifan's death within seven years, additional IHT is now payable on the gift.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is primarily liable for the additional tax?","o":["The beneficiaries of the discretionary trust personally.","No one, because tax has already been paid on the gift.","Ifan's PRs, out of his residuary estate.","Ifan's residuary beneficiaries, personally.","The trustees of the discretionary trust, as the donees."],"a":4,"e":"On death within seven years of a **lifetime chargeable transfer**, the tax is recalculated at death rates (with **taper relief** and **credit** for lifetime tax paid). The **donee**, here the **trustees** of the discretionary trust, is **primarily liable** for any additional tax. The PRs become liable only if the tax is unpaid 12 months after the end of the month of death."},
 {"t":"iht-funding-incidence","q":"Jess died in 2026. Her executors elected to pay the IHT on her house by 10 annual instalments. After paying the first instalment, they sold the house in order to distribute the proceeds.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the sale on the outstanding tax?","o":["All the outstanding tax on the house becomes payable immediately.","The outstanding tax must be paid by the buyer of the house.","The executors can continue to pay the remaining instalments as before.","The outstanding tax is cancelled, because the house is no longer in the estate.","The outstanding tax becomes payable only when the last beneficiary receives the proceeds."],"a":0,"e":"The instalment option is designed to avoid forcing a sale of illiquid assets. If **instalment property is sold**, the reason for the option disappears, and **all outstanding tax** on it (with any interest) becomes **payable immediately** (IHTA s.227(4))."},
 {"t":"iht-funding-incidence","q":"Kian died in 2026. IHT of £60,000 is payable before the grant. His bank accounts are not covered by the Direct Payment Scheme. His daughter Lily, the residuary beneficiary, has just received £100,000 from a life policy that Kian wrote in trust for her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Lily's policy money can help?","o":["The policy money must be paid to the PRs, because a life policy belongs to the estate.","Lily cannot use the money until the grant is obtained.","The policy money must be used to pay the IHT, because HMRC has a charge over it.","Lily can lend or give the policy money to the PRs to pay the IHT.","The policy money is used to reduce the IHT, because it is exempt from tax."],"a":3,"e":"A life policy **written in trust** is paid to the trustees (or beneficiary) on a death certificate, **outside the estate** and without a grant. Lily can therefore **lend or give** the money to the PRs to pay the IHT, and be repaid from the estate after the grant. The money does not belong to the estate, and HMRC has no claim over it."},
 {"t":"iht-funding-incidence","q":"In 2019 Mo gave his holiday cottage to his son Nat but continued to stay there, rent-free, for several months each year until his death in 2026. The cottage is treated as part of Mo's estate for IHT as a gift with reservation of benefit. Mo's will leaves his free estate to his daughter.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is primarily liable for the IHT on the cottage?","o":["Mo's daughter, as residuary beneficiary.","Nat, with Mo's PRs liable only if Nat does not pay.","No one, because the cottage was given away more than three years before death.","Nat and Mo's daughter equally.","Mo's PRs, out of the free estate, because the cottage is part of Mo's estate."],"a":1,"e":"Property subject to a **reservation of benefit** is treated as part of the donor's estate on death. The **donee** (Nat) is **primarily liable** for the IHT attributable to it, and the property bears its own tax. The PRs are liable only if the donee does not pay. Taper relief does not apply to property taxed as part of the death estate."},
 {"t":"administration","q":"Ola's executors placed notices under s.27 of the Trustee Act 1925 in The Gazette and a local newspaper, waited more than two months, made the usual searches and then distributed the estate. Three months later, a creditor, Pip, claims £8,000 that Ola owed him. The executors had not known of the debt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Pip's position?","o":["The executors must reopen the estate and pay Pip from their own funds, then recover from the beneficiaries.","The executors are protected, but Pip may recover from the beneficiaries.","Pip can claim only if he can show the executors acted dishonestly.","The executors are personally liable, because a PR must pay all the deceased's debts.","Pip has no remedy against anyone, because he did not respond to the notices."],"a":1,"e":"Under **Trustee Act 1925 s.27**, PRs who advertise for claims and wait at least **two months** may distribute having regard only to claims they know of, and are **not personally liable** to a creditor who comes forward later. The creditor is not barred entirely: he can **follow the assets** into the hands of the beneficiaries who received them."},
 {"t":"administration","q":"Quinn was the executor of his uncle's estate. He did not place any s.27 notices and, six months after the death, distributed the whole estate to the beneficiaries. A creditor has now appeared with a valid claim for £15,000 that Quinn did not know about.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Quinn's position?","o":["He is liable only if he was a professional executor.","He is not liable, because he did not know about the debt.","He is not liable, because he waited six months, which is longer than the s.27 notice period.","He is personally liable to the creditor for the £15,000.","He is not liable, because the creditor must claim from the beneficiaries instead."],"a":3,"e":"A PR who distributes the estate without paying all debts commits a **devastavit** and is **personally liable** to an unpaid creditor to the extent of the assets distributed. **Section 27 notices** protect a PR against unknown claims, but only if they were actually placed and the time limit has passed. Lack of knowledge is not itself a defence, although the court may relieve an honest and reasonable PR under s.61."},
 {"t":"administration","q":"Ria died on 1 February 2026. Her will leaves a legacy of £20,000 to her friend Sam. In June 2026 Sam asks the executors to pay the legacy immediately. There are enough assets, but the executors are still dealing with the house sale and tax.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sam's position?","o":["He cannot compel payment until 1 February 2027, when interest normally starts to run.","He can compel immediate payment, because there are enough assets in the estate.","He can compel payment six months after the grant of probate.","He cannot be paid until the whole estate has been administered, and no interest is ever due.","He is entitled to interest from the date of death on the legacy."],"a":0,"e":"Under AEA 1925 **s.44**, PRs **cannot be compelled to distribute** before the end of **one year from the death** (the **executor's year**). They may pay earlier if it is safe. If a **pecuniary legacy** is not paid by the **first anniversary** of death, it normally carries **interest** from that date unless the will says otherwise."},
 {"t":"administration","q":"Tia's solvent estate consists of £100,000 of assets after funeral and administration expenses. Her will leaves her car (worth £10,000) to her niece, pecuniary legacies totalling £60,000, and the residue to her son. Her debts are £50,000. The will says nothing about how debts are to be paid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the debts are paid?","o":["The legacies are used first, and the residue is used only if the legacies are exhausted.","The car is sold first, then the residue, and the legacies are paid in full.","The son must pay the debts personally because he is the residuary beneficiary.","The debts are paid equally from the car, the legacies and the residue.","Residue is used first, then the legacies abate, and the car passes intact."],"a":4,"e":"In a solvent estate, unless the will provides otherwise, assets are applied to pay debts in the statutory order (AEA 1925 Sch 1 Pt II): first any **undisposed property**, then **residue**, then the fund retained for **pecuniary legacies**, and only then property **specifically given**. Residue (£100,000 − £10,000 − £60,000 = £30,000) is used first, so the son takes nothing, and the remaining £20,000 comes from the legacies, which abate proportionately. The niece keeps the car."},
 {"t":"administration","q":"Uri, a lay executor, took legal advice and, relying on a reasonable but mistaken interpretation of an ambiguous clause in the will, paid a legacy of £10,000 to the wrong beneficiary. The money cannot now be recovered. The correct beneficiary is suing Uri.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Uri might avoid liability?","o":["By showing that the will was ambiguous, which automatically excuses a PR.","He cannot avoid liability, because a PR is strictly liable for paying the wrong person in every case.","By asking the court to relieve him because he acted honestly and reasonably.","By showing that he did not personally benefit from the mistake.","By relying on s.27 notices, which protect PRs against all claims by beneficiaries."],"a":2,"e":"Paying the wrong person is a **devastavit**, and the PR is liable. However, under **Trustee Act 1925 s.61**, the court may relieve a PR (or trustee) from liability wholly or partly if he acted **honestly and reasonably** and **ought fairly to be excused**. Acting on legal advice about a genuinely ambiguous clause supports relief. Section 27 notices protect against unknown creditors and claimants, not mistakes in distribution."},
 {"t":"administration","q":"Vanessa's will leaves her residue equally to her two children. Her estate includes a painting worth £40,000. Her daughter, Wyn, would like to receive the painting as part of her half share rather than have it sold. The will does not mention appropriation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The PRs can appropriate the painting to Wyn without her consent, because it is part of residue.","The PRs must sell the painting and distribute cash, because residue must be divided in money.","The PRs can give Wyn the painting only if her brother agrees to pay her the difference in value.","Wyn is entitled to the painting at its probate value, whatever its value now.","The PRs can appropriate the painting to Wyn towards her share, with her consent."],"a":4,"e":"Under AEA 1925 **s.41**, PRs may **appropriate** an asset to a beneficiary **in or towards satisfaction** of their entitlement, with the beneficiary's **consent** (unless the will dispenses with it). The asset is valued at the **date of appropriation**, not at probate value, so the division is fair. An appropriation is not a sale, so CGT is not triggered for the estate."},
 {"t":"administration","q":"Xan's will leaves his registered freehold flat to his niece Yana. The executors have paid all debts and IHT and want to transfer the flat to Yana.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the flat should be transferred?","o":["By a written assent, after which Yana registers as proprietor.","By a deed of gift executed by the executors in the presence of witnesses.","By a contract for sale between the executors and Yana, signed by both.","By delivering the title deeds to Yana, which transfers the legal title automatically.","No transfer is needed, because the flat vests in Yana automatically at Xan's death."],"a":0,"e":"PRs pass land to a beneficiary by an **assent** under AEA 1925 **s.36**, which must be **in writing** and signed by the PRs (a deed is not needed). For registered land, the beneficiary then applies to **register** as proprietor (using HM Land Registry form AS1). The land vests in the PRs on death, not automatically in the beneficiary."},
 {"t":"administration","q":"Zoe's will leaves £30,000 to her granddaughter Amy, aged 12. The will contains no provision about payment of legacies to minors. The executors are ready to distribute.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the executors should deal with Amy's legacy?","o":["Pay it to Amy's parents, who can always give a valid receipt for a child.","Pay it into court, because executors cannot hold money for a minor.","Hold it on trust until Amy is 18, or appoint trustees to hold it.","Pay it to Amy's school to be used for her education.","Pay it directly to Amy, because she is old enough to understand the gift."],"a":2,"e":"A **minor cannot give a valid receipt** for a legacy, so paying her would not discharge the executors. Unless the will allows payment to a parent or guardian, the PRs should **hold the legacy on trust** until she is 18, or **appoint trustees** to hold it under AEA 1925 **s.42**. Payment to the parents is good only if the will authorises it."},
 {"t":"administration","q":"Ben's estate is ready for distribution. Ben had personally guaranteed the lease of his company's shop, which still has eight years to run. The company is currently paying the rent, but its finances are uncertain. Ben's residuary beneficiaries want the whole estate distributed now.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the executors should protect themselves?","o":["Refuse to distribute anything until the lease ends in eight years.","Distribute now, because the guarantee ended on Ben's death.","Distribute now, because s.27 notices protect PRs against all future liabilities.","Retain a fund, or obtain indemnities or insurance, before distributing.","Distribute now, because a contingent liability is not a debt of the estate."],"a":3,"e":"A personal **guarantee** usually continues after death and is a **contingent liability** of the estate. Section 27 notices do not protect PRs from liabilities they **know** about. The PRs should **retain** a fund to meet the liability, or take **indemnities** from the beneficiaries or obtain **insurance** (or seek a court order), before distributing. Withholding everything for eight years would usually be unnecessary."},
 {"t":"administration","q":"Carl's executor distributed the estate in 2013 but wrongly left out Carl's daughter, Dee, who was entitled to a quarter of the residue. There was no fraud, and the executor no longer holds any estate property. Dee discovered this in 2026 and now wants to sue the executor.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["Her claim can proceed only against the other beneficiaries, not the executor.","Her claim is likely to be time-barred, because the 12-year limitation period has passed.","Her claim can proceed, because there is no limitation period for claims by beneficiaries.","Her claim can proceed, because time runs only from when she discovered the mistake.","Her claim is time-barred, because claims against PRs must be made within six months of the grant."],"a":1,"e":"Under the **Limitation Act 1980 s.22**, a claim to a **share of a deceased person's estate** must generally be brought within **12 years** of the right accruing. There is no time limit where there has been **fraud** or where the PR still holds estate property, but neither applies here. The six-month time limit applies to claims under the 1975 Act, not to beneficiaries' claims against PRs."},
 {"t":"family-provision","q":"Eli died in 2026, leaving his estate of £600,000 to a charity. His son, Finn, aged 38, is a healthy and successful accountant earning £120,000 a year, with no financial needs. He and Eli had been estranged for 15 years. Finn wants to claim under the Inheritance (Provision for Family and Dependants) Act 1975.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Finn's claim?","o":["He can apply as a child, but is unlikely to succeed because he has no need for maintenance.","He cannot apply, because adult children are not eligible applicants.","He is likely to succeed, because a child is entitled to a share of a parent's estate.","He is likely to succeed, because leaving everything to charity is unreasonable.","He can claim the same share he would have received on intestacy."],"a":0,"e":"A **child** of any age can apply (s.1(1)(c)), but only a spouse or civil partner receives provision on the higher standard. Others are limited to **reasonable financial provision for maintenance** (s.1(2)(b)), meaning what is needed to meet the costs of daily living. An adult, independent, well-off child with no need for maintenance is unlikely to succeed (Ilott v Blue Cross). A gift to charity is not unreasonable in itself."},
 {"t":"family-provision","q":"Gwen divorced her husband Hugh in 2016. The financial order on divorce included a clean break and an order under s.15 of the 1975 Act preventing either of them from applying under the Act on the other's death. Hugh died in 2026, leaving everything to his new partner. Gwen has not remarried and is in financial difficulty.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Gwen can claim?","o":["She can, but only on the higher standard applying to a spouse.","She can, because financial difficulty overrides any earlier order.","She cannot, because she is barred by the order made on the divorce.","She cannot, because a former spouse can never apply under the Act.","She can, because a former spouse who has not remarried is always an eligible applicant."],"a":2,"e":"A **former spouse** who has **not remarried** is an eligible applicant (s.1(1)(b)), limited to the maintenance standard. However, on divorce the court can make an order under **s.15** that a party may **not** apply under the 1975 Act on the other's death, as part of a **clean break**. Because such an order was made, Gwen is barred."},
 {"t":"family-provision","q":"Isaac and Jen lived together as a couple in the same household for five years until Isaac died in 2026. They were not married. Jen earned more than Isaac and was not financially dependent on him. Isaac's will leaves everything to his brother.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Jen's ability to apply under the 1975 Act?","o":["She cannot apply, because only spouses and children can claim.","She can apply only as a person being maintained by Isaac.","She cannot apply, because she was not financially dependent on Isaac.","She can apply on the same higher standard as a surviving spouse.","She can apply as a cohabitant, but only for maintenance."],"a":4,"e":"A **cohabitant** who lived in the **same household** as the deceased **as if a spouse or civil partner** for the whole of the **two years** immediately before death can apply under **s.1(1)(ba)**, whether or not she was dependent. Her claim is limited to the **maintenance standard**. Her financial position will be relevant to the merits under the s.3 factors, but not to eligibility."},
 {"t":"family-provision","q":"Karl married Lia when her son Max was six, and Karl treated Max as his own child, supporting him throughout his childhood. Max, now 17, still lives with his mother. Karl has died, leaving everything to his own brother. Max's mother cannot afford Max's further education.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Max's position?","o":["He can apply on the higher standard as a child of the family.","He can apply as a child of the family, for reasonable maintenance.","He cannot apply, because he is not Karl's biological or adopted child.","He can apply only if he was being maintained by Karl immediately before his death.","He is entitled to a share of the estate under the intestacy rules."],"a":1,"e":"Under **s.1(1)(d)**, a person **treated by the deceased as a child of the family** in relation to a marriage or civil partnership (or family) to which the deceased was a party can apply. Max qualifies as Karl's stepchild. His claim is on the **maintenance** standard, and the court will consider s.3 factors such as his education and Karl's assumption of responsibility for him."},
 {"t":"family-provision","q":"Nina died in July 2026. Her daughter, Opal, wishes to claim under the 1975 Act. No grant of representation has yet been obtained, and the executor is in no hurry to apply.\n\nWhich of the following best describes when Opal can make her claim?","o":["She must wait until a grant has been obtained, and then has six months to claim.","She must claim within six months of the date of death.","She must claim within 12 months of the date of death, whether or not there is a grant.","She can claim now, before any grant has been issued.","She can claim at any time within 12 years of the death."],"a":3,"e":"Under **s.4**, an application must be made within **six months of the date on which representation is first taken out**, unless the court gives permission for a late claim. Since 2014 an application can also be made **before** a grant is issued. The six-month period runs from the grant, not from death."},
 {"t":"family-provision","q":"Pat died in 2026. He owned his home with his new partner, Quin, as beneficial joint tenants, so it passed to Quin by survivorship. The rest of his estate is very small. Pat's 15-year-old daughter, Rhea, who lived with him, is applying under the 1975 Act within six months of the grant.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Pat's share of the home can be used to make provision for Rhea?","o":["It can be used only if Quin agrees.","The whole value of the home is automatically part of the net estate.","It can be used only if Pat intended to defeat Rhea's claim.","The court may treat Pat's severable share as part of his net estate.","It cannot be used, because property passing by survivorship is never part of the net estate."],"a":3,"e":"Under **s.9**, the court may order that the deceased's **severable share** of property held as a **joint tenant** be treated as part of the **net estate**, to such extent as is just, when it makes an order on the application. Since the Inheritance and Trustees' Powers Act 2014 there is no separate six-month limit for s.9. This allows provision to be made from property that passed by survivorship. Intention to defeat a claim is relevant to the anti-avoidance provisions, not s.9."},
 {"t":"family-provision","q":"Sami died in 2026, domiciled in Scotland, where he had lived for 30 years. He owned a holiday home in Cornwall. His will leaves everything to charity. His adult son, who lives in England and is in financial need, wants to claim under the 1975 Act.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the son can claim?","o":["He can claim, but only against the holiday home.","He cannot, because Sami was not domiciled in England and Wales.","He can claim, because the will leaves everything to charity.","He can claim, because the holiday home is in England.","He can claim, because he lives in England."],"a":1,"e":"The 1975 Act applies only where the deceased **died domiciled in England and Wales** (s.1(1)). Sami died domiciled in **Scotland**, so no claim can be made under the Act, even though he owned property in England and the applicant lives there. Scottish law has its own rules on family claims."},
 {"t":"family-provision","q":"Tara, aged 14, lives with her mother. Her father, Ugo, who paid substantial child maintenance, has died, leaving his whole estate to his new wife. Tara's application under the 1975 Act is likely to succeed. The court wants to provide for her living and education costs until she finishes school.\n\nWhich of the following orders is most suitable?","o":["An order giving Tara the share she would have received on intestacy.","An order transferring the whole estate to Tara.","An order setting aside Ugo's will.","An order requiring Ugo's widow to pay Tara from her own income for life.","Periodical payments until Tara completes her education."],"a":4,"e":"Under **s.2**, the court can order **periodical payments**, a **lump sum**, a **transfer** or **settlement** of property, and other orders, out of the **net estate**. Periodical payments for a fixed period, such as until the end of education, suit a minor child's maintenance needs. The court does not set aside the will, cannot order the widow to pay from her own resources, and is not bound by intestacy shares."},
 {"t":"family-provision","q":"Vera died in 2026, leaving her estate to a cats' charity. Her daughter, Wendy, aged 50, is disabled, cannot work and lived with and was supported by Vera until her death. Vera left a signed letter explaining that she had excluded Wendy because she believed the state should support her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will approach Wendy's claim?","o":["It will decide objectively whether reasonable provision was made, weighing the s.3 factors.","It must dismiss the claim, because Vera clearly explained her reasons for excluding Wendy.","It must uphold the claim, because a disabled child is always entitled to provision.","It will award Wendy half of the estate, because she was dependent on Vera.","It will consider only whether Vera acted unreasonably in making her will."],"a":0,"e":"The question is **objective**: whether the will makes **reasonable financial provision** for the applicant, not whether the testator acted unreasonably. The court considers the **s.3 factors**, including the applicant's financial needs and resources, any disability, obligations the deceased had to the applicant, and the size of the estate. The deceased's **reasons**, including a letter, are relevant but **not conclusive**. There is no fixed share."},
 {"t":"family-provision","q":"Xander, knowing he had a terminal illness, gave £300,000 to his friend Yves eight months before his death, with the intention of reducing the estate available to his wife Zara, from whom he was separated. His will leaves the small remaining estate to Yves. Zara has applied under the 1975 Act.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's powers regarding the £300,000?","o":["It can set the gift aside only if Yves knew of Xander's intention.","It can recover the £300,000 only if the gift was made within one month of death.","It may order Yves to provide money or property for Zara's provision.","It can only reduce Zara's claim, because the estate is now small.","It cannot touch the £300,000, because it was given away before Xander's death."],"a":2,"e":"Under the **anti-avoidance** provisions (**s.10**), the court can order the recipient of a **disposition** made within **six years** before death, with the **intention of defeating** an application under the Act, to provide money or property for the applicant. The donee's knowledge is not a requirement, although the court considers all the circumstances. The gift to Yves can therefore be reached."},
 {"t":"iht-lifetime","q":"In 2017 Ava gave £500,000 in cash to her son Bo. She had made no other gifts. Ava died in October 2026, more than seven years later.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the IHT position on the gift?","o":["The gift is added back to Ava's death estate and taxed at 40%.","IHT is payable by Bo at 20% on the amount above the nil rate band.","No IHT is payable, because Ava survived the gift by seven years.","IHT is payable at 40% on the amount above the nil rate band, reduced by taper relief.","IHT was payable at 20% when the gift was made, with no further tax on death."],"a":2,"e":"An outright gift to an individual is a **potentially exempt transfer (PET)**. No tax is due when it is made, and if the donor **survives seven years**, it becomes **fully exempt** and is not cumulated with the death estate (IHTA 1984 s.3A). Ava survived more than seven years, so the gift drops out of account entirely."},
 {"t":"iht-lifetime","q":"In March 2021 Cerys gave £431,000 to her daughter. She had made no other lifetime gifts, and her annual exemptions for 2020/21 and 2019/20 were unused. Cerys died in May 2026, a little over five years later. The nil rate band at death is £325,000.\n\nHow much IHT is payable on the gift?","o":["Nil.","£40,000.","£24,000.","£42,400.","£16,000."],"a":4,"e":"The gift is a **PET** that **fails** because Cerys died within seven years. Deduct two **annual exemptions** (£3,000 for 2020/21 and £3,000 carried forward from 2019/20): £431,000 − £6,000 = £425,000. Deduct the **nil rate band** of £325,000, leaving £100,000 × 40% = £40,000. Death was **5-6 years** after the gift, so **taper relief** reduces the tax to **40%** of the full amount: **£16,000**. The daughter pays it."},
 {"t":"iht-lifetime","q":"In 2021 Dylan gave £200,000 to his nephew. It was his only lifetime gift, and his annual exemptions had already been used. Dylan died in 2026, five years later, leaving his estate of £600,000 to his niece.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the failed gift?","o":["No tax is payable on the gift, but it uses £200,000 of the nil rate band.","IHT is payable on the gift at 40%, reduced by taper relief.","The gift is ignored, because taper relief reduces its value to nil after five years.","The gift reduces the nil rate band available to the estate by only 40% of its value, because of taper relief.","The gift is exempt, because it was made more than three years before death."],"a":0,"e":"A failed PET is **cumulated** at death. The £200,000 gift falls within the nil rate band, so **no tax** is payable on it. **Taper relief** reduces only the **tax** on a gift, not its **value**, so the full £200,000 uses up the nil rate band, leaving £125,000 for the death estate. Gifts within three years of death are not specially exempt."},
 {"t":"iht-lifetime","q":"Elsa earns a high salary and lives comfortably on less than half of it. For the last six years, she has paid her grandson's school fees of £20,000 a year directly from her salary account, under a standing commitment, without affecting her standard of living.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the IHT treatment of the payments?","o":["They are exempt only up to the annual exemption of £3,000 each year.","They are lifetime chargeable transfers taxed at 20%.","They are exempt only because they are paid for a grandchild's education.","They are exempt as normal expenditure out of income.","They are potentially exempt transfers that become exempt only after seven years."],"a":3,"e":"The **normal expenditure out of income** exemption (IHTA s.21) applies to gifts that are part of the donor's **normal (habitual) expenditure**, made out of **income** (not capital), and leave her with enough income to keep her **usual standard of living**. Regular school fees paid from surplus salary qualify, with no upper limit. There is no separate exemption for educational payments as such."},
 {"t":"iht-lifetime","q":"Faye gave £150,000 to Gary, her partner of 20 years, with whom she lives. They have never married or formed a civil partnership. She has made no other gifts and has used her annual exemptions. She is still alive.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the IHT treatment of the gift to Gary?","o":["It is exempt, because it is less than the nil rate band.","It is a potentially exempt transfer, because they are not married.","It is exempt, because Gary is a close family member.","It is exempt, because gifts between long-term partners are treated like gifts between spouses.","It is a lifetime chargeable transfer taxed at 20%."],"a":1,"e":"The **spouse and civil partner exemption** (IHTA s.18) applies only to people who are **married** or in a **civil partnership**. A gift to a **cohabitant** is an ordinary gift to an individual, so it is a **potentially exempt transfer**: no tax now, but it will be taxed if Faye dies within seven years. Being within the nil rate band does not make a gift exempt."},
 {"t":"iht-lifetime","q":"In June 2026 Hamid, who has made no previous lifetime gifts and has already used his annual exemptions for this and last year, gives £375,000 to the trustees of a discretionary trust. The trustees agree to pay any IHT due. The nil rate band is £325,000.\n\nHow much lifetime IHT is payable?","o":["Nil.","£10,000.","£12,500.","£20,000.","£150,000."],"a":1,"e":"A gift into a **discretionary trust** is a **lifetime chargeable transfer (LCT)**, taxed at the **lifetime rate of 20%** on the amount above the available nil rate band. Because the **trustees** pay, there is no grossing up: (£375,000 − £325,000) × 20% = **£10,000**. (If Hamid paid, the transfer would be grossed up, giving £12,500.)"},
 {"t":"iht-lifetime","q":"In 2021 Isla gave £200,000 to a discretionary trust (an LCT). In August 2026 she gave a further £300,000 to another discretionary trust, with the trustees paying the tax. Her annual exemptions for all years have already been used. The nil rate band is £325,000.\n\nHow much lifetime IHT is payable on the 2026 gift?","o":["Nil.","£60,000.","£43,750.","£35,000.","£70,000."],"a":3,"e":"Lifetime chargeable transfers are **cumulated** with other chargeable transfers in the previous **seven years**. The 2021 LCT of £200,000 uses part of the nil rate band, leaving £125,000. The 2026 gift of £300,000 therefore has £175,000 above the available nil rate band, taxed at the lifetime rate of **20%**: **£35,000**, payable by the trustees with no grossing up."},
 {"t":"iht-lifetime","q":"Jasper owns 51% of the shares in a private investment company (which does not qualify for business relief). A 51% holding is worth £510,000, and a 49% holding is worth £290,000. Jasper gives a 2% holding, worth £10,000 on its own, to his son.\n\nWhat is the value transferred for IHT purposes?","o":["£220,000, being the fall in value of Jasper's estate.","£20,000, being 2% of the value of the company.","Nil, because a gift to a child is exempt.","£510,000, being the value of the whole controlling holding.","£10,000, being the value of the shares in the son's hands."],"a":0,"e":"A **transfer of value** is measured by the **loss to the donor's estate** (IHTA s.3), not by the value received by the donee. By giving away 2%, Jasper loses **control**: his holding falls from £510,000 to £290,000, a loss of **£220,000**. That is the value transferred, which will be a PET as the gift is to an individual."},
 {"t":"iht-lifetime","q":"Kemi needed money quickly and sold her vintage car to a dealer she had never met before for £15,000. A month later, she discovered it was worth £25,000. The sale was negotiated at arm's length, and Kemi had no intention of conferring any benefit on the dealer.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the IHT position?","o":["There is a transfer of value of £25,000.","There is a transfer of value of £10,000, but it is covered by the annual exemption.","There is a transfer of value of £10,000, which is a potentially exempt transfer.","There is a lifetime chargeable transfer of £10,000.","There is no transfer of value, because Kemi had no gratuitous intent."],"a":4,"e":"Under IHTA 1984 **s.10**, a disposition is **not** a transfer of value if it was **not intended to confer any gratuitous benefit** and was made in a transaction at **arm's length** between unconnected persons (or was such as might be expected in such a transaction). A bad bargain with a stranger is therefore not a transfer of value, even though Kemi's estate fell by £10,000."},
 {"t":"iht-lifetime","q":"In 2020 Leon gave his holiday cottage to his daughter, Mia. Since the gift, Leon has stayed at the cottage for three weeks every summer, paying Mia a full market rent for each stay under a formal agreement. Leon died in 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the IHT treatment of the cottage?","o":["It is a lifetime chargeable transfer taxed at 20%.","It is part of Leon's estate as a gift with reservation of benefit, because he continued to use the cottage.","It is not reserved property, because Leon paid full rent, so it is a failed PET.","It is completely exempt, because Leon paid rent to Mia.","It is part of Leon's estate, because gifts of land to children are always treated as reserved."],"a":2,"e":"A **gift with reservation of benefit** arises where the donor gives property away but continues to **benefit** from it. There is an exception where the donor's use is for **full consideration** (such as a market rent). Leon paid a full market rent, so there is no reservation. The gift was a **PET**, which **failed** because he died within seven years, so it is taxed as a failed PET (with taper relief available because it was made more than three years before death)."},
 {"t":"iht-death-reliefs","q":"Nell died in 2026, a widow whose husband's nil rate bands were fully used on his death. She made no lifetime gifts. Her net estate (after debts, before reliefs and exemptions) is £2,200,000, including her home worth £700,000, which she leaves to her children.\n\nWhat residence nil rate band (RNRB) is available to her estate?","o":["£175,000.","Nil.","£100,000.","£350,000.","£75,000."],"a":4,"e":"The RNRB is **£175,000**, but it is **tapered** by **£1 for every £2** by which the **net estate** exceeds **£2 million**. Nell's estate exceeds £2 million by £200,000, so the RNRB is reduced by £100,000, leaving **£75,000**. Her husband's RNRB is not available, because his nil rate bands were fully used."},
 {"t":"iht-death-reliefs","q":"Omar died in 2026, unmarried and with no children of his own. He made no lifetime gifts. He leaves his home, worth £300,000, to his stepson Pip, whom he helped raise, and his other assets of £400,000 to his niece.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the nil rate bands available to Omar's estate?","o":["The nil rate band of £325,000 and the full residence nil rate band of £175,000.","Only the nil rate band, because the RNRB is available only to married people.","The nil rate band and an RNRB limited to half the value of the home.","The nil rate band and the RNRB, but only if the niece also receives part of the home.","Only the nil rate band of £325,000, because the RNRB is available only where a home passes to biological children."],"a":0,"e":"The **RNRB** is available where a **qualifying residential interest** (a home the deceased lived in) is **closely inherited**, meaning it passes to **direct descendants**, which include **step-children**, adopted and foster children and their descendants. The home (£300,000) exceeds £175,000, so the full RNRB applies, alongside the **£325,000** nil rate band. Marriage is not required."},
 {"t":"iht-death-reliefs","q":"Pria died in 2026, unmarried, with no lifetime gifts. Her net estate is £500,000. She leaves her flat, in which she lived, worth £120,000, to her daughter and the rest of her estate to a friend.\n\nWhat residence nil rate band is available?","o":["£60,000.","£55,000.","£120,000.","£175,000.","Nil."],"a":2,"e":"The RNRB is the **lower** of the maximum RNRB (**£175,000**) and the **value of the home** (net of any mortgage) **closely inherited** by direct descendants. The flat passing to her daughter is worth £120,000, so the RNRB is limited to **£120,000**. The rest of the estate going to a friend cannot use the RNRB."},
 {"t":"iht-death-reliefs","q":"Quentin died in 2026, unmarried. He lived in rented accommodation, made no lifetime gifts and had a net estate of £1,025,000. His will leaves £75,000 to a registered UK charity and the rest to his nephew.\n\nHow much IHT is payable on his estate?","o":["£234,000.","£250,000.","£252,000.","£225,000.","£280,000."],"a":3,"e":"The charity gift is **exempt**. If at least **10%** of the **baseline amount** goes to charity, the rest is taxed at **36%**. Baseline: £1,025,000 − £75,000 − £325,000 (NRB) = £625,000, plus the £75,000 gift added back = £700,000; 10% is £70,000, and the £75,000 gift exceeds it. Tax: £625,000 × 36% = **£225,000**. No RNRB is available, as there is no home. At 40%, the tax would have been £250,000."},
 {"t":"iht-death-reliefs","q":"Rosa died in 2026, having owned 200 acres of farmland for 12 years. Throughout that time it was let to a tenant farmer, who farmed it, under a farm business tenancy granted in 2014. The agricultural value of the land is £1.5 million. It is the only relievable property in her estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the agricultural property relief available?","o":["50% relief, because the land was let and she did not have vacant possession.","100% relief, because the land was let on a tenancy beginning after 1 September 1995.","No relief, because Rosa did not farm the land herself.","100% relief, but only for the first £1 million, with 50% on the remainder.","No relief, because let land qualifies for business relief instead."],"a":1,"e":"APR is available on land **occupied by the owner** for agriculture for **two years**, or **owned for seven years** and **occupied for agriculture** (by anyone, such as a tenant) throughout. The rate is **100%** if the owner had vacant possession or the land is let on a tenancy beginning on or after **1 September 1995**; otherwise 50%. The £1.5 million is within the **£2.5 million** allowance for 100% relief from 6 April 2026."},
 {"t":"iht-death-reliefs","q":"Sean died in 2026. Eighteen months before his death, he bought 40% of the shares in a private trading company, Sean Tools Ltd, which are worth £600,000 at his death. His will leaves them to his son.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the business property relief available on the shares?","o":["No relief, because Sean had owned the shares for less than two years.","50% relief, because Sean did not have control of the company.","100% relief, because the shares pass to his son.","50% relief, because he owned the shares for less than two years.","100% relief, because unquoted shares in a trading company qualify for full relief."],"a":0,"e":"**Unquoted** shares in a **trading** company qualify for **100% BPR**, whatever the size of the holding. However, the transferor must have **owned** the property for at least **two years** before the transfer (or it must have replaced other relevant business property). Sean owned the shares for only 18 months, so **no relief** is available."},
 {"t":"iht-death-reliefs","q":"Tilly died in July 2026, unmarried. She had held quoted shares on the Alternative Investment Market (AIM) in trading companies for eight years, worth £400,000. She held no other business or agricultural property.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the business property relief on the AIM shares?","o":["100% relief on the first £2.5 million of AIM shares.","50% relief, but only if Tilly controlled the companies.","50% relief, because AIM shares now qualify at the lower rate.","100% relief, because AIM shares are treated as unquoted shares.","No relief, because AIM shares are quoted shares."],"a":2,"e":"AIM shares are 'not listed' on a recognised stock exchange. Before 6 April 2026 they qualified for 100% BPR, but under the **Finance Act 2026**, for transfers on or after **6 April 2026**, they qualify for **50%** relief. Property that qualifies only for 50% relief does **not** use up the **£2.5 million** 100% allowance. Control is relevant only for fully listed shares."},
 {"t":"iht-death-reliefs","q":"Uri died in August 2026, unmarried, owning his sole-trader manufacturing business, which qualifies for business property relief, worth £3,500,000. He owned no other business or agricultural property.\n\nWhat value of the business is chargeable to IHT after business property relief?","o":["£2,500,000.","Nil.","£1,000,000.","£1,750,000.","£500,000."],"a":4,"e":"A sole-trader business qualifies for **100% BPR**, but from **6 April 2026** 100% relief applies only to the **first £2.5 million** of combined 100% BPR/APR property; the excess gets **50%** relief. So £2,500,000 is fully relieved, and the remaining £1,000,000 gets 50% relief, leaving **£500,000** chargeable. Uri was unmarried, so there is no transferred allowance."},
 {"t":"iht-death-reliefs","q":"Vina died in 2026. Two and a half years earlier, she had inherited £400,000 from her father's estate, on which IHT had been paid. Her own estate is now subject to IHT.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the quick succession relief available?","o":["Relief equal to 100% of the tax paid on the earlier transfer.","Relief of 60% of the tax paid on the earlier transfer.","Relief equal to 80% of the tax paid on the earlier transfer.","No relief, because the inheritance was more than two years before her death.","Relief equal to 20% of the tax paid on the earlier transfer."],"a":1,"e":"**Quick succession relief** (IHTA s.141) applies where the deceased received property by a chargeable transfer within **five years** before death. It reduces the tax on the second death by a percentage of the tax paid on the earlier transfer (attributable to the increase in the deceased's estate): **100%** within 1 year, **80%** for 1-2 years, **60%** for **2-3 years**, 40% for 3-4 years and 20% for 4-5 years. Two and a half years gives **60%**."},
 {"t":"iht-death-reliefs","q":"Wally died in 2026. For 20 years he ran a business letting out 30 residential flats he owned, managing them himself, and the business made a good profit. The business is worth £3 million. His will leaves it to his daughter.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the business property relief available?","o":["100% relief, because Wally actively managed the business himself.","50% relief, because it is land used in a business.","100% relief, because he owned the business for more than two years.","No relief, because a property letting business is an investment business.","100% relief on the first £2.5 million and 50% on the remainder."],"a":3,"e":"BPR is **not** available where the business consists **wholly or mainly** of **dealing in securities, land or buildings**, or **making or holding investments** (IHTA s.105(3)). A **property letting** business is treated as holding investments, even if actively managed. So no BPR is available, and the flats are taxed in full."},
 {"t":"income-cgt-administration","q":"Xena's executors are administering her estate. In the 2026/27 tax year the estate's only income is £450 of bank interest. In the 2027/28 tax year it receives £620 of interest.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the executors' liability to income tax?","o":["No tax is due in either year, because PRs are exempt from tax on interest.","Tax is due on both amounts at the higher rate.","No tax is due for 2026/27, and tax is due only on £120 for 2027/28.","No tax is due for 2026/27, but tax is due on the whole £620 for 2027/28.","Tax is due on both amounts, because PRs have no personal allowance."],"a":3,"e":"Since **6 April 2024**, if an estate's total income in a tax year is **£500 or less**, the PRs pay **no income tax** on it. If it is **more than £500**, tax is due on the **whole** amount, not just the excess. PRs have no personal allowance and never pay the higher or additional rates."},
 {"t":"income-cgt-administration","q":"Yann died on 20 July 2026. Before his death in the 2026/27 tax year, he received £9,000 of salary and £1,000 of rent. He had no other income. His executors are dealing with his tax affairs.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Yann's position for the 2026/27 tax year?","o":["He is entitled to a personal allowance only if his executors claim it within 12 months.","He has a full personal allowance, and any tax owed is a debt of the estate.","His personal allowance is reduced in proportion to the part of the tax year before his death.","He has no personal allowance, because he died during the tax year.","His income up to death is taxed as estate income of the PRs."],"a":1,"e":"The PRs must deal with the deceased's tax for the period from 6 April to the **date of death**. The deceased is entitled to a **full personal allowance** for the tax year of death; it is not apportioned. Any tax owed is a **debt** of the estate (deductible for IHT), and any repayment due is an asset of the estate."},
 {"t":"income-cgt-administration","q":"Zara bought shares in 2005 for £20,000. When she died in 2026, they were worth £150,000. Her executors are considering the capital gains tax position.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the CGT position on Zara's death?","o":["CGT is payable on the gain of £130,000, by Zara's PRs out of the estate.","CGT is payable on the gain of £130,000, but it is deducted from the IHT.","No CGT is payable, but the PRs acquire the shares at Zara's original cost of £20,000.","CGT is payable on the gain, but only if the shares are left to someone other than a spouse.","No CGT is payable on death, and the PRs acquire the shares at £150,000."],"a":4,"e":"**Death is not a disposal** for CGT (TCGA 1992 s.62), so the gain accrued during Zara's lifetime is **wiped out**. The PRs acquire the assets at their **market value at death** (probate value), here £150,000, which becomes their base cost for any later sale. This is the 'death uplift'."},
 {"t":"income-cgt-administration","q":"Alfie's shares had a probate value of £80,000. During the administration, when the shares were worth £95,000, his executors transferred them to his daughter, Bella, the residuary beneficiary. A year later, Bella sold them for £110,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the CGT position?","o":["There is no disposal on the transfer, and Bella's gain on sale is £30,000.","Bella's gain on sale is £15,000, because she acquired the shares at their value on the date of transfer.","No CGT is payable by anyone, because the shares came from an estate.","The executors are liable for CGT on the whole gain of £30,000 when Bella sells.","The executors made a gain of £15,000 on the transfer, and Bella's gain on sale is £15,000."],"a":0,"e":"When PRs transfer an asset to a beneficiary **in specie** (by assent or appropriation), there is **no disposal**, and the beneficiary is treated as having acquired the asset **at probate value** at the date of death (TCGA s.62(4)). Bella's gain is £110,000 − £80,000 = **£30,000**, taxed at her own rates, with her own annual exempt amount."},
 {"t":"income-cgt-administration","q":"Cara died on 10 May 2023. The administration of her estate has been delayed. In the 2026/27 tax year, her executors sell a house in the estate, making a chargeable gain of £20,000. They made no other disposals.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the executors' entitlement to an annual exempt amount?","o":["The full annual exempt amount, because the gain arises on a residential property.","None, because PRs are never entitled to an annual exempt amount.","None, because the PRs' entitlement lasted only three tax years.","The full individual annual exempt amount, because PRs are entitled to it every year.","Half the individual annual exempt amount, as for trustees."],"a":2,"e":"PRs receive the **same annual exempt amount as an individual**, but only for the **tax year of death** and the **next two tax years**. Cara died in 2023/24, so the PRs had the exemption in 2023/24, 2024/25 and 2025/26 only. In 2026/27 none is available, and the whole gain is taxed at the PRs' rate. (The 60-day reporting rule also applies to the sale of UK residential property.)"},
 {"t":"income-cgt-administration","q":"Dev's estate includes shares with a probate value of £60,000 that are now worth only £45,000. The estate also holds a painting that has risen in value since death. The residuary beneficiary has no capital gains of her own.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the most tax-efficient approach for the shares?","o":["The PRs should transfer the shares to the beneficiary, because PRs cannot make allowable losses.","The PRs should transfer the shares to the beneficiary, so that she can use the loss against her own future gains.","The PRs should sell the shares themselves, so that the £15,000 loss can be set against the PRs' gains on other estate assets.","It makes no difference, because losses made during the administration are passed on to the beneficiaries automatically.","The PRs should keep the shares until the end of the administration, when the loss is cancelled."],"a":2,"e":"A loss made by the **PRs** can be set against **the PRs' own gains** in the same or later tax years (for example on the painting), but it **cannot** be passed to the beneficiaries. A beneficiary who has no gains would get no immediate benefit from receiving the shares and selling them at a loss. So the PRs should usually sell assets that have **fallen** in value and consider transferring assets that have risen."},
 {"t":"income-cgt-administration","q":"Ezra died in February 2026, holding quoted shares with a probate value of £300,000, on which IHT at 40% is payable. In December 2026 the market fell, and his executors sold all the shares for £240,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the IHT position?","o":["No relief is available, because IHT is always calculated on the value at death.","The executors can claim relief only for CGT purposes, not for IHT.","The executors can claim loss on sale relief only if the shares are sold within six months of death.","The beneficiaries can claim the loss against their own income.","The executors can claim loss on sale relief, using the £240,000 sale price."],"a":4,"e":"**Loss on sale relief** allows the PRs to substitute the **sale price** for the probate value where **quoted shares** are sold within **12 months** of death (or **land** within **four years**) for less than probate value. The sale in December 2026 is within 12 months of the February 2026 death, so the value is reduced by £60,000, cutting the IHT by £24,000."},
 {"t":"income-cgt-administration","q":"Fern's will leaves her collection of shares to her nephew Gil. Gil has been receiving the dividends from the shares from the PRs for several months. He now wants to disclaim the gift so that it falls into residue for his sister.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gil's position?","o":["He cannot disclaim, but he could still make a deed of variation.","He can disclaim, and he can also choose that the shares pass directly to his sister.","He cannot disclaim or vary the gift, because the PRs have already started paying him dividends.","He can disclaim, but only with the consent of Fern's PRs.","He can disclaim at any time within two years of death, whatever benefits he has received."],"a":0,"e":"A **disclaimer** is a refusal of a gift, which then passes as though the beneficiary had died first (here into **residue**). A beneficiary **cannot disclaim** once he has **accepted** any benefit from the gift, such as dividends. Gil can still use a **deed of variation**, within **two years** of death and including the appropriate statements, to redirect the gift."},
 {"t":"income-cgt-administration","q":"Hani's will leaves £150,000 to her son Ivo, who is wealthy. Within a year of her death, Ivo signs a valid deed of variation, with the required statements, redirecting the £150,000 to his own daughter, aged 10. The money is invested and produces £3,000 of income a year.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the tax treatment of the income?","o":["The income is exempt from tax, because the variation was made within two years of death.","The income is taxed as the daughter's income, but only after the first £100.","The income is taxed as the daughter's income, because the variation is read back to Hani's death.","It is taxed as Ivo's income, because he is treated as the settlor.","The income is taxed as Hani's estate's income for the rest of the administration."],"a":3,"e":"A deed of variation can be **read back** to the death for **IHT** (IHTA s.142) and **CGT** (TCGA s.62(6)), but **not** for **income tax**. For income tax, Ivo is treated as the **settlor** of the money he redirected. Where a parent settles money on his own **minor, unmarried child**, if the income is more than **£100** a year, the **whole** of it is taxed as the **parent's** income (ITTOIA 2005 s.629)."},
 {"t":"income-cgt-administration","q":"Jules's will leaves his residue on a discretionary trust for his widow, Kay, and his children. Eighteen months after his death, the trustees appoint the whole trust fund outright to Kay. IHT was paid on the residue on Jules's death.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the IHT effect of the appointment?","o":["The appointment is treated as a potentially exempt transfer by the trustees.","It is read back to Jules's death, so the spouse exemption applies.","There is no IHT effect, because the tax on Jules's death has already been paid.","The appointment is a chargeable transfer by the trustees, taxed at 20%.","The appointment is read back only if Kay signs a deed of variation within two years."],"a":1,"e":"Under **IHTA 1984 s.144**, if property left by will on a **discretionary trust** is **appointed** out by the trustees within **two years** of death, the appointment is **read back** to the death for IHT. An appointment to the **surviving spouse** therefore attracts the **spouse exemption**, and the IHT paid on the residue can be repaid. No deed of variation is needed."}
];

;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["accounts"] = [
 {"t":"framework-client-money","q":"Marsh & Co does legal aid criminal work. It receives a payment of £3,600 from the Legal Aid Agency (LAA) for its costs on a completed matter. The firm's cashier is unsure which bank account to use.\n\nWhich of the following best describes where the payment may be paid?","o":["It must be paid into client account, because no bill has been delivered to the client.","It may be paid straight into business account, because LAA payments for the firm's costs are an exception to the usual rule.","It must be paid into client account and transferred to business account only after a bill for the costs has been delivered to the LAA.","It must be split equally between client and business account.","It must be paid into a separate designated client account in the client's name."],"a":1,"e":"Rule 2.3(b) allows a payment from the Legal Aid Agency for the firm's costs to be paid into business account, even though it may technically be client money. The duty to pay client money into client account otherwise applies, but this is one of the three listed exceptions. No bill to the LAA is needed first."},
 {"t":"framework-client-money","q":"Freya, a partner at Holt Solicitors, is a trustee of a family trust set up by a client. In that capacity she receives £18,000 of dividends on the trust's shares. Nothing in the trust deed conflicts with holding the money in a client account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of the £18,000?","o":["It is client money, because a partner holds it as a trustee, and it must be paid promptly into client account.","It is not client money, because it is held for the trust rather than for a client of the firm.","It is the firm's money, because Freya is a manager of the firm.","It is client money only if the beneficiaries of the trust are clients of the firm.","It is not client money, because trustees are regulated by trust law, not the Accounts Rules."],"a":0,"e":"Client money includes money held or received by you as a trustee or as the holder of a specified office or appointment (r.2.1(c)), and 'you' includes the firm's managers. It must be paid promptly into client account unless that would conflict with the trustee's obligations (r.2.3(a)), which is not the case here. It does not matter whether the beneficiaries are clients."},
 {"t":"framework-client-money","q":"Greaves LLP acts for Pemberton Ltd, the defendant in a contract claim. The claim settles for £45,000. Pemberton's insurer sends the £45,000 to Greaves so that Greaves can pay it to the claimant's solicitors. The claimant is not a client of Greaves.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the status of the £45,000 while Greaves holds it?","o":["It is not client money, because the claimant is not Greaves' client.","It is the firm's money until it is paid out.","It is client money only if Pemberton has paid Greaves' bill.","It is money held as stakeholder between the parties, so the Accounts Rules do not apply until it is paid over to the claimant.","It is client money held for a third party in connection with the firm's regulated services, so it goes into client account."],"a":4,"e":"Client money includes money held or received on behalf of a third party in relation to regulated services the firm delivers (r.2.1(b)). The settlement money is held for paying to the claimant in the course of Greaves' work for Pemberton, so it is client money even though the claimant is not a client. Stakeholder money is also client money under the same rule."},
 {"t":"framework-client-money","q":"Lowry & Co delivers a bill to its client Ms Kaur for profit costs of £2,000 plus VAT of £400, and counsel's fee of £1,000, which Lowry has not yet paid. A week later Ms Kaur sends £3,400 in full payment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm may deal with the £3,400?","o":["It may pay £2,400 into business account, but must pay counsel within two working days of receipt or put the £1,000 into client account.","It must pay £1,000 into client account, because counsel's fee has not yet been paid.","It must pay the whole £3,400 into client account until counsel has been paid.","It may pay all £3,400 into business account, as money received after a bill is not client money, but must pay counsel promptly.","It must pay counsel's fee direct from the £3,400 before banking the rest."],"a":3,"e":"Money for fees and unpaid disbursements is client money only if received before delivery of a bill for them (r.2.1(d)). Once billed, money received is the firm's, even for an unpaid disbursement, so it may all go to business account, though counsel must be paid promptly. The two-working-day rule belonged to the old 2011 rules and no longer exists."},
 {"t":"framework-client-money","q":"Pryor Solicitors acts for Mr Webb in a claim against his building insurer. The insurer settles and sends a cheque for £12,000 made out to the firm. Mr Webb gives the firm a signed letter asking it to pass the cheque straight to his builder, who is repairing his house, rather than banking it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm may do?","o":["It must pay the cheque into client account first and then pay the builder.","It may pass the cheque to the builder only with the SRA's permission.","It must return the cheque to the insurer, because the firm cannot deal with money for a builder.","It may pass the cheque to the builder, because the client has agreed an alternative arrangement in writing.","It must pay the cheque into business account, because the money is not for the firm's own legal work on the claim."],"a":3,"e":"Client money must be paid promptly into a client account unless an exception applies; one exception is where the firm agrees an alternative arrangement in writing with the client in the individual circumstances (r.2.3(c)). The builder's payment is part of the claim the firm is handling, so there is no banking facility issue. SRA permission is not required."},
 {"t":"framework-client-money","q":"On a Monday, Fenn & Co receives a cheque for £8,000 from a client on account of the costs of a planned purchase. The cashier is on holiday, so a partner puts the cheque in a drawer to be banked when the cashier returns in two weeks.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is no breach, because a cheque that has not yet been banked is not client money until it clears into the firm's account.","There is no breach, provided the cheque is banked within 14 days.","There is a breach, because client money must be paid promptly into client account, normally the same or next working day.","There is no breach, because money on account of costs is the firm's money.","There is a breach only if the cheque is lost."],"a":2,"e":"Money received on account of costs before a bill is client money (r.2.1(d)) and must be paid into client account promptly (r.2.3). 'Promptly' is not defined, but it means as soon as reasonably possible, normally the same or the next working day. Holding it for two weeks for staff convenience is a breach; 14 days was never the test for paying in."},
 {"t":"framework-client-money","q":"Shaw LLP delivers a bill for £1,800 to its client Mr Okafor. By mistake, Mr Okafor pays £2,100. The firm notices the overpayment straight away. Mr Okafor has no other matters with the firm.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm should do with the £300 overpayment?","o":["Keep it in business account as a credit against future work.","Return it promptly to Mr Okafor, as there is no longer any proper reason to hold it.","Treat it as a residual balance and pay it to charity.","Keep it in client account until Mr Okafor instructs the firm on a new matter of some kind.","Keep it in business account until the end of the firm's accounting period."],"a":1,"e":"The £300 is client money, as it is not due to the firm under a delivered bill, and it must be returned promptly once there is no longer any proper reason to hold it (r.2.5). Keeping it on account of hypothetical future work is not a proper reason. It is not a residual balance, because Mr Okafor can easily be repaid."},
 {"t":"framework-client-money","q":"After completing a sale for Mrs Dunn, Quill Solicitors finds that £750 remains on her ledger because of a late refund from the lender. Mrs Dunn has moved abroad, and despite reasonable steps the firm cannot trace her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm may do with the £750?","o":["Pay it to a charity of its choice without SRA authorisation, keeping records of its steps.","Transfer it to business account to cover its time spent trying to trace her.","Pay it to charity after deducting its tracing costs.","Keep it in client account indefinitely, as the money can never be withdrawn without Mrs Dunn's instructions.","Apply to the SRA for authorisation before withdrawing it from client account."],"a":4,"e":"Withdrawal of client money is permitted on the SRA's prior written authorisation or in circumstances the SRA prescribes (r.5.1(c)). The prescribed circumstances allow residual balances of £500 or less to be paid to charity without applying; £750 exceeds that, so the firm must apply for authorisation. It may never keep the money for itself or deduct its own costs."},
 {"t":"framework-client-money","q":"When winding up a probate matter, Corley & Co finds £120 left on the estate ledger. The sole beneficiary has died and the firm, after reasonable steps, can find no one entitled to the money. The firm wishes to pay the £120 to a local hospice, which is a registered charity.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligations?","o":["It must first obtain the SRA's written authorisation, because all withdrawals of unclaimed balances need SRA approval in advance.","It may pay the £120 to the charity, but only after holding it for six years.","It may pay the £120 to the charity without applying to the SRA, but must record its steps and keep the records for six years.","It may transfer the £120 to business account, because the amount is below £500.","It must pay the £120 to the court."],"a":2,"e":"Under the SRA's prescribed circumstances, a residual balance of £500 or less may be paid to charity without applying to the SRA, provided the firm has taken reasonable steps to return it, records those steps, keeps a central register and retains the records, with the charity's receipt, for at least six years. The firm can never transfer such money to its own business account."},
 {"t":"framework-client-money","q":"Lennox Solicitors is holding £200,000 for Mr Bain pending the outcome of a dispute expected to last several months. Mr Bain asks in writing for the money to be placed in a 95-day notice deposit account in the firm's name, designated for him, to earn a better rate of interest.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may do so?","o":["Yes, because client money must be available on demand unless otherwise agreed in writing with the client.","No, because client money may never be held on deposit.","Yes, but only with the SRA's prior permission.","No, because client money must always be available on demand.","Yes, provided Mr Bain gives oral instructions which the firm then confirms to him in writing shortly afterwards."],"a":0,"e":"Client money must be available on demand unless the firm agrees an alternative arrangement in writing with the client or third party for whom it is held (r.2.4). Mr Bain's written request allows the money to be placed on notice deposit. SRA permission is not needed, and the notice account remains a client account if properly named."},
 {"t":"client-account-withdrawals","q":"Pike & Partners opens a new bank account at a high street bank in Leeds to hold money for its conveyancing clients. The account is named 'Pike & Partners Conveyancing Deposits'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the account complies with the Accounts Rules?","o":["It complies, because it is held at a bank branch in England and Wales.","It complies, because the account is used only for clients' money.","It does not comply, because the account name must include the word 'client' as well as the firm's name.","It complies, provided the firm's records show the money belongs to clients.","It does not comply, because client accounts must be held at the bank where the firm keeps its business account."],"a":2,"e":"A client account must be at a bank or building society branch (or head office) in England and Wales (r.3.1), and its name must include the name of the authorised body and the word 'client' (r.3.2). This account meets the first requirement but not the second, so it is not a properly designated client account. Records cannot cure a misnamed account."},
 {"t":"client-account-withdrawals","q":"Harding LLP, based in Manchester, is offered a much better interest rate by a bank in Jersey. It proposes to open a client account there for large sums held for estates.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether this is permitted?","o":["It is permitted, provided the account name includes 'client'.","It is permitted, provided the clients agree.","It is not permitted, because estate money cannot be held on deposit.","It is permitted, provided the firm passes on the whole of the extra interest earned in Jersey to the clients concerned.","It is not permitted, because a client account must be held at a bank or building society in England and Wales."],"a":4,"e":"Client money may only be kept in a client account at a branch (or the head office) of a bank or building society in England and Wales (r.3.1). Jersey is outside England and Wales, so this would not be a client account. Client consent or a higher rate of interest does not change the location requirement."},
 {"t":"client-account-withdrawals","q":"Ward & Co acts for Mrs Elliot in her divorce. Under a consent order, Mr Elliot pays a lump sum of £80,000 to Ward & Co for Mrs Elliot. The order requires £30,000 of it to be used to reduce the mortgage on the former family home, now transferred to Mrs Elliot. She asks the firm to pay £30,000 directly to her mortgage lender.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may make the payment?","o":["No, because paying a client's lender directly amounts to providing a banking facility through client account.","No, because the firm does not act for the lender.","Yes, but only with the SRA's authorisation.","Yes, because the payment is linked to the regulated services the firm is delivering in the divorce.","No, because client money may only be paid to the client."],"a":3,"e":"The ban on banking facilities requires payments into and out of client account to be in respect of the delivery by the firm of regulated services (r.3.3). Paying part of a divorce settlement to the client's lender as the consent order requires is part of the legal work, so it is permitted on the client's instructions (r.5.1(b)). The firm need not act for the lender."},
 {"t":"client-account-withdrawals","q":"Corbett Solicitors acts for two unrelated clients, Mr Ross and Ms Tate. Mr Ross agrees to lend Ms Tate £15,000 from his sale proceeds held in client account, to help her complete a purchase the firm is handling. Ms Tate asks the firm to transfer the money between the ledgers today. Mr Ross has said he is happy in a phone call with Ms Tate, but has not contacted the firm.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm should do?","o":["Not make the transfer until it receives instructions from Mr Ross, the lending client, preferably in writing.","Make the transfer, because the overall client account balance is unaffected.","Make the transfer, because Ms Tate has given instructions and the money stays within the firm's general client account.","Not make the transfer at all, because loans between clients are prohibited.","Make the transfer, provided the firm records it in its breach log."],"a":0,"e":"Moving money held for one client so that it is held for another is a withdrawal of the first client's money and needs a proper ground under r.5.1, here the lending client's instructions (r.5.1(b)). Ms Tate's word about Mr Ross's agreement is not enough. Private loans between clients are not prohibited, but the firm should get clear written instructions from the lender."},
 {"t":"client-account-withdrawals","q":"Late on Friday, a trainee at Barton & Co receives an email, apparently from the firm's client Mr Hale, giving new bank details for the £240,000 balance of his sale proceeds. The trainee makes the transfer immediately, without speaking to anyone. The email was from a fraudster.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the main Accounts Rules failure?","o":["The firm used client account as a banking facility.","The firm withdrew the money without a proper ground, because Mr Hale was entitled to it.","The firm failed to ensure that withdrawals were appropriately authorised and supervised.","The firm failed to pay the money into client account promptly.","There is no breach, because the firm acted on what appeared to be the client's instructions."],"a":2,"e":"All withdrawals from client account must be appropriately authorised and supervised (r.5.2). A trainee paying out a large sum alone, on unverified emailed bank details, shows a failure of controls. The firm must also replace the lost money immediately from its own resources (r.6.1) and consider reporting a serious breach."},
 {"t":"client-account-withdrawals","q":"Napier LLP has acted for Mr Grant in a property dispute that has just settled. Mr Grant asks the firm to receive £30,000 from an overseas company he says owes him money, unconnected with the dispute, and to forward it to a friend in another country. The firm has no instructions in any legal matter relating to the overseas company.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm should do?","o":["Refuse, because the payment is not linked to any regulated service, and consider reporting its suspicions to its MLRO.","Agree, provided Mr Grant confirms his instructions in writing and the firm carries out identity checks on the company.","Agree, provided the money is paid out within two working days.","Agree, because Mr Grant is an existing client and the firm knows him well.","Agree, but charge Mr Grant a handling fee."],"a":0,"e":"Payments into and out of client account must be in respect of the delivery by the firm of regulated services (r.3.3). Receiving money from an unknown third party and forwarding it with no underlying legal transaction is a banking facility, whatever the client's instructions or reputation. The unusual flow of funds also raises money laundering concerns, so a report to the Money Laundering Reporting Officer should be considered."},
 {"t":"client-account-withdrawals","q":"Field & Co holds £400 in client account for Ms Lin. It must pay a £650 expert's fee on her behalf today, under an invoice addressed to Ms Lin. The firm decides to pay £400 from client account and the balance from business account.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries for the payment?","o":["Debit Ms Lin's ledger client column £650; credit client cash account £650.","Debit Ms Lin's ledger client column £400 and credit client cash account £400; debit Ms Lin's ledger business column £250 and credit business cash account £250.","Debit Ms Lin's ledger business column £650; credit client cash account £650.","Debit client cash account £400 and credit Ms Lin's ledger client column £400; debit business cash account £250 and credit Ms Lin's ledger business column £250.","Debit Ms Lin's ledger client column £400 and credit client cash account £400; then debit Ms Lin's ledger client column £250 and credit business cash account £250."],"a":1,"e":"A split payment is recorded as two payments: the part from client account debits the client ledger client column and credits the client cash account; the part from business account debits the ledger business column and credits the business cash account. Paying £650 from client account would overdraw her ledger (r.5.3). Each pair of entries must stay within one set of books."},
 {"t":"client-account-withdrawals","q":"Lyle Solicitors is holding £3,000 for Mr Price, received from him to pay Stamp Duty Land Tax (SDLT) on his purchase. Completion has taken place and the SDLT return is due. The fee earner pays the £3,000 to HM Revenue & Customs from client account without asking Mr Price again.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the withdrawal was permitted?","o":["No, because a withdrawal from client account always requires fresh written instructions from the client.","No, because SDLT must be paid from business account.","Yes, but only because the amount is below £5,000.","Yes, because client money may be withdrawn for the purpose for which it is being held.","No, because SDLT money is held for HMRC, which is not a client."],"a":3,"e":"Client money may be withdrawn for the purpose for which it is being held (r.5.1(a)), following the client's instructions (r.5.1(b)), or with SRA authority (r.5.1(c)). The money was received specifically to pay SDLT, so no further instructions are needed. There is no monetary threshold."},
 {"t":"client-account-withdrawals","q":"Osborne LLP will hold £600,000 from the sale of a deceased client's investments for about nine months while the estate is administered. The executors want the estate to earn as much interest as possible and to be able to see the interest clearly.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the most suitable arrangement?","o":["Hold the money in the firm's business account, which earns a higher rate.","Hold the money in a separate designated client account for the estate, so that interest is credited directly to that account for the estate.","Hold the money in the general client account and pay the estate no interest at all, because the whole sum will be distributed within a year.","Pay the money to the executors to hold in their own account, to avoid the firm holding client money.","Hold the money in a joint account with the executors, which is not subject to the Accounts Rules."],"a":1,"e":"A separate designated client account holds money for one client, and interest is credited directly to it, which suits large sums held for a long time. Client money can never be held in business account (r.4.1). Joint accounts are subject to limited rules (r.9), and returning the money to the executors would defeat the purpose of the firm administering the estate."},
 {"t":"client-account-withdrawals","q":"Quarterly VAT of £18,000 is due from Rees & Co to HM Revenue & Customs today, and the firm's business account is short of funds. The managing partner tells the cashier to pay the VAT from the general client account, which holds £900,000, and to transfer the money back from business account next week when fees come in.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the instruction?","o":["It is permitted, because the money will be repaid from business account within a week and no client will be kept waiting for funds.","It is permitted, because the general client account holds far more than £18,000.","It is permitted, provided the partner records it in the breach log.","It is not permitted unless the firm first delivers a bill to its clients.","It is not permitted, because none of the permitted grounds for withdrawing client money applies to paying the firm's own tax bill."],"a":4,"e":"Withdrawals from client account are allowed only on the grounds in r.5.1: the purpose for which the money is held, the instructions of the client or third party for whom it is held, or SRA authority. Paying the firm's own VAT with clients' money fits none of them and is a misuse of client money, however briefly and whatever the size of the total balance. It would have to be replaced immediately (r.6.1) and is likely to be a serious breach that must be reported."},
 {"t":"separation-mixed-payments","q":"Ashworth & Co holds £5,000 on account for Mr Dale. On 1 June it delivers a bill for £2,000 plus VAT of £400. On 31 August, the £2,400 is still sitting in client account because the cashier has been busy.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is no breach, because the money was originally received as client money.","There is a breach, because once billed the £2,400 was the firm's money and should not have stayed in client account.","There is no breach, provided the money is transferred before the next accountant's report.","There is no breach, because leaving extra money in client account provides a cushion that protects the firm's clients.","There is a breach only if Mr Dale objects."],"a":1,"e":"Client money must be kept separate from firm money (r.4.1), which works in both directions. Once a bill has been delivered, the billed sum held in client account belongs to the firm and should be transferred promptly to business account. Leaving it there creates a cushion that could hide a shortfall, so it is a breach, not a benefit to clients."},
 {"t":"separation-mixed-payments","q":"Brennan Solicitors delivered a bill to Ms Fox for £600 including VAT. She then sends a single cheque for £2,600: £600 to pay the bill and £2,000 on account of costs for a new matter. The cashier pays the whole cheque into the business account and plans to transfer £2,000 to client account at the end of the week.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the cashier's action?","o":["It is acceptable, because the cheque included money due to the firm.","It is acceptable, provided the £2,000 is transferred within 14 days.","It is acceptable, because the client chose to send a single cheque.","It is a breach, because a mixed payment must not be paid wholly into business account.","It is a breach, because the whole cheque should have been returned to Ms Fox with a request for two separate payments."],"a":3,"e":"A mixed payment contains both client and firm money and must be allocated promptly to the correct accounts (r.4.2): either split on receipt, or paid wholly into client account and the firm's share transferred promptly. Paying it all into business account means holding client money in business account, a breach of rr.2.3 and 4.1."},
 {"t":"separation-mixed-payments","q":"Carver LLP's written accounts procedure says that, where a mixed payment is paid into client account, the firm's share will be transferred to business account 'within 14 days of receipt'. The COFA asks whether this is compliant.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It is compliant, because the Accounts Rules allow 14 days.","It is compliant, provided the client is told in the client care letter.","It is not compliant, because mixed payments must always be paid entirely into business account and the client's share transferred out.","It is compliant only for sums under £10,000.","It is not automatically compliant, because the rules require the firm's share to be allocated promptly, with no fixed 14-day period."],"a":4,"e":"Rule 4.2 requires mixed payments to be allocated promptly to the correct account. The 14-day period came from the old 2011 rules and has no place in the 2019 rules, so a firm that routinely waits up to 14 days may not be acting promptly. Paying a mixed payment wholly into business account is never permitted."},
 {"t":"separation-mixed-payments","q":"Dorsey & Co holds £4,000 on account for Mr Hart. The fee earner sends Mr Hart an email setting out the work done to date and stating that the costs incurred are £1,500 plus VAT of £300. No formal bill is prepared. The firm then transfers £1,800 from client account to business account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the transfer was permitted?","o":["It was not permitted, because a formal bill of costs must always be delivered to the client before any transfer for costs.","It was permitted, because the costs were notified in writing first and the sum is covered by the money held.","It was not permitted, because notifications of costs must be sent by post.","It was permitted, because money on account belongs to the firm once work has been done.","It was not permitted, because Mr Hart had not agreed the costs."],"a":1,"e":"Before transferring client money to pay its costs, the firm must give a bill of costs or other written notification of the costs incurred (r.4.3(a)-(b)). A clear email setting out the costs counts. The transfer must be for the specific sum identified and covered by funds held for that client (r.4.3(c)), as it is here. The client's agreement to the costs is not a precondition."},
 {"t":"separation-mixed-payments","q":"Ellery Solicitors holds £2,000 on account for Ms Ivy. It delivers a bill for £2,500 plus VAT of £500, a total of £3,000. The firm wants to collect its costs as quickly as possible.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm may do?","o":["Transfer £3,000 from client account to business account, because the bill for that amount has been delivered.","Transfer nothing until Ms Ivy pays the remaining £1,000.","Transfer £2,000 from client account to business account and ask Ms Ivy to pay the remaining £1,000.","Transfer £2,000 from client account and £1,000 from another client's ledger, to be repaid later.","Transfer £3,000 from the general client account, because the total balance is sufficient."],"a":2,"e":"A transfer for costs must be for the sum identified in the bill and covered by the amount held for that client (r.4.3(c)); the firm can never take more than the client's balance (r.5.3). It may take the £2,000 it holds and must look to Ms Ivy for the rest. Using other clients' money would be a serious breach."},
 {"t":"separation-mixed-payments","q":"Fairfax & Co pays a £150 search fee for its client Mr Joyce from its business account because it holds no money for him. A week later, before any bill has been sent, Mr Joyce sends £150 to reimburse the firm.\n\nWhich of the following best describes where the £150 should be paid?","o":["Into client account, because no bill has yet been delivered to Mr Joyce covering the search fee or any costs.","Into client account, because it relates to a disbursement.","Into a separate designated client account.","Into business account, because it reimburses the firm for a disbursement already paid from its own money.","Into client account until the matter is completed."],"a":3,"e":"Money received to reimburse the firm for a disbursement it has already paid out of business account is the firm's own money, not client money, so it goes into business account. Rule 2.1(d) covers money received before a bill for fees and unpaid disbursements; this disbursement has already been paid by the firm."},
 {"t":"separation-mixed-payments","q":"Gale Solicitors acts for Mrs Kemp in a boundary dispute. Before any bill has been sent, Mrs Kemp sends £800 to cover a surveyor's report that the firm will commission next month, under an invoice addressed to Mrs Kemp.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the £800?","o":["It is client money for an unpaid, unbilled disbursement, so it must be paid into client account.","It is the firm's money, because it is for a disbursement.","It is a mixed payment, so it must be split.","It is the firm's money, because Mrs Kemp has agreed to the disbursement.","It is client money, but may be paid into business account if Mrs Kemp agrees to that arrangement orally on the telephone."],"a":0,"e":"Money received for unpaid disbursements before delivery of a bill for them is client money (r.2.1(d)) and must be paid promptly into client account (r.2.3). It becomes available for the purpose when the surveyor's invoice is paid. An alternative arrangement would have to be agreed in writing (r.2.3(c))."},
 {"t":"separation-mixed-payments","q":"Holt & Co delivered a bill to Mr Lord for £1,200 including VAT. Mr Lord then sends £3,200: £1,200 for the bill and £2,000 on account of future costs. The firm's bank allows payments to be split on receipt, and the firm does so.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit business cash account £1,200 and credit Mr Lord's ledger business column £1,200; debit client cash account £2,000 and credit Mr Lord's ledger client column £2,000.","Debit business cash account £3,200; credit Mr Lord's ledger business column £3,200.","Debit client cash account £3,200; credit Mr Lord's ledger client column £3,200.","Debit Mr Lord's ledger business column £1,200 and credit business cash account £1,200; debit Mr Lord's ledger client column £2,000 and credit client cash account £2,000.","Debit client cash account £1,200 and credit Mr Lord's ledger business column £1,200; then debit client cash account £2,000 and credit Mr Lord's ledger client column £2,000."],"a":0,"e":"When a mixed payment is split on receipt, each part is recorded as a receipt in the correct set of books: firm money debits business cash and credits the ledger business column; client money debits client cash and credits the ledger client column. Each transaction's two entries must be in the same set of books. The option with debits to the ledger reverses the entries."},
 {"t":"separation-mixed-payments","q":"Irwin Solicitors receives £5,000 from a client on account of costs, before any bill. The cashier mistakenly pays it into business account and records it as debit business cash, credit client ledger business column. The error is discovered the next day.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the correcting entries?","o":["Debit client cash £5,000; credit client ledger client column £5,000 only.","Debit business cash £5,000; credit client cash £5,000.","Business side: debit client ledger business column £5,000, credit business cash £5,000. Client side: debit client cash £5,000, credit client ledger client column £5,000.","Business side: debit business cash £5,000, credit client ledger business column £5,000. Client side: debit client ledger client column £5,000, credit client cash £5,000.","No entries are needed, provided the money is transferred within 14 days."],"a":2,"e":"Client money paid into business account is a breach that must be corrected promptly (rr.2.3, 4.1, 6.1) by transferring it to client account. A transfer from business to client account needs two pairs of entries: business side, debit the ledger business column and credit business cash; client side, debit client cash and credit the ledger client column."},
 {"t":"separation-mixed-payments","q":"Jarvis LLP acts for Ms Moore in a personal injury claim. The firm sends its bill of costs for £6,000 to the defendant's insurer, which has agreed to pay Ms Moore's costs. The insurer then sends one payment of £36,000: £30,000 damages for Ms Moore and £6,000 for the firm's costs.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm should deal with the payment?","o":["Pay all £36,000 into client account and keep it there until Ms Moore approves the costs.","Pay all £36,000 into business account, then transfer £30,000 to client account.","Return the payment and ask the insurer to send two separate cheques.","Pay all £36,000 into client account and transfer the £6,000 only after sending a separate bill for it to Ms Moore.","Treat it as a mixed payment: pay it into client account and promptly transfer £6,000, or split it on receipt."],"a":4,"e":"The bill or written notification of costs may be given to the client or the paying party (r.4.3(a)), so the £6,000 is the firm's money and the £30,000 is client money: a mixed payment. It must be allocated promptly (r.4.2), by paying it all into client account and transferring the firm's share, or splitting it on receipt. It must never be paid wholly into business account."},
 {"t":"double-entry-basics","q":"Kendall & Co acts for Mr Nash and holds no money for him. It pays a £40 Land Registry search fee for him from its business account.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit Mr Nash's ledger client column £40; credit client cash account £40.","Debit business cash account £40; credit Mr Nash's ledger business column £40.","Debit Mr Nash's ledger business column £40; credit business cash account £40.","Debit Mr Nash's ledger business column £40; credit client cash account £40.","Debit profit costs £40; credit business cash account £40."],"a":2,"e":"A payment out of business account for a client debits the client ledger business column (the client now owes the firm) and credits the business cash account (money out). The client column cannot be used as no client money is held, and an entry mixing client cash with the business column is wrong because both entries must be in the same set of books."},
 {"t":"double-entry-basics","q":"Lister Solicitors pays its monthly office rent of £3,000 from its business account.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit the landlord's client ledger business column £3,000; credit business cash account £3,000.","Debit business cash account £3,000; credit rent account £3,000.","Debit client cash account £3,000; credit rent account £3,000.","Debit rent account £3,000; credit client cash account £3,000.","Debit rent account £3,000; credit business cash account £3,000."],"a":4,"e":"The firm's own expenses have nothing to do with client ledgers. An expense is debited to the relevant expense (nominal) account and the payment out is credited to the business cash account. The rent can never be paid from client account."},
 {"t":"double-entry-basics","q":"Mason & Co pays a £10 oath fee for its client Ms Owen from its petty cash float. It holds no money for her.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit petty cash account £10; credit Ms Owen's ledger business column £10.","Debit Ms Owen's ledger client column £10; credit petty cash account £10.","Debit Ms Owen's ledger business column £10; credit petty cash account £10.","Debit Ms Owen's ledger business column £10; credit client cash account £10.","Debit profit costs £10; credit petty cash account £10."],"a":2,"e":"Petty cash is a float of the firm's own money, so a payment from it for a client is treated like a business account payment: debit the client ledger business column and credit the petty cash account. The client column is not used because no client money is involved."},
 {"t":"double-entry-basics","q":"An extract from the ledger of Mr Patel at Norris LLP shows the client column with a balance of £350 DR.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what this shows?","o":["The firm holds £350 for Mr Patel in client account.","Mr Patel owes the firm £350 for costs.","Mr Patel has paid the firm £350 on account of costs, which is held in client account for him.","The firm has paid out £350 more for Mr Patel than it held for him, which is a breach.","The firm has delivered a bill to Mr Patel for £350."],"a":3,"e":"The normal balance on a client ledger client column is a credit, showing money held for the client. A debit balance means the ledger is overdrawn: the firm has used £350 of other clients' money for Mr Patel, a breach of r.5.3 that must be replaced immediately (r.6.1). A sum owed for costs would appear as a debit on the business column."},
 {"t":"double-entry-basics","q":"At the end of a month, the business cash account of Oakes Solicitors shows a credit balance of £12,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what this shows?","o":["The firm holds £12,000 in its business bank account.","The firm's business account is overdrawn by £12,000, which is permitted as it is the firm's own money.","The firm has breached the Accounts Rules, because a cash account can never be allowed to show a credit balance.","The firm owes £12,000 to its clients.","The firm has £12,000 of client money in its business account."],"a":1,"e":"In a cash account, a debit balance means money in the bank and a credit balance means the account is overdrawn. A business account overdraft is permitted, because it is the firm's own money. It is the client cash account (and client ledger client columns) that must never be overdrawn."},
 {"t":"double-entry-basics","q":"Palmer & Co discovers that it has overpaid £500 from client account on behalf of Ms Quinn, leaving her ledger overdrawn. The firm transfers £500 from business account to client account to replace the shortfall.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit client cash £500; credit Ms Quinn's ledger client column £500 only.","Business side: debit business cash £500, credit Ms Quinn's ledger business column £500. Client side: debit Ms Quinn's ledger client column £500, credit client cash £500.","Debit business cash £500; credit client cash £500.","Debit Ms Quinn's ledger business column £500; credit Ms Quinn's ledger client column £500.","Business side: debit Ms Quinn's ledger business column £500, credit business cash £500. Client side: debit client cash £500, credit Ms Quinn's ledger client column £500."],"a":4,"e":"A transfer from business to client account is two movements of money, so it needs entries in both sets of books. Business side: debit the ledger business column (Ms Quinn now owes the firm) and credit business cash. Client side: debit client cash and credit the ledger client column, bringing it back to nil. There is no single entry that moves money between the two sets of books."},
 {"t":"double-entry-basics","q":"Quayle Solicitors acts for Mr Rowe. These are the only entries: (1) receives £10,000 from Mr Rowe on account; (2) pays £2,500 from client account for him; (3) pays a £100 fee from business account; (4) delivers a bill for £1,500 plus VAT of £300; (5) transfers £1,900 from client account to business account to pay the bill and the £100 fee.\n\nWhich of the following shows the balances on Mr Rowe's ledger after these entries?","o":["Client column £5,600 CR; business column nil.","Client column £7,500 CR; business column £1,900 DR.","Client column £5,600 CR; business column £1,900 DR.","Client column £5,700 CR; business column £100 DR.","Client column £3,700 CR; business column nil."],"a":0,"e":"Client column: £10,000 CR less £2,500 less £1,900 = £5,600 CR. Business column: £100 DR plus £1,800 DR (the bill including VAT) = £1,900 DR, then credited £1,900 by the transfer = nil. So Mr Rowe owes the firm nothing and the firm holds £5,600 for him."},
 {"t":"double-entry-basics","q":"Rigby & Co delivers a bill to Ms Shah for profit costs of £800 plus VAT of £160. No money is received or paid at the same time.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the bill is recorded?","o":["Debit Ms Shah's ledger business column £960; credit profit costs £800 and HMRC (VAT) £160. No cash entry.","Debit client cash £960; credit Ms Shah's ledger client column £960.","Debit business cash £960; credit profit costs £960.","Debit Ms Shah's ledger client column £960; credit profit costs £800 and HMRC £160. No cash account entry is made.","Debit profit costs £800 and HMRC £160; credit Ms Shah's ledger business column £960."],"a":0,"e":"A bill records what the client owes the firm: debit the client ledger business column with the total, credit profit costs (the firm's income) and credit the HMRC VAT account (output tax owed to HMRC). No money moves when a bill is delivered, so no cash account is affected. The reverse entries describe an abatement."},
 {"t":"double-entry-basics","q":"Sutton LLP delivered a bill for £1,200 including VAT to Mr Turner, and holds no money for him. Mr Turner now sends a cheque for £1,200 in payment.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit client cash £1,200; credit Mr Turner's ledger client column £1,200.","Debit business cash £1,200; credit Mr Turner's ledger business column £1,200.","Debit Mr Turner's ledger business column £1,200; credit business cash £1,200.","Debit client cash £1,200; credit Mr Turner's ledger business column £1,200.","Debit business cash £1,200; credit profit costs £1,200."],"a":1,"e":"Money received to pay a bill already delivered is the firm's money, so it goes into business account: debit business cash (money in) and credit the client ledger business column (reducing what he owes to nil). Profit costs were credited when the bill was delivered and are not credited again."},
 {"t":"double-entry-basics","q":"Tyler & Co received a cheque for £2,000 from its client Mrs Usher on account of costs and recorded it in the client books. Before any money was paid out for her, the cheque was dishonoured by her bank.\n\nWhich of the following shows the entries to record the dishonour?","o":["Debit client cash £2,000; credit Mrs Usher's ledger client column £2,000.","Debit Mrs Usher's ledger business column £2,000; credit business cash £2,000.","No entries are needed, because the money was never received.","Debit Mrs Usher's ledger client column £2,000; credit client cash £2,000.","Debit bad debts £2,000; credit Mrs Usher's ledger client column £2,000."],"a":3,"e":"A dishonoured cheque reverses the original receipt: debit the client ledger client column and credit the client cash account. As nothing had been paid out against it, the ledger returns to nil and there is no breach. If money had already been paid out, the ledger would be overdrawn and the firm would have to replace the shortfall."},
 {"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","q":"Underwood Solicitors acts for Ms Vale on her sale and holds the sale proceeds. The estate agent's invoice for £4,000 plus VAT of £800 is addressed to Ms Vale, and she instructs the firm to pay it.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit Ms Vale's ledger client column £4,800; credit client cash £4,800.","Debit Ms Vale's ledger business column £4,000 and HMRC £800; credit business cash £4,800.","Debit Ms Vale's ledger client column £4,000 and HMRC £800; credit client cash £4,800.","Debit Ms Vale's ledger business column £4,800; credit client cash £4,800.","Debit client cash £4,800; credit Ms Vale's ledger client column £4,800."],"a":0,"e":"An invoice addressed to the client is paid under the agency method: the firm pays as the client's agent and does not reclaim the VAT, so the gross amount is paid. Since the firm holds sufficient client money, it may pay from client account: debit the ledger client column and credit client cash. HMRC entries belong to the principal method."},
 {"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","q":"Vickers & Co receives a fee note from counsel, addressed to the firm, for £1,000 plus VAT of £200, for work on its client Mr West's case. The firm holds £3,000 for Mr West in client account.\n\nWhich of the following shows how the firm should pay counsel?","o":["Debit Mr West's ledger client column £1,200; credit client cash £1,200.","Debit Mr West's ledger business column £1,000 and HMRC £200; credit business cash £1,200.","Debit Mr West's ledger business column £1,200; credit business cash £1,200.","Debit Mr West's ledger client column £1,000 and HMRC £200; credit client cash £1,200.","Debit Mr West's ledger business column £1,000; credit business cash £1,000, with the £200 VAT paid from client account."],"a":1,"e":"A fee note addressed to the firm is the firm's own supply, so the principal method applies: it must be paid from business account even though client money is held. The firm reclaims the input VAT, so the entries are debit the ledger business column with the net fee, debit HMRC with the VAT, and credit business cash with the gross amount."},
 {"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","q":"Walsh LLP acts for a buyer client and obtains a local authority search costing £180, which it uses in advising the client. When the firm prepares its bill, the fee earner asks how the search fee should be treated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the correct treatment?","o":["As an agency disbursement listed on the bill with no VAT added by the firm on top of it.","As a disbursement that must be paid from client account.","As an expense of the firm which cannot be charged to the client.","As client money, because it was paid on the client's behalf.","As part of the firm's own supply, so the firm adds VAT on the search fee in its bill."],"a":4,"e":"HMRC's view (Revenue and Customs Brief 6 (2020)) is that property search fees that a solicitor obtains and uses in advising the client are part of the solicitor's own supply, not disbursements for VAT purposes. So they are treated as principal method items: paid from business account, with VAT charged on them in the firm's bill."},
 {"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","q":"Xavier & Co acts for Ms Young. It paid a £150 Land Registry fee for her from client account. It now delivers a bill for profit costs of £1,000 plus VAT, which also lists the Land Registry fee. VAT is 20%.\n\nWhat is the total amount shown as due on the bill?","o":["£1,200","£1,180","£1,380","£1,350","£1,230"],"a":3,"e":"VAT is charged at 20% on the profit costs only: £1,000 + £200 = £1,200. The Land Registry fee is not a VAT supply and is paid as the client's agent, so it is listed without VAT, giving £1,350. Adding VAT to the £150 fee would wrongly treat it as part of the firm's own supply."},
 {"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","q":"Yates Solicitors delivers a bill to Mr Zane for profit costs of £1,500. It has paid counsel's fee of £400, under a fee note addressed to the firm, and a court fee of £100. VAT is 20%.\n\nWhat is the total amount the bill should show as due?","o":["£2,000","£2,300","£2,380","£2,400","£2,180"],"a":2,"e":"Counsel's fee addressed to the firm is a principal method item, so VAT is charged on it with the profit costs: 20% of £1,900 = £380. The court fee is an agency disbursement with no VAT. Total: £1,500 + £400 + £380 + £100 = £2,380. Charging VAT on the court fee too would give £2,400."},
 {"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","q":"Abbott & Co delivered a bill to Mr Bryce for £2,000 plus VAT of £400 eight months ago. Mr Bryce has been declared bankrupt, and the firm accepts that it will never be paid. It writes the debt off and claims VAT bad debt relief.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit profit costs £2,000 and HMRC £400; credit Mr Bryce's ledger business column £2,400.","Debit bad debts £2,400; credit Mr Bryce's ledger business column £2,400.","Debit bad debts £2,000 and HMRC £400; credit Mr Bryce's ledger business column £2,400.","Debit Mr Bryce's ledger business column £2,400; credit bad debts £2,000 and HMRC £400.","Debit bad debts £2,000 and HMRC £400; credit business cash £2,400."],"a":2,"e":"A bad debt is cleared by crediting the client ledger business column with the total owed. Where VAT bad debt relief is claimed, the debit is split: the fees to a bad debts expense account and the VAT to HMRC. Debiting profit costs is how an abatement is recorded, and no cash moves, so business cash is not used."},
 {"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","q":"Carlisle LLP delivered a bill for £1,000 plus VAT of £200 to a client three months ago. The client has refused to pay. The firm writes the debt off and wants to recover the £200 output VAT it has already accounted for to HMRC.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It can recover the VAT immediately, because the debt has been written off in the firm's accounts and the client refuses to pay.","It can recover the VAT by issuing a VAT credit note to the client.","It can never recover output VAT once accounted for.","It can recover the VAT only if the client is insolvent.","It cannot recover the VAT until the debt is at least six months overdue and has been written off in its accounts."],"a":4,"e":"Output VAT on an unpaid bill can be recovered through VAT bad debt relief once the debt is at least six months overdue and has been written off in the firm's accounts. At three months, it is too early. A VAT credit note is used for an abatement, where the firm agrees to reduce the bill, not for a bad debt."},
 {"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","q":"Dalton & Co buys new computers for its office for £5,000 plus VAT of £1,000, paid from its business account. The firm is registered for VAT.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit office equipment account £6,000; credit business cash £6,000.","Debit office equipment account £5,000 and HMRC £1,000; credit business cash £6,000.","Debit business cash £6,000; credit office equipment account £5,000 and HMRC £1,000.","Debit office equipment account £5,000; credit HMRC £1,000 and business cash £4,000.","Debit office equipment account £5,000 and HMRC £1,000; credit client cash £6,000."],"a":1,"e":"VAT the firm pays on its own purchases is input tax, which it can reclaim from HMRC, so it is debited to the HMRC account; the net cost is debited to the expense or asset account, and the gross payment is credited to business cash. A firm purchase is never paid from client account."},
 {"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","q":"Ellis Solicitors keeps copies of its bills only on each client's matter file. There is no single list or electronic record of all bills issued. The firm's COFA asks whether this complies with the Accounts Rules.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It complies, because the bills are kept on file and can be found.","It complies, provided the bills are kept for six years.","It complies, because only bills paid from money in client account need to be recorded in any central record.","It does not comply, because the firm must keep a readily accessible central record of all its bills.","It does not comply, because bills must be filed with the SRA."],"a":3,"e":"Rule 8.4 requires a firm to keep a readily accessible central record of all bills or other written notifications of costs given by it. Copies scattered across matter files do not meet this requirement. Bills are not filed with the SRA, though records must be kept for at least six years (r.13.1)."},
 {"t":"bills-vat-disbursements","q":"Finch & Co acts for Ms Gill and holds no money for her. It receives a fee note for £600 plus VAT of £120 from a medical expert, addressed to Ms Gill, and she asks the firm to pay it. The firm pays from its business account.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit Ms Gill's ledger business column £720; credit business cash £720.","Debit Ms Gill's ledger client column £720; credit client cash £720.","Debit Ms Gill's ledger business column £600 and HMRC £120; credit business cash £720.","Debit Ms Gill's ledger business column £720; credit client cash £720.","Debit business cash £720; credit Ms Gill's ledger business column £720."],"a":0,"e":"A fee note addressed to the client is paid under the agency method, so the firm does not reclaim the VAT and the gross amount is debited to the ledger. As no client money is held, it is paid from business account: debit the ledger business column £720 and credit business cash. Splitting out the VAT to HMRC is only correct for the principal method."},
 {"t":"interest-breaches","q":"Gordon Solicitors acts for Mr Hayes, who is buying a house. On exchange, the seller's solicitors hold Mr Hayes' £40,000 deposit as stakeholder in their general client account. The seller later fails to complete, and the contract is rescinded. The deposit is to be returned to Mr Hayes.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position on interest earned on the deposit?","o":["The seller is entitled to the interest, because the deposit was held by the seller's solicitors.","The seller's solicitors may keep the interest as their fee for holding the deposit.","No interest is payable, because the deposit was held for less than a year.","Interest must be paid to the seller and Mr Hayes in equal shares, because both had an interest in the deposit.","A fair sum of interest must be accounted for to the person entitled to the deposit, here Mr Hayes."],"a":4,"e":"A firm must account for a fair sum of interest on client money to the client or third party for whom it is held (r.7.1). Interest follows the deposit to whoever becomes entitled to it: on completion that is normally the seller, but here the deposit is returned to the buyer, so the interest goes to him. There is no minimum period."},
 {"t":"interest-breaches","q":"Hollis LLP offers its business clients a lower fee if they agree that the firm may keep any interest on money held for them. The client care letter explains how interest would otherwise be calculated, roughly how much a client might forgo, and that the client can refuse. Ms Ito signs a separate agreement accepting the arrangement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the arrangement?","o":["It is permitted, because the firm and client may agree in writing not to pay interest, and Ms Ito gave informed consent.","It is not permitted, because a firm must always pay a fair sum of interest.","It is permitted only if the SRA approves it.","It is not permitted, because interest must always be paid on client balances over £1,000, whatever the client agrees in writing.","It is permitted, but only for sums held for less than eight weeks."],"a":0,"e":"The duty to account for a fair sum of interest (r.7.1) can be displaced by a written agreement with the client, provided the client is given sufficient information to give informed consent (r.7.2). A clear explanation and a separate signed agreement meet that standard. The rules set no minimum sums or periods."},
 {"t":"interest-breaches","q":"Ingram & Co's interest policy pays clients interest on general client account balances at a rate slightly below the rate the firm earns from its bank, taking account of the cost of administration and the need for funds to be instantly available. A client complains that she should receive all the interest the bank paid on her money.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligation?","o":["It must pay her all the interest earned on her money.","It must account to her for a fair sum of interest, which need not be all the interest the firm earns.","It need not pay her any interest at all, because the bank pays the interest to the firm and not to the client.","It must pay her interest at the Bank of England base rate.","It must pay her interest only if the sum held exceeds £10,000."],"a":1,"e":"Rule 7.1 requires a fair sum of interest, not all the interest the firm earns. A fair policy considers the amount and period held, the rate obtainable and the need for funds to be instantly available. The rules prescribe no rate or minimum balance, though a de minimis threshold set too high is unlikely to be fair."},
 {"t":"interest-breaches","q":"Jenner Solicitors holds £300,000 for the executors of Mrs King's estate in a separate designated client account. The bank credits £1,800 interest to the account.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit interest payable account £1,800; credit the estate ledger business column £1,800.","Debit business cash £1,800; credit interest received account £1,800, then pay it on to the estate.","Debit client cash (designated account) £1,800; credit the estate ledger client column £1,800.","Debit the estate ledger client column £1,800; credit client cash £1,800.","Debit interest payable account £1,800; credit business cash £1,800."],"a":2,"e":"With a separate designated client account, the bank credits interest directly to the account held for that client, so it is the client's money from the start. It is recorded as a receipt of client money: debit client cash (the designated account) and credit the client ledger client column. The interest payable and business column entries are used for a general client account."},
 {"t":"interest-breaches","q":"Kemp & Co holds money for Mr Lamb in its general client account. Under its interest policy it owes him £90 interest and decides to pay this to him directly by a payment from business account.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit interest payable £90; credit client cash £90.","Debit client cash £90; credit Mr Lamb's ledger client column £90.","Debit Mr Lamb's ledger client column £90; credit client cash £90.","Debit interest payable £90, credit Mr Lamb's ledger business column £90; then debit Mr Lamb's ledger business column £90, credit business cash £90.","Debit business cash £90; credit interest payable £90."],"a":3,"e":"With a general client account, the bank pays interest to the firm, so the interest paid to the client is the firm's expense. First record what the firm owes: debit interest payable and credit the ledger business column. Then record the payment: debit the ledger business column and credit business cash. Client cash is not involved because the payment comes from business account."},
 {"t":"interest-breaches","q":"Lowe Solicitors discovers that, because of an error by its bank, £20,000 was debited from its client account in error, leaving a shortfall. The bank says it will investigate and refund the money within six weeks.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm must do?","o":["Wait for the bank's investigation, because the bank caused the shortfall.","Report the shortfall to the clients affected and ask them to wait.","Replace the £20,000 immediately from its own money, and recover it from the bank later.","Note the shortfall for the next accountant's report.","Replace the shortfall from its own money only if the bank refuses to refund it after investigating."],"a":2,"e":"Any money improperly withdrawn from client account must be replaced immediately (r.6.1), using the firm's own money, whether or not the firm was at fault. The firm then pursues the bank for the refund. Waiting for the bank leaves clients' money unprotected and is a continuing breach."},
 {"t":"interest-breaches","q":"Mercer LLP's cashier mistakenly pays £20 too much from one client's ledger when paying a search fee. It is spotted at the next routine reconciliation two weeks later and replaced from business account the same day. There is no pattern of similar errors.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligations?","o":["It must report the breach to the SRA immediately, because all breaches must be reported.","It need take no further action, because the money has been replaced.","It must obtain a qualified accountant's report.","It must report the breach to the client and to the SRA, because client money was paid out in error.","It must correct and record the breach, but it is unlikely to be a serious breach that must be reported."],"a":4,"e":"All breaches must be corrected promptly on discovery (r.6.1), and the firm should keep a record, such as a breach log, to demonstrate compliance. Only serious breaches must be reported to the SRA by the COFA (Code for Firms para 9.2). A small, isolated, promptly corrected error is unlikely to be serious, but replacing the money does not erase the breach."},
 {"t":"interest-breaches","q":"Nolan & Co's COFA discovers that a partner transferred £60,000 from client account to the firm's business account to reduce the firm's overdraft before its bank's review. The partner says it was only for a week and has now been paid back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the COFA's obligation?","o":["To record the breach in the firm's breach log only, as the money has been repaid in full and no client suffered any loss.","To report the matter to the SRA only if a client complains.","To wait for the next accountant's report.","To report the matter promptly to the SRA, because misusing client money for the firm's purposes is a serious breach.","To report it to the SRA only if the partner refuses to resign."],"a":3,"e":"The COFA must ensure that a prompt report is made to the SRA of any facts or matters reasonably believed to be capable of amounting to a serious breach of the Accounts Rules (Code for Firms para 9.2). Using client money for the firm's own benefit is misappropriation and is always serious. Repayment does not remove the duty to report."},
 {"t":"interest-breaches","q":"Owen Solicitors discovers that it paid out £1,200 too much from client account for Ms Price, so her client ledger shows a debit balance of £1,200. The firm immediately transfers £1,200 from business account to client account to correct this.\n\nWhich of the following describes the balances on Ms Price's ledger after the correction?","o":["Client column nil; business column nil.","Client column nil; business column £1,200 DR.","Client column £1,200 CR; business column nil.","Client column £1,200 DR; business column £1,200 CR.","Client column nil; business column £1,200 CR."],"a":1,"e":"The correction is a business-to-client transfer. Client side: debit client cash and credit the ledger client column £1,200, bringing it from £1,200 DR back to nil. Business side: debit the ledger business column and credit business cash £1,200, so the business column shows £1,200 DR: Ms Price owes the firm that sum, which it can seek to recover."},
 {"t":"interest-breaches","q":"During an internal review, Patrick & Co finds that £3,000 a client sent on account of costs six weeks ago was paid into business account in error. The firm proposes to deal with it by sending the client a bill for £3,000 now, so that the money becomes the firm's and no transfer is needed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm must do?","o":["Transfer the £3,000 to client account immediately and record the breach; a later bill cannot cure it.","Proceed as proposed, because delivering a bill makes the £3,000 the firm's money, so no transfer is needed at all.","Do nothing, provided the client does not complain.","Return the £3,000 to the client and close the matter.","Transfer the £3,000 to client account within 14 days."],"a":0,"e":"Money improperly withheld from client account must be paid in immediately (r.6.1). The money was client money when received (r.2.1(d)), and keeping it in business account breached rr.2.3 and 4.1. Delivering a bill later cannot regularise the past breach; the firm must correct it, record it and consider whether it is serious enough to report."},
 {"t":"records-reconciliation","q":"Quentin LLP reconciles its client account every six weeks, because that fits its staff rota. Each reconciliation is signed off by a partner and differences are investigated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether this complies with the Accounts Rules?","o":["It complies, because reconciliations are carried out regularly and signed off by a partner.","It complies, because the rules require reconciliation only every two months.","It does not comply, because reconciliations must be carried out weekly.","It complies, provided the accountant's report is unqualified.","It does not comply, because reconciliations must be carried out at least every five weeks."],"a":4,"e":"Rule 8.3 requires a reconciliation of all client accounts at least every five weeks. Every six weeks is too infrequent, even if the process is otherwise sound; monthly reconciliation would comply. Weekly reconciliation is good practice but not required."},
 {"t":"records-reconciliation","q":"Reeve & Co obtains bank statements for its client accounts every four weeks. For its business account it relies on online banking and downloads a statement only once a quarter.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It complies, because bank statements are required only for client accounts.","It does not comply, because statements must be obtained at least every five weeks for business accounts too.","It complies, because the business account holds only firm money.","It does not comply, because statements must be obtained weekly.","It complies, provided the business account is also reconciled at least every five weeks alongside the client accounts."],"a":1,"e":"Rule 8.2 requires statements to be obtained at least every five weeks for all client accounts and business accounts held or operated by the firm. A quarterly business account statement is not enough. The business account does not need a five-weekly reconciliation under r.8.3, but the statement requirement still applies."},
 {"t":"records-reconciliation","q":"At the end of the month, Sharpe Solicitors' client bank statement shows a balance of £80,000. There is an unpresented cheque of £5,000 to HM Revenue & Customs and an outstanding lodgement of £2,000 received late on the last day.\n\nWhat is the adjusted bank balance?","o":["£83,000","£87,000","£80,000","£77,000","£73,000"],"a":3,"e":"The bank balance is adjusted by deducting unpresented cheques (payments made and recorded but not yet cleared by the bank) and adding outstanding lodgements (receipts recorded but not yet shown by the bank): £80,000 - £5,000 + £2,000 = £77,000. This must then agree with the cash book balance and the total of client ledger balances."},
 {"t":"records-reconciliation","q":"Thorpe & Co's monthly three-way reconciliation shows that the adjusted bank balance, the cash book and the total of client ledger balances all agree at £150,000. However, the list of balances shows that one client's ledger has a debit balance of £2,500 on its client column, offset by the credit balances of other clients.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is no problem, because the three figures agree.","There is no breach, provided the client with the debit balance repays the firm within a month of the reconciliation.","There is a breach, because other clients' money has been used, and the firm must replace £2,500 immediately.","There is a breach only if the overall client account is overdrawn.","There is no breach, because debit balances may be netted off against credit balances."],"a":2,"e":"A reconciliation that agrees overall can hide a breach. A debit balance on one client's ledger means the firm has paid out more than it held for that client, using other clients' money (r.5.3). It must not be netted off; the firm must replace the shortfall immediately from its own money (r.6.1) and investigate."},
 {"t":"records-reconciliation","q":"Upton LLP has stored its client ledgers, cash books, reconciliations and bank statements electronically. Its IT manager proposes to delete all accounting records more than three years old to save storage costs.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It is not permitted, because accounting records must be kept securely for at least six years.","It is permitted, provided the records are first printed.","It is permitted, because records need be kept only until the next accountant's report has been obtained.","It is not permitted, because accounting records must be kept for 15 years.","It is permitted for records of matters that are closed."],"a":0,"e":"Rule 13.1 requires all accounting records to be stored securely and retained for at least six years. That covers ledgers, cash books, lists of balances, reconciliations, bank statements and bills. Records of closed matters are not excepted, and electronic storage is fine if it is secure."},
 {"t":"records-reconciliation","q":"Vernon Solicitors' cashier writes up the client ledgers once a month, working from the bank statements when they arrive. Between updates, fee earners check balances by phoning the bank.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether this complies with the Accounts Rules?","o":["It complies, because the ledgers are updated every month.","It does not comply, because client money records must be accurate, contemporaneous and chronological.","It complies, provided the ledgers agree with the bank statements every month once the cashier has written them up.","It complies, because the bank statements are the firm's primary records.","It does not comply, because ledgers must be written up by a partner."],"a":1,"e":"Rule 8.1 requires accurate, contemporaneous (made at the time) and chronological records of client money. Writing up ledgers monthly from bank statements means the firm cannot know at any moment how much it holds for each client, which is essential to checking whether there are sufficient funds before a withdrawal (r.5.3). Who writes up the ledgers is not prescribed."},
 {"t":"records-reconciliation","q":"Walker & Co's reconciliation shows that the total of client ledger balances exceeds the adjusted bank balance by £300. The cashier cannot find the reason and notes that the accountant will look at it at the year-end audit in four months' time.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It is acceptable, because the difference is small.","It is acceptable, provided the unexplained difference is shown and explained in the accountant's report at the year end.","It is not acceptable, because only the SRA may investigate differences.","It is acceptable, because the reconciliation was completed within five weeks.","It is not acceptable, because differences must be investigated and resolved promptly, and any shortfall replaced."],"a":4,"e":"Rule 8.3 requires the firm to promptly investigate and resolve any differences shown by a reconciliation. A ledger total higher than the adjusted bank balance suggests a possible shortfall: the firm owes clients more than it holds. If client money is missing, it must be replaced immediately (r.6.1). The size of the difference does not change the duty."},
 {"t":"records-reconciliation","q":"Xander LLP carries out a monthly check comparing its client bank statement with its client cash book. Both figures always agree, and the check is signed off by the COFA.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether this complies with the reconciliation requirement?","o":["It complies, because the bank and cash book figures are compared every month.","It complies, because it is signed off by the COFA.","It complies, provided the bank statements are obtained at least every five weeks and kept for at least six years.","It does not comply, because the figures must also be compared with the total of client ledger balances.","It does not comply, because the reconciliation must be signed off by the SRA."],"a":3,"e":"Rule 8.3 requires a three-way reconciliation of the bank statement balance, the cash book balance and the client ledger total (from the list of balances under r.8.1(b)). Comparing only the bank and cash book would not show money owed to clients that is missing, or overdrawn ledgers. Sign-off by the COFA or a manager is required but is not enough on its own."},
 {"t":"records-reconciliation","q":"Yeoman & Co records all bills it delivers in a separate 'fee book' maintained by its accounts department. The bills are not entered on the individual client ledgers.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether this complies with the Accounts Rules?","o":["It does not comply, because bills must also be recorded on the business side of each client's ledger.","It complies, because the fee book is a readily accessible central record of all the bills the firm delivers.","It complies, because bills do not involve the movement of money.","It does not comply, because bills must be recorded on the client side of the ledger.","It complies, provided the fee book is kept for six years."],"a":0,"e":"Rule 8.1(a) requires client ledgers to record, on the business side, all receipts and payments that are not client money, and bills of costs. A central record of bills (r.8.4) is also required, but it does not replace entries on the client ledger. Bills are never entered on the client side."},
 {"t":"records-reconciliation","q":"Zeller Solicitors holds several large estate balances in separate designated client deposit accounts at a building society. The building society sends statements only once a quarter, and the firm has never asked for more frequent statements.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It complies, because separate designated deposit accounts are not subject to the five-weekly statement requirement.","It complies, because the building society decides how often to issue statements.","It does not comply, because statements must be obtained at least every five weeks for all client accounts.","It complies, provided the accounts are reconciled once a quarter.","It does not comply, because client money may not be held at a building society."],"a":2,"e":"Rule 8.2 requires the firm to obtain statements at least every five weeks for all client accounts, which include separate designated client accounts. It is the firm's responsibility to obtain them, for example by online access or by request. Client accounts can be held at a building society branch in England and Wales (r.3.1)."},
 {"t":"joint-own-tpma","q":"Anya, a partner at Bell & Co, is attorney for Mr Carr under a lasting power of attorney. She has been signing cheques on Mr Carr's own bank account to pay his care home fees. She now transfers £40,000 from Mr Carr's account into the firm's client account to hold while she sells his house.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the rules that apply to the £40,000 held in client account?","o":["Only the statement, reconciliation and bills record rules apply, as they would to a client's own account.","None of the Accounts Rules apply, because Anya holds the money as attorney.","Only the rules on joint accounts apply.","It is client money held by a manager as attorney, so all the client money rules in Part 2 apply.","The Accounts Rules apply only if Mr Carr is a client of the firm."],"a":3,"e":"While money stays in the client's own account, only rr.8.2, 8.3 and 8.4 apply (r.10). Once it is moved into the firm's client account, it is client money held as the holder of a specified office or appointment, such as donee of a power of attorney (r.2.1(c)), and the full Part 2 rules apply."},
 {"t":"joint-own-tpma","q":"Delia, a partner at Eden LLP, is appointed by the Court of Protection as deputy for Mr Ford and operates his bank account as signatory. In a separate matter, Eden holds a retention in a joint account in the names of Eden and another firm of solicitors.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the main difference in the firm's obligations for the two accounts?","o":["Mr Ford's account is subject to all the Part 2 rules, while the joint account is subject to none.","The joint account must be reconciled at least every five weeks; Mr Ford's account does not need to be reconciled.","Both accounts must be paid into the firm's client account.","Neither account requires bank statements to be obtained.","Mr Ford's account must be reconciled at least every five weeks; the joint account does not need to be reconciled."],"a":4,"e":"For a client's own account operated as signatory (r.10), the firm must obtain statements, reconcile at least every five weeks and keep a central record of bills. For a joint account (r.9), only statements and the bills record apply; there is no reconciliation requirement. Neither must be paid into client account."},
 {"t":"joint-own-tpma","q":"Fraser Solicitors acts for Mrs Grant in a neighbour dispute. As part of a settlement, £10,000 is held in a deposit account in the joint names of Fraser Solicitors and Mrs Grant's neighbour, pending completion of agreed works.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the money must be held in Fraser's client account?","o":["Yes, because it is held in the course of acting for a client.","No, because only the statements and bills record rules apply to money the firm holds jointly with a third party.","Yes, because it is held for a third party.","No, because the Accounts Rules do not apply to money held jointly.","No, but the joint account must be reconciled at least every five weeks in the same way as the firm's client account."],"a":1,"e":"Where, when acting in a client's matter, the firm holds money jointly with the client or a third party, Part 2 does not apply except r.8.2 (statements at least every five weeks) and r.8.4 (bills record) (r.9). So the money need not be in client account and no reconciliation is required, though the firm must still obtain an accountant's report unless exempt."},
 {"t":"joint-own-tpma","q":"Gill & Co, a small firm doing fixed-fee conveyancing, wants to stop holding client money altogether. It proposes to use a third party managed account (TPMA) operated by an FCA-regulated payment provider for all client payments, including its own fees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect if the firm sets up the TPMA arrangement in accordance with the rules?","o":["The firm will still need a client account for its fees.","The firm will need SRA authorisation for each payment out of the TPMA.","The firm will still need to reconcile the TPMA at least every five weeks under r.8.3, as if it were a client account.","The firm will not hold client money, so it may need neither a client account nor an accountant's report.","The firm will be treated as holding the money jointly with the provider."],"a":3,"e":"A TPMA lets payments be made through an account held by a regulated payment provider, so the firm does not receive or hold client money (r.11.1(a)). If it holds no other client money and operates no joint or client's own accounts, it does not need a client account and the accountant's report duty in r.12.1 is not triggered. The firm must obtain regular statements from the provider (r.11.2)."},
 {"t":"joint-own-tpma","q":"Harlow LLP uses a third party managed account (TPMA) for its clients. Its engagement letter, sent after instructions are accepted, mentions the TPMA but does not say who bears the provider's fees or explain the client's right to dispute the firm's payment requests.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It complies, because the TPMA means the firm does not hold client money.","It complies, because the provider's fees are a matter for the provider.","It does not comply, because before accepting instructions the firm must ensure the client understands the TPMA terms, including fees and dispute rights.","It complies, provided all the information about the TPMA, including the provider's fees and the right to dispute payments, is given before the first payment is made.","It does not comply, because a TPMA can be used only with SRA approval."],"a":2,"e":"A firm may use a TPMA only if it takes reasonable steps to ensure, before accepting instructions, that the client is informed of and understands the terms of the arrangement, in particular how fees for its use will be paid and who will bear them, and the client's right to terminate the agreement and dispute payment requests (r.11.1(b)). Telling the client afterwards, or omitting these points, does not comply."},
 {"t":"joint-own-tpma","q":"Irving & Co uses a TPMA. To speed things up, it proposes that clients pay their completion money into the firm's own bank account, after which the firm will move the money into the TPMA.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the proposal?","o":["It is permitted, because the money ends up in the TPMA.","It is not permitted as a TPMA arrangement, because a TPMA must not result in the firm receiving or holding client money.","It is permitted, provided the money is moved into the TPMA within two working days.","It is permitted, provided the clients agree in writing to their money passing through the firm's own bank account first.","It is not permitted, because completion money can never be paid through a TPMA."],"a":1,"e":"A condition of using a TPMA is that it does not result in the firm receiving or holding the client's money (r.11.1(a)). If money passes through the firm's own account, it is client money which must go into client account (r.2.3), and holding it in a business account would breach r.4.1. Client consent does not change this."},
 {"t":"joint-own-tpma","q":"Jackson Solicitors uses a TPMA for all its client payments and holds no client money. The COFA asks what ongoing records duty the firm has in relation to the TPMA.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the duty?","o":["The firm must obtain regular statements from the TPMA provider and check they accurately reflect all transactions.","The firm has no duties, because it holds no client money.","The firm must reconcile the TPMA at least every five weeks and have each reconciliation signed off by a manager of the firm.","The firm must deliver the TPMA statements to the SRA every year.","The firm must keep a client ledger for each TPMA account in the same way as for client account."],"a":0,"e":"Rule 11.2 requires a firm using a TPMA to obtain regular statements from the provider and ensure that they accurately reflect all transactions on the account. The detailed client account rules, including the r.8.3 reconciliation, do not apply because the firm does not hold the money."},
 {"t":"joint-own-tpma","q":"Kezia is a solicitor and partner at Lane LLP. Separately from her work, she is attorney for her elderly mother under a lasting power of attorney, which she was given as a family member, and she signs cheques on her mother's bank account. The firm has no involvement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the Accounts Rules apply to the operation of her mother's account?","o":["No, because the rule on clients' own accounts applies only where the account is operated in the course of practice.","Yes, because the rule on clients' own accounts applies to any solicitor who signs cheques on someone else's bank account.","Yes, because Kezia is a manager of an authorised body.","Yes, but only the rules on bank statements.","No, because a power of attorney never falls within the Accounts Rules."],"a":0,"e":"Rule 10 applies where, in the course of practice, the firm operates a client's own account as signatory. Kezia acts as attorney in a personal family capacity, not in the course of her firm's practice, so the Accounts Rules do not apply. A power of attorney held through the firm's practice can fall within rr.2.1(c) and 10."},
 {"t":"joint-own-tpma","q":"Morgan & Co holds a retention of £25,000 in a joint deposit account in the names of Morgan & Co and the buyer's solicitors, pending a planning approval. The building society sends statements only every six months, and Morgan has not asked for more.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It complies, because joint accounts are outside the Accounts Rules.","It complies, because the other firm is responsible for the statements.","It complies, provided the money is moved to client account within a year.","It does not comply, because the joint account must be reconciled at least every five weeks, like a client account.","It does not comply, because the firm must obtain statements for the joint account at least every five weeks."],"a":4,"e":"For joint accounts, most of Part 2 does not apply, but r.8.2 (statements at least every five weeks) and r.8.4 (central record of bills) do (r.9). Morgan must obtain statements itself; it cannot rely on the other firm. There is no reconciliation requirement for joint accounts."},
 {"t":"joint-own-tpma","q":"Nash LLP's partner, as attorney under a lasting power of attorney, operates Mrs Oakley's own bank account. The firm sends Mrs Oakley (through the partner) bills for the work done, and pays them from Mrs Oakley's account. The bills are kept only on the matter file.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["It complies, because the money never passes through the firm's client account.","It does not comply, because a firm may never be paid from a client's own account.","It does not comply, because the firm must keep a central record of bills for work done in operating a client's own account.","It complies, because only bills paid from client account need a central record.","It does not comply, because the money must be transferred into client account before any of the firm's bills can be paid from it."],"a":2,"e":"When operating a client's own account as signatory, Part 2 does not apply except r.8.2 (statements at least every five weeks), r.8.3 (reconciliation at least every five weeks) and r.8.4 (central record of bills) (r.10). Keeping bills only on the matter file does not meet r.8.4. There is no rule requiring the money to pass through client account."},
 {"t":"accountants-reports","q":"Oliver & Co held client money throughout its accounting period, which ended on 31 December 2025. It is not exempt from the duty to obtain an accountant's report.\n\nBy when must the firm obtain its accountant's report?","o":["31 March 2026","30 June 2026","30 April 2026","31 December 2026","31 December 2025"],"a":1,"e":"A firm that held client money during an accounting period must obtain an accountant's report for that period within six months of the end of the period (r.12.1(a)). Six months after 31 December 2025 is 30 June 2026. The same deadline applies to delivering the report to the SRA, if it must be delivered."},
 {"t":"accountants-reports","q":"Parker LLP obtains its accountant's report for the year ended 30 April 2026 on time. The report is unqualified. The managing partner asks what the firm must do with it under the rules in force for the January 2027 assessment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligation?","o":["It must deliver the report to the SRA within six months of the end of the firm's accounting period.","It must keep the report and produce it if the SRA asks, but need not deliver it to the SRA.","It must deliver the report to the SRA within 28 days of receiving it.","It must send the report to every client for whom it held money.","It must publish the report on its website."],"a":1,"e":"Under r.12.1(b), only a report qualified to show that client money is, has been or is likely to be put at risk must be delivered to the SRA. An unqualified report is obtained and kept. In 2026 the SRA decided that all firms holding client money should submit their reports, but that change needs Legal Services Board approval and was not in force at the exam cut-off."},
 {"t":"accountants-reports","q":"Quinton Solicitors' accountant's report is qualified because, on two occasions, a reconciliation was signed off a few days late. The accountant states that no client money was put at risk.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm must deliver the report to the SRA?","o":["Yes, because all qualified reports must be delivered to the SRA.","Yes, because late reconciliations always put client money at risk, whatever the accountant states in the report.","Yes, but only if the firm has had a qualified report before.","No, because qualified reports are sent by the accountant, not the firm.","No, because a qualified report must be delivered only if client money is, has been or is likely to be at risk."],"a":4,"e":"The duty to deliver a report applies only where it is qualified to show a failure to comply such that money belonging to clients or third parties is, has been or is likely to be placed at risk (r.12.1(b)). A qualification for minor breaches that put no money at risk does not trigger delivery, though the firm must still obtain the report and correct the breaches."},
 {"t":"accountants-reports","q":"During its accounting period, the statement balances of Ross & Co's client account averaged £9,000. The highest balance was £300,000, on the day a large completion took place. The firm holds no LAA money.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm must obtain an accountant's report?","o":["Yes, because the maximum balance exceeded £250,000, so the low balance exemption does not apply.","No, because the high balance was only for one day.","No, because the average balance did not exceed £10,000.","Yes, because any firm that holds client money at any time during the period must obtain an accountant's report.","No, because the firm is a small firm."],"a":0,"e":"A firm is exempt under r.12.2(b) only if its client money statement balance does not exceed an average of £10,000 and a maximum of £250,000 during the period. Both limbs must be met. A single large completion took the maximum over £250,000, so the firm is not exempt."},
 {"t":"accountants-reports","q":"Most of the client money Stanley & Co holds is from the Legal Aid Agency. During the period it also received £1,000 on account of costs from one private client. Its client account statement balances averaged £30,000, with a maximum of £120,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm must obtain an accountant's report?","o":["Yes, because the legal aid exemption needs all client money to be LAA money, and its average balance exceeded £10,000.","No, because the private client money was under £10,000.","No, because almost all of its client money came from the Legal Aid Agency, so the legal aid exemption still applies to it.","No, because its maximum balance did not exceed £250,000.","Yes, because any firm holding LAA money must obtain a report."],"a":0,"e":"The legal aid exemption applies only if all the client money held or received during the period is from the Legal Aid Agency (r.12.2(a)). One private client's money takes the firm outside it. The low balance exemption (r.12.2(b)) is also unavailable, because the average of £30,000 exceeds £10,000, even though the maximum is within £250,000."},
 {"t":"accountants-reports","q":"Taylor LLP needs an accountant's report. To save money, it proposes to ask its long-serving bookkeeper, who holds a bookkeeping qualification but is not a chartered accountant, to prepare and sign the report.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether this is permitted?","o":["It is permitted, provided the bookkeeper is independent of the partners and has at least five years' experience of legal accounts.","It is permitted, provided the COFA countersigns the report.","It is not permitted, because the report must be signed by a chartered accountant who is, or works for, a registered auditor.","It is permitted, because the SRA does not specify who may prepare the report.","It is not permitted, because the report must be prepared by the SRA."],"a":2,"e":"Rule 12.5 requires the accountant's report to be prepared and signed by an accountant who is a member of one of the chartered accountancy bodies and who is, or works for, a registered auditor. A bookkeeper, however experienced, does not qualify, and countersignature by the COFA does not cure this."},
 {"t":"accountants-reports","q":"Upton & Co's client account balances fall within the low balance exemption, so it has not obtained an accountant's report. Following concerns raised by a former employee, the SRA writes to the firm requiring it to obtain and deliver a report for its last accounting period.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's position?","o":["It need not comply, because it is exempt under the rules.","It must comply only if the SRA pays the accountant's fees.","It need comply only if the former employee's concerns are proved, because an exempt firm cannot otherwise be required to obtain one.","It must comply, because the SRA may require a report on reasonable notice if it considers it in the public interest.","It need not comply until its next accounting period."],"a":3,"e":"Under r.12.4, the SRA may require a firm to obtain or deliver an accountant's report on reasonable notice if the firm ceases to operate as an authorised body and to hold or operate a client account, or if the SRA considers it in the public interest. An exemption does not prevent such a requirement."},
 {"t":"accountants-reports","q":"Vane Solicitors is preparing for its annual accountant's report. A partner suggests not telling the accountant about an old client account at another bank, which holds small dormant balances, so as to keep the fee down.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligation?","o":["It may omit the account, because the balances are small.","It may omit the account, because dormant accounts are outside the rules.","It need tell the accountant only about accounts that are overdrawn.","It must give the accountant details of all accounts held or operated in connection with its practice during the period.","It may omit the account, provided it tells the SRA separately about the dormant balances held in it and their total value."],"a":3,"e":"Rule 12.8 requires the firm to provide the accountant with details of all accounts held or operated in connection with the practice at any bank, building society or other financial institution during the period, and all other information and documentation the accountant needs. Withholding an account would also raise serious conduct issues about honesty."},
 {"t":"accountants-reports","q":"Wells LLP's own records show that the balance of money it held for clients averaged £9,500 during its accounting period. Because of timing differences, the balances shown on its client bank statements averaged £10,800. The maximum statement balance was £90,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm is exempt from obtaining an accountant's report?","o":["Yes, because its own accounting records show an average client balance below £10,000 throughout the accounting period.","Yes, because the maximum balance was below £250,000.","No, because the exemption is assessed on bank statement balances, and the average statement balance exceeded £10,000.","Yes, because the difference is due only to timing.","No, because no firm holding more than £50,000 can be exempt."],"a":2,"e":"Under r.12.3, the 'statement or passbook balance' used for the exemption is the total balance of client accounts (and any joint and clients' own accounts) as shown by the bank statements, not the firm's own records. The statement average of £10,800 exceeds £10,000, so the firm is not exempt even though its maximum was within £250,000."},
 {"t":"accountants-reports","q":"An accountant prepared Xenon & Co's accountant's report without checking any of the firm's reconciliations, and failed to spot that several client ledgers had been overdrawn for months. The SRA investigates.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the SRA's power in relation to the accountant?","o":["It has no power over the accountant, because the accountant is regulated only by an accountancy body and never by the SRA in any respect.","It may disqualify the accountant only after a criminal conviction.","It may fine the accountant directly.","It may require the firm to dismiss its COFA.","It may disqualify the accountant from preparing reports if satisfied the accountant failed to exercise due care and skill."],"a":4,"e":"Under r.12.6, the SRA may disqualify an accountant from preparing reports if the accountant has been found guilty of professional misconduct by their professional body, or if the SRA is satisfied that the accountant failed to exercise due care and skill in preparing a report. A criminal conviction is not required."},
 {"t":"conveyancing-accounts","q":"York & Co acts for Mrs Abbott on her sale and her related purchase. The sale contract incorporates the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition) unamended. On exchange, the buyer's solicitors send a £35,000 deposit. Mrs Abbott wants it used as the deposit on her purchase, which is exchanging at the same time.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may use the deposit in this way?","o":["No, because a deposit received on exchange must always be held by the seller's solicitor as stakeholder until completion.","No, because a deposit can be used only after it has been held for 14 days.","No, because the deposit is the buyer's money until completion.","Yes, but only with the SRA's permission.","Yes, because under the Standard Conditions a seller may use the deposit as a deposit on a related home purchase."],"a":4,"e":"Under SC 2.2.5, a seller buying another home in England and Wales may use all or part of the deposit as the deposit on that purchase. Only a deposit not used in that way is held by the seller's conveyancer as stakeholder (SC 2.2.6). The release is for the purpose for which the money is held, so no SRA permission is needed."},
 {"t":"conveyancing-accounts","q":"Brook Solicitors acts for the seller, Mr Cole. On exchange it received a £20,000 deposit, held as stakeholder and credited to a stakeholder ledger. Completion has now taken place.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries for releasing the deposit to Mr Cole?","o":["Debit stakeholder ledger client column £20,000; credit client cash £20,000; debit client cash £20,000; credit Mr Cole's ledger client column £20,000.","Debit client cash £20,000; credit Mr Cole's ledger client column £20,000.","Debit Mr Cole's ledger client column £20,000; credit stakeholder ledger client column £20,000.","Debit stakeholder ledger client column £20,000; credit Mr Cole's ledger client column £20,000. No cash account entries.","Debit stakeholder ledger business column £20,000; credit Mr Cole's ledger business column £20,000."],"a":3,"e":"On completion the deposit becomes the seller's money. Because it stays in client account, this is an inter-client transfer: debit the stakeholder ledger client column and credit the seller's ledger client column, with no cash account entries. The business column is not used because this is client money."},
 {"t":"conveyancing-accounts","q":"Carter LLP acts for Ms Dean and her lender on her purchase. The lender sends the £180,000 mortgage advance two days before completion. The seller then delays completion indefinitely. The lender's instructions require the advance to be returned if completion does not take place within five working days of receipt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm must do when the five days pass?","o":["Hold the advance until completion, because Ms Dean is the firm's main client.","Place the advance in a separate designated client account until completion.","Transfer the advance to Ms Dean, as the borrower.","Use the advance to pay the firm's costs, which have been billed.","Return the advance to the lender as its instructions require."],"a":4,"e":"Where the firm also acts for the lender, the mortgage advance is client money held for the lender until completion, and it may be used only as the lender's instructions allow. If completion is delayed and the lender requires the money back, the firm must return it. Using it for anything else, including the firm's costs, would be an improper withdrawal."},
 {"t":"conveyancing-accounts","q":"Dixon & Co acts for Mr Evans on his purchase for £250,000. He paid a £25,000 deposit on exchange. His lender will advance £180,000. SDLT is £2,500, the Land Registry fee is £150, and the firm has delivered a bill for £900 plus VAT of £180, all to be paid from client account. The firm holds nothing else for him.\n\nHow much must Mr Evans send the firm before completion?","o":["£45,000","£47,500","£48,730","£73,730","£48,550"],"a":2,"e":"Balance of the price: £250,000 - £25,000 = £225,000, less the £180,000 advance = £45,000. Add SDLT £2,500, Land Registry fee £150 and costs including VAT £1,080: £45,000 + £2,500 + £150 + £1,080 = £48,730. The firm needs cleared funds for the whole amount so that no payment overdraws his ledger."},
 {"t":"conveyancing-accounts","q":"Ellison Solicitors completes Ms Faulkner's purchase. It holds £6,000 on her ledger to pay Stamp Duty Land Tax (SDLT) and the Land Registry fee.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how and when the SDLT should be paid?","o":["Within 30 days of completion, from business account, using the principal method.","Within 14 days of completion, from client account, debiting her ledger client column and crediting client cash.","Within 14 days of completion, from business account, with VAT then added to the SDLT figure in the firm's final bill.","Within six months of completion, from client account.","Before completion, from client account, crediting Ms Faulkner's ledger client column."],"a":1,"e":"The SDLT return must be filed and the tax paid within 14 days of completion (the effective date). SDLT is not a VAT supply and is paid as the client's agent, so it is paid from client money held for her: debit the ledger client column and credit client cash. Business account and VAT entries are inappropriate."},
 {"t":"conveyancing-accounts","q":"Foster LLP acts for Mrs Green on her sale. Before completion, the firm gave the buyer's solicitors an undertaking to redeem Mrs Green's mortgage from the sale proceeds. On completion it receives £400,000. Mrs Green asks the firm to send her the full £400,000 so that she can pay off the mortgage herself.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm should do?","o":["Send her the £400,000, as it is her money, she has given clear instructions and she is entitled to the proceeds.","Send her the £400,000, provided she confirms her instructions in writing.","Hold all the proceeds until she agrees to the redemption.","Redeem the mortgage as the undertaking requires, and then promptly account to her for the balance.","Send half the proceeds to her and hold the rest."],"a":3,"e":"Breach of an undertaking is a serious conduct matter, and the client's instructions cannot override it. The firm must use the proceeds to redeem the mortgage as it undertook, then pay the balance (after any billed costs and other authorised payments) to Mrs Green promptly (r.2.5). Holding the whole balance would risk a banking facility."},
 {"t":"conveyancing-accounts","q":"Gibson & Co acts for Mr Hughes on his sale and holds the sale proceeds. The estate agent sends its invoice for £5,000 plus VAT of £1,000, addressed to Mr Hughes, and he asks the firm to pay it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm should pay it?","o":["From client account, debiting Mr Hughes' ledger client column £6,000 and crediting client cash £6,000.","From business account, debiting Mr Hughes' ledger business column £5,000 and HMRC £1,000.","From client account, debiting Mr Hughes' ledger client column £5,000, with the VAT reclaimed by the firm.","From business account, debiting Mr Hughes' ledger business column £6,000.","From client account, but only after the firm has added VAT to its own bill for the agent's fee."],"a":0,"e":"An invoice addressed to the client is paid under the agency method: the firm does not reclaim the VAT and adds no VAT on its own bill for it. As the firm holds sufficient client money and has the client's instructions, it pays the gross £6,000 from client account: debit the ledger client column, credit client cash."},
 {"t":"conveyancing-accounts","q":"Hayward Solicitors acts for Mr Ingham, who is buying a new home before his sale completes. His bank lends him £60,000 under a bridging loan to pay the deposit, on terms that the firm undertakes to repay it from his sale proceeds. The loan is paid to the firm.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the bridging loan should be dealt with?","o":["It is the firm's money, because the firm gave an undertaking, so it is paid into business account.","It is client money, to be repaid from the sale proceeds as the undertaking requires.","It is client money held for the bank, and the firm must pay the bank's interest from business account.","It is client money, but it may be repaid only once Mr Ingham's purchase has completed.","It is not client money, because it is a loan."],"a":1,"e":"A bridging loan paid to the firm for the client is client money: debit client cash, credit the client ledger client column. It is repaid from the sale proceeds (debit ledger client column, credit client cash) in line with the firm's undertaking, before any balance goes to the client. Interest on the loan is the client's liability, not the firm's."},
 {"t":"conveyancing-accounts","q":"Ingle LLP acts for Ms Jones on her purchase. It holds £5,000 for her in client account. It obtains a local authority search for her purchase for £200 and receives the council's invoice.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm should pay the search fee?","o":["From client account, because the firm holds enough money for Ms Jones and the search is for her purchase.","From client account, debiting Ms Jones' ledger client column £200.","From business account, as part of the firm's own supply, with VAT charged on it in the firm's bill.","From business account, debiting Ms Jones' ledger client column.","From client account, after first delivering a bill for it."],"a":2,"e":"Property search fees obtained and used by the solicitor in advising the client are treated as part of the solicitor's own supply (HMRC Revenue and Customs Brief 6 (2020)), so they are principal method items. They must be paid from business account, even if client money is held, and VAT is charged on them in the firm's bill."},
 {"t":"conveyancing-accounts","q":"Kent & Co acts for Mr Lyons and for his lender on his purchase. The mortgage advance of £200,000 arrives a week before completion. The firm has delivered its bill of £1,200 to Mr Lyons, but he has not yet sent the rest of the completion money. The cashier proposes to transfer the £1,200 costs from the advance now.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the proposal?","o":["It is not permitted, because the advance is held for the lender until completion and may be used only as it allows.","It is permitted, because the advance is credited to Mr Lyons' ledger.","It is permitted, because a bill has been delivered.","It is permitted, provided Mr Lyons agrees.","It is not permitted, because a firm's costs may never be taken from client money until after completion has taken place."],"a":0,"e":"Where the firm acts for the lender, the mortgage advance is held for the lender until completion and may be used only for the purpose the lender's instructions allow, which is completing the purchase. Taking the firm's costs from it would be an improper use of the lender's money, even though a bill has been delivered to the buyer. Costs can be taken from money held for the client after a bill."},
 {"t":"probate-accounts","q":"Lamb Solicitors acts for the executors of Mrs Moss. Inheritance tax of £60,000 must be paid before the grant of probate. The executors arrange for Mrs Moss's bank to pay HMRC directly from her account under the direct payment scheme.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the entries in the firm's books?","o":["Debit the estate ledger client column £60,000; credit client cash £60,000.","Debit the estate ledger business column £60,000; credit business cash £60,000, as the bank paid HMRC.","Debit the estate ledger client column £60,000; credit HMRC £60,000.","Debit client cash £60,000; credit the estate ledger client column £60,000.","No entries are needed, because the money never passes through the firm's accounts."],"a":4,"e":"Under the direct payment scheme, the deceased's bank pays HMRC directly from the deceased's account. The money never reaches the firm, so there is nothing to record in its client or business books. If the firm had received and paid the money, client account entries would be needed."},
 {"t":"probate-accounts","q":"Mason & Co acts for the executors of Mr Nolan. To pay inheritance tax before the grant, the executors borrow £40,000 from a bank, which pays the loan to the firm. The firm then pays HMRC.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["No entries are needed, because the loan is the executors' liability.","Receipt: debit business cash, credit estate ledger business column £40,000. Payment: debit estate ledger business column, credit business cash £40,000.","Receipt: debit client cash, credit estate ledger client column £40,000. Payment: debit estate ledger client column, credit client cash £40,000.","Receipt: debit client cash, credit estate ledger client column £40,000. Payment: debit estate ledger business column, credit business cash £40,000.","Receipt: debit estate ledger client column, credit client cash £40,000. Payment: debit client cash, credit estate ledger client column £40,000."],"a":2,"e":"A loan paid to the firm for the executors is client money: debit client cash and credit the estate ledger client column. Paying HMRC from it is a withdrawal for the purpose for which it is held: debit the estate ledger client column and credit client cash. Business account entries are not appropriate because client money is used."},
 {"t":"probate-accounts","q":"Norton LLP acts for the executors of Mrs Owen. Before it holds any money for the estate, it pays the £300 probate application fee from its business account.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit the estate ledger client column £300; credit client cash £300.","Debit the estate ledger business column £300; credit business cash £300.","Debit the estate ledger business column £250 and HMRC £50; credit business cash £300.","Debit business cash £300; credit the estate ledger business column £300.","Debit profit costs £300; credit business cash £300."],"a":1,"e":"The probate application fee is a court fee with no VAT, paid as the client's agent. As the firm holds no money for the estate, it pays from business account: debit the estate ledger business column (the estate owes the firm) and credit business cash. The client column cannot be used because nothing is held."},
 {"t":"probate-accounts","q":"Oakes & Co acts for the executors of Mr Pike. It places the statutory notices to creditors in The Gazette and a local newspaper. The newspaper's invoice for £250 plus VAT of £50 is addressed to the firm. The firm holds £50,000 for the estate in client account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm should pay the invoice?","o":["From client account, debiting the estate ledger client column £300.","From business account, debiting the estate ledger business column £250 and HMRC £50, and crediting business cash £300.","From client account, debiting the estate ledger client column £250, with the £50 VAT element paid from business account.","From business account, debiting the estate ledger business column £300.","From client account, after delivering a bill to the executors."],"a":1,"e":"Because the invoice is addressed to the firm, the principal method applies: the firm received the supply, reclaims the input VAT, and must pay from business account, even though it holds client money. Entries: debit the ledger business column with the net amount, debit HMRC with the VAT, credit business cash with the gross amount. The firm then charges VAT on the £250 in its bill."},
 {"t":"probate-accounts","q":"Parsons Solicitors acts for the executors of Mrs Quayle. The estate is worth £800,000 and the executors have collected in most of the assets, but final income tax figures and the firm's final costs are not yet known. The residuary beneficiaries ask for an early payment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm may do on the executors' instructions?","o":["Nothing, because no payment may be made to beneficiaries until the estate accounts are approved.","Make an interim distribution only with the SRA's authorisation.","Distribute the whole residue now, and recover any shortfall from the beneficiaries later.","Make an interim distribution, keeping back a retention for outstanding liabilities such as income tax and costs.","Distribute the whole residue now, and pay any later liabilities that arise out of the firm's own business account."],"a":3,"e":"Paying legacies and residue is a withdrawal for the purpose for which the money is held, or on the PRs' instructions (r.5.1). Interim distributions may be made before administration is finished, keeping a retention to cover outstanding liabilities. Distributing everything would risk leaving the estate unable to meet its liabilities, and SRA authorisation is not needed."},
 {"t":"probate-accounts","q":"Quinn & Co acts for the two executors of Mr Reid's estate. Mr Reid's daughter, who is entitled to a legacy of £20,000 and is not an executor, phones the firm and asks it to pay her legacy today because she needs the money. The executors have not authorised any distributions.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm should do?","o":["Not pay the legacy unless and until it is authorised by the executors, who are the firm's clients.","Pay the legacy, provided the estate ledger holds enough money.","Pay the legacy, because the daughter is entitled to it under the will and the estate holds enough money.","Pay half the legacy as an interim distribution.","Pay the legacy, provided the daughter confirms her request in writing."],"a":0,"e":"In estate administration, the personal representatives are the firm's clients, not the beneficiaries. A withdrawal must be for the purpose for which money is held or on the client's instructions (r.5.1), and here the executors have not authorised any distribution. The daughter's entitlement under the will does not give the firm authority to pay her."},
 {"t":"probate-accounts","q":"Rhodes LLP holds £350,000 for the executors of Mrs Shaw in its general client account for seven months while the house is sold and the estate administered. There is no agreement with the executors about interest. At the end, the firm proposes to keep the interest it has earned because the estate's legal costs were modest.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The firm must account for a fair sum of interest, unless the executors agreed otherwise in writing with informed consent.","The firm may keep the interest, because estates are not entitled to interest.","The firm may keep the interest, because the bank paid it to the firm.","The firm must pay the estate all of the interest it earned, because the money belonged to the estate throughout the seven months.","The firm must pay interest only if the money was held for more than a year."],"a":0,"e":"A firm must account for a fair sum of interest on client money (r.7.1), unless a different arrangement is agreed in writing with the client after being given sufficient information for informed consent (r.7.2). Large sums held for months, as here, will usually attract interest. A fair sum need not be all the interest earned."},
 {"t":"probate-accounts","q":"Simpson & Co has completed the administration of Mr Tait's estate, and the executors have approved the estate accounts. The sole residuary beneficiary asks the firm to keep her £180,000 inheritance in client account 'for a year or so' and to pay her monthly household bills from it in the meantime.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the firm should do?","o":["Agree, because the beneficiary is entitled to the money and has given instructions.","Agree, provided the firm pays her a fair sum of interest and keeps the money in a separate designated client account.","Agree to hold the money but refuse to pay her bills.","Agree, provided the executors also consent.","Decline, and distribute the residue promptly, because holding it to pay her bills would be a banking facility."],"a":4,"e":"Once the estate accounts are approved, the residue must be distributed promptly as there is no longer a proper reason to hold it (r.2.5). Holding money after the matter has ended and paying household bills has no link to any regulated service and would breach the ban on banking facilities (r.3.3). Paying interest or obtaining consent does not cure this."},
 {"t":"probate-accounts","q":"Turner Solicitors acts for the executors of Mrs Usher, and separately for her son Vic on the purchase of a flat. Vic is entitled to a £25,000 legacy. Vic and the executors both instruct the firm in writing to use the legacy towards Vic's purchase. The firm transfers £25,000 from the estate ledger to Vic's ledger.\n\nWhich of the following shows the correct entries?","o":["Debit Vic's ledger client column £25,000; credit estate ledger client column £25,000.","Debit estate ledger client column £25,000; credit client cash £25,000; debit client cash £25,000; credit Vic's ledger business column £25,000.","Debit estate ledger client column £25,000; credit Vic's ledger client column £25,000. No cash account entries.","Debit estate ledger business column £25,000; credit Vic's ledger business column £25,000.","Debit client cash £25,000; credit Vic's ledger client column £25,000."],"a":2,"e":"A transfer of money held for one client so that it is held for another, with authority (here the executors' instructions, r.5.1(b)), is an inter-client transfer. The money stays in client account, so there is no cash entry: debit the estate ledger client column and credit Vic's ledger client column. The business columns are not involved."},
 {"t":"probate-accounts","q":"Underhill & Co holds £90,000 for the executors of Mr Vance. It delivers a bill to the executors for £3,000 plus VAT of £600. The fee earner tells the cashier to transfer £5,000 to business account 'to cover the bill and the work still to do on the estate'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the instruction?","o":["It is permitted, because the estate holds more than enough money.","It is permitted, because the executors have received a bill.","It is permitted, provided the firm refunds any excess to the estate at the end of the matter when final costs are known.","It is not permitted, because the firm may transfer only the specific sum in a bill or written notification, here £3,600.","It is not permitted, because costs can be taken only when the estate is wound up."],"a":3,"e":"Before taking costs from client money, the firm must give a bill or written notification of costs, and the transfer must be for the specific sum identified in it and covered by the amount held (r.4.3). The extra £1,400 for future work has not been billed, so it remains client money; taking it would be a breach requiring immediate replacement (r.6.1)."}
];

;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["trusts"] = [
 {"t":"certainties","q":"Ellie runs an online furniture business that is struggling. On her accountant's advice she opens a new bank account called 'Customer Prepayments Account' and pays into it every advance payment received from customers, telling her staff that the money must not be touched until each order is delivered. Her terms of business say nothing about trusts. Three months later the business becomes insolvent with £45,000 in the new account.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the customers' position?","o":["They are unsecured creditors, because the word 'trust' was never used in the terms of business or the account name.","They are unsecured creditors, because a trust of money can only be created by signed writing.","The £45,000 is held on trust for them, because Ellie's conduct in separating the money shows an intention to create a trust.","The £45,000 is held on trust for them, but only for those customers who were told about the separate account before they paid their deposits.","They have a proprietary claim only to the extent that their orders were cancelled before the insolvency."],"a":2,"e":"No special words are needed for certainty of intention: the court looks at the substance, including conduct. Paying customers' money into a separate account that is not to be touched until delivery shows an intention to hold it on trust (Re Kayford), so the customers take it ahead of the general creditors. A trust of money (personalty) needs no writing, and the beneficiaries do not need to know about the trust for it to exist."},
 {"t":"certainties","q":"Darren has a savings account in his sole name, into which he paid a personal injury award. Over several years he often told his partner Kim, 'this money is as much yours as mine', and they paid their joint lottery winnings into the account. They never mentioned a trust and never saw a lawyer. Darren has died. His will leaves everything to his brother, who claims the whole balance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kim's position?","o":["Darren held the account on trust for himself and Kim equally, so Kim is entitled to half the balance.","Kim has no claim, because Darren never used the word 'trust' or anything equivalent to it.","Kim has no claim, because a declaration of trust of a bank account must be in signed writing.","Kim is entitled only to the amount of the lottery winnings, because she contributed nothing else.","Kim is entitled to the whole balance, because Darren's words amounted to an immediate gift of the account."],"a":0,"e":"Informal words repeated over time, together with conduct such as paying joint winnings into the account, can show an intention to create a trust even though the word 'trust' is never used (Paul v Constance). 'As much yours as mine' points to equal shares, not an outright gift of the whole. A trust of money is personalty, so no writing is required."},
 {"t":"certainties","q":"Freya owns 80 cases of identical vintage claret stored in a bonded warehouse. She signs a document declaring that she holds '20 cases of my claret' on trust for her nephew Gethin. No cases are labelled or set aside for him. Freya is later declared bankrupt, and her trustee in bankruptcy claims all 80 cases.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gethin's position?","o":["He is entitled to 20 cases, because the cases are all identical, so it does not matter which particular cases are treated as his.","He is entitled to 20 cases, because the declaration was made in signed writing.","He is entitled to a one-quarter share of all 80 cases as a tenant in common.","He is entitled to 20 cases, because Freya declared herself trustee and so no transfer was needed.","He has no proprietary claim, because tangible goods must be separated from the bulk before a trust of them can exist."],"a":4,"e":"For tangible goods taken from a larger bulk, the subject matter is certain only once the goods are segregated (Re London Wine Co). Nothing was set aside, so no trust arose and all 80 cases belong to Freya's estate. The rule allowing an unsegregated trust of identical items (Hunter v Moss) applies only to intangible property such as shares of one class, and signed writing or self-declaration cannot cure uncertain subject matter."},
 {"t":"certainties","q":"Graham owns 1,000 ordinary shares in Hollin Ltd, a private company. He tells his daughter Isla, in front of witnesses, 'I now hold 200 of my Hollin shares on trust for you.' He does not say which 200 shares, and nothing is put in writing. Graham now denies that Isla has any interest.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is no trust, because Graham did not identify which 200 of his 1,000 shares were to be held for Isla, so the subject matter is uncertain.","There is a valid trust of 200 shares, because shares of the same class are identical and need not be separated.","There is no trust, because a declaration of trust of shares in a private company must be in writing.","There is a valid trust of all 1,000 shares, because the shares held for Isla cannot be separated from the rest.","There is no trust, because Graham has not transferred the shares to Isla by stock transfer form."],"a":1,"e":"Intangible property such as shares of the same class in one company does not need to be segregated: it does not matter which 200 shares are held for Isla (Hunter v Moss). Shares are personalty, so an oral declaration is enough. No transfer is needed because Graham declared himself trustee."},
 {"t":"certainties","q":"Hector's will leaves his two holiday cottages, Bramble Cottage and Ivy Cottage, to trustees 'to convey to my daughter Maria whichever one she chooses, and to convey the other to my daughter Jess'. Maria died a month before Hector, without having made any choice. Jess now asks the trustees to transfer a cottage to her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Jess's position?","o":["She is entitled to both cottages, because Maria's gift has lapsed.","She can choose one cottage, because the right of choice passes to her on Maria's death.","The trustees can choose which cottage she receives, because they have an implied discretion.","Her gift fails for uncertainty, because it is impossible to tell which cottage she was to receive.","She is entitled to a half share in each cottage, because equity treats beneficiaries' unclear shares as equal ones."],"a":3,"e":"In a fixed trust each beneficiary's share must be certain. Jess was to take whichever cottage Maria did not choose; with no choice made, Jess's share cannot be identified and her gift fails (Boyce v Boyce). The cottages are held on resulting trust for Hector's estate. The will gives neither Jess nor the trustees any power to choose."},
 {"t":"certainties","q":"Ivan's will leaves £200,000 to trustees 'to distribute among such of my good friends as my trustees in their absolute discretion select'. Ivan had a wide social circle. His residuary beneficiary, his sister, says the gift is void. The trustees point out that several people were plainly Ivan's close friends for many years.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift?","o":["It is valid, because it is enough that some people clearly fall within the class.","It is valid, because the trustees have an absolute discretion and so can decide for themselves which people counted as Ivan's good friends.","It is void, because the trustees cannot draw up a complete list of all Ivan's friends.","It is valid, because difficulty in proving who was a friend does not defeat a discretionary trust.","It is void, because 'good friends' is too vague a concept for it to be said of any person whether they are in the class."],"a":4,"e":"This is a discretionary trust, so the test is whether it can be said of any given person that they are or are not in the class (McPhail v Doulton). 'Good friends' is conceptually uncertain, so the test cannot be met and the trust fails. The complete list test applies only to fixed trusts, and the 'one clear case is enough' approach applies to individual gifts (Re Barlow), not to a discretionary trust of a fund."},
 {"t":"certainties","q":"Jean's will provides that 'any friend of mine who wishes to may buy any of my paintings from my estate at half its probate value'. Her executors are worried because the will does not define 'friend'. Keith, who met Jean every week for 30 years and often stayed with her, asks to buy a painting.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift?","o":["It is valid, because each person claiming to be a friend can be tested individually, and Keith clearly qualifies.","It is void, because 'friend' is conceptually uncertain and so no one can benefit.","It is void, because the executors cannot draw up a complete list of Jean's friends.","It is valid only if the executors can say of every person in the world whether or not that person was one of Jean's friends.","It is valid only if the court is prepared to define 'friend' for the executors in advance."],"a":0,"e":"This is a series of individual gifts (options to buy), not a trust of a fund to be shared among a class. It is enough that a particular claimant can show they clearly qualify (Re Barlow's Will Trusts). Keith, a close friend of 30 years, plainly does, so it does not matter that the boundaries of 'friend' are unclear for others."},
 {"t":"certainties","q":"Lorna's will leaves £500,000 to trustees 'to apply for the benefit of any or all or some of the residents of Greater Manchester as my trustees in their discretion think fit'. The class can be identified from the electoral register but contains about 2.8 million people. Assume the gift is not charitable.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift?","o":["It is valid, because the class is conceptually certain and evidential difficulties do not matter.","It is valid, because it is enough that the trustees can identify some members of the class.","It is void, because a complete list of the residents cannot be drawn up.","It is void, because the class is so wide that the trust is administratively unworkable.","It is valid as a mere power, because the trustees are not obliged to distribute."],"a":3,"e":"Even a conceptually clear class can make a discretionary trust void if it is so wide that the trustees cannot sensibly carry out their duty to distribute (R v District Auditor, ex p West Yorkshire MCC). 'To apply' imposes a duty, so this is a discretionary trust, not a mere power. The complete list test is the wrong test for a discretionary trust."},
 {"t":"certainties","q":"Trustees hold a fund on discretionary trust to distribute the income each year among 'such of the employees and former employees of Kestrel Ltd and their relatives and dependants as the trustees select'. For three years the trustees have paid all the income to six employees they know personally, without considering anyone else. Nell, the daughter of a former employee, has received nothing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nell's position?","o":["She is entitled to an equal share of the income with the six employees already paid.","She has no rights at all, because an object of a discretionary trust has only a hope of benefit and cannot complain about how trustees choose.","She cannot demand a payment, but she can ask the court to require the trustees to consider the whole class properly.","She can call for her share of the capital, because she is an adult object of the trust.","She has no claim, because 'relatives and dependants' is too uncertain a class to be objects."],"a":2,"e":"An object of a discretionary trust has no share of the fund, only a hope of benefit, but they do have the right to be considered and to have the trust properly administered. Trustees who never consider the range of objects are in breach, and the court can intervene. 'Relatives' and 'dependants' are conceptually certain (Re Baden (No 2)), and a single object cannot call for capital."},
 {"t":"certainties","q":"Mark settles £1 million on trustees for his three children in equal shares, but gives the trustees power to appoint any part of the capital to 'anyone in the world except me and my wife'. In default of appointment the children take. The trustees have never used the power. One child argues that the power is void because its class is unlimited.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the power?","o":["It is void, because the trustees could never draw up a list of everyone who might benefit.","It is valid, because a mere power is not invalid just because the class of objects is very wide.","It is void, because it is administratively unworkable in the same way as a discretionary trust for millions of people.","It is valid, but only once the trustees have narrowed the class by excluding everyone outside the family.","It is void, because the children's fixed interests make any power to appoint elsewhere uncertain."],"a":1,"e":"The gift over in default shows this is a mere power: the trustees may, but need not, appoint. The is or is not test applies, and anyone except Mark and his wife is clearly in the class. A mere power is not void merely because the class is very wide (Re Hay's Settlement Trusts); administrative unworkability is a ground for invalidating discretionary trusts."},

 {"t":"formalities","q":"During a telephone call, Olivia tells her son Pavel, 'From today I hold my 5,000 shares in Quarry plc on trust for you.' She writes nothing down. A year later she changes her mind and says the shares are still entirely hers.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Pavel's position?","o":["He has no interest, because a declaration of trust must be in signed writing.","He has no interest, because Olivia did not transfer the shares into his name.","He has an interest, but it is unenforceable against Olivia until she confirms the declaration in signed writing.","He has an equitable interest in the shares, because a trust of personalty can be declared orally.","He has an interest only if he gave Olivia some consideration for the declaration."],"a":3,"e":"Shares are personalty, and a trust of personalty can be declared orally. The writing requirement in LPA 1925 s.53(1)(b) applies only to declarations of trusts of land. Because Olivia declared herself trustee, no transfer is needed, and once declared the trust cannot be revoked just because she changes her mind."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"In March, Ravi orally declared to his sister Sunita that he held his flat in Cardiff on trust for her. In June he signed a letter to her confirming 'the trust of my Cardiff flat that I declared in March'. In August Ravi died. His will leaves everything to a charity.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sunita's position?","o":["She has no interest, because the signed writing must be made at the same time as the oral declaration, not months afterwards.","She is beneficially entitled to the flat, because the later signed letter satisfies the writing requirement.","She has no interest, because a trust of land must be declared by deed.","She has no interest, because the letter was not witnessed.","She has no interest, because the trust was not created by Ravi's will."],"a":1,"e":"LPA 1925 s.53(1)(b) requires a declaration of trust of land to be 'manifested and proved' by signed writing. The writing is evidence, so it can come after the oral declaration, and the trust then dates from the declaration. No deed or witness is needed, so the flat is held for Sunita, not the charity."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"In January, before completing her purchase of Rose Cottage, Wanda signs a note saying 'I hold Rose Cottage on trust for my niece Xena.' She completes the purchase in March and is registered as owner. Nothing else is written or said about a trust.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["No trust arose from the note, because Wanda did not own the cottage when she signed it.","A trust arose in January, because the note was signed by Wanda.","A trust arose automatically in March, when Wanda became the registered owner.","A trust arose in March, but it is unenforceable because the January note was not witnessed.","No trust arose, because a declaration of trust of land must be made by deed."],"a":0,"e":"A person can only declare a trust of property they own at the time; a purported declaration over property not yet acquired is at most a promise, which a volunteer like Xena cannot enforce. So no trust arose in January, and nothing happened in March to create one. Witnesses and deeds are not required for a declaration of trust of land: the problem here is ownership, not form."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Yasmin is entitled to a quarter share of a trust fund of investments held by professional trustees. At a family lunch she tells her brother Zak, 'I give you my share of the trust.' Zak tells the trustees, who note it on their file. Yasmin never signs anything. She now wants the trustees to keep paying the income to her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Zak is entitled, because the trustees were told and recorded the gift.","Zak is entitled, because no formalities are needed for a gift of an interest in personalty such as investments.","Zak is entitled, but cannot enforce his interest in court until Yasmin signs writing.","Zak is entitled, because Yasmin's words created a sub-trust in his favour.","Yasmin remains entitled, because the oral assignment of her existing equitable interest is void."],"a":4,"e":"An assignment of a subsisting equitable interest is a 'disposition' and must be in writing signed by the person disposing or their authorised agent (LPA 1925 s.53(1)(c)). This applies to any property, including investments. Without signed writing the disposition is void, not merely unenforceable, and the trustees' file note does not count."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Alfie is the sole beneficiary of 8,000 shares in Birch plc held for him by his bank as bare trustee. He telephones the bank and tells it to transfer the shares into the name of his goddaughter Cara as a gift. The bank does so and Cara is registered as the owner. Alfie never signs anything about his equitable interest. Alfie has died, and his executors claim the shares.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Cara's position?","o":["She holds the shares on resulting trust for Alfie's estate, because his equitable interest was never disposed of in writing.","She holds only the legal title, because the oral instruction to the bank was void.","She owns the shares outright, because the equitable interest passed with the legal title and no separate writing was needed.","She owns the shares outright only if Alfie confirmed the gift in writing before he died.","She holds the shares on constructive trust for Alfie's estate, because she gave no consideration and the gift was never completed in equity."],"a":2,"e":"Where a beneficiary under a bare trust directs the trustee to transfer the legal title to a third party, intending the beneficial interest to go too, the whole interest passes with the legal title and s.53(1)(c) does not require separate writing (Vandervell v IRC). Contrast a direction to hold the shares on trust for someone else, which is a disposition needing signed writing (Grey v IRC). A gift needs no consideration."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Under her aunt's will trust, Daisy is entitled to a life interest in a fund of shares. As soon as she hears of it, and before receiving any income, Daisy tells the trustees by telephone that she does not want the interest and refuses it. She signs nothing. Two years later, when the fund has grown, she claims the income.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Daisy remains entitled, because a refusal of an equitable interest must be in signed writing.","Daisy remains entitled, because a life interest cannot be refused once the testator has died.","Daisy is not entitled, because a disclaimer is not a disposition and needs no writing.","Daisy is not entitled, but only because she has delayed for more than a year in claiming.","Daisy is not entitled, because her oral refusal took effect as an assignment to the person next entitled."],"a":2,"e":"A disclaimer (refusing an interest before accepting it) is not a 'disposition' of an equitable interest, so s.53(1)(c) does not apply and it can be made orally (Re Paradise Motor Co). Daisy disclaimed before accepting any benefit, so her interest never took effect. It operates as a refusal, not as an assignment to anyone."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Gemma paid £60,000 towards the £240,000 price of a house that was registered in the sole name of her adult brother, Harry, who paid the rest. Nothing was said or written about why. Harry now says that, even if Gemma meant to keep a share, she cannot claim one because there is no signed writing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gemma's position?","o":["She has no claim, because a trust of land must be evidenced by signed writing.","She can recover only £60,000 as a debt, because without writing there can be no trust of land.","She has a claim only if she can show that Harry orally promised her a share.","She has a claim to a share only if the house was intended as a home for both of them.","She is presumed to have a quarter share under a resulting trust, which needs no writing."],"a":4,"e":"Where one person pays part of the price of property put in another's name, equity presumes a resulting trust in proportion to the contribution: here £60,000 of £240,000, a quarter. Resulting, implied and constructive trusts are exempt from the writing rules by LPA 1925 s.53(2). No promise is needed, and there is no presumption of advancement between siblings."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Ian's will, made in 2023, leaves £100,000 'to my friend Jo, on trust for the purposes I have communicated to her'. In fact, it was only in 2024 that Ian told Jo, who agreed, that the money was for his grandson. Ian died in 2026 without changing his will.\n\nWhat happens to the £100,000?","o":["Jo holds it on trust for the grandson, because she accepted the trust before Ian died.","Jo holds it on resulting trust for Ian's estate, because a half-secret trust must be communicated before or at the time the will is made.","Jo takes it beneficially, because the trust was not validly communicated.","Jo holds it on trust for the grandson, because the Wills Act formalities do not apply to secret trusts and communication before death is enough.","The grandson takes it directly under the will, because the will refers to the communication."],"a":1,"e":"This is a half-secret trust: the will shows Jo takes as trustee but not for whom. Such a trust must be communicated and accepted before or at the time of the will (Blackwell v Blackwell). Communication came later, so the secret trust fails, and because the will makes Jo a trustee she cannot keep the money: it results to Ian's estate. A late communication can work only for a fully secret trust."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Kara is absolutely entitled in equity to a fund of shares held by her uncle as bare trustee. She orally declares that she now holds her interest on trust for her two children, with power to decide how much income each receives and to accumulate the rest until they reach 25. Kara signs nothing. Her children later bring a claim against her under the trust.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The declaration is void, because any dealing with an existing equitable interest, including a declaration of a sub-trust over it, must be in signed writing.","The declaration is void, because Kara cannot be a trustee of an interest she holds as a beneficiary.","The declaration is effective only if her uncle agreed to it.","It is likely to be effective without writing, because Kara has created a new sub-trust with active duties rather than disposing of her interest.","The declaration is effective over the income, but not over the capital."],"a":3,"e":"Where a beneficiary declares a sub-trust of their interest and keeps active duties (here discretions over income and accumulation), it is generally treated as a new trust, not a disposition caught by s.53(1)(c). A trust of personalty can be declared orally, and the head trustee's consent is not needed. The position is less clear for a bare sub-trust, where the head beneficiary may drop out, but that is not this case."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Nia is entitled to a half share in equity of a block of flats held on trust by professional trustees. While working overseas, she authorises her friend Owen, by signed letter, to deal with her property interests. On Nia's instructions, Owen signs a document assigning Nia's half share to her brother Pat. Nia signs nothing herself. The trustees ask whether Pat is now the beneficiary.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["Yes, because a disposition of an existing equitable interest can be signed by an agent authorised in writing.","No, because a disposition of an equitable interest in land must always be signed by the beneficiary personally, not an agent.","No, because a disposition of an equitable interest must be made by deed.","Yes, but only once the trustees have consented in writing to the assignment.","No, because an assignment of an equitable interest in land must be registered at HM Land Registry."],"a":0,"e":"Section 53(1)(c) requires a disposition of a subsisting equitable interest to be in writing signed by the person disposing 'or by his agent thereunto lawfully authorised in writing'. Owen was authorised by signed letter, so the assignment is valid. Contrast s.53(1)(b) (declarations of trusts of land), where the writing must be signed by the person able to declare the trust; no deed, trustee consent or registration is required."},

 {"t":"constitution","q":"Quentin wanted to give his 2,000 shares in Rowan Ltd to his niece Sasha. He wrote her a letter saying 'I give you my Rowan shares' and enclosed the share certificate, but he never signed a stock transfer form. Quentin has died, and his will leaves everything to his son, who claims the shares.\n\nWho is entitled to the shares in equity?","o":["Sasha, because Quentin's letter shows he intended to hold the shares on trust for her.","Quentin's son, because the gift was incomplete and equity will not treat a failed gift as a declaration of trust.","Sasha, because Quentin had done everything in his power to transfer the shares.","Sasha, because delivery of the share certificate is enough to transfer shares.","Quentin's son, because shares in a private company can never be given away without the directors first consenting to the transfer."],"a":1,"e":"Legal title to certificated shares passes only on a signed stock transfer form followed by registration. Quentin never signed the form, which only he could do, so he had not done everything in his power and the Re Rose exception does not apply. Equity will not save a failed outright gift by treating it as a declaration of trust (Milroy v Lord), so the shares fall into his estate."},
 {"t":"constitution","q":"Tara signed a TR1 transferring her registered flat to her son Umar as a gift and handed it to him with all the documents needed for registration. Before Umar applied to HM Land Registry, they argued, and Tara told him she wanted the flat back and would not let the transfer be registered.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Tara can stop the gift, because legal title to registered land passes only on registration.","Tara can stop the gift, because Umar gave no consideration.","Umar can insist on the gift only if Tara also declared herself trustee of the flat for him in signed writing when she handed over the transfer.","Tara can stop the gift unless Umar has already moved into the flat.","Umar is entitled in equity, because Tara did everything she needed to do and holds the legal title on trust for him until registration."],"a":4,"e":"A gift is complete in equity once the donor has done everything in their power to transfer the property, even though a third party (here HM Land Registry) still has to act (Re Rose; Mascall v Mascall). Tara signed the transfer and handed over everything needed, so she holds the legal title on trust for Umar and cannot recall the gift. A volunteer can rely on this exception without giving consideration."},
 {"t":"constitution","q":"Victor signed a stock transfer form for his 500 shares in Willow Ltd in favour of his partner Xavi and gave it to her, but kept the share certificate in his safe, saying he would hand it over later. He died before doing so. The company's articles place no restriction on transfers.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Xavi's position?","o":["She is entitled to the shares, because the signed transfer form shows Victor intended an immediate gift.","She is entitled to the shares, because only registration by the company remained to be done.","She has no claim, because Victor had not done everything in his power to transfer the shares.","She has no claim, because a gift of shares must be made by deed.","She is entitled to the shares, because Victor held them on trust for her from the moment he signed the form."],"a":2,"e":"Re Rose applies only where nothing remains that the donor must do. Victor still had to hand over the share certificate, which was in his power to deliver, so the gift was incomplete in equity (Zeital v Kaye). Intention alone does not perfect a gift, and a failed gift is not converted into a declaration of trust."},
 {"t":"constitution","q":"In 2020 Yolanda executed a deed with her trustees, covenanting to transfer to them any property she inherits from her mother, to hold on trust for her nephews. The deed expressly excludes the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999. Her mother died in 2025, leaving Yolanda £300,000, but Yolanda refuses to transfer it. The nephews gave no consideration.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the nephews' position?","o":["They cannot compel Yolanda to transfer the money, because they are volunteers and equity will not assist them.","They can compel the transfer, because the covenant was made by deed.","They can compel the transfer, because the trust was fully constituted when the deed of covenant was signed and delivered.","They can require the trustees to sue Yolanda for damages on their behalf.","They can compel the transfer, because Yolanda now owns the property described in the deed."],"a":0,"e":"A covenant to settle future property does not constitute a trust; the trust is constituted only when the property reaches the trustees. Volunteers cannot obtain specific performance of the covenant, and the court will direct the trustees not to sue for damages on their behalf (Re Kay's Settlement; Re Pryce). The deed makes the promise binding at common law between the parties, but it does not help the nephews, especially as the 1999 Act is excluded."},
 {"t":"constitution","q":"Shortly before her marriage, and in consideration of it, Abby entered into a marriage settlement covenanting to transfer to its trustees any property worth over £10,000 that she later acquired, to be held on trust for herself, her husband and the children of the marriage. She later inherits £80,000 and refuses to transfer it. Her daughter Bea, a child of the marriage, wishes to enforce the covenant.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Bea's position?","o":["She cannot enforce it, because she was not a party to the settlement.","She cannot enforce it, because she was not born when the covenant was made.","She can enforce it only if the settlement expressly allowed third parties to enforce it under the Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999.","She can enforce it, because children of the marriage are within the marriage consideration and are not treated as volunteers.","She can sue only for nominal damages at common law."],"a":3,"e":"Equity treats a spouse and the children of a marriage, under a settlement made before and in consideration of the marriage, as having given consideration. They are therefore not volunteers and can enforce a covenant to settle after-acquired property (Pullan v Koe). It does not matter that Bea was not a party or was not yet born."},
 {"t":"constitution","q":"Cyril, in hospital with terminal cancer and told he had weeks to live, handed his partner Dina the only key to a locked box containing the title deeds to his unregistered cottage, saying 'The cottage is yours if I don't come out of here.' He died ten days later from the cancer. His will leaves the cottage to his brother.\n\nWho is entitled to the cottage?","o":["His brother, because land cannot pass by a gift made in contemplation of death.","His brother, because Cyril did not sign a deed transferring the cottage.","Dina, but only if she can show that she relied on Cyril's words to her detriment, so as to found a proprietary estoppel.","Dina, because the requirements of a gift in contemplation of death, including delivery of dominion, are met.","Dina, because the gift took effect immediately when the key was handed over."],"a":3,"e":"A donatio mortis causa needs contemplation of impending death from an identified cause, a gift conditional on death, and delivery of dominion (control) over the property. All three are met: handing over the key to the box holding the essential title deeds delivered dominion over the land (Sen v Headley). The gift took effect on death, not immediately, and no detrimental reliance is required."},
 {"t":"constitution","q":"Ellen, who was dying from a heart condition, wrote a cheque for £20,000 from her own account in favour of her carer Fiona, saying 'This is for you when I am gone.' Ellen died two days later, before Fiona paid it in, and the bank refused to honour the cheque.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Fiona's position?","o":["She has no claim, because a donor's own cheque cannot be the subject of a gift in contemplation of death.","She is entitled to £20,000 from the estate as a valid gift in contemplation of death.","She is entitled to £20,000, because handing over the cheque delivered dominion over the money.","She is entitled to £20,000 if she can prove that Ellen expected to die soon.","She is entitled to £20,000, because a gift intended to take effect on death is perfected when the donor dies."],"a":0,"e":"A donor's own cheque is only an instruction to their bank to pay, and the bank's authority ends on the donor's death. Handing it over does not deliver dominion over the money, so it cannot found a donatio mortis causa. Fiona therefore has no claim, even though the other conditions (contemplation of death and a gift conditional on death) were met."},
 {"t":"constitution","q":"Gary often told his nephew Hal, 'My boat will be yours when I die.' Gary kept the boat and used it until his death. His will appoints Hal as sole executor and leaves the residue to Gary's daughter, who claims the boat.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hal's position?","o":["He is entitled to the boat, because as executor the legal title to it vests in him.","He is entitled to the boat, because Gary's intention that Hal should have it continued unchanged right up until his death.","He is entitled to the boat as a valid gift in contemplation of death.","He is entitled to the boat by proprietary estoppel, because Gary's promise was repeated.","He has no claim to the boat, because Gary intended a gift on death rather than an immediate lifetime gift."],"a":4,"e":"The rule in Strong v Bird perfects an imperfect gift when the donee becomes executor, but only if the donor intended an immediate lifetime gift that continued until death. 'Will be yours when I die' is a promise of a future gift, which is in substance an informal will. There was no impending death for a donatio mortis causa, and no detrimental reliance for estoppel."},
 {"t":"constitution","q":"In a voluntary deed, Kemal covenanted to transfer his painting to his friend Lara to hold on trust for his nephews. He never transferred it. Kemal has died, and his will appoints Lara as his executor; the painting has vested in her as executor. Kemal's residuary beneficiaries claim the painting.\n\nWho is entitled to the painting in equity?","o":["The residuary beneficiaries, because the nephews are volunteers who cannot enforce the covenant.","The residuary beneficiaries, because Lara holds the painting only in her capacity as executor, not as trustee for the nephews.","The nephews, because the painting has reached the intended trustee and the trust is therefore constituted.","The nephews, but only if Kemal intended an immediate gift that continued until his death.","The residuary beneficiaries, because a covenant to settle is void unless it is made for value."],"a":2,"e":"A trust is constituted once the intended trustee obtains the legal title by any route, including by becoming the settlor's executor (Re Ralli's Will Trusts). The nephews do not need to enforce the covenant, because the property is already in Lara's hands and she holds it on the declared trust. The Strong v Bird conditions concern imperfect gifts, not this situation."},
 {"t":"constitution","q":"Mona signed a share transfer form for 400 shares in Nettle Ltd in favour of her nephew Ned and gave it to her accountant, who told Ned that the shares were his and he need do nothing. Relying on this, Ned agreed to become a director of the company, which required him to hold shares. The form was never delivered to Ned or to the company before Mona died.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The gift fails, because the form was never delivered to Ned or to the company.","The gift is likely to be effective in equity, because it would be unconscionable for Mona to go back on it after Ned acted in reliance.","The gift is effective, because Mona declared herself trustee of the shares for Ned at the moment she signed the share transfer form in his favour.","The gift fails, because the accountant had no authority to make a gift on Mona's behalf.","The gift is effective only if Ned pays Mona's estate the value of the shares."],"a":1,"e":"Normally the transfer form must be delivered for the donor to have done all in their power. But in Pennington v Waine a gift was held complete in equity because, once the donee had been told he need do nothing and had agreed to become a director in reliance, it would have been unconscionable for the donor to resile. Mona intended a gift, not to be a trustee, and no payment is needed."},

 {"t":"beneficial-entitlement","q":"A trust provides for the income to be paid to Olga for life, and on her death the capital to go to her nephew Piers. Piers died last year aged 40, leaving a will giving everything to his wife Rina. Olga has now died.\n\nWho is entitled to the capital?","o":["Rina, because Piers had a vested interest in remainder which passed under his will.","Olga's estate, because Piers's interest failed when he died before Olga.","The settlor or the settlor's estate, under a resulting trust.","Piers's next of kin under the intestacy rules, because a remainder cannot be left by will.","The trustees, who have a discretion to choose between Rina and Olga's family."],"a":0,"e":"A gift of capital 'on her death to Piers' with no condition is vested in interest: Piers did not have to survive Olga. A vested interest is property that passes on the beneficiary's death under their will or intestacy, so it went to Rina under Piers's will and falls into possession now that Olga has died. Only a contingent interest (for example 'if he survives her') would have failed."},
 {"t":"beneficial-entitlement","q":"Tom's will leaves his residuary estate on trust to pay the income to his wife Una for life, and after her death to divide the capital equally among 'such of my children as survive her'. Tom has two adult children, Vera and Will. Una is still alive. Vera asks whether she can sell her interest to an investor now.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Vera's interest?","o":["It is vested in possession, so she can sell it and the buyer takes an immediate right to income.","It is vested in interest, so the buyer will take half the capital on Una's death even if Vera dies before her.","It is a mere hope, like that of an object of a discretionary trust, so she has nothing to sell.","It is contingent on surviving Una, so she can sell it, but the buyer gets nothing if Vera dies before Una.","It cannot be dealt with at all until Una dies, because a remainder is not property."],"a":3,"e":"The words 'such of my children as survive her' make the children's interests contingent on surviving Una. A contingent interest is still property and can be sold, but the buyer takes only what Vera would get, so it fails if she dies first. Una, not Vera, has the interest in possession, and Vera's interest is fixed (not discretionary) once the condition is met."},
 {"t":"beneficial-entitlement","q":"Under a trust of £600,000, Ada is entitled to the income for life and her son Ben is entitled to the capital absolutely on her death. Ada is 62 and Ben is 35, and both have full capacity. They ask the trustees to transfer £200,000 to Ada and £400,000 to Ben now. The trustees object that the settlor wanted the trust to last for Ada's lifetime.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The trustees can refuse, because the beneficiaries cannot override the settlor's wishes.","Ada and Ben can end the trust and divide the fund as they agree, because together they are absolutely entitled.","Ada and Ben can end the trust, but only with the court's approval under the Variation of Trusts Act 1958.","Ben alone can call for the capital, because his interest is vested.","Ada and Ben can end the trust, but the fund must be divided according to the actuarial values of their interests."],"a":1,"e":"Under the rule in Saunders v Vautier, beneficiaries who are all adults of full capacity and together absolutely entitled can end the trust and call for the property, overriding the settlor's wishes. The life tenant and remainderman together own the whole beneficial interest, and they can divide it however they agree. Court approval is needed only where some beneficiaries cannot consent; Ben alone cannot take capital while Ada's life interest continues."},
 {"t":"beneficial-entitlement","q":"A trust gives the income to Cora for life, and then the capital equally to her children Dan, Eve and Finn. Cora is 50. Dan and Eve are adults; Finn is 15. All of them want to end the trust now and divide the fund.\n\nIs there any way they can do so?","o":["Yes, by agreement among themselves, because the majority of the beneficiaries are adults with capacity who consent to ending it.","Yes, if Finn's parent consents on his behalf.","No, the trust must continue at least until Finn reaches 18 in all circumstances.","Yes, because the trustees can end the trust at the request of the adult beneficiaries.","Only by applying to the court to approve an arrangement on Finn's behalf, which it will do only if it is for his benefit."],"a":4,"e":"The beneficiaries can end a trust themselves only if all are adults of full capacity; a minor cannot consent and a parent cannot consent for him. The adults can instead ask the court to approve a variation on Finn's behalf under the Variation of Trusts Act 1958 s.1, which it will do only if the arrangement benefits him. The adults must still consent for themselves."},
 {"t":"beneficial-entitlement","q":"Trustees hold a portfolio of listed shares worth £900,000 on trust for three siblings, Gina, Hugh and Iris, in equal shares absolutely, but the deed says the fund is not to be divided until the youngest reaches 30. All three are adults with capacity; Iris is 24. Gina asks the trustees to transfer her one-third share to her now. Hugh and Iris want the trust to continue.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gina's position?","o":["She cannot call for anything unless all three siblings agree to end the trust.","She must wait until Iris reaches 30, because the settlor's direction binds the beneficiaries.","She can usually call for her one-third share now, because it is vested and listed shares can be split without harming the others.","She can call for her share only if the court approves a variation of the trust.","She can call for the whole fund now, because she is an adult beneficiary of full capacity with a vested interest in the trust property."],"a":2,"e":"Each sibling has a vested, absolute one-third share, with only payment postponed. An adult beneficiary with a vested share in divisible property, such as listed shares, can usually call for that share without the others' agreement, provided this does not harm them. The trustees could refuse only where splitting the property would prejudice the other beneficiaries, such as a house or a controlling shareholding."},
 {"t":"beneficial-entitlement","q":"Jade and Kurt are the only beneficiaries of a trust of investments, entitled in equal shares absolutely. Both are adults with capacity. They do not want to end the trust, but they write to the trustees instructing them to sell the trust's government bonds and invest in a technology fund chosen by Kurt. The trustees think this unwise.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The trustees must follow the instruction, because the beneficiaries are together absolutely entitled.","The trustees must follow the instruction, provided the technology fund is an authorised investment.","The trustees must follow the instruction, unless the trust deed expressly excludes the beneficiaries' power to direct how the fund is invested.","The trustees can ignore the instruction only if they first obtain the court's directions.","The trustees need not follow it: Jade and Kurt can end the trust and take the property, but cannot control the trustees while the trust continues."],"a":4,"e":"The rule in Saunders v Vautier lets beneficiaries who are together absolutely entitled end the trust, but not direct the trustees how to exercise their powers while it continues (Re Brockbank). If Jade and Kurt want to control the investments, they must call for the property and manage it themselves. The trustees must still exercise their own judgement under their investment duties."},
 {"t":"beneficial-entitlement","q":"Trustees hold £400,000 on discretionary trust for 'such of my grandchildren living at my death' as they select, with no gift over. The settlor died in 2024, leaving four grandchildren, who are all now adults with capacity. The four want the fund divided equally between them now; the trustees would prefer to keep it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The grandchildren cannot end the trust, because objects of a discretionary trust have no interest in the fund, even acting together.","The grandchildren can together call for the fund, because the class is closed and all its members are adults who agree.","Each grandchild can call for a quarter share individually, without the others' agreement.","The grandchildren can end the trust only if the trustees consent.","The grandchildren cannot end the trust until the end of the perpetuity period."],"a":1,"e":"Individually, an object of a discretionary trust has only a hope of benefit. But where the class is closed (here fixed at the settlor's death) and all its members are adults of full capacity, together they are absolutely entitled and can end the trust (Re Smith). No single grandchild can claim a share alone, and the trustees' consent is not needed."},
 {"t":"beneficial-entitlement","q":"Lars settled £250,000 on trust for his niece Maya 'if she reaches 30'. There is no gift over. Maya is now 23 and wants the whole fund now to buy a house. Lars is alive and has full capacity.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Maya could obtain the fund now?","o":["She can call for the fund now, because she is an adult and the only named beneficiary, and there is no gift over.","She cannot obtain the fund before she reaches 30 in any circumstances.","She can call for the fund now if she shows that she needs it to buy a home.","She can do so only if Lars, who would take if she failed to reach 30, joins with her in ending the trust.","She can do so only by applying to the court under the Variation of Trusts Act 1958."],"a":3,"e":"Maya's interest is contingent, so she alone is not absolutely entitled. With no gift over, Lars would take the fund on resulting trust if she failed to reach 30, so the beneficial interest is split between them. Both being adults of full capacity, Maya and Lars together could end the trust, and no court application is needed."},
 {"t":"beneficial-entitlement","q":"Noel is entitled under a fixed trust to one-third of a fund absolutely on the death of his aunt, who is still alive. His brother Owen is one of ten objects of a separate discretionary trust of income. Both brothers have been made bankrupt. Their trustee in bankruptcy asks which of these interests forms part of their estates.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["Noel's interest is property that passes to his trustee in bankruptcy; Owen has only a hope of benefit.","Both interests pass to the trustee in bankruptcy, because both brothers are beneficiaries of trusts.","Neither interest passes, because Noel's interest is not yet in possession and Owen's is discretionary.","Owen's interest passes as a one-tenth share of the income, but Noel's does not because his aunt is still alive.","Noel's interest passes only when his aunt dies, and Owen's passes as soon as the trustees pay him anything."],"a":0,"e":"A vested interest in remainder under a fixed trust is a proprietary interest, which can be sold and passes to a trustee in bankruptcy now, even though enjoyment is postponed. An object of a discretionary trust has no share of the fund, only a hope that the trustees will choose to pay them (Gartside v IRC). So there is nothing of Owen's in the fund to pass."},
 {"t":"beneficial-entitlement","q":"Viv's will trust holds £150,000 for her granddaughter Tess 'if she reaches 25, but if she dies under that age, for my nephew Ugo'. Tess died last month aged 22, leaving a will giving everything to her husband. Ugo is alive.\n\nWho is entitled to the £150,000?","o":["Tess's husband, because Tess's interest passed to him under her will when she died.","Viv's residuary beneficiaries, under a resulting trust.","Ugo, because Tess's contingent interest has failed and the gift over takes effect.","Tess's husband and Ugo equally, because both have a claim.","The trustees, who may choose between Tess's husband and Ugo."],"a":2,"e":"Tess's interest was contingent on reaching 25. When she died at 22 the condition failed and her interest ended, so there was nothing to pass under her will. The fund goes to Ugo under the gift over; a resulting trust would arise only if there were no gift over."},

 {"t":"purpose-charitable","q":"Rupert's will leaves land and £200,000 to trustees 'to maintain the land as a sports ground primarily for the benefit of the employees of Sable Ltd, and secondarily for such other persons as the trustees allow', for a period ending 21 years after the death of the last survivor of Rupert's grandchildren living at his death. The residuary beneficiary says the gift is a void purpose trust.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift?","o":["It is void, because it is a trust for a purpose rather than for people, and so no one can enforce it.","It is charitable, because the advancement of amateur sport is a charitable purpose.","It is void, because it exceeds the perpetuity period.","It is valid as one of the recognised exceptions for non-charitable purposes.","It is valid, because it directly and tangibly benefits ascertainable people, who can enforce it."],"a":4,"e":"Although expressed as a purpose, a trust that directly and tangibly benefits ascertainable individuals is treated as a trust for people, who can enforce it (Re Denley's Trust Deed). Its duration is limited to lives in being plus 21 years, so it respects the perpetuity rule. It is not charitable, because employees of one company are linked by a personal nexus and are not a section of the public, and the anomalous exceptions (animals, graves, masses) do not cover it."},
 {"t":"purpose-charitable","q":"Selina's will leaves £15,000 to her executors 'to maintain my family's grave in Northbridge churchyard in perpetuity'. The gift is not charitable. Her residuary beneficiaries challenge it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift?","o":["It is valid, because maintaining graves is a recognised exception to the beneficiary principle.","It is void, because a non-charitable purpose trust must be limited to the perpetuity period, and this one is to last for ever.","It is valid, because the 125-year statutory perpetuity period under the 2009 Act applies to it, and that period has not been exceeded.","It is void, because no non-charitable purpose trust can ever be valid.","It is valid, but the executors may carry it out for no more than 125 years."],"a":1,"e":"Maintaining a grave is one of the anomalous exceptions that can be valid despite the beneficiary principle. But such a trust must not last longer than the common law perpetuity period (a life in being plus 21 years, or 21 years); the 125-year period under the Perpetuities and Accumulations Act 2009 does not apply to the duration of purpose trusts. A gift 'in perpetuity' breaches this, so it fails; words like 'for 21 years' would have saved it."},
 {"t":"purpose-charitable","q":"Tobias's will leaves £40,000 'to the Eastfield Ramblers' Club', an unincorporated members' club. Its rules allow the members, by majority vote at a general meeting, to change the rules and to dissolve the club and divide its property among themselves. The residuary beneficiary argues that the gift is a void purpose trust.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift?","o":["It is void, because the club cannot own property and the gift is for its purposes.","It is void, unless the club's purposes are charitable.","It is valid as a trust for the members as individuals, each taking an equal share immediately.","It is valid as a gift to the current members, to be held according to the club's rules.","It is valid only if the club incorporates as a company within a reasonable time."],"a":3,"e":"A gift to an unincorporated association is normally construed as a gift to its current members, held subject to their contract with each other under the club's rules (Re Recher's Will Trusts). Because the members control the property and can change the rules or dissolve the club and divide the assets, the gift is valid. Each member cannot simply take an immediate share; their rights depend on the rules."},
 {"t":"purpose-charitable","q":"Ursula's will leaves £60,000 'to the Westbury branch of the National Gardeners' Federation' for its general purposes. The Federation is not a charity. Under the Federation's national rules, the branch cannot change its own rules, and all branch property is controlled by the national committee, which can direct how it is used.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift?","o":["It fails, because the branch members cannot control the property, so it cannot take effect as a gift to them.","It is valid as a gift to the current members of the branch, subject to the branch rules and the national rules together.","It is valid as a gift to the national Federation.","It is valid, because a gift to an unincorporated association is always construed as a gift to its members.","It is valid as a non-charitable purpose trust, because its purposes are clear."],"a":0,"e":"The contract-holding analysis works only if the members can control the property, for example by changing the rules and dividing the assets. Where the members are bound by an outside body's rules and cannot do so, the gift cannot take effect as a gift to them (Re Grant's Will Trusts). It then fails as a non-charitable purpose trust, because there is no one to enforce it."},
 {"t":"purpose-charitable","q":"Victor's will leaves £300,000 on trust 'to relieve poverty among the employees and former employees of Vale Brewery Ltd and their families who are in financial need'. Vale employs 400 people. The residuary beneficiary argues that the gift is not charitable.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift?","o":["It is not charitable, because employees of one company are linked to it by a personal nexus and are not a section of the public.","It is not charitable, because 400 employees is too small a class.","It is charitable, because a trust for the relief of poverty can be limited to the employees of a particular company.","It is charitable only if the trustees also make payments to members of the public.","It is not charitable, but it is valid as a private discretionary trust."],"a":2,"e":"The personal nexus test normally prevents a class linked to an employer from being a section of the public (Oppenheim). But trusts for the relief of poverty are an exception: they can be charitable even when limited to employees or relatives (Dingle v Turner). The exception applies only to poverty, not to education or other purposes."},
 {"t":"purpose-charitable","q":"Wendy's will leaves £100,000 to trustees 'to campaign for a change in the law so that all scientific experiments on animals are banned'. Her residuary beneficiaries challenge the gift.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift?","o":["It is not charitable, because its main purpose is to change the law, which is a political purpose.","It is charitable, because the advancement of animal welfare is a recognised charitable purpose under the Charities Act 2011.","It is charitable, because the public benefit of protecting animals is presumed.","It is valid as a non-charitable purpose trust, because its purpose is clearly defined.","It is charitable, provided the trustees also carry out some educational work."],"a":0,"e":"A trust whose main purpose is to change the law is political and not charitable, because the court cannot judge whether the change would benefit the public (National Anti-Vivisection Society v IRC). Campaigning is acceptable only if it is ancillary to a charitable purpose, not the main object. Public benefit is never presumed (Charities Act 2011 s.4), and as a non-charitable purpose trust it fails for want of an enforcer."},
 {"t":"purpose-charitable","q":"Xander died in 2023 leaving £80,000 to the Kingsley Village Library Trust, a registered charity, which received the money. In 2026 the charity closed because its building became unsafe, with £70,000 of the gift unspent. Xander's will contains no other charitable gifts. His family claim the £70,000.\n\nWhat happens to the £70,000?","o":["It returns to Xander's estate on resulting trust, because his will showed no general charitable intention beyond this library.","It is divided equally among the charity's members on its dissolution.","It goes to the Crown as ownerless property.","It can be applied cy-près to similar charitable purposes, because the gift had already taken effect before the failure.","It can be applied cy-près only if Xander's family consent."],"a":3,"e":"This is a subsequent failure: the gift took effect when Xander died, and the purpose failed later. Property already dedicated to charity is applied cy-près to the nearest similar purpose without any need to show general charitable intention (Charities Act 2011 s.62). General charitable intention matters only where the failure occurs before the gift takes effect (initial failure)."},
 {"t":"purpose-charitable","q":"Yara's will leaves £20,000 to each of six named cancer charities. One of them, described as the 'Southdown Cancer Relief Fund', has never existed. The other five are well-known registered charities. The residuary beneficiaries claim the £20,000 intended for the Southdown fund.\n\nWhat is likely to happen to that £20,000?","o":["It lapses into residue, because a gift to a charity that has never existed always fails, whatever the rest of the will says.","It is divided automatically among the five other charities named in the will.","It can be applied cy-près, because the pattern of gifts suggests a general intention to benefit cancer charities.","It goes to the Crown as ownerless property.","It is held on resulting trust for Yara's next of kin, because the failure is initial."],"a":2,"e":"This is an initial failure, so the gift can be applied cy-près only if Yara had a general charitable intention. A gift to a body that never existed, made alongside several gifts to charities with the same purpose, strongly suggests an intention to benefit cancer relief generally rather than one particular institution. A cy-près scheme will direct the money to similar purposes, not automatically to the named charities."},
 {"t":"purpose-charitable","q":"Zelda's will leaves her residuary estate 'to such charitable and benevolent institutions as my trustees select'. Her next of kin argue that the gift is void because 'benevolent' purposes are wider than charitable ones.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the gift?","o":["It is void, because benevolent institutions need not be charitable.","It is void, because the trustees have too wide a discretion.","It is valid as a non-charitable discretionary trust.","It is valid only for half of the residue, which must be given to institutions that are charitable.","It is likely to be valid, because the institutions chosen must be both charitable and benevolent."],"a":4,"e":"A charitable trust must be exclusively charitable. 'Charitable or benevolent' fails because the trustees could choose non-charitable objects (Chichester Diocesan Fund v Simpson). 'Charitable and benevolent' is usually read conjunctively: each institution must be charitable as well as benevolent, so the gift is exclusively charitable and valid."},
 {"t":"purpose-charitable","q":"The trustees of Ashgrove School, a charitable trust running a fee-paying school charging £35,000 a year, propose to end all bursaries and stop letting local state schools use its sports facilities, so that only pupils whose families can pay full fees will benefit.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect on the school's charitable status?","o":["It is unaffected, because the advancement of education is presumed to be for the public benefit.","It is at risk, because a fee-charging charity must provide more than a minimal or token benefit for those who cannot pay.","It is unaffected, because charging fees is irrelevant to public benefit.","It is lost automatically, because a charity may not charge fees for its services.","It is at risk only if the school makes a profit that is distributed to its trustees, since charities may otherwise charge any fees."],"a":1,"e":"Public benefit must be shown for every charitable purpose; there is no presumption (Charities Act 2011 s.4). Charities may charge fees, but the poor must not be excluded: they must receive more than a minimal or token benefit, such as bursaries or shared facilities (R (Independent Schools Council) v Charity Commission). Removing all such benefit puts charitable status at risk."},

 {"t":"resulting-trusts","q":"Bella transferred £100,000 to trustees to hold 'for such of my close associates as the trustees select'. The court has held that the trust fails for uncertainty of objects. The trustees ask what they should do with the money.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["They keep it beneficially, because Bella transferred it to them outright.","They pay it to Bella's closest relatives, as the nearest equivalent to her associates.","They hold it on resulting trust for Bella, because the express trust has failed.","They apply it cy-près to similar purposes.","They pay it to the Crown as ownerless property."],"a":2,"e":"When an express trust fails, the trustees cannot keep the property, because they were never meant to benefit. An automatic resulting trust arises in favour of the settlor (or their estate). Cy-près is available only for charitable gifts, and the Crown takes only if there is no one entitled."},
 {"t":"resulting-trusts","q":"Friends and neighbours of the Carter family raised £30,000 through identifiable donations to pay for specialist treatment abroad for their son Dylan. The appeal leaflet said the money was 'to pay for Dylan's treatment'. Sadly, Dylan died before the treatment, and £24,000 is unspent. Dylan's parents, who inherit his estate, claim the money.\n\nWho is entitled to the £24,000?","o":["Dylan's estate, because the money raised was an outright gift to Dylan that became his property as soon as it was donated.","Dylan's parents, because they organised the appeal.","The Crown, as ownerless property.","A charity chosen by the organisers, under the cy-près doctrine.","The donors, in proportion to their donations, under a resulting trust, because the purpose has failed."],"a":4,"e":"Where money is given for a specific purpose that fails or is fulfilled with a surplus, it is held on resulting trust for the contributors in proportion to their contributions (Re Abbott Fund Trusts). The fund was for Dylan's treatment, not an outright gift to him, so his estate has no claim. The purpose is not charitable, so cy-près does not apply."},
 {"t":"resulting-trusts","q":"Ewan used his own money to buy shares worth £50,000, which were registered in the sole name of his wife, Fay. Nothing was said or written about why. Ewan has died, and his will leaves everything to his son from a previous marriage, who claims the shares.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Fay is presumed to own the shares under the presumption of advancement, unless there is evidence that Ewan did not intend a gift.","Fay holds the shares on resulting trust for Ewan's estate, because Ewan paid for them.","Fay holds the shares on resulting trust for Ewan's estate, because the presumption of advancement between spouses has been abolished by statute.","Fay owns the shares, because the presumption of advancement cannot be rebutted between spouses.","Fay and Ewan's estate are entitled to the shares equally, because they were married."],"a":0,"e":"A purchase by a husband in his wife's name attracts the presumption of advancement: equity presumes a gift. Equality Act 2010 s.199 would abolish it but has never been brought into force, so the presumption still exists, although it is weak and can be rebutted by evidence of Ewan's actual intention. Without such evidence, Fay keeps the shares."},
 {"t":"resulting-trusts","q":"Gwen transfers £80,000 from her savings into the sole bank account of her husband, Hamid. Nothing is said about why. They later separate, and Gwen claims the money back. Ignore the family court's powers on divorce.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Hamid owns the money outright, because voluntary transfers between spouses are always presumed to be gifts, whichever spouse makes them.","Hamid owns the money, because Gwen did not say at the time that it was a loan.","The money belongs to them equally, because they are married.","Hamid is presumed to hold it on resulting trust for Gwen, as advancement has never applied to a wife's transfer to her husband.","Hamid holds the money on trust for Gwen only if she can produce signed writing showing a trust."],"a":3,"e":"A voluntary transfer of personalty raises a presumed resulting trust in favour of the transferor. The presumption of advancement traditionally applies to transfers by a husband to his wife, but not by a wife to her husband, so the resulting trust presumption applies. Resulting trusts need no writing (LPA 1925 s.53(2)), and Hamid can rebut the presumption only with evidence that Gwen intended a gift."},
 {"t":"resulting-trusts","q":"Ivor paid the whole price of a flat registered in the name of his adult friend Jules. Shortly before the purchase, Ivor wrote to Jules: 'I want you to have a home of your own, as my gift.' Two years later, after they fell out, Ivor wrote: 'I always meant the flat to be mine.' Ivor now claims the flat.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Jules holds the flat on resulting trust for Ivor, because Ivor paid the whole price.","Jules owns the flat, because Ivor's letter before the purchase shows a gift, and his later statement cannot be used in his own favour.","Jules holds the flat on resulting trust for Ivor, because Ivor paid the whole price and his later letter confirms what he really intended.","Jules owns the flat, but only because the presumption of advancement applies between friends.","The flat is shared equally, because the evidence is evenly balanced."],"a":1,"e":"Paying for property in another's name raises a presumed resulting trust, but it is rebutted by evidence that the payer intended a gift. Statements made at or before the transaction count for either side, while later statements count only against the person who makes them (Shephard v Cartwright). Ivor's earlier letter shows a gift; his later self-serving letter is no help to him, and advancement does not apply between friends."},
 {"t":"resulting-trusts","q":"Kofi bought a flat with his own money in the name of his brother Lem, so that Kofi could appear to have no assets and fraudulently claim means-tested benefits. Kofi has since confessed, repaid the benefits and been dealt with by the authorities. Lem refuses to transfer the flat back, arguing that Kofi's illegal purpose bars his claim.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kofi's position?","o":["Kofi can never recover the flat, because a claimant who has acted illegally cannot obtain equity's help.","Kofi is likely to recover under a resulting trust, because refusing his claim would not be a proportionate response to the illegality.","Kofi can recover only if he can prove his claim without relying on or mentioning the illegal purpose behind the purchase in Lem's name.","Lem owns the flat absolutely, because the illegal purpose rebuts any resulting trust.","The flat passes to the Crown, because property used for an illegal purpose is forfeited."],"a":1,"e":"Patel v Mirza replaced the old reliance test (Tinsley v Milligan) with a policy-based approach: would denying the claim be contrary to the public interest, considering the purpose of the law broken, other relevant policies and proportionality? Here the illegality has been dealt with and letting Lem keep the flat would be a windfall, so Kofi will usually be able to rely on the presumed resulting trust. The option based on whether Kofi must rely on the illegality states the old reliance test, which is no longer the law."},
 {"t":"resulting-trusts","q":"Mira and her adult daughter Nina bought a house in joint names as a buy-to-let investment, with no declaration of trust. Mira paid £150,000 and Nina £50,000 of the £200,000 price. Neither lived there. They have fallen out and the house is to be sold.\n\nHow is the beneficial interest most likely to be divided?","o":["Equally, because property in joint names is presumed to be held in equal shares.","Equally, because there is no signed declaration of their shares.","In shares the court thinks fair, looking at the whole course of dealing between them, as it would for a couple's jointly owned home.","75% to Mira and 25% to Nina, because the house was bought as an investment and a resulting trust applies.","75% to Mira only if she can show a written agreement to that effect."],"a":3,"e":"The Stack v Dowden presumption of equal shares applies to a couple's home in joint names. Where property is bought as an investment outside that domestic context, the courts generally apply a presumed resulting trust, dividing the interest according to contributions to the price (Laskar v Laskar). That gives Mira 75% and Nina 25%, and resulting trusts need no writing."},
 {"t":"resulting-trusts","q":"Omar transferred 10,000 shares to a trustee company, telling it to hold them 'on trust', but never saying for whom. The company has since received £60,000 in dividends on the shares.\n\nWho is entitled to the shares and dividends in equity?","o":["The trustee company, because it holds the legal title.","The Crown, as ownerless property.","The trustee company's own shareholders.","Omar, at law, because the transfer to the company was void and the shares never left his ownership.","Omar, because the trustee company holds them on resulting trust for him."],"a":4,"e":"Where a settlor transfers property on trust but does not say who is to benefit, the beneficial interest has not been disposed of and results to the settlor under an automatic resulting trust (Vandervell v IRC). The transfer of legal title was effective, so the company holds the shares and dividends as trustee for Omar. A trustee never takes beneficially just because it holds the legal title."},
 {"t":"resulting-trusts","q":"Penn Ltd lent £200,000 to Quill Ltd 'to help with its cash flow'. The money was paid into Quill's general bank account, and nothing in the loan agreement restricted how it could be used. Quill Ltd has gone into insolvent liquidation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Penn Ltd's position?","o":["Penn Ltd is an unsecured creditor, because the money was at Quill Ltd's free disposal and no trust arose.","Penn Ltd can recover the £200,000 in full under a resulting trust, because the loan had a purpose.","Penn Ltd can recover the money only if it is still in a separate account.","Penn Ltd has a charge over Quill Ltd's assets, because it lent money to the company.","Penn Ltd can recover the money, because a loan made to a company that later becomes insolvent is held on trust."],"a":0,"e":"A Quistclose trust arises only where money is lent for a specific purpose on the mutual understanding that it is not to be at the borrower's free disposal. A general purpose such as 'cash flow', with no restriction and payment into the general account, shows the money became Quill's to use as it liked. Penn Ltd is therefore an ordinary unsecured creditor; no charge exists unless one was created."},
 {"t":"resulting-trusts","q":"Tom, an adult, bought a house in his sole name for £200,000, paying the whole price from his savings. His friend Sami, who does not live there, later paid £30,000 for a new kitchen and an extension. There were no discussions about ownership.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Sami has a beneficial interest under a resulting trust?","o":["He has a 15% share under a resulting trust, reflecting his £30,000 contribution.","He has a share under a resulting trust, because the kitchen and extension increased the value of the house by more than £30,000.","He has no share under a resulting trust, because only contributions to the purchase price at the time of purchase count.","He has a half share, because equity presumes equality between friends who contribute to property.","He has no share, because a resulting trust of land must be evidenced in signed writing."],"a":2,"e":"A presumed resulting trust arises from contributions to the purchase price at the time of acquisition. Later payments for improvements do not create one. Sami may have other remedies (for example a debt or an estoppel claim if there were assurances), but not a resulting trust share; writing is not needed for resulting trusts in any event (LPA 1925 s.53(2))."},

 {"t":"family-home","q":"Petra and Quinn, an unmarried couple, bought a house in joint names in 2008 with no declaration of trust and shared all costs equally until Quinn moved out in 2012. Since then Petra alone has paid the mortgage and all the bills, and Quinn has bought a flat of his own. They never discussed their shares. The house is now to be sold.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will decide their shares?","o":["Equally, because the house is in joint names and there was no declaration of trust.","Equal shares are the starting point, but their conduct since 2012 may show a changed intention, and the court may then decide fair shares.","In proportion to their financial contributions over the whole period, calculated as a resulting trust from the date of purchase until the sale.","Petra takes the whole beneficial interest, because Quinn abandoned the house.","Equally, because a common intention can only be formed at the date of purchase."],"a":1,"e":"For a couple's home in joint names, the starting point is equal shares, but this can be displaced by a common intention formed at purchase or later, inferred from conduct. If it is clear they intended to depart from equality but their actual shares cannot be inferred, the court may impute the shares it considers fair having regard to the whole course of dealing (Jones v Kernott, where the claimant received 90%). A resulting trust calculation is not used for a couple's home."},
 {"t":"family-home","q":"Rosa and Sid, an unmarried couple, lived together for 20 years and had three children. They bought a house in joint names with no declaration of trust; Rosa paid 70% of the price. Throughout the relationship they kept their finances strictly separate, each paying particular bills and keeping separate savings and investments. They have now separated.\n\nHow is the beneficial interest most likely to be divided?","o":["Equally, because joint names always means equal shares in the absence of a declaration.","Equally, because they had children together.","70% to Rosa, because a resulting trust calculation applies whenever two cohabitants contribute unequally to the price of a home.","Rosa is likely to receive more than half, because their strictly separate finances can show they did not intend to share equally.","Sid takes nothing, because he paid less than half the price."],"a":3,"e":"Equal shares are the starting point for a home in joint names, but they can be displaced by the whole course of dealing. In Stack v Dowden, a long relationship with children but strictly separate finances and unequal contributions was an unusual case justifying unequal shares (65% to the larger contributor). The court looks at all the circumstances, not a strict resulting trust calculation."},
 {"t":"family-home","q":"Tania lives in a house registered in the sole name of her partner Umar, which he bought before they met. In 12 years together they never discussed her owning any part of it. She redecorated the house, paid for groceries and cared for their children, while Umar paid the mortgage. They have now separated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Tania's position?","o":["She is unlikely to have a beneficial interest, because there is no evidence of a common intention that she should own a share.","She has a half share, because they lived together as a couple for many years.","She has a share decided by what the court considers fair, looking at the whole course of dealing between them over their 12 years together.","She has a share under a resulting trust because of her contributions to the household.","She has a share, because the court can impute an intention to share where that would be fair."],"a":0,"e":"In a sole name case the claimant must first prove an actual common intention to share ownership (express or inferred), plus detrimental reliance (Lloyds Bank v Rosset). There were no discussions, and redecorating, buying groceries and childcare do not show an intention to share the house itself. The court can impute an intention only when quantifying a share, never to find that one exists (Capehorn v Harris)."},
 {"t":"family-home","q":"Vince bought a house in his sole name. When Wanda moved in, he told her, 'This house is as much yours as mine.' Wanda kept her own flat, which she let out, made no payments towards the house or household costs (Vince insisted on paying everything), and did nothing she would not otherwise have done. They have now separated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Wanda's position?","o":["She has a half share, because Vince's words amounted to an express declaration of trust of the house in her favour.","She has a half share, because there was an express common intention to share.","She has a share decided by the court as fair, because there were express discussions.","She has a share under a resulting trust, because she lived in the house.","She is unlikely to have an interest, because she did not act to her detriment in reliance on the common intention."],"a":4,"e":"An oral declaration of trust of land is unenforceable without signed writing (LPA 1925 s.53(1)(b)), so Wanda must rely on a common intention constructive trust. That needs both a common intention to share and detrimental reliance on it. Vince's words show the intention, but Wanda changed nothing in reliance, so no constructive trust arises; she paid nothing towards the price, so there is no resulting trust either."},
 {"t":"family-home","q":"For 30 years Zach worked on his parents' farm for low wages, relying on their repeated assurances that he would inherit a substantial part of it. After a family argument, his parents, who are still alive and living on the farm, made new wills leaving him nothing. The court finds that a proprietary estoppel has been established.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will decide Zach's remedy?","o":["It must transfer to Zach immediately the whole share of the farm he was promised.","It can award only the value of the wages Zach lost by working on the farm.","It will aim to remedy the unconscionability, usually starting from Zach's expectation but adjusting it where appropriate.","It will declare a beneficial share in the farm calculated according to the value of Zach's financial and labour contributions.","It cannot give any remedy until both parents have died, because wills can always be changed."],"a":2,"e":"The court has a flexible discretion to satisfy the equity. Guest v Guest holds that the aim is to remedy the unconscionability of going back on the promise; the starting point is usually to fulfil the expectation, but the court may adjust it, for example with a discount for accelerated receipt or a money award. An assurance about inheritance can found an estoppel even though wills are revocable."},
 {"t":"family-home","q":"Ashok, a property developer, spent £150,000 obtaining planning permission for a site owned by Bree after they reached an oral 'agreement in principle' that he would then buy it at a fixed price. Both knew the agreement was not legally binding. Bree has now refused to sell.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ashok's position?","o":["He can enforce the agreement by proprietary estoppel, because he relied on it to his detriment by spending £150,000 on the planning application.","He can enforce the agreement under a common intention constructive trust.","He has an interest in the site, because the oral agreement satisfies the formalities for land contracts.","His estoppel claim is unlikely to succeed, because both knew the agreement was not binding and he expected a contract, not an interest.","He is entitled to buy the site at the agreed price, because Bree's refusal is unconscionable."],"a":3,"e":"In a commercial setting, where both parties know their arrangement is not binding and the claimant expects a future contract, proprietary estoppel will usually fail (Cobbe v Yeoman's Row Management). An oral agreement does not satisfy LP(MP)A 1989 s.2, which requires signed writing containing all the terms. Ashok may have a restitutionary claim for the value of his work, but not an interest in the land."},
 {"t":"family-home","q":"Cleo and Dev bought a flat together. The TR1 transfer, signed by both, declares that they hold the beneficial interest as joint tenants. Cleo paid 80% of the price. They have separated, and Dev has served a valid notice severing the joint tenancy.\n\nHow is the beneficial interest now held?","o":["80% to Cleo and 20% to Dev, because after severance the shares follow contributions.","In equal shares, because on severance of a declared beneficial joint tenancy each takes an equal share.","In shares decided by the whole course of dealing, because severance brings the declaration of a joint tenancy to an end.","Cleo takes the whole flat by survivorship.","As joint tenants still, because a declaration of a joint tenancy cannot be severed."],"a":1,"e":"An express declaration of the beneficial interests is conclusive, unless set aside for fraud or mistake. On severance, a beneficial joint tenancy becomes a tenancy in common in equal shares, whatever each party paid (Goodman v Gallant). Survivorship only operates on death while the joint tenancy continues."},
 {"t":"family-home","q":"Ellie and Finn own a house in joint names with no declaration of trust. After they separated, Finn told Ellie in a phone call, 'You keep the house, and I'll keep my pension.' Relying on this, Ellie paid the mortgage alone for eight years and gave up any claim to his pension. Finn now claims half the house, arguing that a disposition of his equitable interest needed his signed writing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Ellie is likely to own the whole beneficial interest under a common intention constructive trust, which needs no signed writing.","Finn keeps his half share, because his oral statement was not a signed disposition of his equitable interest in the house, as the law requires.","Finn keeps his half share, because a common intention can be formed only when a house is bought.","Ellie is entitled to an extra share only in proportion to her mortgage payments since separation.","Ellie owns the whole house only if Finn's statement was made in front of witnesses."],"a":0,"e":"A common intention about beneficial shares can be formed after purchase, including on separation. Where one party relies on it to their detriment, a common intention constructive trust arises, and constructive trusts are exempt from writing requirements under LPA 1925 s.53(2) (Hudson v Hathway, where the claimant took the whole interest). The court gives effect to the agreed shares rather than a contributions calculation."},
 {"t":"family-home","q":"Gina's partner Harry bought a house in his sole name, paying the deposit himself. For the next ten years Gina paid half of every monthly mortgage instalment from her salary. They never discussed ownership. They have now separated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gina's position?","o":["She has no interest, because there were no express discussions about sharing ownership.","She can recover only the mortgage money she paid, as a loan.","A common intention can be inferred from her mortgage payments, and her share is decided by the whole course of dealing.","She has exactly half of the beneficial interest, because she paid half of every mortgage instalment throughout the ten years.","She has an interest only if she contributed to the deposit when the house was bought."],"a":2,"e":"Without express discussions, a common intention to share can be inferred from conduct, and direct contributions to the mortgage instalments are the clearest example (Lloyds Bank v Rosset). Her share is then quantified by looking at the whole course of dealing between them, and the court can impute a fair share (Oxley v Hiscock). It is not a strict arithmetic calculation, and deposit contributions are not essential."},
 {"t":"family-home","q":"Ivy has a beneficial interest under a common intention constructive trust in a house registered in the sole name of her partner Jack, where she lives. Without telling her, Jack mortgaged the house to Kestrel Bank, which made no inquiries of Ivy and received no consent from her. Jack has defaulted and the bank seeks possession.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ivy's interest binds the bank?","o":["The bank takes free of Ivy's interest, because her interest was not registered.","The bank takes free, because Ivy's interest was overreached when the bank paid the loan money to Jack as the registered proprietor.","The bank takes free, because a constructive trust interest cannot bind a third party.","The bank is bound only if Ivy contributed to the purchase price in cash.","Ivy's interest is likely to bind the bank, because she was in actual occupation and the money was paid to a sole trustee."],"a":4,"e":"A beneficial interest of a person in actual occupation of registered land can override a later mortgage (LRA 2002 Sch 3 para 2; Williams & Glyn's Bank v Boland), unless the lender asked and the beneficiary failed to disclose it. Overreaching requires the money to be paid to at least two trustees or a trust corporation (City of London BS v Flegg), and here it was paid to Jack alone. A constructive trust interest is a proprietary interest capable of binding third parties."},

 {"t":"fiduciary-duties","q":"Lewis is the solicitor to a family trust that owns 25% of Mill Ltd. Attending company meetings on behalf of the trust, he learns confidential information showing the company is undervalued. The trustees decide not to buy more shares, so Lewis buys a majority stake personally and turns the company round. He makes a large profit, and the trust's own shares also rise in value. He acted honestly but did not obtain the fully informed consent of all the beneficiaries.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Lewis's position?","o":["He may keep the profit, because he acted honestly throughout and the trust's own shares also rose in value.","He may keep the profit, because the trustees had decided not to buy more shares.","He must account for the profit, and no allowance can be made for his work.","He must account for the profit, but the court may make him an allowance for his skill and work.","He need only compensate the trust for any loss it suffered, which is nil."],"a":3,"e":"A fiduciary must account for a profit made from information or opportunities obtained through his position, even if he acted honestly and the trust benefited or could not have made the profit itself (Boardman v Phipps). Only the fully informed consent of all the beneficiaries, the trust instrument or the court could authorise it. Because he acted honestly and his work benefited the trust, the court may make him an equitable allowance for his skill and effort."},
 {"t":"fiduciary-duties","q":"Mabel is the trustee of a trust that holds the lease of a shop for her nephew, aged 15. When the lease expires, the landlord refuses to renew it to the trust because of the nephew's age, but offers a new lease to Mabel personally, which she accepts. The shop now makes good profits.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Mabel holds the new lease on trust for her nephew, because she obtained it through her position as trustee.","Mabel owns the new lease outright, because the landlord would not have granted it to the trust.","Mabel owns the new lease, but must pay her nephew compensation for the loss of the old lease that the trust held.","Mabel holds the new lease on trust only if she acted dishonestly.","Mabel owns the new lease outright, because the old lease had come to an end."],"a":0,"e":"A trustee cannot take for herself an opportunity that came to her through the trust, even if the trust itself could not have taken it (Keech v Sandford). The no profit rule is strict and does not depend on dishonesty. Mabel holds the renewed lease, and its profits, on trust for her nephew."},
 {"t":"fiduciary-duties","q":"Nadia, a chartered accountant, and Olly, a lay person, are the two trustees of a family trust. The trust deed contains no charging clause. Nadia spends 40 hours preparing the trust's tax returns and wants to be paid her usual professional rate. Olly agrees in writing that she should be paid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nadia's position?","o":["She cannot be paid, because trustees must always act without payment unless the trust deed itself contains an express charging clause.","She can be paid only if all the beneficiaries agree.","She can receive reasonable remuneration, because she acts in a professional capacity, is not a sole trustee and Olly agreed in writing.","She can be paid her usual fees only with the court's approval.","She can be paid, but only for work that a lay trustee could not have done."],"a":2,"e":"Where the trust instrument makes no provision, Trustee Act 2000 s.29 entitles a trustee acting in a professional capacity, who is not a sole trustee, to reasonable remuneration if each other trustee agrees in writing (not for charitable trusts). Nadia meets those conditions. Remuneration is limited to what is reasonable, and covers services even if a lay trustee could have provided them."},
 {"t":"fiduciary-duties","q":"Pete is the executor of his late uncle's estate, which includes a successful bicycle shop in Ambridge. Pete is running the shop for the estate until it can be sold. He now plans to open his own bicycle shop two streets away.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["He may do so, as long as he does not use the estate's customer lists or other confidential information about the business.","He may do so, because an executor's duties end when the estate's assets are sold.","He may do so if the estate's business does not actually lose any customers.","He may do so, but must account to the estate for half of his profits.","He can be restrained, because a competing business puts his personal interest in conflict with his duty to the estate."],"a":4,"e":"A fiduciary must not put himself in a position where his personal interest conflicts, or might conflict, with his duty. Setting up a business that competes with one he is running for the estate is a clear conflict, and he can be restrained (Re Thomson). A real sensible possibility of conflict is enough; actual loss of customers does not need to be shown."},
 {"t":"fiduciary-duties","q":"Rhea, a trustee, buys from her adult nephew Sol his interest in remainder under the trust. Before the sale she obtains an independent valuation, tells Sol everything she knows about the trust property and pays him the full valued price. A year later Sol regrets the sale and wants it set aside.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Sol can set the sale aside, because a trustee can never buy anything from a beneficiary, however fair the terms of the sale.","The sale is likely to stand, because Rhea took no advantage of her position, made full disclosure and paid a fair price.","Sol can set the sale aside, because the purchase was not authorised by the trust deed.","The sale stands only if all the other beneficiaries consented to it.","Sol can set the sale aside within six years, whatever the price paid."],"a":1,"e":"A trustee buying a beneficiary's interest is subject to the fair-dealing rule, not the self-dealing rule. The sale stands if the trustee proves she took no advantage of her position, made full disclosure and the transaction was fair and honest (Tito v Waddell (No 2)). Rhea has done all three. The self-dealing rule, which makes a sale voidable however fair, applies to purchases of trust property itself."},
 {"t":"fiduciary-duties","q":"Tara is a trustee of a trust that owns 30% of the shares in Upton Ltd. She was appointed a director of Upton Ltd five years before she became a trustee and has been re-elected every year since by the other shareholders, without the trust's votes being used. She receives £20,000 a year in directors' fees. The trust deed says nothing about directors' fees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["She must account to the trust for all fees received since she became a trustee.","She must account for the fees, because there is a possibility of conflict.","She must account for the fees unless the trust deed expressly allows her to keep fees from companies the trust invests in.","She may keep only the part of her fees that relates to work done before she became a trustee.","She may keep the fees, because she did not obtain the directorship by using the trust's shareholding."],"a":4,"e":"Trustees must account for directors' fees where they obtained the directorship by using the trust's shareholding (Re Macadam). But where the trustee was already a director, or would have been appointed without the trust's votes, the fees are not a profit from the trust position and may be kept (Re Gee). Tara was appointed before becoming a trustee and re-elected without the trust's votes."},
 {"t":"fiduciary-duties","q":"Wes, a trustee of a family trust, retired as trustee so that he could buy a field owned by the trust. Two months after retiring he bought it from the remaining trustees at its full market value, in a private sale. A beneficiary now objects.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The sale stands, because Wes was no longer a trustee when he bought the field.","The sale stands, because he paid the full market price.","The sale can be set aside, because a trustee cannot avoid the self-dealing rule by retiring with a view to the purchase.","The sale can be set aside only if the beneficiaries prove that Wes used confidential information he obtained as a trustee.","The sale stands, provided the remaining trustees agreed to it."],"a":2,"e":"A trustee who buys trust property is caught by the self-dealing rule: the sale is voidable by any beneficiary however fair the price. The rule cannot be dodged by retiring in order to buy (Wright v Morgan). Only the trust instrument, the fully informed consent of all the beneficiaries or the court could authorise it."},
 {"t":"fiduciary-duties","q":"Xavier, a solicitor acting for both a lender and a borrower on a mortgage, carelessly forgets to tell the lender that the borrower has taken out a second loan. Xavier gains nothing from the omission and has no wish to favour the borrower. The lender suffers a loss.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Xavier's liability?","o":["He is liable in negligence, but not for breach of fiduciary duty, because he was careless rather than disloyal.","He is liable for breach of fiduciary duty, because every breach of duty by a fiduciary is a fiduciary breach.","He must account for his fees as a profit made in breach of fiduciary duty.","He has no liability at all, because he gained nothing from the omission and had no wish to favour the borrower over the lender.","He is liable for breach of fiduciary duty, because he acted for two clients."],"a":0,"e":"The distinguishing obligation of a fiduciary is loyalty. A careless failure, without any disloyalty or conflict being acted on, is a breach of the duty of care (negligence), not a breach of fiduciary duty (Bristol and West BS v Mothew). He is still liable for loss caused by his negligence even though he gained nothing."},
 {"t":"fiduciary-duties","q":"Yusuf, a trustee, wants to buy a painting owned by the trust at its independent valuation. All three beneficiaries are adults with full capacity. After Yusuf gives them full details of the painting, the valuation and his interest, they all consent in writing. A year later, the painting has risen in value and one beneficiary wants the sale set aside.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The beneficiary can set the sale aside, because self-dealing is voidable by any beneficiary regardless of consent.","The sale stands, because all the beneficiaries, being adults with capacity, gave fully informed consent.","The sale stands only if the court also approved it.","The beneficiary can set the sale aside, because the trust deed did not authorise it.","The sale stands only if Yusuf paid more than the valuation."],"a":1,"e":"The self-dealing rule makes a trustee's purchase of trust property voidable however fair, unless it is authorised. Authorisation can come from the trust instrument, the court or the fully informed consent of all the beneficiaries, who must be adults with capacity. Here all three gave informed consent, so a beneficiary cannot later change her mind because the painting has risen in value."},
 {"t":"fiduciary-duties","q":"Zara held a senior role at Arc Capital Ltd in which she owed it fiduciary duties. While still in that role, she took for her own new business an investment opportunity that came to her through her work for Arc, and made a profit of £2 million. She argues that she should not have to pay it over, because she could have resigned and lawfully made the same profit a few months later.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Zara's position?","o":["She may keep the profit if she proves she would have made it anyway after leaving.","She must pay over only the part of the profit that she could not have made but for her breach.","She need only compensate Arc for any loss that Arc suffered.","She must account for the profit, because the no profit rule is strict and it is no defence that she could have made it anyway.","She may keep the profit if Arc could not itself have taken the opportunity, because then Arc has lost nothing by her actions."],"a":3,"e":"A fiduciary must account for profits made from opportunities that came through their position. In Rukhadze v Recovery Partners the Supreme Court refused to introduce a 'but for' defence based on what the fiduciary could have done lawfully, reaffirming the strict rule in Regal and Boardman. It is also no defence that the principal could not have taken the opportunity or suffered no loss."},

 {"t":"trustees-appointment","q":"Arlo's will appoints his sister Bel and his nephew Cal, who is 16, as trustees of a trust of shares for Arlo's grandchildren. Arlo has just died.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Cal can be a trustee, but cannot exercise any of his powers until he reaches the age of 18.","Cal can be a trustee if Bel agrees to supervise him.","Cal's appointment is void, and Bel can act alone or a replacement can be appointed.","The whole trust fails, because one of the named trustees cannot act.","Cal's appointment is valid, but he cannot sign documents until he is 18."],"a":2,"e":"A minor cannot be appointed as a trustee: the appointment is void (LPA 1925 s.20). A trust of personalty needs no minimum number of trustees, so Bel can act alone, or another trustee can be appointed under TA 1925 s.36. Equity will not let a trust fail for want of a trustee."},
 {"t":"trustees-appointment","q":"A trust deed gives the settlor, Dora, power to appoint new trustees. One of the three trustees, Eli, has died. The other two, Fin and Gus, want to appoint their accountant in his place, but Dora wants to appoint her son, who is willing to act.\n\nWho has the power to appoint the new trustee?","o":["Fin and Gus, because continuing trustees always have the power to appoint.","The beneficiaries, by majority vote.","The court, because the trustees and Dora disagree.","Fin and Gus, because a settlor drops out of the picture once the trust has been created and has no continuing role.","Dora, because a person nominated in the trust deed to appoint new trustees has the first right to do so."],"a":4,"e":"Under TA 1925 s.36(1) the power to appoint a replacement belongs first to the person nominated in the trust instrument for that purpose. Only if there is no such person, or they are unable or unwilling, do the continuing trustees appoint, and then the personal representatives of the last surviving trustee. Dora is willing, so she appoints in writing."},
 {"t":"trustees-appointment","q":"Hal is one of two trustees of a family trust. He moved to Australia 18 months ago and has stopped responding to correspondence, but he refuses to retire. The trust deed nominates no one to appoint new trustees. The other trustee, Ida, wants to replace him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Ida cannot replace Hal without his consent unless the court orders it, because he has not committed any breach of trust.","Ida can appoint a new trustee in Hal's place in writing, because Hal has remained outside the UK for more than 12 months.","Ida can act as sole trustee for all purposes, because Hal's trusteeship ended automatically.","Only the beneficiaries can remove Hal, by a unanimous written direction.","Ida can replace Hal only if he has committed a breach of trust."],"a":1,"e":"TA 1925 s.36(1) allows a trustee to be replaced if he remains outside the UK for more than 12 months, even without his consent. With no nominated person, the continuing trustee (Ida) makes the appointment in writing, ideally by deed. Hal's office does not end automatically, and no breach of trust is needed."},
 {"t":"trustees-appointment","q":"A trust of investments (no land) has three trustees. They are concerned about the workload and want to appoint two additional trustees, without anyone retiring. The trust deed says nothing about the number of trustees.\n\nCan they appoint both additional trustees?","o":["Yes, because there is no maximum number of trustees of personalty, so the existing trustees may add as many as they like.","No, because additional trustees can be appointed only by the court.","Yes, provided the beneficiaries consent in writing.","No, they can add only one, because an appointment of additional trustees cannot take the total above four.","No, because additional trustees can be appointed only when a trustee retires."],"a":3,"e":"TA 1925 s.36(6) allows the existing trustees (where there are not more than three) to appoint additional trustees in writing, but the appointment must not take the number above four. Although there is no general maximum for trusts of personalty, this statutory power is capped. More than four would be possible only if the trust instrument allowed it."},
 {"t":"trustees-appointment","q":"Jo was the sole surviving trustee of a family trust. She has died, and her will appoints Kit as her executor. The trust deed nominates no one to appoint new trustees. The beneficiaries include minors.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how new trustees can be appointed?","o":["Kit, as Jo's personal representative, can exercise the trustee's powers and appoint new trustees.","The beneficiaries automatically become trustees.","Only the court can appoint new trustees, because there are no continuing trustees left to make the appointment.","The trust ends and the trust property passes under Jo's will.","The settlor's personal representatives must appoint new trustees."],"a":0,"e":"When the last surviving trustee dies, her personal representatives can exercise the trustee's powers until new trustees are appointed (TA 1925 s.18(2)), and they come third in the order of who may appoint under s.36(1). The trust property does not pass under Jo's will for her own beneficiaries. Court appointment is a fallback where no one is able and willing."},
 {"t":"trustees-appointment","q":"Lena is appointed as a new trustee by a deed of appointment signed by the continuing trustees. The trust property consists of money in a bank account and 5,000 shares in a listed company held in certificated form.\n\nWhat further step is needed for Lena to hold the legal title with the other trustees?","o":["The shares must be transferred into the trustees' names and registered by the company, because the deed does not vest shares.","Nothing further, because a deed of appointment automatically vests all the trust property, including shares, in the new and continuing trustees.","The deed must be registered at Companies House for both the shares and the bank account.","The beneficiaries must sign the deed for the vesting to take effect.","The trustees must obtain a vesting order from the court for both the shares and the bank account."],"a":0,"e":"An appointment by deed automatically vests most trust property in the new and continuing trustees (TA 1925 s.40). Shares and stock are excepted (s.40(4)), because legal title to shares passes only by a transfer registered by the company. The bank account will usually vest under the deed, subject to the bank updating its mandate."},
 {"t":"trustees-appointment","q":"A trust of investments has three trustees, Lia, Max and Ned. Lia wishes to retire, but no one is willing to replace her. Max and Ned are content to carry on as trustees and are willing to consent to her retirement. The trust deed does not nominate anyone to appoint trustees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Lia can retire?","o":["She cannot retire without a replacement in any circumstances.","She can retire simply by telling the beneficiaries in writing that she no longer wishes to act as trustee.","She can retire only with the court's permission.","She can retire by deed, because two persons will remain as trustees and they consent by deed.","She can retire only if all the beneficiaries consent."],"a":3,"e":"Under TA 1925 s.39 a trustee may retire without a replacement by deed if, after her discharge, at least two persons (or a trust corporation) will remain as trustees and the co-trustees consent by deed. Max and Ned will remain and consent, so Lia can retire. The beneficiaries' consent is not required."},
 {"t":"trustees-appointment","q":"Mo is one of two trustees of a family trust whose beneficiaries include young children. Relations between Mo and the adult beneficiaries have broken down completely; he refuses to discuss anything with them, and decisions about the trust have stalled for two years. No breach of trust has been proved. The deed contains no power of removal.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Mo can be removed?","o":["Mo cannot be removed unless he is proved to have committed a breach of trust, however bad relations with the beneficiaries are.","Mo can be removed by a majority of the beneficiaries.","Mo can be removed only if he agrees to retire.","Mo can be removed by his co-trustee by deed.","The court can remove Mo if the hostility is preventing proper administration, considering the welfare of the beneficiaries."],"a":4,"e":"The court can remove a trustee under its inherent jurisdiction (or replace him under TA 1925 s.41), guided mainly by the welfare of the beneficiaries. Serious misconduct need not be proved where continued hostility is preventing proper administration (Letterstedt v Broers). The beneficiaries cannot use TLATA 1996 s.19 because some are minors, and a co-trustee has no power to remove another trustee."},
 {"t":"trustees-appointment","q":"A trust of investments is held for three siblings in equal shares absolutely. Two are adults, and the youngest is 16. The trust deed nominates no one to appoint new trustees. All three siblings, and the 16-year-old's parent, want one of the trustees, Nell, replaced by their cousin.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["They can direct the trustees in writing to appoint their cousin, because the majority of the beneficiaries are adults who all agree.","They cannot use the statutory power to direct the trustees, because not all the beneficiaries are adults with full capacity.","They can direct the appointment if the minor's parent consents on his behalf.","They can direct the appointment, because the trust deed does not nominate anyone.","They can end the trust and resettle it with new trustees."],"a":1,"e":"TLATA 1996 s.19 lets beneficiaries direct the retirement or appointment of trustees only if there is no nominated person and the beneficiaries are all of full age and capacity and together absolutely entitled. A 16-year-old cannot join in, and a parent cannot consent for him. For the same reason the siblings cannot end the trust under Saunders v Vautier."},
 {"t":"trustees-appointment","q":"Opal's will leaves her house to five named people as trustees of land for her grandchildren. All five are adults and willing to act.\n\nWho holds the legal title to the house?","o":["All five, because there is no limit on the number of trustees of land appointed by will.","None of them until one disclaims.","Only the first four named who are able and willing to act.","Four of them chosen by the court.","All five, but only four may sign any transfer."],"a":2,"e":"The legal title to land cannot be held by more than four trustees. Where more are named, only the first four named who are able and willing to act become trustees of the land (TA 1925 s.34). No court selection or disclaimer is needed."},

 {"t":"trustee-duties-powers","q":"A will trust arising in 2010 holds £90,000 for the testator's three grandchildren in equal shares, contingent on each reaching 25. There is no prior interest. Hana, aged 20, asks the trustees for £30,000 to start a business. The will says nothing about advancement.\n\nWhat is the maximum the trustees can advance to Hana?","o":["£30,000, the whole of her presumptive share.","£15,000, one half of her presumptive share.","£45,000, one half of the whole fund.","Nothing, because her interest is contingent.","£10,000, one third of her presumptive share."],"a":1,"e":"TA 1925 s.32 allows advancement of capital to a beneficiary with a vested or contingent interest. For trusts created before 1 October 2014 the limit is one half of the beneficiary's presumptive share, unless the trust instrument extends it. Hana's presumptive share is £30,000, so the maximum is £15,000; for a trust arising on or after 1 October 2014 it would be the whole £30,000."},
 {"t":"trustee-duties-powers","q":"Trustees of a family trust have no express investment clause. They want to buy a leasehold flat in Manchester for one of the adult beneficiaries to live in rent-free.\n\nCan they do so?","o":["No, because the general power of investment does not extend to land, and no other statutory power fills that gap.","No, because trustees may buy land only as an investment that produces income.","Yes, but only if the trust deed expressly permits the purchase of land.","Yes, because trustees may acquire freehold or leasehold land in the UK for occupation by a beneficiary.","Yes, but only with the consent of all the beneficiaries."],"a":3,"e":"Trustee Act 2000 s.8 allows trustees to acquire freehold or leasehold land in the United Kingdom as an investment, for occupation by a beneficiary or for any other reason. It is true that the general power of investment in s.3 does not cover land, but s.8 provides a separate power. Land outside the UK would need an express power."},
 {"t":"trustee-duties-powers","q":"The trustees of a £2 million fund appoint a discretionary fund manager after comparing several firms and checking references. They sign a written agreement and give the manager a written policy statement, and they review its performance every quarter. Without warning, an employee of the manager steals £300,000 of the fund.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the trustees' liability?","o":["They are liable, because trustees are always liable for the defaults of their agents.","They are liable, because investment decisions cannot be delegated.","They are liable, because they should have obtained the beneficiaries' consent before appointing any fund manager.","They are liable for half the loss, because they chose the manager.","They are not liable, because they complied with their duty of care in appointing and reviewing the manager."],"a":4,"e":"Trustees may delegate asset management under Trustee Act 2000 Part IV, provided the agreement is in or evidenced in writing and they give a written policy statement (s.15). They are not liable for an agent's default unless they failed to comply with the duty of care in appointing or reviewing the agent (s.23). On these facts they took reasonable care."},
 {"t":"trustee-duties-powers","q":"The trustees of a discretionary trust want to appoint an investment manager to run the trust portfolio. To save time, they also want the manager to decide each year which beneficiaries should receive the income.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what they may do?","o":["They may delegate investment management, but not the decision about which beneficiaries receive income.","They may delegate both functions, provided the agreement is in writing.","They may delegate neither function, because trustees must always act personally.","They may delegate both functions, provided they give the manager a written policy statement covering each of them.","They may delegate the income decision only if the beneficiaries consent."],"a":0,"e":"Trustees may delegate 'delegable functions', including investment management, under Trustee Act 2000 s.11. Decisions about the distribution of trust assets (who benefits) are not delegable, so the trustees must decide that themselves. The duty to act personally is subject to these statutory exceptions."},
 {"t":"trustee-duties-powers","q":"Two lay trustees hold a small fund of £8,000 for a beneficiary who will need it within a year. Without taking any financial advice, they place it in an instant-access deposit account with a major UK bank.\n\nWhich of the following best describes their position?","o":["They are in breach, because trustees must always obtain and consider proper advice before investing, whatever the size of the fund.","They are in breach, because a deposit account is not an investment.","They are probably not in breach, because they may reasonably conclude that advice is unnecessary for a small, short-term fund.","They are in breach unless all the beneficiaries agreed.","They are not in breach, because the duty to take advice applies only to professional trustees."],"a":2,"e":"Trustee Act 2000 s.5 requires trustees to obtain and consider proper advice before investing, unless they reasonably conclude that in all the circumstances it is unnecessary or inappropriate. For a small fund needed in the short term, placing it on deposit without advice is likely to be reasonable. The duty applies to lay and professional trustees alike."},
 {"t":"trustee-duties-powers","q":"Pia is a trustee of a family trust. Because of her personal beliefs, she refuses to allow any investment in companies that sell meat products. The adult beneficiaries do not share her views, the trust deed says nothing about ethical investment, and the exclusion would significantly reduce the fund's returns.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Pia's position?","o":["She is entitled to follow her conscience, because trustees have a wide discretion over investments.","She is entitled to do so if she is the only trustee.","She is in breach only if the trust deed expressly forbids ethical investment.","She is likely to be in breach, because investment powers must be used in the beneficiaries' best financial interests.","She is in breach, because trustees may never take ethical considerations into account."],"a":3,"e":"Investment powers must be exercised in the best financial interests of the beneficiaries, and trustees must put aside their personal, political or moral views (Cowan v Scargill). Ethical investment is allowed where it causes no significant financial detriment, where all the adult beneficiaries agree or where the trust instrument permits it. None of these applies here, but ethical factors are not always forbidden."},
 {"t":"trustee-duties-powers","q":"Quarry Trust Co Ltd, a professional trust company that charges fees, is the trustee of a family trust. Through carelessness it fails to review the trust's investments for five years, and the fund performs badly. It argues that it should be judged by the same standard as an unpaid family member acting as trustee.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the standard of care that applies?","o":["The care and skill expected of a professional trust company, which is higher than that expected of a lay trustee.","The standard of an ordinary prudent person of business, which is the same standard as applies to an unpaid lay trustee.","It is liable only if it acted dishonestly.","There is no duty of care, because the statutory duty does not apply to reviewing investments.","The standard the beneficiaries expected of it when the trust was set up."],"a":0,"e":"The statutory duty of care in Trustee Act 2000 s.1 requires the care and skill reasonable in the circumstances, having regard to any special knowledge or experience the trustee has, and, for a trustee acting in the course of a business or profession, the knowledge and experience reasonably expected of such a person. It applies to investment functions, including reviewing investments (Sch 1). A paid professional trustee is therefore held to a higher standard."},
 {"t":"trustee-duties-powers","q":"Under a will trust arising in 2022, the residuary fund is held for the testator's grandson Owen, aged 12, contingent on his reaching 21. The will says nothing about income. Owen's mother asks the trustees to pay his school fees from the income. Owen also has some income from another trust.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the trustees' power?","o":["They cannot pay anything until Owen reaches 18.","They can pay only if the payment is reasonable in all the circumstances, after first taking account of Owen's other income.","They may apply the income for Owen's maintenance, education or benefit as they think fit, accumulating any surplus.","They must pay all the income to Owen's mother, because she is his parent.","They can use capital, but not income, for school fees."],"a":2,"e":"TA 1925 s.31 allows trustees to apply income for a minor's maintenance, education or benefit where the gift carries the intermediate income (a contingent gift of residue does). For trusts created or arising on or after 1 October 2014 they may pay 'as they think fit'; the older test of reasonableness, taking account of other income, applies only to earlier trusts. Any surplus income must be accumulated."},
 {"t":"trustee-duties-powers","q":"Trustees hold a fund for Pia contingently on her reaching 30, failing which it passes to her brother Rob. When Pia was 22, the trustees advanced £50,000 to her under their statutory power 'to buy a flat', paying it into her personal account without any checks. Pia spent it on gambling and has since died aged 24. Rob complains.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the trustees' position?","o":["They are not liable, because Pia was an adult and the money was hers.","They are not liable, because the power of advancement is a discretion which cannot be challenged once it has been exercised.","They are liable only if they knew that Pia gambled.","They are not liable, because the advancement was within their statutory powers.","They may be liable, because when advancing money for a stated purpose they must take reasonable steps to see it is so used."],"a":4,"e":"Trustees exercising the power of advancement for a stated purpose must take reasonable steps to ensure the money is applied for that purpose (Re Pauling's Settlement Trusts). Paying it straight to Pia with no checks failed to do this. Pia's contingency has now failed, so Rob, who takes the fund on failure, has suffered loss and can claim."},
 {"t":"trustee-duties-powers","q":"A trust gives the income to Sasha for life, then the capital to her nephew Theo. At Sasha's insistence, the trustees invest the whole fund in high-yield bonds that pay a large income but are expected to fall substantially in capital value.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the trustees' position?","o":["They have acted properly, because the life tenant is entitled to the income.","They may be in breach of their duty to act impartially between the life tenant and the remainderman.","They have acted properly, provided the bonds are authorised investments under the trust deed or statute.","They are in breach only if Sasha did not consent in writing.","They have acted properly, because Theo's interest is not yet in possession."],"a":1,"e":"Trustees must act impartially between beneficiaries, balancing the life tenant's interest in income against the remainderman's interest in capital. Investing to maximise income at the expense of capital, at the life tenant's insistence, risks breaching that duty and the standard investment criteria. Theo's interest in remainder is protected now, even though it is not yet in possession, and Sasha's consent could not bind him."},

 {"t":"breach-of-trust","q":"Ada, an unpaid lay trustee, distributed a fund to the person she believed was the only beneficiary, after taking and following a solicitor's advice on the meaning of an unclear clause in the trust deed. The advice turns out to be wrong, and another beneficiary sues her for the share that should have been paid to him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ada's position?","o":["The court may relieve her wholly or partly, because she acted honestly and reasonably and ought to be excused.","She cannot be relieved, because paying the wrong person is a breach of strict liability.","She is automatically relieved, because she is unpaid.","She can be relieved only if the beneficiaries consent.","She cannot be relieved, because she should have applied to the court for directions before distributing any of the fund."],"a":0,"e":"Paying the wrong person is a breach of trust even if done honestly. But TA 1925 s.61 allows the court to relieve a trustee wholly or partly if she acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused, including for not seeking the court's directions. An honest lay trustee who acted on legal advice is a strong candidate, though relief is discretionary, not automatic."},
 {"t":"breach-of-trust","q":"Bank Trust Co, a paid professional trustee, misreads the trust deed and makes an investment that the deed in fact prohibits. It honestly believed the investment was authorised. The investment loses £200,000, and the trust company asks the court for relief.\n\nWhich of the following best describes its position?","o":["It will be relieved, because it acted honestly.","It will be relieved, because the investment was made in good faith on its reading of the deed.","It is unlikely to be relieved, because a paid professional trustee must show it acted reasonably as well as honestly.","It cannot be liable, because a mistake about the meaning of the trust deed is not a breach.","It will be relieved for half the loss, because the court must share the loss fairly between the trustee and beneficiaries."],"a":2,"e":"TA 1925 s.61 requires the trustee to have acted honestly and reasonably and to deserve to be excused; honesty alone is not enough. Courts expect higher standards of paid professional trustees and are slow to relieve them, especially for misreading their own trust deed. Making an unauthorised investment is a breach even if done in good faith."},
 {"t":"breach-of-trust","q":"Cara, an adult beneficiary with capacity, asks the trustees in a signed letter to lend £100,000 of trust money to her friend's business, which is not an authorised investment. The trustees do so and the money is lost. Cara's brother Dan, the other beneficiary, sues the trustees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Dan cannot sue, because a beneficiary consented to the breach.","Dan can sue, and the trustees have no protection at all.","Dan can sue only Cara, because she instigated the breach.","Dan can sue the trustees, but the court may impound Cara's interest to indemnify them.","Dan can sue the trustees, who can recover from Cara only if she was also one of the trustees."],"a":3,"e":"A beneficiary who consents to or instigates a breach cannot sue for it, but that does not bar other beneficiaries like Dan. Under TA 1925 s.62 the court may impound the interest of a beneficiary who instigated, requested or consented in writing to the breach, to indemnify the trustees. Cara's signed request allows this."},
 {"t":"breach-of-trust","q":"Vale Solicitors held Westbank's loan money on bare trust, to be released only on completion of a purchase with a first charge for Westbank. In breach of trust, they paid it to the seller a day early. Completion took place a few days later, and Westbank obtained its first charge. Westbank's loss of £400,000 was caused entirely by a surveyor's negligent overvaluation of the property, which it would have suffered anyway.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Vale Solicitors' liability?","o":["They are liable for the whole loss, because a trustee who pays away trust money in breach of trust must restore it in full.","They are not liable for the loss caused by the overvaluation, because compensation is limited to loss caused by the breach.","They are liable for the whole loss plus compound interest.","They are liable only if they acted dishonestly.","They are liable for half the loss, because the surveyor was also at fault."],"a":1,"e":"Equitable compensation for breach of trust requires a causal link between the breach and the loss, assessed with hindsight at the date of trial using common sense (Target Holdings v Redferns; AIB v Mark Redler). Westbank ended up with exactly what it should have had, and its loss came from the overvaluation, which would have happened anyway. So the solicitors are not liable for it."},
 {"t":"breach-of-trust","q":"In 2010, Eric, a trustee, secretly transferred £100,000 of trust money into his own account and used it to pay off his mortgage. The beneficiaries discovered this only in 2026 and want to sue him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the claim is in time?","o":["It is time-barred, because more than six years have passed since the breach.","It is time-barred, unless a beneficiary was a minor in 2010.","It is in time only if the claim is brought within six years of the date on which the beneficiaries discovered Eric's breach of trust.","It is in time only for any part of the money that Eric still holds.","It is not time-barred, because no limitation period applies to recover trust property converted to the trustee's own use."],"a":4,"e":"The normal six-year limitation period does not apply to an action in respect of a fraudulent breach of trust to which the trustee was a party, or to recover trust property converted to the trustee's own use (Limitation Act 1980 s.21(1)). Eric's deliberate misappropriation falls within both. Discoverability under s.32 is relevant only where a limitation period would otherwise apply."},
 {"t":"breach-of-trust","q":"Since 2012, Fran has been entitled under a trust to the income of a fund for her life. She was born in 2008. In 2015 the trustee negligently invested the fund in a way that greatly reduced its income. There was no fraud and the trustee kept none of the money. Fran, now 18, sues in 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the claim is in time?","o":["It is time-barred, because more than six years have passed since the breach.","It is time-barred, because the six years ran from when her parent discovered the loss.","It is in time only if she can show the trustee concealed the breach.","It is in time only if the trustee still holds the trust money.","It is in time, because time does not run against a minor until she reaches 18."],"a":4,"e":"A claim for an ordinary breach of trust must normally be brought within six years (Limitation Act 1980 s.21(3)). But time does not run against a person under a disability, which includes a minor, until the disability ends (s.28). Fran turned 18 in 2026, so her claim is well within time; concealment and retention of property are not needed."},
 {"t":"breach-of-trust","q":"Gil and Hope are trustees. Gil is also a beneficiary. He persuaded Hope to join him in making an unauthorised investment that promised a high income, which would have benefited him. The investment lost £80,000. Gil's beneficial interest is worth £50,000. The other beneficiaries sue both trustees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the loss will be borne between the trustees?","o":["Gil and Hope share the loss equally between them, because they are jointly and severally liable and both agreed to the investment.","Gil's beneficial interest must be used first to make good the loss, because he instigated the breach for his own benefit.","Hope bears the whole loss, because Gil is a beneficiary.","Gil bears none of the loss, because a beneficiary cannot be liable for a breach of trust.","The loss is borne by the other beneficiaries in proportion to their interests."],"a":1,"e":"Both trustees are jointly and severally liable to the beneficiaries. As between themselves, a trustee who is also a beneficiary and who instigated the breach for his own benefit must have his beneficial interest applied first to make good the loss before he can claim any contribution from his co-trustee (Chillingworth v Chambers). The balance is then shared as is just and equitable under the Civil Liability (Contribution) Act 1978."},
 {"t":"breach-of-trust","q":"Ivy Bank, as trustee of a family trust for 20 years, misunderstood its investment powers and never reviewed the investments. A beneficiary, Jess, sues for the fund's poor performance. The evidence cannot show that a prudent trustee who reviewed the investments properly would have achieved a better result.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Jess's claim?","o":["The bank must pay the difference between the fund's value and that of the best-performing comparable fund over the period.","The bank must pay compensation because it breached its duty to review, whatever the outcome.","The bank must account for its fees as a profit made from the breach.","Her claim is likely to fail, because she must prove that a prudent trustee would have achieved a better result.","Her claim is likely to fail, because a failure to review investments is not a breach of trust."],"a":3,"e":"Failing to review investments is a breach, but compensation requires proof of loss caused by it. Where the complaint is failure to invest properly, the beneficiary must show that a prudent trustee would have produced a better result (Nestle v National Westminster Bank). Without that evidence the claim fails, despite the breach."},
 {"t":"breach-of-trust","q":"Kay, a trustee, adopted a single policy of putting the trust's money into speculative property developments through a company the trust controls. Under that one policy, a development in Leeds made a profit of £150,000 and a development in York lost £400,000. Both were breaches of trust. The beneficiaries sue Kay.\n\nHow much is Kay liable to pay?","o":["£250,000, because the gain and loss arose from the same policy, so the gain can be set off.","£400,000, because a gain from one breach can never be set off against a loss from another.","£325,000, because only half of the gain can be set off.","Nothing, because the trust's investments made money overall in the past.","£550,000, because the gain must also be paid to the trust as an unauthorised profit made by Kay."],"a":0,"e":"A trustee generally cannot set off a gain from one breach against a loss from another (Dimes v Scott). The exception is where both arise from the same policy or transaction, as in Bartlett v Barclays Bank Trust Co, where profits and losses from two speculative developments under one policy were set off. Kay's liability is the net £250,000; the gain already belongs to the trust and is simply credited."},
 {"t":"breach-of-trust","q":"Hana joined her co-trustees in making an unauthorised investment in 2022. She retired as trustee in 2024, for reasons unconnected with the investment. In 2026 the investment collapses and the beneficiaries sue all the trustees. Hana argues that she is no longer a trustee and so cannot be liable.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hana's position?","o":["She is not liable, because she ceased to be a trustee before the investment collapsed and before any claim was made against her.","She is not liable, because her replacement took over responsibility for past breaches.","She remains liable for the 2022 breach, because a retired trustee is liable for breaches committed while in office.","She is liable only if she retired in order to allow the breach to happen.","She is liable for only half the loss, because she is no longer a trustee."],"a":2,"e":"A trustee who retires remains liable for breaches committed while she was in office. A new trustee is not liable for breaches before his appointment, though he should act on them. The 'retired to facilitate a breach' rule concerns liability for breaches committed after retirement, which is not this case."},

 {"t":"strangers","q":"Jago, a trustee, honestly but mistakenly believes that the trust deed allows him to lend trust money to a business owned by Lou. Kelly, Lou's adviser, knows that the deed prohibits such loans but persuades Jago to make the loan, for which Lou pays Kelly a commission from his own funds. The loan is lost. The beneficiaries sue Kelly.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kelly's position?","o":["She is not liable, because the trustee did not act dishonestly.","She is not liable, because she did not receive any trust property.","She is liable only if she also received trust property for her own benefit, rather than a commission from Lou.","She may be liable for dishonest assistance, because it is her dishonesty, not the trustee's, that matters.","She is liable for knowing receipt, because she was paid a commission."],"a":3,"e":"Dishonest assistance requires a breach of trust, assistance in or procurement of it, and dishonesty by the defendant (Royal Brunei Airlines v Tan). The trustee need not be dishonest, and the assistant need not receive any trust property. Kelly's commission came from Lou's own money, not trust property, so knowing receipt does not apply."},
 {"t":"strangers","q":"Mia, a solicitor, receives £300,000 into her firm's client account from a client who she knows is a trustee of a family trust. On his instructions she pays it straight on to an offshore company he owns. She strongly suspects he is taking trust money for himself but tells herself it is not her business. She keeps none of the money apart from her normal fee, which the client pays separately.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mia's position?","o":["She is liable for knowing receipt, because the trust money passed through her firm's client account on its way to the offshore company.","She is not a knowing recipient, because she received the money only as an agent, but she may be liable for dishonest assistance.","She has no liability, because she did not keep any of the money.","She is liable for knowing receipt only if her fee came out of the trust money.","She has no liability, because she acted on her client's instructions."],"a":1,"e":"Knowing receipt requires beneficial receipt: a solicitor receiving money into client account only to pay it on is a ministerial recipient, not a beneficial one. But she may be a dishonest assistant: by the standards of ordinary decent people, deliberately turning a blind eye to strong suspicion of misappropriation is dishonest (Barlow Clowes; Ivey). Acting on instructions is no defence."},
 {"t":"strangers","q":"Nico received a gift of £50,000 from his uncle, a trustee, with no reason to suspect anything. He spent £20,000 of it on a car. He then learned that the money had been taken from a trust in breach of trust, but spent the remaining £30,000 on a holiday anyway. The uncle is bankrupt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Nico's liability for knowing receipt?","o":["He is liable for the full £50,000, because he received trust property and the trustee who gave it to him is bankrupt.","He is not liable at all, because he had no knowledge when he received the money.","He is liable for £50,000, because ignorance of the breach is no defence to knowing receipt.","He is liable only for £20,000, the value of the car.","He is liable for the £30,000 he spent after he learned of the breach, because his conscience was then affected."],"a":4,"e":"An innocent volunteer is not personally liable for knowing receipt for property dealt with before he knew of the breach. But once he learns the truth while still holding the property, it becomes unconscionable for him to treat it as his own, so he is liable for the £30,000 he spent afterwards (BCCI v Akindele test). The beneficiaries may also have a proprietary claim to the car, which still exists."},
 {"t":"strangers","q":"The directors of Petrov Ltd, in breach of their fiduciary duties, caused the company to pay Oscar a large return under an investment agreement that looked like a normal commercial deal. Oscar had no knowledge of the directors' wrongdoing, and nothing about the transaction was unusual in his line of business. The company's liquidator sues him for knowing receipt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Oscar's position?","o":["He is liable, because he received the money for his own benefit and it was traceable to a breach of fiduciary duty.","He is liable, because he should have investigated the source of such a large payment.","He is not liable, because his knowledge was not such as to make it unconscionable for him to keep the benefit.","He is not liable, because knowing receipt requires the recipient to have acted dishonestly.","He is liable only for the amount of his return above a normal commercial rate."],"a":2,"e":"The test for knowing receipt is whether the recipient's state of knowledge made it unconscionable for him to retain the benefit (BCCI v Akindele). Dishonesty is not required, but receipt alone is not enough either. In commercial transactions courts are reluctant to expect recipients to investigate, and Oscar knew nothing suspicious."},
 {"t":"strangers","q":"Pip, a bank employee, helped a trustee move £500,000 of trust money into a series of offshore accounts, knowing that the trustee was taking it for himself. The trustee paid Pip £5,000 for his help. The money has disappeared and the trustee cannot be found.\n\nWhat is the extent of Pip's liability?","o":["He is jointly and severally liable with the trustee for the whole £500,000 loss.","He is liable only for the £5,000 he received.","He is liable only if the trustee cannot pay.","He is liable for half the loss, sharing it equally with the trustee who took the money.","He has no liability, because he never received the trust money."],"a":0,"e":"A dishonest assistant is jointly and severally liable with the trustee for the loss caused by the breach he assisted, so the beneficiaries can recover the whole £500,000 from Pip. No receipt of trust property is needed. Alternatively they could claim an account of his £5,000 profit, but not both."},
 {"t":"strangers","q":"In 2017 Rory, an accountant who was never a trustee, dishonestly helped a trustee divert trust money. The beneficiaries discovered everything in 2018, and nothing was concealed from them after that. They first bring a claim against Rory for dishonest assistance in 2026.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the claim is in time?","o":["It is time-barred, because the six-year period applies to a dishonest assistant who was never a trustee.","It is not time-barred, because there is no limitation period for any claim involving fraud by a trustee.","It is not time-barred, because time runs only from when the trust property is recovered.","It is time-barred only if the trustee's own liability is also time-barred.","It is not time-barred, because the assistant is treated as a trustee for all purposes."],"a":0,"e":"The 'no limitation period' rule for fraudulent breaches in Limitation Act 1980 s.21(1) applies only to true trustees. A dishonest assistant or knowing recipient who was never a trustee has the normal six-year period (Williams v Central Bank of Nigeria). Time could be postponed for concealment under s.32, but the beneficiaries knew everything by 2018, so even on that basis time expired in 2024 and the 2026 claim is out of time."},
 {"t":"strangers","q":"After a family trust's only trustee lost mental capacity, Sven, the settlor's nephew, who was never appointed as a trustee, took control of the trust's bank account and investment portfolio and began managing them as if he were the trustee. His decisions caused heavy losses.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sven's position?","o":["He has no liability, because he was never appointed as a trustee.","He is liable only if he acted dishonestly.","He is treated as a trustee, with the same duties and liabilities, because he took it upon himself to act.","He is liable only as a knowing recipient.","He is liable only to the extent that he received fees."],"a":2,"e":"A person who is not appointed but takes it upon himself to act as trustee by intermeddling with trust property is a trustee de son tort (Mara v Browne). He is treated as a real trustee, with the same duties (including the duty of care) and liabilities. Dishonesty is not required."},
 {"t":"strangers","q":"A trustee, in breach of trust, gave Sophie £40,000 of trust money as a birthday present. Sophie had no reason to know the money came from the trust. She has put it in a separate savings account and has not touched it. The trustee is bankrupt.\n\nWhat claim do the beneficiaries have against Sophie?","o":["No claim, because Sophie did not know of the breach of trust.","A personal claim for knowing receipt of £40,000.","A claim for dishonest assistance.","A claim only if Sophie gave value for the money.","A proprietary claim to the £40,000, because she is an innocent volunteer still holding the traceable trust money."],"a":4,"e":"An innocent volunteer is not personally liable for knowing receipt, because she had no knowledge making it unconscionable to receive the money. But the beneficiaries' equitable interest continues in the money she still holds, so they can trace and make a proprietary claim to it. Only a bona fide purchaser for value takes free; a change of position defence would apply only if she had changed her position in good faith."},
 {"t":"strangers","q":"Tim, a conveyancer, helps a client move money out of a family trust. Tim knows the client is a trustee and that the money is being taken for the client's own use. Tim genuinely believes that, as a matter of his own moral code, doing what clients ask is never dishonest.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will decide whether Tim was dishonest?","o":["He is not dishonest, because he genuinely believed he was doing nothing wrong.","He is dishonest only if he also realised that ordinary decent people would regard his conduct as dishonest.","He is not dishonest, because he was acting on his client's instructions.","Given what he knew, the question is whether ordinary decent people would regard his conduct as dishonest.","He is dishonest because he was negligent in failing to make inquiries."],"a":3,"e":"Under Ivey v Genting Casinos, the court first finds the defendant's actual knowledge or belief about the facts, then asks whether, given that, his conduct was dishonest by the objective standards of ordinary decent people. There is no further requirement that he realised his conduct was dishonest by those standards (rejecting the Twinsectra combined test). Negligence alone is not dishonesty."},
 {"t":"strangers","q":"Uma, a trustee, in breach of trust sold a valuable trust sculpture to Victor, who paid full value in good faith with no notice of the trust. Victor later gave the sculpture to his daughter Wren, who by then had learned all about Uma's breach. The beneficiaries sue Wren for knowing receipt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Wren's position?","o":["Wren is liable, because she knew of the breach of trust when she received the sculpture.","Wren is not liable, because the beneficiaries' interest ended when Victor bought in good faith without notice.","Wren is liable, because she gave no value for the sculpture.","Wren is liable for dishonest assistance instead of knowing receipt.","Wren is not liable, because only the person who first receives trust property can ever be liable as a knowing recipient."],"a":1,"e":"Knowing receipt requires the claimant to still have an equitable proprietary interest in the property when the defendant receives it (El Ajou; Byers v Saudi National Bank). A bona fide purchaser for value without notice takes free of the trust, so the beneficiaries' interest ended when Victor bought, and Wren received property no longer subject to it. The beneficiaries can instead trace into the price Uma received."},

 {"t":"remedies-tracing","q":"Zeb, a trustee, paid £20,000 of trust money into a new, empty personal bank account. A week later he paid in £10,000 of his own salary. He then withdrew £10,000 and spent it on a holiday, leaving £20,000. Zeb is now bankrupt.\n\nHow much of the account can the beneficiaries claim?","o":["£13,333, a two-thirds proportionate share of the remaining balance.","£10,000, because the first money paid in is treated as the first money paid out.","£20,000, because the trustee is presumed to have spent his own money first.","Nothing from the account, only a personal claim against Zeb.","£20,000 only if Zeb intended to preserve the trust money."],"a":2,"e":"Where a trustee mixes trust money with his own, he is presumed to spend his own money first (Re Hallett's Estate). So the £10,000 spent on the holiday was Zeb's own, and the £20,000 left is trust money. The first in, first out rule (Clayton's Case) applies only between innocent parties and is never used to help a wrongdoing trustee."},
 {"t":"remedies-tracing","q":"Amos, a trustee, used £40,000 of trust money and £60,000 of his own money to buy a painting for £100,000. The painting is now worth £300,000. Amos is bankrupt.\n\nWhat is the beneficiaries' best claim?","o":["A 40% share of the painting, now worth £120,000, or a lien for £40,000, whichever they choose.","A lien over the painting for £40,000 only, because Amos contributed most of the purchase price himself.","The whole painting, because Amos is a wrongdoer.","A personal claim for £40,000 as an unsecured creditor.","A 40% share of the painting only if Amos intended to buy it for the trust."],"a":0,"e":"Where trust money and the trustee's own money are used together to buy an asset, the beneficiaries can choose either a proportionate share of the asset, including any increase in value, or a lien to secure repayment of the trust money (Foskett v McKeown). A 40% share, worth £120,000, is plainly better than a £40,000 lien, and as a proprietary claim it takes priority over Amos's creditors."},
 {"t":"remedies-tracing","q":"Bryn, a trustee, paid £15,000 of trust money into his personal account, which was £20,000 overdrawn at the time. A month later he paid in £30,000 of his own salary, leaving a credit balance of £25,000. Bryn is now bankrupt.\n\nWhat proprietary claim do the beneficiaries have against the account?","o":["£15,000, because Bryn later paid in enough to replace it.","£15,000, because a trustee who mixes funds is presumed to spend his own money before any trust money.","A proportionate share of the £25,000 balance.","Nothing, because the trust money was used to reduce the overdraft and has been dissipated.","£15,000, because the bank had notice of the trust."],"a":3,"e":"Paying trust money into an overdrawn account uses it to discharge a debt, so it is dissipated and cannot be traced (Bishopsgate Investment Management v Homan). Later deposits of the trustee's own money are not treated as repaying the trust unless he clearly intended this, and the lowest intermediate balance after the trust money went in was nil. The beneficiaries have only a personal claim."},
 {"t":"remedies-tracing","q":"Cass is the trustee of two separate family trusts. She paid £30,000 from Trust A into a current account, and later £20,000 from Trust B. She then dishonestly withdrew £25,000 and spent it on herself, leaving £25,000. Cass is bankrupt, and both trusts claim the balance.\n\nHow is the remaining £25,000 likely to be shared between the two trusts?","o":["£5,000 to Trust A and £20,000 to Trust B, because the first money paid in is treated as the first paid out.","£25,000 to Trust A and nothing to Trust B, because Trust A's money was paid in first.","£12,500 to each trust equally.","Neither trust can claim from the account, because the money was mixed.","In proportion to their contributions, £15,000 to Trust A and £10,000 to Trust B."],"a":4,"e":"As between two innocent beneficiaries, the traditional first in, first out rule (Clayton's Case) would leave Trust A with only £5,000. The courts now usually displace it as arbitrary and unfair, sharing the balance pari passu (in proportion to contributions) (Barlow Clowes v Vaughan). Trust A contributed 60% and Trust B 40%, giving £15,000 and £10,000."},
 {"t":"remedies-tracing","q":"Dev, a trustee, sold shares belonging to the trust to Erin for £50,000, in breach of trust. Erin paid full value in good faith and had no notice of the trust. Dev paid the £50,000 into a new account that holds nothing else. Dev is now bankrupt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the beneficiaries' position?","o":["They can recover the shares from Erin, because the sale was in breach of trust.","They cannot recover the shares from Erin, but they can trace into the £50,000 sale proceeds in Dev's account.","They can recover the shares only if they repay Erin's £50,000.","They have only a personal claim against Dev, as unsecured creditors.","They can recover the shares, because a buyer of shares must always check that the seller is entitled to sell them."],"a":1,"e":"A bona fide purchaser for value without notice takes free of the trust, so Erin keeps the shares. But tracing follows value: the £50,000 Dev received is the substitute for the trust shares, so the beneficiaries can claim it from his account in priority to his creditors. Their proprietary claim is not limited to a personal claim."},
 {"t":"remedies-tracing","q":"Executors mistakenly paid £20,000 of estate money to Faye, who was not entitled to it. Faye, acting in good faith and believing the money was hers, gave £15,000 to a charity she would not otherwise have supported. She still has £5,000. The persons entitled to the estate claim the money from her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Faye's position?","o":["She must repay £20,000, because she received money that was not hers.","She has a defence to the extent she changed her position in good faith, so she will probably repay only about £5,000.","She need repay nothing, because she received the money innocently.","She must repay the full £20,000 unless she can show that the charity received the £15,000 in good faith and still has it.","She must repay £15,000, the amount she gave away."],"a":1,"e":"Change of position is a defence for an innocent recipient who, in good faith, has changed her position because of the receipt so that it would be inequitable to require full repayment (Lipkin Gorman v Karpnale). Faye made a gift she would not otherwise have made, so the defence covers the £15,000. She must still return the £5,000 she has left."},
 {"t":"remedies-tracing","q":"Gail contracted to buy 30% of the shares in Hart Ltd, a private family company, from Ivo for their market value. The shares are not available from anyone else, and Gail wants them in order to gain influence over the company. Ivo now refuses to complete.\n\nWhich remedy is Gail most likely to obtain?","o":["Damages only, because specific performance is never available for contracts to sell shares.","Specific performance as of right, because the contract is binding.","An interim injunction requiring Ivo to sell the shares.","Damages only, because equitable remedies are available only in trust cases.","Specific performance may be ordered, because these shares are not available elsewhere and damages may be inadequate."],"a":4,"e":"Specific performance is discretionary and is granted where damages are inadequate. Shares in a private company cannot be bought on the market, so damages would not give Gail what she bargained for, making specific performance likely. It is never available 'as of right', and equitable remedies are not confined to trust cases."},
 {"t":"remedies-tracing","q":"Zak agreed to buy a field from Abe. Abe failed to complete on the agreed date. Zak did nothing for four years, during which Abe, believing Zak had lost interest, spent £100,000 building stables on the field. Zak now seeks specific performance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's position?","o":["It may refuse specific performance, because Zak's unreasonable delay has prejudiced Abe.","It must order specific performance, because contracts for land are always specifically enforceable.","It must order specific performance, because the six-year limitation period has not expired.","It may refuse only if Zak acted dishonestly.","It must award damages, because equitable remedies are never available after a delay of more than three years."],"a":0,"e":"Equitable remedies are discretionary and can be refused for laches: unreasonable delay that makes it unjust to grant relief, particularly where the defendant has changed his position. Abe spent £100,000 believing Zak had lost interest. A limitation period not having expired does not prevent the court refusing equitable relief, and there is no fixed time bar."},
 {"t":"remedies-tracing","q":"The beneficiaries of a family trust have strong evidence that their trustee has taken £2 million of trust money. They learn that he is about to transfer his remaining assets overseas before they can bring their claim to trial.\n\nWhich order should they seek urgently?","o":["A search order, to allow them to enter his home and look for documents and other evidence of the theft.","Specific performance of the trust deed.","Rectification of the trust deed.","A freezing injunction, to stop the trustee disposing of or moving his assets before judgment.","Rescission of the trust."],"a":3,"e":"A freezing injunction prevents a defendant from dissipating or moving his assets out of reach before judgment, which is exactly the risk here. A search order is for preserving evidence, not assets. Specific performance, rectification and rescission do not address the threatened removal of assets."},
 {"t":"remedies-tracing","q":"Kit, a trustee, used £100,000 of trust money, and nothing else, to buy shares that are now worth only £60,000. Kit is bankrupt but has some other assets that will go to his general creditors.\n\nWhat is the beneficiaries' best claim?","o":["A claim to the shares only, losing the remaining £40,000.","A personal claim for £100,000 only, as unsecured creditors.","A charge over the shares to secure the £100,000, together with a personal claim for the £40,000 shortfall.","A claim to the shares, plus a proprietary claim against Kit's other assets for the £40,000 shortfall in value.","Nothing, because the trust money was dissipated in shares that fell in value."],"a":2,"e":"Where an asset is bought wholly with trust money, the beneficiaries can take the asset itself or a charge over it securing the trust money. Where the asset has fallen in value, the charge is better: they take the £60,000 in priority and prove as unsecured creditors for the £40,000 shortfall. They have no proprietary claim to Kit's other, untraced assets."}
];

;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["land"] = [
 {"t":"nature-of-land","q":"Amira holds a 15-year lease of a shop, which ends next month. During the lease she bolted shelving units and a refrigerated display counter to the walls and floor for her grocery business. The lease says nothing about fixtures. The landlord says everything attached to the building now belongs to him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Amira's position?","o":["She may remove the shelving and counter before the lease ends, making good any damage, because they are tenant's trade fixtures.","She may not remove them, because anything bolted to the building becomes part of the land and belongs to the landlord.","She may remove them at any time within six months after the lease ends, because they were bought with her own money.","She may remove them only if the landlord consents, because the lease does not expressly allow removal.","She may not remove them, but the landlord must pay her their value when the lease ends."],"a":0,"e":"Fixtures normally become part of the land, but a tenant may remove **tenant's trade fixtures** (items fixed for the purposes of the tenant's business), and ornamental and domestic fixtures, provided she does so **before the lease ends** and makes good any damage. No landlord consent or express term is needed. There is no general right to compensation, and the right is lost if she leaves them behind after the lease ends."},
 {"t":"nature-of-land","q":"Bernard owns a field on which he built a timber bungalow. The bungalow rests on concrete pillars by its own weight and is not bolted down, but it was built in place and could only be removed by demolishing it. Bernard has contracted to sell the field to Chloe. The contract does not mention the bungalow.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the bungalow passes to Chloe?","o":["It does not pass to Chloe, because it is not mentioned in the contract and so remains Bernard's property.","It passes to Chloe only if she can show that Bernard intended it to be a fixture when he built it.","It passes to Chloe, because a structure that cannot be removed without being destroyed has become part of the land.","It passes to Chloe only if it is fixed to the pillars by bolts or mortar before completion.","It does not pass to Chloe, because an item resting by its own weight is presumed to be a chattel."],"a":2,"e":"Land includes **buildings and structures** forming part of it. A house that can only be removed by **demolition** is part of the land itself, not a chattel (Elitestone v Morris), whatever the method of attachment. The presumption that an item resting by its own weight is a chattel is easily rebutted here, and the absence of any mention in the contract does not matter because the land passes with everything that forms part of it."},
 {"t":"nature-of-land","q":"Dafydd owns the freehold of an office building. A developer next door uses a tower crane whose arm regularly swings through the airspace about 30 metres above Dafydd's roof. The crane causes no damage and does not interfere with Dafydd's use of the building. Dafydd has never given permission.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Dafydd's position?","o":["The crane is a nuisance but not a trespass, because airspace is not part of the land.","There is no trespass, because airspace belongs to the Crown above the height of the roof.","There is no trespass, because a landowner owns no airspace above the height of the buildings on the land.","There is no trespass, because the crane causes no damage and so Dafydd has suffered no loss.","It is a trespass, because land includes the airspace needed for its ordinary use and enjoyment."],"a":4,"e":"**Land** includes the **airspace** above it to such height as is necessary for the ordinary use and enjoyment of the land and structures on it. A crane arm oversailing at low height invades that airspace and is a **trespass**, which is actionable without proof of damage, so an injunction may be granted. High-level overflight (such as aircraft) is different, but a crane over a building is within the owner's airspace."},
 {"t":"nature-of-land","q":"Elin's will left her farm 'to my husband Fergus for the rest of his life, and then to my daughter Gwen'. Elin has died and her executors are administering the estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the nature of Fergus's interest?","o":["It is a legal fee simple, because Fergus is entitled to possession immediately.","It is an equitable life interest under a trust of land, not a legal estate.","It is a legal estate, because it gives Fergus the right to possession of the farm for his lifetime.","It is a legal lease for life, which takes effect as a 90-year lease ending on Fergus's death.","It is a mere licence to occupy, which ends if Fergus sells his interest."],"a":1,"e":"Only two **legal estates** can exist: the **fee simple absolute in possession** (freehold) and the **term of years absolute** (lease) (LPA 1925 s.1(1)). A **life interest** is not a fee simple ('absolute' excludes it) and is not a term of years, so it can exist only in **equity**, behind a **trust of land**. The 90-year conversion applies to leases for life granted at a rent or premium, not to gifts by will."},
 {"t":"nature-of-land","q":"Huw owns the freehold of a house, which he let to a tenant for 20 years at a market rent five years ago. He lives elsewhere and collects the rent. His friend says that because the tenant is in possession, Huw no longer owns a legal estate in the house.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Huw's interest?","o":["He holds only an equitable interest in the house until the lease ends and he regains physical possession.","He holds a legal fee simple in remainder, which becomes 'in possession' when the lease ends.","He holds no estate in the house while the lease lasts, only a right to the rent.","He still holds a legal fee simple in possession, which includes receiving the rent.","He holds a legal term of years, because the freehold has been converted into a reversionary lease."],"a":3,"e":"For the fee simple to be **in possession**, the owner need not physically occupy the land: under LPA 1925 s.205, **possession includes the receipt of rents and profits** or the right to receive them. So a freeholder who has let the land still holds a **legal estate**, subject to the tenant's **legal lease**. Both legal estates exist at the same time in the same land."},
 {"t":"nature-of-land","q":"Isla owns a registered freehold. Over the years she has entered into several arrangements with her neighbour, Jamal, each made by deed.\n\nWhich of the following rights, if properly registered, is capable of existing as a legal interest?","o":["A right for Jamal to use Isla's drive until Jamal marries.","A right for Jamal to buy Isla's house at a fixed price within 10 years.","A right for Jamal to cross Isla's garden for the rest of Jamal's life.","A right of way for Jamal over Isla's drive for a period of 25 years.","A promise by Isla not to build anything on her back garden."],"a":3,"e":"An **easement** can be **legal** only if it is held for a period equivalent to a fee simple absolute in possession or a **term of years absolute** (LPA 1925 s.1(2)(a)), so a 25-year right of way granted by deed (and registered) can be legal. A right for life or until marriage is not for either period, so it is equitable. A **restrictive covenant** and an **option** are not in the list of legal interests and are always **equitable**, whether or not a deed is used."},
 {"t":"nature-of-land","q":"Kieran is buying a house from Lara. On his final visit he sees that Lara has taken down a heavy mirror that was screwed to the wall above the fireplace, and has dug up an established rose bed in the garden, planning to take both with her. The contract and the fittings and contents form are silent about both.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The mirror is a fixture and must stay, but the roses are Lara's personal property and she may take them.","The established roses form part of the land and should stay, but the mirror is likely to be a chattel that Lara may take.","Lara may take both, because any item not listed in the contract remains the seller's property.","Both the mirror and the roses are chattels, because each can be removed without damaging the building.","Both the mirror and the roses belong to Kieran, because anything attached to the land passes on a sale."],"a":1,"e":"Plants **growing in the soil** are part of the land and pass with it unless excluded. A mirror screwed to the wall merely so that it can be **enjoyed as an object** (rather than to improve the land permanently) is likely to remain a **chattel**, because the **purpose of annexation** carries most weight. Whether an item is listed in the contract matters only if the contract deals with it; silence leaves the general rules to decide."},
 {"t":"nature-of-land","q":"Mared is digging foundations for an extension on the registered freehold she owns. Her builder, Nico, unearths an old brooch buried about a metre below the surface. It is not treasure. Nico claims it as the finder.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to the brooch?","o":["Mared and Nico equally, because both contributed to its discovery.","Nico, because a finder of lost property has a better claim than anyone except the true owner.","Nico, because Mared did not know the brooch was there and so had no intention to control it.","The Crown, because all objects buried in land belong to the Crown unless the owner is traced.","Mared, because objects found in or attached to land belong to the landowner rather than the finder."],"a":4,"e":"Ownership of land extends **below the surface**, and objects found **in or attached to the land** belong to the landowner, even if she did not know they were there, rather than to the finder (Waverley BC v Fletcher). The finder's rule applies to items lying loose on the surface in some cases, not to items buried in the soil. Only **treasure** under the Treasure Act 1996 vests in the Crown."},
 {"t":"nature-of-land","q":"Owen agreed in writing with his neighbour Priya that she may park her car on his shared driveway whenever she needs to. The arrangement was not made by deed. The document was signed by both, and Priya pays Owen £40 a month. Owen now wishes to sell his house.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the nature of Priya's right?","o":["It is at most an equitable interest, because no deed was used, so it cannot be a legal interest.","It is a legal easement, because it was made in signed writing and Priya pays for it.","It is a legal lease, because Priya pays a monthly sum for the use of the driveway.","It is a legal interest, because it is of the kind listed in LPA 1925 s.1(2).","It is a legal profit, because it allows Priya to take a benefit from Owen's land."],"a":0,"e":"Even a right **capable** of being legal (such as an easement) is legal only if created by **deed** (LPA 1925 s.52) and, for registered land, completed by **registration**. Without a deed, the best Priya can have is an **equitable** right (if the signed writing is a valid contract or grant in equity), or merely a **contractual licence**. A right to park on a shared drive gives no **exclusive possession**, so it is not a lease, and payment does not make it a legal easement."},
 {"t":"nature-of-land","q":"Rhodri owns a house. He bought it with a mortgage from Ash Bank, created by a deed charging the house by way of legal mortgage, which has been registered. Rhodri asks you what kind of interest the bank holds.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the bank's interest?","o":["A legal estate, because the bank holds a long lease of the house until the loan is repaid.","A contractual right only, because the bank's rights arise from the loan agreement.","A legal interest, because a charge by way of legal mortgage can exist at law.","A legal fee simple held on trust for Rhodri until the loan is repaid.","An equitable interest, because a mortgage is only security for a debt and gives the bank no estate."],"a":2,"e":"A **charge by way of legal mortgage** is one of the interests capable of existing at law (LPA 1925 s.1(2)(c)); made by **deed** and **registered**, it is a **legal interest** in Rhodri's land. Rhodri keeps the legal fee simple. Since the LRA 2002, a registered mortgage cannot be made by granting the lender a long lease, and a mortgage does not transfer the fee simple to the lender."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Sian owns a small flat. She orally agreed with Tomos that he could rent it for two years at the full market rent, starting immediately. No premium was paid and nothing was put in writing. Tomos moved in and pays the rent monthly. Sian now says Tomos has no legal lease because nothing was written down.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Tomos's position?","o":["He holds a legal lease only if he registers it at HM Land Registry.","He holds no interest, because an oral agreement to grant a lease is void.","He holds a legal lease, because no formalities are needed for this short lease.","He holds only an equitable lease, because a legal lease must always be made by deed.","He holds only a licence, because a lease of land must be in writing signed by both parties."],"a":2,"e":"A legal lease normally requires a **deed**, but LPA 1925 **s.54(2)** creates an exception for a lease that (1) takes effect **in possession**, (2) is for **three years or less**, and (3) is at the **best rent reasonably obtainable without taking a fine** (premium). Such a lease can be created **orally**. Leases of seven years or less are not registrable, so no registration is needed."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Ursula agreed to let her shop to Vikram for two years at a market rent, in return for Vikram also paying her a premium of £15,000 at the start. The lease was put in writing and signed by both, but it was not executed as a deed. Vikram has paid the premium and moved in.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Vikram's interest?","o":["He holds a legal lease, because a premium is treated as advance rent and so the best rent was obtained.","He holds a legal lease, because the lease is for three years or less and was made in writing.","He holds a legal lease, because he has paid the premium and taken possession.","He holds only a licence, because no deed was executed and a lease without a deed is void.","He does not hold a legal lease, because the premium means a deed was needed."],"a":4,"e":"The s.54(2) exception covers only short leases at the **best rent reasonably obtainable without taking a fine**. A **premium** (a 'fine') takes the lease outside the exception, so a **deed** was required for a legal lease. Because the signed written agreement satisfies LP(MP)A 1989 s.2, Vikram is likely to have an **equitable lease** under the Walsh v Lonsdale principle, not a mere licence."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Wyn is selling his house to Xavier. The solicitors prepared two identical copies of the contract containing all the agreed terms. Wyn signed one copy and Xavier signed the other. The solicitors then exchanged the copies by post. Xavier now wants to withdraw, arguing that no single document was signed by both parties.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is a binding contract, because exchange of identical copies, each signed by one party, satisfies the statutory formalities.","There is no contract, because the law requires one document signed by both the seller and the buyer.","There is no contract until completion, because a contract for the sale of land takes effect only when the transfer is signed.","There is a binding contract only if the copies were also witnessed, because a land contract must be a deed.","There is a binding contract only if Xavier has paid a deposit, because payment is part of the formalities."],"a":0,"e":"LP(MP)A 1989 **s.2** requires a contract for the sale of land to be **in writing**, to **incorporate all the expressly agreed terms**, and to be **signed by or on behalf of each party**. Where contracts are **exchanged**, the signatures may be on **separate identical copies**, one signed by each party (s.2(3)). No deed, witness or deposit is required for the contract itself."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"In 2024 Yasmin granted Zac an option to buy her field for £90,000 within five years. The option agreement was in writing, contained all the terms and was signed by both of them. Last month Zac sent Yasmin a letter, signed only by him, stating that he exercises the option. Yasmin refuses to sell, arguing that the letter was not signed by her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The option has not been exercised, because the letter needed to be executed as a deed.","Zac has validly exercised the option only if Yasmin replies accepting the exercise in writing.","The option is void, because an option is not a contract for the sale of land.","Zac has validly exercised the option, because only the grant of the option needs to satisfy the s.2 formalities.","The option has not been exercised, because the exercise creates a contract that must be signed by both parties."],"a":3,"e":"An **option** is a contract for the sale of land, so its **grant** must comply with LP(MP)A 1989 s.2, as this one did. Its **exercise** is a unilateral act by the grantee, so the notice of exercise need only comply with the option's own terms and does **not** require the grantor's signature (Spiro v Glencrown Properties). No deed and no acceptance by Yasmin are needed."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Aled bought a plot of building land at a public auction. When the hammer fell, the auctioneer recorded the sale in his book, but Aled and the seller did not sign a written contract. The seller now refuses to complete, arguing that there is no written contract signed by both parties.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is a binding contract only if Aled can rely on proprietary estoppel.","There is a binding contract, because auction sales are exempt from s.2.","There is a binding contract only if the sale price was paid in full on the day of the auction.","There is no contract, because every contract for the sale of land must be in writing signed by both parties.","There is no contract, because the auctioneer's record is not signed by Aled."],"a":1,"e":"LP(MP)A 1989 s.2(5) lists exceptions to the writing requirement, including contracts **made in the course of a public auction**. The contract is formed when the hammer falls, so it binds both parties even though it is not in signed writing. (In practice auction contracts are signed on the day, but the statute does not require it.)"},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"For 20 years Bryn worked full time on his mother Carys's farm for very low wages. Carys repeatedly told him, 'One day this farm will all be yours,' and Bryn turned down well-paid jobs elsewhere because of it. Carys has now died, leaving the farm by will to a charity. Nothing about the farm was ever put in writing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Bryn's claim?","o":["He may claim only unpaid wages, because the remedy for estoppel is limited to compensation for work done.","He may succeed in proprietary estoppel, with the court deciding the appropriate remedy.","He will fail, because a promise about land is unenforceable unless made in a signed written contract.","He will fail, because a promise to leave property by will can always be changed and so cannot be relied on.","He will succeed automatically to the whole farm, because estoppel always gives the claimant exactly what was promised."],"a":1,"e":"**Proprietary estoppel** arises where the owner gives an **assurance** that the claimant has or will acquire an interest in land, the claimant **relies** on it, and suffers **detriment**, so that it would be unconscionable to go back on it. A promise about inheritance can found an estoppel where it was intended to be relied on. The court has a **discretion** to award the **minimum needed to satisfy the equity**, which may be the farm, a share or money; it is not automatically the full promise."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Dana bought a house in her sole name ten years ago, using her savings for the deposit and a mortgage in her name. Her partner, Ellis, moved in a year later. Dana told Ellis, 'This is our home, and it's as much yours as mine,' and Ellis then paid half of every mortgage instalment and funded a new kitchen. They have now separated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Ellis can establish a beneficial interest?","o":["He cannot claim any interest, because the legal title is in Dana's sole name and there is no signed declaration of trust.","He automatically has a half share, because cohabitants who share a home are treated as joint owners.","He can claim a share only under a resulting trust, limited to what he contributed to the original purchase price.","He must prove a common intention that he would share beneficially, and detrimental reliance on it.","He can claim a share only if he and Dana were married or in a civil partnership."],"a":3,"e":"Where the home is in **one name**, the non-owner must prove a **common intention constructive trust**: an express or inferred **common intention** to share beneficially, and **detrimental reliance** on it. Constructive trusts are exempt from the writing requirement (LPA 1925 s.53(2)). Dana's express statement and Ellis's mortgage payments are strong evidence, and the size of his share is then decided on the **whole course of dealing**. There is no automatic share for cohabitants."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Farah and Gethin, an unmarried couple, bought a house in their joint names as their family home, with no declaration of how the beneficial interest was held. Farah paid 80% of the price and Gethin 20%. They shared household bills and raised two children there. They have now separated, and Farah claims 80% of the equity.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely starting point?","o":["They are presumed to own the beneficial interest equally, and Farah must prove a different common intention to depart from that.","Farah owns the whole beneficial interest, because she paid the larger share of the price.","The court will divide the beneficial interest in whatever shares it considers fair, without regard to their intentions.","They hold as tenants in common in equal shares, because unequal contributions sever any joint tenancy.","They own the beneficial interest 80:20 under a resulting trust, because shares follow the contributions to the price."],"a":0,"e":"In a **domestic** case where the home is in **joint names** with no express declaration, **equity follows the law**: the starting point is **joint beneficial ownership** (equal shares) (Stack v Dowden). The party claiming unequal shares must show a different **common intention**, judged on the whole course of dealing. The resulting trust presumption based on contributions does not apply to family homes, and the court cannot simply impose what it thinks fair."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Huw is the sole beneficiary under a trust of a block of flats held by trustees. In a phone call, Huw told his sister Iola that he was giving her his entire beneficial interest. Nothing was written down. Huw has since died, and Iola claims the interest from his estate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Iola's claim fails, because a transfer of an equitable interest must be made by deed.","Iola's claim succeeds, because the formality rules do not apply to gifts between family members.","Iola's claim succeeds, because a transfer of an equitable interest can be made orally if the trustees are told.","Iola's claim succeeds, because only declarations of trust of land, not transfers, require writing.","Iola's claim fails, because a disposition of an equitable interest must be in signed writing."],"a":4,"e":"Under LPA 1925 **s.53(1)(c)**, a **disposition of a subsisting equitable interest** must be **in writing signed** by the person disposing of it (or their authorised agent); otherwise it is **void**. An oral assignment, even with notice to the trustees, is ineffective. A deed is not required, but signed writing is."},
 {"t":"formalities","q":"Jac signed a document transferring his registered freehold to his niece Kira as a gift. The document was signed by Jac in the presence of a witness, who also signed it, and it was handed to Kira. However, nothing in the document stated or showed that it was intended to be a deed; it was headed 'Letter of Gift'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the document is a valid deed?","o":["It is a valid deed only if Kira also signed it in the presence of the witness.","It is a valid deed, because it was signed, witnessed and delivered.","It is not a valid deed, because a deed must make clear on its face that it is intended to be a deed.","It is a valid deed, because a transfer of land is always treated as a deed if it is signed by the owner.","It is not a valid deed, because a deed must be sealed by the person executing it."],"a":2,"e":"Under LP(MP)A 1989 **s.1**, an instrument is a **deed** only if (1) it makes **clear on its face** that it is intended to be a deed (for example by describing itself as a deed or being expressed to be executed as one), and (2) it is validly **executed**: for an individual, **signed in the presence of a witness who attests** the signature, and **delivered**. Seals are no longer needed, and only the person transferring need sign."},
 {"t":"registered-land","q":"In June 2026 Lewis gave his unregistered freehold cottage to his son Mabon by a deed of gift. Mabon's solicitor forgot to apply for first registration, and it is now September 2026. No application has been made.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Mabon holds the legal title, because registration is voluntary for a gift of land.","Mabon holds the legal title, because the obligation to register applies only to transfers for value.","The transfer is void entirely, so Lewis owns the cottage both legally and beneficially.","Mabon holds the legal title and will be fined, but the transfer itself is unaffected.","The legal title has reverted to Lewis, who holds it on a bare trust for Mabon."],"a":4,"e":"A **gift** of an unregistered freehold is a trigger for **compulsory first registration** (LRA 2002 s.4). The application must be made within **two months**. If it is not, the transfer becomes **void as regards the legal estate**, which reverts to the transferor, who holds it on a **bare trust** for the transferee (s.7). Mabon keeps the beneficial interest and can still register (late registration is possible with the Registrar's consent, or after a fresh transfer)."},
 {"t":"registered-land","q":"Nia owns a registered freehold shop. Three years ago she granted Osian a legal lease of the shop for five years by deed. Osian has never registered or noted the lease, and has sublet the shop to a tenant who trades there. Nia has now sold the freehold to Padrig, who did not know about the lease.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Padrig is bound by Osian's lease?","o":["He is bound, because a legal lease of seven years or less overrides a registered disposition.","He is bound only because Osian's subtenant was in actual occupation of the shop.","He is not bound, because he did not know of the lease and could not discover it.","He is bound only if Osian paid a premium for the lease.","He is not bound, because the lease was not protected by a notice on the register."],"a":0,"e":"A **legal lease** granted for **seven years or less** is not a registrable disposition and is an **overriding interest** under LRA 2002 **Sch 3 para 1**. It binds a buyer of the registered estate whether or not it is on the register, whether or not the buyer knew of it, and whether or not the tenant is in occupation. Occupation by the subtenant is therefore irrelevant to this route."},
 {"t":"registered-land","q":"Rhiannon contributed a third of the price of a registered house bought in the sole name of her partner, Steffan, and has lived there with him since. Steffan sold the house to Tirion. Before exchange, Tirion asked Rhiannon directly whether she had any interest in the property, and she said she did not, as she did not want to cause trouble. Tirion has now been registered.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Rhiannon's interest binds Tirion?","o":["It does not bind Tirion, because a beneficial interest can only bind a buyer if protected by a notice.","It binds Tirion, because Rhiannon's occupation was obvious on a reasonably careful inspection.","It does not bind Tirion, because Rhiannon failed to disclose her interest when asked about it.","It binds Tirion, because Rhiannon was in actual occupation at the date of the disposition.","It binds Tirion, because a beneficial interest under a trust can never be defeated by a sale by one trustee."],"a":2,"e":"An interest of a person in **actual occupation** overrides under LRA 2002 **Sch 3 para 2**, but not if the buyer made **inquiry** of that person and she **failed to disclose** the interest when she could reasonably have been expected to do so (para 2(b)). Rhiannon's denial therefore prevents her interest overriding. Beneficial interests under a trust cannot be protected by a notice at all (they are protected by a restriction)."},
 {"t":"registered-land","q":"Ulrich and Vanya are married. Their home is registered in Ulrich's sole name and was bought entirely with his money; Vanya has no beneficial interest in it. Vanya lives there. Ulrich has just sold the house to Wanda, and nothing was entered on the register for Vanya.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Vanya can remain in the house against Wanda?","o":["She cannot, because home rights end automatically when the owning spouse sells the house.","She can, because she was in actual occupation at the time of the sale.","She can, because a buyer of a matrimonial home always takes subject to the non-owning spouse's rights.","She cannot, because home rights need a notice and cannot override.","She can, unless Wanda made inquiries of her before completion."],"a":3,"e":"A spouse's **home rights** under the Family Law Act 1996 are personal statutory rights, not a proprietary interest of the kind that can override. The Act provides that they **cannot be an overriding interest** even if the spouse is in **actual occupation**; they bind a buyer only if protected by a **notice** on the register. Vanya has no beneficial interest to protect, so Wanda takes free."},
 {"t":"registered-land","q":"Yestin owns a registered freehold. He wishes to grant his daughter a lease of a flat in the building for 99 years. You are asked about the steps needed for the daughter to obtain a legal lease.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what is required?","o":["A deed only, because registration is required only for leases of 21 years or more.","A deed and then registration, because the lease is for more than seven years.","Signed writing only, because a lease between family members does not need a deed.","A deed and a notice of the lease on Yestin's title, but no separate registration.","Nothing beyond a written contract, because the daughter's occupation will protect her."],"a":1,"e":"A legal lease of more than three years requires a **deed** (LPA 1925 s.52). In registered land, the grant of a lease of **more than seven years** is a **registrable disposition** (LRA 2002 s.27): it is legal only once **registered**, when it receives its own title and is noted on the landlord's title. Until registration, the daughter would hold only an equitable lease."},
 {"t":"registered-land","q":"Zara and her brother Aron bought a registered house as tenants in common, but only Aron is the registered proprietor. Zara is worried that Aron might sell or mortgage the house without her knowledge and wants to protect her beneficial share on the register.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the appropriate entry?","o":["A restriction, so that her interest is overreached on any sale or mortgage.","A unilateral notice, because Aron would not agree to an agreed notice.","A caution against dealings, which will require her consent to any sale.","No entry, because her beneficial interest is automatically shown in the proprietorship register.","An agreed notice, which will make her beneficial interest binding on any buyer or lender."],"a":0,"e":"An interest under a **trust of land** **cannot be protected by a notice** (LRA 2002 s.33). The correct protection is a **restriction** (commonly Form A), which prevents registration of a disposition by a sole proprietor unless the money is paid to **two trustees** (or a trust corporation), so that Zara's interest is **overreached** into the proceeds. Cautions against dealings can no longer be entered under the 2002 Act."},
 {"t":"registered-land","q":"In 2023 Bethan granted her neighbour Carwyn a written option, signed by both, to buy a strip of her registered land. Carwyn never protected the option on the register and does not occupy the strip. Bethan has now sold and transferred all her land to Dyfan for full value. Dyfan knew all about the option before completion. Dyfan has been registered.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the option binds Dyfan?","o":["It binds Dyfan, because an option is an overriding interest under the Land Registration Act 2002.","It does not bind Dyfan, because an option can never bind anyone other than the person who granted it.","It does not bind Dyfan, because it was unprotected and his knowledge is irrelevant.","It binds Dyfan, because he had actual knowledge of it before completion.","It binds Dyfan, because a buyer who knows of an interest is acting in bad faith if he ignores it."],"a":2,"e":"Under LRA 2002 **s.29**, a registered disposition **for valuable consideration** takes priority over any interest that is not **protected on the register** or **overriding**. An option is protectable by **notice**; it was not protected, and Carwyn was not in actual occupation. The buyer's **knowledge is irrelevant**: the register, not notice, decides priority. Carwyn's remedy is in contract against Bethan."},
 {"t":"registered-land","q":"Elis's house was first registered many years ago. Following a Land Registry mistake, the register shows a strip of garden belonging to his neighbour Fflur as part of Elis's title. Elis has fenced and gardened the strip ever since and did nothing to cause the mistake. Fflur has just discovered the error and applies to have the register corrected.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["The register will be rectified only if Fflur applies within 12 months of the mistake.","The register will always be rectified, because the registration was a mistake by the Land Registry.","The register will be rectified, but Elis will receive an indemnity for the loss of the strip.","The register will not be rectified, and Fflur has no remedy because the register is conclusive.","Rectification is unlikely because Elis is in possession, but Fflur may claim an indemnity."],"a":4,"e":"Correcting a mistake that prejudices the registered proprietor's title is **rectification** (LRA 2002 Sch 4). Where the proprietor is **in possession**, rectification is not made without his consent unless he **caused or substantially contributed** to the mistake by fraud or lack of care, or it would be **unjust** not to rectify. If rectification is refused, the person who suffers loss (Fflur) can claim an **indemnity** from the Land Registry (Sch 8)."},
 {"t":"registered-land","q":"Gareth has occupied a strip of land belonging to his neighbour Heledd, a registered proprietor, for 12 years, treating it as part of his garden. He knew it was Heledd's land. He applies to be registered as proprietor. Heledd receives notice of the application and serves a counter-notice requiring it to be dealt with under the special conditions.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["Gareth will be registered as proprietor, because he has been in adverse possession for more than 10 years.","The application will fail, because no special condition is met, and Heledd has two years to evict him.","Gareth will be registered as proprietor, because Heledd's title was extinguished after 12 years of adverse possession.","The application will be rejected, and Gareth must wait a further 10 years before he can apply again.","Gareth will be registered with possessory title, which Heledd can challenge within 12 years."],"a":1,"e":"Under LRA 2002 Sch 6, a squatter may apply after **10 years' adverse possession**. If the registered proprietor objects by counter-notice, the squatter succeeds only if he meets one of three conditions: **estoppel**, some **other entitlement**, or a **reasonable boundary mistake** (with a reasonable belief that the land was his). Gareth knew the land was Heledd's, so he fails. Heledd then has **two years** to evict him; if she does not, he may re-apply. The old 12-year extinguishment rule does not apply to registered land."},
 {"t":"registered-land","q":"Iwan owns a registered freehold with an old restrictive covenant against using it for trade, entered by notice on the charges register. Iwan is selling to Jodie, who wants to run a café there. Jodie's solicitor points out the notice.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the effect of the notice?","o":["It has no effect on Jodie unless she has actual knowledge of the covenant before completion.","It prevents Iwan from selling to Jodie without the consent of the person who benefits from the covenant.","It converts the covenant into a legal interest that binds every future owner.","Jodie takes subject to the covenant if valid, but its validity is not guaranteed.","It guarantees that the covenant is valid and enforceable against Jodie by the person who benefits."],"a":3,"e":"Entry of a **notice** ensures that the interest, **if valid**, keeps its priority and binds a later registered disponee (LRA 2002 s.29 and s.32(3)). A notice **does not guarantee validity**: the covenant must still be shown to be enforceable (for example, that its benefit has passed). A notice does not stop a sale (that is the role of a restriction), and it does not make an equitable interest legal."},
 {"t":"unregistered-land","q":"Kelvin is buying an unregistered freehold house from Lowri. Lowri's title deeds include a conveyance on sale to her in 2022, a conveyance on sale in 2009, a mortgage of 1998 and a conveyance on sale in 1990.\n\nWhich document should Kelvin's solicitor require as the root of title?","o":["The 1998 mortgage, because a mortgage is the best evidence of the owner's title.","No root is needed, because Kelvin can rely on a search of the Land Charges Register.","The 2022 conveyance, because it is the most recent document dealing with the whole legal and beneficial interest.","The 2009 conveyance, because it is the most recent good root that is at least 15 years old.","The 1990 conveyance, because the root must be at least 30 years old."],"a":3,"e":"Under LPA 1925 s.44, the buyer can require title to be deduced from a **good root of title at least 15 years old**. A good root is a document dealing with the whole **legal and equitable interest**, describing the land adequately and casting no doubt on the title: a **conveyance on sale** is ideal. The 2009 conveyance is the most recent document that is at least 15 years old (it is 17 years old in 2026). A mortgage is not ideal, and the 2022 conveyance is too recent."},
 {"t":"unregistered-land","q":"In 2005 Morgan granted his neighbour Nerys a right of way over his unregistered farm by deed, for the benefit of her land for ever. In 2026 Morgan sold the farm to Olwen. The track is overgrown and Olwen knew nothing of the right. No land charge was registered.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Olwen is bound?","o":["She is not bound, because a right of way that is not visibly used is presumed abandoned.","She is bound, because a legal easement binds every later owner of unregistered land regardless of notice.","She is not bound, because the easement was not registered as a Class D(iii) land charge.","She is not bound, because she was a purchaser of the legal estate without notice.","She is bound only if the easement is noted on the title when the farm is first registered."],"a":1,"e":"In unregistered land, **legal estates and interests bind the world**: a legal easement created by **deed** for a period equivalent to a fee simple binds every later owner, whether or not they knew of it. **Class D(iii)** covers only **equitable** easements created after 1925. Mere non-use does not show an intention to abandon. On first registration the easement should be entered, but Olwen is bound already."},
 {"t":"unregistered-land","q":"Pryce owns an unregistered house subject to a first mortgage to Elm Bank, which holds the title deeds. In 2024 he granted a second legal mortgage to his friend Quentin, who did not register anything. In 2026 Pryce granted a further mortgage to Rowan Finance, which searched the Land Charges Register and found nothing.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Quentin's mortgage?","o":["It binds Rowan Finance, because a legal mortgage binds the world in unregistered land.","It binds Rowan Finance, because it was created before Rowan Finance's mortgage.","It is void against Rowan Finance only if Rowan Finance had no actual knowledge of it.","It binds Rowan Finance, because only equitable mortgages need to be registered.","It is void against Rowan Finance, because it was not registered as a land charge."],"a":4,"e":"A legal mortgage **not protected by deposit of title deeds** is a **puisne mortgage**, the main exception to the rule that legal interests bind the world. It must be registered as a **Class C(i)** land charge; if not, it is **void against a purchaser for value of any interest** in the land (LCA 1972 s.4(5)), which includes a later mortgagee. Knowledge is irrelevant for land charges."},
 {"t":"unregistered-land","q":"Sabrina is buying an unregistered house. The title deeds include a 1912 conveyance containing a covenant by the then buyer, for himself and his successors, not to use the land for any trade. Sabrina's solicitor did not read the pre-root deeds, but the covenant is referred to in the 1990 root conveyance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Sabrina will be bound by the covenant (if its benefit has passed)?","o":["She will be bound, because the doctrine of notice applies and she has constructive notice.","She will not be bound, because she personally had no actual knowledge of the covenant.","She will be bound only if the covenant is registered against the name of the 1912 buyer.","She will not be bound, because a covenant over 100 years old is automatically unenforceable.","She will not be bound, because the covenant was never registered as a Class D(ii) land charge."],"a":0,"e":"**Restrictive covenants created before 1926** cannot be registered as land charges (Class D(ii) covers only those created after 1925). They are governed by the **doctrine of notice**. A buyer has **constructive notice** of what a proper investigation would reveal, and **imputed notice** of what her solicitor knew or should have found. Since the covenant appears in the root, Sabrina is bound. Age alone does not end a covenant."},
 {"t":"unregistered-land","q":"In 2018 Tegid agreed in writing to sell an unregistered field to Ulla. Ulla registered a Class C(iv) land charge against the name 'Tegid Jones', but the owner's full name is 'Tegid Rhys Jones'. Tegid has now sold the field to Vaughan, whose solicitor searched against 'Tegid Rhys Jones' and received a clear official search certificate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Vaughan is bound by Ulla's estate contract?","o":["He is bound, because Tegid's name was registered in a recognisable form.","He is not bound, because an estate contract cannot be registered as a land charge.","He is not bound, because his clear search certificate is conclusive.","He is bound, because the land charge was registered before he bought the field.","He is bound, because registration of a land charge is actual notice to everyone."],"a":2,"e":"The Land Charges Register is a register of **names**. Where a charge is registered against an incorrect version of the name and the buyer searches against the **correct full name** and obtains a **clear official certificate**, the certificate is **conclusive** in the buyer's favour (LCA 1972 s.10(4); Diligent Finance v Alleyne). Ulla's remedy is against Tegid for breach of contract. An estate contract is a Class C(iv) land charge."},
 {"t":"unregistered-land","q":"In 2015 Wendy covenanted, by deed, with her neighbour Xander not to build on part of her unregistered garden. Xander registered the covenant as a Class D(ii) land charge against Wendy's name. In 2026 Wendy sold the house to Yannick, whose solicitor forgot to make a land charges search. Yannick knew nothing of the covenant and has started building.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Yannick's position?","o":["He is not bound, because a restrictive covenant can only bind the original covenantor.","He is not bound, because he had no actual knowledge of the covenant.","He is bound, because registration of a land charge is actual notice to everyone.","He is not bound, because he bought the legal estate for money without notice.","He is bound only if his solicitor would have discovered the covenant by inspecting the land."],"a":2,"e":"Under LPA 1925 **s.198**, **registration** of a land charge is deemed to be **actual notice** to everyone for all purposes. A registered Class D(ii) covenant therefore binds a later buyer whether or not he searched or knew of it. Yannick's remedy, if any, is against his solicitor for failing to search."},
 {"t":"unregistered-land","q":"Zelda's husband Alun is the sole owner of their unregistered home. Zelda has no beneficial interest but, as his spouse, has statutory home rights. She registered a Class F land charge against Alun's name. Alun has now sold the house to Bridget, who says she did not know Zelda had any rights.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Zelda's rights bind Bridget?","o":["They do not bind Bridget, because home rights are personal and can never bind a buyer.","They do not bind Bridget, because she did not know of them before completion.","They bind Bridget only if Zelda was in actual occupation when Bridget bought the house.","They do not bind Bridget, because home rights end automatically when the house is sold.","They bind Bridget, because a registered Class F land charge binds a later buyer."],"a":4,"e":"A spouse's **home rights** under the Family Law Act 1996 are registrable in unregistered land as a **Class F** land charge. Once registered, the charge is **actual notice** to everyone (LPA 1925 s.198), so it binds a buyer regardless of her knowledge. Actual occupation is irrelevant to whether a land charge binds in unregistered land."},
 {"t":"unregistered-land","q":"Caradog is the sole owner of the legal title to an unregistered house, which he holds on trust for himself and his partner Dilys, who contributed half the price. Dilys lives there openly with him. Caradog mortgages the house to Fern Bank, which pays the loan to him alone. Fern Bank's surveyor visited the house and saw women's clothing and a second toothbrush, but the bank made no further inquiries.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Dilys's interest binds Fern Bank?","o":["It binds the bank, because there was no overreaching and the bank had constructive notice.","It does not bind the bank, because she did not register her beneficial interest as a land charge.","It binds the bank, because she was in actual occupation, which always protects a beneficiary in unregistered land.","It does not bind the bank, because a mortgage lender is never bound by an unregistered interest.","It does not bind the bank, because her interest was overreached when the bank paid the mortgage money."],"a":0,"e":"A beneficial interest under a trust is **not a land charge**. It is **overreached** only if the money is paid to **two trustees** or a trust corporation; here it was paid to a **sole trustee**, so the **doctrine of notice** applies. A purchaser (including a mortgagee) has **constructive notice** of what reasonable inspection and inquiries would reveal; signs of a second occupant should have prompted inquiry (Kingsnorth Finance v Tizard). Occupation is relevant only as giving notice, not as an automatic protection."},
 {"t":"unregistered-land","q":"Eira owns an unregistered cottage. She encouraged her neighbour Ffion to build a garage partly on Eira's land, assuring her that she could use the land for it for ever. Ffion built the garage at great expense. Eira has now sold the cottage to Gwyn, who saw the garage and knew Ffion used it, but Ffion never registered anything.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ffion's rights bind Gwyn?","o":["They bind Gwyn only if Ffion had a signed written contract with Eira.","They do not bind Gwyn, because he bought the legal estate for money.","They do not bind Gwyn, because an estoppel equity must be registered as a Class D(iii) land charge.","They are likely to bind Gwyn, because estoppel rights depend on notice, which he had.","They do not bind Gwyn, because estoppel rights are personal and only bind the person who gave the assurance."],"a":3,"e":"An **equity arising by proprietary estoppel** is not one of the classes of **land charge**, so it cannot be registered under the LCA 1972. It is therefore governed by the **doctrine of notice** (Ives v High). Gwyn saw the garage and knew of its use, so he had **notice** and is bound (subject to the court's discretion as to the remedy). The protection for purchasers 'for money' applies only to registrable land charges."},
 {"t":"unregistered-land","q":"Heulwen agreed in writing in 2019 to grant Ianto an option to buy her unregistered field. Ianto did not register a land charge. In 2026 Heulwen transferred the field to her new husband, Jared, in consideration of their marriage; he paid no money.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ianto's option binds Jared?","o":["It does not bind Jared, because an option ends when the land is transferred to a family member.","It binds Jared, because he did not buy the legal estate for money or money's worth.","It does not bind Jared, because an unregistered estate contract is void against any purchaser for value.","It does not bind Jared, because marriage is treated as valuable consideration and defeats unregistered land charges.","It binds Jared only if he knew of the option before the transfer."],"a":1,"e":"An unregistered **Class C(iv)** estate contract (which includes an **option**) is void only against a **purchaser of a legal estate for money or money's worth** (LCA 1972 s.4(6)). **Marriage consideration** is value but is not 'money or money's worth', so Jared is not protected, and the option binds him. The wider protection for any purchaser for value applies only to Classes C(i)-(iii) and F. Knowledge is irrelevant either way."},
 {"t":"co-ownership","q":"Five friends, Ali, Bea, Cal, Dev and Eve, all aged over 18, buy a registered house together. The transfer names all five as buyers, in that order. They contribute equally to the price.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who holds the legal title?","o":["All five, as joint tenants of the legal estate on trust for themselves.","The first four named, Ali, Bea, Cal and Dev, as joint tenants on trust for all five.","All five, as tenants in common of the legal estate in equal shares.","Ali alone, as the first named, holding on trust for all five.","The first two named, Ali and Bea, because a trust of land needs exactly two trustees."],"a":1,"e":"The legal title to land can be held by **no more than four** trustees (Trustee Act 1925 s.34; LPA 1925 s.34). Where more than four are named, the **first four named who are of full age** hold the legal estate, as **joint tenants** (the legal title can only be held jointly), on trust for all five. Eve still has a beneficial interest. Two trustees are needed to give a valid receipt for capital money, but up to four can hold the legal title."},
 {"t":"co-ownership","q":"Farid and Grace bought a registered house. The transfer is in both their names and says they hold the property 'on trust for themselves equally'. Nothing else is stated. Farid has died, leaving his estate by will to his brother.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who is entitled to Farid's beneficial share?","o":["Grace, because the right of survivorship applies to all co-owners of land.","His brother and Grace in equal shares, because the will severs the joint tenancy.","Grace, because a will cannot pass any interest in co-owned land.","His brother, because the word 'equally' created a tenancy in common in equity.","Grace, because co-owners named on the transfer are always beneficial joint tenants."],"a":3,"e":"An express declaration is conclusive. **Words of severance** such as '**equally**', 'in equal shares' or 'to be divided between' show that each co-owner has a distinct share, creating a **tenancy in common** in equity. There is no right of survivorship, so Farid's half passes under his **will** to his brother. Grace remains sole legal owner, holding on trust for herself and the brother."},
 {"t":"co-ownership","q":"Hari and Ivan are business partners. They bought a warehouse together for use in their business, in their joint names, with no declaration of their beneficial interests. Hari paid 70% of the price and Ivan 30%. Ivan has died.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the beneficial ownership?","o":["Equity presumes a tenancy in common in commercial situations such as this, so Ivan's 30% share passes to his estate.","Ivan's estate is entitled to half, because business partners always share property equally.","Hari owns the whole warehouse, because he paid the greater share of the price.","Hari owns the whole warehouse by survivorship, because they held as joint tenants with no words of severance.","Hari and Ivan's estate hold the warehouse equally, because a joint purchase in joint names creates equal shares."],"a":0,"e":"Without an express declaration or words of severance, **equity** presumes a **tenancy in common** in certain situations, including where the co-owners are **business partners** buying property for the business, and where they contribute in **unequal shares** (a resulting trust in proportion to contributions). Survivorship is regarded as inappropriate in commercial dealings. Ivan's estate therefore takes his 30%."},
 {"t":"co-ownership","q":"Jade and Kwame own a registered house as beneficial joint tenants. Without Kwame's knowledge, Jade forged his signature on a legal charge of the house in favour of Larch Bank to secure her personal loan. Jade has now died. Her will leaves everything to her sister.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the beneficial ownership of the house?","o":["The bank owns Jade's half outright, because the charge transferred her share to it.","Kwame owns the whole house by survivorship, because the forged charge was void and had no effect at all.","Kwame owns the whole house by survivorship, free of the charge, because a joint tenant cannot deal with her share alone.","Jade's sister owns the whole house, because the charge severed the legal title.","The joint tenancy was severed, so Jade's half passes under her will, subject to the charge."],"a":4,"e":"A joint tenant who **deals with her own share** (by sale or mortgage) severs the joint tenancy as an **act operating on her own share** (Williams v Hensman). A forged charge cannot bind the non-signing co-owner, but it takes effect as an **equitable charge over the forger's beneficial share** and so **severs** the joint tenancy. Jade's half therefore passes under her will, still **subject to** the bank's charge; Kwame keeps his half."},
 {"t":"co-ownership","q":"Leon and Megan, former partners, own a house as beneficial joint tenants. Leon now lives elsewhere. He sent a signed notice of severance by first-class post to the house, where Megan still lives. It arrived, but Megan, who was ill, never opened it. A week later Leon died.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the joint tenancy was severed?","o":["It was not severed, because a notice is effective only when the recipient has read and understood it.","It was not severed, because a notice of severance must be sent by recorded delivery to be valid.","It was severed, because the notice was validly served even though never read.","It was severed only if Megan acknowledged receipt in writing.","It was not severed, because a notice of severance takes effect only one month after service."],"a":2,"e":"Under LPA 1925 **s.36(2)**, a joint tenant may sever by **notice in writing** to the other joint tenants. By s.196, a notice is sufficiently served if **left at** the recipient's last known place of abode; if sent by registered post or recorded delivery it is deemed served when it would normally be delivered. Once delivered, the notice is effective **even if never read** (Kinch v Bullard). No acknowledgement or waiting period is needed."},
 {"t":"co-ownership","q":"Nadia, aged 60, and her brother Omar, aged 55, owned a holiday cottage as beneficial joint tenants. Both were killed instantly in the same car crash, and it cannot be determined who died first. Nadia's will leaves everything to her son; Omar's will leaves everything to his daughter.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who inherits the cottage?","o":["Nadia's son, because Nadia, as the elder, is presumed to have survived Omar.","Neither, because the cottage passes under the intestacy rules where the order of death is unknown.","Nadia's son and Omar's daughter equally, because simultaneous death automatically severs the joint tenancy.","Nadia's son and Omar's daughter equally, because neither joint tenant survived the other.","Omar's daughter, because Omar, as the younger, is presumed to have survived."],"a":4,"e":"Where two people die in circumstances in which it is uncertain who died first, LPA 1925 **s.184** (the **commorientes** rule) presumes that the **elder died first**. Omar, as the younger, is treated as surviving Nadia, so he took the whole cottage by **survivorship**, and it then passes under his will to his daughter. Simultaneous death does not sever a joint tenancy."},
 {"t":"co-ownership","q":"Pippa, Quinn and Ross are the trustees and beneficiaries of a house held on trust for them in equal shares. All three were entitled to live there. After a dispute, the trustees exercised their power to exclude Ross from occupation, and Pippa and Quinn continue to live in the house.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the trustees may do about Ross?","o":["They may require Pippa and Quinn to make compensation payments to Ross for his exclusion.","They may exclude Ross only with a court order, and no compensation can be required.","They may exclude Ross, and he is entitled to an immediate sale of the house.","They may not exclude Ross at all, because every beneficiary has an absolute right to occupy.","They may exclude Ross, but he loses his beneficial share for as long as he is excluded."],"a":0,"e":"A beneficiary with an interest in possession has a **right to occupy** under TLATA 1996 **s.12**. Where two or more are entitled, the trustees may **exclude or restrict** some (but not all) of them under **s.13**, acting reasonably, and may impose **conditions** on those who remain, such as making **compensation payments** to the excluded beneficiary. Exclusion does not affect Ross's beneficial share, and he may apply to court under s.14 if he disagrees."},
 {"t":"co-ownership","q":"Sam and Tara, who are not married, bought a house as beneficial joint tenants five years ago specifically as a home in which to bring up their children, now aged 6 and 9. They have separated. Tara and the children still live there. Sam has applied to court for an order for sale so he can release his capital.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will approach the application?","o":["It will apply the bankruptcy rules, under which a sale is ordered after one year.","It must order a sale, because a co-owner is always entitled to realise his share.","It will weigh the statutory factors, including the children's welfare, and may postpone a sale.","It must refuse a sale, because the house was bought as a family home.","It will order a sale only if Tara has failed to pay her share of the mortgage."],"a":2,"e":"On an application under TLATA 1996 **s.14**, the court considers the **s.15 factors**: the **intentions** of those who created the trust, the **purposes** for which the property is held, the **welfare of any minor** who occupies it as a home, and the interests of any secured creditor. Where the house was bought as a **family home** for children, and that purpose continues, the court may refuse or postpone a sale. There is no automatic right to sale. The one-year rule applies only to bankruptcy under s.335A."},
 {"t":"co-ownership","q":"Ulrika is the sole registered proprietor of a house, which she holds on trust for herself and her father Victor, who contributed 40% of the price and lives there. Nothing is entered on the register. Ulrika sells the house to Wilma for £400,000, paid to Ulrika alone. Wilma did not ask Victor anything, although he was living there at the time.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Victor's interest binds Wilma?","o":["It does not bind Wilma, because his interest was overreached when she paid the purchase price.","It does not bind Wilma, because his interest was not protected by a notice on the register.","It binds Wilma only if she actually knew that he had contributed to the price.","It binds Wilma, because it was not overreached and Victor was in actual occupation.","It does not bind Wilma, because a beneficiary under a trust has only a personal claim against the trustee."],"a":3,"e":"**Overreaching** requires capital money to be paid to **at least two trustees** (or a trust corporation) (LPA 1925 ss.2 and 27). Payment to a **sole trustee** does not overreach, so the question is whether Victor's interest binds Wilma under the LRA 2002. A beneficial interest of a person in **actual occupation** overrides under **Sch 3 para 2**, and Wilma made no inquiry of him (Williams & Glyn's Bank v Boland). Knowledge is not required."},
 {"t":"co-ownership","q":"Xavier and Yvonne own their home as beneficial joint tenants. Xavier was convicted of the manslaughter of Yvonne, having killed her deliberately in a violent attack. Yvonne's will leaves everything to her mother.\n\nWhich of the following best describes who owns the beneficial interest in the home?","o":["Yvonne's estate alone, because Xavier forfeits his own share as well as hers.","Xavier and Yvonne's estate, because the forfeiture rule severs the joint tenancy.","Xavier alone, because the forfeiture rule applies only to murder, not manslaughter.","Yvonne's estate alone, because a criminal conviction automatically severs the legal title.","Xavier alone, because he survived Yvonne and takes by survivorship."],"a":1,"e":"The **forfeiture rule** prevents a person who has **unlawfully killed** another from benefiting from the death, and it applies to manslaughter involving deliberate violence as well as murder. For co-owned land, the joint tenancy is treated as **severed**: the killer keeps his **own share**, but Yvonne's share passes under her will to her mother. (The court can modify the rule under the Forfeiture Act 1982 in some manslaughter cases, but not usually where the killing was deliberate and violent.)"},
 {"t":"easements","q":"Adele owns a canal-side boatyard. Her neighbour Bryn, who owns the canal and its towpath, granted her by deed 'the exclusive right to hire out pleasure boats on the canal'. Bryn has since let a rival operate boats on the canal, and Adele claims this infringes her easement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Adele's right?","o":["It is only a licence, because it does not accommodate her land.","It is a profit à prendre, because it allows her to take a benefit from Bryn's land.","It is an easement only if she has used the canal for more than 20 years.","It is an easement, because it was granted by deed for the benefit of her land.","It is an easement, because the canal and the boatyard are next to each other."],"a":0,"e":"An easement must **accommodate the dominant tenement**: it must benefit the land itself, not merely give a **personal or commercial advantage** to its current owner (Re Ellenborough Park). A monopoly right to run a business on the servient land is a commercial right that is not connected to the normal use of the land, so it is only a **licence** binding Bryn in contract (Hill v Tupper). Proximity alone is not enough, and the right does not involve taking anything from the land, so it is not a profit."},
 {"t":"easements","q":"Carys owns a block of flats with a large shared courtyard containing about 20 unmarked parking spaces. When she sold a flat to Dewi, she granted him by deed 'the right to park one private car anywhere in the courtyard'. Carys's successor now argues that a right to park cannot be an easement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Dewi's right?","o":["It cannot be an easement, because a right to park does not benefit the flat itself.","It cannot be an easement, because a right to park amounts to exclusive possession of the servient land.","It can be an easement, because the owner keeps possession and reasonable use.","It cannot be an easement, because rights involving vehicles are only licences.","It is capable of being an easement only if a particular space is allocated to Dewi."],"a":2,"e":"A **right to park** can be an easement, provided it does not amount to **exclusive possession** or leave the servient owner without any **reasonable use** of its land (the 'ouster' principle). A right to park one car anywhere in a large courtyard plainly leaves the owner in possession and control, so it is capable of being an easement. A right to park benefits the flat by making it more useful as a home. A designated space can still be an easement, but allocation is not a requirement."},
 {"t":"easements","q":"Elwyn owns a registered freehold. By a deed dated last year, he granted his neighbour Ffion a right of way across his garden for the benefit of her house for ever. Ffion's solicitor did not apply to register the easement. Elwyn now argues that Ffion has no legal easement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ffion's right?","o":["It is legal, because it was granted by deed for a period equivalent to a fee simple.","It is legal, because express easements over registered land override without registration.","It is a mere licence, because it was never registered.","It is legal only if Ffion has used the path for at least one year.","It is only equitable, because the grant has not been registered."],"a":4,"e":"An easement capable of being legal must be created by **deed**, and in **registered land** the express grant of a legal easement is a **registrable disposition** (LRA 2002 s.27(2)(d)) that operates at law only when **registered**. Until then, Ffion holds an **equitable easement**. Expressly granted easements do not override under Sch 3 para 3, which applies only to legal easements, mainly those arising by implication or prescription."},
 {"t":"easements","q":"For the past 25 years Gwil has driven across his neighbour Hana's field to reach his barn. Each January, Hana's solicitor sent Gwil a letter confirming that he was permitted to use the track 'for the coming year, until further notice'. Hana has now blocked the track, and Gwil claims an easement by prescription.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gwil's claim?","o":["It will succeed, because Hana knew of the use and did nothing to stop it.","It will fail, because the use was with Hana's permission and so was not 'as of right'.","It will succeed under the Prescription Act 1832, which does not require the use to be as of right.","It will fail only if Hana can show that the track was used secretly.","It will succeed, because he has used the track for more than 20 years."],"a":1,"e":"Prescription requires **20 years' use 'as of right'**: without force, without secrecy and **without permission** (nec vi, nec clam, nec precario). Written **permission**, renewed periodically, means the use was by licence and cannot found a prescriptive easement, however long it continued. Mere knowledge and acquiescence by the owner supports a claim, but positive permission defeats it."},
 {"t":"easements","q":"From 1985 to 2010 the owners of a cottage openly walked across a neighbouring field to reach a bus stop, without permission, force or secrecy. In 2010 the bus route was withdrawn and the path was not used again until 2025. The current owner of the field has now fenced it off.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the cottage has an easement?","o":["It has an easement under the Prescription Act 1832 based on the use from 1985 to 2010.","It has no easement, because a footpath to a bus stop cannot accommodate a dominant tenement.","It has no easement, because there must be 20 years' use immediately before legal proceedings are begun.","It may have an easement by lost modern grant, based on the 20 years' use from 1985 to 2010.","It has no easement, because the easement was abandoned when it was not used for 15 years."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **Prescription Act 1832**, the 20 years' use must be **next before** the action, so the gap since 2010 defeats a claim under the Act. The **doctrine of lost modern grant** presumes a grant after **20 years' use as of right at any time**, so the 1985-2010 use is enough. **Non-use alone** does not amount to abandonment, which requires a clear **intention** to give up the right permanently."},
 {"t":"easements","q":"In 2000 Iolo's predecessor was granted by deed a right of way over a private lane 'for all purposes' to reach a field. Iolo has now obtained planning permission to build six houses on the field, which will greatly increase traffic along the lane. The lane owner says the easement cannot be used for access to the houses.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The houses can use the right of way only for pedestrian access, because vehicular use would be excessive.","The houses cannot use the right of way, because an easement is limited to the use being made when it was granted.","The houses can use the right of way only if the lane owner consents to the increase in traffic.","The houses can use the right of way, because it was granted 'for all purposes'.","The easement has been extinguished, because the dominant land has changed its character."],"a":3,"e":"The scope of an **express easement** depends on the **words of the grant**, construed in context. A right of way granted '**for all purposes**' is not confined to the use at the date of the grant, so it can be used for a new purpose such as access to houses, and an increase in use is permitted. The stricter limits on a radical change in the character of the dominant land apply mainly to easements arising by **prescription** or implication. A change of use does not extinguish the easement."},
 {"t":"easements","q":"Jana owns a registered freehold. In 2020, by a signed written agreement that was not a deed, she granted her neighbour Kofi a right to use her drive to reach his garage. Kofi used the drive daily. Nothing was entered on Jana's register. Jana has now sold her house for full value to Lemar, who has blocked the drive.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Lemar is bound?","o":["He is bound, because the drive was in regular use and the easement was obvious on inspection.","He is not bound, because an unprotected equitable easement cannot override.","He is bound, because Kofi was in actual occupation of the drive when Lemar bought the house.","He is bound, because an easement binds every later owner of the servient land.","He is not bound only if he had no actual knowledge of Kofi's use."],"a":1,"e":"Without a deed, Kofi has at most an **equitable easement**. Under LRA 2002 **Sch 3 para 3**, only **legal** easements can override a registered disposition, so an equitable easement binds a buyer for value only if protected by a **notice** (s.29). Using a right of way is not **actual occupation** of the land under Sch 3 para 2. The buyer's knowledge is irrelevant."},
 {"t":"easements","q":"Mabli owns a house which has had the benefit of a legal right of way over her neighbour Nathan's driveway for many years. Nathan has now died, and Mabli has bought Nathan's house from his executors. She now occupies both houses, using Nathan's house as a home office and studio.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the easement?","o":["It continues, because Mabli uses the two houses for different purposes.","It is suspended while Mabli owns both houses, and revives automatically if she sells either of them.","It continues until Mabli registers a formal release of the easement.","It continues, because an easement is a permanent right attached to the land.","It has been extinguished by common ownership and possession of both houses."],"a":4,"e":"An easement requires the dominant and servient land to be **owned and occupied by different persons**. When both come into the **same ownership and possession**, the easement is **extinguished**; it does not revive automatically on a later sale, although a new easement may be granted expressly or implied."},
 {"t":"easements","q":"Olwen's house has had an uninterrupted view across open fields to the sea for over 40 years. Her neighbour has obtained planning permission to build a barn that will completely block the view. Olwen claims she has an easement of a view by prescription.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Olwen's claim?","o":["It will fail, because a right to a view is too vague and wide to be capable of existing as an easement.","It will fail only because the neighbour has planning permission for the barn.","It will succeed, because a right to a view is treated in the same way as a right to light.","It will succeed, because she has enjoyed the view for more than 20 years without interruption.","It will succeed, because the view accommodates her house by increasing its value."],"a":0,"e":"To be an easement, a right must be **capable of forming the subject matter of a grant**, which means it must be sufficiently **definite** and not too wide. There is **no easement of a view**, because it is incapable of precise definition and would restrict the neighbour's use of an unlimited area. A **right to light** through a defined window can exist, but it is different. Planning permission does not decide private rights."},
 {"t":"easements","q":"Paul holds a 10-year lease of a ground-floor shop from Quilla, who owns the rest of the building and the yard behind it. The lease, made by deed, grants Paul a right to load and unload deliveries in Quilla's yard. Quilla argues that a tenant cannot have an easement over his own landlord's land.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Paul can have an easement only once he has used the yard for 20 years.","Paul cannot have an easement, because the landlord and tenant are treated as owning the same land.","Paul can have an easement, because he and Quilla occupy different land.","Paul cannot have an easement, because only a freeholder can hold an easement.","Paul can have only a licence, because a right for business deliveries cannot be an easement."],"a":2,"e":"An easement requires different persons to own **or occupy** the dominant and servient land. A **tenant** can hold an easement over other land retained by his **landlord**, because the tenant's leasehold estate is distinct from the yard the landlord keeps. Easements can exist for a **term of years**, so a leaseholder can hold one, and a right of access for deliveries accommodates a shop. It is created expressly by the lease, so no period of use is required."},
 {"t":"covenants","q":"In 2012 Rhys sold part of his garden to Sara, who covenanted, for herself and her successors, to maintain a boundary fence. In 2020 Sara sold the land to Tom. Tom has let the fence collapse. Rhys still owns his house.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rhys's remedy?","o":["He can obtain an injunction against Tom, because the covenant was made for Sara's successors.","He has no remedy against anyone, because the covenant ended when Sara sold the land.","He can sue Sara for damages, because she remains liable on her covenant.","He can sue Tom for damages, because the burden of a covenant passes with the land at common law.","He can sue Sara only if Tom has already been sued and cannot pay."],"a":2,"e":"The **original covenantor** remains liable in **contract** for breaches of the covenant even after she has parted with the land, because the covenant is expressed to be made for herself and her successors (LPA 1925 s.79). The burden of a **positive** covenant does **not run** to successors at common law or in equity, so Tom is not directly liable. In practice, Sara will have taken an **indemnity covenant** from Tom so she can recover from him."},
 {"t":"covenants","q":"Ursula owns a house on a private estate. The estate road is owned by a management company. Ursula's predecessor covenanted to contribute to the cost of maintaining the road and was granted a right of way over it. Ursula uses the road every day but refuses to pay, arguing that the burden of a positive covenant cannot bind her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["She must pay, because positive covenants bind successors in equity where they have notice.","She need not pay, but the company may forfeit her house for non-payment.","She must pay only if she personally signed a deed of covenant with the company.","She need not pay, because the burden of a positive covenant never runs to a successor.","She must pay if she wishes to keep using the road, under the benefit and burden principle."],"a":4,"e":"Although the burden of a positive covenant does not run, the **benefit and burden** principle (Halsall v Brizell) means a successor who **chooses to take the benefit** of a right (here the right of way) must accept a burden that is **conditional on or reciprocal to** it (the road maintenance contribution). Ursula can avoid paying only by giving up use of the road. There is no forfeiture of freehold land for breach of covenant."},
 {"t":"covenants","q":"In 2015 Victor sold part of his land to Wendy, who covenanted not to build anything on the land sold, to protect the view from Victor's retained house. The covenant was protected by a notice on Wendy's registered title. Wendy has sold to Xena, who has started building a garage. Victor still owns his house.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Victor can stop Xena?","o":["Yes, because the burden of the restrictive covenant runs in equity.","Yes, but only by claiming damages, because an injunction is not available against a successor.","No, because a restrictive covenant ends when the original covenantor sells the land.","No, because Xena was not a party to the covenant and there is no privity of contract between them.","No, because the burden of a covenant never runs with freehold land."],"a":0,"e":"The **burden** of a **restrictive** covenant runs in **equity** (Tulk v Moxhay) if it is **negative** in substance, it **accommodates** land retained by the covenantee, the original parties **intended** it to bind successors (presumed under LPA 1925 s.79), and it is **protected** (by notice in registered land). All are satisfied, so Victor can seek an **injunction**, the usual equitable remedy, against Xena."},
 {"t":"covenants","q":"In 2018 Yusuf sold a field to Zoe, who covenanted to keep the hedges along the boundary with Yusuf's farm properly trimmed. Zoe still owns the field. In 2025 Yusuf sold his farm to Aisha. Zoe has let the hedges grow wildly, damaging Aisha's crops.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Aisha can sue Zoe?","o":["No, because Aisha was not a party to the covenant and is not bound by privity of contract.","No, because the benefit of a positive covenant can never pass to a successor.","No, because only the original covenantee, Yusuf, can enforce the covenant.","Yes, because the benefit of the covenant passed to her at common law.","Yes, but only if the benefit was expressly assigned to Aisha in the transfer of the farm."],"a":3,"e":"Here the **original covenantor** (Zoe) is being sued, so only the passing of the **benefit** matters. At **common law**, the benefit of a covenant (positive or negative) passes to a successor of the covenantee if it **touches and concerns** the land, the parties intended it to run (helped by LPA 1925 s.78), the original covenantee held a **legal estate**, and the successor takes a legal estate. A hedge-trimming covenant benefits the farm, so Aisha can sue. Express assignment is one route, not a requirement."},
 {"t":"covenants","q":"In 1995 a developer sold a plot to Ben, who covenanted with the developer 'and its successors in title to the land edged red on the attached plan' not to use the plot for business purposes. The developer later sold part of the red land to Cleo. Ben's successor now runs a garage business on the plot. Nothing was said about the covenant in the transfer to Cleo.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Cleo can enforce the covenant?","o":["No, because the benefit was not expressly assigned to Cleo when she bought.","Yes, because the benefit was annexed to every part of the land.","No, because Cleo bought only part of the benefited land.","Yes, but only if Cleo can show that a building scheme exists.","No, because the covenant was made with the developer, not with Cleo."],"a":1,"e":"Under LPA 1925 **s.78**, a covenant that **relates to** the covenantee's land is deemed to be made with its successors, which **annexes** the benefit to the land (Federated Homes v Mill Lodge). Where the land is **identifiable** (here, the red land on a plan), the benefit is annexed to **each and every part** of it, so a buyer of part takes the benefit automatically without express assignment."},
 {"t":"covenants","q":"Dina's house is next to land owned by Eli, which is subject to a covenant, protected on the register and benefiting Dina's land, against building above two storeys. Eli built a three-storey block of flats. Dina knew of the work from the start but took no action until the flats were finished and sold to residents.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the remedy Dina is most likely to obtain?","o":["Nothing, because a covenant against building ends once a building has been completed.","Damages in lieu of an injunction, because demolition would be oppressive and she delayed until the flats were complete.","An order setting aside the sale of the flats to the residents.","Nominal damages only, because she suffered no financial loss.","A mandatory injunction requiring the top storey to be demolished, because an injunction is always granted for breach of a restrictive covenant."],"a":1,"e":"The usual remedy for breach of a restrictive covenant is an **injunction**, but it is **discretionary**. Where the claimant has **delayed** while the building was completed and an injunction requiring demolition would be **oppressive**, the court is likely to award **damages in lieu** (now under the Senior Courts Act 1981 s.50). These can be assessed as a sum the claimant might reasonably have demanded for releasing the covenant, so they are not merely nominal."},
 {"t":"covenants","q":"Fern owns a large house on land subject to a 1960 covenant restricting it to use as 'a single private dwelling'. The area has since become a busy commercial district, and the land benefited by the covenant is now offices. Fern wants to convert the house into flats.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Fern can be released from the covenant?","o":["She can obtain release only by agreement, because a court has no power to remove a covenant.","She can apply to the local planning authority, whose planning permission overrides the covenant.","She can simply ignore the covenant, because covenants over 50 years old are automatically unenforceable.","She can apply to the Upper Tribunal (Lands Chamber) to discharge or modify it.","She can ask HM Land Registry to remove the covenant from her title because the area has changed."],"a":3,"e":"Under LPA 1925 **s.84**, the **Upper Tribunal (Lands Chamber)** can **discharge or modify** a restrictive covenant on grounds including that it is **obsolete** because of changes in the character of the neighbourhood, that it **impedes reasonable use** without securing practical benefits of substantial value, that the beneficiaries **agree**, or that discharge will cause **no injury**. Covenants do not expire with age, and **planning permission** does not override private covenants."},
 {"t":"covenants","q":"In the 1980s a developer laid out a defined estate of 40 houses and sold every plot subject to the same covenants, including a ban on business use, intending them to be mutually enforceable by all plot owners. Gina bought plot 30 in 1988. The owner of plot 5, sold in 1985 before Gina's purchase, now runs a nursery business from it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Gina can enforce the covenant?","o":["She can, because the estate is subject to a building scheme binding every plot.","She cannot, because a business use does not affect the value of plot 30.","She cannot, because plot 5 was sold before plot 30, so its covenant was not made for Gina's benefit.","She cannot, because covenants can only be enforced by the original developer.","She can, but only by claiming damages, because injunctions are not available under a building scheme."],"a":0,"e":"A **building scheme** exists where a developer lays out a **defined area** in plots, imposes **common covenants** intended to benefit all plots, and buyers purchase on that basis. Every plot owner can then enforce the covenants against every other, **regardless of the order** of purchase, which overcomes the normal problem that an earlier buyer's covenant could not be made with land the developer had already sold."},
 {"t":"covenants","q":"Hywel owns a registered freehold subject to a covenant, protected by notice, prohibiting its use 'for any trade or business'. The covenant benefits the neighbouring house owned by Ines. Hywel has granted a three-year lease to Jasper, who has opened a tattoo studio there. Hywel lives abroad and says he cannot control his tenant.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Ines can take action against Jasper?","o":["She cannot, because only the freehold owner of the burdened land can be bound by the covenant.","She cannot, because Jasper's lease is for less than seven years and so is not affected by the covenant.","She can sue Jasper only for damages, because he did not give the covenant himself.","She cannot, because covenants protected by notice bind only buyers, not tenants.","She can seek an injunction, because the covenant binds Jasper in equity."],"a":4,"e":"The **burden** of a **restrictive** covenant runs in **equity** and binds not only successors to the freehold but **anyone who occupies** the burdened land, including **tenants** and licensees, unless they are protected purchasers without notice. Here the covenant was protected by notice, so Jasper is bound and Ines can obtain an **injunction** against him, as well as pursuing Hywel."},
 {"t":"covenants","q":"In 2010 Kai covenanted with his neighbour Lena, owner of the adjoining house, not to build on his garden. The covenant benefited Lena's house and was protected by notice. In 2024 Kai bought Lena's house and now owns and lives in both properties. He wishes to sell his original house with permission to build on the garden.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the covenant?","o":["It is suspended while Kai owns both houses and will revive automatically when he sells one of them.","It remains enforceable, but only by Lena personally.","It has been extinguished by the unity of ownership and possession.","It can be removed only by an order of the Upper Tribunal (Lands Chamber).","It remains enforceable and will bind the buyer, because it is protected by notice on the register."],"a":2,"e":"A restrictive covenant is generally **extinguished** when the **benefited and burdened land** come into the **same ownership and possession** (unity of seisin), because a person cannot enforce a covenant against himself. It does not revive automatically on a later sale; a fresh covenant would have to be imposed. Kai should apply to remove the notice from his register. (Under a building scheme the covenants may revive, but there is none here.)"},
 {"t":"mortgages","q":"Mared borrowed £200,000 from a private lender, Nevin, secured by a legal charge over her farm. In the same mortgage deed, she granted Nevin an option to buy the farm at a fixed price at any time during the next 10 years. Mared has now repaid the loan in full, and Nevin seeks to exercise the option.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The option is valid unless Mared can prove undue influence by Nevin.","The option is void only if the fixed price is below the market value of the farm.","The option is valid, because Mared freely agreed to it in a deed.","The option is valid, because the loan has now been repaid, so the mortgage is no longer relevant.","The option is void, because it is a clog on the equity of redemption."],"a":4,"e":"Equity protects the borrower's **equity of redemption**: the right to recover the property free of the mortgage on repayment. An **option to purchase** the mortgaged property granted to the lender **as part of the mortgage transaction** is a **clog** on redemption and is **void** (Samuel v Jarrah Timber), whatever its price or the borrower's consent. An option granted in a separate, later and independent transaction may be valid."},
 {"t":"mortgages","q":"Ortho Ltd, a large property company, borrowed £20 million from a pension fund on the security of a legal charge over its office portfolio. Both parties were advised by solicitors. The mortgage provides that the loan cannot be repaid for 40 years. Ortho Ltd now wishes to redeem early to refinance at a lower rate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The postponement is likely to be valid, as it is not oppressive or unconscionable.","The postponement is valid only if Ortho Ltd pays the lender all interest for the full 40 years.","The postponement is void, because any postponement of the right to redeem is a clog on the equity of redemption.","The postponement is void, because a mortgage cannot last longer than 25 years.","Ortho Ltd can redeem at any time after the legal date for redemption, whatever the mortgage says."],"a":0,"e":"A **postponement** of the date for redemption is not automatically a clog. It will be upheld if it does not make the right to redeem illusory and is not **oppressive or unconscionable**. In a **commercial** mortgage negotiated at **arm's length** between parties of equal bargaining power with legal advice, a long postponement is likely to be valid (Knightsbridge Estates v Byrne). There is no fixed maximum term."},
 {"t":"mortgages","q":"Pritam's mortgage of his house to Quay Bank was made by deed, and the legal date for redemption has passed. He is one month behind with his instalments, and the bank has served no notice on him. The bank has sold the house to Rani without going to court, and Rani has been registered. Pritam claims the sale should be set aside.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The sale is void, because the bank's power of sale had not arisen, so Pritam keeps the house.","The sale is valid and Pritam has no remedy, because the legal date for redemption had passed.","Rani has good title, but Pritam may claim damages from the bank for the improper sale.","The sale is void, because a lender can only sell a borrower's home with a court order for sale.","The sale is valid, but Rani holds the house on trust for Pritam until he can repay the loan."],"a":2,"e":"The statutory power of sale **arises** (LPA 1925 s.101) when the mortgage is by deed and the **legal date for redemption has passed**. It becomes **exercisable** (s.103) only when notice requiring repayment has been served and three months' default has passed, interest is two months in arrears, or another covenant is broken. A buyer is protected if the power has **arisen**, even if it was not exercisable (s.104); the borrower's remedy is **damages** against the lender for the improper exercise."},
 {"t":"mortgages","q":"Seren's house was subject to a first legal charge to Teal Bank securing £150,000 and a second legal charge to Uplands Finance securing £40,000. Teal Bank exercised its power of sale and sold the house for £180,000. Its costs of sale were £5,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Teal Bank must apply the surplus after paying its own debt and costs?","o":["It may keep the £25,000 surplus as compensation for the trouble of selling.","It must pay the £25,000 surplus into court, to be divided equally between Uplands Finance and Seren.","It must pay the surplus to Seren, and Uplands Finance must sue Seren personally for its debt.","It must pay the £25,000 surplus to Uplands Finance, which is entitled to it before Seren.","It must pay the £25,000 surplus to Seren, because she is the owner of the equity of redemption."],"a":3,"e":"Under LPA 1925 **s.105**, the selling lender holds the proceeds on trust to pay: (1) any prior incumbrances, (2) the **costs** of sale, (3) the **mortgage debt** owed to it, and then (4) the **residue** to the person entitled to the mortgaged property, which means any **subsequent mortgagee** before the borrower. The surplus here is £180,000 − £5,000 − £150,000 = £25,000, all of which goes to Uplands Finance (owed £40,000); Seren receives nothing, and Uplands can sue her on her personal covenant for the remaining £15,000."},
 {"t":"mortgages","q":"Viking Bank holds a legal charge over a block of shops owned by Wren Ltd. The shops are let and Wren Ltd is in arrears on the loan. The bank does not want to take possession itself, because of the risk of liability as a mortgagee in possession, but it wants the rents to be applied to the debt.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the bank's most suitable remedy?","o":["Suing Wren Ltd on its personal covenant to pay, which automatically diverts the rents to the bank.","Appointing a receiver, who acts as Wren Ltd's agent in collecting the rents.","Forfeiting the tenants' leases, so that the bank can re-let the shops itself.","Taking possession, because only a mortgagee in possession can collect rent from tenants.","Applying for foreclosure, so that the rents become payable to the bank."],"a":1,"e":"A lender can **appoint a receiver** (LPA 1925 s.101(1)(iii) and s.109, once the power of sale is exercisable). The receiver is deemed to be the **borrower's agent**, so the lender is not liable for the receiver's acts in the way it would be as a **mortgagee in possession** (which carries a strict duty to account). It is the usual remedy for income-producing property. Foreclosure is rarely granted and not designed for collecting rent."},
 {"t":"mortgages","q":"Xenia defaulted on a mortgage of her investment property, which is worth much more than the debt. The lender, Yew Bank, would like to become the outright owner of the property rather than sell it, and has started foreclosure proceedings.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["The court is unlikely to grant foreclosure and, on Xenia's request, may order a sale instead so that she receives the surplus.","Foreclosure is available only where the property is worth less than the debt, so the bank must sue on the personal covenant instead.","Foreclosure will be granted automatically, because the borrower is in default.","Foreclosure will be granted, and Yew Bank must pay Xenia the difference between the debt and the property's value.","The bank can foreclose without a court order by registering itself as proprietor."],"a":0,"e":"**Foreclosure** extinguishes the borrower's equity of redemption and vests the property in the lender. It requires a **court order** and is now very **rarely** granted, because it can give the lender a windfall where the property is worth more than the debt. Under LPA 1925 **s.91**, the court can order a **sale** instead at the request of the borrower or any interested party, so the surplus goes to the borrower."},
 {"t":"mortgages","q":"Zack owns a registered house. On 1 March he granted a legal charge to Alder Bank. On 10 March he granted a second legal charge to Birch Finance. Birch Finance registered its charge on 15 March. Alder Bank did not register its charge until 20 March. Neither lender knew of the other.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the priority of the two charges?","o":["Alder Bank has priority, because its charge was created first.","Alder Bank has priority, because a first legal charge always ranks ahead of a second charge.","Birch Finance has priority, because its charge was registered first.","They rank equally, because neither lender knew of the other.","Birch Finance has priority only if Alder Bank's delay in registering was unreasonable."],"a":2,"e":"In registered land, **registered charges** rank according to the **order in which they are entered on the register**, not the order of creation (LRA 2002 s.48). Birch Finance registered first, so it has priority. An unregistered charge takes effect only in equity until registered, and lenders protect themselves by making an official search with **priority** before completion. Knowledge is irrelevant."},
 {"t":"mortgages","q":"Cerys needed a loan of £10,000 from her friend Dilwyn. As security, she handed him her original title deeds and a copy of her registered title, saying, 'Keep these until I repay you.' Nothing was put in writing. Cerys has now been made bankrupt, and Dilwyn claims to be a secured creditor.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Dilwyn's position?","o":["He has a legal mortgage, because Cerys intended to give him security over her house.","He has a lien over the house, because he holds the title documents.","He has an equitable mortgage only if he registers a notice on Cerys's title.","He has an equitable mortgage by deposit of title deeds, which gives him priority over unsecured creditors.","He has no mortgage, because there is no written contract signed by both parties."],"a":4,"e":"An **equitable mortgage** is a contract to create a mortgage, so it must satisfy LP(MP)A 1989 **s.2** (writing signed by both parties containing all the terms). The old rule that a mere **deposit of title deeds** created an equitable mortgage did not survive s.2 (United Bank of Kuwait v Sahib). Dilwyn therefore has no security and is an **unsecured** creditor. Registration cannot create an interest that does not exist."},
 {"t":"mortgages","q":"Emyr's house is mortgaged to Fir Bank. Emyr defaulted, and the bank took possession with a court order. Property prices are currently falling sharply, and agents advise that waiting a year would produce a much higher price. The bank sells immediately at full current market value after proper marketing. Emyr claims the bank should have waited.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the bank's liability?","o":["It is not liable, because a mortgagee owes the borrower no duties at all when selling.","It is not liable, because it may choose when to sell, provided it obtains the market value.","It is liable, because it must obtain the court's permission before selling in a falling market.","It is liable, because a mortgagee must act in the borrower's best interests and wait for the market to recover.","It is liable, because a mortgagee may only sell when the price will cover the whole debt."],"a":1,"e":"A selling mortgagee must act in **good faith** and take **reasonable care to obtain the true market value** (or best price reasonably obtainable) **at the time** it chooses to sell, which includes proper marketing (Cuckmere Brick v Mutual Finance). But it may **choose the timing** of the sale to suit itself and is **not** obliged to wait for the market to improve (Silven Properties v RBS). It owes some duties, so it is not entirely free of obligations."},
 {"t":"mortgages","q":"Gwen and her husband Hari jointly applied to Ivy Bank for a loan of £100,000, secured on their jointly owned home, stating that it was to buy a holiday cottage in their joint names. In fact Hari used undue influence to obtain Gwen's agreement and spent the money on his gambling debts. The bank knew nothing of this. Gwen now argues the charge should not bind her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The charge does not bind Gwen, because she was a victim of undue influence and the bank's knowledge is irrelevant.","The charge does not bind Gwen, because the bank is always fixed with notice of undue influence between spouses.","The charge does not bind Gwen, because the bank did not insist that she receive independent legal advice.","The charge binds Gwen, because a joint loan to a couple for their stated joint purposes does not put the bank on inquiry.","The charge binds Gwen only if she signed it in the presence of a bank official."],"a":3,"e":"A lender is **put on inquiry** where a wife (or other person in a non-commercial relationship) stands as **surety** for her husband's debts (Royal Bank of Scotland v Etridge (No 2)). Where the loan is a **joint advance** to both for their **joint purposes**, the lender is **not** put on inquiry, because nothing suggests that one party is being disadvantaged (CIBC Mortgages v Pitt). Unless the bank had actual notice, the charge binds Gwen."},
 {"t":"leases-nature","q":"In 2014 the local council granted Jonah, by deed, the right to occupy a plot of land next to his house as a private garden 'until the council requires the land for road widening', at a rent of £1,200 a year. He has paid the rent annually ever since. The council does not yet need the land, but it now gives him one month's notice to leave.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Jonah's position?","o":["He holds a lease for a term of 12 years, inferred from the period he has occupied, which has now come to an end.","He is a licensee, because the agreement does not give a certain term, so one month's notice is enough.","He has a lease for life, converted into a 90-year term ending on his death or when the council needs the land, so the notice is ineffective.","He has a yearly periodic tenancy, implied from the annual rent, so the council can end it by giving at least half a year's notice to quit expiring at the end of a year.","He is a tenant at will, so the council can end his occupation immediately."],"a":2,"e":"A lease must have a **certain term**, so a term 'until the land is required for road widening' cannot take effect as written (Prudential Assurance v London Residuary Body); for a company tenant the result would be a yearly periodic tenancy implied from the rent. But where the tenant is an **individual** and the parties intended the arrangement to be binding, it is treated as a **lease for life**, which LPA 1925 **s.149(6)** converts into a **90-year term** ending on the tenant's death and still determinable on the agreed event (Mexfield Housing Co-operative v Berrisford). The council does not yet need the land, so its one month's notice is ineffective. Jonah has exclusive possession at a rent, so he is neither a licensee nor a tenant at will."},
 {"t":"leases-nature","q":"Kerry rents a studio flat from Lior under a document called a 'licence agreement', which says she is not a tenant. She pays £900 a month. She lives there alone, and Lior provides no services. Lior keeps a key, which he may use only in an emergency or with notice to inspect repairs.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kerry's status?","o":["She is a licensee, because Lior has kept a key to the flat.","She is a tenant, because she has exclusive possession.","She is a tenant only if the agreement was made by deed.","She is a licensee, because she pays monthly and has no fixed term.","She is a licensee, because the agreement expressly says she is not a tenant."],"a":1,"e":"The test is whether the occupier has **exclusive possession** for a **term** (normally at a **rent**) (Street v Mountford). The **label** the parties use is not decisive. A landlord's retention of a key for limited purposes, such as emergencies or inspecting repairs, is consistent with exclusive possession; it is unrestricted access to provide services that would point to a licence. A monthly letting can be a (periodic) lease, and a lease of three years or less needs no deed."},
 {"t":"leases-nature","q":"Mostyn works as the resident caretaker of a school. His employment contract requires him to live in a flat on the school site so that he can respond to alarms and lock and unlock the buildings. He has exclusive use of the flat and a reduced salary to reflect it. His employment has now ended.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mostyn's status?","o":["He is a tenant at will, because his occupation depended on his employment.","He is a licensee only if he never paid anything for the flat.","He is a tenant, because he had exclusive possession of the flat in return for a reduced salary.","He is a tenant, because a deduction from salary counts as rent.","He is a licensee, because he was a service occupier required to live there."],"a":4,"e":"Exclusive possession for a term at a rent normally creates a lease, but there are **exceptions** where there is no tenancy, including a **service occupancy**: an employee **required to live** in accommodation for the **better performance of his duties**. Such an occupier is a **licensee**, and the right to occupy ends with the employment. A salary reduction does not change this."},
 {"t":"leases-nature","q":"Nadia granted her uncle Osian the right to live in her cottage 'for the rest of his life' in return for a premium of £30,000 and a monthly rent of £200. The grant was made by deed. Osian is 70.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Osian's interest?","o":["A lease for a term of 90 years that can be ended by notice after Osian's death.","A periodic monthly tenancy, because rent is payable monthly.","A legal life estate in the cottage.","A licence, because a lease cannot be granted for an uncertain period such as a life.","An equitable life interest under a trust of land, with no lease."],"a":0,"e":"A lease for **life** granted at a **rent or for a premium** would be void for uncertainty, but LPA 1925 **s.149(6)** converts it into a lease for **90 years**, which can be ended after the death by at least one month's notice expiring on a quarter day. A life estate cannot exist at law (it is equitable under a trust of land), but the statutory conversion applies because Osian pays a rent and premium."},
 {"t":"leases-nature","q":"Petra owns a registered office building. She granted Quentin a lease of one floor for exactly seven years, starting on the date of the grant, by deed at a market rent. Quentin did not register the lease. Petra has now sold the building to Rhodri, who did not know about the lease.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Rhodri is bound by Quentin's lease?","o":["He is not bound, because a lease of seven years or more must be registered with its own title.","He is bound only if Quentin was in actual occupation and Rhodri should have discovered him.","He is bound, because the lease is a legal lease that overrides.","He is not bound, because the lease was never protected by a notice on Petra's title.","He is bound only if the lease is for less than three years."],"a":2,"e":"In registered land, only a lease of **more than seven years** is a **registrable disposition** (LRA 2002 s.27). A lease of **exactly seven years** created by deed is therefore **legal** without registration and **overrides** a registered disposition under **Sch 3 para 1**, whether or not the tenant is in occupation and whatever the buyer knew. The three-year limit relates only to leases that can be created without a deed."},
 {"t":"leases-nature","q":"Sion owns a farm. In 2026 he agreed by deed to grant his granddaughter Tirion a lease of a barn for 30 years, starting in 2050, when he expects her to have qualified as a vet.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the validity of the lease?","o":["It is valid, but takes effect only as an equitable lease until 2050.","It is valid, provided it is registered before it takes effect in possession.","It is void, because the term starts more than 21 years after the grant.","It is void, because a lease cannot be granted to start at a future date.","It is valid, because it was made by deed and the term of 30 years is certain."],"a":2,"e":"A lease can be granted to start at a future date (a **reversionary lease**), but under LPA 1925 **s.149(3)** a term that is to start more than **21 years** after the instrument creating it is **void**. A start date 24 years after the grant is too late, so the lease is void, however certain its length and even though made by deed."},
 {"t":"leases-nature","q":"Uma owns a four-bedroom house. Over the last year she let rooms to four people who each moved in on different dates. Each signed a separate agreement, paying a different monthly sum, under which Uma may choose who occupies the house and may move in a new occupier whenever one leaves. All four share the whole house.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the occupiers' status?","o":["They are joint tenants, because the separate agreements are shams.","They are tenants at will, because they moved in on different dates.","They are joint tenants, because together they have exclusive possession of the whole house.","They are each tenants of their own room, because they each pay a separate rent.","They are licensees, because the four unities needed for a joint tenancy are absent."],"a":4,"e":"Several occupiers can together have exclusive possession only as **joint tenants**, which requires the **four unities** (possession, interest, title and time). Different start dates, separate agreements and different payments mean there is no unity of title, interest or time, so they cannot be joint tenants (AG Securities v Vaughan). No one has exclusive possession of any part individually, so each is a **licensee**. The arrangements reflected genuine shared living, not a sham."},
 {"t":"leases-nature","q":"Vince and Wynne were negotiating the terms of a long lease of a warehouse. Before agreement was reached, Wynne allowed Vince to move in and start using the warehouse so that he could prepare his stock. No rent was agreed or paid. Negotiations have now broken down.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Vince's status?","o":["He is a tenant at will, which either party can end at any time.","He holds a legal lease of up to three years, because he took possession.","He is a periodic tenant, because he has taken exclusive possession of the warehouse.","He holds an equitable lease on the terms being negotiated.","He is a tenant at sufferance, because he holds over without agreement."],"a":0,"e":"A person let into possession **while the terms of a lease are being negotiated** is usually a **tenant at will**, which either party can end at any time (Javad v Aqil). No periodic tenancy arises because no rent has been agreed or paid on a periodic basis, and there is no concluded contract for an equitable lease. A tenancy at sufferance arises only when a tenant holds over after a lease ends."},
 {"t":"leases-nature","q":"Xander started using a lock-up workshop owned by Yolande with her agreement eight months ago. There is nothing in writing. He pays her £1,000 at the start of every calendar month, which she accepts. There is no fixed end date and no ongoing negotiation of terms. Yolande wants him to leave next week.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Xander's interest?","o":["A licence, because there is no written agreement, so it can be ended at any time.","A tenancy at will, because there is no fixed term, so it can be ended immediately.","An equitable lease, because a legal lease must be made by deed.","A legal monthly periodic tenancy, ended by at least one month's notice.","A yearly periodic tenancy, which requires half a year's notice."],"a":3,"e":"Where a person occupies with exclusive possession and pays and the landlord accepts rent by reference to a regular **period** (here a month), with no intention to the contrary, a **periodic tenancy** of that period arises. It is a lease for less than three years at the best rent taking effect in possession, so it is **legal** without formalities (LPA 1925 s.54(2)). At common law, a monthly periodic tenancy is ended by a notice to quit of at least **one period** expiring at the end of a period."},
 {"t":"leases-nature","q":"In July 2026 Zaynab, a private landlord, granted Alun a residential letting of a flat in England as his home. The agreement, described as an assured shorthold tenancy, states that it is for a fixed term of 12 months. Zaynab says she can recover possession at the end of the 12 months using a 'no fault' notice.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Alun holds a licence, because fixed-term residential lettings can no longer be created.","Alun holds an assured periodic tenancy, and Zaynab can recover possession only on a statutory ground through the court.","Alun holds a 12-month fixed term, after which the tenancy ends automatically without any notice.","Alun holds a periodic tenancy, which Zaynab can end by giving two months' notice to quit.","Alun holds a 12-month assured shorthold tenancy, and Zaynab can recover possession at the end of it by a section 21 notice."],"a":1,"e":"Since **1 May 2026**, the **Renters' Rights Act 2025** has turned most private residential lettings in England into **assured periodic tenancies**: fixed terms cannot be created for these lettings, and **section 21 'no fault' evictions** have been abolished. The landlord can recover possession only on a **statutory ground** (section 8) through the **court**. The tenant can end the tenancy by giving two months' notice; the landlord cannot simply serve a notice to quit."},
 {"t":"leases-covenants","q":"In 1990 Arbor Estates granted Bex a 40-year lease of a shop. In 2015 Bex assigned the lease, with consent, to Cato. Cato has failed to pay the rent for the quarter due on 24 June 2026. Arbor Estates served a valid notice on Bex under s.17 of the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995 within three months of the rent falling due.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Bex's liability?","o":["Bex is liable only if she gave an authorised guarantee agreement when she assigned the lease.","Bex is liable, because the original tenant of a pre-1996 lease remains liable for the whole term.","Bex is liable only for rent falling due within six months after the assignment.","Bex is not liable, because she was released from the tenant covenants when she assigned the lease with consent.","Bex is not liable, because only the current tenant, Cato, is liable under privity of estate."],"a":1,"e":"The lease was granted **before 1 January 1996**, so it is an **old lease**. The **original tenant** remains liable on the covenants for the **whole term** under **privity of contract**, even after assignment. The automatic release on assignment applies only to new leases under the LTCA 1995. Because a valid **s.17 notice** was served within **six months** of the rent falling due, the landlord can recover the rent from Bex (who may claim an indemnity from Cato)."},
 {"t":"leases-covenants","q":"In 2018 Delta Properties granted Ewan a 15-year lease of a warehouse, with a covenant not to assign without the landlord's consent. In 2023 Ewan assigned the lease to Fion Ltd with the landlord's consent. The landlord did not require Ewan to enter into an authorised guarantee agreement. Fion Ltd has now failed to pay the rent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ewan's liability for the rent?","o":["He is liable for the first three years after the assignment only.","He is liable, because the original tenant remains liable throughout the term under privity of contract.","He is liable, but only if the landlord serves a s.17 notice within six months.","He is not liable, because he was released on a lawful assignment.","He is liable, because he remains guarantor of the next assignee automatically."],"a":3,"e":"The lease was granted **on or after 1 January 1996**, so it is a **new lease** under the **LTCA 1995**. On a **lawful assignment** (one not in breach of covenant), the tenant is **automatically released** from the tenant covenants (s.5). He can remain liable only under an **authorised guarantee agreement** (AGA), and none was given. A s.17 notice is needed only where a former tenant is still liable."},
 {"t":"leases-covenants","q":"In 2019 Gable Ltd granted Hana a 10-year lease of a shop, containing a covenant not to assign without the landlord's consent. In 2024 Hana assigned the lease to Indigo Ltd without asking for consent. Indigo Ltd has now fallen into arrears with the rent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Hana's position?","o":["She remains liable with Indigo Ltd, because the assignment was in breach of covenant.","She is released, because the 1995 Act releases a tenant on any assignment of a new lease.","She is released, but she can be required to enter into an authorised guarantee agreement.","She is not liable, but Indigo Ltd's assignment is void and the lease remains vested in her.","She remains liable only until the landlord accepts rent from Indigo Ltd."],"a":0,"e":"Under the LTCA 1995, a tenant is released only on an assignment that is **not in breach** of covenant. An assignment made **in breach** of an alienation covenant is an **excluded assignment** (s.11): the assignment still passes the lease to Indigo Ltd, but Hana is **not released** and remains liable with Indigo until the **next lawful assignment**. The breach may also allow the landlord to forfeit."},
 {"t":"leases-covenants","q":"In 2017 Juno Ltd granted Kit a 20-year lease of a flat. The landlord covenanted to repair the structure. In 2025 Juno Ltd sold the freehold reversion to Lark Ltd. Juno Ltd did not serve any notice on Kit about being released. In 2026 the roof developed serious disrepair, which neither landlord has repaired, and Lark Ltd has gone into liquidation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Kit can sue Juno Ltd?","o":["No, because only the current landlord, Lark Ltd, is liable on the landlord covenants.","Yes, but only if Juno Ltd entered into an authorised guarantee agreement.","No, because the landlord's repairing covenant ends when the reversion is sold.","No, because a landlord is automatically released from its covenants when it sells the reversion.","Yes, because Juno Ltd did not apply for release when it sold the reversion."],"a":4,"e":"Under the LTCA 1995, the **landlord** of a new lease is **not automatically released** when it assigns the reversion. It must **apply for release** by serving notice on the tenant within **four weeks** of the assignment (ss.6-8); if the tenant objects, the court decides. Juno Ltd served no notice, so it remains liable on the landlord covenants alongside Lark Ltd. AGAs apply only to tenants."},
 {"t":"leases-covenants","q":"In 1988 Mint Ltd granted a 50-year lease of an office to Nova Ltd. In 2005 Nova Ltd assigned the lease to Oak Ltd, which in 2020 assigned it to Pine Ltd. Pine Ltd has now breached the repairing covenant. Oak Ltd gave no direct covenant to the landlord.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Oak Ltd's liability for Pine Ltd's breach?","o":["Oak Ltd is liable jointly with Nova Ltd and Pine Ltd under privity of contract.","Oak Ltd is liable only if the landlord serves a s.17 notice within six months.","Oak Ltd is not liable, because the breach happened after it assigned the lease.","Oak Ltd is liable, because every assignee of an old lease remains liable throughout the term.","Oak Ltd is liable, because it is treated as guarantor for its immediate assignee."],"a":2,"e":"For an old lease, an **assignee** is liable to the landlord through **privity of estate** only for breaches committed **while the lease is vested in it**. Once Oak Ltd assigned to Pine Ltd, it ceased to be liable for later breaches. Only the **original tenant** (Nova Ltd) remains liable throughout under privity of contract, or an assignee that gave a **direct covenant** to the landlord, which Oak Ltd did not."},
 {"t":"leases-covenants","q":"Quinn holds an assured periodic tenancy of a flat as his home. The written tenancy agreement says that the tenant is responsible for all repairs, including to the roof and external walls. The roof has started leaking badly, and Quinn has told the landlord, who refuses to act, relying on the agreement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the landlord's position?","o":["The landlord must repair the roof only if Quinn pays for the cost of the work.","The landlord need not repair the roof, because the agreement expressly puts the obligation on the tenant.","The landlord must repair the roof only if the lease contains a covenant for quiet enjoyment.","The landlord need not repair the roof, because statutory repairing obligations apply only to leases of more than seven years.","The landlord must repair the roof, because of its non-excludable statutory obligation."],"a":4,"e":"Under the Landlord and Tenant Act 1985 **s.11**, in a residential lease of **less than seven years**, the landlord must keep in repair the **structure and exterior** and the installations for water, gas, electricity, sanitation and heating. Any term putting this liability on the tenant is **void** (s.11(4) and s.12) unless authorised by the court. The obligation arises once the landlord has **notice** of the disrepair, which it has."},
 {"t":"leases-covenants","q":"Rafa holds a 12-year commercial lease of a shop with a covenant not to assign without the landlord's consent, 'such consent not to be unreasonably withheld'. On 1 March he applied in writing for consent to assign to a strong tenant, supplying full references. The landlord has not replied, and it is now 1 June.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Rafa's position?","o":["The landlord is in breach of statutory duty, so Rafa may claim damages and assign.","Rafa must wait for the landlord's reply, because consent is not given until the landlord says so in writing.","Rafa may assign only after applying to court for a declaration, and has no claim for damages.","The landlord's silence is treated as a refusal, which is presumed reasonable unless Rafa proves otherwise.","Rafa may assign only after serving a further notice giving the landlord a final 28 days to reply."],"a":0,"e":"Under the Landlord and Tenant Act **1988 s.1**, where consent is not to be unreasonably withheld, the landlord must, **within a reasonable time**, give consent (unless it is reasonable not to) and serve **written notice** of its decision, with **reasons** for any refusal or conditions. The **burden** of proving reasonableness is on the landlord. Three months' silence is likely to be unreasonable delay, a **breach of statutory duty** giving a claim for **damages**, and the tenant may assign without being in breach."},
 {"t":"leases-covenants","q":"Saffron's lease of a flat contains a covenant that the tenant 'shall not sublet the premises or any part of them'. She has found a reliable subtenant and asked the landlord for consent. The landlord refuses without giving reasons.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Saffron's position?","o":["She can sublet, because the landlord must give written reasons within a reasonable time under the 1988 Act.","She can apply to court for a declaration that the refusal is unreasonable.","She cannot sublet, because the covenant is absolute and the landlord may refuse for any reason or none.","She can sublet if she pays the landlord a reasonable sum for consent.","She can sublet, because consent cannot be unreasonably withheld and the landlord gave no reasons."],"a":2,"e":"An **absolute** covenant prohibits subletting altogether. The statutory implication that consent 'shall not be unreasonably withheld' (LTA 1927 s.19(1)(a)) and the duties under the LTA 1988 apply only to **qualified** covenants (not to assign or sublet **without consent**). Under an absolute covenant, the landlord may refuse for **any reason or none**, although it may choose to waive the covenant."},
 {"t":"leases-covenants","q":"In 2020 Thorn Ltd granted Umbra Ltd a 15-year lease of a warehouse. The lease provides that the landlord may withhold consent to assignment unless the proposed assignee has net profits of at least three times the annual rent in each of its last three years. Umbra Ltd wishes to assign to Vale Ltd, a sound company whose profits are only twice the rent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Thorn Ltd may refuse consent?","o":["It may refuse only if it obtains a court declaration that the condition is reasonable.","It may not refuse, because pre-agreed conditions are void under the anti-avoidance provisions of the 1995 Act.","It may not refuse, because Vale Ltd is a sound company and refusal would be unreasonable.","It may refuse, because the pre-agreed condition in the lease has not been met.","It may not refuse, because a lease cannot fix in advance the circumstances in which consent may be withheld."],"a":3,"e":"For **new commercial leases** (granted on or after 1 January 1996, not residential), LTA 1927 **s.19(1A)** allows the parties to agree in advance **conditions** or **circumstances** in which the landlord may withhold consent. If the landlord withholds consent because such a condition is not met, it is **not** treated as unreasonable. A profits test of this kind is a common, valid pre-agreed condition."},
 {"t":"leases-covenants","q":"Wren Ltd holds a head lease of a building, which contains a covenant not to use the premises other than as offices. Wren Ltd sublet one floor to Xylo Ltd, which has turned it into a nightclub. The head landlord, Yarrow Ltd, has no contract with Xylo Ltd. Xylo Ltd knew of the user covenant when it took the sublease.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Yarrow Ltd can take action against Xylo Ltd?","o":["It can forfeit Xylo Ltd's sublease directly, without involving Wren Ltd.","It can seek an injunction against Xylo Ltd, which is bound in equity.","It cannot, because there is no privity of contract or privity of estate between a head landlord and a subtenant.","It can sue Xylo Ltd for damages under privity of estate.","It cannot, because only Wren Ltd can enforce covenants against its own subtenant."],"a":1,"e":"There is **no privity of contract or estate** between a head landlord and a subtenant, so positive covenants cannot be enforced directly. However, a **restrictive** covenant in the head lease (such as a user restriction) binds a subtenant in **equity** under Tulk v Moxhay principles if the subtenant has **notice** of it. Yarrow Ltd can therefore seek an **injunction** against Xylo Ltd, and may also take action against Wren Ltd (including forfeiture of the head lease)."},
 {"t":"leases-remedies","q":"Zola holds a 10-year lease of a shop. The lease contains a covenant not to use the premises for the sale of hot food, but it contains no forfeiture clause (proviso for re-entry). Zola has started selling hot takeaway food. The landlord wants to end the lease.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the landlord's position?","o":["It cannot forfeit for lack of a forfeiture clause, but it can seek an injunction or damages.","It can forfeit after serving a s.146 notice, because a right of re-entry is implied into every lease.","It can forfeit immediately by peaceable re-entry, because the breach is irremediable.","It can forfeit, but only by court proceedings, because the lease is silent on re-entry.","It cannot take any action, because the lease does not provide a remedy for breach."],"a":0,"e":"**Forfeiture** is available only if the lease contains an express **right of re-entry** (forfeiture clause), or the obligation is drafted as a condition. No such right is implied into a lease for breach of a tenant's covenant. The landlord can still enforce the covenant by claiming an **injunction** to stop the hot food sales and **damages** for any loss."},
 {"t":"leases-remedies","q":"Arwel holds a commercial lease that contains a forfeiture clause for non-payment of rent 'whether formally demanded or not'. He is three months in arrears. The landlord's solicitor asks whether a notice under s.146 of the Law of Property Act 1925 must be served before forfeiting.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["A s.146 notice must be served, and the landlord must also make a formal demand at the premises.","The landlord may forfeit only if at least half a year's rent is in arrears.","No s.146 notice is needed, and the landlord may forfeit for the arrears.","The landlord must first use CRAR before it can forfeit for unpaid rent.","A s.146 notice must be served, giving Arwel a reasonable time to pay the arrears."],"a":2,"e":"A **s.146 notice** is required before forfeiture for breaches **other than non-payment of rent**. For rent, the common law requires a **formal demand**, but this is almost always **excluded** by the words 'whether formally demanded or not', as here. The landlord may forfeit (subject to the tenant's right to relief). The half-year rule concerns when a formal demand is unnecessary in the High Court, and CRAR is an alternative, not a precondition."},
 {"t":"leases-remedies","q":"Briallen holds a 99-year lease of a flat, in which she lives. She has breached a covenant by keeping a large dog. The lease contains a forfeiture clause. The landlord, after serving a valid s.146 notice, plans to change the locks one morning while Briallen is out.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the lawfulness of the landlord's plan?","o":["It is unlawful only because keeping a dog is an irremediable breach.","It is lawful, provided the landlord gives Briallen 14 days to collect her belongings.","It is lawful, because peaceable re-entry is permitted if no one is present when the locks are changed.","It is lawful, because the landlord has served a valid s.146 notice.","It is unlawful, because the landlord must obtain a court order to recover possession."],"a":4,"e":"Where premises are **let as a dwelling**, it is unlawful to enforce a right of re-entry **otherwise than by court proceedings** while any person is lawfully residing there (**Protection from Eviction Act 1977 s.2**). Peaceable re-entry is therefore not available for occupied residential premises, and doing so may also be a criminal offence. (For long residential leases, further restrictions also apply before a s.146 notice can be served.)"},
 {"t":"leases-remedies","q":"Cadi Ltd's lease of a warehouse contains a forfeiture clause. Cadi Ltd fell into rent arrears of £30,000, and the landlord peaceably re-entered the empty warehouse and changed the locks. Two months later, Cadi Ltd applied to court for relief, offering to pay all the arrears and the landlord's costs immediately.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["Relief is available only if the tenant also proves the arrears were caused by circumstances beyond its control.","Relief is likely to be granted, because the tenant will pay all arrears and costs.","Relief will be refused, because relief is not available once the landlord has peaceably re-entered.","Relief will be refused, because the tenant must apply before the landlord re-enters.","Relief will be granted only if the landlord consents."],"a":1,"e":"Equity treats a forfeiture clause for rent as **security for the rent**. A tenant who **pays all arrears and costs** will normally be granted **relief**, almost as of right. After **peaceable re-entry**, the tenant can still apply, and should do so promptly, normally within **six months** (by analogy with the statutory time limits). Two months is well within that period."},
 {"t":"leases-remedies","q":"Dwynwen holds a lease of a shop containing a covenant not to use the premises for any illegal or immoral purpose, and a forfeiture clause. The landlord discovers that, with Dwynwen's knowledge, the upper floor has for months been used as a brothel. The landlord serves a s.146 notice.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the notice must do and how soon the landlord can forfeit?","o":["The notice must require the breach to be remedied within a reasonable time, which is usually three months.","The landlord need not serve any notice, because an illegal use ends the lease automatically.","The notice must offer Dwynwen the chance to pay compensation instead of ending the lease.","The breach is irremediable, so the landlord can forfeit after a short period.","The landlord cannot forfeit, because the breach is remediable by stopping the use."],"a":3,"e":"A s.146 notice must specify the breach, require it to be **remedied** if it is capable of remedy, and require **compensation** if the landlord wants it. Use for **illegal or immoral purposes** carries a lasting **stigma** and is regarded as **irremediable** where the tenant knew of it (Rugby School v Tannahill). The notice need not require remedy, and the landlord need only allow a short time (around **14 days**) before forfeiting."},
 {"t":"leases-remedies","q":"Efa's 15-year lease of an office ended last month. She had covenanted to keep the premises in repair but left them in poor condition. The landlord's surveyor estimates repair costs of £60,000. However, valuers agree that the disrepair has reduced the value of the landlord's reversion by only £15,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the maximum damages the landlord can recover?","o":["£60,000, because the lease has ended and the statutory limits apply only during the lease.","Nothing, because the lease has ended, so the repairing covenant can no longer be enforced.","£37,500, being the average of the repair cost and the diminution in value.","£15,000, because damages are capped at the fall in value of the reversion.","£60,000, because the landlord is entitled to the full cost of putting the premises into repair."],"a":3,"e":"Under the Landlord and Tenant Act 1927 **s.18(1)**, damages for breach of a tenant's repairing covenant **cannot exceed the diminution in value of the landlord's reversion**. The cap applies to claims during the term and at its end, so the landlord can recover no more than £15,000. (Nothing at all is recoverable for failing to leave premises in repair at the end of the lease if they are to be demolished or altered so that the repairs would be valueless.)"},
 {"t":"leases-remedies","q":"Fflur Ltd holds a 20-year lease of a warehouse with 12 years left, containing a tenant's repairing covenant. The lease also gives the landlord the right to enter, carry out any repairs the tenant fails to do after notice, and recover the cost from the tenant as a debt. The tenant has ignored the landlord's repair notice, so the landlord has done the work.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the landlord can recover the cost?","o":["Only by forfeiting the lease and claiming the cost as part of its losses.","As a debt under the lease, free of the 1938 Act and the statutory cap.","Only by deducting the cost from the tenant's rent deposit, because no other remedy is available.","As damages for breach of the repairing covenant, but only with the court's permission under the Leasehold Property (Repairs) Act 1938.","As damages limited to the diminution in value of the reversion."],"a":1,"e":"A '**Jervis v Harris**' clause allows the landlord to enter, do the repairs and recover the cost as a **debt**, not as damages. Because the claim is not for damages for breach of the repairing covenant, the **Leasehold Property (Repairs) Act 1938** (which requires the court's leave where the lease has at least 3 years left) and the **s.18** cap on damages do not apply. This is a key landlord remedy for disrepair."},
 {"t":"leases-remedies","q":"Gethin holds an assured periodic tenancy of a flat. A water pipe inside the flat has burst and damaged the ceiling, but Gethin has not told the landlord, assuming it would find out from its annual inspection. Six weeks later, the ceiling collapses and damages Gethin's furniture. He claims damages from the landlord.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the landlord's liability?","o":["It is liable only if Gethin carried out the repair himself first.","It is liable, because its statutory repairing obligation is absolute and arises when the disrepair occurs.","It is liable, because it should have discovered the burst pipe by inspection.","It is not liable at all, because repairs inside the flat are always the tenant's responsibility.","It is not liable for the period before it had notice of the disrepair."],"a":4,"e":"A landlord's obligation to repair (including under the LTA 1985 **s.11**) is breached only once it has **notice** of the disrepair **within the demised premises** and fails to repair within a reasonable time (O'Brien v Robinson). Gethin did not tell the landlord, so there was no breach before notice. Internal installations such as water pipes are within s.11, so the obligation is the landlord's once it knows."},
 {"t":"leases-remedies","q":"Heledd holds a 10-year commercial lease with a tenant's break clause allowing her to end the lease on the fifth anniversary by giving six months' notice, 'provided that the tenant has paid all rent due under the lease' by the break date. She served a valid notice on time. On the break date she had paid all the rent except £12 of interest on a late payment, which the lease defines as rent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the lease has ended?","o":["It has not ended, because the break condition was not strictly met.","It has ended, because the shortfall was trivial and the court will disregard it.","It has ended, because a valid notice was served on time, which is the only requirement.","It has ended, but Heledd must pay the £12 with interest.","It has not ended, because a break clause can be exercised only by the landlord."],"a":0,"e":"A break clause is an option, and any **conditions** attached to it must be **strictly** complied with. If the clause requires payment of all rent due by the break date and any sum defined as rent (however small) is unpaid, the condition is not met and the **break fails**, so the lease continues. The court has no power to excuse a trivial shortfall."},
 {"t":"leases-remedies","q":"Ifor held a seven-year lease of a shop with four years left. His business failed, so he posted the keys to the landlord with a note saying he was giving the shop back. The landlord kept the keys, changed the locks and let the shop to a new tenant two weeks later. The landlord now claims rent from Ifor for the rest of the term.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The lease continues, because a tenant cannot end a fixed-term lease early without a break clause.","The lease has ended by merger, because the landlord now holds both the lease and the reversion.","The lease has ended by surrender by operation of law, so no further rent is due.","The lease continues, but the landlord must give credit for rent received from the new tenant.","The lease continues, because a surrender of a lease of more than three years must be made by deed."],"a":2,"e":"A surrender normally needs a **deed**, but a **surrender by operation of law** arises where the parties act in a way that is **inconsistent** with the lease continuing, such as the tenant giving up possession and the landlord **accepting** it and **re-letting** to a new tenant. The landlord is then estopped from denying that the lease has ended, so no further rent is due. Merger occurs when the tenant acquires the reversion, not the other way round."}
];

;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["property"] = [
 {"t":"transaction-overview","q":"Priya, a solicitor, is instructed by Martin to sell his flat. Priya would like to buy the flat herself at the full asking price. Martin is delighted and says he is happy for Priya's firm to continue acting for him on the sale.\n\nWhat should Priya do?","o":["Act for Martin on the sale, because he has agreed to sell at the full asking price.","Act for Martin, provided she obtains his informed consent given in writing.","Decline to act for Martin on the sale, because her own interest conflicts with his.","Act for both herself and Martin, because they share a substantially common interest.","Act for Martin, provided another solicitor in her firm takes over the file."],"a":2,"e":"This is an **own interest conflict** (SRA Code para 6.1): Priya wants to pay as little as possible, while her duty is to get the best deal for Martin. Unlike a conflict between two clients, there are **no exceptions** and client consent cannot cure it. The conflict rules apply to the whole firm, so passing the file to a colleague does not help."},
 {"t":"transaction-overview","q":"Lowri and Neil are both existing clients of Hart & Co. Each wants to buy the same development plot and they are bidding against each other. Both ask Hart & Co to act for them on their bids.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Hart & Co may act for both?","o":["It may act for both only if each gives informed written consent, effective safeguards protect their confidential information, and it is reasonable to act.","It may never act for both, because two buyers of the same property always have a conflict that cannot be cured.","It may act for both without consent, because nothing is binding until contracts are exchanged.","It must act for whichever client instructed it first, and it need not tell the other client why it has declined, because the reason is confidential to the first client.","It may act for both, provided each client is told the amount of the other's bid."],"a":0,"e":"Two buyers bidding for the same property are **competing for the same objective**, one of the two exceptions in Code para 6.2. Even then the firm may act only if both clients give **informed consent in writing**, **effective safeguards** protect each client's confidential information, and it is **reasonable** to act. Telling each client the other's bid would breach confidentiality, and the fact that nothing is binding before exchange does not remove the conflict."},
 {"t":"transaction-overview","q":"Hamid acts for Greta on the sale of her house. On completion he gives the buyer's solicitor an unconditional undertaking to redeem Greta's mortgage with Kestrel Bank. After completion he discovers that the redemption statement he relied on was out of date, and the sale money he holds is £6,000 short of the amount needed. Greta has no other money.\n\nWhat is Hamid's position?","o":["His undertaking is discharged, because he relied in good faith on the lender's redemption statement.","Only Greta is liable for the shortfall, because she is the borrower under the mortgage.","The undertaking cannot be enforced against him, because undertakings bind only the client.","The buyer must accept the property subject to the remaining £6,000 of the charge.","He must make good the shortfall himself, because his undertaking binds him personally."],"a":4,"e":"An **undertaking** binds the solicitor **personally**, and Code para 1.3 requires it to be performed. Because Hamid's undertaking was **unconditional**, he must pay off the mortgage even though the sale money is short. That is why a solicitor should only undertake what is within their control, with an up-to-date redemption statement, and why the buyer does not have to accept the charge staying on the title."},
 {"t":"transaction-overview","q":"Ines acts for Jago, a cash buyer of a £900,000 house. Jago's funds arrive from three overseas accounts in other people's names, and he will not explain why. Ines suspects money laundering. Jago then phones to ask why exchange is being delayed.\n\nWhat should Ines do?","o":["Tell Jago she must report him to the authorities unless he explains where the money came from.","Report her suspicion to the firm's MLRO and say nothing to Jago that suggests a report has been or may be made.","Proceed to exchange, because a cash buyer does not have to show where the money came from.","Report directly to the police rather than to the MLRO, and tell Jago that he must find another solicitor because a report has been made about him.","Ask Jago to sign a declaration that the funds are lawful and then proceed to exchange."],"a":1,"e":"Ines has a suspicion, so she must **report to the firm's MLRO** (Money Laundering Reporting Officer). Failing to report is an offence in the regulated sector (Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 s.330), and going ahead with the deal without consent could be the **arranging** offence. Telling Jago that a report may be made risks **tipping off**, and a signed declaration from Jago does nothing to remove the suspicion."},
 {"t":"transaction-overview","q":"Kerry is buying a flat. The day before completion she receives an email that appears to come from her solicitor's firm. It tells her to send the balance of the price to a 'new' client account because the firm has changed banks. She phones her solicitor to ask what to do.\n\nWhat should the solicitor advise?","o":["Send the money to the new account, provided the email carries the firm's usual logo and signature.","Send half the money to each account to reduce the risk of loss.","Reply to the email asking the sender to confirm the new account details in writing.","Send nothing to the new account: the firm will never change its bank details by email, so pay only to details already confirmed by phone.","Send the money to the new account, because the firm's professional indemnity insurance will cover any loss that is caused by a fraudulent email."],"a":3,"e":"This is a typical **'Friday afternoon' fraud**: a fraudster takes over or copies an email account to divert completion money. Firms should warn clients at the start that their bank details will **never change by email**, and clients should check any account details by phone using a number they already have. Replying to the suspect email, or trusting its logo, only plays into the fraudster's hands."},
 {"t":"transaction-overview","q":"Oakley Solicitors pays a local estate agent £150 for each seller the agent refers to it. The agent refers Rosa, who is selling her house, and she instructs Oakley.\n\nWhat must Oakley do?","o":["Tell Rosa about the referral fee arrangement and make sure its advice to her stays independent.","Nothing, because the arrangement is a private commercial matter between the firm and the agent, not between the firm and Rosa.","Refuse to act, because referral fees are banned in all conveyancing work.","Deduct the £150 from Rosa's bill so that she does not bear the cost.","Get the agent's consent before telling Rosa about the arrangement."],"a":0,"e":"Referral fees are allowed in conveyancing, but under Code para 5.1 the client must be **told about any financial arrangement** with an introducer, and the solicitor's advice must stay **independent** and in the client's best interests. The ban on referral fees applies to personal injury work, not conveyancing. There is no rule that the fee must be credited to the client, and the agent has no say over what the client is told."},
 {"t":"transaction-overview","q":"Ashworth Ltd is buying a freehold warehouse for its distribution business. The trainee on the file plans to send the seller's solicitors a TA6 and a TA10 and to run the matter under the Law Society Conveyancing Protocol.\n\nWhat is the best advice to the trainee?","o":["Send the TA6 and TA10, because the Protocol is compulsory for Conveyancing Quality Scheme firms in all transactions, whether residential or commercial.","Raise no enquiries, because caveat emptor means a commercial seller need not answer them.","Raise enquiries using the Commercial Property Standard Enquiries (CPSE), because the Protocol and TA forms are for residential transactions.","Send both the TA forms and the CPSE so that nothing is missed.","Rely on the estate agent's particulars instead of raising formal enquiries."],"a":2,"e":"The Conveyancing Protocol and its **TA forms** apply only to **residential** sales and purchases. In a commercial transaction the buyer's solicitor raises the **CPSE** enquiries, and the contract normally incorporates the Standard Commercial Property Conditions. Caveat emptor makes enquiries **more** important, not unnecessary, and agent's particulars are no substitute for formal replies from the seller."},
 {"t":"transaction-overview","q":"Boris's solicitors send £600,000 of completion money to the seller's solicitors, who release it to their client. It later turns out that the 'seller' was an impostor posing as the registered owner, who knew nothing of the sale. Neither firm of solicitors was negligent.\n\nWhat is the most likely position of Boris's solicitors?","o":["They are not liable to Boris, because they were not negligent.","Boris becomes the owner, because he relied on the register and so the real owner loses the property.","Only the seller's solicitors can be liable, because they were the ones who dealt with the impostor and released the money to him.","Boris must bear the loss himself, because he chose the property and agreed the price.","They are liable to Boris for breach of trust, because they held the money on trust to release only on a genuine completion."],"a":4,"e":"Completion money is held **on trust**, to be released only for a **genuine completion**. Because the seller was an impostor there was no genuine completion, so Boris's solicitors paid the money away in **breach of trust** even though they were not negligent, and the courts have refused to excuse this (Dreamvar). A forged transfer does not take the property away from the real owner, so Boris does not get good title."},
 {"t":"transaction-overview","q":"Cartwright Estates Ltd asks Pell & Co to act on the grant of a 15-year lease of a shop to Dunn Ltd. Dunn Ltd, a long-standing client of the firm, also asks Pell & Co to act for it on the same lease to save costs. Both companies say they consent.\n\nWhat should Pell & Co do?","o":["Act for both, because they share a substantially common interest in completing the lease.","Act for only one of them, because the landlord and tenant will be negotiating the lease terms against each other.","Act for both, provided different fee earners handle each side and an effective information barrier is kept in place throughout.","Act for both, because the Code for Leasing Business Premises allows a single adviser.","Act for neither, because the firm already acts for both companies on other matters."],"a":1,"e":"On the grant of a lease the landlord and tenant negotiate the rent, repair, alienation and other terms **against each other**, so there is a **conflict** and neither exception in Code para 6.2 applies. Consent and information barriers cannot cure a conflict where no exception applies. The firm can act for one of them, and acting for both on other unrelated matters is not a bar in itself."},
 {"t":"transaction-overview","q":"Elsie agreed 'subject to contract' to buy Farrah's house for £350,000 and has spent £1,800 on searches and a survey. Before exchange, Farrah accepts a higher offer of £370,000 from Gil and tells Elsie she will sell to Gil unless Elsie matches it.\n\nWhat is Elsie's position?","o":["She can obtain specific performance, because the price had been agreed.","She can recover her £1,800 of wasted costs, because Farrah has acted in bad faith.","She can complain to the SRA, because Farrah's solicitor must refuse to act where a seller gazumps.","She has no claim against Farrah, because there is no binding contract until exchange.","She can enforce the oral agreement, because she has started to perform it by paying for searches."],"a":3,"e":"This is **gazumping**, which is lawful in England and Wales because a sale of land is not binding until contracts are **exchanged**. A contract for the sale of land must be in writing and signed by both parties, and 'subject to contract' negotiations create no obligations. Elsie bears her own wasted costs, and Farrah's solicitor commits no misconduct by acting on her instructions."},
 {"t":"title","q":"Hana is buying a registered freehold house. The proprietorship register names Ken and Lily as proprietors and contains no restriction. Ken died last year and Lily is selling on her own.\n\nWhat should Hana's solicitor require?","o":["Lily must appoint a second trustee so that the price is paid to two trustees.","Ken's personal representatives must join in the transfer.","A court order authorising Lily to sell alone.","A certified copy of Ken's death certificate, after which Lily can transfer alone.","A statutory declaration from Lily that nobody else lives at the property."],"a":3,"e":"With **no Form A restriction**, Ken and Lily held as **beneficial joint tenants**, so on Ken's death the whole property passed to Lily by **survivorship**. Lily can sell alone and give a valid receipt; HM Land Registry needs evidence of Ken's death to remove him from the register. A second trustee is needed only where a Form A restriction shows a **tenancy in common**."},
 {"t":"title","q":"Emrys is buying unregistered land in 2026. The epitome of title contains: (1) a 1962 assent by the personal representatives of Fay to Gwen; (2) a 1986 conveyance on sale from Gwen to Huw, the seller; (3) a 1994 legal mortgage by Huw, discharged in 2010; and (4) a 2018 deed by which Huw granted a neighbour a right of way over the land.\n\nWhich document is the best root of title?","o":["The 1962 assent, because it is the oldest document in the epitome.","The 1986 conveyance on sale, because it is a sale of the whole interest more than 15 years old.","The 1994 legal mortgage, because a mortgage deals with the whole legal estate.","The 2018 deed of grant, because it is the most recent document dealing with the land.","None of them, so the seller must register the land before exchange."],"a":1,"e":"A good root must be at least **15 years old**, deal with the **whole legal and equitable interest**, describe the land and cast no doubt on the title. The 1986 **conveyance on sale** meets every test, and a sale is the strongest root because the buyer then would have checked title before paying. The mortgage and the grant of an easement do not deal with the whole legal and equitable interest, and an **assent** is a weaker root even though it is older. The land is still unregistered because none of these deeds triggered compulsory first registration: a sale did so everywhere only from 1 December 1990, and a first legal mortgage, assent or gift only from 1 April 1998."},
 {"t":"title","q":"Jules is buying an unregistered house. The epitome starts with a 1974 conveyance on sale from Kemp to Lamb, followed by a 1988 conveyance on sale from Lamb to Moss, the seller.\n\nAgainst whom should Jules's solicitor make the Land Charges Department search (K15)?","o":["Kemp, Lamb and Moss, each for their period of ownership.","Moss only, because only the current owner can have registered a charge that binds Jules.","Lamb and Moss only, because Kemp's ownership ended more than 40 years ago.","Nobody, provided an index map search confirms that the land is unregistered.","Moss only, for the period since the date of the root of title."],"a":0,"e":"Land charges are registered against the **name of the estate owner** who created them, so the K15 search must be made against **every owner named in the epitome** for their period of ownership. A land charge registered against Kemp or Lamb (for example a restrictive covenant registered as a D(ii) charge) binds Jules even though they no longer own the land. An index map search shows only whether the land is registered; it does not reveal land charges."},
 {"t":"title","q":"Nell is buying an unregistered house. The epitome starts with a good root in 1972. The next document is a conveyance on sale to the seller dated October 1990, which is signed by the previous owner, an individual, but has no witness's signature.\n\nWhat is the significance of the missing witness?","o":["None, because deeds made after 31 July 1990 need only be signed by the person making them and delivered, without any witness.","None, because only the root of title needs to be validly executed.","None, provided the October 1990 conveyance was sealed by the previous owner.","None, because HM Land Registry will correct the defect on first registration.","The October 1990 conveyance was not validly executed as a deed, so the seller must deal with the defect before exchange."],"a":4,"e":"Since 31 July 1990 an individual executes a deed by signing it **in the presence of a witness who attests** the signature, and delivering it (LP(MP)A 1989 s.1). Without a witness, the October 1990 conveyance was not validly executed, which breaks the chain of title. The buyer's solicitor should raise this before exchange and ask the seller to cure it (for example by a confirmatory deed); sealing is no longer the test and the Land Registry will not simply ignore it."},
 {"t":"title","q":"Olwen died leaving her registered house to her nephew. Probate was granted to her two executors, Pat and Quin. Pat is handling the sale; Quin lives abroad and would rather not be involved.\n\nWho must execute the transfer to the buyer?","o":["Pat alone, because one personal representative can deal with all of the estate's assets, including land, without the other.","The nephew, because he is entitled to the house under the will.","Both Pat and Quin, because all the personal representatives who proved the will must join in a transfer of land.","Pat alone, provided he appoints a second trustee to receive the price.","Pat alone, provided a Form A restriction is entered on the register."],"a":2,"e":"Where there are several **personal representatives**, all of those who have taken out the grant must join in a **conveyance or transfer of land** (Administration of Estates Act 1925 s.2(2)), even though one PR can usually deal with other assets alone. The nephew has no power to sell until the property is vested in him. Appointing a second trustee is the answer to a Form A restriction with a surviving co-owner, not to a sale by PRs."},
 {"t":"title","q":"Rhiannon is buying a house registered in the sole name of Simon. The charges register contains a notice of home rights in favour of Simon's wife, Tess, from whom he is separated. Simon says Tess moved out last year.\n\nWhat should Rhiannon's solicitor require?","o":["Nothing, because a spouse's home rights end automatically when she stops living in the home, so the notice is now ineffective.","Tess's signed release of her home rights and an application to cancel the notice, on or before completion.","Tess to be appointed as a second trustee so that her rights are overreached.","A statutory declaration from Simon confirming that Tess has moved out.","Indemnity insurance against any claim by Tess, without contacting her."],"a":1,"e":"A spouse's **home rights** under the Family Law Act 1996 bind a buyer once protected by a **notice**, and they do **not** end simply because the spouse moves out. The seller must supply Tess's **release** and the application to **cancel** the notice by completion. Home rights are a personal right of occupation, not a beneficial interest under a trust, so overreaching does not apply."},
 {"t":"title","q":"Una is buying a house from Vale Bank, which repossessed it from the borrower, Will, and is selling under the power of sale in its registered charge. Una's solicitor wonders whether she must check that Will was in default and that the bank's power of sale had become exercisable.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["Una must obtain Will's written consent to the sale.","Una must see evidence of Will's arrears and of the notices the bank served on him, to check that the power of sale was exercisable.","Will must sign the transfer, because he is still the registered proprietor.","Una will take subject to Will's right to redeem until a court confirms the sale.","Una need only be satisfied that the power of sale has arisen; she need not check whether it has become exercisable."],"a":4,"e":"A buyer from a mortgagee need only check that the **power of sale has arisen** (in practice, that the mortgage money has become due). It **need not enquire** whether the power has become **exercisable**, for example whether there was a default (LPA 1925 s.104). The buyer takes free of the borrower's interest and of later charges, and the borrower's consent or signature is not needed."},
 {"t":"title","q":"Xavier is buying a house and plans to build a second house in the large garden. The charges register contains a 1972 covenant not to build more than one dwelling on the land. The seller's agent has already written to the company that has the benefit of the covenant, and it replied objecting to any further building.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Xavier?","o":["Take out restrictive covenant indemnity insurance before exchange and then proceed.","Proceed, because restrictive covenants more than 50 years old cannot be enforced.","Seek a release from the company or apply to the Upper Tribunal to discharge or modify the covenant, as insurance is unlikely to be available.","Proceed once planning permission is granted, because a grant of planning permission overrides any private restrictive covenant affecting the land.","Proceed, because a restrictive covenant does not bind later owners of freehold land."],"a":2,"e":"Insurers will usually refuse restrictive covenant indemnity cover once the person with the benefit has been **contacted**, and especially where it has **objected**. The realistic options are a negotiated **release** or an application to the **Upper Tribunal (Lands Chamber)** under LPA 1925 s.84, or not proceeding with the plan. Planning permission does not override a private covenant, and restrictive covenants can run with freehold land whatever their age."},
 {"t":"title","q":"Yvette is buying a registered freehold shop with vacant possession. When she inspects, she finds Zak running a business there under a written five-year lease granted two years ago. Nothing about the lease appears on the register.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["Zak's lease will bind Yvette as an overriding interest, so this must be resolved before exchange.","Zak's lease will not bind Yvette, because it is not entered on the register.","Zak's lease will bind Yvette only if it is protected by a notice before completion.","Zak has only a personal licence, which ends when the seller sells.","Zak's lease will bind Yvette only if Zak tells the seller's solicitor about it."],"a":0,"e":"A legal lease of **7 years or less** is an **overriding interest** (LRA 2002 Sch 3 para 1), and Zak is also in **actual occupation**, so the lease binds a buyer even though it is not on the register. Yvette's solicitor must raise this before exchange: the seller must end the lease and give vacant possession, or the deal must be renegotiated. A notice is not needed for a short lease to bind."},
 {"t":"title","q":"Aled is buying a cottage that can only be reached along a track over a neighbour's field. The register shows no right of way over the track. The seller says that she and earlier owners have used the track openly and without permission for over 30 years.\n\nWhat is the best course of action before exchange?","o":["Take no action, because a track used for 30 years becomes a public right of way.","Require the seller to apply to the Upper Tribunal to discharge the neighbour's title to the track, so that the seller becomes the owner of the track.","Proceed, because the seller now owns the track by adverse possession.","Ask the seller to obtain a deed of grant or, failing that, a statutory declaration of the 30 years' use, and consider indemnity insurance.","Proceed, relying on the seller's title guarantee to cover any problem with access."],"a":3,"e":"Without a legal right of access the cottage could be landlocked. The best solution is a **deed of grant** of an easement from the neighbour; failing that, an easement may have been acquired by **prescription** (20 years' use as of right), which should be supported by a **statutory declaration** and, if needed, **insurance**. Using a track does not make the user its owner, and the title guarantee is no substitute for securing the right before exchange."},
 {"t":"searches","q":"Bethan is buying a house in a former coal-mining village in South Yorkshire. Her solicitor has ordered the usual local authority, drainage and water and environmental searches.\n\nWhich additional search should the solicitor make?","o":["An optional enquiry of the local authority about common land.","A coal mining search (CON29M) from the Mining Remediation Authority.","A chancel repair search.","An index map search at HM Land Registry.","A full search of the Land Charges register."],"a":1,"e":"In a coal-mining area the buyer should make a **CON29M** search, which reveals past and present coal workings, mine entries and **subsidence** claims. It is made to the **Mining Remediation Authority** (formerly the Coal Authority). The other searches answer different questions: commons, chancel liability, and whether land is registered or subject to land charges."},
 {"t":"searches","q":"Dilys bought a house after the seller answered 'No' on the TA6 to the question whether there had been any disputes with neighbours. After completion she discovers that the seller had been in a long dispute with next door about the boundary fence, and the neighbour has now issued proceedings.\n\nWhat remedy is Dilys most likely to have?","o":["None, because caveat emptor means the buyer takes the property as she finds it.","A claim against her own solicitor for failing to discover the dispute by a local search.","A claim against the seller under the full title guarantee only.","None, because replies to pre-contract enquiries stop being representations once the contract has been signed and exchanged.","A claim against the seller for misrepresentation, because the reply was untrue and induced the contract."],"a":4,"e":"Replies to pre-contract enquiries are **representations**. An untrue reply that induced the buyer to enter the contract gives a claim for **misrepresentation**, with damages under the Misrepresentation Act 1967 s.2(1). Caveat emptor means the seller need not volunteer information, but it does not let the seller give false answers. A local search would never reveal a private neighbour dispute."},
 {"t":"searches","q":"Ewan is buying a house with a mortgage. The seller's solicitor has supplied a search pack, but the local authority search in it is eight months old. Exchange is planned for next week.\n\nWhat should Ewan's solicitor do?","o":["Rely on it, because there is no legal time limit on how long a search remains valid.","Rely on it, because local searches remain valid for 12 months by law.","Check the lender's requirements and obtain a new local search, because it is likely to be treated as out of date.","Rely on it, provided the seller confirms in the contract that nothing affecting the property has changed since the search was made.","Ask the seller to sign a statutory declaration that no new entries have been made."],"a":2,"e":"There is no legal expiry date, but **lenders and search insurers** usually treat a search as out of date after about **6 months**. The buyer's solicitor should check the lender's requirements and obtain a fresh search (or an update and insurance acceptable to the lender) before exchange. The seller's word cannot replace a search of the council's records."},
 {"t":"searches","q":"Ebony's solicitor obtained a clear local authority search on the house she was buying. After completion, the council grants permission for a large block of flats on the field next to her garden. The planning application had been made before she exchanged contracts.\n\nWhich of the following best explains why the local search did not reveal the application?","o":["A standard local search covers only the property searched against, not neighbouring land.","The local search reveals applications within one mile, so the council must have made a mistake.","The application would have appeared only on the drainage and water search.","Planning applications are never recorded by the local authority until they are granted.","The environmental search, not the local search, is the one that reveals planning applications."],"a":0,"e":"The local search (LLC1 and CON29) answers questions about **the property searched against**. It will not normally reveal a planning application or proposed development on **neighbouring land** unless wider checks or optional enquiries are made. So where neighbouring development matters to a client, the solicitor should make further checks, such as of the council's online planning register."},
 {"t":"searches","q":"Fergus's local land charges search (LLC1) showed no entries. After completion he discovers a financial charge for work the council had carried out on the property, which should have been registered as a local land charge but was not.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["The charge is void against Fergus because it was not registered.","The seller alone remains liable for the charge, because it arose during her ownership.","Fergus can rescind the purchase because of the undisclosed charge.","The charge binds Fergus, but he can claim compensation for his loss because he searched.","The charge binds Fergus and he has no remedy of any kind."],"a":3,"e":"A local land charge **binds a buyer even if it was not registered**. But a buyer who searched and suffers loss because of an unregistered charge can claim **compensation** from the registering authority (Local Land Charges Act 1975 s.10). That is one reason to make an official search rather than rely on informal information. Rescission after completion is not available on these facts."},
 {"t":"searches","q":"Glyn Ltd is buying a site that was used as a petrol station for 40 years. The environmental search shows a high risk of contamination. The company that ran the petrol station was dissolved years ago.\n\nWhat is the main risk for Glyn Ltd?","o":["As the new owner it could be required to pay for cleaning up the land, because the original polluter cannot be found.","None, because only the person who caused the pollution can ever be made to pay.","The local authority must pay the whole cost of any clean-up of the site.","The liability passes to Glyn Ltd only if the contract expressly says so, because statutory clean-up liability otherwise stays with the seller.","The risk affects only Glyn Ltd's lender, not Glyn Ltd itself."],"a":0,"e":"Under **Part 2A of the Environmental Protection Act 1990**, if the original polluter cannot be found, the **current owner or occupier** can be made to pay for **remediation** (clean-up) of contaminated land. So Glyn Ltd should commission a further environmental survey and consider a price reduction, indemnity or insurance before exchange. The liability arises by statute, not from the contract."},
 {"t":"searches","q":"Hana is buying a stone cottage built in the 1890s, which has been altered several times. Her lender's valuation has come back satisfactory, and she tells her solicitor she will not bother with a survey.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["A basic condition report is enough, because the lender's valuer has inspected the property.","She can rely on the valuation, because she can always sue the valuer if defects appear.","She should get her own building survey, because a lender's valuation does not check the condition of an old, altered property.","She can rely on the seller's answers on the TA6, which guarantee the condition of the property.","No survey is needed, because the standard conditions let her rescind the contract if serious defects are discovered before completion."],"a":2,"e":"A **mortgage valuation** only tells the lender whether the property is adequate security; it is not a check of condition. Because of **caveat emptor**, the buyer must find out about defects herself, and an old, altered building calls for a full **building survey** (RICS Level 3). The standard conditions put the risk of the physical state on the buyer, and the TA6 is not a guarantee of condition."},
 {"t":"searches","q":"Iolo is buying a newly built house on an estate. The drainage and water search shows that the foul sewers serving the estate have not yet been adopted by the water company.\n\nWhat should Iolo's solicitor ask the developer for before exchange?","o":["An agreement with the highway authority for adoption of the estate roads.","A build-over agreement allowing building over the public sewer.","A planning agreement with the council for affordable housing contributions.","An NHBC certificate, which on its own covers the cost of making up the sewers.","An adoption agreement with the water company for the sewers, supported by a bond."],"a":4,"e":"Sewers that are not yet adopted should be covered by an **adoption agreement** with the water company under the Water Industry Act 1991 s.104, backed by a **bond** so the work is paid for if the developer fails. Without one, the owners could be left to maintain private sewers. A road agreement deals with roads, not sewers, and a build-over agreement deals with building over an existing public sewer."},
 {"t":"searches","q":"Jasper's solicitor, at a firm in the Conveyancing Quality Scheme, is acting on a residential purchase. The seller's solicitor has sent a contract package including a completed TA6, TA10 and official copies. Jasper's solicitor plans to send her firm's standard list of 120 general enquiries as well.\n\nWhat should she do?","o":["Send the full list, so that every possible issue is covered before exchange.","Raise no enquiries at all, because answers given on the TA6 are binding warranties that make any further enquiries unnecessary.","Ask the seller's solicitor to complete the CPSE.1 as well.","Raise only additional enquiries that arise specifically from the title, searches, survey or the TA forms.","Send the enquiries to the local authority instead of to the seller."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **Protocol**, the seller provides the standard **TA forms** in the contract package, and the buyer's solicitor should raise only **specific additional enquiries** arising from the documents and results. Long lists of general enquiries are discouraged because they waste time. The CPSE is for commercial deals, and TA6 replies are representations, not warranties that make further enquiries unnecessary."},
 {"t":"searches","q":"Kian is buying a house and plans to fell a large oak tree in the front garden to build a double garage. The local land charges search shows a tree preservation order on the oak.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Kian?","o":["The order binds only the owner on whom it was served, so it will not legally affect Kian once the property has been transferred to him.","He will need the council's consent to fell or lop the tree, which may be refused, so he should consider this before exchange.","Permitted development rights for garages override a tree preservation order.","The order will lapse automatically when the property is sold.","He may fell the tree without consent, provided he plants a replacement."],"a":1,"e":"A **tree preservation order** is a local land charge that **binds successive owners**. The protected tree must not be cut down or lopped without the council's **consent**, and consent may be refused. Because Kian's plans depend on felling the tree, his solicitor should report this before exchange so that he can decide whether to go ahead."},
 {"t":"finance-lender","q":"Lewis is buying a flat with a mortgage from Mere Bank, for which his solicitor also acts. Contracts are exchanged on 2 June with completion fixed for 16 June. Part 2 of the UK Finance Mortgage Lenders' Handbook says Mere Bank needs the certificate of title five working days before completion.\n\nWhen should the solicitor send the certificate of title?","o":["After exchange, no later than five working days before 16 June, so the advance arrives in time for completion.","Before exchange, together with the report on title to Lewis.","On the morning of 16 June, once the seller's solicitor has confirmed that the seller is ready, willing and able to complete that day.","Within 14 days after completion, together with the SDLT return.","Only after the priority period of the pre-completion search has ended."],"a":0,"e":"The **certificate of title** confirms to the lender that the title is good and **requests the mortgage advance**. It is sent **after exchange**, once the completion date is fixed, and in time to meet the lender's Part 2 notice period, so the money arrives before completion. Sending it on completion day is too late and could make Lewis liable for late completion; before exchange is too early because the date is not fixed."},
 {"t":"finance-lender","q":"Lara is buying her first home with a mortgage. Her father, Mal, is giving her £40,000 towards the price and will live with her in the property. Her solicitor also acts for the lender.\n\nWhat will the lender typically require in relation to Mal?","o":["Mal must be added as a joint borrower under the mortgage.","Nothing, because gifts from close family members do not need to be disclosed to a lender, provided the solicitor holds the money first.","Mal must hold a share of the property as tenant in common with Lara.","Written confirmation that the £40,000 is a gift giving Mal no interest in the property, and Mal's signed occupier's consent.","Mal must pay the £40,000 directly to the seller rather than through the solicitor."],"a":3,"e":"A lender needs to know that a **gifted deposit** is a true gift and that the giver will claim **no interest** in the property that could compete with its charge. Because Mal will live there and is aged 17 or over, he should also sign an **occupier's consent** waiving any rights against the lender. Paying money direct to the seller is a mortgage fraud warning sign, not a requirement."},
 {"t":"finance-lender","q":"Nico is buying a house with a mortgage. His mortgage offer expires on 30 April. The seller wants completion on 15 May, and Nico is keen to exchange contracts today.\n\nWhat should Nico's solicitor do?","o":["Exchange today, because the offer only needs to be valid at the date of exchange.","Before exchange, ask the lender to extend the offer or agree an earlier completion date, as the offer must be valid at completion.","Exchange today, because once contracts have been exchanged the lender is legally obliged to honour the offer even after its expiry date.","Exchange today and arrange bridging finance after exchange if the offer expires.","Ask the seller to accept a reduced deposit in case the loan falls through."],"a":1,"e":"The buyer's solicitor must check that the mortgage offer will still be **valid at completion**, because that is when the money is needed. If Nico exchanges and the offer then expires, he is still bound to complete and could lose his deposit and face a claim from the seller. The problem must be solved **before exchange**, either by an extension or by an earlier completion date."},
 {"t":"finance-lender","q":"Ollie is buying a flat for £260,000 with a mortgage, and his solicitor also acts for the lender under the UK Finance Mortgage Lenders' Handbook. The official copies show that the seller bought the flat only two months ago for £180,000.\n\nWhat should the solicitor do?","o":["Nothing, because the price is a matter for the lender's valuer, not the solicitor.","Refuse to act, because a quick resale at a higher price is unlawful.","Advise Ollie to renegotiate the price before telling anyone else.","Report the matter straight to the National Crime Agency without telling the lender.","Report it to the lender, because the Handbook requires disclosure where the seller has owned the property for less than six months."],"a":4,"e":"The lender's instructions require the solicitor to report matters that may affect the **valuation**, including a seller who has owned the property for **less than 6 months**. A quick resale at a large uplift can be a sign of mortgage fraud, so the lender must be told so that it can decide whether to proceed. Back-to-back sales are not unlawful in themselves, and there is nothing yet to justify a report to the NCA."},
 {"t":"finance-lender","q":"Pia is buying a house with a 25-year interest-only mortgage. She asks her solicitor what this means for her.\n\nWhich explanation is correct?","o":["Each monthly payment covers interest and part of the loan, so the debt is cleared after 25 years.","The interest rate is fixed for the full 25 years of the mortgage.","Her payments cover only interest, so the whole loan must be repaid at the end from another source, and the lender will expect a repayment plan.","She cannot be charged an early repayment charge, because interest-only loans are always flexible and can be repaid at any time without penalty.","The loan is secured only on her savings and not on the house."],"a":2,"e":"With an **interest-only** mortgage the monthly payments cover **interest only**, so the **whole capital** is still owed at the end of the term and must be repaid from another source, such as savings, an investment or a sale. Lenders therefore require a credible **repayment strategy**. Explaining the terms of an offer Pia already has is permitted; recommending a mortgage product would need FCA authorisation."},
 {"t":"finance-lender","q":"Tomos is selling his house. Two years ago he took out a mortgage with a five-year fixed interest rate. The sale is expected to complete in about two months.\n\nWhat should Tomos's solicitor do at an early stage?","o":["Nothing, because early repayment charges cannot be enforced when a home is sold.","Ask the buyer to take over Tomos's mortgage so the fixed rate is not broken.","Obtain a redemption statement, because an early repayment charge may be payable when the mortgage is paid off on completion.","Leave the mortgage to the buyer's solicitor, who will deal with redeeming it on completion as part of the buyer's own arrangements.","Wait until after completion before contacting the lender."],"a":2,"e":"Repaying a loan during a **fixed-rate** period often triggers an **early repayment charge**. The seller's solicitor should obtain a **redemption statement** early so that Tomos knows the exact figure and can be sure the sale price will cover the loan, the charge and costs. Without that figure, the solicitor cannot safely give an undertaking to redeem on completion."},
 {"t":"finance-lender","q":"Ulla Ltd is buying an office block with a loan from Vega Bank under a facility agreement that the parties have negotiated in detail. Ulla Ltd asks its solicitors to act for Vega Bank as well, to save costs.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["The firm may act for both, because a buyer and its lender always share a substantially common interest in the title being good.","The firm may act for both, provided Vega Bank consents in writing.","The firm must act for Vega Bank, because only the borrower's solicitor can check the title.","The firm should act for Vega Bank only and refer Ulla Ltd elsewhere.","The firm should not normally act for Vega Bank, because the individually negotiated loan terms create a conflict."],"a":4,"e":"Acting for buyer and lender is usually acceptable only where the mortgage is on the lender's **standard terms**, giving a substantially common interest. Where loan terms are **individually negotiated**, as in most commercial deals, borrower and lender want different things, so there is a **conflict** and the lender normally instructs **separate solicitors**. Consent alone does not cure a conflict where no exception applies."},
 {"t":"finance-lender","q":"Wyn is buying a house and wants to exchange contracts today because the seller is threatening to sell to someone else. His broker says his mortgage has been 'agreed in principle', but no formal offer has been issued yet.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["Do not exchange until a satisfactory formal offer is received, because after exchange he must complete even if the loan is refused.","Exchange today, because an agreement in principle binds the lender to lend.","Exchange today on a 'subject to finance' basis, which is standard in residential contracts.","Exchange today, but tell the seller that the deposit must be returned if the loan is refused.","Exchange today, because the standard conditions let a buyer rescind the contract and recover the deposit if the mortgage is later refused."],"a":0,"e":"Exchange makes the contract **binding**, so the buyer must complete whether or not the loan comes through. If Wyn exchanges without a formal offer and the loan is refused, he risks losing his **10% deposit** and being sued for the seller's further losses. An agreement in principle is not binding, and the standard conditions contain no right to rescind if finance fails."},
 {"t":"finance-lender","q":"Xena's mortgage offer was based on a purchase price of £300,000. Before exchange, the survey reveals damp and the seller agrees to reduce the price to £285,000. Xena's solicitor also acts for her lender.\n\nWhat should the solicitor do?","o":["Nothing, because a lower price can only benefit the lender.","Keep the contract price at £300,000 and record the £15,000 reduction as an allowance paid to Xena outside the contract itself.","Tell the lender only if the reduction is more than 10% of the price.","Tell the lender about the price reduction, because it may affect the valuation and the amount the lender will lend.","Send the certificate of title at the original price and tell the lender after completion."],"a":3,"e":"A solicitor acting for a lender must report anything that affects the **valuation** or the loan, and a **price reduction** is a standard example under the Handbook. The lender may want to adjust the advance or re-value. Keeping a higher price in the contract while paying less would be **mortgage fraud**, and there is no 10% threshold."},
 {"t":"finance-lender","q":"Yusra is buying a house with a mortgage. The contract incorporates the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition) without amendment, and her solicitor also acts for her lender.\n\nFrom when should Yusra's solicitor advise that the property be insured?","o":["From completion, because that is when the lender's security takes effect.","From exchange of contracts, because under the standard conditions the buyer bears the risk of damage to the property from the date of exchange.","From registration of the charge at HM Land Registry.","From the date of the mortgage offer.","From the date the seller accepted Yusra's offer, subject to contract."],"a":1,"e":"Under SC 5.1.1 of the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition, 2018 revision) the property is at the **buyer's risk from exchange**, and under SC 5.1.2 the seller is under no obligation to insure. Yusra must therefore insure from exchange, or she will have to complete at the full price even if the house is damaged. The lender's security starts on completion, and the UK Finance Mortgage Lenders' Handbook requires cover to be in place **from completion at the latest**, so insurance from exchange satisfies the lender too. Registration of the charge, the mortgage offer and acceptance of the offer are not the relevant dates."},
 {"t":"contract","q":"Joe is selling his registered freehold house. The charges register shows a registered charge in favour of Zephyr Bank and a 1962 restrictive covenant against using the property for business. Joe's solicitor is drafting the contract.\n\nWhat should the contract list as specified incumbrances?","o":["Both the charge and the covenant, because both appear on the charges register.","Neither, because the buyer is treated as knowing about every entry on the register.","The charge only, because the buyer needs to know which mortgage is being repaid.","Neither, because incumbrances are dealt with only in the transfer deed.","The covenant but not the charge, because the charge will be paid off on completion."],"a":4,"e":"**Specified incumbrances** are the burdens the buyer agrees to take the property subject to, such as the restrictive covenant. The **mortgage** is not specified, because the seller will **discharge** it on completion and the buyer must take free of it. Under the standard conditions the property is sold free of incumbrances other than those specified and a few other categories, so the covenant must be listed."},
 {"t":"contract","q":"Rhona and Saul are the executors of their late uncle's will and are selling his house. Neither of them ever lived there or knows much about its history.\n\nWhich title guarantee should the contract and transfer normally give?","o":["Full title guarantee, because the Standard Conditions of Sale require it in every sale.","No title guarantee, because the executors never owned the house themselves.","Limited title guarantee, because personal representatives know little about the property's history.","Full title guarantee, because the grant of probate proves the executors' title.","Limited title guarantee, because the house must have possessory title."],"a":2,"e":"**Personal representatives**, trustees and repossessing lenders usually give **limited title guarantee**, under which they promise only that they have not themselves incumbered the property and are not aware that anyone else has since the last sale. Full title guarantee also covers incumbrances the seller could reasonably know about, which is unfair on sellers with no personal knowledge. Executors do have a title to sell, so 'no title guarantee' is unnecessary."},
 {"t":"contract","q":"Abel agreed to buy a house under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition) without amendment, and the seller agreed to accept a 5% deposit. Abel failed to complete on the completion date, and the seller, who is ready to complete, has served a valid notice to complete.\n\nWhat is the effect on the deposit?","o":["Abel must immediately pay a further 5%, so that a full 10% deposit has been paid.","The deposit stays at 5%; the seller can claim the balance only as damages after rescinding.","Abel can now rescind the contract, because the deposit was reduced.","The seller must return the 5% deposit to Abel until completion takes place.","Abel must pay the whole balance of the price within 24 hours."],"a":0,"e":"Where a buyer has paid less than 10%, the standard conditions require it to pay the **balance of a full 10% deposit** immediately on receiving a **notice to complete** (SC 6.8.3). This protects the seller, which can then forfeit the full 10% if the buyer still fails to complete. The notice gives 10 working days to complete, not 24 hours."},
 {"t":"contract","q":"Contracts for the sale of Carmel's house were exchanged using the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition). By mistake, the completion date was left blank on both parts of the contract.\n\nWhen must completion take place?","o":["Ten working days after the date of the contract.","On any date that the seller later chooses by giving notice to the buyer.","28 days after the date of the contract.","Twenty working days after the date of the contract.","Never, because the contract is void for uncertainty without a completion date."],"a":3,"e":"Under the standard conditions, if no completion date is agreed, completion is **20 working days after the date of the contract** (SC 6.1.1; the SCPC says the same). The contract is not void, because the conditions fill the gap. Ten working days is the period allowed after a notice to complete, which is a different rule."},
 {"t":"contract","q":"Dinah is selling her house under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition). On the completion date, a Friday, the buyer's completion money reaches Dinah's solicitor's client account at 2.45pm, and completion takes place that afternoon.\n\nWhat is the effect of the time the money arrived?","o":["Completion did not take place, and Dinah may rescind immediately.","The money is treated as received on the next working day, so the buyer may have to pay compensation for late completion.","There is no effect, because the money arrived on the completion date.","Dinah's solicitor must return the money and complete on Monday instead.","The buyer must pay interest at Bank of England base rate for the period after 2pm, but the completion itself is unaffected."],"a":1,"e":"Under SC 6.1.2, money received after **2pm** is treated as received on the **next working day** for the purposes of apportionments and **compensation for late completion**. Completion itself can still happen that day, but the buyer is treated as having been late. Compensation is at the **contract rate**, not base rate, and Dinah cannot rescind without first serving a notice to complete."},
 {"t":"contract","q":"Ewan is buying a house for a total of £300,000, which includes carpets, curtains and freestanding furniture that the parties genuinely value at £4,000.\n\nHow should the contract deal with these items?","o":["Include them in a single price of £300,000, because SDLT is charged on everything the buyer pays for.","State a separate contents price of £4,000, on which no SDLT is payable.","State a contents price of £20,000, to bring the land price into a lower SDLT band.","Do not mention them, because they pass automatically to the buyer as fixtures.","Deal with them in a separate contract to be made after completion."],"a":1,"e":"SDLT is charged only on **land**. Chattels (movable items) are not land, so a **genuine** price apportioned to them in the contract is not subject to SDLT, and the contents part takes effect as a sale of goods under the standard conditions. Inflating the contents price to cut tax would be **evasion**, and chattels do not pass automatically as fixtures would."},
 {"t":"contract","q":"Faye is buying a house under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition). After exchange she discovers that, through an innocent error, the contract plan shows the rear garden as 30 metres long when it is in fact 27 metres. The house is otherwise exactly as described.\n\nWhat is Faye's remedy?","o":["She may rescind the contract and recover her deposit.","She has no remedy, because the buyer accepts the property in its physical state at exchange.","She may rescind, but only if the seller refuses to reduce the price.","She may claim damages for the material difference, but cannot rescind.","She may only seek specific performance with a reduction in price."],"a":3,"e":"Under the errors and omissions condition in the standard conditions, a buyer is entitled to **damages** where an error causes a material difference between the property as represented and as it is. **Rescission** is available only if the error results from **fraud or recklessness**, or the buyer would be obliged to accept something **substantially different**. An innocent three-metre shortfall in an otherwise accurate description gives damages, not rescission."},
 {"t":"contract","q":"When Gemma bought her house she gave a covenant to maintain a boundary wall. She is now selling and remains personally liable on that covenant after she sells.\n\nWhat should the contract and transfer provide?","o":["The buyer should give Gemma an indemnity covenant in the transfer, promising to observe the covenant and indemnify her against breach.","Nothing, because positive covenants run with freehold land and bind the buyer automatically.","Gemma must obtain a release of the covenant before exchange.","The buyer should give a fresh positive covenant directly to the original covenantee, which then releases Gemma from her own liability on it.","Gemma should take out indemnity insurance to cover any future breach by the buyer."],"a":0,"e":"The burden of a **positive covenant** does not run with freehold land at law, but the original covenantor stays liable after selling. So the seller protects herself by taking an **indemnity covenant** from the buyer, creating a **chain of indemnity**; the standard conditions require one where the seller remains liable. A release is not needed, and a positive covenant does not bind the buyer automatically."},
 {"t":"contract","q":"Carys agrees to sell a field to Dean for £80,000, with a 10% deposit and completion in six weeks. Dean signs a letter setting out the price and a description of the field, but it says nothing about the deposit or the completion date. Carys does not sign anything.\n\nIs there a binding contract?","o":["Yes, because the price and the property, which are the essential terms, have been agreed in writing and signed by the buyer.","Yes, because Dean has signed a written document.","Yes, because the parties have partly performed the agreement.","No, because a contract for the sale of land must be made by deed.","No, because the contract must be in writing, contain all the agreed terms and be signed by both parties."],"a":4,"e":"A contract for the sale of land must be **in writing**, incorporate **all the terms expressly agreed**, and be **signed by or on behalf of each party** (LP(MP)A 1989 s.2). Here the letter omits the agreed deposit and completion date, and Carys has not signed, so there is no contract. A deed is needed for the transfer, not for the contract, and part performance no longer saves an informal contract for land."},
 {"t":"contract","q":"Fern Insurance plc, which cannot recover VAT, is buying a ten-year-old office building. The seller has not opted to tax. The contract will incorporate the Standard Commercial Property Conditions (3rd edition).\n\nWhat should Fern's solicitor ask for in the contract?","o":["Nothing, because under the SCPC the price is inclusive of VAT.","The Part 2 condition that the sale is a transfer of a going concern.","The Part 2 condition under which the seller warrants that the sale is not a taxable supply and will not opt to tax.","A provision that Fern will opt to tax the building on completion.","A provision that Fern will pay any VAT charged on the price and reclaim the full amount from HMRC in its next VAT return."],"a":2,"e":"Under the SCPC the price is **exclusive** of VAT, so if the seller opted to tax before completion VAT would be added on top, which is a real cost to an insurer that cannot recover it. The optional **Part 2** VAT condition protects Fern: the seller warrants the sale is **not a taxable supply** and that it will not opt. A going concern transfer is for let property bought as a rental business, and Fern cannot reclaim VAT anyway."},
 {"t":"exchange","q":"Elin is at the bottom of a chain of three linked sales. Her solicitor speaks to each solicitor up the chain in turn using Formula C. Each confirms they hold their client's signed contract, and a release deadline of 4pm is agreed.\n\nWhat does each solicitor commit to under Part I of Formula C?","o":["Contracts are exchanged at once when Part I is agreed.","Each solicitor undertakes to exchange if the other solicitor calls to release contracts by the agreed deadline that day.","Each solicitor must send the deposit immediately on agreeing Part I.","Part I is not binding until it is confirmed in writing by each solicitor.","Each solicitor may withdraw at any time before the agreed deadline without breaching any undertaking, because nothing binds until Part II."],"a":1,"e":"**Formula C** works in two stages. Under **Part I**, each solicitor confirms it holds its client's signed contract and **undertakes to exchange** if the other calls to **release** by the agreed deadline that day; this lets commitments be built up link by link. Exchange itself happens under **Part II**, when the release call is made, and the deposit is then sent as directed."},
 {"t":"exchange","q":"Farid is selling his flat and buying a house, and both transactions are ready to exchange today with the same completion date. He needs the sale proceeds to fund the purchase.\n\nHow should his solicitor exchange?","o":["Exchange on the purchase first, so Farid is sure of his new home, then on the sale later in the week.","Exchange on both and agree the completion dates afterwards.","Exchange on the purchase now and on the sale once his buyer is ready, using bridging finance in the meantime.","Exchange on the sale first and then immediately on the purchase, with the same completion date.","Exchange on both by post, so the contracts are made at the same moment."],"a":3,"e":"The solicitor must make sure Farid is never bound to **buy** without being bound to **sell**, and that the completion dates match. So the **sale is exchanged first** and the **purchase immediately afterwards**, in the same series of calls (or using Formula C). Exchanging the purchase first risks leaving Farid committed with no buyer, and postal exchange gives no control over timing."},
 {"t":"exchange","q":"Contracts for the sale of Gwen's house are exchanged by post under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition). The buyer's solicitor posts the buyer's signed part on Monday. Gwen's solicitor posts Gwen's signed part on Tuesday, and it arrives on Thursday.\n\nWhen was the contract made?","o":["On Tuesday, when the last part was posted.","On Monday, when the buyer's part was posted.","On Thursday, when the last part was received.","When both parts have been received and the deposit has cleared.","When the completion date was inserted in the contract."],"a":0,"e":"Under the Standard Conditions of Sale, where exchange is by post or document exchange the contract is made when the **last part is posted** (or deposited at the document exchange). Gwen's solicitor posted the last part on Tuesday, so that is when the parties became bound. This uncertainty about timing is one reason telephone exchange is used in chains."},
 {"t":"exchange","q":"Hal and Iona are buying a house together. They both signed the contract three months ago. Iona now phones to authorise exchange today, but Hal is travelling and cannot be contacted.\n\nWhat should their solicitor do?","o":["Exchange today, because one joint buyer can authorise exchange for both.","Exchange today, because signing the contract gave authority to exchange at any time.","Not exchange until Hal has also confirmed his authority to exchange.","Exchange today and ask Hal to ratify the exchange when he returns.","Exchange today, provided Iona agrees to indemnify the firm."],"a":2,"e":"A solicitor needs the **express authority of every client** before exchanging. Signing the contract usually implies authority, but after three months the solicitor should confirm that nothing has changed. Exchanging **without authority** is a breach of duty to the client and could leave the firm liable, and an indemnity from one client does not cure that."},
 {"t":"exchange","q":"Jade, a buyer's solicitor, has just exchanged contracts by telephone with the seller's solicitor.\n\nWhat should Jade's attendance note of the exchange record?","o":["Only the date of exchange and the agreed completion date.","Only the amount of the deposit and the account it was sent to.","Only the names of the clients and the purchase price.","Only the time of exchange and which Law Society formula was used, because the other details already appear in the contract itself.","The date and time, the solicitors involved, the formula, any variations, the completion date and the deposit arrangements."],"a":4,"e":"Immediately after a telephone exchange, each solicitor should make a full **attendance note** of the **date and time**, the **identities** of the solicitors, the **formula** used, any agreed **variations**, the **completion date** and the **deposit** arrangements. This is the evidence of the binding contract and of the undertakings given. A partial note could leave a later dispute about the terms unresolved."},
 {"t":"exchange","q":"Contracts were exchanged for the sale of Iris's house to Jac, who paid a 10% deposit to Iris's solicitor as stakeholder. Before completion, Iris is made bankrupt.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["The contract is automatically void because of Iris's bankruptcy.","Jac must complete by paying the balance to Iris personally.","Jac can complete by registering the transfer himself without anyone else's involvement, because he became owner in equity on exchange.","The deposit vests in Iris's trustee in bankruptcy as part of her estate.","The contract binds Iris's trustee in bankruptcy, who may complete or disclaim it, and the stakeholder deposit is not Iris's money."],"a":4,"e":"Bankruptcy does not end the contract: it binds the **trustee in bankruptcy**, who may complete it or **disclaim** it as onerous (Insolvency Act 1986 s.315), leaving the buyer to prove in the bankruptcy for its loss. A deposit held as **stakeholder** belongs to neither party until completion, so it does not form part of Iris's estate. This is why stakeholder capacity protects buyers."},
 {"t":"exchange","q":"Kemi has exchanged contracts to buy a plot from a developer, with completion due in nine months once the house is built. She is worried the developer might sell or charge the plot to someone else in the meantime.\n\nWhat should Kemi's solicitor do?","o":["Protect the contract by applying for a notice on the developer's registered title.","Make a pre-completion official search now, which gives nine months' priority.","Do nothing, because a contract to buy land binds everyone who later acquires it.","Apply for a Form A restriction on the developer's title.","Make a full search of the Land Charges register against the developer."],"a":0,"e":"After exchange the buyer has an **estate contract**, which in registered land should be protected by a **notice** on the seller's title, especially where completion is a long way off. Without protection, a later buyer or lender for value could take free of it. An official search gives only **30 working days'** priority, and a Form A restriction relates to tenancy in common, not to contracts."},
 {"t":"exchange","q":"Contracts have been exchanged for Lorna's sale of her house, which includes the fitted kitchen units. Before completion, Lorna takes out the kitchen units to use in her new home.\n\nWhat is the buyer's position?","o":["Lorna remains the legal owner until completion, so she can do as she wishes with the property, including removing the kitchen units.","Because the risk passed to the buyer on exchange, the buyer must bear the loss.","Lorna holds the property as a qualified trustee and must take reasonable care of it, so the buyer has a claim for the units.","The buyer can claim only if the units were listed on the TA6.","The buyer's only remedy is to refuse to complete and recover the deposit."],"a":2,"e":"Once contracts are exchanged the buyer becomes the owner **in equity**, and the seller holds the legal title as a kind of **qualified trustee** who must take **reasonable care** of the property until completion. Deliberately removing fixtures that are included in the sale is a breach, for which the buyer can claim. The passing of **risk** concerns accidental damage, not the seller's own acts."},
 {"t":"exchange","q":"Mo's solicitor has just exchanged contracts on his purchase of a house with a mortgage.\n\nWhich steps should the solicitor take immediately after exchange?","o":["Make the pre-completion official search of the register.","Confirm insurance is in place, report exchange to Mo and the lender, and send the certificate of title in good time.","File the SDLT return and pay the tax.","Apply to HM Land Registry to register Mo as owner.","Send the seller's solicitor the redemption figures for the seller's mortgage, so that it can give the usual undertaking on completion."],"a":1,"e":"Immediately after exchange the buyer's solicitor should make sure **insurance** is arranged (from exchange if the contract puts the risk on the buyer, and in any event from completion as the lender requires), **report** exchange to the client and the **lender**, and send the **certificate of title** so the advance arrives before completion. The official search is made later, close to completion, and the SDLT return and registration come **after completion**. Redemption figures are the seller's solicitor's job."},
 {"t":"exchange","q":"A month ago Nadia gave her solicitor written authority to exchange on her house purchase. Yesterday she phoned the firm and told a receptionist that she no longer wishes to proceed, but the message never reached her solicitor. This morning the solicitor exchanged contracts.\n\nWhat is the most likely position?","o":["The contract is void, because the solicitor had no authority to exchange.","Nadia can withdraw without liability within 24 hours of exchange.","The contract binds Nadia, and the firm has no liability to her because her message was informal and was not given to her own solicitor.","The contract probably binds Nadia because of the solicitor's apparent authority, but the firm may be liable to her.","The contract binds Nadia only if her deposit has already been paid."],"a":3,"e":"Nadia withdrew her authority by telling the firm, so the solicitor exchanged **without authority**, which is a breach of duty. But the seller's solicitor reasonably relied on the solicitor's **ostensible (apparent) authority**, so the contract is likely to bind Nadia, and the firm may be **liable** to her for the loss. There is no cooling-off period after exchange."},
 {"t":"pre-completion","q":"Nia is selling part of her large garden as a building plot and keeping her house. The buyer will need rights of access and drainage over Nia's remaining land.\n\nWhich form of transfer should be used, and what must it contain?","o":["A TR1, because a transfer of registered land always uses that form.","An AP1, which transfers the plot and updates the register at the same time.","A TP1, with a plan, granting and reserving the necessary rights and setting out any new covenants.","A TR1 with a plan attached, but no rights, because easements arise automatically on a sale of part.","A traditional conveyance, because the plot has never been registered separately."],"a":2,"e":"A transfer of **part** of a registered title uses form **TP1**. It must include a **plan** identifying the part sold and should expressly **grant** the buyer rights (access, drainage, services) and **reserve** rights for the seller's retained land, with any new **covenants**. A TR1 is for a transfer of the **whole** title, and AP1 is an application form, not a transfer."},
 {"t":"pre-completion","q":"Owen and Pam are buying a house together and want to own it as tenants in common in shares of 60% and 40%. The transfer will be on form TR1.\n\nWho must execute the TR1?","o":["The seller only, because the buyers are not giving anything under the transfer.","The seller and Owen only, as the first-named buyer.","Owen and Pam only, because they are the ones acquiring the property.","The seller and the buyer's solicitor, on the buyers' behalf.","The seller, Owen and Pam, because the transfer contains a declaration of trust."],"a":4,"e":"The **seller always** executes the transfer. The **buyers must also** execute it where it contains a **declaration of trust** (here, the tenancy in common in 60:40 shares) or covenants by the buyer, such as an indemnity covenant. Both buyers must sign because both are declaring the trust."},
 {"t":"pre-completion","q":"Quinn, who is selling his registered house, lives abroad. His brother will sign the TR1 on Quinn's behalf under a general power of attorney that Quinn granted two years ago.\n\nWhat will the buyer's solicitor need to send to HM Land Registry?","o":["Nothing extra, because an attorney signs the transfer in his own name as seller, and HM Land Registry never asks to see the power itself.","A certified copy of the power of attorney and evidence that it had not been revoked, because the power was more than 12 months old.","Quinn's own signature on the transfer as well as his brother's.","An order of the court approving the sale by an attorney.","Confirmation that the power is registered at HM Land Registry."],"a":1,"e":"An attorney signs **in the name of the donor**, so the buyer's solicitor must check that the power is valid, covers the sale and has not been revoked. HM Land Registry needs a **certified copy** of the power and, because it was **more than 12 months old** when the transfer was made, **evidence of non-revocation**, such as a statutory declaration (Powers of Attorney Act 1971 s.5). No court order is needed."},
 {"t":"pre-completion","q":"Rosie is selling her house. She signs the TR1 at home while her neighbour watches over a video call and then signs as witness after the document is posted to her.\n\nIs the transfer validly executed?","o":["No, because the witness must be physically present when Rosie signs and must attest her signature.","Yes, because video witnessing of deeds has been accepted by HM Land Registry for all deeds since the pandemic in 2020.","Yes, provided Rosie's solicitor later certifies the signature.","Yes, because a TR1 does not need to be witnessed.","Yes, provided the neighbour signs within 24 hours of the video call."],"a":0,"e":"An individual executes a deed by signing it **in the presence of a witness** who attests the signature (LP(MP)A 1989 s.1). HM Land Registry requires the witness to be **physically present**, and it does **not** accept video witnessing for registration. Rosie must sign again in front of a witness who is physically there."},
 {"t":"pre-completion","q":"Sami is buying an unregistered house. His solicitor's full Land Charges search (K15) result is dated 1 March. Completion is delayed and will now take place on 3 April.\n\nWhat should Sami's solicitor do?","o":["Nothing, because a K15 search gives 30 working days' protection.","Nothing, because a K15 search remains valid for six months.","Nothing, because only entries made before the root of title can bind Sami.","Make a fresh K15 search before completion, because the 15-working-day protection will have expired.","Make an official search of the register (OS1) instead of a new K15, because the OS1 gives a longer 30-working-day priority."],"a":3,"e":"A buyer who completes within **15 working days** of a K15 search takes free of any entry made after the search (Land Charges Act 1972 s.11(5)). From 1 March to 3 April is more than 15 working days, so the protection will have run out and a **fresh search** is needed before completion. The 30-working-day priority period belongs to the OS1 search, which is for registered land."},
 {"t":"pre-completion","q":"Tilly is buying a leasehold flat under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition). The service charge is £2,190 a year (£6 a day), and the seller has paid it in advance up to 31 December. Completion is on 31 October.\n\nHow will the service charge be apportioned on the completion statement?","o":["£366 is credited to Tilly, reducing the amount she pays.","£372 is added to the amount Tilly pays.","Nothing, because service charges are never apportioned on a residential sale.","£366 is added to the amount Tilly pays.","£1,824 is credited to Tilly, reducing the amount she pays."],"a":3,"e":"The seller is treated as owning the property for the **whole of the completion day** (SC 6.3), so the buyer is responsible from 1 November to 31 December: 30 + 31 = **61 days × £6 = £366**. The seller has **paid in advance** for that period, so Tilly must **reimburse** the seller, and the £366 is **added** to the sum she pays. Counting 62 days wrongly includes the completion day."},
 {"t":"pre-completion","q":"Uma is buying a house for £350,000. She paid a 10% deposit on exchange. She is also paying £3,000 for contents, and she owes the seller £250 as an apportionment of a charge the seller paid in advance.\n\nWhat balance must be paid on completion?","o":["£318,250","£353,250","£317,750","£315,250","£312,250"],"a":0,"e":"Start with the price of £350,000, **deduct** the £35,000 deposit already paid, **add** the £3,000 contents price and **add** the £250 apportionment the buyer owes: £350,000 − £35,000 + £3,000 + £250 = **£318,250**. The other figures come from forgetting the deposit, getting the apportionment the wrong way round, leaving out the contents or deducting them."},
 {"t":"pre-completion","q":"Vince is selling his house for £220,000. The redemption statement from his lender shows £223,000 is needed to pay off his mortgage on completion. The buyer's solicitor asks Vince's solicitor for the usual undertaking to redeem the mortgage.\n\nWhat should Vince's solicitor do?","o":["Give the undertaking, because Vince has promised to pay the shortfall after completion.","Give the undertaking, provided the estate agent agrees to waive its commission.","Ask the buyer to take the property subject to the remaining part of the charge.","Give the undertaking and cover the shortfall from other money in the firm's client account.","Not give the undertaking unless Vince first provides the money to cover the shortfall."],"a":4,"e":"An undertaking binds the solicitor **personally**, so it should be given only if the solicitor will hold **enough money** to redeem. Here the sale price does not cover the mortgage (**negative equity**), so Vince must provide the shortfall before completion, or the lender must agree how its charge will be released. Using other clients' money would breach the SRA Accounts Rules, and the buyer cannot be expected to take subject to the charge."},
 {"t":"pre-completion","q":"Wanda is buying a registered freehold house with a mortgage. Her solicitor has made an official search with priority (OS1) of the seller's title and wonders whether to make a bankruptcy search against the seller as well.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["A bankruptcy search against the seller is essential in every purchase, because a bankrupt seller cannot pass good title to the buyer.","A full Land Charges search against the seller is needed instead.","No bankruptcy search against the seller is needed, as it would show on the OS1 result; the lender will want one against Wanda.","A company search against the seller is needed instead.","No pre-completion searches of any kind are needed for registered land."],"a":2,"e":"In registered land, a bankruptcy petition or order against the **seller** would appear on the register as a notice or restriction, so it would be revealed by the **OS1** result. The lender requires a **bankruptcy-only search (K16)** against its own **borrower**, Wanda. A full Land Charges search is for unregistered land, and a company search is needed only where a party is a company."},
 {"t":"pre-completion","q":"Xander is buying a house under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition) without amendment. Completion is fixed for Friday 28 June. His solicitor sends the draft transfer to the seller's solicitor on Monday 24 June.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["There is no breach, because the standard conditions set no timetable for the transfer.","Xander is in breach of the timetable, which required the draft transfer to be sent at least 12 working days before completion.","There is no breach, because under the standard conditions it is the seller's solicitor who is responsible for drafting the transfer.","The seller may rescind the contract immediately because the draft was late.","There is no breach, because the draft is due only five working days before completion."],"a":1,"e":"The buyer's solicitor drafts the transfer, and the standard conditions require the draft to be sent at least **12 working days** before completion, with the engrossment at least **5 working days** before. Sending it four days before completion breaches that timetable, and any delay it causes is relevant when working out **compensation for late completion**. A late draft does not on its own give the seller a right to rescind."},
 {"t":"completion","q":"Yasmin's purchase is to be completed under the Law Society Code for Completion by Post.\n\nIn what capacity does the seller's solicitor act on completion?","o":["As agent for both seller and buyer, because it holds the documents for both.","As the seller's agent only, with no duties to the buyer's solicitor.","As stakeholder for both parties, charging a fee for that service.","As the buyer's solicitor's agent, without charge, following its instructions.","As the buyer's lender's agent, because it receives the mortgage advance."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **Code for Completion by Post**, the **seller's solicitor acts as the buyer's solicitor's agent** for the purpose of completion, **without charge**, and must follow its instructions (for example, to check documents and date the transfer). It also gives undertakings about redeeming the seller's mortgages. It does not act for both parties, and it charges no agency fee."},
 {"t":"completion","q":"Zeb completed his purchase of a registered freehold house on 1 September. His solicitor's application to register the transfer reached HM Land Registry on 5 September, and the register was updated on 20 October.\n\nWhen did Zeb become the legal owner?","o":["On registration, which takes effect from 5 September; until then the seller held the legal title on bare trust for him.","On 1 September, when completion took place and the transfer was dated.","On exchange of contracts, when Zeb became bound to buy.","When the SDLT return was filed and the tax paid.","On 20 October, the day the register was actually changed, because legal title passes only when the register is updated, and not before."],"a":0,"e":"For registered land the **legal title passes only on registration** (LRA 2002 s.27), and registration takes effect from the time the **application was received**. During the registration gap, the seller holds the legal title on **bare trust** for the buyer, who owns the property in equity and is protected by the OS1 priority period. Completion alone passes legal title only in unregistered land."},
 {"t":"completion","q":"Ravi is buying a house in England. With the seller's agreement he moves in and takes possession on 1 March, before completion, which takes place on 1 April.\n\nWhen must the SDLT return be filed and the tax paid?","o":["Within 14 days after completion on 1 April.","Within 30 days after completion on 1 April.","Within 14 days after 1 March, because taking possession is substantial performance.","Within 30 days after 1 March, because taking possession is substantial performance.","Only once HM Land Registry has registered Ravi as owner."],"a":2,"e":"The SDLT return and payment are due within **14 days after the effective date**. The effective date is normally completion, but it is **earlier** if there is **substantial performance**, such as the buyer taking possession. Ravi took possession on 1 March, so the 14 days run from then. The 30-day period applies to Land Transaction Tax in Wales."},
 {"t":"completion","q":"On completion, the seller's solicitor sends the money to redeem the seller's mortgage, as it undertook to do. Two weeks later, the lender's electronic discharge has still not been confirmed, and the buyer's solicitor wants to register the purchase.\n\nWhen is the seller's solicitor's undertaking discharged?","o":["When the redemption money was sent to the lender.","On completion, when the transfer was dated.","When the buyer's solicitor applies to register the transfer.","Never, because the buyer must obtain the discharge directly from the lender, so the seller's solicitor has no further responsibility.","Only when evidence of discharge, such as confirmation of the electronic discharge or a signed DS1, is provided."],"a":4,"e":"The usual undertaking is to **redeem** the mortgage **and** to provide **evidence of discharge**. It is therefore discharged only when that evidence is supplied, for example confirmation that the lender has sent an electronic discharge to HM Land Registry or a signed **DS1**. Until then, the seller's solicitor should chase the lender, and the buyer's solicitor can rely on the undertaking."},
 {"t":"completion","q":"Sion Ltd has just been granted a 10-year lease of an office out of its landlord's registered freehold title.\n\nWhat must Sion Ltd's solicitor do to make sure Sion Ltd holds the legal lease?","o":["Nothing, because a lease of under 21 years takes effect as an overriding interest.","Apply to HM Land Registry to register the lease with its own title.","Only ask the landlord to enter a notice of the lease on the freehold title.","Register the lease only if a premium was paid for it.","Register the lease at Companies House within 21 days."],"a":1,"e":"A lease granted for **more than 7 years** out of a registered title is a disposition that must be **registered**, with its own title, before it operates at law (LRA 2002 s.27). Only leases of 7 years or less are overriding interests. A notice on the landlord's title follows from registration but is not enough on its own, and Companies House registration is for charges, not leases."},
 {"t":"completion","q":"Tamsin has completed her purchase of a leasehold flat with a mortgage. The lease requires notice of any assignment or charge to be given to the landlord within one month, with a fee.\n\nWho should give the notice?","o":["The seller's solicitor, as part of the seller's post-completion steps.","Tamsin's solicitor, by serving notice of the assignment and of the charge on the landlord with the fee.","HM Land Registry, which notifies the landlord automatically when it registers the transfer and the new charge on the title.","Nobody, because once the transfer is registered notice is not needed.","Only Tamsin's lender, which must notify the landlord of its own charge."],"a":1,"e":"Serving **notice of assignment** (and of any new **charge**) on the landlord is a **buyer's** post-completion step, usually done by the buyer's solicitor, who also acts for the lender. It must be done within the time and with the fee the lease requires, otherwise the buyer may be in breach of the lease. HM Land Registry does not notify landlords."},
 {"t":"completion","q":"Umar bought a house with vacant possession under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition). After completion he finds that the seller's lodger is still living there. The seller argues that the contract merged with the transfer on completion, so Umar has no claim.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["The seller is right: once completion has taken place the contract merges with the transfer and the contract can no longer be sued on.","Umar's only claim is in misrepresentation.","Umar's only claim is under the covenants for title in the transfer.","Umar has a claim only if he served a notice to complete before completion.","Umar can sue for breach of the vacant possession term, because completion does not end liability for outstanding obligations."],"a":4,"e":"At common law the contract **merges** with the transfer on completion, but the standard conditions displace this: completion does **not** cancel liability to perform any **outstanding obligation**. So Umar can sue for breach of the obligation to give **vacant possession**. Covenants for title concern the title, not occupation by a lodger."},
 {"t":"completion","q":"Vera's solicitor completes a sale under the Law Society Code for Completion by Post at 11am, as soon as the buyer's money arrives.\n\nWhen must Vera's solicitor send the transfer and other completion documents to the buyer's solicitor?","o":["Within 14 days, together with the evidence of discharge.","Only once the evidence of discharge of Vera's mortgage arrives.","When the buyer's application to register has been completed.","The same day, by first class post, document exchange or another agreed method.","On the next working day after the keys have been released."],"a":3,"e":"Under the Code, once completion has taken place the seller's solicitor must confirm it to the buyer's solicitor and send the **dated transfer**, any deeds and the evidence of discharge (or the undertaking to provide it) by first class post, DX or other agreed method **the same day**. Waiting for the discharge would delay the buyer's registration unnecessarily, since the undertaking covers it."},
 {"t":"completion","q":"Wes, an individual, completed his purchase of a registered freehold house in England on Monday 2 March with a mortgage. His solicitor's OS1 search was made on 20 February.\n\nWhich post-completion deadline falls first?","o":["Filing the SDLT return and paying the tax, within 14 days after completion.","Applying to register the transfer and charge, within the OS1 priority period.","Applying for first registration, within two months of completion.","Registering the charge at Companies House, within 21 days.","Giving notice of the purchase to the local authority for council tax."],"a":0,"e":"The **SDLT** return and payment are due **14 days** after the effective date, here 16 March. The **OS1** priority period is **30 working days** from 20 February, which runs into early April. First registration does not apply because the land is already registered, and Companies House registration applies only where the borrower is a **company**."},
 {"t":"completion","q":"Yolanda's purchase is being completed under the Law Society Code for Completion by Post. The estate agent holds the keys and asks when it may release them to Yolanda.\n\nWhat is the correct answer?","o":["On exchange of contracts, because the buyer is then bound to complete.","As soon as Yolanda's solicitor sends the payment reference for the completion money.","Once the seller's solicitor confirms that completion has taken place.","At 2pm on the completion date, whether or not the money has arrived.","Once Yolanda has paid the estate agent's fee."],"a":2,"e":"Completion happens when the seller's solicitor receives the money and treats the matter as completed. Only then should the keys be released, so the agent should wait for the seller's solicitor's **confirmation of completion**. Releasing keys earlier gives the buyer possession before the seller has been paid, and the buyer does not pay the seller's agent."},
 {"t":"delayed-completion","q":"A house is sold for £500,000 under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition) and a £50,000 deposit is paid. The contract rate is 7.3% a year. Completion is late. The buyer's total period of default is 7 days and the seller's is 3 days.\n\nWhat compensation is payable?","o":["The buyer pays £630.","The buyer pays £400.","The seller pays £300.","No compensation is payable, because both parties were at fault.","The buyer pays £360."],"a":4,"e":"Under the late completion condition the party with the **greater period of default** pays, for the period by which its default **exceeds** the other's: here the buyer, for 7 − 3 = **4 days**. When the buyer pays, the rate is applied to the price **less the deposit**: £450,000 × 7.3% ÷ 365 = £90 a day, so 4 days = **£360**. £400 wrongly uses the full price, and £630 ignores the seller's default."},
 {"t":"delayed-completion","q":"Arden Ltd agreed to buy an industrial unit under the Standard Commercial Property Conditions (3rd edition) without amendment. The seller completed 10 days late, through its own fault. Because of the delay, Arden Ltd had to pay an extra £4,000 in rent for its existing premises.\n\nWhat can Arden Ltd recover?","o":["Compensation at the contract rate on the full price for 10 days, which replaces any claim for its actual losses.","No contractual compensation, but common law damages for its proven losses, such as the £4,000.","Compensation at the contract rate on the price less the deposit for 10 days.","Nothing at all, because time was not of the essence.","Rescission of the contract and return of its deposit, as of right."],"a":1,"e":"Under the SCPC, contractual compensation for late completion is payable **only by the buyer**. A late **seller** pays no contractual compensation, so the buyer must claim **common law damages** and prove its loss, such as the extra rent. Late completion is still a breach even though time is not of the essence, but it does not give an immediate right to rescind."},
 {"t":"delayed-completion","q":"Completion of a house sale under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition) was due on Wednesday 3 June but did not happen because the buyer's funds were not ready. The seller, who is ready, able and willing to complete, serves a valid notice to complete on Thursday 4 June. Assume there are no bank holidays.\n\nWhat is the last day for completion under the notice?","o":["Sunday 14 June","Monday 15 June","Wednesday 17 June","Thursday 18 June","Friday 19 June"],"a":3,"e":"After a notice to complete, completion must take place within **10 working days**, **excluding the day the notice is given**, and time is then of the essence (SC 6.8.2). Counting from Friday 5 June as day 1, the tenth working day is **Thursday 18 June**. Sunday 14 June wrongly counts calendar days, and Wednesday 17 June wrongly includes the day of service."},
 {"t":"delayed-completion","q":"Bryn sold his own home and was due to complete his purchase of Cerys's house on the same day under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition). Cerys completed four days late through her own fault. Bryn spent £1,200 on a hotel and storage during the delay. Contractual compensation for the four days comes to £300.\n\nWhat can Bryn recover?","o":["The £300 compensation and a further £900 in damages.","The £300 compensation only, because compensation replaces damages.","The £300 compensation and a further £1,200 in damages.","Nothing, because time was not of the essence for the completion date.","Nothing yet, unless he terminates the contract."],"a":0,"e":"Late completion is a **breach of contract** even though time is not of the essence, so the innocent party can claim **damages** for its losses. Contractual compensation under the standard conditions is **in addition** to damages, but any damages for the same delay are **reduced** by the compensation paid. So Bryn's total recovery is £1,200: £300 compensation plus £900 damages."},
 {"t":"delayed-completion","q":"After contracts are exchanged, house prices in the area rise sharply. The seller, Viv, tells the buyer, Will, that she will not complete unless he pays more. Will wants the house, not money.\n\nWhat is Will's best remedy?","o":["Damages only, because the court will not force a seller to transfer her home against her will after prices have risen.","Rescission of the contract and return of his deposit.","Specific performance, because each piece of land is unique and damages would not be adequate.","Registration of the transfer himself, without Viv's signature.","A claim under the covenants for title implied in the transfer."],"a":2,"e":"**Specific performance** is an order forcing a party to complete. It is generally available for contracts for the sale of land because **land is unique**, so damages are not an adequate remedy. Rescission would end the contract, which is the opposite of what Will wants, and covenants for title only arise once a transfer has been made."},
 {"t":"delayed-completion","q":"Xavi agreed to buy a house under the Standard Conditions of Sale (5th edition) and paid a 10% deposit. The seller failed to complete, and Xavi, who was ready, able and willing, served a valid notice to complete. The seller has not completed within the time allowed.\n\nWhat can Xavi do?","o":["Rescind, but the seller keeps the deposit as compensation for the cancelled sale.","Claim damages only, because the contract cannot be ended.","Rescind, recover the deposit with accrued interest and claim damages, or instead seek specific performance.","Serve a second notice to complete, because the first notice only fixes a new completion date and does not allow rescission.","Wait six months and then rescind if the seller has still not completed."],"a":2,"e":"Where the **seller** fails to comply with a notice to complete, the buyer may **rescind**, recover the **deposit with accrued interest**, and claim **damages** under the standard conditions. Alternatively, the buyer can affirm the contract and seek **specific performance**. A seller in default never keeps the buyer's deposit."},
 {"t":"delayed-completion","q":"Yanis failed to comply with a valid notice to complete on his £300,000 purchase, for which he had paid a £30,000 deposit. The seller rescinded, kept the deposit and resold the house for £260,000, incurring £2,000 of extra costs.\n\nWhat can the seller recover from Yanis?","o":["Keep the £30,000 deposit and claim a further £12,000.","Keep the £30,000 deposit and claim a further £42,000.","Keep the £30,000 deposit, but make no further claim.","Return the deposit and claim £42,000 in damages.","Keep the £30,000 deposit and claim a further £40,000."],"a":0,"e":"The seller may **rescind, forfeit the deposit, resell and claim damages** under the standard conditions, but must **give credit** for the deposit against its loss. Its loss is the £40,000 shortfall on resale plus £2,000 costs = £42,000; after crediting the £30,000 deposit it can claim a further **£12,000**. Claiming the full £42,000 on top of the deposit would compensate it twice."},
 {"t":"delayed-completion","q":"Zara failed to complete her purchase after a notice to complete, and the seller rescinded and kept her 10% deposit. Zara asks whether the court can order the deposit to be returned to her.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["The deposit must be returned whenever the seller resells at a profit.","The court has no power to order the return of a deposit that has been forfeited under the express terms of a valid contract for sale.","The deposit must always be returned if the buyer acted in good faith.","The court has a discretion to order its return, but uses it only rarely, where keeping the deposit would be unconscionable.","The deposit can be returned only if it was more than 10% of the price."],"a":3,"e":"Under LPA 1925 s.49(2) the court **may**, if it thinks fit, order the **return of a deposit**. The courts use this power **sparingly**, only where it would be **unconscionable** for the seller to keep the deposit on the facts. Normally a buyer who fails to complete after a notice to complete loses the 10% deposit."},
 {"t":"delayed-completion","q":"Before completion, Abby discovers that the seller's reply on the TA6 that the property had 'never flooded' was untrue: the seller knew the ground floor had flooded twice. Abby relied on the reply when she exchanged.\n\nWhat remedy does Abby have?","o":["None until after completion, when she can claim damages.","She may rescind the contract for misrepresentation, recovering her deposit, and/or claim damages.","None, because of caveat emptor.","Only compensation under the errors and omissions condition, with no right to rescind.","Only specific performance of the contract with a reduced price."],"a":1,"e":"A false reply to an enquiry that induced the contract is a **misrepresentation**. The buyer may **rescind** (set the contract aside and recover the deposit) and/or claim **damages** under the Misrepresentation Act 1967. Caveat emptor does not protect a seller who gives untrue answers, and Abby need not complete first."},
 {"t":"delayed-completion","q":"Bella bought a registered freehold house from Clive with full title guarantee. After completion she discovers that a neighbour has a right of way across the garden, which Clive knew about but which was not on the register, was not disclosed and was not apparent on inspection. Bella did not know of it.\n\nWhat remedy does Bella have?","o":["None, because the contract merged with the transfer on completion, so Bella can no longer bring any claim about matters affecting the title.","She may rescind the transfer and recover the price.","Only a claim under the errors and omissions condition of the contract.","A claim only if Clive had given limited title guarantee.","A claim on the implied covenant that the property is free from incumbrances, other than those Clive could not reasonably know about."],"a":4,"e":"**Full title guarantee** implies a covenant that the property is free from charges, incumbrances and third-party rights, **other than** those the seller does not and could not reasonably know about (LP(MP)A 1994 s.3). Clive knew of the right of way and Bella did not, so she can sue on the covenant after completion. The covenants for title are designed to give remedies after completion, despite merger."},
 {"t":"lease-contents","q":"Cobb Ltd's office lease gives it the right to break on 29 September, on condition that it has 'fully complied with all the tenant's covenants in the lease'. Cobb Ltd served a valid break notice and has paid all its rent, but on the break date a few internal doors are damaged, in breach of its repairing covenant.\n\nWhat is the most likely outcome?","o":["The break is likely to fail, because break conditions are applied strictly.","The break is effective, because the breach is minor.","The break is effective, provided Cobb Ltd repairs the doors within a reasonable time afterwards.","The break is effective, but the landlord can claim damages for the disrepair instead.","The break is effective, because the notice itself was served correctly."],"a":0,"e":"Conditions attached to a break clause are applied **strictly**: if the condition is not met exactly on the break date, the break fails and the lease continues. A condition of **full compliance with all covenants** can be failed by even minor disrepair. That is why a tenant should negotiate conditions limited to paying the **principal rent** and giving **vacant possession**."},
 {"t":"lease-contents","q":"Dorrit Ltd's lease requires rent to be paid quarterly in advance on the usual quarter days. It has a tenant's break clause, and Dorrit Ltd validly breaks the lease on 10 April, having paid the full quarter's rent due on 25 March. The lease says nothing about refunds.\n\nCan Dorrit Ltd recover the rent for the period after 10 April?","o":["Yes, because a term requiring the landlord to refund the apportioned rent for the period after the break is implied into the lease.","Yes, because the landlord is not entitled to rent after the lease has ended.","No, because the lease contains no term requiring the landlord to refund rent paid in advance.","Yes, less a reasonable deduction for the landlord's administration costs.","Yes, by deducting it from the next service charge payment."],"a":2,"e":"Rent payable in advance on a quarter day is **not refundable** for the period after a break date unless the lease **expressly** says so; the courts will not imply a refund term (Marks and Spencer v BNP Paribas). A tenant negotiating a lease with a break clause should therefore insist on an express **refund** clause."},
 {"t":"lease-contents","q":"Eldon Ltd's lease allows it to break on 29 September by giving six months' written notice. Its notice, served in good time and in the correct form, mistakenly gives the break date as 28 September.\n\nIs the notice likely to be effective?","o":["No, because a break notice must comply exactly with the clause, so a notice giving the wrong date is always ineffective.","Only if the landlord agrees to accept it.","Only if Eldon Ltd serves a corrected notice within seven days.","Only once a court has declared it valid.","Yes, because a reasonable landlord would understand that Eldon Ltd meant to break on the correct date."],"a":4,"e":"A notice with an obvious minor error can still be effective if a **reasonable recipient** would understand what was intended (Mannai v Eagle Star). Here the landlord would understand that Eldon Ltd meant the permitted break date. By contrast, a notice served **late**, after the deadline, cannot be saved."},
 {"t":"lease-contents","q":"Fable Ltd's lease of a shop contains a covenant 'not to change the use of the premises without the landlord's consent'. Fable Ltd wants to change from a bookshop to a café. The landlord says it will consent only if Fable Ltd pays it £5,000.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["The landlord must act reasonably and cannot ask for any payment.","The landlord cannot demand a payment for its consent, although it is not obliged to act reasonably in deciding whether to consent.","The landlord can demand any sum it likes, because the covenant is not fully qualified and so the landlord has complete discretion over it.","The covenant is absolute, so Fable Ltd cannot change the use at all.","The landlord can demand a reasonable sum, but not £5,000."],"a":1,"e":"For a **qualified user covenant**, LTA 1927 s.19(3) prevents the landlord from charging a **fine** (a payment) for its consent, though it may ask for reasonable expenses. Unlike covenants against assignment or improvements, there is **no implied term** that consent cannot be unreasonably withheld, unless the lease says so."},
 {"t":"lease-contents","q":"Gale Estates is granting a 15-year lease of a shop with five-yearly open market rent reviews. It wants the user clause to allow only the sale of greeting cards, to keep a balanced mix of shops in its parade.\n\nWhat effect is the narrow user clause likely to have on the rent at review?","o":["It will increase the rent, because the premises will be more exclusive.","It will have no effect, because the valuer assesses the rent of the premises themselves, not the use to which they may be put.","The valuer must ignore the user clause on review.","It is likely to depress the rent, because fewer tenants would bid for premises with such a restricted use.","It will make the lease contracted out of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954."],"a":3,"e":"On an open market review, the valuer assumes a hypothetical letting on the **same terms** as the actual lease, other than rent. A **narrow user clause** shrinks the pool of potential tenants, so it tends to **depress the rent** on review. The landlord must balance control of the tenant mix against rental value."},
 {"t":"lease-contents","q":"Hollis Ltd is negotiating a 10-year lease of a whole building. The landlord will insure the building and recover the premium from Hollis Ltd as insurance rent.\n\nWhich package of protections should Hollis Ltd seek in the insurance provisions?","o":["A right to insure the building itself, alongside the landlord's policy, to make sure it is covered.","None, because if the building is destroyed the lease will be frustrated and the rent will stop.","Suspension of the rent until the building is reinstated, however long that takes, with no right for either party to end the lease in the meantime, whatever the delay.","A landlord's covenant to insure and reinstate, rent suspension while unusable, a right to terminate if not reinstated, and insured damage excluded from repair.","An obligation to pay any excess under the landlord's policy, whoever caused the damage."],"a":3,"e":"A tenant should make sure the landlord must **insure and reinstate**, that **rent is suspended** while insured damage makes the premises unusable (usually capped to match loss of rent cover), that it can **terminate** if reinstatement fails, and that insured damage is **excluded** from its repairing covenant. Frustration of a lease is very rare, so the tenant cannot rely on it, and double insurance wastes money."},
 {"t":"lease-contents","q":"Ivo Ltd spent £200,000, with the landlord's consent, installing a mezzanine floor in its warehouse. The lease has an open market rent review with the usual assumptions and disregards. The first review is now due.\n\nWhat effect does the mezzanine have on the reviewed rent?","o":["Its value is included, because it now forms part of the premises.","Its value is disregarded, so Ivo Ltd does not pay rent on an improvement it paid for.","Half its value is included, to share the benefit between landlord and tenant.","Its value is disregarded only if the lease obliged Ivo Ltd to install it.","The review cannot take place until the mezzanine is removed."],"a":1,"e":"The usual **disregards** on an open market review include the effect on rent of the tenant's **improvements**, as well as its occupation and goodwill. Without that disregard, the tenant would pay rent on value it created at its own cost. Improvements carried out under an **obligation** to the landlord are usually *not* disregarded, so that is the wrong way round."},
 {"t":"lease-contents","q":"Jarrow Ltd and its landlord are negotiating the rent review clause. They want any dispute decided quickly by a valuer who will use his own knowledge and investigation, whose decision will be final, and who could be sued in negligence if he gets it wrong.\n\nWho should the clause appoint to decide disputes?","o":["An independent expert.","An arbitrator.","The county court.","A mediator.","The landlord's own surveyor."],"a":0,"e":"An **independent expert** uses their own knowledge and investigations, their decision is **final**, and they can be sued in **negligence**. An **arbitrator** acts judicially on the evidence and submissions put by the parties, under the Arbitration Act 1996, with limited rights of appeal, and is not liable in negligence. A mediator cannot impose a decision."},
 {"t":"lease-contents","q":"Kara takes a lease of a shop with a user clause permitting use as a restaurant. After she has fitted it out, she discovers that the premises do not have planning permission for restaurant use and the council will not grant it.\n\nCan Kara claim against her landlord?","o":["Yes, because a landlord impliedly warrants that premises are fit for the permitted use.","Yes, under the landlord's covenant for quiet enjoyment.","Yes, because the landlord must obtain any planning permission needed for the use the lease permits before the term starts.","Yes, by rescinding the lease and recovering her fitting-out costs.","No, because a landlord gives no implied warranty that the permitted use is lawful under planning law."],"a":4,"e":"A landlord gives **no implied warranty** that the use permitted by the lease is lawful under planning law, or that the premises are fit for it (Hill v Harris). The tenant must check the **planning position** before taking the lease. The user clause controls what the landlord allows, not what the planning authority allows."},
 {"t":"lease-contents","q":"Lark Ltd, the anchor tenant in a shopping centre, closes its store halfway through the lease, in breach of a covenant to keep the premises open for trade during normal hours. The landlord wants a court order forcing Lark Ltd to reopen.\n\nWhat is the landlord most likely to obtain?","o":["An order for specific performance, because keep-open covenants are routinely enforced.","An injunction requiring Lark Ltd to trade, as a matter of course.","Damages, because the court will not usually order a tenant to keep trading.","Automatic forfeiture of the lease without any court proceedings.","Commercial Rent Arrears Recovery for the loss of footfall."],"a":2,"e":"The courts will not usually order **specific performance** of a **keep-open covenant**, because it would require continuing supervision and could force a business to trade at a loss (Co-operative Insurance v Argyll). The landlord's remedy is normally **damages**. CRAR is only for unpaid rent, not for losses from breach of other covenants."},
 {"t":"lease-dealings","q":"In 1992 a landlord granted a 35-year lease of a shop to Tern Ltd. In 2005 Tern Ltd assigned the lease to Usk Ltd with the landlord's consent. Usk Ltd has now failed to pay a quarter's rent. Within three months of the rent falling due, the landlord served a notice on Tern Ltd under section 17 of the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995.\n\nIs Tern Ltd liable for the unpaid rent?","o":["No, because Tern Ltd was released from all the tenant covenants when it lawfully assigned the lease with consent.","No, because it gave no authorised guarantee agreement.","No, because the notice had to be served within one month.","Yes, because as original tenant of a lease granted before 1996 it remains liable for the whole term.","Only for rent that fell due before the assignment."],"a":3,"e":"For a lease granted **before 1 January 1996**, the original tenant stays liable for the **whole term** through **privity of contract**, even after assigning. Its liability for a fixed charge such as rent depends on the landlord serving a **s.17 notice within 6 months** of the sum falling due, which was done here. Release on assignment applies only to leases granted on or after 1 January 1996."},
 {"t":"lease-dealings","q":"In 2015 a landlord granted a lease to Vale Ltd, which later assigned it with consent to Wren Ltd, giving an authorised guarantee agreement (AGA). Wren Ltd failed to pay the rent due on 25 March 2026. The landlord first serves a notice under section 17 of the Landlord and Tenant (Covenants) Act 1995 on Vale Ltd in November 2026.\n\nIs Vale Ltd liable for that rent?","o":["No, because the notice was not served within six months of the rent falling due.","Yes, because Vale Ltd guaranteed Wren Ltd under the AGA.","Yes, because an AGA removes the need for a section 17 notice.","Only for the interest on the late rent.","Only once the landlord has obtained judgment against Wren Ltd."],"a":0,"e":"To recover a fixed charge such as rent from a **former tenant** or its guarantor, the landlord must serve a **s.17 notice within 6 months** of the sum falling due. The rent fell due on 25 March 2026, so the deadline was 25 September 2026, and a notice in November is too late. The rule applies to former tenants under an AGA as well as to original tenants of old leases."},
 {"t":"lease-dealings","q":"Xeno Ltd holds a lease granted in 2018 that contains a qualified covenant against assignment. It assigns the lease to Yule Ltd without asking for the landlord's consent.\n\nWhat is the effect of the assignment?","o":["It is void, so Xeno Ltd remains the tenant.","It is effective, and Xeno Ltd is released from the tenant covenants.","It transfers the lease, but it is a breach of covenant, Xeno Ltd is not released and the landlord may be able to forfeit.","It is effective, but Yule Ltd is never liable on the tenant covenants.","It is effective, and the landlord must accept Yule Ltd as its tenant without any remedy, because the lease has already passed."],"a":2,"e":"An assignment made **without** a required consent still **transfers** the lease, but it is a **breach of covenant**, which may allow the landlord to **forfeit**. For a lease granted after 1995 it is an **excluded assignment**, so the outgoing tenant is **not released** (LTCA 1995 s.11). Yule Ltd, as the tenant in possession, is bound by the covenants."},
 {"t":"lease-dealings","q":"Zest Ltd holds a lease granted in 2019. The alienation clause says the landlord may withhold consent to an assignment unless the proposed assignee has annual net profits of at least three times the rent. Zest Ltd's proposed assignee is solvent but has net profits of only twice the rent, and the landlord refuses consent.\n\nIs the refusal lawful?","o":["No, because the assignee is solvent, so refusal is unreasonable.","No, because the statutory requirement of reasonableness overrides any condition in the lease.","Only if the landlord can also show that the assignee poses a real financial risk.","Only if the landlord first applies to court for a declaration.","Yes, because the landlord is relying on a pre-agreed condition in a lease granted after 1995."],"a":4,"e":"For leases granted on or after **1 January 1996**, LTA 1927 s.19(1A) allows the lease to set out **pre-agreed circumstances** or conditions for consent. A landlord who relies on them is **not** acting unreasonably. The assignee fails the agreed profits test, so the landlord may lawfully refuse."},
 {"t":"lease-dealings","q":"Abbot Ltd asks its landlord for consent to assign its shop lease to a financially strong retailer. The lease contains a qualified covenant against assignment. The landlord says it will consent only if Abbot Ltd first agrees to vary the lease to increase the rent.\n\nIs the landlord acting reasonably?","o":["Yes, because a landlord may always protect its own financial interests.","No, because it is seeking a collateral advantage that has nothing to do with the proposed assignment.","Yes, because the lease does not expressly say that the landlord's consent to an assignment cannot be unreasonably withheld.","Yes, because consent to assign is entirely at the landlord's discretion.","Yes, provided the landlord gives its reasons in writing."],"a":1,"e":"Under a qualified covenant, consent to assign is **not to be unreasonably withheld** (LTA 1927 s.19(1)(a)), even if the lease does not say so. A landlord may refuse only for reasons connected with the **landlord and tenant relationship** in the lease, such as the assignee's finances or use, not to obtain a **collateral advantage** like a higher rent. Giving reasons in writing does not make an unreasonable condition reasonable."},
 {"t":"lease-dealings","q":"A landlord granted a lease in 1990. In 2010 the lease was assigned from Brook Ltd to Crane Ltd, and in the licence to assign Crane Ltd gave the landlord a direct covenant to observe the tenant covenants for the rest of the term. In 2020 Crane Ltd assigned to Dove Ltd, which has now stopped paying rent.\n\nCan the landlord recover the arrears from Crane Ltd?","o":["No, because Crane Ltd was released when it assigned under the 1995 Act.","Yes, after serving a section 17 notice in time, because Crane Ltd's direct covenant makes it liable for the whole term.","No, because only the current tenant, Dove Ltd, is liable.","Only if Crane Ltd gave an authorised guarantee agreement.","Yes, without the need for any notice, because Crane Ltd gave a direct covenant and so is liable just like the original tenant."],"a":1,"e":"In an **old lease** (granted before 1996), an assignee is liable only while it holds the lease, unless it gave the landlord a **direct covenant**, which creates **privity of contract** for the **whole term**. Crane Ltd did so, so it remains liable. But the landlord must still serve a **s.17 notice** within 6 months of the arrears falling due, which applies to old and new leases."},
 {"t":"lease-dealings","q":"Dawn Ltd is taking an underlease of one floor of an office building from Echo Ltd, which holds the head lease of the whole building. Echo Ltd is currently in breach of the repairing covenant in the head lease.\n\nWhy should Dawn Ltd's solicitor be particularly concerned about the head lease?","o":["Because Dawn Ltd will be liable to pay the head lease rent to the head landlord.","Because the head landlord cannot take any action that affects an undertenant.","Because Dawn Ltd will automatically become the head landlord's direct tenant.","Because the underlease will survive any forfeiture of the head lease as an overriding interest, so Dawn Ltd need not worry about the head lease at all.","Because forfeiture of the head lease for Echo Ltd's breach would also end the underlease, subject to Dawn Ltd applying for relief."],"a":4,"e":"An underlease is carved out of the head lease, so if the **head lease is forfeited** the **underlease ends** with it, though the undertenant may apply to court for **relief**. The undertenant must therefore check the head lease and whether the tenant is complying with it. The undertenant does not pay the head rent, unless the head landlord serves notice under CRAR."},
 {"t":"lease-dealings","q":"Fenn Properties owns the freehold of a shop let on a lease granted in 2010. It sells the freehold and wants to be released from the landlord's covenants in the lease.\n\nHow can Fenn Properties obtain a release?","o":["It is released automatically when the sale completes.","It can never be released, because it was the original landlord.","It may serve notice on the tenant within four weeks of the sale, and is released if the tenant does not object in time or the court agrees.","It is released only if the tenant signs a deed of release.","It is released automatically when the buyer registers the transfer at HM Land Registry, because the buyer then takes over all the landlord covenants."],"a":2,"e":"For a lease granted on or after 1 January 1996, a landlord who sells its reversion is **not released automatically**. It may apply for release by serving **notice** on the tenant within **4 weeks** of the sale; it is released if the tenant does not object within **4 weeks**, or if the court decides release is reasonable (LTCA 1995 ss.6-8). A tenant's deed is not required."},
 {"t":"lease-dealings","q":"Gilt Ltd wants to assign its shop lease to Hart Ltd. The lease contains a qualified covenant against assignment, and the landlord is willing to consent.\n\nWho normally prepares the licence to assign, and who pays the landlord's costs?","o":["The landlord's solicitor prepares it, and Gilt Ltd, as outgoing tenant, normally pays the landlord's reasonable costs.","Hart Ltd's solicitor prepares it, because Hart Ltd is giving the covenants, and the landlord pays its own costs of the consent.","Gilt Ltd's solicitor prepares it, and Hart Ltd pays everyone's costs.","HM Land Registry issues it on a prescribed form, at no cost.","No licence is needed, because consent cannot be unreasonably withheld."],"a":0,"e":"The **licence to assign** is the landlord's formal consent, usually a deed between the landlord, the outgoing tenant and the assignee. It is drafted by the **landlord's solicitor**, and the **tenant** normally pays the landlord's reasonable costs. Consent still has to be obtained even though it cannot be unreasonably withheld."},
 {"t":"lease-dealings","q":"Ivy Ltd wants to take a lease of a retail unit that the landlord has not yet finished refurbishing. Both parties want to be legally committed now, with the lease to start once the works are done.\n\nWhat is the best way to structure the deal?","o":["Grant the lease now, with the term starting immediately, and let the landlord finish the refurbishment works later while Ivy Ltd pays full rent.","Give Ivy Ltd an option to take a lease, which it may exercise once the works are finished.","Sign binding heads of terms under the Code for Leasing Business Premises.","Enter into an agreement for lease setting out the works, a long-stop date and the agreed form of lease, to be completed after the works.","Use a contract incorporating the Standard Conditions of Sale for a freehold."],"a":3,"e":"An **agreement for lease** binds both parties now to grant and take a lease later. It is used where the landlord must first carry out **works**, and sets out the works, a **long-stop date**, the agreed form of lease and when the lease will be completed (for example, after **practical completion**). Heads of terms are not normally binding, and an option binds only the landlord."},
 {"t":"lease-remedies","q":"Jessop Ltd lets a building comprising a ground-floor shop and a flat above, under one written lease. The tenant's manager lives in the flat. The tenant owes three months' rent.\n\nCan the landlord use Commercial Rent Arrears Recovery (CRAR)?","o":["Yes, for all the arrears, because the lease is a business lease.","Yes, but only for the rent attributable to the shop.","No, because CRAR is not available where any part of the premises is let or occupied as a dwelling.","Yes, after giving 14 days' notice of enforcement.","Yes, but only with a court order."],"a":2,"e":"CRAR is available only for leases of **purely commercial** premises. If **any part** of the premises is let or occupied as a **dwelling**, CRAR cannot be used at all (Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act 2007 s.75). The landlord would need another remedy, such as a debt claim or forfeiture."},
 {"t":"lease-remedies","q":"Kendal Ltd's tenant of an office building, let under a written lease of purely commercial premises, owes £8,000 of rent, £3,000 of service charge and £1,000 of insurance rent. The lease reserves all three sums as rent. The landlord plans to use Commercial Rent Arrears Recovery (CRAR).\n\nHow much can be recovered using CRAR?","o":["£12,000, because the lease reserves all the sums as rent.","£11,000, being the rent and service charge.","Nothing, because the landlord must first obtain judgment.","£9,000, being the rent and insurance rent.","£8,000, together with any interest and VAT on it."],"a":4,"e":"CRAR can recover only **pure rent**: the amount payable for possession and use of the premises, plus **interest** and **VAT** on it (TCEA 2007 s.76). Service charge and insurance rent are excluded **even if the lease calls them rent**. No court order is needed, but the landlord must give at least 7 clear days' notice of enforcement."},
 {"t":"lease-remedies","q":"Lyle Ltd's tenant is 30 days late paying rent. The lease has a forfeiture clause allowing re-entry if rent is 21 days in arrears 'whether formally demanded or not'. The premises are a shop, empty at night. The landlord wants the premises back as quickly as possible.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["The landlord may forfeit by peaceable re-entry, without serving a section 146 notice, though the tenant may apply for relief.","The landlord must first serve a section 146 notice and give the tenant time to pay.","The landlord can forfeit only by court proceedings, because the premises are a shop and peaceable re-entry is limited to offices.","The landlord must first make a formal demand for the rent at the premises.","The landlord cannot forfeit for rent and must use Commercial Rent Arrears Recovery."],"a":0,"e":"For **non-payment of rent**, no **s.146 notice** is needed, and the words 'whether formally demanded or not' remove the need for a formal demand. Where nobody lives at the premises, the landlord may forfeit by **peaceable re-entry**, for example by changing the locks at night. The tenant can still apply for **relief**, which is usually granted if it pays all arrears and costs."},
 {"t":"lease-remedies","q":"Moss Ltd holds a 10-year lease with five years left. Its landlord serves a section 146 notice for breach of the repairing covenant, intending to forfeit.\n\nWhat right does Moss Ltd have?","o":["None, because the lease has more than three years left.","It can apply for relief only after the landlord has re-entered.","It can serve a counter-notice at any time within six months, which brings the landlord's claims for forfeiture and damages to an end.","It can serve a counter-notice within 28 days, after which the landlord needs the court's permission to forfeit or claim damages.","None, because a section 146 notice is not needed for disrepair."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **Leasehold Property (Repairs) Act 1938**, where a lease was granted for **7 years or more** and has **3 years or more** left, the s.146 notice must tell the tenant it may serve a **counter-notice** within **28 days**. If it does, the landlord needs the **court's permission** to forfeit or sue for damages, and must prove one of the statutory grounds."},
 {"t":"lease-remedies","q":"Nash Ltd's lease of an old warehouse has just ended, leaving the building in serious disrepair in breach of the tenant's repairing covenant. The landlord intends to demolish the warehouse and build flats, and sues for the £150,000 cost of the repairs.\n\nWhat damages is the landlord likely to recover?","o":["The full £150,000, because that is the cost of putting the building into the state of repair required by the tenant's repairing covenant.","Little or nothing, because damages are capped at the fall in value of the landlord's interest, and the building is to be demolished.","The £150,000 cost of repairs plus the rent lost while the repairs are done.","One year's rent, which is the statutory cap for disrepair claims.","Nothing, because a claim for disrepair cannot be made once the lease has ended."],"a":1,"e":"Damages for breach of a tenant's repairing covenant are **capped at the diminution in value of the reversion** (LTA 1927 s.18(1)), meaning the fall in value of the landlord's interest caused by the disrepair. If the landlord is going to **demolish** the building, the disrepair may not reduce its value at all, so damages may be **nil**. A claim can be made at the end of the lease."},
 {"t":"lease-remedies","q":"Oriel Ltd's tenant has been using a shop as a hot food takeaway, in breach of the user covenant, since January. The landlord knew about it and accepted the March quarter's rent. The breach is still continuing in June, and the landlord now wants to forfeit.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["The right to forfeit has been permanently waived by accepting the March rent.","The right has been waived unless the rent was accepted 'without prejudice'.","There was no waiver, because waiver applies only when rent is demanded, not accepted.","The landlord cannot forfeit for breach of a user covenant.","The landlord can still forfeit, because the breach is continuing, so the waiver covers only the past period."],"a":4,"e":"Accepting rent with knowledge of a breach **waives** the right to forfeit for it. But where the breach is **continuing**, such as an unauthorised use, the waiver covers only the period before the acceptance, and the right to forfeit **revives** as the breach continues. A once-and-for-all breach, such as an unauthorised assignment, is waived permanently, and 'without prejudice' wording does not prevent waiver."},
 {"t":"lease-remedies","q":"Pryce Ltd's lease is guaranteed by its director, Quentin. Later the landlord and Pryce Ltd agree to vary the lease to increase the rent substantially. Quentin is not asked to consent, and the guarantee says nothing about variations. Pryce Ltd now cannot pay the rent.\n\nWhat is Quentin's position?","o":["He is liable for the full increased rent, because he guaranteed all the tenant's obligations.","He is liable only for the rent as it was before the variation.","He is likely to have been released from his guarantee by the variation made without his consent.","He is liable only once the landlord has forfeited the lease.","He is liable only if the landlord serves a section 17 notice on him within six months."],"a":2,"e":"A guarantor may be **released** by a material **variation** of the lease made **without its consent**, unless the guarantee allows variations. That is why landlords draft guarantees to permit them. The rule that a former tenant is not liable for increases caused by later variations (LTCA 1995 s.18) is a different rule, and a s.17 notice is about former tenants and their guarantors."},
 {"t":"lease-remedies","q":"Rook Ltd, the tenant of a shop with four years of its lease left, hands the keys back to its landlord and leaves. The landlord accepts the keys and, a month later, grants a new lease of the shop to another tenant. No deed was signed.\n\nWhat is the status of Rook Ltd's lease?","o":["It has ended by surrender by operation of law.","It continues, because a surrender must be made by deed.","It continues, because the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954 procedure was not followed.","It has ended by merger.","It has been forfeited, because the tenant abandoned the premises."],"a":0,"e":"A lease can end by **surrender by operation of law**, without a deed, where both parties act in a way that is inconsistent with the lease continuing. Here the tenant handed back the keys and the landlord accepted them and **re-let** the premises. The s.38A procedure is needed only for an **agreement to surrender in the future**, not an immediate surrender."},
 {"t":"lease-remedies","q":"Sable Ltd's landlord is in breach of its covenant to repair the roof of the building, and water is coming into Sable Ltd's shop. The lease says rent is payable 'without any deduction or set-off'. Sable Ltd is considering doing the repairs and deducting the cost from its rent.\n\nWhat is the best advice?","o":["It may do the repairs and deduct the cost from its rent, because the landlord is in breach and the no set-off wording is unenforceable.","It may stop paying rent altogether until the landlord repairs the roof.","It may forfeit the landlord's interest in the building.","It should not deduct the cost, because the lease excludes set-off, but it can claim damages or specific performance.","It has no remedy while it remains in occupation."],"a":3,"e":"A tenant can sometimes do the landlord's repairs and **set off** the cost against rent, but not where the lease requires rent to be paid **without deduction or set-off**, as commercial leases usually do. Sable Ltd should instead claim **damages** for the landlord's breach or seek **specific performance** of the repairing covenant, which is more readily granted against a landlord. Withholding rent risks forfeiture."},
 {"t":"lease-remedies","q":"Tolly Ltd has left its office premises and stopped paying rent. It owes £9,000. The landlord holds a £15,000 rent deposit under a rent deposit deed that allows it to draw on the deposit for unpaid rent.\n\nWhat is the landlord's quickest remedy?","o":["Sue Tolly Ltd for the debt before touching the deposit.","Draw £9,000 from the rent deposit under the deed and require Tolly Ltd to top it up.","Use Commercial Rent Arrears Recovery, which must be used before the deposit.","Forfeit the lease before it can use the deposit.","Hold the deposit untouched until the lease ends."],"a":1,"e":"A **rent deposit** is security for the tenant's obligations. Under the **rent deposit deed** the landlord can draw on it for arrears, quickly and without court proceedings, and can usually require the tenant to **top it up**. The landlord does not have to sue, use CRAR or forfeit first."},
 {"t":"lta1954","q":"Ivy runs a pop-up shop under a five-month fixed-term tenancy. The tenancy contains no provision for renewal, and Ivy has never occupied the premises before. Ivy wants to know whether she will have a right to a new lease when it ends.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["She is protected by the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954, because she occupies the premises for business purposes and the lease was not contracted out.","She is not protected: a term of six months or less is excluded unless it can be extended or she has occupied for over 12 months.","She is protected, because the landlord did not serve a warning notice to contract out.","She is protected once the term ends and the tenancy continues.","She is protected, because she has exclusive possession of the premises."],"a":1,"e":"Fixed-term tenancies of **6 months or less** are excluded from the 1954 Act, unless the tenancy contains a provision for renewal or extension beyond 6 months, or the tenant (and any predecessor in the same business) has already occupied for **more than 12 months** (s.43(3)). Neither exception applies, so Ivy has no security of tenure, without any need to contract out."},
 {"t":"lta1954","q":"Jet Ltd holds a 10-year lease of a shop, which was not contracted out of the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954. Two years before it ends, Jet Ltd sublets the whole shop to Kite Ltd and moves its own business elsewhere.\n\nWill Jet Ltd have a right to renew its lease when it ends?","o":["Yes, because the lease was not contracted out.","Yes, because Kite Ltd's business occupation of the shop is treated as occupation by Jet Ltd, its immediate landlord.","Yes, because it occupied for business purposes for most of the term.","No, because it no longer occupies the premises for its own business, although Kite Ltd may be protected.","No, because subletting automatically forfeits the lease."],"a":3,"e":"The Act protects a tenancy only where the premises are **occupied by the tenant** for its business (s.23). If the tenant **sublets the whole** and moves out, it is no longer in occupation and loses protection; the **subtenant**, which is the one in occupation, may be protected instead. Occupation by a separate unconnected company does not count as the tenant's occupation."},
 {"t":"lta1954","q":"Lark Ltd's protected business lease expires on 29 September 2027. On 1 August 2026 its landlord serves a section 25 notice specifying 29 September 2027 as the termination date.\n\nIs the notice valid?","o":["No, because it was given more than 12 months before the termination date it specifies.","Yes, because the termination date is not earlier than the contractual expiry date.","Yes, because a section 25 notice may be served at any time during the lease.","No, because the termination date must be at least six months after the contractual expiry date.","Yes, unless the tenant objects within two months."],"a":0,"e":"A **s.25 notice** must be given **not less than 6 nor more than 12 months** before the termination date it specifies, and that date cannot be earlier than the contractual expiry date. From 1 August 2026 to 29 September 2027 is almost 14 months, so the notice is **invalid**. The landlord should serve a fresh notice between 29 September 2026 and 29 March 2027."},
 {"t":"lta1954","q":"Mint Ltd holds a 15-year protected business lease. It serves a valid section 26 request for a new tenancy. The landlord does nothing for three months and then says it will oppose renewal because it intends to redevelop.\n\nCan the landlord oppose renewal?","o":["Yes, because redevelopment is a mandatory ground that can be raised at any time.","Yes, provided it applies to court before the date proposed in the request.","Yes, because the landlord has six months to serve a counter-notice.","Yes, but only if it pays the tenant double compensation.","No, because it did not serve a counter-notice stating its grounds within two months of the request."],"a":4,"e":"After a tenant's **s.26 request**, a landlord who wishes to oppose renewal must serve a **counter-notice** within **2 months**, stating its grounds. If it does not, it **cannot oppose** the grant of a new tenancy, although it can still argue about the terms. The tenant has then secured its right to renew."},
 {"t":"lta1954","q":"Nook Ltd's landlord has served a hostile section 25 notice relying on ground (f), redevelopment. At the hearing, the landlord's director admits that the works are mainly intended to get rid of Nook Ltd and that the landlord would not carry them out if Nook Ltd left voluntarily.\n\nWill the landlord succeed on ground (f)?","o":["Yes, because it has a firm intention to carry out the works.","Yes, because ground (f) is mandatory once the works are proved to be substantial.","No, because its intention to redevelop is conditional on the tenant claiming a new tenancy.","No, because intention is judged at the date the notice was served.","Yes, provided it pays Nook Ltd compensation."],"a":2,"e":"For ground (f), the landlord's intention to redevelop must exist **whether or not the tenant claims a new tenancy**. An intention that depends on the tenant staying (the 'if the tenant goes, I won't bother' test) does not satisfy the ground (S Franses v Cavendish Hotel). Intention is judged at the date of the **hearing**, not when the notice was served."},
 {"t":"lta1954","q":"Oak Ltd has occupied its premises for its business for 16 years under a protected lease. Its landlord, who has owned the freehold for 20 years, successfully opposes renewal under ground (g), own occupation. The rateable value of the premises is £30,000, and the multiplier is 1.\n\nHow much compensation is Oak Ltd entitled to?","o":["£60,000","£30,000","£90,000","Nothing, because ground (g) is not a no-fault ground.","Nothing, because the landlord has owned the freehold for more than five years."],"a":0,"e":"A tenant refused a new lease on one of the no-fault grounds **(e), (f) or (g)** is entitled to compensation of the **rateable value × the multiplier (1)**. Because Oak Ltd has occupied for the whole of the **14 years** before the termination date, compensation is **doubled**: 2 × £30,000 = **£60,000**. The five-year rule limits when a landlord can rely on ground (g), not compensation."},
 {"t":"lta1954","q":"Pine Ltd's protected business lease contains a clause excluding any compensation under the Landlord and Tenant Act 1954. Pine Ltd has occupied for its business for seven years. The landlord successfully opposes renewal on ground (f). The rateable value is £25,000, and the multiplier is 1.\n\nWhat compensation will Pine Ltd receive?","o":["Nothing, because the lease validly excludes compensation.","£50,000, because the exclusion clause is void.","Nothing, because ground (f) does not carry compensation.","£25,000, because the exclusion is ineffective where the tenant has occupied for five years or more.","£12,500, because the exclusion clause halves the compensation."],"a":3,"e":"A lease may exclude compensation, **except** where the tenant (or its predecessors in the same business) has occupied for business purposes for **5 years or more** (s.38(2)-(3)). Pine Ltd has occupied for seven years, so the exclusion does not apply and it receives the normal compensation of **1 × rateable value = £25,000**. Double compensation needs 14 years' occupation."},
 {"t":"lta1954","q":"Quay Ltd's landlord opposes renewal of its protected business lease under ground (b), persistent delay in paying rent. Quay Ltd paid late several times three years ago, during a cash-flow problem, but has paid on time ever since.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court will approach this ground?","o":["The landlord will succeed automatically once it proves any late payment.","The court will ask whether, because of the late payments, Quay Ltd ought not to have a new tenancy, and may still grant one.","The landlord will succeed, but must pay Quay Ltd compensation.","The ground fails automatically, because the late payments happened more than two years before the landlord served its notice.","The landlord will succeed only if it also offers suitable alternative accommodation."],"a":1,"e":"Grounds **(a), (b), (c) and (e)** are **discretionary**: even if the landlord proves the facts, the court must also decide that the tenant **ought not** to have a new tenancy because of them. The tenant's improved record since the problem is relevant to that decision. No compensation is payable where the landlord succeeds on a fault ground such as (b)."},
 {"t":"lta1954","q":"Reed Ltd is renewing its protected business lease. Under the current lease the landlord is responsible for repairing the structure. The landlord proposes that the new lease should make Reed Ltd pay for all structural repairs through the service charge, and offers a slightly lower rent in return. Reed Ltd refuses, and the court must decide the terms.\n\nWhat is the court most likely to do?","o":["Adopt the landlord's proposal, because the court follows current market practice.","Adopt the landlord's proposal, because the landlord is free to set the terms of a new lease, subject only to the rent being reasonable.","Adopt the landlord's proposal, because a rent reduction has been offered.","Grant a new lease of no more than five years on the landlord's terms.","Keep the existing repairing terms, because the party seeking a change must justify it and the old lease is the starting point."],"a":4,"e":"Under s.35, the court decides the other terms of a new lease having regard to the **current tenancy**, which is the starting point. A party proposing a **change** must justify it as fair and reasonable, and shifting the risk of structural repair costs to the tenant is a fundamental change the courts have refused even with a rent reduction (O'May). The maximum term the court can order is 15 years."},
 {"t":"lta1954","q":"Sage Ltd's protected business lease expires on 24 June. It is now 1 May. Sage Ltd has decided not to renew and plans to move out before 24 June. Neither party has served any notice.\n\nWhat must Sage Ltd do to make sure its liability ends on 24 June?","o":["It must serve a section 27(1) notice, but it is now too late to do so, so the tenancy will continue beyond 24 June.","It must serve a section 26 request stating that it does not want a new lease.","It need only stop occupying the premises for its business by 24 June, which ends the tenancy on that date.","It must wait for the landlord to serve a section 25 notice.","It will remain liable for rent until the court makes an order ending the tenancy."],"a":2,"e":"A tenant that does not want to renew can serve a **s.27(1) notice** at least 3 months before contractual expiry, but it can also end the tenancy simply by **ceasing to occupy** for business purposes by the **contractual expiry date** (s.27(1A)). If it stayed in occupation past 24 June, the tenancy would continue and it would need to give 3 months' notice under s.27(2)."},
 {"t":"planning","q":"Tegan owns a house in Cardiff. In June 2022, without planning permission, she built a large detached garage, which was substantially completed that month. The local planning authority has just discovered it in November 2026.\n\nCan the authority take enforcement action?","o":["Yes, because the time limit for operational development is 10 years.","Yes, because there is no time limit for building works without planning permission.","Yes, because the 4-year rule was abolished across England and Wales in April 2024.","Yes, until June 2032, because the breach is less than 10 years old.","No, because in Wales operational development becomes immune four years after substantial completion."],"a":4,"e":"In **Wales**, the time limit for enforcement against **operational development** is still **4 years** from substantial completion. The garage was completed in June 2022, so it became **immune** in June 2026. The 10-year rule introduced on 25 April 2024 applies only in **England**."},
 {"t":"planning","q":"In February 2022, Uri converted a barn in England into a single house and moved in, without planning permission. The local planning authority has just discovered it in November 2026.\n\nCan the authority take enforcement action against the change of use?","o":["Yes, because all breaches of planning control in England are now subject to a single 10-year time limit for enforcement, whenever they occurred.","Yes, because the change of use was deliberately concealed.","No, because the change of use to a single dwelling took place before 25 April 2024, so the 4-year rule applies and it is now immune.","Yes, because a change of use to a dwelling has no time limit.","No, because a change of use of a farm building is never development."],"a":2,"e":"In England the 4-year limit for a **change of use to a single dwellinghouse** was replaced by **10 years** on **25 April 2024**, but the old **4-year** rule still applies where the change took place before that date. Uri's change in February 2022 became immune in February 2026. There is nothing in the facts to suggest deliberate concealment."},
 {"t":"planning","q":"Vik owns a shop in a high street in England, used within Use Class E. He wants to change the use to a hot food takeaway. He will make no external alterations.\n\nDoes Vik need planning permission?","o":["Yes, because a hot food takeaway is a sui generis use, so the change is a material change of use.","No, because selling hot food is within Class E like other shops and restaurants.","No, because no building works are involved.","No, because a change of use is development only if it lasts more than 28 days a year.","Yes, but only building regulation approval, not planning permission."],"a":0,"e":"**Hot food takeaways** are **sui generis** (in no use class), so changing to or from that use is a **material change of use** and therefore development needing planning permission, unless the GPDO grants it. Restaurants and cafés are within Class E, which is the common trap. A change of use can be development even with no building works."},
 {"t":"planning","q":"Wendy owns a Grade II listed house in England. She wants to remove an internal wall and put up new internal partitions. The works will not affect the exterior of the building.\n\nWhat consents does she need?","o":["Planning permission, because all works to a listed building, inside or out, are treated as development needing permission.","No consent at all, because internal works are not development.","Planning permission and building regulation approval only.","Listed building consent, because although the works are not development they affect the character of a listed building.","Only a lawful development certificate."],"a":3,"e":"Works affecting only the **interior** of a building are **not development** (TCPA 1990 s.55(2)), so no planning permission is needed. But works that affect the character of a **listed building**, inside or out, need **listed building consent**, and doing them without it is a **criminal offence**. Building regulation approval may also be needed for structural work."},
 {"t":"planning","q":"Xenia owns a detached house in England. She plans to convert her detached garage into a separate one-bedroom flat to let to a tenant, with no external alterations.\n\nDoes the conversion need planning permission?","o":["No, because using buildings in the garden of a house is always incidental to the enjoyment of the house, whoever happens to live in them.","Yes, because creating a separate dwelling is not incidental to the enjoyment of the house and amounts to a material change of use.","No, because there are no external alterations.","No, because the use stays within Class C3 dwellinghouses.","Only if the flat is let for more than six months."],"a":1,"e":"Using buildings within the **curtilage** of a house for purposes **incidental** to enjoying the house (for example a home gym or hobby room) is not development. Using the garage as a **separate dwelling** let to someone else is not incidental: it creates a new planning unit, which is a **material change of use** needing planning permission."},
 {"t":"planning","q":"Yasmin owns a house in a conservation area in England. She wants to build a small single-storey rear extension that would normally be permitted development under the General Permitted Development Order. The council has made an Article 4 direction removing householder permitted development rights in the area.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["She can build without an application, because the extension is within the size limits.","She needs express planning permission, because the Article 4 direction removes the permitted development rights.","She needs only building regulation approval.","She needs a lawful development certificate, which the council must grant.","She can build without permission, because an Article 4 direction applies only to commercial property, not to houses."],"a":1,"e":"The GPDO grants deemed permission for listed classes of development, but an **Article 4 direction** can **remove** specified permitted development rights in a particular area. Where it applies, the owner must apply for **express planning permission**. Building regulation approval is a separate requirement."},
 {"t":"planning","q":"Zane runs a car repair workshop in England under a planning permission with a condition limiting opening hours to 8am-6pm. He regularly opens until 9pm. The council serves a breach of condition notice requiring him to comply, and he wants to appeal.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["He can appeal to the Secretary of State, which suspends the notice.","He can appeal to the magistrates' court, which suspends the notice.","The notice has no effect until the council also serves an enforcement notice, which he can then appeal to the Secretary of State.","There is no right of appeal against a breach of condition notice, and failing to comply is a criminal offence.","He can ignore the notice, because the council must obtain an injunction first."],"a":3,"e":"A **breach of condition notice** requires compliance with a planning **condition**, and there is **no right of appeal** against it; non-compliance is a **criminal offence**. By contrast, an **enforcement notice** can be appealed to the Secretary of State, which suspends it. This makes a breach of condition notice an effective tool for clear breaches of conditions."},
 {"t":"planning","q":"In January 2024 Amir was granted full planning permission in England to build a house on a plot he owns. The permission says nothing about when development must begin. It is now November 2026, and he has not started work.\n\nWhat is the position?","o":["He must begin the development by January 2027 or the permission will lapse.","The permission lasts indefinitely, because no time limit was stated.","The permission has already lapsed, because work had to begin within two years.","He has five years from the grant to begin the development.","He must complete the house by January 2027."],"a":0,"e":"A **full planning permission** is subject to a statutory condition that development must **begin** within **3 years** of the grant, unless the permission says otherwise (TCPA 1990 s.91). The permission was granted in January 2024, so Amir must start by January 2027; he does not have to finish by then."},
 {"t":"planning","q":"Bea is buying a house in England. The seller's TA6 shows a loft conversion completed in 2023 with no building regulation completion certificate. The seller has already asked the council's building control department about it.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Bea?","o":["Obtain building regulation indemnity insurance before exchange and proceed.","Proceed, because the council can only take any enforcement action within 12 months of the work being completed, and that period has now passed.","Proceed, because building regulations are a matter for the seller only.","Proceed, because planning permission covers building regulation compliance.","Ask the seller to obtain a regularisation certificate and get a survey, as insurance is unlikely to be available and the council can still act."],"a":4,"e":"In England the council can serve a notice requiring removal or alteration of non-compliant work within **10 years** of completion (extended from 12 months by the Building Safety Act 2022). **Indemnity insurance** is normally unavailable once the council has been **contacted**. The best route is a **regularisation certificate** and a survey, with the issue reported to the lender; planning permission and building regulation approval are separate."},
 {"t":"planning","q":"Carl obtained planning permission in England to build a hay barn. Instead he built a house disguised as a barn, lived in it for five years without the council knowing, and then claimed the house was immune from enforcement.\n\nWhat is the most likely outcome?","o":["The house is immune, because the time limit for enforcement had passed before the council found out, and the reason for its delay is irrelevant.","The house is immune, because he had planning permission for a building on the site.","Carl cannot rely on the time limit, because he deliberately concealed the breach, and the council may seek a planning enforcement order.","The council can take action only by bringing criminal proceedings for the deception.","The house is immune, provided Carl applies for a lawful development certificate."],"a":2,"e":"An owner who **deliberately conceals** a breach of planning control cannot rely on the enforcement **time limits** to make it lawful (Welwyn Hatfield v Secretary of State). The council can also apply for a **planning enforcement order**, allowing enforcement outside the normal time limit where a breach was concealed (TCPA 1990 s.171BA). A lawful development certificate would not be granted on these facts."},
 {"t":"tax","q":"Mia is a first-time buyer purchasing a house in England for £520,000 to live in. Assume the following SDLT residential rates: 0% up to £125,000; 2% on £125,001 to £250,000; 5% on £250,001 to £925,000. First-time buyers' relief gives 0% up to £300,000 and 5% from £300,001 to £500,000, but is not available if the price exceeds £500,000.\n\nHow much SDLT is payable?","o":["£11,000","£1,000","£10,000","£16,000","Nothing"],"a":3,"e":"First-time buyers' relief is **lost entirely** when the price is **over £500,000**, so the standard rates apply. SDLT = 0% on £125,000 + 2% on £125,000 (£2,500) + 5% on £270,000 (£13,500) = **£16,000**. £11,000 wrongly applies the relief, and £1,000 wrongly charges only the slice above £500,000."},
 {"t":"tax","q":"Noah owns his home in England and buys a flat to let out for £240,000. Assume the SDLT residential rates are 0% up to £125,000 and 2% on £125,001 to £250,000, and that a 5% surcharge applies on top of each band to purchases of additional dwellings.\n\nHow much SDLT is payable?","o":["£2,300","£14,300","£12,000","£16,800","£6,250"],"a":1,"e":"Noah will own **more than one dwelling** and is not replacing his main residence, so the **higher rates** apply: each band is increased by 5%. SDLT = 5% on £125,000 (£6,250) + 7% on £115,000 (£8,050) = **£14,300**. £2,300 ignores the surcharge, and £12,000 wrongly charges 5% on the whole price instead of using slices."},
 {"t":"tax","q":"Olu is buying a new main home in England before he has sold his old one, so at the end of the day of purchase he will own two dwellings.\n\nWhat is the SDLT position?","o":["He pays the standard rates, because he intends to sell his old home.","He pays no SDLT until his old home is sold.","He pays the higher rates, with no possibility of a refund.","He can choose which of the two properties the higher rates apply to, by electing within 36 months of the purchase.","He pays the higher rates, but can claim a refund of the surcharge if he sells his old home within 36 months."],"a":4,"e":"Because Olu will own **more than one dwelling** at the end of the day of purchase and has not yet disposed of his old main residence, the **higher rates** apply. If he sells his previous main residence within **36 months**, he can claim a **refund** of the surcharge. An intention to sell is not enough to avoid paying it at the time."},
 {"t":"tax","q":"Pria is buying a building in England comprising a ground-floor shop and a flat above, for a single price of £400,000.\n\nWhich SDLT rates apply?","o":["The non-residential rates on the whole price, because the property is mixed-use.","The residential rates on the whole price, because the property includes a dwelling.","The residential rates on the flat and the non-residential rates on the shop, apportioned.","The higher residential rates on the whole price.","No SDLT, because mixed-use property is exempt."],"a":0,"e":"**Mixed-use** property, part residential and part commercial, is charged at the **non-residential** rates on the whole price. The residential rates apply only where the whole property is residential. The non-residential rates are generally lower at this level of price, and mixed-use property is not exempt."},
 {"t":"tax","q":"Quill Developments Ltd completed a new office building 18 months ago and is now selling the freehold. It has not opted to tax.\n\nWhat is the VAT position on the sale?","o":["It is exempt, because the seller has not opted to tax.","It is zero-rated, because it is the first sale of a new building by the developer that constructed it, whatever its use.","It is standard-rated, because it is the sale of the freehold of a new commercial building less than three years old.","It is outside the scope of VAT, because land is never subject to VAT.","It is standard-rated only if the buyer is VAT registered."],"a":2,"e":"The sale of the **freehold** of a **new commercial building** (less than **3 years** from completion) is **compulsorily standard-rated** at 20%, whether or not the seller has opted to tax. **Zero-rating** applies to the first sale of a new **dwelling** by its builder, not to offices. After three years the sale would be exempt unless the owner had opted to tax."},
 {"t":"tax","q":"Rydal Homes Ltd, a house builder, is selling the freehold of a newly built house to Sam.\n\nWhat is the VAT position?","o":["It is standard-rated, so Sam must pay VAT at 20% on top of the price.","It is exempt, so Rydal Homes Ltd cannot recover the VAT on its building costs.","It is zero-rated, so no VAT is charged to Sam but Rydal Homes Ltd can recover its input VAT.","It is outside the scope of VAT because Sam is a private individual.","It is standard-rated only if Rydal Homes Ltd has opted to tax."],"a":2,"e":"The first grant of a **major interest** (the freehold, or a lease of more than 21 years) in a **new dwelling** by the person constructing it is **zero-rated** (VAT Act 1994 Sch 8 Group 5). No VAT is added to the price, but because a zero-rated supply is a taxable supply, the builder can **recover** its input VAT. The option to tax does not apply to dwellings."},
 {"t":"tax","q":"Pip exchanged contracts to sell a buy-to-let flat in England on 20 March 2026, with completion on 10 April 2026. She will make a chargeable gain.\n\nWhen is the disposal treated as made for capital gains tax, and when must the gain be reported and the tax paid?","o":["The disposal is on 20 March 2026, in 2025/26, and the gain must be reported and the tax paid within 60 days of completion.","The disposal is made on 10 April 2026, in the 2026/27 tax year, and the tax is due by the following 31 January in the usual way.","The disposal is made on 20 March 2026, and the gain must be reported within 30 days of exchange.","The disposal is made on 10 April 2026, and the gain must be reported within 14 days of completion.","The disposal is made on 20 March 2026, and the tax is payable only once HM Land Registry registers the buyer."],"a":0,"e":"For CGT, the time of disposal under an unconditional contract is the date of the **contract (exchange)**, not completion (TCGA 1992 s.28), so this disposal falls in **2025/26**. But a disposal of **UK residential property** on which CGT is due must be **reported and the tax paid within 60 days of completion**. The 14-day period belongs to SDLT."},
 {"t":"tax","q":"Quinn owned his house for 12 years. He lived in it as his only home for the first 4 years, then spent 2 years travelling abroad (not for work), and then moved back in and lived there for the last 6 years before selling at a gain. He had no other residence.\n\nHow much of the gain is exempt under Private Residence Relief?","o":["10/12 of the gain, because he was absent for two years.","Only the gain for the first 4 years plus the final 9 months.","All of the gain except the part relating to the two years he was away, because only periods of actual residence can ever qualify.","None of the gain, because he left the property for more than a year.","The whole gain, because the absence is covered by the up-to-3-years 'any reason' rule and he lived there before and after it."],"a":4,"e":"Periods of absence of up to **3 years in total for any reason** are treated as **deemed occupation**, provided the owner **actually lived** in the property **before and after** the absence. Quinn's two-year absence qualifies, so he is treated as occupying for the whole 12 years and the **whole gain** is exempt. Partial relief would apply only if part of the ownership did not count."},
 {"t":"tax","q":"Rhodri Ltd has bought an old office building for £3 million plus VAT, because the seller had opted to tax. It now plans a £1 million refurbishment and will then let the building. All its prospective tenants are VAT-registered businesses that make taxable supplies.\n\nWhat is the best advice on whether Rhodri Ltd should opt to tax?","o":["It should not opt, because opting would make VAT a real cost to its tenants.","It should opt, so it can recover input VAT on the purchase and the works; its tenants can recover the VAT on their rent.","It should not opt, because the option to tax cannot apply to a building bought from a seller that had already opted to tax it.","It need not opt, because it can recover input VAT on the building whether or not it opts.","It should opt only if it intends to sell the building within three years."],"a":1,"e":"Without an **option to tax**, Rhodri Ltd's lettings would be **exempt** and it could not recover the VAT on the purchase price and the refurbishment. Opting makes its rents **standard-rated**, so it can **recover its input VAT**. Because its tenants make taxable supplies, they can recover the VAT on the rent, so opting costs them nothing in real terms."},
 {"t":"tax","q":"Sorrel Ltd is taking the grant of a new 10-year lease of a shop in England. It will pay a premium of £100,000 to the landlord and an annual rent.\n\nOn what is SDLT chargeable?","o":["Only on the premium, because rent is never subject to SDLT.","Only on the first year's rent, at the non-residential rates.","Nothing, because leases of less than 21 years are exempt from SDLT.","On the premium and on the net present value of the rent payable over the term.","Only on the total rent payable over the 10 years, at 5%."],"a":3,"e":"On the **grant** of a lease, SDLT is charged on any **premium** (at the normal rates) and on the rent, by calculating the **net present value** of the total rent over the term and charging 1% on the part above the threshold (£150,000 for non-residential leases). There is no general exemption for short leases."}
];

;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["crimlaw"] = [
 {"t":"actus-reus","q":"Gary is walking his dog past a shallow paddling pond in a public park when he sees a toddler, whom he does not know, fall in face down. Gary could easily wade in and lift the child out, but he is late for work and walks on. A few minutes later another passer-by pulls the child out, but the child has died.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gary's criminal liability for the child's death?","o":["He is guilty of gross negligence manslaughter, because the risk of death was serious and obvious to him.","He is guilty of unlawful act manslaughter, because walking away from a drowning child is a dangerous act.","He is not guilty, because English law imposes no general duty to act on a stranger, however easy the rescue.","He is not guilty, because the other passer-by's failure to act sooner broke the chain of causation.","He is guilty of murder by omission, because he must have realised that death was virtually certain."],"a":2,"e":"An omission is only criminal where the defendant was under a legal duty to act, such as one arising from statute, contract, a special relationship, an assumption of care or creating the danger. Gary is a stranger with none of these, so he commits no offence even though he had a strong moral duty to help. Gross negligence manslaughter and murder by omission both need a duty first, and unlawful act manslaughter cannot be based on an omission at all."},
 {"t":"actus-reus","q":"Helen is helping her friend clear out a garage. She absent-mindedly drops a lit cigarette end into a box of old papers. Ten minutes later she notices smoke rising from the box. Not wanting to admit what she has done, she shuts the garage door and goes home without telling anyone or calling the fire brigade. The fire spreads and destroys the garage.\n\nCan Helen be guilty of arson?","o":["Yes, because having created the danger and become aware of it, she was under a duty to take reasonable steps to deal with it.","No, because she did not intend or foresee any damage when she dropped the cigarette, so there is no coincidence of actus reus and mens rea.","No, because arson can only be committed by a positive act of setting fire to property, not by a failure to act.","No, because the garage belonged to her friend, who was under the primary duty to deal with any fire on her property.","Yes, because dropping a lit cigarette among papers is objectively reckless whatever Helen herself foresaw at the time."],"a":0,"e":"A person who innocently creates a dangerous situation comes under a duty to take reasonable steps to deal with it once they become aware of it (Miller). Helen's failure to act after noticing the smoke is the actus reus, and her mens rea is judged from that moment, so the earlier lack of foresight does not help her. Recklessness is subjective, so the objective option is wrong, and the owner having a duty does not remove Helen's."},
 {"t":"actus-reus","q":"Ian wants to inherit from his wealthy uncle, Ken. He puts a large dose of poison into Ken's evening drink, intending to kill him. Before the poison has had any effect, Ken suffers a massive stroke caused by a long-standing medical condition and dies. Medical evidence shows the poison played no part in his death.\n\nWhat is Ian's most likely criminal liability?","o":["Murder, because he intended to kill Ken and Ken died shortly after drinking the poison.","Manslaughter only, because the poison was not the cause of death but was an unlawful and dangerous act.","No offence, because Ken's death was caused entirely by his medical condition and nothing Ian did.","Murder, because Ian must take his victim as he finds him, including Ken's medical condition.","Attempted murder, because he did not cause death but intended to kill."],"a":4,"e":"Ken would have died when and as he did even without the poison, so Ian fails the 'but for' test of factual causation and cannot be guilty of murder or any homicide (White). But Ian intended to kill and did an act that was more than merely preparatory, so he is guilty of attempted murder. The thin skull rule applies only once the defendant has caused the result; it does not create causation."},
 {"t":"actus-reus","q":"Lily stabs Mark in the arm during a quarrel. The wound is serious but treatable. Mark is a devout member of a faith that forbids blood transfusions, and he refuses the transfusion doctors say he needs. He dies from loss of blood. Lily argues that Mark caused his own death.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Lily caused Mark's death?","o":["She did not cause death, because Mark's refusal was a free, deliberate and informed choice that broke the chain.","She caused death, because she must take Mark as she finds him, including his beliefs.","She did not cause death, because his refusal of treatment was not a reasonably foreseeable reaction to a stab wound.","She caused death only if she knew before the attack that Mark's beliefs would lead him to refuse a transfusion.","She caused death only if the jury decides that Mark's refusal of treatment was reasonable in the circumstances."],"a":1,"e":"Under the thin skull rule the defendant takes the victim as they find them, and this covers the victim's beliefs as well as physical conditions (Blaue). A victim's refusal of treatment does not break the chain of causation, and the stab wound remained an operating and substantial cause of death. Lily's knowledge of Mark's beliefs, and whether his refusal was reasonable, are irrelevant."},
 {"t":"actus-reus","q":"Nathan bursts into Olivia's first-floor flat waving a large knife and shouting that he will cut her. Terrified, Olivia climbs out of the window and jumps to the garden below, breaking both ankles. Nathan says he never touched her and that jumping from a window was an extreme overreaction.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Nathan caused Olivia's injuries?","o":["He did not cause them, because she chose to jump and the injuries were self-inflicted by her own free act.","He caused them only if he foresaw that she might jump from the window to escape him.","He did not cause them, because a reasonable person would have stayed in the flat rather than risk the fall.","He caused them, as jumping in panic was within the range of expected responses.","He caused them only if the injuries would have been serious enough to amount to grievous bodily harm."],"a":3,"e":"A victim's attempt to escape breaks the chain only if it is so 'daft' or unexpected that no reasonable person could foresee it (Roberts, Williams). The jury should bear in mind that Olivia acted in panic in the agony of the moment, and jumping to escape a man with a knife is within the range of expected responses. Causation does not depend on what Nathan foresaw, nor on the level of injury."},
 {"t":"actus-reus","q":"Paul, a drug dealer, prepares a syringe of heroin and hands it to his friend Quentin, an experienced adult user, at Quentin's request. Quentin injects himself and dies of an overdose. Paul leaves before Quentin shows any sign of being unwell.\n\nDid Paul cause Quentin's death for the purposes of unlawful act manslaughter?","o":["No, because Quentin's free, deliberate and informed decision to inject himself broke the chain of causation.","Yes, because supplying heroin is a criminal offence and is plainly dangerous to the person who receives it.","Yes, because Paul prepared the syringe himself, which makes him the person who administered the drug.","Yes, because a drug dealer always owes a duty of care to the people to whom he supplies drugs.","No, because Paul did not intend Quentin to die or suffer serious harm when he handed him the syringe."],"a":0,"e":"Where an adult freely and knowingly injects himself, that free, deliberate and informed act breaks the chain, so the supplier does not cause death (Kennedy (No 2)). Preparing the syringe is not administering it; the position would be different if Paul had injected Quentin. Lack of intent to kill is not the reason, since unlawful act manslaughter never needs intent to kill, and supply alone does not automatically create a duty of care."},
 {"t":"actus-reus","q":"Robert, armed with a handgun, grabs a bank clerk, Sara, and holds her in front of him as a shield while he fires at armed police officers. The officers return fire in self-defence and a police bullet kills Sara.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Robert caused Sara's death?","o":["He did not, because the fatal bullet was fired by a police officer, who is the only legal cause of her death.","He did not, because the officers' decision to return fire was a free, deliberate and informed act that broke the chain of causation.","He did, because the officers' reasonable act of self-defence was not a free and deliberate act and did not break the chain.","He did only if the officers were acting lawfully and could not have avoided hitting Sara by any means at all.","He did only if he intended that Sara would be killed when he used her as a shield against the police."],"a":2,"e":"A third party's free, deliberate act normally breaks the chain, but a reasonable, instinctive act of self-defence or performance of a legal duty does not (Pagett). Robert's conduct provoked the return of fire and remained an operating and substantial cause of death. Intention is a mens rea issue, not part of causation, and the defendant's act need not be the only cause."},
 {"t":"actus-reus","q":"Tom attacks Uma with an iron bar, causing severe head injuries. She is taken to hospital and put on a ventilator. Two days later, after tests confirm that she is brain-stem dead, doctors switch off the ventilator. Tom argues that the doctors, not he, caused her death.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the legal position?","o":["The doctors caused death, because switching off a ventilator is a positive act that ends life.","The doctors caused death unless the decision to switch off was taken with the consent of Uma's family.","Neither caused death in law, because Uma was already dead when the ventilator was switched off, so Tom has no liability.","Tom did not cause death, because the chain of causation is broken by any deliberate medical decision.","Tom caused death, because switching off life support did not break the chain and his attack was still the operating cause."],"a":4,"e":"Switching off the life support of a brain-stem dead victim does not break the chain of causation (Malcherek). Death in law is brain-stem death, which Tom's attack caused, so his injuries remained the operating and substantial cause. The third option is wrong because it treats the timing of death as clearing Tom, when in fact it confirms that his attack caused it."},
 {"t":"actus-reus","q":"Victor stabs William in the stomach. William is treated in hospital and after two weeks the wound has almost completely healed. A doctor then gives William a large dose of an antibiotic to which, as his notes clearly record, he is seriously allergic, and also gives him grossly excessive fluids. William dies from the reaction. Expert evidence describes the treatment as palpably wrong.\n\nIs Victor likely to have caused William's death?","o":["Yes, because medical negligence never breaks the chain of causation where the victim is in hospital because of the defendant's attack.","Yes, because William would not have been in hospital at all but for the stab wound inflicted by Victor.","Yes, because Victor must take his victim as he finds him, including William's allergy to the antibiotic.","No, because the wound had almost healed and the palpably wrong treatment was the sole operating cause of death.","No, because any negligent medical treatment that contributes to death automatically breaks the chain."],"a":3,"e":"Medical treatment breaks the chain only where it is so independent and potent that the defendant's contribution becomes insignificant, as where the wound had mainly healed and the treatment was palpably wrong (Jordan). On these facts the stab wound was no longer an operating cause, so Victor did not cause death, though he remains liable for the stabbing. Ordinary negligent treatment would not break the chain (Cheshire), so the last option overstates the rule."},
 {"t":"actus-reus","q":"Xavier is driving carefully along a country road with his window open when a swarm of bees flies into the car and attacks his face. He loses all control of his arms as he tries to protect his eyes, and the car mounts the pavement and injures a pedestrian, Yasmin.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Xavier's position?","o":["He is liable, because he chose to drive with the window open and so voluntarily took the risk of something entering the car through it.","He is not liable, because his movements were not willed: a total loss of control caused by an external factor is automatism.","He is liable, because automatism is only available where the loss of control is caused by an internal medical condition.","He is not liable, because automatism is a defence where the defendant's control is reduced or impaired, even if not wholly lost.","He is liable, because any offence connected with driving is one of strict liability, so voluntariness is irrelevant."],"a":1,"e":"The actus reus must be voluntary. A total loss of control caused by an external factor, such as a swarm of bees, is automatism and leads to an acquittal (Hill v Baxter). An internal cause would be insanity, not automatism, and merely reduced or impaired control is not enough. Even strict liability offences generally require a voluntary act."},
 {"t":"mens-rea","q":"Zoe wants to kill her business rival, Adam. She fires a rifle at him from about 300 metres away. She is an inexperienced shooter and genuinely believes she has only about a one in ten chance of hitting him. The bullet strikes Adam in the head and kills him.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Zoe's mens rea?","o":["She did not intend to kill, because she foresaw death as unlikely rather than virtually certain.","She was reckless as to death, because she foresaw a risk of death and it was unjustified to take it.","She intended to kill only if the jury finds that she appreciated death was a virtual certainty.","She directly intended to kill, because death was her aim, however unlikely she thought success to be.","She intended to kill only if a reasonable person in her position would have expected the shot to succeed."],"a":3,"e":"Direct intention exists where the result is the defendant's aim or purpose, and it does not matter how unlikely the defendant thinks success is (Mohan). Zoe's aim was Adam's death, so she has the intention to kill needed for murder. The virtual certainty test applies only to oblique intention, where the result is a side-effect rather than the defendant's purpose."},
 {"t":"mens-rea","q":"Ben sets fire to a disused warehouse at night to claim on the insurance. He knows that homeless people sometimes sleep inside and admits that he thought it 'highly probable' that someone would be badly hurt, although he hoped no one was there. A man asleep inside dies in the fire.\n\nCan the jury find that Ben intended to cause death or serious injury?","o":["Yes, because foresight of serious injury as highly probable is sufficient for oblique intention.","No, because foresight that serious injury was highly probable, rather than virtually certain, is not enough.","Yes, because the jury must find intention whenever the defendant foresees that serious injury is the likely result.","No, because oblique intention can only apply where the defendant desired the consequence in addition to his main aim.","Yes, because an intention to commit arson is automatically transferred to the man who died in the fire."],"a":1,"e":"Oblique intention requires that death or serious injury was a virtual certainty and that the defendant appreciated this (Woollin). Foresight that harm was 'highly probable' is recklessness, not intention, so this is not murder and the jury should look at manslaughter. Transferred malice cannot move an intent to damage property onto a person, and desire is not required for intention."},
 {"t":"mens-rea","q":"Callum throws his partner's laptop through a closed upstairs window in a rage, knowing that their neighbour is standing directly below. The neighbour is struck and suffers a fractured skull and dies. Callum says he only wanted to destroy the laptop. The jury is sure that serious injury was a virtual certainty and that Callum appreciated this.\n\nHow should the jury be directed on intention?","o":["The jury is entitled to find that Callum intended serious injury, but it is not obliged to do so.","The jury must find that Callum intended serious injury, because both parts of the virtual certainty test are met.","The jury cannot find intention, because Callum's purpose was only to destroy the laptop, not to harm anyone.","The jury may find intention only if Callum also desired the neighbour to be injured as a means to his end.","The jury should decide whether a reasonable person would have intended serious injury in Callum's position."],"a":0,"e":"Where the result was a virtual certainty and the defendant appreciated it, the jury is entitled to find intention, but foresight of virtual certainty is evidence of intention, not intention itself, so it is not obliged to (Woollin; Matthews and Alleyne). The test is subjective as to what Callum appreciated, not what a reasonable person would intend, and desire is irrelevant."},
 {"t":"mens-rea","q":"Dara, aged 40, lights a bonfire in her garden on a very windy day to burn hedge cuttings. Her neighbour's wooden fence is two metres away. It does not cross her mind that sparks might blow onto the fence, although any ordinary person would have seen the risk. The fence catches fire and is destroyed.\n\nWas Dara reckless as to damaging the fence?","o":["Yes, because the risk of damage would have been obvious to any reasonable person standing in her garden that day.","Yes, because an adult of normal intelligence is presumed to foresee the obvious risks of what she does.","Yes, because lighting a fire is inherently dangerous and recklessness is presumed in cases involving fire.","No, unless the jury finds that the destruction of the fence was virtually certain from what she did.","No, because recklessness requires that Dara herself was aware of a risk, and she gave the risk no thought."],"a":4,"e":"Recklessness is subjective for all offences since R v G: the defendant must actually be aware of a risk and it must be unreasonable to take it. Dara never foresaw the risk, so she was not reckless, however obvious it would have been to others. The 'obvious to a reasonable person' test from Caldwell has been overruled, and virtual certainty is the test for oblique intention, not recklessness."},
 {"t":"mens-rea","q":"In a crowded bar, Ellis swings a punch at Fraser, with whom he is arguing. Fraser ducks and the punch lands on Gemma, who is standing behind him, splitting her lip. Ellis says he never meant to touch Gemma and did not even know she was there.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ellis's liability for the injury to Gemma?","o":["He is not liable, because he had no mens rea in relation to Gemma, whom he did not know was there.","He is liable only if he was subjectively reckless as to the risk of hitting someone standing behind Fraser.","He is liable, because his intention to strike Fraser transfers to Gemma, as the harm is of the same type.","He is not liable, because transferred malice applies only to murder and not to non-fatal offences.","He is liable only for an attempted battery on Fraser, as the person he actually intended to hit."],"a":2,"e":"Under transferred malice, where the defendant intends harm to one person but the same type of harm happens to another, the defendant's mens rea transfers (Latimer). Ellis intended to strike a person, and a person was struck, so he is liable for the injury to Gemma as if he had intended it. There is no need to prove separate recklessness as to Gemma, and the doctrine is not limited to murder."},
 {"t":"mens-rea","q":"Harry throws a bottle at Isaac across a pub car park, intending to hit him. The bottle misses Isaac and smashes the windscreen of a parked car. Harry says he never thought about the cars when he threw it.\n\nIs Harry guilty of criminal damage to the windscreen?","o":["Yes, because his intention to hit Isaac transfers to the windscreen under the doctrine of transferred malice.","Yes, because he intended to commit an offence, and the law treats that intent as covering any harm that follows.","Yes, because damaging property is a less serious harm than injuring a person, so the greater intent covers it.","No, unless the prosecution can prove that the damage to the windscreen was a virtually certain consequence.","No, because an intent to harm a person cannot transfer to property, and he did not foresee any risk to the car."],"a":4,"e":"Transferred malice applies only between the same type of harm; an intention to harm a person does not transfer to property (Pembliton). Criminal damage needs intention or recklessness as to damaging property, so Harry would be guilty only if he foresaw a risk of hitting a car. He gave it no thought, so he lacked the mens rea, though he may be guilty of assault on Isaac."},
 {"t":"mens-rea","q":"Jade strangles Kieran in his flat, intending to kill him. Believing him to be dead, she drags him into the woods and buries him in a shallow grave to hide the body. The post-mortem shows that Kieran was still alive when buried and died of suffocation from the earth.\n\nWhat is Jade's liability for Kieran's death?","o":["She is guilty of murder, because the strangling and burial formed one series of acts and she had the intent during it.","She is guilty only of attempted murder, because the strangling with intent to kill did not in fact cause death.","She is guilty only of manslaughter, because she believed Kieran was already dead when she buried him.","She is not guilty of homicide, because when the burial caused death she had no intention to kill a living person.","She is guilty of murder only if she foresaw that Kieran might still be alive when she buried him."],"a":0,"e":"Actus reus and mens rea must coincide, but the courts treat a series of acts as a single transaction, so mens rea at any point during it is enough (Thabo Meli; Church). Jade intended to kill when she began, and the burial was part of the same transaction, so she is guilty of murder. She need not have foreseen that Kieran was still alive."},
 {"t":"mens-rea","q":"Liam is unloading a heavy crate from a van when it slips and lands on Maya's foot, trapping it. Maya shouts at Liam to lift it off. Liam, who dislikes her, decides to leave it there and stands watching for a minute before finally lifting it. Maya's foot is bruised.\n\nCan Liam be guilty of battery?","o":["No, because when the crate fell on Maya's foot Liam had no intention or recklessness as to applying force.","No, because battery requires a positive act of force and Liam's later conduct was merely an omission.","Yes, because the force was a continuing act and he formed the intent during it.","Yes, because the crate falling was careless, and negligence is enough for the mens rea of battery.","No, because a battery cannot be committed indirectly through an object rather than the defendant's own body."],"a":2,"e":"Where the actus reus is a continuing act, it is enough that the defendant forms the mens rea at some point while it continues (Fagan v MPC). The force on Maya's foot continued, and it became a battery once Liam decided to keep the crate there. Battery needs intention or subjective recklessness, not negligence, and force can be applied through an object."},
 {"t":"mens-rea","q":"Nadia owns a newsagent's. Her assistant sells a National Lottery ticket to a 15-year-old boy who shows a very convincing fake identity card stating that he is 18. The relevant offence of selling a lottery ticket to a person under the minimum age says nothing about knowledge or belief, and is a regulatory offence aimed at protecting young people.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["Not guilty, because the presumption of mens rea applies to every offence and can never be displaced.","Guilty, because the offence is likely to be strict liability as to age.","Not guilty, because the assistant made an honest and reasonable mistake about the boy's age.","Guilty, because the assistant was negligent in failing to spot that the identity card was a forgery.","Not guilty, because strict liability can only apply where the defendant has acted deliberately."],"a":1,"e":"Courts presume mens rea is required, but the presumption is more easily displaced for regulatory offences of social concern, where strict liability encourages vigilance (Gammon). Selling lottery tickets to children has been held to be strict liability as to age, so even a reasonable mistake is no defence (Harrow LBC v Shah). Liability does not depend on negligence."},
 {"t":"mens-rea","q":"Oscar lets his flat to three students. Without his knowledge, they regularly smoke cannabis there. Oscar is charged with being concerned in the management of premises used for smoking cannabis. The statute does not mention any mental element, and conviction carries real social stigma and a sentence of imprisonment.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the court is likely to approach the mental element?","o":["As strict liability, because Parliament did not include any words of knowledge or intention in the offence.","As strict liability, because drug misuse is a matter of social concern and that always displaces mens rea.","As requiring only negligence, so that Oscar is guilty if a reasonable landlord would have found out.","As requiring mens rea, because the presumption applies and is especially strong for truly criminal offences.","As requiring mens rea only if Oscar can prove he took all reasonable precautions to prevent drug use."],"a":3,"e":"Courts presume Parliament intended mens rea, even where the statute is silent, and the presumption is particularly strong for 'truly criminal' offences carrying stigma (Sweet v Parsley). On almost identical facts the House of Lords held that knowledge was required, so a landlord unaware of the drug use was not guilty. Silence in the statute is not enough to displace the presumption."},
 {"t":"non-fatal-offences","q":"Patrick and Ryan argue in a car park. Patrick raises his fist, steps towards Ryan and shouts 'I'm going to punch your lights out!'. Ryan flinches, expecting to be hit. Before Patrick can swing, his friend grabs his arm and pulls him away. No contact is made.\n\nWhat offence, if any, has Patrick committed?","o":["Assault, because Ryan apprehended the immediate application of unlawful force.","Battery, because raising a fist at someone close by counts as applying force.","Attempted battery, because Patrick tried but failed to punch Ryan.","No offence, because no force was applied and Ryan suffered no injury.","No offence, because words alone can never amount to an assault."],"a":0,"e":"Assault is causing the victim to apprehend immediate unlawful force; no touching is needed. Ryan expected to be hit there and then, and Patrick intended that, so it is assault. There is no battery as no force was applied, and attempted battery is not an offence because battery is summary only. Words can in any event amount to an assault (Ireland)."},
 {"t":"non-fatal-offences","q":"At a party, Sam's friend Toby makes a joke about Sam's wife. Sam grips a kitchen knife on the counter and says, calmly and clearly, 'If we weren't at my mother's birthday, I'd stab you for that.' He then puts the knife down and walks away. Toby is shocked.\n\nHas Sam committed an assault?","o":["Yes, because picking up a knife while making a threat always causes apprehension of immediate force.","Yes, because Toby was shocked, and causing shock or distress is enough for the actus reus of assault.","Yes, because a conditional threat is an assault whenever the defendant is holding a weapon.","Yes, because threatening words are sufficient for assault even if they show no force will follow.","No, because Sam's words made clear he would not use force, so Toby could not apprehend immediate force."],"a":4,"e":"Words can negate an assault where they show that the defendant will not use force (Tuberville v Savage). Sam's words made clear he would not stab Toby because of the occasion, so Toby could not apprehend immediate force. Shock or distress alone is not the test; the question is whether the victim apprehended immediate unlawful force."},
 {"t":"non-fatal-offences","q":"Uri points a replica pistol at a shop assistant, Vera, and tells her to open the till. Vera believes the gun is real and is terrified. Uri knows it cannot fire. Before Vera can do anything, a customer tackles Uri and he drops the replica.\n\nHas Uri committed an assault on Vera?","o":["No, because the replica could not fire, so there was never any possibility of force being applied.","No, because Uri knew the gun could not fire, so he could not intend Vera to apprehend force.","Yes, because Vera apprehended immediate unlawful force, and it does not matter that the force was impossible.","No, because Uri's demand was conditional on Vera refusing to open the till, so the threat was not immediate.","Yes, but only if Vera's belief that the gun was real was one that any reasonable person would have shared."],"a":2,"e":"The offence turns on what the victim apprehends, so pointing an imitation gun at a victim who believes it is real is an assault (Logdon v DPP). Uri intended Vera to believe force was coming, which is the required mens rea. A conditional threat is still an assault where the defendant has no right to impose the condition (Read v Coker)."},
 {"t":"non-fatal-offences","q":"Wendy shouts threats at her neighbour, Xena, across the garden fence and then slaps her once across the face, leaving a red mark that fades within the hour. Xena is upset and has trouble sleeping for a few nights, but a psychiatrist finds no recognised psychiatric condition.\n\nWhat is the most serious offence Wendy has committed?","o":["Assault occasioning actual bodily harm, because Xena's sleeplessness and distress amount to actual bodily harm.","Assault and battery, because neither the transient mark nor the distress amounts to actual bodily harm.","Assault occasioning actual bodily harm, because any visible mark on the skin amounts to actual bodily harm.","Inflicting grievous bodily harm, because the psychological effects on Xena were serious and long-lasting.","No offence, because the slap caused no lasting injury and Xena suffered no recognised psychiatric harm."],"a":1,"e":"Actual bodily harm must be more than transient or trifling, and psychiatric harm counts only if it is a recognised clinical condition proved by expert evidence (Chan-Fook). A red mark that fades quickly and ordinary distress or sleeplessness do not qualify. Wendy has committed assault (the threats) and battery (the slap), and no injury is needed for battery."},
 {"t":"non-fatal-offences","q":"After their relationship ends, Aaron waits for his ex-girlfriend Bella outside her gym, grabs her hair from behind and cuts off her long ponytail with scissors. Bella feels no pain and suffers no other injury, but is very distressed.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate offence for Aaron?","o":["Battery only, because Bella felt no pain and the hair will grow back.","Criminal damage, because hair once cut is property capable of being damaged.","Wounding under s.20, because cutting through the hair breaks the outer layer of the body.","Assault occasioning actual bodily harm, because hair is part of the body.","Grievous bodily harm under s.18, because the cut was deliberate and made with a dangerous weapon."],"a":3,"e":"Hair is part of the body, and cutting off a substantial amount without consent can be actual bodily harm even without pain (DPP v Smith (Michael)). The cutting was a battery that caused ABH, so s.47 applies. There is no wound because the skin was not broken, and nothing suggests really serious harm."},
 {"t":"non-fatal-offences","q":"During a scuffle outside a nightclub, Callum punches Declan in the mouth, intending to hurt him. The blow causes a small cut that breaks through both layers of skin on the inside of Declan's lip. It bleeds briefly and heals within a week.\n\nWhat is the most serious offence Callum is likely to have committed?","o":["Assault occasioning actual bodily harm, because a cut that heals in a week is too minor to be a wound.","Battery, because the injury healed quickly and left no lasting harm.","Maliciously wounding under s.20, because both layers of skin were broken and Callum foresaw some harm.","Wounding with intent under s.18, because any deliberate blow that breaks the skin is a wound with intent.","Inflicting grievous bodily harm under s.20, because any bleeding injury is grievous bodily harm."],"a":2,"e":"A wound is a break in both layers of the skin, including the inner skin of the mouth or lip (Moriarty v Brookes), however minor. Section 20 needs only foresight of some harm, which Callum had because he intended to hurt Declan. Section 18 needs an intent to cause grievous bodily harm, not just an intent to hurt, and a minor bleeding cut is not really serious harm."},
 {"t":"non-fatal-offences","q":"Elena is furious with her flatmate Faye. She takes a craft knife and slashes Faye's forearm once, intending to leave a cut 'to teach her a lesson' but not intending any serious injury. The cut needs four stitches and heals fully.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Elena's liability?","o":["She is guilty under s.18, because she intended to wound Faye and an intent to wound is enough for s.18.","She is guilty under s.18, because using a knife shows that she intended really serious harm.","She is guilty of assault occasioning actual bodily harm only, because the injury healed fully.","She is guilty under s.20, because she intended to wound but not to cause grievous bodily harm.","She is not guilty of any wounding offence, because the cut was not serious enough to be a wound."],"a":3,"e":"The cut broke both layers of the skin, so it is a wound. Section 18 needs an intent to cause grievous bodily harm, and an intent merely to wound is not enough, so Elena commits s.20, which needs only foresight of some harm. The use of a knife is evidence for the jury, but the facts say she did not intend serious injury."},
 {"t":"non-fatal-offences","q":"George swings a heavy metal bar at Hassan's head, intending to fracture his skull. Hassan ducks and the bar only grazes his scalp, causing a small cut through both layers of skin. Hassan is otherwise unhurt.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate offence for George?","o":["Wounding with intent to cause grievous bodily harm under s.18.","Maliciously wounding under s.20, because the injury was only minor.","Assault occasioning actual bodily harm under s.47, because the cut was small.","Attempted wounding under s.20, because George failed to cause the injury he intended.","Battery, because Hassan suffered no harm beyond a graze."],"a":0,"e":"The small cut breaks both layers of the skin, so it is a wound, and George intended to cause really serious harm. Section 18 is committed where the defendant wounds with intent to cause grievous bodily harm, so a minor wound with that intent is enough. The seriousness of the injury does not matter once both the wound and the intent are proved."},
 {"t":"non-fatal-offences","q":"Isla and Jack are in a relationship. At Jack's request, Isla repeatedly whips him with a belt during sex for their mutual sexual gratification, leaving extensive bruising and some broken skin. Jack later reports Isla to the police after they separate. Isla says Jack consented throughout.\n\nIs Jack's consent a defence for Isla?","o":["Yes, because consent is a defence to any non-fatal offence where the activity took place in private between adults.","Yes, because adults in a relationship may consent to any level of harm during sexual activity.","No, because consent is never a defence to any offence involving the application of force.","Yes, because rough horseplay is a recognised exception where there was no intention to injure.","No, because consent is no defence to actual bodily harm or worse inflicted for sexual gratification."],"a":4,"e":"Consent is a defence to assault and battery, but not to actual bodily harm or worse unless a recognised exception applies (Brown). Section 71 Domestic Abuse Act 2021 confirms that a person cannot consent to ABH or worse inflicted for sexual gratification. The horseplay exception does not cover deliberate injury for sexual gratification, and privacy does not matter."},
 {"t":"non-fatal-offences","q":"Kirsty, in a rage, punches Laura, who is holding her six-month-old son in her arms. Laura stumbles and drops the baby, who strikes his head on the pavement and suffers bruising. Kirsty says she never touched the baby.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kirsty's liability towards the baby?","o":["She is not liable, because battery requires the defendant to apply force directly to the victim's body.","She can be liable for an offence against the baby, because force can be applied indirectly through another person.","She is not liable, because she intended to strike only Laura and transferred malice does not apply to battery.","She is liable only for criminal damage to the baby's clothing, as no direct force touched the baby.","She is not liable, because the baby could not have apprehended any force and so there was no assault."],"a":1,"e":"Battery can be committed by indirect force, such as punching a person so that they drop the child they are holding (Haystead). The force was applied to the baby through Laura, and the bruising can be actual bodily harm under s.47. The baby's lack of apprehension matters only for assault, not battery."},
 {"t":"theft","q":"Megan befriends Norman, a lonely 80-year-old man with limited mental capacity. Over several months she persuades him to give her £40,000 from his savings as a gift. Norman understands he is giving her the money and civil lawyers advise that the gift is probably valid in civil law. Megan is charged with theft.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["She cannot be guilty, because Norman consented to the transfer, so there was no appropriation.","She can be guilty, because receiving even a valid gift can be an appropriation, so the key question is dishonesty.","She cannot be guilty, because ownership of the money passed to her under a valid gift, so it did not belong to another.","She can be guilty only if she made a false representation to Norman to persuade him to give her the money.","She cannot be guilty, because theft requires the property to be taken without the owner's knowledge."],"a":1,"e":"Appropriation is any assumption of the rights of an owner and can occur even with the owner's consent, including receipt of a valid gift (Hinks; Gomez). So the case turns on whether Megan was dishonest under the Ivey test. No false representation is needed for theft, and the property belonged to Norman at the moment of appropriation."},
 {"t":"theft","q":"In a supermarket, Oliver peels the price label from a cheap bottle of wine and sticks it on an expensive bottle of champagne, intending to pay the lower price at the till. A store detective sees him do it and stops him before he reaches the checkout.\n\nHas Oliver appropriated the champagne?","o":["No, because there is no appropriation until he leaves the store without paying the full price.","No, because the supermarket consents to customers handling goods on the shelves.","No, because he intended to pay something for the champagne, so he was not assuming any of the owner's rights.","Yes, but only if the store detective was actually deceived about the price of the champagne.","Yes, because switching the labels assumed one of the rights of the owner, which is enough for appropriation."],"a":4,"e":"Assuming any one of the owner's rights is an appropriation, and switching price labels is something only the owner has the right to do (Morris). The appropriation took place when he switched the labels, so theft could be complete before he reached the till. A willingness to pay something does not prevent dishonesty (s.2(2) Theft Act 1968), and no one needs to be deceived."},
 {"t":"theft","q":"Priya shares a house with two friends. They each give her £60 so that she can pay the electricity bill on the due date. Priya instead spends the £180 on a weekend away, intending to tell her friends the bill was paid late. The bill remains unpaid.\n\nCan Priya be guilty of theft of the £180?","o":["No, because her friends handed the money to her voluntarily, so it became her own property.","Yes, because she was legally obliged to use that money in a particular way.","No, because she has a moral obligation only, and moral obligations do not make property belong to another.","No, because she could repay the money later, so she did not intend to permanently deprive her friends.","Yes, but only for her friends' £120, because she cannot steal her own share of the money."],"a":1,"e":"Under s.5(3) Theft Act 1968, property received from or on account of another, under an obligation to deal with it in a particular way, is treated as belonging to the other (Davidge v Bunnett). Priya was obliged to use the money to pay the bill, so spending it dishonestly is theft. Planning to replace the money with other money does not prevent an intention to permanently deprive (Velumyl)."},
 {"t":"theft","q":"Quinn leaves his car at a garage for repairs. When he collects it, the bill is £900, which he cannot afford. That night he goes back and drives the car away from the garage forecourt using his spare key, intending never to pay the bill.\n\nCan Quinn be guilty of stealing his own car?","o":["No, because a person can never steal property of which he is the legal owner.","No, because the garage only has a claim for the debt, which is a civil matter and not theft.","No, because he did not intend to permanently deprive the garage, which never owned the car.","Yes, because the garage had possession and control of it, so it belonged to the garage.","Yes, but only if the garage had a written contract giving it the right to keep the car until payment."],"a":3,"e":"Property belongs to anyone with possession or control of it (s.5(1) Theft Act 1968), so an owner can steal his own property from someone in possession (Turner (No 2)). The garage had possession and control of the car, and Quinn intended to deprive it of that permanently. No written contract is needed."},
 {"t":"theft","q":"Rhian, a student, finds the unattended office of her law lecturer and sees next week's exam paper on the desk. She reads it carefully and memorises the questions, then leaves the paper exactly where she found it.\n\nIs Rhian guilty of theft?","o":["No, because confidential information is not property that can be stolen.","Yes, because she appropriated the information in the paper, intending to use it permanently.","Yes, because the exam paper was confidential and she had no right to read it.","No, because she left the office without taking anything at all, and theft needs physical removal.","Yes, because the information has lost its value now that she knows the questions."],"a":0,"e":"Confidential information is not property under s.4 Theft Act 1968, so it cannot be stolen (Oxford v Moss). She did not intend to permanently deprive the university of the paper itself, which she left behind. Physical removal is not required for appropriation in general, but there must be property capable of being stolen."},
 {"t":"theft","q":"Sian shares a flat with Tara. Sian runs out of milk one morning and uses most of Tara's carton for her cereal while Tara is away. Tara often lends Sian food and has said 'help yourself if you're stuck', and Sian genuinely believes Tara would not mind. In fact Tara is annoyed and reports it.\n\nIs Sian dishonest?","o":["Yes, because Tara did not actually consent, so Sian's belief in consent is irrelevant.","Yes, because ordinary decent people would regard taking someone else's food as dishonest.","No, because she genuinely believed Tara would have consented had she known of the circumstances.","Yes, because her belief in Tara's consent was not reasonable in the circumstances.","No, because the milk was of trivial value, and theft does not cover items of minimal value."],"a":2,"e":"Under s.2(1)(b) Theft Act 1968, an appropriation is not dishonest if the defendant believes the owner would have consented had they known of the appropriation and its circumstances. The belief need only be honest, not reasonable, and Tara's actual reaction does not matter. Where a s.2(1) belief applies, there is no need to apply the Ivey test."},
 {"t":"theft","q":"Umar is walking along a busy high street when he sees a £20 note blowing along the pavement. Nobody is nearby and there is nothing to identify who dropped it. He picks it up and spends it on lunch.\n\nIs Umar likely to be guilty of theft?","o":["Yes, because lost property still belongs to its owner and he knew it was not his.","Yes, because he should have handed the money in to the police before spending it.","Yes, because ordinary decent people would regard keeping found money as dishonest.","No, because money found in the street is abandoned and belongs to no one.","No, because he believed the owner could not be discovered by taking reasonable steps."],"a":4,"e":"An appropriation is not dishonest if the defendant believes the owner cannot be discovered by taking reasonable steps (s.2(1)(c) Theft Act 1968). Umar's belief is genuine and reasonable on these facts, so he is not dishonest. Lost money is not abandoned, as it still belongs to the owner, but the s.2(1)(c) belief prevents theft."},
 {"t":"theft","q":"Vince, an employee of a large supermarket chain, regularly takes food that is close to its sell-by date and gives it to a homeless shelter. Store policy forbids this and requires the food to be destroyed. Vince knows the policy but believes that what he does is morally right and not dishonest at all.\n\nIs Vince dishonest for the purposes of theft?","o":["Yes, if ordinary decent people would regard his conduct as dishonest, whatever his own view.","No, because he genuinely believes that what he is doing is morally justified and honest.","No, because he does not realise that ordinary decent people would regard his conduct as dishonest.","No, because he gains nothing personally and the food would otherwise have been thrown away.","Yes, because an employee who breaks a store policy is automatically dishonest."],"a":0,"e":"Under the Ivey test, the jury identifies the defendant's actual knowledge or belief as to the facts, then asks whether the conduct was dishonest by the standards of ordinary decent people. The defendant's own moral standards are irrelevant, and there is no longer any requirement that the defendant realised others would regard the conduct as dishonest (Barton and Booth). Theft does not require personal gain, so the 'Robin Hood' defence fails."},
 {"t":"theft","q":"Will manages a café. On Friday he takes £300 in banknotes from the till to pay his rent, intending to put back £300 from his wages the following Monday before the owner checks the takings. He does put the money back on Monday, but the owner has already discovered the shortfall.\n\nDid Will intend to permanently deprive the owner?","o":["No, because he intended to replace the money and did so before the owner suffered any loss.","No, because he was only borrowing the money and borrowing is never theft.","Yes, because he intended to deprive the owner of the actual notes he took.","No, because he returned the money on Monday, which shows he never intended to keep it.","Yes, but only because the owner discovered the shortfall before the money was replaced."],"a":2,"e":"Taking cash intending to replace it later with other notes or coins is an intention to permanently deprive the owner of the original notes (Velumyl). The intention is judged at the time of appropriation, so replacing the money later does not undo the theft. Whether the owner discovered the shortfall is irrelevant."},
 {"t":"theft","q":"Xavier takes his colleague's annual gym membership card from her locker in January without asking, intending to use it himself and return it. He uses the gym every day and slips the card back into her locker the following January, the day after the membership expires.\n\nCan Xavier be guilty of theft of the card?","o":["No, because he always intended to return the card and in fact did so.","No, because borrowing can never amount to an intention to permanently deprive.","No, because the card itself is a cheap piece of plastic with no real value.","Yes, because returning it with its value used up equals an outright taking.","Yes, because any unauthorised use of another person's property is theft."],"a":3,"e":"Borrowing is an intention to permanently deprive only if it is for a period and in circumstances making it equivalent to an outright taking, as where the item is returned with its goodness or virtue gone (s.6(1) Theft Act 1968; Lloyd). A membership card returned after it expires has lost all its practical value, so this is theft. Ordinary borrowing of an item returned unharmed would not be."},
 {"t":"robbery-burglary","q":"Yusuf believes that his former flatmate, Zak, owes him £200 and genuinely thinks he is legally entitled to take it. He confronts Zak in the street, pushes him against a wall and takes £200 from his wallet. In fact Zak owes him nothing.\n\nIs Yusuf guilty of robbery?","o":["Yes, because he used force on Zak immediately before and in order to take the money.","Yes, because Zak owed him nothing, so Yusuf had no right to the money in law.","Yes, because a belief in a legal right is relevant only to theft, not robbery.","Yes, but only if a reasonable person would also have believed the money was owed.","No, because his belief in a legal right means there was no dishonest theft."],"a":4,"e":"Robbery requires a completed theft, and an appropriation is not dishonest if the defendant believes he has a legal right to the property (s.2(1)(a) Theft Act 1968; Robinson). The belief need only be honest, not reasonable or correct. With no dishonesty there is no theft and therefore no robbery, though Yusuf may be guilty of an assault offence."},
 {"t":"robbery-burglary","q":"Abel walks past Bryony, who is standing at a bus stop holding a lit cigarette between her fingers. He plucks the cigarette from her fingers without touching her hand and walks off smoking it. Bryony is startled but unhurt.\n\nWhat is Abel's most likely liability?","o":["Robbery, because snatching property from someone's hand always involves force on the person.","Robbery, because Bryony was frightened by the sudden snatch.","Theft but not robbery, because no force was used on Bryony herself.","Assault with intent to rob, because the snatch was a threat of force.","No offence, because a single cigarette is too trivial to be property."],"a":2,"e":"Robbery requires force on a person, or putting or seeking to put a person in fear of force then and there. Taking an item from someone's fingers without touching them is not force on the person (P v DPP), so this is theft. Contrast wrenching a bag from someone's grip, where force is transmitted through the property to the person (Clouden)."},
 {"t":"robbery-burglary","q":"Carla approaches Dennis in a pub and tells him: 'Give me your watch now, or next week I'll have my brothers come round and beat you up.' Dennis is frightened and hands over his watch.\n\nIs Carla guilty of robbery?","o":["Yes, because she put Dennis in fear of force in order to steal his watch.","Yes, because Dennis was actually frightened, which is all that robbery requires.","No, because Dennis handed over the watch voluntarily, so there was no appropriation.","No, because the threat was of force in the future, not of force then and there.","No, because the force threatened was to be used by other people, not by Carla."],"a":3,"e":"Robbery requires force, or putting or seeking to put a person in fear of being subjected to force then and there (s.8 Theft Act 1968). A threat of force at some future time is not enough for robbery; demanding property with such a threat is likely to be blackmail. The appropriation is not prevented by Dennis handing the watch over, and force by others could count if it were threatened then and there."},
 {"t":"robbery-burglary","q":"Elliot takes two bottles of whisky from a supermarket shelf, hides them in his coat and walks past the tills without paying. At the exit a security guard, Fiona, blocks his way. Elliot pushes her to the ground so that he can get out with the whisky.\n\nWhat is Elliot's most likely liability?","o":["Theft and battery, because the theft was complete before he used any force on Fiona.","Theft only, because the force was used on a security guard rather than on the owner of the property.","Robbery, because the theft was still continuing when he used force in order to steal.","Theft and assault occasioning actual bodily harm, because force used during an escape is never robbery.","Burglary, because he entered the shop intending to steal and used force inside the building."],"a":2,"e":"Appropriation can be a continuing act, so force used while the defendant is still in the course of stealing, including getting away from the shop, can make it robbery (Hale; Lockley). Elliot used force on Fiona in order to steal, and force on any person counts, not just the owner. Burglary is unlikely, as there is nothing to show he entered the shop as a trespasser."},
 {"t":"robbery-burglary","q":"Gareth and Hugh punch Ivor in a park because of a long-running feud. Ivor runs away, leaving his bicycle. Only after he has gone do Gareth and Hugh decide to take the bicycle, which they ride off and dump later that evening.\n\nAre Gareth and Hugh guilty of robbery?","o":["Yes, because they used force on Ivor immediately before taking his bicycle.","No, because the force was not used in order to steal the bicycle.","Yes, because force and theft were part of a single transaction in the same place.","No, because they dumped the bicycle later, so there was no intention to permanently deprive.","Yes, because Ivor was afraid and fled, which allowed them to take the bicycle."],"a":1,"e":"Robbery requires force to be used in order to steal (s.8 Theft Act 1968). Where the defendant decides to take the property only after the force has been used for another reason, the force was not used to steal, so there is no robbery (Vinall). They may still be guilty of theft and an assault offence; dumping the bike does not necessarily mean there was no intention to permanently deprive."},
 {"t":"robbery-burglary","q":"Jasmine walks into a jeweller's shop where a customer, Kelvin, is browsing. She grabs Kelvin, holds a knife to his throat and tells the assistant, Lucy, to empty the display cabinet into a bag or she will cut him. Lucy does so and Jasmine leaves with the jewellery.\n\nIs Jasmine guilty of robbery?","o":["No, because she threatened Kelvin, who had no interest in the jewellery.","No, because Lucy handed over the jewellery voluntarily, so Jasmine did not appropriate it.","Yes, but only if Lucy herself was put in fear of being subjected to force.","No, because the threat was conditional on Lucy refusing to hand over the jewellery.","Yes, because the threat of force may be against any person, not just the owner."],"a":4,"e":"Robbery requires force or a threat of force on 'any person', who need not be the owner or the person in possession of the property. Jasmine threatened Kelvin then and there in order to steal the jewellery, so this is robbery. Appropriation can occur even where property is handed over, and a threat to use force unless a demand is met is still a threat of force."},
 {"t":"robbery-burglary","q":"Marcus breaks a ground-floor window of a house at 3am intending to steal. He reaches in with his head and one arm to grab a laptop from a table just inside, but his arm is too short and he is arrested before he can climb further in.\n\nHas Marcus entered the building for the purposes of burglary?","o":["Yes, because entry need only be effective, and putting part of his body inside is enough.","No, because he was unable to reach the laptop, so his entry was not effective.","No, because the entry must be substantial, and only part of his body was inside.","No, because the offence requires his whole body to cross the threshold of the building.","Yes, but only if he had actually succeeded in taking hold of the laptop."],"a":0,"e":"Entry need only be effective; it need not be substantial or of the whole body (Brown). A man stuck in a window with only his head and arm inside has entered, even though he could not steal anything in that position (Ryan). Marcus entered as a trespasser intending to steal, so this is burglary under s.9(1)(a)."},
 {"t":"robbery-burglary","q":"Nina, who is homeless, climbs through an open window into a stranger's house to shelter from a storm, knowing she has no permission to be there. She has no intention to steal or cause damage. Later, angry at being cold and hungry, she deliberately smashes an expensive vase.\n\nWhat offence has Nina committed in relation to the vase?","o":["Burglary under s.9(1)(b), because she entered as a trespasser and then committed criminal damage.","Burglary under s.9(1)(a), because she entered as a trespasser and damaged property inside.","Aggravated burglary, because she damaged property inside a dwelling.","Criminal damage only, because damage is not an ulterior offence for burglary under s.9(1)(b).","No offence, because she had no intention to cause damage when she entered."],"a":3,"e":"Unlawful damage is an ulterior offence only for s.9(1)(a), where the defendant must have the intention at the moment of entry. Nina had no such intent when she entered, and s.9(1)(b) covers only stealing or inflicting grievous bodily harm (or attempting them), not damage. So she commits criminal damage, not burglary."},
 {"t":"robbery-burglary","q":"Owen is shopping in a department store. When the assistant goes to the stockroom, Owen lifts the flap of the counter, goes behind it into the area marked 'Staff only' and takes cash from the till.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Owen's liability?","o":["Theft only, because he entered the store lawfully as a customer.","Burglary, because he entered part of the building as a trespasser.","Burglary only if he intended to steal when he first entered the store.","Theft only, because a counter area is not a building or part of a building.","Robbery, because he took cash from a till in the presence of other customers."],"a":1,"e":"Burglary covers entering 'part of a building' as a trespasser, such as an area from which customers are excluded (Walkington). Owen went beyond his permission by entering the staff-only area and then stole, so this is burglary under s.9(1)(b); if he intended to steal when he went behind the counter, it is also s.9(1)(a). His lawful entry into the store itself does not matter."},
 {"t":"robbery-burglary","q":"Pete breaks into a house at night intending to steal. He carries nothing with him. When the householder, Rosa, comes downstairs, Pete pushes his fingers forward inside his jacket pocket so that it looks as if he has a gun, and tells her to hand over her purse. She does so.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Pete's liability?","o":["Burglary only, because his fingers cannot be an imitation firearm.","Aggravated burglary, because his fingers were being used as a weapon of offence.","Aggravated burglary, because he pretended to have a firearm while stealing.","Aggravated burglary, because Rosa genuinely believed he had a gun.","Burglary only if he intended to use violence when he first entered the house."],"a":0,"e":"Aggravated burglary requires the defendant to have with him a firearm, imitation firearm, weapon of offence or explosive. Fingers under a jacket are part of the body, not a 'thing', so they cannot be an imitation firearm (Bentham). Pete is guilty of burglary (he entered as a trespasser intending to steal), and may also be guilty of robbery, but not aggravated burglary."},
 {"t":"criminal-damage","q":"Amira, a protester, draws large pictures on the pavement outside the town hall in water-soluble chalk. Rain would have washed them away within a few days, but the council pays contractors £400 to remove them the next morning with high-pressure water jets.\n\nHas Amira damaged the pavement?","o":["No, because the chalk was water-soluble and would have washed away naturally with the next rain.","No, because the pavement was not physically altered and its surface was left completely intact.","Yes, because the drawings impaired the pavement's value or usefulness and cost money to put right.","Yes, because any interference with property belonging to another automatically amounts to damage.","No, because the drawings were a political protest and therefore made with a lawful excuse."],"a":2,"e":"Damage includes temporary harm or impairment of value or usefulness that costs time, effort or money to put right (Hardman). The council had to pay to remove the drawings, so the pavement was damaged even though nothing was broken. A political motive is not a lawful excuse, and not every interference with property is damage."},
 {"t":"criminal-damage","q":"During an argument in a car park, Barney flicks a handful of loose dust at Clara's car. It leaves a faint film on the paintwork, which Clara wipes off with a tissue straight away. There is no mark, and nothing further is needed to restore the car.\n\nHas Barney committed criminal damage?","o":["Yes, because he intended to dirty Clara's car, and that is enough for criminal damage.","Yes, because any substance deliberately applied to another person's property is damage.","Yes, because the dust temporarily altered the appearance of the car's paintwork.","No, because criminal damage requires property to be broken or permanently impaired.","No, because the dust was removed at once with no cost or effort, so there was no damage."],"a":4,"e":"Whether there is damage is a question of fact and degree. Something wiped off at once, with no cost or effort and no effect on value or usefulness, is not damage (A (a juvenile) v R, spit on a raincoat). Damage need not be permanent, so the fourth option is wrong, but there must be some real impairment."},
 {"t":"criminal-damage","q":"Duncan's team loses a cup final. In a rage, he smashes his own large television, which he bought outright and owns alone, with a hammer. He lives alone and nobody else is endangered.\n\nWhat offence, if any, has Duncan committed?","o":["Simple criminal damage, because he intentionally destroyed property.","Aggravated criminal damage, because the offence covers damage to the defendant's own property.","Simple criminal damage, because he had no lawful excuse for smashing the television.","No offence, because simple criminal damage requires property belonging to another.","Criminal damage, but only if the value of the television was more than £5,000."],"a":3,"e":"Simple criminal damage under s.1(1) Criminal Damage Act 1971 requires property 'belonging to another'. Duncan was the sole owner, so there is no offence. Aggravated damage under s.1(2) can cover the defendant's own property, but only where the defendant intends or is reckless that life will be endangered, which is not the case here."},
 {"t":"criminal-damage","q":"Erin rents a flat. During her tenancy she fits shelves to the walls with heavy brackets. When she moves out she rips them off, damaging the plaster, because she genuinely believes the shelves are hers to take. In law they had become fixtures belonging to her landlord.\n\nIs Erin guilty of criminal damage?","o":["No, because her honest belief that it was hers means she lacked mens rea.","Yes, because a mistake about the law of fixtures is no defence to any criminal offence.","Yes, because her belief was not reasonable, as a tenant should know that fixtures belong to the landlord.","No, because a tenant can never be guilty of damaging property in her own rented home.","Yes, because she intentionally damaged the walls, which belonged to her landlord."],"a":0,"e":"The mens rea for criminal damage must extend to the property belonging to another. An honest belief that the property is the defendant's own, even if mistaken in law and unreasonable, means the defendant lacks mens rea (Smith (David)). The general rule that ignorance of the law is no excuse does not prevent such a mistake from negating mens rea."},
 {"t":"criminal-damage","q":"Felix wants to claim on his home insurance. He sets fire to the sofa in his own house at night, knowing that his lodger is asleep in the room above and realising there is a risk the fire could spread upstairs. The fire is put out quickly and the lodger is unharmed.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate offence?","o":["No offence of damage, because Felix owns the house and the sofa, though he may have committed fraud.","Simple arson, because property was damaged by fire but nobody was actually harmed.","Aggravated arson, because he was reckless as to the damage endangering life.","Aggravated arson only if the lodger's life was actually put in danger by the fire.","Simple criminal damage, because the fire was put out before serious harm occurred."],"a":2,"e":"Aggravated criminal damage under s.1(2) covers any property, including the defendant's own, where the defendant intends or is reckless as to whether life will be endangered by the damage. Committed by fire, it is aggravated arson. Life need not actually be endangered; what matters is Felix's recklessness about endangering the lodger's life (Dudley)."},
 {"t":"criminal-damage","q":"After a dispute, Gavin fires an air rifle at his neighbour's living-room window while the neighbour is sitting just behind it. The pellet breaks the window and narrowly misses the neighbour. Gavin realised the pellet might hit and injure the neighbour, but did not think about any danger from the broken glass.\n\nIs Gavin guilty of aggravated criminal damage?","o":["Yes, because he was reckless as to whether the neighbour's life would be endangered.","No, because any danger to life came from the pellet, not from the damage to the window.","Yes, because he intentionally damaged the window while the neighbour was close by.","No, because aggravated criminal damage cannot be committed with a firearm.","Yes, because the neighbour's life was actually endangered by what Gavin did."],"a":1,"e":"For s.1(2), the danger to life must come from the damage itself, not from the act that caused it (Steer). Gavin foresaw danger only from the pellet, not from broken glass, so aggravated criminal damage is not made out. He is guilty of simple criminal damage, and other offences may apply."},
 {"t":"criminal-damage","q":"On a hot day, Hana sees a dog panting heavily and collapsing in a locked car in a supermarket car park. She cannot find the owner and believes the dog will die within minutes. She smashes a rear window with a rock to get the dog out. The owner later complains.\n\nDoes Hana have a lawful excuse?","o":["No, because the dog was not her property, and protection of property covers only the defendant's own property.","No, because she should have called the police instead, so her chosen means was objectively unreasonable.","No, because lawful excuse is available only where the owner has actually consented to the damage.","Yes, but only if a court agrees that breaking the window was in fact the only way to save the dog.","Yes, because she honestly believed the dog was in immediate need of protection and her means were reasonable."],"a":4,"e":"Under s.5(2)(b) Criminal Damage Act 1971, the defendant has a lawful excuse if she damaged property to protect property (which can be another person's, and an animal is property) and believed it was in immediate need of protection and her means were reasonable. Under s.5(3), it is enough that these beliefs were honestly held. Breaking the window was capable of protecting the dog, so the objective part is also satisfied."},
 {"t":"criminal-damage","q":"Ivo sprays red paint over the gates of an arms factory. He sincerely believes that weapons made there will be used to destroy homes abroad and that his protest will help to prevent this. He is charged with criminal damage and relies on lawful excuse.\n\nIs his defence likely to succeed?","o":["No, because his act was too remote to protect any property.","Yes, because he honestly believed that the homes abroad were in need of protection.","Yes, because s.5 makes it immaterial whether his belief was justified, as long as he held it.","Yes, because damage done as a sincere political protest is a recognised form of lawful excuse.","No, because lawful excuse is available only to protect property in England and Wales."],"a":0,"e":"Although the defendant's beliefs under s.5(2)(b) are judged subjectively, whether the defendant acted 'in order to protect property' is judged objectively (Hill and Hall). Painting a factory gate could not protect homes abroad from a future attack, so the act is too remote. A sincere political belief is not a lawful excuse (Kelleher), and nothing limits the property to England and Wales."},
 {"t":"criminal-damage","q":"Jodie throws a brick through the windscreen of her friend's car while he is sitting in the driving seat with the engine running, realising the flying glass could cause him to lose control. She says her friend had asked her to smash the car so he could claim on the insurance, and she genuinely believed he consented to it being damaged in that way. She is charged with aggravated criminal damage.\n\nCan Jodie rely on her belief in his consent?","o":["Yes, because an honest belief that the owner consented is a lawful excuse to any criminal damage offence.","No, because the lawful excuse defence does not apply to aggravated criminal damage.","Yes, but only if the friend had in fact consented, rather than Jodie merely believing that he had.","No, because a belief in consent is never a defence where the damage was part of a planned insurance fraud.","Yes, because the property belonged to the person who consented, so it was not damage to another's property."],"a":1,"e":"The s.5 lawful excuse defences, including belief in consent, apply only to simple criminal damage under s.1(1), not to aggravated damage under s.1(2). So Jodie's belief cannot defend the s.1(2) charge, though it could be a lawful excuse to a simple damage charge (Denton). General defences such as self-defence could still apply to s.1(2)."},
 {"t":"criminal-damage","q":"Kai kicks a neighbour's garden wall in temper, causing damage that will cost £900 to repair. He is charged with simple criminal damage. Nothing about the wall makes it a memorial, and it was not damaged by fire.\n\nHow will the case be tried?","o":["In the Crown Court, because criminal damage is an either-way offence and the defendant always has a right to elect.","In the magistrates' court or the Crown Court, depending on Kai's choice, with a maximum of 10 years.","In the Crown Court, because any deliberate damage to a person's home is indictable only.","In the magistrates' court as if it were summary only, with no right to elect Crown Court trial.","In the magistrates' court, with a maximum sentence of 12 months' imprisonment and an unlimited fine."],"a":3,"e":"Criminal damage is normally either way, but where the value of the damage is £5,000 or less (and it is not arson or damage to a memorial), the magistrates must try it as if it were summary only (s.22 Magistrates' Courts Act 1980). Kai has no right to elect Crown Court trial, and the maximum is 3 months' imprisonment and a fine."},
 {"t":"murder","q":"Lewis is owed money by Martin. He goes to Martin's house with a baseball bat, intending to break both of Martin's legs so he will 'never forget to pay again'. He hits Martin repeatedly on the legs. Martin suffers a rare blood clot as a result of the fractures and dies. Lewis says he never wanted Martin to die.\n\nWhat is Lewis's most likely liability?","o":["Manslaughter, because he did not intend to kill and did not foresee that death might occur.","Murder, because he intended to cause grievous bodily harm, which is sufficient mens rea for murder.","Murder only if the jury finds that death was a virtual certainty and that he appreciated this.","Manslaughter, because a rare blood clot is an unforeseeable event that breaks the chain of causation.","Grievous bodily harm with intent only, because the death was not the result he intended."],"a":1,"e":"The mens rea of murder is intention to kill or intention to cause grievous bodily harm (implied malice: Vickers; Cunningham). Breaking someone's legs is really serious harm, and it was Lewis's aim, so he has the mens rea for murder even though he did not intend or foresee death. The blood clot does not break the chain: the defendant takes the victim as he finds him."},
 {"t":"murder","q":"Nicole attacks her ex-partner Olga, who is eight months pregnant, kicking her in the stomach intending to kill the unborn child. Olga survives, but the child dies in the womb as a result of the attack and is stillborn.\n\nCan Nicole be guilty of the murder of the child?","o":["Yes, because she intended to kill the child and her attack caused the child's death.","Yes, because the child was capable of being born alive and so was a reasonable creature in being.","Yes, because her intention to harm Olga transfers to the child under the doctrine of transferred malice.","No, because the child died before birth and so was not a person in being capable of being murdered.","No, because murder requires an intention to kill an adult or child who has been registered at birth."],"a":3,"e":"Murder requires the killing of a 'reasonable creature in being', which means a child fully born with an existence independent of its mother. A child who dies in the womb is not a person in being, so killing a foetus is not murder, whatever the defendant intended. Other offences, such as child destruction, may apply, and Nicole is liable for her attack on Olga."},
 {"t":"murder","q":"Paddy stabs his pregnant partner, Rhiannon, in the abdomen. She recovers, but the baby is born prematurely as a result of the stabbing, lives for three months and then dies from complications caused by the premature birth. Paddy intended to cause Rhiannon serious harm but not to harm the baby.\n\nWhat is Paddy's most likely liability for the baby's death?","o":["Murder, because his intention to cause grievous bodily harm transfers to the baby once born.","Murder, because the baby was born alive and was a person in being when she died.","No liability, because the baby was a foetus when the injury was inflicted.","No liability, because the baby died from premature birth, not from the stab wound itself.","Unlawful act manslaughter, because malice cannot transfer twice, via the foetus."],"a":4,"e":"On these facts, the House of Lords held that an intent to harm the mother cannot be transferred first to the foetus and then to the child once born (Attorney-General's Reference (No 3 of 1994)), so this is not murder. But the stabbing was an unlawful and dangerous act which caused the death of a person born alive, so unlawful act manslaughter is possible. The premature birth was caused by the stabbing, so causation is satisfied."},
 {"t":"murder","q":"Quinton fires a shotgun at Rory in a crowded street, intending to kill him. Rory dives to the ground and the shot hits Sean, a passer-by, who dies instantly. Quinton did not know Sean and had no wish to harm him.\n\nWhat is Quinton's liability for Sean's death?","o":["Murder, because his intention to kill Rory transfers to Sean, who was killed instead.","Manslaughter, because he had no intention to kill or cause serious harm to Sean.","Attempted murder of Rory only, because Sean's death was an accident.","Manslaughter, because transferred malice applies only to non-fatal offences.","Murder only if Quinton foresaw that he might hit a bystander when he fired."],"a":0,"e":"Under transferred malice, a defendant who intends to kill one person but kills another is guilty of murdering the actual victim, because the harm is of the same type (Latimer principle). Quinton's intent to kill Rory transfers to Sean. He is also guilty of the attempted murder of Rory, but that does not reduce his liability for Sean's death."},
 {"t":"murder","q":"Tariq is attacked in a car park by Ursula, who swings an umbrella at him. Tariq genuinely believes he needs to defend himself, but he pulls a knife from his pocket and stabs Ursula through the heart, intending to cause her serious harm. The jury finds that stabbing her was unreasonable force against an umbrella. Ursula dies.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Tariq's liability?","o":["He is guilty of manslaughter, because there is a partial defence of using excessive force in self-defence.","He is not guilty, because he genuinely believed that he needed to defend himself against Ursula.","Murder, because there is no partial defence of excessive force, though loss of control may apply.","He is guilty of manslaughter, because his honest belief that force was needed negates the intention for murder.","He is not guilty, because a person under attack cannot be expected to weigh the force needed to a nicety."],"a":2,"e":"Self-defence is all or nothing: if the force used was unreasonable, the defence fails entirely and, with intent to cause serious harm, the defendant is guilty of murder (Clegg). There is no partial defence of excessive self-defence. Loss of control with the fear trigger may still reduce murder to manslaughter if its elements are met."},
 {"t":"murder","q":"Vanessa and her brother-in-law William have never met before a family wedding. At the reception William insults Vanessa's late mother. Without any planning and in a single moment of anger, Vanessa picks up a heavy glass vase and smashes it onto William's head, intending to kill him. He dies.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Vanessa's liability?","o":["Manslaughter, because murder requires malice aforethought, which means premeditation.","Manslaughter, because she bore William no long-standing ill will before the wedding.","Murder only if she had formed the intention to kill before she arrived at the wedding.","Manslaughter, because an insult about a deceased relative is a qualifying trigger for loss of control.","Murder, because malice aforethought needs neither premeditation nor ill will."],"a":4,"e":"Despite its name, malice aforethought needs neither malice (ill will) nor premeditation: an intention to kill or cause grievous bodily harm formed on the spur of the moment is enough. Vanessa intended to kill, so this is murder. An insult alone is unlikely to be 'circumstances of an extremely grave character' for loss of control, and the facts do not suggest a loss of self-control."},
 {"t":"murder","q":"Xander drinks a bottle of vodka and, in a drunken fury, stabs his friend Yves once in the chest during an argument. Yves dies. The evidence suggests Xander was so drunk that he may not have formed any intention to kill or cause serious harm.\n\nIf the jury is not sure that Xander formed the intent, what is the most likely verdict?","o":["Guilty of manslaughter, as intoxication may negate murder's specific intent but not manslaughter.","Not guilty of any homicide offence, because Xander lacked the mens rea at the time of the stabbing.","Guilty of murder, because voluntary intoxication is never relevant to whether the defendant formed the mens rea.","Guilty of murder, because a drunken intent is still an intent and Xander chose to drink.","Guilty of manslaughter by diminished responsibility, because his intoxication impaired his judgment."],"a":0,"e":"Murder is a specific intent offence, so evidence of voluntary intoxication can show that the defendant did not form the intention to kill or cause grievous bodily harm. If the jury is not sure he did, he is not guilty of murder but is guilty of manslaughter, a basic intent offence to which voluntary intoxication is no defence (Majewski; Lipman). Voluntary intoxication alone cannot found diminished responsibility."},
 {"t":"murder","q":"Zara and her partner Alan deliberately stop feeding Zara's four-year-old son, intending that he will become seriously ill so that they can claim extra benefits. Alan lives with them and takes Zara's money to buy the family's food. The child dies of starvation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes their liability?","o":["Only Zara can be liable, because only a parent owes a legal duty to feed a child.","Neither can be guilty of murder, because murder cannot be committed by an omission.","Both can be guilty of murder, because each owed a duty to the child and intended serious harm.","Both can be guilty only of gross negligence manslaughter, because they did not intend the child to die.","Both can be guilty only of unlawful act manslaughter, because starving a child is an unlawful act."],"a":2,"e":"Murder can be committed by omission where the defendant is under a duty to act (Gibbins and Proctor). Zara owed a parental duty and Alan had assumed a duty by living with them and taking money for food. Both intended serious harm, which is enough for murder, so they need not have intended death. Unlawful act manslaughter cannot be based on an omission."},
 {"t":"murder","q":"Bethany is convicted of murder after a trial in the Crown Court. There is no evidence of any partial defence. Her counsel asks the judge to consider a suspended sentence because she has young children and no previous convictions.\n\nWhat sentence must the judge impose?","o":["A sentence of life imprisonment, but only if the judge considers that she is dangerous.","A sentence of life imprisonment, with a minimum term to be served before parole can be considered.","Any sentence the judge thinks just, up to a maximum of life imprisonment.","A determinate sentence of at least 15 years, with release automatically at the halfway point.","A suspended sentence, because her personal mitigation is exceptional."],"a":1,"e":"Murder carries a mandatory life sentence, so the judge has no discretion over the sentence itself. The judge sets a minimum term, using the starting points in Schedule 21 to the Sentencing Act 2020, which must be served before release on parole can be considered. Mitigation affects only the length of the minimum term; a discretionary sentence up to life applies to manslaughter, not murder."},
 {"t":"murder","q":"Callum, a British citizen living in London, travels to Spain on holiday. There he deliberately kills a Spanish national, intending to kill him, and then flies home before he can be arrested. The Spanish authorities do not seek his extradition.\n\nCan Callum be tried for murder in England and Wales?","o":["No, because criminal law applies only to offences committed within England and Wales.","No, because only the courts of the country where the killing happened can try a murder.","Yes, but only if the victim was also a British citizen.","Yes, because murder committed abroad by a British citizen can be tried in England and Wales.","Yes, but only if the Spanish authorities consent to the trial taking place in England."],"a":3,"e":"Most offences are limited to conduct in England and Wales, but murder is an exception: a British citizen who commits murder anywhere in the world can be tried here (s.9 Offences Against the Person Act 1861). The victim's nationality does not matter, and no consent from the foreign state is required."},
 {"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","q":"For two years Dina's husband, Elwyn, has belittled and threatened her. One evening he tells her he is going to hurt their daughter. Over the next hour Dina's distress builds up until she loses her self-control and hits him with a heavy pan, killing him. The prosecution argues that loss of control cannot apply because her reaction was not sudden.\n\nIs the prosecution's argument correct?","o":["Yes, because a loss of self-control must be sudden and temporary to qualify for the defence.","No, because the loss of self-control need not be sudden, though delay is relevant.","Yes, because a delay of an hour shows that Dina acted out of a considered desire for revenge.","No, because loss of control is presumed whenever the defendant has suffered a long history of abuse.","Yes, because only diminished responsibility is available in a long-term abuse case."],"a":1,"e":"Under s.54(2) Coroners and Justice Act 2009, the loss of self-control need not be sudden, which helps 'slow-burn' cases that failed under the old law of provocation. A delay is relevant evidence, as it may suggest the killing was considered rather than the result of lost control, but it does not automatically exclude the defence. There is no presumption of loss of control, and both partial defences can be considered in an abuse case."},
 {"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","q":"Felix discovers that his neighbour, Gwen, has been spreading malicious lies about him at work. He broods on it overnight. The next morning he buys a hammer, waits for Gwen outside her house and kills her with it. He says he was consumed with anger and wanted to make her pay for what she had done.\n\nIs loss of control likely to be available to Felix?","o":["Yes, because the loss of self-control no longer needs to be sudden.","Yes, because malicious lies are things said that give a justifiable sense of being seriously wronged.","No, because Felix acted in a considered desire for revenge, which excludes the defence.","Yes, because the jury must decide the issue whenever the defendant says he lost his temper.","No, because loss of control is only available where the defendant fears serious violence from the victim."],"a":2,"e":"The defence is excluded where the defendant acted in a considered desire for revenge (s.54(4) Coroners and Justice Act 2009). Felix brooded overnight, bought a weapon and lay in wait, and said he wanted to make Gwen pay, which points to considered revenge rather than a loss of self-control (compare Jewell). The anger trigger exists, but it does not help where the defence is excluded."},
 {"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","q":"Harriet's ex-partner, Ivan, has previously beaten her badly. He breaks into her flat at night carrying a knife and shouts that he is going to kill her. Terrified, Harriet loses her self-control and stabs him repeatedly with a kitchen knife, continuing after he has fallen. He dies. The jury rejects self-defence because the force was excessive.\n\nWhich partial defence is most likely to be available?","o":["Loss of control based on the anger trigger, because Ivan's threats caused her a justifiable sense of being seriously wronged.","Diminished responsibility, because fear of serious violence is a recognised medical condition.","None, because the rejection of self-defence means that the jury must convict her of murder.","Loss of control is unavailable, because she continued stabbing after Ivan had fallen to the floor.","Loss of control based on the fear trigger, because she lost control through fear of serious violence from Ivan."],"a":4,"e":"The fear trigger applies where the defendant's loss of self-control was attributable to fear of serious violence from the victim against the defendant or another identified person (s.55(3) Coroners and Justice Act 2009). It is designed for cases where the defendant overreacts in fear and self-defence fails because the force was excessive. Fear is not itself a recognised medical condition, and the failure of self-defence does not rule out the partial defence."},
 {"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","q":"Jake breaks into a house to steal. The householder, Kenneth, catches him in the act and calls him a 'pathetic thief' and 'scum'. Jake loses his self-control and strikes Kenneth with a crowbar, killing him.\n\nCan Jake rely on the anger trigger for loss of control?","o":["Yes, because the insults were things said which caused Jake to lose his self-control.","Yes, because whether Jake felt seriously wronged is judged only from his own point of view.","No, because the anger trigger only applies to things done, not to things said.","No, because a burglar caught in the act has no justifiable sense of being seriously wronged.","Yes, because a person of Jake's sex and age might have reacted in the same way to such insults."],"a":3,"e":"The anger trigger requires things done or said that constituted circumstances of an extremely grave character and caused the defendant to have a justifiable sense of being seriously wronged, both judged objectively (s.55(4)). A burglar insulted by the householder he is burgling has no justifiable sense of being seriously wronged (Bowyer). The trigger can be based on words, but the objective requirements are not met here."},
 {"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","q":"Lucas wants an excuse to attack Mo, whom he hates. In a pub he deliberately taunts Mo about his family until Mo, as Lucas intended, punches him. Lucas then claims to lose his self-control and beats Mo to death.\n\nCan Lucas rely on loss of control?","o":["No, because he incited the punch for the purpose of providing an excuse for violence.","Yes, because being punched is a thing done that can give a justifiable sense of being seriously wronged.","Yes, because the incitement exclusion applies only where the defendant is responsible for planning the whole incident.","No, because a punch in a pub fight can never amount to circumstances of an extremely grave character.","Yes, because the jury must consider any trigger, whoever caused it, once loss of control is raised."],"a":0,"e":"A trigger must be disregarded if the defendant incited the thing done or said for the purpose of providing an excuse to use violence (s.55(6) Coroners and Justice Act 2009). Lucas deliberately provoked Mo for exactly that purpose, so he cannot rely on the punch. Merely behaving badly would not exclude the defence (Dawes), but deliberate incitement for this purpose does."},
 {"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","q":"Nick, who is very drunk, is mocked by a stranger, Owain, in a bar about his recent divorce. Nick loses his self-control and hits Owain with a bottle, killing him. The defence argues that a person who had drunk as much as Nick might well have reacted in the same way.\n\nHow should the jury apply the third element of loss of control?","o":["By asking how a person with Nick's level of intoxication would have reacted, as that is part of his circumstances.","By asking whether Nick himself, given his drunkenness, could have controlled his reaction.","By asking how a sober person of Nick's sex and age with normal tolerance and self-restraint might have reacted.","By disregarding Nick's sex and age and asking only how a reasonable adult would have reacted.","By asking whether the killing was a reasonable reaction to what Owain said, given Nick's drunkenness."],"a":2,"e":"The jury asks whether a person of the defendant's sex and age, with a normal degree of tolerance and self-restraint and in the defendant's circumstances, might have reacted in the same or a similar way (s.54(1)(c)). Voluntary intoxication only reduces self-restraint, so it is excluded and the comparison is with a sober person (Asmelash). The defendant's sex and age are expressly part of the test."},
 {"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","q":"Paloma kills her partner. Psychiatrists agree she was suffering from a recognised mental disorder at the time. One says it impaired her ability to form a rational judgment to a degree that was more than trivial but not important or weighty; the other describes the impairment as minimal.\n\nIs the 'substantial impairment' element of diminished responsibility likely to be satisfied?","o":["Yes, because any impairment that is more than trivial counts as substantial.","Yes, because the existence of a recognised medical condition is enough on its own.","Yes, because the jury must accept the defence if there is any expert evidence supporting it.","No, because 'substantially' means important or weighty, not merely more than trivial.","No, because diminished responsibility requires an impairment of all three listed abilities."],"a":3,"e":"The abnormality must substantially impair the defendant's ability to understand her conduct, form a rational judgment or exercise self-control, and 'substantially' has its ordinary meaning of important or weighty, not merely more than trivial (Golds). Impairment of any one of the three abilities is enough. On this evidence the impairment falls below that level, so the element is unlikely to be met."},
 {"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","q":"Quentin is charged with murdering his brother. He has a long history of schizophrenia and the defence calls two psychiatrists to support a plea of diminished responsibility. The judge must direct the jury on the burden and standard of proof for that defence.\n\nWhat is the correct direction?","o":["The prosecution must disprove diminished responsibility beyond reasonable doubt once it is raised.","Quentin must raise sufficient evidence, and the prosecution must then disprove it on the balance of probabilities.","The prosecution must disprove the defence beyond reasonable doubt, but only if supported by medical evidence.","Quentin must prove each element of diminished responsibility beyond reasonable doubt.","Quentin must prove each element of diminished responsibility on the balance of probabilities."],"a":4,"e":"Under s.2(2) Homicide Act 1957, the burden of proving diminished responsibility is on the defendant, on the balance of probabilities (more likely than not). This contrasts with loss of control, where the defendant need only raise sufficient evidence and the prosecution must then disprove it beyond reasonable doubt. The defendant never has to prove a defence beyond reasonable doubt."},
 {"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","q":"Ryan drinks heavily one evening at home. He has no mental health history and is not dependent on alcohol. While very drunk, he stabs his flatmate to death during an argument. A psychiatrist confirms that acute intoxication is listed in the international medical classifications and that it substantially impaired Ryan's self-control.\n\nCan Ryan rely on diminished responsibility?","o":["No, because voluntary acute intoxication on its own is not a recognised medical condition for this defence.","Yes, because acute intoxication appears in the recognised medical classifications.","Yes, because his intoxication substantially impaired his ability to exercise self-control.","Yes, but only if the jury also finds that he lost his self-control as a result of a qualifying trigger.","No, because diminished responsibility is never available where the defendant had consumed any alcohol."],"a":0,"e":"Voluntary acute intoxication is not a 'recognised medical condition' for diminished responsibility, even though it appears in medical classifications (Dowds). So Ryan cannot rely on it. The position is different where the defendant has alcohol dependency syndrome, or another recognised condition that substantially impaired him despite the drink (Dietschmann), so having drunk alcohol does not rule out the defence."},
 {"t":"voluntary-manslaughter","q":"Sophie is attacked by her neighbour with a broken bottle. In fear of serious violence, she loses her self-control and stabs him with a screwdriver, intending to kill him. He survives after emergency surgery. Sophie is charged with attempted murder and wants to rely on loss of control.\n\nCan she rely on it?","o":["Yes, because she lost her self-control through fear of serious violence from the victim.","No, because loss of control is a partial defence to murder only, not to attempted murder.","Yes, and it will reduce the charge from attempted murder to attempted manslaughter.","No, because the fear trigger applies only where the defendant faces violence from a weapon such as a gun.","Yes, because partial defences apply to any offence where the defendant intended to kill."],"a":1,"e":"Loss of control and diminished responsibility are partial defences available only on a charge of murder. They do not apply to attempted murder or any other offence, so Sophie cannot use loss of control here, though the circumstances may be relevant to sentence or to self-defence. There is no offence of attempted manslaughter based on a partial defence."},
 {"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","q":"Tilly is standing on a pier. Without looking, she throws a heavy wooden crate belonging to her friend into the sea, as he had asked her to get rid of it. The crate strikes a swimmer below, who drowns. Throwing the crate in these circumstances was not itself a criminal offence.\n\nCan Tilly be guilty of unlawful act manslaughter?","o":["Yes, because throwing a heavy object into the sea was obviously dangerous to anyone swimming below.","Yes, because any act that causes death and is not lawful in civil law is an unlawful act.","Yes, because she did not check whether anyone was below, which makes her conduct reckless.","No, because there was no criminal offence, though gross negligence may apply.","No, because unlawful act manslaughter requires the defendant to intend or foresee some harm to the victim."],"a":3,"e":"The unlawful act in unlawful act manslaughter must be a criminal offence, not merely a civil wrong (Franklin). As Tilly committed no criminal offence, unlawful act manslaughter is not available, but gross negligence manslaughter may be if the other elements are met. The defendant need not foresee harm for unlawful act manslaughter, so the last option is wrong."},
 {"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","q":"Uma's ten-week-old baby develops a high fever. Uma knows he is very ill but does not call a doctor because she is afraid social services will become involved. The baby dies of an infection that would almost certainly have been treated successfully. The prosecution is considering unlawful act manslaughter.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["Unlawful act manslaughter cannot be based on an omission, but gross negligence may apply.","Unlawful act manslaughter is available, because neglecting a child is a criminal offence.","Unlawful act manslaughter is available, because a reasonable person would see a risk of harm.","Neither form of manslaughter is available, because Uma did nothing that caused the baby's death.","Murder is the correct charge, because she knew the baby was very ill and did nothing."],"a":0,"e":"Unlawful act manslaughter requires an act, not an omission (Lowe). Where the defendant fails to act, the route is gross negligence manslaughter, provided the defendant owed a duty, as a parent does. Murder would need an intention to kill or cause serious harm, which the facts do not show."},
 {"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","q":"Vikram and his close friend Will are playing with a revolver they believe to be safe because there is no bullet in the chamber opposite the barrel. Neither understands that the cylinder rotates when the trigger is pulled. As a joke, Vikram points it at Will, who laughs, and pulls the trigger. It fires and Will dies.\n\nIs Vikram guilty of unlawful act manslaughter?","o":["Yes, because pointing a gun at someone is a dangerous act that a reasonable person would recognise.","Yes, because his ignorance about the mechanism of the revolver is no excuse.","No, because there was no assault, and so no unlawful act on which to base it.","No, because Will had consented to the risk by joining in the game.","Yes, because Vikram intentionally pulled the trigger, which is a battery."],"a":2,"e":"Unlawful act manslaughter requires a criminal offence committed with its own mens rea. Will did not apprehend any force, and Vikram neither intended nor foresaw causing apprehension, so there was no assault and no unlawful act (Lamb). Gross negligence manslaughter may be considered instead."},
 {"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","q":"Two 16-year-olds, Xander and Yan, push a heavy concrete block off a motorway bridge onto the road below as a prank. It smashes through a car windscreen and kills the driver. They say it never occurred to them that anyone could be hurt.\n\nAre they guilty of unlawful act manslaughter?","o":["No, because they did not foresee any risk of harm, so the act was not dangerous.","No, because the reasonable person must be given their age, and teenagers would not see the risk.","No, because they intended only to damage property, so they had no mens rea as to harming a person.","Yes, but only if they intended to cause at least some harm to the people in the cars below.","Yes, because all sober and reasonable people would recognise a risk of some harm, whatever they foresaw."],"a":4,"e":"Dangerousness is judged objectively: would all sober and reasonable people recognise that the act risked some harm (Church)? The defendant need not recognise the risk himself (Newbury and Jones), and the reasonable person is an adult, not someone of the defendant's age (F (J) and E (N)). Criminal damage is the unlawful act, and it need not be aimed at a person."},
 {"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","q":"Zack and Ali break into a house at night to steal. Inside they find the occupant, a visibly frail 90-year-old man, and shout abuse at him while they search the house. He suffers a heart attack and dies. The medical evidence shows the shock of the burglary caused the heart attack.\n\nWas the burglary a dangerous act for unlawful act manslaughter?","o":["No, because causing fear or shock can never amount to physical harm.","Yes, because once his frailty was obvious, a risk of physical harm was plain.","No, because a reasonable person would not have foreseen a heart attack in particular.","Yes, because burglary of a dwelling is always a dangerous act for this purpose.","No, because Zack and Ali did not touch the man at any point during the burglary."],"a":1,"e":"The reasonable person is given the knowledge the defendant gained during the crime, so once the man's age and frailty were obvious, a reasonable person would see a risk of some physical harm, such as shock causing a heart attack (Watson). Contrast Dawson, where the victim appeared healthy. The kind of harm need not be foreseen precisely, and no physical contact is needed."},
 {"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","q":"Beth and Carl are drug users. At Carl's request, Beth prepares a syringe of heroin and injects it into his arm. He collapses and dies of an overdose. Beth did not intend him to come to any harm.\n\nIs Beth likely to be guilty of unlawful act manslaughter?","o":["No, because Carl consented to the injection and freely chose to take the drug.","No, because supplying drugs does not cause death where the user voluntarily takes them.","Yes, because injecting Carl was a dangerous unlawful act that caused his death.","No, because she did not intend or foresee that Carl would come to any harm.","Yes, but only if Beth was the person who originally supplied the heroin."],"a":2,"e":"Where the defendant injects the victim, the defendant commits the offence of administering a noxious thing (s.23 Offences Against the Person Act 1861), which can be the unlawful act for manslaughter (Cato). The act was dangerous and Beth's injection caused death, as there was no free act of self-injection to break the chain. The self-injection rule in Kennedy (No 2) does not apply, and foresight of harm is not required."},
 {"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","q":"Dev wants his council to rehouse him. He sets fire to his own rented council flat at night, intending to make it look like a racist attack. He does not intend to harm anyone, but the fire spreads and his neighbour dies of smoke inhalation.\n\nIs Dev likely to be guilty of unlawful act manslaughter?","o":["No, because the act was aimed at property rather than at any person.","No, because Dev lived in the flat himself, so he cannot be guilty of damaging it.","No, because he had no intention to harm anyone, so he lacked the mens rea for manslaughter.","Yes, but only if Dev foresaw that his neighbour might be harmed by the fire.","Yes, because arson was a criminal and dangerous act, even though it was aimed at property."],"a":4,"e":"The unlawful act need not be aimed at a person; arson of property is enough if it is dangerous on the objective test (Goodfellow). The flat belonged to the council, so it was another's property. A reasonable person would recognise a risk of some harm from a fire in a block of flats, and Dev's lack of foresight is irrelevant."},
 {"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","q":"Elsa, an optometrist, carries out a routine eye test on a child. She fails to examine the back of his eyes as she should have done. Had she done so, she would have seen swelling indicating a life-threatening condition. The child dies months later. Elsa did not see the swelling at any point.\n\nIs Elsa likely to be guilty of gross negligence manslaughter?","o":["No, because the serious and obvious risk of death must be judged on what she actually knew at the time.","Yes, because she would have seen the risk of death if she had carried out the examination properly.","Yes, because a professional who fails to carry out a basic part of her job is always grossly negligent in law.","No, because an optometrist does not owe a duty of care to a patient for conditions affecting life.","Yes, because her failure was a 'but for' cause of the child's death."],"a":0,"e":"For gross negligence manslaughter, it must be reasonably foreseeable that the breach gave rise to a serious and obvious risk of death, judged on the information available to the defendant at the time, not what she would have known had she not been negligent (Rose). Elsa never saw the swelling, so the risk of death was not obvious to her. She did owe a duty and breached it, but the risk element fails."},
 {"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","q":"Felix supplies drugs to his girlfriend Grace at a festival. Over several hours she becomes very ill and Felix does not seek medical help. She dies. The experts agree that if Felix had called for help, Grace would have had a good chance of surviving, but they cannot say for sure that she would have survived.\n\nIs Felix guilty of gross negligence manslaughter?","o":["Yes, because he owed her a duty and his failure to seek help was grossly negligent.","No, because the prosecution cannot prove that Grace would have survived (the jury could not be sure).","Yes, because his failure to call for help made a more than minimal contribution to her death.","No, because a person who supplies drugs never owes a duty of care to the person taking them.","Yes, because supplying drugs is an unlawful act and the drugs caused her death."],"a":1,"e":"In omission cases, the prosecution must prove causation to the criminal standard: the jury must be sure that if the defendant had acted, the victim would have survived. A good chance, or even a 90% chance, is not enough (Broughton). The experts could not say that, so causation is not proved and Felix is not guilty. Supplying drugs may give rise to a duty where the defendant creates the danger (Evans), but the case fails on causation."},
 {"t":"involuntary-manslaughter","q":"Harvey is a lorry driver smuggling ten people into the country in a sealed container. To avoid detection at the port, he closes the only air vent. When the container is opened, several of the people have suffocated. Harvey argues that he owed them no duty of care because they were all taking part in an illegal enterprise.\n\nIs Harvey's argument likely to succeed?","o":["Yes, because the law will not impose a duty of care between people engaged in a joint criminal venture.","Yes, because the victims consented to the risks of travelling in a sealed container.","Yes, because only professionals such as doctors owe a duty of care for gross negligence manslaughter.","No, because a duty of care can arise even though the parties are engaged in a criminal enterprise.","No, because unlawful act manslaughter applies, so no duty of care needs to be proved."],"a":3,"e":"In gross negligence manslaughter, an illegal enterprise does not prevent a duty of care arising (Wacker). Harvey had taken charge of the people in the container and owed them a duty, which he breached by closing the vent in a situation with an obvious risk of death. Duties are not limited to professionals."},
 {"t":"fraud","q":"Ivy orders a three-course meal in a restaurant, intending to pay. After the main course she discovers she has left her purse at home. She decides not to pay and, when the waiter goes to the kitchen, she leaves without paying.\n\nHas Ivy committed fraud by false representation?","o":["No, because she intended to pay when she ordered, so her representation was true when made.","No, because she said nothing to the waiter, and silence cannot be a representation.","No, because the restaurant was never actually deceived into giving her the food.","No, because she made no gain, since the food had already been eaten when she left.","Yes, because by staying seated she impliedly represented an intention to pay."],"a":4,"e":"A representation can be implied by conduct, and ordering a meal implies a representation of a present intention to pay (s.2(3)-(4) Fraud Act 2006). That representation continues while the defendant remains at the table, and becomes false when she changes her mind (DPP v Ray). Fraud is a conduct crime, so nobody needs to be deceived, and gain includes keeping what one has, here the price of the meal."},
 {"t":"fraud","q":"Jack hires a builder, Kerry, to repair his roof. When she finishes, Jack tells her, 'I'll pay you in full on Friday when my wages come in.' In fact he has already decided never to pay and plans to move abroad on Thursday.\n\nHas Jack made a false representation?","o":["No, because a promise about the future is not a representation of fact.","Yes, because it falsely represented his present intention to pay.","No, because Kerry had already done the work before Jack made the statement.","No, because the debt is a civil matter that Kerry can recover through the courts.","Yes, but only if Kerry actually believed him and delayed taking action as a result."],"a":1,"e":"A representation can be as to the state of mind of the person making it (s.2(3) Fraud Act 2006). 'I'll pay you on Friday' represents a present intention to pay, which was false because Jack had already decided not to. He intended to gain by keeping what he has (s.5(3)), and it does not matter whether Kerry believed him."},
 {"t":"fraud","q":"Lara buys a list of stolen credit card details on the internet. She enters one set of details on a retailer's website to order a laptop. The retailer's automated system rejects the card before any order is processed, and no human ever sees the transaction.\n\nIs Lara guilty of fraud by false representation?","o":["No, because a representation must be made to a human being, not to a computer system.","No, because the transaction was rejected, so she gained nothing and caused no loss.","Yes, because a representation can be made to a machine, and none needs to succeed.","No, because she only attempted to obtain the laptop, so the correct charge is attempted fraud.","Yes, but only if the card holder suffered some financial loss as a result."],"a":2,"e":"A representation can be made to a machine or system designed to respond to communications without human intervention (s.2(5) Fraud Act 2006). Fraud is a conduct crime, so it is complete once Lara makes the false representation dishonestly, intending to gain or cause loss, whether or not anything is obtained. So this is the full offence, not an attempt."},
 {"t":"fraud","q":"Martin sells his car privately. A buyer asks whether it has ever been in an accident. Martin says 'No, never', although he bought it second-hand and suspects it may have been repaired after a crash; he is not sure either way. In fact it was badly damaged in a crash two years ago.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Martin's position under s.2 Fraud Act 2006?","o":["He can be guilty, because he knew his statement might be untrue.","He cannot be guilty, because he did not know for certain that the car had been in an accident.","He cannot be guilty, because the buyer could have checked the car's history for himself.","He can be guilty only if the buyer goes ahead and pays a higher price for the car as a result.","He cannot be guilty, because a seller in a private sale has no duty to disclose a car's history."],"a":0,"e":"A representation is false if it is untrue or misleading and the defendant knows that it is, or might be, untrue or misleading (s.2(2) Fraud Act 2006). Martin suspected the car had been in a crash, so he knew his answer might be untrue, and he is guilty if he was also dishonest and intended to gain. A duty to disclose is relevant to s.3, not to a positive false statement under s.2."},
 {"t":"fraud","q":"Nadia owns a small manufacturing company. When renewing the company's commercial property insurance, she deliberately does not mention that the premises suffered two serious fires last year, because she knows this would raise the premium. She is not asked about previous fires on the form and says nothing false.\n\nWhich form of fraud, if any, is Nadia most likely to have committed?","o":["Fraud by false representation, because staying silent about the fires implied that there had been none.","Fraud by abuse of position, because as owner she was expected to safeguard the insurer's interests.","No fraud, because she made no false statement and was not asked about previous fires.","Fraud by failing to disclose, because as a business applicant she had a legal duty to.","No fraud, because the insurer suffered no loss unless and until a claim is made."],"a":3,"e":"Fraud under s.3 Fraud Act 2006 is committed where the defendant dishonestly fails to disclose information she is under a legal duty to disclose, intending to gain or cause loss. A business applicant for insurance must make a fair presentation of the risk (Insurance Act 2015), so Nadia was under such a duty. No loss is required, and she occupied no position of trust towards the insurer."},
 {"t":"fraud","q":"Owen sells his second-hand bicycle to a neighbour for £150. He knows the gears sometimes slip, but the neighbour does not ask about the gears and Owen does not mention it. There is no contract term or other legal duty requiring him to disclose the fault.\n\nHas Owen committed fraud?","o":["No, because he was under no legal duty to disclose the fault and made no false representation.","Yes, under s.3, because he was under a moral duty to tell the neighbour about the fault.","Yes, under s.2, because offering the bicycle for sale impliedly represented that it had no known faults.","Yes, under s.4, because a seller occupies a position of trust towards the buyer.","Yes, because he intended to gain by obtaining a higher price than the bicycle was worth."],"a":0,"e":"Fraud by failing to disclose under s.3 requires a legal duty to disclose; a moral duty is not enough. Owen made no false statement and had no contractual or other legal duty to mention the fault, so there is no s.3 fraud. An ordinary private seller does not occupy a position of trust for s.4, and an intention to gain alone is not enough without the conduct element."},
 {"t":"fraud","q":"Paula owes her landlord £1,200 in rent arrears. To stop him taking action, she sends him a fake bank screenshot showing that she has already paid. She intends to pay eventually, but wants more time. She has no intention of causing the landlord any permanent loss.\n\nDoes Paula have the intention required for fraud?","o":["No, because she intended to pay in the end, so she intended no gain or loss at all.","No, because she gained nothing new, as she already had the money in her account.","Yes, because gain includes keeping what one has, and gain or loss may be temporary.","No, because fraud requires an intention to cause a permanent loss to the victim.","Yes, but only if the landlord actually delayed taking action because of the screenshot."],"a":2,"e":"Gain and loss under s.5 Fraud Act 2006 can be temporary or permanent, and gain includes keeping what one has. By sending the fake screenshot Paula intended to keep her money for longer, which is an intention to gain, and to cause the landlord a loss by not getting what he might get. Fraud is a conduct crime, so the landlord need not have acted on it."},
 {"t":"fraud","q":"Quinn is a purchasing manager for a supermarket chain. A supplier offers the chain a valuable discount for a large order. Quinn deliberately lets the offer lapse without telling anyone, so that his brother's company, which competes with that supplier, can win the contract instead. He makes no false statement.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Quinn's position?","o":["He has committed no offence, because he made no false statement and took no positive step.","He has committed fraud by false representation, because his silence misled his employer.","He has committed theft, because he appropriated the benefit of the discount for his brother.","He may have committed fraud by abuse of position, which can be committed by an omission.","He has committed fraud by failing to disclose information, but only if his contract required him to report offers."],"a":3,"e":"Fraud by abuse of position under s.4 Fraud Act 2006 is committed where the defendant occupies a position in which he is expected to safeguard another's financial interests and dishonestly abuses it, intending to gain or cause loss. An employee responsible for purchasing occupies such a position, and s.4(2) states that the abuse can be by omission. No false statement or deception is required."},
 {"t":"fraud","q":"Rhys, a builder, has done work for two elderly sisters for many years and they trust him completely. He quotes them £25,000 for new windows, knowing that a fair price would be about £8,000. He says nothing untrue about the work, and the sisters agree to the price.\n\nCan Rhys be guilty of fraud by false representation?","o":["No, because there is no legal limit on the price a builder may charge for his work.","Yes, because in a relationship of trust, quoting a price can impliedly represent that it is fair.","No, because he made no express statement that was false.","No, because the sisters were free to obtain other quotes before agreeing to the price.","Yes, because any price substantially above the market rate is automatically a false representation."],"a":1,"e":"A representation may be implied by conduct (s.2(4) Fraud Act 2006). Where there is a relationship of mutual trust, quoting a price can impliedly represent that it is fair, so a grossly excessive quote can be a false representation (Silverman). That depends on the relationship of trust, not on the price alone; in an ordinary arm's length deal a high price is not fraud."},
 {"t":"fraud","q":"Sara works in a shop. She submits false expense claims to her employer, a large multinational company, for taxi journeys she never took. She genuinely believes that what she is doing is acceptable because 'everyone does it' and the company makes huge profits.\n\nIs Sara dishonest for the purposes of fraud?","o":["No, because she genuinely believed her conduct was acceptable, which means she was not dishonest.","No, because she did not realise that ordinary decent people would regard what she did as dishonest.","Yes, but only if she knew that her conduct was a breach of her employer's rules.","No, because a belief that the victim would not suffer any real loss prevents dishonesty.","Yes, if ordinary decent people, knowing what she knew, would regard her conduct as dishonest."],"a":4,"e":"Dishonesty is decided by the Ivey test: the jury establishes the defendant's actual knowledge or belief as to the facts, then asks whether her conduct was dishonest by the standards of ordinary decent people. The defendant's own view of what is acceptable is irrelevant, and there is no need for her to realise that others would regard it as dishonest (Barton and Booth). Making false claims for journeys never taken would clearly be dishonest by those standards."},
 {"t":"general-defences","q":"Theo decides to kill his business partner, Ursula, and buys a knife. To give himself the courage to go through with it, he drinks a bottle of whisky, then goes to her house and stabs her to death. He was very drunk at the time of the stabbing.\n\nCan Theo rely on his intoxication to reduce murder to manslaughter?","o":["Yes, because murder is a specific intent offence and he may not have formed the intent at the time he stabbed her.","Yes, because he was so drunk at the time of the stabbing that his intent cannot be proved.","No, because intoxication is never relevant to any offence of homicide.","No, because he formed the intent to kill while sober and drank to give himself Dutch courage.","No, but only because he was voluntarily intoxicated when he bought the knife."],"a":3,"e":"Where the defendant forms the intention while sober and then gets drunk to give himself courage to carry it out, he cannot rely on his intoxication even for a specific intent offence (Attorney-General for Northern Ireland v Gallagher). Theo is guilty of murder. Intoxication can be relevant to murder in other cases, as it may show the defendant did not form the intent."},
 {"t":"general-defences","q":"Vera's drink is secretly spiked with a strong drug by Will at a party. The drug lowers her inhibitions. She then punches another guest, Xena, intending to hurt her, because she has long resented her. She says she would never have done it if she had not been drugged.\n\nCan Vera rely on involuntary intoxication?","o":["Yes, because involuntary intoxication is a complete defence to any offence.","Yes, because she would not have committed the offence if her drink had not been spiked.","No, because she still formed the mens rea, and a drugged intent is still an intent.","No, because involuntary intoxication is a defence only to specific intent offences.","Yes, but only if the drug was one that is normally known to cause aggression."],"a":2,"e":"Involuntary intoxication is relevant only if it prevented the defendant from forming the mens rea. Where the defendant still formed it, intoxication is no defence, however much it lowered her inhibitions (Kingston). Vera intended to hurt Xena, so she has no defence. Where involuntary intoxication does prevent mens rea, it can be a defence to both specific and basic intent offences."},
 {"t":"general-defences","q":"Yolanda's soft drinks at a work party are secretly laced with vodka by a colleague. She has never drunk alcohol before and does not realise she is intoxicated. Becoming confused and disoriented, she swings her arms wildly, hits a waiter and breaks his nose. She did not intend or foresee hitting anyone.\n\nCan Yolanda rely on her intoxication on a charge of assault occasioning actual bodily harm?","o":["Yes, because her intoxication was involuntary and she did not form the mens rea for the battery.","No, because s.47 is a basic intent offence, to which intoxication of any kind is never a defence.","No, because she should have realised from the taste that her drinks had been laced.","No, because a person who becomes intoxicated is judged as if she were sober.","Yes, but only if she can prove that she would not have hit the waiter had she been sober."],"a":0,"e":"Involuntary intoxication, such as a spiked drink, can be a defence to any offence, including a basic intent offence like s.47, if the defendant did not form the mens rea. The Majewski rule that intoxication is no defence to basic intent offences applies only to voluntary intoxication. Yolanda did not intend or foresee applying force, so she lacks the mens rea for the battery."},
 {"t":"general-defences","q":"Zane knows he is drinking homemade cider at a barbecue but does not realise how strong it is. He becomes very drunk and pushes a guest into a fire, causing serious burns. He is charged with inflicting grievous bodily harm under s.20. He argues that his intoxication was involuntary because he did not know the cider's strength.\n\nIs Zane's argument likely to succeed?","o":["Yes, because he did not know how strong the cider was, so he did not choose to become intoxicated.","Yes, because underestimating the strength of a drink is treated in the same way as having a drink spiked.","No, because s.20 is a strict liability offence, so his state of mind is irrelevant.","Yes, but only if a reasonable person would also have underestimated the strength of the cider.","No, because he knew he was drinking alcohol, so he was voluntarily intoxicated."],"a":4,"e":"A person who knows he is drinking alcohol but underestimates its strength is voluntarily intoxicated (Allen). Section 20 is a basic intent offence, so voluntary intoxication is no defence and the jury asks whether Zane would have foreseen some harm if sober (Majewski; Richardson and Irwin). Section 20 is not strict liability: it requires foresight of some harm."},
 {"t":"general-defences","q":"After a stressful week, Aled takes several of his partner's prescribed sedative tablets to calm down. He has never taken them before and has no reason to think they might affect him except by making him drowsy. Unexpectedly, they make him confused and aggressive, and he smashes a neighbour's window. He is charged with criminal damage.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the relevance of his intoxication?","o":["It is irrelevant, because criminal damage is a basic intent offence and he took the tablets voluntarily.","It is irrelevant, because the tablets were not prescribed for him, which makes his intoxication unlawful.","It is a complete defence, because any intoxication from medication is involuntary.","It may be relied on to show lack of mens rea, unless he was reckless in taking the tablets.","It is relevant only if the offence charged is one of specific intent."],"a":3,"e":"Taking a drug normally regarded as non-dangerous, such as a sedative, which has an unexpected effect may be treated like involuntary intoxication (Hardie). Aled can rely on it to show he lacked mens rea, even for a basic intent offence like criminal damage, unless the jury finds he was reckless in taking the tablets. The rule for alcohol and dangerous drugs (Majewski) does not apply."},
 {"t":"general-defences","q":"After a long night out, Bianca, who is very drunk, picks up a black coat from the cloakroom of a club and takes it home. She genuinely believes it is her own coat, which is similar. In fact it belongs to another guest. She is charged with theft.\n\nCan Bianca's intoxication help her?","o":["No, because voluntary intoxication is no defence to any offence involving property.","Yes, because her drunken belief can show she was not dishonest.","No, because her belief was caused by voluntary intoxication, so it must be disregarded.","No, because a sober person would have realised that the coat was not hers.","Yes, but only if her drinks had been spiked without her knowledge."],"a":1,"e":"Theft is a specific intent offence: it requires dishonesty and an intention to permanently deprive. If Bianca genuinely believed the coat was hers, even because she was drunk, she was not dishonest and did not intend to deprive another, so she is not guilty. The rule disregarding drunken mistakes applies to self-defence (s.76(5) CJIA 2008), not to this situation."},
 {"t":"general-defences","q":"Callum, who is sober, sees a man pinning a teenager to the ground in a dark street and punching him. Callum believes the teenager is being mugged and pulls the man off, punching him once. In fact the man is a plain-clothes police officer lawfully arresting the teenager. A reasonable person might not have made the same mistake.\n\nCan Callum rely on defence of another?","o":["No, because the officer was acting lawfully, so there was no unlawful attack to defend against.","Yes, because he is judged on the facts as he honestly believed them to be.","Yes, but only if the jury finds that his belief was based on reasonable grounds.","No, because his mistake was not one that a reasonable person would have made.","No, because defence of another applies only to protecting members of the defendant's own family."],"a":1,"e":"The defendant is judged on the facts as he genuinely believed them, whether or not the belief was mistaken or reasonable (s.76(3)-(4) CJIA 2008; Gladstone Williams). The jury then decides whether a single punch was reasonable force in the circumstances as Callum believed them. The reasonableness of his belief matters only to whether it was genuinely held, and the defence protects any person, not just family."},
 {"t":"general-defences","q":"Dafydd is approached in an empty car park by Euan, who has threatened him before. Euan pulls out a knife and says 'You're dead'. Before Euan can move any closer, Dafydd punches him in the face, breaking his nose.\n\nCan Dafydd rely on self-defence?","o":["No, because he struck first, and self-defence requires the defendant to have been attacked before using force.","No, because he should have run away rather than using force against Euan.","Yes, because he was attacked with a knife, so any degree of force was justified.","No, because Euan had not yet used any force, so there was no attack to defend against.","Yes, because a pre-emptive strike can be justified if the force used was reasonable."],"a":4,"e":"The defendant need not wait to be struck before acting: a pre-emptive strike can be justified (Beckford). Dafydd honestly believed force was necessary, and a single punch against a man threatening him with a knife is likely to be reasonable. There is no duty to retreat, and the force must still be reasonable, not unlimited."},
 {"t":"general-defences","q":"Fiona is confronted in the street by Gary, who swings a punch at her. She could easily have walked away into a nearby shop, but instead she stands her ground and pushes Gary away, causing him to fall and graze his elbow. She is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm.\n\nWhat is the relevance of Fiona's opportunity to retreat?","o":["It means she cannot rely on self-defence, because she was under a duty to retreat if she safely could.","It is irrelevant, because the opportunity to retreat is never considered in self-defence.","It is a factor for the jury in deciding whether her force was reasonable, but there is no duty to retreat.","It means self-defence is available only if she first showed she was unwilling to fight.","It means her force will be unreasonable unless she can show that retreat was impossible."],"a":2,"e":"There is no duty to retreat, but the possibility that the defendant could have retreated is a factor the jury may take into account in deciding whether the force was reasonable (s.76(6A) CJIA 2008). The defendant does not have to show she was unwilling to fight (Bird). A push in response to an attempted punch is likely to be reasonable force."},
 {"t":"general-defences","q":"Harry is approached outside a pub by Ian, who shoves him and shouts abuse. Harry genuinely believes he is about to be attacked. He responds by hitting Ian repeatedly over the head with a heavy bottle, fracturing his skull. The jury finds the force was disproportionate but not grossly so.\n\nCan Harry rely on self-defence?","o":["No, because outside a householder case, disproportionate force is never reasonable.","Yes, because force that is disproportionate but not grossly so may still be reasonable.","Yes, because he honestly believed he was about to be attacked, which is enough on its own.","Yes, because a person in danger cannot be expected to weigh the exact force needed to a nicety.","No, because he could have retreated into the pub rather than using any force at all."],"a":0,"e":"In an ordinary case, force that is disproportionate in the circumstances as the defendant believed them is not reasonable (s.76(6) CJIA 2008). The more generous rule, under which disproportionate but not grossly disproportionate force may be reasonable, applies only in householder cases involving a dwelling. Harry's honest belief satisfies the first stage, but the force fails the second."},
 {"t":"parties","q":"Jamal is walking home when he sees his friend Kyle repeatedly punching a stranger in the street. Jamal stops and watches, saying nothing and doing nothing. Kyle does not notice Jamal is there. The stranger suffers serious injuries.\n\nIs Jamal liable as an accessory to Kyle's offence?","o":["Yes, because he deliberately chose to watch the attack rather than intervene.","No, because mere presence, without intended and actual encouragement, is not enough.","Yes, because he was a friend of Kyle and his presence at the scene was evidence of support.","Yes, because as a witness he was under a duty to try to stop the offence.","No, but only because Jamal did not know the victim personally."],"a":1,"e":"Mere presence at the scene of a crime is not enough for accessory liability, even if the defendant does nothing to stop it, unless the presence actually encourages the principal and the defendant intends it to (Clarkson). Kyle did not even notice Jamal, so there was no encouragement. There is no general duty to intervene to prevent a crime."},
 {"t":"parties","q":"Liam owns a powerful sports car. He lets his friend Mira drive it while he sits in the passenger seat. Mira drives at 110 mph on a busy road. Liam, who could tell her to slow down or stop, says nothing because he is enjoying the speed. Mira is convicted of dangerous driving.\n\nCan Liam be liable as an accessory?","o":["No, because he did nothing except sit in the passenger seat while Mira drove.","No, because only the person actually driving the car can commit dangerous driving.","Yes, because any passenger who fails to object to dangerous driving is an accessory.","No, because Mira did not ask for or need Liam's encouragement to drive fast.","Yes, because as owner he had a right to control Mira and chose not to use it."],"a":4,"e":"A person with a legal right to control the principal's actions who deliberately fails to exercise it may be an accessory (Du Cros v Lambourne; Tuck v Robson). As owner, Liam could have stopped Mira and chose not to, which can amount to abetting. An ordinary passenger with no right of control would not be liable simply for staying silent."},
 {"t":"parties","q":"Nora wants to steal jewellery from her neighbour's house. She persuades her eight-year-old nephew, Ollie, to climb through the neighbour's open window and bring out a jewellery box, telling him it is a game. Ollie does so and gives the box to Nora.\n\nWhat is Nora's liability?","o":["She is the principal, because Ollie is an innocent agent under the age of 10.","She is an accessory to theft by Ollie, because she procured him to take the box.","She is not guilty, because Ollie was the person who physically took the jewellery box.","She is guilty only of encouraging a child to commit an offence, not of theft itself.","She is an accessory to burglary, but Ollie is the principal offender."],"a":0,"e":"An innocent agent is a person who carries out the actus reus but is not guilty, for example because they are under 10, the age of criminal responsibility. The person who uses the innocent agent is the principal. Nora is therefore guilty as principal, and Ollie is not guilty of any offence."},
 {"t":"parties","q":"Petra encourages her friend Rob to 'put his foot down' while he drives her home. Rob speeds up a little but continues to drive carefully and within the speed limit. A child suddenly runs into the road and is hit. The court finds that Rob was not driving carelessly or dangerously and could not have avoided the child.\n\nCan Petra be guilty as an accessory to any driving offence?","o":["Yes, because she encouraged Rob to drive faster, intending him to do so.","Yes, because her encouragement was a cause of the accident in which the child was injured.","No, because accessory liability depends on an offence actually being committed by the principal.","Yes, because an accessory can be convicted even where the principal is acquitted.","No, but only because she did not know that a child might run into the road."],"a":2,"e":"Accessory liability is derivative: there must be a principal offence (at least its actus reus) for the defendant to be an accessory to (Thornton v Mitchell). Rob committed no driving offence, so there is nothing for Petra to have aided or encouraged. An accessory can be convicted where the principal is acquitted for lack of mens rea or a personal defence, but not where no offence was committed at all."},
 {"t":"parties","q":"Sam sells Tanya a set of industrial bolt-cutters. Tanya tells him she plans to use them to break into a commercial building to steal, but does not say which building or when. Sam intends to help her and supplies them. A month later Tanya uses them to break into a warehouse and steal stock.\n\nCan Sam be liable as an accessory to the burglary?","o":["No, because he did not know which building Tanya would break into or when.","No, because he was simply carrying on his business by selling goods to a customer.","No, because he played no part at the time the burglary was committed.","Yes, because he intended to assist and knew she intended a crime of the type that was committed.","Yes, because anyone who supplies equipment later used in a crime is automatically an accessory."],"a":3,"e":"An accessory need not know the precise details of the crime; it is enough that the defendant knew the principal intended a crime of the type committed (Bainbridge). Sam intended to help Tanya commit a burglary and knew its essential nature, so he can be an accessory. Aiding can take place before the crime, and a seller who intends to assist is not protected by acting in the course of business (National Coal Board v Gamble)."},
 {"t":"parties","q":"Ursula pays a hitman, Vince, £10,000 to kill her husband. Vince goes to the husband's house intending only to pretend to carry out the killing and keep the money. When the husband confronts him, Vince panics and deliberately stabs him to death.\n\nIs Ursula guilty of murder?","o":["No, because Vince had abandoned the plan, so her counselling did not cause the killing.","Yes, because counselling needs no causal link, and Vince acted within its scope.","No, because Vince killed in panic, which was outside the scope of her agreement with him.","Yes, but only of manslaughter, because she was not present at the scene of the killing.","No, because she can only be guilty of conspiracy to murder, not murder itself."],"a":1,"e":"Counselling requires no causal link between the counselling and the offence, only that the principal acted within the scope of the authority or advice given and not by accident (Calhaem). Vince deliberately killed the person Ursula wanted dead, so she is guilty of murder as an accessory and receives the mandatory life sentence. Presence at the scene is not required for counselling."},
 {"t":"parties","q":"Wes and Xander agree to rob a post office. Wes knows Xander is carrying a knife, and they agree that Xander will use it to stab anyone who tries to stop them, so as to cause serious injury. During the robbery a customer tackles Xander, who stabs him, intending serious harm. The customer dies. Wes did not want anyone to die.\n\nIs Wes guilty of murder?","o":["No, because he did not personally stab the customer or intend him to die.","No, because he only foresaw that Xander might use the knife, which is not enough for murder.","Yes, because he intended, conditionally, that Xander would cause serious harm if they were challenged.","No, because a conditional intention to assist is not enough for accessory liability.","Yes, because he took part in a robbery, and any death in the course of a joint enterprise is murder."],"a":2,"e":"After Jogee, an accessory is guilty of murder if he intended to assist or encourage the principal to kill or cause grievous bodily harm, and that intention can be conditional. Wes agreed that Xander would stab anyone who intervened to cause serious injury, so he had the mens rea for murder. Mere foresight is no longer enough, but here there was an intention, not just foresight."},
 {"t":"parties","q":"Yara and three friends get into an argument outside a bar, and a spontaneous fight starts with another group. Yara throws a punch, then thinks better of it and walks away up the street before anyone is seriously hurt. After she has gone, one of her friends kicks a man in the head, killing him.\n\nCan Yara have effectively withdrawn from the joint attack?","o":["No, because withdrawal is only effective if she clearly told the others she was leaving.","No, because she took part in the violence at the start, so she remains liable for its consequences.","No, because withdrawal requires her to take reasonable steps to prevent the killing.","No, because withdrawal is never possible once any violence has been used.","Yes, because in spontaneous violence walking away before the fatal blow may be enough to withdraw."],"a":4,"e":"In cases of spontaneous violence, a party who walks away before the fatal act may withdraw without communicating it (Mitchell and King). The stricter requirement of clear, timely communication, and possibly steps to prevent the crime, applies to planned crimes (Becerra; Rook). Yara left before the fatal kick, so she may have withdrawn."},
 {"t":"parties","q":"Zach and Aaron plan to burgle a house. Zach gives Aaron a knife to use 'if anyone gets in the way'. During the burglary the occupant appears. Zach shouts 'Someone's coming, let's go!' and climbs out of the window. Aaron stays and stabs the occupant to death with the knife.\n\nHas Zach effectively withdrawn?","o":["No, because his last-minute words did not neutralise his help, including the knife.","Yes, because his words made clear to Aaron that he no longer wished to take part.","Yes, because he left the house before the fatal stabbing took place.","Yes, because withdrawal is always effective if the defendant leaves the scene before the principal offence.","No, because once a crime has been agreed it is never possible to withdraw from it."],"a":0,"e":"For a planned crime, withdrawal requires timely and unequivocal communication, and where the defendant has given material help, such as a weapon, the defendant may need to take reasonable steps to neutralise it or prevent the crime (Becerra). Shouting 'let's go' as the occupant appeared was neither timely nor sufficient, especially having supplied the knife. Withdrawal is possible in principle, but not on these facts."},
 {"t":"parties","q":"Bryn agrees to drive the getaway car for a bank robbery planned by Cara. He waits outside the bank while Cara threatens staff with a gun and takes cash, then drives her away. Bryn never enters the bank.\n\nWhat is Bryn's liability?","o":["He is guilty only of assisting an offender, because he played no part in the robbery itself.","He is guilty of theft only, because he never used or threatened force on anyone.","He is guilty of robbery, but the maximum sentence is lower for an accessory than for a principal.","He is guilty of robbery as an accessory, and can be tried and punished as if he were the principal.","He is not guilty of robbery, because an accessory must be present at the scene of the force."],"a":3,"e":"Under s.8 Accessories and Abettors Act 1861, anyone who aids, abets, counsels or procures an indictable offence is liable to be tried and punished as a principal offender. By agreeing to drive the getaway car, Bryn aided the robbery, so he is guilty of robbery and faces the same maximum sentence as Cara. He need not use force himself."},
 {"t":"attempts","q":"Dylan is angry with his ex-wife's new partner, Ed. He gets into the passenger seat of Ed's car, points a loaded shotgun at him and says 'You're not going to like this.' The safety catch is still on. Ed grabs the barrel and pushes it away before Dylan can do anything more.\n\nIs Dylan guilty of attempted murder?","o":["No, because he had not yet reached the last act, as he still had to release the safety catch and pull the trigger.","No, because pointing a gun is merely preparatory to firing it.","No, because Ed was never in any real danger while the safety catch was still on.","No, because attempted murder requires that the victim was actually injured.","Yes, if he intended to kill, because pointing a loaded gun at Ed was more than merely preparatory."],"a":4,"e":"An attempt requires an act more than merely preparatory, done with intent to commit the full offence (s.1(1) Criminal Attempts Act 1981). The defendant need not reach the last act; on very similar facts, pointing a loaded gun at the victim was held to be more than merely preparatory although the defendant still had to remove the safety catch and pull the trigger (Jones). Attempted murder requires an intention to kill."},
 {"t":"attempts","q":"Fergus is found by a caretaker in the toilets of a primary school, carrying a knife, rope and masking tape. He admits he planned to abduct a pupil. He has not spoken to or approached any child.\n\nIs Fergus likely to be guilty of attempted kidnapping?","o":["Yes, because he was on the school premises with equipment and a clear plan.","No, because he had only got ready and equipped himself, not tried to commit it.","Yes, because his presence in the school was the last act before the kidnapping.","Yes, because he admitted his intention, which is enough on its own for an attempt.","No, because kidnapping is a summary offence and cannot be attempted."],"a":1,"e":"The question is whether the defendant has actually tried to commit the offence, or only got ready, put himself in a position, or equipped himself (Geddes). On very similar facts, a man found in school toilets with a knife, rope and tape, who had not contacted any pupil, had not gone beyond preparation. Intention alone is not enough, and kidnapping is indictable, so it can be attempted."},
 {"t":"attempts","q":"Gemma and Hal plan to burgle a house. At 2am they go to the back door, and Hal starts levering at the lock with a crowbar while Gemma keeps watch. A neighbour sees them and calls the police, who arrest them before the door opens.\n\nAre Gemma and Hal guilty of attempted burglary?","o":["No, because they had not yet entered the building, which is the actus reus of burglary.","No, because forcing a lock is merely preparation for entering the house.","No, because the burglary was never completed as the door did not open.","Yes, but only Hal, because he alone was physically damaging the door.","Yes, because by forcing the lock they had embarked on the crime proper."],"a":4,"e":"Physically starting on the door or lock of the target premises is usually enough to show that the defendant has embarked on the crime proper (Boyle and Boyle; Tosti). The defendant does not need to have entered. Gemma, keeping watch as part of the joint plan, is guilty as an accessory to the attempt."},
 {"t":"attempts","q":"Imran agrees to deliver a package he believes contains cocaine to a buyer, for payment. Police seize the package on the way and find it contains only flour. Imran is charged with attempting to supply a controlled drug.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the likely outcome?","o":["Not guilty, because it was impossible to supply cocaine when the package contained only flour.","Guilty, because he is judged on the facts as he believed them, and factual impossibility is no defence.","Not guilty, because supplying flour is not a crime and he cannot be convicted of an imaginary offence.","Guilty only of possession of flour with intent to deceive the buyer.","Not guilty, because he did not know what the package contained for certain."],"a":1,"e":"The defendant can be guilty of an attempt even though the facts make the full offence impossible, and he is judged on the facts as he believed them (s.1(2)-(3) Criminal Attempts Act 1981; Shivpuri). Imran believed the package contained cocaine, so he intended to supply a controlled drug. Legal impossibility, where the intended act is not a crime at all, is different; supplying cocaine is a crime."},
 {"t":"attempts","q":"Jess creeps into her neighbour Kenny's bedroom at night and stabs him repeatedly, intending to kill him. Unknown to Jess, Kenny had died of a heart attack in his sleep an hour earlier.\n\nIs Jess guilty of attempted murder?","o":["No, because you cannot kill a person who is already dead, so the offence is legally impossible.","No, because her actions did not cause Kenny's death.","No, because the correct offence is interfering with a dead body, not attempted murder.","Yes, because she is judged on the facts as she believed them to be.","Yes, but only if she had some reason to think that Kenny might still be alive."],"a":3,"e":"Factual impossibility is no defence to an attempt; the defendant is judged on the facts as she believed them (s.1(3) Criminal Attempts Act 1981). Jess believed Kenny was alive and intended to kill him, and stabbing him was more than merely preparatory. Killing a person is a crime, so this is not legal impossibility."},
 {"t":"attempts","q":"Leon swings a punch at Max in the street, but Max does not see it coming and Leon misses completely. Max only learns what happened from a witness afterwards. The police want to charge Leon with attempted battery.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Leon's liability?","o":["He is guilty of attempted battery, because he did a more than merely preparatory act intending to hit Max.","He is guilty of assault, because he intended Max to apprehend immediate force.","He has committed no offence of attempt, because battery is summary only and cannot be attempted.","He is guilty of battery, because swinging a punch at someone close by counts as applying force.","He is guilty of attempted assault occasioning actual bodily harm, because he tried to injure Max."],"a":2,"e":"Only offences triable on indictment can be attempted, so there is no offence of attempting a summary-only offence such as battery or common assault (s.1(4) Criminal Attempts Act 1981). Max did not apprehend any force, so there is no assault, and no force was applied, so there is no battery. Attempted s.47 would need proof of intent to cause actual bodily harm, which the facts do not show."},
 {"t":"attempts","q":"Nina plans to set fire to her former employer's office. She breaks in at night, pours petrol over the carpet and takes out a lighter. Suddenly she has second thoughts, puts the lighter away and leaves without starting a fire. She is later identified from CCTV.\n\nCan Nina rely on her change of mind?","o":["No, because the attempt was complete once she went beyond mere preparation with intent.","Yes, because she voluntarily abandoned the plan before causing any damage by fire.","Yes, because withdrawal is a defence to an attempt if the defendant communicates it before the full offence.","Yes, because she never actually lit the lighter, so she did not go beyond preparation.","No, because her change of mind was caused by fear of being caught, not by genuine remorse."],"a":0,"e":"Once the defendant has done an act more than merely preparatory with intent to commit the offence, the attempt is complete, and abandoning the plan, even voluntarily, is no defence; it is relevant only to sentence. Pouring petrol in the target building with a lighter in hand is well beyond preparation. Withdrawal is a concept that applies to accessories, not to attempts."},
 {"t":"attempts","q":"Oliver throws a stone at a cat sitting on his neighbour's greenhouse, intending to scare it off. He realises the stone might break the glass but does not want that to happen. The stone misses both the cat and the greenhouse.\n\nIs Oliver guilty of attempted criminal damage to the greenhouse?","o":["Yes, because recklessness is enough for criminal damage, so it is also enough for an attempt.","Yes, because he did an act more than merely preparatory and foresaw that damage might result.","No, because an attempt needs intention as to the result, not recklessness.","No, because throwing a stone is merely preparatory to damaging property.","No, because a cat is not property and so there could be no criminal damage."],"a":2,"e":"An attempt requires an intention to commit the full offence, and recklessness as to a result or consequence is not enough (Mohan), even where the full offence can be committed recklessly. Oliver foresaw a risk of damage but did not intend it, so there is no attempted criminal damage. Had the glass broken, recklessness would have been enough for the full offence."},
 {"t":"attempts","q":"Paige throws a petrol bomb at a car in which she knows four people are sitting, intending to destroy the car. She realises the fire might endanger their lives but does not intend that. The bomb misses and hits a wall, causing no damage.\n\nCan Paige be guilty of attempted aggravated arson?","o":["Yes, because recklessness as to endangering life is enough for the attempt.","No, because an attempt always requires intention as to every element of the full offence.","No, because the bomb hit a wall and caused no damage, so no life was ever endangered.","Yes, but only if a reasonable person would have realised that the occupants' lives would be endangered.","No, because she did not intend to endanger life, so the most she can be guilty of is attempted simple arson."],"a":0,"e":"For an attempt, the defendant must intend the conduct and result of the full offence, but where the full offence requires only recklessness as to a circumstance, recklessness as to that circumstance is enough (Attorney-General's Reference (No 3 of 1992); Khan). Paige intended to damage property by fire and was reckless as to endangering life, which is enough for attempted aggravated arson. The recklessness required is subjective, not what a reasonable person would realise."},
 {"t":"attempts","q":"In a crowded café, Quentin slips his hand into a stranger's open handbag and rummages through it, intending to take anything of value he finds. The bag contains only tissues and a hairbrush, so he takes nothing and moves away. He is arrested by a plain-clothes officer.\n\nWhat is Quentin's most likely liability?","o":["Theft of the tissues and hairbrush, because he appropriated them by handling them.","No offence, because a conditional intention to steal is not an intention to permanently deprive.","No offence, because it was impossible to steal anything of value from the handbag.","Attempted theft of some or all of the contents of the handbag, as his conditional intention is enough.","Attempted theft of the hairbrush, because that was an item he actually touched."],"a":3,"e":"A conditional intention to steal whatever is worth taking is not enough for theft of specific items the defendant did not want (Easom), but it is enough for an attempt if the charge is framed as attempting to steal 'some or all of the contents' (Attorney-General's References (Nos 1 and 2 of 1979)). Impossibility is no defence to an attempt. A charge of attempting to steal the specific hairbrush would fail because he never intended to take it."}
];

;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["crimprac"] = [
 {"t":"police-station-detention","q":"Liam is arrested in the street at 14:00 on Monday on suspicion of burglary of a shop. He is kept in a police van while officers search the area and arrives at the police station at 15:15. The custody officer authorises his detention at 15:45 to obtain evidence by questioning.\n\nWhen will the basic 24-hour limit on Liam's detention without charge expire?","o":["14:00 on Tuesday","15:45 on Tuesday","15:15 on Tuesday","21:45 on Monday","15:15 on Wednesday"],"a":2,"e":"The detention clock runs from the 'relevant time', which is the time of arrival at the first police station (15:15) or 24 hours after arrest, whichever is earlier (PACE s.41). So the 24-hour limit expires at 15:15 on Tuesday. 14:00 wrongly uses the time of arrest, 15:45 uses the authorisation time (which only matters for reviews), and 21:45 on Monday is when the first review would be due."},
 {"t":"police-station-detention","q":"Marcus is arrested for driving with excess alcohol, a summary-only offence, and taken to the police station. He asks for his partner to be told that he has been arrested. The inspector on duty wants to delay this because he suspects Marcus's partner may move Marcus's car before officers can examine it.\n\nCan the police delay Marcus's right to have his partner informed?","o":["No, because the offence Marcus is suspected of is summary only","Yes, for up to 36 hours, because an inspector has authorised the delay","Yes, but only if a superintendent rather than an inspector authorises it","Yes, for up to 24 hours, because Marcus has not yet been charged","No, because the right to have someone informed can never be delayed"],"a":0,"e":"The right to have someone informed (PACE s.56) can be delayed only where the suspect is detained for an indictable offence (either way or indictable only), has not been charged, and an inspector has reasonable grounds for one of the listed harms. Drink driving is summary only, so no delay is possible at all. The right can be delayed in other cases, so E is wrong, and the rank of the officer is irrelevant where the offence is summary."},
 {"t":"police-station-detention","q":"Nadia is arrested for fraud by false representation (an either-way offence). The relevant time is 08:00 on Monday and her detention was authorised at 08:30. A single review of her detention took place at 14:00. At 18:00 on Monday, a superintendent wishes to authorise her continued detention up to 36 hours, as the investigation is being carried out diligently and further questioning is needed.\n\nCan the superintendent authorise the extension at 18:00?","o":["Yes, because the extension is being authorised within the first 24 hours","Yes, because the offence is indictable and the investigation is diligent","No, because only the magistrates' court can extend detention beyond 24 hours","No, because an extension can be authorised only in the final hour of the 24-hour period of detention","No, because an extension cannot be authorised before the second review has taken place"],"a":4,"e":"A superintendent's extension to 36 hours (PACE s.42) requires an indictable offence, a diligent and expeditious investigation and continuing necessity, and it must be given before the 24 hours expire but not before the second review. Only one review has taken place, so the extension cannot yet be given, even though the other conditions appear to be met. The magistrates are needed only beyond 36 hours, and there is no 'final hour' rule."},
 {"t":"police-station-detention","q":"Owen is arrested for assault occasioning actual bodily harm on his neighbour. After interview, the police need about six weeks for medical evidence before the Crown Prosecution Service can make a charging decision. They are concerned that Owen may approach the neighbour in the meantime.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate way for the police to deal with Owen?","o":["Release him under investigation with a condition not to contact the neighbour","Release him on pre-charge bail with a condition not to contact the neighbour","Keep him in police detention until the medical evidence is available","Charge him now and release him on bail to attend the magistrates' court","Apply to the magistrates for a warrant of further detention for six weeks"],"a":1,"e":"Pre-charge bail lets the police release a suspect with a date to return and conditions, such as no contact with the complainant, where that is necessary and proportionate; the initial period is 3 months. Release under investigation carries no conditions, so A is impossible. Detention cannot last six weeks (the absolute maximum is 96 hours), and charging requires sufficient evidence, which the police do not yet have."},
 {"t":"police-station-detention","q":"Priya is charged with robbery at 19:00 on Tuesday. The custody officer refuses her police bail because there are reasonable grounds for believing she will fail to appear at court. The local magistrates' court sits every weekday morning.\n\nWhen must Priya be brought before the magistrates' court?","o":["Within 24 hours of charge, whether or not a court is sitting","Within 36 hours of the relevant time, unless a superintendent extends","Only once a superintendent has reviewed the refusal of bail","As soon as practicable, and no later than the first court sitting after charge","Within 96 hours of the relevant time, as for detention before charge"],"a":3,"e":"A person charged and refused police bail must be brought before the magistrates' court as soon as practicable, and in any event not later than the first sitting after charge, which here is Wednesday morning. The 24, 36 and 96-hour limits apply to detention before charge, not to a charged person awaiting court. No superintendent review of the bail refusal is required."},
 {"t":"police-station-detention","q":"Quinn, aged 32, is arrested for theft and brought to the police station. When the custody officer explains his rights, Quinn says he does not want a solicitor because 'it will only slow things down and I want to get home'.\n\nWhat must the custody officer do?","o":["Tell Quinn he can speak to a solicitor by telephone, ask why he declines, and remind him of the right before any interview","Record that Quinn has declined legal advice, after which he need not be offered it again at any point during his detention in custody","Insist that Quinn speaks to the duty solicitor, because a suspect cannot be interviewed without legal advice","Arrange an appropriate adult for Quinn, because refusing legal advice suggests he may be vulnerable","Tell Quinn that if he declines advice now he cannot change his mind later in his detention"],"a":0,"e":"Where a suspect declines legal advice, the custody officer must point out that they can speak to a solicitor by telephone and ask why they do not want advice; the suspect must also be reminded of the right before every interview. The right to free legal advice is a continuing right that can be exercised at any time, so B and E are wrong. Advice cannot be forced on an adult, and declining it is not itself a sign of vulnerability."},
 {"t":"police-station-detention","q":"Rhys, aged 15, is arrested for burglary of a dwelling. An inspector has reasonable grounds for believing that if Rhys's father is told of the arrest, the father will hide the stolen property. The inspector authorises a delay in Rhys's right to have someone informed and wants to delay telling Rhys's father anything.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the police's duty to inform Rhys's father?","o":["The father need not be told while the delay authorised by the inspector lasts, up to 36 hours","The father can be told only after a superintendent confirms the inspector's decision","The father must still be told of the arrest, where Rhys is held and why, as this duty cannot be delayed","The father must be told only once Rhys has been interviewed with a solicitor present","The father need not be told, because a social worker can act as appropriate adult instead"],"a":2,"e":"For a juvenile, the police must inform the person responsible for the child's welfare of the arrest, where the child is held and why (PACE s.57), and must call an appropriate adult. This duty is separate from the s.56 right and cannot be delayed, even when s.56 is lawfully delayed. Using a social worker as appropriate adult does not remove the duty to inform the parent."},
 {"t":"police-station-detention","q":"Sam is arrested for robbery and asks for a named solicitor. A superintendent authorises a delay in access to legal advice, recording as the only reason that 'this solicitor is known to advise clients to give no comment interviews, which would hamper the investigation'.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the superintendent's decision?","o":["It is lawful, because robbery is indictable and Sam has not yet been charged","It is lawful, because a superintendent rather than an inspector authorised it","It is lawful, provided that Sam is allowed access to the named solicitor no later than 24 hours after the relevant time","It is unlawful, because legal advice can only be delayed for summary offences","It is unlawful, because delay cannot be authorised on the ground that the solicitor may advise silence"],"a":4,"e":"Delay of legal advice needs an indictable offence, no charge, a superintendent's authority and reasonable grounds for believing the solicitor would cause one of the listed harms (interference with evidence or people, alerting suspects, hindering recovery of property). Code C Annex B says expressly that delay cannot be authorised because the solicitor might advise the suspect not to answer questions. Having the right rank and type of offence is not enough without a proper ground."},
 {"t":"police-station-detention","q":"Tom has been detained without charge for suspected arson for eight hours. His solicitor telephones the police station shortly before Tom's second review is due and asks to argue that his continued detention is no longer necessary.\n\nWho should carry out the review, and must the solicitor be heard?","o":["The investigating inspector, who may choose whether to hear the solicitor","An inspector not directly involved in the investigation, who must let the solicitor make representations","The custody officer, who need not hear representations before Tom is charged","A superintendent, because this is the second review, who must hear representations only if it is held in person","Any officer of inspector rank, who may accept representations only in writing"],"a":1,"e":"Before charge, reviews are carried out by an inspector who is not directly involved in the investigation (PACE s.40). Before deciding, the review officer must give the suspect (unless asleep) or their solicitor the opportunity to make representations about why detention should end. The custody officer reviews detention only after charge, and an officer involved in the investigation cannot act as review officer."},
 {"t":"police-station-detention","q":"Uma is arrested for common assault, a summary-only offence, and arrives at the police station at 10:00 on Thursday. At 08:00 on Friday, officers tell the custody officer that they need another six hours to trace and interview an independent witness before deciding whether to charge.\n\nWhat must happen by 10:00 on Friday?","o":["A superintendent may extend Uma's detention to 22:00 on Friday","The police may apply to the magistrates for a warrant of further detention","Uma may be held until the next review, which must be within 9 hours","Uma must be charged or released, because her detention cannot be extended","Uma must be charged, because the police have already interviewed her"],"a":3,"e":"The basic limit of detention without charge is 24 hours from the relevant time. Extensions by a superintendent (to 36 hours) and by the magistrates (up to 96 hours) are available only for indictable offences, so for summary-only common assault there is no extension. Uma must be charged or released (with or without bail) by 10:00 on Friday; there must be sufficient evidence before she can be charged, so E is wrong."},

 {"t":"police-interview","q":"You are advising Vince at the police station before his interview about a burglary. In private, Vince admits to you that he committed the burglary, but he wants to tell the police that he was at his brother's house all evening and asks you to help him prepare that account.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate course of action?","o":["Help him prepare the alibi, because the duty of confidentiality prevents you disclosing his admission","Refuse to help with a false alibi, but you may advise him to answer 'no comment'; withdraw if he insists on the lie","Tell the investigating officer that Vince has admitted the offence, as you must not mislead the police","Advise Vince to give a prepared statement containing the alibi, which is less risky than answering questions","Withdraw immediately and without explanation, because you can never act for a client who admits guilt"],"a":1,"e":"A solicitor must not mislead anyone, including the police (SRA Code of Conduct para 1.4), so cannot help a client put forward a positive account the solicitor knows is false. The solicitor can still act and advise 'no comment' or put the police to proof, because silence misleads no one; if the client insists on lying, the solicitor must withdraw. Confidentiality means the solicitor must not tell the police what Vince said, so C is wrong."},
 {"t":"police-interview","q":"Wanda, aged 28, is arrested for criminal damage. Her support worker tells the custody officer that Wanda has a learning disability and often agrees with whatever she is told. Wanda says she understands everything and does not want anyone with her in interview.\n\nWhat should the custody officer do?","o":["Proceed with the interview without an appropriate adult, since Wanda is an adult and has declined one","Proceed without an appropriate adult provided Wanda has a solicitor present in the interview","Postpone the interview until a doctor confirms in writing that Wanda has a mental disorder","Treat Wanda as vulnerable and arrange for an appropriate adult to attend her interview","Ask the support worker to sit in on the interview as Wanda's solicitor instead"],"a":3,"e":"If an officer has any reason to suspect that a person may be vulnerable (for example, may have difficulty understanding or be prone to suggestion or compliance), they must be treated as vulnerable and an appropriate adult must be called (Code C para 1.13(d)). A suspect's own wish not to have one does not remove the requirement, and a solicitor cannot act as appropriate adult. No medical diagnosis is needed first."},
 {"t":"police-interview","q":"Xander, aged 14, is arrested for theft from a shop. Before the police arrived, he told his mother that he had taken the goods. His mother is at the police station. The available adults are listed below.\n\nWho is the most appropriate person to act as Xander's appropriate adult?","o":["A local authority social worker who has no involvement in the case","His mother, because she is his parent and is already at the police station","The duty solicitor who will advise Xander in the interview","A civilian detention officer employed by the police at the station","His brother, aged 17, who has no involvement in the case"],"a":0,"e":"A person who has received admissions from the suspect before acting cannot be the appropriate adult, so Xander's mother is ruled out. The next choice is a local authority social worker. The suspect's solicitor and anyone employed by the police cannot act, and an appropriate adult must be 18 or over, which excludes the brother."},
 {"t":"police-interview","q":"During a recorded interview of Yasmin for fraud, her solicitor repeatedly answers questions on Yasmin's behalf before she can speak, despite the officer's requests to stop. The interviewing officer, a sergeant, wants the solicitor removed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the solicitor may be removed?","o":["The sergeant conducting the interview may remove the solicitor at once, and Yasmin must then continue to be interviewed without legal advice","The custody officer may remove the solicitor, but only with Yasmin's written consent","The solicitor cannot be removed, because a suspect's right to legal advice cannot be limited during interview","The interview must stop and the matter must be referred to the magistrates' court to decide","A superintendent (or an inspector if none is available) may remove the solicitor after speaking to them; Yasmin may consult another"],"a":4,"e":"A solicitor can be removed only if their conduct prevents or unreasonably obstructs proper questions being put, for example by answering on the client's behalf (Code C paras 6.9-6.11). The decision is for a superintendent, or an inspector if no superintendent is readily available, after speaking to the solicitor, and the suspect must be given the chance to consult another solicitor. The sergeant cannot decide, and the court has no role."},
 {"t":"police-interview","q":"Zac is arrested for possession of drugs with intent to supply. When his solicitor sees him before interview, Zac is sweating and shaking, says he is 'clucking' from heroin withdrawal, and says he will 'say whatever they want' if it gets him out of the station.\n\nWhat should the solicitor do first?","o":["Advise Zac to answer 'no comment' and let the interview go ahead as planned","Prepare a short written statement for Zac and let the interview go ahead","Ask the custody officer to have a healthcare professional assess whether Zac is fit to be interviewed","Ask the custody officer to call an appropriate adult, because drug withdrawal makes Zac a vulnerable person","Advise Zac to answer questions quickly so that he can leave the station sooner"],"a":2,"e":"A suspect may be unfit for interview if questioning could harm them or anything they say might be unreliable because of their condition, such as drug withdrawal (Code C Annex G). The solicitor should raise fitness and ask for a healthcare professional's assessment before any interview takes place. Advising on silence or a prepared statement comes after fitness is resolved, and withdrawal alone does not make an adult 'vulnerable' in the appropriate adult sense."},
 {"t":"police-interview","q":"Ava and Ben are arrested for the same robbery. Their solicitor has already advised Ava, who says Ben planned it and she was only a lookout under pressure from him. Ben now asks the same solicitor to advise him and says Ava was the organiser.\n\nWhat should the solicitor do?","o":["Advise Ben as well, provided the solicitor does not tell him what Ava said","Advise Ben as well, because the police station is only an early stage of the case","Advise Ben instead of Ava, because Ben is likely to face the more serious charge","Decline to advise Ben, because their interests conflict, and keep Ava's information confidential","Advise both together in one consultation so their accounts can be made consistent"],"a":3,"e":"Each suspect blames the other, so there is a conflict of interest and the solicitor cannot act for both (SRA Code of Conduct para 6.2). The solicitor continues to owe Ava confidentiality (para 6.3) and must not use her information for Ben. Keeping their accounts apart does not cure the conflict, and helping them make their accounts consistent would be improper."},
 {"t":"police-interview","q":"Cal is arrested at 02:00 in the back garden of a house that has just been burgled. In interview he is asked why he was in the garden. The officer intends to ask the court to draw an inference if Cal does not explain why he was there.\n\nWhat must the officer do for an inference to be possible from Cal's failure to account for his presence?","o":["Nothing more, because the standard caution given at the start of the interview is enough","Give Cal a special warning, in ordinary language, about the consequences of failing to account for his presence","Ask Cal to sign a written statement confirming that he refuses to explain his presence","Obtain a superintendent's written authority before asking Cal any questions about his presence in the garden at night","Wait until Cal is charged and then ask him to explain his presence on charge"],"a":1,"e":"An inference from failure to account for presence at a place (CJPOA s.37) needs the suspect to have been arrested there and given a special warning in ordinary language: the offence, the fact to be explained, the officer's belief that it may be due to taking part in the offence, that a court may draw an inference, and that a record is being made. The standard caution is enough for s.34, but not for s.36 or s.37. No superintendent authority is needed."},
 {"t":"police-interview","q":"Dee is being interviewed about a fraud. The interview has run for two and a half hours without a pause. Dee tells her solicitor she is tired and finding it hard to concentrate. There is no urgency in the investigation.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate step for the solicitor to take?","o":["Ask for a short break, as interviews should normally include refreshment breaks about every two hours","Advise Dee to answer 'no comment' to all further questions because she is tired","Ask for the interview to be stopped until Dee has had a continuous 24 hours' rest","Ask for the interview to be ended, because interviews may not exceed two hours in total","Say nothing, as the solicitor may only intervene to challenge improper questions"],"a":0,"e":"Code C requires breaks at meal times and short refreshment breaks approximately every two hours (para 12.8), as well as 8 hours' continuous rest in any 24 hours (not 24 hours' rest). The solicitor may intervene to ask for a break, so E is wrong. There is no rule that an interview may not exceed two hours in total."},
 {"t":"police-interview","q":"Eli is arrested for kidnapping and asks for a solicitor. Before the solicitor arrives, a superintendent lawfully authorises an urgent interview because waiting would put the victim, who has not been found, at risk of physical harm.\n\nWhich caution should be given to Eli at the start of this interview?","o":["The usual caution, because the urgent interview has been properly and lawfully authorised in writing by a superintendent","The usual caution, together with a special warning about his failure to answer","The restricted caution, because no inference can be drawn while he has not been allowed to consult a solicitor","No caution, because an urgent interview is not an interview under caution","The restricted caution, but only if Eli is under 18 or vulnerable"],"a":2,"e":"Where a suspect has asked for legal advice and is interviewed before being allowed to consult a solicitor, no inference from silence can be drawn (CJPOA s.34(2A)), so the restricted caution is used: 'You do not have to say anything, but anything you do say may be given in evidence.' The authorisation makes the interview lawful but does not allow inferences. An urgent interview is still an interview and must be under caution."},
 {"t":"police-interview","q":"Fay is arrested for theft from her employer. As she was arrested at work, she said to the arresting officer, 'I only took what they owed me.' The officer recorded this. Fay is now about to be interviewed at the police station.\n\nWhat should the interviewing officer do about Fay's remark at the start of the interview?","o":["Nothing, because remarks made before the caution was given can never be used","Ask Fay's solicitor to confirm whether Fay made the remark","Leave the remark out unless Fay raises it herself during the interview","Put the remark to Fay only if she chooses to answer questions, as otherwise it would breach her right to silence","Put the remark to Fay and ask her to confirm or deny it, and whether she wants to add anything"],"a":4,"e":"At the start of the interview, the officer must put to the suspect any significant statement or silence that occurred before the interview and ask them to confirm or deny it and whether they wish to add anything (Code C para 11.4). Fay's remark is capable of being used against her, so it is a significant statement. The solicitor must not answer for the client, and the duty does not depend on whether the suspect is answering questions."},

 {"t":"identification","q":"Gus is arrested after a fight outside a pub in which a man was injured. A witness says she saw the fight and could identify the man who threw the first punch. In interview, Gus says he was there and was involved, but that the other man attacked him first.\n\nMust the police hold an identification procedure?","o":["Yes, because the witness says she is able to identify the attacker","Yes, because Gus is a known suspect who is available to take part","No, because the witness did not know Gus before the incident","No, because Gus admits being at the scene, so the dispute is about what happened","No, because identification procedures are only needed for indictable-only offences"],"a":3,"e":"A procedure must normally be held where a witness may be able to identify a suspect who disputes being the person seen (Code D para 3.12). It need not be held where it would serve no useful purpose, for example where the suspect admits being at the scene and the dispute is about what happened. Here identity is not in issue, only who started the fight."},
 {"t":"identification","q":"Hana tells the police that she recognised the man who snatched her phone as Ivan, whom she says she knew at school ten years ago. Ivan is arrested. He denies the offence and says he has never met Hana and did not attend her school.\n\nShould an identification procedure be held?","o":["Yes, because Ivan disputes that Hana knows him, so it is not an undisputed recognition","No, because Hana has named Ivan and so this is a recognition case","No, because Hana's identification is strong evidence that Ivan is the snatcher","No, because an identification procedure could only confirm what Hana already says","Yes, but only if Ivan asks for one in writing after being given the Code D notice"],"a":0,"e":"The 'no useful purpose' exception for recognition applies only where it is not disputed that the suspect is already well known to the witness. Ivan disputes knowing Hana at all, so a procedure must be held. The duty is mandatory once the conditions are met; the police cannot skip a procedure because they think it would add nothing, and it does not depend on a request from the suspect."},
 {"t":"identification","q":"Jack disputes that he is the man who robbed a newsagent. The shopkeeper says he could identify the robber. Video identification can be arranged within a few days. Jack's solicitor asks for an identification parade instead, without giving any particular reason.\n\nWhich procedure should the identification officer arrange?","o":["An identification parade, because the suspect, through his solicitor, is entitled to choose which procedure is used","A group identification, because it is the most natural way to view a suspect","A video identification, unless a parade is practicable and more suitable, having considered the representations","A confrontation, because the shopkeeper has said he can identify the robber","Whichever procedure the investigating officer in the case considers will be quickest"],"a":2,"e":"Video identification is the normal first choice and must be offered unless it is not practicable, or a parade is both practicable and more suitable (Code D paras 3.14-3.16). The identification officer must consider the suspect's representations, but the suspect does not choose. Confrontation is a last resort, and the investigating officer takes no part in arranging the procedure."},
 {"t":"identification","q":"Kara is suspected of an assault. The victim's first description, recorded before any procedure, says the attacker had 'a large spider tattoo on her neck'. Kara has such a tattoo. The identification officer is putting together images for a video identification.\n\nWhat should the identification officer do about the tattoo?","o":["Leave Kara's tattoo visible, because the witness specifically described it","Use only images of other people who have no tattoos at all, so that the comparison with Kara is fair","Abandon the video identification and hold a confrontation instead","Show the witness an image of the tattoo alone first, to check that it matches","Conceal the tattoo, or replicate a similar one, so that it appears the same on all the images"],"a":4,"e":"Where a suspect has a distinctive feature such as a tattoo or scar, it can be concealed or replicated on all images so that the suspect does not stand out (Code D Annex A). Leaving it visible on Kara alone would make the procedure unfair and could lead to an application to exclude under s.78 PACE. Showing the tattoo on its own first would contaminate the witness's memory."},
 {"t":"identification","q":"Leon has agreed to take part in a video identification for a robbery he denies. His solicitor asks you, a trainee at the same firm, what the solicitor is entitled to do in relation to the procedure.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the solicitor's entitlements?","o":["To be present with Leon in the room while each witness views the images, so that both can observe the witness","To see all the images before they are shown, to object to any of them, and to attend the viewing","To choose the other people whose images are included in the set","To be told after each viewing whether the witness picked out Leon","To question each witness about their first description before the viewing"],"a":1,"e":"Under Code D Annex A, the suspect's solicitor must be given a reasonable opportunity to see the complete set of images before they are shown and to object, and may attend the viewing. The suspect is not present at the viewing. Selecting the images is for the identification officer, and witnesses must not be told whether they picked out the suspect."},
 {"t":"identification","q":"Mo is charged with burglary and the only evidence against him is a video identification by a neighbour. When preparing for trial, his solicitor discovers that the neighbour's first description of the burglar was never recorded before the procedure took place.\n\nWhat is the most likely consequence of this?","o":["The identification is automatically inadmissible because Code D was breached","The prosecution must hold a new video identification before the trial","The defence may apply to exclude it under s.78 PACE, and if admitted the jury may be directed about the breach","The breach is irrelevant once the neighbour has positively identified Mo","The case must be stopped as an abuse of process, because the breach means the identification can never be tested fairly"],"a":2,"e":"Recording the first description before any procedure is required by Code D para 3.1, so that it can be compared with the suspect's appearance. A breach does not make the evidence automatically inadmissible; the defence can apply under s.78 PACE to exclude it if its admission would make the proceedings unfair, and if it is admitted the judge can direct the jury about the breach. A stay for abuse of process is reserved for much more serious misconduct."},
 {"t":"identification","q":"An hour after a street robbery, the police have no suspect. An officer shows the victim, Nell, an album of six photographs and says, 'Have a good look at number four; we've had our eye on him for a while.' Nell picks out number four.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the procedure used?","o":["It complied with Code D, because photographs may be shown where there is no known suspect","It complied with Code D, because Nell made her own choice from the album","It breached Code D only because a video identification should have been used instead","It breached Code D only because the suspect's solicitor was not present","It breached Code D, because Nell should have been shown at least 12 photographs and not prompted"],"a":4,"e":"Photographs may be shown where there is no known suspect, but under Code D Annex E the witness must be shown at least 12 images at a time and must not be prompted or have their attention directed to any one. Both rules were broken here. There was no known suspect, so a video identification could not yet have been held, and there was no suspect's solicitor to be present."},
 {"t":"identification","q":"Olly and Pete are brothers of very similar appearance. Both are arrested for the same assault and both dispute being involved. The police decide to show them together in a single video identification to the one eyewitness.\n\nHow many other people's images must be included?","o":["At least 12","At least 8","At least 16","At least 10","At least 6"],"a":0,"e":"A video identification normally includes the suspect and at least eight other people who resemble them. Where two suspects of roughly similar appearance are shown together, at least 12 other people must be included (Code D Annex A). The other figures are not the required numbers."},
 {"t":"identification","q":"Quentin disputes that he is the man who robbed a bank. The video identification is arranged and run by Detective Constable Rowe, the officer in the case. The witness picks out Quentin.\n\nWhy was the procedure in breach of Code D?","o":["Because a detective constable may run a procedure only with Quentin's written consent","Because a video identification procedure should always be run by the superintendent in charge of the police station","Because a video identification must always be run by the Crown Prosecution Service","Because it should have been run by an officer of at least inspector rank who is not involved in the investigation","Because only the magistrates' court can authorise a video identification"],"a":3,"e":"Code D requires the identification officer to be at least an inspector and not involved in the investigation; officers involved in the investigation must take no part. Running the procedure through the officer in the case is a breach, which could support an application to exclude the identification under s.78 PACE. Consent, the CPS and the court are irrelevant to who runs the procedure."},
 {"t":"identification","q":"Rory is arrested for robbery. The witness described the robber as having 'a full black beard and long hair'. Rory, who has a beard and long hair, is given the Code D notice for a video identification next week. He tells his solicitor he plans to shave and have his hair cut short before then.\n\nWhat should the solicitor tell Rory?","o":["That he is free to change his appearance, and nothing can be said about it at trial","That significantly altering his appearance may be given in evidence, and the police may make other arrangements","That deliberately changing his appearance after receiving the notice is an offence of perverting the course of justice","That the police must cancel the procedure if his appearance has changed","That he must ask the identification officer's permission before having a haircut"],"a":1,"e":"The Code D notice warns the suspect that if they significantly alter their appearance before the procedure, this may be given in evidence, and the police may make other arrangements to test the witness's ability to identify (for example using images taken earlier, or a covert procedure). It is not automatically an offence, and it does not force the police to cancel the procedure."},

 {"t":"bail","q":"Sasha is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm. The magistrates grant her conditional bail. The prosecutor believes that Sasha will contact the complainant and wants to challenge the decision.\n\nWhat must the prosecutor do?","o":["Tell the court orally at the end of the hearing, before Sasha is released, and serve written notice within 2 hours","Serve written notice of appeal on the Crown Court and the defence within 24 hours of Sasha being released from custody on bail","Ask the magistrates to reconsider the decision at the next hearing using the same information","Apply to the High Court by way of case stated within 21 days of the decision","Ask the police to arrest Sasha again so that bail can be reconsidered"],"a":0,"e":"The prosecution can appeal against the grant of bail for an imprisonable offence (Bail (Amendment) Act 1993 s.1). It must give oral notice at the end of the hearing, before the defendant is released, and serve written notice within 2 hours; the defendant stays in custody and the appeal is heard within 48 hours. The other routes are not the correct procedure for challenging a grant of bail."},
 {"t":"bail","q":"Taz is charged with three burglaries of commercial premises, all committed between midnight and 4 a.m. He has a stable home address and a good record of attending court. The prosecution says there is a real risk he will commit further night-time burglaries if released.\n\nWhich condition is most likely to address the prosecution's objection?","o":["Reporting to the local police station three times a week, at times set by the court","A surety from his father in the sum of £1,000","A curfew at his home address overnight, possibly electronically monitored","Surrender of his passport to the police","Residence at his home address"],"a":2,"e":"Bail conditions should be aimed at the specific risk raised. The objection is a risk of offending at night, which an overnight curfew (often with electronic monitoring) directly addresses. Reporting, a surety, residence and passport surrender mainly address the risk of absconding, which is not the concern here."},
 {"t":"bail","q":"Uri is on unconditional bail for theft. He fails to attend the magistrates' court on the date of his trial. When he is later arrested, he says he forgot about the hearing. There is no other explanation.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Uri's position?","o":["He has breached a condition of his bail, so he can be arrested and brought before the court but has committed no offence","He has committed no offence, because his bail was unconditional","He can be dealt with only by having his bail withdrawn, not by any penalty","He has committed an offence only if he had previously failed to surrender","He may have committed the offence of failing to surrender without reasonable cause, and a warrant is likely"],"a":4,"e":"Failing to surrender to custody without reasonable cause is an offence under Bail Act 1976 s.6, whether bail was conditional or unconditional, and the court will usually issue a warrant for arrest (s.7(1)). Forgetting is unlikely to be reasonable cause. Breaching a condition, by contrast, is not itself an offence; that is the s.7(3) arrest route."},
 {"t":"bail","q":"Vera, aged 24, is charged with theft of a bicycle worth £150 from a front garden. She has one previous conviction for failing to surrender, three years ago, and no other convictions. The prosecution accepts that the likely sentence is a fine or a community order, but objects to bail on the ground that she will fail to surrender.\n\nWhat is the best argument for Vera's solicitor?","o":["Vera's previous failure to surrender was over two years ago, so it must be ignored","The fail to surrender exception cannot be relied on, because there is no real prospect of a custodial sentence","The court cannot refuse bail for theft, because theft is a summary-only offence","Vera has a right to bail which can be refused only if she is charged with an indictable-only offence","A surety would remove all risk, so the court must grant unconditional bail"],"a":1,"e":"For a defendant aged 18 or over, the main exceptions in Schedule 1 Part I para 2 (including failure to surrender) do not apply where there is no real prospect of a custodial sentence (para 1A). The prosecution accepts the likely sentence is a fine or community order, so this is Vera's strongest point. Theft is either way, not summary only, and an old failure to surrender is relevant to bail, not automatically ignored."},
 {"t":"bail","q":"Will, aged 35, first appears before the magistrates' court charged with murder. He has no previous convictions, lives with his family and has a job. His solicitor wants to make a full bail application at this hearing.\n\nWhat will happen?","o":["The magistrates will hear the application and may grant bail with strict conditions","The magistrates will grant bail only if Will provides a substantial surety","The magistrates will remand Will in custody for 8 days before hearing any application","The magistrates cannot grant bail; Will must be sent to the Crown Court, where a judge will decide bail","The magistrates will refer the bail decision to the High Court because the offence is murder"],"a":3,"e":"Only a Crown Court judge can grant bail to a person charged with murder (Coroners and Justice Act 2009 s.115). The magistrates must send the case to the Crown Court, where the judge applies the test that bail may not be granted unless there is no significant risk of Will committing an offence likely to cause physical or mental injury to another. The magistrates cannot hear the application themselves."},
 {"t":"bail","q":"Xavi is charged with rape. Twelve years ago he was convicted of rape and served a prison sentence. He now lives with his partner, works full time and has complied with every condition of his licence.\n\nOn what basis can the court grant Xavi bail?","o":["Only if the prosecution fails to show substantial grounds for one of the usual exceptions","Only if a Crown Court judge rather than the magistrates hears the application","Only if Xavi agrees to electronic monitoring and a curfew","Only if Xavi's previous conviction is spent","Only if the court is satisfied that there are exceptional circumstances which justify it"],"a":4,"e":"Where a defendant charged with murder, attempted murder, manslaughter, rape or certain other serious sexual offences has a previous conviction for one of those offences, bail may be granted only if the court is satisfied there are exceptional circumstances (CJPOA 1994 s.25). This displaces the usual presumption in favour of bail. It is not limited to the Crown Court; that restriction applies to murder."},
 {"t":"bail","q":"Yvette is charged with fraud. The magistrates are minded to grant bail but are concerned that she may abscond. Her aunt is willing to help and asks the solicitor what it would mean for her to stand as a surety.\n\nWhat is the best explanation of a surety?","o":["The aunt promises to pay a sum fixed by the court, which she may forfeit if Yvette fails to surrender","The aunt must deposit a sum of money with the court before Yvette is released, which is returned after the trial","The aunt agrees to let Yvette live with her and to report any breach of conditions","The aunt becomes liable to be prosecuted if Yvette commits an offence on bail","The aunt promises to pay Yvette's fine if Yvette is convicted at trial"],"a":0,"e":"A surety (Bail Act 1976 s.3(4)) is a person who promises to pay a sum (a recognisance) to the court if the defendant fails to surrender; they may lose that sum if the defendant absconds. Depositing money in advance is a security (s.3(5)), which is different. A surety is not liable for the defendant's offences or fines."},
 {"t":"bail","q":"Zane is arrested late on Sunday and charged with robbery. He appears before the magistrates on Monday morning. The prosecution has been unable, for lack of time, to check the address he has given or obtain his record, and asks for a short remand in custody.\n\nWhich exception to the right to bail is the prosecution relying on?","o":["That there are substantial grounds to believe Zane will fail to surrender because his address is unverified","That Zane must be kept in custody for his own protection","That Zane was on bail at the time of the alleged offence","That it has not been practicable to obtain sufficient information because of lack of time","That Zane has been arrested for breaching a bail condition"],"a":3,"e":"Schedule 1 Part I para 5 allows the court to refuse bail where it has not been practicable to obtain sufficient information to take the bail decision for want of time since the proceedings began. It is typically used at a first appearance and lasts only until the information can be obtained. The facts do not support the other exceptions."},
 {"t":"bail","q":"Ada is charged with burglary of a shop. At the time of the alleged burglary, she was on bail for an unrelated theft. She has a fixed address and no previous failures to surrender, and the likely sentence for the burglary includes immediate custody.\n\nWhich exception to the right to bail is most clearly available to the prosecution?","o":["That Ada has previously failed to surrender in these proceedings and is therefore likely to abscond again","That Ada should be kept in custody for her own protection","That Ada was already on bail in other criminal proceedings when the offence was allegedly committed","That there is insufficient information to make a decision","That Ada is already serving a custodial sentence"],"a":2,"e":"Schedule 1 Part I para 2A provides that a defendant charged with an indictable imprisonable offence need not be granted bail if it appears they were on bail in other criminal proceedings when the offence was allegedly committed. Being on bail at the time is also a factor the court weighs. None of the other exceptions fits the facts."},
 {"t":"bail","q":"Ben is charged with an either-way offence of assault occasioning actual bodily harm and the magistrates grant him bail. The next day, the prosecution receives his full record for the first time, showing four convictions for failing to surrender. This information was not available at the hearing. Ben has already been released.\n\nWhat can the prosecution do?","o":["Appeal to the Crown Court against the grant of bail, as it is still within 48 hours","Apply to the magistrates' court to reconsider the grant of bail on the basis of the new information","Nothing, because a grant of bail can never be revisited once the defendant is released","Ask the police to arrest Ben for breach of bail so that bail can be reconsidered","Apply to the High Court for judicial review of the decision to grant bail"],"a":1,"e":"For an indictable (including either-way) offence, the prosecution can ask the magistrates to reconsider a grant of bail on the basis of information not available when the decision was made (Bail Act 1976 s.5B). A prosecution appeal to the Crown Court requires oral notice at the end of the hearing before release, which was not given. Ben has breached no condition, so a s.7 arrest is not available."},

 {"t":"first-hearings","q":"Cara, a registered nurse with no previous convictions, is charged with theft of £120 from a patient at the hospital where she works. The offence is unlikely to lead to custody. A conviction would almost certainly lead to her being struck off the nursing register. She applies for a representation order.\n\nWhich interests of justice factor most strongly supports Cara's application?","o":["She may be unable to understand the proceedings","The case involves the tracing of witnesses","She is likely to lose her liberty","It is in the interests of another person that she is represented","She is likely to lose her livelihood"],"a":4,"e":"The interests of justice test (LASPO 2012 s.17(2)) is met where the defendant is likely to lose their livelihood, and a nurse struck off for dishonesty would lose her job; damage to reputation would also be relevant for a person of good character charged with dishonesty. Loss of liberty is unlikely here, and nothing suggests she cannot understand the case or that witnesses need tracing."},
 {"t":"first-hearings","q":"Dev, aged 40, is a well-paid IT manager. He is charged with robbery and his case has been sent to the Crown Court. His disposable income is above the magistrates' court limit but well below the level at which Crown Court legal aid is refused.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Dev's position on criminal legal aid?","o":["He will not get legal aid, because his income is above the magistrates' court limit","He is likely to qualify, but may have to pay income contributions and, if convicted, a capital contribution","He will get legal aid free of charge, because in the Crown Court the interests of justice test is met automatically","He will get legal aid only if he can show he is likely to lose his livelihood","He must pay for his defence privately and recover the costs if he is acquitted"],"a":1,"e":"In the Crown Court the interests of justice test is automatically met, and nearly everyone passes the means test except those with a very high disposable income. Those who qualify may be required to pay income contributions during the case and a capital contribution at the end if convicted. The magistrates' court means limit does not apply in the Crown Court."},
 {"t":"first-hearings","q":"Eve, aged 22, is charged with robbery of a mobile phone. She makes her first appearance in the magistrates' court. Her solicitor has read the initial details of the prosecution case, and Eve says she will plead not guilty.\n\nWhat will happen at this hearing?","o":["Eve will enter a not guilty plea and the magistrates will fix a date for trial","Eve will indicate her plea and the magistrates will decide whether to accept jurisdiction","Eve will be asked whether she wants to elect trial in the Crown Court","No plea will be taken; Eve will be sent to the Crown Court straight away and bail will be decided","Eve will be committed to the Crown Court for sentence once she pleads"],"a":3,"e":"Robbery is indictable only, so at the first hearing no plea is taken; the magistrates must send the case to the Crown Court forthwith (Crime and Disorder Act 1998 s.51) and decide bail. Plea before venue, allocation and election apply only to either-way offences. The next hearing will be the Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing in the Crown Court."},
 {"t":"first-hearings","q":"Finn is charged with burglary of a house. The prosecution alleges that when the occupier came downstairs, Finn threatened to hit her with a torch before running off with her laptop.\n\nHow is this offence classified?","o":["Indictable only, because the occupier was subjected to a threat of violence","Either way, because burglary of a dwelling is always triable either way","Summary only, because the property taken was of relatively low value","Either way, but it must be tried in the magistrates' court as the value is low","Indictable only, because all burglaries of a dwelling are indictable only"],"a":0,"e":"Most burglaries, including most dwelling burglaries, are either way. But burglary of a dwelling becomes indictable only where any person in the dwelling was subjected to violence or the threat of violence. Not all dwelling burglaries are indictable only, and value does not affect the classification of burglary."},
 {"t":"first-hearings","q":"Gina is arrested on Sunday and charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm. She is kept in custody and appears before the magistrates' court on Monday morning. She has not instructed a solicitor, has no money with her and the prosecution is opposing bail.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Gina can be represented on her bail application at this hearing?","o":["She must apply for a representation order and wait for it to be granted before anyone can act","She can be represented only if she passes the magistrates' court means test","She can use the court duty solicitor, whose help at the first hearing is not means tested","She must represent herself at the first hearing and instruct a solicitor afterwards","She can be represented only if the court decides it is in the interests of justice"],"a":2,"e":"The court duty solicitor is available, free and without a means test, to advise and represent unrepresented defendants at their first appearance, including on a bail application. Ongoing representation will need a representation order, which involves the interests of justice and means tests, but Gina does not have to wait for that before being helped today."},
 {"t":"first-hearings","q":"Hal and Ian are jointly charged with theft from their employer. Hal tells the solicitor that he intends to plead guilty and give evidence that Ian planned the theft. Ian says he knew nothing about it and will plead not guilty. Both ask the same solicitor to represent them.\n\nWhat should the solicitor do?","o":["Act for both, as long as each consents in writing after being told of the risks","Decline to act for both, because there is a conflict of interest between them","Act for both, as long as a different solicitor at the firm deals with each one","Act for both until trial, and then decide which one to continue representing","Act for Ian only, because he is pleading not guilty and needs more help"],"a":1,"e":"Where one co-defendant intends to plead guilty and give evidence against the other, their interests conflict and the solicitor cannot act for both (SRA Code of Conduct para 6.2). Consent and using different fee earners at the same firm do not cure a conflict in criminal litigation of this kind. The solicitor should not pick one client on the basis of who needs more help, and must keep both clients' information confidential."},
 {"t":"first-hearings","q":"Jade is charged with robbery. At her first hearing in the magistrates' court, her solicitor tells the court that Jade intends to plead guilty. The case is sent to the Crown Court, and Jade formally pleads guilty when the indictment is put to her at the Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing.\n\nWhat is the maximum reduction in sentence available for Jade's guilty plea?","o":["One quarter, because the plea was entered at the Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing","One tenth, because the plea was entered in the Crown Court","One sixth, because robbery is an indictable-only offence","No reduction, because no plea can be taken in the magistrates' court for robbery","One third, because she indicated her plea at the first stage of proceedings"],"a":4,"e":"The maximum one-third reduction is available where a guilty plea is indicated at the first stage of proceedings. For an indictable-only offence sent to the Crown Court, that is normally the first hearing in the magistrates' court, where the defendant can indicate an intention to plead guilty. One quarter applies where the plea is first indicated after that stage, and one sixth is the cap for murder only."},
 {"t":"first-hearings","q":"Kim is charged with burglary and remanded in custody overnight. At her first hearing the next morning, the prosecution has served only a summary of the circumstances of the offence and Kim's previous convictions. Her solicitor asks the court to adjourn until the witness statements have been served.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The prosecution has breached the rules, because it must always serve all witness statements before the first hearing","The court must adjourn, because a defendant cannot be asked about plea without the full prosecution evidence","The prosecution has served the minimum required for a defendant in custody, so it has not breached the rules","The prosecution need serve nothing at all before the first hearing, so the request is misconceived","The court must dismiss the case, because initial details have not been fully served"],"a":2,"e":"The prosecution must serve initial details of the prosecution case at or before the first hearing; where the defendant is in custody, this must include at least a summary of the circumstances and the defendant's record (Criminal Procedure Rules Part 8). The court should not ask for a plea until the defence has had the chance to consider the material it needs, but there is no rule that all witness statements must be served first, and the case would not be dismissed."},
 {"t":"first-hearings","q":"Leo is charged with common assault, a summary-only offence. At his first hearing in the magistrates' court, he pleads not guilty, saying he acted in self-defence.\n\nWhat will the court do next?","o":["Complete a Preparation for Effective Trial form, give directions and fix a trial date","Hold a plea before venue and allocation hearing to decide where the trial will take place","Ask Leo whether he wishes to elect trial by jury in the Crown Court","Send the case to the Crown Court for a Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing","Adjourn for a pre-sentence report before fixing the trial"],"a":0,"e":"For a summary-only offence, the defendant pleads at the first hearing; on a not guilty plea, the court gives case management directions using the Preparation for Effective Trial form and fixes a trial date. Plea before venue, allocation and election apply only to either-way offences, and sending applies to indictable-only offences or those sent after allocation. A pre-sentence report is for after conviction."},
 {"t":"first-hearings","q":"Max, aged 45, is charged with sexual assault on a 13-year-old girl, who will be the main prosecution witness. Max says he will defend the case himself and cross-examine the girl if he does not get legal aid. His income is low enough for the magistrates' court means test.\n\nWhich interests of justice factor most directly supports his application for a representation order?","o":["He is likely to lose his livelihood","He may be unable to understand the proceedings","The case involves a substantial question of law about cross-examination","It is in the interests of another person that he is represented","He needs witnesses to be traced and interviewed"],"a":3,"e":"One interests of justice factor is that it is in someone else's interests that the defendant is represented, for example so that the defendant does not personally cross-examine a child or the complainant in a sexual offence case. That fits these facts directly. Nothing suggests a question of law, difficulty understanding, or witnesses to be traced."},

 {"t":"allocation-sending","q":"Nora, aged 35, is charged with fraud involving £180,000 taken from elderly victims. She indicates a not guilty plea. The magistrates decide the case is unsuitable for summary trial. Nora tells her solicitor she would prefer to be tried in the magistrates' court because it will be quicker.\n\nWhat will happen to Nora's case?","o":["She can insist on summary trial, because she has indicated a not guilty plea","She can consent to summary trial, but may then be committed for sentence","She will be sent to the Crown Court for trial and has no choice in the matter","The magistrates must ask her whether she consents to Crown Court trial","The case will be adjourned for the prosecution to decide where it should be tried"],"a":2,"e":"Where the magistrates decide an either-way offence is unsuitable for summary trial, the defendant must be sent to the Crown Court (Crime and Disorder Act 1998 s.51). The defendant has a choice only if the court decides the case is suitable for summary trial. A defendant can never insist on summary trial of an either-way offence the court has declined."},
 {"t":"allocation-sending","q":"Oscar is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm and indicates a not guilty plea. The magistrates accept jurisdiction. Oscar asks for an indication of sentence, and the magistrates indicate that a non-custodial sentence would be more likely if he pleaded guilty now. Oscar then pleads guilty.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the court's sentencing powers?","o":["The court may impose any sentence within its powers, as the indication is only guidance","The court may commit Oscar to the Crown Court for sentence if it later thinks its powers are inadequate","The court may impose custody only if a pre-sentence report recommends it","The court must impose a fine, because that is the least severe non-custodial sentence","The court cannot impose a custodial sentence, nor commit Oscar for sentence on the basis its powers are inadequate"],"a":4,"e":"If the court gives an indication of sentence and the defendant then pleads guilty, the court is bound by it: it cannot impose a custodial sentence where it indicated a non-custodial one, and it cannot commit for sentence on the basis that its powers are inadequate (Magistrates' Courts Act 1980 s.20A). The indication does not dictate which non-custodial sentence must be imposed."},
 {"t":"allocation-sending","q":"Pia, aged 29, is charged with burglary of commercial premises. At plea before venue, she indicates a guilty plea. After hearing the facts and her record, the magistrates consider that the offence is so serious that the Crown Court should have the power to sentence her.\n\nWhat should the magistrates do?","o":["Commit Pia to the Crown Court for sentence; she keeps the full one-third reduction for her early plea","Send Pia to the Crown Court for trial, as her guilty indication is set aside","Sentence Pia themselves, because they cannot commit a defendant who has indicated guilty","Commit Pia to the Crown Court for sentence; she loses the reduction because she will be sentenced there","Adjourn for allocation, because a guilty indication does not count as a plea"],"a":0,"e":"A guilty indication at plea before venue is treated as a guilty plea, and the magistrates may commit to the Crown Court for sentence if they consider their powers inadequate (Sentencing Code s.14). The defendant still receives the full one-third reduction for indicating guilt at the first stage, even when committed. There is no trial and no allocation after a guilty indication."},
 {"t":"allocation-sending","q":"Quinn is charged with criminal damage to a shop window and display. The prosecution says the damage is worth £5,500; the defence says it is worth about £4,000. The magistrates cannot tell from the information whether the value is more or less than £5,000.\n\nWhat procedure should the court follow?","o":["Treat the offence as indictable only and send the case to the Crown Court","Hold a hearing with expert evidence to decide the exact value before going further","Treat the offence as summary only, because the defence value is below £5,000","Ask Quinn whether he consents to summary trial; if he does, deal with it as a low-value case","Proceed to allocation as for any either-way offence, ignoring the value"],"a":3,"e":"Where it is unclear whether the value involved in criminal damage exceeds £5,000, the court asks the defendant whether he consents to summary trial; if he consents, the case is dealt with as if summary only, with the lower maximum penalty (Magistrates' Courts Act 1980 s.22). If he does not consent, the court proceeds as for an ordinary either-way offence. The court does not hear evidence to fix the value."},
 {"t":"allocation-sending","q":"Rita is charged with arson, having set fire to her neighbour's shed causing £400 of damage. She indicates a not guilty plea, and the magistrates decide the case is suitable for summary trial. Rita wants a jury trial.\n\nCan Rita elect trial in the Crown Court?","o":["No, because the damage is under £5,000, so the case must be tried summarily as a low-value offence","Yes, because the low-value rule does not apply to arson, so it is an ordinary either-way offence","No, because the magistrates have already accepted jurisdiction","Yes, but only with the agreement of the prosecution","No, because arson is a summary-only offence when the value is low"],"a":1,"e":"The rule that criminal damage of £5,000 or less is tried as if summary only (Magistrates' Courts Act 1980 s.22) does not apply to arson, damage by fire or aggravated criminal damage. So arson is an ordinary either-way offence and, once the magistrates accept jurisdiction, Rita may elect Crown Court trial. The prosecution's agreement is not needed."},
 {"t":"allocation-sending","q":"Sid, aged 26, is charged with robbery and, arising from the same incident, theft of a car used to escape. Both charges come before the magistrates at his first appearance.\n\nHow will the magistrates deal with the theft charge?","o":["Take Sid's plea on the theft and, if not guilty, decide allocation in the usual way","Adjourn the theft charge until the Crown Court has dealt with the robbery","Ask Sid whether he wants the theft tried in the magistrates' court","Try the theft charge in the magistrates' court and send only the robbery","Send the theft to the Crown Court with the robbery, without plea before venue or allocation"],"a":4,"e":"Where an adult is sent to the Crown Court for an indictable-only offence (robbery), a related either-way offence is sent with it without plea before venue or allocation (Crime and Disorder Act 1998 ss.50A and 51). This keeps related matters together in one court. Sid gets no choice about the theft."},
 {"t":"allocation-sending","q":"Tia, aged 30, is sent to the Crown Court for robbery. She is also charged with a related summary offence of using threatening behaviour, which is imprisonable. She intends to plead not guilty to both.\n\nWhat will happen to the threatening behaviour charge?","o":["It must stay in the magistrates' court because it is a summary offence","It will be tried by the jury in the Crown Court together with the robbery, as both charges arise from the same facts","It can be sent to the Crown Court, but if Tia does not plead guilty to it there, it returns to the magistrates","It must be withdrawn by the prosecution because Tia has been sent for trial","It can be sent only if Tia consents to it being dealt with in the Crown Court"],"a":2,"e":"A related summary offence can be sent with an indictable offence if it is imprisonable or carries disqualification from driving (Crime and Disorder Act 1998 s.51). In the Crown Court, the judge can deal with it only if the defendant pleads guilty; otherwise it goes back to the magistrates. Sending a summary offence does not mean the jury will try it with the robbery."},
 {"t":"allocation-sending","q":"Uma, a teacher of good character, is charged with theft from a charity she volunteers for. The magistrates have accepted jurisdiction. The main prosecution evidence is a confession in interview, which Uma says was obtained after a police officer promised she would not be charged if she 'came clean'. She asks which court her solicitor recommends.\n\nWhat is the strongest reason for Uma to elect trial in the Crown Court?","o":["The judge rules on the confession in the jury's absence, so the jury never hears it if it is excluded","The Crown Court has lower maximum sentences than the magistrates' court","A defence statement is voluntary in the Crown Court, so Uma need not reveal any of her case before the trial","The Crown Court is quicker and cheaper than the magistrates' court","Crown Court trials always result in lower legal aid contributions"],"a":0,"e":"In the Crown Court, admissibility is decided by the judge in a voir dire (a trial within a trial) without the jury, so if the confession is excluded the jury never hears it. In the magistrates' court the same bench decides law and fact, so it hears the disputed confession even if it then excludes it. The other options are wrong: the Crown Court has higher powers, a defence statement is compulsory there, and it is slower and more costly."},
 {"t":"allocation-sending","q":"Vic, aged 40, is charged with a single offence of theft from his employer. He indicates a not guilty plea. The prosecution invites the magistrates to decline jurisdiction because 'theft from an employer is serious'. Applying the relevant sentencing guideline to the prosecution's facts, the likely sentence after trial is 9 months' custody. There is no unusual complexity.\n\nWhat is the magistrates' most likely decision?","o":["Decline jurisdiction, because theft in breach of trust from an employer must always be tried in the Crown Court","Accept jurisdiction, because the likely sentence is within their 12-month power and the case is not complex","Decline jurisdiction, because the likely sentence is custodial","Accept jurisdiction only if Vic agrees that he will not elect Crown Court trial","Adjourn for a pre-sentence report before deciding allocation"],"a":1,"e":"Under the Allocation guideline, either-way offences should be tried summarily unless the likely sentence would clearly exceed the court's powers or the case is unusually complex. The magistrates' maximum for a single either-way offence is 12 months, so a likely 9-month sentence is within their powers. A custodial sentence alone does not mean the case must go to the Crown Court, and Vic keeps his right to elect."},
 {"t":"allocation-sending","q":"Wes is charged with fraud. The magistrates accepted jurisdiction and Wes consented to summary trial. Before the trial starts, the prosecution obtains evidence that the fraud was much larger than first thought, so that the likely sentence would now clearly exceed the magistrates' powers.\n\nWhat can the prosecution do?","o":["Nothing, because the allocation decision is final once Wes has consented","Discontinue the case and recharge Wes with an indictable-only offence","Ask the court to commit Wes to the Crown Court for sentence now, before the summary trial takes place, as its powers are inadequate","Apply before trial for the case to be sent to the Crown Court, on the basis that sentencing powers would be inadequate","Appeal to the Crown Court against the allocation decision"],"a":3,"e":"Before a summary trial begins, the prosecution can apply for the case to be sent to the Crown Court, and the court may do so only if satisfied its sentencing powers would be inadequate (Magistrates' Courts Act 1980 s.25). Committal for sentence happens only after conviction. There is no appeal to the Crown Court against an allocation decision, and fraud is not indictable only."},

 {"t":"case-management-disclosure","q":"Xena is charged with a street robbery. She is 5 feet 2 inches tall and has no tattoos. Among the unused material is a statement from a passer-by, whom the prosecution does not intend to call, describing the robber as 'about 5 foot 10 with a tattoo on her hand'.\n\nMust the prosecution disclose the passer-by's statement?","o":["No, because the prosecution does not intend to rely on the passer-by at trial","Yes, because it might reasonably be considered capable of undermining the prosecution case or assisting the defence","No, unless the defence specifically asks for it in a defence statement","Yes, but only if the court orders disclosure after a public interest immunity hearing, because it concerns a non-witness","No, because unused material only needs to be listed on the sensitive schedule"],"a":1,"e":"The initial disclosure test (CPIA s.3) requires disclosure of any unused material that might reasonably be considered capable of undermining the prosecution case or assisting the defence. A description that does not match Xena plainly meets the test on identity. It does not matter that the prosecution will not call the witness or that the defence has not asked for it; public interest immunity is only for sensitive material."},
 {"t":"case-management-disclosure","q":"Yan pleads not guilty to an assault in the magistrates' court. The prosecution has served initial disclosure. Yan's solicitor believes the police seized CCTV from a nearby bar that would show the complainant started the fight, but it has not been disclosed. No defence statement has been served.\n\nWhat must the defence do before applying to the court for disclosure of the CCTV?","o":["Nothing, because the prosecution must disclose anything the defence asks for","Write to the investigating officer, who must hand the CCTV over within 14 days","Apply for a witness summons against the owner of the bar requiring him to bring the original CCTV recording to court","Serve a defence statement, as an application for specific disclosure can only be made after one is served","Wait until the trial and cross-examine the officer about the CCTV"],"a":3,"e":"An application for specific disclosure under CPIA s.8 can be made only after the defence has served a defence statement. In the magistrates' court a defence statement is voluntary, but without one the defence cannot use s.8. The prosecution's duty is to disclose material meeting the test, not everything the defence asks for."},
 {"t":"case-management-disclosure","q":"Zoe is charged with burglary and her case is in the Crown Court. Her defence is that at the time of the burglary she was at a friend's flat, many miles away, with her friend Ali.\n\nWhat must Zoe's defence statement contain about this?","o":["The nature of her alibi defence and the name, address and date of birth of Ali (or as much as is known)","Only a general statement that she denies the offence, as the alibi can be raised at trial","Ali's signed witness statement, which must be attached to the defence statement","Nothing about the alibi, as an alibi is served in a separate notice after the trial date is fixed","The alibi, but not Ali's details, which are protected by legal professional privilege"],"a":0,"e":"A defence statement must set out the nature of the defence, including any particular defence relied on, and where the defence is an alibi it must give the name, address and date of birth of each alibi witness, or as much as is known (CPIA s.6A). Failing to give these details is a fault which can lead to comment and adverse inferences (s.11). A witness statement need not be attached."},
 {"t":"case-management-disclosure","q":"Abe is tried for assault in the magistrates' court. He voluntarily served a defence statement saying he acted in self-defence. At trial, he gives evidence that he was not there at all and that the complainant has mistaken him for someone else.\n\nWhat is the most likely consequence of the change in defence?","o":["Abe's evidence about not being there will be ruled inadmissible","Abe will be convicted automatically because his defences are inconsistent","There are no consequences, because a defence statement is voluntary in the magistrates' court","The trial must be adjourned so that the prosecution can serve fresh evidence","The court may comment on the change, and may draw such adverse inferences as appear proper"],"a":4,"e":"Once a defence statement is served, even voluntarily in the magistrates' court, putting forward a different defence at trial is a fault under CPIA s.11. The court may comment on it and draw such inferences as appear proper, but the defence evidence is not excluded and Abe cannot be convicted solely on the inference. So the voluntary nature of the statement does not protect him."},
 {"t":"case-management-disclosure","q":"Bea is charged with fraud and her case is sent to the Crown Court. She is on bail. At the Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing (PTPH), she pleads not guilty to all counts.\n\nWhat will happen at the PTPH after Bea's plea?","o":["The judge will adjourn for a pre-sentence report","The case will be sent back to the magistrates' court for trial","The judge will fix a trial date and set a timetable of stage dates for the parties","The prosecution must immediately serve its defence statement","The judge will now decide whether Bea's case is suitable to be tried summarily instead"],"a":2,"e":"At the PTPH the defendant is arraigned (the indictment is put and they plead). On a not guilty plea, the judge fixes the trial date and sets stage dates, for example for service of the prosecution case and initial disclosure, the defence statement and defence witness notice, and later applications. A pre-sentence report follows a guilty plea, and it is the defence, not the prosecution, that serves a defence statement."},
 {"t":"case-management-disclosure","q":"At a case management hearing, the judge asks Cole's solicitor to say what the real issues in the trial are. Cole's instructions are that he was not the person seen on CCTV. He would prefer to say nothing about his defence until trial.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate response for the solicitor?","o":["Identify that identity is in dispute, without waiving privilege or giving up Cole's right not to give evidence","Refuse to answer, because Cole has an absolute right to silence at every stage","Disclose Cole's full instructions, including what he has told the solicitor in confidence","Say that the defence case will be decided at trial depending on how the prosecution evidence comes out in cross-examination","Ask the judge to adjourn the case so that Cole can decide whether to plead guilty"],"a":0,"e":"Under the Criminal Procedure Rules, every participant must help the court further the overriding objective, which includes identifying the real issues early. The defence must say what is in dispute (here, identity), but this does not require the defendant to waive legal professional privilege or give up the right to silence. A refusal or evasive answer would breach the duty to assist active case management."},
 {"t":"case-management-disclosure","q":"Dan is charged with assault and has served a defence statement saying the complainant attacked him first. After receiving the statement, the prosecution discovers that the complainant has two recent convictions for assault. The prosecution had already made initial disclosure.\n\nWhat must the prosecution do?","o":["Nothing, because initial disclosure has already been made and the duty has ended","Disclose the convictions only if Dan makes a successful application to the court for specific disclosure","Apply for public interest immunity before disclosing the convictions","Disclose the convictions as soon as reasonably practicable under its continuing duty of disclosure","Disclose the convictions only if the complainant consents"],"a":3,"e":"The prosecution has a continuing duty to keep disclosure under review and to disclose material meeting the test as soon as reasonably practicable, especially after receiving the defence statement (CPIA s.7A). The complainant's convictions for violence are capable of assisting a self-defence case. Dan need not make a s.8 application for material the prosecution must disclose anyway, and there is no public interest immunity point."},
 {"t":"case-management-disclosure","q":"Ed's solicitor drafted and served a defence statement in the Crown Court without first getting Ed to approve it. At trial, the prosecution cross-examines Ed about inconsistencies between the defence statement and his evidence. Ed says he never saw the statement.\n\nHow is the defence statement treated?","o":["It cannot be used, because Ed did not sign it","It is treated as given with Ed's authority unless the contrary is proved","It can be used only against the solicitor, not against Ed","It is treated as given with Ed's authority only if he confirms it in evidence","It must be withdrawn and a new statement served before the trial can continue"],"a":1,"e":"A defence statement served on the defendant's behalf by their solicitor is treated as given with the defendant's authority unless the contrary is proved (CPIA s.6E). This is why the solicitor must take careful instructions and have the client approve the statement before it is served. Inconsistencies may therefore lead to comment and inferences."},
 {"t":"case-management-disclosure","q":"Fern is due to be tried for theft in the magistrates' court. She has decided not to serve a defence statement. She intends to call her colleague, Gail, to give evidence that Fern was in a meeting at the time of the theft.\n\nWhat must Fern do in relation to Gail?","o":["Nothing, because defence disclosure is entirely voluntary in the magistrates' court","Serve Gail's signed witness statement on the prosecution","Ask the court's permission at trial to call Gail","Include Gail's details only if Fern later serves a defence statement","Serve a defence witness notice giving Gail's name, address and date of birth"],"a":4,"e":"A defence witness notice (CPIA s.6C), naming any witnesses the defendant intends to call with their details, is required in both the magistrates' court and the Crown Court, even where no defence statement is served. Failing to serve it, or calling a witness not included, is a fault that may lead to comment and inferences. Only the defence statement itself is voluntary in the magistrates' court."},
 {"t":"case-management-disclosure","q":"Gil's case is in the Crown Court. The prosecution served initial disclosure on 3 March. Gil's solicitor has not applied for any extension of time.\n\nBy what date must Gil's defence statement be served?","o":["17 March","24 March","31 March","2 April","28 April"],"a":2,"e":"In the Crown Court, a defence statement is compulsory and must be served within 28 days of the prosecution's initial disclosure, so here by 31 March. 17 March applies the 14-day limit for a voluntary defence statement in the magistrates' court. The time limit can be extended only on an application made before it expires."},

 {"t":"evidence-identification-silence","q":"Hugo is on trial for wounding. He gives evidence that the complainant came at him with a broken bottle and that he struck out to defend himself. In summing up, the judge tells the jury that Hugo must prove, on the balance of probabilities, that he acted in self-defence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the judge's direction?","o":["It is correct, because self-defence is a defence that the defendant raises","It is correct, because the defendant bears a reverse legal burden for all defences, including self-defence, that he raises","It is wrong, because once Hugo has raised self-defence the prosecution must disprove it so the jury is sure","It is wrong, because Hugo must prove self-defence beyond reasonable doubt","It is wrong, because the judge should not mention self-defence unless the prosecution raises it"],"a":2,"e":"The defendant bears only an evidential burden for self-defence, meaning he must raise enough evidence for the issue to go to the jury. Once that is done, the legal burden is on the prosecution to disprove self-defence beyond reasonable doubt, so the jury must be sure he was not acting in self-defence. A reverse legal burden applies only to insanity and some statutory defences, such as diminished responsibility."},
 {"t":"evidence-identification-silence","q":"Iris is charged with murder. She accepts that she killed the victim but says she was suffering from an abnormality of mental functioning that substantially impaired her ability to exercise self-control, which she says amounts to diminished responsibility.\n\nWho bears the burden of proof on diminished responsibility, and to what standard?","o":["Iris must prove it on the balance of probabilities","The prosecution must disprove it beyond reasonable doubt","Iris must prove it beyond reasonable doubt","The prosecution must disprove it on the balance of probabilities","Iris need only raise evidence of it, after which the judge decides"],"a":0,"e":"Diminished responsibility (Homicide Act 1957 s.2) is one of the exceptions where the defendant bears the legal burden, but only to the civil standard of the balance of probabilities. For most defences, such as self-defence, the defendant bears only an evidential burden and the prosecution must then disprove the defence. The defendant never has to prove anything beyond reasonable doubt."},
 {"t":"evidence-identification-silence","q":"Jules is charged with criminal damage to his former partner's car. The only witness, his former partner, says she watched him damage it from her window in good light. Jules's defence is that she has invented the whole story because she is angry about their break-up; he does not suggest that she has confused him with someone else.\n\nShould the judge give the jury a Turnbull warning?","o":["Yes, because the case depends wholly on the evidence of a single witness","Yes, because a Turnbull warning must be given in every case involving identification","No, because the witness viewed the incident in good light","Yes, because recognition of a former partner can still be mistaken","No, because the defence is that the witness is lying, not that she is mistaken"],"a":4,"e":"A Turnbull warning is needed where the case depends wholly or substantially on an identification which the defence says is mistaken. Where the defence is that the witness is lying rather than mistaken, the issue is credibility, not mistaken identification, so the warning is not required. Good lighting goes to the quality of an identification, not to whether Turnbull applies at all."},
 {"t":"evidence-identification-silence","q":"Kian is on trial for burglary. A neighbour glimpsed the burglar for a few seconds at dusk from about 20 metres away. She later picked Kian out at a video identification. Kian's DNA was also found on a glove dropped inside the burgled house, and he has given no explanation for it. Kian says the neighbour is mistaken.\n\nWhat should the judge do about the identification evidence?","o":["Withdraw the case from the jury, because the identification was a fleeting glimpse at dusk from 20 metres and is of poor quality","Leave the case to the jury with a Turnbull warning, pointing to the DNA as capable of supporting the identification","Leave the case to the jury without any warning, because there is supporting evidence","Exclude the identification evidence under s.78 PACE as unreliable","Direct the jury to convict if they accept the DNA evidence"],"a":1,"e":"Where the identification is poor but supported by other evidence, the judge leaves the case to the jury with a Turnbull warning and points to the evidence capable of supporting it, here the DNA on the glove. The case should be withdrawn only if the identification is poor and unsupported. The warning is still needed even where there is supporting evidence, and the judge never directs a jury to convict."},
 {"t":"evidence-identification-silence","q":"Lara is charged with handling stolen goods. In interview, on legal advice, she handed in a prepared statement explaining that she bought the goods from a market trader for a fair price and had no idea they were stolen, and then answered 'no comment'. At trial, her evidence is the same as the prepared statement and adds no new facts.\n\nCan the jury draw an adverse inference under s.34 CJPOA 1994?","o":["Yes, because she refused to answer the police's questions","Yes, because silence on legal advice never prevents an inference","Yes, but only if the prosecution proves the advice was wrong","No, because the prepared statement contained all the facts she relied on at trial","No, because a defendant can never be criticised for following legal advice"],"a":3,"e":"Section 34 permits an inference only where the defendant failed to mention a fact later relied on in their defence. If a prepared statement contained all the facts relied on at trial, there is no such failure, so no inference should be drawn even though the defendant then answered 'no comment'. Legal advice is relevant to whether silence was reasonable, but it is not the reason why there is no inference here."},
 {"t":"evidence-identification-silence","q":"Milo is charged with assault. In interview he handed in a prepared statement saying he was at home all evening, then answered 'no comment'. At trial he gives evidence that he was at home and adds, for the first time, that his flatmate was with him all evening and can confirm this.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether an adverse inference may be drawn?","o":["No inference may be drawn, because Milo gave a prepared statement","An inference may be drawn about his whole defence, because he refused to answer any questions after giving the statement","An inference may be drawn in relation to the new fact that his flatmate was with him, which was not in the statement","No inference may be drawn, because an alibi can be raised for the first time at trial","An inference may be drawn only if the flatmate gives evidence"],"a":2,"e":"A prepared statement protects against a s.34 inference only for the facts it contains. Milo now relies on a new fact (that his flatmate was with him) which he could reasonably have mentioned in interview, so an inference is possible in relation to that fact. No inference arises about the facts that were in the statement."},
 {"t":"evidence-identification-silence","q":"Nell answered 'no comment' in her interview about a theft. At trial, she does not give evidence or call any witnesses. Her advocate cross-examines the prosecution witnesses only to test the reliability of their accounts, without putting any positive case to them, and argues that the prosecution has not proved its case.\n\nCan an adverse inference be drawn under s.34 CJPOA 1994 from her silence in interview?","o":["Yes, because she remained silent in interview after being cautioned","Yes, because she has failed to give evidence at trial","Yes, but only if the judge is satisfied her silence was not on legal advice","No, because s.34 inferences are only available in the Crown Court","No, because she has not relied on any fact in her defence"],"a":4,"e":"Section 34 applies only where the defendant later relies on a fact not mentioned in interview. A defendant who simply puts the prosecution to proof, calling no evidence and putting no positive case, relies on no fact, so no s.34 inference can arise. Her failure to give evidence is a separate matter under s.35, and s.34 applies in both the magistrates' court and the Crown Court."},
 {"t":"evidence-identification-silence","q":"Omar is arrested for robbery and asks for a solicitor. A superintendent lawfully delays his access to legal advice, and he is interviewed under caution before seeing a solicitor. He answers 'no comment'. At trial he says, for the first time, that he was at a cinema at the time.\n\nCan the jury draw an adverse inference from his failure to mention the cinema in interview?","o":["No, because he had not been allowed to consult a solicitor before he was questioned","Yes, because the delay of legal advice was lawfully authorised","Yes, because an alibi is a fact he could reasonably have been expected to mention","No, because an alibi must be disclosed only in a defence statement","Yes, but only if he was given a special warning before the interview"],"a":0,"e":"No inference can be drawn under s.34 if the defendant was at an authorised place of detention and had not been allowed an opportunity to consult a solicitor before being questioned (s.34(2A)). This applies even when the delay was lawful. A special warning relates to ss.36-37, not s.34."},
 {"t":"evidence-identification-silence","q":"Pia is on trial for burglary. The only prosecution evidence linking her to the burglary is that, when interviewed under caution, she failed to mention that she had been at a friend's house that night, a fact she now relies on. There is no forensic, identification or other evidence against her. At the close of the prosecution case, the defence submits that there is no case to answer.\n\nHow should the judge rule?","o":["Reject the submission, because the jury is entitled to draw an inference from her silence in interview","Reject the submission, because an alibi raised late is likely to be false","Adjourn so that the prosecution can obtain more evidence","Allow the submission, because a case cannot be left to the jury solely on an inference from silence","Allow the submission only if Pia agrees to give evidence"],"a":3,"e":"Under CJPOA s.38(3), a defendant cannot be convicted solely on an inference from silence, and a submission of no case to answer cannot be rejected solely on such an inference. With no other evidence against Pia, there is no case to answer. Allowing the prosecution time to find evidence after it has closed its case is not appropriate."},
 {"t":"evidence-identification-silence","q":"Rex answered 'no comment' in interview on his solicitor's advice. At his trial for assault, he relies on self-defence, which he did not mention in interview. He wants to tell the jury that his solicitor advised silence because the police had disclosed almost nothing about the evidence.\n\nWhat is the effect of Rex giving the reasons for the advice?","o":["The jury must not draw any inference, because he followed legal advice","He waives privilege and may be cross-examined on the advice; the jury decides if he genuinely and reasonably relied on it","His solicitor becomes a compellable prosecution witness and must give evidence","The reasons are inadmissible, because communications between a client and his solicitor are always privileged and can never be revealed","The judge must direct the jury to ignore his silence altogether"],"a":1,"e":"Silence on legal advice does not automatically prevent an inference; the jury must decide whether the defendant genuinely and reasonably relied on the advice or stayed silent because he had no answer that would stand up to questioning. If the defendant gives the reasons for the advice, he waives legal professional privilege and can be cross-examined about what passed between him and the solicitor. Simply saying he was advised to stay silent would not waive privilege."},

 {"t":"hearsay-confessions","q":"Sol is charged with blackmail. The prosecution calls the victim, Tess, to say that Sol told her, 'Pay me £500 by Friday or I'll tell your husband about your affair.' The defence objects that this is hearsay.\n\nIs Tess's evidence of what Sol said hearsay?","o":["No, because it is relied on to prove that the words were said, as they form the demand, not to prove that anything stated is true","Yes, because it is a statement made outside court, so it can be admitted only if one of the statutory or preserved common law hearsay routes applies","Yes, but it is admissible as a confession because it is adverse to Sol","No, because Tess is giving oral evidence and can be cross-examined","Yes, and it must be excluded because Sol cannot be cross-examined about it"],"a":0,"e":"Hearsay is an out-of-court statement relied on as evidence of the truth of the matter stated (CJA 2003 ss.114-115). Here the words are the demand itself and are relied on to prove that they were said, not that their content is true, so they are not hearsay. The fact that Tess gives live evidence does not by itself answer the hearsay question, because hearsay is about the use made of the statement."},
 {"t":"hearsay-confessions","q":"Uri is charged with assault. The key prosecution witness, Val, gave a statement to the police and then moved to Australia for a two-year work contract. The prosecution applies to read her statement under s.116 CJA 2003, saying only that she is abroad. It has not asked whether she could give evidence by live link.\n\nWhat is the defence's best argument against admission?","o":["Hearsay can never be admitted where the maker is alive","Val's absence abroad is a valid reason only if she had already left the UK before she made her statement to the police","The prosecution has not shown that securing her attendance, including by live link, is not reasonably practicable","A statement from a witness abroad can be admitted only with the defence's agreement","Val's statement can be admitted only if she is unfit to give evidence"],"a":2,"e":"Under s.116(2)(c), being outside the UK is a reason for admitting a statement only if it is not reasonably practicable to secure the witness's attendance. The court should consider whether she could give evidence by live link, so the prosecution's failure to explore this is the defence's strongest point. The maker of a statement need not be dead for it to be admitted, and defence agreement is not required."},
 {"t":"hearsay-confessions","q":"Wyn is charged with theft. A witness, Zed, gave a statement to police but has since moved and cannot be contacted. The prosecution sent one letter to his last known address, which was returned unopened, and made no other enquiries. It applies to read his statement because he cannot be found.\n\nIs Zed's statement likely to be admitted under s.116 CJA 2003?","o":["Yes, because the letter was returned unopened, which proves that he can no longer be found at any address","No, because such steps as it is reasonably practicable to take to find him have not been taken","Yes, because the defence can attack his credibility under s.124","No, because s.116 never applies to a witness who is alive","Yes, but only if the prosecution also shows that Zed is in fear"],"a":1,"e":"A statement is admissible under s.116(2)(d) only if the witness cannot be found although such steps as it is reasonably practicable to take to find them have been taken. One returned letter, with no other enquiries such as phone, employer or police databases, is unlikely to be enough. The defence's ability to attack credibility under s.124 does not satisfy the conditions for admission."},
 {"t":"hearsay-confessions","q":"Ali is charged with causing grievous bodily harm. The prosecution wishes to prove the victim's injuries by relying on hospital records made by the treating nurse at the time, describing what she saw on examination. The nurse cannot now be traced.\n\nUnder which provision are the hospital records most likely to be admissible?","o":["Only with the defence's agreement, because the nurse cannot be cross-examined","As res gestae, because they were made at the time of treatment","As the statement of a witness who cannot be found, but only with the court's leave because the nurse is absent","They are not admissible, because medical records are always excluded hearsay","As a business document created in the course of a profession by someone with personal knowledge"],"a":4,"e":"Section 117 CJA 2003 admits a statement in a document created in the course of a trade, business, profession or office, where the person who supplied the information had personal knowledge of it. Hospital records made by a treating nurse fit this. Res gestae applies to statements made by someone emotionally overpowered by an event, and leave under s.116 is needed only for the fear ground."},
 {"t":"hearsay-confessions","q":"Bo was stabbed in the street. Seconds afterwards, bleeding heavily and screaming, he shouted to a passer-by, 'Kai stabbed me!' Bo died in hospital two hours later. Kai is charged with murder and the prosecution wants the passer-by to repeat what Bo shouted.\n\nIs Bo's statement admissible?","o":["No, because the statement was not made on oath","No, because Bo cannot be cross-examined about it","Yes, under the res gestae rule, as he was so emotionally overpowered that concoction can be disregarded","Yes, but only if the defence agrees to it being admitted","Yes, as a business document, because the passer-by's account of it was later recorded by a police officer in his notebook"],"a":2,"e":"Res gestae is a preserved common law exception (CJA 2003 s.118): a statement made by someone so emotionally overpowered by an event that the possibility of concoction or distortion can be disregarded is admissible. Bo's shout seconds after being stabbed fits this. Hearsay is often unsworn and not cross-examined, which is why it needs a route, but that does not make it inadmissible; s.117 concerns business documents."},
 {"t":"hearsay-confessions","q":"Cy and Dee are jointly charged with burglary. In his police interview, Cy said, 'Dee and I did it together; she kept watch while I went in.' Dee denies any involvement. Neither Cy's confession nor its admissibility against him is challenged.\n\nHow may Cy's confession be used at the joint trial?","o":["As evidence against Cy only, not as evidence against Dee","As evidence against both Cy and Dee, because it was made under caution","As evidence against Dee only if Cy refuses to give evidence","As evidence against both, unless Dee proves on the balance of probabilities that it is false","It cannot be used at all, because it implicates a co-defendant"],"a":0,"e":"A confession is admissible against the person who made it (PACE s.76(1)), but it is not evidence against a co-defendant whom it implicates. The judge will direct the jury to consider Cy's interview only in Cy's case. If Cy gives evidence at trial implicating Dee, that live evidence can be used against her, but the interview itself cannot."},
 {"t":"hearsay-confessions","q":"Eli is charged with burglary. In an interview later excluded under s.76 PACE, he told police that the stolen jewellery was hidden under the floorboards of his garden shed. The police found it there.\n\nWhat use can the prosecution make of the discovery of the jewellery?","o":["None, because the jewellery was found only as a result of an excluded confession","It can prove the jewellery was found and also tell the jury that Eli had told the police where it was hidden","It can use the discovery only if Eli consents","It can prove the jewellery was found in Eli's shed, but cannot say it was found because of what he said","It can use the discovery only if the confession is later admitted under s.78"],"a":3,"e":"Under PACE s.76(4), the exclusion of a confession does not affect the admissibility of facts discovered as a result of it. The prosecution can prove that the jewellery was found in Eli's shed, but cannot tell the jury that it was found because of what he said (unless Eli's own evidence raises it). Exclusion of the confession does not make the physical evidence inadmissible."},
 {"t":"hearsay-confessions","q":"Fay is interviewed about a fraud. The officer tells her, truthfully, that her business partner has also been arrested and has blamed everything on her. Fay becomes very upset and confesses. The interview is otherwise conducted properly, with her solicitor present.\n\nHow likely is an argument that the confession was obtained by oppression to succeed?","o":["Very likely, because Fay was distressed when she confessed","Very likely, because the police must not tell a suspect what a co-accused has said","Unlikely, because telling a suspect upsetting but true information is not oppression","Unlikely, because oppression requires the use or threat of physical violence","Very likely, because the confession was not volunteered spontaneously"],"a":2,"e":"Oppression (PACE s.76(2)(a)) includes torture, inhuman or degrading treatment and the use or threat of violence, and more generally the exercise of power in a burdensome, harsh or wrongful manner, usually involving some impropriety. Telling a suspect true but upsetting information, in a properly conducted interview, is not oppression. Oppression is not limited to physical violence, so D is wrong."},
 {"t":"hearsay-confessions","q":"Gus has no previous convictions and no involvement in drugs. Over several weeks, an undercover officer repeatedly asks Gus to obtain cocaine for him, offers him very large sums of money and tells him that he is desperate. Gus eventually obtains some cocaine and sells it to the officer. He is charged with supplying a Class A drug.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate application for the defence to make?","o":["An application to exclude Gus's interview under s.76 PACE because it was obtained by oppression","A submission of no case to answer at the close of the prosecution case","An application for a Newton hearing on the basis of plea","An application to exclude the officer's evidence as hearsay","An application to stay the proceedings as an abuse of process because of entrapment"],"a":4,"e":"Where police conduct goes beyond providing an unexceptional opportunity to commit an offence and instead causes someone to commit an offence they would not otherwise have committed, the main remedy is a stay of the proceedings as an abuse of process; exclusion under s.78 PACE is an alternative. The officer's evidence is direct evidence, not hearsay, and a Newton hearing is about the factual basis of a guilty plea."},
 {"t":"hearsay-confessions","q":"Hal is interviewed about an arson in the presence of his solicitor. Before the interview, the officer falsely tells the solicitor that Hal's fingerprints were found on a petrol can at the scene. Relying on this, the solicitor advises Hal that the evidence is strong, and Hal confesses. No fingerprints were in fact found.\n\nWhat is the defence's strongest argument?","o":["The confession must be excluded under s.76(2)(a), because any lie told by the police to a suspect or to a solicitor is oppression","The confession should be excluded under s.78 PACE, as deceiving the solicitor undermined the advice and the trial's fairness","The confession is inadmissible hearsay because it was made outside court","The confession must be excluded because Hal was not given a special warning","The interview should be excluded because the solicitor should not have advised Hal at all"],"a":1,"e":"Section 78 PACE gives the court discretion to exclude prosecution evidence if its admission would have such an adverse effect on fairness that it ought not to be admitted. Deliberately deceiving the solicitor, so that the legal advice was based on false information, is a strong ground for exclusion. A lie is not automatically oppression, and confessions are admissible against their maker as an exception to the hearsay rule."},

 {"t":"bad-character","q":"Ivan is charged with possession of cocaine with intent to supply. His defence is that the arresting officers planted the drugs in his car because they dislike him. Ivan has previous convictions for drug supply.\n\nWhich gateway is most clearly opened by Ivan's defence for the prosecution to adduce his convictions?","o":["Gateway (a): agreement of the parties","Gateway (e): substantial probative value in relation to a co-defendant","Gateway (f): correcting a false impression","Gateway (g): an attack on another person's character","Gateway (c): important explanatory evidence"],"a":3,"e":"Alleging that police officers fabricated evidence or planted drugs is an attack on another person's character, because it suggests they committed an offence or behaved reprehensibly; this opens gateway (g) (CJA 2003 s.106). The prosecution might also rely on gateway (d) for propensity, but the question asks what the defence opens. Gateway (e) is for co-defendants only, and Ivan has said nothing about his own character to trigger (f)."},
 {"t":"bad-character","q":"Jo is on trial for theft from a shop. In evidence-in-chief she says, 'I've never been in trouble with the police in my life.' In fact she has two convictions for theft and one for driving without insurance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the prosecution may do?","o":["Adduce all of Jo's convictions under gateway (f), including the driving offence, because she has lied on oath","Adduce evidence under gateway (f) to correct the false impression, but no more than is necessary","Nothing, because Jo has not attacked anyone else's character","Adduce her convictions only if Jo agrees, under gateway (a)","Apply for Jo to be charged with perjury before the trial continues"],"a":1,"e":"Jo has given a false impression about herself, which opens gateway (f) (CJA 2003 s.105). The evidence admitted under this gateway must go no further than necessary to correct the false impression. Gateway (f) does not depend on an attack on another person, and the prosecution does not need Jo's agreement."},
 {"t":"bad-character","q":"Kit is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm. His only previous conviction is for common assault 15 years ago, when he was 19. The prosecution has applied to adduce it under gateway (d) to show a propensity for violence. The defence wants to keep it out.\n\nWhat is the defence's best application?","o":["To exclude it under s.78 PACE, because gateway (d) evidence can never be excluded under the CJA 2003 itself once the gateway is passed","To argue it is not bad character, because it is unconnected with the facts of the offence","To exclude it under gateway (e), because it is old","To ask the jury to disregard it after it has been admitted","To apply under s.101(3) to exclude it as unfair, the court having particular regard to the length of time since the conviction"],"a":4,"e":"Under CJA 2003 s.101(3), on the defendant's application the court must not admit evidence under gateway (d) or (g) if it would have such an adverse effect on fairness that it ought not to be admitted, and s.101(4) requires the court to have particular regard to the length of time involved. A single old conviction will often not show a propensity. A previous conviction is bad character even though unconnected with the facts; gateway (e) is for co-defendants."},
 {"t":"bad-character","q":"Lex is charged with burglary of a house. He has previous convictions for robbery and for handling stolen goods within the last four years. The prosecution wishes to adduce them to show his propensity to commit offences of the kind charged.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how these convictions can show propensity?","o":["They are in the same category of offence as burglary, so they can show propensity under gateway (d)","They cannot show propensity, because they are not for burglary","They can show propensity only if Lex attacks the character of a prosecution witness and so opens gateway (g)","They can be admitted only under gateway (c) as important explanatory evidence","They can show propensity only if Lex agrees to them being admitted"],"a":0,"e":"Under CJA 2003 s.103, propensity to commit offences of the kind charged may be shown by convictions for offences of the same description or of the same category. The theft category includes theft, robbery, burglary and handling stolen goods. So these convictions can be relied on under gateway (d), subject to the court's power to exclude them if unjust or unfair."},
 {"t":"bad-character","q":"Mia is charged with assault. Her defence is an alibi: she says she was at home and the prosecution witnesses are mistaken. The prosecution says her alibi is a lie. She has two convictions in the last three years for making false statements to obtain state benefits.\n\nThrough which route are Mia's convictions most likely to be admissible?","o":["Gateway (g), because by saying the witnesses are mistaken her alibi attacks the prosecution witnesses' character","Gateway (f), because by denying the offence she gives a false impression","Gateway (d), as showing a propensity to be untruthful, because it is suggested that her case is untrue","Gateway (e), because they have substantial probative value","They are not admissible at all, because they are not offences of violence"],"a":2,"e":"Gateway (d) covers an important matter in issue between the defendant and the prosecution, including propensity to be untruthful (CJA 2003 s.103). Convictions involving false statements can show such a propensity where it is suggested that the defendant's case is untruthful, as here. Saying witnesses are mistaken is not an attack on their character, and simply denying the offence is not a false impression."},
 {"t":"bad-character","q":"Ned is charged with assaulting a work colleague. He has no previous convictions or cautions. The prosecution wants to call evidence from two other colleagues that, in the year before the assault, Ned regularly bullied and racially abused them at work.\n\nHow should this evidence be treated?","o":["It is not bad character evidence, because Ned has no convictions","It is bad character evidence, because it shows reprehensible behaviour, so it needs a gateway to be admitted","It is automatically admissible, because it concerns the same workplace","It is bad character evidence, but as Ned has no convictions or cautions it can be admitted only with his agreement","It is inadmissible, because only convictions can be used as bad character evidence"],"a":1,"e":"Bad character means evidence of misconduct, which includes the commission of an offence or other reprehensible behaviour (CJA 2003 ss.98 and 112). Bullying and racial abuse are reprehensible behaviour even without a conviction, so the evidence is bad character and must pass through a gateway in s.101. It does not 'have to do with the alleged facts' of this assault, so it is not outside the definition."},
 {"t":"bad-character","q":"Oli is tried for sexual offences against two complainants. The prosecution relies on each complainant's evidence as bad character evidence in relation to the other count. During the trial, it emerges that the complainants discussed their accounts in detail and may have tailored them to match.\n\nWhat must the judge do if satisfied that the evidence is contaminated so that a conviction would be unsafe?","o":["Direct the jury to give the evidence less weight but allow them to continue","Exclude the evidence under s.101(3) and allow the trial to continue","Adjourn the trial so that the police can re-interview the complainants","Direct the jury to acquit or discharge the jury","Ask the prosecution whether it wishes to continue with the case"],"a":3,"e":"Under CJA 2003 s.107, where bad character evidence admitted under gateways (c) to (g) is contaminated (false or misleading because of collusion or the influence of others) and a conviction would be unsafe, the judge must direct an acquittal or discharge the jury. A warning about weight is not enough in that situation, and the decision is the judge's, not the prosecution's."},
 {"t":"bad-character","q":"Pip is on trial for shoplifting. The judge has admitted his five previous convictions for shoplifting under gateway (d) to show propensity. The judge is now preparing the summing up.\n\nWhat must the judge tell the jury about the previous convictions?","o":["That the convictions prove Pip committed this offence unless he has an explanation","That the convictions are relevant only to Pip's credibility as a witness, and not to whether he committed the offence","That the jury must ignore the convictions unless Pip gave evidence","That the convictions are relevant only to sentence if Pip is convicted","Why they were admitted, how they may be used, and that the jury must not convict wholly or mainly because of them"],"a":4,"e":"When bad character is admitted, the judge must explain why it was admitted and how the jury may use it, that they should consider whether it really shows a propensity, that propensity does not by itself prove guilt, and that they must not convict wholly or mainly because of it. Once admitted, evidence may be used for any purpose for which it is relevant, so it is not limited to credibility."},
 {"t":"bad-character","q":"Rae is charged with assault occasioning actual bodily harm. She has a single conviction, eight years ago, for shoplifting. Her defence is that she has never been a violent person, and she wants the jury to know about her minor, non-violent conviction.\n\nThrough which gateway can Rae's conviction be admitted?","o":["Gateway (b), because it is adduced by Rae herself","Gateway (d), because it shows a propensity to commit offences","Gateway (f), because it corrects a false impression","Gateway (g), because she has attacked another person's character","It cannot be admitted, because a defendant may not adduce her own bad character"],"a":0,"e":"Gateway (b) allows a defendant to adduce evidence of her own bad character, for example to show that her only conviction is minor and not for violence. Gateways (d), (f) and (g) are used by the prosecution against the defendant, not by the defendant. Rae will not then be entitled to a full good character direction, but may gain credibility by being open."},
 {"t":"bad-character","q":"Sid is charged with assault. He says the complainant, Ty, attacked him first. Ty has three recent convictions for violence. Sid's solicitor intends to apply under s.100 CJA 2003 for leave to adduce Ty's convictions. Sid has two convictions for assault.\n\nWhat should the solicitor warn Sid about before making the application?","o":["That the application will automatically be refused, because Ty is the victim","That Sid will lose his right not to give evidence","That by attacking Ty's character, Sid may open gateway (g), letting the prosecution adduce his own convictions","That Ty's convictions can be admitted only if Ty agrees","That the jury will be told that Sid applied to adduce Ty's convictions and may hold that against him when deciding"],"a":2,"e":"Adducing Ty's convictions to show he was the aggressor is an attack on another person's character, which can open gateway (g) (CJA 2003 s.106) so that the prosecution may apply to adduce Sid's own convictions. Sid could then apply to exclude them under s.101(3). The s.100 application itself needs the court's leave, but not Ty's agreement."},

 {"t":"trial-procedure","q":"Tom is charged with causing actual bodily harm to his 15-year-old stepson. Tom's wife, Uma, saw the incident and gave a statement to the police, but now refuses to give evidence against her husband.\n\nCan Uma be compelled to give evidence for the prosecution?","o":["No, because a spouse can never be compelled to give evidence against her husband","No, because she is competent but not compellable for the prosecution in any case","Yes, but only if she has already given a statement to the police","No, because the victim is not her own biological child","Yes, because the offence involves an assault on a person who was under 16 at the time"],"a":4,"e":"A spouse is competent for the prosecution, but compellable for it only for specified offences (PACE s.80(3)), which include an offence involving an assault on, or injury or threat of injury to, a person who was under 16 at the time. The stepson is 15, so Uma is compellable. It does not matter whether the child is hers or whether she gave a statement."},
 {"t":"trial-procedure","q":"Vic and Wyn are jointly charged with robbery. Before the trial, Vic pleads guilty. The prosecution now wishes to call Vic to give evidence against Wyn, but Vic does not want to.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Vic's position?","o":["Vic is not competent for the prosecution, because he is a co-defendant","Vic is competent for the prosecution, but cannot be compelled to give evidence against his former co-accused","Vic is competent and compellable for the prosecution, because he is no longer a co-defendant","Vic can be compelled only if he is offered a reduction in his sentence","Vic can give evidence only for Wyn, not for the prosecution"],"a":2,"e":"A co-defendant is not competent for the prosecution while still jointly tried. Once he has pleaded guilty, been acquitted or is tried separately, he is no longer a co-defendant and becomes competent and compellable like any other witness. So Vic can be compelled to testify, and a sentence reduction is not a condition of compellability."},
 {"t":"trial-procedure","q":"In a Crown Court trial for burglary, the jury retired to consider its verdict at 11:00. At 12:45, the jury sends a note saying it has reached a verdict by a majority of 10 to 2.\n\nWhat should the judge do?","o":["Not accept a majority verdict yet, as the jury has not deliberated for long enough","Accept the verdict, because 10 to 2 is an acceptable majority with 12 jurors at any stage","Discharge the jury, because it has failed to reach a unanimous verdict","Accept the verdict only if the defence agrees","Direct the jury that it must now return a unanimous verdict or none"],"a":0,"e":"A majority verdict may be accepted only after the jury has deliberated for at least 2 hours (Juries Act 1974 s.17), and in practice 2 hours and 10 minutes. Although 10 to 2 is an acceptable majority, the jury has deliberated for only 1 hour 45 minutes, so the judge should ask it to continue trying for a unanimous verdict. Discharge is premature."},
 {"t":"trial-procedure","q":"During a Crown Court trial, two jurors are discharged through illness, leaving 10 jurors. After more than three hours of deliberation, the jury is unable to agree unanimously.\n\nWhat is the minimum majority verdict the judge can accept?","o":["8 to 2","10 to 0 only, because a reduced jury must be unanimous","7 to 3","9 to 1","Any majority, because the jury has deliberated long enough"],"a":3,"e":"With 12 jurors the acceptable majorities are 11-1 or 10-2; with 11 jurors, 10-1; with 10 jurors, 9-1. Only a jury reduced to 9 must be unanimous. So with 10 jurors the minimum acceptable majority is 9 to 1."},
 {"t":"trial-procedure","q":"Ari is on trial for assault and will give evidence. His defence is an alibi supported by his friend, Bea. Ari's advocate wants to call Bea first, so that Ari can hear her evidence before he gives his own.\n\nIs this permissible?","o":["Yes, because the defence may call its witnesses in any order it chooses","No, because Ari must give evidence before any other defence witness","Yes, provided the prosecution does not object","No, because an alibi witness can be called only after the defendant has been cross-examined by the judge","Yes, but only in the magistrates' court"],"a":1,"e":"Where the defendant is going to give evidence, he must be called before any other defence witness (PACE s.79), unless the court directs otherwise. This stops the defendant tailoring his evidence to what his witnesses have said. The rule applies in both the magistrates' court and the Crown Court."},
 {"t":"trial-procedure","q":"Cal is on trial for robbery. The main prosecution witness, Dot, is 16 years old. She saw the robbery and is frightened of seeing Cal in court. The prosecution is considering applying for special measures.\n\nOn what basis is Dot eligible for special measures?","o":["She is eligible only if she is the complainant in a sexual offence","She is eligible only if Cal or his associates have made threats against her before the trial","She is not eligible, because only children under 14 can have special measures","She is not eligible, because special measures apply only in the Crown Court","She is eligible as a vulnerable witness, because she is under 18 at the time of the hearing"],"a":4,"e":"A witness is eligible for special measures as a vulnerable witness if under 18 at the time of the hearing (YJCEA 1999 s.16). Dot is 16, so she is eligible regardless of whether she has been threatened. The age of 14 is relevant to whether a witness gives sworn evidence, not to special measures, which are available in all criminal courts."},
 {"t":"trial-procedure","q":"Eve is charged with sexual assault on a 6-year-old girl. The prosecution intends to call the girl as a witness. The defence argues that she is too young to give evidence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the test the court will apply?","o":["Whether she can understand questions put to her as a witness and give answers that can be understood","Whether the girl understands the nature and solemnity of an oath; if she does not, she cannot give evidence at all","Whether the girl is at least 7 years old, which is the minimum age for a witness","Whether the girl's parents consent to her giving evidence","Whether the girl can give sworn evidence, because unsworn evidence is not admissible"],"a":0,"e":"Every person is competent to give evidence unless they cannot understand questions put to them as a witness and give answers that can be understood (YJCEA 1999 s.53). There is no minimum age. A witness under 14 gives unsworn evidence (s.55), so the girl does not need to understand the oath."},
 {"t":"trial-procedure","q":"Fin is on trial for assault. The prosecution calls Gail, who gave a police statement saying she saw Fin punch the victim. In the witness box, Gail says she saw nothing, refuses to look at the prosecutor and says, 'I'm not helping you lot.' The prosecutor believes she is deliberately not telling the truth.\n\nWhat can the prosecutor do?","o":["Nothing, because a party cannot challenge its own witness","Read Gail's police statement to the jury as evidence of its truth, without asking her any further questions about it, because she is refusing to help","Ask for leave to treat Gail as hostile, cross-examine her and put her previous statement, which can then be evidence of its truth","Ask the judge to charge Gail with contempt and adjourn the trial","Withdraw Gail as a witness and rely only on the defence's evidence"],"a":2,"e":"A witness who shows hostility (unwillingness to tell the truth, not merely unhelpful evidence) may, with the court's leave, be cross-examined by the party who called her, including on a previous inconsistent statement. If the previous statement is proved, it is evidence of the truth of its contents (CJA 2003 s.119). The prosecutor cannot simply read the statement without following this procedure."},
 {"t":"trial-procedure","q":"Hari, who lives in Swansea, is charged with fraud and will be tried at Swansea Crown Court. He and two of his witnesses speak Welsh as their first language and want to give evidence in Welsh. The Plea and Trial Preparation Hearing is next week.\n\nWhat should Hari's solicitor do?","o":["Nothing until the trial, when Hari and the witnesses can simply begin speaking Welsh and the court will arrange an interpreter","Apply for the trial to be moved to England, where interpreters are easier to arrange","Apply for a jury made up only of Welsh speakers","Tell the court early, such as at the PTPH, so a Welsh-speaking judge and simultaneous translation can be arranged","Advise Hari that he must give evidence in English, because the indictment is in English"],"a":3,"e":"In proceedings in Wales, any party or witness may speak Welsh (Welsh Language Act 1993 s.22). The defence should tell the court at the earliest opportunity, for example at the PTPH, so that the court can list the case before a Welsh-speaking judge and arrange simultaneous translation. Jurors cannot be selected for their ability to speak Welsh, so translation is provided for non-Welsh-speaking jurors."},
 {"t":"trial-procedure","q":"Ike is on trial for robbery. The prosecution's identification witness gave a clear description that matches Ike, but in cross-examination she gave some inconsistent answers about the lighting and how long she saw the robber. At the close of the prosecution case, Ike's advocate submits that there is no case to answer because the witness is unreliable.\n\nHow should the judge rule?","o":["Allow the submission, because any inconsistency in a witness's evidence means there is no case to answer","Reject the submission, because the witness's reliability is for the jury where there is evidence on which it could properly convict","Allow the submission, because the defence has raised a reasonable doubt","Reject the submission, because a submission of no case to answer can only be made in the magistrates' court, not in a trial before a judge and jury","Adjourn the trial so that the witness can attend a further identification procedure"],"a":1,"e":"On a submission of no case to answer, the judge stops the case only if there is no evidence, or the evidence taken at its highest is so tenuous that a properly directed jury could not properly convict on it. Where the strength of the case depends on a witness's reliability, which is generally for the jury, and there is evidence on which a jury could properly convict, the case should be left to the jury. The judge does not decide whether there is reasonable doubt at this stage."},

 {"t":"sentencing","q":"Jan pleads guilty to theft from her employer. She says she took £2,000 on one occasion. The prosecution says she took £20,000 over a year, which would put the offence in a much higher category under the guideline. The prosecution does not accept her basis of plea.\n\nWhat should happen before sentence?","o":["The judge must sentence on the prosecution's version, because by pleading guilty Jan has accepted the prosecution case","The judge should hold a Newton hearing, at which the prosecution must prove its version so that the judge is sure","The judge must sentence on Jan's version, because the prosecution cannot challenge a guilty plea","A jury must be sworn to decide which version is correct","Jan must withdraw her plea and the case must proceed to trial"],"a":1,"e":"Where a defendant pleads guilty on a factual basis that differs from the prosecution's case and the difference would materially affect sentence, the judge must either accept the defence version or hold a Newton hearing. At a Newton hearing, held without a jury, the prosecution must prove its version to the criminal standard; if it cannot, the judge sentences on the defendant's version."},
 {"t":"sentencing","q":"Kai indicated a guilty plea to assault occasioning actual bodily harm at his first hearing, but on the basis that he punched the victim once. After a Newton hearing, the judge rejects his version and finds that he kicked the victim repeatedly while he was on the ground.\n\nWhat is the likely effect on the reduction for Kai's guilty plea?","o":["He keeps the full one-third reduction, because he indicated his plea at the first stage","He loses all reduction, because his version was rejected","His reduction is limited to one tenth, as if he had pleaded on the first day of trial","His reduction is limited to one quarter, as if he had pleaded after the first stage","His reduction will normally be halved"],"a":4,"e":"Under the Sentencing Council guilty plea guideline, if a defendant's version of the facts is rejected at a Newton hearing, the reduction which would have been available is normally halved. So Kai's one-third reduction would normally become about one sixth. He does not automatically lose all credit, and the one-quarter and one-tenth reductions depend on when the plea was indicated."},
 {"t":"sentencing","q":"Leah pleads guilty to theft of food from a supermarket. The prosecution accepts the facts of the offence. In mitigation, Leah says she stole because she had no money left after paying her rent and her children had not eaten for a day. The prosecution has no information either way about this.\n\nHow should the court deal with Leah's explanation?","o":["Hold a Newton hearing, at which the prosecution must disprove the explanation so that the court is sure it is untrue","Ignore the explanation, because mitigation must be supported by documents","It is extraneous mitigation within her own knowledge, which she must prove on the balance of probabilities","Accept the explanation automatically, because the prosecution cannot contradict it","Adjourn for a jury to decide whether the explanation is true"],"a":2,"e":"A Newton hearing is for disputes about the facts of the offence. Extraneous mitigation, such as the defendant's reasons for offending, is within the defendant's own knowledge and the prosecution usually cannot contradict it; the defendant must prove it on the balance of probabilities, usually by giving evidence. The court is not obliged to accept it simply because the prosecution cannot contradict it."},
 {"t":"sentencing","q":"Max is convicted of burglary of a shop. At the time of the burglary he was on bail for an unrelated offence of theft. He has no previous convictions.\n\nHow must the court treat the fact that Max committed the burglary while on bail?","o":["As an aggravating factor that the court must take into account","As irrelevant, because he has not been convicted of the theft","As a factor which the court may consider only if the theft is serious","As a reason why Max cannot be given credit for any mitigation","As a factor relevant only to bail, not to sentence"],"a":0,"e":"Under the Sentencing Code s.64, if an offence was committed while the offender was on bail, the court must treat that as an aggravating factor. This is a statutory aggravating factor, so the court has no discretion to ignore it, though the offender may still have mitigation such as his lack of previous convictions."},
 {"t":"sentencing","q":"Nia is convicted of an either-way offence. The court decides that the offence is serious enough for a community order and proposes a single drug rehabilitation requirement, as she is addicted to heroin. She has a full-time job and could pay a fine. There is nothing exceptional about her circumstances.\n\nWhat must the court also do?","o":["Nothing more, because one requirement is enough for a community order","Make the order last at least 3 years","Impose a suspended sentence instead, because the drug rehabilitation requirement is not punitive","Include at least one requirement imposed for the purpose of punishment, or impose a fine, or both","Order a pre-sentence report on whether Nia consents to a fine"],"a":3,"e":"A community order must include at least one requirement imposed for the purpose of punishment, or the court must impose a fine, or both, unless there are exceptional circumstances that would make that unjust. A drug rehabilitation requirement is primarily rehabilitative, so the court must add a punitive element such as unpaid work, a curfew or a fine. A community order can last up to 3 years, but there is no minimum of 3 years."},
 {"t":"sentencing","q":"Olu is convicted of criminal damage to bus shelters causing extensive damage. The court imposes a community order and wishes to include as much unpaid work as possible to reflect the extent of the damage.\n\nWhat is the maximum number of hours of unpaid work the court can order?","o":["240 hours","200 hours","300 hours","350 hours","400 hours"],"a":2,"e":"An unpaid work requirement in a community order must be between 40 and 300 hours. The figure of 240 hours is the maximum for unpaid work in a youth rehabilitation order for 16 and 17-year-olds, not for adults."},
 {"t":"sentencing","q":"Pat, aged 40, is convicted in October 2026 of assaulting his former partner, committed in breach of a restraining order that prohibited contact with her. The court decides that the custody threshold is passed and that the appropriate term is 6 months.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the court must suspend the sentence?","o":["The court need not suspend it, as the presumption does not apply to an offence in breach of a court order, but it may choose to","The court must suspend it, because the sentence is 12 months or less and Pat is over 18","The court must suspend it unless Pat refuses to agree to a community requirement, because the sentence is 12 months or less and he is an adult","The court cannot suspend it, because a sentence for breaching a court order must be served immediately","The court must suspend it, because Pat was convicted after 22 March 2026"],"a":0,"e":"For offenders aged 18 or over convicted on or after 22 March 2026, a custodial sentence of 12 months or less must be suspended unless there are exceptional circumstances (Sentencing Act 2026, inserting ss.264A and 277A into the Sentencing Code). But the presumption does not apply where, among other things, the offence was committed in breach of a court order such as a restraining order. The court may still suspend the sentence in its discretion; it is not barred from doing so."},
 {"t":"sentencing","q":"Quin is convicted of dangerous driving and of driving while disqualified, both committed on the same journey. The court decides that each offence merits a custodial sentence.\n\nHow should the court normally structure the sentences?","o":["Consecutively, because they are two separate offences","Consecutively, because driving while disqualified is always an aggravating factor","Consecutively, but with each sentence reduced by half","Concurrently, as they arise from the same journey, with no adjustment needed to reflect the overall offending","Concurrently, as they arise out of the same incident, while ensuring the total reflects all the offending"],"a":4,"e":"Under the Totality guideline, sentences are normally concurrent where the offences arise out of the same incident or facts, such as offences committed on the same journey. The court must still make sure the overall sentence is just and proportionate and reflects all the offending, which may mean increasing the lead sentence. Consecutive sentences are more usual for unrelated incidents."},
 {"t":"sentencing","q":"Rob, aged 23, pleads guilty in the magistrates' court to an either-way offence of assault. He has no previous convictions. The magistrates are considering whether the custody threshold is passed and, if so, the length of any sentence. No report has been prepared.\n\nWhat should the magistrates do before deciding?","o":["Impose a custodial sentence straight away, because Rob pleaded guilty","Obtain a pre-sentence report, unless they consider it unnecessary","Commit Rob to the Crown Court for a report to be prepared","Impose a community order, because a first-time offender cannot receive custody","Ask the prosecution to recommend a sentence"],"a":1,"e":"Before deciding whether the custody or community thresholds are passed, or the length of a custodial sentence, the court must obtain a pre-sentence report from the Probation Service unless it considers one unnecessary (Sentencing Code s.30). A first-time offender can receive custody if the threshold is passed, and the prosecution does not recommend sentence."},
 {"t":"sentencing","q":"Sue has pleaded guilty to theft of a handbag. While her solicitor is preparing the plea in mitigation, Sue says, 'Tell the court I only picked it up because I thought it was mine.'\n\nWhat should the solicitor do?","o":["Include the point, because the solicitor must follow the client's instructions","Include the point, but tell the court that it comes from Sue and has not been checked","Include the point only if the prosecution agrees","Not put it forward, as it contradicts her guilty plea, and advise Sue about her plea","Tell the court that Sue is guilty and ask for the maximum reduction"],"a":3,"e":"Mitigation must not contradict the guilty plea: a belief that the bag was hers would be a defence to theft (no dishonesty), not mitigation. The solicitor must not mislead the court and should advise Sue that if she maintains this account, her plea may need to be reconsidered. The solicitor must not simply follow instructions that would mislead the court."},

 {"t":"appeals","q":"Tod is tried in the magistrates' court for assault. The magistrates acquit him after ruling that a key piece of prosecution evidence was inadmissible. The prosecution believes this ruling was wrong in law.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate way for the prosecution to challenge the acquittal?","o":["By applying to the magistrates to state a case for the opinion of the High Court","By appealing to the Crown Court for a full rehearing","By applying for leave to appeal to the Court of Appeal","By asking the Attorney General to refer the case as unduly lenient","By asking the magistrates to reopen the case under s.142"],"a":0,"e":"Either party may appeal by way of case stated to the High Court where the magistrates' decision was wrong in law or in excess of jurisdiction (Magistrates' Courts Act 1980 s.111), and the application must be made within 21 days. This is the prosecution's main way to challenge a magistrates' acquittal. The prosecution has no right of appeal to the Crown Court, and the unduly lenient scheme concerns Crown Court sentences."},
 {"t":"appeals","q":"Una pleaded guilty in the magistrates' court to theft. When asked how she pleaded, she said, 'Guilty, but I honestly thought the coat was mine.' The magistrates accepted the plea and sentenced her. She now wants to appeal against conviction.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Una's position?","o":["She cannot appeal against conviction, because she pleaded guilty","She can appeal against conviction to the Court of Appeal with leave","She can apply to the High Court by way of case stated, because accepting a plea that was a mistake is an error of law","She can appeal to the Crown Court, which may remit the case for a not guilty plea because her plea was equivocal","She can appeal only against sentence and ask the Crown Court to give her an absolute discharge"],"a":3,"e":"A defendant who pleaded guilty in the magistrates' court can normally appeal against sentence only. But where the plea was equivocal (the defendant said 'guilty' but added words suggesting a defence), the Crown Court can remit the case to the magistrates to enter a not guilty plea. Una's words amount to a denial of dishonesty, so her plea was equivocal."},
 {"t":"appeals","q":"Vin is convicted after trial in the magistrates' court of assault occasioning actual bodily harm and is given a community order. He thinks the sentence is too harsh and wants to appeal against it to the Crown Court.\n\nWhat should his solicitor advise him about the risk of appealing?","o":["There is no risk, because a defendant can never be given a more severe sentence on his own appeal","The Crown Court can impose any sentence the magistrates could have imposed, which may be more severe, including custody","The Crown Court can increase the sentence up to the maximum available in the Crown Court for the offence of assault occasioning ABH","The Crown Court can increase the sentence only if the prosecution cross-appeals","The Crown Court can increase the sentence only if new evidence emerges at the appeal"],"a":1,"e":"On appeal from the magistrates' court, the Crown Court may impose any sentence the magistrates could have imposed, which can be more severe than the original sentence (Senior Courts Act 1981 s.48). The limit is the magistrates' powers, not the Crown Court's. This contrasts with the Court of Appeal, which cannot deal with a defendant more severely on his own appeal."},
 {"t":"appeals","q":"Wade is convicted of theft in the magistrates' court. He believes the magistrates misinterpreted the meaning of 'dishonestly' and applies to them to state a case for the High Court. A week later, he decides he would rather appeal to the Crown Court for a full rehearing.\n\nCan Wade now appeal to the Crown Court?","o":["Yes, because he is still within 15 business days of sentence","Yes, because the two routes can be pursued at the same time","Yes, but only with the leave of the Crown Court","No, because an appeal to the Crown Court is available only against sentence","No, because once he applied for a case to be stated, he lost his right of appeal to the Crown Court"],"a":4,"e":"Under Magistrates' Courts Act 1980 s.111(4), once a defendant applies for the magistrates to state a case, he loses his right of appeal to the Crown Court. So Wade must choose his route carefully. Being within the 15-business-day time limit does not help once the right has been lost."},
 {"t":"appeals","q":"Xia is convicted of robbery in the Crown Court and receives a sentence that the victim's family consider far too lenient. The family asks the victim's solicitor what can be done.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The victim can appeal to the Court of Appeal against the sentence within 28 days","The prosecution can appeal to the Crown Court for the sentence to be increased","The Attorney General may refer the sentence to the Court of Appeal as unduly lenient, with leave, within 28 days","Nothing can be done, because a sentence can never be increased after it is passed","The Criminal Cases Review Commission can refer the sentence to the Court of Appeal immediately at the family's request"],"a":2,"e":"The Attorney General (or Solicitor General) may refer a Crown Court sentence for an indictable-only offence, such as robbery, or certain either-way offences to the Court of Appeal as unduly lenient, with leave, within 28 days of sentence (CJA 1988 s.36). The Court of Appeal can then increase the sentence. Victims have no right of appeal, and the CCRC deals with possible miscarriages of justice against defendants."},
 {"t":"appeals","q":"Yuri is on trial in the Crown Court for burglary. At the close of the prosecution case, the judge rules that the only evidence of identification is inadmissible, which means there is no case to answer. The prosecution believes the ruling is wrong.\n\nWhat can the prosecution do?","o":["Appeal against the ruling to the Court of Appeal, agreeing that Yuri will be acquitted if the appeal fails or is abandoned","Appeal to the High Court by way of case stated within 21 days","Ask the Attorney General to refer the acquittal as unduly lenient","Ask the judge to discharge the jury and order a retrial before a fresh jury, where the admissibility of the evidence can be argued again","Nothing, because the prosecution has no right to challenge any ruling in the Crown Court"],"a":0,"e":"The prosecution can appeal against a terminating ruling in a Crown Court trial, such as a ruling of no case to answer or one excluding vital evidence, to the Court of Appeal (CJA 2003 s.58). It must agree that the defendant should be acquitted if the appeal fails or is abandoned. Case stated is for the magistrates' court (or the Crown Court on appeals from it), and the unduly lenient scheme concerns sentences."},
 {"t":"appeals","q":"Zara is convicted of fraud in the Crown Court. She applies for leave to appeal against conviction. A single judge of the Court of Appeal considers her application on the papers and refuses leave.\n\nWhat can Zara do next, and what risk should she be warned about?","o":["Nothing, because the single judge's decision is final","Apply directly to the Supreme Court for leave to appeal, with no risk to her sentence because the single judge has already refused","Appeal to the High Court by way of case stated, which is quicker","Renew the application to the full court, usually within 14 days, at the risk of losing time served if it lacks merit","Renew the application to the trial judge, who can grant a certificate at any time with no risk"],"a":3,"e":"If the single judge refuses leave, the defendant can renew the application to the full Court of Appeal, usually within 14 days. If the court considers the application to be without merit, it can direct that time spent in custody pending the appeal does not count towards the sentence (Criminal Appeal Act 1968 s.29). The Supreme Court route needs a certified point of law of general public importance."},
 {"t":"appeals","q":"Abe is convicted of robbery in the Crown Court. His defence was alibi, but his alibi witness could not be traced before trial. Two months after his conviction, the witness comes forward and confirms that Abe was with her at the time. Abe has applied for leave to appeal.\n\nWhat will the Court of Appeal consider in deciding whether to receive the witness's evidence?","o":["Only whether the witness is willing to give evidence on oath","Whether it is capable of belief, may afford a ground of appeal, would have been admissible, and why it was not adduced at trial","Only whether the evidence, if it had been heard at the trial, would definitely have led the jury to acquit Abe of the robbery charge","Whether the prosecution consents to the evidence being admitted","Whether the trial judge certifies that the case is fit for appeal"],"a":1,"e":"The Court of Appeal may receive fresh evidence if it thinks it necessary or expedient in the interests of justice, considering whether it appears capable of belief, may afford a ground for allowing the appeal, would have been admissible at trial, and whether there is a reasonable explanation for the failure to adduce it at trial (Criminal Appeal Act 1968 s.23). The prosecution's consent is not required, and the test for allowing the appeal is whether the conviction is unsafe."},
 {"t":"appeals","q":"Bea is convicted after trial in the magistrates' court on 1 March. The case is adjourned for a pre-sentence report, and she is sentenced on 29 March. She wants to appeal against her conviction to the Crown Court.\n\nWithin what time must Bea serve her notice of appeal?","o":["Within 15 business days of 1 March, the date of conviction","Within 21 days of 1 March, the date of conviction","Within 28 days of 29 March, the date of sentence","Within 3 months of 29 March, the date of sentence","Within 15 business days of 29 March, the date of sentence"],"a":4,"e":"Notice of appeal from the magistrates' court to the Crown Court must be served within 15 business days after sentence, and where sentence is adjourned after conviction, time runs from the date of sentence. So time runs from 29 March. Twenty-one days applies to case stated, 28 days to the Court of Appeal, and 3 months to judicial review."},
 {"t":"appeals","q":"Cy was convicted of murder in the Crown Court five years ago, and his appeal to the Court of Appeal was dismissed. New scientific evidence has now emerged which was not available at his trial or appeal and casts serious doubt on the prosecution's forensic case.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate step for Cy to take?","o":["Apply to the Court of Appeal for a second appeal as of right","Apply to the Crown Court for a retrial","Apply to the Criminal Cases Review Commission, which may refer the conviction back to the Court of Appeal","Ask the Attorney General to refer the conviction as unsafe","Apply to the High Court for judicial review of the original conviction within 3 months of discovering the new evidence"],"a":2,"e":"The Criminal Cases Review Commission investigates possible miscarriages of justice and can refer a conviction to the appropriate appeal court where there is a real possibility it would not be upheld, normally because of new evidence or argument, and normally only after an appeal has failed. There is no second appeal as of right, and the Attorney General's references concern lenient sentences or points of law after acquittal."},

 {"t":"youth-court","q":"Dan, aged 15, is charged with burglary of a house while the occupiers were away. He has two previous convictions for theft. Applying the Sentencing Children and Young People guideline to the prosecution's facts, the likely sentence would be a detention and training order of around 12 months.\n\nWhere should Dan's case be tried?","o":["In the Crown Court, because domestic burglary is a grave crime and Dan already has previous convictions","In the Crown Court, because Dan has previous convictions","In the youth court, as there is no real prospect of a sentence beyond its powers","In the adult magistrates' court, because Dan is over 14","In the youth court, but only if Dan consents to summary trial"],"a":2,"e":"Domestic burglary is a grave crime, but the youth court should send a child to the Crown Court only if there is a real prospect that the sentence needed is beyond the 2-year maximum of a detention and training order. A likely sentence of around 12 months is within the youth court's powers, so the case stays there. A child has no right to elect Crown Court trial and does not need to consent to summary trial."},
 {"t":"youth-court","q":"Eli, aged 16, is charged with manslaughter after a fight in which another teenager died. He has no previous convictions and appears before the youth court.\n\nWhat must happen to Eli's case?","o":["It must be sent to the Crown Court, because it is a homicide offence","It must be tried in the youth court, because Eli is under 18","It will be tried in the youth court unless the prosecution applies for it to be sent","It must be sent to the Crown Court only if Eli is jointly charged with an adult","The youth court will decide whether it is a grave crime before deciding where it is tried"],"a":0,"e":"A child charged with homicide (murder or manslaughter) must be sent to the Crown Court (Crime and Disorder Act 1998 s.51A). There is no discretion and no need to apply the grave crimes test. The youth court deals with most offences by children, but homicide is an exception."},
 {"t":"youth-court","q":"Fay, aged 14, has no previous convictions. She pleads not guilty to theft, an imprisonable offence, and is found guilty after trial in the youth court. The court considers that a community sentence is appropriate.\n\nWhich sentence is the court most likely to impose?","o":["A compulsory referral order, because she is a first-time offender","A discretionary referral order, because the offence is imprisonable","A detention and training order, because she has been found guilty of an imprisonable offence after trial","A suspended sentence order, because she is under 18","A youth rehabilitation order, because a referral order is not available after a not guilty plea"],"a":4,"e":"A referral order requires a guilty plea; a child found guilty after a not guilty plea cannot receive one. So the main community sentence available is a youth rehabilitation order. A detention and training order would be available for a 14-year-old only if she is a persistent offender and the custody threshold is passed, and suspended sentence orders are for adults."},
 {"t":"youth-court","q":"Gus, aged 16, has one previous conviction, for which he received a referral order last year. He now pleads guilty to criminal damage, an imprisonable offence. The court is considering a community sentence.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the availability of a referral order?","o":["A referral order is compulsory, because Gus has pleaded guilty to an imprisonable offence","The court may make a referral order, because the discretionary conditions are met as Gus has pleaded guilty","A referral order is not available, because a child who has already had one referral order can never have another","A referral order is not available, because Gus has a previous conviction","A referral order is available only if Gus agrees to it"],"a":1,"e":"The compulsory referral conditions require the child never to have been convicted before, so they do not apply to Gus. But the discretionary conditions allow the court to make a referral order where the child pleads guilty to at least one offence, including where the child has previous convictions or has had a referral order before. So the court may, but need not, make one."},
 {"t":"youth-court","q":"Hal, aged 17, is due to appear in the youth court charged with theft. His mother tells his solicitor that she does not intend to come to court because of work.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position on his mother's attendance?","o":["She must attend every hearing, because Hal is under 18 and the court must have a parent present","She must attend unless Hal objects to her presence","She cannot attend, because the youth court is closed to the public","The court may require her to attend, but it is not obliged to, because Hal is aged 16 or over","She must attend only the hearing at which Hal is sentenced"],"a":3,"e":"A parent or guardian must attend every hearing if the child is under 16 (unless that would be unreasonable), and the court may require them to attend if the child is 16 or 17 (Children and Young Persons Act 1933 s.34A). Hal is 17, so the court has a discretion. The youth court is closed to the general public, but parents are directly involved and may attend."},
 {"t":"youth-court","q":"Ivy, aged 15, appears in the youth court charged with assault. A local journalist attends the hearing and wants to publish Ivy's name and school in a newspaper report.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The journalist may publish Ivy's details, because the press may attend the youth court","The journalist may publish Ivy's details only if the court makes an order allowing it before the hearing","The journalist cannot attend, because the youth court is closed to the press","The journalist may publish Ivy's name but not her school","Automatic reporting restrictions prevent publication of anything likely to identify Ivy, unless the court lifts them"],"a":4,"e":"Automatic reporting restrictions apply in the youth court (Children and Young Persons Act 1933 s.49): nothing may be published that is likely to identify the child, such as her name, address or school. The court can lift them only in limited cases. The press may attend the youth court even though the public may not."},
 {"t":"youth-court","q":"Jay, aged 16, is found guilty of robbery in the youth court. The court decides that the custody threshold is passed and that a youth rehabilitation order with intensive supervision and surveillance is not appropriate. It considers that the right term of detention is 9 months.\n\nWhat sentence can the court impose?","o":["A detention and training order of 9 months","A suspended sentence order of 9 months","A detention and training order of a permitted fixed length, such as 8 or 10 months, as 9 months is not available","Long-term detention of 9 months, because the youth court can impose a custodial term of any length up to 2 years for a 16-year-old","A youth rehabilitation order, because custody cannot be imposed on a 16-year-old"],"a":2,"e":"A detention and training order can be made only for fixed terms of 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 18 or 24 months, so 9 months is not available and the court must choose a permitted term. Long-term detention under s.250 can be imposed only by the Crown Court, and suspended sentence orders are for adults. A 16-year-old can receive a detention and training order once the custody threshold is passed."},
 {"t":"youth-court","q":"Kai committed a theft when he was 17. He first appeared in the youth court at 17 and pleaded not guilty. Before the trial, he turned 18.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how Kai's case will be dealt with?","o":["The youth court may continue to deal with the case even though Kai is now 18","The case must be transferred to the adult magistrates' court for trial","The case must be sent to the Crown Court because Kai is now an adult","The case must be discontinued and Kai recharged as an adult","Kai can now elect trial in the Crown Court because he is an adult"],"a":0,"e":"For venue, what matters is the defendant's age at the first appearance. If the defendant turns 18 after proceedings have begun, the youth court can continue to deal with the case (or remit it to the adult court). If convicted, the court's starting point for sentence will be the sentence likely to have been imposed at the date of the offence."},
 {"t":"youth-court","q":"Lily, aged 14, has no previous convictions. She pleads not guilty to robbery and is found guilty after a trial in the youth court. The court considers the offence so serious that, but for the availability of intensive alternatives, custody would be appropriate. It is considering a youth rehabilitation order with intensive supervision and surveillance.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether this order is available?","o":["It is available, because the offence is imprisonable and passes the custody threshold","It is available, because robbery is a grave crime","It is not available, because the youth court cannot make orders with intensive supervision","It is not available, because Lily is under 15 and is not a persistent offender","It is not available, because a child found guilty after trial can receive only a fine or a discharge"],"a":3,"e":"A youth rehabilitation order with intensive supervision and surveillance is available only where the offence is imprisonable and crosses the custody threshold, and, if the child is under 15, only if the child is a persistent offender. Lily is 14 with no previous convictions, so it is not available. A child found guilty after trial can still receive a youth rehabilitation order (without intensive supervision), so E is wrong, and whether robbery is a grave crime affects venue, not this order."},
 {"t":"youth-court","q":"Mo, aged 15, is charged with robbery. The youth court refuses him bail because there are substantial grounds for believing he would commit further offences.\n\nWhere will Mo be remanded?","o":["To an adult prison, because bail has been refused","To local authority accommodation, or youth detention accommodation if strict conditions are met","To his parents' home under a curfew, because a child under 16 cannot be remanded in custody of any kind","To a police station until his next hearing","To a secure hospital, because he is under 18"],"a":1,"e":"A child refused bail is remanded to local authority accommodation, or, if strict conditions about the offence and risk are met, to youth detention accommodation. Children are not remanded to adult prisons. A child can be refused bail, so he will not simply go home, and a police station or hospital is not the proper place of remand."}
];

;
window.SQE_BERT = window.SQE_BERT || {};
window.SQE_BERT["ethics"] = [
 {"t":"principles-competence","q":"Martin is a solicitor at a high street firm. His personal social media profile names the firm and describes him as a solicitor. One evening, at home, he posts a series of abusive and demeaning messages about a particular ethnic group. No client is involved.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Martin's position under the SRA Principles?","o":["He has not breached the Principles, because conduct outside practice is a matter for his private life alone, unless it is criminal.","He has breached only the duty to act in the best interests of each client, as his clients may be offended.","He has breached the Principle of honesty, because the messages he posted were untrue and unfair.","He is likely to have failed to uphold public trust and confidence in the profession, even though he acted outside practice.","He has not breached the Principles, because no client was affected and he did not post during working hours."],"a":3,"e":"The Principles reach conduct **outside practice** where it affects the trust the public places in solicitors, and abusive discriminatory posts by someone who identifies as a solicitor plainly do (Principle 2, and Principle 6 on equality, diversity and inclusion). Private life is not a complete shield, and it does not matter that no client was involved. Honesty is not the issue, because nothing suggests he was deceiving anyone."},
 {"t":"principles-competence","q":"Ayesha acts for a buyer of commercial premises. The seller's solicitor asks her on the telephone whether her client's lender has approved the lease terms. Ayesha has not checked, but assumes the answer is yes because the lender usually approves such leases, and says: \"Yes, the lender has approved them.\" In fact the lender had raised objections. Ayesha genuinely believed what she said.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ayesha's conduct?","o":["She is likely to have acted without integrity, because she recklessly made a misleading statement, even if she was not dishonest.","She acted dishonestly, because the statement she made to the seller's solicitor was in fact untrue.","She did not breach any Principle, because she genuinely and reasonably believed her statement to be true when she made it to the seller's solicitor.","She made an honest mistake that raises only a question about her competence, not her integrity.","She did not breach any Principle unless the seller suffered a financial loss by relying on her statement."],"a":0,"e":"**Integrity** is wider than honesty: it means meeting the ethical standards of the profession, and **recklessly** making a misleading statement (stating something as fact without any basis for checking it) is a classic example of lack of integrity. She is probably not **dishonest** under the Ivey test, because she genuinely believed it was true. It is more than a mere competence slip because she presented a guess as a fact, and loss is not required for a breach."},
 {"t":"principles-competence","q":"Bryony acts for Fenwick Ltd, which is negotiating to buy a small software company. Fenwick Ltd's managing director instructs Bryony to tell the seller's solicitor that Fenwick Ltd has received a \"firm offer\" from a rival company to buy the same business for £2 million, so the seller should lower its price. No such offer exists. The managing director says this is normal negotiating tactics and in the company's best interests.\n\nWhat should Bryony do?","o":["Make the statement, because the duty to act in the client's best interests requires her to follow its instructions in the negotiations.","Make the statement, because exaggeration in commercial negotiations is accepted and does not mislead anyone.","Refuse to make the statement, because her duties of honesty and integrity take precedence over her client's interests.","Make the statement only if the managing director confirms the instructions in writing before she does so.","Make the statement, but only orally, so that nothing misleading is recorded in writing between the parties."],"a":2,"e":"Where the Principles conflict, those protecting the wider public interest, including **honesty** (Principle 4) and **integrity** (Principle 5), take precedence over the client's interests (Principle 7). Inventing a rival offer is a positive lie, not acceptable negotiating puffery, and it would also mislead the other side (Code para 1.4). Written instructions or giving the lie orally make no difference."},
 {"t":"principles-competence","q":"Claudia is an in-house solicitor employed by a utility company. The company's chief executive tells her to sign a statement to the industry regulator confirming that the company has complied with certain safety reporting duties. Claudia knows that several reports were never filed. The chief executive reminds her that he pays her salary and that she must \"support the business\".\n\nWhich of the following best describes Claudia's position?","o":["She may sign the statement, because an in-house solicitor's main duty is to follow the instructions of her employer.","She may sign the statement, because the SRA Principles do not apply to solicitors who work in-house.","She may sign the statement if she adds a note that it was prepared on the chief executive's instructions.","She must sign the statement but should then report the chief executive to the regulator afterwards.","She must not sign the statement, because she must act with independence and honesty even towards her employer."],"a":4,"e":"The Principles apply to in-house solicitors in the same way as to those in private practice. **Independence** (Principle 3) means she must not let her employer compromise her professional judgement, and signing a statement she knows is false would be **dishonest** (Principle 4). Adding a note does not cure a knowingly false statement, and she cannot sign first and report later."},
 {"t":"principles-competence","q":"Gavin acts for Mrs Lowe in a debt claim. He forgot to issue the claim form last week as promised. When Mrs Lowe telephones anxiously asking for news, Gavin tells her the claim \"was issued on Monday\", intending to issue it the next morning so that she will not worry. He issues it the next day and the delay causes her no loss.\n\nHow is Gavin's statement most likely to be judged?","o":["As honest, because he intended only to reassure his anxious client, and he did in fact issue the claim the very next day.","As honest, because Mrs Lowe suffered no loss as a result of the short delay in issuing her claim.","As dishonest, because he knew it was untrue and ordinary decent people would regard such a lie as dishonest.","As a lack of competence only, because the real problem was his failure to issue the claim on time.","As dishonest only if Gavin himself realised that other people would consider the statement dishonest."],"a":2,"e":"Under the **Ivey test** the tribunal asks what Gavin actually knew (that the claim had not been issued) and then whether, on that knowledge, ordinary decent people would regard his conduct as dishonest. A deliberate lie to a client is dishonest even if the motive was kind and no loss followed. The missed deadline was a competence issue, but covering it up with a lie turns it into dishonesty, which usually leads to strike-off."},
 {"t":"principles-competence","q":"Mr Iqbal telephones a small firm to ask whether a solicitor can help him with a lease of a shop for his business. The partner, Gerald, asks about him and, on learning of Mr Iqbal's religion, says the firm is \"too busy\" and declines to act. Gerald later tells a colleague that he would \"rather not deal with people of that faith\". He says solicitors are free to choose their clients.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gerald's conduct?","o":["It is permitted, because solicitors are generally free to decide whether or not to accept instructions from any prospective client.","It is permitted, because he gave a neutral reason to Mr Iqbal and so did not discriminate against him.","It breaches the Code only if Mr Iqbal could not find another solicitor to act for him on the lease.","It breaches the Code, because he must not allow personal views to lead him to refuse a client on discriminatory grounds.","It is permitted, because the duty not to discriminate applies only once someone has become a client."],"a":3,"e":"Solicitors are generally free to refuse instructions, but **not for a discriminatory reason**: Code para 1.1 forbids letting personal views affect professional relationships, and Principle 6 requires solicitors to encourage equality, diversity and inclusion (the Equality Act 2010 also applies to prospective clients). Giving a false neutral reason makes matters worse, not better. It is irrelevant whether Mr Iqbal could go elsewhere."},
 {"t":"principles-competence","q":"The SRA has referred allegations against Rhiannon, a solicitor, to the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal. It alleges that she misled a client about the progress of a matter. Rhiannon's advocate argues that the Tribunal can find the allegations proved only if it is sure of them, because her career is at stake.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the standard of proof the Tribunal will apply?","o":["The criminal standard, so the allegations must be proved beyond reasonable doubt.","The civil standard, so the allegations must be proved on the balance of probabilities.","The criminal standard for dishonesty allegations and the civil standard for all other allegations.","A heightened civil standard requiring clear and convincing evidence because strike-off is possible.","No fixed standard, because the Tribunal decides what is fair and reasonable in each case."],"a":1,"e":"Since the Solicitors (Disciplinary Proceedings) Rules 2019 came into force on **25 November 2019**, the Tribunal decides allegations on the **civil standard**, the balance of probabilities (more likely than not). It used to apply the criminal standard, which is why that option is a tempting distractor. There is no separate higher standard for dishonesty, and \"fair and reasonable\" is the Legal Ombudsman's approach, not the Tribunal's."},
 {"t":"principles-competence","q":"Following an inspection, the SRA finds that Harrow & Co, a traditional partnership, has for several years failed to carry out proper customer due diligence and to have adequate anti-money laundering controls. There is no suggestion of dishonesty. The SRA considers that a fine of £150,000 would be proportionate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the SRA can impose this fine itself?","o":["It cannot, because the SRA's own power to fine a traditional firm is limited to £25,000 in every case.","It can, because there is no limit on the fines the SRA can impose for economic crime matters such as these.","It cannot, because only the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal may fine a firm where no one has been dishonest.","It can, but only if the firm agrees to the fine and admits the breaches of the money laundering rules.","It cannot, because the SRA may only rebuke or impose conditions, and all fines are for the Tribunal."],"a":1,"e":"The SRA's own fining power for individuals and traditional firms is normally capped at **£25,000**, but since the **Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act 2023** the cap does not apply to **economic crime** matters, which include money laundering failures. So the SRA may impose an unlimited fine here. Outside economic crime, a fine above £25,000 would have to go to the Tribunal."},
 {"t":"principles-competence","q":"Priyanka acts for Mr Cole in a boundary dispute. The neighbour has offered £5,000 to settle. Priyanka considers the claim is worth about £20,000 and that Mr Cole has good prospects at trial. Mr Cole says he is tired of the dispute and wants to accept the offer straight away.\n\nWhat should Priyanka do?","o":["Advise him clearly of her view and the risks, then act on his decision if he still wishes to accept.","Refuse to accept the offer, because acting in his best interests means obtaining the best financial outcome.","Accept the offer immediately without further advice, because a client's instructions must always be followed.","Stop acting for Mr Cole, because she cannot act on instructions she considers are against his interests.","Delay responding to the offer until Mr Cole has had time to reconsider and change his mind."],"a":0,"e":"Acting in the client's **best interests** (Principle 7) and with **independence** (Principle 3) means giving her honest professional view, even if unwelcome, so that he makes an informed decision (Code para 8.6). It does not mean overriding a lawful decision that belongs to the client. Refusing to accept, stopping acting, or stalling would all disregard his instructions, while accepting without advice fails to give him the information he needs."},
 {"t":"principles-competence","q":"Owen is a partner in a law firm. Outside work he has a consensual relationship with another adult who has no connection with the firm, its clients or his work. A colleague who disapproves tells Owen he should report himself to the SRA for a lack of integrity.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Owen's position?","o":["He has breached the Principle of integrity, because integrity requires solicitors to meet high moral standards in all areas.","He has breached the duty to uphold public trust, because any personal conduct that others disapprove of damages trust.","He must report the relationship to the SRA, which will decide whether it amounts to a breach of integrity.","He has breached the Principles unless he discloses the relationship to his partners and the firm's COLP.","The Principles are unlikely to be engaged, because the conduct is private and does not touch his professional standing."],"a":4,"e":"The Principles apply outside practice only so far as conduct realistically affects the trust the public places in the solicitor and the profession. Integrity is **not a free-standing code of morality**: it is measured against the standards in the SRA's rules (as the High Court held in a case about a senior partner's private conduct). A purely private, lawful relationship with no connection to his work does not engage the Principles, so there is nothing to report."},
 {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"Imran acts for the buyer of a house. On a telephone call before exchange, the seller's solicitor asks what will happen to the 10% deposit. Imran says: \"I will hold the deposit and not release it to my client until the seller's planning certificate arrives.\" The seller's solicitor makes a note and exchanges contracts. The certificate has not yet arrived, and Imran's client now demands the deposit back.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Imran's position?","o":["He may release the deposit, because the word \"undertake\" was never used in the telephone conversation.","He may release the deposit, because an undertaking must be given in writing to be binding on a solicitor.","He must not release the deposit, because his oral statement was an undertaking that the other solicitor relied on.","He must release the deposit, because his client's instructions override any promise he made on the client's behalf.","He may release the deposit if he first gives the seller's solicitor reasonable notice of his intention to do so."],"a":2,"e":"An **undertaking** is any statement, **oral or written**, whether or not it uses the word \"undertake\", made to someone who reasonably relies on it, that the solicitor will do or not do something. Imran's promise was relied on when contracts were exchanged, so it binds him (Code para 1.3). The client cannot release him; only the seller's solicitor can, and giving notice does not change that."},
 {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"Lauren acts for a seller of a flat. The buyer's solicitor asks when he will receive the original lease. Lauren replies by email: \"I expect to be able to send the lease next week, once it comes back from storage.\" The storage company loses the lease and it cannot be sent for three weeks.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Lauren's position?","o":["She has breached an undertaking, because any written statement to another solicitor about a future act is one.","She has probably not breached an undertaking, because her email stated an expectation rather than a promise.","She has breached an undertaking, because she should not have mentioned a date she could not guarantee.","She has breached an undertaking, but is excused because the delay was caused by the storage company.","She has not breached an undertaking, because only statements about money can amount to undertakings."],"a":1,"e":"An undertaking must be a **promise** to do or refrain from doing something. Words such as \"I expect\" or \"I will try\" express a hope or expectation, so they are probably not undertakings. If she had promised, the storage company's fault would be no excuse, because undertakings must be performed even when performance becomes difficult. Undertakings are not limited to money."},
 {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"Neil acts for a client who is remortgaging. He gives the outgoing lender an undertaking \"subject to receipt of the advance from Northway Bank, to redeem your mortgage within five working days of completion\". Northway Bank later withdraws its offer and the advance never arrives. The outgoing lender complains that its mortgage has not been redeemed.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Neil's position?","o":["He has breached the undertaking and must redeem the mortgage from his firm's own money.","He has breached the undertaking, because a solicitor cannot attach any conditions to an undertaking given to another lender.","He has breached the undertaking unless his client agrees to release him from performing it.","He has not breached the undertaking, because it was conditional on receiving funds that never arrived.","He has breached the undertaking, but the SRA will waive it because the bank withdrew its offer."],"a":3,"e":"Good practice is to make an undertaking about money **conditional** on receiving the funds, and Neil did so. Because the condition (receipt of the advance) was never met, the obligation never arose. Conditions are perfectly proper, and the client's release would be irrelevant in any event, because only the recipient can release an undertaking."},
 {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"Toby acts for a defendant in a civil claim. To secure an adjournment, he gives the claimant's solicitors a written undertaking that \"we will pay your client's wasted costs of £8,000 within 14 days\". His client then fails to put him in funds and stops answering his calls.\n\nWhat is Toby's position?","o":["He is not bound, because he gave the undertaking on his client's behalf and only the client is liable to pay.","He is not bound, because his client's failure to put him in funds means that performing the undertaking has become impossible for him.","He is bound only if the claimant's solicitors can prove that they had a contract with him for the payment.","He may ask the SRA to release him from the undertaking because his client has failed to provide the money.","He is personally bound and must make sure the £8,000 is paid within 14 days, even though his client has not paid him."],"a":4,"e":"An undertaking binds the solicitor (and the firm) **personally**, even if given on the client's behalf, unless personal liability was clearly excluded. It must be performed even if it becomes costly or the client lets him down, which is why money undertakings should be given only when funds are held or made conditional on receipt. The SRA cannot release an undertaking, and no contract need be shown because the court can enforce it against him as an officer of the court."},
 {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"On completion of a sale, Hannah undertakes to the buyer's solicitor \"to send the discharge of the seller's mortgages\". The seller had two mortgages, one with Lark Bank and one with Swift Finance. Hannah sends the discharge for the Lark Bank mortgage only, saying she meant only the main mortgage. The buyer's solicitor says the undertaking covered both.\n\nHow is the undertaking most likely to be interpreted?","o":["In favour of Hannah, as covering only the main mortgage, because that is what she intended when she gave it.","In favour of the buyer's solicitor, as covering both mortgages, because ambiguity is read against the giver.","As void for uncertainty, so that Hannah is under no obligation to send either discharge.","In favour of Hannah, because an undertaking is read narrowly in favour of the solicitor who gives it.","As covering only the mortgage that was redeemed first, because that was discharged on completion."],"a":1,"e":"An ambiguous undertaking is generally interpreted **in favour of the recipient**, because the giver chose the words and could have made them clear. \"The seller's mortgages\" naturally covers both. Hannah's private intention does not cut it down, and the court will not treat a professional promise as void merely because she drafted it loosely."},
 {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"Lena, a solicitor, gave the claimant's solicitors an undertaking to hold the proceeds of sale of a property to their order. In breach of it, she paid the money to her own client, who has disappeared. The claimant has lost £40,000. The claimant's solicitors want their client compensated.\n\nWhich of the following is the claimant's best route to obtain compensation?","o":["Apply to the High Court to exercise its supervisory jurisdiction over Lena as an officer of the court.","Complain to the SRA, which can order Lena to pay compensation for breach of the undertaking.","Complain to the Legal Ombudsman, which can award compensation up to £50,000 for breach of an undertaking.","Sue Lena for breach of contract, which is the only way an undertaking can be enforced.","Apply to the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal for an order that Lena compensates the claimant."],"a":0,"e":"Solicitors are **officers of the court**, so the High Court can use its **supervisory jurisdiction** to order a solicitor to perform an undertaking or compensate the recipient, without any need to prove a contract. The SRA and the Tribunal deal with breach as **misconduct** but do not award compensation to the recipient. The Legal Ombudsman deals with complaints by a solicitor's own clients about service, and the claimant was not Lena's client."},
 {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"Jasmine acts for the buyer of a house. The seller is represented by Max, who calls himself a \"property consultant\" and is not a solicitor or regulated by any legal regulator. Max offers to give an undertaking to redeem the seller's mortgage from the sale price on completion.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the risk of relying on Max's undertaking?","o":["It is as safe as a solicitor's undertaking, because any undertaking given in a conveyancing transaction can be enforced by the court.","It is safe to rely on, provided Max puts the undertaking in writing and uses the word \"undertake\".","It can be enforced by the SRA, because the SRA regulates anyone who gives undertakings in conveyancing.","It cannot be enforced through the SRA or the court's supervisory jurisdiction over solicitors, so Jasmine should not rely on it.","It is safe to rely on, because the Legal Ombudsman can compensate the buyer if Max fails to comply."],"a":3,"e":"The special protection for undertakings comes from **solicitors' professional obligations**: the SRA can discipline a solicitor for breach, and the court can enforce it against a solicitor as an officer of the court. An unregulated person answers to no regulator, and the court's supervisory jurisdiction does not apply to him. Written wording does not change this, and the Legal Ombudsman only covers authorised lawyers."},
 {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"Kieran acts for a builder who is owed £3,000 by a homeowner, Mrs Dunn, for work on her kitchen. Mrs Dunn has no solicitor. The builder asks Kieran to write telling her that unless she pays within 7 days, \"we will report you to the police for fraud and you may be sent to prison\". There is no evidence that Mrs Dunn has acted dishonestly; she disputes the quality of the work.\n\nWhat should Kieran do?","o":["Send the letter, because he must follow his client's instructions and act in his client's best interests.","Send the letter, because it is not misleading to mention a possible criminal complaint to the debtor.","Refuse to send that letter, but send a letter setting out the builder's civil claim and a reasonable deadline.","Send the letter only after first advising Mrs Dunn in writing to take independent legal advice.","Refuse to write to Mrs Dunn at all, because she has no solicitor and he cannot contact her directly."],"a":2,"e":"Threatening criminal proceedings or imprisonment to extract payment of a disputed civil debt takes **unfair advantage** of an unrepresented person (Code para 1.2), misstates the consequences (para 1.4) and fails to uphold public trust (Principle 2). Kieran can still pursue the client's genuine claim firmly in a measured letter. He may write directly to an unrepresented person; recommending independent advice does not make an improper threat acceptable."},
 {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"Selina acts for a seller of a house. She knows from her client that the neighbour has been complaining about a shared drive for months and has threatened to involve solicitors, but has not yet made a formal claim. In replying to the buyer's pre-contract enquiries, Selina proposes to answer the question \"Are you aware of any disputes with neighbours?\" with: \"The seller has not received any formal claim.\"\n\nWhich of the following best describes the proposed answer?","o":["It is acceptable, because the statement is literally true and no formal claim has been made.","It is acceptable, because a seller's solicitor has no duty to volunteer facts that are unhelpful.","It is acceptable, because the buyer can make its own enquiries of the neighbour before exchange.","It is acceptable, provided her client approves the wording of the reply in writing.","It would mislead the buyer by a half-truth, so she must not give it in that form."],"a":4,"e":"Code para 1.4 is wider than lying: a **half-truth** designed to create a false impression is misleading. The question asks about disputes, and a literally true answer that hides a known dispute misleads. While a solicitor need not volunteer every unhelpful fact, once she answers a question, the answer must not mislead, and the client's approval cannot authorise misleading the other side."},
 {"t":"trust-fairness-undertakings","q":"Ravi is a manager (partner) at a law firm. He regularly sees another partner shouting at a trainee, mocking her work in front of others and setting her impossible deadlines. The trainee has not complained and Ravi is not her supervisor.\n\nWhat does the Code require of Ravi?","o":["He must challenge the behaviour, because managers must challenge colleagues who bully or harass others.","He need do nothing, because the trainee has not complained and he is not responsible for her supervision.","He need do nothing unless the behaviour amounts to discrimination on the ground of a protected characteristic.","He must report the other partner to the police, because bullying at work is a criminal offence.","He need do nothing, because treatment of staff is a matter of employment law, not professional conduct."],"a":0,"e":"Code para 1.5 requires solicitors to treat colleagues fairly and with respect and not to bully or harass them, and **managers must challenge** behaviour that falls below that standard. This applies whether or not the victim complains and whether or not Ravi supervises her. Bullying is a conduct issue, not only an employment issue, and it need not amount to discrimination."},
 {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"Hugo acts for the claimant in a professional negligence claim. His client suggests that the expert surveyor they want to instruct should be paid 10% of any damages recovered, instead of an hourly fee, to keep costs down if the claim fails. The expert is willing.\n\nWhat should Hugo advise?","o":["The arrangement is permitted, because experts are independent and may agree any fee basis with the client.","The expert must not be paid a fee that depends on the outcome, but may be paid a proper fee for his work.","The arrangement is permitted, provided the expert's fee arrangement is disclosed to the court and the defendant.","The arrangement is permitted, because the rule against outcome-linked payments applies only to witnesses of fact.","The expert must not be paid anything at all, because any payment to a witness risks influencing the evidence."],"a":1,"e":"Code para 2.3 forbids providing or offering any benefit to a witness that depends on the nature of their evidence or the outcome of the case, so a **contingency fee for an expert** is not allowed. An expert may of course be paid a proper fee for the work done. Disclosure does not cure the problem, and the rule covers experts as well as witnesses of fact."},
 {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"Orla is defending Mr Grey on a charge of criminal damage. She learns that a bystander, Mrs Hart, who saw the incident, has given a statement to the police and has been listed as a prosecution witness. Orla wants to interview Mrs Hart herself.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Orla may do so?","o":["She must not interview her, because Mrs Hart is a prosecution witness and contact would interfere with evidence.","She may interview her, because no party owns a witness, but it is good practice to tell the prosecution first.","She may interview her only with the permission of the court, which must be sought at a hearing.","She may interview her only if Mrs Hart agrees to give evidence for the defence instead of the prosecution.","She must not interview her unless the prosecution consents and a police officer is present throughout."],"a":1,"e":"There is **no property in a witness**: no party has an exclusive claim to a witness, so a defence solicitor may interview a prosecution witness. In criminal cases it is good practice to tell the prosecution first and offer them the chance to be present, to avoid any suggestion of interfering with the evidence. Court permission or prosecution consent is not required, and she must not try to change the witness's evidence (Code para 2.2)."},
 {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"Felix acts for a claimant in a road traffic claim. The claimant's witness, Bella, is very nervous about giving evidence. Felix proposes to meet her to explain what happens in court and what cross-examination involves. He also plans to go through her statement with her and suggest \"better\" ways of describing the speed of the defendant's car.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Felix's proposal?","o":["He may do everything he proposes, because preparing a nervous witness is in his client's best interests.","He must not meet Bella at all before the trial, because any contact with a witness amounts to coaching.","He may explain the procedure and cross-examination, but must not suggest how she should describe the facts.","He may suggest different wording for the speed, provided that Bella genuinely agrees that the new wording is accurate.","He may do everything he proposes, provided that the meeting is recorded and disclosed to the defendant."],"a":2,"e":"A solicitor may **prepare** a witness by explaining the procedure, the layout of the court and what cross-examination involves. Suggesting what the witness should say, or rehearsing \"better\" evidence, is **coaching** and seeks to influence the substance of the evidence (Code para 2.2). Recording the session or getting her agreement does not make coaching acceptable."},
 {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"Archie is charged with theft from his employer. In his first interview with his solicitor, Kemi, he said he was not at work that day. At a later conference he says he did go into work briefly but did not take anything. He has never told Kemi that he committed the theft. Kemi suspects he is lying.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Kemi's position?","o":["She may continue to act and present his defence, after questioning him about the change in his account.","She must withdraw, because the change in his account shows that he is guilty of the theft.","She must tell the court that her client has given her two inconsistent accounts of his movements.","She may continue to act, but only by putting the prosecution to proof and not calling Archie to give evidence.","She must withdraw unless Archie signs a statement confirming that his latest account is true."],"a":0,"e":"**Inconsistent instructions are not an admission of guilt.** Kemi may question Archie about the change, but she would only be knowingly misleading the court if he told her his defence was false. Deciding guilt is for the court, not the solicitor. The limits that apply when a client admits guilt (put the prosecution to proof, no positive defence) do not apply here, and confidentiality prevents her telling the court about the earlier account."},
 {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"Priya acts for the claimant in a personal injury claim. Her client's witness statement, verified by a statement of truth and already served and filed, says he can no longer play football. Priya then sees recent photographs on the client's social media of him playing in a football match. When she raises it, he admits the statement is untrue but refuses to let her correct it.\n\nWhat should Priya do?","o":["Continue to act, because the false statement was made by her client and not by her personally.","Tell the court and the defendant that the witness statement is untrue, because her duty to the court comes first.","Cease to act, without telling the court or the defendant why, because she cannot continue without misleading the court.","Continue to act but avoid referring to the football point at the trial, so that she does not herself repeat the falsehood to the court.","Continue to act, provided she advises the client in writing about the risks of contempt of court."],"a":2,"e":"Once she knows the court has been misled, Priya must ask the client for consent to correct the position; if he refuses, continuing would make her complicit (Code para 1.4), so she must **withdraw**. **Confidentiality** (para 6.3) means she must not reveal the reason to the court or the defendant. Staying on and simply avoiding the point would still be relying on evidence she knows is false."},
 {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"Dean appears before the magistrates on a charge of shoplifting. When the legal adviser asks for his name and date of birth, Dean gives his brother's details, because he has several previous convictions in his own name. His solicitor, Rosa, knows this. Dean tells Rosa to say nothing.\n\nWhat should Rosa do?","o":["Advise Dean that he must correct his details and, if he refuses, withdraw from the case.","Continue to act, because she has no duty to tell the court about her client's previous convictions.","Continue to act, because it was Dean, not Rosa, who gave the false details to the court.","Immediately tell the court Dean's true identity, because her duty to the court overrides confidentiality.","Continue to act, but refuse to make any submissions about Dean's character or background."],"a":0,"e":"There is no duty to reveal previous convictions, but giving a **false name or date of birth** actively misleads the court, and continuing would make Rosa complicit (Code para 1.4). She must advise Dean to correct it and, if he refuses, **withdraw**. Confidentiality prevents her from revealing the truth to the court herself."},
 {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"Halima withdrew from a criminal case after her client insisted on giving evidence that she knew to be false. She told the court only that she was withdrawing for professional reasons. The client has now instructed a new firm, whose solicitor telephones Halima to ask why she stopped acting.\n\nWhat should Halima tell the new solicitor?","o":["The full reason, because solicitors owe each other a duty of candour when a client changes representation.","The full reason, because the new solicitor needs to know so that the court is not misled again.","Only that the client admitted guilt to her, so that the new solicitor can decide whether to act.","The full reason, but only orally and on the understanding that it will not be repeated to anyone else.","Nothing about the reason, unless the client consents, because she still owes the client confidentiality."],"a":4,"e":"The duty of **confidentiality** (Code para 6.3) continues after the retainer ends, so Halima must not disclose what the client told her without consent. The new solicitor will form their own view based on what the client tells them. There is no general duty of candour between solicitors that overrides confidentiality, and an informal or partial disclosure is still a breach."},
 {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"Corinne acts for a defendant in a contract dispute. While preparing for trial, her client tells her about a former employee, Sam, who saw the events and would give evidence strongly supporting the claimant. The claimant's solicitors do not know about Sam, and Sam is not mentioned in any document that must be disclosed.\n\nWhat must Corinne do about Sam?","o":["Tell the claimant's solicitors about Sam, because she must not allow the court to be misled.","Tell the court about Sam, because she must draw the court's attention to all relevant evidence, whether helpful or not.","Call Sam as a witness, because she must not prevent relevant evidence from being given.","Nothing: she need not reveal an unhelpful witness, provided she does not mislead the court about the facts.","Withdraw from the case, because continuing while knowing of Sam would mislead the court."],"a":3,"e":"The duty under Code para 2.7 is to draw the court's attention to relevant **law** and procedural irregularities, not to volunteer damaging **facts** or witnesses. Silence about a fact the other side has not discovered is not misleading, as long as Corinne makes no positive misleading statement (for example, asserting that no one else saw the events). Withdrawing or disclosing would be unnecessary and would breach her duties to her client."},
 {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"Saul acts for a tenant in a possession claim. He has identified an argument that the landlord's notice was invalid. The point is supported by one first-instance decision but most practitioners think it will fail. His client wants him to run it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Saul may advance the argument?","o":["He must not advance it, because he may only put forward arguments he believes are likely to succeed.","He must not advance it, because doing so would waste the court's time when the argument is weak.","He may advance it only if he first tells the court that he personally believes it will fail.","He may advance it only if the landlord's solicitors agree that the point can be raised at trial.","He may advance it, because an argument he thinks will probably lose may still be properly arguable."],"a":4,"e":"Code para 2.4 allows a solicitor to make submissions that are **properly arguable**. An argument with genuine legal support is properly arguable even if it will probably lose. What he must not do is make factual assertions he knows are false or run points with no proper basis. He need not tell the court his personal view, and the opponent's agreement is irrelevant."},
 {"t":"duty-to-court","q":"Wendy acts for a company in a commercial claim. During disclosure, the other side disclosed internal emails showing that one of its directors had been negotiating with a rival business. Wendy's client wants to send the emails to a trade journalist to embarrass the other side. The emails have not been read out in court.\n\nWhat should Wendy advise?","o":["The client may pass on the documents, because once disclosed they belong to the receiving party.","The client may pass on the documents, provided it first gives the other side seven days' notice.","The client may pass on the documents, because the journalist is not a party to the proceedings.","The documents may be used only for the purposes of the proceedings, so passing them on could be a contempt of court.","The client may pass on the documents, but only if the journalist first agrees in writing not to publish the directors' names."],"a":3,"e":"Documents obtained on **disclosure** may be used only for the purpose of the proceedings in which they were disclosed (unless they have been read in open court, or the court or owner permits). Misusing them for another purpose can be a **contempt of court**, and Code para 2.5 requires Wendy not to place herself in contempt or assist her client to do so. Notice or conditions on publication do not make the misuse lawful."},
 {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"Mrs Elkins, aged 84, instructs Noah to make a new will leaving her entire estate to an animal sanctuary, cutting out her three children. She explains clearly what she owns, who her children are and why she is disappointed with them. Her eldest son telephones Noah and says the will \"proves she has lost her mind\".\n\nWhich of the following best describes Noah's position?","o":["He must not act until a doctor has confirmed in writing that Mrs Elkins has the mental capacity needed to make a valid will.","He must not act, because leaving everything away from close family suggests that she lacks capacity.","He may act only if the children are told about the will and given a chance to object to its terms.","He may act if satisfied she has capacity, because an unwise or unusual decision does not show a lack of capacity.","He must not act, because her son has raised a doubt and capacity is presumed absent once doubted."],"a":3,"e":"Under the Mental Capacity Act 2005 an adult is **presumed to have capacity**, and a person is not to be treated as lacking capacity merely because they make an **unwise decision** (s.1). Mrs Elkins understands her assets, her family and her reasons. A medical opinion is good practice where there is real doubt, but a relative's objection alone does not displace the presumption, and Noah cannot discuss her will with the children without her consent."},
 {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"Mr Okoro, a long-standing client, has had a stroke. His speech is badly affected, but he can understand what is said to him and can answer questions by writing short notes and pointing to a letter board. He wants to give instructions to sell some shares. His solicitor, Tess, finds the meeting slow and difficult.\n\nWhat should Tess do?","o":["Use all practicable means to help him, such as writing and the letter board, before judging capacity.","Conclude that he lacks capacity, because he cannot communicate his decision by speaking.","Take instructions from his wife instead, because she can explain his wishes more quickly.","Decline to act until he has recovered his speech, because instructions must be given orally.","Apply to the Court of Protection for a deputy, because he cannot give instructions in the normal way."],"a":0,"e":"A person must not be treated as unable to make a decision unless **all practicable steps** to help them have been taken without success (Mental Capacity Act 2005 s.1). Communication **by any means** counts, including writing and a letter board (s.3), and the duty to take account of his needs (Code para 3.4) points the same way. His wife has no authority to instruct for him, and a deputy is needed only where a person lacks capacity."},
 {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"Ffion is acting for Mr Barton in the sale of his flat. Before contracts are exchanged, Mr Barton suffers a severe brain injury and his doctors confirm that he now lacks capacity to manage his property and affairs. He never made a power of attorney. His daughter tells Ffion to carry on with the sale.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Ffion's position?","o":["She may continue on the daughter's instructions, because the daughter is his closest relative.","Her authority to act has ended, so she needs instructions from someone with legal authority, such as a deputy.","She may continue, because Mr Barton gave clear instructions to sell before he lost capacity.","She may continue if she is satisfied that completing the sale is in Mr Barton's best interests.","She may continue on the daughter's instructions, provided the daughter agrees in writing to be responsible for her fees."],"a":1,"e":"The retainer depends on a client able to give instructions, so if a client **loses capacity** and there is no attorney or deputy, the solicitor's authority to act **ends**. A relative has no automatic right to instruct, and earlier instructions or a belief about best interests do not give Ffion authority. An application to the **Court of Protection** for a deputy (or other order) would be needed."},
 {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"Mrs Price has advanced dementia and now lacks capacity to manage her finances. Three years ago, while she had capacity, she signed a lasting power of attorney for property and financial affairs appointing her son, Dylan. It has never been registered. Dylan instructs a solicitor to sell her house to pay for her care.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the solicitor may act on Dylan's instructions?","o":["Not yet, because the power must first be registered with the Office of the Public Guardian.","Yes, because the power was validly signed while Mrs Price still had capacity to make it.","Yes, because a lasting power of attorney takes effect automatically once the donor loses capacity.","No, because a lasting power of attorney can never be used to sell the donor's home.","Yes, provided Dylan confirms in writing that the sale is in his mother's best interests."],"a":0,"e":"A **lasting power of attorney** must be **registered** with the **Office of the Public Guardian** before it can be used, so the solicitor cannot act on Dylan's instructions yet. Registration can still be applied for after the donor loses capacity. A property and financial affairs attorney can sell the donor's home within the scope of the power once it is registered."},
 {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"Vale Solicitors acts for Brook Ltd. An email arrives from the company's finance manager, whom the firm has never dealt with, instructing it to sell the company's warehouse urgently and to pay the proceeds to a new bank account. The firm's usual contact is the managing director.\n\nWhat should Vale Solicitors do before acting?","o":["Act on the email, because the finance manager is a senior employee of the company and so has authority to instruct the firm.","Act on the email, but only after the finance manager confirms the instructions by telephone.","Refuse to act, because only a company's shareholders can instruct a solicitor to sell its property.","Check that the instructions genuinely come from the company and that the finance manager is authorised to give them.","Act on the email, because the firm's client is the individual who gives the instructions."],"a":3,"e":"The client is the **company**, which acts through people it authorises (Code para 8.1). Under para 3.1 a solicitor acts only on instructions from the client or someone properly authorised, and must satisfy themselves where there is reason to suspect instructions do not reflect the client's wishes. An unfamiliar sender, urgency and a new bank account are warning signs, so the firm should verify authority with the company itself (for example, the board or managing director), not just with the sender."},
 {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"Leo qualified as a solicitor two years ago and practises residential conveyancing at a two-partner firm. A friend asks him to advise her company on a complex cross-border tax restructuring. No one at the firm has tax experience, and Leo has never done this work. He thinks he could \"read up on it\".\n\nWhat should Leo do?","o":["Accept the work, because a solicitor with a current practising certificate is entitled to carry out any type of legal work.","Accept the work, provided he warns the company in writing that he has no experience of tax law.","Decline or refer the work elsewhere, unless he can obtain the expertise and supervision needed to do it competently.","Accept the work, provided he charges a reduced fee to reflect his inexperience in this area.","Accept the work, because the company's consent to him acting removes any competence concern."],"a":2,"e":"Code para 3.2 requires a **competent** and timely service, so a solicitor must not take on work he cannot do properly. The right course is to get the necessary expertise and supervision, refer the client, or decline. A warning, the client's consent or a discount does not make an incompetent service acceptable."},
 {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"Gareth, a partner, delegates the preparation of a client's lease to an experienced paralegal and does not review the draft. The lease contains a serious error and the client complains. Gareth says the paralegal is responsible because she drafted it.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Gareth's position?","o":["He is not accountable, because the paralegal was experienced and the error was hers.","He remains accountable for the work and should have supervised it effectively.","He is not accountable, because only the firm, not an individual partner, is responsible for staff work.","He is accountable only if the paralegal was not competent to carry out the work delegated.","He is not accountable, because the paralegal is not regulated by the SRA and so bears no duty."],"a":1,"e":"Under Code para 3.5, a solicitor who supervises or manages others remains **accountable** for the work carried out through them and must **effectively supervise** it. Delegating the work does not delegate the responsibility, however experienced the paralegal. The firm also has duties, but they sit alongside Gareth's personal duty."},
 {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"Mr Doherty, who has a learning disability, instructs Sunita about a dispute with his landlord. He finds long letters full of legal terms very hard to follow and has told her so. Sunita's standard practice is to send detailed written advice in technical language.\n\nWhat does the Code require of Sunita?","o":["She may continue her standard practice, because all clients must be treated in exactly the same way, whatever their personal circumstances.","She must decline to act, because he cannot fully understand legal advice given in writing.","She must take instructions from a relative or carer, who can explain the advice to him.","She must obtain a medical report confirming his capacity before giving him any further advice.","She must take account of his needs, for example by explaining advice in plain language and in a format he can follow."],"a":4,"e":"Code para 3.4 requires solicitors to consider and take account of the client's **attributes, needs and circumstances**, including making **reasonable adjustments**, and para 8.6 requires information to be given in a way the client can understand. Treating everyone identically is not what equality requires. A learning disability is not in itself a reason to doubt capacity or to take instructions from someone else."},
 {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"Ines, a solicitor, is defending Mr Lyle in a criminal case. Mr Lyle's employer has agreed to pay her fees. The employer's HR director telephones Ines and asks what Mr Lyle has told her about the incident and how she rates his chances.\n\nWhat should Ines do?","o":["Answer the questions, because the employer is paying the fees and so is entitled to updates.","Answer the questions, but only about her view of his prospects, not what Mr Lyle told her.","Answer the questions, because the employer is treated as a joint client when it pays the fees.","Stop acting, because a third party paying the fees creates a conflict of interest that cannot be cured.","Decline to discuss the matter unless Mr Lyle consents, because the employer is not her client."],"a":4,"e":"Paying the fees does **not** make the employer the client. Ines owes Mr Lyle **confidentiality** (Code para 6.3), so she may not discuss his affairs, including her view of his prospects, without his consent, and she must act in his interests only. A third-party funder is common and does not of itself create a conflict requiring her to stop acting."},
 {"t":"instructions-capacity-service","q":"Mr Hale ends his retainer with Burns & Co in the middle of a court case and instructs a new firm. He owes Burns & Co £6,000 in unpaid fees. A hearing is listed in ten days and the new firm needs the file urgently. Burns & Co says it will keep the file until it is paid.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The firm has no right to keep any of the papers, because the client may end the retainer at any time.","The firm may keep the papers until it is paid in full, whatever the effect on the court case.","It generally has a lien, but should not use it where this would seriously prejudice the client's case.","The firm must hand over the papers only if Mr Hale first pays half of the outstanding fees.","The firm may keep the papers, because Mr Hale was not entitled to end the retainer before the case finished."],"a":2,"e":"A client may end the retainer **at any time**, but remains liable for work done. The firm generally has a **lien** (a right to keep the papers until the bill is paid), but it should not be used where it would **seriously prejudice** the client or obstruct court proceedings, and the court can order the papers to be handed over on terms (for example, the new firm undertaking to protect the fees). There is no rule requiring part payment first."},
 {"t":"client-money","q":"Pryce Solicitors receives £2,000 from a new client, Ms Yates, \"on account of costs\" for her divorce. No work has yet been done and no bill has been sent. The practice manager wants to pay the money into the firm's business account to help with this month's rent and \"sort it out when we bill\".\n\nWhat should the firm do with the £2,000?","o":["Pay it into business account, because money paid towards the firm's own costs is always firm money.","Pay it promptly into client account, because money received for costs before a bill is client money.","Pay it into business account, provided the firm records it on Ms Yates's ledger as a payment on account.","Pay it into client account, but move it to business account as soon as any work is done on the matter.","Pay half into each account, because it is a mixed payment of client money and firm money."],"a":1,"e":"Money received for the firm's **costs before a bill or other written notification of costs** has been given is **client money** (Accounts Rules rule 2.1(d)). It must be paid promptly into client account and kept separate from firm money. It can only be transferred to business account once a bill or written notification has been given, not simply because work has started."},
 {"t":"client-money","q":"Ash & Co sent Mr Neville a bill for £1,200 (its fees of £1,000 plus VAT) on completion of his lease. The bill includes no disbursements. A week later Mr Neville pays the full £1,200.\n\nHow should the firm deal with the payment?","o":["Treat it as firm money and pay it into business account, because it pays a bill already delivered for the firm's fees.","Treat it as client money and pay it into client account, because it was received from a client.","Treat it as client money until the firm has paid the VAT element to HMRC.","Pay it into client account and transfer it to business account only after Mr Neville has confirmed in writing that he agrees the bill.","Treat the VAT as client money and the fees as firm money, splitting the payment between the two accounts."],"a":0,"e":"A payment of a bill that has **already been delivered** for the firm's own fees is **firm money**, so it belongs in business account. Client money must be kept separate from firm money, so paying it into client account would be wrong. VAT on the firm's fees is part of the firm's money (the firm accounts to HMRC for it), and no written agreement of the bill is needed."},
 {"t":"client-money","q":"Seren arranges a legal expenses insurance policy for her client, Mr Rhodes, in connection with his claim. The insurer pays Seren's firm a commission of £300 for introducing the business. Mr Rhodes has not been told about the commission.\n\nWhat must the firm do?","o":["Keep the £300, because it was paid by the insurer and not out of Mr Rhodes's own money.","Keep the £300, provided the commission is mentioned to Mr Rhodes on his final bill once the matter has ended.","Pay the £300 to the SRA Compensation Fund, because a solicitor may never keep a commission.","Account to Mr Rhodes for the £300, unless he agrees, with proper information, that the firm may keep it.","Keep the £300, because commissions under £500 do not need to be disclosed to the client."],"a":3,"e":"Code para 4.1 requires solicitors to **properly account** to clients for any **financial benefit** received as a result of their instructions, **unless the client has agreed otherwise**. The commission arose from Mr Rhodes's matter, so it belongs to him unless he agrees (having been properly informed) that the firm keeps it. Mentioning it after the event is not agreement. There is no small-sum exception, and the Compensation Fund has nothing to do with it."},
 {"t":"client-money","q":"Clara is a freelance solicitor who practises on her own account and is not an authorised firm. A new client asks her to hold a £25,000 deposit for a share purchase in a bank account until completion. Clara proposes to open a separate personal account for the deposit.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Clara may hold the deposit?","o":["She may, provided the account is in her name and kept separate from her own money.","She may, provided the client gives informed consent in writing to the money being held in a separate personal account.","She may, provided she obtains an annual accountant's report on the account she uses.","She may, because the Accounts Rules apply only to firms and not to individual solicitors.","She may not, because a freelance solicitor cannot hold client money of this kind or operate a client account."],"a":4,"e":"Code para 4.3 says a solicitor does not **personally hold client money** except as permitted. A **freelance** solicitor may receive only limited money, such as fees and disbursements paid in advance, and cannot operate a client account. A £25,000 deposit is client money that must be held by an authorised firm (or through a third-party managed account), so separation or client consent does not help."},
 {"t":"client-money","q":"Bell Solicitors acts for Mrs Shah on the purchase of a house. Her ledger shows a nil balance. A search fee of £350 must be paid today, and Mrs Shah's money will not arrive until tomorrow. The firm's client account holds over £500,000 for other clients.\n\nHow should the search fee be paid today?","o":["From client account, because the total balance in the account is more than enough to cover it.","From client account, provided the money is replaced as soon as Mrs Shah's funds arrive.","From client account, provided Mrs Shah authorises the payment in writing before it is made.","From client account, using money held for another client who has agreed to the short-term loan.","From the firm's business account, because there are no funds held for Mrs Shah in client account."],"a":4,"e":"A firm may withdraw client money for a client only if it holds **sufficient funds for that specific client**. Using other clients' money would create a **shortfall** on their ledgers, even briefly. If the payment must be made today, the firm can pay it from its own business account and recover it from Mrs Shah later. Her authority, or replacing the money later, does not allow the use of other clients' money."},
 {"t":"client-money","q":"Jonas acted for Mr Fry on a house sale, which completed three months ago. All bills and disbursements have been paid, but the firm still holds £20,000 of the sale proceeds. Mr Fry says he \"might buy another property next year\", so Jonas suggests keeping the money in client account for convenience.\n\nWhat should the firm do?","o":["Keep the money, because Mr Fry may instruct the firm on a future purchase.","Keep the money in client account, provided the firm pays Mr Fry a fair sum of interest while it is held.","Transfer the money to business account until Mr Fry gives further instructions.","Return the money to Mr Fry promptly, because there is no longer a proper reason to hold it.","Keep the money for up to one year, after which it must be paid to the SRA."],"a":3,"e":"The Accounts Rules require client money to be **returned promptly** once there is no longer any proper reason to hold it. A possible future purchase is not a current matter for which the money is held, and holding it \"for convenience\" risks using client account as a **banking facility**. Paying interest does not cure this, and client money must never be moved to business account without a proper basis."},
 {"t":"client-money","q":"Kaur & Co is holding £400,000 for Mr Adebayo for four months while a complex property purchase is delayed. The firm has a written interest policy, and Mr Adebayo has not agreed to give up interest. He asks whether he will receive anything for the use of his money.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's obligation?","o":["It must account to him for a fair sum of interest in accordance with its written interest policy.","It need not pay interest, because the money is held only for the purpose of his purchase.","It must pay him the exact interest the bank actually paid the firm on the client account.","It need pay interest only if the money was held in a separate designated deposit account.","It need not pay interest, because interest on client account belongs to the firm."],"a":0,"e":"The Accounts Rules require a firm to account to clients for a **fair sum of interest** on client money held, in line with its **written interest policy**, unless it has agreed otherwise in writing with the client. The rule is a \"fair sum\", not necessarily the exact amount the bank paid, and it applies whether the money is in a general or a designated account."},
 {"t":"client-money","q":"Wilde LLP's new finance director proposes that, to save time, the firm should reconcile its client account bank statements with its cash book and client ledgers once every three months instead of monthly.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the proposal?","o":["It is permitted, provided the firm's COFA approves the change in writing.","It is permitted, because the rules require only an annual reconciliation by the accountant.","It is not permitted, because client accounts must be reconciled at least every five weeks.","It is permitted, provided the firm's accountant's report confirms no breaches during the year.","It is not permitted, because client accounts must be reconciled at the end of every week."],"a":2,"e":"Rule 8.3 of the Accounts Rules requires client accounts to be reconciled **at least every five weeks**, so that the bank statement, the cash book and the client ledgers agree and any shortfall is spotted quickly. Quarterly reconciliation would breach the rule, and neither the COFA nor the accountant's report can authorise a breach. Weekly reconciliation is permitted but not required."},
 {"t":"client-money","q":"Moss Solicitors is a small wills firm. In its last accounting period, the average balance it held in client account was £8,000, and the highest balance on any day was £210,000. None of its client money came from the Legal Aid Agency.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm must obtain an accountant's report for that period?","o":["It must obtain a report, because every firm holding client money must have an annual report.","It need not obtain a report, because its average and maximum client balances are within the exemption limits.","It must obtain a report, because the exemption applies only to firms whose client money is paid entirely by the Legal Aid Agency.","It must obtain a report, because its highest balance exceeded £10,000 on at least one day.","It need not obtain a report, because firms with fewer than ten staff are always exempt."],"a":1,"e":"A firm need not obtain an accountant's report if its client money **average balance** for the period did not exceed **£10,000** and its **maximum balance** did not exceed **£250,000** (or all its client money came from the Legal Aid Agency). Moss Solicitors meets both limits. The £10,000 limit is for the average, not the highest balance, and there is no exemption based on staff numbers."},
 {"t":"client-money","q":"Bea, a partner at Tidy LLP, discovers that £15,000 was transferred from client account to the firm's business account a month ago to pay the firm's costs on a matter where no bill had been sent to the client. The client has not complained.\n\nWhat must the firm do?","o":["Do nothing until the client complains, because the firm is owed costs on the matter.","Send the client a bill now, which will retrospectively justify the transfer.","Replace the £15,000 in client account immediately from its own money and correct the breach.","Ask the client to agree in writing that the firm may keep the £15,000 already transferred to business account.","Report the matter to the client's bank, which will reverse the transfer if appropriate."],"a":2,"e":"Costs may be taken from client money only after a **bill or other written notification of costs** has been given, so this was an improper withdrawal. Breaches must be **corrected promptly**, and money improperly withdrawn must be **replaced immediately** from the firm's own money. A later bill or client agreement does not cure the breach, and the COFA should consider whether it is serious enough to report to the SRA."},
 {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"A claims management company offers to refer road traffic accident personal injury clients to Barnes Solicitors in return for £250 per client. The company says each client will be told about the payment in writing and asked to consent to it.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Barnes Solicitors?","o":["It may agree, provided each client is told about the payment and consents in writing.","It must not agree, because personal injury referral fees are banned by statute despite consent.","It may agree, provided the arrangement is set out in a written fee-sharing agreement.","It may agree, provided its advice to each client remains independent of the company.","It may agree, provided the payment is described as a marketing fee rather than a referral fee."],"a":1,"e":"The **Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012 (s.56)** bans paying or receiving referral fees in **personal injury** (and death) claims. Client consent cannot cure a statutory ban. Relabelling the payment as a marketing fee will not help either, because the SRA treats a payment as a referral fee unless shown otherwise (Code para 5.2)."},
 {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"Daisy is a partner at a firm that writes wills. She agrees orally with a local accountant that, for every client he refers who makes a will, the firm will pay him 15% of its fee. Daisy does not plan to mention the arrangement to clients because \"it does not affect the price\".\n\nWhat does the Code require?","o":["Nothing further, because fee sharing is allowed in wills matters and the price to the client is unaffected.","Only that clients be told about the arrangement; the agreement itself may remain oral.","The fee-sharing agreement must be put in writing, and clients must be told about it and the accountant's interest.","Only that the agreement be put in writing; clients need not be told because they pay no more.","The arrangement must end, because solicitors may not share their fees with non-lawyers."],"a":2,"e":"Fee sharing and referral payments are allowed in matters such as wills, but Code para 5.1 requires that clients are told of any **fee sharing arrangement** and of any financial interest the introducer has in referring them, and that the **fee-sharing agreement is made in writing**. Both requirements apply, whether or not the price is affected."},
 {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"An estate agent refers many buyers to Lucas, a conveyancing solicitor, under a properly disclosed and documented arrangement. The agent tells Lucas that buyers should not be \"scared off\" by survey reports. Lucas's client's survey reveals serious subsidence. The agent asks Lucas not to advise the client to renegotiate, as it might cause the sale to fall through.\n\nWhat should Lucas do?","o":["Follow the agent's request, because the arrangement with the agent has been properly disclosed to the client.","Tell the client about the survey but not advise on it, so as to remain neutral between the client and the agent.","Refer the client back to the agent for advice on the survey, as the agent arranged the transaction.","Give the client independent advice on the survey, including renegotiating or withdrawing if appropriate.","Follow the agent's request unless the client specifically asks him what he thinks about renegotiating."],"a":3,"e":"An introducer arrangement must never compromise the solicitor's **independence** (Principle 3) or the duty to act in the client's **best interests** (Principle 7). Disclosure of the arrangement does not allow the introducer to shape the advice. Lucas must give full, independent advice on the survey even if it is unwelcome to the agent."},
 {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"Nadia is a solicitor at an SRA-authorised firm. She also owns a financial planning business that is not regulated by the SRA or any legal regulator. She would like to refer her probate clients to the financial planning business for investment advice.\n\nWhat must Nadia ensure before making such a referral?","o":["The client is told that the business is part of her firm and covered by the firm's insurance.","The financial planning business pays a referral fee to her firm, which is disclosed to the client in writing before the referral.","Nothing, because a solicitor may refer clients to any business that she personally owns.","The client gives informed consent, understanding among other things that the business is not regulated by the SRA.","The SRA has approved the business as a separate business before any referral is made."],"a":3,"e":"Under Code para 5.3 a solicitor may refer a client to a **separate business** only with the client's **informed consent**. That means the client must understand, among other things, that the business is **not regulated by the SRA**, so the SRA protections (such as the Compensation Fund and compulsory insurance) do not apply. She must never present it as SRA-regulated (para 8.10), and there is no SRA approval process for separate businesses."},
 {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"Lomax LLP pays a website operator £100 for each person who instructs it on a residential conveyancing matter after clicking through from the website. The firm describes the payment as a \"marketing fee\" and does not tell clients about it, saying it is not a referral fee.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the SRA's likely approach?","o":["It will accept the firm's description, because only payments that are expressly described as referral fees are caught by the Code.","It will treat the payment as unlawful, because referral fees are banned in conveyancing matters.","It will treat the payment as a referral fee only if the website operator is regulated by the SRA.","It will accept the firm's description, provided the payment is less than the firm's fee for the work.","It will treat the payment as a referral fee unless the firm shows otherwise, so clients should be told about it."],"a":4,"e":"Under Code para 5.2, where it appears a solicitor has made or received a referral fee, the payment is **treated as a referral fee unless the solicitor shows otherwise**. A per-client payment for introductions looks like a referral fee, whatever its label. Referral fees are lawful in conveyancing, but clients must be told about them (para 5.1)."},
 {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"An introducer offers to send will-writing clients to Rowan Solicitors. Rowan's partner discovers that the introducer obtains clients by cold-calling elderly homeowners at home and offering \"free will checks\". The introducer is not regulated by the SRA.\n\nWhat is the best advice to Rowan Solicitors?","o":["It may accept the referrals, because the introducer is not regulated by the SRA and its methods are its own affair.","It may accept the referrals, provided the clients are told about the introducer's methods.","It must not accept referrals obtained in this way, because a solicitor could not lawfully make such approaches.","It may accept the referrals, because the ban on unsolicited approaches does not apply to will-writing.","It may accept the referrals, provided no referral fee is paid to the introducer for them."],"a":2,"e":"Code para 5.1(e) requires that a client referred by an introducer has not been acquired in a way that would breach the SRA's rules if the introducer were regulated by the SRA. **Cold calling** members of the public is an **unsolicited approach** that a solicitor could not make (para 8.9), so the firm cannot use the introducer to get round the rule. That is so whether or not a fee is paid or clients are told."},
 {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"Hollis Solicitors has launched a new lasting powers of attorney service. It proposes to write individually to all its former private clients from the last five years, telling them about the service and its fees.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the proposal?","o":["It is not permitted, because any unsolicited letter to an individual member of the public is an unsolicited approach.","It is permitted only for clients whose matters were closed within the last twelve months.","It is not permitted unless each former client has previously agreed to receive marketing.","It is permitted only if the letter is sent by email rather than through the post.","It is permitted, because the rule against unsolicited approaches does not apply to current or former clients."],"a":4,"e":"Code para 8.9 bans **unsolicited approaches** to members of the public to advertise legal services, but it expressly **excepts current and former clients**. So the firm may write to its former clients, provided the letter is accurate and not misleading (para 8.8). The Code sets no twelve-month limit, though the firm must still comply with data protection and marketing laws."},
 {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"A firm opening a new branch arranges for a leaflet advertising its wills and conveyancing services to be delivered to every home in the surrounding postcode area. The leaflet is accurate.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the leaflet drop?","o":["It is permitted, because general advertising to every home is not a targeted personal approach.","It is not permitted, because delivering leaflets to homes is an unsolicited approach to the public.","It is permitted only if each household has been a client of the firm in the past.","It is not permitted unless the leaflets are addressed to named individuals living at each home.","It is permitted only if the firm also publishes the same information on its website."],"a":0,"e":"The ban in Code para 8.9 is aimed at **targeted personal approaches** such as cold calling, door-stepping or approaching people at hospitals or accident scenes. **General advertising**, such as leaflets delivered to every home in an area, online or TV advertisements, is not caught, as long as it is accurate and not misleading (para 8.8). Addressing leaflets to named individuals would make the approach more personal, not less."},
 {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"Quinn Solicitors advertises on its website: \"House purchase: all-inclusive fixed fee £499.\" In fact the £499 excludes VAT, search fees and Land Registry fees, which typically add over £600. This is explained only in the terms of business sent after instruction.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the advertisement?","o":["It is acceptable, because the full costs are set out in the terms of business before work begins.","It breaches the Code, because publicity about charges must be accurate and not misleading.","It is acceptable, because disbursements are paid to third parties and need not be mentioned in advertising.","It breaches the Code only if a client complains that the true cost was higher than expected.","It is acceptable, because the firm's own fee for the work is in fact £499."],"a":1,"e":"Code para 8.8 requires all **publicity** to be **accurate and not misleading**, including publicity about **charges**. Describing a price as \"all-inclusive\" when VAT and disbursements are extra is misleading, and later disclosure in the terms of business does not cure it. A breach does not depend on a complaint."},
 {"t":"business-referrals-publicity","q":"Lewis, a solicitor, wants to set up a will-writing business that will not be authorised by the SRA or any other legal regulator. He plans to call it \"Lewis Solicitors Will Services\" and to be its sole director.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Lewis's plan?","o":["He must not, because a solicitor cannot manage an unauthorised business with \"solicitors\" in its name.","He may use the name, because will-writing is not a reserved legal activity.","He may use the name, provided the business carries professional indemnity insurance.","He may use the name, because he is a solicitor and so is entitled to describe himself as one.","He may use the name, provided he tells clients that the business is not regulated by the SRA."],"a":0,"e":"Code para 5.4 says a solicitor must not be a manager, employee, member or interest holder of a business whose name includes the word \"**solicitors**\", or that describes its work as done by solicitors, unless it is an **authorised body**. It is true that will-writing is not reserved, so he can run an unregulated will-writing business, but not under that name. Telling clients it is unregulated does not cure a misleading name."},
 {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"Mr Byrne is selling his house to Ms Lowry, a stranger he met through an estate agent. The price has been agreed but other terms, such as the completion date and what fixtures are included, have not. To save costs, both ask Cooper Solicitors to act for them.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may act for both?","o":["It must not act for both, because a buyer and seller have opposing interests and no exception applies.","It may act for both, because the price has been agreed and so there is no conflict between them.","It may act for both, provided both give informed consent in writing to the firm acting.","It may act for both under the substantially common interest exception, because both want the sale to happen.","It may act for both, provided separate solicitors in the firm act for each of them."],"a":0,"e":"A buyer and seller in the same transaction have **opposing interests** (terms, timing, price), so there is a **client conflict** (Code para 6.2). Wanting the sale to happen is not a **substantially common interest** in how it is achieved, particularly where terms remain to be negotiated, and the clients are not \"competing for the same objective\". Without an exception, consent and separate fee-earners cannot allow the firm to act."},
 {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"Jade acts for Mr Ellis on his house purchase. His mortgage lender, a high street bank, asks the firm to act for it too, on its standard mortgage terms, with instructions limited to the usual requirements of the UK Finance Mortgage Lenders' Handbook.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether Jade may act for both?","o":["She must not act for both, because a borrower and lender always have opposing interests.","She may act for both only under the competing for the same objective exception.","She may act for both only if she first obtains the SRA's permission to do so.","She may act for both, because a standard residential mortgage on usual terms rarely creates a conflict.","She must not act for both unless the mortgage terms have been individually negotiated between Mr Ellis and the bank."],"a":3,"e":"Acting for a **borrower and lender** on a standard residential mortgage is usually permitted, because the lender's terms are standard and the instructions are limited to the usual requirements, so their interests do not significantly conflict. A conflict is more likely if terms are **individually negotiated**, which is the opposite of the last option. No SRA permission is needed, and the competing-objective exception is irrelevant."},
 {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"Leila acts for Mr Grant on a house purchase and for his lender on the mortgage. Mr Grant mentions that the seller has secretly agreed to pay him £15,000 \"cashback\" after completion, so the real price is lower than the price stated to the lender. He tells Leila not to mention it to the lender.\n\nWhat should Leila do?","o":["Continue to act for both, because the cashback is a private matter between the buyer and the seller.","Continue to act for both, but report the cashback to the lender because her duty to the lender comes first.","Stop acting for the lender, and probably Mr Grant, because she cannot tell the lender without his consent.","Continue to act for the lender only, and tell it about the cashback without Mr Grant's consent.","Continue to act for both, provided Mr Grant confirms in writing that the cashback will be repaid."],"a":2,"e":"The cashback is information **material** to the lender (para 6.4), but it is **confidential** to Mr Grant (para 6.3), so a conflict has arisen and Leila cannot properly act for the lender without telling it. Since she cannot reveal it without consent, she must stop acting for the lender, and probably for Mr Grant as well. She should also consider whether this is mortgage fraud, which raises money laundering reporting issues."},
 {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"Ian, a solicitor, is asked by a client to bring a substantial damages claim against Bluewater plc. Ian and his wife together own a large shareholding in Bluewater plc, worth about half their savings. The client knows this and says she does not mind.\n\nWhat should Ian do?","o":["Decline to act, because his financial interest creates an own interest conflict that consent cannot cure.","Act for the client, because she has given informed consent knowing all about the size of the shareholding.","Act for the client, provided her consent is given or evidenced in writing.","Act for the client, provided he sells his own shares but his wife keeps hers.","Act for the client, provided an information barrier is set up between him and his wife."],"a":0,"e":"An **own interest conflict** arises where a solicitor's duty to a client conflicts, or there is a significant risk it may conflict, with the solicitor's own interests, including those of people close to him (such as his wife). Ian's significant shareholding in the opponent gives him an interest in the outcome. Under Code para 6.1 there are **no exceptions**, so the client's consent, written or not, cannot cure it."},
 {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"Mrs Patel, a wealthy client, has just received damages after a claim that Marcus handled. Knowing that Marcus wants to buy a house, she offers to lend him £60,000 at a low rate of interest, and asks him to draw up the loan agreement for her.\n\nWhat should Marcus do?","o":["Act on the loan, provided the interest rate is at least as good as a bank's rate.","Act on the loan, provided Mrs Patel gives informed consent in writing to him acting.","Act on the loan, but ask a colleague in his firm to draft the agreement instead.","Decline, because it is an own interest conflict, and advise her to take independent advice.","Act on the loan, because he is the borrower and the agreement is in his favour."],"a":3,"e":"A client wanting to **lend money to the solicitor** creates an **own interest conflict**: his interest in favourable terms conflicts with his duty to advise her. Para 6.1 has no exceptions, so consent does not help. A conflict for one solicitor is usually a conflict for the whole firm, so passing it to a colleague would not solve it. Mrs Patel should take independent advice."},
 {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"Hart LLP acts for Orion Ltd. Orion Ltd's board has asked the firm to prepare a new service agreement for its managing director, Sophia, including her salary, bonus and notice period. Sophia asks the firm to act for her personally on the same agreement as well.\n\nWhat should the firm do?","o":["Act for both, because Sophia is the managing director and so her interests are the same as the company's.","Act for both under the substantially common interest exception, with their written consent.","Act for Orion Ltd only and advise Sophia to take separate advice, because their interests in the terms conflict.","Act for Sophia only, because a company acts through its directors and she can instruct for both.","Act for both, provided a different partner deals with each client's instructions."],"a":2,"e":"On a director's service agreement the company wants the best terms for itself and the director wants the best terms for herself, so there is a **client conflict** (Code para 6.2). Their interests in the terms are not substantially common, so no exception applies. The firm's client is Orion Ltd, and Sophia should be advised to take **separate advice**."},
 {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"Mr Moss, aged 90, wants to transfer his house to his daughter, Kate, who is his main carer. Kate has made all the arrangements and asks Ward Solicitors to act for both of them to save money. In a short meeting, Mr Moss seems uncertain about the consequences and keeps looking to Kate before answering.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may act for both?","o":["It may act for both, because family members transferring property have a substantially common interest.","It should not act for both, because his vulnerability makes it unreasonable to do so.","It may act for both, provided both Mr Moss and Kate give informed consent in writing.","It may act for both, provided the firm sees Mr Moss alone for part of the meeting.","It may act for Kate only, because she is the person who gave the firm its instructions."],"a":1,"e":"Even where a substantially common interest might apply, the firm may act for both only if it is satisfied it is **reasonable** to do so (Code para 6.2(iii)). Here Mr Moss is elderly, uncertain and apparently dependent on Kate, who benefits from the transfer, so there is a real risk of **undue influence** and he needs separate, independent advice. Consent does not make acting for both reasonable, and Kate is not the one whose instructions matter most."},
 {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"Two long-standing clients of Pike & Co, Alder Ltd and Birch Ltd, are both tendering for the same contract to supply a local council. Both want Pike & Co to prepare their tenders. Alder Ltd agrees in writing to the firm acting for both. Birch Ltd refuses, saying it wants exclusive advice.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may act for both?","o":["It may act for both, because the clients are competing for the same objective.","It may act for both, provided it sets up effective information barriers between the two teams.","It may act for both, because one client's written consent is enough if the firm acts reasonably.","It may act for both, because tendering for a public contract never creates a conflict of interest between two bidders.","It must not act for both, because every client must give written informed consent for the exception to apply."],"a":4,"e":"Tendering for the same contract is \"**competing for the same objective**\" (Code para 6.2(b)), but the firm may only act if **all** the conditions are met: written informed consent from **all** the clients, effective safeguards where appropriate, and it being reasonable to act. Birch Ltd has refused, so the firm cannot act for both, however good its information barriers."},
 {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"Mrs Jenkins asks Alan, a solicitor, to prepare a new will leaving £40,000 to Alan's son, Tom, who has helped her with her garden for several years. Alan suggests she takes independent advice about the gift, but she refuses, saying she wants Alan to do everything.\n\nWhat should Alan do?","o":["Prepare the will, because the gift is to his son and not to Alan himself.","Prepare the will, provided Mrs Jenkins confirms in writing that she does not want independent advice.","Prepare the will, provided Tom is not appointed as an executor or witness.","Prepare the will, because Tom earned the gift by helping her over several years.","Decline to prepare the will, because a significant gift to his son creates an own interest conflict."],"a":4,"e":"A solicitor's own interests include those of **people close to him**, so a significant gift by will to his son creates an **own interest conflict**. The client should take **independent advice** about the gift and, if she refuses, Alan should **not act** in preparing the will (Code para 6.1). Written confirmation or careful drafting does not cure the conflict, and the reason for the gift does not matter."},
 {"t":"conflicts-of-interest","q":"Kerr Solicitors acts for Mr Rees in a long-running dispute with his landlord about service charges. Mr Rees's landlord, Mrs Fox, now asks the firm to act for her on the purchase of a holiday cottage in another county, which has nothing to do with Mr Rees or his flat. The firm holds no confidential information about Mrs Fox.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may act for Mrs Fox?","o":["It must not act, because it cannot act for anyone on the other side of a current client's dispute.","It may act, because the matters are unrelated and it holds no relevant confidential information.","It may act only with the written informed consent of both Mr Rees and Mrs Fox.","It may act only under the substantially common interest exception, with safeguards in place.","It must not act, because Mrs Fox would be a party to a matter in which Mr Rees is a client."],"a":1,"e":"A **client conflict** under Code para 6.2 arises only where the duties to two clients conflict in the **same or a related matter**. A cottage purchase in another county is unrelated to the service charge dispute, and the firm holds no confidential information about Mrs Fox that is material to Mr Rees, so para 6.5 is not engaged either. The firm should still keep the position under review and consider any reputational or relationship concerns."},
 {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"A well-known footballer instructed Gale & Co in a dispute that settled without any publicity. A journalist telephones one of the firm's solicitors, Erin, and asks: \"Can you at least confirm that he is a client of yours?\"\n\nWhat should Erin do?","o":["Confirm it, because the fact that someone is a client is not confidential information.","Confirm it, provided she gives no details about the dispute or the settlement.","Confirm it, because the dispute has now been settled and the retainer has ended.","Deny it, because that is the only way to protect the client's confidentiality.","Decline to confirm or deny it, because the fact that someone is a client is itself confidential."],"a":4,"e":"The duty of confidentiality (Code para 6.3) covers **all** information about a client's affairs, including the **fact that the person is a client**. It continues after the retainer ends. Erin should neither confirm nor deny, because a false denial would be misleading (para 1.4) and confirmation would breach confidentiality."},
 {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"Ms Akhtar was a client of Davies Solicitors for many years before she died. Her nephew, who expected to inherit but did not, asks the firm to send him copies of her file notes about her will instructions. Her executor, her sister, has not been asked and has not consented. No court order has been made.\n\nWhat should the firm do?","o":["Decline unless the executor consents or the law requires it, as confidentiality survives death.","Send the copies, because the duty of confidentiality ends when the client dies.","Send the copies, because the nephew was a close relative of the client.","Send the copies, but remove any information about other members of the family.","Decline permanently, because no one can ever see a deceased client's confidential file."],"a":0,"e":"The duty of confidentiality continues after the client's **death**, and the right to waive it passes to the **personal representatives** (here the executor). Without the executor's consent or a legal requirement (such as a court order), the firm must not disclose. It is not a permanent bar, because the executor can consent or a court can order disclosure."},
 {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"Linden & Co acts for a company buying a warehouse. There is no dispute and none is expected. Through its solicitors, the company commissions a surveyor's report on the building's condition. A year later the company sues the seller and the seller seeks disclosure of the survey report.\n\nIs the report likely to be protected by legal professional privilege?","o":["No, because it is a communication with a third party made when no litigation was reasonably in prospect.","Yes, because it was obtained through the company's solicitors and so attracts legal advice privilege.","Yes, because all documents in a solicitor's file are confidential and so are privileged from disclosure.","Yes, because litigation later followed and the report is now relevant to that litigation.","No, because legal professional privilege never applies to a company, only to individuals."],"a":0,"e":"**Legal advice privilege** protects confidential communications between **lawyer and client** for the purpose of legal advice; it does not cover a third party's report. **Litigation privilege** covers communications with third parties only where litigation was **in progress or reasonably in prospect** and the dominant purpose was that litigation. Neither applies here. Confidentiality is wider than privilege but can be overridden by a disclosure obligation, and companies can claim privilege."},
 {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"Mr Kirby is facing large debts. He asks his solicitor, Zara, how he could transfer his house to his brother for £1, with a secret understanding that his brother will transfer it back once his creditors have given up. Zara refuses to help. Mr Kirby's creditors later bring proceedings and seek disclosure of his communications with Zara.\n\nAre those communications likely to be privileged?","o":["Yes, because communications seeking legal advice from a solicitor are always privileged.","Yes, because Zara refused to help, so no fraud was committed using her advice.","No, because legal professional privilege does not apply to advice about property transactions.","No, because there is no privilege in communications made to further a fraud.","Yes, because only Mr Kirby can waive privilege and he has not done so."],"a":3,"e":"Under the **crime/fraud exception**, there is no legal professional privilege in communications made for the purpose of furthering a crime or fraud, such as a sham transfer to defeat creditors. It is the client's purpose that matters, so Zara's refusal does not restore privilege. Privilege is otherwise very strong and can be waived only by the client, which is why the other options are tempting."},
 {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"Nina acts for Mrs Ford, who is buying a business. Nina also acts for a bank in an unrelated matter and, from that work, knows in confidence that the business Mrs Ford is buying is about to lose its main customer. This would affect what Mrs Ford is willing to pay. The bank will not consent to disclosure.\n\nWhat should Nina do?","o":["Tell Mrs Ford, because the duty of disclosure to a current client overrides the duty of confidentiality.","Continue acting for Mrs Ford without telling her, because the information came from a different matter.","Not disclose the information, and stop acting for Mrs Ford unless an exception to the duty of disclosure applies.","Tell Mrs Ford only that she should look carefully at the business's customer contracts.","Tell the bank that Mrs Ford is buying the business, so that it can decide what to do."],"a":2,"e":"Nina owes the bank **confidentiality** (para 6.3) and owes Mrs Ford a duty to disclose all **material** information she knows (para 6.4). Confidentiality wins, so she cannot tell Mrs Ford, even by hinting, and she cannot continue without telling her. She must therefore stop acting for Mrs Ford unless an exception applies (for example, Mrs Ford gives written informed consent to not being told). Telling the bank about Mrs Ford would breach Mrs Ford's confidentiality."},
 {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"Five years ago, Pemberton LLP acted for Dart Ltd on a property purchase. The file was closed long ago. Now a new client wants the firm to bring an unrelated employment claim against Dart Ltd. The firm holds no confidential information about Dart Ltd that is relevant to the employment claim. Dart Ltd objects.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may act?","o":["It must not act, because a firm can never act against a former client without its consent.","It must not act, because Dart Ltd has objected and its objection must be respected.","It may act only if it sets up an information barrier and Dart Ltd consents in writing.","It may act only if five years have passed since the end of the previous retainer.","It may act, because it holds no confidential information about Dart Ltd relevant to the claim."],"a":4,"e":"Code para 6.5 prevents acting against a current or former client only where the firm holds **confidential information** of that client that is **material** to the new matter. Here the earlier work is unrelated and the firm holds no relevant information, so Dart Ltd's objection does not prevent it acting. There is no time limit in the rule; what matters is the information."},
 {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"Grange LLP is a large national firm. Its London corporate team acted for Hollow plc on a merger last year and holds material confidential information about it. A new client wants the firm's Manchester litigation team to sue Hollow plc. The firm has a permanent, established information barrier system: the teams share no staff, IT access or supervisors and work in different cities. Hollow plc refuses consent.\n\nWhich of the following best describes whether the firm may act?","o":["It must not act, because Hollow plc has refused consent and consent is always required.","It may act if the measures in place mean there is no real risk of disclosure of Hollow plc's information.","It may act only under the substantially common interest exception, with written consent.","It must not act, because a firm can never act against a former client in litigation.","It may act, because Hollow plc's confidential information is held in a different office in a different city from the litigation team."],"a":1,"e":"Under Code para 6.5 a firm holding material confidential information may act against a former client if **effective measures** mean there is **no real risk of disclosure**, **or** the former client gives written informed consent. A permanent, established barrier in a large firm with separate staff, systems and supervisors is the kind most likely to succeed, although the burden of showing no real risk is heavy. Geography helps but is not enough on its own, which is why the last option is not the best answer."},
 {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"Tariq acts for Mr Webb on a property purchase. Tariq suspects that the purchase money is the proceeds of crime and has made a suspicious activity report to the National Crime Agency. Mr Webb rings to ask why completion has been delayed and whether \"anything is wrong\".\n\nWhat should Tariq tell Mr Webb?","o":["About the report, because a solicitor must tell the client all information that is material to the matter he is handling.","Nothing about the report, because the duty of disclosure does not apply where disclosure is prohibited by law.","About the report, provided he also explains that he is under a legal duty to make such reports.","That the delay is because the seller's solicitors have not yet replied to his enquiries.","About the report, but only once completion has taken place and the transaction is finished."],"a":1,"e":"The duty to disclose material information (Code para 6.4) does not apply where disclosure is **prohibited by legal restrictions** imposed in the interests of preventing crime. Telling Mr Webb about the report could be the offence of **tipping off** (POCA s.333A) if it is likely to prejudice an investigation. Tariq must not lie, though, so inventing a false reason would breach para 1.4; he should say nothing about the report and seek guidance from his MLRO (money laundering reporting officer)."},
 {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"Helen acts for Mr Carr in a clinical negligence claim. A medical report shows that he has a much shorter life expectancy than he believes. The doctor who wrote the report warns Helen in writing that Mr Carr is very fragile and that learning this now is likely to cause him serious psychological harm.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Helen's duty?","o":["She must tell him at once, because she must make the client aware of all material information.","She must tell his family instead, so that they can decide whether and when to tell him.","She may withhold the information only if Mr Carr has agreed in writing not to receive it.","She may withhold the information, because she has reason to believe disclosure will cause him serious mental injury.","She must withdraw from acting, because she cannot continue to act without telling her client all material information."],"a":3,"e":"The duty of disclosure in Code para 6.4 does not apply where the solicitor has **reason to believe** that **serious physical or mental injury** will be caused to the client or another if the information is disclosed (para 6.4(c)). The doctor's written warning gives Helen that reason. She must not tell the family, which would breach confidentiality, and written consent is a different exception (para 6.4(b)) that is not needed here."},
 {"t":"confidentiality-disclosure","q":"Police officers investigating a fraud visit Lake Solicitors and ask informally to see the file of a client, Mr Dunn. They have no warrant, court order or production order, and Mr Dunn has not consented. The officers say it would be \"helpful\" and \"in everyone's interests\".\n\nWhat should the firm do?","o":["Hand over the file, because a solicitor must cooperate with the police in a criminal investigation.","Hand over the file, but remove any documents that are protected by legal professional privilege.","Decline to hand over the file, because there is no client consent and no legal requirement to disclose it.","Hand over the file, provided the officers confirm in writing that it will be kept confidential.","Hand over the file, because Mr Dunn's confidentiality is outweighed by the public interest in fraud prevention."],"a":2,"e":"Confidentiality may be overridden only by the client's **consent** or where disclosure is **required or permitted by law** (Code para 6.3), for example under a warrant or production order. An informal police request is neither. The firm should decline, while considering separately whether it has any money laundering reporting obligation; removing privileged documents or asking for confidentiality does not make voluntary disclosure lawful."},
 {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"Adele, a solicitor, is charged with assault after an argument outside a bar on a Saturday night. She intends to plead not guilty and thinks she will be acquitted, so she does not want to tell anyone until the trial is over.\n\nWhat does the Code require Adele to do?","o":["Nothing until the trial, because she must notify the SRA only if she is convicted.","Notify the SRA promptly that she has been charged, whatever the likely outcome.","Nothing, because the conduct happened outside work and has nothing to do with her practice.","Tell her firm's partners, but notify the SRA only if the firm decides that it should be told.","Notify the SRA only if the charge involves dishonesty or the misuse of client money."],"a":1,"e":"Code para 7.6 requires a solicitor to notify the SRA **promptly** if they are subject to any **criminal charge**, conviction or caution (subject to the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974). The duty arises on charge, not just on conviction, and applies to conduct outside work. It is not limited to dishonesty offences."},
 {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"Gideon, a solicitor, has run into serious personal debt. He has just entered into an individual voluntary arrangement (IVA) with his creditors. He has told no one at work because it is a private financial matter and his practice is unaffected.\n\nWhat must Gideon do?","o":["Notify the SRA promptly, because an IVA is a relevant insolvency event.","Nothing, because his personal finances are a private matter unconnected with his practice.","Notify the SRA only if he is made bankrupt, because an IVA avoids bankruptcy.","Tell his firm's COFA, who will decide whether the SRA needs to know.","Notify the SRA when he next renews his practising certificate."],"a":0,"e":"Under Code para 7.6 a solicitor must notify the SRA **promptly** if a **relevant insolvency event** occurs in relation to them, which includes an **individual voluntary arrangement**, as well as bankruptcy and debt relief orders. Waiting for renewal is not prompt. He could satisfy the duty through his firm's compliance officer only if he expects them to pass it on, not by leaving the decision to them."},
 {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"Lucia, an associate, finds clear evidence that her firm's COLP, Peter, has been taking money from the client account of a deceased client's estate. Peter is also the managing partner.\n\nWhat should Lucia do?","o":["Report the matter to Peter, because reporting to the firm's COLP always satisfies her own duty to report.","Ask Peter to repay the money and report to the SRA only if he refuses to do so.","Investigate further to establish exactly how much was taken before reporting anything.","Tell the deceased client's family, who are the people with an interest in the estate.","Report the matter promptly and directly to the SRA, because she cannot expect Peter to report himself."],"a":4,"e":"Taking client money is dishonest and therefore a **serious breach** that must be reported promptly (Code para 7.7). Reporting to the COLP satisfies the duty only if she can expect the COLP to pass it on (para 7.12), which she cannot when the COLP is the wrongdoer, so she must report **directly** to the SRA. She need not investigate fully first, and repayment does not remove the duty."},
 {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"Nathan, a solicitor, notices that a payment of £45 was posted to the wrong client ledger in his firm's client account. The error was a simple keying mistake. It was spotted and corrected the same day, and no client suffered any loss.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what must be done?","o":["Report the breach to the SRA immediately, because every breach of the Accounts Rules must be reported, however minor.","Report the breach to the SRA, because any error involving client money is a serious breach.","Tell the client whose ledger was affected and ask for written consent to the correction.","Do nothing at all, because breaches that are corrected the same day are not breaches.","Record the breach in the firm's records; a report to the SRA is usually not needed for a minor, corrected breach."],"a":4,"e":"Only facts **capable of amounting to a serious breach** must be reported (Code para 7.7). A minor, isolated, inadvertent breach that is put right promptly with no loss is usually not serious, but it is still a breach and the firm must **record** it. The firm's COFA should be aware of it and would watch for any pattern."},
 {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"Ruth acts for a claimant in a civil claim. She discovers that the defendant's solicitor, Mark, at another firm, has served a witness statement he knew was fabricated, because he drafted it himself after the witness refused to sign a true version.\n\nWhat should Ruth do?","o":["Do nothing, because her duty to report applies only to people in her own firm.","Raise the matter only with the court at the trial, which is the proper place to deal with it.","Report Mark's conduct promptly to the SRA, because the duty covers anyone regulated by the SRA.","Write to Mark and give him the chance to withdraw the statement before reporting him.","Tell her client and leave it to the client to decide whether to complain to the SRA."],"a":2,"e":"Code para 7.7 requires solicitors to report promptly facts they reasonably believe are capable of amounting to a **serious breach** by **any person regulated by the SRA**, including solicitors at other firms. Fabricating evidence is dishonest and very serious. The duty is Ruth's own; she should not delay by giving Mark the chance to withdraw or leave it to her client, and she must also deal properly with the evidence in the case."},
 {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"Owen, a solicitor, instructs a barrister in a commercial case. During the hearing Owen hears the barrister tell the judge that a key document \"has never been seen by my client\", when Owen knows the client received and read it. The barrister refuses to correct the statement.\n\nTo whom should Owen report the barrister's conduct?","o":["To the SRA, because the conduct occurred in a case in which Owen is the solicitor.","To the Legal Ombudsman, because it deals with complaints about all authorised lawyers.","To the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal, which hears allegations against all advocates.","To the Bar Standards Board, because it is the regulator of barristers.","To no one, because a solicitor has no duty to report misconduct by a barrister."],"a":3,"e":"The reporting duty in Code para 7.7 is to report to the SRA, **or another approved regulator as appropriate**, serious breaches by persons regulated by them. Barristers are regulated by the **Bar Standards Board**, so that is the right body. The Legal Ombudsman deals with service complaints by clients, not misconduct. Owen must also consider his own duty not to be complicit in misleading the court."},
 {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"Bethan, a junior solicitor, comes across a file note that appears to have been altered after the event by her supervising partner, so that it records advice the partner did not give at the time. She is not certain what happened and feels she should look into it thoroughly over the next few months before saying anything.\n\nWhat should Bethan do?","o":["Investigate fully first, because she must not report a partner unless she is sure of the facts.","Ask the partner to explain the alteration, and report only if the explanation is unsatisfactory.","Report promptly, because she need not be sure a serious breach occurred before the SRA should be told.","Do nothing, because a file note is an internal document that is not shown to the court or client.","Wait until the client complains, because only then will it be clear whether a breach has occurred."],"a":2,"e":"The duty in Code para 7.7 is triggered by facts she **reasonably believes are capable of amounting to** a serious breach, and para 7.8 requires her to inform the SRA of matters it should know about so that it can **investigate**. Altering a file note to record advice not given suggests dishonesty. She should report promptly (directly or via the COLP if confident they will pass it on) rather than conduct her own lengthy investigation or confront the partner."},
 {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"Cerys discovers that a fellow solicitor at her firm, Liam, took £3,000 from client account to pay a personal debt. When she confronts him, he says he has already put the money back and that no client lost anything. He begs her not to tell anyone.\n\nWhat should Cerys do?","o":["Do nothing, because the money has been repaid and no client has suffered any loss.","Report it only if Liam does the same thing again, because a single repaid breach need not be reported.","Tell the client whose money was taken, and leave it to that client to report the matter.","Report it promptly, because the conduct is dishonest and repayment does not remove the duty to report.","Ask the COFA to confirm that the money has been repaid, and then close the matter."],"a":3,"e":"Taking client money for personal use is **dishonest** and so always a **serious breach** that must be reported promptly to the SRA (Code para 7.7), directly or via the COLP or COFA if she is confident they will report it. Putting the money back does not remove the duty. Concealing it to protect a colleague would itself be a breach."},
 {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"The SRA writes to Dominic, a solicitor, asking him to provide within 14 days copies of documents from a client file as part of an investigation into his firm. Dominic is very busy and also thinks the documents are confidential to the client, so he plans to ignore the request for a few months.\n\nWhat should Dominic do?","o":["Ignore the request, because client confidentiality means he cannot give client documents to the SRA.","Respond promptly with full and accurate information and documents, because he must cooperate with the SRA.","Respond when he has more time, because the SRA's deadlines are guidance rather than requirements.","Ask the client for permission and refuse to provide the documents if the client says no.","Provide only a summary of the documents, so as to protect the client's confidential information."],"a":1,"e":"Code paras 7.3 and 7.4 require solicitors to **cooperate** with the SRA and to **respond promptly**, providing full and accurate explanations, information and documents. The SRA has statutory powers to require documents, so disclosure to it is **required by law** and does not breach confidentiality. A summary would not be a full response."},
 {"t":"cooperation-reporting","q":"Imogen acts for Mr Shaw on his house purchase. After completion she realises she submitted the Land Registry application with a minor error in the property description. The Land Registry will correct it free of charge within a few days, and Mr Shaw has suffered no loss and will suffer none.\n\nWhat should Imogen do?","o":["Tell Mr Shaw openly what happened and put it right; she may continue acting if no conflict with her own interests remains.","Correct the error without telling Mr Shaw, because he has suffered no loss and the error is minor.","Stop acting immediately and tell Mr Shaw to instruct another firm, because any mistake by a solicitor creates an own interest conflict.","Tell Mr Shaw what happened and ask him to sign a waiver of any claim before she corrects it.","Report herself to the SRA, because any error by a solicitor must be reported as a serious breach."],"a":0,"e":"Code para 7.11 requires solicitors to be **honest and open** with clients when things go wrong and to **put matters right**. A mistake creates an **own interest conflict** only if there is a significant risk that her interest in avoiding a claim conflicts with the client's interests; where the error is corrected and causes no loss, she may continue acting. Asking for a waiver would be improper, and a minor corrected error is not a serious breach to report."},
 {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"Coastal Haulage Ltd employs 50 people and has an annual turnover of £6 million. It is unhappy with the service it received from its solicitors on a property lease and, after receiving the firm's final response, wants to complain to the Legal Ombudsman.\n\nCan the Legal Ombudsman deal with the complaint?","o":["Yes, because any client of an authorised lawyer may complain about poor service.","Yes, because the company has already received the firm's final response.","No, because the Legal Ombudsman deals only with complaints about litigation.","No, because the company is too large to be an eligible complainant.","Yes, but only if the company limits its claim to £50,000 or less."],"a":3,"e":"Only certain complainants are **eligible**, including individuals and **micro-enterprises** (fewer than 10 employees and turnover or balance sheet of no more than €2 million). A company with 50 staff and £6 million turnover is too large. It could still pursue the firm in the courts or complain to the SRA about any misconduct."},
 {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"Mr Hughes runs a plumbing business as a limited company with six employees and an annual turnover of about €900,000. The company's solicitors handled a commercial lease badly. The company complained to the firm, received a final response two months ago and now wants to go to the Legal Ombudsman.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the company's position?","o":["It is not eligible, because only individuals, not companies, can complain to the Legal Ombudsman.","It is not eligible, because the complaint is about business advice rather than a personal matter.","It is eligible only if Mr Hughes complains personally rather than through the company.","It is not eligible, because the Legal Ombudsman only deals with complaints within 8 weeks of the final response.","It is eligible to complain, because it is a micro-enterprise with fewer than 10 employees and a small turnover."],"a":4,"e":"A **micro-enterprise**, meaning a business with **fewer than 10 employees** and turnover or balance sheet of no more than **€2 million**, is an eligible complainant, even if it is a company. The company also brought its complaint within six months of the final response. The client is the company, so it should complain in its own name."},
 {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"Mrs Lane is selling her house. She is very unhappy with the buyer's solicitor, who has been slow and rude in correspondence with her own solicitor, causing the sale to be delayed by two months. She wants to complain to the Legal Ombudsman about the buyer's solicitor.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mrs Lane's position?","o":["She cannot complain to the Legal Ombudsman about the buyer's solicitor, because she was not that solicitor's client.","She can complain to the Legal Ombudsman, because the delay has caused her a financial loss.","She can complain to the Legal Ombudsman, but only after complaining to the buyer's solicitor first.","She can complain to the Legal Ombudsman, because it deals with poor service by any authorised lawyer, whoever their client was.","She cannot complain to anyone, because the buyer's solicitor owes duties only to its own client."],"a":0,"e":"The Legal Ombudsman deals with complaints from people who received, or asked for, the lawyer's services; someone complaining about the **other side's** solicitor is not eligible. Mrs Lane can raise the issue with her own solicitor, and if she believes there has been **misconduct** (for example, taking unfair advantage) she can report it to the **SRA**, so it is wrong to say she can complain to no one."},
 {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"Ms Darby complained to her solicitors about delay in her divorce. On 1 February 2026 the firm sent its final response, which clearly explained her right to go to the Legal Ombudsman and the six-month time limit. She took no further action until 15 September 2026, when she contacted the Legal Ombudsman. There are no exceptional circumstances.\n\nWhat is the Legal Ombudsman most likely to do?","o":["Accept the complaint, because she brought it within one year of the act complained of.","Accept the complaint, because the six-month limit runs from the date of the act complained of.","Accept the complaint, because the time limits apply only to complaints about money.","Decline to accept the complaint, because it was not brought within six months of the final response.","Refer the complaint back to the firm to issue a new final response with a fresh time limit."],"a":3,"e":"A complaint must reach the Legal Ombudsman within **six months of the firm's final response**, provided that response told the client about the Ombudsman and the six-month limit. Six months from 1 February 2026 ended on 1 August 2026, so 15 September is too late. Being within the one-year outer limit does not help, because **both** time limits must be met."},
 {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"Mr Kwame's solicitor made an error in his lease on 10 October 2025, which he discovered on 1 November 2025. He complained to the firm on 10 November 2025. The firm's final response, sent on 1 December 2025, rejected the complaint but did not mention the Legal Ombudsman or any time limit. Mr Kwame contacted the Legal Ombudsman on 20 July 2026.\n\nIs his complaint likely to be within time?","o":["No, because more than six months have passed since the firm sent its final response.","Yes, because the six-month limit never started and he is within one year of the error.","No, because a complaint must be made within eight weeks of the firm's final response.","Yes, because there is no time limit where the firm rejected the complaint in full.","No, because he should have gone to the Legal Ombudsman as soon as he discovered the error."],"a":1,"e":"The six-month limit runs from the final response only if that response told the client about the Legal Ombudsman and the six-month limit, which this one did not. He must still meet the outer limit of **one year from the act or omission** (or from when he should reasonably have known), and 20 July 2026 is within a year of the 10 October 2025 error. Eight weeks is the period after which a client may go to the Ombudsman if the firm has not resolved the complaint, not a deadline."},
 {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"Miss Ahmed's probate solicitor delayed the administration of her late father's estate for over a year without explanation and did not return her calls. She has not lost any money as a result, but has been caused great stress and inconvenience. Her complaint is properly before the Legal Ombudsman.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what the Legal Ombudsman can do?","o":["Nothing, because she has not suffered any financial loss as a result of the delay.","Direct the firm to pay her compensation for distress and inconvenience, among other remedies.","Strike the solicitor off the roll for failing to provide a proper service.","Fine the firm up to £25,000 for its failure to deal with the estate promptly.","Order the firm to pay compensation only if a court would find it negligent."],"a":1,"e":"The Legal Ombudsman can direct a firm to apologise, refund or reduce fees, put things right and pay **compensation**, including for **distress and inconvenience**, up to a total of £50,000. It decides what is **fair and reasonable**, not only what a court would award for negligence. It cannot strike off or fine; those are disciplinary powers of the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal and the SRA."},
 {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"The Legal Ombudsman has issued a final decision on Mr Pope's complaint about his solicitors, directing them to reduce their fees by £1,500. Mr Pope believes he deserves much more and rejects the decision. He wants to sue the firm in negligence for £30,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mr Pope's position?","o":["He may not sue, because the Legal Ombudsman's decision is binding once it has been made.","He may not sue, because complaining to the Legal Ombudsman removes the right to go to court.","He may sue, because a decision rejected by the complainant is not binding on him.","He may sue, but only to enforce the £1,500 reduction directed by the Legal Ombudsman.","He may sue only if he first appeals the Legal Ombudsman's decision to the High Court."],"a":2,"e":"An Ombudsman's final decision becomes binding (and enforceable through the courts) only if the complainant **accepts** it, and he then cannot sue over the same matter. If the complainant **rejects** it, it is not binding and the complainant remains free to bring court proceedings. There is no appeal to the High Court from the decision."},
 {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"Tate & Co's terms of business say that clients who wish to make a formal complaint must pay an administration fee of £150, which will be refunded if the complaint is upheld. The firm says this discourages trivial complaints.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the firm's policy?","o":["It breaches the Code, because complaints must be dealt with free of charge.","It is permitted, because the fee is refunded if the complaint is upheld.","It is permitted, provided the fee is set out in writing at the start of the matter.","It is permitted, because the Code allows firms to recover reasonable administrative costs.","It breaches the Code only if the fee exceeds the firm's actual cost of handling a complaint."],"a":0,"e":"Code para 8.5 requires complaints to be dealt with **promptly, fairly and free of charge**. A fee for making a complaint, even if refundable, breaches that requirement and could deter clients from complaining. Setting it out in the terms of business does not make it acceptable."},
 {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"At the start of Ms Grey's boundary dispute, her solicitor estimated total costs to trial at £8,000 plus VAT. Six months later the neighbour instructed experts and made several applications, and the solicitor now realises costs are likely to reach about £20,000. He decides to tell Ms Grey only when he sends the final bill.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the solicitor's duty?","o":["He need do nothing, because his original estimate was given in good faith at the time.","He need do nothing, because a costs estimate is not binding and the client must pay the actual costs.","He must cap his fees at £8,000 plus VAT, because the client relied on his original estimate.","He need only tell her if the final costs exceed his estimate by more than 50%.","He must update Ms Grey promptly on the likely overall cost, so she can make informed decisions."],"a":4,"e":"Code para 8.7 requires solicitors to give clients the best possible information about the **likely overall cost**, both at the outset and **as the matter progresses**. When an estimate becomes too low, the client must be told promptly so that she can decide whether to continue, settle or change approach (para 8.6). The Code does not automatically cap fees at the estimate, and there is no 50% tolerance."},
 {"t":"complaints-legal-ombudsman","q":"Mr Lin complains that his solicitor took months to deal with simple matters on his house sale and did not return calls. He also believes the solicitor lied to him about having sent documents to the buyer's solicitor, when in fact they had not been sent. He wants both matters investigated.\n\nWhich of the following best describes where these complaints should go?","o":["Both matters to the Legal Ombudsman, which deals with all complaints about solicitors.","Both matters to the SRA, which deals with all complaints about solicitors' work.","The poor service to the Legal Ombudsman after the firm's procedure, and the alleged dishonesty to the SRA.","Both matters to the Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal, which hears all complaints from clients.","The poor service to the SRA, and the alleged dishonesty to the Legal Ombudsman."],"a":2,"e":"The **Legal Ombudsman** deals with **poor service** complaints (after the firm's own complaints procedure has had its chance), while the **SRA** deals with **misconduct**, such as dishonesty. The two bodies can refer matters to each other. The Solicitors Disciplinary Tribunal hears serious cases brought by the SRA, not complaints made directly by clients."},
 {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"Fern Solicitors, a partnership, negligently failed to register a client's purchase at the Land Registry in time, and another charge was registered first. The client has lost £120,000. The firm is still trading.\n\nWhere should the client mainly look for compensation?","o":["The SRA Compensation Fund, because the loss was caused by a solicitor's mistake.","A negligence claim against the firm, which its compulsory professional indemnity insurance will meet.","The Legal Ombudsman, which can award compensation for any amount of loss.","The SRA, which can order the firm to compensate a client who has suffered loss.","The Solicitors Indemnity Fund, which pays negligence claims against solicitors' firms that are still trading."],"a":1,"e":"A loss caused by a firm's **negligence** is met by a civil claim against the firm, backed by its compulsory **professional indemnity insurance (PII)**. The Compensation Fund is for **dishonesty** or failure to account where recovery is not possible elsewhere, not negligence. The Legal Ombudsman's remedies are capped at £50,000, the SRA does not award compensation, and the Solicitors Indemnity Fund deals only with claims against closed firms after the six-year run-off period."},
 {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"Three solicitors are setting up a new firm as a limited liability partnership (LLP). They ask what minimum level of professional indemnity insurance cover the SRA requires the firm to hold.\n\nWhich of the following is correct?","o":["£2 million for each and every claim, because that applies to every authorised firm.","£2 million in total for all claims made in any one year.","£3 million for each and every claim, because the firm is an LLP.","£1 million for each and every claim, increasing as the firm grows.","No minimum, because LLP members are personally liable for their own negligence."],"a":2,"e":"Under the SRA's **Minimum Terms and Conditions**, the minimum cover is **£2 million** for each and every claim, rising to **£3 million** for firms that are **LLPs or companies**, because clients generally cannot sue the owners of an incorporated firm personally. The cover is per claim, not an annual aggregate."},
 {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"Moor Solicitors closed three years ago when its sole principal retired. No other firm took over its practice. A former client now discovers that the firm negligently drafted his lease and he has lost £80,000.\n\nWhere should the former client look for compensation?","o":["The firm's professional indemnity insurer, under the six years of run-off cover.","The SRA Compensation Fund, because the firm has closed and cannot pay.","The Solicitors Indemnity Fund, because the firm closed without a successor practice.","The Legal Ombudsman, because complaints about closed firms are handled only by it.","Nowhere, because no claims can be brought against a firm that has closed."],"a":0,"e":"When a firm closes with **no successor practice**, its PII policy provides **six years' run-off cover** for claims made after closure. Three years after closure, the claim falls within that period and should be made against the firm with its insurer meeting it. The Solicitors Indemnity Fund deals with claims arising after the run-off period ends, and the Compensation Fund is not for negligence."},
 {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"Birch & Co closed eight years ago without a successor practice. A former client has only now discovered that the firm made a negligent error in his property purchase, causing him loss.\n\nWhere should he look for compensation?","o":["The firm's professional indemnity insurer, because its run-off cover lasts for ten years.","The SRA Compensation Fund, because the firm no longer has insurance.","The Legal Ombudsman, which can award up to £50,000 against a closed firm.","The Solicitors Indemnity Fund, which handles claims after the run-off period ends.","The firm's former principal personally, because no other protection exists."],"a":3,"e":"Run-off cover lasts **six years**. Claims against closed firms that arise after that period are dealt with by the **Solicitors Indemnity Fund (SIF)**, which the SRA has managed since 1 October 2023. The Compensation Fund is for dishonesty and failure to account, not negligence."},
 {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"Rose Solicitors is a small firm. It wants to appoint its practice manager, Tom, who is an accountant but not a lawyer, as its Compliance Officer for Finance and Administration (COFA), and its senior partner, a solicitor, as its Compliance Officer for Legal Practice (COLP).\n\nWhich of the following best describes the proposal?","o":["It is permitted, provided the SRA approves both appointments, because a COFA need not be a lawyer.","It is not permitted, because both compliance officers must be solicitors.","It is not permitted, because the COLP and COFA must be the same person in a small firm.","It is permitted, and no approval by the SRA is required for either appointment.","It is not permitted, because a partner cannot also act as a compliance officer."],"a":0,"e":"The **COLP** must be a lawyer, but the **COFA** need not be (it is often a finance director or practice manager). Both must be **approved by the SRA**. In a small firm one person may hold both roles, but this is not required. The proposed rule stopping managers in higher-risk firms acting as compliance officers was paused by the SRA and was not in force at the examinable date."},
 {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"The SRA finds that Kemp LLP has repeatedly failed to keep proper records showing compliance with its regulatory obligations. One of the four members, Alice, says she cannot be held responsible because compliance is the COLP's job and she was focused on her own clients.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Alice's position?","o":["She is not responsible, because only the COLP is responsible for the firm's compliance with the rules.","She is not responsible, because an LLP is a separate legal entity and only the firm can be sanctioned.","She is responsible only if she was personally involved in the failures to keep records.","She is responsible only if the COLP reported the failures to her and she took no action.","She is responsible, because managers share joint and several responsibility for compliance."],"a":4,"e":"Under the Code of Conduct for Firms, **managers** (here, LLP members) are **responsible for compliance** by the firm, and this responsibility is **joint and several** where management is shared. The COLP has particular duties, but that does not relieve the other managers of theirs. The firm can also be sanctioned, but that is in addition to individual responsibility."},
 {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"Mr Bell's solicitor dishonestly took £2.5 million from client account when Mr Bell sold his business. The firm has collapsed, the solicitor is bankrupt and there is no other source of recovery. Mr Bell applies to the SRA Compensation Fund, saying he is entitled to be paid the full £2.5 million.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Mr Bell's position?","o":["He is entitled as of right to the full £2.5 million, because the loss was caused by dishonesty.","Any payment is discretionary and is usually limited to a maximum of £2 million per claim.","He is entitled to the full £2.5 million, but only once the firm's insurer has refused to pay.","He can recover nothing, because the Compensation Fund covers only losses under £50,000.","He can recover nothing, because the Compensation Fund does not cover losses on business sales."],"a":1,"e":"The **Compensation Fund** is a **discretionary** fund of last resort: there is no right to a payment. It can cover losses caused by a solicitor's **dishonesty** that cannot be recovered elsewhere, but payments are usually capped at **£2 million** per claim. The £50,000 figure is the Legal Ombudsman's limit, not the Fund's."},
 {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"Two solicitors run a successful firm as a recognised body. A wealthy businessman who is not a lawyer wants to invest in the firm and take a 30% ownership stake and a seat on its management board.\n\nWhich of the following best describes how the firm would need to be authorised?","o":["As a recognised body, because the solicitors would still hold most of the ownership of the firm.","As a recognised sole practice, because only one of the owners would not be a lawyer.","It would need no change in authorisation, because ownership by a minority investor does not affect how a firm is regulated.","As a licensed body (an alternative business structure), because a non-lawyer would have an ownership and management role.","It could not be authorised at all, because non-lawyers can never own any part of a law firm."],"a":3,"e":"A firm in which **non-lawyers** have an ownership or management role must be authorised as a **licensed body**, also called an **alternative business structure (ABS)**, under the Legal Services Act 2007. A recognised body is owned and managed by lawyers. Non-lawyer ownership is lawful in an ABS."},
 {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"Wyatt Solicitors negligently lost a client's personal injury claim. The client's loss is agreed at £90,000. The firm's indemnity policy has an excess of £10,000. The firm tells the client that the insurer will pay £80,000 and that she must pursue the firm separately for the remaining £10,000.\n\nWhich of the following best describes the position?","o":["The client receives £80,000 from the insurer and must sue the firm for the excess.","The client receives £80,000 and must apply to the Compensation Fund for the excess.","The client receives £80,000, because insurers may always deduct the excess from a claim.","The client receives £90,000 only if she agrees not to complain to the SRA.","The client receives the full £90,000, because the excess is a matter for the insurer and the firm."],"a":4,"e":"Under the SRA's **Minimum Terms and Conditions**, the excess must not reduce what the claimant receives: the insurer pays the claim in full and the firm bears the excess, which the insurer can recover from it. The Compensation Fund is for dishonesty, not negligence. Making payment conditional on not complaining to the SRA would breach Code para 7.5."},
 {"t":"firms-compliance-protections","q":"Sian is a solicitor working for an unregulated legal advice business (not authorised by the SRA or any other legal regulator). A client of the business wants Sian to issue county court proceedings and conduct the claim for her.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Sian's position?","o":["She can conduct the litigation, because she is a solicitor with a practising certificate.","She can conduct the litigation, provided the client agrees in writing that the business is unregulated.","She cannot conduct the litigation through the business, because it is a reserved legal activity.","She can conduct the litigation, provided the business has professional indemnity insurance.","She can conduct the litigation, because only rights of audience are reserved legal activities."],"a":2,"e":"Solicitors may work in unregulated businesses, but they cannot carry out **reserved legal activities**, such as **conducting litigation**, rights of audience and reserved conveyancing and probate work, for the public there. She must also make sure clients understand whether and how her services are regulated and which protections apply (Code paras 8.10 and 8.11). Client consent or insurance does not change what is reserved."},
 {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"Grove Ltd is settling a claim by a former sales director who is moving to a competitor. Grove Ltd asks its solicitor, Imran, to draft a settlement agreement with a clause keeping confidential the amount paid and Grove Ltd's customer pricing information. The director is represented by his own solicitor.\n\nWhich of the following best describes Imran's position?","o":["He must refuse to draft any confidentiality clause, because the SRA prohibits non-disclosure agreements.","He may draft a clause protecting the settlement terms and trade secrets, with clear permitted disclosures.","He may draft any confidentiality clause the client wants, because the director has his own solicitor.","He may draft the clause, but only if Grove Ltd first obtains the SRA's approval for its wording.","He must refuse unless the director agrees to the clause before receiving any legal advice."],"a":1,"e":"NDAs are lawful and can legitimately protect **settlement terms** and **trade secrets**. What the SRA warning notice forbids is using or wording an NDA to prevent or deter reporting to regulators or the police, protected disclosures (whistleblowing) or disclosures required by law. Good drafting makes the permitted disclosures clear. Representation on the other side helps, but does not license improper terms, and no SRA approval is needed."},
 {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"Rae acts for an employer settling a discrimination complaint by an employee, Ben. The employer wants a clause requiring Ben to repay the whole settlement if he discusses \"any aspect of his employment\" with anyone, with no exceptions, including his doctor, his own lawyer and his accountant.\n\nWhat should Rae do?","o":["Refuse to draft the clause in that form, because it uses a clawback to deter disclosures that should be permitted.","Draft the clause, because the employer is entitled to protect the confidentiality of its settlement in any way it chooses.","Draft the clause, because Ben can choose not to sign it if he does not like its terms.","Draft the clause, but tell Ben privately that the employer is unlikely to enforce it.","Draft the clause, provided the repayment is limited to half of the settlement sum."],"a":0,"e":"The SRA warning notice says solicitors should not use **clawback** clauses (requiring repayment) or similar terms to deter **permitted disclosures**, such as to the person's own legal, medical or tax advisers, regulators or the police. A clause with no exceptions is also likely to be unenforceable, and the SRA expects solicitors not to include terms they know are unenforceable. Reducing the clawback, or privately telling Ben it will not be enforced, would not cure the problem."},
 {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"Lena acts for a company settling a grievance with a junior employee, Maya, who is not legally represented. The company wants Lena to give Maya the settlement agreement at 4pm and tell her she must sign it by 6pm the same day or the offer will be withdrawn. It also wants Lena to tell Maya there is \"no need to involve a lawyer\".\n\nWhat should Lena do?","o":["Follow the instructions, because a short deadline is a legitimate negotiating tactic.","Follow the instructions, because Maya is not her client and she owes Maya no duties.","Follow the instructions, provided she gives Maya a copy of the agreement to keep.","Refuse to impose the deadline or discourage advice, and ensure Maya has a fair chance to take independent advice.","Refuse to act for the company at all, because settlements with unrepresented employees are prohibited."],"a":3,"e":"The SRA warning notice says solicitors must not apply **undue pressure**, impose **oppressive time limits** or discourage the other party from taking **independent legal advice**. Code para 1.2 forbids taking **unfair advantage** of others, especially unrepresented people. For a valid employment settlement agreement the employee must in any event receive independent advice, so the deal can proceed, just not on those terms."},
 {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"Two years ago, a company's former employee, Sophie, signed a settlement agreement containing a confidentiality clause. She now says she was sexually assaulted by a manager and wants to report it to the police. The company asks its solicitor, Victor, to write warning her that reporting would breach the agreement and that the company will sue to recover the settlement.\n\nWhat should Victor do?","o":["Send the letter, because she freely signed the agreement and must keep to its terms.","Refuse to send that letter, because the clause cannot lawfully stop her reporting a crime to the police.","Send the letter, but make clear that the company will only sue if she also speaks to the press.","Send the letter, provided it also offers her an increased payment for keeping silent.","Refuse to act further, but tell the police himself what Sophie has alleged against the manager."],"a":1,"e":"A solicitor must not use an NDA to prevent or deter someone from **reporting an offence to the police** (SRA warning notice; Code paras 1.2, 1.4 and 7.5). Section 17 of the Victims and Prisoners Act 2024 (which makes such terms **void** for victims of crime) does not help here, because it applies only to agreements made on or after 1 October 2025. But at common law a confidentiality clause cannot validly prevent a person from reporting a crime to the police, whatever its terms. Telling the police himself would breach the company's confidentiality."},
 {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"A businessman asks his solicitor, Paul, to write to a local campaigner who has posted online that the businessman's development will damage a nature reserve. The businessman wants the letter headed \"Without Prejudice\" so that the campaigner \"cannot show it to anyone\". The letter would not make any offer to settle.\n\nWhat should Paul do?","o":["Refuse to use that heading, because \"without prejudice\" applies only to genuine attempts to settle a dispute.","Use the heading, because any letter between parties to a dispute may be marked without prejudice.","Use the heading, because it means the campaigner is legally prevented from publishing the letter.","Use the heading, provided the letter also contains a nominal offer to settle the matter.","Use the heading, but only if the campaigner has a solicitor who will explain what it means."],"a":0,"e":"\"**Without prejudice**\" protects only **genuine attempts to settle** a dispute. Using it on a letter with no settlement offer, to suggest the recipient cannot show or publish it, is misleading, which the SLAPP warning notice identifies as misusing labels (Code para 1.4). Adding a token offer just to justify the label would be equally improper."},
 {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"A newspaper journalist emails Esme's client, a large company, on a Monday at 9am, asking for comment by Thursday on an article about alleged accounting irregularities. The company asks Esme to reply demanding that the journalist provide all her sources and documents within two hours, failing which proceedings will be issued immediately.\n\nWhat should Esme do?","o":["Send the reply, because the company is entitled to protect its reputation from false allegations.","Send the reply, because journalists must disclose their sources to anyone they write about.","Send the reply, provided she copies it to the newspaper's editor and its lawyers.","Decline to act at all, because a solicitor must not act for anyone against a journalist.","Decline, because such a short deadline and excessive demands may be oppressive conduct."],"a":4,"e":"The SLAPP warning notice gives as examples of **oppressive conduct** unreasonably **short deadlines** and **excessive demands** for information, particularly when directed at a journalist's right of reply. Esme can still respond on the company's behalf, identifying any genuine inaccuracies within a reasonable time. Acting against a journalist is not itself improper, so refusing to act at all is the wrong extreme."},
 {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"A national newspaper publishes an investigation into a property developer's finances. The article was written by a freelance journalist with limited means. The developer asks his solicitor, Gary, to threaten a defamation claim against the journalist personally, but not against the newspaper, \"because she'll fold quickly\".\n\nWhich of the following best describes the instruction?","o":["It is acceptable, because a claimant may always choose which of several possible defendants to sue.","It is acceptable, because the journalist wrote the article and so is the only proper defendant.","It is a red flag of improper purpose, because it targets the weaker individual instead of the publisher.","It is acceptable, provided the letter does not mention the journalist's lack of resources.","It is acceptable, provided the developer genuinely believes the article is defamatory."],"a":2,"e":"The SLAPP warning notice lists **targeting an individual journalist rather than the publisher** that is the proper defendant, especially to exploit an imbalance of resources, as a sign of **improper purpose**. Gary must exercise independent judgement (Principle 3) and not simply follow instructions designed to stifle publication. The client's genuine belief does not remove the improper purpose behind the choice of target."},
 {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"A well-known chef tells her solicitor, Henry, that a food blogger has falsely claimed online that her restaurant was closed down for hygiene breaches. She wants a pre-action letter sent today. Henry has not seen the post or checked the restaurant's inspection history.\n\nWhat should Henry do before sending the letter?","o":["Send the letter today, because he can rely entirely on his client's account of the facts.","Send the letter today, but mark it \"private and confidential\" so the blogger cannot publish it.","Refuse to send any letter, because all claims against bloggers are treated as SLAPPs.","Take sufficient instructions and reasonable steps to check that the claim is properly arguable.","Send the letter today, provided he includes the words \"subject to further instructions\"."],"a":3,"e":"The SLAPP warning notice and Code para 2.4 require that a solicitor only puts forward assertions that are **properly arguable**, so before sending a pre-action letter Henry should take sufficient instructions and check the basis of the claim (for example, by reading the post and the inspection records). A genuine claim is not a SLAPP, so refusal is wrong. A \"private and confidential\" label used to suggest the recipient cannot publish would be misleading."},
 {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"A small accountancy firm's website has been the subject of a blog post stating, as fact, that the firm's founder was convicted of fraud. The founder has never been convicted of anything, and the post has caused clients to leave. He asks his solicitor, Amara, to act.\n\nWhich of the following best describes what Amara may do?","o":["Nothing, because any threat of legal action against a blogger is a strategic lawsuit against public participation.","Send a letter threatening the blogger with imprisonment unless the post is removed within 24 hours.","Send a measured letter setting out the false statement and a properly arguable claim, with a fair deadline.","Send a letter only if the blogger is a professional journalist working for a newspaper.","Send a letter only after the founder has reported the blogger to the police for defamation."],"a":2,"e":"Defamation claims are lawful, and protecting a client from a clearly false and damaging statement is legitimate. The SRA's concern is with **meritless, exaggerated or oppressive** threats. A measured letter that sets out a properly arguable claim with a reasonable deadline is appropriate. Threatening imprisonment in a civil claim would be an exaggerated remedy, and defamation is not a crime to report to the police."},
 {"t":"ndas-slapps","q":"Nikhil acts for an investigative journalist. She receives a letter from a solicitor at another firm on behalf of a businessman, threatening an \"immediate injunction, imprisonment and damages of £10 million\" if she does not abandon her article within 12 hours. The letter makes no specific allegation of what in the draft is untrue. Nikhil believes the threats are baseless and designed to intimidate.\n\nWhat should Nikhil consider doing about the other solicitor's conduct?","o":["Nothing, because the other solicitor was only following his client's instructions.","Nothing, because a solicitor's duty to report covers only people in his own firm.","Reporting it to the Legal Ombudsman, which deals with complaints about other firms' letters.","Writing to the businessman directly to warn him that his solicitor is acting improperly.","Reporting it to the SRA, because it may be capable of amounting to a serious breach."],"a":4,"e":"Exaggerated threats (such as imprisonment in a civil claim), vague unparticularised allegations and oppressive deadlines are examples of improper conduct in the SLAPP warning notice. Solicitors must report facts they reasonably believe are capable of amounting to a **serious breach** by anyone the SRA regulates (Code para 7.7). Following instructions is no defence, the Legal Ombudsman deals only with clients' service complaints, and writing to the businessman directly would bypass his solicitor and achieve nothing."}
];

;
